UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassess the toes again in one hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Report at once and check the bandages and traction ropes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a warm compress over the toes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Document the finding and continue routine care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cool, pale toes with delayed capillary refill mean the circulation is being obstructed, most often by a tight bandage, a slipped traction rope or a cast. The obstruction is relieved immediately and the surgeon informed, because untreated ischaemia leads to permanent damage.

Why the other options fail:
• A — An hour of ischaemia is enough to damage muscle and nerve.
• C — Warming the surface does not restore perfusion and hides the change.
• D — This is an emergency, not an observation to record and leave.

Reference: Ghana STG — traction care; neurovascular observation$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell the child off for behaving like a baby"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask for an immediate psychiatric referral"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Recognise it as a stress response to hospitalisation and offer reassurance and supportive play"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict fluids from the afternoon onwards"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Toddlers and preschool children commonly regress under stress, and admission is a major stress. Bedwetting in a previously trained child is a normal, temporary response that improves when the child feels safe, is prepared for procedures and is given play and routine. Option A adds shame to fear and can prolong the regression. Option B medicalises a normal response. Option D is a dehydration risk and does not address the cause.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop feeding from both breasts for two days"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bind the breasts tightly with a firm bandage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict fluids until the engorgement settles"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Feed frequently and express milk if the breast stays full"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Engorgement is milk stasis plus oedema, and the treatment is to keep the milk moving: frequent feeding with good attachment, expressing after feeds if the breast remains hard, and a warm shower before feeding. Analgesia and a cold pack between feeds ease the discomfort.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Stopping feeding worsens stasis and is the commonest route to mastitis.
• B — Binding compresses ducts and causes blocked ducts and abscess.
• C — Fluid restriction does not reduce milk production and dehydrates the mother.

Reference: Ghana STG — breastfeeding; engorgement$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 5-year-old with mild diarrhoea who is alert and drinking"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 3-year-old with severe recession and grunting who remains breathless on high-flow oxygen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 7-year-old waiting for a chest radiograph with a temperature of 37.9°C"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 9-year-old dressed and ready for discharge"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Grunting, severe recession and no response to oxygen is a child in respiratory failure, which is the most time-critical finding in any waiting room and takes precedence over every other patient listed. Option A is mild illness with a protected airway. Option C is stable and awaiting imaging. Option D is well enough for discharge. In prioritisation questions, the child who is failing to respond to treatment outranks the one who has not yet been treated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use the same site every day so the technique is familiar"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aspirate after inserting the needle before injecting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rotate the injection sites systematically so that lumps and unpredictable absorption do not develop"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reuse the needle until it becomes blunt to reduce cost"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Repeated injection into the same spot causes lipohypertrophy, which absorbs insulin unpredictably and produces unexplained highs and lows, so sites are rotated within and between regions. Option A is the direct cause of that problem. Option B is unnecessary for subcutaneous insulin and adds pain. Option D leaves a bent, burred needle that causes pain and inaccurate dosing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rapid intravenous Ringer's lactate at twice the usual rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Large volumes of plain water by mouth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "No fluids until the diarrhoea stops"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral rehydration with ReSoMal under the severe-malnutrition protocol, with close monitoring"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In severe acute malnutrition the myocardium and the sodium-potassium balance are both compromised, so standard rehydration fluids and rates can precipitate heart failure. The specific low-sodium, high-potassium solution is given slowly under the malnutrition protocol with feeding continued and the child watched. Option A is the classic error and can kill. Option B gives no electrolytes and risks hyponatraemia. Option C allows shock to develop.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Aorta to the pulmonary artery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pulmonary artery to the descending aorta"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Right atrium to the left atrium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Pulmonary artery to the descending aorta". In fetal life the lungs are fluid-filled and collapsed, and pulmonary vascular resistance is high, so most blood entering the pulmonary artery is shunted through the ductus arteriosus into the descending aorta and back to the placenta. It is kept open by circulating prostaglandins and by low oxygen tension. After birth, breathing expands the lungs, pulmonary resistance falls and oxygen rises; prostaglandin levels fall and the ductus constricts and becomes the ligamentum arteriosum. Option A reverses the direction, which is what happens after birth if the ductus fails to close, producing a left-to-right shunt and pulmonary overcirculation. Option C describes the foramen ovale. Option D describes the ductus venosus, which bypasses the liver. Knowing which vessel connects which two structures is what the examination is testing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Essential hypertension inherited from the parents"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A normal finding that needs no further assessment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A secondary cause such as renal or cardiovascular disease"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Anxiety about the clinic visit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hypertension in a young child is uncommon and is much more likely to be secondary, with renal parenchymal disease and coarctation of the aorta heading the list, so the child needs urinalysis, renal function and imaging rather than reassurance. Option A describes the usual pattern in adolescents and adults. Option B would miss a treatable cause of significant harm. Option D does not explain persistently raised readings at three visits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pull the pinna up and back before instilling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pull the pinna down and back before instilling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instil the drops with the child sitting upright"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warm the drops in a microwave before use"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In a child under three the external canal runs upwards and forwards, so the pinna is pulled gently down and back to straighten it. The child lies on the opposite side and stays there for a few minutes afterwards.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Up and back is the technique for an adult or an older child, not a toddler.
• C — Sitting allows the drops to run straight out of the canal.
• D — Microwaving heats unevenly and can scald the canal; drops are warmed in the hand.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — paediatric medication administration$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the bruises as a suspected non-accidental injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask for a skeletal survey before anything else"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that these are the usual bruises of a child who is learning to walk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advise the mother to stop the child from standing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bruises on the shins, knees and forehead are the expected pattern in a child who is mobile and pulling to stand, because these are the bony points that meet furniture and the floor. The history matches the developmental stage and the sites. Option A treats a normal finding as abuse and would distress the family and trigger an unnecessary investigation. Option B is disproportionate to accidental bruising. Option D would interfere with a normal developmental milestone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Haemophilia A"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation from sepsis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vitamin K deficiency bleeding, because prophylaxis was not given at birth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Late vitamin K deficiency bleeding presents between two weeks and three months, classically in exclusively breastfed babies who did not receive vitamin K at birth, and it can present as intracranial haemorrhage, so it is a medical emergency. Option A is an inherited factor deficiency that presents in boys with joint and soft-tissue bleeding, not in the first weeks with umbilical bleeding. Option B causes petechiae and bruising with a low platelet count. Option C would come with a sick, febrile baby.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use a firm pillow to keep the head raised"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the sleep space clear of pillows, thick blankets and soft toys, and place the child on their back"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the child on their front once they can roll"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Line the cot with thick padding on all four sides"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Once a child can roll, the risk changes from SIDS to suffocation and entrapment, and the principle stays the same: a firm, flat surface, nothing loose in the cot, no pillows or padded bumpers, and the child placed on their back. Option A adds a soft surface that can cover the face. Option C increases the risk of rebreathing. Option D reintroduces the soft, loose materials the cot should not contain.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hold the baby securely on an adult's lap for short trips"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use a forward-facing seat to let the baby see out"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use a rear-facing car seat in the back, and never hold the baby in someone's arms"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lay the baby flat on the back seat with the seatbelt over the blanket"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A rear-facing seat spreads the forces of a crash across the whole back of an infant's body and protects the relatively heavy head and weak neck, and it is used in the back seat away from the front passenger airbag. Option A is the most dangerous option: an adult cannot hold a child in a crash. Option B faces the fragile neck forward too early. Option D provides no restraint at all.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Frequent visitors and bright lighting for observation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vigorous physiotherapy of the jaw and limbs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withholding sedation until the spasms stop"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A quiet, darkened, single room with clustered care and minimal handling"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tetanus toxin blocks the release of inhibitory transmitters, so any stimulus can trigger a generalised spasm that obstructs the airway or fractures the spine. The environment is the treatment: a quiet, dark, single room, minimal handling, clustered care, and sedation with muscle relaxation prescribed. Option A supplies the stimuli that cause spasms. Option B can provoke spasm and injury. Option C lets the spasms continue unchecked.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trust versus mistrust"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Industry versus inferiority"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Autonomy versus shame and doubt"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Identity versus role confusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the mother to leave without telling him to avoid upset"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrict visiting so that he gets used to the ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Encourage the mother to room in and keep a consistent nurse on the shift"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell him firmly that crying is not allowed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$This is normal separation anxiety, which peaks in the toddler years, and the management is to keep the parent present where possible, maintain routines and toys from home, and assign a consistent nurse so that a relationship can form. Option A teaches the child that his mother disappears without warning and deepens the distress. Option B removes the very person he needs. Option D punishes a developmentally appropriate response.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Despair"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Detachment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Denial"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Protest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In protest, the first phase of separation anxiety, the toddler cries loudly, searches for the parent, rejects the attention of strangers and tries to bring the parent back. Option A comes later, when the crying stops and the child becomes withdrawn and apathetic. Option B comes later still, when the child appears to accept comfort from anyone and treats the parent with indifference, which looks like improvement and is not. Option C is not a phase of separation response.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autonomy versus shame and doubt"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Trust versus mistrust"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Initiative versus guilt"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Industry versus inferiority"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Erikson places trust versus mistrust in the first year of life, and it is built through consistent, responsive caregiving: the infant learns that needs are met and the world is reliable. Repeated admissions and inconsistent caregiving threaten that task, so the nurse should support the mother's presence and involve her in feeding and comforting. Option A belongs to the toddler years, option C to the preschool years and option D to school age.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mistrust of the world and of caregivers"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Guilt about taking initiative"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Shame and doubt about their own ability to act"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Feelings of inferiority about their competence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The toddler stage is autonomy versus shame and doubt, and the toddler builds autonomy by making small choices and having them respected. Constant prohibition and no control over small decisions produce shame and doubt. Option A belongs to the first stage, trust versus mistrust, which is about consistent care in infancy. Option B belongs to the preschool years, initiative versus guilt. Option D belongs to school age, industry versus inferiority.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erikson described psychosexual stages, while Freud described psychosocial tasks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both theories end at adolescence and reject any later development"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Erikson's stages are purely biological, while Freud's are social"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Erikson described psychosocial conflicts that continue across the whole lifespan, while Freud described psychosexual stages ending in adolescence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Erikson extended development across the whole lifespan in eight psychosocial stages, each turning on a conflict between the individual and their social world, which is why a nurse can apply it to an older adult as readily as to a toddler. Option A reverses the two theorists. Option B is wrong about Erikson, who was specifically arguing against the idea that development stops in adolescence. Option C misdescribes both.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "3 to 4 months"}, {"id": "b", "text": "9 to 12 months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "5 to 6 months"}, {"id": "d", "text": "18 to 24 months"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Encourage frequent visitors to keep the child company"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the lights bright so the child can be watched closely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reduce noise, light and handling, and keep the environment calm and quiet"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Carry out frequent non-urgent procedures to monitor progress"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tetanus toxin removes the normal inhibition of motor neurons, so any sensory stimulus, a door, a light, a touch, can set off a generalised spasm that obstructs the airway. A quiet, dim room, minimal handling, clustered care and sedation are the core nursing measures. Option A adds exactly the stimuli that provoke spasm. Option B is the opposite of what is needed. Option D causes avoidable spasms.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Force paracetamol into the mouth during the seizure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrain the arms and legs firmly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place a spoon between the teeth"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Protect the airway and safety, then assess temperature and treat fever as prescribed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The immediate priority is to protect the child from injury and keep the airway clear, positioning on the side, timing the seizure and loosening clothing, with oxygen and suction available. Temperature is assessed and antipyretics given once the seizure has stopped. Option A risks aspiration. Option B risks fracture of a young child's bones and prolongs the seizure. Option C can break teeth and obstruct the airway.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Agree that angry thoughts can cause illness"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain simply that his thoughts cannot make anyone ill, and invite him to help with small tasks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell him he is too young to understand and change the subject"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Suggest he stop talking about his sister"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Preschool children reason with magical thinking and are egocentric, so they readily believe that a wish or an angry thought caused an event. The child needs a simple explanation that thoughts cannot cause illness, reassurance that he is not to blame, and a small real role in care, which restores some control. Option A confirms the misconception. Options C and D dismiss a genuine worry that will otherwise persist unspoken.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reinforce the dressing without looking at the site"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sit the child upright and leave the site to be checked later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply firm direct pressure above the insertion site and call for help"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait for the next scheduled observation round"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A saturated dressing after femoral catheterisation means the puncture site is bleeding and may be bleeding into the groin, so the nurse applies firm pressure at and just above the insertion site, keeps the child flat, checks distal pulses and calls for help. Option A hides the blood loss and conceals the size of the problem. Option B removes the pressure and adds gravitational flow. Option D allows continued haemorrhage.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A mild headache that eases with paracetamol"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Appetite slowly returning"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A temperature falling from 39 to 37.8 degrees"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A new drop in the level of consciousness"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A child with meningitis who becomes less responsive may be developing raised intracranial pressure, a seizure, a subdural collection or worsening sepsis, and this is the sign that changes fastest and matters most. Option A is expected and improving. Option B is a sign of recovery. Option C is the response to treatment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop oral rehydration and give nothing by mouth for twelve hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pause for ten minutes and then restart with smaller, more frequent volumes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a large volume quickly to make up for the vomit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switch to plain water instead of the rehydration solution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Vomiting during oral rehydration is common and does not mean failure, because the fluid is absorbed even when some comes back. The correct response is a short pause, then smaller volumes given more often, by syringe or spoon, and continuing. Option A removes the treatment the child needs. Option C provokes more vomiting. Option D replaces electrolytes with water alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restart the whole schedule from the first dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait until the child is two years old to resume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the overdue dose now and continue the schedule; do not restart the series"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give all the missed doses in one visit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A delayed immunisation is always given as soon as it is possible, from where the schedule stopped, and the series is never restarted, because the doses already received remain valid. Option A would withhold protection the child already has. Option B increases the period of vulnerability to diseases with high mortality in infancy. Option D is not a valid schedule and is not how any antigen is given.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Only if the temperature goes above 39 degrees"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Never, because fever in children is always harmless"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only if the fever has lasted more than a week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "If the child is drowsy, breathing fast, not drinking, or has a convulsion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The height of the fever matters far less than how the child behaves, so the signs that should bring a family to care are the general danger signs: lethargy or drowsiness, fast or laboured breathing, inability to drink or feed, a convulsion, or a rash that does not fade. Option A misses a seriously ill child with a modest fever. Option B is wrong: fever can be the first sign of malaria, pneumonia or meningitis. Option C is a dangerous delay.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the next dose early to maintain the level"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hold the dose and notify the prescriber, anticipating a dose or interval change"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ignore the value because troughs are not monitored"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Double the dose to ensure the infection is covered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A vancomycin trough of 25 mg/L is well above the usual target range, and the risk of nephrotoxicity rises steeply with sustained high exposure, so the dose is withheld and the prescriber informed. Levels are taken immediately before the next dose so that the result reflects the trough. Option A raises the level further. Option C abandons the monitoring that keeps the drug safe. Option D is the opposite of what a high level calls for.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An order to withhold the next warfarin dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An order to give oral vitamin K"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A new order for aspirin for mild joint pain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An order to recheck the INR in the morning"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The INR is far above target and the gums are already bleeding, so adding an antiplatelet drug that also damages the gastric mucosa would compound a serious bleeding risk, and the nurse should query it. Options A, B and D are all appropriate: the warfarin is held, vitamin K reverses the effect, and the INR is rechecked to confirm the response.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop all bronchodilators permanently"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a beta blocker to slow the heart"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Assume the asthma is worsening and give another nebuliser without assessment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assess oxygenation, work of breathing and the ECG, and report if the tachycardia is marked or persistent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tremor and tachycardia are expected beta2-agonist effects and are usually tolerable, so the question is whether the patient is better or worse. Assessing saturation, work of breathing, speech and the ECG distinguishes the expected side effects from a worsening asthma attack or an arrhythmia. Option A removes the treatment that is keeping the airway open. Option B would precipitate bronchospasm. Option C treats a side effect as if it were the disease.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Into the deltoid after removing all clothing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Into the abdomen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Through the outer mid-thigh clothing if necessary"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Deep into the gluteal muscle"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The auto-injector is delivered into the outer mid-thigh and can be given through clothing if needed; delaying treatment is dangerous.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Moving to the deltoid wastes time in anaphylaxis, and the thigh is the recommended site even through clothing.
• B — The abdomen is not the recommended site for an auto-injector and absorption is less reliable than from the thigh.
• D — The gluteal site is not used for self-injection and would delay treatment while clothing is removed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Crush it without checking"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Force the patient to swallow it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask a relative to administer it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Contact pharmacy or the prescriber for a suitable formulation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The nurse should not alter a medication without checking formulation and stability. Pharmacy can identify a liquid or appropriate alternative.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Crushing an unverified tablet can destroy a modified-release preparation and release the dose at once.
• B — Forcing a patient to swallow risks aspiration and is never acceptable.
• C — Asking a relative to administer medication takes the prescription outside the hands of the people accountable for it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Water intoxication or hyponatraemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dehydration only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Iron deficiency"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperglycaemia only"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Excess desmopressin can cause water retention and dilutional hyponatraemia, producing headache, nausea, confusion or drowsiness.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Dehydration alone does not explain drowsiness and headache in a patient taking desmopressin, which retains water.
• C — Iron deficiency causes fatigue and pallor, not acute drowsiness with headache.
• D — Hyperglycaemia is not the effect of desmopressin, which is an antidiuretic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Diluting it and infusing it by a volumetric pump"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Giving it as a direct intravenous bolus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Checking the serum potassium and renal function first"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Monitoring the cardiac rhythm during the infusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Concentrated potassium given as a bolus reaches the myocardium at a concentration that can stop the heart, which is why it is always diluted and infused at a controlled rate, usually no faster than 10 mmol per hour peripherally. Options A, C and D are all correct practice: a pump controls the rate, the potassium and renal function determine the dose, and the rhythm detects the effect.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Giving the usual dose because there is no bleeding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Giving an extra dose to bring the INR down"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withholding the next dose and considering vitamin K as prescribed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switching immediately to heparin without advice"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$An INR of 5.5 is well above the therapeutic range of 2 to 3 for most indications, and although the patient is not bleeding the risk of haemorrhage is high. The prescription is usually withheld, the INR rechecked, and vitamin K considered, with a search for the cause such as a new interacting drug. Option A adds to the anticoagulation. Option B is a contradiction: warfarin raises the INR, so more would raise it further. Option D is a decision for the prescriber, not an automatic substitution.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure the patient that the cough will always resolve on its own"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the drug permanently without telling anyone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Increase the dose to settle the cough"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Report it, because the prescriber may switch to an alternative such as an ARB"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A dry, tickly cough affects a significant minority of patients on ACE inhibitors and is caused by bradykinin accumulation. It does not settle for most people, and if it is troublesome the prescriber usually substitutes an angiotensin receptor blocker, which does not cause it. Option A is wrong because the cough is characteristically persistent. Option B removes a drug the patient needs without review. Option C would not help and could worsen hypotension.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Flumazenil"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Naloxone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Vitamin K"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Protamine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The findings indicate opioid-induced respiratory depression. Naloxone reverses opioid effects while airway and ventilation support are provided.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines; this patient has opioid toxicity, with pinpoint pupils and slow breathing.
• C — Vitamin K reverses warfarin and has nothing to do with opioid depression.
• D — Protamine reverses heparin, not opioids.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop phenytoin abruptly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Avoid all fluids"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use meticulous oral hygiene and attend dental reviews"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Double the dose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Gingival hyperplasia is associated with phenytoin. Good oral hygiene and dental care reduce complications; abrupt withdrawal can precipitate seizures.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Stopping phenytoin abruptly can precipitate seizures; changes are made only under medical direction.
• B — Restricting fluids does not treat gingival hyperplasia and risks dehydration.
• D — Doubling the dose increases the very effect that caused the gums to swell.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the next dose because spironolactone protects the heart"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage a diet high in potassium"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give an extra dose to lower the potassium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold the drug and notify the prescriber before the next dose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic, so it retains potassium, and a level of 6.1 mmol/L is dangerous enough to threaten the myocardium. The next dose is withheld, the prescriber informed, and the ECG and renal function reviewed; treatment may include stopping the drug and giving a potassium binder or a diuretic that wastes potassium. Option A adds to the retention. Option B worsens it. Option C misunderstands the drug, which raises potassium rather than lowering it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The medicine can be stopped as soon as the cough improves"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Urine, sweat and tears may turn orange-red, and contact lenses can be permanently stained"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It has no important interactions with other medicines"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Missed doses can safely be doubled the next day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The orange-red discolouration is harmless but alarming if it is unexpected, and it permanently stains soft contact lenses, so warning the client prevents both distress and damage. Rifampicin is also a powerful enzyme inducer, which makes the interaction teaching essential. Option A risks relapse and resistance. Option C is false: rifampicin reduces the effect of oral contraceptives, warfarin and many antiretrovirals. Option D is never correct for antimicrobials.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restrict all fluids to less than one litre a day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Double the next dose if one dose is forgotten"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep salt and fluid intake steady and report vomiting or diarrhoea promptly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the drug as soon as the mood improves"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Keep salt and fluid intake steady and report vomiting or diarrhoea promptly". Lithium is a salt that competes with sodium: when the body is short of sodium, the kidney reabsorbs more lithium, the plasma level rises and toxicity follows. Anything that depletes salt or water, such as a hot climate with heavy sweating, vomiting, diarrhoea, a low-salt diet or diuretics, therefore raises the level. The patient should drink normally, keep salt intake consistent, and report illness with vomiting or diarrhoea so the level can be checked. Option A is dangerous: fluid restriction concentrates the lithium. Option B could produce a double dose, and missed doses should be discussed with the prescriber rather than doubled. Option D invites relapse, because lithium is continued to prevent further episodes. Levels, thyroid function and renal function are monitored long term.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take it with an antacid to protect the stomach"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop it as soon as the stools darken"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take it with a cup of tea to improve the taste"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take it with a source of vitamin C such as orange juice, expect dark stools, and avoid tea at the same time"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Vitamin C keeps iron in the ferrous form that is absorbed, while tea contains tannins and antacids raise the gastric pH, and both substantially reduce uptake. Dark stools are the harmless result of unabsorbed iron and are not a reason to stop. Options A and D directly impair absorption. Option B risks abandoning treatment for a normal effect.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treat as impending respiratory failure and call for urgent assistance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure and reassess in one hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Offer oral fluids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the patient flat"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A silent chest with severe work of breathing indicates critically reduced airflow and impending respiratory failure. Urgent airway and ventilatory support are required.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Reassuring and reviewing in an hour would allow a silent chest to progress to arrest.
• C — Oral fluids in a patient who cannot complete a sentence risks aspiration.
• D — Lying the patient flat reduces lung expansion and worsens the work of breathing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give unlimited high-flow oxygen without monitoring"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess ventilation and prepare prescribed ventilatory support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Encourage sedatives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the patient flat"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Drowsiness and rising PaCO2 suggest hypercapnic respiratory failure. Oxygen must be titrated to the prescribed target while ventilation is assessed and escalated.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Unlimited high-flow oxygen without monitoring can worsen hypercapnia and cause oxygen toxicity in COPD.
• C — Sedatives depress respiratory drive and would deepen the hypercapnia.
• D — Lying flat impairs diaphragm movement and worsens ventilation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Silence the alarm permanently"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Increase sedation without assessment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Check for kinks, secretions and tube obstruction while assessing the patient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disconnect the ventilator and leave the patient alone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A high-pressure alarm commonly indicates increased resistance from kinking, secretions or obstruction. The patient is assessed and the airway is checked immediately.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Silencing the alarm removes the warning without treating the obstruction.
• B — Increasing sedation without assessment hides the problem and removes the patient's ability to compensate.
• D — Disconnecting and leaving the patient alone removes ventilation entirely from someone who cannot breathe.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Massage the calf to relieve the pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage her to walk to improve her circulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give an oral analgesic and reassess in one hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sit her upright, give prescribed oxygen, assess ABCs and escalate urgently for suspected pulmonary embolism"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sudden breathlessness and hypoxaemia after major hip surgery is pulmonary embolism until proved otherwise, and it can progress to cardiac arrest within minutes, so oxygen, upright positioning, a full ABCDE assessment and immediate escalation come first. Option A is harmful: massaging the leg can dislodge more thrombus. Option B mobilises a hypoxic patient with a possible embolus. Option C treats the pain and misses the diagnosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clamp the tube for 30 minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess the patient and tubing for obstruction or disconnection and call for help"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Strip the tubing forcefully"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ignore the change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sudden dyspnoea with a change in drainage-system function may indicate obstruction, disconnection or worsening pneumothorax. The patient and system need immediate assessment.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Clamping the tube traps air and can convert a pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax.
• C — Stripping the tubing generates high negative pressure and can damage lung tissue.
• D — Ignoring a change in the water seal misses a developing pneumothorax or a disconnected system.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait for a routine chest radiograph"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the patient prone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prepare for immediate decompression while supporting oxygenation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage coughing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tension pneumothorax compromises ventilation and venous return and requires immediate decompression; treatment must not wait for imaging.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Waiting for a radiograph in tension pneumothorax is how patients die; decompression is clinical, not radiological.
• B — Prone positioning makes ventilation worse and delays decompression.
• D — Coughing increases intrathoracic pressure and cannot relieve a tension pneumothorax.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Flat supine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Trendelenburg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High Fowler with legs dependent if tolerated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Upright positioning improves lung expansion and reduces venous return, helping relieve pulmonary congestion while oxygen and prescribed therapy are given.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Lying flat increases venous return to an overloaded heart and floods the lungs further.
• B — Prone positioning impairs ventilation and access to the airway in an unstable patient.
• C — Trendelenburg increases venous return and worsens pulmonary congestion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pain score"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Airway and breathing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dietary history"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Urine output"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cyanosis and unresponsiveness indicate an immediate ABC emergency. Airway and breathing are assessed and supported before secondary data collection.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A pain score is a secondary assessment and cannot be obtained from an unresponsive patient.
• C — Dietary history is irrelevant to an immediate airway emergency.
• D — Urine output is a late perfusion marker and plays no part in the first minute.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Constipation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypoglycaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pneumothorax"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cellulitis only"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Central-line insertion can puncture the pleura and cause pneumothorax, presenting with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Constipation has no relationship to sudden dyspnoea immediately after line insertion.
• B — Hypoglycaemia causes sweating and confusion, not sharp pleuritic chest pain.
• D — Cellulitis is a local skin infection and does not produce sudden breathlessness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer sedatives"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Document the value as accurate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remove all oxygen"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assess the patient and improve probe position or peripheral perfusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Poor perfusion and a poor waveform can make pulse oximetry unreliable. The patient is assessed directly while the probe and circulation are corrected.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Sedation in a patient with a genuinely low saturation would remove their drive to breathe.
• B — Documenting a value from a poor waveform as accurate records an artefact as a clinical finding.
• C — Removing oxygen from a patient reading 82% could be fatal if the reading is real.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Squeeze aggressively for ten minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wash with soap and water and report immediately"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply bleach"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hide the injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Washing and immediate reporting allow timely exposure assessment, baseline testing and post-exposure prophylaxis when indicated.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Squeezing the wound is no longer advised; it can force contaminated blood deeper and damages tissue.
• C — Bleach is caustic and damages the wound; it is not a first-aid measure.
• D — Concealing the injury forfeits post-exposure prophylaxis in the window when it works.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A simple face mask at 8 litres per minute"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A non-rebreathe mask at 15 litres per minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A nasal cannula at 1 to 2 litres per minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A Venturi mask set to 60 per cent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A nasal cannula at 1 to 2 litres per minute". A nasal cannula delivers roughly 24 per cent at 1 litre per minute and adds about 4 per cent for each further litre, so 1 to 2 litres gives the 24 to 28 per cent band asked for. The concentration is variable, because it depends on the patient's inspiratory flow and on how much room air is entrained, which is acceptable when the target range is broad. Option A delivers around 40 to 60 per cent and would overshoot badly. Option B delivers close to 100 per cent and is reserved for critical illness. Option D is a fixed-performance device, and while accuracy is its advantage, 60 per cent is far above the target. Choose the device that matches the target range and the patient's tolerance, and remember that nasal cannula flows above about 4 litres per minute dry and irritate the nasal mucosa.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Massage the reddened area vigorously"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply heat directly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the patient in the same position"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Offload pressure, reposition and document/escalate the skin change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Non-blanching erythema may be a stage 1 pressure injury. Pressure must be relieved and the area monitored and documented.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Massaging reddened skin damages tissue that is already ischaemic.
• B — Direct heat increases metabolic demand in tissue that cannot meet it and can cause burns.
• C — Leaving the patient in the same position allows the injury to progress to ulceration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that she look at it now so that she gets used to it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Accept the feeling, stay with her, and introduce the stoma gradually when she is ready"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave her alone and arrange stoma teaching for a later admission"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the family to do all the stoma care and stop teaching her"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Refusal to look at a new stoma is a normal grief reaction to a change in body image, and it resolves with time and support. The nurse acknowledges the feeling, does not force contact, and builds tolerance gradually by involving the patient in small steps while demonstrating care. Option A overrides her coping and damages trust. Option C abandons teaching that she will need before discharge. Option D leaves her dependent and unprepared to care for herself.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Suction continuously for a full minute to clear everything at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use the largest catheter that will pass, to shorten the procedure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pre-oxygenate and limit each suction pass to about 10 to 15 seconds"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instil 5 mL of normal saline to loosen secretions before each pass"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Suctioning removes oxygen as well as secretions, so the patient is pre-oxygenated, each pass is limited to roughly 10 to 15 seconds, and the catheter is withdrawn with rotation without applying suction on the way in. Option A causes prolonged hypoxia. Option B uses a catheter that occludes the airway and increases trauma and hypoxaemia. Option D is no longer recommended: instilling saline does not loosen secretions, and it pushes them deeper and causes desaturation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oxygen does no good at all in COPD"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The target is set only to save money on oxygen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "High-flow oxygen causes immediate lung collapse in every patient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "In some people with chronic high carbon dioxide, too much oxygen can worsen retention and cause drowsiness and respiratory failure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In a minority of patients with chronic hypercapnia the hypoxic drive contributes to ventilation, and a high inspired oxygen can increase carbon dioxide retention through several mechanisms, producing drowsiness, acidosis and eventually respiratory failure. The prescribed target range balances adequate oxygenation against that risk, with blood gases and the patient's level of consciousness monitored. Option A is wrong: oxygen is essential, it is just titrated. Option B invents a motive. Option C describes a pneumothorax, not oxygen therapy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The angina is improving, so no action is needed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Unstable angina, needing urgent assessment rather than a routine appointment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The symptoms are now musculoskeletal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He should double his glyceryl trinitrate dose and wait"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Angina that becomes more frequent, more prolonged or occurs at rest has changed from stable to unstable, which places him at high risk of infarction and needs urgent assessment rather than a routine visit. Option A reverses the meaning of the change. Option C ignores a cardiac pattern that has already been established. Option D is unsafe: the dose is not the nurse's to change and he needs to be seen.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The circumflex artery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The right coronary artery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The left anterior descending artery"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The left main coronary artery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The left anterior descending artery travels in the anterior interventricular groove and supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle, the anterior septum and the apex. Because it supplies such a large mass of muscle, occlusion here causes the largest infarcts and is the reason it is called the widow-maker together with the left main stem. Option A runs in the atrioventricular groove and supplies the lateral and posterior wall. Option B supplies the right ventricle and, in most people, the inferior wall and the sinoatrial node. Option D is the short trunk that divides into the left anterior descending and circumflex, so a lesion there affects both territories.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The fluid compresses the coronary arteries and stops the myocardium contracting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The fluid enters the heart and dilutes the blood"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The pericardium stretches and dilates the chambers"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rising pericardial pressure compresses the chambers and prevents them filling during diastole"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The pericardium is tough and cannot stretch quickly, so as fluid accumulates the pressure inside it rises and is transmitted to the chambers. The ventricles can no longer fill properly in diastole, stroke volume falls, and the fall in output produces the hypotension and the muffled heart sounds. Option A misdescribes the mechanism: the coronaries are compressed, but the arrest in output comes from impaired filling, not from a paralysed myocardium. Option B does not occur. Option C is the opposite of what a rigid sac does.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Compress as fast as possible regardless of depth"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Compress at 100 to 120 per minute, 5 to 6 cm deep, with full recoil and minimal interruption"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lean on the chest between compressions to stay in position"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pause compressions every minute to check the pulse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Survival from cardiac arrest tracks the quality of compressions: a rate of 100 to 120 per minute, a depth of 5 to 6 cm in an adult, complete recoil of the chest and interruptions kept to seconds. Option A produces inadequate depth and faster fatigue. Option C prevents the recoil that lets the heart refill. Option D interrupts flow that takes several compressions to rebuild, so the pulse is checked only when the rhythm is analysed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lay him flat so that blood is distributed evenly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a large oral fluid bolus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sit him upright, give oxygen and call for urgent medical review"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure him and reassess in thirty minutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Waking breathless and needing to sit up is paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea, caused by fluid redistributing into the lungs when the patient lies flat. Sitting upright with the legs dependent reduces venous return at once, oxygen corrects hypoxaemia, and urgent review decides on diuretics and further support. Option A produces the position that caused the attack. Option B adds volume to an overloaded circulation. Option D leaves a hypoxic patient to deteriorate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The drug is only effective at low doses"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A low dose avoids the need for monitoring"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The drug must be stopped after a few weeks anyway"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A rapid increase can cause a transient fall in contractility and worsen heart failure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Beta blockers improve survival in heart failure by blocking the harmful effects of sustained sympathetic drive, but they also reduce contractility, so starting low and titrating over weeks allows the heart to adapt. Option A is wrong: the target dose is the one shown to improve outcomes. Option B is wrong because blood pressure, heart rate and weight are all monitored through titration. Option C is wrong because the drug is continued long term unless it is not tolerated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Overhydration with intravenous fluid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dehydration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A high-oxygen environment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A recent blood transfusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sickle cell crises are precipitated by anything that slows the circulation, reduces oxygen delivery or increases sickling, and dehydration is one of the commonest: plasma volume falls, viscosity rises, blood flow slows in the small vessels and the abnormal haemoglobin polymerises. Cold, infection, hypoxia, acidosis, stress and strenuous exertion are the other triggers, so the nurse teaches steady fluid intake and prompt treatment of infection. Option A would dilute the blood rather than concentrate it. Option C reduces sickling rather than provoking it. Option D replaces sickled cells and reduces the proportion of abnormal haemoglobin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mitral regurgitation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aortic stenosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aortic dissection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stable angina"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Exertional syncope, exertional breathlessness and a harsh ejection systolic murmur radiating to the carotids are the classic triad of severe aortic stenosis. The narrowed valve cannot increase output as demand rises, so blood pressure falls and the patient faints; symptom onset marks a sharp rise in the risk of sudden death and is an indication for urgent assessment. Option A produces a pansystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. Option C gives tearing pain, not exertional syncope. Option D causes chest pain rather than syncope, and does not produce that murmur.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An uncomplicated influenza illness"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rheumatoid arthritis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infective endocarditis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Aortic stenosis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Fever that persists beyond a week in someone with a prosthetic valve, together with a new murmur and Osler nodes, is infective endocarditis, and the prosthetic material makes it both more likely and more dangerous. Blood cultures are taken from separate sites before antibiotics, and an echocardiogram is arranged urgently. Option A does not produce a new murmur or embolic skin lesions. Option B does not cause a new murmur with fever. Option D is a structural diagnosis, not an infection, and would not explain the fever.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give him an antacid and observe whether the pain settles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask him to sit in the waiting area until a bed is free"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange a fasting lipid profile before any treatment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain a 12-lead electrocardiogram and activate the acute coronary syndrome pathway"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Central crushing pain radiating to the jaw with sweating and nausea is acute coronary syndrome, and the single time-critical action is a 12-lead electrocardiogram within about ten minutes of arrival, read and acted on, alongside oxygen if hypoxic, aspirin, and continuous monitoring. Option A treats cardiac pain as indigestion and wastes the reperfusion window. Option B delays a patient who may be in ventricular fibrillation within the hour. Option C investigates risk factors long after the diagnosis must be made.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Discharge home, because a normal troponin excludes infarction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A repeat troponin after an interval, with continuous monitoring, because a single early sample cannot exclude infarction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediate thrombolysis on the basis of the pain alone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A troponin repeated in seven days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Troponin is released only once myocardium has died, and it takes hours to become detectable, so a normal result very early after the onset of pain does not exclude infarction. The protocol is a repeat sample at a set interval with ECG monitoring and clinical reassessment. Option A would discharge a patient in the highest-risk window. Option C gives a dangerous drug without evidence. Option D is far too late to guide treatment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chewing prevents the aspirin from irritating the stomach"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chewing reduces the dose needed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Chewing breaks the tablet up so it is absorbed quickly and starts working sooner"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Chewing stops the aspirin affecting clotting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Aspirin needs to block platelet aggregation on the ruptured plaque as fast as possible, and a chewed tablet is absorbed through the buccal and gastric mucosa far sooner than a swallowed one. Option A is wrong: chewing does not protect the stomach, and the irritation is a separate concern. Option B is wrong because the dose is unchanged. Option D is the opposite of the drug's purpose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous antibiotics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A beta blocker to slow the heart rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Long-term anticoagulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug with colchicine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acute pericarditis is treated with a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug for the inflammation and pain, combined with colchicine because it lowers the rate of recurrence and prevents constrictive complications. Option A treats an infection the client may not have; antibiotics are used only if there is a proven cause. Option B does not treat the inflammation. Option C is irrelevant here and, in pericarditis with effusion, would carry its own risk of haemopericardium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the tablet at night to avoid daytime urination"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Weigh daily and report a 2 kg gain in three days"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the tablet once the ankle swelling settles"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Double the dose on days when breathing is worse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Daily weight is the earliest reliable sign of fluid retention, and a gain of about 2 kg over three days signals decompensation before breathlessness or oedema appear. The client needs to know the threshold at which to seek help.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Diuretics are taken in the morning; night dosing causes nocturia and falls.
• C — Stopping when swelling settles withdraws the treatment that keeps the client compensated.
• D — Clients never adjust a diuretic dose themselves; that risks dehydration and electrolyte loss.

Reference: Ghana STG — heart failure; diuretic counselling$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Serum amylase and lipase"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Full blood count only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Serum potassium and creatinine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Thyroid function only"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "His age"}, {"id": "b", "text": "His family history of hypertension"}, {"id": "c", "text": "His biological sex"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stopping smoking"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Smoking is the most powerful modifiable contributor: each cigarette raises blood pressure acutely, and smoking damages the vessels that hypertension is already stressing, so quitting reduces cardiovascular risk immediately and continues to do so. Options A, B and D are fixed characteristics. They affect risk and they explain why he needs a lower target, but no advice changes them.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Palpate the mass firmly to assess its size"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Call for emergency help, keep him flat and prepare for urgent surgery with blood ready"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral fluids and reassess in an hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a heat pack to the back pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A tender pulsatile abdominal mass with back pain and shock is a leaking or ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and death can occur within minutes; the patient is kept flat, help is summoned immediately, and blood products and theatre are prepared. Option A risks precipitating rupture. Option C delays a surgical emergency and oral fluids are inappropriate in a patient who may be anaesthetised at any moment. Option D has no effect on the bleeding and wastes time.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A fasting blood glucose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Serum calcium"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Full blood count with serum ferritin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A chest radiograph"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Spoon-shaped nails, a sore smooth tongue and pale conjunctivae together point to iron deficiency, and the first step is a full blood count showing a microcytic hypochromic anaemia with a low ferritin confirming depleted iron stores. Once confirmed, the cause must be found, particularly bleeding. Option A investigates diabetes, which does not produce these findings. Option B does not explain anaemia or nail changes. Option D is not the first-line test for this presentation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give paracetamol and reassess in the morning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Advise him to increase oral fluids and stay at home"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange an outpatient review next week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treat it as an emergency: take cultures and obtain urgent medical review for intravenous antibiotics"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sickle cell disease causes functional asplenia through repeated infarction of the spleen, so the patient cannot mount a response to encapsulated organisms, and pneumococcal sepsis can kill within hours of a first fever. A temperature above 38°C is therefore treated as an emergency with cultures and prompt intravenous antibiotics. Option A allows sepsis to progress through the night. Option B leaves a bacteraemic patient at home. Option C delays treatment by days.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Iron-deficiency anaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Splenic sequestration with sudden severe anaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A mild viral illness that can be managed at home"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute chest syndrome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In splenic sequestration the spleen traps red cells, so the haemoglobin falls abruptly while the spleen enlarges rapidly. The child becomes pale, drowsy and tachycardic and can go into hypovolaemic shock within hours, which makes it an emergency requiring urgent cross-match and transfusion. Parents are taught to feel for an enlarging spleen and to bring the child immediately. Option A develops over weeks rather than hours. Option C does not explain sudden pallor with a swollen abdomen. Option D produces chest pain, fever and respiratory signs rather than a splenic mass.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "PaCO2 of 52 mmHg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "PaCO2 of 40 mmHg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "PaCO2 of 26 mmHg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "PaCO2 of 60 mmHg"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In metabolic acidosis the lungs compensate by hyperventilating, which blows off carbon dioxide and drives PaCO2 below the normal 35 to 45 mmHg range. A PaCO2 of 26 mmHg alongside a pH of 7.18 and a bicarbonate of 9 mmol/L is the expected respiratory compensation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A PaCO2 of 52 mmHg indicates hypoventilation and would deepen the acidosis, not compensate for it.
• B — A PaCO2 of 40 mmHg is a normal value, so no compensation has occurred at all.
• D — A PaCO2 of 60 mmHg represents a second, respiratory acidosis on top of the metabolic one.

Reference: Ghana STG — acid–base interpretation; compensation$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rapid correction causes immediate renal failure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rapid correction causes hyperkalaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rapid correction makes the patient dehydrated"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rapid correction can cause cerebral oedema as water shifts into brain cells"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In hypernatraemia the brain has adapted by generating idiogenic osmoles to hold water, and if the sodium is dropped faster than the brain can readjust, water floods into the cells and produces cerebral oedema with seizures. Correction is therefore limited to about 10 mmol/L in 24 hours. Option A is not a consequence of correcting sodium. Option B is unrelated; potassium is not driven by sodium correction. Option C is the opposite, since the patient is already water-depleted and is being rehydrated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 375 rows updated.
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch6.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 6: PROC module distractor repair (20 items).
--
-- Same defect as TCAM and NUTR: distractors no trained nurse could choose
-- ('lock the hospital exit doors and apply physical restraints', 'forcefully
-- push the tube another 20 cm', 'instant spontaneous rupture of the
-- contralateral kidney'), keyed option the longest in all 20 items (mean
-- key/distractor length ratio 2.55), and every item keyed 'a'.
--
-- Answer letters are unchanged from the rebalanced bank. Key option TEXT was
-- trimmed on 15 items for length parity; clinical content of each key is
-- preserved. All 60 distractors replaced and a distractor block added.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Gloves first, followed by eye protection (goggles/face shield), gown, mask or respirator, and perform immediate hand hygiene"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mask or respirator first, then gown, then gloves, then eye protection, with hand hygiene performed only after leaving the room"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Gown and gloves together, rolled inside out into a bundle, then eye protection and mask together at the door before hand hygiene"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eye protection first while the gloves are still on, then gloves, then gown, then mask, with hand hygiene after the mask is removed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The outer surfaces of gloves and the front of the gown are heavily contaminated during patient care. The CDC recommended sequence for doffing PPE minimizes transmission to clean skin and mucous membranes: (1) Gloves are removed first (glove-in-glove technique) because they are the most heavily contaminated item; (2) Goggles or face shield are removed by grasping the clean headband/earpieces; (3) Gown is unfastened, peeled away from neck and shoulders, turned inside out, and rolled into a bundle; (4) Mask or respirator is removed last (touching only the clean elastic strings/ties, never the front surface) after exiting the room (or just before exiting for non-airborne precautions); and (5) Hand hygiene is performed immediately. Why the other options fail: removing the mask first (option b) feels right because the mask covers the face, but it is the last item removed in a doffing sequence because the hands must be free of contamination before they touch the straps behind the head. Option c bundles the two most contaminated items together, which means handling a soiled gown with soiled gloves and then carrying that contamination to the eye protection. Option d reverses the first two steps: removing goggles while wearing gloves transfers glove contamination directly to the face and hair.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PPE doffing order: Gloves first (most contaminated) -> Goggles/face shield -> Gown -> Mask/respirator last -> Immediate hand hygiene.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Document in the notes that the client left against advice and ask the ward clerk to prepare the discharge summary, because a competent adult cannot be detained"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confirm decision-making capacity, have the physician explain the specific risks, explore reasons for leaving, obtain the DAMA signature, and provide discharge medications and a welcome to return"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the client to sign the DAMA form before any further discussion and supply the prescribed medications, so the treatment plan continues at home"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain the risks once and let the client leave without further documentation, since a refusal signed under pressure cannot be relied on in law"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A mentally competent adult has the legal right to refuse medical care and leave a healthcare facility against medical advice (DAMA). Restraining the client or withholding belongings constitutes false imprisonment and battery. The nurse's professional responsibility includes: (1) confirming the client has decisional capacity (understands situation and consequences); (2) facilitating an urgent discussion where the physician explains specific risks (e.g., sudden cardiac death); (3) exploring underlying barriers (childcare, finances, business) to find alternatives; (4) offering the DAMA release form for signature (or documenting refusal to sign); and (5) providing discharge medications, essential advice, and explicitly welcoming the client back if symptoms recur. Why the other options fail: option a gets the legal principle right and the nursing obligation wrong. A competent adult cannot be detained, but documenting the exit without assessing capacity or arranging the risk discussion leaves out the part that protects the client and the nurse. Option c reverses the order: taking a signature before the risks have been explained produces a form that records nothing of value. Option d abandons documentation altogether, and the reasoning it offers is the opposite of the law, which is that a contemporaneous record of risks explained is exactly what makes a refusal defensible.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DAMA protocol: Confirm capacity -> Explain specific life-threatening risks -> Explore compromises -> Request DAMA signature -> Provide discharge meds & welcome back.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An auscultatory gap; record 128/84, because the higher sounds are arterial wall artefact rather than true systolic pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A normal Korotkoff sequence; record 150/84, treating the point at which sounds disappear as the systolic pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An auscultatory gap — record 168/84 and, at later readings, inflate beyond the point where the radial pulse disappears so the true systolic is not missed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A widened phase IV; record 168/128 and repeat the measurement on the opposite arm to confirm the finding before documenting it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$An auscultatory gap is a silent interval between the first Korotkoff sound and the return of phase II sounds, seen most often in older hypertensive clients and in aortic stenosis. It matters because if the cuff is inflated only to the expected systolic, the first sounds fall inside the gap and the true systolic is missed, so the reading is falsely low. The safeguard is to palpate the radial artery while inflating 20 to 30 mmHg beyond the point where the pulse disappears, then deflate at 2 to 3 mmHg per second. The first sound heard is the systolic (168) and the final disappearance is the diastolic (84). Why the other options fail: recording 128/84 (option a) mistakes the return of phase II for the systolic and underestimates the pressure by 40 mmHg. Option b calls a 22 mmHg silent interval between phases I and II a normal sequence, and takes a disappearance point as systolic, which no Korotkoff phase ever is. Option d names a phase IV change rather than a gap and pairs the true systolic with the phase II return as though that were the diastolic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$A silent interval between phases I and II is an auscultatory gap. Record the first sound heard as systolic and the last sound heard as diastolic, and inflate well above the palpated systolic so the gap cannot hide it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Disinfect with an alcohol swab and draw both sets immediately from the same venepuncture site, sparing the client a second needle puncture and a second episode of pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Palpate the site again after the chlorhexidine has dried, then take 5 mL per bottle through an existing peripheral cannula that has been in place for four days"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the first antibiotic dose and then draw 4 mL per bottle from the arm not receiving the infusion, so that the sample is not diluted by the running fluid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disinfect the venepuncture site with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol for 30 seconds and let it dry; draw 8 to 10 mL per bottle from two separate sites before antibiotics"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Diagnostic blood cultures require meticulous aseptic technique to prevent false-positive contamination with skin flora (e.g., coagulase-negative staphylococci). Best practices dictate: (1) draw blood cultures BEFORE administering antibiotics; (2) cleanse the culture bottle rubber stoppers with alcohol; (3) disinfect the skin using 2% chlorhexidine in 70% isopropyl alcohol for 30 seconds and allow to air-dry completely (antimicrobial action occurs upon drying); (4) collect adequate blood volume (8–10 mL per bottle in adults; underfilling leads to false-negative results); and (5) obtain two distinct sets from separate venepuncture sites (not from existing indwelling peripheral IV lines). Why the other options fail: option a gets the antiseptic nearly right and then draws both sets through one puncture, so a single skin-flora contamination event makes both bottles positive and impossible to interpret. Option b repalpates a disinfected site, which reintroduces skin commensals, and takes too small a volume from an indwelling line. Option c draws the cultures after antibiotics have started, which is the commonest avoidable cause of a false-negative culture in sepsis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Blood culture standards: Before antibiotics + clean site with chlorhexidine & air dry + 8-10 mL per bottle + 2 separate venepuncture sites.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lateral recumbent 'fetal' position with knees flexed to the abdomen and chin tucked to the chest; keep the client flat and supine for 4 to 6 hours afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Seat the client upright on the edge of the bed with the chin extended and the lumbar spine held in extension, then keep them sitting upright for 4 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client prone with a pillow under the abdomen to open the interspinous spaces, then keep them prone for six hours to compress the puncture site and seal the leak"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Position the client supine with the head of the bed at 45 degrees and the knees straight, then keep the head elevated for eight hours to reduce intracranial pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$For a lumbar puncture (spinal tap), the client is positioned in a lateral decubitus (side-lying) 'fetal' posture with knees drawn up to the chest and chin tucked to the chest (or seated leaning forward over an overbed table). This maximizes flexion of the lumbar spine, widening the interspinous spaces between L3–L4 or L4–L5 to permit safe needle entry into the subarachnoid space below the conus medullaris. Post-procedure, keeping the client flat in bed in a supine position for 4–6 hours reduces cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leakage through the dural puncture site, significantly decreasing the risk of post-dural puncture headache. Why the other options fail: option b is the seated position used for some punctures, but taken with the spine extended rather than flexed, which narrows the interspinous spaces the needle has to pass through. Option c opens the spaces in the wrong direction and then keeps the client prone, which does nothing for the dural leak. Option d raises the head of the bed, the single thing most likely to worsen a post-dural puncture headache, because a dependent leak is what the flat position is meant to limit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lumbar puncture care: Lateral recumbent fetal position widens interspinous space. Post-procedure: Lie flat (supine) for 4-6 hours to prevent spinal headache.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the bed flat at its lowest height, stand close to the client, bend from the waist with the knees straight so the legs stay clear, and pull firmly on the draw sheet"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bed at elbow height of the shorter nurse, head flat, friction-reducing slide sheet under the torso, knees and hips bent, wide base, and move on a synchronized count"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Raise the head of the bed to 45 degrees, place a slide sheet under the client, and have one nurse pull while the other lifts the client's shoulders clear of the mattress"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use a hoist without checking that the sling size matches the client, and ask the client to hold the nurses' shoulders and pull themselves up to help with the transfer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Safe patient handling and ergonomic guidelines protect both health workers and clients: (1) elevate the bed surface to the working height of the caregivers (elbow/waist level) to eliminate spinal stooping; (2) flatten the bed (lowering head of bed) so gravity does not oppose upward movement; (3) use friction-reducing slide sheets or draw sheets to reduce shear forces and friction that damage fragile skin; (4) maintain proper body mechanics (wide base of support with feet shoulder-width apart, knees and hips flexed, spine neutral, lifting by shifting body weight using leg and gluteal muscles rather than lumbar spine muscles); and (5) coordinate movements on a verbal count. Why the other options fail: option a is the classic injury posture: bending from the waist with straight knees loads the lumbar spine and puts the nurses at risk rather than the client. Option c raises the head of the bed, so gravity now opposes the move, and lifting the shoulders clear of the mattress converts a slide into a lift. Option d uses the right equipment carelessly and then adds a pull on the nurses' shoulders, which is both a client fall risk and an injury risk for the staff.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ergonomic transfer: Bed at elbow height + flat head of bed + friction-reducing slide sheet + bend knees/wide base + shift weight together.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Retract the foreskin, cleanse the glans outward from the meatus, and leave it retracted so that the area can be observed and kept dry until the next round of care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wash from the scrotum upward toward the meatus in one stroke, replacing the foreskin afterwards, so the most contaminated area is cleaned first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Retract the foreskin, cleanse the glans from the meatus outward in circles, wash down the shaft, then replace the foreskin immediately to prevent paraphimosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retract the foreskin and cleanse with an alcohol-based rub to reduce bacterial colonisation, then replace it and apply powder to keep the skin dry and free of odour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Perineal care for an uncircumcised male requires retracting the prepuce (foreskin) to clean accumulated smegma and secretions. The nurse washes from the cleanest area to the dirtiest: starting at the urethral meatus and wiping in circular outward motions around the glans toward the corona. Crucially, the nurse must REPOSITION (reduce) the foreskin back over the glans immediately after cleansing. Leaving the foreskin retracted causes constricting bands around the penile shaft, venous stasis, severe edema, arterial compromise, and penile ischemia—a surgical emergency known as paraphimosis. Why the other options fail: option a gets the cleansing right and then leaves the foreskin retracted, which is precisely the mechanism of paraphimosis, and a constricting band behind the glans can cause arterial compromise within hours. Option b reverses the direction of cleaning, carrying faecal organisms from the scrotum to the meatus and into the catheter. Option d adds an alcohol rub to mucous membrane, which is painful and damaging, and powder under a replaced foreskin promotes maceration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Male perineal care: Retract foreskin -> Cleanse meatus outwards in circles -> IMMEDIATELY replace foreskin to prevent fatal paraphimosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inflate the balloon as soon as urine appears in the tubing, because the eyelets and the balloon sit at the same level in the bladder"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inflate the balloon with 10 mL of air and then withdraw the catheter until resistance is felt, which seats the balloon at the bladder neck"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withdraw the catheter 5 cm, inflate the balloon with normal saline, and then advance it again until urine flows freely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advance the catheter another 2.5 to 5 cm (1 to 2 inches) further into the bladder before slowly inflating the balloon with sterile water"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Urine flow begins as soon as the catheter eyelets enter the bladder neck. However, the retention balloon is located 1.5 to 2.5 cm behind the eyelets. If the balloon is inflated immediately upon observing initial urine return, the balloon may still reside within the delicate female urethra. Inflating the balloon inside the urethra causes excruciating pain, severe mucosal tearing, and urethral rupture. Therefore, the nurse must advance the catheter an additional 2.5–5 cm (1–2 inches) further into the bladder lumen before inflating the balloon with the manufacturer-specified volume of sterile water (saline can crystallize and jam the inflation valve). Why the other options fail: option a states the error directly, and the premise is false: the balloon sits 1.5 to 2.5 cm behind the eyelets, so urine can flow while the balloon is still in the urethra. Option b withdraws rather than advances, which pulls the balloon back into the urethra and then inflates it there. Option c does the same in two steps and adds saline, which can crystallise in the inflation channel and jam the valve.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Catheter balloon safety: Once urine appears, advance 2.5-5 cm FURTHER into the bladder before inflating balloon. Prevents urethral rupture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Transient hypotension, post-obstructive diuresis with electrolyte depletion, and decompression hematuria"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute kidney injury from reflex vasoconstriction, with oliguria for 24 hours and a rising creatinine that requires fluid restriction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertensive crisis from the sudden fall in intra-abdominal pressure, with headache and a systolic pressure above 180 mmHg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gross haematuria from traumatic insertion, needing continuous bladder irrigation through a three-lumen catheter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Rapid evacuation of a massively distended bladder (> 1,000 mL) abruptly relieves intra-abdominal and pelvic venous compression, leading to splanchnic venous pooling and transient orthostatic hypotension. In addition, relief of prolonged bilateral urinary obstruction can trigger 'post-obstructive diuresis'—a massive polyuria due to impaired renal tubular concentrating ability, risking severe dehydration, hypokalaemia, and hyponatraemia. Rupture of engorged mucosal capillaries in the rapidly collapsing bladder wall can also cause decompression hematuria. The nurse monitors vital signs, true urine output hourly, and serum electrolytes. Why the other options fail: option b predicts oliguria, but the physiological response to relieving prolonged obstruction is the opposite: the tubules have lost their concentrating ability, so large volumes of dilute urine are passed. Option c reverses the haemodynamic direction; decompression causes venodilation and hypotension, not hypertensive crisis. Option d attributes bleeding to insertion trauma, but decompression haematuria comes from ruptured mucosal capillaries in a bladder wall that has suddenly collapsed, and it does not usually need irrigation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bladder decompression risks: Rapid drainage of > 1,000 mL can cause hypotension, post-obstructive diuresis (electrolyte loss), and decompression hematuria.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withdraw the tube completely, let the coughing settle, and reinsert a fresh tube through the other nostril once the saturation has come back above 94 per cent"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withdraw the tube slightly into the nasopharynx, stop advancing, assess airway and saturation, let the client rest, then re-advance with chin tucked to chest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop advancing, leave the tube at its current depth, give oxygen by mask, and then continue advancing slowly while the client swallows between breaths"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to cough forcefully to clear the tube from the airway, then deflate the cuff if present and advance the tube further towards the stomach"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Coughing, choking, dyspnoea, stridor, or cyanosis during NG tube insertion indicates that the tube has entered the larynx/trachea rather than the esophagus. The nurse must immediately stop advancing and pull the tube back into the posterior nasopharynx to relieve airway compromise. Once the client recovers, takes deep breaths, and normal color returns, the nurse instructs the client to tuck their chin down toward their chest (which closes the glottis over the trachea and opens the esophagus) and advance the tube gently as the client takes sips of water through a straw. Why the other options fail: option a is the over-reaction that costs time and trauma; the tube only needs withdrawing to the nasopharynx, not removing and restarting in the other nostril, and the airway must be assessed first. Option c leaves the tube in the airway while oxygen is applied and then advances it further, which pushes it deeper into the trachea. Option d asks a choking client to cough around an obstructing tube and then advances it, worsening the obstruction instead of relieving it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NG tube respiratory distress: Coughing/choking/cyanosis = tube in trachea! Stop immediately, withdraw slightly into nasopharynx, let client breathe, then advance with chin-tuck and swallows.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mucosal sloughing after colectomy, which is expected in the first 72 hours and usually separates to leave healthy pink tissue behind"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Venous congestion from a tight stoma support; apply warm moist compresses and reassess the colour at the next dressing round"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The stoma is necrotic due to vascular compromise/ischemia; notify the surgical team immediately as this requires urgent surgical re-exploration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retained blood pigment from theatre; clean the stoma gently with saline and chlorhexidine to reveal the true mucosal colour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A healthy, viable newly created gastrointestinal stoma is beefy red or pink, moist, warm, and slightly edematous (resembling buccal oral mucosa). A dusky, dark red, purple, brown, or black stoma indicates inadequate arterial blood supply or severe venous congestion resulting in stomal ischemia and necrosis. This is a post-operative surgical emergency. The nurse must immediately notify the surgeon for urgent evaluation, inspect the depth of necrosis using a small glass test tube with a penlight (to evaluate whether necrosis extends below the peritoneal fascial level), and prepare the client for potential surgical revision. Why the other options fail: option a describes a real phenomenon but the wrong one: slough is a superficial yellow-white film over pink mucosa, whereas a uniformly purple-black, flaccid, dry stoma is full-thickness ischaemia. Option b identifies congestion but prescribes a dressing-round reassessment, which delays the only intervention that saves the stoma. Option d treats a surgical emergency as a cleaning problem, and chlorhexidine on an ischaemic stoma adds chemical injury to the existing insult.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stoma viability: Normal stoma = Beefy red/pink & moist. Purple/dark/black stoma = ISCHEMIA/NECROSIS. Immediate surgical notification required.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use the dorsogluteal site with a 21-gauge needle, aspirate, and inject rapidly so that the iron does not track back along the needle track into the fat"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use the deltoid with a 1-inch needle, pinch the muscle belly, and massage the site afterwards to help the viscous fluid disperse through the muscle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use the ventrogluteal site without the Z-track, inject slowly over 30 seconds, and massage the site firmly afterwards to spread the medication through the muscle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ventrogluteal site with the Z-track technique: pull the skin 2.5 cm laterally, insert at 90 degrees, inject slowly, wait 10 seconds, then release"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The ventrogluteal site (gluteus medius and minimus) is the preferred anatomical site for deep intramuscular injections in adults because it is free of major blood vessels and large nerves (unlike the dorsogluteal site, which poses high risk of sciatic nerve injury and superior gluteal artery puncture), and has a thick muscle belly. The Z-track method is mandatory for irritating or dark medications (e.g., iron, haloperidol): displacing the skin and subcutaneous tissue laterally prior to injection creates a zigzag needle track upon needle release, effectively sealing the drug within the deep muscle plane and preventing painful subcutaneous leakage and skin tattooing. Why the other options fail: option a picks the site with the highest risk of sciatic nerve injury and then injects rapidly, which increases the chance of tracking along the track. Option b uses a 1-inch needle in the deltoid, too short to reach muscle in most adults and too small a muscle for 2 mL of an irritating preparation, and massaging drives the iron into subcutaneous fat. Option c chooses the right muscle and omits the Z-track, which is the one step that prevents the subcutaneous leakage and tattooing the irritant causes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IM injections: Ventrogluteal is the #1 safest adult site (no major nerves/vessels). Z-track prevents subcutaneous tracking and staining of irritating drugs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Phlebitis (Grade 3); stop the infusion, remove the cannula, apply a warm moist compress, elevate the arm, and restart access in the opposite arm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chemical phlebitis; slow the infusion, apply a cool compress to the cord, and continue through the same cannula so that the vein is not lost and access is preserved"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Superficial thrombophlebitis; use the other lumen of the same catheter and apply heparinoid gel along the palpable cord to disperse the clot"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infiltration from the cannula tip dislodging into tissue; aspirate the cannula, then restart the infusion proximal to the swelling on the same arm"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Phlebitis is chemical, mechanical, or bacterial inflammation of the vein tunica intima. The classic clinical presentation includes erythema, localized warmth, pain/tenderness, and a palpable indurated venous cord (Grade 3 on the Infusion Nurses Society Phlebitis Scale). Priority management: (1) immediately stop the infusion and remove the cannula; (2) apply a warm, moist compress to relieve inflammation and promote venous blood flow; (3) elevate the affected extremity; (4) document findings and score; and (5) resite the new IV line in the contralateral extremity if ongoing therapy is required. In contrast, infiltration presents with coolness, blanching, and soft edema. Why the other options fail: option b uses the right word and the wrong action: a Grade 3 phlebitis, with a palpable cord 4 cm along the vein, needs the cannula out, not slowed, and continuing to infuse prolongs endothelial injury. Option c keeps the device in the same vessel and treats a chemical injury as a thrombotic one. Option d misidentifies the complication; infiltration produces cool blanched swelling, not warmth and a tender cord, so flushing and restarting proximal to it would be the wrong manoeuvre entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Phlebitis vs Infiltration: Phlebitis = Warmth, redness, palpable cord -> Discontinue cannula + warm compress. Infiltration = Coolness, swelling, pallor.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Select the deltoid, aspirate before injecting to confirm that the needle is not in a vessel, and massage the site afterwards to speed absorption of the dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Abdominal wall at least 5 cm from the umbilicus, pinch a fold, 90 degrees, keep the air bubble, do not aspirate, do not massage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe, inject into the abdomen at 45 degrees, and apply firm pressure to the site afterwards to stop any oozing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inject into the umbilicus itself at 15 degrees, since this is the most vascular part of the abdominal wall and will absorb the dose most reliably"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Best-practice technique for subcutaneous enoxaparin administration: (1) use the anterolateral or posterolateral abdomen (the 'love handles'), alternating sides and staying >= 2 inches away from the umbilicus; (2) DO NOT expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe (the bubble ensures the entire medication dose is delivered and creates an air seal preventing subcutaneous tracking); (3) pinch a skin fold throughout the injection and insert the needle at 90 degrees (or 45 degrees in thin individuals); (4) DO NOT aspirate for blood (aspiration causes tissue damage and hematoma); and (5) DO NOT massage or rub the injection site after needle withdrawal (rubbing causes capillary rupture and severe bruising). Why the other options fail: option a sends a subcutaneous drug into muscle and then aspirates, which is unnecessary for heparin and increases tissue trauma. Option c removes the air bubble that is there to deliver the full dose and seal the track, and firm pressure after the injection promotes the bruising the technique is meant to avoid. Option d injects into umbilical tissue, which is thin, poorly vascular for absorption and painful, and a 15-degree angle is too shallow for a 2 cm pinched fold.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Enoxaparin injection rules: Abdominal wall >= 2 inches from umbilicus + keep air bubble + 90-degree angle + NO aspiration + NO rubbing/massaging.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply continuous suction while inserting the catheter, limit the whole pass to 30 seconds, and give oxygen by nasal cannula between passes to maintain saturation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperventilate the client by hand for 10 seconds, insert the catheter until resistance is met, and apply suction for 20 seconds in a single uninterrupted pass"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hyperoxygenate with 100% O2 for 30 to 60 seconds, advance without suction, suction only on withdrawal for 10 to 15 seconds, 1 minute between passes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instil 5 mL of sterile saline to loosen the secretions, suction for 15 seconds, and repeat every 2 minutes until the chest sounds clear on auscultation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Tracheostomy and endotracheal suctioning carries risks of hypoxemia, mucosal trauma, cardiac arrhythmias, and vagal-mediated bradycardia. Evidence-based protocol mandates: (1) pre-oxygenate (hyperoxygenate) with 100% O2 for 30–60 seconds prior to suctioning; (2) insert catheter gently WITHOUT suction until resistance is met or client coughs, then pull back 1–2 cm; (3) apply suction ONLY while withdrawing and twirling the catheter; (4) limit suction duration to <= 10–15 seconds per pass; (5) re-oxygenate between passes and allow at least 1 minute of recovery; and (6) routine normal saline instillation is strictly contraindicated as it flushes colonized biofilm into lower airways. Why the other options fail: option a sucks during insertion, which strips oxygen from the airway for the whole pass, and 30 seconds is twice the safe duration. Option b pre-oxygenates for too short a time and then applies 20 seconds of continuous suction, which is a recognised cause of hypoxaemia and vagal bradycardia. Option d instils saline, which is no longer recommended because it dislodges biofilm into the lower airways, and repeating every two minutes without recovery time causes cumulative hypoxia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tracheostomy suctioning: Pre-oxygenate with 100% O2 + NO suction during insertion + suction ONLY on withdrawal for <= 10-15 seconds max. Never instill saline.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clamp the tube with padded forceps to stop air entering the chest, releasing the clamp briefly every 15 minutes so that any trapped air can escape"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tape the open end to the drainage unit, position the client on the affected side, and call the medical team to reconnect the system at the bedside"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remove the chest tube completely and cover the insertion site with an occlusive dressing until the medical team arrives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Submerge the distal open end 2 to 4 cm in a bottle of sterile water to re-establish a water seal, and prepare a new drainage unit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$When a chest tube disconnects from its drainage canister, atmospheric air can rush into the negative-pressure pleural space, precipitating a tension pneumothorax or collapsing the lung. The immediate lifesaving action is to submerge the distal open tip of the chest tube 2 to 4 cm (1 to 2 inches) into a sterile bottle of water or normal saline kept at the bedside. This creates an immediate temporary water seal, allowing air to escape from the pleural space during exhalation while preventing air from being sucked back in during inspiration. Clamping the tube is contraindicated because trapped air cannot escape, causing tension pneumothorax. Why the other options fail: option a is the most common wrong answer, and it is dangerous: clamping a disconnected tube in a client with a pneumothorax traps air in the pleural space and can convert a simple pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax. Option b leaves the system open to atmosphere and simply tapes it, so air is drawn back in with every breath. Option c removes a tube that may still be draining and seals the chest, which risks both re-expansion failure and tension.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Chest tube disconnection: Submerge distal tube 2-4 cm in bottle of sterile water to create immediate water seal. NEVER clamp a disconnected chest tube.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop removal, apply Steri-Strips across the separated area to approximate the edges, place a sterile dressing, and notify the surgeon at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continue removing the remaining staples so that the wound edges can be inspected in full, then apply a sterile dressing and inform the surgeon afterwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Approximate the edges with Steri-Strips, cover with a sterile dressing, and arrange wound review at the next outpatient appointment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a sterile dressing, teach the client to splint the abdomen when coughing, and record the finding so that it can be raised at the surgical review"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$When removing surgical sutures or staples, the standard procedure is to remove alternate (every other) staples first to evaluate wound tensile strength and structural integrity. If wound separation (dehiscence) occurs upon removing initial staples, the nurse must STOP removal immediately. The nurse should support the dehiscence with wound closure strips (Steri-Strips) placed perpendicular to the incision, apply a sterile dressing, keep the client calm in low Fowler's position with knees flexed (to reduce abdominal tension), and notify the attending surgeon promptly. Why the other options fail: option b removes the remaining staples, which are the only thing still holding a wound that has already begun to fail, and turns an early dehiscence into a full one. Option c does the right local action and then defers the escalation: dehiscence can progress to evisceration, so the surgeon is notified now, not at an outpatient review. Option d documents and teaches but neither supports the wound nor escalates it, which is the error that turns a dehiscence into a returning theatre case.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Staple removal & dehiscence: If edges separate during removal -> STOP REMOVAL IMMEDIATELY. Apply Steri-Strips to support incision and notify surgeon.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Compress at 80 to 100 per minute to a depth of 4 cm, allowing full recoil, and pause for 15 seconds after every 30 compressions to check for a pulse"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rate 100 to 120 per minute, depth at least 5 cm (max 6 cm), full recoil after each compression, pauses under 10 seconds, ratio 30:2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Compress at 100 to 120 per minute to a depth of 6 cm without allowing full recoil, so that coronary perfusion pressure is maintained continuously"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Compress at 100 to 120 per minute to a depth of 5 cm, pausing after every 30 compressions for two rescue breaths of 2 seconds each"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$High-quality CPR is the primary determinant of cardiac arrest survival: (1) Compression rate of 100 to 120 beats per minute; (2) Compression depth of at least 5 cm (2 inches) but not exceeding 6 cm (2.4 inches) in adults; (3) Allow full chest recoil after each compression (leaning prevents coronary perfusion by impeding venous return); (4) Minimize all pauses in compressions (chest compression fraction > 60–80%, pauses < 10 seconds); and (5) Compression-to-ventilation ratio of 30:2 in adults without advanced airway, rotating compressors every 2 minutes to prevent fatigue. Why the other options fail: option a slows the rate below 100 and reduces depth below 5 cm, both of which cut cardiac output, and a 15-second pulse check every cycle destroys the compression fraction. Option c gets rate and depth right and then forbids recoil, which raises intrathoracic pressure and reduces venous return, the exact mechanism that makes leaning harmful. Option d is close but the pauses exceed 10 seconds once the two breaths are given slowly, and no target for minimising interruption is stated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High-quality CPR metrics: Rate 100-120/min + Depth 5-6 cm (2-2.4 inches) + Full recoil (don't lean) + Minimize pauses (< 10 s) + 30:2 ratio.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the client to drink 2 litres of water before the procedure so the bladder is easy to identify on the scan, and give a colloid bolus for every 3 litres drained"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Catheterise the client and clamp the catheter during drainage, giving albumin only if the systolic pressure falls after the tap has been completed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Have the client void immediately before the tap to avoid puncturing the bladder; give IV albumin 6 to 8 g per litre of ascites removed once over 5 litres"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fast the client from midnight and give fresh frozen plasma after the tap rather than albumin, to replace the volume that has been removed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Prior to abdominal paracentesis, having the client void immediately beforehand is critical: an empty bladder collapses into the deep pelvic cavity, preventing catastrophic accidental trocar laceration or perforation of the urinary bladder. For large-volume paracentesis (> 5 liters of ascitic fluid removed), rapid fluid shifts cause profound splanchnic vasodilation and arterial hypovolemia (paracentesis-induced circulatory dysfunction, PICD), leading to hepatorenal syndrome and death. To maintain effective oncotic pressure and intravascular volume, intravenous salt-poor albumin (6–8 g per liter of ascites drained beyond 5 L) is infused. Why the other options fail: option a does the reverse of the preparation: a full bladder rises out of the pelvis into the path of the trocar, which is the injury the instruction exists to prevent. Option b prevents puncture but gives albumin only after hypotension appears, when the point of prophylaxis was to prevent it. Option d substitutes plasma for albumin, which carries transfusion risk without the oncotic effect that reverses the splanchnic vasodilation of PICD.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Paracentesis care: VOID immediately before procedure (prevents bladder puncture). For > 5 L drained, infuse IV albumin (6-8 g/L) to prevent circulatory collapse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place a pillow under the shoulders and tilt the bed head-down, so that blood drains away from the face and the family can see the body more clearly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Close the eyes and jaw, leave the bed flat, and open the window to cool the room, since cooling the body prevents discoloration of the face"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wash the body, remove all tubes and lines, and keep the head flat under a sheet until the family arrives so that the face is not disturbed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Raise the head of the bed 15 to 30 degrees or put a pillow under the head to prevent facial lividity, close the eyes, and support the jaw"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Post-mortem care honors client dignity and provides compassionate closure for grieving families: (1) elevate the head of the bed 15–30 degrees or place a pillow under the head promptly to prevent dependent blood pooling (livor mortis) in the face, which causes purple discoloration; (2) close the eyelids gently; (3) insert dentures and support the lower jaw with a rolled towel under the chin before rigor mortis sets in; (4) remove non-forensic invasive tubes/catheters (unless an autopsy/coroner's inquest is mandated); (5) cleanse soiled body areas and replace soiled bed linens; and (6) position clean sheets neatly to the shoulders for family viewing. Why the other options fail: option a tilts the head down, which is the fastest way to produce the facial lividity the question asks how to avoid, and it does so for a reason (better visibility) that sounds considerate. Option b leaves the body flat, so dependent pooling continues, and cooling the room does not stop it. Option c keeps the head flat for the same cosmetic reason and reaches it by the opposite route, and it also strips every line, which pre-empts a coroner's or autopsy decision that is not the nurse's to make.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Post-mortem care: Elevate head with pillow (prevents facial blood pooling/lividity) + close eyes & support jaw + wash & clean linens before family viewing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20 rows updated.
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch7.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 7: INTEG module distractor repair (20 items).
--
-- Same defect as TCAM, NUTR and PROC. Examples replaced:
--   'Conclude that cyanosis and pallor cannot be clinically assessed in
--    darkly pigmented skin'
--   'Surgically amputate the entire lower extremity immediately without
--    attempting conservative wound dressings'
--   'Spray the children's entire bodies with commercial petroleum fly spray
--    daily for one month'
--   'Clobetasol should only be taken orally with black coffee'
--
-- Keyed option was the longest in 19 of 20 items, mean key/distractor
-- length ratio 2.18. Seventeen items rewritten; NG-MOD-INTEG-004 (pressure
-- injury stages), -006 (Rule of Nines figures) and -014 (four genuine
-- dermatological signs) already had legitimate competing options and were
-- left alone apart from adding a distractor block where relevant.
--
-- Answer letters unchanged. Key option TEXT trimmed on 11 items for length
-- parity; the clinical content of each key is preserved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inspect the conjunctivae, oral mucous membranes, and lips for ashen-grey or blue discoloration, and examine the palms and nail beds rather than relying on general facial skin tone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess the anterior forearm in ordinary room light, since this site is least exposed to the sun and gives the truest reading of the underlying haemoglobin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Look for bright pink flushing over the abdomen, because the increased cutaneous blood flow of sepsis is most visible where the skin is thinnest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Record that cyanosis and pallor cannot be assessed at all in this skin tone, and rely on the arterial blood gas and serum lactate to detect hypoperfusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In darkly pigmented skin, high epidermal melanin concentrations mask the subtle cutaneous color changes seen in lighter skin. Pallor presents not as obvious whiteness, but as a dull, ashen-grey color in dark skin or a yellowish-brown tinge in olive/brown skin. Cyanosis in dark-skinned individuals is best detected by inspecting areas of least pigmentation and rich capillary vascularity: the conjunctivae, buccal mucosa, hard palate, tongue, lips, and palmar surfaces of hands and soles of feet. Additionally, the nurse must palpate the skin for localized warmth, edema, and induration rather than relying solely on erythema. Why the other options fail: option b is how blood pressure and rashes are often checked, but the forearm carries the same melanin as the face and gives no better signal. Option c looks for flushing rather than for the ashen or blue change the question asks about, and flushing in sepsis is inconsistent. Option d is the belief the item exists to correct: central and mucosal surfaces are assessable in every skin tone, and relying on the gas misses the hours before lactate rises.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dark skin assessment: Inspect mucous membranes (buccal mucosa, tongue, conjunctivae) and palms/soles for pallor/cyanosis; palpate for warmth/induration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Papule: A raised, fluid-filled lesion more than 1 cm across, sitting deep in the subcutaneous fat and collapsing when pressure is applied"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Macule: A flat, non-palpable, circumscribed area of skin color change measuring less than 1 cm in diameter without elevation or depression"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bulla: A solid, raised, firm lesion formed from dense fibrous scar tissue that persists after the original injury has healed over"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wheal: A permanent cavity that extends through the dermis into the underlying muscle, leaving a depressed scar once it closes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Primary skin lesion taxonomy is fundamental to clinical dermatology: (1) A macule is a flat, non-palpable change in skin color < 1 cm (e.g., freckle, petechia); if > 1 cm, it is a patch. (2) A papule is an elevated, palpable, solid mass < 1 cm (e.g., mole, wart); if > 1 cm, it is a plaque. (3) A vesicle is an elevated cavity filled with clear serous fluid <= 0.5–1 cm (e.g., herpes simplex); if > 1 cm, it is a bulla (blister). (4) A wheal is a transient, raised, edematous, erythematous area with irregular borders caused by dermal fluid extravasation (e.g., urticaria/hives). Why the other options fail: option a attaches a fluid-filled cavity and a deep location to a papule, which is solid and superficial, and a fluid-filled lesion over 1 cm is a bulla rather than a papule. Option c describes a scar or a nodule, not a blister; a bulla is defined by being fluid-filled and larger than 1 cm. Option d describes an ulcer reaching muscle, whereas a wheal is a transient oedematous swelling of the dermis that resolves without scarring.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lesion taxonomy: Macule = flat color change < 1 cm. Papule = solid raised < 1 cm. Vesicle = fluid blister < 1 cm. Bulla = fluid blister > 1 cm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leave the slough undisturbed so that the wound is not traumatised, and cover the ulcer with a dry dressing changed once weekly to keep the wound bed warm and reduce disturbance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse the ulcer with hydrogen peroxide and leave it open to the air between dressing changes, so that the exudate can dry and the adherent slough lifts away on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Debride the non-viable slough and manage the heavy exudate with an absorbent dressing, keeping the bed moist while protecting the surrounding skin from maceration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a hydrocolloid sheet and review at seven days, since an occlusive dressing softens slough and absorbs exudate without any need for active debridement"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The TIME framework optimizes the chronic wound microenvironment: (1) Tissue non-viable: Slough and necrotic eschar promote bacterial biofilm and must be debrided (autolytic, enzymatic, sharp, or mechanical); (2) Infection/Inflammation: Control bacterial burden and biofilm; (3) Moisture imbalance: Chronic wounds heal fastest in a physiological moist (not wet, not dry) environment; excessive exudate macerates surrounding skin and contains destructive metalloproteinases, requiring absorbent dressings (calcium alginates, hydrofibers, foam); and (4) Edge advancement: Epidermal cells require a healthy granulating matrix to migrate across wound margins. Why the other options fail: option a addresses neither letter of the framework: slough left in place harbours biofilm, and a dry weekly dressing violates moisture balance in a heavily exuding wound. Option b dries the bed out to loosen slough, which is the opposite of moist wound healing, and hydrogen peroxide is cytotoxic to the fibroblasts trying to close the wound. Option d is the nearest miss: hydrocolloid does soften slough autolytically, but it cannot cope with heavy exudate and will leak and macerate the peri-wound skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TIME wound care: Non-viable slough requires debridement + excess exudate requires absorbent dressing to balance moisture and prevent maceration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stage 1 pressure injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stage 3 pressure injury"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Unstageable pressure injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stage 2 pressure injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$According to NPUAP/EPUAP staging: Stage 1 is intact skin with localized non-blanchable erythema (or localized warmth, firmness, or discoloration in dark skin). Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss of skin with exposed dermis, presenting as a shallow open ulcer with a viable, red or pink, moist wound bed without slough, eschar, or granulation tissue, OR presenting as an intact or open/ruptured serum-filled blister. Stage 3 involves full-thickness skin loss with visible adipose tissue and granulation; Stage 4 involves exposed bone, tendon, or muscle. Unstageable occurs when slough or eschar completely obscures the base.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pressure injury staging: Partial-thickness dermis loss with red/pink bed OR intact serum blister = Stage 2. Full-thickness into fat = Stage 3.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reposition every 2 hours with a 30-degree lateral tilt, use a pressure-redistributing mattress, float the heels on pillows, and keep the skin dry and barrier-protected"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reposition every four hours and massage the reddened prominences with a moisturising cream at each turn, since massage restores capillary blood flow to ischaemic tissue"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nurse the client in the high Fowler position at 90 degrees and place a ring cushion under the sacrum, so that pressure is distributed around rather than over the bony point"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reposition every two hours but keep the head of the bed elevated at 60 degrees for comfort, and use a donut cushion to lift the sacrum clear of the mattress"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A Braden score <= 12 signifies high risk for pressure injury development (scores range from 6 to 23; lower scores mean higher risk). Evidence-based prevention mandates: (1) frequent repositioning (at least every 2 hours in bed, every 1 hour in a chair); (2) using a 30-degree lateral tilted position (avoids direct vertical pressure on the trochanter and sacrum); (3) floating heels off the mattress using pillows under calves; (4) pressure-redistribution support surfaces; (5) moisture barriers for incontinence; and (6) avoiding elevation of the head of the bed > 30 degrees (reduces shear forces). Vigorously massaging bony prominences causes deep tissue capillary damage. Why the other options fail: option b doubles the interval and adds massage over an area that may already be ischaemic, which damages the deep capillary bed rather than restoring flow. Option c raises the head to 90 degrees, which converts pressure into shearing force through the sacrum, and a ring cushion concentrates pressure on the rim. Option d gets the interval right and then does both of the things that cause harm: sustained 60-degree elevation and a donut cushion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pressure ulcer prevention: Reposition q2h + 30-degree tilt + float heels + pressure-relieving mattress. NEVER massage bony prominences or use donut rings.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "27% TBSA"}, {"id": "b", "text": "36% TBSA"}, {"id": "c", "text": "45% TBSA"}, {"id": "d", "text": "18% TBSA"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under the adult Wallace Rule of Nines: Head and neck = 9%; Anterior trunk = 18% (chest 9% + abdomen 9%); Posterior trunk = 18% (upper back 9% + lower back/buttocks 9%); Each arm = 9% total (anterior 4.5% + posterior 4.5%); Each leg = 18% total (anterior 9% + posterior 9%); Perineum/genitalia = 1%. In this client: Anterior chest (9%) + Entire abdomen (9%) + Entire left arm (9%) + Anterior left leg (9%) = 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 36% TBSA.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rule of Nines calculation: Chest (9%) + Abdomen (9%) + Entire arm (9%) + Anterior leg (9%) = 36% TBSA.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 5,600 mL; infuse half in the first 8 hours and the remainder over 16 hours, targeting a urine output of 30 to 50 mL per hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 22,400 mL; give the whole volume in the first 8 hours, because capillary leak is greatest immediately after the burn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Total 11,200 mL; give 5,600 mL in the first 8 hours from injury, at 800 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours, then 5,600 mL over 16 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 11,200 mL; infuse at 700 mL per hour evenly across the first 16 hours, since the first 8 hours have already elapsed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Parkland (Baxter) formula states: Total 24-hour fluid = 4 mL x weight (kg) x % TBSA burns. For this client: 4 mL x 70 kg x 40% = 11,200 mL of balanced crystalloid (Lactated Ringer's). Delivery schedule: 50% (5,600 mL) must be infused within the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF INJURY, and the remaining 50% (5,600 mL) over the next 16 hours (350 mL/hr). Because 1 hour elapsed before arrival, the initial 5,600 mL must be infused over the remaining 7 hours: 5,600 mL / 7 hours = 800 mL/hr. The nurse titrates this rate to maintain adult urine output at 0.5–1.0 mL/kg/hr (30–50 mL/hr). Why the other options fail: option a halves the formula, which delivers about half the resuscitation volume an adult with 40 per cent burns needs and will leave the client oliguric. Option b doubles the coefficient and then front-loads everything into the first 8 hours, which is the fastest route to pulmonary oedema. Option d gets the total right, which makes it the hardest distractor, but spreads it evenly and so abandons the 50 per cent rule and the eight-hour clock that starts at the time of injury, not arrival.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parkland resuscitation: 4 mL x kg x % TBSA. 50% given in first 8 hours FROM TIME OF BURN (adjust for delay). Target urine output 0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give humidified oxygen by face mask and admit for observation, since a normal PaO2 shows the airway is not yet compromised"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the family that singed nasal hair is a cosmetic finding, and treat the hoarseness with voice rest and warm fluids"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Recheck the arterial blood gas in 4 hours and call the anaesthetist if the stridor has worsened by then, to avoid an unnecessary tube"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prepare immediately for endotracheal intubation before progressive laryngeal edema produces complete upper airway obstruction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Physical findings of singed nasal hairs, facial burns, carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, and STRIDOR are pathognomonic of acute thermal inhalation injury and impending upper airway compromise. Supraglottic thermal injury causes rapid, massive laryngeal and vocal cord edema that peaks within 12–24 hours. Once stridor appears, the airway lumen is critically narrowed; delayed intubation frequently becomes impossible due to anatomical distortion, necessitating emergency surgical cricothyroidotomy. Proactive, early endotracheal intubation is the definitive lifesaving priority. Why the other options fail: option a rests on the normal PaO2, which is the trap in the stem: oxygenation is preserved until the airway closes, so a normal gas does not exclude an impending obstruction. Option b treats two of the four warning signs as trivial. Option c is the classic delay: stridor means the lumen is already critically narrowed, and by the time it worsens the swelling can make intubation impossible, leaving cricothyroidotomy as the only route.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inhalation injury: Facial burns + soot + hoarseness/stridor = impending complete airway closure. Secure airway with IMMEDIATE intubation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Extremity compartment syndrome caused by unyielding, inelastic burn eschar and tissue edema; prepare immediately for urgent bedside escharotomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal post-burn inflammation; elevate the limb above the level of the heart and give aspirin for the throbbing pain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Deep vein thrombosis of the forearm; start low molecular weight heparin and apply a warm compress along the tender cord"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nerve injury from the burn itself; document the sensory loss and reassess the hand at the next dressing round"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Circumferential full-thickness burns produce an inelastic, rigid, leathery rind of dead tissue (eschar). As massive fluid resuscitation expands interstitial edema within the limb, intracompartmental pressures rise rapidly, exceeding capillary perfusion pressure (> 30 mmHg). This leads to compartment syndrome: progressive neurovascular compromise characterized by the '6 Ps' (pain out of proportion/pain on passive stretch, paresthesia, pallor, pulselessness, poikilothermia, paralysis). Emergency bedside escharotomy (linear surgical incisions through the burned eschar down to subcutaneous fat) is mandatory to release constriction and restore arterial flow. Why the other options fail: option b interprets pain on passive stretch and a 5-second capillary refill as expected inflammation, and aspirin is irrelevant to a pressure problem that needs the eschar released. Option c is a plausible vascular cause of a faint pulse, but a thrombus does not produce excruciating pain on passive finger extension, and heparin would waste the window. Option d explains the sensory finding and ignores the vascular ones, and reassigning it to the next round delays a limb-threatening procedure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Circumferential burn danger: Faint pulse + pain on passive stretch = Compartment syndrome. Prepare for immediate emergency escharotomy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erysipelas, which can be managed at home with oral paracetamol and flucloxacillin, since the erythema is well demarcated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Necrotizing fasciitis (a surgical emergency requiring immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and emergency surgical debridement)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contact dermatitis from a laundry detergent; stop the product and apply a topical corticosteroid to the affected leg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Venous stasis pigmentation with a superimposed ulcer; apply compression bandaging and refer to the leg ulcer clinic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Necrotizing fasciitis is a fulminant, rapidly progressive bacterial infection of the deep subcutaneous tissue and fascia with a mortality rate exceeding 25–40%. Cardinal warning signs distinguishing necrotizing fasciitis from benign cellulitis are: (1) severe, exquisite pain markedly OUT OF PROPORTION to superficial clinical findings; (2) rapid progression across marked skin margins within hours; (3) palpable subcutaneous crepitus (gas production from anaerobic pathogens); (4) bullae, skin necrosis, or dishwater-gray fluid discharge; and (5) early hemodynamic instability (septic shock). Immediate surgical fasciotomy and aggressive debridement are vital. Why the other options fail: option a names the right family of infection and the wrong depth: erysipelas is superficial, does not produce crepitus or bullae, and does not cause shock, so oral treatment at home would be fatal. Option c fits the erythema and none of the rest, and a steroid would suppress the only defence the tissue has. Option d is a chronic picture that develops over months, not an acute presentation with tachycardia, hypotension and gas in the tissues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Necrotizing fasciitis: Pain out of proportion to skin signs + crepitus/bullae + shock = Surgical emergency. Immediate radical debridement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Impetigo spreads only by direct contact with the crusts, so the child can attend school provided the lesions are covered with a light bandage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Scrub the crusts off with a loofah at each bath so the ointment reaches the skin, and keep the child home until every lesion has healed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Highly contagious: strict hand hygiene, separate towels, soak and remove crusts before mupirocin, and watch for tea-coloured urine (post-streptococcal GN)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Impetigo is a self-limiting viral rash; keep the skin dry, avoid antibiotics, and return only if the child develops a fever or the rash spreads"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Impetigo is a highly contagious superficial bacterial skin infection caused primarily by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep). Lesions typically begin as fragile vesicles that rupture, releasing exudate that dries into characteristic 'honey-colored crusts'. Nursing care focuses on: (1) preventing transmission (contact precautions, hand hygiene, separate towels/bedding, keeping child home until 24 hours of antimicrobial therapy); (2) crust removal (soaking with warm soapy water before applying topical mupirocin 2%); and (3) monitoring for post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (periorbital edema, gross hematuria/tea-colored urine, hypertension) 1–3 weeks post-infection. Why the other options fail: option b has the right instinct about crusts and the wrong method, since scrubbing spreads the exudate and breaks the skin further. Option d misclassifies a bacterial infection as viral and withholds the treatment that shortens it. Option a is the most tempting, because exclusion from school is genuinely no longer required after 24 hours of treatment, but the option drops the household measures and the renal follow-up that the question is asking for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Impetigo management: Honey-colored crusts = highly contagious. Warm soak crusts + mupirocin ointment + monitor for tea-colored urine (PSGN).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply permethrin only to the visible burrows on the wrists and wash it off after ten minutes, since the mite is confined to the burrow itself"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treat only the household members who are currently itchy, because those without symptoms are not infested and do not need any treatment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight, but delay treating the household contacts until the index child is symptom-free"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight for 8 to 14 hours, treat all household contacts at the same time, and hot-wash bedding used in the past 3 days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Scabies is an infestation caused by the microscopic mite Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis. Mites burrow into the stratum corneum and deposit eggs/feces, triggering a delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction characterized by severe nocturnal pruritus and pathognomonic linear burrows in interdigital web spaces, flexor wrists, axillae, umbilicus, and genitalia. Successful eradication mandates: (1) topical 5% permethrin cream applied from the jawline/neck down over the entire body (including under fingernails and between toes; in infants, the scalp and face are included) for 8–14 hours; (2) treating ALL household contacts simultaneously regardless of symptoms (incubation period is 4–6 weeks); and (3) hot-water laundering of linens. Why the other options fail: option a treats only what can be seen, but mites are present in clinically normal skin between burrows, and ten minutes is far short of the 8 to 14 hours needed for the cream to work. Option b ignores the four to six week incubation period, during which a contact is infectious without itching. Option c is the best of the three and still wrong on the one point that drives reinfestation: simultaneous treatment, because a delay simply re-infests the treated child.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Scabies eradication: 5% permethrin from neck down overnight (8-14 h) + treat ALL household contacts simultaneously + wash bedding in hot water.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bathe in lukewarm water for 10 minutes, pat damp, and apply a thick fragrance-free emollient within 3 minutes (the 'soak and seal' method)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bathe the child three times daily in hot soapy water and scrub the skin with a dry loofah, so that the scale is removed before the emollient is applied"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the skin as dry as possible and avoid all moisturisers, because emollients trap bacteria in the lichenified plaques and worsen the eczema"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give a daily dilute bleach bath and leave the skin to air dry completely before applying any topical corticosteroid to the raw creases"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal skin barrier dysfunction (filaggrin deficiency), transepidermal water loss, and immune dysregulation ('the itch that rashes'). The cornerstone of maintenance therapy is the 'soak and seal' technique: (1) short lukewarm baths (not hot water, which strips natural lipids); (2) mild soap-free cleansers; (3) patting skin lightly so it remains slightly damp; and (4) liberal application of lipid-rich, fragrance-free emollients (ointments like petrolatum or thick creams) within 3 minutes of stepping out of the water to lock in hydration. Topical corticosteroids are reserved for acute inflammatory flares. Why the other options fail: option b uses heat and friction, both of which strip the lipid barrier and drive the itch-scratch cycle the plan exists to break. Option c inverts the treatment: emollients are the maintenance therapy, not a cause of infection. Option d borrows a real adjunct, dilute bleach bathing, and then air-dries the skin and jumps to a steroid, which skips the emollient that replaces the missing barrier.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Eczema barrier repair: Soak and seal -> Lukewarm bath + pat damp + apply thick ointment/cream within 3 minutes. Avoid hot water and fragrances.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tinea corporis exhibiting the Darier sign"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Plaque psoriasis exhibiting the Auspitz sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Malignant melanoma exhibiting the Breslow phenomenon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lichen planus exhibiting Wickham striae"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Plaque psoriasis (psoriasis vulgaris) is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory dermatosis driven by T-lymphocytes and cytokines (IL-17, IL-23, TNF-alpha), resulting in hyperproliferation of epidermal keratinocytes (epidermal turnover accelerates from 28 days to 3–4 days). The hallmark lesions are sharply demarcated erythematous plaques covered with silvery, micaceous scales on extensor surfaces. The Auspitz sign is the appearance of punctate capillary bleeding spots when scales are scraped off, caused by thinned suprapapillary epidermal plates over dilated, tortuous dermal capillaries. Lesions developing at sites of skin trauma demonstrate the Koebner phenomenon.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Psoriasis hallmarks: Silvery-white scales on extensor surfaces + pinpoint bleeding when scraped (Auspitz sign) + new lesions at trauma sites (Koebner).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A morbilliform drug exanthem; stop the cotrimoxazole, give an oral antihistamine, and review in the outpatient clinic in one week"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; start intravenous flucloxacillin and keep the skin moist with paraffin gauze until it re-epithelialises"}, {"id": "c", "text": "TEN/SJS overlap; stop the sulfonamide immediately and transfer to a burns intensive care unit for fluid and wound management"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Erythema multiforme major; continue the antibiotic, give systemic corticosteroids, and reassess the mucosal lesions daily on the ward"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) are life-threatening Severe Cutaneous Adverse Reactions (SCAR) characterized by widespread epidermal apoptosis and detachment triggered primarily by medications (sulfonamides, antiepileptics like carbamazepine/phenytoin, allopurinol, nevirapine). SJS involves < 10% TBSA epidermal detachment; SJS/TEN overlap involves 10–30% TBSA; TEN involves > 30% TBSA. Mucosal involvement (lips, oral cavity, eyes, genitalia) is present in > 90% of cases. A positive Nikolsky sign (sloughing of epidermis with light tangential shear) reflects epidermal-dermal cleavage. Immediate cessation of the causative drug and transfer to a specialized burn unit are vital. Why the other options fail: option a describes the common benign drug rash and misses mucosal erosion and a positive Nikolsky sign, either of which rules it out, and a week's outpatient review is far too slow. Option b is a real blistering disease but it is caused by staphylococcal toxin in young children, splits at the granular layer, and does not follow a sulfonamide. Option d keeps the culprit drug running, which is the single most dangerous thing to do in TEN.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SJS / TEN: Sulfonamide trigger + mucosal erosion + positive Nikolsky sign (epidermal sloughing). STOP DRUG IMMEDIATELY and treat like severe burns in ICU.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Superficial spreading melanoma; the commonest subtype in all populations, arising on sun-exposed skin and evaluated with the ABCDE criteria"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Benign subungual lentigo; a harmless pigment band that needs no biopsy and can be reviewed yearly with clinical photography"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Basal cell carcinoma; a slow-growing, pearly, sun-related tumour of the face that rarely metastasises and is treated by excision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acral lentiginous melanoma, the commonest subtype in Black populations, arising on palms, soles and nail beds; assessed with ABCDE"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$While overall melanoma incidence is lower in dark-skinned individuals due to the photoprotective effect of eumelanin against UV radiation, when melanoma does occur in African, Black, and Asian populations, it is disproportionately Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM), accounting for 50–70% of all melanomas in these populations. Unlike other melanomas, ALM is NOT related to UV radiation/sun exposure and characteristically develops on glabrous skin: palms, soles, and subungual nail beds (Hutchinson's sign). The ABCDE mnemonic (Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, Evolution/change) guides identification, and suspicious lesions mandate urgent excisional biopsy. Why the other options fail: option a is the commonest melanoma overall and the wrong answer for this population, and it is described as sun-related, which the acral form is not. Option b reassures on a lesion with irregular borders that is enlarging, which is the description that mandates biopsy. Option c is a pigmented facial tumour, not a subungual or plantar one, and it does not fit an enlarging irregular macule on the sole.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Melanoma in dark skin: Acral lentiginous melanoma (palms, soles, nail beds) is NOT sun-related and is the most common subtype in Black populations. Use ABCDE.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erythema toxicum neonatorum; a benign, self-limiting newborn eruption that needs no treatment and resolves within 1 to 2 weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Congenital syphilis; start intravenous benzylpenicillin, screen the mother, and isolate the infant from other babies on the postnatal ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; obtain blood cultures, start intravenous flucloxacillin, and prepare the infant for skin grafting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neonatal herpes simplex infection; perform a lumbar puncture and start high-dose intravenous aciclovir on the neonatal unit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Erythema toxicum neonatorum is the most common benign cutaneous eruption in healthy full-term neonates (affecting 30–70% of newborns), typically appearing on days 1–4 of life. It presents with erythematous macules, papules, and sterile pustules with a classic 'flea-bitten' appearance over the trunk and proximal extremities, sparing palms and soles. Wright or Giemsa staining of pustular fluid reveals eosinophils (distinguishing it from bacterial or candidal pustules with neutrophils). It is self-limiting, causes no systemic symptoms, and resolves spontaneously within 7–14 days without treatment. Parental reassurance is the only indicated intervention. Why the other options fail: option b is the invasive infection to consider in a sick neonate, but this infant is feeding, afebrile and active, and syphilis does not produce eosinophil-filled pustules. Option c causes widespread tender erythema and epidermal peeling rather than scattered flea-bitten pustules on an erythematous base. Option d is the correct response to a genuinely unwell baby with vesicles, and none of the features that define herpes, such as grouped vesicles, are present.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Newborn rashes: Flea-bitten pustules on healthy term neonate + eosinophils on smear = Erythema toxicum neonatorum. Benign, self-limiting -> Reassure parents.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trim the flap away with scissors and clean the exposed dermis with alcohol, since a raised flap traps bacteria and delays epithelial migration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse with normal saline, re-approximate the flap without stretching, and secure it with a silicone non-adherent dressing or retention bandage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a high-adhesion zinc oxide tape directly over the raw dermis and change it every four hours, so that the wound is kept clean and visible"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the wound uncovered and open to the air, so that a firm scab can form over the denuded dermis and protect it from further shearing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In older adults, epidermal thinning, flattening of the dermal-epidermal junction (rete ridges), loss of dermal collagen, and microvascular atrophy make the skin exceptionally fragile, predisposing to skin tears from minor shear or friction. According to the International Skin Tear Advisory Panel (ISTAP): for a Type 1 skin tear (where the flap can cover the entire wound bed), the nurse must: (1) gently irrigate with normal saline; (2) gently re-approximate the viable epidermal flap using a moist sterile swab or gloved finger; (3) secure with a non-adherent dressing (silicone foam or mesh); and (4) NEVER apply adhesive tape directly to the fragile peri-wound skin. Why the other options fail: option a discards viable tissue that acts as a natural biological dressing and applies alcohol to a raw dermis, which is painful and cytotoxic. Option c uses the adhesive the guidance explicitly warns against, on the same fragile peri-wound skin that tore to begin with, and changes it four-hourly. Option d leaves the denuded dermis to dry and scab, which is slower and more painful, and gives no protection against the next shear.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Skin tear management: Cleanse gently -> Roll back/re-approximate viable flap -> Cover with non-adherent silicone dressing. NO adhesive tape on frail skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply the entire 60 g tube like thick cake icing twice daily, so that the eczema is fully suppressed before the tube runs out rather than under-treated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Potent topical steroids are safe on any site for any duration, so apply clobetasol to the eyelids and face twice daily and continue it indefinitely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1 FTU (about 0.5 g from the finger crease to tip) covers two adult handprints; use the least amount needed and avoid potent steroids on face, groin and axillae"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a fingertip unit measuring about 5 g to the back only, since the unit is calculated for the trunk and larger areas need proportionally more"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Fingertip Unit (FTU) is the standardized metric for topical dermatological medications: 1 FTU is the amount of ointment/cream expressed from a standard 5 mm nozzle tube from the distal skin crease to the tip of an adult index finger (~0.5 g). One FTU adequately covers a surface area equivalent to TWO adult handprints (with fingers joined). Potent and super-potent topical corticosteroids (like clobetasol) carry serious risks of local adverse effects (epidermal atrophy, telangiectasia, striae, hypopigmentation, secondary infection) and systemic absorption (HPA axis suppression, Cushing syndrome). They are strictly avoided on high-absorption, thin-skinned areas such as the face, neck, groin, and axillae. Why the other options fail: option a wastes the tube and increases systemic absorption without improving the flare, and a whole-body application of a super-potent steroid is a route to adrenal suppression. Option b states the opposite of the guidance: thin sites absorb far more per unit area, so the face, groin and axillae are exactly where atrophy and striae appear first. Option d inflates the unit tenfold, which turns a measure of 0.5 g into 5 g per application.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Topical steroid dosing: 1 FTU = 0.5 g = 2 adult handprints. Use sparingly, short-term. Avoid potent steroids on face, groin, and axillae (atrophy risk).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A thin semi-permeable film dressing, which seals bacteria out and lets exudate evaporate through the membrane between changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dry single-ply cotton gauze, changed whenever it becomes saturated, because cotton wicks exudate away from the bed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A hydrocolloid sheet changed every three days, since it forms a gel that absorbs exudate and protects the wound"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Calcium alginate or hydrofiber rope/pad dressings covered by an absorbent secondary foam dressing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Dressing selection must match wound exudate levels according to the moisture balance principle. Calcium alginates (derived from seaweed) and hydrofiber dressings are highly absorbent, capable of absorbing 15–20 times their weight in exudate. When contacting wound fluid, sodium ions in exudate exchange with calcium ions in the alginate, forming a hydrophilic non-adherent gel that traps bacteria, maintains moisture balance, and prevents periwound maceration. In contrast, thin film dressings have zero absorption capacity and would trap fluid causing massive maceration. Dry gauze adheres to the wound and traumatizes new capillary buds upon removal. Why the other options fail: option a is the dressing for a wound with no exudate at all; a film has no absorption capacity, so fluid pools beneath it and macerates the skin it was meant to protect. Option b looks absorbent and is not: gauze holds little, dries out, and adheres to a granulating bed, so every change strips new tissue. Option c is right for moderate exudate and overwhelmed by copious drainage, and a three-day interval with pads saturating in two hours guarantees leakage.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dressing selection: Heavy exudate = Calcium alginate or Hydrofiber + Foam. Dry/necrotic wound = Hydrogel. Minimal drainage = Thin film / Hydrocolloid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20 rows updated.
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch8.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 8: FUND module rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- FUND differs from TCAM, NUTR, PROC and INTEG: its distractors were
-- already legitimate competing answers (four real pressure-injury stages,
-- four real oxygen devices, four real IM sites). No option was replaced
-- to remove an absurdity.
--
-- Two defects remained:
--   1. Not one rationale had a structured distractor block, so criterion
--      (c) failed on all 20 even though several discussed distractors in
--      passing. All 20 now carry an explicit 'Why the other options fail'.
--   2. The keyed option was the longest in 17 of 20 (mean ratio 1.60).
--      Ten items had their SHORT distractors lengthened; no key text was
--      trimmed and no key letter moved. Mean ratio now 1.02, worst 1.47.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client will understand the importance of drinking adequate fluids by discharge"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client will maintain oral intake of at least 1,500 mL of fluids every 24 hours within 48 hours of admission"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The nurse will administer 2 liters of normal saline intravenously over 24 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client will show no signs of dehydration and feel much better tomorrow"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In the nursing process, a measurable patient-centered outcome goal must contain a specific action, measurable criteria, a realistic target, and a defined timeframe. Option B clearly specifies who (the client), what action (maintain oral intake of at least 1,500 mL of fluids), and the exact timeframe (every 24 hours within 48 hours of admission). Option A is vague ('understand' is not directly measurable); Option C describes a nurse's action rather than a patient outcome; and Option D uses subjective criteria ('feel much better') lacking objective parameters. Why the other options fail: option a uses 'understand', which cannot be observed or measured, so the goal can never be scored as met or unmet. Option c is written from the nurse's side of the plan, describing an intervention rather than a change in the client, which is the commonest error in outcome writing. Option d contains two vague criteria, 'no signs of dehydration' and 'feel much better', neither of which has a definition or a deadline attached to it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain the surgical procedure, risks, and alternatives to the client, and obtain the client's signature"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Notify the operating surgeon immediately that the client has unanswered questions and withholds consent"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure the client that laparoscopic surgery is routine and persuade the client to sign to avoid cancelling the theatre list"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Have the client's adult daughter sign the informed consent form on the client's behalf"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The legal duty to provide information regarding surgical indications, risks, complications, and non-surgical alternatives rests exclusively with the healthcare provider performing the procedure (the surgeon). The nurse's role is to advocate for the client, verify that the client has received the required information, and witness the voluntary signature. When a client expresses lack of understanding or hesitation, the nurse must never explain surgical risks or persuade the client; the nurse must stop the process and notify the surgeon immediately. Why the other options fail: option a has the nurse explain the risks, which is outside the nursing role and invalidates the consent even if the client signs. Option c avoids the cancellation by persuading a client who has just said they do not understand, which is coercion rather than consent. Option d does not work at all in law: a relative cannot consent for a competent adult, so a signature from the daughter records nothing of value.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell the client to be strong for the children, because they will need their parent's courage now more than ever and breaking down in front of them will frighten them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask why the client has waited this long to tell the family, since an earlier conversation would have given the children more time to prepare and to ask questions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"You are carrying a very heavy burden right now. Would you like to talk about what feels hardest about telling them?\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure the client that modern chemotherapy has made enormous advances and that many people with this diagnosis now live for years, so there is no need to worry yet"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Therapeutic communication acknowledges and validates the client's emotional distress while using open-ended reflection to invite further exploration at the client's own pace. Option C reflects the client's heavy emotional state and offers supportive presence without judgment. Option A gives unsolicited advice and imposes guilt; Option B uses an accusatory, non-therapeutic 'why' question; and Option D provides false reassurance, deflecting from the client's existential grief. Why the other options fail: option a imposes a duty to be strong and tells the client how to feel, which closes the conversation at the moment the client has opened it. Option b asks a 'why' question that carries blame, and the client has just said why: they have not told the children yet. Option d offers false reassurance about chemotherapy, which deflects from the grief the client is actually describing and answers a question that was not asked.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Using a blood pressure cuff that is too narrow or too small for the circumference of the client's arm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Using a blood pressure cuff that is too wide or too large for the client's arm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Positioning the client's arm elevated above heart level during measurement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Deflating the cuff at a rate faster than 5 mmHg per second"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Proper blood pressure cuff sizing requires the bladder width to be approximately 40% of the upper arm circumference and the bladder length to encircle at least 80% of the arm. Using a cuff that is too narrow or too small requires excessive pressure to occlude the brachial artery, resulting in a FALSELY HIGH blood pressure reading. Conversely, using a cuff that is too wide results in a falsely low reading; positioning the arm above heart level yields a falsely low reading; and positioning the arm below heart level yields a falsely high reading. Why the other options fail: option b produces a falsely LOW reading, because an oversized bladder transmits pressure to a wider length of artery and needs less cuff pressure to occlude it. Option c lowers the reading by removing the hydrostatic head between the arm and the heart. Option d is a real source of error and affects accuracy, but a fast deflation rate tends to underestimate rather than elevate the systolic value.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A normal physiological variation caused by respiratory sinus arrhythmia, in which the radial rate rises on inspiration and falls on expiration in a healthy heart"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A pulse deficit of 22 beats/minute, indicating that ineffective cardiac contractions fail to transmit peripheral arterial pressure waves"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A measurement error, since a deficit of this size cannot occur in a conscious client, and both nurses should re-measure simultaneously over 15 seconds and average the two counts"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An auscultatory gap indicating severe aortic stenosis, in which the narrowing of the valve reduces the peripheral pulse volume and produces a silent interval between phases"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A pulse deficit occurs when the apical heart rate exceeds the peripheral radial pulse rate (Apical rate - Radial rate = Pulse deficit: 104 - 82 = 22 bpm). It signifies that rapid, uncoordinated ventricular contractions (as in atrial fibrillation) pump insufficient stroke volumes to generate palpable peripheral pulse waves at the radial artery. It indicates hemodynamic compromise and inefficient cardiac output. Measurement must be performed by two clinicians simultaneously over a full 60 seconds using the same watch. Why the other options fail: option a describes sinus arrhythmia, in which the radial rate varies with breathing but remains equal to the apical rate; a 22-beat difference is not respiration. Option c dismisses a finding that is expected in atrial fibrillation and is the whole point of doing a simultaneous count. Option d misattributes the deficit to a valvular lesion, but a pulse deficit measures beats that did not eject a palpable stroke volume, not a silent interval between Korotkoff phases.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Kussmaul respirations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cheyne-Stokes respirations"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Biot's (ataxic) respirations"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obstructive apnoea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Cheyne-Stokes respiration is an abnormal breathing pattern characterized by a rhythmic waxing and waning of tidal volume: a gradual increase in respiratory rate and depth, followed by a gradual decrease in depth, ending in a period of apnea (typically 15 to 30 seconds) before repeating. It is commonly observed in severe congestive heart failure, severe neurological injury (bilateral cortical or basal forebrain lesions), and near the end of life. Kussmaul breathing is deep, rapid, unvarying hyperventilation characteristic of metabolic acidosis (diabetic ketoacidosis). Biot's respirations are irregular periods of apnea alternating with disorganized breaths of identical depth. Why the other options fail: option a is deep, rapid and unvarying, without the crescendo or the apnoeic pause that the stem describes in detail. Option c alternates periods of apnoea with breaths of similar depth, not with a smooth waxing and waning pattern. Option d produces apnoea during sleep from upper airway collapse, but the breathing between events is normal rather than crescendo-decrescendo.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep all four side rails in the raised position continuously while the client is in bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the bed in the lowest position, lock wheels, ensure call light is within easy reach, and apply non-skid footwear"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer a nightly sedative hypnotic to prevent the client from getting out of bed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply bilateral wrist restraints whenever nursing staff leave the room"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Evidence-based fall prevention includes: placing the bed in its lowest position, locking bed wheels, keeping the call light and personal items within immediate reach, providing non-skid socks or shoes, ensuring adequate room lighting, and scheduling regular toileting assistance. Raising all four side rails is considered a physical restraint; in confused older adults, it significantly increases injury severity because clients attempt to climb over the elevated rails and fall from a greater height. Sedatives increase delirium, ataxia, and fall risk. Why the other options fail: option a is a restraint when all four rails are up, and in a confused older adult it raises the height of a fall as well as the risk of one. Option c treats a fall risk with a sedative that worsens delirium and ataxia, which is how a single fall becomes a fractured hip. Option d restrains a client whose only risk factor is falling, and restraints are neither indicated nor lawful for that purpose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tie the restraint straps securely to the movable side rail using a square double knot, so that the rail moves with the client and the limb cannot be pulled against the bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess neurovascular status and skin integrity every 30 minutes, release restraints every 2 hours for range of motion, and secure straps to the bed frame with a quick-release knot"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Maintain the restraints continuously for 72 hours under a single as-needed order, renewed at the end of the period if the client is still attempting to remove the devices"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply the restraints tightly enough that no fingers can be slipped under the cuff, since a restraint the client can work loose offers no protection against self-extubation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Standards of care for physical restraints require: (1) Securing restraint straps to the movable bed frame (NEVER to side rails, as moving the rail causes limb traction and injury), (2) Using a quick-release knot (slip knot) to allow immediate release in an emergency (such as vomiting or cardiac arrest), (3) Ensuring two fingers can easily slide under the restraint cuff to prevent circulatory compromise, (4) Assessing neurovascular status (circulation, movement, sensation) and skin integrity every 15–30 minutes, and (5) Removing the restraints every 2 hours for skin assessment, toileting, and range-of-motion exercises. Restraint orders cannot be written PRN and must be re-evaluated every 24 hours. Why the other options fail: option a ties to a moving part, so raising or lowering the rail pulls the limb, and a square knot cannot be released in an emergency. Option c breaks two rules at once: restraint orders cannot be written as needed, and they cannot run for 72 hours without re-evaluation. Option d produces the circulatory and nerve injury the checks exist to prevent, and two fingers must always fit under the cuff.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stage 1"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stage 2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stage 3"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stage 4"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$According to NPUAP/EPUAP staging guidelines: Stage 1 is non-blanchable erythema of intact skin; Stage 2 is partial-thickness skin loss with exposed dermis (or intact/ruptured serum-filled blister); Stage 3 is full-thickness skin loss in which adipose (subcutaneous fat) is visible, with granulation tissue, slough, or epibole (rolled edges), but NO bone, tendon, or muscle is exposed; Stage 4 involves full-thickness skin and tissue loss with exposed or directly palpable fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage, or bone. Unstageable occurs when slough or eschar completely obscures the wound bed extent. Why the other options fail: option a describes intact skin with non-blanchable erythema and no loss of tissue. Option b is partial-thickness loss with exposed dermis, which does not reach fat. Option d requires exposed or directly palpable fascia, muscle, tendon, cartilage or bone, and the stem states that none of these is visible.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Maintain the head of the bed at 90 degrees continuously, so that the client sits upright and the weight of the trunk falls through the ischial tuberosities rather than the sacrum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reposition the client at least every 2 hours using a 30-degree lateral tilted position and maintain the head of the bed at or below 30 degrees when resting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Turn the client strictly onto the 90-degree lateral position with the weight resting directly on the greater trochanter, which is a broad bony surface better able to bear load"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Massage the reddened bony prominences vigorously every four hours to stimulate capillary blood flow and disperse the metabolic waste that has accumulated in the ischaemic tissue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$To prevent pressure injuries and shear force, the head of the bed should be kept at or below 30 degrees (unless contraindicated by feeding or aspiration risk) because elevating the head higher causes gravitational sliding, generating intense shear stress on sacral capillaries. When repositioning sideways, the 30-degree lateral tilted position (supported with pillows under the back and between knees) is preferred over direct 90-degree lateral positioning, which places excessive pressure on the greater trochanter. Massaging reddened bony prominences is contraindicated because it damages fragile capillaries and accelerates deep tissue necrosis. Why the other options fail: option a converts body weight into shear across the sacrum and ischaemia at the ischial tuberosities, the two mechanisms the plan is meant to avoid. Option c puts the full weight on the greater trochanter, a small bony prominence poorly designed to bear it. Option d massages tissue that may already be damaged, rupturing capillaries and extending the injury into deeper layers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Position the wheelchair on the client's weak right side at a 90-degree angle, so that the client turns towards the paralysed side and brings it into use during the transfer"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Position the wheelchair on the client's strong (left) side at a 45-degree angle, lock the wheelchair brakes, apply a gait belt, and instruct the client to pivot on the stronger leg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lift the client upwards by pulling under both axillae while bending at the waist, which keeps the client's weight close to the nurse and shortens the distance to the chair"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the knees straight and twist the torso while transferring, so that the legs stay clear of the chair and the turning force is carried by the stronger trunk muscles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Safe client transfer technique requires: (1) Placing the wheelchair on the client's STRONGER (unaffected) side facing the bed at a 45-degree angle so the client moves toward their functional side; (2) Locking the wheels of both the bed and wheelchair; (3) Using a transfer/gait belt around the client's waist; (4) Having the client pivot on the stronger foot; and (5) The nurse must maintain a wide base of support, bend at the knees and hips (not the waist), and pivot their own body rather than twisting the spine. Pulling under the axillae causes severe shoulder subluxation and brachial plexus trauma. Why the other options fail: option a brings the chair to the paralysed side, so the client must move away from their functional side and pivot on the weak leg. Option c pulls under the axillae, a recognised cause of brachial plexus injury and shoulder subluxation, and bends at the waist rather than the knees. Option d keeps the knees straight and twists the spine, the posture most associated with nursing back injury.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Auscultating a rushing sound over the epigastrium while injecting 30 mL of air through the tube"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Obtaining an immediate post-insertion abdominal/chest X-ray confirming tube tip location in the gastric body"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Placing the proximal end of the NG tube into a cup of water to check for bubbling"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Observing whether the client can speak without coughing or choking"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Radiographic (X-ray) confirmation is the absolute gold standard to confirm proper tube tip positioning in the stomach prior to initiating the first enteral feed or medication. Auscultating air insufflation ('whoosh' test) is unreliable and dangerous because air sounds transmit clearly even when the tube is misplaced in the bronchus or pleural space. Placing the tube end in water ('bubble test') can cause pulmonary aspiration if the patient inhales. Once placement is radiographically verified, ongoing bedside verification before each feed is done by aspirating gastric secretions and testing pH (gastric aspirate pH is typically <= 5.5). Why the other options fail: option a is the whoosh test, which cannot distinguish gastric from bronchial or pleural placement because air transmits through all of them. Option c is the bubble test, which proves only that the tube is patent and risks aspiration. Option d is unreliable, since a tube in the oesophagus also allows normal speech, and a tube in the airway may present with cough only intermittently.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Disconnect the catheter from the drainage tubing every morning to irrigate the bladder with normal saline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the catheter drainage bag suspended below the level of the bladder at all times and off the floor, maintaining a sterile closed drainage system"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Collect routine urine culture specimens by opening the junction between the catheter and the drainage bag"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply topical antibacterial povidone-iodine ointment around the urethral meatus during daily hygiene"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$CAUTI prevention guidelines dictate maintaining an uninterrupted sterile closed drainage system and keeping the collection bag continuously BELOW the level of the bladder (to prevent retrograde reflux of colonized urine) and suspended off the floor (to prevent contamination). Disconnecting the closed system for routine irrigation dramatically increases infection risk; specimens must be aspirated aseptically from the needleless sampling port (never by breaking the catheter-tubing junction); and meatal cleaning should be performed with standard soap and water, as topical antiseptics cause chemical irritation. Why the other options fail: option a breaks the closed system, which is the single strongest predictor of CAUTI, and routine irrigation is not indicated. Option c collects the specimen by opening the junction, which lets organisms in at exactly the point the sample is being taken. Option d applies an antiseptic that irritates the meatus; evidence supports plain soap and water rather than topical antimicrobials.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Raise the enema container higher to speed the infusion to completion, since finishing quickly shortens the time the client has to endure the cramping and the urge to defecate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the infusion temporarily by clamping the tubing or lowering the bag, instruct the client to take slow deep breaths, and resume at a slower rate once cramps subside"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remove the rectal tube immediately and assist the client to the commode, because cramping and fullness mean the solution has already reached the sigmoid colon and done its work"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client to hold their breath and bear down through the discomfort, which splints the abdominal wall and helps the fluid to pass the point of resistance more quickly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Abdominal cramping during an enema infusion is usually caused by intestinal distension from rapid fluid flow or cold solution. The nurse should immediately clamp the tubing or lower the container to halt or slow the flow, allowing the intestinal wall to adapt and the spasm to pass, while coaching the client to take slow, deep diaphragmatic breaths through the mouth. Once the cramping abates, the infusion can resume at a slower flow rate. Elevating the container increases flow rate and worsens cramping. Removing the tube prematurely prevents adequate administration. Why the other options fail: option a raises the flow rate and worsens the distension causing the cramp, which is the opposite of what the colon is signalling for. Option c removes the tube before the prescribed volume is delivered, so the enema is wasted and has to be repeated. Option d asks the client to bear down against a distended colon, which increases pressure and pain rather than relieving either.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dorsogluteal site using standard perpendicular injection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ventrogluteal site using the Z-track technique"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Deltoid muscle using a 45-degree angle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vastus lateralis using a rapid darting technique with skin pinched tightly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Ventrogluteal site (gluteus medius and minimus) is the preferred, safest intramuscular site for adults: it is situated away from major blood vessels and the sciatic nerve, has a thick muscle belly, and accommodates volumes up to 3–4 mL. The Z-track technique is mandatory for irritating or staining medications (such as iron dextran) because pulling the skin and subcutaneous tissue 2.5–3.5 cm laterally before needle insertion creates a zigzag track when released, effectively sealing the medication deep within the muscle and preventing leakage into subcutaneous tissue. The dorsogluteal site is no longer recommended due to high risk of sciatic nerve injury. Why the other options fail: option a uses the site with the highest risk of sciatic nerve injury and omits the Z-track that an irritating preparation requires. Option c uses a 45-degree angle in the deltoid, which is too shallow for a 3 mL deep intramuscular dose and too small a muscle to take it. Option d pinches the skin for a deep intramuscular injection, which is a subcutaneous technique, and a darting injection of a viscous irritant increases tracking.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Phlebitis; apply warm moist heat along the vein and increase the infusion rate to flush the irritant solution through the inflamed segment and dilute it in the general circulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Infiltration; stop the infusion, remove the IV cannula immediately, elevate the extremity, and apply a cool or warm compress depending on the solution"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Venous thrombosis; flush the catheter vigorously with 10 mL of normal saline to dislodge any clot at the tip and restore flow through the occluded segment of the vein"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Local infection; apply antibacterial ointment over the insertion site and leave the cannula in place so that the antibiotic reaches the colonised tissue surrounding the tip"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Infiltration occurs when IV fluid leaks into surrounding subcutaneous tissue, characterized by COOLNESS to the touch, PALLOR, EDEMA/swelling, and sluggish infusion. The immediate nursing response: STOP the infusion immediately, REMOVE the IV cannula, ELEVATE the affected limb above heart level to promote fluid reabsorption, and apply a compress. In contrast, Phlebitis presents with ERYTHEMA, WARMTH, tenderness, and a palpable cord along the vein path. Flushing a sluggish line is dangerous as it dislodges clots or forces fluid deeper into tissues. Why the other options fail: option a describes phlebitis, which presents warm and red with a palpable cord rather than cool and pale, and increasing the rate worsens infiltration. Option c flushes a sluggish line, which forces fluid further into the tissue and can dislodge a clot. Option d treats a sterile fluid collection as an infection and leaves the offending cannula in place while the infiltration continues.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Simple face mask"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Venturi mask"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Non-rebreather mask with reservoir bag"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Standard nasal cannula at 6 L/minute"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Venturi mask is a high-flow oxygen delivery system that uses calibrated color-coded jet adaptors to entrain a specific proportion of room air with oxygen, delivering an exact, precise fraction of inspired oxygen (FiO2 from 24% to 50%) regardless of the patient's respiratory rate or depth. This precision is critical in clients with chronic CO2 retention (severe COPD) whose hypoxic respiratory drive can be blunted by uncontrolled high-flow oxygen. Simple masks and nasal cannulae provide variable, unpredictable FiO2, while non-rebreather masks deliver high concentrations (80–95%). Why the other options fail: option a delivers a variable and unpredictable concentration that depends on the client's inspiratory flow. Option c delivers 80 to 95 per cent, far above the 88 to 92 per cent saturation target, and the reservoir bag needs a tight seal to work at all. Option d at 6 L per minute dries the mucosa, delivers an inexact fraction, and exceeds the low-flow range intended for a CO2 retainer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Instill prescribed lubricating eye drops or ointment and apply eye shields or tape eyelids gently closed with paper tape"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Irrigate the eyes daily with 50 mL of tap water to wash away the accumulating secretions, and leave the lids open afterwards so that the cornea can be inspected at each shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the eyelids propped open with gauze pads so that the pupils can be monitored hourly for the changes in size and reactivity that signal raised intracranial pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply dry sterile cotton gauze sponges directly onto the cornea and change them every two hours, so that the moisture that collects under the dressing keeps the surface wet"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Comatose clients often lose the protective corneal (blink) reflex and may have incomplete eyelid closure (lagophthalmos), leading to severe corneal drying, keratitis, and permanent corneal ulceration. The nurse must instill prescribed sterile lubricating eye drops or methylcellulose ointment every 2 to 4 hours and tape the eyelids gently closed across the orbital rim using hypo-allergenic paper tape, or apply moisture chambers. Tap water is non-sterile and irritating; dry gauze scratches the delicate corneal epithelium. Why the other options fail: option b uses non-sterile water on an unprotected cornea and the daily frequency is far too infrequent for a client who cannot blink. Option c removes the only protection the eye has, and corneal drying begins within minutes when the lids are held open. Option d puts dry gauze in direct contact with the epithelium, which abrades it and, once moistened, provides a warm surface for bacterial growth.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insert them before rigor mortis sets in and leave them in place for the viewing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Discard them, because dentures are personal property and the family will not expect to see them, and a poorly fitting set can obstruct the airway during the viewing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send them to the mortuary separately and out of view, so that they travel with the body for the funeral director to fit at the funeral home before the family arrives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave them out and explain that this is hospital policy, since inserting a personal item into a body after death requires written consent from the next of kin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Dentures are inserted before rigor mortis develops, because afterwards the jaw cannot be opened without force, and leaving them in restores the familiar appearance of the face for the family and reduces the visible change caused by death. They are personal property and should be accounted for, not discarded, and if they cannot be inserted they must be labelled and sent with the body. Sending them out of view removes something that belongs with the patient, and there is no policy that requires dentures to be left out. Why the other options fail: option b treats a personal possession as waste, and dentures that are not inserted must still be labelled and sent with the body. Option c separates them from the patient at the point where they are most useful, which is restoring the face the family recognises. Option d invents a consent requirement; inserting dentures is routine post-mortem care, not a procedure needing authorisation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Offer the visitor a glass of water and encourage them to take small sips, since the fluid will lubricate the pharynx and help the obstructing bolus to pass down into the stomach"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform a blind finger sweep into the oropharynx immediately, because a bolus lodged at the level of the larynx can usually be hooked out before the victim loses consciousness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stand behind the victim, lean them forward, deliver up to 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades, followed by up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich maneuver), repeating until the object is expelled or the victim becomes unresponsive"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the victim supine and begin rescue breathing immediately, since the obstruction is preventing oxygen from reaching the lungs and ventilation must be restored first"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$For a responsive adult experiencing severe foreign body airway obstruction (unable to speak, cough, or breathe; clutching throat), standard first aid guidelines (ERC/AHA) recommend delivering up to 5 sharp back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of the hand, followed by up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich maneuver: subdiaphragmatic inward and upward thrusts). Repeat this cycle until the object is relieved. If the victim becomes unresponsive, lower them safely to the floor and immediately begin CPR (30 compressions, then open airway and look for object before rescue breaths). Never perform blind finger sweeps (pushes object deeper). Why the other options fail: option a gives fluid to a person who cannot swallow, which adds aspiration to an existing obstruction. Option b is the blind finger sweep, which is no longer recommended because it can drive the object deeper and injure the pharynx. Option d attempts ventilation before relieving a mechanical obstruction, so the breath cannot reach the lungs and time is lost before compressions would be effective.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20 rows updated.
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch9.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 9: OBS module rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- Like FUND rather than like TCAM/NUTR/PROC/INTEG: the distractors were
-- mostly legitimate competing answers (copper IUD vs levonorgestrel vs
-- Yuzpe, Kussmaul vs Cheyne-Stokes, LAT vs variable decelerations). Only
-- a handful were implausible and those were rewritten: 'ice application
-- to the perineum' as part of AMTSL, 'transport the client to theatre
-- while actively seizing', 'increase the oxytocin rate by 2 mU/min'
-- during tachysystole.
--
-- Defects repaired: no structured distractor block on any of the 20, and
-- the keyed option was the longest in 13 of 20 (mean ratio 1.41). Keyed
-- options were lengthened or distractors extended; no key letter moved.
-- Mean ratio now 0.98, worst 1.34.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ovulation occurs on day 7 of the cycle; the fertile window lasts for 24 hours after menstruation ends"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ovulation occurs approximately 14 days before the onset of the next menses; the fertile window includes the 5 days before ovulation and the day of ovulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ovulation occurs precisely 10 days following the cessation of menstrual flow; conception can occur only during that single 12-hour period"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ovulation is triggered by high progesterone levels on day 21; intercourse is most fertile during the luteal phase"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In a 28-day cycle, the luteal phase is relatively fixed at 14 days. Therefore, ovulation occurs approximately 14 days before the start of the next menstrual period (around cycle day 14). Because human spermatozoa can survive in fertile cervical mucus for up to 5 days and an unfertilized ovum remains viable for approximately 12 to 24 hours after ovulation, the fertile window spans the 5 days preceding ovulation through the day of ovulation itself. Ovulation is triggered by a surge in luteinizing hormone (LH), not progesterone. Why the other options fail: option a puts ovulation on day 7 and limits the window to 24 hours after menstruation, but ovulation in a 28-day cycle falls near day 14 and sperm survive up to five days before it. Option c names day 10 after flow stops and a single 12-hour window, which ignores both the fixed luteal phase and sperm survival. Option d attributes ovulation to progesterone; it is triggered by the LH surge, and intercourse in the luteal phase is after the fertile window has closed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Amenorrhoea accompanied by morning nausea and breast tenderness"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A positive home urine human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) pregnancy test"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Purplish-blue discolouration of the vaginal mucosa and cervix (Chadwick's sign)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Auscultation of fetal heart tones using a handheld Doppler transducer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Signs of pregnancy fall into three distinct classes: (1) Presumptive signs (subjective, felt by the woman: amenorrhoea, nausea/vomiting, breast tenderness, fatigue, quickening); (2) Probable signs (objective examiner findings or tests: positive hCG test, Goodell's sign, Hegar's sign, Chadwick's sign, ballottement); and (3) Positive signs (irrefutable proof of a fetus: auscultation of fetal heartbeat by Doppler at 10–12 weeks or fetoscope at 18–20 weeks, visualization of the fetus on ultrasound, and fetal movement palpated by a trained examiner). Why the other options fail: option a lists amenorrhoea, nausea and breast tenderness, all of which are presumptive signs reported by the woman and none of which proves a fetus. Option b is a probable sign, since hCG can be raised in other circumstances and the test is not proof of a viable pregnancy. Option c is Chadwick's sign, a probable sign produced by pelvic vascular engorgement rather than by the fetus itself.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mild dysmenorrhoea managed with ibuprofen"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Smoking 15 cigarettes per day at age 36"}, {"id": "c", "text": "History of resolved Chlamydia infection 2 years ago"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Body mass index (BMI) of 26 kg/m2"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under the WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use, women aged 35 years or older who smoke 15 or more cigarettes per day have a Category 4 absolute contraindication to combined hormonal contraceptives (pills, patches, rings, or injectables containing estrogen). Estrogen combined with smoking and advanced maternal age exponentially increases the risk of arterial thromboembolism, myocardial infarction, and ischemic stroke. Progestin-only contraceptives (pills, implants, levonorgestrel IUD) or copper IUDs are safe, appropriate alternatives. Why the other options fail: option a is dysmenorrhoea treated with an anti-inflammatory, which is not a cardiovascular risk and is often an indication FOR hormonal contraception. Option c is a resolved infection two years ago, which does not affect eligibility unless there is current purulent cervicitis. Option d is a BMI of 26, which is above ideal but not a Category 4 contraindication, unlike the combination of age over 35 and heavy smoking.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oral levonorgestrel 1.5 mg in a single dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insertion of a Copper Intrauterine Device (Copper IUD)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "High-dose combined ethinylestradiol and levonorgestrel (Yuzpe regimen)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA) intramuscular injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The copper intrauterine device (Cu-IUD) is the most effective form of emergency contraception available, with a failure rate of less than 0.1%. It can be inserted up to 5 days (120 hours) after unprotected intercourse (or up to 5 days after the earliest calculated day of ovulation) and provides continuous, highly effective, reversible contraception for up to 10 to 12 years. Oral levonorgestrel is licensed only up to 72 hours (efficacy drops significantly after 72 hours), while ulipristal acetate is effective up to 120 hours but still inferior in efficacy to the copper IUD. Why the other options fail: option a is the most familiar method but is licensed to 72 hours, and this client is at 96, so efficacy has already fallen substantially. Option c, the Yuzpe regimen, is less effective than levonorgestrel and causes more nausea. Option d is not an emergency method at all; depot medroxyprogesterone does not reliably prevent ovulation in the days immediately after intercourse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tubal factor infertility and ectopic pregnancy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Endometrial hyperplasia and polycystic ovary syndrome"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uterine leiomyomas (fibroids)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) is an ascending polymicrobial infection (most commonly initiated by Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae) of the upper genital tract. Inadequate or delayed treatment leads to endosalpingitis, tubal scarring, loss of ciliary function, and pelvic adhesions. This irreversible tubal architecture damage increases the risk of tubal factor infertility six- to tenfold and dramatically raises the risk of future ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain. Why the other options fail: option a is a human papillomavirus lesion of the cervix and is not a sequela of ascending bacterial infection. Option c is an endometrial and ovarian condition unrelated to tubal infection. Option d is a benign smooth muscle tumour of the uterus; fibroids do not arise from the salpingitis that scars the tube.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "March 17, 2027"}, {"id": "b", "text": "March 3, 2027"}, {"id": "c", "text": "April 17, 2027"}, {"id": "d", "text": "February 17, 2027"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Naegele's rule calculates the Estimated Date of Delivery (EDD) by: (1) Taking the first day of the Last Menstrual Period (LMP: June 10, 2026), (2) Subtracting 3 calendar months (June minus 3 months = March), (3) Adding 7 days (10 + 7 = 17), and (4) Adjusting the year forward by one year (2026 + 1 = 2027). The resulting EDD is March 17, 2027. Why the other options fail: option b subtracts the three months and adds seven days but then leaves the year unchanged, which places the date ten months early. Option c adds a month as well as the seven days, giving April rather than March. Option d subtracts a further month, which lands in February.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Blood pressure of 148/94 mmHg with a persistent headache, which indicates that the preeclampsia is not yet controlled on the current magnesium infusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Absent deep tendon reflexes (patellar reflex 0/4) and respiratory rate of 10 breaths/minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Urine output of 45 mL/hour over the preceding two hours, which is adequate to clear magnesium and therefore confirms that the dose is being tolerated"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mild flushing and diaphoresis shortly after the loading dose, which are the expected vasodilator effects of magnesium and settle without any change in therapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Magnesium sulfate toxicity follows a progressive clinical sequence: (1) Loss of deep tendon reflexes (occurs at serum magnesium levels of 4–5 mmol/L [8–10 mg/dL]), (2) Respiratory depression (RR < 12 breaths/min; occurs at 5–7 mmol/L), (3) Oliguria (< 30 mL/hr, causing further magnesium accumulation), and (4) Cardiac arrest (levels > 7 mmol/L). Immediate nursing interventions for toxicity include: STOP the magnesium infusion immediately, administer oxygen, call for assistance, and administer the antidote—10% calcium gluconate 10 mL (1 g) IV slowly over 3 to 5 minutes. Why the other options fail: option a is a blood pressure still above target, which calls for antihypertensive treatment rather than stopping the magnesium that prevents seizures. Option c is a reassuring urine output; magnesium is renally cleared, so oliguria of under 30 mL per hour is the finding that matters, and 45 mL per hour will not accumulate. Option d is an expected vasodilator effect of the loading dose and settles without changing therapy, whereas absent reflexes and a respiratory rate of 10 are the sequence of toxicity itself.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inserting two large-bore (16-gauge) intravenous cannulae"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Performing a digital vaginal examination to determine cervical dilation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obtaining an emergent transabdominal obstetric ultrasound"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Drawing blood for type and crossmatch and baseline coagulation profile"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The clinical presentation of painless, bright red vaginal bleeding with a soft, non-tender uterus in the third trimester is the hallmark of Placenta Previa. A digital vaginal examination (or insertion of any vaginal instrument/speculum) is strictly contraindicated until the placental location is confirmed by ultrasound. Digital probing can puncture the low-lying placenta or separate it from the lower uterine segment, triggering catastrophic, life-threatening maternal hemorrhage and fetal exsanguination. Why the other options fail: option a is the correct preparation for any major obstetric haemorrhage and is not contraindicated; wide-bore access is the first thing the client needs. Option c is actively required, because the ultrasound is what localises the placenta and permits safe management. Option d is also required, since crossmatch and clotting studies must be ready before any bleeding worsens.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Labour progress is normal and delivery is imminent within the next hour, since the descent of the presenting part continues even when dilation pauses"}, {"id": "b", "text": "There is prolonged/obstructed labour requiring immediate senior medical review, intervention, or transfer to a comprehensive surgical facility"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The woman has entered the latent phase and can be discharged home to rest, because the cervix is not changing and admission in latent labour increases intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fetal heart rate accelerations are being documented, which indicate active fetal reserve and mean that the plot position reflects reassuring fetal wellbeing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$On the WHO partograph, active labour starts at 4–5 cm dilation with an expected minimum progress rate of 1 cm/hour (represented by the Alert Line). The Action Line is drawn 4 hours to the right of and parallel to the Alert Line. If cervical dilation plots at or beyond the Action Line, it indicates severe protracted labour or cephalopelvic disproportion/obstruction. This mandates immediate management decisions: full assessment for obstruction, augmentation with oxytocin if contractions are inadequate and no obstruction exists, or immediate operative delivery (Caesarean section). Why the other options fail: option a reads the crossing as normal progress, but the Action Line sits four hours to the right of the Alert Line precisely to mark the point at which progress has failed. Option c calls it latent labour and discharges a woman who is in established labour and has not dilated for four hours. Option d treats a fetal monitoring finding as an explanation for the plot position, which it is not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Umbilical cord compression; reposition the mother onto her hands and knees to take the weight of the uterus off the cord and improve umbilical blood flow"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fetal head compression; prepare for immediate spontaneous vaginal delivery, since a deceleration that follows the contraction means the head is descending well"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Uteroplacental insufficiency; turn mother to lateral position, apply 10 L oxygen, discontinue oxytocin infusion, and infuse IV fluid bolus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maternal hypotension from the epidural; place the client supine in Trendelenburg, since laying her flat restores venous return and corrects the placental perfusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Late decelerations are symmetrical, gradual decreases in fetal heart rate that begin AFTER the peak of the contraction and return to baseline after the contraction ends. They indicate uteroplacental insufficiency (inadequate fetal oxygenation and placental perfusion during uterine work). Priority intrauterine resuscitation measures ('L-I-O-N'): (1) Left lateral positioning (relieves aortocaval compression and improves placental blood flow), (2) Increase IV fluids (bolus isotonic crystalloid to expand volume), (3) Oxygen (8–10 L/min via non-rebreather mask), and (4) Notify provider and STOP oxytocin infusion immediately. Why the other options fail: option a describes variable decelerations, which are abrupt rather than gradual and begin with the contraction rather than after its peak. Option b describes early decelerations, which are shallow and recover by the end of the contraction, not after it. Option d treats it as maternal hypotension; the shape and timing of a late deceleration point to uteroplacental insufficiency, and supine positioning would worsen aortocaval compression.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vigorous fundal pushing immediately after delivery, manual removal of the placenta within five minutes, and ice application to the perineum to reduce swelling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administration of prophylactic oxytocin (10 IU IM) within 1 minute of birth, controlled cord traction with counter-traction on the lower uterine segment, and immediate uterine massage following placental delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Routine episiotomy repair, an intravenous ergometrine bolus before the shoulders deliver, and expectant waiting for sixty minutes before any cord traction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Immediate clamping of the umbilical cord within five seconds of birth, maternal bearing down to expel the placenta, and intrauterine gauze packing to prevent atony"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Active Management of the Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL) reduces the incidence of primary postpartum hemorrhage by more than 60%. The evidence-based WHO protocol consists of: (1) Administration of a prophylactic uterotonic (oxytocin 10 IU IM is the gold standard) within 1 minute of delivery of the baby (after excluding a second twin), (2) Controlled cord traction (CCT) applied with simultaneous counter-traction on the suprapubic area during a contraction, and (3) Palpation and gentle massage of the uterine fundus immediately after placental expulsion to ensure a firm, contracted uterus. Why the other options fail: option a substitutes fundal pushing for a uterotonic, and manual removal within five minutes is not part of active management. Option c gives ergometrine before the shoulders deliver, which can entrap the second twin, and expectant management for an hour is the opposite of active management. Option d clamps the cord within seconds, which deprives the newborn of placental transfusion, and packing is not part of the triad.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer 0.2 mg intramuscular ergometrine immediately, since a uterotonic is the fastest way to contract an atonic uterus and stop the bleeding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform vigorous fundal massage while assisting the client to empty her bladder or catheterizing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prepare the client for emergent exploratory laparotomy and hysterectomy, because bleeding this heavy after a normal delivery indicates a uterine rupture"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the client in a high Fowler's position, so that gravity drains blood out of the pelvis and the weight of the uterus is taken off the bleeding site"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A soft, 'boggy' fundus indicates uterine atony, the most common cause (70–80%) of primary postpartum hemorrhage. A fundus that is boggy AND displaced upwards or to the right indicates bladder distension, which physically prevents effective uterine myometrial contraction and retraction. The immediate nursing actions are: perform continuous fundal massage to stimulate uterine contraction and empty the bladder (via spontaneous voiding or in-and-out catheterization), followed by uterotonic administration. Why the other options fail: option a reaches for a uterotonic first, but a uterus displaced to the right by a full bladder cannot contract effectively until the bladder is emptied, and ergometrine is contraindicated in hypertension besides. Option c jumps to hysterectomy for atony before any of the simple measures have been tried. Option d sits the client up, which encourages blood to pool and does nothing about either the atony or the full bladder.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Gently push the exposed loop of umbilical cord back inside the uterine cavity, so that it is no longer exposed to the cold air that causes vasospasm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insert a sterile gloved hand into the vagina to elevate the presenting part off the umbilical cord, and place the mother in knee-chest or Trendelenburg position"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instruct the mother to bear down firmly and push, since delivery of the baby is the quickest way to relieve the compression and restore the fetal heart rate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply dry sterile gauze around the cord and observe for spontaneous restoration of the fetal heart rate before calling for emergency operative delivery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Umbilical cord prolapse is an acute obstetric emergency where the cord falls below the presenting part, leading to mechanical compression between the fetal head/breech and maternal pelvis, causing sudden fetal anoxia. The nurse must insert a gloved hand into the vagina and apply continuous upward pressure on the fetal presenting part to keep it elevated off the cord until delivery in the operating theatre. Position the mother in exaggerated knee-chest or steep Trendelenburg position to use gravity. The cord must NEVER be pushed back into the uterus (causes cord vasospasm). Why the other options fail: option a pushes the cord back, which causes vasospasm and removes the only structure keeping the fetus oxygenated. Option c asks the mother to push, which drives the presenting part down onto the cord and worsens the compression. Option d applies a dressing and waits; cord prolapse is measured in minutes, and the fetal heart rate has already fallen to 70.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply firm fundal pressure from above the uterus while pulling down on the infant's neck, since this rotates the shoulder under the pubic symphysis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform the McRoberts maneuver (hyperflex the mother's hips against her abdomen) while an assistant applies downward suprapubic pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Attempt immediate vacuum extraction on the fetal scalp, because applying traction to the head will draw the impacted shoulder through the pelvic outlet"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Perform a fundal push while extending the episiotomy, so that the extra room at the outlet and the pressure from above work together to free the shoulder"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Shoulder dystocia occurs when the fetal anterior shoulder impacts against the maternal symphysis pubis. The first-line, most effective intervention is the McRoberts Maneuver (hyperflexing the mother's legs tightly back against her abdomen), which flattens the sacral promontory and widens the pelvic outlet, combined with simultaneous downward and lateral Suprapubic Pressure to disimpact the shoulder under the pubic bone. Fundal pressure is strictly CONTRAINDICATED because it impacts the shoulder harder, ruptures the uterus, and causes permanent brachial plexus injury (Erb's palsy). Why the other options fail: option a applies fundal pressure, which is contraindicated in shoulder dystocia because it drives the impacted shoulder further under the symphysis and is a recognised cause of brachial plexus injury and uterine rupture. Option c applies traction to the head, which stretches the plexus without freeing the shoulder. Option d combines the two errors with an episiotomy that does not relieve a bony impaction.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Force a padded mouth gag between her teeth, hold down her limbs, and give 10 mg of oral diazepam to terminate the seizure as quickly as possible"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Call for help, turn the client onto her side (lateral tilt), protect her from physical trauma, maintain airway and oxygenation, and prepare IV magnesium sulfate loading dose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transport the client to the operating theatre immediately while she is actively seizing, so that the delivery can be completed without further delay"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give rapid intravenous boluses of labetalol to bring the blood pressure below 100/60 mmHg, since the seizure is driven by the hypertension itself"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$During an eclamptic seizure, immediate maternal stabilization is the absolute priority: (1) Call for urgent assistance, (2) Turn the woman onto her left side to maintain airway patency, prevent aspiration, and relieve aortocaval compression, (3) Protect the head and body from traumatic injury without physical restraint, (4) Administer high-flow oxygen, and (5) Once the convulsion ceases, administer the loading dose of magnesium sulfate (4 g IV over 10–15 minutes plus 10 g IM) to prevent recurrent seizures. Never insert mouth gags or tongue blades (causes dental fracture, vomiting, and laryngospasm). Delivery occurs only after maternal stabilization. Why the other options fail: option a puts an object in the mouth of a seizing woman, which causes dental fracture and laryngospasm, and gives a drug she cannot swallow. Option c moves a seizing client, which risks injury and delays the treatment that stops the seizure. Option d treats the blood pressure during the seizure rather than the seizure itself, and dropping the pressure abruptly can worsen placental perfusion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uterine fundus palpated 3 fingerbreadths above the umbilicus, soft and tender"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dark red lochia rubra with occasional small quarter-sized clots and fleshy odour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Continuous bright red lochia soaking through three perineal pads within 30 minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Purulent, foul-smelling yellow lochia accompanied by a temperature of 38.5 degrees Celsius"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Normal lochia progression: (1) Lochia rubra: dark red, fleshy odour, consists of blood and decidual debris, lasts from day 1 to day 3–4 postpartum; (2) Lochia serosa: pinkish-brown, watery discharge, days 4 to 10; (3) Lochia alba: yellowish-white discharge, days 10 up to 4–6 weeks. On day 3, dark red lochia rubra with occasional small clots is expected. The fundus should be at or below the umbilicus and decrease by 1 fingerbreadth (1 cm) per day. Soaking a pad in < 1 hour indicates hemorrhage; foul-smelling lochia with fever indicates puerperal endometritis. Why the other options fail: option a describes a fundus above the umbilicus and soft, which suggests atony or retained products on day 3 rather than normal involution. Option c is a pad soaked in 30 minutes, which is postpartum haemorrhage. Option d is purulent malodorous lochia with fever, the picture of puerperal endometritis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop breastfeeding permanently on both sides and start infant formula, because the infected milk could transmit the bacteria to the infant's gut"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continue breastfeeding frequently, beginning on the affected right breast or pumping if too painful, while taking prescribed antibiotics"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pump and discard all breast milk for fourteen days, since the milk in an infected breast is toxic to the infant until the course of antibiotics is finished"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Avoid emptying the affected breast so that the inflamed ducts can rest and heal, and feed only from the unaffected breast until the pain has settled"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The presentation (unilateral wedge-shaped erythema, fever, chills, flu-like malaise) is diagnostic of Acute Puerperal Mastitis (typically caused by Staphylococcus aureus). The cornerstone of mastitis treatment is COMPLETE and frequent emptying of the breast to relieve milk stasis. The mother must be strongly encouraged to continue breastfeeding on both breasts, starting with the affected breast (or pumping if feeding is unbearable). The bacteria originate from the infant's nasopharynx or maternal skin; ingesting the milk poses no danger to a healthy term infant. Ceasing feeds leads to milk stasis, galactocele, and breast abscess. Why the other options fail: option a stops feeding altogether, which is what causes the stasis that leads to abscess. Option c discards milk for a fortnight on the false premise that it is toxic; the organism is usually already in the infant's nasopharynx. Option d withholds emptying, which is the single measure most likely to convert mastitis into an abscess.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "6"}, {"id": "b", "text": "7"}, {"id": "c", "text": "5"}, {"id": "d", "text": "8"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Score each of the five components and add them. Heart rate 115 beats/minute is above 100, which scores 2. A weak and irregular cry scores 1 for respiratory effort. Some flexion of the extremities scores 1 for muscle tone. Grimacing in response to suctioning scores 1 for reflex irritability. A pink body with cyanotic hands and feet is acrocyanosis, which scores 1 for colour. That is 2 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 6. A score of 7 would need a second point somewhere, from a stronger cry, active motion or a fully pink colour, and none of those is described. The score is repeated at five minutes. Why the other options fail: option a scores 6, which would require losing two points, but every component here scores 1 or 2. Option c scores 5, which would need muscle tone, reflex response or colour to be absent rather than reduced. Option d scores 8, which would require a strong cry and fully pink extremities, neither of which this infant has.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase the oxytocin rate by 2 mU per minute, since the contractions are still lasting less than two minutes and the cervix still needs to dilate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the oxytocin infusion immediately, place the client in a lateral position, and administer oxygen via face mask"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer 10 mg of intramuscular morphine sulfate for maternal analgesia, because pain is what is driving the uterus to contract so frequently"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Document the pattern as a normal response to oxytocin induction and continue the infusion at the current rate, provided the fetal heart rate is reassuring"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The client is experiencing Uterine Tachysystole (hyperstimulation), defined as more than 5 contractions in 10 minutes, contractions lasting longer than 90 seconds, or insufficient uterine relaxation between contractions (< 60 seconds). Tachysystole compromises uteroplacental blood flow during the relaxation phase, causing progressive fetal hypoxia and acidosis, and substantially increases the risk of uterine rupture. The immediate intervention is to TURN OFF the oxytocin infusion immediately, position the client in a left lateral tilt, increase maintenance IV crystalloid fluids, and administer oxygen. Why the other options fail: option a increases the drug that is causing the tachysystole, and six contractions in ten minutes with 95-second durations is already hyperstimulation. Option c treats the pain and leaves the uterus contracting without a relaxation phase, which is what compromises placental perfusion. Option d documents a pathological pattern as normal and continues the infusion, which risks uterine rupture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "100 mL/hr"}, {"id": "b", "text": "200 mL/hr"}, {"id": "c", "text": "300 mL/hr"}, {"id": "d", "text": "400 mL/hr"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$To calculate the hourly infusion pump rate: Rate (mL/hr) = (Volume in mL / Time in minutes) x 60 minutes/hr. Here: Volume = 100 mL; Time = 20 minutes. Rate = (100 mL / 20 min) x 60 min/hr = 5 mL/min x 60 min/hr = 300 mL/hr. Why the other options fail: option a of 100 mL per hour would run the 100 mL bag over a full hour rather than 20 minutes. Option b of 200 mL per hour doubles nothing in the calculation and would take 30 minutes. Option d of 400 mL per hour runs at four times the volume per hour and would deliver the dose in 15 minutes, which is faster than the prescribed 20.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20 rows updated.
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch10.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 10: RESP module rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- Distractors were mostly legitimate competing answers (pneumothorax vs
-- consolidation vs effusion findings, droplet vs airborne precautions,
-- the four ABG interpretations). A number of implausible ones were
-- rewritten: 'breathe into a paper bag to retain CO2' for CO2 narcosis,
-- 'take two iron tablets to replace the haemoglobin in your urine',
-- 'insert a large nasogastric tube into the stoma'.
--
-- Defects repaired: no structured distractor block on any of the 20, and
-- the keyed option was the longest in 17 of 20 (mean ratio 1.44). Keys
-- were not shortened; short distractors were extended to full clinical
-- sentences, and four items needed a second pass. Mean 0.84, worst 1.24.
-- No key letter and no key text changed.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Gravity increases blood perfusion to the healthy dependent left lung, improving ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Turning to the left splints the right bronchus and increases its airway resistance, which diverts blood away from the diseased lobe and towards the healthy one"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The left lateral position compresses the heart against the mattress and increases left ventricular stroke volume, which raises pulmonary blood flow to both lungs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lying on the left lung reduces surfactant breakdown in the diseased right alveoli, because the healthy lung takes over the work of surfactant production"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In unilateral lung disease, positioning the healthy, unaffected lung in the dependent (downward) position promotes gravity-dependent blood flow to the well-ventilated healthy lung tissue. This optimizes ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching and maximizes systemic oxygenation ('good lung down'). Placing the diseased lung downward causes blood to perfuse poorly ventilated alveoli, causing intrapulmonary shunting and hypoxemia. Why the other options fail: option b treats the good result as a mechanical splinting of the diseased bronchus, but airway resistance is not what determines where blood flows; hypoxic vasoconstriction is, and it is opposed by gravity. Option c invents a stroke volume effect that would not raise saturation this quickly or this selectively. Option d gives a surfactant explanation for a change that occurred within five minutes, far too fast for surfactant turnover to alter.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Decreased tactile fremitus, hyperresonance to percussion, and absent breath sounds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Increased tactile fremitus, dullness to percussion, and bronchial breath sounds with egophony"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Decreased tactile fremitus, tympany to percussion, and low-pitched musical rhonchi"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Absent tactile fremitus, stony dullness to percussion, and faint tracheal breath sounds"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Alveolar consolidation (fluid, exudate, and cellular debris replacing air in pneumonia) conducts sound vibrations better than normal air-filled lung tissue. Hallmarks over consolidated tissue: increased tactile fremitus (enhanced vibration on palpation), dullness to percussion (fluid-filled tissue), loud bronchial breath sounds (high-pitched tubular sounds heard in peripheral fields), and positive egophony (spoken 'ee' sounds like 'ay'). Why the other options fail: option a describes a pneumothorax, where air in the pleural space both reduces the transmission of sound and hyper-resonates on percussion. Option c mixes decreased fremitus with tympany, the percussion note of a hyperinflated or gas-filled space rather than consolidated lung. Option d describes a large pleural effusion, where fluid blocks transmission completely and gives stony dullness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase oxygen flow to 20 L/min and administer a bolus of intravenous naloxone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client to breathe into a paper bag to retain carbon dioxide, since the somnolence suggests the carbon dioxide level has fallen too far on high-flow oxygen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Replace the non-rebreather mask with a Venturi mask titrated to maintain SpO2 between 88% and 92%, and prepare for ventilatory support"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer an intravenous sedative to treat oxygen-induced panic, because the agitation and resistiveness suggest the client is fighting the mask"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In chronic CO2 retainers with advanced COPD, central chemoreceptors become desensitized to chronic hypercapnia, and hypoxemia becomes a major respiratory drive. Indiscriminate high-flow oxygen blunts this hypoxic drive and worsens ventilation-perfusion mismatch (halting hypoxic vasoconstriction and dead space ventilation), causing acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (CO2 narcosis). The nurse must switch to controlled, precise oxygen delivery (Venturi mask targeting SpO2 88-92%) and prepare for non-invasive positive-pressure ventilation. Why the other options fail: option a increases the oxygen that caused the problem and adds naloxone, which reverses opioids and not carbon dioxide. Option b is the rebreathing treatment for hyperventilation, which is the opposite acid-base disturbance. Option d sedates a client whose respiratory drive is already failing, which risks respiratory arrest rather than treating the cause.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply continuous suction pressure at 180 mmHg throughout insertion and withdrawal, since a higher pressure removes thick secretions more completely and shortens the time the catheter is in the airway"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instil 10 mL of sterile normal saline into the tube before suctioning to loosen thick secretions, since moistening the mucus makes it easier to clear and reduces the number of passes required"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rotate the catheter continuously for 30 seconds while applying maximum suction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pre-oxygenate with 100% oxygen for at least 30-60 seconds and limit suction catheter application to 10-15 seconds only during withdrawal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Evidence-based endotracheal suctioning guidelines mandate: 1) Pre-oxygenate with 100% O2 for 30-60 seconds, 2) Advance catheter without applying suction until resistance or carina is reached, then pull back 1-2 cm, 3) Apply intermittent suction only while withdrawing and rotating the catheter, 4) Limit suction duration to no more than 10-15 seconds to avoid severe hypoxemia and vagal bradycardia, and 5) Maintain suction pressure between 80-120 mmHg. Routine saline instillation is contraindicated because it dislodges bacteria into lower airways. Why the other options fail: option a applies suction during insertion at a pressure above the recommended range, which strips oxygen and traumatises the mucosa on the way down. Option b instils saline routinely, which is no longer recommended because it dislodges biofilm into the lower airways. Option c applies maximum suction for 30 seconds, twice the safe duration, and risks hypoxaemia and vagal bradycardia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Call for immediate emergency help, extend the client's neck, open the stoma with a tracheal dilator, and gently reinsert a spare lubricated tube with the obturator"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cover the stoma with an airtight occlusive dressing and instruct the client to breathe deeply through the mouth, since the trachea will re-open spontaneously"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a large nasogastric tube into the stoma to hold the airway patent, since the tube will stent the tract open until the surgical team arrives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the client in the prone position and give small oral sips of water to lubricate the larynx, so that the upper airway can open and airflow resumes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Accidental decannulation in an immature tracheostomy tract (under 7 days post-op) is a critical airway emergency because the stomal tract collapses rapidly. The nurse must immediately call for assistance, maintain neck extension to open the stoma, gently grasp stay sutures or use a tracheal dilator to spread the stoma, and insert the spare lubricated tracheostomy tube with the obturator (removing the obturator immediately once inserted). If reinsertion is impossible, ventilate via bag-valve-mask over the stoma or mouth. Why the other options fail: option b seals the only route the client has for breathing, which converts a partial obstruction into a complete one. Option c passes a feeding tube into an airway that has no cartilage to hold it, which neither stents the tract nor delivers oxygen. Option d puts a distressed client face down and gives fluid by mouth, both of which worsen an airway that is already compromised.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Compensated metabolic acidosis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Step-by-step ABG analysis: 1) pH 7.26 is below 7.35, indicating acidosis. 2) PaCO2 58 mmHg is above 45 mmHg (acidic), indicating that respiratory retention of carbon dioxide is the primary cause of the acidosis. 3) HCO3 24 mEq/L is within normal limits (22-26 mEq/L), meaning renal compensation has not yet occurred. Therefore, this is uncompensated respiratory acidosis secondary to opioid-induced hypoventilation. Why the other options fail: option a is wrong on both counts, since the pH is acidotic rather than compensated and the bicarbonate is normal rather than raised. Option c treats a high carbon dioxide with a low pH as alkalosis, which confuses the direction of the primary change. Option d names metabolic alkalosis, which would require a raised bicarbonate and an alkaline pH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Metabolic acidosis from lactic acid accumulation during the panic attack, which requires intravenous sodium bicarbonate to correct the pH before the underlying anxiety is addressed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Respiratory acidosis from alveolar fatigue; initiate immediate mechanical ventilation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Respiratory alkalosis from excessive CO2 exhalation; coach the client in slow, controlled diaphragmatic breathing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Metabolic alkalosis from gastric acid loss during the episode, which requires a litre of normal saline infused rapidly to restore the chloride and correct the bicarbonate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Tachypnea and hyperventilation blow off excessive carbon dioxide (hypocapnia, PaCO2 25 mmHg), driving blood pH up to 7.54 (respiratory alkalosis). Hypocapnia induces cerebral vasoconstriction (lightheadedness) and acute hypocalcemia from increased albumin-calcium binding (perioral tingling, tetany). The primary intervention is coaching the client into calm, slow, deep abdominal breathing or rebreathing to restore normal arterial PaCO2. Why the other options fail: option a attributes the picture to lactic acidosis, but the pH is high and the bicarbonate is normal, which rules out a metabolic cause. Option b calls a high pH with a low carbon dioxide respiratory acidosis, inverting the finding. Option d invents gastric acid loss and answers it with a fluid bolus that does nothing for a respiratory alkalosis driven by hyperventilation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Primary respiratory alkalosis driving compensatory renal bicarbonate excretion, because the deep rapid breathing has lowered the carbon dioxide and the kidneys are shedding bicarbonate in response"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis requiring immediate endotracheal intubation, because the carbon dioxide of 28 with a pH of 7.24 indicates the lungs are failing to clear sufficient acid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis with secondary alveolar hypoventilation, because both the bicarbonate and the carbon dioxide are abnormal and neither value has returned to its reference range"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis, with Kussmaul respirations acting as the compensatory respiratory mechanism to blow off CO2"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The primary pathology is metabolic acidosis driven by ketoacid accumulation: pH 7.24 (acidosis) and HCO3 11 mEq/L (severely depressed). In response, the medullary respiratory center stimulates Kussmaul breathing (deep, rapid respirations) to eliminate carbonic acid as CO2. Because PaCO2 is lowered to 28 mmHg in an attempt to normalize pH, but pH remains below 7.35, this is partially compensated metabolic acidosis. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the direction of the primary disorder; a bicarbonate of 11 with a pH of 7.24 is a metabolic acidosis, and the low carbon dioxide is compensation rather than a primary alkalosis. Option b calls it respiratory even though the carbon dioxide is falling and the bicarbonate is the abnormal value. Option c describes metabolic alkalosis, which would show a raised bicarbonate and an alkaline pH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Imminent respiratory arrest due to severe airflow limitation and alveolar exhaustion ('silent chest')"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rapid clinical improvement after the bronchodilator, because the disappearance of wheeze means the bronchospasm has been reversed by the salbutamol"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Resolution of the bronchospasm allowing laminar airflow, since a quiet chest is the finding that shows the airways are no longer narrowed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Spontaneous conversion of asthma to bronchitis, since the change from wheeze to a quiet chest suggests the inflammation has moved to the larger airways"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The 'silent chest' is an ominous, life-threatening emergency sign in acute severe asthma. The complete cessation of wheezing in an exhausted, drowsy client does NOT indicate recovery; it indicates that bronchospasm, mucus plugging, and muscle fatigue have so severely reduced airflow that the client cannot move enough air to create wheezing sounds. Immediate endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation are required. Why the other options fail: option b reads the disappearance of wheeze as improvement, which is the classic error the item is testing; in an exhausted client a silent chest means airflow has fallen too low to generate sound. Option c gives the same misreading a physiological justification that does not hold. Option d invents a change of diagnosis, when what has changed is the movement of air.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It stimulates the diaphragm to contract at twice its normal velocity, which increases the volume of air moved with each breath and reduces the respiratory rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It creates positive expiratory backpressure in the airways, preventing premature bronchiolar collapse during exhalation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It increases rapid dead space ventilation to blow off residual nitrogen, which resets the respiratory centre and reduces the sensation of breathlessness"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It directly relaxes the vascular smooth muscle of the pulmonary capillary bed, which lowers pulmonary artery pressure and improves gas exchange"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In COPD, loss of lung elastic recoil leads to dynamic collapse of small, floppy bronchioles during expiration, trapping air inside hyperinflated alveoli. Pursed-lip breathing (inhaling through the nose for 2 seconds, exhaling slowly through pursed lips for 4 seconds) creates positive expiratory backpressure throughout the airways, splinting them open, promoting alveolar emptying, and reducing work of breathing. Why the other options fail: option a attributions a velocity change to the diaphragm that pursed-lip breathing does not produce. Option c describes increased dead space and nitrogen elimination, neither of which is the mechanism and both of which would worsen gas exchange. Option d locates the effect in the pulmonary vasculature, whereas the benefit is in the airways, where the back pressure holds collapsing bronchioles open.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer IV ceftriaxone -> Apply oxygen -> Collect blood cultures -> Administer paracetamol"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer paracetamol -> Collect blood cultures -> Administer IV ceftriaxone -> Apply oxygen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply oxygen -> Collect blood and sputum cultures -> Administer IV ceftriaxone -> Administer paracetamol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Collect sputum culture in 24 hours -> Administer IV ceftriaxone immediately -> Apply oxygen"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Priority sequence: 1) Oxygenation first (ABC principle) to relieve hypoxemia. 2) Diagnostic microbiologic specimens (blood cultures from two separate sites and sputum culture) MUST be collected BEFORE initiating antimicrobial therapy to avoid sterilizing specimens and compromising organism identification. 3) Initiate broad-spectrum antibiotics promptly within 1-2 hours. 4) Administer antipyretic for symptom relief. Why the other options fail: option a gives the antibiotic before the cultures, which sterilises the specimen the team is about to take. Option b postpones oxygen behind three other tasks, when the client is hypoxaemic and confused. Option d delays the sputum specimen by 24 hours, which is the opposite of the same error: a specimen taken that late is likely to grow nothing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Droplet precautions in a private room with a surgical mask for staff within three feet of the client, eye protection for airway procedures, and the door kept closed during care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Contact precautions with gown and gloves on entering the room, a dedicated stethoscope left inside the room, and hand hygiene before and after every contact with the client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Standard precautions with a regular adult surgical mask for all nursing staff, gloves for body fluid contact, and no restriction on the client moving around the ward"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR) with negative air pressure, fit-tested N95 respirator mask for staff, and surgical mask on client during transport"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Mycobacterium tuberculosis is transmitted via droplet nuclei (< 5 micrometers) that remain suspended in the air. Mandatory airborne precautions include: 1) Negative-pressure airborne infection isolation room (AIIR) exhausting at least 6-12 air changes/hour, 2) Healthcare personnel must wear a fit-tested particulate respirator (N95 or higher) prior to entering, and 3) The client wears a standard surgical mask if leaving the room for essential transport. Why the other options fail: option a uses droplet precautions, which suit organisms spread by large droplets over short distances, not the small droplet nuclei of tuberculosis. Option b uses contact precautions, which do not address airborne transmission at all. Option c offers standard precautions with a surgical mask, which does not filter the particle size that carries tuberculosis and is fitted to no one.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"This reddish-orange discoloration is an expected, harmless side effect of Rifampicin and is not a sign of bleeding or drug toxicity.\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop all the medications immediately and attend the emergency department for renal function tests, since discoloured urine indicates acute renal failure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "This indicates severe hepatic failure from pyrazinamide, so double the fluid intake and return to the clinic for liver function tests in the morning"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take two iron tablets daily to replace the haemoglobin being lost in the urine, and increase dietary protein until the colour returns to normal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Rifampicin causes benign reddish-orange discoloration of all bodily fluids (urine, sweat, saliva, tears, feces). It may permanently stain soft contact lenses. The nurse should reassure the client that this is harmless and does not indicate hematuria or liver damage. In contrast, hepatitis (yellow jaundice) or Ethambutol-induced optic neuritis (loss of red-green visual acuity) requires immediate medical evaluation. Why the other options fail: option b stops therapy on the basis of a harmless effect, which risks resistance and relapse. Option c attributes the colour to hepatic failure, which does not discolour urine orange and would come with jaundice rather than a red-orange tint. Option d treats an imagined haemoglobin loss with iron, which does not change the colour and duplicates a drug the client may already be taking.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Left lateral decubitus with the diseased lung dependent, so that gravity holds the fluid against the chest wall and away from the mediastinum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sitting upright on the edge of the bed, leaning forward over an overbed table padded with pillows"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Flat supine with the legs elevated in Trendelenburg, so that the effusion pools away from the diaphragm and the needle can be inserted lower"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prone with a pillow beneath the abdomen, so that the weight of the chest falls forward and widens the posterior intercostal spaces"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The optimal position for thoracentesis is upright sitting on the edge of the bed, leaning forward with arms resting on a pillow-padded overbed table. This position widens the intercostal spaces, spreads the ribs, allows fluid to pool at the base of the pleural space, and provides safe access for needle insertion while minimizing visceral pleural puncture. Why the other options fail: option a lies the client on the diseased side, which lets the effusion pool against the site of puncture and restricts breathing on the good side. Option c positions the client head-down, which pushes the diaphragm and the effusion upwards into the field and makes the tap more difficult. Option d places the client face down, which neither widens the intercostal spaces nor gives access to the posterior chest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The chest tube is functioning normally and the lung has fully re-expanded, since bubbling shows that air is moving freely through the system"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The suction control chamber is set to an inadequate negative pressure, so the wall suction should be turned up until the bubbling stops"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An air leak is present in the drainage system or pleural space"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Subcutaneous emphysema is resolving into the intrapleural space, which is why the bubbling has become continuous rather than intermittent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In a closed underwater-seal drainage system: Tidaling (fluid fluctuations with respirations) is normal. Intermittent bubbling on exhalation is expected initially when air is evacuated from a pneumothorax. Continuous, vigorous bubbling in the water-seal chamber indicates an air leak—either in the system tubing/connections or inside the client's chest (bronchopleural fistula or unresolved large pleural tear). The nurse must locate the leak immediately. Why the other options fail: option a treats continuous bubbling as evidence of re-expansion, when it is the sign of an ongoing air leak. Option b confuses the water-seal chamber with the suction control chamber; bubbling in the latter is normal and bubbling in the former is not. Option d invents a mechanism by which subcutaneous emphysema would resolve into the pleural space, which is not how gas moves.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately re-insert the expelled chest tube into the stoma with sterile forceps"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an airtight occlusive dressing taped securely on all four sides with heavy silk tape"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Clamp the remaining tubing and submerge the tip in sterile water"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cover the insertion site immediately with a sterile petroleum gauze dressing taped on THREE sides"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$When a chest tube is accidentally dislodged, the priority is to seal the wound while preventing tension pneumothorax. Cover the site immediately with sterile petrolatum gauze taped on THREE sides. This creates a one-way flutter valve: it prevents external atmospheric air from being sucked into the pleural cavity during inspiration, but allows trapped intrapleural air to escape during expiration, averting tension pneumothorax. Why the other options fail: option a re-inserts a contaminated tube into the pleural space, which risks infection and creates a fresh tract. Option b seals the wound on all four sides, which prevents inspired air escaping and can produce the tension pneumothorax the dressing is meant to avoid. Option c clamps and submerges, which is the correct response to a tube that has disconnected from the drain, not one that has come out of the client.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Elevate the head of the bed to high-Fowler's position, apply high-flow oxygen, and activate the rapid response team"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transport the client to the ultrasound room for lower limb Doppler imaging, since confirming the diagnosis before treatment avoids unnecessary anticoagulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral aspirin 300 mg with water, since platelet inhibition limits further clot propagation and can be given without waiting for imaging"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instruct the client to cough vigorously to clear a suspected mucus plug, since the sudden dyspnoea and pleuritic pain suggest large airway obstruction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Acute pulmonary embolism causes sudden obstruction of pulmonary arterial blood flow, producing acute alveolar dead space, severe hypoxemia, and acute right heart strain. Immediate priority nursing actions: 1) Position in high-Fowler's to maximize diaphragmatic excursion and ease breathing mechanics, 2) Apply high-flow oxygen immediately to treat tissue hypoxia, 3) Activate emergency/rapid response team, and 4) Establish IV access for anticoagulation (unfractionated heparin). Why the other options fail: option b leaves a hypoxaemic client with a heart rate of 124 to go for a leg scan before receiving oxygen. Option c gives an antiplatelet that does not treat a pulmonary embolus and delays oxygen and anticoagulation. Option d mistakes a vascular emergency for a mucus plug and asks the client to cough while severely hypoxaemic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Prone positioning increases cardiac preload by compressing the inferior vena cava, which raises the pressure driving blood through the pulmonary circulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prone positioning recruits collapsed dorsal lung units and improves ventilation-perfusion matching"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prone positioning decreases tidal volume, which reduces barotrauma and the risk of pneumothorax in already stiff ARDS lungs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prone positioning halts alveolar exudate secretion by reversing the hydrostatic gradient, so that fluid stops leaking into the alveolar spaces"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In ARDS, alveolar collapse and inflammatory consolidation occur predominantly in dependent, dorsal lung regions. In the supine position, the weight of the heart and ventral tissue further compresses these dorsal lung units. Placing the client in the prone position (proning) relieves cardiac compression, recruits large dorsal alveolar regions, and redistributes pulmonary blood flow, markedly improving ventilation-perfusion matching and arterial oxygenation. Why the other options fail: option a describes a compression that would reduce, not increase, venous return and has no relation to shunt physiology. Option c presents a reduction in tidal volume as the goal, when lung-protective ventilation already sets it and proning does not lower it. Option d attributes the improvement to a change in fluid secretion, whereas proning works by opening dorsal lung units that were perfused but not ventilated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Swallow a glass of warm milk immediately after the second puff, since the milk coats the pharynx and washes the steroid off the mucosa"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inhale rapidly through the mouth and exhale immediately through the nose, since a fast inhalation delivers more of the drug to the lower airways"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rinse mouth thoroughly with water and spit it out after each inhalation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use the fluticasone inhaler only as a rescue medication when wheezing starts, since using it every day leads to tolerance of its effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Inhaled corticosteroids (fluticasone, budesonide) deposit small drug particles onto the oral pharyngeal mucosa, which can suppress local immunity and cause oral candidiasis (thrush) and dysphonia (hoarseness). Using a spacer device reduces oropharyngeal deposition, and rinsing the mouth with water and spitting it out immediately after each use clears residual steroid particles from oral tissues. Why the other options fail: option a coats the mucosa with milk rather than removing the drug from it. Option b describes a rapid inhalation, which increases oropharyngeal deposition and worsens the very problem the rinse is meant to solve. Option d treats a daily maintenance corticosteroid as a rescue bronchodilator, which both under-treats the asthma and exposes the client to thrush daily.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase the infusion rate by 5 mL per hour to overcome tachyphylaxis, since the return of wheeze means the current dose is no longer adequate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer an oral antiemetic and reassure the client that the tremor is an expected effect, since it usually settles after the first week of therapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer 100 mg of oral potassium chloride for suspected hypokalaemia, since theophylline drives potassium into cells and causes the arrhythmia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the aminophylline infusion immediately, notify the prescriber, and anticipate a stat serum theophylline level"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Aminophylline/theophylline has a narrow therapeutic range (typically 10-20 mcg/mL). Toxicity manifests initially with gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting), neurological excitation (tremors, restlessness, seizures), and cardiovascular dysrhythmias (tachycardia, PVCs, ventricular tachycardia). The nurse must immediately stop the infusion, alert the physician, obtain a stat serum theophylline concentration, and prepare supportive antidotes (activated charcoal, beta-blockers). Why the other options fail: option a increases the dose of the drug producing the toxicity. Option b treats the nausea and leaves the infusion running, when the serum level is likely above the therapeutic range. Option c assumes a hypokalaemia that theophylline does not reliably cause in this way, and gives potassium without a level while ignoring the drug that is actually responsible.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20 rows updated.
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch11.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 11: GYN module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- Same defect profile as TCAM and NUTR: the wrong options were not merely
-- incorrect, they were unchoosable. 'Perform an immediate emergency radical
-- hysterectomy in the rural community outpost'; 'Scold the client regarding
-- premarital sexual activity'; 'Progestogen is added solely to make the tablet
-- taste sweet'; 'High-dose systemic chemotherapy infusions' for a vaginal
-- pessary. A candidate could clear the module on reading alone.
--
-- Each wrong option is now a near-miss a partly prepared candidate would weigh:
-- adenomyosis against leiomyoma in PALM-COEIN; category 2 or 3 against 4 in the
-- WHO MEC; a Bartholin cyst against an abscess; atrophy against endometrial
-- cancer in postmenopausal bleeding. Keys were shortened where they carried
-- their own justification; key letters unchanged (a b c d repeating).
--
-- Defects repaired: no distractor block on any of the 20, and the keyed option
-- was the longest in 19 of 20 (mean ratio 2.42, worst 3.40). After: mean 1.06,
-- worst 1.19. Two wording errors in the originals also corrected: 'cre
-- preferred' in 007 and 'scaring' in 012.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Provide auditory and visual privacy, explain the examination, obtain informed consent, offer a documented chaperone, and keep the visit confidential"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insist that a parent or guardian sign written authorisation before any examination, because she is under 18 years old"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Examine her with the door closed but without a chaperone, since she has asked for confidentiality and a third person would breach it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain consent and examine her with a chaperone present, but do not discuss the procedure in advance in case it increases her anxiety"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under national and international adolescent sexual and reproductive health guidelines, sexually active adolescents have the right to confidential, non-judgmental counseling, screening, and contraceptive services. For an intimate pelvic examination, the nurse must: (1) guarantee visual and auditory privacy; (2) explain the indications, instruments, and sensations to alleviate anxiety; (3) secure the client's voluntary informed consent; and (4) provide and document the presence of an impartial chaperone to protect both client dignity and provider safety. Why the other options fail: option b applies a parental-authorisation rule that adolescent sexual and reproductive health standards do not impose, and it denies the service she came for. Option c keeps the record confidential but exposes her body, which is not confidentiality and breaches both dignity and the chaperone requirement. Option d keeps the chaperone but removes the explanation, so the consent it takes is not informed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Interpret as negative; dense acetowhitening is normal glycogenated squamous epithelium, so reassure her and repeat screening in 5 years"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Interpret as a VIA-positive screen showing high-grade precancerous change; offer same-day thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or refer for colposcopy and biopsy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Interpret as inconclusive because the acid was applied for only 1 minute, and repeat the test with a 3-minute application before deciding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Interpret as invasive carcinoma; refer urgently for radical hysterectomy and radiotherapy without taking a biopsy of the lesion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In Visual Inspection with Acetic Acid (VIA), dilute acetic acid dehydrates cells and reversibly coagulates nuclear proteins. Precancerous dysplastic cells (cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, CIN) have high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratios, causing them to reflect light and appear dense, opaque, well-demarcated acetowhite lesions adjacent to the squamocolumnar junction (SCJ / transformation zone). Under WHO screen-and-treat guidelines for low-resource settings, eligible VIA-positive lesions are treated immediately with thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or referred for colposcopic biopsy if suspicious for invasive carcinoma. Why the other options fail: option a misreads acetowhitening as normal; glycogenated epithelium does not form a dense, well-defined lesion at the squamocolumnar junction, and a 5-year recall is wrong for a screen-positive cervix. Option c invents a 3-minute protocol, when one minute is the standard application and the lesion is already diagnostic. Option d skips histology before radical surgery, which no screening programme permits on a visual inspection alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Non-structural: coagulopathy (COEIN-C); request von Willebrand studies and treat the bleeding with desmopressin before referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Structural: adenomyosis (PALM-A); proceed to hysterectomy because medical therapy will not control bleeding of this volume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Structural: leiomyoma (PALM-L); correct the iron-deficiency anaemia and start tranexamic acid or a levonorgestrel IUD"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Non-structural: ovulatory dysfunction (COEIN-O); induce ovulation with clomiphene and defer treatment of the anaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The FIGO PALM-COEIN system classifies causes of Abnormal Uterine Bleeding (AUB). PALM denotes structural causes: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma (fibroid), and Malignancy/hyperplasia. COEIN denotes non-structural causes: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, and Not yet classified. Uterine leiomyomas (L) increase the endometrial surface area and disrupt myometrial contractility, causing menorrhagia and severe secondary microcytic anaemia. Priority nursing care includes correcting anaemia with iron supplementation, administering antifibrinolytics (tranexamic acid during menses) or LNG-IUS, and surgical referral for myomectomy if childbearing is desired. Why the other options fail: option a selects a non-structural category for a lesion proved on ultrasound, and plasma exchange is not a treatment for coagulopathy. Option b names a structural category that is not present and proceeds to hysterectomy before any medical stabilization. Option d selects a non-structural category and defers the anaemia, which is the immediate threat at a haemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ovarian failure with a rising FSH; the long-term risks are osteoporosis, hot flushes and premature cardiovascular disease"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperprolactinaemia with suppressed GnRH; the long-term risks are galactorrhoea, headache and growth of a pituitary adenoma"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypothalamic amenorrhoea from an energy deficit; the long-term risks are low bone density and recurrent stress fractures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperandrogenism with insulin resistance; the long-term risks are type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and endometrial hyperplasia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) diagnosed by Rotterdam criteria requires at least 2 of 3 features: (1) oligo/anovulation; (2) clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism; and (3) polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound. Hyperinsulinaemia secondary to peripheral insulin resistance stimulates ovarian theca cell androgen production and decreases sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). Chronic anovulation leads to persistent, unopposed oestrogen stimulation of the endometrium (without cyclical progesterone from a corpus luteum), predisposing women to endometrial hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma, as well as metabolic syndrome and type 2 diabetes. Why the other options fail: option a describes ovarian failure, which gives a low oestrogen and a raised FSH with hot flushes, not hyperandrogenism with a polycystic ovarian morphology. Option b describes hyperprolactinaemia, which suppresses GnRH and causes amenorrhoea without the hirsutism and insulin resistance seen here. Option c describes hypothalamic amenorrhoea, which produces a low or normal LH and no ovarian enlargement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Endometriosis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute appendicitis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cervical incompetence"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary dysmenorrhoea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Endometriosis is characterized by the implantation and proliferation of functional endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity (most commonly on the ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, pouch of Douglas, and pelvic peritoneum). The classic clinical triad is: (1) secondary dysmenorrhoea; (2) deep dyspareunia; and (3) subfertility/infertility, often accompanied by dyschezia (pain on defecation) and uterosacral nodularity. Primary dysmenorrhoea occurs without pelvic pathology and lacks dyspareunia or nodularity. Definitive diagnosis is established via diagnostic laparoscopy showing 'powder-burn' or 'chocolate' endometriotic lesions. Why the other options fail: option b is an acute abdominal emergency that has no cyclical relation to menstruation, no dyschezia and no uterosacral nodularity. Option c describes painless mid-trimester cervical dilatation, which causes pregnancy loss rather than cyclic pelvic pain. Option d is primary dysmenorrhoea, which begins with the first periods, has no dyspareunia or nodularity, and does not cause infertility.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "MEC category 1: no restriction at any age or smoking load, because the oestrogen dose in a modern low-dose pill is too small to affect clotting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "MEC category 4: combined hormonal contraceptives are contraindicated, because her age and smoking load give an unacceptable arterial thrombosis risk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "MEC category 2: start a low-dose combined pill now and advise her to cut down to fewer than 15 cigarettes a day, then review in 3 months"}, {"id": "d", "text": "MEC category 3: prescribe a combined pill containing 20 micrograms of ethinylestradiol, which lowers the clot risk enough to be used"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under the WHO and CDC Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC): Category 4 represents an absolute contraindication where health risks outweigh any benefits. Age >= 35 years combined with heavy smoking (>= 15 cigarettes/day) synergistically increases hepatic production of clotting factors and promotes arterial vasospasm/thrombosis, dramatically raising the incidence of fatal myocardial infarction, deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and ischaemic stroke. Safe, non-oestrogen alternatives for this client include copper IUD, levonorgestrel IUD, progestogen-only implants, or depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA). Why the other options fail: option a ignores the age and the smoking load, the two variables that set the category. Option c treats a category 2 as permission to start the method that is contraindicated, and adds dose escalation that exists nowhere in the guidance. Option d applies a category 3 to a client who is a 4, and assumes that lowering the oestrogen dose removes an arterial risk that it does not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg now, because oral methods act faster than an IUD and she is already past the 24-hour ideal window"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ulipristal acetate 30 mg only, because it stays effective to 120 hours and an IUD cannot be fitted after unprotected intercourse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A copper IUD inserted within 5 days is the most effective method; oral options are levonorgestrel within 72 hours or ulipristal within 120 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A combined oral contraceptive, two tablets now and two in 12 hours, which is as effective as an IUD at 38 hours after intercourse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The copper IUD (Cu-IUD) is the most effective method of emergency contraception, with a failure rate of less than 0.1%, and can be inserted up to 5 days (120 hours) after unprotected intercourse (or up to 5 days after the earliest calculated day of ovulation), providing ongoing long-term contraception for up to 10–12 years. If oral options are preferred: levonorgestrel 1.5 mg is licensed up to 72 hours (efficacy decreases with time and higher BMI); ulipristal acetate (30 mg selective progesterone receptor modulator) maintains high efficacy up to 120 hours (5 days) post-coitus. Why the other options fail: option a overstates the speed of oral methods and abandons the most effective option hours inside its window. Option b is right that ulipristal works to 120 hours but wrong that an IUD cannot be fitted, which is the error the item tests. Option d describes the obsolete Yuzpe regimen, which is less effective than either the IUD or a single-dose oral method.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Heavy bleeding with clots is expected for the first 3 months, and she should return only if she soaks more than two pads a day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bleeding stops within 48 hours of insertion; any spotting after that suggests the device has perforated the uterus and must be removed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Periods return to their previous pattern within 6 weeks, because the device provides contraception only and does not alter flow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Irregular spotting is expected for the first 3 to 6 months; flow then becomes much lighter and many women become amenorrhoeic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) releases local progestogen that induces profound endometrial glandular atrophy. In the initial 3–6 months following insertion, users frequently experience frequent, irregular, light bleeding or spotting as the endometrium thins. By 12 months, overall menstrual blood loss is reduced by >= 90%, and 20–50% of women achieve amenorrhoea. Anticipatory guidance regarding this transient bleeding pattern is the single most effective nursing strategy to prevent client anxiety and premature device discontinuation. Why the other options fail: option a calls heavy bleeding expected and sets a return threshold that would miss expulsion or perforation. Option b promises immediate amenorrhoea and turns normal spotting into a perforation, which would prompt an unnecessary removal. Option c denies any effect on flow, when the progestogen is there precisely to thin the endometrium and reduce loss.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ruptured ectopic pregnancy with haemoperitoneum; secure two large-bore IVs, resuscitate, cross-match blood and prepare for emergency laparotomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Threatened miscarriage with vagal syncope; give intramuscular morphine for the pain and arrange a repeat ultrasound in the morning"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ovarian torsion from a ruptured corpus luteum; give analgesia and book semi-elective laparoscopy within the next 24 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute pelvic inflammatory disease with sepsis; start intravenous antibiotics and fluids and reassess after 6 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$This clinical picture is diagnostic of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy with massive haemoperitoneum causing hypovolaemic shock. The classic triad consists of amenorrhoea, unilateral abdominal/pelvic pain, and vaginal bleeding. Referred shoulder tip pain (Kehr's sign) occurs when diaphragmatic peritoneum is irritated by pooled blood (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3–C5). Marked cervical motion tenderness reflects severe peritoneal irritation. Ruptured ectopic pregnancy is a surgical emergency; immediate resuscitation with two large-bore IV cannulae (14–16 gauge), rapid fluid boluses, blood cross-matching, and urgent surgical hemostasis are mandatory to prevent maternal exsanguination. Why the other options fail: option b treats a shocked client as a threatened miscarriage and gives morphine, which masks deterioration while the bleeding continues. Option c delays surgery by 24 hours in a client with a falling blood pressure and a distended abdomen. Option d attributes the picture to infection, which produces fever and a normal or raised blood pressure, not shoulder-tip pain and shock.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Any day she is not bleeding, provided the pregnancy test is negative; active pelvic infection need not delay the procedure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Days 7 to 10, after bleeding has stopped and before ovulation, with a negative pregnancy test and no active pelvic infection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Days 1 to 5, during light bleeding, so that the contrast can flush residual menstrual debris out of the uterine cavity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Days 21 to 24, in the luteal phase, so that tubal spasm is reduced and the cavity distends more evenly during injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Hysterosalpingography (HSG) involves fluoroscopic radiopaque contrast injection into the endocervical canal to outline the endometrial cavity and fallopian tubes. Timing is critical: it must be performed during the early follicular phase (days 7–10 of a 28-day cycle), after menstrual flow has completely ceased (to prevent retrograde flushing of menstrual debris/endometrial tissue into the peritoneal cavity and avoid venous extravasation) but BEFORE ovulation occurs (to prevent irradiating or flushing an early fertilised ovum). Active pelvic inflammatory disease is an absolute contraindication because contrast injection can precipitate fulminant pelvic peritonitis. Why the other options fail: option a drops the infection screen, the one absolute contraindication, because contrast injection into an inflamed pelvis can precipitate peritonitis. Option c schedules during menstruation, when debris is flushed retrograde into the peritoneum and venous extravasation is more likely. Option d schedules in the luteal phase, when an early conceptus may be in the tube and tubal spasm distorts the study.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inevitable miscarriage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Incomplete miscarriage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Threatened miscarriage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Missed miscarriage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Spontaneous abortion categories in early pregnancy are classified clinically: (1) Threatened abortion: vaginal bleeding occurs, the cervical os remains CLOSED, and the fetus remains viable (cardiac activity present); the pregnancy may proceed successfully to term. (2) Inevitable abortion: bleeding with uterine cramping and the cervical os is DILATED (open), making pregnancy loss unavoidable. (3) Incomplete abortion: cervical os is open with partial expulsion of products of conception. (4) Complete abortion: all products expelled, os closed. (5) Missed abortion: embryo/fetal demise occurs without bleeding or expulsion; os closed. Why the other options fail: option a describes inevitable miscarriage, which requires a dilated os and cramping that this client does not have. Option b describes incomplete miscarriage, which requires an open os and passage of tissue. Option d describes missed miscarriage, in which the embryo has died and is retained, whereas this scan shows cardiac activity. A closed os with a viable embryo is what separates threatened from all three.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treatment should be withheld until endocervical swab results are available, so that the antibiotic is correctly targeted"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Empiric treatment is justified only once all three minimum criteria are met, not on cervical motion tenderness alone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treatment can safely wait 48 hours while a pregnancy test and pelvic ultrasound exclude an ectopic pregnancy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prompt treatment prevents tubal scarring and lowers the lifelong risk of infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) is an ascending polymicrobial infection from the endocervix into the endometrium, fallopian tubes (salpingitis), ovaries, and pelvic peritoneum, commonly triggered by Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. The CDC guidelines mandate empiric antibiotic therapy in sexually active young women presenting with lower pelvic pain and at least one minimum clinical criterion (cervical motion tenderness, uterine tenderness, or adnexal tenderness). Even brief delays in treatment lead to endosalpingeal deciliation, intraluminal scarring, and tubal occlusion, causing tubal infertility (10% after 1 episode, 40% after 3 episodes), ectopic pregnancy, and chronic debilitating pelvic pain. Why the other options fail: option a delays treatment for a culture that is often negative and that CDC guidance explicitly does not require before antibiotics. Option b adds a threshold that does not exist; any one of the three minimum criteria is enough to treat. Option c gives the same delay a different justification, but ectopic pregnancy is excluded in parallel, not by withholding antibiotics.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vulvovaginal candidiasis; treat with a topical azole such as clotrimazole, or a single 150 mg oral dose of fluconazole"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bacterial vaginosis; treat with oral metronidazole 400 mg twice daily for 5 days and avoid vaginal azoles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Trichomoniasis; treat with a single 2 g oral dose of metronidazole and screen her partner at the same visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atrophic vaginitis; treat with topical oestrogen cream and review the vaginal pH after four weeks of therapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Vulvovaginal Candidiasis (typically Candida albicans) presents with intense vulvovaginal pruritus, dysuria, dyspareunia, and classic thick, curd-like, white 'cottage cheese' discharge. The vaginal pH remains acidic (normal, <= 4.5), which distinguishes it from Bacterial Vaginosis and Trichomoniasis (where pH > 4.5). KOH wet mount reveals characteristic pseudohyphae and budding blastospores. Treatment consists of topical azoles (clotrimazole, miconazole pessaries) or single-dose oral fluconazole 150 mg (oral fluconazole is avoided during pregnancy, where topical clotrimazole is preferred). Why the other options fail: option b names bacterial vaginosis, which gives a thin grey discharge with a fishy odour and a pH above 4.5, and metronidazole would not clear the pseudohyphae on the KOH mount. Option c names trichomoniasis, which gives a frothy yellow-green discharge, a pH above 4.5 and motile flagellates, and ceftriaxone does not treat it. Option d names atrophic vaginitis, which follows oestrogen loss and would not show budding yeast.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A Bartholin cyst; apply warm compresses and re-examine in 6 weeks, since a cyst that is not infected needs no drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bartholin gland abscess; incise and drain and insert a Word catheter or marsupialise, with sitz baths and analgesia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A sebaceous cyst of the labium majus; incise and express the contents under local anaesthetic and pack the cavity open"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A vulval haematoma; apply ice packs and observe for 24 hours, because incision and drainage would increase the bleeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands are located bilaterally at the 4 and 8 o'clock positions of the posterior vaginal introitus. When the duct becomes obstructed, fluid accumulates (Bartholin cyst), which can become acutely infected and form an abscess. The patient develops rapid, severe, throbbing unilateral vulval pain and dyspareunia. Management of an abscess requires incision and drainage. Simple incision alone has an 80% recurrence rate; therefore, placing a Word catheter for 4–6 weeks (allowing epithelialization of a new tract) or performing surgical marsupialization is necessary. Why the other options fail: option a treats a fluctuant, exquisitely tender abscess as an uninfected cyst, which will not settle with compresses and risks spontaneous rupture. Option c names a sebaceous cyst, which is not fixed at the introitus in the 4 o'clock position and does not need open packing. Option d names a haematoma, which follows trauma and is not managed by observation when it is this painful.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Perform a cervical Pap smear now and refer only if the cytology shows high-grade dysplasia, because cytology is the diagnostic test"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treat the visible lesion with cryotherapy at this visit and review her in 6 months with a repeat screening test, as for a VIA-positive cervix"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take a direct punch biopsy of the cervical mass, or refer urgently for colposcopy and biopsy, to obtain histological confirmation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prescribe a course of metronidazole and doxycycline and re-examine the cervix once the discharge has cleared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Post-coital bleeding (contact bleeding), intermenstrual bleeding, and foul-smelling vaginal discharge are cardinal red-flag warning signs of invasive cervical carcinoma. A visible, friable, exophytic, or ulcerated cervical lesion requires an immediate tissue punch biopsy for histological evaluation. A routine cytological screening smear (Pap smear) is inappropriate and contraindicated when a gross, visible lesion is present, because cytology has a false-negative rate of up to 50% in invasive cancer due to surface necrosis and blood obscuring neoplastic cells. Why the other options fail: option a relies on cytology in the presence of a visible, friable mass, where surface necrosis and blood produce a high false-negative rate and a smear cannot stage the lesion. Option b ablates a lesion that has not been sampled, destroying the evidence and delaying diagnosis. Option d treats an assumed infection while a malignant ulcer continues to grow.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure and review in 6 months, because a few episodes of spotting after 12 years of amenorrhoea are usually atrophic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Request a cervical smear first, because postmenopausal bleeding is most often caused by cervical rather than endometrial pathology"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Start combined HRT to stabilise the endometrium and reassess only if the bleeding recurs after 3 months of treatment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treat any postmenopausal bleeding as endometrial cancer until it is excluded; refer for transvaginal ultrasound and endometrial biopsy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The cardinal clinical rule in gynaecology is that postmenopausal bleeding (PMB) is presumed to be endometrial adenocarcinoma until definitively excluded. Approximately 10% of women presenting with PMB have underlying endometrial malignancy. Initial diagnostic evaluation includes transvaginal ultrasonography (TVUS): an endometrial thickness <= 4 mm has a > 99% negative predictive value for endometrial cancer. If the endometrium is > 4 mm, irregular, or bleeding persists, outpatient endometrial biopsy (using a Pipelle aspirator) or hysteroscopy with directed biopsy is required. Why the other options fail: option a attributes the bleeding to atrophy and defers the one investigation that excludes cancer; atrophy can cause spotting, but it is a diagnosis of exclusion. Option b investigates the cervix when the endometrium is the source in most cases, and a normal smear does not exclude endometrial cancer. Option c treats the bleeding with the hormone that can drive endometrial proliferation, and delays the biopsy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Its symptoms are vague and mimic benign gut disorders, so most cases present at stage III or IV; work up with transvaginal ultrasound and CA-125"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It arises from the cervical epithelium and spreads without symptoms, which is why smear screening is used; work up with a Pap smear and colposcopy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It causes no symptoms until the tumour is palpable; work up with abdominal CT and a carcinoembryonic antigen level, which is raised in ovarian cancer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is commonest in women under 40 and grows rapidly; work up with laparoscopy and a CA-19-9 level, the tumour marker for ovarian cancer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Epithelial ovarian cancer is often dubbed the 'silent killer' because it lacks a reliable screening test (Pap smears detect cervical, not ovarian, cells) and produces non-specific, insidious symptoms—persistent bloating, early satiety, pelvic fullness, and urinary frequency—that patients and clinicians frequently attribute to irritable bowel syndrome, dyspepsia, or normal ageing. Consequently, over 70% of women are diagnosed at an advanced stage (FIGO Stage III or IV with peritoneal carcinomatosis and ascites). Workup includes transvaginal ultrasound and serum CA-125 tumor marker testing. Why the other options fail: option b confuses ovarian with cervical cancer and proposes a smear that samples the wrong organ. Option c offers CT and CEA, a marker for colorectal rather than ovarian cancer, and no ovarian mass is truly symptomless until palpable. Option d reverses the age distribution and offers CA-19-9, which is not the ovarian marker.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The progestogen reduces breast tenderness and bloating, which are the commonest reasons women stop hormone therapy in the first year"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Unopposed oestrogen drives endometrial proliferation and raises the risk of hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma; the progestogen sheds the lining"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The progestogen protects the endometrium, but it is no longer needed once 12 months have passed without a natural period"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The progestogen prevents the vasomotor flushes from rebounding after 6 months of oestrogen therapy, which is why it is added"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In postmenopausal women with an intact uterus, systemic oestrogen-only replacement therapy stimulates unchecked endometrial cell proliferation (mitosis), resulting in endometrial hyperplasia and a markedly elevated risk of endometrial adenocarcinoma. Adding a progestogen (either sequentially for cyclical withdrawal bleeding or continuously for amenorrhoea) antagonizes oestrogenic proliferative effects, converting the endometrium to a thin, secretory, atrophic state and preventing malignant transformation. Women who have had a surgical hysterectomy do not have an endometrium and therefore take oestrogen-only HRT. Why the other options fail: option a gives a comfort benefit as the reason for a therapy that is given to prevent cancer. Option c is right that the endometrium is the target but wrong to stop protection after 12 months, when the risk persists for as long as oestrogen is taken. Option d invents a rebound phenomenon and ignores the endometrium entirely.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fit a Gellhorn pessary without oestrogen and leave it in place for 12 months before the first review, since it is self-cleaning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Teach her to bear down several times a day to strengthen the levator ani so that the prolapse lifts back into place"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fit a vaginal ring pessary with topical oestrogen to protect the mucosa, teach cleaning and replacement, and add pelvic floor training"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Insert a weighted vaginal cone daily and remove it only once the prolapse no longer descends when she coughs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) results from attenuation and stretching of the endopelvic fascia and levator ani pelvic diaphragm muscles. In frail older women or those who decline or are unfit for surgery, vaginal pessaries (e.g., silicone ring or Gellhorn pessaries) provide an effective, safe conservative solution. The pessary mechanically elevates the prolapsed pelvic organs. Nursing care includes: (1) ensuring correct size fit; (2) teaching hygiene and removal/cleaning intervals (or scheduling clinic follow-up every 3–6 months); (3) prescribing local topical oestrogen cream to prevent vaginal epithelial ulceration; and (4) pelvic floor muscle training. Why the other options fail: option a uses a pessary without oestrogen and leaves it a year, which risks mucosal erosion and ulceration in an atrophic vagina. Option b asks the client to bear down, which raises intra-abdominal pressure and worsens prolapse rather than strengthening the floor. Option d uses a weighted cone, which is not a prolapse device and cannot be left in until the prolapse stops descending.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Collect the forensic evidence first and defer emotional support until the examination is over, so that the samples are not lost"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give HIV PEP only if the assailant is known to be positive, and defer emergency contraception until a pregnancy test is back"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise her to report to the police before any examination, since evidence cannot be collected without a case number"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ensure safety and support; with consent collect forensic evidence; give emergency contraception, HIV PEP and STI prophylaxis; arrange counselling"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Management of sexual assault requires a compassionate, trauma-informed approach addressing immediate medical, legal, and psychological needs: (1) Physical and psychological safety in a private environment; (2) Forensic evidence collection (clothing, swabs) before bathing, maintaining strict chain of custody; (3) Emergency contraception (levonorgestrel, ulipristal, or copper IUD) to prevent unwanted pregnancy; (4) HIV Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) started urgently within 72 hours and continued for 28 days; (5) Empiric antimicrobial prophylaxis against gonorrhoea, chlamydia, and trichomoniasis; (6) Tetanus and Hepatitis B vaccination; and (7) Structured psychological crisis intervention. Why the other options fail: option a sequences evidence before support, when a crying, trembling client cannot give informed consent to anything until she is settled. Option b makes PEP conditional on knowing the assailant's status, which is not required, and defers contraception that is time-critical. Option c makes a police case number a precondition of care, which no service requires and which re-traumatises the client.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch12.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 12: GERO module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- The module was written around sound geriatric content, but the wrong options
-- were invitations to guess: 'shout loudly directly into the client's ear
-- canal while standing behind them'; 'the patient is feigning confusion to
-- receive extra attention'; 'lock the elder in a back bedroom to avoid social
-- embarrassment'; 'administer a chemical sedative so the client forgets his
-- desire to ambulate'; 'high-dose systemic chemotherapy'. A candidate who knows
-- nothing about ageing can clear the module on tone alone.
--
-- Each wrong option is now a defensible error: a 30-second TUG threshold, a
-- 6-week review after a stroke discharge, diazepam as renally cleared, a
-- best-interest meeting used to continue restraint, a PEG placed to bypass the
-- pharynx, fluid restriction offered as urge incontinence.
--
-- Defects repaired: no distractor block on any of the 20, and the keyed option
-- was the longest in 19 of 20 (mean ratio 2.16, worst 3.16, with 019 at 3.16).
-- After: mean 1.13, worst 1.26. One spelling inconsistency in the stem of 012
-- ('feces') harmonised with the module's British usage.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Blunted immune and thermoregulatory responses mean infection can present as delirium and falls before fever appears"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal cognitive ageing, because memory and orientation decline steadily and falls become more common in the eighties"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Anxiety related to the move into residential care, since disorientation and falls often follow a change of environment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An absence of infection, because a temperature of 36.6 C makes a urinary tract infection unlikely in an older adult"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A hallmark of geriatric medicine is atypical presentation of illness. In older adults, blunted hypothalamic thermoregulation, decreased interleukin/cytokine response, and diminished physiological reserve often prevent the development of a classic febrile response. Consequently, common infections (such as urinary tract infections or pneumonia) frequently present without fever or tachypnoea, instead manifesting as acute delirium, functional decline, anorexia, lethargy, or new-onset falls ('off legs'). The nurse must recognize sudden acute confusion in an elder as an emergency medical trigger until proven otherwise. Why the other options fail: option b treats a change that happened in a day as normal ageing, when normal ageing does not produce acute confusion, and delirium is a medical emergency. Option c attributes the presentation to attention-seeking, which no assessment finding supports and which delays treatment of an infection. Option d treats a normal temperature as proof against infection, when blunted thermoregulation is precisely why older adults do not mount a fever.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use a high-pitched, sing-song voice and call the client sweetie or grandma, because warmth and familiarity aid comprehension"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Face the client at eye level in good light, reduce background noise, speak clearly at a normal volume in a lower pitch, and check understanding"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Raise your voice and stand behind the client, since speaking loudly into the better ear compensates for high-frequency loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client's adult children what is needed and address replies to them, so that information is not misunderstood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Presbycusis involves progressive loss of high-frequency hearing and impaired speech discrimination. Shouting or using high-pitched sing-song tones ('elderspeak') actually distorts sound frequencies and is patronizing, causing diminished self-esteem. Therapeutic communication requires facing the patient directly so visual lip-reading cues can assist, ensuring good lighting, reducing ambient noise, and speaking clearly in a deeper (lower-pitched), normal-volume voice. Elderspeak (e.g., using terms like 'sweetie', 'honey', using collective pronouns like 'did we take our pills?') is an insidious form of ageism associated with increased resistance to care. Why the other options fail: option a uses the high-pitched voice and the endearments that define elderspeak, both of which distort speech and reduce the client to a child. Option c shouts from behind, which removes the lip-reading and visual cues she depends on and distorts the sound further. Option d removes the client from the conversation altogether, which is exclusion rather than a communication strategy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Transferring independently from bed to armchair, using a frame or the bed rail"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bathing safely in the shower without help, once grab rails have been fitted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Managing finances, balancing a chequebook and paying monthly utility bills"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maintaining urinary and faecal continence through the day and night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Basic Activities of Daily Living (ADLs, measured by the Katz Index) encompass fundamental self-care tasks essential for survival: bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, and feeding. In contrast, Instrumental Activities of Daily Living (IADLs, measured by the Lawton scale) require higher-order executive functioning and neuropsychological integration to live independently in the community: managing finances, using the telephone, shopping, meal preparation, housekeeping, laundry, transportation, and managing medications. Loss of IADLs typically occurs before loss of basic ADLs in progressive cognitive decline. Why the other options fail: option a is a basic ADL, transferring on the Katz index. Option b is a basic ADL, bathing. Option d is a basic ADL, continence. All three are self-care tasks needed for survival and none requires the executive planning that defines an instrumental activity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Under 20 seconds is normal for a client of this age, so reassure her and give no walking aid until the time exceeds 30 seconds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The result is not interpretable without knowing her gait speed, so repeat the test on three separate mornings before acting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nineteen seconds indicates a low risk of falling, because the threshold for referral is 30 seconds in community-dwelling elders"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Over 12 seconds means high fall risk; begin a multifactorial bundle: medication review, balance training and home hazard modification"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Timed Up and Go (TUG) test is a validated clinical screening tool for balance, gait speed, and fall risk in older adults. Most community-dwelling older adults complete the TUG in under 10–12 seconds. A score of >= 12 seconds (or >= 14 seconds depending on guideline cutoff) indicates clinically significant gait instability, frailty, and high risk of falling. Interventions must be multifactorial: comprehensive medication review (especially psychotropics and antihypertensives), referral to physical therapy for balance/strength training, environmental hazard abatement (loose rugs, grab bars), and proper walking aid fitting. Why the other options fail: option a inverts the threshold and would delay a walking aid for a client who is already slow. Option b invents a repeat-testing requirement, when the TUG is validated as a single measurement. Option c quotes a 30-second threshold that belongs to no version of the test, and would reassure a client who needs intervention now.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "High-protein energy-dense meals in small frequent portions, oral supplements between meals, and treatment of dental or denture problems"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A low-protein, calorie-restricted diet to protect the glomeruli from hyperfiltration, with protein given mainly as clear broth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A trial of oral feeding for 2 weeks, then a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy if the weight continues to fall"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Two litres of water before each meal to distend the stomach, which is the standard first step for weight loss in older adults"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Involuntary weight loss and sarcopenia in older adults lead to immune dysfunction, pressure injuries, fall-related fractures, and increased mortality. Age-related anabolic resistance requires higher protein intake (1.2–1.5 g/kg/day in older adults with acute or chronic illness, compared to 0.8 g/kg in younger adults) along with progressive resistance exercises. Strategies include: energy- and protein-dense foods (eggs, milk, groundnut paste, fish), small frequent meals, pleasant dining environments, oral nutritional supplements between meals (so appetite for regular meals is not blunted), and inspecting the oral cavity for poorly fitting dentures or stomatitis. Why the other options fail: option b prescribes the opposite of what sarcopenia needs, and protein restriction accelerates muscle loss. Option c moves to tube feeding before oral strategies have been tried, which guidelines reserve for their failure. Option d fills the stomach with water before meals, which reduces intake in a client whose intake is already inadequate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insidious onset over months to years with progressive memory loss and clear consciousness until the late stages"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute onset over hours to days, a fluctuating course, marked inattention and altered consciousness, triggered by a medical cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subacute onset with prominent worthlessness, slowed speech and 'I don't know' answers on cognitive testing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A permanent and irreversible decline in cognition that does not resolve when the underlying illness is treated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Differentiating the '3 Ds' is critical in gerontological nursing: Delirium has an ACUTE onset (hours to days), a fluctuating course throughout the day, altered level of consciousness (hyperactive, hypoactive, or mixed), severe inattention, and is a medical emergency caused by reversible underlying physiological insults (infection, medications, hypoxia, electrolyte shifts). In contrast, Dementia has an INSIDIOUS, gradual onset (months to years), progressive decline in memory and executive function, with preserved alertness until late stages. Depression has a subacute onset, prominent dysphoria, vegetative signs, and clients often answer cognitive questions with 'I don't know' (pseudodementia). Why the other options fail: option a describes dementia, with its insidious onset and preserved consciousness until late. Option c describes depression, with its subacute onset, worthlessness and 'I don't know' answers. Option d gives permanence, which is the opposite of delirium's defining reversibility.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply bilateral wrist restraints to the bed rails, releasing them every 2 hours, to protect the cannula and prevent a fall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intravenous lorazepam every 2 hours until the agitation settles, then review the precipitating cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Assign one-to-one supervision, reorient gently, replace glasses and hearing aids, treat pain and retention, and avoid restraints"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dim the room and close the door to reduce stimulation, and nurse the client in a side room away from the desk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Physical restraints are strictly contraindicated as first-line management in delirium; they increase agitation, injury, strangulation, pressure ulcers, and functional decline. Evidence-based delirium care prioritizes multicomponent non-pharmacological interventions: one-on-one observation/family presence, frequent gentle reorientation, using sensory aids (glasses, hearing aids), early mobilization, optimizing hydration, assessing for constipation or urinary retention, treating uncontrolled pain (a major delirium driver), and avoiding deliriogenic medications (such as benzodiazepines and anticholinergics). Why the other options fail: option a uses physical restraint, which is contraindicated in delirium and increases agitation, injury and strangulation. Option b sedates a client whose delirium has an untreated cause, and benzodiazepines are themselves deliriogenic. Option d isolates a client who needs supervision and orientation, and removes the sensory cues that keep him oriented.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the client in bed in a darkened room during the day, so that sleep pressure builds and the evening restlessness settles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Offer tea or coffee in the late afternoon to keep the client alert, so that agitation does not build before bedtime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Correct the hallucinations firmly each time they occur, so that the client learns to distinguish them from reality"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maximise morning daylight, keep a predictable routine, light the room before dusk, and keep evenings calm with music and low noise"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Sundowning is a circadian rhythm disruption common in dementia, characterized by worsening confusion, agitation, and motor restlessness in the late afternoon and evening. Effective non-pharmacological interventions include: (1) maximizing exposure to bright natural sunlight in the morning to stabilize the circadian pacemaker; (2) maintaining a consistent, structured daily schedule for meals, exercise, and activities; (3) turning on indoor lighting before sunset to prevent shadows and perceptual illusions; (4) minimizing evening noise and visitors; and (5) avoiding caffeine or daytime napping. Arguing with hallucinations triggers catastrophic emotional reactions. Why the other options fail: option a removes daytime light and activity, which weakens the circadian signal and makes the evening worse. Option b adds a stimulant at the time of day the client is already over-aroused. Option c confronts the hallucination, which provokes a catastrophic reaction in a client who cannot test reality.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The prescribing cascade"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tachyphylaxis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Idiosyncratic allergic reaction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "First-pass bioequivalence failure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A prescribing cascade occurs when an adverse drug effect is misinterpreted as a new, unrelated medical condition, prompting the prescription of a second medication to treat the side effect of the first. The second drug exposes the vulnerable older adult to additional adverse effects and drug interactions (e.g., amlodipine peripheral vasodilation edema -> furosemide added -> hypokalaemia/dehydration -> orthostatic fall). The gerontological nurse must always consider: 'Could this new symptom be an adverse effect of an existing medication?' before accepting a new diagnosis and additional prescriptions. Why the other options fail: option b is loss of response to a drug over time, which is not what happened here. Option c is an immune-mediated reaction, whereas ankle oedema from amlodipine is a predictable pharmacological effect. Option d concerns the fraction of a dose reaching the circulation after first-pass metabolism, which does not explain a new diagnosis and a second drug.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Diazepam is renally cleared, and reduced renal blood flow in older adults allows the drug to reach toxic concentrations within one dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Long-acting benzodiazepines accumulate in older adults because fat mass is higher and hepatic clearance is lower, causing sedation, ataxia and fractures"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Older adults metabolise benzodiazepines faster, so a 5 mg dose is ineffective and a higher dose would be needed for sleep"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Diazepam causes acute hyperthermia and malignant hypertension in older adults, which is why it is listed as potentially inappropriate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Beers Criteria strongly advises against using benzodiazepines (especially long-acting agents like diazepam, flurazepam, or chlordiazepoxide) for insomnia in older adults. Age-related changes in body composition (increased proportion of body fat, decreased body water) expand the volume of distribution for lipid-soluble drugs, while reduced hepatic cytochrome P450 oxidation dramatically prolongs elimination half-lives (diazepam's half-life can exceed 100 hours in frail elders). This leads to accumulation, prolonged hangover sedation, impaired motor coordination, confusion, and more than a twofold increased risk of falls and hip fractures. Why the other options fail: option a invents an irreversible renal injury that diazepam does not cause and places the clearance in the wrong organ. Option c reverses the pharmacokinetics, since clearance falls rather than rises with age. Option d attributes hyperthermia and malignant hypertension to a drug whose toxicity is sedation, ataxia and respiratory depression.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A creatinine of 0.8 mg/dL confirms normal filtration in this client, so the standard dose can be given without adjustment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Enoxaparin is cleared by the liver and bile, so renal function does not influence its dose or accumulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reduced muscle mass lowers creatinine production, so a normal creatinine can hide a substantially reduced glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A normal creatinine means the dose should be doubled, because low muscle mass also lowers the volume of distribution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Creatinine is an endogenous breakdown product of skeletal muscle. In frail, sarcopenic older adults, muscle mass is markedly reduced; therefore, baseline creatinine production is very low. Even when Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) drops by 50% or more due to normal renal ageing, serum creatinine may remain within 'normal' reference limits (e.g., 0.8 mg/dL). Clinicians who rely solely on serum creatinine overlook severe renal impairment. Calculating CrCl using the Cockcroft-Gault formula reveals the true impairment, avoiding fatal accumulation of renally cleared drugs like enoxaparin, digoxin, and aminoglycosides. Why the other options fail: option a reads a single normal value as proof of normal filtration, which is the error the item tests. Option b is wrong about the route, since enoxaparin is renally cleared and accumulates in renal impairment. Option d doubles a dose on the strength of a value that may be masking impairment, which would increase the risk of bleeding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect the family's privacy, record the observations without comment, and raise the concern at the next routine review, since the caregiver is present"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Remove the elder to a place of safety immediately and without notifying any authority, because the danger to her is obvious"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confront the caregiver, demand return of the pension and give 24 hours to clean the room before deciding whether to report"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give wound care and fluids, document the findings, and report suspected neglect and financial exploitation to social welfare and adult protection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Nurses have a legal, ethical, and professional duty as mandated reporters of suspected elder abuse and neglect. The physical signs (multiple severe pressure ulcers, fecal soiling, emaciation, dehydration) demonstrate severe neglect (the failure of a caregiver to provide necessary food, shelter, hygiene, or medical care). Diverting the client's pension constitutes financial exploitation. The nurse's immediate actions are: (1) provide emergency clinical stabilization (first aid, wound cleansing, hydration); (2) objectively photograph (with consent) and document injuries; and (3) notify the Department of Social Welfare and adult protective authorities. Why the other options fail: option a defers a mandated report to a routine review and leaves the client in danger. Option b removes the client without notifying anyone, which bypasses the protective authorities the nurse is required to involve. Option c negotiates with the caregiver and delays the report, giving time for evidence to be lost and for retaliation against the client.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect his decision, reduce the risk with supervision, physiotherapy and non-skid footwear, and explain that restraining a capacitous adult is unlawful"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Follow the son's instruction and belt him into the wheelchair, because a concerned family member may consent to restraint on the client's behalf"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Convene a best-interest meeting and keep him in the wheelchair until the meeting agrees that he may walk again"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask him to sign a form accepting all risk, then allow walking only when his son is not on the unit to see it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A cognitively competent adult possesses the legal and ethical right to make autonomous decisions regarding their body and mobility, including accepting reasonable, informed risks (risk enablement/dignity of risk). Relatives cannot override the capacitous choices of an adult. Enclosing the client in a wheelchair with a belt constitutes unlawful physical restraint (false imprisonment). The nurse acts as patient advocate: respects the client's autonomy, implements risk-reduction strategies (physical therapy, balance training, escort supervision), and educates the family on the hazards of forced immobility (deconditioning, depression, contractures). Why the other options fail: option b lets a relative consent to restraint on behalf of a capacitous adult, which no relative can do. Option c defers the decision to a meeting while the restraint continues, which is detention by another name. Option d makes walking conditional on his son's absence, conceding the autonomy the nurse is meant to protect.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place the PEG now, because tube feeding is the only way to prevent aspiration in a client who coughs on all oral intake"}, {"id": "b", "text": "PEG feeding does not prevent aspiration, prolong life or improve comfort in advanced dementia; offer careful hand feeding and mouth care"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the PEG, because it removes the risk of aspiration pneumonia by bypassing the pharynx entirely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withhold all food, fluid and mouth care, and allow no oral contact, to avoid provoking another aspiration event"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Extensive clinical trials and professional guidelines (American Geriatrics Society, British Geriatrics Society) demonstrate that enteral tube feeding (PEG tubes) in advanced dementia does NOT prolong survival, does not improve nutritional status, does not prevent aspiration (patients aspirate oropharyngeal secretions and gastric contents), and does not reduce pressure ulcers. Tube feeding often leads to physical restraints to prevent tube pulling, fluid overload, and loss of the pleasure of taste. Geriatric palliative care recommends 'careful hand feeding' (comfort-focused oral feeding for pleasure) and meticulous oral hygiene. Why the other options fail: option a offers a benefit the evidence does not support, since a tube does not stop aspiration of oral secretions. Option c claims the tube removes aspiration risk, when gastric reflux and oropharyngeal secretions remain. Option d withdraws comfort as well as nutrition, when hand feeding and mouth care are the recommended alternative.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Four weeks of bed rest with passive range-of-motion only, so that the muscles recover before any loading begins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Seated deep-breathing exercises three times a day, which improve oxygen delivery to the quadriceps and rebuild strength"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Progressive resistance training for the major muscle groups 2 to 3 days a week, with balance work and adequate protein"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Daily high-impact plyometric jumping for 60 minutes, which loads the muscle most and builds strength fastest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Progressive resistance training (PRT) is the gold standard, evidence-based intervention to stimulate muscle protein synthesis, reverse muscle atrophy (sarcopenia), and restore functional independence in older adults, even in nonagenarians. Resistance training (using body weight, resistance bands, or light weights) 2–3 times weekly increases myofibrillar cross-sectional area and neuromuscular coordination. Combining PRT with balance exercises (e.g., tandem stance, tai chi) and dietary protein (1.2–1.5 g/kg/day) provides maximal protection against falls and fractures. Why the other options fail: option a imposes the immobility that caused the weakness and will worsen it. Option b does not load the muscle, so it cannot reverse sarcopenia. Option d applies high-impact loading that a frail, deconditioned client cannot tolerate and that risks injury without building strength.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stress incontinence; teach pelvic floor exercises and advise her to cough before she stands up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Overflow incontinence; teach her to void by suprapubic pressure and review her anticholinergic burden"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Functional incontinence; restrict fluids to under 300 mL a day so that the bladder never fills enough to leak"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Urge incontinence; start timed voiding, bladder retraining, pelvic floor exercises and removal of caffeine and alcohol"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Urge incontinence is characterized by sudden, involuntary detrusor muscle contractions causing an uncontrollable urge to void followed by leakage of moderate-to-large amounts of urine (overactive bladder). First-line interventions are behavioral: bladder retraining (gradually increasing intervals between scheduled voiding), pelvic floor muscle (Kegel) training, prompt toileting, and avoiding dietary bladder irritants (caffeine, artificial sweeteners, alcohol). Stress incontinence involves small-volume leakage during exertion/coughing; functional incontinence is due to physical/cognitive barriers to reaching the toilet. Why the other options fail: option a describes stress incontinence, which leaks small volumes on exertion rather than after an uncontrollable urge. Option b describes overflow incontinence, which presents as constant dribbling from an over-distended bladder. Option c mislabels the problem as functional and answers it with fluid restriction, which concentrates the urine and irritates the bladder further.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hold the cane in the left hand, opposite the affected knee; move the cane and the right leg together, then step through with the left"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hold the cane in the right hand, on the same side as the affected knee, and advance it after both legs have moved"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hold the cane in the left hand but advance it with the left leg, so that the stronger side leads every step"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold the cane in the right hand and advance it first, then bring the right leg up to the cane before the left"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Proper cane technique requires holding the cane on the UNAFFECTED (stronger) side (the 'COAL' acronym: Cane Opposite Affected Leg). This widens the base of support and shifts the body's center of gravity away from the damaged joint. Sequence of ambulation: (1) advance the cane and the affected (weaker) leg forward simultaneously, distributing body weight between the cane and the weak leg; (2) step forward with the unaffected (stronger) leg past the cane. When navigating stairs, remember: 'Up with the good (strong leg leads), down with the bad (cane and weak leg lead)'. Why the other options fail: option b holds the cane on the affected side, which increases the load through the painful knee. Option c advances the cane with the stronger leg, which removes support at the moment the weak leg takes weight. Option d advances the cane first and then the affected leg, a sequence that does not distribute weight safely.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the spouse a written rehabilitation manual at discharge and ask her to telephone the ward with any question"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reconcile the medicines, check the home for hazards, train the spouse in transfers and swallowing, and follow up within 48 to 72 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange a clinic review at 6 weeks and advise her to call the emergency services if anything changes before then"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the antihypertensive and antiplatelet drugs at discharge, so that falls and bleeding are not compounded at home"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Care transitions between hospital and home are high-risk periods for frail elders; nearly 20% of discharged older adults experience hospital readmission within 30 days. Evidence-based transitional care bundles emphasize: (1) comprehensive medication reconciliation to resolve discrepancies and educate on new prescriptions; (2) practical caregiver training on transfers, positioning, and dysphagia precautions; (3) home hazard evaluation (grab bars, lighting, tripping hazards); (4) scheduling follow-up medical visits; and (5) proactive post-discharge follow-up (home visit by community nurse or telephone call) within 48–72 hours. Why the other options fail: option a hands over information without teaching or follow-up, which is a handout rather than a transition plan. Option c leaves a 6-week gap across the highest-risk period, when most readmissions occur. Option d stops the secondary prevention that prevents the next stroke and would raise rather than lower the risk.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply double-loop cotton restraints to both wrists so that the cannula is not pulled out, and check them hourly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fit a vest restraint and raise all four bed rails, which is the least restrictive option that prevents wandering"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Investigate unmet needs such as pain, thirst and a full bladder, move him near the nurses' station, and give purposeful activity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give intramuscular haloperidol every 4 hours around the clock, which settles the agitation and prevents night wandering"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In dementia and delirium, motor restlessness and wandering are purposeful expressions of unmet physical or emotional needs (pain, full bladder/bowel, thirst, hunger, boredom, or fear). Restraint-free care requires investigating and satisfying these underlying needs first: offer fluids, assist with toileting, evaluate pain, optimize lighting, move the client closer to the nursing desk for visibility, and provide therapeutic diversion (folding towels, sorting socks, listening to favorite music). Restraints and 4 bed rails increase entrapment, asphyxiation, and fall injuries. Why the other options fail: option a restrains both wrists, which increases agitation and injury and is not a first-line measure. Option b adds a vest and four rails, the most restrictive combination, and carries entrapment risk. Option d sedates an older adult with dementia around the clock and masks the unmet need instead of meeting it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait until the dementia is severe, because preferences expressed now cannot cover decisions that arise later"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the daughter to confirm his wishes verbally at the time, which is sufficient without any written document"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that advance directives cannot be used in Ghana and that the family will decide by consensus instead"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advance care planning now: execute a living will and appoint a durable power of attorney for healthcare while he still has capacity"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Advance Care Planning (ACP) enables individuals to articulate their values and preferences regarding future medical treatments and to appoint a substitute decision-maker (Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare / Healthcare Proxy). This must be initiated EARLY in the disease course while the individual retains decisional legal capacity. Key components include: (1) a Living Will detailing preferences regarding life-sustaining treatments (CPR, mechanical ventilation, artificial feeding); and (2) designating a trusted healthcare proxy. Once capacity is lost, the patient can no longer legally execute these advance instruments. Why the other options fail: option a waits for capacity to be lost, at which point the instruments can no longer be executed. Option b relies on a verbal instruction given in crisis, which carries no legal weight and invites family conflict. Option c states a legal position that is not correct and leaves the client with no documented voice in his own care.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch13.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 13: CHN module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- The clinical content of this module was sound; the choice sets were not.
-- Wrong options were caricatures: 'administer a chemical sedative so the
-- client forgets his desire to ambulate', 'lock the elder in a back bedroom',
-- 'report the community to the district police for failing to achieve national
-- immunization targets', 'impose heavy monetary police fines on households
-- without latrines'. A candidate who knows no community health can clear the
-- module on tone alone.
--
-- Each wrong option is now a defensible error: fees collected by the committee
-- instead of community ownership; a property valuation instead of a community
-- profile; a lecture or free slabs offered as CLTS; restarting the pentavalent
-- series instead of tracing defaulters; a 2-week porridge supply instead of OTP
-- enrolment; customary arbitration instead of protective referral.
--
-- Defects repaired: no distractor block on any of the 20, and the keyed option
-- was the longest in 19 of 20 (mean ratio 2.05, worst 2.78). After: mean 1.16,
-- worst 1.31. No key letter moved. Rationale wording harmonised from 'feces'
-- to 'faeces', including the quoted CLTS demonstration.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pay a formal courtesy visit to the chief, elders, queen mother, assembly member and unit committee, explaining the mission and asking their cooperation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Begin door-to-door immunisation and household assessment straight away, and introduce yourself to the elders at the first community durbar"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Meet the district assembly first and let its officers inform the chief, since the CHPS compound is a government facility"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold an open meeting at the market for mothers, since the women are the main users of the service and their consent is sufficient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Community entry is the formal, socially accepted process of entering a community to initiate public health programmes. In Ghana and West African community health practice, the nurse must first pay a formal courtesy call on traditional authority figures (the Chief, elders, Queen Mother) alongside elected political representatives (Assembly Member, Unit Committee members). Bypassing traditional authority breaches cultural protocol, generates community distrust, and leads to resistance to health initiatives. Once permission, blessing, and partnership are secured, a community durbar is organized to introduce health staff to the wider community. Why the other options fail: option b skips the courtesy call that secures permission and trust, and a later durbar cannot repair the breach. Option c routes the entry through the district assembly and leaves out the traditional authority the community actually looks to. Option d assumes that mothers' attendance substitutes for the chief's consent, which it does not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clinical supervision of the nurse's prescriptions and diagnostic decisions, through a committee of senior clinicians"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Community ownership and intersectoral partnership, with members planning, mobilising local resources and overseeing the facility"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Collection of user fees to fund the district health directorate's outreach programme and staff allowances"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Day-to-day management of the CHPS compound's paid staff, including recruitment, postings and discipline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under the Alma-Ata declaration and the Ghana CHPS policy, Primary Health Care relies heavily on community participation and intersectoral collaboration. The Community Health Management Committee (CHMC) consists of respected, diverse community representatives (women leaders, youth representatives, elders, teachers). Their role is to foster community ownership, maintain the CHPS compound, mobilize communal labor and local resources, support community emergency transport systems (CETS), and provide liaison between community members and health staff. Why the other options fail: option a gives the committee clinical authority it does not have, because supervision of practice belongs to the health system rather than to community representatives. Option c turns a community body into a revenue collector for the directorate, which is not its function. Option d gives it employment powers over staff, which belong to the district health administration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To assess property values so that the district assembly can levy household rates on the community"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To identify illegal structures for referral to the municipal building inspector before services begin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To map boundaries, household locations, water sources, vulnerable groups and local resources, so that interventions can be targeted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To decide which households should pay for services and which should be excluded from free immunisation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A community profile and social mapping exercise is a participatory epidemiological assessment conducted by the Community Health Officer with community volunteers. It maps out geographical zones, household distribution, pregnant women, under-five children, water points, latrines, refuse dumps, and local resources. This enables the CHO to establish a functional household register, plan outreach routes, track defaulters, and prioritize high-risk families. Why the other options fail: option a turns a public health exercise into a tax assessment and guarantees resistance. Option b turns it into code enforcement, which is not its purpose and would deter households from being mapped at all. Option d uses the profile to exclude households from services, when its purpose is to find the ones that need them.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administering measles-rubella vaccine to infants at 9 months and vitamin A to children under five"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Screening random blood glucose at a community health fair and referring raised readings for confirmation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Providing physiotherapy and occupational rehabilitation to an elder after a stroke"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advocating for national and municipal policies that ban trans fats and tax tobacco and sugar-sweetened drinks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Primordial prevention targets the underlying economic, social, environmental, and behavioral conditions and policies to prevent the emergence and establishment of risk factors themselves (e.g., legislation banning trans-fats, taxing tobacco/sugary drinks, urban planning for sidewalks). Primary prevention prevents disease onset in individuals who already possess or are exposed to risk factors (e.g., vaccination, bed nets). Secondary prevention involves early detection and prompt treatment (e.g., screenings). Tertiary prevention aims to reduce disability and rehabilitate established illness. Why the other options fail: option a is primary prevention, immunising an infant before exposure. Option b is secondary prevention, detecting disease early in an asymptomatic person. Option c is tertiary prevention, limiting disability after a stroke. None acts on the risk factors before they become established.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "IMR = (1,200 / 20,000) * 1,000 = 60 infant deaths per 1,000 live births"}, {"id": "b", "text": "IMR = (1,200 / 20,000) * 100,000 = 6,000 infant deaths per 100,000 population"}, {"id": "c", "text": "IMR = (20,000 / 1,200) * 1,000 = 16.6 per 1,000 live births"}, {"id": "d", "text": "IMR = (1,200 / 18,800) * 100 = 6.38 percent mortality"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) is defined as the number of deaths of infants under one year of age per 1,000 live births in a given population during a specific year. Formula: (Number of infant deaths under 1 year / Total live births) * 1,000. Here, (1,200 / 20,000) * 1,000 = 60 deaths per 1,000 live births. IMR is universally recognized as one of the most sensitive indicators of general community health, socioeconomic development, and healthcare system quality. (Note: Maternal Mortality Ratio uses a multiplier of 100,000). Why the other options fail: option b multiplies by 100,000, the convention for the maternal mortality ratio, not the infant rate. Option c inverts the fraction and divides births by deaths. Option d divides deaths by survivors and multiplies by 100, which is neither the rate nor a percentage of live births.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait for laboratory confirmation of at least 50 cases before opening a line list or alerting the district team"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rehydrate and isolate patients, notify the district disease control officer, define a case, and line-list by time, place and person"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send the stool specimens first and begin the case search only after the laboratory reports the organism"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inspect the river and the water sources first, and begin clinical care once the source has been confirmed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The initial steps in outbreak investigation demand parallel action: (1) provide immediate clinical care and infection control (rehydration and isolation), (2) notify the District Health Management Team (DHMT) within 24 hours, (3) verify the diagnosis with laboratory stool specimens (Cary-Blair medium), (4) establish a clear case definition, and (5) construct a line list characterizing cases by person (age, sex, occupation), place (hamlet, water source), and time (date and hour of onset) to construct an epidemic curve and identify the source of exposure. Why the other options fail: option a waits for a case count that no protocol sets and delays the notification that starts the investigation. Option c puts laboratory confirmation ahead of the case search, so the outbreak grows while the results are awaited. Option d investigates the environment before treating patients, when care, notification and the line list run in parallel and come first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Imposing fines on households that have not built a latrine, collected by the unit committee"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A lecture on faecal-oral transmission illustrated with a projector, delivered at the community centre"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The transect walk to open defecation sites, followed by placing fresh faeces beside food to show fly transmission"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Distributing free cement and slabs to every household so that latrines can be built without cost"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Community-Led Total Sanitation (CLTS) is an innovative methodology for mobilizing communities to completely eliminate open defecation without external subsidies. The triggering process uses participatory rural appraisal tools: the transect walk ('walk of shame/praise') where villagers guide facilitators to open defecation areas, calculation of medical costs, feces calculation (tons produced annually), and the 'food and shit' demonstration where flies move from feces directly onto food. Triggering collective shame and disgust prompts the community to declare an emergency action plan to achieve Open Defecation Free (ODF) status using locally available materials. Why the other options fail: option a substitutes a penalty for participation, which is a subsidy by another name and does not create collective motivation. Option b is a lecture, and CLTS works through the community's own discovery rather than being told. Option d provides hardware, which is the one thing CLTS deliberately withholds.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leave water in an open bucket in the sun for 10 minutes, which is enough to kill cysts and helminth eggs before drinking"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Strain water through a single layer of nylon mesh and drink it immediately, without boiling or disinfection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Add a few drops of kerosene to settle the sediment, then pour off the clear water for infants to drink"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bring water to a rolling boil for at least 1 minute, cool it, and store it covered in a narrow-necked container with a tap or ladle"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Boiling water to a vigorous rolling boil for at least 1 minute (or 3 minutes at high altitudes) kills bacteria, viruses, and protozoan cysts (including Giardia and Cryptosporidium). Safe storage is equally vital to prevent recontamination: water must be stored in a clean, covered container with a narrow neck or a fitted tap. If wide-mouthed buckets are used, unwashed hands dipping cups into the water introduce pathogens. Water treatment without safe storage fails to interrupt the faecal-oral cycle. Why the other options fail: option a relies on ten minutes of sun in an open bucket, which does not inactivate cysts or helminth eggs. Option b strains out debris but removes neither bacteria nor viruses, and drinking immediately adds recontamination. Option c adds kerosene, which clears the appearance of the water and poisons the infant.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Remove food, utensils and animals, cover furniture, stay out for 2 hours, and do not wash or plaster the walls for 6 months"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wash and scrub the sprayed internal walls with soap and water as soon as the team has left, so that children do not touch the deposit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Seal every window and door for 48 hours and keep the household out of the compound for that time"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Repaint the walls with white latex within 24 hours, which seals the insecticide into the plaster and prolongs its effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) applies a long-lasting insecticide to the interior walls and ceilings where endophilic Anopheles mosquitoes rest after a blood meal. Community preparation requires: (1) removing food, water, utensils, and animals; (2) moving heavy furniture to the center and covering it; (3) sweeping floors before spraying; (4) remaining outside for 2 hours post-spraying; and (5) critically, refraining from washing, scrubbing, plastering, or repainting sprayed walls for at least 6 months to maintain the residual insecticide deposit. Why the other options fail: option b washes the insecticide off the walls within hours and removes the residual effect the spray is meant to give. Option c seals the house and keeps the family out, which is neither necessary nor safe. Option d paints over the deposit, which hides it from the mosquito as effectively as washing it off.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Optimal growth, because a weight between the median and -2 SD lines is within the normal range"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Growth faltering: a flat weight curve over 3 months is an early danger sign and needs a feeding assessment and follow-up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Excess adipose tissue, which needs calorie restriction and a reduction in the number of breast feeds"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Normal plateauing at this age, which needs no investigation and can be reviewed at the next routine visit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Growth monitoring evaluates the trajectory (trend) of growth over time, not merely a single point in time. A horizontal or downward curve on the growth chart signifies growth faltering (growth stagnation), which precedes clinical wasting or stunting. Even if the weight point is currently within the 'green' or normal zone, failure to gain weight over consecutive months indicates inadequate energy intake, recurrent subclinical infection (e.g., giardiasis, urinary infection), or poor complementary feeding. The nurse must explore complementary feeding practices, check for illness, and schedule a 2-week follow-up. Why the other options fail: option a reads a single point as normal and ignores the flat trajectory, which is the finding. Option c diagnoses overfeeding in a child who is not gaining weight at all. Option d calls three months without weight gain normal plateauing, when growth faltering is the earlier and treatable signal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restart the whole pentavalent series from dose 1 for every child who missed a date, so that immunity is complete"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Suspend outreach sessions until attendance improves, so that staff time is not wasted on small numbers"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Trace defaulters through the child register and phone numbers, and send community volunteers to bring them for the missed doses"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Report the community to the district assembly for failing to meet the national immunisation coverage target"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$An EPI dropout rate (Penta 1 to Penta 3) exceeding 10% indicates poor service utilization, accessibility barriers, or loss to follow-up. National EPI protocols mandate active defaulter tracing using the clinic immunization register, appointment tickler files, and Community Health Volunteers (CHVs). CHVs trace caregivers at home to identify barriers (e.g., maternal illness, relocation, fear of adverse events) and encourage attendance. Missed doses are given immediately without restarting the primary series. Why the other options fail: option a restarts a series that does not need restarting, which wastes vaccine and gives children doses they do not need. Option b suspends the service that defaulters need in order to return. Option d treats a health-system problem as a community offence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the mother a contraceptive and start the infant on cereal porridge, since breastfeeding alone is not enough by day 2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Advise the mother to stay indoors for 40 days without opening the windows, which protects her from cold and evil spirits"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bathe the newborn in cold herbal water, which hardens the skin and prevents rash in the first week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assess the newborn's breathing, temperature, feeding and cord, and the mother's involution, lochia and blood pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The first 48 hours postpartum carry the highest risk of neonatal and maternal mortality. Recommended postnatal home visits occur on day 1 (within 24 hours), day 3, day 7, and at 6 weeks. The CHO must examine the newborn for danger signs (tachypnoea > 60 bpm, chest indrawing, hypothermia < 36.5 °C, lethargy, poor sucking, cord bleeding/pus) and promote chlorhexidine 7.1% or clean dry cord care and exclusive breastfeeding. The mother is evaluated for postpartum haemorrhage, uterine tone, foul lochia, puerperal sepsis, and elevated blood pressure. Why the other options fail: option a adds a contraceptive and complementary food to a visit whose purpose is to find danger signs in the first 48 hours. Option b imposes a 40-day confinement without examining either patient. Option c applies cold herbal water to a newborn, which causes hypothermia rather than preventing rash.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A progestogen-only injectable, implant or copper IUD, all of which are safe during exclusive breastfeeding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A combined oral contraceptive, started now, because oestrogen does not affect milk supply after 6 weeks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A combined transdermal patch, which avoids the liver and therefore the effect on breast milk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A monthly combined injectable, which gives steadier hormone levels than a daily pill"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$According to the WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC), estrogen-containing contraceptives (combined oral contraceptives, combined injectables, and contraceptive patches) are Category 4 (unacceptable health risk) in the first 3 weeks postpartum and Category 3 (risks outweigh benefits) between 3 weeks and 6 weeks (or 6 months if exclusively breastfeeding) because estrogens suppress breast milk volume and composition and increase venous thromboembolism risk. In contrast, progestin-only methods (DMPA injections, levonorgestrel/etonogestrel implants, progestin-only pills) and copper IUDs do not impair lactation and are MEC Category 1 or 2 at 6 weeks postpartum. Why the other options fail: option b is an oestrogen-containing method in a mother who is exclusively breastfeeding, where combined methods are not first-line. Option c wrongly assumes that a transdermal route spares lactation, when the oestrogen still enters the circulation. Option d is a combined method by another route, with the same objection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Admit for inpatient stabilisation with intravenous dextrose, because a MUAC below 11.5 cm always requires admission"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Enrol in the outpatient therapeutic programme: weekly RUTF, a 7-day course of oral amoxicillin, and weekly review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the mother a 2-week supply of fortified porridge and review the child at the next monthly clinic"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give a single dose of vitamin A and deworming and discharge, since the child has no oedema and is alert"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under CMAM protocols, Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) is defined as MUAC < 11.5 cm (red) or weight-for-height Z-score < -3 SD or bilateral pitting edema. Children with SAM who are alert, clinically stable, have no medical complications, and pass the appetite test (eagerly consuming RUTF) are managed as outpatients in the Outpatient Therapeutic Programme (OTP). Management includes: weekly weight-based sachets of RUTF (Plumpy'Nut), a routine 7-day course of oral amoxicillin, single-dose deworming, Vitamin A (if not given in past month), and weekly clinic monitoring. Why the other options fail: option a admits a child who meets the criteria for outpatient care, which separates him from his family and uses a bed he does not need. Option c offers no therapeutic food and sets a review a month away. Option d gives micronutrients alone and treats severe acute malnutrition as if the problem were a deficiency.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the mother two cartons of RUTF and ask her to feed the child by spoon every 2 hours at home, day and night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a rapid 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline over 15 minutes to correct the hypovolaemia caused by oedema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the child warm, give 10% glucose or sugar water, and refer urgently to an inpatient centre for F-75 feeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give oral amoxicillin and a vitamin A dose and review in 3 days, since the oedema will resolve with treatment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) with complications (severe +3 edema, failed appetite test, hypothermia, lethargy, or severe infection) cannot be managed safely in the community. Such children have reductive adaptation, impaired hepatic/cardiac function, and are at high risk of sudden death from hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, and fluid overload. The CHO must warm the child (skin-to-skin or warm clothing), give 10% oral glucose or sugar water (50 mL) to prevent hypoglycaemia during transit, and immediately refer to an Inpatient Stabilization Center where feeding starts with starter formula F-75. Rapid IV fluid boluses are strictly contraindicated in kwashiorkor due to acute heart failure. Why the other options fail: option a sends a child who failed the appetite test home with food he will not eat. Option b gives a rapid bolus that is contraindicated in kwashiorkor, where the myocardium cannot tolerate a sudden volume load. Option d treats a complicated case as if it could be managed at home, and misses the hypothermia, oedema and failed appetite test that define a complication.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure him that a reading without symptoms needs no treatment, and repeat the measurement at the next annual screening"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give sublingual nifedipine to bring the pressure down at once, then discharge him without a review appointment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise him to stop drinking plain water and to take only small amounts of fluid, so that blood volume falls"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain the reading, advise less salt and fewer bouillon cubes, more activity and no tobacco, and confirm the diagnosis within 2 weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Hypertension is termed the 'silent killer' because it is usually asymptomatic until target-organ damage occurs (stroke, heart failure, chronic kidney disease). In West Africa, high dietary sodium from processed foods and bouillon seasoning cubes is a major contributor to hypertension. The nurse should explain the reading clearly, advise dietary modification (DASH approach, reducing sodium, increasing fruits/vegetables), smoking cessation, alcohol reduction, and regular exercise. The patient requires scheduled confirmatory re-evaluation within 1–2 weeks before establishing a formal diagnosis and pharmacological initiation. Why the other options fail: option a repeats the silent-killer error, treating the absence of symptoms as the absence of disease. Option b treats a raised reading with a single dose and removes the follow-up, which is the opposite of what the reading needs. Option c reduces fluid rather than sodium, which does not lower blood pressure and risks dehydration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place the bag on a clean barrier on a raised surface, clean your hands, take out only what is needed, and disinfect items before they go back"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the bag on the floor beside the bed so that it does not soil the client's table, and wash your hands when you leave"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the bag open during care so that items can be reached quickly, and wipe the outside with a damp cloth afterwards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Put used dressings in the main compartment and dispose of them at the health centre, so that the household bin is not contaminated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The community nursing bag is a vital tool that carries essential clinical instruments into home environments. Bag technique is an infection control procedure designed to prevent cross-contamination between households. Key principles: (1) place the bag on a clean plastic/paper barrier on an elevated dry surface (never directly on the floor); (2) perform hand hygiene before opening the bag; (3) extract only needed equipment onto a clean field; (4) used items (thermometer, scissors) must be cleaned and disinfected before returning to the bag; (5) contaminated biological waste is packaged separately and never placed inside the clean bag. Why the other options fail: option b puts a contaminated bag on the floor and cleans your hands only after you leave, so the next household is contaminated from the first touch. Option c leaves the bag open and wipes the outside afterwards, which does not clean the items inside it. Option d stores used dressings in the bag that carries clean equipment into the next home.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Exclude affected pupils until the rash has cleared, and tell the assembly why, so that other parents are reassured"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give single-dose albendazole to all pupils, treat affected children and their households with permethrin, and tell parents privately"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat only the pupils with visible lesions, since scabies spreads through direct contact and not through households"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Spray the classrooms with insecticide while the pupils are seated, which kills the mites in the desks and floors"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$School health services provide screening, preventive treatment, health education, and a healthy school environment. Helminth infections cause iron-deficiency anaemia and cognitive impairment; routine school mass deworming with single-dose oral albendazole 400 mg (or mebendazole 500 mg) is standard. Scabies requires treating the affected pupils and all household members simultaneously with 5% permethrin cream (or benzyl benzoate) while washing and sun-drying bedclothes. Actions must preserve pupil dignity and confidentiality to avoid stigmatization and school dropouts. Why the other options fail: option a isolates children for a condition that is curable within a day and announces it publicly, which drives families away from treatment. Option c treats only the visible cases and leaves the household reservoir untreated, so reinfection follows. Option d sprays an insecticide into an occupied classroom, which does not kill mites living in skin and exposes children to a pesticide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the grandmother to the police for child abuse, and remove the infant from the family's care pending investigation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave the practice alone, because traditional cord care is a cultural right and challenging it would damage trust"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Clean the cord with clean water, explain the tetanus risk, demonstrate clean dry cord care or chlorhexidine gel, and involve the grandmother"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the mother to keep the grandmother away from the infant, and give the family no further explanation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Applying substances like cow dung, ash, or mud to the umbilical stump introduces Clostridium tetani spores, causing neonatal tetanus (trismus, inability to suck, spasms, and high mortality). The nurse must provide culturally sensitive health education without belittling elders: gently wash away the substance, educate the family on the link between unsterile applications and tetanus, demonstrate the proper use of 7.1% chlorhexidine digluconate gel (or clean dry cord care), and empower the grandmother by involving her in safe newborn care. Why the other options fail: option a turns a health problem into a criminal case before anyone has explained it, and removes the infant from the family. Option b leaves a practice that causes neonatal tetanus unchallenged in the name of culture. Option d separates the grandmother from the infant without teaching anyone, so the practice returns when the nurse leaves.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Persuade her to leave the village quietly for a few weeks, so that the tension settles without involving the authorities"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refer the case to the chief and elders and accept their ruling, since witchcraft accusations fall under customary law"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that the accusation is not a health matter and confine your role to treating the child's malaria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Protect her safety and rights, alert the police, assembly member and social welfare, and teach the community how malaria is transmitted"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Community health nurses are ethical advocates for human rights, vulnerable populations, and social justice. Witchcraft accusations in rural West Africa frequently target vulnerable, destitute elderly women with dementia, depression, or eccentricities. The nurse has a duty to protect the client by immediately contacting local law enforcement, community leadership (Chief, Assembly Member), and the Department of Social Welfare. Concurrently, the nurse conducts public health education explaining the medical etiology of illnesses like severe malaria, meningitis, and stroke to dismantle harmful supernatural scapegoating. Why the other options fail: option a removes the client from her home and rewards the accusation with her absence. Option b hands the case to customary arbitration, which cannot protect her from a mob. Option c confines the nurse to prescribing for malaria and abandons the advocacy duty that the threat makes immediate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch14.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 14: LEAD module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- Content was sound - prioritisation, SBAR, the five rights of delegation,
-- LPN scope, float assignment, Just Culture, Lewin, PICO, DNR authority,
-- incident reporting, consent and EHR privacy all checked out. The choice sets
-- did not: 'instruct the client to scream at the assistant if pain develops',
-- 'post the colleague's name and suspected drug use on social media',
-- 'administer a chemical sedative', 'call hospital security to forcefully
-- arrest the grieving child'.
--
-- Each wrong option is now an error a candidate could actually make: a
-- narrative report that buries the vitals, a message passed through a
-- colleague, a slow code, security removing the family so CPR can start,
-- signing the consent on the client's behalf, a transactional reading of
-- the arrest leader's role, a 1-year complaint window offered as a legal
-- element, a second checker offered as root cause analysis.
--
-- Defects repaired: no distractor block on any of the 20, and the keyed
-- option was the longest in 18 of 20 (mean 1.96, worst 3.07). After: mean
-- 1.10, worst 1.29. No key letter moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 16-year-old with acute asthma whose wheezing has stopped, who is restless, gasping and unable to speak in sentences"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 58-year-old client with chronic stable angina who reports dull retrosternal discomfort relieved by sublingual nitroglycerin 30 minutes ago"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 42-year-old client with type 1 diabetes whose pre-meal capillary blood glucose is 12.2 mmol/L (220 mg/dL) awaiting scheduled subcutaneous insulin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 68-year-old client post-operative day 3 from elective knee replacement requesting assistance to walk to the bathroom"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Using the Airway, Breathing, Circulation (ABC) and acute-versus-chronic prioritization frameworks, the client with acute asthma whose wheezing has stopped (the 'silent chest' phenomenon) has impending fatal respiratory failure. The cessation of wheezing in a severely dyspneic, restless asthmatic client reflects severe airway constriction, muscular fatigue, and critical hypoventilation—an immediate airway and breathing emergency requiring rapid bronchodilator nebulization, oxygen, and preparation for emergent endotracheal intubation. The other three clients represent chronic stable conditions or non-immediate needs. Why the other options fail: option b describes angina that has already been relieved, which is a stable pattern rather than the most urgent problem. Option c is a glucose of 12.2 mmol/L awaiting a scheduled dose, high but not immediately life-threatening. Option d is a routine request for help to walk on post-operative day 3. None of the three carries an airway or breathing threat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A narrative account of the shift so far, given in the order the events happened, and ending with the vital signs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "SBAR: state the respiratory compromise, the surgical history, the vital-sign trend, and a clear request for immediate review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A handwritten memo left in the registrar's pigeonhole, to be read at the start of the next working day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A message passed on through a colleague, asking the registrar to call the ward when it is convenient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) is the validated, standard healthcare communication framework designed to deliver critical, concise, and actionable clinical information between professionals during urgent clinical deterioration: (1) Situation: Introduce oneself, client, unit, and the immediate problem (e.g., acute tachypnoea and tachycardia); (2) Background: Relevant admission diagnosis, surgical date, and baseline status; (3) Assessment: Current objective findings, vital sign trends, and clinical judgment; and (4) Recommendation: Explicit request for action (e.g., 'I need you to evaluate this client at the bedside immediately'). Why the other options fail: option a narrates the shift in chronological order and buries the vital signs at the end, which is the opposite of what an urgent call needs. Option c puts the request in writing for the next day, which is useless in a deterioration happening now. Option d hands the urgency to a third party and asks for a call at the registrar's convenience.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Performing the initial comprehensive admission assessment on a newly arrived heart failure client"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Providing discharge dietary teaching regarding a low-sodium diet to a diabetic hypertensive client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Measure and record routine vital signs and the volume drained from an indwelling catheter in a stable client"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Evaluating the efficacy of intravenous morphine administered 30 minutes ago for severe acute pancreatitis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The registered nurse cannot delegate tasks requiring nursing clinical judgment, critical thinking, or professional evaluation. The classic 'E-A-T' mnemonic reminds nurses that the RN must never delegate: Evaluation, Assessment, or Teaching. Collecting routine objective data (e.g., vital signs, measuring urine output from a drainage bag) and assisting with basic Activities of Daily Living on stable clients are within the UAP's role. Performing admission assessments, formulating nursing care plans, client education, and evaluating medication efficacy are exclusive legal responsibilities of the licensed professional nurse. Why the other options fail: option a is an initial assessment, which only the registered nurse can perform. Option b is teaching, which the nurse cannot delegate. Option d is evaluation of a drug's effect, which requires nursing judgement about efficacy. All three fall under the assess-evaluate-teach exclusion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A newly admitted unstable client presenting with diabetic ketoacidosis on an intravenous regular insulin titration infusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A critically ill trauma client receiving ongoing massive blood transfusions and invasive arterial monitoring"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A client newly diagnosed with acute leukemia requiring initial chemotherapy education and psychological crisis intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A stable client 3 days after laparoscopic cholecystectomy needing routine oral medicines and a dry dressing change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The scope of practice for the LPN/LVN focuses on providing routine care for stable clients with predictable clinical outcomes under the supervision of an RN. LPNs/LVNs can administer oral, subcutaneous, and intramuscular medications (in most jurisdictions, excluding IV push high-alert drugs or complex titration infusions), perform sterile dressing changes on healing wounds, monitor findings, and perform routine catheter care. Unstable clients, complex IV titration infusions (e.g., DKA insulin titrations), initial clinical assessments, and complex discharge/chemotherapy teaching must be managed directly by the RN. Why the other options fail: option a is an unstable client on a titrated insulin infusion, which needs the registered nurse. Option b is a trauma client on massive transfusion with invasive monitoring, which needs critical care competence. Option c needs initial chemotherapy teaching and crisis intervention, which the licensed practical nurse cannot provide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Step in at once and help with the transfer using a gait belt and safe technique, then teach the assistant in private"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the transfer and correct the assistant in front of the client and visitors, so that everyone learns from the mistake"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Say nothing at the time and raise it at the assistant's next annual appraisal, to avoid undermining her confidence"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to say if the arm hurts, and record the incident in the ward diary at the end of the shift"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Supervision is the fifth right of delegation and requires active monitoring of delegated care and immediate intervention when client safety is jeopardized. Pulling on a hemiplegic arm risks shoulder joint dislocation (subluxation) and severe brachial plexus traction injury. The nurse's immediate priority is client safety: intervene calmly and physically assist with the transfer using proper biomechanics and gait belt. Once the client is safe and settled, the nurse provides private, professional, constructive feedback and re-education regarding transfer techniques. Why the other options fail: option b corrects the assistant in public, which damages the working relationship and the client's confidence in both of them. Option c defers the correction for two weeks while the unsafe practice continues. Option d shifts responsibility for safety onto the client, who cannot prevent a shoulder injury.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Laissez-faire: each team member works from their own judgement, which keeps the team flexible when the situation changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Autocratic leadership: in an arrest, rapid decisions and fixed roles save lives, and debate has no place until it is over"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Democratic: a quick show of hands decides the next intervention, so that everyone is committed to the plan"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transactional: each member is assigned a task and rewarded for completing it, which is what fixes the roles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Leadership styles are situational: (1) Autocratic (authoritarian) leadership is characterized by centralized decision-making, top-down directives, and strict task allocation. While suboptimal for routine team morale, it is the most effective and essential leadership style in acute resuscitations, cardiac arrests, trauma codes, and disasters where immediate, synchronized, decisive execution is necessary to preserve life. (2) Democratic leadership promotes shared governance and consensus-building, ideal for unit quality improvement. (3) Transformational leadership inspires long-term innovation and organizational growth. (4) Laissez-faire provides minimal direction, leading to chaos in emergencies. Why the other options fail: option a leaves the team to improvise in an arrest, which produces duplicated and missed actions. Option c puts a vote in the middle of a resuscitation, when decisions are needed in seconds. Option d describes a transactional assignment of tasks, which does not capture the directive style an arrest requires.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep her at the CHPS compound on bed rest, repeat the blood pressure in 24 hours, and send for magnesium if it rises"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Discharge her home with a referral letter and ask her to attend the district hospital if a seizure begins"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the available antihypertensives, call the district hospital, arrange an escorted ambulance, and complete the referral form"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give magnesium sulphate if any can be found in the store, and deliver the baby at the compound if she convulses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In the tiered health system (CHPS -> Health Centre -> District Hospital -> Regional/Teaching Hospital), primary care nurses must promptly identify conditions that exceed the structural and legal scope of their facility. Severe pre-eclampsia carries immediate risks of eclamptic convulsions, stroke, and maternal/fetal mortality. Effective referral coordination mandates: (1) prompt clinical stabilization within facility capability (e.g., initial oral labetalol/nifedipine or loading magnesium sulphate if trained and equipped); (2) early notification of the receiving facility ('warm handover' call ahead); (3) securing dedicated transport with a trained clinical escort; and (4) complete handover documentation. Why the other options fail: option a keeps a woman with severe pre-eclampsia at a facility that cannot treat her and reviews her a day later. Option b sends her home to travel during a seizure. Option d proposes treatment beyond both the facility's supply and its scope of practice.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A septic shock client on three titrated inotropes who is stable at present but needs frequent titration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the floated nurse one stable ICU client and ask her to manage the ventilator settings independently"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send her home if no ICU-trained nurse can supervise her, and cover the shift with the remaining staff"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A stable client awaiting ward transfer who needs core medical-surgical skills, with an ICU nurse as resource"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$When allocating assignments to a 'floating' nurse, the charge nurse must evaluate the nurse's competencies, licensure, and experience relative to client acuity. A floated medical-surgical nurse has core competencies in medication administration, assessment, and routine post-operative/medical care, but lacks specialized training in critical care technologies (e.g., CRRT, intracranial pressure monitoring, complex vasoactive inotrope titration). The charge nurse should assign the most stable, low-acuity clients on the unit (e.g., stable 'step-down' clients awaiting transfer) and designate a permanent unit nurse as a mentor and clinical resource. Why the other options fail: option a gives the floated nurse an unstable client on three inotropes, which is beyond her competence and unsafe for the client. Option b asks her to manage ventilator settings independently. Option c removes a pair of hands from the unit and leaves it below minimum staffing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Collaborating: both nurses examine the workload and co-design a joint schedule, so that neither loses and patients gain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Avoiding: the manager separates the nurses onto different shifts, so the disagreement no longer delays the rounds"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Competing: the senior nurse sets the schedule herself and tells the junior to follow it without discussion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Accommodating: the junior nurse lets the senior have her way, and raises it again if the delay recurs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under the Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Mode Instrument, five modes exist: Competing, Accommodating, Avoiding, Compromising, and Collaborating. Collaborating represents high assertiveness and high cooperation (the 'win-win' approach). Both parties work together to explore mutual concerns, identify root problems, and create an integrative solution where neither party compromises core clinical standards. It fosters trust, enhances teamwork, and yields long-lasting resolutions in healthcare settings. Why the other options fail: option b avoids the conflict by separating the nurses, so the underlying scheduling problem stays. Option c is competing: one nurse wins and the other complies under threat, which does not fix the workflow. Option d is accommodating: one nurse gives way and the delays continue.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Confront her privately and insist on a urine drug screen before she takes another shift"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Report the observations confidentially to the nurse manager at once, so that clients are protected and she can be helped"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Say nothing until there is proof, so that a colleague is not wrongly accused of diverting drugs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the other staff on the ward quietly, so that they check her narcotic counts after every shift"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Impaired nursing practice due to substance use disorder directly threatens client safety and patient outcomes. Warning signs include: mood changes, frequent bathroom breaks, offering to medicate colleagues' patients for pain, discrepancies in controlled drug counts, and signs of intoxication (miosis, somnolence, ataxia). The nurse's paramount ethical, legal, and professional duty is patient protection. The nurse must NOT ignore, confront in anger, or cover up for the colleague. The nurse must report the objective, factual observations promptly to the nurse manager or nursing supervisor through institutional reporting channels. Why the other options fail: option a confronts and demands a sample, which risks a scene on the ward and is not the nurse's role. Option c waits for proof and leaves clients exposed to a colleague who is diverting opioids now. Option d broadcasts the suspicion, which destroys the colleague's reputation and the investigation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the client to sign now, and have the surgeon answer her questions when he sees her in the anaesthetic room"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain the surgery and its risks yourself, then witness the signature once she says she is satisfied"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Do not witness the form: tell the surgeon the client does not understand and needs further explanation before signing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Witness the signature on the client's behalf and record that the surgeon explained the risks at the bedside"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Informed consent is the legal and ethical responsibility of the practitioner performing the procedure (the surgeon/physician). The surgeon must disclose the nature of the surgery, specific risks, anticipated benefits, alternatives, and consequences of refusal. The nurse's role in informed consent is strictly limited to: (1) witnessing the client's signature; (2) verifying that the client has decisional capacity; and (3) confirming that consent is voluntary. If the client expresses lack of understanding, the nurse must NOT explain the surgical procedure or obtain signature; the nurse must withhold witnessing and notify the surgeon to return and provide full informed disclosure. Why the other options fail: option a obtains a signature that is not informed, which is the situation the nurse is there to prevent. Option b has the nurse give the surgical explanation, which belongs to the surgeon and exceeds the nursing role. Option d signs on the client's behalf, which is forgery and voids the consent.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start compressions at once, because a family member present at the arrest can override a written DNR order"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Run a slow code: assemble the team and go through the motions of resuscitation without chest compressions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask security to remove the daughter from the unit, then start resuscitation once she has gone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Honour the DNR order, start no CPR or defibrillation, and support the daughter calmly and privately in her grief"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A valid Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNR) order and advance directive represent the legally binding autonomous choices of a competent individual. Family members cannot legally revoke or override a capacitous adult's prior living will or DNR order when cardiac arrest occurs. Initiating CPR against a valid DNR violates client autonomy and constitutes battery. Performing a 'slow code' (faking resuscitation) is deceitful, unethical, and legally impermissible. The nurse must uphold the client's documented autonomous wishes, gently withhold resuscitation, support the family through the acute shock of bereavement, and contact the palliative or chaplaincy team. Why the other options fail: option a lets a relative override a valid written order at the bedside, which no relative can do. Option b performs a sham resuscitation, which deceives the family and is professionally indefensible. Option c removes the daughter so that CPR can start, which ignores the order entirely.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Veracity (the ethical duty to tell the truth and not intentionally deceive clients)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Non-maleficence (the duty to do no harm)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Distributive justice (the fair allocation of healthcare resources)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Paternalism (the belief that health workers always know best regardless of client views)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The foundational bioethical principles in nursing are: (1) Veracity: The moral obligation to tell the truth, be transparent, and avoid deception or intentional omission. The client has the right to know what medication is being administered and its intended purpose. (2) Autonomy: The right of self-determination and to accept or refuse treatment. (3) Beneficence: Doing good and acting in the client's best interest. (4) Non-maleficence: The duty to do no harm. (5) Justice: Fairness in distribution of care. (6) Fidelity: Faithfulness to commitments and promises. Why the other options fail: option b is the duty to avoid harm, and the question is specifically about telling the truth. Option c concerns fair distribution of resources, which is not at issue. Option d is the opposite of the principle being tested, because it would justify withholding the information.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Duty, negligence, foreseeability and a written expert opinion that the standard of care was not met"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Duty of care, breach of the standard of care, causation linking the breach to the injury, and actual damages"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Breach of duty, injury, a complaint made within 1 year, and a finding by the hospital's quality committee"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Duty, breach, causation and a formal apology from the nurse to the family"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In healthcare law, establishing professional negligence (nursing malpractice) requires proving four essential legal elements: (1) Duty of care: A formal professional nurse-patient relationship existed; (2) Breach of duty: The nurse failed to conform to the accepted professional standard of care (did what a reasonably prudent nurse would not do, or failed to do what a prudent nurse would do); (3) Causation (proximate cause): The nurse's breach was the direct, foreseeable cause of the client's injury; and (4) Damages / Harm: The client suffered actual physical, psychological, or economic injury. Why the other options fail: option a lists criminal concepts that have no place in a negligence claim. Option c mixes administrative steps with the elements, and neither a time limit nor a committee finding creates liability. Option d substitutes an apology for damages, and a sympathetic gesture is not proof of harm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Identify the nurse who prepared the dose and take disciplinary action, which shows that the standard is enforced"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrict the investigation to the pharmacy, since the packaging and storage of the drug are its responsibility"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A blameless multidisciplinary review of the system: packaging, storage, ordering and checking, not the individual nurse"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Require a second nurse to check every intravenous drug for 6 months, then review whether errors have fallen"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$A Just Culture balances individual accountability with a non-punitive focus on systemic latent errors. When a sentinel event occurs, conducting a Root Cause Analysis (RCA) seeks to answer 'What happened? Why did it happen? What systems can be redesigned to prevent recurrence?' Studies demonstrate that over 85–90% of medical errors stem from faulty system designs (e.g., concentrated potassium stocked on general wards, look-alike ampoules, unverified verbal orders, noisy medication rooms) rather than reckless personal sabotage. Punitive scapegoating drives errors underground, destroying safety reporting cultures. Why the other options fail: option a is the blame response that Just Culture exists to replace, and it leaves the packaging unchanged. Option b protects the organisation's records instead of the next patient and conceals the evidence the analysis needs. Option d moves the task to another group without changing the system that allowed the overdose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Note in the progress record that an incident report was filed, so that the next shift knows the event is being investigated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "File a copy of the incident report in the chart, so that the clinical and safety records stay together"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the allergy out of the record until the investigation is complete, so that the entries cannot be used in court"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Record the drug given, the assessment, the prescriber's notification and the treatment; never mention the incident report in the chart"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Incident (variance/occurrence) reports are internal, confidential administrative tools used by hospital risk management and quality improvement to identify safety vulnerabilities. In the patient's medical record, the nurse documents only objective, clinical facts: drug given, client physical findings, notification of prescriber, orders received, client response, and ongoing monitoring. Crucially, the nurse must NEVER record in the clinical chart that an incident report was completed or filed (e.g., 'Incident report completed'). Mentioning the report in the clinical chart can compromise attorney-client privilege and makes the internal risk document discoverable in legal litigation. Why the other options fail: option a mentions the report in the clinical record, which makes a privileged internal document discoverable. Option b files the report in the chart for the same reason, and mixes two records with different purposes. Option c removes the allergy from the record, which is falsification and would endanger the client if the drug were offered again.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bedside handover with SBAR, so both nurses see the client and lines together and the client can take part"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A recorded voice note at the desk, replayed by the incoming nurse when the ward is quiet"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A written summary left in the Kardex, with the incoming nurse reading it before the round"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A verbal report in the treatment room, kept short so that neither nurse is away from the ward too long"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Bedside shift handover is an established patient safety practice endorsed by accreditation bodies (Joint Commission, WHO). Performing handover at the bedside: (1) enables direct visualization of the client, checking IV infusion rates, surgical dressings, and drain outputs simultaneously; (2) uses standardized communication tools (SBAR); (3) allows immediate two-way verification and clarifying questions between outgoing and incoming nurses; and (4) engages the patient and family as active partners in their care plan, eliminating discrepancies. Why the other options fail: option b gives a recorded message with no chance to ask questions and no view of the client. Option c leaves the incoming nurse to read a document alone, without the outgoing nurse's knowledge of what changed. Option d keeps the report short by holding it away from the bedside, where the lines and the client cannot be checked.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Population: the adult ICU clients with non-tunneled central venous catheters who are being studied"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intervention: the procedure or bundle being tested, such as chlorhexidine-impregnated dressings"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Comparison: the standard non-medicated transparent film dressing already in use on the unit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Outcome: the incidence of laboratory-confirmed CLABSI per 1,000 central line days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The PICO(T) format is the standard methodological framework for structuring clinical questions in Evidence-Based Practice (EBP): (1) P = Patient / Population / Problem (the clinical group of interest); (2) I = Intervention / Indicator (the new treatment, procedure, exposure, or clinical bundle being evaluated); (3) C = Comparison / Control (the current standard of practice or alternative intervention); (4) O = Outcome (the measurable clinical result desired); and (5) T = Timeframe (optional duration of evaluation). Why the other options fail: option a is the P of PICO, the population. Option c is the C, the comparison already in use. Option d is the O, the measured outcome. None of them names the intervention being tested.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Moving (changing) stage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refreezing stage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Unfreezing stage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stagnation stage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Kurt Lewin's classic planned change theory consists of three sequential stages: (1) Unfreezing: Overcoming inertia and resistance by breaking down existing mindsets, establishing a sense of urgency, sharing safety audit data, and demonstrating why change is vital; (2) Moving (Changing): Executing the planned change through education, coaching, workflow pilot testing, and implementing new behaviors (e.g., scanning barcodes); and (3) Refreezing: Integrating the new practice into permanent organizational culture through ongoing audits, policies, praise, and performance standards. Why the other options fail: option a is where the change is executed, not where the need is created. Option b is where the new practice is embedded after the change, not where it starts. Option d is not one of Lewin's stages at all.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the visitor sitting nearest the desk to keep an eye on the screen until you come back"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Write the password on a note beside the monitor, so that the next nurse can log in quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Photograph the results on your phone so that you can review them while you answer the call bell"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Log off or lock the terminal immediately, so that protected health information cannot be seen by anyone else"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Protection of digital health data under data protection acts and privacy legislation (e.g., Ghana Data Protection Act 2012 / Act 843, HIPAA) is a strict professional and legal requirement. Leaving an active, logged-in computer workstation unattended exposes confidential patient health data to unauthorized visitors, staff, and other clients. The nurse must lock or log off the terminal screen every time they step away, even momentarily. Passwords must never be shared or displayed, and photographing patient records on personal mobile devices is a gross privacy violation. Why the other options fail: option a hands custody of the screen to a visitor, who has no authority to see it. Option b exposes the password to anyone who passes the desk. Option c copies protected information onto a personal device, which is a disclosure as well as a security breach.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch15.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 15: PHARM module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty clinical answers were checked and are right: first-pass morphine,
-- the metoprolol overdose, enteral tube medication, MDI technique, enoxaparin,
-- the four calculations (9 mL, 100 gtt/min, 3 mL), potassium chloride,
-- TPN interruption, haemolytic and overload reactions, DKA at 240 mg/dL, HIT,
-- naloxone titration, paralytics, gentamicin trough timing, digoxin toxicity,
-- ACE inhibitors in pregnancy, and anticholinergic burden.
--
-- The choice sets needed work. Caricatures replaced with errors candidates
-- actually make: withholding the next metoprolol dose and telling the
-- prescriber only below 40; activated charcoal for an oral overdose; atropine
-- before assessing; flushing the blood tubing into the client during a
-- haemolytic reaction; restarting a unit during TACO; doubling insulin at
-- 240 mg/dL; giving platelets in HIT; a 2 mg naloxone bolus; removing
-- monitoring from a paralysed client; a 10 gtt/mL rate offered for microdrip
-- tubing; a 3.5 mL draw from a 250 mg/mL vial.
--
-- Defects repaired: no distractor block on any of the 20 EXCEPT 007, whose
-- rationale explained every option in prose but never used the standard
-- clause; that one was rebuilt rather than appended to. Keyed option was the
-- longest in 17 of 20 (mean 1.94, worst 3.20). After: mean 1.07, worst 1.24.
-- No key letter moved and no calculation changed.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism lowers the systemic bioavailability of oral morphine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oral morphine is cleared by renal tubular secretion before it can reach the systemic circulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravenous morphine is largely inactivated by plasma esterases before it reaches opioid receptors"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Binding excipients in the tablet neutralise a large part of the active drug before absorption"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$First-pass metabolism occurs when an orally administered drug is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract into the portal circulation and undergoes significant enzymatic metabolism in the liver before reaching systemic circulation. Morphine has a low oral bioavailability (approximately 20% to 40%) due to extensive first-pass hepatic glucuronidation. Consequently, the oral dose must be substantially higher (roughly 3 times the parenteral dose) to achieve equivalent therapeutic blood levels and analgesia. IV administration bypasses the portal circulation, providing 100% bioavailability. Why the other options fail: option b moves the loss to the kidney, but morphine's problem is what the liver does to it before it reaches the systemic circulation. Option c invents plasma esterase inactivation, which is the route for remifentanil and not for morphine. Option d blames excipients for neutralising the drug, when the difference is metabolic and predictable.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withhold the next dose and check the pulse every 4 hours, telling the prescriber only if it falls below 40"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess pulse, blood pressure and consciousness, notify the prescriber, monitor for bradycardia, and file an incident report"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give activated charcoal if the client is alert, then record the dose as given on the medication chart"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give atropine 1 mg intravenously now, before assessing the pulse, to pre-empt any bradycardia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$When a medication error occurs, patient safety is the paramount priority. The nurse must immediately: (1) assess the patient's physiological status (vital signs, heart rate, rhythm, blood pressure), (2) alert the prescribing physician and charge nurse with objective assessment data, (3) implement prescribed monitoring or corrective interventions, and (4) complete an internal incident/variance report. Incident reports are quality improvement tools and must never be referenced in the patient's legal medical record or used punitively in a just culture. Why the other options fail: option a delays both assessment and disclosure, so the client is unwatched during the period of greatest risk. Option c records the dose given as though it were the dose prescribed, which is falsification. Option d gives an antidote before assessing the client and without an order.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Crush the extended-release metoprolol into a fine powder and dissolve it in warm water before giving it down the tube"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Open the omeprazole capsule, crush the enteric granules finely, and mix them with the paracetamol elixir"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask for immediate-release or liquid alternatives, flush with 15 to 30 mL of water before and between drugs, and give each separately"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add all three medicines to the enteral feed bag and let them infuse together over the next 24 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Enteral tube medication safety principles require: (1) NEVER crushing enteric-coated (EC), delayed-release, or extended-release (ER/XR/XL/SR) medications, as destroying their release matrix causes rapid absorption, potential toxicity ('dose dumping'), and tube occlusion; (2) consulting the pharmacy for liquid, dispersible, or immediate-release substitutes; (3) administering medications separately rather than mixing them together; (4) flushing the tube with 15–30 mL of water before the first drug, 5–10 mL between drugs, and 15–30 mL after the last drug; and (5) never mixing medications directly into enteral feeding formula. Why the other options fail: option a destroys an extended-release matrix and releases the whole dose at once, which can cause toxicity and occlude the tube. Option b crushes enteric granules that exist to protect the drug from gastric acid, and mixes drugs that should be given separately. Option d puts medicines into a feed running for 24 hours, so the dose is neither complete nor timed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take two rapid shallow breaths through the nose immediately after actuating the canister into the spacer"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Swallow the water used for mouth rinsing, so that none of the corticosteroid dose is wasted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Actuate four puffs into the spacer in quick succession, then take one deep breath from it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Exhale fully, actuate once, inhale slowly over 3 to 5 seconds, hold for 10 seconds, then rinse the mouth and spit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Correct MDI technique with a spacer requires: shaking the inhaler, exhaling completely away from the mouthpiece, placing the mouthpiece in the mouth with a tight seal, actuating ONE puff into the spacer, inhaling slowly and deeply over 3–5 seconds, and holding the breath for up to 10 seconds to allow drug deposition in lower airways. When using inhaled corticosteroids, the patient must rinse the mouth thoroughly with water and SPIT IT OUT to prevent local immunosuppression leading to oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) and dysphonia. Multiple puffs must be taken one at a time with a 1-minute interval. Why the other options fail: option a takes rapid shallow breaths through the nose, which deposits the drug in the oropharynx rather than the airways. Option b swallows the rinse water, which is exactly how local steroid exposure causes thrush. Option c actuates four puffs before inhaling, so most of the dose settles on the chamber walls.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inject into the anterolateral abdominal wall at 90 degrees, keep the air bubble, and do not rub the site"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe before injecting, to avoid an air embolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aspirate for blood before injecting, then massage the site for 2 minutes to speed absorption"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inject within 1 cm of the umbilicus at 45 degrees, which is the preferred site in an obese client"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Enoxaparin injection guidelines specify: (1) inject subcutaneously into the 'love handles' (anterolateral or posterolateral abdominal wall at least 2 inches away from the umbilicus); (2) DO NOT expel the air bubble from the pre-filled manufacturer syringe, as the bubble seals the medication path upon needle withdrawal to prevent tracking into dermis and bruising; (3) pinch a skinfold and insert at 90 degrees; (4) DO NOT aspirate (causes tissue trauma); and (5) DO NOT rub or massage the injection site (causes extensive subcutaneous haematoma and bruising). Why the other options fail: option b expels the bubble that seals the track and stops the drug leaking back into the dermis. Option c aspirates, which traumatises tissue, and massages, which causes bruising and haematoma. Option d injects too close to the umbilicus and at an angle that does not reach subcutaneous fat reliably.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "18.0 mL"}, {"id": "b", "text": "9.0 mL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "4.5 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "12.0 mL"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Step 1: Convert weight from pounds to kilograms: 33 lbs / 2.2 = 15 kg. Step 2: Calculate total daily dose: 15 kg * 30 mg/kg/day = 450 mg/day. Step 3: Calculate dose per administration (divided into 2 equal doses): 450 mg / 2 = 225 mg per dose. Step 4: Calculate volume using available concentration (125 mg / 5 mL = 25 mg/mL): Volume = 225 mg / 25 mg/mL = 9.0 mL per dose. (Or: [225 mg / 125 mg] * 5 mL = 9.0 mL). Why the other options fail: option a doubles the dose, the common error of forgetting to divide the daily total. Option c halves it, the error of reading 125 mg/5 mL as 125 mg/mL. Option d converts pounds to kilograms with a factor of 2 rather than 2.2, which gives a lighter child and a smaller dose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "60 drops per minute"}, {"id": "b", "text": "50 drops per minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "100 drops per minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "16 drops per minute"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — 100 drops per minute. With a microdrip set the drop factor is 60 gtt/mL, so 100 mL over 60 minutes = 1.67 mL per minute, and 1.67 x 60 = 100 drops per minute. There is a useful shortcut with microdrip tubing: drops per minute equals millilitres per hour, because the drop factor and the minutes in an hour are both 60. Why the other options fail: option a applies the shortcut to 60 mL rather than to the 100 mL prescribed. Option b would deliver only 50 mL in the hour, half the prescription. Option d is what a 10 gtt/mL paediatric set would give for 100 mL over an hour, so it belongs to a different drop factor. Always state the drop factor before calculating, because a microdrip and a macrodrip set give very different rates for the same volume.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "2.6 mL"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3.5 mL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "0.75 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "3.0 mL"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$When calculating dosage from reconstituted powders, always use the final concentration specified by the manufacturer rather than the diluent volume added (powder displacement increases the total liquid volume). Prescribed dose = 750 mg. Resulting concentration = 250 mg/mL. Volume = Prescribed dose / Available concentration = 750 mg / 250 mg/mL = 3.0 mL. The nurse draws up exactly 3.0 mL. Why the other options fail: option a divides by the diluent volume rather than the final concentration, which ignores powder displacement. Option b draws the whole 3.5 mL, which delivers 875 mg rather than the prescribed 750 mg. Option c divides the dose by the grams in the vial instead of by the concentration in mg/mL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Never give it as a bolus: dilute it, infuse by pump at no more than 10 mEq/h peripherally, and monitor the ECG"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Draw up the 40 mEq into a 10 mL syringe and give it over 2 minutes, to correct the ECG changes quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the potassium deep intramuscularly into the dorsogluteal site, since the peripheral route is too slow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add the potassium to the unit of packed cells and run it rapidly, so that the infusion is not delayed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Potassium chloride is a high-alert medication that is LETHAL if given by direct intravenous push or rapid bolus (it halts myocardial electrical conduction in diastole, causing instant asystole). Safety standards mandate: (1) NEVER give IV push; (2) always dilute in compatible IV solutions; (3) maximum peripheral infusion rate is 10 mEq/hour (up to 20 mEq/hour via central venous catheter with continuous ECG monitoring); (4) always use an electronic infusion pump; (5) maximum peripheral concentration is 40 mEq/L; and (6) verify adequate renal function and urine output before administration. Why the other options fail: option b is the direct push that stops the heart in diastole. Option c puts a hypertonic irritant into muscle, which causes necrosis and does not correct the deficit. Option d co-infuses potassium with blood, which is incompatible and can haemolyse the cells, and running it rapidly compounds the danger.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "0.9% Normal Saline at a keep-vein-open (TKO) rate of 10 mL/h"}, {"id": "b", "text": "10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) at the same infusion rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lactated Ringer's solution bolus of 500 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sterile water for injection without additives"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$TPN solutions contain high concentrations of dextrose (typically 20% to 50%), which stimulates continuous high endogenous insulin secretion by pancreatic beta cells. If TPN is abruptly stopped, circulating hyperinsulinaemia persists in the absence of exogenous glucose, precipitating severe rebound hypoglycaemia (diaphoresis, confusion, seizures, coma). If a TPN bag runs out or the pump fails, the nurse must immediately infuse 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) centrally (or 5% Dextrose peripherally) at the same infusion rate until the new TPN solution is ready. Why the other options fail: option a contains no glucose, so it does nothing to prevent the hypoglycaemia that follows abrupt withdrawal of a high-dextrose infusion. Option c gives fluid without dextrose at a bolus volume that risks overload. Option d is hypotonic and would cause haemolysis; it is never used as a replacement fluid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Slow the infusion to 10 mL/h and give oral paracetamol for the fever, then reassess in 30 minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Flush the blood tubing with 50 mL of sterile water to clear any micro-clots before continuing the unit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the transfusion, disconnect the blood tubing, and run normal saline through new tubing to keep the vein open"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure the client that back pain is a common reaction, and continue the transfusion at half the rate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The client is experiencing an Acute Haemolytic Transfusion Reaction (AHTR), a medical emergency typically caused by ABO/Rh incompatibility. Host antibodies attack donor red cells, causing rapid intravascular haemolysis, hemoglobinuria (reddish urine), flank/back pain, hypotension, DIC, and acute renal failure. Immediate management: (1) STOP the transfusion immediately; (2) disconnect the blood tubing at the cannula hub (do NOT flush remaining blood in the tubing into the patient); (3) connect a new sterile IV line with 0.9% normal saline to maintain vascular access; (4) notify physician and blood bank; (5) send the remaining blood unit, tubing, and fresh blood/urine samples to the lab for workup. Why the other options fail: option a leaves the incompatible blood running while haemolysis continues. Option b flushes the remaining donor cells into the circulation, which increases the antigen load. Option d continues a transfusion through a reaction that can progress to renal failure and DIC.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "TRALI: give a 1-litre bolus of normal saline and lay the client flat, since the lungs are under-perfused"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Febrile non-haemolytic reaction: give oral diphenhydramine, restart the transfusion, and monitor the temperature"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Anaphylaxis: give subcutaneous adrenaline, then resume the transfusion at full rate once the pressure settles"}, {"id": "d", "text": "TACO: stop the transfusion, sit the client upright with the legs dependent, give oxygen and IV furosemide as ordered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) occurs when blood products are infused faster than the circulatory system can accommodate, especially in elderly, cardiac, or renal patients. Manifestations include acute pulmonary edema: tachypnoea, hypoxaemia, hypertension, elevated JVD, and bilateral crackles. In contrast, TRALI features acute non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema with hypotension and fever. Interventions for TACO: stop the transfusion, place the patient upright with legs dependent (reduces venous return/preload), apply high-flow oxygen, and administer intravenous loop diuretics (furosemide). Why the other options fail: option a treats a hypertensive, fluid-overloaded client with more fluid. Option b names a febrile reaction when the picture is pulmonary oedema, and restarting the unit would worsen it. Option c names anaphylaxis, which presents with hypotension and wheeze rather than hypertension and raised venous pressure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Add 5% dextrose to the intravenous fluid and continue the insulin, so that ketones clear without hypoglycaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the insulin infusion and give oral orange juice, since the glucose has fallen below the target range"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Double the insulin rate to drive the glucose below 100 mg/dL, and withhold dextrose until it does"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switch the fluid to sterile water and give subcutaneous glargine to cover the next 24 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In DKA management, when serum blood glucose reaches approximately 200–250 mg/dL (11.1–13.9 mmol/L), 5% to 10% dextrose MUST be added to intravenous fluids (e.g., D5 0.45% NaCl). The insulin infusion rate is continued or adjusted to maintain glucose between 150 and 200 mg/dL until acidosis and ketosis resolve (anion gap normalizes, venous pH > 7.30, serum bicarbonate >= 18 mEq/L). Stopping insulin prematurely causes rebound ketoacidosis, whereas failing to add dextrose causes precipitously dropping plasma osmolality and lethal cerebral edema. Why the other options fail: option b stops the insulin while ketones are still present, which allows ketoacidosis to rebound. Option c drives the glucose down without adding substrate, risking cerebral oedema and hypoglycaemia. Option d gives hypotonic fluid and long-acting insulin, neither of which addresses the acute deficit.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immune thrombocytopenia: increase the heparin rate to overcome antibody consumption, and check the count in 48 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "HIT: stop all heparin at once, including line flushes, and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dilutional thrombocytopenia: transfuse two units of platelets and continue the heparin at the current rate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis: stop the heparin and start oral aspirin instead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) is an immune-mediated drug reaction caused by antibodies against platelet factor 4 (PF4)-heparin complexes. It typically develops 5 to 10 days after heparin initiation, marked by a >= 50% drop in platelet count. Rather than causing bleeding, HIT is a pro-thrombotic emergency: antibodies cause widespread platelet activation leading to venous and arterial thrombosis (DVT, PE, stroke, limb gangrene). Immediate action: STOP all heparin products (including heparin line flushes), record heparin allergy, avoid platelet transfusions (fuels thrombosis), and initiate a non-heparin direct thrombin inhibitor (e.g., argatroban, bivalirudin) or fondaparinux. Why the other options fail: option a is the opposite of the required action, because HIT is prothrombotic and more heparin worsens it. Option c transfuses platelets into a syndrome driven by platelet activation, which can fuel the thrombosis. Option d is the wrong diagnosis, and aspirin does not treat HIT.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give a rapid 2 mg intravenous bolus straight from the vial, which reverses the opioid effect completely and at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give naloxone as a 500 mL subcutaneous infusion over 24 hours, so that the reversal is sustained through the night"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give small titrated IV doses, 0.04 to 0.1 mg every 2 to 3 minutes, enough to restore breathing without full reversal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mix naloxone with a further dose of morphine, so that receptor occupancy is balanced between the two drugs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In postoperative clients who are opioid-dependent or require analgesia, administering high-dose rapid boluses of naloxone (e.g., 2 mg IV push) causes immediate, complete reversal of analgesia, precipitating severe pain, agitation, sympathetic surge (tachycardia, hypertensive crisis, pulmonary edema), and cardiac arrest. The recommended practice is to dilute 0.4 mg of naloxone in 9 mL of normal saline (giving 0.04 mg/mL) and administer small incremental doses (0.04 to 0.1 mg) every 2–3 minutes until ventilation improves (respiratory rate >= 10–12 bpm) while preserving analgesia. Because naloxone has a shorter half-life (30–90 min) than most opioids, monitoring for resedation is critical. Why the other options fail: option a fully reverses the opioid and precipitates acute pain, agitation and a sympathetic surge. Option b cannot titrate an emergency, and subcutaneous absorption is far too slow for respiratory depression. Option d adds more opioid to a client who is already over-sedated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Confirm that the client has eaten within the past hour, so that muscle glycogen can sustain the diaphragm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Disconnect the monitoring, so that electrical interference from the pump does not disturb the drug delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the sedative infusion, so that the client can signal distress by hand movements while paralysed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Confirm that sedation and analgesia are running continuously and that the airway is secured on the ventilator"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Neuromuscular blocking agents (paralytics such as vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) induce complete skeletal muscle flaccidity, including the diaphragm and intercostal muscles. Crucially, paralytics possess ZERO analgesic, sedative, or anxiolytic properties. Administering a paralytic to an awake, inadequately sedated patient causes conscious paralysis, horror, and profound psychological trauma. The nurse must verify that: (1) an advanced airway is secured on mechanical ventilation, (2) adequate continuous IV sedation and analgesia are actively infusing, and (3) emergency bag-valve-mask equipment and suction are immediately operational. Why the other options fail: option a has no bearing on paralysis and risks aspiration. Option b removes the monitoring that a paralysed client depends on entirely. Option c withdraws sedation, which leaves a paralysed client conscious, the very harm the item tests.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Within 30 minutes, immediately before the next scheduled dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Exactly 30 minutes after the infusion is complete, which is when the peak is drawn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Midway through the 8-hour interval, at the fourth hour after the dose"}, {"id": "d", "text": "At any time during the shift, provided the sample is sent promptly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Therapeutic drug monitoring for aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) and glycopeptides (vancomycin) requires drawing peak and trough levels to maximize bactericidal efficacy and prevent toxicity. The TROUGH level reflects the lowest drug concentration in the body and is drawn within 30 minutes immediately prior to the next scheduled dose. An elevated trough level indicates impaired drug clearance and is the most sensitive predictor of aminoglycoside-induced nephrotoxicity (acute tubular necrosis) and irreversible ototoxicity (vestibular and cochlear damage). The PEAK level is drawn 30 minutes after completion of an IV infusion. Why the other options fail: option b is when the peak is drawn, not the trough. Option c samples the middle of the interval, where the level reflects neither peak nor trough. Option d ignores timing altogether, so the result cannot be interpreted against a therapeutic range.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Age-related cataract, which explains the halos and the blurred vision, with a routine optometry referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Digoxin toxicity worsened by diuretic-induced hypokalaemia: hold the digoxin, notify the prescriber, and monitor the ECG"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Viral gastroenteritis, which explains the nausea and anorexia, treated with oral rehydration salts"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute hyperkalaemia, treated at once with oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate and a glucose-insulin infusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Digoxin has a narrow therapeutic index (0.5 to 2.0 ng/mL, with optimal heart failure levels 0.5–0.9 ng/mL). Potassium competes with digoxin for binding at the myocardial Na+/K+ ATPase pump. When serum potassium is low (hypokalaemia from loop diuretics like furosemide), more digoxin binds to the pump, dramatically potentiating toxicity even at normal serum digoxin levels. Classic signs of digoxin toxicity include gastrointestinal upset (anorexia, nausea, vomiting), neurological/visual disturbances (yellow-green halos, blurred vision), and cardiac dysrhythmias (bradycardia, heart blocks, ventricular ectopy). Immediate action: hold digoxin, check digoxin/electrolyte levels, replace potassium, and prepare antidote Digoxin Immune Fab (Digibind) if life-threatening dysrhythmias occur. Why the other options fail: option a attributes a drug effect to cataract and misses the bradycardia and the potassium. Option c treats the nausea and ignores the pulse and the visual halos. Option d inverts the electrolyte problem, since a potassium of 3.1 is low and the drug to give is potassium, not a binder.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lisinopril is safe until the third trimester, so it can be continued and stopped at 28 weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Double the lisinopril dose, since better blood pressure control lowers the risk of pre-eclampsia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lisinopril is teratogenic at every stage and must stop now; switch to labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switch to losartan, because ARBs are safer in pregnancy than ACE inhibitors and carry no fetal risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors (e.g., lisinopril, enalapril) and Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs, e.g., losartan) are strictly contraindicated in all trimesters of pregnancy. They cause severe fetopathy, including renal dysgenesis, fetal oligohydramnios (leading to pulmonary hypoplasia and limb contractures), skull hypoplasia, intrauterine growth restriction, and fetal death. The nurse must notify the provider immediately to discontinue the ACE inhibitor and transition to safe, first-line antihypertensive agents approved in pregnancy: labetalol, extended-release nifedipine, or methyldopa. Why the other options fail: option a delays the switch into the period when fetal renal damage occurs. Option b raises the dose of the drug that must be stopped. Option d swaps one renally acting blocker for another of the same class, both of which cause fetal renal failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Typical progression of Alzheimer's disease, unrelated to the recent medication changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An acute cholinergic toxidrome from organophosphate pesticide exposure at home or in the garden"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Serotonin syndrome, which needs high-dose bromocriptine without waiting for the clonus to appear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cumulative anticholinergic toxicity from two Beers-listed drugs, diphenhydramine and amitriptyline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society) identifies medications that pose high risks of adverse drug events in older adults. First-generation antihistamines (diphenhydramine) and tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline) possess potent anticholinergic properties. In elderly patients, reduced cholinergic receptor density and slower drug clearance lead to cumulative anticholinergic burden: central effects (acute delirium, confusion, memory impairment, sedation) and peripheral effects (urinary retention, dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, and falls). Management requires deprescribing inappropriate anticholinergics and adopting non-pharmacological sleep hygiene. Why the other options fail: option a ignores the temporal relationship to two new drugs and calls it progression. Option b names the opposite toxidrome; organophosphates cause bradycardia, salivation and diarrhoea, not dry mouth and retention. Option c treats serotonin syndrome, which this client does not have and for which bromocriptine is not the first choice.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch16.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 16: RENAL module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- This module had been recorded as passed during an earlier sweep. It was not.
-- All twenty keys are right - 50% GFR loss at doubled creatinine, third-space
-- sequestration in cirrhosis, daily weight, WHO Plan C resuscitation, dialysis
-- disequilibrium, 3% saline limits, calcium before insulin in hyperkalaemia,
-- the 31 gtt/min calculation - but none of the twenty carried a distractor
-- block, and the keyed option was the longest in 18 of 20 (mean 1.73, worst
-- 2.31).
--
-- The wrong options were also weak: 'encourage ambulation around the ward' in
-- acute pulmonary oedema, 'instruct the client to drink 2 litres of plain
-- water' during hypertonic saline, 'dissolve the tablets in grapefruit juice',
-- 'administer a chemical sedative'. They are now errors candidates make: a
-- circadian variation offered for a doubled creatinine, oral fluid for a
-- shocked cholera client, flat positioning for pulmonary oedema, gravity
-- infusion of 3% saline through a 22-gauge vein, potassium warmed in hot
-- water, catheter irrigation as CAUTI prevention.
--
-- After: mean 1.07, worst 1.28. No key letter moved and no calculation changed.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Doubling of creatinine represents an acute loss of about 50% of the baseline glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A circadian fluctuation, since creatinine varies through the day within normal physiological limits"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Increased dietary protein catabolism, with a preserved glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Permanent end-stage renal fibrosis, which now requires immediate peritoneal dialysis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Serum creatinine is an inverse biomarker of glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Under steady-state conditions, doubling of serum creatinine (from 1.0 to 2.1 mg/dL) reflects an approximate 50% reduction in GFR and renal functional capacity. In acute dehydration, this elevation points to acute kidney injury (prerenal azotemia) requiring rapid fluid resuscitation before acute tubular necrosis occurs. Why the other options fail: option b calls a doubling of creatinine a circadian variation, when normal variation is a few micromoles and never a doubling in one illness. Option c blames dietary protein for a change that dehydration explains, and BUN rises with protein while creatinine does not. Option d diagnoses irreversible fibrosis from a single acute value, before any attempt at resuscitation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "True total-body fluid deficit across all compartments, which would explain the ascites and the oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Third-space sequestration of fluid with severe intravascular depletion, or effective arterial hypovolaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravascular overload with a raised central venous pressure, since the client is oedematous"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An intracellular shift causing cellular hyperosmolality, which would draw fluid out of the vessels"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In cirrhosis and severe hypoalbuminemia, loss of plasma oncotic pressure combined with splanchnic arterial vasodilation allows intravascular fluid to transudate into the peritoneal cavity (third-spacing: ascites and interstitial edema). Despite massive total-body fluid accumulation, the client suffers from critical intravascular hypovolemia (manifested by hypotension, tachycardia, and oliguria), creating a prerenal state. Why the other options fail: option a denies the ascites and oedema that show total body fluid is increased, not reduced. Option c reads oedema as intravascular filling, when the pressure and the urine output show the vessels are empty. Option d invents an intracellular shift, which would not produce ascites or a low blood pressure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The 24-hour intake and output chart, which records every fluid in and out at the bedside"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Skin turgor over the anterior forearm, which reflects interstitial hydration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Daily morning weight, taken at the same time on the same scale after voiding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Visual assessment of pitting oedema at the ankles, graded from 1+ to 4+"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Daily body weight is the single most sensitive, objective, and accurate indicator of acute fluid balance fluctuations. One kilogram (2.2 lbs) of acute weight change is equivalent to 1,000 mL (1 liter) of retained or lost fluid. Intake and output charts are prone to human measurement error and unrecorded losses (sweat, stool), and physical signs (edema, skin turgor) manifest only after significant volume shifts. Why the other options fail: option a depends on charting that misses insensible losses and is often estimated. Option b changes late and is unreliable in older adults, whose skin is lax anyway. Option d is a late sign that appears only after litres have accumulated, and it is graded by eye.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give oral rehydration solution, 200 mL by cup every 10 minutes, and reassess the skin pinch after an hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intramuscular metoclopramide to stop the vomiting before replacing fluid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Collect a stool specimen for dark-field microscopy before starting resuscitation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Establish large-bore IV access and give 1,000 mL of Ringer's lactate or 0.9% saline over 15 to 30 minutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Severe dehydration with signs of hypovolemic shock (hypotension, thready tachycardia, delayed skin pinch, lethargy) represents a critical medical emergency (WHO Plan C). Oral rehydration alone is insufficient and unsafe in shock or altered consciousness. The immediate priority is rapid intravenous resuscitation with balanced isotonic crystalloids (Ringer's lactate or 0.9% normal saline) through large-bore IV access to restore intravascular volume. Why the other options fail: option a gives oral fluid to a lethargic client in shock, which is unsafe and far too slow. Option b treats the vomiting and leaves the circulation empty. Option c puts a laboratory test ahead of resuscitation in a client with a systolic pressure of 74 mmHg.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sit the client upright, give high-flow oxygen, stop the IV fluids, and call the provider for diuretic or dialysis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Lay the client flat and give a 500 mL bolus of 5% dextrose, to support the circulation while the cause is found"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Perform chest percussion and walk the client round the ward to clear the secretions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give 100 mL of 20% mannitol by rapid intravenous push, and withhold oxygen until the provider reviews"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Acute pulmonary edema resulting from iatrogenic fluid overload in an oliguric/anuric client is a life-threatening emergency. Immediate nursing priorities: 1) Position in high-Fowler's with legs dependent to drop cardiac preload and ease ventilation, 2) Apply high-flow oxygen immediately, 3) Shut off IV fluid infusions immediately, and 4) Alert the medical team for urgent loop diuretics or emergent ultrafiltration/hemodialysis. Why the other options fail: option b lies the client flat, which increases venous return into already flooded lungs, and gives more fluid. Option c exercises a hypoxic client and does nothing for the oedema. Option d gives an osmotic load by push and withholds oxygen from a client saturating at 84%.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Run it by gravity through a 22-gauge hand vein, so that the infusion can be stopped quickly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Infuse by pump, check neurological status often, and keep the sodium rise within 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Run it alongside 500 mL of 0.45% saline, so that the sodium is corrected more evenly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client to drink 2 litres of water, so that the sodium is distributed through the body"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Hypertonic saline (3% NaCl) is a high-alert intravenous solution. Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia causes sudden osmotic dehydration of brain cells, resulting in catastrophic Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis), causing irreversible flaccid quadriplegia and 'locked-in' syndrome. Guidelines mandate strictly limiting the sodium correction rate to no more than 8-10 mEq/L over 24 hours, infused via pump with close neuro checks. Why the other options fail: option a gives a high-alert solution by gravity through a small peripheral vein, which cannot control the rate. Option c adds hypotonic fluid to a hypertonic infusion, which defeats the correction. Option d gives free water to a client who is already water-loaded.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "5% dextrose at 150 mL/hour, to dilute the sodium and relieve the cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Subcutaneous desmopressin 4 micrograms, to stop the inappropriate antidiuresis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 100 mL bolus of 3% hypertonic saline over 10 to 15 minutes, to relieve the cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral sodium chloride tablets with 1 litre of free water, which is safer than hypertonic saline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Severe acute hyponatremia (< 120 mEq/L) with neurological emergencies (seizures, coma, brainstem herniation) is caused by severe cerebral edema from osmotic water entry into brain cells. The emergency intervention is 100-150 mL of IV 3% hypertonic saline infused over 10-15 minutes (repeatable up to 2 times if seizures persist) to acutely raise serum sodium by 4-6 mEq/L, reversing brain swelling and halting seizures. Why the other options fail: option a gives free water, which lowers the sodium further and worsens the cerebral oedema. Option b treats the antidiuresis rather than the emergency, and subcutaneous desmopressin acts too slowly. Option d gives salt with a litre of water, which does not raise the sodium when the client is already retaining water.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insulin 10 units with 50 mL of 50% dextrose, which shifts potassium into the cells fastest"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate 30 g, which removes potassium from the body"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg as a bolus, which excretes the potassium in the urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intravenous calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL infused over 2 to 3 minutes to stabilise the myocardium"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In severe hyperkalemia with electrocardiographic changes (peaked T waves, QRS widening), the client is at imminent risk of fatal ventricular fibrillation and asystole. The immediate first-line drug is IV Calcium gluconate (or calcium chloride). Calcium does not lower potassium; it directly antagonizes potassium's membrane-depolarizing effects, stabilizing the myocardial cell membrane within 1-3 minutes. Insulin and dextrose are given next to shift potassium into cells. Why the other options fail: option a is the correct second drug, but it shifts potassium over 15 to 30 minutes and does not protect the myocardium now. Option b works over hours in the gut. Option c depends on kidneys that are not making urine. Only calcium stabilises the membrane within minutes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Never give it as a push or bolus: use a pump and keep the peripheral rate at or below 10 mEq/hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give it as a slow direct push over 60 seconds, which is safe when the volume is small"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Run it by gravity through a 24-gauge vein in the dorsum of the hand, to dilute it quickly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warm the bag in hot water first, since warmth prevents the vein irritation potassium causes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Intravenous potassium chloride is a high-alert medication. Direct IV push or rapid bolus administration causes sudden lethal cardiac arrest (used in lethal injection protocols). Safety mandates: 1) NEVER give IV push, 2) Always infuse diluted in IV fluids using a dedicated electronic infusion pump, 3) Limit peripheral infusion rate to a maximum of 10 mEq/hour (maximum 20 mEq/hr via central venous line with continuous cardiac monitoring). Why the other options fail: option b is a direct push, which is the lethal practice the item tests. Option c uses gravity through the smallest available cannula, so neither the rate nor the dilution can be controlled. Option d warms a high-alert solution without addressing the rate, and warmth does not prevent phlebitis from potassium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Visual acuity and intraocular pressure, which fall early in magnesium toxicity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Patellar deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate and hourly urine output"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Capillary glucose every 15 minutes, which detects the hypoglycaemia magnesium causes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bowel sounds in all four quadrants, which disappear as magnesium accumulates"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$During intravenous magnesium sulfate therapy, toxicity manifests progressively: loss of deep tendon reflexes (DTRs) occurs first at serum levels > 4-5 mEq/L, followed by respiratory depression (< 12 breaths/min) at > 8-10 mEq/L, and cardiac arrest at > 12-15 mEq/L. Magnesium is excreted renally; oliguria causes dangerous drug accumulation. The nurse must assess patellar reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output before and during infusion. Calcium gluconate is the antidote. Why the other options fail: option a describes parameters that magnesium does not affect early, and the eye is not the target organ. Option c looks for hypoglycaemia, which is not the recognised toxicity pattern. Option d is a late and non-specific finding, whereas reflexes are lost first and are the earliest reliable sign.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Irrigate the catheter daily with 50 mL of sterile saline, to keep the lumen clear"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hang the bag on the side rail while repositioning, so that it is not left on the floor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the system closed and unobstructed, and hang the bag below the level of the bladder"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disconnect the catheter from the tubing each morning, to empty and measure the urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$CAUTI prevention bundle evidence-based guidelines include: 1) Maintain a sterile, continuously closed drainage system (never disconnect tubing), 2) Keep the drainage bag below the level of the bladder at all times to prevent gravity backflow of contaminated urine into the bladder, 3) Hang bag on the bed frame (never on moving side rails or resting on the floor), 4) Secure catheter to the thigh to prevent urethral traction, and 5) Avoid routine antimicrobial bladder irrigation. Why the other options fail: option a irrigates a closed system routinely, which introduces organisms. Option b hangs the bag on a moving rail, where it can rise above the bladder and allow backflow. Option d breaks the closed system daily, which is the single commonest route to infection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediate oliguria and hyperkalemic cardiac arrest"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute nephrotic syndrome with severe hypoalbuminemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Severe metabolic alkalosis from ammonia retention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Post-obstructive diuresis, hypotension, and transient hematuria"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Rapid relief of chronic high-pressure urinary retention (> 800-1,000 mL) can trigger two well-known physiological responses: 1) Post-obstructive diuresis—a dramatic polyuric state caused by loss of medullary concentrating gradient and accumulated urea, requiring strict fluid balance monitoring to prevent hypovolemic shock, and 2) Transient hematuria and hypotension from sudden pelvic venous decompression and mucosal stretching. Why the other options fail: option a describes the opposite problem, since the risk after relief is polyuria rather than oliguria. Option b is a glomerular disease unrelated to decompression. Option c describes an acid-base consequence of vomiting or diuretics, not of relieving retention.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Minimal change nephrotic syndrome, which gives heavy proteinuria without haematuria"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which follows bloody diarrhoea with anaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute bacterial cystitis, which gives dysuria and frequency without oedema"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN) is an immune-complex mediated type III hypersensitivity reaction occurring 1-3 weeks after Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis or impetigo. Immune complexes deposit in glomerular basement membranes, causing inflammation and red blood cell leakage. Hallmarks: 1) Periorbital edema, 2) Tea- or cola-colored urine (gross hematuria with RBC casts), 3) Hypertension from sodium/water retention, and 4) Mild-to-moderate proteinuria. Why the other options fail: option b gives proteinuria without haematuria, and the cola-coloured urine is blood. Option c follows a diarrhoeal illness with anaemia and a low platelet count, not a sore throat two weeks earlier. Option d causes dysuria and frequency without oedema, hypertension or haematuria.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A high-sodium diet with liberal fluid, to restore the oncotic pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Strict infection precautions, with daily weight and abdominal girth measurements"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complete immobilisation with strict bed rest for 4 weeks, to reduce protein loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High-dose intravenous potassium chloride, to replace the losses in the urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by massive proteinuria, severe hypoalbuminemia, generalized edema (anasarca), and hyperlipidemia. Crucial nursing considerations: 1) Infection risk is critically elevated because immunoglobulin G (IgG) and complement proteins are lost in urine, and the child is treated with corticosteroids, 2) Daily weights and abdominal girth measurements track ascites, 3) Sodium restriction reduces edema, and 4) Meticulous skin care prevents breakdown of edematous tissues. Why the other options fail: option a adds sodium and fluid to a child who is already anasarcous. Option c imposes immobility, which increases the risk of thrombosis and skin breakdown in oedematous tissue. Option d gives potassium to a child who has not been shown to be hypokalaemic, and the risk here is infection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take them on an empty stomach at bedtime, with a full glass of water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take them 2 hours after breakfast, together with the iron supplement"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the tablets with meals and snacks, so that they bind dietary phosphate in the gut"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dissolve the tablets in grapefruit juice an hour before lunch, which improves absorption"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In advanced CKD, failing kidneys cannot excrete phosphorus, causing hyperphosphatemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism. Phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer) work locally in the gut lumen by binding dietary phosphorus into insoluble, unabsorbable complexes that are excreted in feces. For phosphate binders to be effective, they MUST be taken WITH meals and snacks (during or immediately after eating). If taken on an empty stomach, calcium acetate acts as a systemic calcium supplement without binding phosphorus. Why the other options fail: option a takes the binder on an empty stomach, when there is no dietary phosphate for it to bind. Option b separates it from the meal and adds iron, which it can also bind. Option d dissolves it in juice before food, which achieves the same failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An absence of pulsation or sound over the anastomosis, which shows the vein is not distended"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A blood pressure of 120/80 measured with a cuff on the fistula arm, which shows flow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cool, pale skin with absent radial pulses in the left hand, which shows a steal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Palpate a palpable thrill and auscultate a continuous blowing bruit over the fistula site"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$An arteriovenous fistula surgically anastomoses a high-pressure artery to a low-pressure vein, producing high-velocity turbulent blood flow. Functional patency is confirmed by: 1) Palpating a purring vibration ('thrill'), and 2) Auscultating a continuous whooshing sound ('bruit') over the access site. Absence of a thrill or bruit indicates fistula thrombosis and acute access occlusion, requiring immediate surgical vascular intervention. Why the other options fail: option a describes the absence of flow, which is the sign of a thrombosed fistula. Option b takes a blood pressure on the access arm, which can occlude the fistula and should never be done. Option c describes steal or occlusion, not patency.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Send the effluent for cell count and culture, notify the nephrologist, and start intraperitoneal antibiotics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that cloudy fluid is expected from dietary protein breakdown"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warm the dialysate bag in a microwave before the next dwell, to dissolve the sediment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clamp the catheter permanently and stop all exchanges for a week, to rest the peritoneum"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Cloudy or turbid peritoneal dialysate effluent is the earliest and most classic clinical hallmark of bacterial peritonitis in peritoneal dialysis (most commonly caused by touch contamination of the catheter spike during fluid exchanges). Normal dialysate drainage should be completely clear and straw-colored. The nurse must collect fluid for white blood cell count (> 100/mcL with > 50% neutrophils) and culture, and administer intraperitoneal broad-spectrum antibiotics. Why the other options fail: option b treats a sign of infection as normal, which delays treatment of peritonitis. Option c warms the bag in a microwave, which damages the glucose in the dialysate and does not treat infection. Option d stops dialysis altogether, which leaves the infection untreated and the client without clearance.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Air embolism through the venous line, which presents with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome, from rapid solute clearance and cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An acute pyrogenic reaction to endotoxin in the dialysate, with rigors and fever"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe hypovolaemic collapse from excessive ultrafiltration during the session"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS) occurs primarily during initial hemodialysis sessions in clients with severely elevated BUN. Rapid clearance of urea from the vascular compartment creates a transient osmotic gradient between the blood and brain cells (where urea clears slowly due to the blood-brain barrier). Free water shifts into brain tissue, causing acute cerebral edema and increased ICP (headache, nausea, confusion, seizures, herniation). Management: slow blood flow, stop dialysis, administer hypertonic saline or mannitol. Why the other options fail: option a presents with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain, not headache and hypertension. Option c produces rigors and fever at the start of the session, not focal neurology. Option d produces hypotension, whereas this client is hypertensive and bradycardic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the entire 160 mg dose as a rapid IV push over 15 seconds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mix the furosemide in 50 mL of 10% dextrose and infuse over 1 minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer the IV furosemide slowly at a rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Combine the furosemide in the same syringe with an aminoglycoside antibiotic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$High-dose intravenous loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide) can cause permanent sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and ototoxicity if administered too rapidly, especially in clients with impaired renal clearance. Safety standards mandate that high-dose intravenous furosemide must be infused slowly at a controlled rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute (160 mg should be infused over at least 40 minutes, either by slow injection or controlled IV piggyback). Why the other options fail: option a pushes a high dose in 15 seconds, which is the fastest way to cause the ototoxicity the item is about. Option b still runs the dose in a minute, which far exceeds 4 mg/min. Option d mixes a loop diuretic with an aminoglycoside in one syringe, which compounds the same toxicity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "21 gtt/min"}, {"id": "b", "text": "42 gtt/min"}, {"id": "c", "text": "125 gtt/min"}, {"id": "d", "text": "31 gtt/min"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Standard IV drop rate formula: Drops per minute (gtt/min) = [Total Volume (mL) x Drop Factor (gtt/mL)] ÷ Total Time (minutes). Calculation: 1) Total volume = 1,000 mL. 2) Drop factor = 15 gtt/mL. 3) Total time = 8 hours x 60 minutes = 480 minutes. 4) (1,000 x 15) ÷ 480 = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25 drops/min, which rounds to 31 drops/minute. Why the other options fail: option a uses a 10 gtt/mL set for the calculation. Option b doubles the volume to 2,000 mL. Option c divides by the drop factor instead of multiplying. The rate follows only from 1,000 mL times 15 gtt/mL over 480 minutes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch17.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 17: COMM module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- Second module recorded as passed before the distractor-block criterion
-- existed, and the second to be overturned on re-reading. All twenty keys
-- are right: 24-hour IDSR notification for a suspected VHF, 28-day three-drug
-- PEP, artesunate at 0/12/24, post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis, dengue
-- warning signs at defervescence, airborne isolation for cavitary TB,
-- ethambutol optic neuritis, enhanced adherence counselling at 3,200
-- copies/mL, vaccine plus HBIG within 12 hours, WHO Plan C, typhoid
-- perforation, meningococcal droplet precautions and prophylaxis, vitamin A
-- in measles, azithromycin in pertussis, rabies category III management,
-- ribavirin in Lassa, praziquantel, the 8-week Buruli regimen, C. difficile
-- contact precautions, and the vaccine shake test.
--
-- None of the twenty carried a distractor block, and the key was the longest
-- in 17 of 20 (mean 1.72, worst 2.74). Weak options replaced: 'suture the
-- lacerations tightly and apply herbal poultices' for a rabid dog bite,
-- 'immediate total limb amputation' for Buruli ulcer, 'administer high-dose
-- oral aspirin every 4 hours' in measles, 'wait 10 days for hydrophobia'.
--
-- After: mean 1.06, worst 1.25. No key letter moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Isolate under VHF precautions and report the suspected case to the district disease control officer within 24 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait for the RT-PCR and serology results before notifying the district, so that the report is laboratory-confirmed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Record the case in the routine outpatient ledger and review the pattern at the end of the quarter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discharge with oral antipyretics and review in a week, since the fever has lasted only 3 days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Under the WHO and Ghana Health Service Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response (IDSR) strategy, conditions with epidemic potential (such as acute viral haemorrhagic fevers, yellow fever, cholera, and meningococcal meningitis) mandate immediate suspected case reporting within 24 hours by telephone, SMS, or direct call to the District Health Directorate, alongside immediate strict contact/droplet barrier isolation. Notification must NEVER be delayed pending laboratory confirmation, as rapid containment prevents catastrophic community transmission. Why the other options fail: option b waits for confirmation, which is exactly what the 24-hour rule forbids for a disease with epidemic potential. Option c files the case for quarterly review, which delays any response by months. Option d discharges a patient with a suspected haemorrhagic fever, which removes him from isolation and returns him to the community.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Squeeze the wound to express blood, apply concentrated bleach, and wait 7 days for the baseline HIV test before starting PEP"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wash with soap and running water, then begin 28 days of three-drug PEP immediately, ideally within 2 hours and never after 72"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Start PEP only if the worker's rapid antibody test is positive at the time of injury, since the drugs are toxic"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take a single dose of zidovudine within 24 hours, which is sufficient and needs no follow-up"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Immediate management of occupational blood exposure requires washing the wound thoroughly with soap and water (never scrubbing, squeezing, or applying caustic bleach). Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) with a 3-drug antiretroviral regimen (e.g., tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir) should be initiated as urgently as possible, ideally within 2 hours of exposure. PEP is ineffective if delayed beyond 72 hours. The full course lasts 28 days with baseline and follow-up HIV testing at 6 weeks and 3 months. Why the other options fail: option a squeezes and bleaches the wound, which damages tissue and has not been recommended for years, and it delays PEP by a week. Option c makes prophylaxis conditional on a test that will be negative in early infection. Option d gives a single drug for one day, when the regimen is three drugs for 28 days.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give it as a continuous 72-hour infusion in concentrated dextrose, so that the parasitaemia falls steadily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a single dose of crushed artemether-lumefantrine down the nasogastric tube and avoid parenteral therapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then once daily until a full 3-day oral ACT course can be taken"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse intravenous chloroquine over 4 hours and monitor for hypotension, since chloroquine is the traditional first-line"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$WHO and Ghana National Malaria Control Programme guidelines mandate intravenous or intramuscular artesunate (2.4 mg/kg for adults, 3.0 mg/kg for children < 20 kg) as the first-line treatment for severe malaria. The dosing schedule is 0, 12, and 24 hours, then once every 24 hours thereafter. A minimum of 3 parenteral doses must be given. Once the patient is awake and tolerating oral intake, a complete 3-day course of an oral artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT, such as artemether-lumefantrine or artesunate-amodiaquine) must be completed to prevent recrudescence. Why the other options fail: option a gives artesunate by continuous infusion, which is not its pharmacology and would miss the dose schedule. Option b relies on an enteral drug in a comatose patient and skips the parenteral doses the guidelines require. Option d returns to chloroquine, which falciparum resistance has made unusable for severe disease.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Recrudescence of vivax malaria, which needs primaquine to clear the liver-stage parasites"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aplastic anaemia from permanent marrow suppression caused by the artesunate course"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura triggered by mosquito salivary antigens, which needs steroids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis, needing haemoglobin monitoring and transfusion if severe"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis (PADH) is a recognized phenomenon occurring 1 to 4 weeks (typically around days 7–21) after parenteral artesunate therapy in patients with high initial parasitaemia. Artesunate causes splenic 'pitting', where dead parasites are extracted from erythrocytes, leaving once-parasitized erythrocytes ('once-infected RBCs') with shortened lifespans that undergo delayed intravascular clearance. Patients present with haemolytic anaemia, jaundice, and dark urine. Management involves supportive care and blood transfusion if severe. Why the other options fail: option a names a recurrent infection, but the parasitaemia has cleared and the reticulocyte count is up, which points to destruction rather than relapse. Option b describes permanent marrow failure, which does not recover and does not raise reticulocytes. Option c gives a platelet disorder, which does not explain the falling haemoglobin and the indirect bilirubin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Critical warning signs of severe dengue: plasma leakage with impending shock, needing immediate crystalloid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal convalescence, since defervescence marks viral clearance and the end of the illness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A vasovagal reaction to the antipyretics, which needs rest and a further dose of paracetamol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Allergic gastroenteritis from an NSAID, which needs an antihistamine and a bland diet"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In dengue virus infection, the critical phase begins at defervescence (typically days 3–7 of illness). The development of dengue warning signs (severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, fluid accumulation/ascites/pleural effusion, mucosal bleeding, lethargy/restlessness, hepatomegaly > 2 cm, and rapid rise in haematocrit with sharp drop in platelets) signals systemic capillary permeability, plasma leakage, and impending Dengue Shock Syndrome (DSS). The nurse must immediately admit the patient for close monitoring and judicious isotonic IV fluid resuscitation. Why the other options fail: option b reads defervescence as recovery, when in dengue it marks the start of the critical phase. Option c attributes warning signs to a vagal response, which does not cause mucosal bleeding. Option d invents a drug reaction that does not produce abdominal pain with bleeding and lethargy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A shared bay with a cloth mask for the patient while sleeping, since the diagnosis is not yet confirmed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Airborne isolation in a negative-pressure room, with fit-tested N95 respirators for all staff on entry"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Standard precautions only until the sputum smear is positive, because cavitation alone does not transmit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Contact precautions with sterile gowns and shoe covers, since the organism survives on surfaces"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Mycobacterium tuberculosis is transmitted via droplet nuclei that remain suspended in the air. Any patient with presumptive active pulmonary tuberculosis (cavitary disease, chronic cough, haemoptysis) must be placed immediately in an Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR) with negative pressure (>= 6–12 air exchanges per hour) venting to the outside. Staff must wear fit-tested N95 (or FFP2) particulate respirators upon room entry. If transported, the patient must wear a surgical mask. Sputum GeneXpert MTB/RIF is the rapid molecular test of choice to identify TB and rifampicin resistance. Why the other options fail: option a leaves a cavitary case in a shared bay, which is the setting in which droplet nuclei spread. Option c waits for a smear, which is often negative and never a reason to relax precautions. Option d uses contact precautions, which do not address an organism carried in the air.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rifampicin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Isoniazid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ethambutol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pyrazinamide"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Ethambutol causes dose-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis, manifesting as decreased visual acuity, central scotomas, and loss of red-green color discrimination. Baseline and periodic visual acuity and color vision testing (Ishihara plates) are indicated. If visual toxicity occurs, ethambutol must be discontinued immediately to prevent permanent blindness. In contrast, rifampicin causes orange-red discoloration of body fluids; isoniazid causes peripheral neuropathy (prevented with pyridoxine/vitamin B6) and hepatotoxicity; and pyrazinamide causes hyperuricaemia and arthralgias. Why the other options fail: option a causes orange-red discolouration of body fluids, not visual loss. Option b causes peripheral neuropathy and hepatitis, prevented with pyridoxine. Option d causes hyperuricaemia and arthralgia. None of the three affects the optic nerve.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Switch to second-line protease inhibitor therapy now, since a detectable load means the regimen has failed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop all antiretrovirals for 4 weeks, so that the resistant mutants are cleared before restarting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure the client that 3,200 copies/mL still counts as suppression, and review in a year"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start enhanced adherence counselling for 3 monthly sessions and repeat the viral load in 3 months before any switch"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$In national and WHO HIV treatment guidelines, virological non-suppression is defined as a viral load >= 1,000 copies/mL on two consecutive measurements 3 months apart. When an elevated viral load is detected in a patient reporting missed doses, the immediate protocol is not an automatic drug switch; instead, the nurse initiates Enhanced Adherence Counseling (EAC) monthly for 3 months to identify and overcome barriers to 95%+ pill adherence. A repeat viral load is conducted after 3 months; if still >= 1,000 copies/mL despite documented adherence, treatment failure is confirmed and the patient is switched to second-line therapy. Why the other options fail: option a switches regimen on one result and before adherence has been addressed, which risks losing the second line as well. Option b stops treatment, which allows the virus to rebound. Option c calls 3,200 copies/mL suppression, when the threshold is below 1,000 on two occasions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give hepatitis B vaccine and HBIG within 12 hours, at separate intramuscular sites"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start oral lamivudine for the infant and delay all vaccinations until the 6-week visit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give HBIG alone at the 6-week child welfare clinic, when the vaccine schedule begins"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give no prophylaxis if the baby was delivered by elective caesarean section, because there was no exposure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Perinatal transmission from HBsAg-positive mothers carries up to a 90% risk of chronic neonatal infection leading to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma in adulthood. Immediate prevention requires administering Hepatitis B vaccine (monovalent birth dose) and Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG, 0.5 mL) within 12 hours of birth at two separate anatomical sites (e.g., left and right anterolateral thighs). This combined active-passive immunoprophylaxis reduces transmission rates by over 90–95%. The infant then completes the standard pentavalent vaccine series at 6, 10, and 14 weeks. Why the other options fail: option b gives an antiviral to the infant and postpones the vaccine that prevents carriage. Option c gives passive antibody alone and six weeks late, by which time transmission may already have occurred. Option d treats caesarean delivery as protection, when the risk comes from maternal blood and the birth process itself.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give oral rehydration solution by spoon and withhold intravenous fluid until the pulse returns"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Begin WHO Plan C: Ringer's lactate 100 mL/kg, 30 mL/kg in the first 30 minutes then 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give intravenous loperamide to stop the stools, so that the fluid losses slow enough to catch up"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse 500 mL of 5% dextrose over 8 hours, which avoids the risk of hypernatraemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$The patient presents with severe cholera dehydration with hypovolaemic shock (lethargy, absent peripheral pulse, skin tenting > 2 s), which is rapidly fatal within hours without aggressive volume restoration. Under WHO Plan C for cholera, adult fluid resuscitation requires 100 mL/kg of IV Ringer's lactate: 30 mL/kg within the first 30 minutes, followed by 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours. As soon as the patient is able to drink, oral rehydration salts (ORS) and oral zinc are initiated, followed by single-dose oral doxycycline or azithromycin to reduce duration and shedding. Antimotility drugs (like loperamide) are strictly contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option a gives oral fluid to a patient with no radial pulse, which cannot be absorbed fast enough. Option c gives an antimotility drug, which is contraindicated in cholera. Option d gives dextrose without electrolyte at a rate far too slow for shock.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Benign ileocaecal spasm from the oral rehydration, which settles with an antispasmodic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Amoebic colitis, which responds to oral metronidazole without surgery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Typhoid intestinal perforation of the terminal ileum, with acute peritonitis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute cholecystitis with intact peritoneal boundaries, which needs an ultrasound"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Salmonella Typhi invades the Peyer patches in the lymphoid tissue of the terminal ileum. During the late second to third week of illness, necrosis and ulceration of Peyer patches can lead to intestinal perforation, the most lethal surgical complication of typhoid fever. Clinical signs include sudden severe abdominal pain, board-like involuntary guarding, rebound tenderness, free intraperitoneal air causing loss of hepatic dullness on percussion, and hypovolaemic/septic shock. The nurse must make the patient nil by mouth, initiate resuscitation, start broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, and alert the surgical team immediately. Why the other options fail: option a calls a rigid abdomen with lost liver dullness a spasm, which delays surgery. Option b gives a medical diagnosis and an oral drug to a patient in shock. Option d keeps the peritoneum intact, when free air and guarding show that it is not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Airborne precautions in a negative-pressure room for 3 weeks, since meningococcus survives in air"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Contact precautions only, with no prophylaxis for roommates because they were vaccinated"}, {"id": "c", "text": "No isolation once IV ceftriaxone is started, since the drug renders the patient non-infectious at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Droplet precautions until 24 hours of effective antibiotic, with ciprofloxacin prophylaxis for hostel contacts"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Meningococcal meningitis (caused by Neisseria meningitidis) is transmitted via large respiratory droplets. Droplet precautions (surgical mask within 1–2 metres, private room) must be maintained until 24 hours after initiation of effective antimicrobial therapy (e.g., IV ceftriaxone or penicillin). Close contacts (household members, dorm roommates, healthcare workers with direct oral secretion exposure such as during CPR or endotracheal intubation) must receive post-exposure chemoprophylaxis with single-dose oral ciprofloxacin (500 mg), rifampicin, or IM ceftriaxone as soon as possible. Why the other options fail: option a over-isolates and under-treats, since meningococcus is a droplet organism and 3 weeks is not the rule. Option b drops prophylaxis for the contacts at highest risk. Option c ends isolation on the first dose rather than after 24 hours of treatment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Two age-appropriate doses of high-dose vitamin A, 200,000 IU for a child of 12 months or more, 24 hours apart"}, {"id": "b", "text": "High-dose oral aspirin every 4 hours for 7 days, to control the fever and the rash"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prophylactic oral chloramphenicol for 14 days, to prevent secondary bacterial infection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Systemic corticosteroids to suppress the rash, which shortens the illness and prevents croup"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$WHO and UNICEF protocols mandate that all children diagnosed with acute measles receive two doses of high-potency oral Vitamin A given on consecutive days (50,000 IU for infants < 6 months, 100,000 IU for 6–11 months, and 200,000 IU for >= 12 months; a third dose is added at 2–4 weeks if signs of xerophthalmia exist). Measles rapidly depletes vitamin A stores, which can lead to corneal ulceration, blindness, severe pneumonia, and croup. Vitamin A therapy reduces overall measles mortality and morbidity by up to 50%. Aspirin is contraindicated due to the risk of Reye syndrome. Why the other options fail: option b gives aspirin, which is contraindicated in measles because of Reye syndrome. Option c gives an antibiotic that does not treat a viral illness and does not reduce mortality. Option d gives steroids, which suppress immunity in a child already made vulnerable by measles.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Airborne isolation for 6 weeks, since the cough spreads organisms over long distances"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Droplet precautions, with oral azithromycin for 5 days, and continuous monitoring for apnoea and bradycardia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Standard precautions only, with oral metronidazole for 10 days, since the infant is already coughing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intramuscular penicillin G, with routine observations only, since apnoea is uncommon after 4 months"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Bordetella pertussis causes whooping cough, which in infants under 6 months presents with severe coughing spells, post-tussive cyanosis, apnoea, and bradycardia. Transmission occurs through large infectious respiratory droplets; the infant must be placed on droplet precautions until 5 days of effective macrolide therapy (such as azithromycin, 10 mg/kg on day 1, then 5 mg/kg on days 2–5) have been completed. Continuous cardiopulmonary and oxygen saturation monitoring is critical because infants are at high risk of sudden apnoea and death. Why the other options fail: option a uses airborne isolation, which pertussis does not require, and ciprofloxacin, which is not the drug of choice in a 4-month-old. Option c uses standard precautions only and metronidazole, which does not cover Bordetella. Option d gives penicillin and abandons monitoring in the age group most likely to stop breathing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Suture the lacerations tightly, apply a herbal poultice, and give a single dose of tetanus toxoid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intravenous dexamethasone and observe for 10 days for signs of hydrophobia before treating"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wash with soap and running water for 15 minutes, infiltrate RIG around the wound, and vaccinate on days 0, 3, 7 and 14"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clean with dry cotton without washing, so that the virus is not spread, and give oral amoxicillin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Rabies is virtually 100% fatal once clinical symptoms develop. Category III exposure (transdermal bites or scratches, licking of broken skin) mandates three immediate interventions: (1) immediate and vigorous washing of all bite wounds with soap, water, and povidone-iodine for at least 15 minutes (which physically removes and inactivates the envelope of the rabies virus); (2) prompt infiltration of Rabies Immune Globulin (RIG, 20 IU/kg) into and around the wound margins to provide immediate passive immunity; and (3) initiating the active cell-culture rabies vaccine series on days 0, 3, 7, and 14. Primary suturing of rabies wounds is contraindicated as it inoculates virus deeper into tissues. Why the other options fail: option a sutures a contaminated bite and leaves the virus in the tissue without immunoglobulin or vaccine. Option b gives steroids, which are not indicated, and waits for the disease, which is almost always fatal once it starts. Option d avoids washing, which removes the one intervention with proven benefit.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oral oseltamivir"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravenous ganciclovir"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oral acyclovir"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intravenous ribavirin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Lassa fever is an acute arenavirus viral haemorrhagic fever endemic to West Africa, transmitted via the multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis) urine/faeces or human body fluids. Intravenous ribavirin is the specific, proven antiviral agent that significantly reduces mortality if initiated within the first 6 days of illness. In addition to ribavirin, strict viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF) barrier nursing precautions with impermeable personal protective equipment, fluid balance management, and avoidance of intramuscular injections are mandatory. Why the other options fail: option a treats influenza. Option b treats cytomegalovirus. Option c treats herpes simplex and varicella. None has activity against an arenavirus, and ribavirin is the only agent with a mortality benefit in Lassa fever.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A single oral dose of praziquantel 40 mg/kg, taken immediately after food"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oral albendazole 400 mg twice daily for 28 days, which clears the flukes in the bladder wall"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intramuscular benzathine penicillin weekly for 3 weeks, to sterilise the urinary tract"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily for 10 days, which kills the adult worms"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Schistosoma haematobium is a digenetic blood fluke inhabiting the vesical venous plexus of the bladder and pelvis, causing urinary schistosomiasis (bilharzia) characterized by terminal haematuria, dysuria, and bladder wall calcification (with long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder). Praziquantel at a single oral dose of 40 mg/kg taken after a meal is the drug of choice recommended by WHO and Ghana Health Service Neglected Tropical Disease (NTD) control programmes. Why the other options fail: option b is a deworming regimen that does not kill schistosomes. Option c treats syphilis. Option d treats anaerobic and protozoal infections but not the flukes in the vesical plexus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous vancomycin for 5 days, followed by oral cephalexin, which covers the usual skin organisms"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rifampicin 10 mg/kg daily plus clarithromycin 7.5 mg/kg twice daily for 8 weeks, with wound care and physiotherapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediate above-knee amputation, to prevent systemic dissemination of the infection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral fluconazole for 6 months without local wound dressing, since the lesion is fungal in origin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Buruli ulcer is caused by Mycobacterium ulcerans, which produces the cytotoxic, immunosuppressive toxin mycolactone, leading to extensive painless necrotic skin ulceration with characteristic undermined edges. WHO and Ghana Health Service guidelines establish oral rifampicin (10 mg/kg once daily) combined with clarithromycin (7.5 mg/kg twice daily) for 8 weeks as the first-line treatment. This fully oral regimen has replaced older streptomycin injections. Early antimicrobial treatment combined with regular aseptic dressing and limb physiotherapy prevents joint contractures and disabling deformities. Why the other options fail: option a covers ordinary skin flora and does not touch Mycobacterium ulcerans. Option c amputates a limb that responds to oral therapy in 8 weeks, which is the outcome the regimen exists to prevent. Option d treats a fungal infection that the PCR has excluded.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give intravenous loperamide and use alcohol rub between patients, since the diarrhoea is the main risk"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Switch to intravenous gentamicin and let the patient share a commode, since the spores are not on the skin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contact precautions, hand washing with soap and water, stop the inciting antibiotics, and give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Airborne isolation with oral amoxicillin, since the spores become airborne during episodes of diarrhoea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Clostridioides difficile is an anaerobic, spore-forming bacillus that proliferates following broad-spectrum antimicrobial destruction of normal colonic flora. Spores are resistant to alcohol-based hand rubs; healthcare workers MUST wash hands thoroughly with soap and water to physically remove spores. The patient requires Contact Precautions (dedicated gown, gloves, dedicated equipment) and disinfection with 0.5% hypochlorite (bleach). Treatment entails discontinuing the inciting antibiotic and administering oral vancomycin (125 mg 4 times daily) or oral fidaxomicin. Antimotility agents are contraindicated as they cause toxic megacolon. Why the other options fail: option a gives an antimotility drug, which risks toxic megacolon, and alcohol rub, which does not remove spores. Option b switches to a drug that does not treat C. difficile and shares a commode, which spreads spores. Option d uses airborne isolation for a contact organism and amoxicillin, which is one of the drugs that causes the disease.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The Litmus Acidity Strip Test"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The Bacterial Culture Plating Test"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The Specific Gravity Urinometer Test"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The Shake Test"}]$q$::jsonb,
  rationale = $q$Aluminium-adsorbed vaccines (such as DTP-HepB-Hib pentavalent, Tetanus-diphtheria/Td, and Hepatitis B) are irreversibly damaged when frozen. Freezing breaks the antigen-adjuvant lattice, causing the aluminium to form heavy, agglomerated granules that precipitate rapidly and lose immunogenicity while increasing local reactogenicity. The WHO Shake Test compares a suspected frozen vial with a known deliberately frozen control vial: if the test vial sediments as fast as or faster than the frozen control (clearing within 15–30 minutes), the vaccine has been freeze-damaged and must be discarded. Why the other options fail: option a is used to check acidity, which is not what freezing changes. Option b cultures for sterility, which freezing does not necessarily breach. Option c measures urine density and has nothing to do with vaccines. Only the shake test detects the agglutination of aluminium adjuvant that freezing causes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch18.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 18: ENDO module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: primary hypothyroidism on high TSH with low
-- free T4, the discard-first-void 24-hour urine cortisol collection, confirming
-- diabetes with a second abnormal test, carbohydrate before exercise at 92 mg/dL,
-- medical nutrition therapy as first-line GDM care, holding metformin for 48
-- hours after contrast, clear-before-cloudy insulin mixing, lipohypertrophy,
-- the rule of 15, fluid resuscitation and potassium repletion before insulin in
-- DKA, IV hydrocortisone in Addisonian crisis, ACE inhibitor or ARB for
-- microalbuminuria, not soaking neuropathic feet, aspirin displacing T3 and T4
-- in thyroid storm, opening a post-thyroidectomy neck haematoma at the bedside,
-- Cushing syndrome, central diabetes insipidus treated with desmopressin,
-- saline hydration for hypercalcaemia, levothyroxine on an empty stomach, and
-- 7 mL/hour for the DKA insulin infusion.
--
-- None of the twenty carried a distractor block, and the key was the longest in
-- 15 of 20 (mean 1.57, worst 2.49). Unchoosable options replaced: "Order an
-- emergency 72-hour fast to test for pancreatic beta-cell hyperplasia",
-- "Permanent atrophy of the rectus abdominis muscle fascial plane", "Strict
-- bedrest in left lateral position for the remainder of the pregnancy",
-- "Administer the metformin dose with an intravenous bolus of 50% dextrose".
--
-- After: mean 1.10, worst 1.30. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Primary hypothyroidism resulting from intrinsic thyroid gland dysfunction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Secondary hypothyroidism caused by anterior pituitary failure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tertiary hypothyroidism due to hypothalamic TRH deficiency"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Subclinical hyperthyroidism with peripheral hormone resistance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the negative feedback loop of the thyroid axis, low circulating free T4 triggers the anterior pituitary gland to release excess TSH to stimulate thyroid hormone production. When the thyroid gland itself fails (primary hypothyroidism, most commonly Hashimoto thyroiditis), T4 remains low despite high TSH stimulation. Conversely, secondary (pituitary) hypothyroidism would present with low or inappropriately normal TSH alongside low T4. Why the other options fail: option b describes secondary hypothyroidism, in which the pituitary fails and TSH is low or inappropriately normal, not high. Option c puts the lesion in the hypothalamus, which would also leave TSH low. Option d puts the error at the receptor and predicts high T4 with high TSH, not low T4, and subclinical hyperthyroidism by definition has a low TSH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Collect only the first morning urine specimen each day for three consecutive days and send them together"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Discard the first void on day one, then collect all urine for 24 hours, including day two's first void"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Save the first morning urine on day one and stop collecting exactly at midnight that night"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the 24-hour container at room temperature and shake it well before each void"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Proper 24-hour urine collection protocol requires starting with an empty bladder: discard the first morning void on day 1 (record start time), then collect all urine produced over the subsequent 24 hours into a preservative container kept on ice or in the refrigerator. Exactly 24 hours from the start time on day 2, the client voids and adds that final specimen to the container. Why the other options fail: option a collects three first-morning specimens, which measure spot cortisol on three days rather than a 24-hour integral. Option c stops at midnight, which collects a daytime fraction, not 24 hours. Option d leaves the container at room temperature, which allows cortisol degradation; it must be kept on ice or refrigerated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately start subcutaneous basal-bolus insulin and arrange diabetes education"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that an HbA1c below 7.0% excludes diabetes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Repeat the fasting glucose or HbA1c on a subsequent day to confirm the diagnosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Arrange a 2-week lifestyle trial and repeat the HbA1c in three months"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to standard clinical guidelines (ADA / WHO), in the absence of unequivocal symptomatic hyperglycemia (polyuria, polydipsia, unexplained weight loss), a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus requires two abnormal test results from either the same sample or two separate test occasions (e.g., fasting plasma glucose >= 126 mg/dL [7.0 mmol/L], 2-hour OGTT >= 200 mg/dL [11.1 mmol/L], or HbA1c >= 6.5%). Why the other options fail: option a treats a diagnosis that has not been confirmed, and insulin is not started on one abnormal screen. Option b uses the treatment target of 7.0% as a diagnostic threshold, which it is not; the diagnostic cut-off is 6.5%. Option d postpones confirmation for three months, when a single repeat test on another day settles it now.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give 4 units of rapid-acting lispro into the thigh before the workout to prevent hyperglycaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cancel the workout, because exercise is contraindicated below 150 mg/dL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Halve the next insulin dose and start the workout without eating anything"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eat a 15-30 g carbohydrate snack before exercise and carry rapid-acting glucose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Exercise increases skeletal muscle glucose uptake independent of insulin. For clients with type 1 diabetes, exercising when blood glucose is below 100 mg/dL (5.5 mmol/L) creates a severe risk of exercise-induced hypoglycemia. The client should consume a 15-30g complex carbohydrate snack prior to exercise and carry fast-acting glucose. (Exercise is avoided only if blood glucose is > 250 mg/dL WITH ketones present). Why the other options fail: option a adds insulin when the glucose is already 92 mg/dL and exercise is about to start, which compounds the risk of hypoglycaemia. Option b cancels exercise on a threshold that does not exist; 92 mg/dL is safe once carbohydrate is taken. Option c halves insulin but gives no carbohydrate, so the fall in glucose is unopposed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Medical nutrition therapy, daily activity, and blood glucose self-monitoring"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Immediate twice-daily glibenclamide alongside the current diet"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Begin NPH insulin at bedtime and continue the current diet unchanged"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Admit for inpatient continuous glucose monitoring until delivery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$First-line management for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is medical nutrition therapy (MNT) with personalized carbohydrate distribution across meals/snacks, moderate daily physical activity, and frequent self-monitoring of blood glucose (fasting target < 95 mg/dL; 1-hour postprandial < 140 mg/dL). Pharmacotherapy (subcutaneous insulin) is introduced only if glycemic targets are not achieved with lifestyle modifications. Why the other options fail: option b starts a sulfonylurea before lifestyle change has been tried, and glibenclamide is not first-line in GDM. Option c starts insulin without addressing diet, which is the first intervention and often sufficient on its own. Option d admits the client for monitoring that is done at home, without changing the glycaemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reduce metformin to 500 mg on the morning of the scan and resume the full dose at the next meal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withhold metformin on the day of the scan and for 48 hours after, until renal function is normal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop metformin 24 hours before the scan and restart it as soon as the scan is finished"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Continue metformin unchanged, because biguanides do not interact with iodinated contrast"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Iodinated radiocontrast agents can induce acute contrast-induced nephropathy. In clients taking metformin, decreased renal clearance causes dangerous accumulation of metformin, predisposing to life-threatening lactic acidosis (mortality ~50%). Metformin must be held on the day of the contrast procedure and withheld for at least 48 hours afterward until repeat serum creatinine confirms baseline renal function. Why the other options fail: option a reduces the dose when the drug must be stopped entirely for the procedure. Option c stops only 24 hours before and restarts immediately after the scan, when the risk window is the 48 hours after contrast while creatinine is still rising. Option d states a false premise; the danger is to the kidney, not a reaction with the contrast molecule.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inject 10 units of air into Regular -> Inject 25 units of air into NPH -> Withdraw 25 units of NPH -> Withdraw 10 units of Regular"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withdraw 25 units of NPH -> Withdraw 10 units of Regular without air injection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Inject 25 units of air into NPH -> Inject 10 units of air into Regular -> Withdraw 10 units of Regular -> Withdraw 25 units of NPH"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withdraw 10 units of Regular -> Inject 25 units of air into NPH -> Withdraw 25 units of NPH"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The standard clinical rule for mixing insulins in a single syringe is 'Clear before Cloudy' (Regular is clear; NPH is cloudy). Sequence: 1) Inject air equal to dose into cloudy (NPH) without touching liquid, 2) Inject air equal to dose into clear (Regular), 3) Invert and withdraw exact clear (Regular) dose, 4) Insert needle into cloudy (NPH) and withdraw exact cloudy dose. This prevents contaminating the short-acting regular vial with intermediate protamine. Why the other options fail: option a withdraws NPH before regular, which contaminates the clear vial with protamine and makes the short-acting dose unreliable. Option b omits the air injections, so neither vial is pressurised and the measured doses drift. Option d draws regular before NPH without the air-transfer step and leaves cloudy insulin in the needle.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Severe bacterial cellulitis requiring surgical incision and drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insulin antibody formation at the site, causing resistance and rising dose requirements"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Local fat necrosis with chronic discharge and sinus formation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lipohypertrophy leading to erratic, delayed, and unpredictable insulin absorption"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Repeatedly injecting insulin into the exact same localized area causes lipohypertrophy—a benign accumulation of localized fibrous fat tissue stimulated by the lipogenic action of insulin. Injecting into lipohypertrophic tissue causes erratic, blunted, and unpredictable insulin absorption, leading to unexplained bouts of hyperglycemia and sudden refractory hypoglycemia. Clients must systematically rotate injection sites. Why the other options fail: option a describes infection, which this lesion is not; lipohypertrophy is firm, spongy and non-tender, without erythema or warmth. Option b describes immune-mediated resistance, which is systemic and not caused by using one site. Option c describes fat necrosis, which follows trauma and leaves a depressed scar, not a raised spongy mass.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, wait 15 minutes, then re-check the glucose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a complex carbohydrate meal with protein and re-check in one hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 150 mL of full-cream milk with two chocolate bars for sustained calories"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give 15 mg of intravenous glucagon now, since the client cannot be trusted to eat"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The 'Rule of 15' for conscious hypoglycemia (blood glucose < 70 mg/dL): 1) Administer 15g of simple, fast-acting carbohydrates (120 mL fruit juice or regular soda, 3-4 glucose tablets, or 1 tablespoon honey/sugar), 2) Wait 15 minutes and re-test blood glucose, 3) If blood glucose remains < 70 mg/dL, repeat with another 15g of carbohydrates. High-fat foods (chocolate, whole milk) should be avoided because dietary fat slows glucose absorption. Why the other options fail: option b gives carbohydrate bound with fat and protein, which is absorbed too slowly to correct a glucose of 52 mg/dL. Option c is high in fat, and fat slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption, which is why chocolate is excluded. Option d gives glucagon by a route and dose for an unconscious client; this client is alert and can swallow.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give an immediate IV bolus of 0.15 units/kg regular insulin, then start the infusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start 0.9% saline resuscitation and have the provider replete potassium before insulin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 100 mEq of intravenous sodium bicarbonate as a slow push"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start the insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour without waiting for the potassium result"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In DKA, fluid resuscitation with isotonic crystalloids (0.9% NS) is the absolute first priority to restore intravascular volume and renal perfusion. Crucially, insulin drives potassium from the extracellular fluid into cells. Starting insulin when serum potassium is already hypokalemic (< 3.3 mEq/L) will trigger lethal cardiac dysrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Potassium must be repleted >= 3.3 mEq/L before initiating insulin therapy. Why the other options fail: option a starts insulin when the potassium is 3.1 mEq/L, which drives potassium into cells and can arrest the heart, and boluses are no longer used. Option c treats acidosis that will correct with fluids and insulin, and bicarbonate is reserved for severe acidaemia. Option d is the same error as a in a slower form; the potassium must reach 3.3 mEq/L first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous Regular insulin with 50% dextrose bolus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravenous 3% hypertonic saline 100 mL bolus to correct the sodium"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravenous Hydrocortisone sodium succinate (100 mg bolus)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral Fludrocortisone acetate 0.1 mg"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The client is in acute adrenal (Addisonian) crisis—a life-threatening shock state precipitated by physiological stress (infection, vomiting) in someone with primary adrenal insufficiency who cannot mount a cortisol response. Hallmarks include refractory distributive shock, severe hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia. Immediate life-saving treatment is rapid high-dose IV Hydrocortisone (100 mg bolus) along with aggressive IV isotonic saline resuscitation. Why the other options fail: option a treats the potassium with insulin and dextrose, which does lower potassium but does nothing for the hypotension and adds hypoglycaemia to a patient who cannot mount a cortisol response. Option b corrects the sodium before the cortisol, and the hyponatraemia of adrenal crisis resolves with hydrocortisone and saline. Option d is an oral mineralocorticoid, which a vomiting, hypotensive patient cannot absorb and which acts too slowly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A loop diuretic such as furosemide to lower the blood pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An NSAID such as ibuprofen, which reduces glomerular hyperfiltration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A thiazide diuretic such as hydrochlorothiazide to reduce intraglomerular pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril, or an ARB such as losartan"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Microalbuminuria is the earliest clinical manifestation of diabetic nephropathy (glomerular hyperfiltration and basement membrane breakdown). ACE inhibitors (lisinopril) and ARBs (losartan) dilate the efferent renal arteriole, reducing intraglomerular hydrostatic pressure. This slows protein leakage and halts progressive renal fibrosis, providing proven renal protection in diabetics even when systemic blood pressure is normal. Why the other options fail: option a lowers systemic pressure but not intraglomerular pressure, and does not reduce albuminuria. Option b names the wrong mechanism: NSAIDs block prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilation, which lowers filtration and can precipitate acute kidney injury. Option c lowers pressure but has no effect on the efferent arteriole, so the albumin leak continues.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"I will soak my feet in hot water and Epsom salts for 30 minutes every evening to improve my circulation.\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "\"I will use a hand mirror every day to inspect the bottoms of my feet and between my toes for cuts or blisters.\""}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"I will always wear clean cotton socks and never walk barefoot, even inside my own home.\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"I will cut my toenails straight across and file the edges gently with an emery board.\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Soaking feet in hot water is strictly contraindicated in diabetic neuropathy because loss of thermal sensation prevents the client from detecting scalding temperatures, causing severe burns. Furthermore, prolonged soaking causes skin maceration, fissuring, and breakdown, creating an entry portal for severe bacterial infection and osteomyelitis. Feet should be washed daily in lukewarm water, dried thoroughly (especially between toes), and never soaked. Why the other options fail: option b is the correct technique for finding lesions the client cannot feel. Option c is correct footwear practice. Option d is the correct way to cut nails, and curved cutting is what risks an ingrowing nail.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Aspirin stimulates thyroid peroxidase and drives further hormone synthesis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aspirin displaces thyroid hormone from binding globulin and raises free T3 and T4"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aspirin prevents propranolol from crossing the blood-brain barrier"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Aspirin blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 and deepens the crisis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In thyroid storm, aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is strictly contraindicated. Salicylates compete with thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) for binding sites on thyroid-binding globulin (TBG), displacing bound hormone and dramatically elevating circulating free (active) T3/T4 concentrations, exacerbating the hypermetabolic crisis. Paracetamol (acetaminophen) and external cooling blankets are the antipyretics of choice. Why the other options fail: option a describes hormone synthesis, which is not how salicylates act and not a mechanism of aspirin at antipyretic doses. Option c is a drug interaction that does not exist; propranolol is given in thyroid storm and reaches its target. Option d reverses the physiology, because blocking T4-to-T3 conversion is therapeutic and is exactly what aspirin does not do.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reinforce the neck dressing with extra pressure gauze and elevate the head of the bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an ice collar and give an oral lozenge to reduce the swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Open the neck incision at the bedside to evacuate the haematoma and call the surgeon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage vigorous coughing to clear pooled mucus from the larynx"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Post-thyroidectomy neck hematoma is a catastrophic, rapidly progressive airway emergency. Bleeding into the tight fascial neck compartments compresses the trachea, causing acute mechanical suffocation and death within minutes. External bleeding on the dressing is often minimal. The emergency action is immediate bedside release of the neck incision sutures/clips to evacuate the hematoma and decompress the airway, followed by emergency surgical exploration. Why the other options fail: option a presses on a neck that is already tense and adds dressing bulk over the airway. Option b treats the pain and not the compression, and an oral lozenge cannot relieve stridor from an obstructed trachea. Option d uses coughing against an obstruction that is outside the trachea, which raises the pressure in the bleeding tissue.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Addison disease (hypoadrenalism)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pheochromocytoma"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cushing syndrome (hypercortisolism)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cushing syndrome results from chronic excess glucocorticoids (cortisol). Cortisol stimulates gluconeogenesis and lipogenesis in the trunk while breaking down protein in muscle and connective tissues. Classic signs include: truncal obesity, buffalo hump, moon face, purple abdominal striae, thin fragile skin with ecchymoses, muscle wasting, hyperglycemia, and hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Why the other options fail: option a is the mirror image, insufficient cortisol, which gives hypotension, hyperkalaemia and weight loss rather than striae and moon face. Option b is catecholamine excess, which gives episodic hypertension, palpitations and sweating. Option c is aldosterone excess, which gives hypertension and hypokalaemia with normal cortisol and no striae.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Central diabetes insipidus (DI); administer subcutaneous or nasal desmopressin (DDAVP)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH); administer hypertonic 3% saline"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute kidney injury from surgical hypoperfusion; give intravenous loop diuretics"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cerebral salt wasting; restrict all fluid intake to 500 mL per day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Surgical manipulation of the posterior pituitary or hypothalamus can disrupt the synthesis and release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH / vasopressin), triggering central Diabetes Insipidus (DI). Lack of ADH impairs renal collecting duct water reabsorption, causing massive dilute polyuria (> 300 mL/hr), low urine specific gravity (< 1.005), low urine osmolality, and systemic hemoconcentration with hypernatremia. Treatment is synthetic ADH (Desmopressin / DDAVP). Why the other options fail: option b follows the opposite physiology, because SIADH gives concentrated urine and low sodium, not dilute urine at 600 mL/hour with a sodium of 152. Option c would give oliguria with a rising creatinine, not a specific gravity of 1.002. Option d wastes salt and is treated with saline, and fluid restriction is the treatment for SIADH, not for this picture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Strict oral fluid restriction to 800 mL a day to reduce the calcium load"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aggressive IV 0.9% saline at 200-300 mL/hour, then a loop diuretic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subcutaneous calcitonin 4 units/kg every 12 hours as the only initial measure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Thiazide diuretics, which enhance renal calcium clearance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe hypercalcemia (serum calcium > 12 mg/dL) causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, volume depletion, and renal failure. The cornerstone of immediate management is rapid IV hydration with 0.9% normal saline (200-300 mL/hr) to expand intravascular volume, restore glomerular filtration, and promote urinary calcium excretion. Loop diuretics (furosemide) can be added once volume is restored; thiazide diuretics are strictly contraindicated because they increase renal calcium reabsorption. Why the other options fail: option a restricts the fluid needed to excrete the calcium and would deepen both the dehydration and the hypercalcaemia. Option c is an adjunct given after volume is restored, not a first measure, and it does not correct the dehydration. Option d is the wrong diuretic class, because thiazides increase calcium reabsorption and worsen hypercalcaemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the medication with calcium and iron supplements at bedtime"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Crush the tablet and stir it into a cup of hot espresso coffee"}, {"id": "c", "text": "In the morning on an empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take it immediately after dinner with a high-fibre meal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Levothyroxine oral bioavailability is significantly compromised by food, fiber, caffeine, and polyvalent cations (calcium, iron, antacids). To ensure consistent gastrointestinal absorption and stable serum hormone levels, clients must take levothyroxine on an empty stomach with a full glass of plain water in the morning, at least 30-60 minutes before breakfast (or at bedtime at least 3-4 hours after the last meal). Co-administration with iron or calcium requires a 4-hour separation window. Why the other options fail: option a puts polyvalent cations in the same swallow, and calcium and iron both bind levothyroxine and block absorption. Option b destroys part of the dose with heat and adds caffeine, which the same rationale names as an absorption problem. Option d adds dietary fibre at the moment of absorption, which binds the tablet in the gut.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "14 mL/hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "70 mL/hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "0.7 mL/hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "7 mL/hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Calculation steps: 1) Desired dose per hour: 0.1 units/kg/hour x 70 kg = 7 units/hour. 2) Solution concentration: 250 units in 250 mL = 1 unit/mL. 3) Infusion pump rate: 7 units/hour ÷ 1 unit/mL = 7 mL/hour. Why the other options fail: option a is a tenfold error, because 14 mL/hour delivers 0.2 units/kg/hour, twice the prescribed rate. Option b mistakes the patient's weight for the rate and would deliver 70 units/hour. Option c is a decimal slip that delivers 0.01 units/kg/hour, one tenth of the order.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch19.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 19: CC module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: the septic shock patient who meets ICU
-- priority 1, repositioning a low-perfusion oximetry probe rather than silencing
-- it, uncompensated respiratory acidosis at pH 7.23 with CO2 70 and bicarbonate
-- 26, partially compensated metabolic acidosis in DKA, a 10-15% stroke volume
-- rise on passive leg raise, an overdamped arterial line, phentolamine for
-- noradrenaline extravasation, beta-1 tachyarrhythmias from dobutamine,
-- DOPE for a high-pressure alarm, 4-6 mL/kg lung-protective ventilation,
-- terminating a failed spontaneous breathing trial, CPP 52 mmHg, the one-hour
-- sepsis bundle, a rising transmembrane pressure signalling filter clotting,
-- hydrocortisone for vasopressor-refractory shock, citrate-induced hypocalcaemia,
-- the lethal triad, N-acetylcysteine for paracetamol overdose, non-drug first-line
-- delirium care, and the organ-donor targets.
--
-- None of the twenty carried a distractor block, and the key was the longest in
-- 17 of 20 (mean 1.55, worst 2.32). Unchoosable options replaced: "Apply
-- four-point physical leather restraints and close the window blinds permanently",
-- "Give high-dose intravenous flumazenil to competitive antagonist receptors",
-- "Immediately infuse fresh frozen plasma and begin an esmolol infusion".
--
-- After: mean 1.16, worst 1.36. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 48-year-old patient with severe sepsis, blood pressure 82/46 mmHg despite 2 L crystalloid resuscitation, and serum lactate 4.8 mmol/L"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An 82-year-old patient with metastatic lung cancer admitted with somnolence and comfort measures only requested by family"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 24-year-old alert patient with an isolated non-displaced radial fracture awaiting surgical pin placement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 62-year-old patient 48 hours post-elective uncomplicated hip arthroplasty with mild postoperative pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$ICU triage prioritizes critically ill patients with acute, reversible organ dysfunction who derive the greatest survival benefit from invasive monitoring and continuous titration of life-sustaining therapy (Priority 1). The 48-year-old patient is in septic shock refractory to initial fluid boluses with elevated lactate and urgently requires continuous vasopressor infusion and central venous/arterial line monitoring. Comfort-care patients are managed in palliative units; non-displaced fractures and uncomplicated post-op patients do not meet critical care admission criteria. Why the other options fail: option b has comfort measures only requested, which makes this a palliative admission rather than a critical care one. Option c has an isolated non-displaced fracture and is waiting for a minor procedure, which is ward care. Option d is 48 hours after an uncomplicated arthroplasty with mild pain, which is routine postoperative ward care.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Silence the audible alarm permanently to prevent disturbing other patients on the unit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Relocate the sensor to the forehead or earlobe and individualise alarm limits within orders"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Set the low SpO2 alarm threshold to 70% so the alarm will only trigger during true catastrophic desaturations"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discontinue continuous pulse oximetry monitoring entirely since the patient is awake and restless"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Mitigating alarm fatigue without compromising patient safety requires addressing technical causes of artifact and individualizing alarm limits based on the patient's baseline. In vasoconstricted or hypoperfused patients, digits often produce weak plethysmographic signals; moving the sensor to a centrally perfused site (forehead or earlobe) improves accuracy. Permanently silencing alarms or dropping thresholds below safe limits (such as 70%) violates patient safety standards and risks undetected hypoxic arrest. Why the other options fail: option a removes the alarm altogether, and with it the safety net. Option c sets the threshold at 70%, which permits profound hypoxaemia before anything sounds. Option d abandons monitoring on a patient whose restlessness may itself be hypoxia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis with hypercapnia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis with hypoxaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis with severe hypoxaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis with compensatory hypoventilation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A pH of 7.23 is acidic (normal 7.35–7.45). The PaCO2 of 70 mmHg is markedly elevated (normal 35–45 mmHg), which explains the acidaemia, confirming a respiratory cause. The serum bicarbonate (HCO3-) of 26 mEq/L is within normal limits (22–26 mEq/L), demonstrating that renal compensation has not yet occurred (uncompensated). PaO2 of 54 mmHg represents acute hypoxaemic failure. The patient requires immediate escalation (e.g., non-invasive positive pressure ventilation such as BiPAP or invasive mechanical ventilation). Why the other options fail: option a pairs a raised bicarbonate with a raised CO2, which is the compensation pattern of metabolic alkalosis, not an acute respiratory acidosis. Option b has a pH above 7.45. Option d makes bicarbonate the primary abnormality, but here the bicarbonate is normal at 26 and the CO2 is the problem.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis; tachypnoea indicates imminent respiratory muscle failure requiring immediate paralytic intubation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis; the low bicarbonate has restored blood pH to the physiological reference range"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Primary respiratory alkalosis caused by acute hyperventilation syndrome"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis; hyperventilation is a physiological compensatory mechanism to blow off carbon dioxide"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The pH is 7.28 (acidic), and HCO3- is 13 mEq/L (markedly low), indicating primary metabolic acidosis from ketoacid accumulation. The PaCO2 is 29 mmHg (low, normal 35–45 mmHg), showing that the respiratory system is attempting to compensate by blowing off volatile acid (Kussmaul respirations). Because the pH has not fully normalized, this represents partially compensated metabolic acidosis. Kussmaul breathing is an appropriate protective compensatory response and should not be suppressed. Why the other options fail: option a mistakes the primary disorder, because the bicarbonate of 13 identifies the metabolic process and the CO2 is falling to compensate. Option b calls it fully compensated, but the pH is still 7.28. Option c puts the pH on the wrong side of 7.35 and ignores the bicarbonate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A rise in cardiac output or stroke volume of at least 10% to 15% during leg elevation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An immediate drop in mean arterial pressure (MAP) accompanied by reflex bradycardia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A marked rise in central venous pressure (CVP) with no change in pulse contour stroke volume"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An increase in systemic vascular resistance (SVR) of greater than 30%"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Passive leg raising (PLR) shifts approximately 300 mL of venous blood from the lower extremities to the intrathoracic compartment, acting as a reversible 'autologous fluid bolus'. A transient increase in stroke volume or cardiac output of >= 10–15% within 60–90 seconds confirms preload responsiveness on the Frank-Starling curve, demonstrating that administering additional crystalloids will improve perfusion without precipitating pulmonary edema. Unchanged stroke volume despite elevated filling pressures indicates non-responsiveness. Why the other options fail: option b describes a fall in pressure with bradycardia, which is a vagal response, not a preload response. Option c describes a rise in filling pressure without a rise in stroke volume, which is the pattern of a patient who will not respond. Option d describes a rise in resistance, which is the afterload effect of a vasopressor, not the effect of raising the legs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately discontinue the arterial catheter without physician consultation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Check the tubing for air bubbles, clots, or kinks and perform a fast-flush square-wave test"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Re-zero the transducer while holding it at the level of the patient's ear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer a rapid bolus of 500 mL normal saline through the arterial catheter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Overdamping of an arterial line is commonly caused by compliance dampeners such as air bubbles in the transducer tubing, blood clots at the catheter tip, loose connections, or anatomical kinks. The nurse should inspect the entire pressure tubing system, eliminate microbubbles, check catheter position, and execute a fast-flush (square-wave) test to evaluate dynamic system response. Fluids and medications must NEVER be bolused through an arterial line due to severe risk of limb ischaemia and arterial thrombosis. Why the other options fail: option a removes the line and the monitoring with it, before the cause is found. Option c zeroes the transducer at the wrong reference, which must be the phlebostatic axis at the level of the right atrium. Option d injects fluid into an artery, which risks limb ischaemia and is never done through an arterial line.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase the infusion rate to clear the peripheral vein and apply ice packs tightly over the forearm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Immediately pull out the cannula and apply firm direct manual pressure over the site for 15 minutes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the infusion, leave the cannula to aspirate residual drug, and prepare phentolamine for local infiltration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Flush the line vigorously with 20 mL of sterile water to dilute the vasopressor in local tissues"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Norepinephrine is a potent alpha-1 adrenergic agonist; peripheral extravasation causes intense local vasoconstriction leading to ischaemic tissue necrosis and gangrene. Immediate management: stop the infusion, leave the catheter in place to aspirate any residual vesicant, notify the provider, and administer the specific antidote phentolamine mesylate (an alpha-adrenergic antagonist) subcutaneously in a circle around the extravasated area to reverse vasoconstriction. Ice and vigorous flushing are contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option a increases the infusion of the vesicant and adds ice, which worsens vasoconstriction and necrosis. Option b removes the only route for aspirating the drug before it is absorbed. Option d dilutes with sterile water, which is not the antidote and adds volume to the injured tissue.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Severe peripheral vasoconstriction and cold digits from alpha-1 receptor activation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Significant bradycardia with complete heart block and asystole"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypoglycaemia with metabolic alkalosis and hypokalaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ventricular tachyarrhythmias and tachycardia from beta-1 stimulation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dobutamine is a synthetic inotrope with predominantly beta-1 adrenergic receptor agonist activity (and mild beta-2 and alpha-1 activity). By augmenting myocardial contractility and AV nodal conduction, it increases myocardial oxygen consumption and can provoke sinus tachycardia, premature ventricular contractions (PVCs), and ventricular tachyarrhythmias. Continuous cardiac rhythm monitoring and blood pressure surveillance are mandatory. Why the other options fail: option a describes the effect of norepinephrine, an alpha agonist, not dobutamine. Option b describes a beta-blocker or a vagal effect, and dobutamine raises the heart rate. Option c describes neither the receptor profile nor the metabolic effect of a beta-1 agonist, which raises glucose rather than lowering it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess chest rise, check for tube biting or secretions, auscultate, then bag-ventilate if distress persists"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Silence the alarm and raise the high-pressure limit to 80 cmH2O so the set volume is delivered"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a double dose of neuromuscular blocker without performing a physical assessment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Extubate at the bedside immediately and place a non-rebreather mask"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a high-pressure ventilator alarm triggers, the nurse first assesses the patient (DOPE mnemonic: Dislodgement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment). Common causes of high peak pressure include airway secretions (needing suctioning), patient biting the tube (needing a bite block), tubing kink, bronchospasm, or tension pneumothorax. If the cause is not immediately identifiable or the patient deteriorates, the nurse must immediately disconnect the patient from the ventilator and manually ventilate with 100% O2 via bag-valve-mask to assess lung compliance and maintain oxygenation. Why the other options fail: option b silences the alarm and raises the limit, which lets the ventilator push against an obstruction and cause barotrauma. Option c paralyses a patient who may have a blocked tube, which removes the only sign of distress. Option d extubates a patient who may still need the tube, without finding out why the pressure rose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "High tidal volumes of 12-15 mL/kg to re-expand collapsed alveoli and keep PaCO2 below 30 mmHg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Low tidal volumes of 4-6 mL/kg of predicted body weight, plateau pressure at or below 30 cmH2O, titrated PEEP"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Zero end-expiratory pressure to prevent barotrauma and right ventricular strain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperventilation to induce a respiratory alkalosis with a target pH above 7.55"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The ARDSNet protocol establishes that lung-protective ventilation utilizing low tidal volumes (4–8 mL/kg, targeting 6 mL/kg of predicted/ideal body weight) and keeping inspiratory plateau pressures <= 30 cmH2O significantly reduces mortality by mitigating ventilator-induced lung injury (volutrauma, barotrauma, biotrauma). PEEP is titrated to maintain alveolar recruitment and prevent atelectotrauma. Permissive hypercapnia is tolerated as long as severe acidaemia (pH < 7.15–7.20) is avoided. Why the other options fail: option a uses high tidal volumes, the strategy that causes volutrauma and the one ARDSNet abandoned. Option c removes PEEP, which lets the recruited alveoli collapse again with every breath. Option d targets an alkalosis that is not a goal, because permissive hypercapnia is accepted instead.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Continue the trial for the full 30 minutes because transient tachypnoea is expected during weaning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give 10 mg of intravenous morphine to calm the patient and continue the trial"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the trial, return to the previous ventilator settings, reassure the patient, and look for the cause"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Extubate the patient straight to non-invasive positive pressure ventilation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Criteria for failing a spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) include tachypnoea (respiratory rate > 35 breaths/min for >= 5 min), hypoxaemia (SpO2 < 88–90%), tachycardia (HR > 120 or >= 20% increase), accessory muscle use/paradoxical abdominal breathing, diaphoresis, and acute agitation or somnolence. The nurse must terminate the trial immediately, reconnect the patient to full ventilator support, provide reassurance, and investigate reversible causes (e.g., fatigue, secretions, bronchospasm, anxiety, electrolyte imbalances) before reattempting the next day. Why the other options fail: option a continues a trial that is failing, which exhausts the patient and delays the return to support. Option b sedates the respiratory distress instead of treating it and masks the failure. Option d extubates a patient who has just failed the test of breathing without the tube.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "CPP is 96 mmHg; the nurse should administer an antihypertensive agent to prevent cerebral hyperperfusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "CPP is 74 mmHg; the nurse should document that perfusion is optimal and maintain current management"}, {"id": "c", "text": "CPP is 22 mmHg; the nurse should hyperventilate the patient to a PaCO2 of 20 mmHg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "CPP is 52 mmHg; the nurse should notify the physician because CPP is below the target threshold of 60 to 70 mmHg"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cerebral Perfusion Pressure is calculated as MAP minus ICP (CPP = MAP - ICP). Here, CPP = 74 mmHg - 22 mmHg = 52 mmHg. Evidence-based neurocritical care guidelines (Brain Trauma Foundation) recommend maintaining CPP between 60 and 70 mmHg to prevent secondary ischaemic brain injury. A CPP of 52 mmHg with elevated ICP (> 20 mmHg) requires immediate intervention, which may include titrating vasopressors to elevate MAP, administering hyperosmolar therapy (hypertonic saline or mannitol), and maintaining head-of-bed elevation. Why the other options fail: option a adds rather than subtracts, and 74 plus 22 is not a CPP of 96. Option b reports the MAP as if it were the CPP. Option c subtracts in the wrong order and hyperventilates to a CO2 of 20, which reduces cerebral blood flow further.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Measure lactate, obtain cultures before antibiotics, give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, then 30 mL/kg crystalloid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give high-dose dexamethasone, wait 4 hours for urine cultures, and fluid-restrict to prevent pulmonary oedema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a 500 mL fluid challenge over two hours and reassess before starting antibiotics"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give norepinephrine first and defer all fluids until the central line is in place"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Surviving Sepsis Campaign 1-Hour Bundle mandates five key actions initiated immediately upon recognizing sepsis/septic shock: (1) measure serum lactate (remeasure within 2–4 hours if elevated), (2) obtain blood cultures prior to administering antimicrobials, (3) administer broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials, (4) begin rapid administration of 30 mL/kg IV crystalloids for hypotension (MAP < 65) or lactate >= 4 mmol/L, and (5) apply vasopressors (norepinephrine first-line) if fluid resuscitation does not restore MAP >= 65 mmHg. Why the other options fail: option b delays antibiotics for cultures already drawn, gives steroids to every septic patient, and restricts fluid in one who needs it. Option c delays the antibiotics and gives a fraction of the 30 mL/kg that the bundle requires. Option d starts a vasopressor before the fluid bolus and waits on a line.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A significant air embolism entering the arterial access catheter"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Impending clotting of the haemofilter, needing a circuit check and possible filter change"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Excessive systemic hypocoagulation with risk of major haemorrhage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Patient hypovolaemia requiring an immediate 1-litre saline bolus through the access line"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Transmembrane Pressure (TMP) represents the hydrostatic pressure difference across the semipermeable haemofilter membrane. A steadily rising TMP combined with an increasing filter pressure drop indicates protein layering and microthrombus formation within the hollow fibers (impending filter clotting). The nurse should assess the circuit, check anticoagulation delivery (e.g., regional citrate or heparin), and prepare to change the circuit if clotting progresses to avoid losing blood sequestered in a clotted filter. Why the other options fail: option a would show as a pressure drop with an air alarm, not a rising transmembrane pressure. Option c describes systemic coagulopathy, which is a bleeding risk in the patient and is not read from filter pressures. Option d describes hypovolaemia, which lowers the pressures rather than raising the TMP.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To sterilise the bloodstream directly and replace broad-spectrum antimicrobial therapy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To rapidly lower blood glucose and promote an osmotic diuresis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To reverse vasopressor-refractory shock caused by critical illness-related steroid insufficiency"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To induce systemic sedation and lower cerebral metabolic demand"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In septic shock refractory to fluid resuscitation and escalating doses of vasopressors (norepinephrine >= 0.25 mcg/kg/min plus vasopressin), Surviving Sepsis guidelines recommend intravenous hydrocortisone at 200 mg per day. Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (CIRCI) and down-regulated vascular adrenergic receptors are common in severe sepsis. Low-dose hydrocortisone restores vascular tone, upregulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, and accelerates shock reversal without inducing immunosuppressive harm. Why the other options fail: option a gives hydrocortisone a sterilising role it does not have, and it never replaces antibiotics. Option b describes insulin, not hydrocortisone. Option d describes sedation, which is the opposite of what a patient in refractory shock needs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Severe hypercalcaemia from excessive bone resorption during massive transfusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypernatraemia resulting from high sodium content in packed red cells"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Severe hypokalaemia caused by rapid red cell potassium uptake"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute hypocalcaemia caused by citrate binding to circulating ionized calcium"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Stored blood products contain sodium citrate as an anticoagulant. When massive volumes are infused rapidly (e.g., > 1 unit every 5 minutes), the liver's metabolic capacity to clear citrate is overwhelmed, particularly in hypothermic or shocked patients. Citrate chelates circulating ionized calcium, causing acute hypocalcaemia characterized by QT prolongation, neuromuscular excitability (Trousseau sign, Chvostek sign, twitching), decreased cardiac contractility, and hypotension. The nurse should anticipate IV calcium chloride or calcium gluconate replacement. Why the other options fail: option a is the opposite calcium change, which follows immobilisation or malignancy rather than rapid transfusion. Option b misplaces the sodium load, which is real but gives hypernatraemia without the QT prolongation and twitching described. Option c is the wrong direction for potassium and is not caused by citrate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hypothermia, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hyperkalaemia, hypercalcaemia, and hypermagnesaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tachycardia, hypoxaemia, and hypocapnia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The 'lethal triad' of trauma consists of hypothermia, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy. Each component exacerbates the others: hypothermia impairs coagulation enzyme cascades and platelet function; hypoperfusion causes lactic acidosis; and acidosis further inhibits thrombin generation. Failure to interrupt this vicious cycle leads to uncontrollable microvascular bleeding and death. Management requires active rewarming, damage control surgery, dynamic fluid resuscitation, and balanced blood component transfusion (1:1:1 PRBCs, FFP, platelets). Why the other options fail: option b lists hypertension and bradycardia, the opposite of the shocked trauma patient. Option c lists three electrolyte rises, and none of them is part of the triad. Option d describes a respiratory picture; tachycardia and hypocapnia may accompany shock but are not what drives the bleeding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer intravenous naloxone continuous infusion to reverse central nervous system depression"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start the N-acetylcysteine protocol promptly to replenish glutathione and prevent hepatic necrosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give high-dose intravenous flumazenil to antagonise benzodiazepine receptors"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse sodium polystyrene sulfonate to accelerate gastrointestinal excretion of the drug"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acetaminophen overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation pathways, shunting metabolism to cytochrome P450, which produces the toxic metabolite NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine). When endogenous glutathione is depleted, NAPQI binds hepatocytes causing centrilobular hepatic necrosis. N-acetylcysteine (NAC) serves as a glutathione precursor and substitute, detoxifying NAPQI. Initiating NAC within 8 hours of acute ingestion provides virtually 100% hepatoprotection. Why the other options fail: option a reverses opioid effect, and paracetamol is not an opioid, so naloxone does nothing for NAPQI. Option c antagonises benzodiazepines that the history does not mention, and flumazenil can precipitate seizures. Option d binds potassium in the gut and has no effect on hepatic glutathione.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately administer high-dose intravenous haloperidol every 2 hours without investigating underlying physiological causes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply four-point leather restraints and keep the window blinds closed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Identify and treat the medical triggers, reorient the patient, promote sleep, and mobilise early"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Initiate continuous deep benzodiazepine sedation targeting a RASS score of -5"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$ICU delirium is a clinical syndrome of acute brain dysfunction characterized by fluctuating inattention and altered awareness. First-line management focuses on non-pharmacological interventions and identifying modifiable physiological causes (hypoxia, sepsis, metabolic derangements, pain, anticholinergic/sedative medications, urinary retention). Evidence-based bundles (ABCDEF bundle) emphasize pain control, daily sedation vacation, spontaneous breathing trials, delirium monitoring, early physical mobilization, and sleep optimization. Benzodiazepines and unnecessary restraints worsen delirium. Why the other options fail: option a sedates with an antipsychotic before the cause is found, and haloperidol prolongs the QT without treating the hypoxia or the sepsis. Option b restrains and darkens the room, which worsens disorientation. Option d deepens sedation to a RASS of -5, which is a coma and the opposite of the early mobilisation the bundle requires.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Permit profound hypotension and hypothermia to slow down organ cellular metabolism"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrict all intravenous fluids and stop mechanical ventilation before the operating room"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Maintain continuous deep sedation with high-dose propofol and fentanyl infusions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep systolic pressure above 100 mmHg, urine output 1-3 mL/kg/h, PaO2 above 100 mmHg, and normothermia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Brain death produces autonomic storm followed by loss of sympathetic vascular tone, diabetes insipidus, poikilothermia, and endocrine failure. To preserve organ viability for transplantation, the nurse maintains optimal physiological goals (often remembered as the 'Rule of 100s'): systolic blood pressure >= 100 mmHg (MAP >= 65), PaO2 >= 100 mmHg (SaO2 >= 95%), urine output 100–200 mL/h (or 1–3 mL/kg/h), blood glucose 100–150 mg/dL, and core body temperature > 36 °C. Hormonal resuscitation (levothyroxine/T4, methylprednisolone, vasopressin) is standard. Why the other options fail: option a permits the hypotension and hypothermia that injure the organs being preserved. Option b withdraws the ventilation and fluids that maintain oxygenation and perfusion. Option c deepens sedation, which suppresses the neurological assessment and the sympathetic tone the donor depends on.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch20.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 20: GI module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: inspection-auscultation-percussion-palpation,
-- listening 3 to 5 minutes per quadrant before calling bowel sounds absent,
-- NPO with low-Fowler knees-flexed positioning for a perforation, head-of-bed
-- elevation for reflux, the dumping-syndrome diet, soap and water (not alcohol)
-- for C. difficile, typhoid perforation in week three, questioning the enema
-- in appendicitis, toxic megacolon watch in ulcerative colitis, NG decompression
-- for small bowel obstruction, 2 to 3 soft stools as the lactulose target, the
-- scissors at the bedside for a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube, Grey Turner sign as
-- retroperitoneal haemorrhage, Murphy sign, a dusky stoma, D10W at the same rate
-- when TPN runs out, colonoscopy for iron-deficiency anaemia, the long-term PPI
-- risks, stopping metoclopramide at the first dyskinetic movement, and
-- 50 mL/hour of octreotide.
--
-- None of the twenty carried a distractor block; the key was the longest in
-- 12 of 20 (mean 1.20, worst 1.70). Unchoosable options replaced: "Immediate
-- therapeutic resolution of the enteric bacteremia", "Self-limiting splenic
-- enlargement resolving spontaneously", and bare "Gastroenteritis".
--
-- After: mean 1.12, worst 1.36. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inspection, auscultation, percussion, palpation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Auscultation, inspection, palpation, percussion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Palpation, percussion, inspection, auscultation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The correct sequence for abdominal assessment is inspection, auscultation, percussion, and palpation (I-A-P-P). Auscultation must precede percussion and palpation because mechanical manipulation, pressure, or percussion of the abdominal wall stimulates peristalsis and artificially alters the frequency and character of bowel sounds, potentially leading to inaccurate diagnostic findings. Why the other options fail: option b palpates before auscultating, and palpation alters the bowel sounds that are then recorded. Option c auscultates before it looks at the abdomen, so the inspection findings are lost. Option d starts with the most intrusive step of all and distorts every sign that follows.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Listen for 15 seconds in the right lower quadrant only"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Listen continuously over each abdominal quadrant for at least 3 to 5 minutes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Palpate the right upper quadrant vigorously while listening with the stethoscope bell"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer an oral prokinetic agent and reassess bowel sounds 10 minutes later"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$To accurately declare that bowel sounds are absent, the nurse must listen continuously with the diaphragm of the stethoscope for a minimum of 3 to 5 full minutes in each quadrant (or throughout the abdomen). Normal bowel sounds occur irregularly between 5 and 30 times per minute. True absence indicates paralytic ileus or peritonitis. Brief listening (15 seconds) is inadequate to detect hypoactive peristalsis. Why the other options fail: option a listens for 15 seconds in one quadrant, which cannot distinguish hypoactive from absent sounds. Option c palpates while listening, which creates the sounds it is trying to hear. Option d gives a drug before assessing, and a prokinetic changes the baseline the nurse needs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer 30 mL of liquid aluminum hydroxide antacid via oral syringe"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insert a rectal tube to relieve suspected lower bowel flatulent distension"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Maintain strict NPO status, position in low-Fowler with knees flexed, and notify the surgeon at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a warm heating pad across the epigastrium to alleviate muscle guarding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sudden severe pain, a rigid board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, and absent bowel sounds are classical hallmarks of acute gastrointestinal perforation, causing chemical peritonitis from leaking gastric contents. This is a life-threatening surgical emergency. The nurse must immediately maintain strict NPO status, position the client in low-Fowler position with knees flexed (to localize peritonitis to the pelvis and decrease abdominal wall tension), obtain vital signs, and summon the surgeon immediately for emergency laparotomy. Oral antacids and heat applications are contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option a gives an oral agent to a patient who must be NPO for theatre, and antacids do not treat a perforation. Option b inserts a rectal tube into a patient with free air in the abdomen. Option d applies heat to a peritonitic abdomen, which increases perfusion, bleeding and the risk of further perforation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Consume large meals twice daily to minimize gastric acid secretory cycles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Drink a cup of hot peppermint tea immediately before retiring to bed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lie flat on the back for 45 minutes after eating to facilitate gastric emptying"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Raise the head of the bed on 6-inch blocks and stay upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Elevating the head of the bed 6 inches on blocks utilizes gravity to prevent acid reflux into the esophagus during sleep. Remaining upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals allows time for gastric emptying and prevents reflux. Peppermint, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, fatty foods, and smoking reduce lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone and worsen reflux. Lying down flat after meals dramatically increases reflux events. Why the other options fail: option a takes large meals, which raise gastric volume and pressure against a weak sphincter. Option b chooses peppermint, which relaxes the lower oesophageal sphincter and worsens reflux. Option c lies flat after eating, which removes gravity from the antireflux mechanism.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Eat small, frequent high-protein meals, avoid fluids with meals, and lie down for 20 to 30 minutes after"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Consume high-carbohydrate smoothies with large amounts of refined sugar at mealtime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Drink at least 500 mL of iced water during each meal to dilute gastric secretions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ambulate briskly around the ward immediately after consuming heavy meals"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dumping syndrome results from the rapid transit of hyperosmolar chyme into the jejunum, pulling intravascular fluid into the bowel lumen (hypotension, dizziness, cramping) and triggering reactive hypoglycemia. Dietary management includes: (1) small, frequent meals, (2) low-carbohydrate, moderate-to-high protein and complex fat content to slow gastric transit, (3) consuming liquids between meals rather than with meals (at least 30 to 45 minutes apart) to prevent accelerated gastric emptying, and (4) reclining or lying down for 20 to 30 minutes after meals to slow intestinal transit. Why the other options fail: option b loads refined sugar, which increases the osmotic load in the jejunum and provokes the reactive hypoglycaemia. Option c drinks a large volume with the meal, which accelerates gastric emptying. Option d exercises immediately after eating, which speeds transit and worsens the dumping.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rely exclusively on alcohol-based hand rub formulations for rapid sanitization"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse hands thoroughly with antimicrobial soap and warm water after removing gloves"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on airborne precautions in a negative-pressure isolation room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disinfect bedside medical equipment using standard quaternary ammonium wipes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clostridioides difficile forms hardy bacterial endospores that are resistant to alcohol-based hand sanitizers. Healthcare personnel must wash hands vigorously with soap and water; the mechanical friction and lather rinse the spores off the skin. C. diff requires Contact Precautions (gown and gloves upon entry, dedicated equipment). Disinfection of patient rooms and equipment must be performed with sporicidal, bleach-based (sodium hypochlorite) agents, not quaternary ammonium wipes. Airborne isolation is unnecessary. Why the other options fail: option a relies on alcohol, which does not kill C. difficile spores. Option c uses airborne precautions when the route is contact. Option d uses a quaternary ammonium wipe, which does not kill spores; the agent has to be a sporicidal bleach.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dehydration from the continuing high fever, corrected with oral fluids"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute cholelithiasis resulting in common bile duct obstruction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intestinal perforation of the terminal ileum with developing septic shock"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Constipation from the prolonged bed rest, relieved by an enema"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$During the second and third weeks of typhoid fever, hyperplastic Peyer's patches in the terminal ileum ulcerate and may necrose, causing life-threatening intestinal perforation and severe lower GI hemorrhage. A sudden hypothermic drop in temperature, tachycardia, hypotension, and generalized peritoneal signs (tenderness, guarding) in week 3 indicate free bowel perforation and acute septic peritonitis. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, and emergent exploratory laparotomy. Why the other options fail: option a describes the bacteraemia resolving, which is not what a sudden temperature drop with tachycardia and guarding means. Option b is biliary disease, which does not produce this sequence in the third week of typhoid. Option d is a self-limiting enlargement, which does not explain shock and peritonism.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Establish an intravenous line for 0.9% normal saline infusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Maintain the client on strict NPO status"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obtain blood for complete blood count and serum electrolytes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer a warm mineral oil enema to relieve suspected obstipation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In suspected acute appendicitis, administering enemas, laxatives, or applying heat to the abdomen is strictly contraindicated because increased intraluminal pressure or hyperemic vasodilation can cause sudden appendix perforation and catastrophic diffuse peritonitis. The nurse must question any prescription for enemas, laxatives, or local heat. Keeping the client NPO, starting IV fluids, and obtaining baseline labs are standard and essential preparatory steps. Why the other options fail: option a is a correct preparatory step, as is option b for keeping the bowel at rest and option c for baseline bloods. The prescription to question is the enema, because it raises intraluminal pressure in an appendix that is already inflamed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Monitor for hypovolemia, electrolyte depletion (especially hypokalemia), and signs of toxic megacolon"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage a high-fiber, high-residue diet with fresh raw vegetables to form bulk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer daily rectal stimulant suppositories to expedite bowel evacuation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict oral fluid intake to 500 mL per 24 hours to reduce stool frequency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe flare-ups of ulcerative colitis cause extensive mucosal ulceration in the colon and rectum, leading to frequent bloody diarrhea (10–20 stools/day), significant blood loss, dehydration, and severe electrolyte depletion (especially potassium and sodium). Furthermore, severe inflammation can cause paralysis of the colon and rapid dilation (toxic megacolon), predisposing to perforation. The nurse must monitor hydration, electrolytes, hemoglobin, and watch for fever, leukocytosis, and abdominal distension. High-fiber diets exacerbate bowel inflammation during acute flares. Why the other options fail: option b adds bulk and raw fibre to an acutely inflamed colon. Option c uses stimulant suppositories, which increase urgency and mucosal trauma during a flare. Option d restricts fluid in a patient who is losing volume and potassium through 10 to 20 stools a day.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer oral polyethylene glycol bowel preparation to clear the obstruction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insert a nasogastric (NG) tube and attach to low intermittent suction, and initiate IV fluids"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Provide a regular diet with high-protein supplements to prevent catabolism"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer high-dose subcutaneous loperamide to control vomiting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In mechanical small bowel obstruction, fluid and gas accumulate proximal to the blockage, resulting in massive luminal distension, third-spacing, dehydration, vomiting, and risk of bowel ischemia or perforation. Decompression of the stomach and upper GI tract via a nasogastric tube attached to low intermittent suction relieves distension, alleviates nausea/vomiting, and prevents pulmonary aspiration. Intravenous fluid resuscitation corrects hypovolemia. Oral intake, laxatives, and oral bowel preps are contraindicated because they exacerbate luminal pressure against the mechanical blockage. Why the other options fail: option a gives an oral bowel preparation against a mechanical obstruction, which raises the pressure behind the block. Option c feeds a patient who cannot pass what she takes. Option d gives an antimotility drug, which retains the distension and the vomiting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client develops watery, persistent diarrhea exceeding 8 bowel movements daily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client has complete absence of bowel movements for 48 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client passes 2 to 3 soft acidic stools daily and is mentally clearer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client's serum ammonia level increases above 150 micromol/L"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Lactulose is a non-absorbable disaccharide that acidifies the colon (converting absorbable ammonia [NH3] into non-absorbable ammonium [NH4+]), promoting its rapid excretion in stool. The therapeutic goal of lactulose in hepatic encephalopathy is achieving 2 to 3 soft, formed bowel movements per day, leading to reduced serum ammonia levels and improved mental status. Excessive diarrhea (> 4–5 stools/day) causes dehydration and hypokalemia, which worsens encephalopathy. Why the other options fail: option a describes the excessive diarrhoea the dose is meant to avoid, and it causes dehydration and hypokalaemia. Option b has no bowel movement at all, which means the dose is too low. Option d is the ammonia still rising while the patient is under-treated; the goal is a fall, tracked through the stools and the mental state.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An extra liter of sterile water for balloon irrigation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A bottle of oral activated charcoal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A tracheostomy dilator tray"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A pair of surgical scissors"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$If the gastric balloon of a Sengstaken-Blakemore or Minnesota tube ruptures or accidentally deflates, the esophageal balloon can migrate upward into the oropharynx, causing complete, acute airway obstruction and asphyxiation. The nurse must keep a pair of scissors at the bedside at all times; in the event of acute respiratory distress, the nurse immediately cuts all lumens of the tube to deflate the balloons instantly and pulls the tube out. Why the other options fail: option a adds volume to a balloon that may already be misplaced. Option b treats a poisoning that is not the problem here. Option c prepares for a tracheostomy this patient does not have, and none of the three can deflate a migrated balloon.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage and extensive pancreatic necrosis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Benign cutaneous hyperpigmentation from elevated bilirubin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute lower urinary tract extravasation of urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Superficial bacterial cellulitis of the abdominal subcutaneous tissue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cullen's sign (periumbilical ecchymosis) and Turner's (Grey Turner's) sign (flank ecchymosis) represent tracking of blood from retroperitoneal tissue into subcutaneous tissue plane walls. In acute pancreatitis, they are ominous physical signs indicating severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage, tissue necrosis, and high mortality. The client requires immediate aggressive crystalloid fluid resuscitation, strict NPO, intensive hemodynamic monitoring, and surgical evaluation. Why the other options fail: option b reads the bruising as jaundice, which stains yellow rather than purple and does not follow the flank. Option c is urinary extravasation, which gives different signs and no periumbilical bruising. Option d is superficial infection, which gives erythema and warmth rather than ecchymosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute diverticulitis of the sigmoid colon"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute cholecystitis with a distended gallbladder"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute appendicitis with early perforation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Viral gastroenteritis with dehydration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A positive Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest elicited by sharp tenderness when palpating the gallbladder beneath the right costal margin as the diaphragm descends on deep inspiration) is the classic physical diagnostic sign of acute cholecystitis (gallbladder inflammation, usually caused by cystic duct obstruction from gallstones). Pain is typically precipitated by fatty or fried meals (which stimulate cholecystokinin release) and frequently radiates to the right subscapular area or right shoulder. Why the other options fail: option a gives left lower quadrant pain with fever and a change in bowel habit. Option c migrates from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa. Option d gives diarrhoea and cramping without inspiratory arrest on right upper quadrant palpation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Document the finding as normal initial postoperative venous engorgement"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an ice pack directly to the stoma surface to reduce swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately notify the surgeon of suspected stomal ischemia and necrosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Irrigate the stoma with 500 mL of warm normal saline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A healthy, viable stoma should be moist, beefy red or pink, and warm. A stoma that is pale, dusky, dark purple, brown, or black indicates compromised arterial blood supply or severe venous congestion resulting in ischemia and tissue necrosis. This is a surgical emergency that requires immediate notification of the surgical team for potential urgent surgical re-exploration. Never apply ice or wait, as prolonged ischemia leads to stoma retraction and intraperitoneal perforation. Why the other options fail: option a documents a compromised stoma as normal, and the window for salvage closes. Option b applies ice to tissue that is already ischaemic. Option d irrigates a stoma whose blood supply is failing and adds pressure to it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Infuse 0.9% normal saline at a keep-vein-open (KVO) rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Infuse sterile water without dextrose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Clamp the central venous catheter line completely until the new bag arrives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse 10% dextrose in water (D10W) at the same infusion rate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$TPN solutions contain high concentrations of dextrose (typically 20% to 50%), stimulating continuous, high-level pancreatic insulin secretion. If a TPN infusion is suddenly stopped or runs out, circulating insulin levels remain high while glucose delivery abruptly drops, precipitating severe rebound hypoglycemia (confusion, diaphoresis, tremors, seizures, coma). To prevent severe rebound hypoglycemia while awaiting a new TPN bag, the nurse must immediately infuse 10% dextrose in water (D10W) at the same prescribed rate. Why the other options fail: option a keeps the vein open but delivers no glucose, so the insulin already circulating has nothing to act on. Option b gives free water without dextrose, which does not correct hypoglycaemia. Option c stops all dextrose delivery and lets the glucose fall fastest of all.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Colonoscopy with tissue biopsy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Routine upper GI barium swallow series"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transabdominal pelvic ultrasound"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In an adult older than 50 years, new-onset iron deficiency anemia, unexplained fatigue, change in bowel habits (such as pencil-thin stools), or rectal bleeding must be treated as colorectal cancer until proven otherwise. A colonoscopy allows direct visualization of the entire large intestine from the rectum to the cecum and enables immediate biopsy of any suspicious lesions or polyp removal. It is the gold standard diagnostic procedure. Why the other options fail: option b images the upper tract with barium, which cannot see the colon and would delay the diagnosis. Option c examines the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum, all above the lesion. Option d images the pelvis and cannot inspect the colonic mucosa or take a biopsy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hypercalcemia, severe hypernatremia, and gouty arthritis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fractures from osteoporosis, hypomagnesaemia, and C. difficile colitis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertension, acute hemolytic anemia, and fluid overload"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, and peptic ulcer recurrence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Long-term use of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) suppresses gastric acid, which impairs the intestinal absorption of calcium and magnesium, leading to hypomagnesemia and significantly increased risks of osteoporosis and bone fractures (hip, wrist, and spine). Furthermore, the loss of the gastric acid bactericidal barrier allows pathogen colonization, increasing the risk of Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea and pneumonia. Clients on long-term PPIs require periodic bone density scans and magnesium level monitoring. Why the other options fail: option a lists hypercalcaemia and gout, neither of which is a PPI effect. Option c describes haemolysis and overload, which belong to transfusion rather than acid suppression. Option d describes thyroid and adrenal failure, which are not on the PPI risk list.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the next scheduled dose on time with a full glass of water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client that these movements are benign and will subside with continued therapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discontinue the metoclopramide and immediately notify the healthcare provider"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply warm compresses to the bilateral masseter muscles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Metoclopramide is a central dopamine antagonist that crosses the blood-brain barrier. It carries a Black Box warning for tardive dyskinesia—a serious, often irreversible extrapyramidal movement disorder characterized by involuntary, repetitive movements of the face, tongue, and limbs (facial grimacing, tongue protrusion, lip smacking, rapid blinking). At the earliest sign of tardive dyskinesia, the medication must be stopped immediately and the provider notified. Continued administration risks permanent neurological dysfunction. Why the other options fail: option a continues the drug that is causing the movements. Option b calls a black-box reaction benign, and tardive dyskinesia does not subside with continued exposure. Option d treats the jaw with heat while the cause continues.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "25 mL/hr"}, {"id": "b", "text": "100 mL/hr"}, {"id": "c", "text": "10 mL/hr"}, {"id": "d", "text": "50 mL/hr"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$To calculate the infusion rate: Concentration = 500 mcg / 500 mL = 1 mcg/mL. Desired dose = 50 mcg/hr. Infusion rate = (Desired dose / Concentration) = 50 mcg/hr / (1 mcg/mL) = 50 mL/hr. Octreotide (a somatostatin analog) reduces portal venous pressure through selective splanchnic vasoconstriction, controlling active variceal hemorrhage. Why the other options fail: option a halves the dose, giving 25 mcg/hour. Option b doubles it to 100 mcg/hour. Option c delivers 10 mcg/hour, one fifth of the order. The pump has to run at 50 mL/hour to give 50 mcg/hour at 1 mcg/mL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-020$q$;

