-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch23.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 23: AP1 module option rewrite and rationale block (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: negative feedback exemplified by insulin,
-- the transverse plane, tension pneumothorax, hypertonic saline and osmosis,
-- the ischaemic ATP cascade, squamous metaplasia, osteoblast-osteoclast coupling
-- with PTH, the epiphyseal plate, osteoarthritis against rheumatoid arthritis,
-- the sliding filament theory, myasthenia gravis at the motor end-plate, the
-- ventrogluteal site, pernicious anaemia after gastrectomy, bile salts and
-- bilirubin, villous atrophy in coeliac disease, keratinocyte migration through
-- the epidermis, hypothalamic heat dissipation, full-thickness burn and eschar,
-- fat embolism syndrome, and mechanical small bowel obstruction.
--
-- Same profile as AP2 and AP3: keys of 157 to 348 characters against distractors
-- of 46 to 117, mean 2.72 and worst 4.86, with options such as "the stomach has
-- completely ceased acid production, causing air to ferment into alcohol" and
-- "immediate breakdown of subcutaneous adipose tissue to release chilling ice
-- crystals". Every option was rewritten in the same register, and the
-- mechanistic detail moved into the rationale.
--
-- After: mean 1.10, worst 1.34. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Elevated blood glucose triggers pancreatic beta-cells to secrete insulin, which promotes cellular glucose uptake and returns blood glucose back toward normal baseline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Uterine cervical stretch during labor triggers oxytocin secretion, which produces stronger uterine contractions that further increase cervical stretch until delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Platelet adhesion to a ruptured vessel wall triggers the release of ADP and thromboxane A2, recruiting more platelets in an accelerating cascade until a clot forms"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lactation suckling stimulates prolactin and oxytocin release, which triggers milk let-down and stimulates further vigorous infant suckling"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A Negative Feedback mechanism is a primary homeostatic regulatory loop that detects deviations from a set point and triggers biological responses that reverse or oppose the initial stimulus, restoring equilibrium. When blood glucose rises, insulin release lowers glucose back to baseline (opposing the change). In contrast, Positive Feedback mechanisms amplify, reinforce, and accelerate the initial stimulus until a discrete endpoint is reached, as in oxytocin-mediated labor contractions (b), platelet aggregation in hemostasis (c), and the suckling-milk ejection reflex (d). Why the other options fail: option b is a positive feedback loop, because contractions increase the stimulus that produced them. Option c is also positive feedback, with each platelet recruiting more. Option d is positive feedback again, since suckling begets more suckling rather than returning the system to baseline.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Midsagittal (median) plane, dividing the body into equal right and left halves"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transverse (horizontal or axial) plane, dividing the body into superior and inferior portions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Frontal (coronal) plane, dividing the body into anterior and posterior portions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Parasagittal plane, dividing the body into unequal right and left portions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Transverse plane (also called horizontal or axial plane) runs horizontally, dividing the body or an organ into superior (upper) and inferior (lower) sections; standard axial CT and MRI cross-sections are viewed along this plane. A Sagittal (or midsagittal) plane (a, d) divides the body vertically into right and left halves. A Frontal (or coronal) plane (c) divides the body vertically into anterior (ventral/front) and posterior (dorsal/back) portions. Why the other options fail: option a splits the body into right and left halves, not upper and lower. Option c splits it into anterior and posterior. Option d is a vertical cut parallel to the midline, which still produces left and right portions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fluid accumulating in the retroperitoneal space, displacing the left kidney and splinting the diaphragm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Air trapped in the subarachnoid space after a dural tear, raising intracranial pressure and splinting the chest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Air entered the pleural cavity between the visceral and parietal pleura, abolishing the negative intrapleural pressure and collapsing the lung"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bile leaking between the visceral and parietal peritoneum, irritating the diaphragm and causing referred chest pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The lungs are enclosed by a double-layered serous membrane: the visceral pleura (covering the lung surface) and the parietal pleura (lining the thoracic wall), separated by the pleural cavity containing a thin film of serous fluid under negative pressure. When penetrating trauma breaches this seal, air rushes into the potential space (pneumothorax). In a tension pneumothorax, a one-way valve effect traps air, increasing intrapleural pressure, collapsing the ipsilateral lung, shifting the mediastinum and trachea to the opposite side, and compressing the vena cava, causing obstructive shock. Option c accurately describes this pleural pathology. Why the other options fail: option a puts fluid in the retroperitoneum, which lies behind the abdominal cavity and does not collapse the lung. Option b puts air in the subarachnoid space, which is inside the skull and spinal canal. Option d puts bile in the peritoneum, which irritates the diaphragm but leaves the pleural space intact.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Water moves from the intravascular space into swollen brain cells, because the hypertonic fluid cannot cross the capillary wall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sodium pulls water into the brain interstitium first, and the oedema resolves only when the kidneys excrete the excess"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The hypertonic gradient draws sodium into brain cells, and water follows, so the intracellular volume rises"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Water moves from the swollen brain interstitium into the hypertonic intravascular space, reducing cerebral oedema and intracranial pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Osmosis is the net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration (higher water concentration) to an area of higher solute concentration (lower water concentration). Hypertonic solutions (such as 3% sodium chloride, osmolarity ~1,026 mOsm/L) have a higher solute concentration than normal intracellular fluid (~285 mOsm/L). Infusing 3% saline raises intravascular osmolarity, creating an osmotic gradient that pulls excess water out of swollen brain cells and interstitial tissue into cerebral capillaries, thereby reducing brain volume and intracranial pressure (ICP). Option a describes hypotonic fluids (e.g., 0.45% saline), which would fatally worsen cerebral edema. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the gradient, because water leaves the brain when the plasma is hypertonic. Option b describes a delayed renal effect rather than the immediate osmotic shift. Option c moves sodium and water into the cells, which is the effect of hypotonic fluid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, the sodium-potassium pump fails, so sodium and water enter and the cell swells and acidifies"}, {"id": "b", "text": "ATP depletion opens the potassium channels first, so potassium enters the cell and water follows, causing swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis raises ATP above normal, and the excess energy drives calcium into the cell"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The membrane becomes impermeable to all ions, so the cell cannot regulate its volume and swells"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. Hypoxia causes: 1) cessation of aerobic ATP synthesis; 2) switch to anaerobic glycolysis, generating lactic acid and lowering cellular pH; 3) failure of the ATP-dependent Na+/K+ pump, causing sodium (and obligate water) to accumulate inside the cell while potassium leaks out, leading to hydropic cellular swelling; and 4) influx of extracellular calcium activating destructive intracellular enzymes (proteases, lipases, endonucleases), culminating in irreversible necrosis. Option a accurately details this universal mechanism of hypoxic cell injury. Why the other options fail: option b has potassium entering the cell first, when the failing pump loses its gradient and sodium accumulates inside. Option c has anaerobic glycolysis raising ATP, which it cannot do as efficiently as oxidation. Option d makes the membrane impermeable to ions, which is the opposite of pump failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hypertrophy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Metaplasia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Atrophy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperplasia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Metaplasia is a reversible cellular adaptation in which one adult mature cell type (e.g., pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium) is replaced by another mature adult cell type (e.g., stratified squamous epithelium) that is better able to tolerate chronic physical or chemical irritation (such as hot cigarette smoke). However, this adaptation results in the loss of vital mucus secretion and ciliary escalator function, predisposing to chronic bronchitis, and if irritation persists, can progress to dysplasia and bronchogenic squamous cell carcinoma. Hypertrophy (a) is increased cell size; Atrophy (c) is decreased cell size; Hyperplasia (d) is increased cell number. Why the other options fail: option a increases cell size without changing cell type. Option c shrinks cells without changing type. Option d increases the number of the same cells, which is not the substitution of one mature epithelium for another that the biopsy shows.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Osteoblasts resorb bone while osteoclasts build it, and PTH lowers serum calcium by blocking both"}, {"id": "b", "text": "PTH raises serum calcium by increasing intestinal absorption alone, without acting on bone cells"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Osteoblasts build bone, osteoclasts resorb it, and PTH drives osteoclastic resorption to raise serum calcium when it falls"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoclasts both build and resorb bone, and PTH switches them from one role to the other"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bone is dynamic living tissue continuously remodeled by two primary cell types: 1) OSTEOBLASTS ('bone builders') synthesize collagen matrix and promote mineral deposition (osteogenesis); 2) OSTEOCLASTS ('bone chewers/resorbers') secrete lysosomal enzymes and acids to break down bone matrix, releasing ionized calcium into systemic circulation. When serum calcium falls below normal, Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) is secreted, stimulating osteoclast activity, renal calcium reabsorption, and vitamin D activation to elevate serum calcium. Calcitonin opposes PTH. In post-menopausal osteoporosis, estrogen deficiency accelerates osteoclastic resorption over osteoblastic formation. Why the other options fail: option a swaps the two cells. Option b describes calcitonin, which lowers calcium, rather than PTH. Option d removes both cells and hormones from bone turnover, which occurs continuously.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The plate is the growth zone, and injury can cause premature or asymmetric closure with length discrepancy or angulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The plate is the weakest part of the bone, so fractures there heal more slowly than shaft fractures"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The plate carries the blood supply to the epiphysis, so injury causes avascular necrosis of the whole bone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The plate has no nerve supply, so injuries there are painless and go unrecognised until deformity appears"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In growing children, longitudinal bone elongation occurs at the epiphyseal plate (growth plate or physis), located between the epiphysis and diaphysis of long bones. It contains active chondrocytes undergoing endochondral ossification. Traumatic disruption (Salter-Harris fracture) can disrupt vascular supply and damage proliferating cartilage, leading to premature bony bridging (epiphyseal arrest), asymmetric bone growth, angular deformities, or significant limb-length discrepancies. Bone marrow in the medullary cavity and flat bones produces RBCs (a); muscles attach to the periosteum via tendons (b). Why the other options fail: option a makes the plate the site of red cell production, which is marrow. Option b has muscle attaching directly to marrow. Option c gives the plate no blood supply and a malignant outcome, neither of which is the concern described.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Osteoarthritis is degenerative cartilage loss with subchondral remodelling; rheumatoid arthritis is autoimmune synovitis with pannus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Osteoarthritis follows a low-grade synovial infection, while rheumatoid arthritis is a purely genetic disorder of cartilage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rheumatoid arthritis affects weight-bearing joints asymmetrically, while osteoarthritis is always symmetric and seronegative"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoarthritis affects the synovium first, while rheumatoid arthritis begins in subchondral bone and spares the synovium"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A synovial (diarthrodial) joint consists of articular hyaline cartilage, a fibrous capsule lined by a synovial membrane, and synovial lubricating fluid. OSTEOARTHRITIS is primarily a biomechanical degenerative disorder characterized by progressive degradation of articular cartilage, osteophyte (bone spur) formation, and joint space narrowing, classically exacerbated by weight-bearing and use. RHEUMATOID ARTHRITIS is a systemic autoimmune disease where autoantibodies target the synovial membrane, causing inflammatory synovitis, microvascular proliferation, and invasive hyperplastic pannus that erodes articular cartilage and bone symmetrically. Option a accurately contrasts these conditions. Why the other options fail: option b makes osteoarthritis infectious, when it is mechanical and degenerative. Option c reverses the symmetry and distribution of the two diseases. Option d denies the articular cartilage that both diseases destroy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Calcium binds tropomyosin, which pulls troponin aside so that myosin heads can bind actin and shorten the sarcomere"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, moving tropomyosin so myosin can cross-bridge and execute the power stroke"}, {"id": "c", "text": "ATP hydrolysis extends actin filaments while myosin filaments shorten, which draws the Z lines together"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sodium entering the T-tubules binds troponin and exposes the actin sites for the myosin heads"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The sliding filament mechanism of skeletal muscle contraction follows a precise molecular sequence: 1) Motor neuron action potential releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction; 2) End-plate depolarization propagates down transverse tubules (T-tubules); 3) Voltage-gated channels trigger calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; 4) Calcium binds to TROPONIN, inducing a conformational shift in TROPOMYOSIN that uncovers active myosin-binding sites on ACTIN; 5) Energized MYOSIN heads bind to actin, forming cross-bridges; 6) The power stroke pulls actin filaments toward the sarcomere center (M-line), hydrolyzing ATP; 7) New ATP binding releases myosin from actin. Filaments do not change length (c); they slide past each other. Why the other options fail: option a dissolves the myosin heads, which are not consumed by contraction. Option c lengthens actin and shortens myosin, when the filaments slide past each other without changing length. Option d gives the trigger to sodium and collagen, when calcium and troponin are the switch.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autoantibodies block nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor end-plate, so the muscle cannot depolarise"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Autoantibodies destroy the presynaptic calcium channels, so acetylcholine is never released into the cleft"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Excess acetylcholinesterase at the end-plate breaks down acetylcholine before it can act"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autoantibodies block the muscarinic receptors on the muscle membrane, preventing depolarisation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Myasthenia Gravis (MG) is an autoimmune disease targeting the neuromuscular junction. Circulating autoantibodies (primarily against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor [AChR] on the postsynaptic muscle sarcolemma) block acetylcholine binding and accelerate receptor degradation. With repetitive motor neuron firing, acetylcholine stores deplete while fewer functional receptors remain available, resulting in progressive muscle fatigability that worsens with activity and improves with rest (e.g., ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, and potentially fatal myasthenic respiratory crisis). ALS destroys motor neurons (a); Guillain-Barré causes demyelination (b). Why the other options fail: option a puts the lesion in the upper motor neuron, which would give spasticity rather than fatigable weakness. Option b destroys presynaptic calcium channels, which is Lambert-Eaton and improves with use. Option c removes acetylcholine too quickly, which is anticholinesterase excess rather than the disease.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It lies away from the sciatic nerve and major vessels, and it has a thick muscle mass for a viscous drug"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It has fewer nerve endings, so an irritating drug causes less pain and is absorbed more slowly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It lies over the gluteus maximus, which has a larger surface area than the dorsogluteal site"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It avoids the superior gluteal artery, and it lies within the safe triangle bounded by the iliac crest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Anatomical landmarks make the Ventrogluteal site (comprising the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus muscles, land-marked by the greater trochanter, anterior superior iliac spine, and iliac crest) the gold standard for adult deep IM injections. It provides a deep muscle belly, consistent subcutaneous fat thickness, and is entirely free of major nerves and large blood vessels. In contrast, the traditional Dorsogluteal site carries documented hazards of accidental needle impalement of the Sciatic Nerve (causing permanent foot drop and paralysis) or superior gluteal artery injury, as well as unpredictable deposition into deep adipose tissue rather than muscle. Why the other options fail: option a says the muscle has no blood supply, which no muscle has. Option b prefers a site for its nerve supply, when the aim is to avoid nerves. Option c compares the dorsogluteal fat with the deltoid, which does not address the risk of sciatic injury.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum, causing pernicious anaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of chief cells removes pepsinogen, so protein cannot be digested and iron absorption falls, causing microcytic anaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Loss of acid allows bacterial overgrowth, which consumes vitamin B12 and causes a macrocytic anaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Loss of intrinsic factor reduces folate absorption in the jejunum, causing a macrocytic anaemia that responds to folate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The gastric mucosa contains specialized secretory glands in the fundus and body: 1) PARIETAL CELLS secrete Hydrochloric Acid (HCl, establishing an acidic pH for pepsin activation and pathogen destruction) and INTRINSIC FACTOR (IF). Intrinsic factor binds dietary Vitamin B12 (cobalamin), protecting it from digestive degradation and facilitating receptor-mediated absorption in the terminal ileum. Following total gastrectomy (or autoimmune destruction of parietal cells in pernicious anemia), intrinsic factor is completely absent, preventing B12 absorption and resulting in macrocytic megaloblastic anemia and progressive neurological demyelination (subacute combined degeneration). Chief cells secrete pepsinogen (b). Why the other options fail: option b blames chief cells and pepsinogen for calcium, which they do not regulate. Option c blames acid loss for sickle cell disease, which is inherited. Option d blames pepsinogen for haemophilia, which is a clotting factor deficiency.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bile salts from the liver emulsify fat and carry bilirubin into the gut, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea and pale stools"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bile salts from the gallbladder emulsify fat, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea, while bilirubin is excreted by the kidney"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bile salts are needed for fat digestion, but bilirubin reaches the gut in the blood and is excreted in the urine instead"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bile acids activate pancreatic lipase in the duodenum, and obstruction blocks the enzyme rather than the bile"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bile is produced continuously by hepatocytes in the liver, concentrated in the gallbladder, and released into the duodenum via the common bile duct in response to cholecystokinin (CCK). Bile salts act as biological detergents, emulsifying large dietary fat globules into microscopic micelles to provide a vast surface area for pancreatic lipase digestion. Bile also excretes conjugated bilirubin into the intestinal lumen, where normal bacterial flora metabolize it into stercobilin (giving feces their brown color). When choledocholithiasis obstructs bile outflow, fats cannot be absorbed (steatorrhea), feces lack stercobilin (clay-colored stools), and water-soluble conjugated bilirubin backs up into circulation, spilling into urine (dark amber) and skin (jaundice). Why the other options fail: option a makes the pancreas the source of bile, which the liver is. Option b sends bilirubin to the kidney when obstruction diverts it to the blood, and bile comes from the liver rather than the gallbladder. Option c treats bilirubin as blood-borne and the stools as normal, when the stools are pale from absent stercobilin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of villi removes the brush border enzymes and the surface area, so fat, carbohydrate, protein, iron and folate all malabsorb"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of villi reduces the surface area for water absorption alone, producing diarrhoea without nutrient loss"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Villous atrophy blocks the lymphatic lacteals, so only fat is malabsorbed while sugars and proteins are spared"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The villi regenerate within days after gluten withdrawal, so the absorptive defect is transient and self-limiting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum) is structurally engineered for maximum nutrient absorption. Three levels of mucosal folding—circular folds (plicae circulares), finger-like mucosal villi, and apical enterocyte microvilli (the brush border)—amplify the internal absorptive surface area hundreds of times. In celiac disease, immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by gluten ingestion destroys enterocytes, blunting villi and causing crypt hyperplasia. This drastic loss of functional surface area and brush border enzymes (lactase, peptidases) causes severe global malabsorption, chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, iron deficiency anemia, and metabolic bone disease. Villi do not produce acid (a). Why the other options fail: option a gives the villi an acid-secreting role, which belongs to the stomach. Option b makes the villi a filter for red cells. Option d says recovery is immediate and complete, when untreated coeliac disease causes continuing malabsorption.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stem cells in the basal layer divide and migrate upward, keratinise and die to form the dead, anucleate stratum corneum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keratinocytes are produced by the dermal collagen and migrate inward toward the subcutaneous fat"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The basal layer contains melanocytes but no dividing cells, so the epidermis is renewed from the dermis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The corneum retains living nuclei and divides to replace cells lost from the basal layer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The epidermis is a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium organized into 5 layers (from deep to superficial: Stratum Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum [thick skin only], and Corneum). The Stratum Basale contains single-layered stem cells undergoing active mitosis. As daughter keratinocytes are pushed superficially, they synthesize keratin intermediate filaments, produce keratohyalin and lamellar lipid granules in the granulosum (providing a hydrophobic water barrier), lose their nuclei and organelles, and flatten into the Stratum Corneum—a tough, horn-like layer of 15–30 sheets of dead keratin-packed squames that desquamates continuously (taking roughly 28–40 days from birth to shed). Option d correctly details this epidermal life cycle. Why the other options fail: option a puts living dividing cells in the corneum, which is dead. Option b moves keratinocyte production to the dermis and reverses the direction of migration. Option c denies the dividing stem cells and melanocytes that the basal layer contains.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cutaneous vasodilation increases skin blood flow and eccrine sweating drives evaporative cooling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cutaneous vasoconstriction and piloerection trap warm air against the skin and conserve heat"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sweating is suppressed and blood is shunted to the viscera, so heat is retained in the core"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eccrine glands secrete a hypertonic fluid that cools the skin by conduction rather than evaporation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The preoptic anterior hypothalamus functions as the body's primary thermoregulatory thermostat. When core temperature rises above the set point, hypothalamic autonomic signaling initiates two primary heat-loss mechanisms: 1) CUTANEOUS VASODILATION: Dermal precapillary sphincters relax, redirecting warm blood from the body core to superficial subpapillary dermal capillary loops, allowing heat to radiate, conduct, and convect into the cooler ambient environment; 2) ECCRINE SWEAT GLAND ACTIVATION: Cholinergic sympathetic nerves stimulate eccrine glands across the body to secrete watery sweat, which absorbs thermal energy (latent heat of vaporization) as it evaporates from the skin. Option b describes heat-retention responses to cold. Why the other options fail: option b constricts the skin vessels and raises the hairs, which conserves heat rather than losing it. Option c suppresses sweating and sends blood to the viscera, which is the cold response. Option d has sweat cooling by conduction and being hypertonic, when sweat is hypotonic and cools by evaporation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Full-thickness loss of the epidermis and dermis, including nerves and appendages, leaving a leathery insensate eschar"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Destruction of the epidermis and the upper dermis only, with pain preserved because the nerve endings remain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Destruction of the epidermis alone, with the dermis intact and the wound moist and painful"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Destruction of the dermis and subcutaneous fat, with the epidermis spared and the surface blistered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Full-thickness (third-degree) burns extend completely through the epidermis and the entire thickness of the dermis, often extending into the underlying hypodermis (subcutaneous tissue). Because all dermal appendages—including the dermal capillary plexus and sensory nerve endings (nociceptors, Meissner's, Merkel's, and free nerve terminals)—are completely incinerated, the wound appears leathery, waxy white, brown, or charred (eschar) and is completely insensitive to light touch and pinprick (anesthetic). However, peripheral zones of partial-thickness burn surrounding the wound remain intensely painful. Option b accurately identifies this full-thickness depth. Why the other options fail: option a confines the injury to the corneum, which would leave the skin intact and painful. Option b spares the deep dermis and keeps sensation, which the painless eschar contradicts. Option d spares the epidermis and blisters, which does not describe a leathery white wound.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fat embolism syndrome: marrow fat enters torn venous sinusoids, lodges in the pulmonary capillaries and causes petechiae"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pulmonary embolism from a deep vein thrombosis, which gives dyspnoea and hypoxia but no petechial rash"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute respiratory distress syndrome from the trauma itself, which gives bilateral infiltrates without petechiae"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sepsis from osteomyelitis at the fracture site, which gives fever and hypotension but not the rash"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Fat Embolism Syndrome (FES) is a life-threatening systemic complication occurring 24 to 72 hours following closed fractures of major long bones (especially the femur or tibia) or pelvic crush trauma. Disrupted bone marrow adipose tissue and medullary venous sinusoids allow fat globules to enter the systemic venous circulation. These fat emboli occlude pulmonary microvascular capillaries, triggering lipolysis into toxic free fatty acids that induce diffuse alveolar endothelial injury, acute lung injury (ARDS), ventilation-perfusion mismatch (severe hypoxemia), cerebral microvascular occlusion (confusion, altered mental status), and thrombocytopenic microvascular fragility (pathognomonic non-palpable petechiae over the neck, axilla, and chest). Option c correctly identifies FES. Why the other options fail: option b gives pulmonary embolism, which does not cause a petechial rash on the neck and conjunctivae. Option c gives ARDS, which produces infiltrates without the rash. Option d gives sepsis, which produces fever and hypotension but not this distribution of petechiae.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Proximal muscle contracts against the block while distal bowel is quiet, and fluid is lost into the lumen"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The bowel proximal to the block dilates and becomes atonic, so sounds are absent above and tinkling below"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The obstruction reverses peristalsis, so the bowel empties proximally and the abdomen becomes silent"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperactive sounds reflect increased secretion rather than contraction, and the fluid loss is into the peritoneum"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In mechanical small bowel obstruction (e.g., from postoperative adhesions or incarcerated hernias), physical blockage prevents the distal progression of chyme. Proximally, the smooth muscle layers (muscularis externa: circular and longitudinal fibers) mount vigorous hyper-peristaltic contractions to overcome the resistance, manifesting early as high-pitched, metallic 'tinkling' sounds and audible peristaltic rushes. Proximal intraluminal accumulation of swallowed air and digestive secretions (7–8 L/day of saliva, gastric juice, bile, and pancreatic fluids) increases wall tension, compresses venous drainage, causes bowel wall edema, and sequesters massive fluid into the third space, leading to hypovolemic shock and electrolyte collapse. Option d accurately explains this pathophysiological trajectory. Why the other options fail: option b reverses the findings, because the bowel above the block is hyperactive and the bowel below is quiet. Option c has peristalsis running backwards and the abdomen silent, when the sounds are high-pitched and rushing. Option d attributes the sounds to secretion and puts the fluid in the peritoneum, when it is lost into the lumen.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch24.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 24: INFO module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: clinical wisdom in the DIKW framework,
-- standardised nursing terminologies, DHIMS-2 for national surveillance, the
-- copy-forward safety hazard, point-of-care charting, questioning an unsafe
-- heparin order, downtime procedure, unauthorised record access, credential
-- sharing, photographing a wound on a personal phone, a CDSS allergy alert,
-- barcode workarounds, telehealth triage of chest pain, mHealth in rural Ghana,
-- Boolean search construction, a run chart of CAUTI rates, jigsaw identification
-- in a group chat, AI triage as decision support, ransomware downtime, and
-- charting without losing the client.
--
-- The keyed option ran 158 to 321 characters against 70 to 117 character
-- distractors (mean 2.47, worst 3.63), and several options were not answers at
-- all: "It is an unmonitored digital archive designed purely for long-term paper
-- recycling", "It triggers automatic electrical power surges across the hospital
-- electrical grid", "Transfer the client directly to the morgue based on the
-- predictive sepsis algorithm". All four options were rewritten as plausible
-- positions a nurse could actually hold.
--
-- After: mean 0.99, worst 1.33. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Synthesising the data with clinical knowledge to recognise septic shock and start the sepsis bundle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Recognising that the readings are abnormal and alerting the team before the end of the shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Documenting the vital signs accurately in the electronic record so the trend can be reviewed later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Checking the monitor calibration and repeating the observations to confirm the readings are real"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The DIKW framework illustrates how raw data transitions into clinical action: 1) DATA = discrete objective facts (BP 82/44, HR 124); 2) INFORMATION = data organized and given context (vital signs show hypotension, tachycardia, tachypnea, and pyrexia); 3) KNOWLEDGE = synthesizing information with nursing science (recognizing that this cluster represents Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome / severe sepsis); 4) WISDOM = using clinical knowledge, judgment, and ethics to take optimal, timely clinical action (immediately triggering the sepsis resuscitation bundle, fluids, blood cultures, and antibiotics). Options b, c, and d reflect unprocessed data or negligent avoidance. Why the other options fail: option b is data without interpretation. Option c is data transcribed onto paper, which is information at most. Option d suppresses the signal and treats the heart rate as anxiety, which is the opposite of clinical wisdom.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A shared vocabulary that lets diagnoses, interventions and outcomes be compared across systems and used for quality measurement"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A coding scheme that shortens documentation by allowing nurses to enter single numbers instead of sentences"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A billing structure that converts nursing interventions into chargeable items for reimbursement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A translation tool that converts nursing notes into medical diagnoses for the physician record"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Standardized nursing terminologies (e.g., International Classification for Nursing Practice [ICNP], SNOMED-CT, NANDA/NIC/NOC) create a consistent, structured language that represents nursing care across multidisciplinary platforms. This standardization ensures semantic interoperability between diverse electronic systems, makes nursing contributions visible in clinical documentation, allows aggregation of Big Data for nursing research, and enables evaluation of nurse-sensitive client outcomes. It does not replace assessment (a), monetize nursing inappropriately (c), or displace medical diagnoses (d). Why the other options fail: option b describes shorthand, not a terminology. Option c makes documentation a billing exercise, which is not its clinical purpose. Option d replaces medical diagnosis with slang and destroys the comparability the standard exists to create.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It aggregates facility and district data so outbreaks can be detected and coverage and resources tracked nationally"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It stores the monthly returns so that facilities can print their own reports at the end of the year"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It links the payroll and attendance records of health staff to the national human resource database"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It replaces the paper register at facility level, with the national office collecting the forms quarterly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$DHIMS-2 (District Health Information Management System) is the primary web-based platform utilized by the Ghana Health Service (GHS) and Ministry of Health for routine national health information management. Frontline facility data (from CHPS compounds, health centers, and hospitals) are aggregated into DHIMS-2 to monitor disease incidence (e.g., malaria, cholera, vaccine-preventable illnesses), track maternal-child health metrics, evaluate health system performance, and guide targeted budgetary and logistical resource distribution. It is not an ad platform (a) or an employee punch clock (d). Why the other options fail: option a treats DHIMS-2 as an advertising platform. Option b describes an archive with no analysis. Option d reduces it to attendance monitoring.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It carries forward findings that may no longer be true, masks deterioration and creates a record that misrepresents the assessment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It slows the record down and consumes storage, so the system becomes unusable over time"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It breaches the software licence, because the documentation belongs to the vendor rather than the clinician"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It duplicates the previous shift's work, so the same assessment is charged to the client twice"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cloning documentation via 'copy and paste' in the electronic health record is a high-risk practice condemned by regulatory bodies and patient safety organizations. Copying prior assessments propagates outdated physical findings (e.g., documenting surgical dressings as 'clean, dry, and intact' when an incision has actually dehisced or infected), masks acute deterioration, creates inaccurate legal records, and can be considered fraudulent billing in court. Every clinical assessment must reflect contemporaneous, independent evaluation. Server space (a) and copyright laws (b) are not the primary clinical safety concerns. Why the other options fail: option a confuses storage with safety. Option b invents a copyright claim over clinical text. Option c invents an electrical effect. The harm is that yesterday's findings are presented as today's assessment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It improves accuracy and timeliness, makes the data available to the team at once and involves the client"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It shortens the shift by removing the end-of-shift documentation period, so nurses can leave on time"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It guarantees that no medication errors can occur, because every dose is scanned at the bedside"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It removes the need to wash hands between clients, because the device is cleaned between uses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Point-of-care documentation involves recording assessments, vital signs, and medication administration in real time at the bedside. Evidence demonstrates that point-of-care charting dramatically reduces cognitive recall errors (which peak during end-of-shift batch charting), ensures real-time availability of critical lab values and vitals across the multidisciplinary team, and invites the client into the care dialogue. It should never replace eye contact or interpersonal connection (b), does not provide absolute immunity from all errors (c), and never waives hand hygiene protocols (d). Why the other options fail: option b shortens the shift, which is a convenience and not the rationale. Option c promises something no system can guarantee. Option d removes hand hygiene, which no documentation method can do.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withhold the dose, hold the order in the system, contact the prescriber and notify the pharmacist"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the dose as prescribed, because the prescriber is accountable for the order"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Change the order to 5,000 units yourself and document the correction in the progress note"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cancel the order without telling anyone, because no dose has been given"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Computerized Provider Order Entry (CPOE) significantly reduces illegibility errors but does not eliminate prescribing errors (such as wrong dose or wrong frequency selections from drop-down menus). The nurse remains the ultimate safety barrier before medication administration. When an unsafe or questionable order appears in CPOE, the nurse must withhold the drug, flag or hold the order, contact the prescriber for clarification, and consult pharmacy. Assuming computer orders are infallible (a) leads to fatal bleeding; modifying an order without authorization (c) or deleting records (d) violates pharmacy and nursing practice acts. Why the other options fail: option b assumes the order is correct because it is electronic, which is exactly the false assumption CPOE was meant to end. Option c changes the order without the prescriber, which is a second error. Option d deletes the record, which destroys the evidence of the error.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Activate the downtime protocol, move to paper charts and shadow records, and designate staff to track orders and admissions"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop giving medicines and stop monitoring vital signs until the servers are restored"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the nurses to record observations and doses on their phones and enter them when the system returns"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discharge the stable clients so the unit has fewer records to reconstruct when the system returns"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$When electronic health systems experience scheduled or unscheduled downtime, clinical operations must maintain uninterrupted patient safety through an established downtime policy and disaster recovery protocol. The charge nurse activates downtime standard operating procedures: accessing downtime emergency paper binders, deploying paper medication administration records (MARs) and progress notes, tracking client movement manually, and utilizing runner messengers for urgent laboratory and pharmacy orders. Ceasing care (b) causes preventable deaths; discharging patients recklessly (a) or relying on human memory (d) causes catastrophic medical errors. Why the other options fail: option b stops care because the computer is down, which harms patients. Option c records on personal phones, which is not an approved downtime record. Option d discharges clients to reduce paperwork, which is not a clinical decision.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An unlawful breach of role-based access and need-to-know, subject to discipline and regulatory reporting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A breach only if the records were printed or shared on social media, and not otherwise"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A minor policy deviation that carries no institutional or regulatory liability"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An acceptable practice, because the nurse knows the clients personally and meant no harm"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Access to electronic protected health information (ePHI) is legally restricted by Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and the statutory 'need-to-know' principle under data privacy statutes (such as the Ghana Data Protection Act 2012 [Act 843], HIPAA, and GDPR). Healthcare personnel may only open the records of clients directly assigned to their clinical care. Snooping into the records of neighbors, acquaintances, or public figures without clinical justification is an intentional privacy violation resulting in disciplinary action, termination, and professional council investigation. Why the other options fail: option b makes access lawful as long as nothing is printed. Option c makes it a mistake with no consequence, when the access was deliberate. Option d rewards curiosity about neighbours, which is exactly what access controls exist to prevent.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Decline, log off the workstation, and explain that sharing credentials breaks accountability and policy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Log in for the physician but stay at the keyboard while the order is entered"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Write the username and password on a note beside the workstation for the team to use"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Let the physician enter the order under the nurse's name and countersign it themselves afterwards"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In healthcare cybersecurity and electronic documentation law, an individual's unique login credentials (username and password) represent their legal electronic signature. Sharing credentials compromises the entire audit trail, breaches hospital security policies, and legally binds the nurse to any order, medication, or charting entered under their account. The nurse must refuse credential sharing, log out of the computer, and direct the prescriber to IT support or emergency phone/verbal order protocols. Handing over sessions (b) or pasting passwords (c) are severe security violations. Why the other options fail: option b leaves the session open and the physician unverified. Option c publishes credentials, which is worse than sharing them once. Option d attributes the order to the wrong clinician, which is the harm the nurse is avoiding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Personal devices and consumer messaging apps are not encrypted or approved, so the image breaches privacy law and policy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The image is likely to be of poor quality, so the second opinion would be unreliable"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The photograph is stored on a device the hospital cannot back up, so it may be lost before review"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Second opinions are not permitted for a client who has not been formally transferred to another facility"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Capturing client clinical images on personal, unencrypted mobile devices and transmitting them across consumer messaging platforms violates client privacy rights, institutional BYOD (Bring Your Own Device) policies, and statutory health data protection laws. Digital clinical photography requires: 1) explicit informed client consent; 2) utilization of enterprise-secured, encrypted, institutional hardware and HIPAA/Data Protection Act-compliant platforms; and 3) direct storage into the client's official medical record. Camera resolution (a) and battery usage (c) are irrelevant to data privacy laws. Why the other options fail: option b addresses image quality rather than privacy. Option c addresses backup rather than consent or encryption. Option d overstates the rule: second opinions are permitted, they simply have to travel by a lawful route.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Read the alert, withhold the ampicillin, recheck the allergy record and ask the prescriber for a non-penicillin drug"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Override the alert after checking that the dose lies within the normal range for the client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the ampicillin with an antihistamine premedication, so any reaction is blunted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dismiss the alert because documented allergies are often out of date and the reaction is usually mild"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) provide real-time, evidence-based alerts at the point of care to intercept adverse drug events. High-tier critical alerts (such as severe drug-allergy interactions) represent life-threatening safety hazards. The nurse must not succumb to 'alert fatigue' (mindlessly overriding pop-ups). The nurse must review the alert, withhold the drug, verify the documented penicillin allergy, and notify the prescriber to order a safe therapeutic alternative. Automatic overrides (a) cause fatal anaphylaxis; antacids (d) do not prevent immune-mediated anaphylactic shock. Why the other options fail: option b overrides the alert without resolving the allergy. Option c premedicates and gives the drug anyway. Option d dismisses a documented anaphylaxis as stale, which is not a judgement the nurse can make at the keyboard.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A dangerous workaround that bypasses bedside patient identification and raises the risk of a wrong-patient error"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An efficient adaptation to a damaged wristband, provided the copied barcode is legible and matches the client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A software interoperability problem, since the scanner cannot read a wrinkled label, and not a nursing practice issue"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A benign technical malfunction with no implication for patient safety, because the nurse knows the client"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A 'workaround' is an informal bypass of an established safety system created by staff to cope with workflow obstacles or save time. Photocopying client barcodes onto carts, badges, or door frames completely dismantles the core safety mechanism of BCMA—which is verifying the Right Patient at the bedside simultaneously with the Right Drug. If a barcode is unreadable, the nurse must reprint and apply a new wristband immediately. Labeling workarounds as innovations (a) or benign (c) ignores root causes of severe wrong-patient medication administration fatalities. Why the other options fail: option b calls the shortcut efficient, which is how workarounds spread. Option c blames the scanner rather than the practice it was substituted for. Option d says the risk is nil because the nurse knows the client, which is precisely the assumption that produces wrong-patient errors.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Recognise possible ACS, tell the client to call emergency services, confirm the exact location, stay on the line and advise aspirin if safe"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Arrange an elective appointment with the client's physician and advise rest until then, since the consultation is virtual"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the client to take an antacid and call back if the pain has not settled within the hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "End the call and document it, because a telehealth service cannot take responsibility for an emergency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Telehealth nurses must uphold the same standards of clinical judgment and care as bedside clinicians. When acute life-threatening symptoms (e.g., acute myocardial infarction) are identified during virtual triage, the nurse must: 1) verify the client's physical location/address immediately; 2) coordinate emergency EMS dispatch; 3) provide interim emergency guidance (rest, chew aspirin if protocol allows and no contraindications); and 4) stay on the connection until emergency responders arrive. Delaying care (b), recommending physical exertion (c), or hanging up (d) constitutes gross telehealth malpractice. Why the other options fail: option b delays care that must happen in minutes. Option c treats chest pain with an antacid and a callback. Option d abandons the client, when the nurse is the only clinician present.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It allows offline data capture and decision support on the tablet, with records synchronised when connectivity returns"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It replaces paper registers entirely, because tablets work in every setting and need no network at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It moves the burden of record keeping to the mother, who reports her own visits through the application"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It provides real-time video consultation with specialists, which is the only way to close the rural gap"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$mHealth (mobile health) applications on portable smartphones and tablets revolutionize rural primary health care (such as CHPS compounds) by supporting: 1) offline functionality with store-and-forward data synchronization; 2) algorithmic point-of-care guidance for early recognition of obstetric and neonatal danger signs; 3) automated SMS text reminders for antenatal care and infant vaccinations; and 4) real-time referral communication with district hospitals. It enhances frontline human care rather than replacing clinicians with robots (a) or imposing unaffordable hardware burdens (c). Why the other options fail: option b overstates it, because tablets do need power, maintenance and a network eventually. Option c shifts the record onto the mother. Option d makes video the only solution, when offline capture and decision support are the functions described.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use Boolean AND to combine every synonym of the topic, then truncate each term to capture variants"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use NOT to exclude irrelevant populations, so the search returns only postoperative delirium studies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use OR within each concept and AND between concepts, so that each concept is captured without losing studies"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use phrase searching in quotation marks alone, which returns the most precise set with no Boolean operators"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) structure bibliographic database queries: 'OR' broadens a search by including synonyms within a concept (e.g., 'aged OR elderly'); 'AND' narrows the search by requiring that all distinct concepts must be present in the retrieved records. Grouping synonyms in parentheses with OR and connecting the concept sets with AND produces targeted, high-yield results. Using 'NOT' inappropriately (a) excludes the target population; using 'OR' exclusively (b) returns millions of irrelevant records; natural language phrases (d) fail in structured indexing systems. Why the other options fail: option a ANDs every synonym, which returns almost nothing. Option b uses NOT on the very population the question is about. Option d writes a sentence rather than a query and uses no Boolean logic at all.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Form an interprofessional improvement team, analyse insertion and dwell-time data, and trial a nurse-driven removal reminder"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withdraw the metric from the dashboard until the rate has returned to the benchmark on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Introduce a policy restricting catheter use and audit compliance monthly, without examining the existing data"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dock the pay of the night shift nurses until the rate falls below the national benchmark"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clinical dashboards aggregate EHR data into visual indicators (run charts, control charts) to monitor nurse-sensitive indicators (e.g., CAUTI, CLABSI, pressure injuries, falls). When run charts demonstrate non-random negative trends (e.g., 6 consecutive points above benchmark), informatics-guided leadership investigates the underlying data (e.g., catheter dwell times), implements evidence-based interventions (such as EHR automated reminders for early catheter removal or nurse-driven discontinuation protocols), and tracks post-intervention metrics. Deleting data (a) is fraudulent; punitive blaming (b) destroys safety culture. Why the other options fail: option b hides the metric, which removes the signal without changing the rate. Option c audits a policy without examining the data. Option d punishes staff before investigating, which is not an improvement method.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A confidentiality breach by jigsaw identification; private chats are not exempt and the nurse faces discipline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acceptable, because the messages are end-to-end encrypted and the name was left out"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acceptable educational discussion, because no full name, date of birth or address was included"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A protected disclosure, because the nurse was raising a clinical concern in good faith"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Confidentiality breaches frequently occur through 'jigsaw identification'—combining indirect identifiers (clinical photograph, specific unit, room number, tribal background, rare congenital defect) that allow community members or hospital staff to readily identify the individual. Furthermore, using consumer messaging apps (even with encryption) to transmit protected patient health data without consent violates national data privacy statutes and professional codes. Omitting names (c) does not prevent identification; regulatory bodies routinely revoke nursing licenses for social media and messaging app disclosures. Why the other options fail: option b confuses encryption in transit with lawful use. Option c assumes that omitting the name is sufficient, when the room, the defect and the background identify the child. Option d is not a protected disclosure, because the nurse was seeking advice rather than reporting wrongdoing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use the alert as decision support, assess the client at the bedside, check the labs and escalate as the findings warrant"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ignore the alert, because an algorithm cannot interpret borderline vital signs in an older adult"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Follow the alert without assessment, because the algorithm has been validated on a large dataset"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Repeat the observations until the algorithm withdraws the alert, then continue routine care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Artificial Intelligence (AI) and machine learning models in healthcare serve as assistive decision support, NOT replacements for professional clinical judgment. Predictive models identify subtle patterns in massive EHR datasets that human clinicians might overlook (e.g., early sepsis trajectories). The nurse must validate AI alerts by performing an in-person physical assessment, synthesizing clinical context, and verifying objective diagnostic data. Total dismissal of AI (a) misses early warning opportunities; blind subservience to AI (c) risks algorithmic bias and error. Why the other options fail: option b discards a validated signal. Option c follows the alert without assessment, which is automation bias. Option d treats the alert as noise to be waited out rather than a finding to be checked.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Move to manual downtime procedures, protect patients, switch to paper, disconnect the network and notify incident command"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pay the ransom from petty cash so that monitoring is restored as quickly as possible"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transfer every inpatient to another facility and close the unit until the systems are restored"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep charting electronically on the ward computers, since the encryption affects only the servers"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A ransomware cyberattack represents a combined IT disaster and clinical emergency. The nursing team's absolute priority is maintaining client physical safety by transitioning to full manual paper-based business continuity procedures (manual vitals, paper MARs, independent medication double-checks). Concurrently, nursing leadership coordinates with hospital incident command and disconnects devices from network ports to prevent malware propagation. Paying ransoms (a) is a law enforcement decision; abandoning clients (b) or vigilante counter-hacking (d) are dangerous and illegal. Why the other options fail: option b pays criminals from petty cash, which funds further attacks and restores nothing quickly. Option c moves every patient out of a hospital that can still function on paper. Option d keeps using the compromised network.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Position the mobile computer so eye contact is kept, explain what is being entered and involve the client in the plan"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Chart at the bedside without speaking, so the record is finished before the conversation begins"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complete the documentation after the shift from memory, so the whole shift is spent with clients"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to enter their own history into the tablet while the nurse attends to other tasks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Technology must serve the patient, not replace the human therapeutic relationship. Nurses can humanize technology by: 1) positioning the computer screen so it does not form a physical barrier between nurse and client; 2) maintaining regular eye contact and verbal communication; 3) explaining what is being documented ('I am entering your blood pressure reading into your electronic chart now'); and 4) actively involving the client in reviewing goals, lab trends, and discharge plans. Refusing to document (a) is illegal negligence; hiding behind computers (b) destroys the therapeutic alliance. Why the other options fail: option b documents without speaking, which is the complaint the nurse made. Option c leaves documentation to memory, which produces inaccuracy. Option d delegates the history, which is not assessment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch25.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 25: RSCH module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: research against quality improvement,
-- independent and dependent variables, a directional hypothesis, the three
-- properties of a true trial, case-control design, data saturation, member
-- checking, stratified random sampling, Cronbach alpha, inter-rater reliability,
-- parental permission with child assent, the three forms of research misconduct,
-- the median in skewed data, incidence against prevalence, a type I error, an
-- odds ratio whose confidence interval excludes 1.0, the independent samples
-- t-test, sensitivity and specificity, the five A steps of evidence-based
-- practice, and closing the audit loop.
--
-- The keyed option ran 138 to 243 characters against 30 to 140 character
-- distractors (mean 1.94, worst 5.22), and many options were category errors
-- rather than plausible alternatives: "Bribery, extortion, and false imprisonment",
-- "Libel, slander, and defamation", "The study has an 81% mortality rate among ICU
-- clients". Options were rewritten as the misconceptions a student actually holds,
-- including the reversal of incidence and prevalence and the confusion of
-- reliability with validity.
--
-- After: mean 1.01, worst 1.30. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Research tests hypotheses to produce generalisable knowledge; QI improves processes and outcomes within one facility"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Research is always local and unpublished, while QI is always multicentre and generalisable"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Research needs board review only when it is funded, while QI never requires ethical oversight"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Research and QI differ only in name, and the same methods and approvals apply to both"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nursing research is a systematic scientific inquiry designed to generate new generalizable knowledge, discover relationships, or test biological/behavioral hypotheses that can be applied beyond the local institution. Quality Improvement (QI), by contrast, uses established evidence and systematic data-guided activities to bring about immediate, practical improvements in local healthcare delivery processes and outcomes within a specific clinical setting. Research typically requires formal IRB ethical oversight (b); research is intended for dissemination (c); and they have distinct methodologies and governance (d). Why the other options fail: option b reverses the ethical requirement, because both research and QI can need review depending on risk. Option c makes publication the distinction, which is false. Option d collapses a real methodological and regulatory difference into a matter of naming.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The independent variable is hospital discharge status; the dependent variable is room temperature"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The independent variable is the 10-minute warm foot bath; the dependent variable is sleep quality"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The independent variable is sleep quality; the dependent variable is the warm foot bath"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The independent variable is client age; the dependent variable is the nurse's years of experience"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In scientific research, the Independent Variable (IV) is the experimental intervention, treatment, or condition manipulated by the researcher (the presumed cause—here, the 10-minute warm foot bath). The Dependent Variable (DV) is the outcome or response measured to determine the effect of the intervention (the presumed effect—here, the client's sleep quality). Option c reverses the cause and effect; options a and d identify unrelated demographic or confounding factors. Why the other options fail: option a names variables that the study does not manipulate or measure. Option c reverses independent and dependent. Option d names the client's age, which is not the intervention, and the nurse's experience, which is not an outcome.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Null hypothesis, predicting no difference between the two groups"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Non-directional hypothesis, predicting a difference without stating which group will do better"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Directional research hypothesis, predicting that one named group will score lower than the other"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Complex qualitative hypothesis, drawn from themes rather than a measured outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A directional research hypothesis explicitly predicts the direction of the expected relationship or difference between groups (e.g., specifying that group A will have 'lower anxiety' or 'higher scores' than group B). A non-directional hypothesis (b) predicts a difference exists without specifying which group will be higher or lower (e.g., 'there will be a difference in anxiety scores'). A null hypothesis (a) states that no relationship or difference exists between the variables (e.g., 'there will be no difference in anxiety scores'). Qualitative studies (d) explore phenomena rather than testing statistical hypotheses. Why the other options fail: option a predicts no difference, which this hypothesis does not. Option b is non-directional, but this hypothesis names which group will do better. Option d is not a qualitative hypothesis, because it specifies groups and a measured outcome.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Manipulation of the independent variable, a control group and random allocation to the arms"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pre-test and post-test measurement, a convenience sample and blinding of the statistician only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Randomisation and a sample size calculation, because manipulation is implied by the intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Manipulation and control alone, because randomisation is optional if the sample is large"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A true experimental design or Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) requires three essential scientific pillars: 1) MANIPULATION: The researcher actively introduces an intervention (the independent variable) to some participants; 2) CONTROL: The researcher maintains a control or comparison group that does not receive the experimental treatment (e.g., placebo, standard care); 3) RANDOMIZATION: Participants are randomly assigned to groups, ensuring every subject has an equal probability of assignment and balancing confounding baseline characteristics. Option d correctly identifies all three pillars. Why the other options fail: option a omits randomisation, which is what distinguishes a true trial from a quasi-experiment. Option b describes a weak observational design. Option c lists desirable features but not the three defining properties.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Case-control study, comparing past exposures in people with and without the outcome"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prospective cohort, following exposed and unexposed people forward for the outcome"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cross-sectional survey, measuring exposure and outcome at the same time in one sample"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retrospective cohort, following a defined exposed group back through the records for the outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A case-control study is a retrospective observational design that begins with the outcome: individuals who already have the disease or condition (cases) are matched with individuals who do not (controls), and researchers look back in time (retrospectively) to determine differences in prior exposure to risk factors (calculating an Odds Ratio). A prospective cohort study (b) starts with exposed and unexposed groups free of disease and follows them forward in time. A cross-sectional study (c) measures exposure and outcome simultaneously at a single point in time. An RCT (d) involves active experimental randomization. Why the other options fail: option b follows people forward from exposure, which is the opposite direction. Option c measures exposure and outcome at one time. Option d starts from exposure, not from the outcome.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A statistical power calculation of 80%, which sets the number of participants needed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Data saturation, the point at which further interviews yield no new themes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A type II statistical error, which arises when the sample is too small to detect an effect"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cronbach alpha of 0.88, which shows the interview guide is internally consistent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In qualitative research, sample size is not determined by pre-study statistical mathematical formulas (power analysis). Instead, sampling continues until 'Data Saturation' (or informational redundancy) is achieved—the point at which gathering additional participant data no longer reveals new perspectives, properties, or themes. Statistical power (a), Type II errors (c), and Cronbach's alpha (d) are quantitative psychometric and inferential statistical concepts irrelevant to qualitative sampling closure. Why the other options fail: option a is a quantitative sample-size calculation. Option c is a statistical error about failing to detect an effect. Option d is a measure of internal consistency, not of thematic exhaustion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Double-blind randomisation of transcripts to two coders, so neither knows the hypothesis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Member checking, returning transcripts and themes to participants to confirm the findings"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cluster sampling of participants, so that each adolescent's account is weighted equally"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prolonged engagement in the field, so that the researcher becomes a familiar presence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Member checking (also known as participant validation) is a core method used in qualitative research to establish Credibility (the qualitative parallel to internal validity). The researcher shares transcripts, analytic categories, or preliminary thematic findings with study participants to allow them to confirm whether the interpretations accurately and authentically represent their true lived realities. Randomization (a), cluster sampling (b), and regression (d) belong exclusively to quantitative statistical methods. Why the other options fail: option a blinds coders, which guards against bias but does not return findings to participants. Option c changes how participants are sampled. Option d extends time in the field, which is a different technique.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stratified random sampling, with the roster divided into tiers and participants drawn at random from each"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Quota sampling, with the researcher filling each regional tier to a set number by convenience"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cluster sampling, with whole facilities selected at random and every nurse in them surveyed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Purposive sampling, with key informants chosen from each region and type of hospital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Stratified random sampling is a probability sampling method in which the target population is first divided into mutually exclusive, homogeneous subgroups or strata (e.g., geographical regions, hospital tiers, clinical cadres), and random samples are drawn proportionally from each stratum. This ensures that smaller or minority sub-populations are adequately and representatively reflected in the final sample, minimizing sampling error. Snowball (a), convenience (b), and purposive (c) are non-probability sampling methods prone to severe selection bias. Why the other options fail: option b fills tiers by convenience rather than at random. Option c samples whole facilities rather than individuals within tiers. Option d selects informants deliberately rather than randomly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It shows strong internal consistency, so the items correlate and measure the same construct"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It shows the scale is invalid, because alpha must be below 0.05 to be significant"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It shows an unacceptable error rate of 88%, so the tool needs revision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It shows high content validity, because a high alpha means the items cover the construct"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cronbach's alpha ($\alpha$) is a measure of internal consistency reliability for multi-item psychometric scales, evaluating the degree to which items that propose to measure the same general construct produce similar scores. A Cronbach's alpha of 0.70 to 0.79 is considered acceptable, 0.80 to 0.89 is good/strong, and $\ge 0.90$ is excellent. An alpha of 0.88 confirms strong internal reliability. It is not a p-value where $< 0.05$ is sought (b); it does not denote an error rate (c); and reliability does not automatically guarantee content validity (d), which evaluates whether items cover the full conceptual domain. Why the other options fail: option b applies a p-value threshold to a reliability coefficient. Option c misreads 0.88 as an error rate. Option d treats internal consistency as evidence of content coverage, which it is not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inter-rater reliability, because two raters scoring the same clients are being compared"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Test-retest reliability, because the same clients are assessed on two occasions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Predictive validity, because the Braden score forecasts who will develop a pressure injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Internal consistency, because the six Braden subscales are summed into one score"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Inter-rater (or inter-observer) reliability measures the degree of consistency or agreement between two or more independent raters or observers scoring the same subjects using the same instrument at the same point in time (often quantified by Cohen's kappa or intra-class correlation). High inter-rater reliability ensures that assessment scores reflect true client characteristics rather than idiosyncratic examiner biases. Predictive validity (a) examines whether a score predicts future events; construct validity (c) tests whether an instrument measures a theoretical trait; face validity (d) is superficial inspection. Why the other options fail: option b compares one rater on two occasions, which is test-retest. Option c asks whether the score predicts an outcome. Option d asks whether the items hang together, when the question is whether two raters agree.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Obtain parental permission and the child's developmentally appropriate assent, respecting a refusal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Obtain parental permission alone, because the child is legally unable to assent before age 18"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obtain the child's assent alone, because the child is the participant and the parent cannot consent"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain permission from the school head teacher, who acts in loco parentis during school hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In pediatric research ethics, minors lack legal competence to provide legal informed consent. Ethical standards mandate obtaining: 1) legal informed consent (permission) from the parent(s) or legal guardian; and 2) affirmative, developmentally appropriate 'Assent' from the child (typically children aged 7 and older who possess cognitive capability). If a child refuses to assent to a non-therapeutic research protocol, their dissent must be respected and cannot be overridden by parents or teachers. Community permission (a) complements but never replaces individual family consent. Why the other options fail: option b takes parental permission alone, which omits the child's assent. Option c inverts the requirement by taking assent alone. Option d delegates consent to a teacher, who cannot give it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fabrication, falsification and plagiarism: making up data, altering data, and using text without attribution"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fabrication, falsification and duplication, where duplication is publishing the same study twice"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Plagiarism, self-plagiarism and salami slicing, where the same data are split across papers"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Misconduct, negligence and malpractice, which are the three legal categories of research fraud"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The internationally recognized federal and academic definition of Scientific Research Misconduct encompasses 'FFP': 1) FABRICATION: Making up data or results and recording or reporting them as real; 2) FALSIFICATION: Manipulating research materials, equipment, or processes, or changing/omitting data such that research is not accurately represented; and 3) PLAGIARISM: Appropriating another person's ideas, processes, results, or words without giving appropriate credit. Options a, b, and c refer to clinical torts, crimes, or communications rather than scientific research misconduct. Why the other options fail: option b substitutes duplication for plagiarism, but the three named acts are fabrication, falsification and plagiarism. Option c names three related publishing practices, none of which is the invented data. Option d uses legal categories rather than the research integrity categories.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The median, because it is resistant to extreme outliers and reflects the midpoint of a skewed distribution"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The mean, because the arithmetic average uses every value and is always the best summary"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The mode, because the most frequent value is the only valid average for skewed data"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The standard deviation, because it describes the spread around the arithmetic centre"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When continuous data are normally distributed (bell-shaped symmetrical curve), the Mean and standard deviation are the preferred parametric measures. However, when data are skewed (asymmetrical with extreme outliers, such as length of stay or healthcare costs), the arithmetic Mean is heavily distorted by extreme values. The Median (the 50th percentile) is non-parametric and resistant to outliers, making it the most accurate measure of central tendency for skewed datasets. The standard deviation (d) is a measure of dispersion, not central tendency. Why the other options fail: option b is pulled upward by the 90-day outliers, which is the problem. Option c ignores all but the most frequent value. Option d measures spread rather than centre.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "250 is the incidence and 1,200 the prevalence, because incidence counts new cases and prevalence all existing cases"}, {"id": "b", "text": "250 is the prevalence and 1,200 the incidence, because prevalence counts new cases each year"}, {"id": "c", "text": "250 is the incidence rate and 1,200 the cumulative incidence over the same period"}, {"id": "d", "text": "250 is the case fatality rate and 1,200 the attack rate among contacts of the cases"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Incidence and Prevalence are fundamental epidemiological metrics: INCIDENCE refers to the number of NEW cases of a disease that develop in a population at risk during a specified time interval (here, 250 new TB cases in 2025). PREVALENCE refers to the TOTAL number of all existing cases (both new and pre-existing) in the population at a given time point or period (here, 1,200 total cases). Option c reverses these definitions; options a and d identify unrelated mortality or immunological parameters. Why the other options fail: option b reverses incidence and prevalence. Option c invents a cumulative measure for 1,200, which is the number under treatment at one time. Option d uses mortality and attack rates, which describe deaths and contacts.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A type I error, rejecting a true null hypothesis and declaring an inert drug effective"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A type II error, failing to detect a real effect because the sample was too small"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A sampling error, because the sample was not representative of the population"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A confounding error, because a third variable produced the apparent effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In hypothesis testing: A TYPE I ERROR (alpha error, false positive) occurs when the researcher rejects the null hypothesis when the null hypothesis is actually true in reality (concluding that a treatment works when it is actually ineffective). A TYPE II ERROR (beta error, false negative) occurs when the researcher fails to reject the null hypothesis when it is actually false (concluding that a treatment does not work when it actually does, often due to inadequate sample size/power). Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error. Why the other options fail: option b is the error of missing a real effect, which did not happen here. Option c is about representativeness rather than inference. Option d invokes confounding, which the question excludes by stating the drug is inert.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The interval excludes 1.0, so the reduction in odds is statistically significant and the rinse is protective"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The interval is wide, so the finding is inconclusive until a much larger trial is completed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The point estimate of 0.62 lies close to 1.0, so the effect is clinically negligible"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An odds ratio below 1.0 means the rinse increases the odds of pneumonia rather than reducing them"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$For ratio statistics such as Odds Ratios (OR) and Relative Risk (RR), the null value (indicating no difference between groups) is 1.0. If the 95% Confidence Interval does NOT include 1.0, the finding is statistically significant at $p < 0.05$. An OR of 0.62 with a 95% CI of [0.48 to 0.81] means that clients receiving chlorhexidine have 38% lower odds of developing pneumonia, and because the upper bound (0.81) is below 1.0, the protective effect is statistically significant. If the CI crossed 1.0 (e.g., [0.75 to 1.25]), it would not be statistically significant. Why the other options fail: option b asks for a larger trial although the interval already excludes 1.0. Option c judges clinical importance from how close the point estimate lies to 1.0. Option d reverses the direction of an odds ratio below 1.0.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An independent samples t-test, which compares two independent group means"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A chi-square test of independence, which compares two categorical variables"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A Kruskal-Wallis test, which compares three or more independent group medians"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A Kaplan-Meier analysis, which compares time to an event between groups"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An Independent Samples Student's t-test is the standard parametric inferential statistical test used to determine whether there is a statistically significant difference between the means of two independent groups on a continuous, normally distributed dependent variable (e.g., mean blood pressure in mmHg). A Chi-square test (b) compares categorical proportions between groups. Kruskal-Wallis (c) is non-parametric for 3 or more groups. Kaplan-Meier (d) analyzes time-to-event survival data. Why the other options fail: option b compares categorical variables. Option c compares medians across three or more groups. Option d compares time to an event.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sensitivity 96% and specificity 92%, because 96 of 100 cases and 92 of 100 non-cases were correctly classified"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sensitivity 92% and specificity 96%, because the healthy clients supply the true positive rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sensitivity 96% and specificity 92%, and the positive predictive value must equal the sensitivity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sensitivity 4% and specificity 8%, because these are the proportions of false results in each group"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$SENSITIVITY is the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals who HAVE the disease (True Positive Rate = True Positives / Total Diseased = 96 / 100 = 96%). SPECIFICITY is the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals who DO NOT HAVE the disease (True Negative Rate = True Negatives / Total Non-diseased = 92 / 100 = 92%). High sensitivity rules OUT disease when negative (SnNOut); high specificity rules IN disease when positive (SpPIn). Option b correctly calculates both metrics. Why the other options fail: option b exchanges the two measures, giving the non-case rate to sensitivity. Option c adds a predictive value that cannot be derived from these numbers. Option d reports the false rates rather than the true rates.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask, acquire, appraise, apply and assess, in that order, beginning with a PICO question"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acquire, appraise, apply, assess and ask, with the question formed after the search"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Assess, apply, appraise, acquire and ask, working backwards from the outcome"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask, appraise, acquire, apply and assess, with appraisal done before the search"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The evidence-based practice process follows the classic 5 A's sequential framework: 1) ASK: Convert clinical need into a structured, answerable clinical inquiry (PICO); 2) ACQUIRE: Efficiently search biomedical databases for the best available evidence; 3) APPRAISE: Critically evaluate the evidence for validity, reliability, and clinical applicability; 4) APPLY: Integrate critical evidence with clinical expertise, patient values/circumstances, and available resources; 5) ASSESS: Evaluate the outcomes of the practice change and identify areas for ongoing improvement. Why the other options fail: option a begins with the search before the question. Option b puts appraisal before the question. Option d appraises before acquiring the evidence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Closing the audit loop, because the re-audit shows the changes achieved the standard"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The first audit, because the baseline measurement is what starts the cycle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The research phase, because a re-audit is equivalent to a controlled trial"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The reporting phase, because the loop closes when the results are published"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A clinical audit is incomplete without 'closing the audit loop'. The complete audit cycle consists of: 1) setting standards; 2) measuring baseline practice; 3) comparing practice against standards; 4) implementing targeted changes; and 5) re-auditing practice. The re-audit verifies whether implemented interventions achieved sustained compliance with standards, closing the loop and establishing clinical governance. Audits are quality improvement activities, not punitive misconduct trials (a) or falsifications (b). Why the other options fail: option b describes the first audit, which happened before the changes. Option c equates a re-audit with a trial, which it is not. Option d closes the loop on publication rather than on measured improvement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch26.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 26: BEH module distractor, rationale and length repair (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: the biopsychosocial model, the sick role,
-- Kleinman's explanatory model, Piaget's concrete operational stage, the protest
-- phase of separation anxiety, identity against role confusion, ego integrity,
-- Selye's exhaustion stage, displacement, regression, Aguilera's balancing
-- factors, the social determinants of health, a licorice-herb interaction in
-- heart failure, precontemplation, the health belief model, motivational
-- interviewing, the PHQ-9, direct suicide assessment, culturally congruent
-- psychosis care, and panic-level anxiety.
--
-- The keyed option ran 180 to 272 characters against 66 to 111 character
-- distractors (mean 1.92, worst 4.02), and the wrong options were often
-- caricatures: "Snatch the herbal tea bottle away angrily and pour it down the
-- toilet", "Shout loudly into the client's ear to snap them out of the hysterical
-- episode", "Arrive with armed police, insult the spiritual elders, and forcibly
-- drag the client away". They were replaced with the misconceptions students
-- actually hold, such as treating a structural barrier as a reminder problem or
-- waiting for the client to raise suicide.
--
-- After: mean 1.00, worst 1.36. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explore how the disease, psychological stressors and social circumstances interact to shape the client's attendance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Focus on beta-cell failure and the HbA1c, because the metabolic problem is what the treatment targets"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat the missed appointments as a competence problem and arrange closer supervision at each visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add an antidepressant first, because depression is the most common reason for missing appointments"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$George Engel's Biopsychosocial Model posits that health, illness, and healthcare delivery cannot be fully understood by biological pathology alone. It requires examining the dynamic interplay of biological factors (glycemic control, genetics), psychological components (illness perceptions, coping styles, emotional distress, self-efficacy), and social determinants (socioeconomic status, family dynamics, work schedule, cultural beliefs). Narrow biomedical reductionism (b) ignores the real-world behavioral drivers of chronic disease; shaming the client (c) and unwarranted neuroimaging (d) are non-therapeutic. Why the other options fail: option b stays inside the biomedical lens the question asks the nurse to move beyond. Option c replaces assessment with judgement. Option d adds a treatment for a problem the assessment has not yet established.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The right to be excused from work indefinitely, with no obligation to seek treatment or return to normal roles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The right to be temporarily excused from normal roles, with the obligation to see illness as undesirable and to cooperate with treatment to get well"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The right to decide the treatment plan alone, with the obligation to refuse any advice offered by clinicians"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The right to be cared for without question, with the obligation to remain dependent on the family until cured"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Talcott Parsons' functionalist sociological model describes the 'Sick Role' as carrying two institutionalized rights and two core obligations: Rights: 1) The sick person is exempt from normal social and work responsibilities (proportional to illness severity); 2) The sick person is not blamed or held morally responsible for their condition. Obligations: 1) The person must define sickness as undesirable and want to get well; 2) The person must seek competent professional medical help and cooperate with the treatment regimen. Option b correctly pairs these rights and obligations. Why the other options fail: option a makes the exemption permanent and removes the obligation. Option c gives the client the clinical decisions alone. Option d keeps the client dependent indefinitely, which the sick role does not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Elicit the client's own explanation of the illness, then negotiate a plan that respects it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain that the biomedical model is the only evidence-based one, and ask the client to accept the prescription first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refer the client to a cultural broker who can persuade them that the spiritual explanation is wrong"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Adjust the prescription to fit the client's belief, and give a lower dose so the drug does not cool the body too quickly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Arthur Kleinman's Explanatory Model framework encourages clinicians to explore how clients and families understand their sickness: etiology, timing, pathophysiology, severity, and acceptable treatment. Eliciting the client's perspective fosters trust and cultural humility, enabling the nurse to bridge clinical recommendations with the client's cultural framework (negotiating safe medical adherence while respecting spiritual practices that do not cause harm). Ridiculing the client (a) or issuing ultimatums (b) causes immediate care disengagement. Endorsing abandonment of evidence-based therapy (d) is dangerous malpractice. Why the other options fail: option b dismisses the belief and ends the negotiation before it starts. Option c contradicts the belief rather than exploring it. Option d accepts the belief so completely that it abandons effective treatment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Concrete operational stage: use models and show the plaster so the child can see and touch what will happen"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Preoperational stage: explain in simple stories and use play to prepare the child for the cast"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Formal operational stage: explain the mechanics of the reduction in abstract terms, because the child can reason logically"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sensorimotor stage: prepare the child through touch and movement, since reasoning is not yet developed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Children aged 7 to 11 years are in Piaget's Concrete Operational stage of cognitive development. They can perform logical operations on tangible, concrete objects and understand cause-and-effect, conservation, and physical realities, but they struggle with purely abstract hypotheses. Explaining procedures with tangible models, letting them handle plaster casting gauze, and explaining how the cast holds the bone straight matches their cognitive capabilities. Sensorimotor (a) is ages 0–2; Preoperational (c) is ages 2–7 (prone to magical thinking and guilt); Formal operational (b) begins around age 12 with abstract deductive reasoning. Why the other options fail: option b describes the preoperational child, who is younger. Option c describes formal operations, which a 9-year-old has not reached, and abstract mechanics would not help. Option d describes infancy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Protest, with crying, screaming and rejecting the nurse's comfort"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Despair, with crying that has stopped and a withdrawn, apathetic child"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Detachment, with the child appearing cheerful and indifferent to the parent's return"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autistic isolation, with withdrawal that predates the separation rather than following it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$John Bowlby and James Robertson described three sequential stages of separation anxiety in young children (especially 6 to 30 months): 1) PROTEST: The child vigorously cries, screams, clings, watches eagerly for parents, and angrily rejects unfamiliar caregivers; 2) DESPAIR: The crying stops, motor activity declines, and the child appears depressed, withdrawn, sad, and apathetic; 3) DETACHMENT (Denial): The child superficially adapts, appears interested in toys, but shows detachment from returning parents (a defense mechanism against deep emotional pain). Option a accurately describes the protest stage. Why the other options fail: option b is despair, when the crying has stopped and the child is withdrawn. Option c is detachment, when the child seems content and ignores the returning parent. Option d is not a phase of separation anxiety at all.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trust versus mistrust, because the adolescent cannot rely on adults to keep them safe"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Identity versus role confusion, because peer opinion shapes the adolescent's sense of self"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Initiative versus guilt, because the adolescent feels guilty about needing help"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Generativity versus stagnation, because the adolescent wants to contribute to others"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Adolescents (ages 12 to 18 years) navigate Erikson's developmental stage of Identity versus Role Confusion. The primary developmental task is developing a sense of self, personal values, and peer group belonging. Fitting in with peers is paramount; chronic illnesses and visible regimens (like injections or blood glucose testing) threaten peer conformity and identity formation. The nurse should address this by integrating peer support groups, exploring discrete injection routines, and empowering autonomous self-care. Trust vs Mistrust (a) is infancy; Initiative vs Guilt (c) is early childhood (3–6 yrs); Generativity vs Stagnation (d) is middle adulthood (40–65 yrs). Why the other options fail: option a belongs to infancy. Option c belongs to the preschool years. Option d belongs to midlife, when the task is contributing to the next generation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autonomy versus shame, because the client is struggling to remain independent in hospital"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Industry versus inferiority, because the client measures their worth by past achievement"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ego integrity versus despair, because the client is reviewing life and seeking peace with it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intimacy versus isolation, because the client is seeking closeness in later life"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Older adulthood (65+ years) centers on Erikson's stage of Ego Integrity versus Despair. The developmental task is life review: looking back over one's life to achieve a sense of fulfillment, meaning, and coherence (Ego Integrity) versus experiencing bitter regret, despair, and fear of impending death. Engaging in life review is a healthy, therapeutic process that nurses should facilitate through active listening and validation. Autonomy vs Shame (a) is toddlerhood (1–3 yrs); Industry vs Inferiority (b) is school age (6–12 yrs); Intimacy vs Isolation (d) is young adulthood (18–40 yrs). Why the other options fail: option a belongs to toddlerhood. Option b belongs to school age. Option d belongs to young adulthood.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The alarm stage, with the initial surge of catecholamines and a brief rise in blood pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The resistance stage, with cortisol maintaining glucose and blood pressure despite the strain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The exhaustion stage, with adrenal atrophy, hypoglycaemia and collapse after prolonged stress"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A eustress phase, in which beneficial stress improves performance and adaptation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hans Selye's General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) comprises three distinct biological stages: 1) ALARM REACTION: Acute 'fight-or-flight' activation of the sympathetic nervous system and hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis (tachycardia, hypertension, hyperglycemia); 2) RESISTANCE: Sustained corticosteroid release and physiological adaptation to resist the ongoing stressor; 3) EXHAUSTION: Depletion of adaptive energy reserves following prolonged, relentless stress, resulting in organ system breakdown, adrenal insufficiency, blunted immune function, shock, or death. Option d accurately describes this terminal decompensatory phase. Why the other options fail: option a is the first stage, with a catecholamine surge rather than collapse. Option b is not a stage of the syndrome. Option c is where the body holds out at a cost, before its resources run down.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Displacement, because anger at the employer is directed at a safer target, the nurse"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sublimation, because the anger has been channelled into a socially useful activity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reaction formation, because the client behaves in a way opposite to their true feeling"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intellectualisation, because the client reasons about the situation without feeling it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Displacement is an unconscious defense mechanism in which emotional anger, hostility, or distress triggered by a powerful, threatening person or situation (e.g., the corporate employer) is redirected onto a less threatening, safer substitute target (e.g., the triage nurse). Sublimation (b) is redirecting unacceptable impulses into socially constructive pursuits (e.g., vigorous exercise). Reaction Formation (c) is expressing the exact opposite of unconscious unacceptable feelings (e.g., behaving with fawning affection toward someone you detest). Intellectualization (d) is analyzing an emotional event purely through sterile logic to avoid feeling. Why the other options fail: option b channels anger into something useful, which is not what shouting at a nurse does. Option c would show behaviour opposite to the feeling, such as excessive friendliness. Option d would show detached reasoning rather than abuse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Regression, when a child under stress returns to earlier behaviours such as bedwetting and thumb sucking"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Repression, when the child pushes the frightening memory of admission out of awareness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fixation, when the child becomes stuck at the stage at which the trauma occurred"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reaction formation, when the child behaves in the opposite way to how they actually feel"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Regression is a common, adaptive, and temporary ego defense mechanism in young children facing severe stress, physical illness, hospitalization, or separation from family. The child retreats to behaviors associated with an earlier, safer developmental stage (e.g., thumb-sucking, enuresis, wanting a bottle) where they felt completely nurtured and protected. Reassuring parents that this behavior is normal and will resolve as health and security return prevents punitive parental reactions that worsen distress. Punishment (c) increases anxiety; attributing this to brain damage (a) or manipulation (d) is clinically inaccurate. Why the other options fail: option b is repression, which is unconscious forgetting rather than a return to earlier behaviour. Option c is fixation, a developmental arrest rather than a temporary retreat. Option d would show behaviour opposite to the child's fear.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client's financial reserves, insurance cover and the availability of legal aid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client's physiological stress markers, including cortisol and blood pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Realistic perception of the event, adequate situational supports and effective coping mechanisms"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client's previous psychiatric history and adherence to past treatment plans"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Donna Aguilera's Crisis Intervention methodology identifies three critical 'balancing factors' that determine whether a stressful life event triggers a state of emotional equilibrium or decompensates into a psychological crisis: 1) Realistic perception of the event (accurate understanding without catastrophic distortion or denial); 2) Adequate situational supports (available family, friends, community, and social resources to buffer distress); and 3) Adequate coping mechanisms (positive past behavioral strategies for problem-solving and emotional regulation). If any balancing factors are absent, crisis ensues. Financial metrics (a) and lab values (b) do not constitute Aguilera's model. Why the other options fail: option a is financial capital, not a balancing factor in the model. Option b is physiological measurement. Option d is background history, none of which predicts whether this crisis will resolve.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Conduct a home visit to find structural barriers and link the family to transport, childcare and social support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain the risks of missed appointments and send a formal reminder before the next visit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Report the mother to social services for neglect, because three children are at risk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transfer her care to a clinic nearer her home and close the record if she does not attend"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The WHO Social Determinants of Health (SDOH) emphasizes that conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, and age (e.g., poverty, transport deficits, childcare lack, insecure employment) drive more than 80% of health outcomes and clinic non-attendance. Labeling individuals 'non-compliant' ignores structural inequities. A compassionate home visit uncovers real barriers and allows tailored solutions (CHPS home visits, mobile outreach, transport subsidies, flexible clinic hours). Punitive threats (a and b) and patient abandonment (c) worsen maternal-fetal mortality and violate human rights. Why the other options fail: option b treats a structural problem as a reminder problem. Option c attributes the missed visits to the mother's character. Option d relocates care without removing the barrier that stopped her.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain the interaction, validate the wish for strength, and agree a safe alternative with the family"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Remove the tea from the bedside, explain why it is unsafe, and document the conversation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Allow the tea because family beliefs matter, and monitor the electrolytes and weight closely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the family to stop bringing remedies and restrict their visits until the client stabilises"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cultural competence requires balancing respect for traditional cultural health practices with patient physiological safety. Licorice root contains glycyrrhizin, which inhibits 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, leading to mineralocorticoid excess, severe hypokalemia, hypertension, and heart failure decompensation. The nurse explains the mechanism respectfully without denigrating their beliefs, validates their positive intention to heal, and negotiates safe alternatives (e.g., safe herbal teas without cardiac interactions or prayer/rituals). Aggressive confiscation (b) destroys trust; passive negligence (c) precipitates fatal heart failure; security ejection (d) alienates the support system. Why the other options fail: option b removes the tea but not the belief, and the family will bring it again. Option c allows a substance that is actively harmful in heart failure. Option d punishes the family and guarantees that the next remedy is hidden.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Precontemplation, because the client does not see the behaviour as a problem and has no intention of changing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Contemplation, because the client has begun to think about quitting within the next six months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Preparation, because the client has set a date and made a plan for the next month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maintenance, because the client has already stopped and is sustaining the change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Transtheoretical Model (TTM) stages are: 1) PRECONTEMPLATION: No intention to change behavior in the foreseeable future (typically defined as within the next 6 months); unaware or in denial of negative consequences; 2) CONTEMPLATION: Aware a problem exists and seriously considering change within 6 months, but ambivalent; 3) PREPARATION: Intending to take action within the next 30 days and taking initial small steps; 4) ACTION: Actively modifying behavior (within the past 6 months); 5) MAINTENANCE: Sustaining behavior change for over 6 months and preventing relapse. This client has zero intention to quit, fitting Precontemplation. Why the other options fail: option b would need the client to be considering quitting, which they explicitly are not. Option c would need a date and a plan. Option d would need the client to have already stopped.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Perceived susceptibility and perceived barriers, because she does not feel at risk and sees the clinic as far away"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perceived severity and self-efficacy, because she needs to fear cancer before she will act"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cues to action alone, because a reminder letter is what brings women to screening"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Perceived benefits alone, because screening uptake follows once the purpose is explained"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Health Belief Model (HBM) posits that health-seeking behavior depends on: 1) Perceived Susceptibility (belief about chances of contracting a condition); 2) Perceived Severity (belief about seriousness and consequences); 3) Perceived Benefits (belief in efficacy of advised action); 4) Perceived Barriers (tangible and psychological costs of the action); 5) Cues to Action; and 6) Self-Efficacy. The woman exhibits low perceived susceptibility ('I feel healthy, so it can't happen to me') and high perceived barriers ('clinics are too far'). Psychoanalytic (a) and Pavlovian (b) models do not guide health screening behavior. Why the other options fail: option b addresses fear and confidence rather than the two beliefs she actually stated. Option c isolates reminders from the beliefs that make them work. Option d ignores both of her statements.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reflect both sides of the ambivalence and ask what steps might protect the marriage and the liver"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Point out that the client is in denial and that continued drinking will kill them"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise switching from spirits to beer while the client considers cutting down"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explore what the client enjoys about drinking before discussing any change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Motivational Interviewing (MI) is a collaborative, person-centered counseling approach designed to strengthen personal motivation for and commitment to change by resolving ambivalence. Key principles include expressing empathy, developing discrepancy between current behavior and core values, rolling with resistance, and supporting self-efficacy. Reflecting ambivalence ('On one hand... but on the other hand...') and asking an open question invites the client to voice their own arguments for change ('change talk'). Confrontational labeling (a), minimizing alcoholism (b), and shaming (c) trigger defensiveness and entrench resistance. Why the other options fail: option b argues with the client, which raises resistance. Option c colludes with continued drinking. Option d explores the behaviour without moving toward change talk.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A score of 21 with active suicidal ideation, which indicates severe depression needing urgent review"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A score of 15, which indicates moderately severe depression and a routine referral"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A score of 10, which is the threshold for mild depression and needs no action"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A score of 5, which indicates minimal symptoms despite the reported weight loss"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The PHQ-9 is a standardized 9-item depression screening tool scored from 0 to 27: 0–4 = minimal/none; 5–9 = mild; 10–14 = moderate; 15–19 = moderately severe; 20–27 = severe depression. A score of 20 or higher signifies severe major depressive disorder. Any endorsement of item 9 (thoughts of being better off dead or hurting oneself) demands immediate comprehensive suicide risk assessment and emergency psychiatric referral. Involuntary ECT (c) is never indicated for mild depression; discharging a suffering client (d) constitutes medical negligence. Why the other options fail: option b scores the symptoms as minimal, which the weight loss and anhedonia contradict. Option c treats a mild score as an emergency and names a treatment that requires consent and referral. Option d calls the presentation malingering, which no score supports.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask directly about suicidal thoughts, plan and means, and keep the client under one-to-one observation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask about mood and sleep first, and raise suicide only if the client mentions it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure the client that things will improve and return to the assessment later in the shift"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Promise confidentiality so the client feels safe enough to disclose more"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Suicide risk assessment requires direct, unambiguous, objective questioning. Asking directly about suicidal ideation does NOT plant the idea in a person's head; rather, it provides relief and opens communication. The nurse must assess: 1) ideation; 2) plan specificity; 3) lethality of chosen method; and 4) immediate access to means. If risk is present, the client must never be left alone (initiating continuous 1-to-1 visual observation and removing environmental hazards). False reassurance (a) trivializes despair; promising secrecy (c) is dangerous malpractice; leaving the client alone (d) allows suicide completion. Why the other options fail: option b waits for the client to raise suicide, when the nurse must ask. Option c reassures without assessing. Option d promises a secrecy the nurse cannot keep and must not offer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Engage the family and prayer camp leaders, explain psychosis as a treatable brain condition, and agree that prayer and medication can run together"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain that antipsychotics are essential and that the prayer camp is harmful, and ask the family to choose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Accept the family's decision, because the client's community determines the care that is acceptable"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Arrange an involuntary admission so that treatment can continue without the family's involvement"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In many West African settings, mental health care intersects with deeply rooted traditional and faith-based healing frameworks. Antagonistic approaches or police raids (a) alienate families and drive patients into hiding. Complete abandonment (b) leaves vulnerable clients without care. The evidenced-based, culturally congruent approach championed by the Mental Health Authority and WHO is intersectoral collaboration: engaging faith leaders and family elders respectfully, delivering non-shaming psychoeducation, and negotiating co-existence (allowing prayers/supportive faith rituals to continue while ensuring continuous medical antipsychotic therapy). Why the other options fail: option b forces a choice between treatment and belief, which the family will resolve in favour of the camp. Option c accepts the decision and leaves the psychosis untreated. Option d removes the client from the family and loses the support that sustains adherence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stay with the client, reduce stimulation, speak in short calm sentences and guide slow breathing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Teach the client about the autonomic nervous system once the attack has settled"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Move the client to a quiet room and check back every fifteen minutes until the attack passes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage the client to breathe into a paper bag until the tingling stops"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$During panic-level anxiety (the highest level on Peplau's anxiety continuum), perceptual fields are completely closed, logical reasoning is suspended, and the individual experiences terror and physiological fight-or-flight surges. The nurse must: 1) stay with the client (never abandon someone in panic); 2) reduce environmental stimuli (dim lights, quiet room); 3) use a calm, low, reassuring tone; 4) speak in concise, simple sentences; and 5) guide physical breathing to reverse respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. Lengthy teaching (a) cannot be processed; leaving the client alone (b) intensifies terror; shouting (c) escalates panic. Why the other options fail: option b teaches during the attack, when the client cannot process it. Option c leaves the client alone at the peak of panic. Option d uses a paper bag, which is no longer recommended and can worsen hypoxaemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch27.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 27: INTEG distractor blocks on the three staging, calculation and
-- sign-recognition items that the earlier pass left without one.
--
-- The three are the items in that module whose options are labels rather than
-- sentences - the pressure-injury stages, the Rule of Nines percentages, and
-- four named dermatological signs - and the earlier pass had treated them as
-- self-explanatory. Each now states why the other three labels are wrong.
--
-- After: INTEG carries a block on 20 of 20. Mean stays 1.03, worst 1.25.
-- Key letters unchanged.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stage 1 pressure injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stage 3 pressure injury"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Unstageable pressure injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stage 2 pressure injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to NPUAP/EPUAP staging: Stage 1 is intact skin with localized non-blanchable erythema (or localized warmth, firmness, or discoloration in dark skin). Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss of skin with exposed dermis, presenting as a shallow open ulcer with a viable, red or pink, moist wound bed without slough, eschar, or granulation tissue, OR presenting as an intact or open/ruptured serum-filled blister. Stage 3 involves full-thickness skin loss with visible adipose tissue and granulation; Stage 4 involves exposed bone, tendon, or muscle. Unstageable occurs when slough or eschar completely obscures the base. Why the other options fail: option a is intact skin with non-blanchable erythema and no loss of skin, which is not the shallow open wound described. Option b extends through the dermis into subcutaneous fat, which this wound does not. Option c cannot be staged because slough or eschar obscures the base, when here the base is visible and pink.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "27% TBSA"}, {"id": "b", "text": "36% TBSA"}, {"id": "c", "text": "45% TBSA"}, {"id": "d", "text": "18% TBSA"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the adult Wallace Rule of Nines: Head and neck = 9%; Anterior trunk = 18% (chest 9% + abdomen 9%); Posterior trunk = 18% (upper back 9% + lower back/buttocks 9%); Each arm = 9% total (anterior 4.5% + posterior 4.5%); Each leg = 18% total (anterior 9% + posterior 9%); Perineum/genitalia = 1%. In this client: Anterior chest (9%) + Entire abdomen (9%) + Entire left arm (9%) + Anterior left leg (9%) = 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 36% TBSA. Why the other options fail: option a at 27% omits the whole left arm. Option c at 45% adds the posterior trunk, which the description does not burn. Option d at 18% counts only the trunk and leaves out both the arm and the leg.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tinea corporis exhibiting the Darier sign"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Plaque psoriasis exhibiting the Auspitz sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Malignant melanoma exhibiting the Breslow phenomenon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lichen planus exhibiting Wickham striae"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Plaque psoriasis (psoriasis vulgaris) is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory dermatosis driven by T-lymphocytes and cytokines (IL-17, IL-23, TNF-alpha), resulting in hyperproliferation of epidermal keratinocytes (epidermal turnover accelerates from 28 days to 3–4 days). The hallmark lesions are sharply demarcated erythematous plaques covered with silvery, micaceous scales on extensor surfaces. The Auspitz sign is the appearance of punctate capillary bleeding spots when scales are scraped off, caused by thinned suprapapillary epidermal plates over dilated, tortuous dermal capillaries. Lesions developing at sites of skin trauma demonstrate the Koebner phenomenon. Why the other options fail: option a gives tinea the Darier sign, but Darier sign is the urticarial response produced by rubbing a mastocytoma. Option c pairs melanoma with Breslow correctly, but Breslow thickness is a histological measurement rather than a bedside sign. Option d pairs lichen planus with Wickham striae correctly, but the silvery scale and pinpoint bleeding described belong to psoriasis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-014$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch28.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 28: PRO2 module option rewrite and distractor blocks (20 items).
--
-- All twenty keys verified correct: autocratic leadership in a resuscitation,
-- managing an unsafe staffing shortfall, witnessed wasting of a controlled drug,
-- andragogy for experienced learners, preceptorship against mentorship,
-- feedback after a sterile breach, the comparator element of a PICO question,
-- systematic review as level I evidence, consent under the Declaration of
-- Helsinki, the Do phase of PDSA, latent conditions in the Swiss cheese model,
-- human error under Just Culture, pre-eclampsia management at a CHPS compound,
-- the objective of the NHIS, nursing voice on a district management team,
-- HeFRA and NMC requirements for a private maternity home, the profession's
-- public voice, organisational responses to burnout, unfreezing in Lewin's model,
-- and closing the audit loop.
--
-- This module had the smallest length spread of any read so far - mean 1.81,
-- worst 2.58 - but the same two defects. The keyed option was a paragraph while
-- the distractors were one-liners, and the wrong options were absurdities:
-- "Abandon the unit immediately and go home to avoid personal malpractice
-- liability", "Hand the remaining 8 mg to the client's visiting family member",
-- "Falsify the surgical site infection numbers in the official ledger".
--
-- Every option was rewritten to the same length and register, and all twenty
-- items now carry a distractor block.
--
-- After: mean 1.07, worst 1.15. Key letters stay a5 b5 c5 d5; no key moved.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autocratic leadership, with brief orders, named roles and closed-loop communication"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Democratic leadership, with the team voting on each shock energy before it is delivered"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Laissez-faire leadership, with each member acting on their own judgement and reporting later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transformational leadership, with the leader motivating the team by long-term vision"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In acute life-or-death resuscitations where seconds dictate survival, an autocratic (directive) leadership style is clinically indicated. The code leader must maintain command, make rapid high-stakes decisions, assign clear roles (chest compressions, airway, defibrillator, medication recorder), and demand closed-loop communication. Laissez-faire (b) leads to chaos and delayed defibrillation; democratic consensus voting (c) wastes critical seconds when immediate CPR is required; transformational lecturing (d) is completely inappropriate during an emergency arrest. Why the other options fail: option b removes the leader in an arrest, when seconds decide the outcome. Option c puts a vote where a decision is needed. Option d motivates over the long term, which is not what a pulseless patient needs in the next thirty seconds.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the assistant to take the stable patients and give antibiotics while nurses do dressings"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess acuity, prioritise time-critical care, delegate basic care and escalate the shortfall in writing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lock the unit doors and refuse emergency transfers until the manager has been contacted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Do the work of three nurses without escalation, because reporting looks like incompetence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$When faced with an acute staffing shortage, the charge nurse must: 1) re-triage and prioritize care by client acuity, protecting time-critical treatments (airway, urgent medications, vital signs, post-op monitoring); 2) delegate routine, non-complex tasks within assistive personnel scope; and 3) escalate the staffing crisis formally and in writing to nursing management/supervision to seek float coverage. Delegating IV medications to an assistant (a) is illegal malpractice; unilaterally locking doors (c) violates institutional emergency response; abandoning the shift (d) constitutes gross patient abandonment. Why the other options fail: option a gives an unqualified assistant a delegated clinical task while the two nurses split across tasks. Option c closes the unit to emergencies, which is not within the charge nurse's authority. Option d abandons the unit and the licence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Discard it alone at the sink and document the waste later in the same shift"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Return the 8 mg to pharmacy at the end of the shift for credit against ward stock"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Waste the 8 mg into the approved receptacle with a second nurse watching, then both co-sign"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the remaining 8 mg in a labelled syringe for the client's next scheduled dose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Controlled substances (such as opioids) are subject to strict statutory chain-of-custody protocols to prevent diversion and abuse. Any unused portion of a single-use narcotic must be destroyed immediately in the presence of a second licensed healthcare professional (RN or pharmacist). Both practitioners must directly witness the physical destruction and immediately sign/authenticate the narcotic record. Stashing leftover narcotics in pockets (a) is illegal and facilitates diversion; un-witnessed sink wasting (b) violates drug regulations; giving hospital narcotics to families (d) constitutes illicit drug trafficking. Why the other options fail: option a wastes without a witness and documents after the fact. Option b returns a controlled drug to stock, which breaks the chain of custody. Option d gives a controlled drug to a family member, which is diversion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A lecture series covering the protocols from first principles, with a written exam at the end"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Self-directed reading of the unit policies, with a short quiz at the start of each shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Observing a senior clinician for two weeks, with no set objectives and no feedback"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Problem-centred simulation built on their own clinical experience, with self-directed solving"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Malcolm Knowles' Andragogy theory emphasizes that adult learners: 1) are self-directed and internally motivated; 2) bring rich, diverse life and clinical experience that serves as a foundation for new learning; 3) are problem-centered and task-oriented (preferring real clinical dilemmas over theoretical rote memorization); and 4) learn best when the information has immediate, practical clinical utility. Passive rote lectures (a), infantilizing adult practitioners (b), and punitive shaming (c) create hostility and inhibit adult learning. Why the other options fail: option a teaches protocols from first principles to people who already hold them. Option b leaves learning to reading with no application. Option c offers exposure without objectives or feedback.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Preceptorship is time-limited and competence-focused; mentorship is long-term and career-focused"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Preceptorship is a formal teaching qualification, while mentorship is informal peer support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Preceptorship is for new graduates only, while mentorship is for failed competency reviews"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both are structured programmes of the same length, differing only in whether they are paid"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Preceptorship is a formal, assigned, time-limited partnership between an experienced staff nurse (preceptor) and a novice nurse/student to teach clinical skills, socialize them into the unit, ensure patient safety, and verify clinical competence. In contrast, mentorship is typically an ongoing, voluntary, mutually agreed-upon professional relationship focused on long-term career growth, leadership development, scholarly aspirations, and personal fulfillment. Option a correctly outlines these distinct professional development models. Why the other options fail: option b turns preceptorship into a qualification and strips mentorship of any purpose. Option c invents eligibility rules for both. Option d makes them identical, which is the distinction the question asks for.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the procedure and send the new nurse out while a senior colleague finishes the catheter"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pause to protect sterility, restore asepsis, then debrief privately using the SBI framework"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Say nothing during the procedure and raise it at the next annual appraisal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Correct the technique at the bedside in front of the client so that it is remembered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a break in sterility occurs, the preceptor's first duty is client safety—gently stopping the breach and replacing sterile supplies without shaming the novice in front of the client. Afterward, constructive feedback should occur in a private, supportive setting using structured models like SBI (Situation: during catheter insertion; Behavior: touching the unsterile bed rail with sterile gloves; Impact: introduces infection risk). This preserves psychological safety and fosters clinical growth. Public humiliation (a) damages morale; ignoring contamination (c) causes CAUTI; jumping to board revocation (d) is inappropriate for a learning error. Why the other options fail: option a removes the learner from the procedure, so no learning occurs. Option c delays the feedback until it cannot change behaviour. Option d corrects in front of the client, which teaches fear rather than technique.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Adult intensive care clients with central venous catheters, which is the population"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% isopropyl alcohol, which is the intervention"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Povidone-iodine topical skin preparation, the comparison intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The incidence of catheter-related bloodstream infection, which is the outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The PICO framework structures clinical research questions: P = Population/Patient problem (Adult ICU clients with central lines); I = Intervention (Chlorhexidine skin prep); C = Comparison/Control intervention (Povidone-iodine); O = Outcome (CLABSI incidence rate). In this scenario, povidone-iodine is the standard/comparator intervention ('C'). Option a is the Population ('P'), option b is the Intervention ('I'), and option d is the Outcome ('O'). Why the other options fail: option a is the population, which is the P. Option b is the intervention, the I rather than the C. Option d is the outcome, the O.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A single large randomised controlled trial published in a high-impact journal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A prospective cohort study of two thousand patients followed for five years"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An expert consensus guideline written by a national professional body"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A systematic review and meta-analysis of several rigorous randomised trials"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the hierarchy of clinical evidence, systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) occupy the peak (Level I) because they synthesize data across multiple high-powered, rigorously controlled experimental studies, minimizing bias. Single case-control studies (c) and qualitative phenomenological reports (b) provide lower levels of evidence (Levels IV–VI). Expert opinion, editorials, and blogs (a) reside at the very bottom (Level VII). Why the other options fail: option a is a single trial, which sits below a synthesis of trials. Option b is observational, which sits below any randomised design. Option c is expert opinion, which sits near the base of the pyramid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Voluntary written consent, with risks, benefits and the right to withdraw explained"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Written consent taken by the ward clerk on the day, with the risks explained afterwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Verbal consent from a relative, because the client is poor and may not understand the form"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A consent form with the risks left out, so that the client is not frightened away"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Declaration of Helsinki and modern Institutional Review Board (IRB) codes mandate that human clinical research must uphold participant autonomy through voluntary informed consent, full disclosure of risks and benefits, and guarantees that participants may withdraw at any time without compromising clinical care. Coercive financial incentives (b), withholding standard curative care from control groups as in the unethical Tuskegee study (c), and publishing confidential identifiers (d) are gross violations of bioethics and international human rights law. Why the other options fail: option a takes consent after the event. Option b takes consent from someone who cannot give it. Option c withholds the risks, which voids the consent.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Define the problem, set the aim and identify the root causes of the delay"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Run the change as a small pilot, document what happens and note unexpected problems"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Analyse the results of the pilot and compare them against the predicted baseline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Implement the change across the whole hospital as a standing policy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Deming/IHI PDSA cycle operates as follows: 1) PLAN = define the problem, aim, metrics, and intervention; 2) DO = test the change on a small scale (pilot), execute the plan, and record observations/obstacles; 3) STUDY = analyze post-intervention data against baseline and evaluate lessons learned; 4) ACT = decide whether to adopt, adapt, or abandon the change based on results. Option b describes the 'Do' stage. Option a is 'Plan', option c is 'Study', and option d is 'Act'. Why the other options fail: option a is the Plan phase, which comes before the test of change. Option b is the Study phase. Option c is the Act phase, which follows the study of the results.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Individual carelessness by the nurse, to be handled through the disciplinary process"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Active failures alone, because latent conditions cannot contribute to one overdose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Latent system conditions that aligned with active failures to defeat the defences"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An unforeseeable event, which no model of accident causation can explain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$James Reason's Swiss Cheese Model posits that complex organizational accidents rarely stem from isolated individual carelessness. Instead, defense barriers (layers of cheese) contain inherent holes (latent failures: look-alike drug packaging, poor equipment maintenance, excessive mandatory overtime, poor lighting/distractions). When these holes align across multiple institutional defenses, an active human error penetrates and harms the client. System engineering focuses on eliminating latent hazards rather than merely blaming the last human touchpoint. Why the other options fail: option a blames the individual and stops the analysis where the model begins. Option b separates active failures from latent conditions, when the model holds that they align. Option c calls the event unforeseeable, which the model exists to disprove.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "At-risk behaviour: coach the nurse and remove the confusing menu in the next update"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reckless behaviour: refer the nurse for a fitness-to-practise review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sabotage: remove the nurse from clinical duties pending the investigation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Human error: console the nurse, examine the pump interface and add a safeguard"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Just Culture categorizes workplace behaviors into three distinct categories: 1) Human error (inadvertent mistake, slip, or lapse while performing care) -> console the worker and fix system/device vulnerabilities; 2) At-risk behavior (taking shortcuts where risk is mistakenly believed to be negligible or justified) -> coach the worker and remove incentives for shortcuts; 3) Reckless behavior (conscious, intentional disregard of substantial, unjustifiable risk) -> disciplinary sanction. Misprogramming due to confusing user-interface design is a classic human error deserving consolation and system redesign. Why the other options fail: option a is at-risk behaviour, which requires a known risk the nurse accepted. Option b is reckless behaviour, which requires conscious disregard. Option c is sabotage, which requires intent to harm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give magnesium sulphate and an antihypertensive, stabilise, then refer urgently with handover"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give magnesium and keep her at the CHPS compound until the blood pressure settles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral antihypertensives and send her home to return if the headache worsens"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Arrange a taxi to the district hospital without magnesium, since loading needs a hospital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under Ghana Health Service primary care and CHPS protocols, severe pre-eclampsia is an obstetric emergency requiring urgent stabilization followed by expedited referral up the healthcare cascade. The Community Health Officer administers a loading dose of magnesium sulphate (anticonvulsant) and rapid-acting antihypertensive (such as oral labetalol or hydralazine) within standing orders, secures transport, notifies the receiving District Hospital, and accompanies or coordinates skilled handover. Retaining the patient at CHPS (b) causes eclamptic seizures and maternal death; discharging home (c) is fatal malpractice; uncoordinated commercial transport (d) fails basic emergency transfer standards. Why the other options fail: option b delays referral for a condition that can progress to eclampsia within hours. Option c sends a woman with severe pre-eclampsia home. Option d moves her without the loading dose that standing orders permit.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Free care for registered members, with the cost recovered from higher non-member charges"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Removing financial barriers at the point of care and preventing catastrophic out-of-pocket payment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A contributory scheme that covers the formal sector and expands to the informal sector"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A basic package for every citizen, funded from general taxation with no contribution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS) was established (under Act 650, revised Act 852) to eliminate the punitive 'cash-and-carry' system that forced patients to pay out-of-pocket before receiving emergency or essential healthcare. Its primary objective is risk pooling and social financial protection to ensure that all citizens, especially the vulnerable and indigent, can access essential healthcare without experiencing catastrophic poverty, advancing Universal Health Coverage (UHC). NHIS is a social public health protection mechanism, not a private for-profit venture (a). Why the other options fail: option a recovers cost from charges, which is the opposite of removing them. Option b describes cover that stops at the formal sector. Option c describes a tax-funded service, which is not what the scheme is.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Present the mortality figures to the assembly and ask the members to fund a new vehicle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Lobby for more midwives at the district hospital, where most deliveries take place"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use district data to find the worst maternal outcomes, target resources there and build transport"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Campaign for a maternity wing at every facility, starting nearest the district capital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nurses in health administration and policy leadership exercise policy voice by using empirical public health data (such as DHIMS-2 metrics) to highlight healthcare disparities, maternal-neonatal morbidity trends, and resource bottlenecks. Advocating for targeted resource allocation, ambulance networks, and training for frontline primary healthcare providers drives systemic population-level improvements. Apathy (a), urban misallocation (b), and eliminating nursing cadres (d) actively worsen health outcomes. Why the other options fail: option a asks for a vehicle rather than using the data to target the worst outcomes. Option b concentrates staff where deliveries already happen. Option c spreads resources evenly rather than to where mortality is highest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Register the business with the assembly and open after an environmental health inspection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confirm NMC registration and open, since a licence to practise licenses the facility"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obtain HeFRA accreditation and open, since the facility regulator also covers the nurse"}, {"id": "d", "text": "HeFRA facility accreditation, NMC good standing, and practice within scope and referral protocols"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Health entrepreneurship requires strict adherence to statutory regulatory frameworks. Under Ghanaian law (Health Institutions and Facilities Act 829), all healthcare facilities—including nurse/midwife-led private clinics and maternity homes—must be inspected, accredited, and licensed by the Health Facilities Regulatory Agency (HeFRA). Furthermore, the practitioner must maintain active licensure and good standing with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC), ensure a defined clinical scope, and maintain formal emergency referral links with a higher-level facility. Operating without HeFRA licensing (a) is illegal and subject to criminal closure. Why the other options fail: option a registers a business without licensing the facility or checking professional standing. Option b treats a personal licence as a facility licence. Option c accredits the building and leaves the practitioner unchecked.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Publish research, write in the media, and join policy panels and professional associations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A national advertising campaign showing nurses at work, funded by the association"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Speak only through the ministry's communications office so the message stays consistent"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Focus on clinical work and let the image improve as patients experience good care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The public image of nursing directly influences public trust, recruitment of top candidates, legislative healthcare funding, and the profession's policy-making influence. The International Council of Nurses (ICN) and national associations advocate for proactive media engagement: publishing clinical research, writing thought-leadership articles, speaking as expert panelists on national health topics, and showcasing nursing's scientific, autonomous clinical contributions. Silence (d) and unkempt presentation (c) perpetuate harmful stereotypes. Why the other options fail: option a spends money on advertising rather than on evidence and expertise. Option b channels the profession's voice through another body. Option c assumes that good care alone changes public perception, which the evidence does not support.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mandatory resilience training for the nurses, with attendance recorded and appraised"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Structured debriefing, safe staffing ratios, confidential counselling and peer support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A roster giving each nurse three consecutive days off every month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rotate staff through the oncology unit quickly so no one is exposed for long"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Compassion fatigue and burnout are occupational hazards in high-stress, emotionally demanding healthcare specialties. Evidence-based organizational interventions include: regular clinical multidisciplinary debriefings (such as Schwartz Center Rounds) to discuss emotional aspects of care, ensuring safe staffing ratios, providing confidential mental health support programs (EAP), and fostering peer support. Punitive threats (a), increasing workload (c), and demanding emotional apathy (d) exacerbate psychological trauma and accelerate nurse turnover. Why the other options fail: option a makes the individual responsible for a system problem. Option b adds rest without changing the workload that caused the exhaustion. Option c reduces exposure instead of reducing the harm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Implement the system overnight and train afterwards, so no one has time to resist"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Set a compliance target and audit it monthly, reporting the results to the manager"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Help staff see the limits of paper charting and address their anxieties, creating readiness"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Set up a staff committee to decide whether the system should be introduced at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Kurt Lewin's 3-Step Change Theory consists of: 1) UNFREEZING = creating motivation and readiness for change by challenging the status quo, showing the need for innovation (e.g., highlighting medication error risks in paper records), and addressing emotional resistance; 2) MOVING (CHANGING) = executing the transition, providing hands-on training, and coaching through the new workflow; 3) REFREEZING = stabilizing and institutionalizing the change into permanent standard operating procedures. Option c describes Unfreezing. Option b is Refreezing. Why the other options fail: option a skips unfreezing entirely and guarantees resistance. Option b is the refreezing stage, with audit and policy. Option c delegates a decision the manager has already been given.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Present the findings to the surgical team and agree to review the rate again next year"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Introduce the WHO checklist and record compliance, without repeating the audit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refer the unit for external accreditation review, since internal audit cannot change practice"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Analyse causes, apply evidence-based changes, and re-audit after a set period to close the loop"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A clinical audit is a quality improvement process that seeks to improve client care and outcomes through systematic review of care against explicit criteria and the implementation of change. The complete clinical audit cycle requires: 1) identifying standard criteria; 2) measuring current practice; 3) comparing results with standards; 4) implementing corrective evidence-based changes; and 5) re-auditing practice to verify that improvements occurred ('closing the audit loop'). Hiding the report (a) or falsifying data (b) violates clinical governance and ethics. Why the other options fail: option a records the finding without changing anything. Option b introduces the change but never measures it, so the loop stays open. Option c hands the problem to another body, which does not close the cycle.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

