-- Batch 29: the last two thin rationales in the live bank.
--
-- NG-FLUID-0002 and NG-FLUID-0003 carried correct keys and correct clinical
-- content, but 136 and 155 character rationales with no analysis of the wrong
-- options - the only two items in the 5,510 that fail the 200-character floor
-- once the rest of this repair is applied. Both now explain why each wrong
-- option fails; neither key nor any option changes.
--
-- After: rationales under 200 characters = 0 for the whole bank.
-- UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  rationale = $q$IV calcium gluconate stabilises the myocardium within minutes by restoring the threshold potential, which is what the peaked T waves are warning about. It does not lower the potassium; insulin with glucose, and then potassium removal, follow as prescribed. Why the other options fail: option b is oral potassium chloride, which adds to a potassium of 7.1 mmol/L and would deepen the hyperkalaemia. Option c is a thiazide diuretic, which is not a treatment for hyperkalaemia and acts far too slowly for a patient with ECG changes. Option d is hypotonic fluid, which dilutes the extracellular potassium transiently without protecting the membrane and can worsen the sodium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLUID-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  rationale = $q$A potassium of 2.6 mmol/L with new ventricular ectopy is severe hypokalaemia with cardiac involvement, so the priority is to escalate and prepare prescribed replacement with continuous ECG monitoring while the cause is corrected. Why the other options fail: option a defers treatment by a day in a patient whose rhythm is already abnormal. Option c gives sodium, which addresses neither the potassium nor the arrhythmia and does nothing for the ongoing losses. Option d restricts fluid in a patient who is volume depleted from prolonged vomiting and needs the deficit replaced.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLUID-0003$q$;

-- Verify: expect 2
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch30.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 30: EMERG. Regenerated from src/db after the letter-reference cleanup.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Open femur fracture, alert, respiratory rate 22/min and capillary refill of 1.5 seconds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Snoring respirations that clear on airway opening, respiratory rate 34/min and a weak rapid radial pulse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Open skull injury with brain matter exposed, apnoeic after two attempts to open the airway"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ambulatory with superficial lacerations and bilateral forearm contusions, walking to the tag point"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the START triage algorithm, patients are assessed on three objective physiological criteria: Respirations, Perfusion, and Mental status (RPM). Victims tagged RED (Immediate) have life-threatening injuries that are salvageable with immediate intervention: respiratory rate > 30 breaths/min, absent radial pulse or capillary refill > 2 seconds, or inability to follow simple commands. The 28-year-old has a respiratory rate of 34 bpm and compromised perfusion, requiring immediate red-tag prioritization. The 40-year-old is Yellow (Delayed), the apnoeic client with catastrophic head trauma is Black (Expectant), and the walking wounded client is Green (Minor). Why the other options fail: option a meets no red criterion, since its respiratory rate, perfusion and mentation are all normal, making it Delayed (yellow). Option c is apnoeic after airway opening and is therefore Expectant (black). Option d is walking wounded and is Minor (green).$q$,
  strategy = $q$START Triage Rules: Walking wounded = Green. Not breathing even after opening airway = Black. Breathing only after opening airway OR RR > 30 OR Cap refill > 2s / no radial pulse OR unable to follow commands = RED (Immediate). All others = Yellow.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Auscultate both lung bases for crackles and wheezes before any other assessment is made"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stabilise the cervical spine manually and inspect the oropharynx for blood or a foreign body"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert two 14-gauge peripheral lines and infuse warmed normal saline under pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Score the Glasgow Coma Scale and check both pupils for size and reactivity to light"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In trauma and emergency primary survey, the sequential ABCDE approach dictates that threats to life must be identified and managed immediately before proceeding to the next letter. 'A' stands for Airway assessment and maintenance with Cervical Spine protection. The nurse must establish manual in-line cervical spine stabilization while assessing airway patency, suctioning secretions or blood, and removing foreign material. Breathing auscultation belongs to 'B', IV access belongs to 'C' (Circulation), and GCS scoring belongs to 'D' (Disability). Why the other options fail: option a is the Breathing step, option c is the Circulation step and option d is the Disability step, so each comes after the airway has been secured. None of them opens or protects an airway that is still at risk.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In trauma resuscitation, Airway and C-spine are inextricably linked in Step A. Never move to Breathing (B) or Circulation (C) until a patent, protected airway is confirmed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Head-tilt chin-lift maneuver, because the client is unconscious and needs air now"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Neck hyperextension with a chin pull, the fastest way to clear a snoring airway"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Modified jaw-thrust without neck extension, holding the head in neutral alignment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prone positioning with the head turned to one side so that secretions can drain away"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In any trauma client with suspected cervical spine injury (such as motor vehicle collisions or head/facial injuries), the modified jaw-thrust maneuver is the only acceptable manual method to open the airway. The head-tilt chin-lift maneuver hyperextends the cervical spine and can sever the spinal cord in the presence of an unstable cervical vertebral fracture. The jaw-thrust lifts the mandible forward, displacing the tongue away from the posterior pharynx without moving the cervical vertebrae. Why the other options fail: options a and b both extend the neck, which can transect a cord already compromised at the fracture. Option d leaves the airway obstructed by the tongue and secretions while preventing reassessment and ventilation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Airway opening in trauma: Always JAW-THRUST without head tilt. Head-tilt chin-lift is strictly reserved for non-trauma medical victims.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sized from mouth corner to angle of jaw, inserted tip up, rotated 180 degrees, and withheld with a gag reflex"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sized from the tip of the nose to the chin, inserted concave down, and used for conscious clients"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lubricated with petroleum jelly, inserted straight back along the tongue in every adult"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Contraindicated in basilar skull fracture, where a nasopharyngeal airway is the preferred adjunct"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An oropharyngeal airway (OPA) is sized by measuring from the corner of the client's mouth to the angle of the jaw (mandible). In adults, it is inserted upside down (tip toward the hard palate) and rotated 180 degrees as it passes the soft palate to prevent pushing the tongue back into the pharynx. An OPA is used ONLY in unconscious/unresponsive clients who lack a gag reflex. Inserting an OPA in a conscious or semi-conscious patient with an intact gag reflex triggers vomiting, laryngospasm, and pulmonary aspiration. Nasopharyngeal airways (NPAs) are contraindicated in basilar skull fractures. Why the other options fail: option b uses nasopharyngeal sizing and the wrong indication, since an oropharyngeal airway is for the unresponsive client. Option c describes unsafe lubrication and insertion. Option d reverses the rule: the oropharyngeal airway is safe in basilar skull fracture, while the nasopharyngeal one is contraindicated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$OPA rule: Unconscious ONLY (no gag reflex). Correct size: Corner of mouth to angle of jaw. Rotation: Insert tip up, rotate 180 degrees.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Anterior superior iliac spine, entered through the thickest part of the iliac crest"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mid-shaft of the femur, driven through the rectus femoris into the medullary canal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Distal radius, immediately proximal to the styloid process at the wrist joint"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Proximal tibia, about 2 cm medial to the tibial tuberosity on the flat anteromedial surface"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$When rapid peripheral intravenous access cannot be established within 60 to 90 seconds in a critically ill or shocked adult, intraosseous (IO) access provides an immediate, non-collapsible vascular route that accommodates fluids, blood products, and all resuscitation medications at doses identical to IV administration. The proximal tibia is the primary site: located approximately 2 cm (two fingerbreadths) medial to the tibial tuberosity along the broad, flat anteromedial surface of the tibia. Another common adult site is the proximal humerus. Why the other options fail: option a is not an intraosseous site. Option b is not d site either, and criving through muscle risks fracture and extravasation. Option c is d distal site reserved for when proximal sites are unavailable, so it is not the most common choice.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IO access anatomical landmark: Proximal tibia (2 cm medial and slightly proximal to the tibial tuberosity, where the bone surface is flat and superficial).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "1 unit of packed red cells, 1 unit of fresh frozen plasma and 1 unit of platelets"}, {"id": "b", "text": "4 units of 0.9% sodium chloride for every 1 unit of packed red blood cells given"}, {"id": "c", "text": "6 units of packed red cells followed by 500 mL of 20% albumin as the only colloid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cryoprecipitate alone, repeated until the fibrinogen level exceeds 4 g/L"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In severe hemorrhagic shock, modern 'Damage Control Resuscitation' emphasizes early balanced blood component therapy using a 1:1:1 ratio: 1 unit of Packed Red Blood Cells, 1 unit of Fresh Frozen Plasma, and 1 unit of Platelets. This reconstituted whole blood approach prevents the lethal trauma triad (hypothermia, acidosis, and trauma-induced coagulopathy). Infusing large volumes of clear crystalloids (like normal saline) is harmful because it causes dilutional coagulopathy, hypothermia, and disrupts nascent clots. Why the other options fail: option b is crystalloid, which dilutes clotting factors and worsens the lethal triad. Option c transfuses red cells alone with an artificial colloid. Option d replaces one component only, so the coagulopathy of the other two is untouched.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Massive Transfusion Protocol (MTP) golden ratio: 1:1:1 (1 PRBC : 1 FFP : 1 Platelets) to prevent dilutional coagulopathy and the lethal trauma triad.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hypovolaemic shock from an unrecognised internal haemorrhage after the fall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cardiogenic shock from blunt myocardial contusion at the moment of impact"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Septic shock from aspirated water and sand after the immersion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neurogenic shock from loss of sympathetic outflow above the level of the cord injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Neurogenic shock occurs with high spinal cord injuries (typically at or above T6). Disruption of sympathetic outflow causes unopposed vagal (parasympathetic) tone, leading to profound systemic vasodilation and loss of cardiac sympathetic stimulation. The diagnostic hallmark that differentiates neurogenic shock from all other forms of shock is: HYPOTENSION accompanied by BRADYCARDIA (heart rate < 60 bpm) and warm, dry, flushed skin (due to loss of vasomotor tone and blood pooling in the periphery). In hypovolemic or cardiogenic shock, compensatory sympathetic activation causes tachycardia and cool, clammy extremities. Why the other options fail: options a, b and c all preserve sympathetic tone, so each produces tachycardia with cool, clammy skin. Only neurogenic shock removes that tone, and so only it pairs hypotension with bradycardia and warm, dry skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neurogenic shock hallmark: Hypotension + BRADYCARDIA + Warm, dry skin (caused by sympathetic blockade above T6). All other shocks present with tachycardia and cool clammy skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intramuscular adrenaline 1:1,000, 0.3 to 0.5 mg into the anterolateral mid-thigh"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravenous diphenhydramine 50 mg, given slowly over five minutes to block histamine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravenous hydrocortisone 200 mg, given as the first drug in the anaphylaxis protocol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nebulised salbutamol, repeated back-to-back until the inspiratory stridor resolves"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Intramuscular Epinephrine (1:1,000 concentration, dose 0.3–0.5 mg in adults) injected into the anterolateral mid-thigh (vastus lateralis) is the single life-saving, first-line medication for anaphylaxis. Epinephrine rapidly stimulates alpha-1 receptors (causing vasoconstriction to reverse hypotension and upper airway edema), beta-1 receptors (increasing cardiac inotropy/chronotropy), and beta-2 receptors (producing profound bronchodilation and inhibiting further mast cell mediator release). Antihistamines and corticosteroids are second-line adjuvant therapies that take 30 to 120 minutes to work and NEVER substitute for prompt epinephrine. Why the other options fail: options b and c are second-line drugs that take 30 to 120 minutes and do not reverse airway oedema or vasodilation. Option d relieves bronchospasm alone and leaves the airway swelling, hypotension and mediator release untreated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anaphylaxis reflex: EPINEPHRINE IM into the outer thigh FIRST. Antihistamines and steroids NEVER come before epinephrine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pause for ten seconds to palpate a carotid pulse and listen for heart sounds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give amiodarone 300 mg as an immediate bolus before any further compressions are done"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Resume chest compressions at once and continue for two minutes before rechecking the rhythm"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Deliver a second shock immediately at 300 Joules, back to back with the first"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to ACLS and ERC resuscitation guidelines, immediately following any defibrillation shock, the team must resume CPR (chest compressions) immediately without pausing to check the pulse or re-analyze the rhythm. After defibrillation, the stunned myocardium takes time to establish an organized rhythm and adequate perfusion; pausing compressions produces myocardial ischemia. CPR must continue uninterrupted for a full 2-minute cycle (5 cycles of 30:2 or continuous compressions) before pausing for a brief (< 10 second) rhythm and pulse check. Why the other options fail: option a pauses perfusion at the exact moment the myocardium is stunned and delays the compressions that restore flow. Option b spends time on c drug when compressions should already have restarted. Option d exceeds the maximum biphasic energy and back-to-back shocks are not indicated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ACLS rule after shock: SHOCK -> IMMEDIATELY RESUME CHEST COMPRESSIONS. Never check a pulse or pause immediately following defibrillation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Synchronised cardioversion at 100 Joules, because an organised rhythm is present"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continue CPR, give adrenaline 1 mg every 3 to 5 minutes, and correct the 5 Hs and 5 Ts"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Push amiodarone 300 mg, since PEA is treated like refractory ventricular fibrillation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply transcutaneous pacing at 80 mA, because the rhythm is bradycardic and organised"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pulseless Electrical Activity (PEA) and Asystole are non-shockable cardiac arrest rhythms. Defibrillation is completely ineffective and contraindicated. Management relies on: (1) High-quality chest compressions with minimal interruptions, (2) Epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO administered as early as possible and repeated every 3 to 5 minutes, and (3) Rapid identification and correction of reversible causes: the 5 Hs (Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion [acidosis], Hypo/Hyperkalemia, Hypothermia) and 5 Ts (Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade [cardiac], Toxins, Thrombosis [pulmonary PE], Thrombosis [coronary MI]). Why the other options fail: option a cannot help, because there is no pulse to synchronise with and cardioversion requires a perfusing rhythm. Option c has no role in PEA. Option d treats a symptomatic bradycardia with a pulse, not a pulseless arrest.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PEA & Asystole: NEVER shock! High-quality CPR + Epinephrine 1 mg q3-5min + Find and fix the 5 Hs and 5 Ts (most common causes: hypovolemia and hypoxia).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "High-dose intravenous dexamethasone, to suppress cerebral oedema after the arrest"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperventilation to keep PaCO2 below 25 mmHg, to constrict the cerebral vessels"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rapid 5% dextrose to keep serum glucose above 14 mmol/L after resuscitation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Targeted temperature management at 32 to 36 degrees Celsius for at least 24 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Targeted Temperature Management (TTM) is the standard of care for comatose adult patients who achieve ROSC after cardiac arrest. Maintaining a constant core body temperature between 32°C and 36°C for at least 24 hours (and actively preventing fever for at least 72 hours) suppresses cerebral metabolic demand, inhibits oxygen free-radical generation, and decreases cerebral apoptosis. Routine steroids are ineffective; hyperventilation causes severe cerebral ischemia; and hyperglycemia exacerbates ischemic brain injury. Why the other options fail: option a has no outcome benefit in post-arrest coma. Option b lowers PaCO2 so far that cerebral flow falls and ischaemia deepens. Option c worsens the injury, because hyperglycaemia after arrest is associated with worse neurological recovery.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Post-cardiac arrest comatose patient care: Targeted Temperature Management (32–36°C) for 24 hours + Prevent fever + Avoid hyperventilation + Maintain MAP >= 65 mmHg.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Decompress with a 14-gauge needle at the second intercostal space, midclavicular line"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Send to radiology for an erect chest X-ray to confirm the diagnosis first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intubate and start high-pressure positive pressure ventilation at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse two litres of normal saline under pressure to restore the blood pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The presentation (unilateral absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation AWAY from the affected side, hypotension, jugular venous distension) is diagnostic of a left-sided Tension Pneumothorax. Air under positive pressure collapses the lung, shifts the mediastinum, compresses the vena cava, and halts venous return to the heart, leading to imminent arrest. Tension pneumothorax is a CLINICAL diagnosis; waiting for a chest X-ray is fatal. Immediate needle decompression converts the tension pneumothorax into a simple pneumothorax, restoring venous return and hemodynamics, followed by formal tube thoracostomy. Why the other options fail: option b delays treatment for b clinical diagnosis and the client arrests before the film is done. Option c raises intrathoracic pressure and accelerates the tension. Option d cannot relieve the mechanical obstruction to venous return, so the blood pressure does not recover.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tension Pneumothorax is a CLINICAL emergency: Tracheal deviation + Absent breath sounds + JVD + Shock. NEVER wait for an X-ray! Decompress immediately with needle thoracostomy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An occlusive dressing taped on three sides, leaving the fourth side free as a flutter valve"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An airtight occlusive dressing taped securely on all four sides to seal the wound"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tightly packed dry sterile gauze, changed every four hours until the chest tube is in"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Left open to air, covered only by a loose gauze swab held in place with tape strips"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An open sucking chest wound allows atmospheric air to enter the pleural space during inspiration. The proper initial dressing is a sterile occlusive dressing taped on THREE SIDES only. This creates a one-way flutter-valve mechanism: on inspiration, the dressing sucks against the chest wall to prevent external air from entering the thorax; on expiration, the untaped fourth side flutters open, allowing trapped intrapleural air to escape. Taping all four sides traps escaping air, rapidly converting an open pneumothorax into a life-threatening tension pneumothorax. Why the other options fail: option b seals all four sides, trapping air and converting the open wound into a tension pneumothorax. Option c packs the wound and restricts ventilation without any valve. Option d lets air enter freely on each breath and creates no flutter valve.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Open 'sucking' chest wound rule: THREE-SIDED dressing (flutter valve). Taping all four sides turns it into a deadly tension pneumothorax!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Flumazenil, given as a bolus and repeated until the client is fully alert"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Deferoxamine, infused over eight hours with serial iron studies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "N-acetylcysteine, started at once and continued on the weight-based regimen"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pralidoxime, with atropine, until the muscarinic effects settle"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$N-acetylcysteine (NAC) is the specific antidote for acetaminophen toxicity. In acetaminophen overdose, hepatic glutathione stores are depleted, allowing the toxic metabolite NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine) to bind to hepatocellular proteins, causing centrilobular hepatic necrosis. NAC acts as a glutathione precursor and substitute, binding and detoxifying NAPQI. When administered within 8 hours of ingestion, NAC provides near 100% protection against severe hepatotoxicity. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines; Deferoxamine chelates iron; Pralidoxime treats organophosphates. Why the other options fail: Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and would do nothing to a paracetamol load; deferoxamine chelates iron; atropine with pralidoxime is the organophosphate antidote. None restores glutathione or neutralises NAPQI, so hepatic necrosis would proceed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Acetaminophen/Paracetamol antidote = N-ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC). It replenishes glutathione to neutralize the toxic metabolite NAPQI.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Its 12 to 24 hour duration means a single dose protects the client through the night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Repeated doses given within six hours cause permanent loss of renal function"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Its 30 to 90 minute duration is shorter than most opioids, so respiratory depression can return"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is a partial opioid agonist, so higher doses deepen the sedation and hypoventilation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Naloxone is a pure opioid competitive antagonist with a relatively short half-life (duration of action typically 30 to 90 minutes). Many opioids (such as methadone, sustained-release oxycodone, or high-dose fentanyl) have half-lives extending for several hours or days. Once naloxone dissociates from the mu-opioid receptors, the patient can rapidly slip back into life-threatening hypoventilation, coma, and apnea ('rebound overdose'). The nurse must continuously monitor respiratory status for at least several hours and prepare for repeat doses or a continuous naloxone infusion. Why the other options fail: option a overstates the duration several-fold. Option b invents a renal toxicity that naloxone does not have. Option d gets the pharmacology wrong, because naloxone is an antagonist and does not sedate or depress respiration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Naloxone wears off BEFORE the opioid wears off! Always monitor for rebound respiratory depression as the antidote clears.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "2,800 mL"}, {"id": "b", "text": "5,600 mL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "11,200 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1,400 mL"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Using the Parkland formula: Total 24-hour volume = 4 mL x weight (kg) x % TBSA burn. Total 24-hour volume = 4 mL x 70 kg x 40 = 11,200 mL of Ringer's lactate. The Parkland schedule requires: Half (50%) of the total calculated volume administered in the first 8 hours calculated from the TIME OF INJURY: 11,200 mL / 2 = 5,600 mL in the first 8 hours. The remaining half (5,600 mL) is infused over the subsequent 16 hours. Resuscitation is titrated to maintain a minimum adult urine output of 0.5 to 1.0 mL/kg/hour (30–50 mL/hr). Why the other options fail: option c is the full 24-hour total, not the first eight hours. Option a is one quarter of the total and option d is one eighth, so both under-resuscitate the client during the most critical window.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parkland Formula: Total = 4 mL x kg x % TBSA. First 8 hours = 50% (half) of total. Next 16 hours = remaining 50%. Total: 4 x 70 x 40 = 11,200 mL. First 8 hours = 5,600 mL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase PEEP by 5 cmH2O and reassess the oxygen saturation in five minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a bolus dose of a neuromuscular blocking agent to stop the agitation first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Silence the alarm and allow the ventilator five minutes to recalibrate on its own"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disconnect from the ventilator and bag manually with 100% oxygen while troubleshooting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a ventilated patient suddenly deteriorates and the cause is not immediately obvious, the universal rule of critical care is: Disconnect the patient from the ventilator immediately and begin manual ventilation using a bag-valve-mask (BVM) reservoir connected to 100% high-flow oxygen. This immediately differentiates between a machine/circuit failure and a patient problem. While manually bagging, the nurse troubleshoots using the DOPE mnemonic: Displacement of tube, Obstruction (suction secretions), Pneumothorax (listen for breath sounds), Equipment failure. Why the other options fail: option a raises pressure in d circuit that is already alarming and does not address the obstruction. Option b paralyses the client and hides the deterioration. Option c wastes the minutes in which the cause must be found and corrected.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sudden ventilator distress rule: DISCONNECT from ventilator and BAG manually with 100% O2 first! Then troubleshoot using DOPE (Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autonomy, giving every arrival equal access to intensive care regardless of prognosis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "First come, first served, with allocation decided strictly by order of arrival"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Utilitarianism, directing scarce resources to the greatest number of salvageable lives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Non-maleficence, withholding invasive care until specialist teams are all assembled"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under routine emergency conditions, care is guided by individual patient beneficence and autonomy (providing every available resource to the single patient). In disaster mass casualty triage and crisis standards of care, the ethical framework shifts fundamentally to utilitarianism: the duty to allocate scarce medical resources (ventilators, blood, surgical suites, staff) to produce the greatest good for the greatest number of salvageable individuals. Patients with catastrophic injuries who have minimal chance of survival despite maximal resources are categorized as expectant/palliative (black tag) so that available resources save those who can survive. Why the other options fail: option a describes routine autonomy, which cannot be honoured equally once resources are exhausted. Option b allocates by queue position and ignores prognosis. Option d withholds care, which harms the very clients it claims to protect.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Disaster triage ethics: Utilitarian framework (Greatest good for the greatest number). Focus resources on salvageable patients with highest likelihood of survival.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dilute in 100 mL of normal saline and infuse over 15 minutes through a peripheral line"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inject into the deltoid muscle and massage the site firmly for at least one minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Slow IV push over two minutes, watching the monitor for a rise in blood pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rapid IV push over 1 to 2 seconds through a large antecubital vein, then a 20 mL saline flush"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Adenosine has an extremely short biological half-life of less than 10 seconds because it is rapidly cleared and taken up by vascular endothelial cells and erythrocytes. To reach the AV node in therapeutic concentration before degrading, it must be administered as an ultra-rapid IV push (over 1 to 2 seconds) through a proximal, large-bore intravenous site (preferably the antecubital fossa), followed immediately by a rapid 20 mL normal saline flush and brief elevation of the arm. The nurse must warn the client about transient feelings of facial flushing, chest tightness, or impending doom, and anticipate a brief period of asystole on the monitor. Why the other options fail: Diluting and infusing over fifteen minutes slows a drug whose half-life is under ten seconds; an intramuscular injection never reaches the atrioventricular node in time; a slow push over two minutes delivers too little, too late.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Adenosine administration: Rapid IV push (1-2 seconds) + Immediate 20 mL saline flush + Antecubital site (closest to heart). Warn patient of brief chest tightness/asystole.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "13.1 mL/hr"}, {"id": "b", "text": "26.3 mL/hr"}, {"id": "c", "text": "35.0 mL/hr"}, {"id": "d", "text": "52.5 mL/hr"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Step 1: Calculate the total mcg per minute required: 70 kg x 0.1 mcg/kg/min = 7 mcg/min. Step 2: Convert mcg/min to mcg/hour: 7 mcg/min x 60 min/hr = 420 mcg/hour. Step 3: Determine the drug concentration in mcg/mL: 4 mg in 250 mL = 4,000 mcg / 250 mL = 16 mcg/mL. Step 4: Calculate the pump rate in mL/hr: 420 mcg/hr / 16 mcg/mL = 26.25 mL/hr (rounds to 26.3 mL/hr). Why the other options fail: options a and d come from using double the true concentration and double the dose respectively. Option c follows a transposed figure in the calculation. Each would deliver a dose other than the prescribed 0.1 mcg/kg/min.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vasoactive infusion formula: Rate (mL/hr) = (Dose x Weight x 60) / Concentration. Rate = (0.1 x 70 x 60) / 16 = 420 / 16 = 26.25 mL/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-EMERG-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  mojibake-repair.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- NurseGrid Prep — repair of UTF-8 text that was decoded as CP1252 ("mojibake")
-- Symptoms: an em dash stored as â€”, curly quotes as â€œ/â€™, degrees as Â°, é as Ã©.
-- Safe: only rows that actually contain a damaged sequence are touched, and the
-- script is idempotent, so it can be run more than once.
-- Run AFTER question-quality-fixes.sql.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem      = replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(stem,'â€”','—'),'â€“','–'),'â€œ','“'),'â€™','’'),'â€˜','‘'),'â€¢','•'),'â€¦','…'),'Ã—','×'),'Â°','°'),'Â±','±'),'Âµ','µ'),'Ã©','é'),'Ã¨','è'),'Ã¡','á'),'Ã³','ó'),'Ãº','ú'),'Ã±','ñ'),'Ã¼','ü'),'Ã´','ô'),'Ã§','ç'),'Ã¢','â'),'Å“','œ'),'â€¦','…'),
  rationale = replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(rationale,'â€”','—'),'â€“','–'),'â€œ','“'),'â€™','’'),'â€˜','‘'),'â€¢','•'),'â€¦','…'),'Ã—','×'),'Â°','°'),'Â±','±'),'Âµ','µ'),'Ã©','é'),'Ã¨','è'),'Ã¡','á'),'Ã³','ó'),'Ãº','ú'),'Ã±','ñ'),'Ã¼','ü'),'Ã´','ô'),'Ã§','ç'),'Ã¢','â'),'Å“','œ'),'â€¦','…'),
  strategy  = replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(strategy,'â€”','—'),'â€“','–'),'â€œ','“'),'â€™','’'),'â€˜','‘'),'â€¢','•'),'â€¦','…'),'Ã—','×'),'Â°','°'),'Â±','±'),'Âµ','µ'),'Ã©','é'),'Ã¨','è'),'Ã¡','á'),'Ã³','ó'),'Ãº','ú'),'Ã±','ñ'),'Ã¼','ü'),'Ã´','ô'),'Ã§','ç'),'Ã¢','â'),'Å“','œ'),'â€¦','…'),
  choices   = (replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(replace(choices::text,'â€”','—'),'â€“','–'),'â€œ','“'),'â€™','’'),'â€˜','‘'),'â€¢','•'),'â€¦','…'),'Ã—','×'),'Â°','°'),'Â±','±'),'Âµ','µ'),'Ã©','é'),'Ã¨','è'),'Ã¡','á'),'Ã³','ó'),'Ãº','ú'),'Ã±','ñ'),'Ã¼','ü'),'Ã´','ô'),'Ã§','ç'),'Ã¢','â'),'Å“','œ'),'â€¦','…'))::jsonb
WHERE position('â€' in stem)>0 OR position('Â°' in stem)>0 OR position('Ã©' in stem)>0 OR position('â€' in rationale)>0 OR position('Â°' in rationale)>0 OR position('Ã©' in rationale)>0 OR position('â€' in strategy)>0 OR position('Â°' in strategy)>0 OR position('â€' in choices::text)>0 OR position('Ã©' in choices::text)>0;

-- Expect a row count well below 5,510; check with:
--   SELECT count(*) FROM questions WHERE stem LIKE '%â€%' OR rationale LIKE '%â€%';
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch31-rekey.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 31: re-key. Regenerated from src/db after the letter-reference cleanup.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uterine cervical stretch during labor triggers oxytocin secretion, which produces stronger uterine contractions that further increase cervical stretch until delivery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Platelet adhesion to a ruptured vessel wall triggers the release of ADP and thromboxane A2, recruiting more platelets in an accelerating cascade until a clot forms"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Elevated blood glucose triggers pancreatic beta-cells to secrete insulin, which promotes cellular glucose uptake and returns blood glucose back toward normal baseline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lactation suckling stimulates prolactin and oxytocin release, which triggers milk let-down and stimulates further vigorous infant suckling"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A Negative Feedback mechanism is a primary homeostatic regulatory loop that detects deviations from a set point and triggers biological responses that reverse or oppose the initial stimulus, restoring equilibrium. When blood glucose rises, insulin release lowers glucose back to baseline (opposing the change). In contrast, Positive Feedback mechanisms amplify, reinforce, and accelerate the initial stimulus until a discrete endpoint is reached, as in oxytocin-mediated labor contractions, platelet aggregation in hemostasis, and the suckling-milk ejection reflex. Why the other options fail: option a is a positive feedback loop, because contractions increase the stimulus that produced them. Option b is also positive feedback, with each platelet recruiting more. Option d is positive feedback again, since suckling begets more suckling rather than returning the system to baseline.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Homeostasis: Negative feedback opposes/reverses the stimulus to restore baseline equilibrium (e.g., blood glucose, body temperature). Positive feedback amplifies the stimulus (e.g., labor, clotting).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Midsagittal (median) plane, dividing the body into equal right and left halves"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Frontal (coronal) plane, dividing the body into anterior and posterior portions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Parasagittal plane, dividing the body into unequal right and left portions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transverse (horizontal or axial) plane, dividing the body into superior and inferior portions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Transverse plane (also called horizontal or axial plane) runs horizontally, dividing the body or an organ into superior (upper) and inferior (lower) sections; standard axial CT and MRI cross-sections are viewed along this plane. A Sagittal (or midsagittal) plane divides the body vertically into right and left halves. A Frontal (or coronal) plane divides the body vertically into anterior (ventral/front) and posterior (dorsal/back) portions. Why the other options fail: option a splits the body into right and left halves, not upper and lower. Option b splits it into anterior and posterior. Option c is a vertical cut parallel to the midline, which still produces left and right portions.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anatomical planes: Transverse (axial) = superior/inferior. Sagittal = right/left. Coronal (frontal) = anterior/posterior.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Air entered the pleural cavity between the visceral and parietal pleura, abolishing the negative intrapleural pressure and collapsing the lung"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fluid accumulating in the retroperitoneal space, displacing the left kidney and splinting the diaphragm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Air trapped in the subarachnoid space after a dural tear, raising intracranial pressure and splinting the chest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bile leaking between the visceral and parietal peritoneum, irritating the diaphragm and causing referred chest pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The lungs are enclosed by a double-layered serous membrane: the visceral pleura (covering the lung surface) and the parietal pleura (lining the thoracic wall), separated by the pleural cavity containing a thin film of serous fluid under negative pressure. When penetrating trauma breaches this seal, air rushes into the potential space (pneumothorax). In a tension pneumothorax, a one-way valve effect traps air, increasing intrapleural pressure, collapsing the ipsilateral lung, shifting the mediastinum and trachea to the opposite side, and compressing the vena cava, causing obstructive shock. Why the other options fail: option b puts fluid in the retroperitoneum, which lies behind the abdominal cavity and does not collapse the lung. Option c puts air in the subarachnoid space, which is inside the skull and spinal canal. Option d puts bile in the peritoneum, which irritates the diaphragm but leaves the pleural space intact.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thoracic anatomy: Pleural cavity lies between visceral pleura (on lungs) and parietal pleura (on chest wall). Air entry collapses negative pressure, causing pneumothorax.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Water moves from the intravascular space into swollen brain cells, because the hypertonic fluid cannot cross the capillary wall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Water moves from the swollen brain interstitium into the hypertonic intravascular space, reducing cerebral oedema and intracranial pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sodium pulls water into the brain interstitium first, and the oedema resolves only when the kidneys excrete the excess"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The hypertonic gradient draws sodium into brain cells, and water follows, so the intracellular volume rises"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Osmosis is the net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration (higher water concentration) to an area of higher solute concentration (lower water concentration). Hypertonic solutions (such as 3% sodium chloride, osmolarity ~1,026 mOsm/L) have a higher solute concentration than normal intracellular fluid (~285 mOsm/L). Infusing 3% saline raises intravascular osmolarity, creating an osmotic gradient that pulls excess water out of swollen brain cells and interstitial tissue into cerebral capillaries, thereby reducing brain volume and intracranial pressure (ICP). Why the other options fail: option a reverses the gradient, because water leaves the brain when the plasma is hypertonic. Option c describes a delayed renal effect rather than the immediate osmotic shift. Option d moves sodium and water into the cells, which is the effect of hypotonic fluid.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tonicity & Osmosis: Hypertonic fluids pull water OUT of cells into blood vessels (shrinks swollen cells). Hypotonic fluids push water INTO cells (cells swell).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "ATP depletion opens the potassium channels first, so potassium enters the cell and water follows, causing swelling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, the sodium-potassium pump fails, so sodium and water enter and the cell swells and acidifies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis raises ATP above normal, and the excess energy drives calcium into the cell"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The membrane becomes impermeable to all ions, so the cell cannot regulate its volume and swells"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. Hypoxia causes: 1) cessation of aerobic ATP synthesis; 2) switch to anaerobic glycolysis, generating lactic acid and lowering cellular pH; 3) failure of the ATP-dependent Na+/K+ pump, causing sodium (and obligate water) to accumulate inside the cell while potassium leaks out, leading to hydropic cellular swelling; and 4) influx of extracellular calcium activating destructive intracellular enzymes (proteases, lipases, endonucleases), culminating in irreversible necrosis. Why the other options fail: option a has potassium entering the cell first, when the failing pump loses its gradient and sodium accumulates inside. Option c has anaerobic glycolysis raising ATP, which it cannot do as efficiently as oxidation. Option d makes the membrane impermeable to ions, which is the opposite of pump failure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cellular pathophysiology: Hypoxia -> ATP depletion -> Na+/K+ pump failure -> intracellular Na+ and water accumulation -> cell swelling and lactic acidosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Metaplasia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypertrophy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Atrophy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperplasia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Metaplasia is a reversible cellular adaptation in which one adult mature cell type (e.g., pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium) is replaced by another mature adult cell type (e.g., stratified squamous epithelium) that is better able to tolerate chronic physical or chemical irritation (such as hot cigarette smoke). However, this adaptation results in the loss of vital mucus secretion and ciliary escalator function, predisposing to chronic bronchitis, and if irritation persists, can progress to dysplasia and bronchogenic squamous cell carcinoma. Hypertrophy is increased cell size; Atrophy is decreased cell size; Hyperplasia is increased cell number. Why the other options fail: option b increases cell size without changing cell type. Option c shrinks cells without changing type. Option d increases the number of the same cells, which is not the substitution of one mature epithelium for another that the biopsy shows.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cellular adaptations: Metaplasia = conversion of one mature cell type to another (e.g., respiratory ciliated columnar to stratified squamous in smokers).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Osteoblasts resorb bone while osteoclasts build it, and PTH lowers serum calcium by blocking both"}, {"id": "b", "text": "PTH raises serum calcium by increasing intestinal absorption alone, without acting on bone cells"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Osteoclasts both build and resorb bone, and PTH switches them from one role to the other"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoblasts build bone, osteoclasts resorb it, and PTH drives osteoclastic resorption to raise serum calcium when it falls"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bone is dynamic living tissue continuously remodeled by two primary cell types: 1) OSTEOBLASTS ('bone builders') synthesize collagen matrix and promote mineral deposition (osteogenesis); 2) OSTEOCLASTS ('bone chewers/resorbers') secrete lysosomal enzymes and acids to break down bone matrix, releasing ionized calcium into systemic circulation. When serum calcium falls below normal, Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) is secreted, stimulating osteoclast activity, renal calcium reabsorption, and vitamin D activation to elevate serum calcium. Calcitonin opposes PTH. In post-menopausal osteoporosis, estrogen deficiency accelerates osteoclastic resorption over osteoblastic formation. Why the other options fail: option a swaps the two cells. Option b describes calcitonin, which lowers calcium, rather than PTH. Option c removes both cells and hormones from bone turnover, which occurs continuously.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bone cells: Osteoblasts = Build bone (deposition). Osteoclasts = Chew/break down bone (resorption). PTH stimulates osteoclasts to raise serum calcium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The plate has no nerve supply, so injuries there are painless and go unrecognised until deformity appears"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The plate is the growth zone, and injury can cause premature or asymmetric closure with length discrepancy or angulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The plate is the weakest part of the bone, so fractures there heal more slowly than shaft fractures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The plate carries the blood supply to the epiphysis, so injury causes avascular necrosis of the whole bone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In growing children, longitudinal bone elongation occurs at the epiphyseal plate (growth plate or physis), located between the epiphysis and diaphysis of long bones. It contains active chondrocytes undergoing endochondral ossification. Traumatic disruption (Salter-Harris fracture) can disrupt vascular supply and damage proliferating cartilage, leading to premature bony bridging (epiphyseal arrest), asymmetric bone growth, angular deformities, or significant limb-length discrepancies. Bone marrow in the medullary cavity and flat bones produces RBCs; muscles attach to the periosteum via tendons. Why the other options fail: option b makes the plate the site of red cell production, which is marrow. Option c has muscle attaching directly to marrow. Option d gives the plate no blood supply and a malignant outcome, neither of which is the concern described.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pediatric bone anatomy: Epiphyseal plate fractures (Salter-Harris) damage growth cartilage, risking premature growth arrest and permanent limb discrepancy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Osteoarthritis follows a low-grade synovial infection, while rheumatoid arthritis is a purely genetic disorder of cartilage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Osteoarthritis is degenerative cartilage loss with subchondral remodelling; rheumatoid arthritis is autoimmune synovitis with pannus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rheumatoid arthritis affects weight-bearing joints asymmetrically, while osteoarthritis is always symmetric and seronegative"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoarthritis affects the synovium first, while rheumatoid arthritis begins in subchondral bone and spares the synovium"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A synovial (diarthrodial) joint consists of articular hyaline cartilage, a fibrous capsule lined by a synovial membrane, and synovial lubricating fluid. OSTEOARTHRITIS is primarily a biomechanical degenerative disorder characterized by progressive degradation of articular cartilage, osteophyte (bone spur) formation, and joint space narrowing, classically exacerbated by weight-bearing and use. RHEUMATOID ARTHRITIS is a systemic autoimmune disease where autoantibodies target the synovial membrane, causing inflammatory synovitis, microvascular proliferation, and invasive hyperplastic pannus that erodes articular cartilage and bone symmetrically. Why the other options fail: option a makes osteoarthritis infectious, when it is mechanical and degenerative. Option c reverses the symmetry and distribution of the two diseases. Option d denies the articular cartilage that both diseases destroy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Synovial joint pathology: Osteoarthritis = cartilage wear-and-tear (subchondral bone spurs). Rheumatoid arthritis = autoimmune synovial inflammation (pannus formation).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Calcium binds tropomyosin, which pulls troponin aside so that myosin heads can bind actin and shorten the sarcomere"}, {"id": "b", "text": "ATP hydrolysis extends actin filaments while myosin filaments shorten, which draws the Z lines together"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sodium entering the T-tubules binds troponin and exposes the actin sites for the myosin heads"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, moving tropomyosin so myosin can cross-bridge and execute the power stroke"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The sliding filament mechanism of skeletal muscle contraction follows a precise molecular sequence: 1) Motor neuron action potential releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction; 2) End-plate depolarization propagates down transverse tubules (T-tubules); 3) Voltage-gated channels trigger calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; 4) Calcium binds to TROPONIN, inducing a conformational shift in TROPOMYOSIN that uncovers active myosin-binding sites on ACTIN; 5) Energized MYOSIN heads bind to actin, forming cross-bridges; 6) The power stroke pulls actin filaments toward the sarcomere center (M-line), hydrolyzing ATP; 7) New ATP binding releases myosin from actin. Filaments do not change length; they slide past each other. Why the other options fail: option a dissolves the myosin heads, which are not consumed by contraction. Option b lengthens actin and shortens myosin, when the filaments slide past each other without changing length. Option c gives the trigger to sodium and collagen, when calcium and troponin are the switch.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Muscle contraction sequence: Action potential -> Calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum -> Calcium binds troponin -> Tropomyosin moves -> Myosin-actin cross-bridge power stroke.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autoantibodies block nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor end-plate, so the muscle cannot depolarise"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Excess acetylcholinesterase at the end-plate breaks down acetylcholine before it can act"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Autoantibodies destroy the presynaptic calcium channels, so acetylcholine is never released into the cleft"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autoantibodies block the muscarinic receptors on the muscle membrane, preventing depolarisation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Myasthenia Gravis (MG) is an autoimmune disease targeting the neuromuscular junction. Circulating autoantibodies (primarily against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor [AChR] on the postsynaptic muscle sarcolemma) block acetylcholine binding and accelerate receptor degradation. With repetitive motor neuron firing, acetylcholine stores deplete while fewer functional receptors remain available, resulting in progressive muscle fatigability that worsens with activity and improves with rest (e.g., ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, and potentially fatal myasthenic respiratory crisis). ALS destroys motor neurons; Guillain-Barré causes demyelination. Why the other options fail: Excess acetylcholinesterase would break acetylcholine down faster than it is released, which is not the antibody mechanism; destruction of the presynaptic calcium channels is Lambert-Eaton, which improves with use; muscarinic blockade on the muscle membrane is not how the end-plate fails.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neuromuscular junction: Myasthenia Gravis = autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors -> muscle weakness and fatigability.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It lies away from the sciatic nerve and major vessels, and it has a thick muscle mass for a viscous drug"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It has fewer nerve endings, so an irritating drug causes less pain and is absorbed more slowly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It avoids the superior gluteal artery, and it lies within the safe triangle bounded by the iliac crest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It lies over the gluteus maximus, which has a larger surface area than the dorsogluteal site"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Anatomical landmarks make the Ventrogluteal site (comprising the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus muscles, land-marked by the greater trochanter, anterior superior iliac spine, and iliac crest) the gold standard for adult deep IM injections. It provides a deep muscle belly, consistent subcutaneous fat thickness, and is entirely free of major nerves and large blood vessels. In contrast, the traditional Dorsogluteal site carries documented hazards of accidental needle impalement of the Sciatic Nerve (causing permanent foot drop and paralysis) or superior gluteal artery injury, as well as unpredictable deposition into deep adipose tissue rather than muscle. Why the other options fail: option a says the muscle has no blood supply, which no muscle has. Option b prefers a site for its nerve supply, when the aim is to avoid nerves. Option d compares the dorsogluteal fat with the deltoid, which does not address the risk of sciatic injury.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IM injection anatomy: Ventrogluteal (gluteus medius/minimus) is safest—deep muscle belly, no major nerves. Dorsogluteal is unsafe due to proximity to the Sciatic Nerve.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of chief cells removes pepsinogen, so protein cannot be digested and iron absorption falls, causing microcytic anaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of acid allows bacterial overgrowth, which consumes vitamin B12 and causes a macrocytic anaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Loss of intrinsic factor reduces folate absorption in the jejunum, causing a macrocytic anaemia that responds to folate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum, causing pernicious anaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The gastric mucosa contains specialized secretory glands in the fundus and body: 1) PARIETAL CELLS secrete Hydrochloric Acid (HCl, establishing an acidic pH for pepsin activation and pathogen destruction) and INTRINSIC FACTOR (IF). Intrinsic factor binds dietary Vitamin B12 (cobalamin), protecting it from digestive degradation and facilitating receptor-mediated absorption in the terminal ileum. Following total gastrectomy (or autoimmune destruction of parietal cells in pernicious anemia), intrinsic factor is completely absent, preventing B12 absorption and resulting in macrocytic megaloblastic anemia and progressive neurological demyelination (subacute combined degeneration). Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Why the other options fail: option a blames chief cells and pepsinogen for calcium, which they do not regulate. Option b blames acid loss for sickle cell disease, which is inherited. Option c blames pepsinogen for haemophilia, which is a clotting factor deficiency.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gastric physiology: Parietal cells secrete HCl and Intrinsic Factor. Intrinsic Factor is mandatory for Vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bile salts from the gallbladder emulsify fat, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea, while bilirubin is excreted by the kidney"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bile salts are needed for fat digestion, but bilirubin reaches the gut in the blood and is excreted in the urine instead"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bile salts from the liver emulsify fat and carry bilirubin into the gut, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea and pale stools"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bile acids activate pancreatic lipase in the duodenum, and obstruction blocks the enzyme rather than the bile"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bile is produced continuously by hepatocytes in the liver, concentrated in the gallbladder, and released into the duodenum via the common bile duct in response to cholecystokinin (CCK). Bile salts act as biological detergents, emulsifying large dietary fat globules into microscopic micelles to provide a vast surface area for pancreatic lipase digestion. Bile also excretes conjugated bilirubin into the intestinal lumen, where normal bacterial flora metabolize it into stercobilin (giving feces their brown color). When choledocholithiasis obstructs bile outflow, fats cannot be absorbed (steatorrhea), feces lack stercobilin (clay-colored stools), and water-soluble conjugated bilirubin backs up into circulation, spilling into urine (dark amber) and skin (jaundice). Why the other options fail: Saying bilirubin reaches the gut in the blood and leaves in the urine, making the gallbladder the source of bile and sending bilirubin to the kidney, and having bile acids activate pancreatic lipase are all wrong: the liver secretes bile, bile carries bilirubin into the gut, and obstruction backs conjugated bilirubin into the blood.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hepatobiliary physiology: Bile emulsifies dietary fats and excretes bilirubin. Common bile duct obstruction causes steatorrhea, clay-colored stools, and jaundice.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of villi removes the brush border enzymes and the surface area, so fat, carbohydrate, protein, iron and folate all malabsorb"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of villi reduces the surface area for water absorption alone, producing diarrhoea without nutrient loss"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The villi regenerate within days after gluten withdrawal, so the absorptive defect is transient and self-limiting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Villous atrophy blocks the lymphatic lacteals, so only fat is malabsorbed while sugars and proteins are spared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The small intestine (duodenum, jejunum, ileum) is structurally engineered for maximum nutrient absorption. Three levels of mucosal folding—circular folds (plicae circulares), finger-like mucosal villi, and apical enterocyte microvilli (the brush border)—amplify the internal absorptive surface area hundreds of times. In celiac disease, immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by gluten ingestion destroys enterocytes, blunting villi and causing crypt hyperplasia. This drastic loss of functional surface area and brush border enzymes (lactase, peptidases) causes severe global malabsorption, chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, iron deficiency anemia, and metabolic bone disease. Villi do not produce acid. Why the other options fail: option a gives the villi an acid-secreting role, which belongs to the stomach. Option b makes the villi a filter for red cells. Option c says recovery is immediate and complete, when untreated coeliac disease causes continuing malabsorption.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Intestinal histology: Villi and microvilli maximize mucosal absorptive surface area. Villi atrophy in celiac disease causes generalized nutrient malabsorption.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stem cells in the basal layer divide and migrate upward, keratinise and die to form the dead, anucleate stratum corneum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The corneum retains living nuclei and divides to replace cells lost from the basal layer"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keratinocytes are produced by the dermal collagen and migrate inward toward the subcutaneous fat"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The basal layer contains melanocytes but no dividing cells, so the epidermis is renewed from the dermis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The epidermis is a keratinized stratified squamous epithelium organized into 5 layers (from deep to superficial: Stratum Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum [thick skin only], and Corneum). The Stratum Basale contains single-layered stem cells undergoing active mitosis. As daughter keratinocytes are pushed superficially, they synthesize keratin intermediate filaments, produce keratohyalin and lamellar lipid granules in the granulosum (providing a hydrophobic water barrier), lose their nuclei and organelles, and flatten into the Stratum Corneum—a tough, horn-like layer of 15–30 sheets of dead keratin-packed squames that desquamates continuously (taking roughly 28–40 days from birth to shed). Why the other options fail: option a puts living dividing cells in the corneum, which is dead. Option c moves keratinocyte production to the dermis and reverses the direction of migration. Option d denies the dividing stem cells and melanocytes that the basal layer contains.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Epidermal layers (deep to superficial): Basale (mitosis/stem cells) -> Spinosum -> Granulosum -> Lucidum -> Corneum (dead keratinized protective barrier).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cutaneous vasoconstriction and piloerection trap warm air against the skin and conserve heat"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sweating is suppressed and blood is shunted to the viscera, so heat is retained in the core"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cutaneous vasodilation increases skin blood flow and eccrine sweating drives evaporative cooling"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eccrine glands secrete a hypertonic fluid that cools the skin by conduction rather than evaporation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The preoptic anterior hypothalamus functions as the body's primary thermoregulatory thermostat. When core temperature rises above the set point, hypothalamic autonomic signaling initiates two primary heat-loss mechanisms: 1) CUTANEOUS VASODILATION: Dermal precapillary sphincters relax, redirecting warm blood from the body core to superficial subpapillary dermal capillary loops, allowing heat to radiate, conduct, and convect into the cooler ambient environment; 2) ECCRINE SWEAT GLAND ACTIVATION: Cholinergic sympathetic nerves stimulate eccrine glands across the body to secrete watery sweat, which absorbs thermal energy (latent heat of vaporization) as it evaporates from the skin. Why the other options fail: option a constricts the skin vessels and raises the hairs, which conserves heat rather than losing it. Option b suppresses sweating and sends blood to the viscera, which is the cold response. Option d has sweat cooling by conduction and being hypertonic, when sweat is hypotonic and cools by evaporation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thermoregulation in heat: Hypothalamus triggers cutaneous vasodilation (radiates heat) and eccrine sweating (evaporative cooling).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Full-thickness loss of the epidermis and dermis, including nerves and appendages, leaving a leathery insensate eschar"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Destruction of the epidermis and the upper dermis only, with pain preserved because the nerve endings remain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Destruction of the epidermis alone, with the dermis intact and the wound moist and painful"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Destruction of the dermis and subcutaneous fat, with the epidermis spared and the surface blistered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Full-thickness (third-degree) burns extend completely through the epidermis and the entire thickness of the dermis, often extending into the underlying hypodermis (subcutaneous tissue). Because all dermal appendages—including the dermal capillary plexus and sensory nerve endings (nociceptors, Meissner's, Merkel's, and free nerve terminals)—are completely incinerated, the wound appears leathery, waxy white, brown, or charred (eschar) and is completely insensitive to light touch and pinprick (anesthetic). However, peripheral zones of partial-thickness burn surrounding the wound remain intensely painful. Why the other options fail: Epidermis and upper dermis only is a partial-thickness burn with pain preserved; epidermis alone is superficial and stays moist and painful; dermis and fat with the epidermis spared and a blistered surface is not a leathery insensate eschar.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Burn depth anatomy: Full-thickness (3rd degree) destroys epidermis AND dermis, incinerating sensory nerve endings, making the core eschar painless and dry/leathery.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute respiratory distress syndrome from the trauma itself, which gives bilateral infiltrates without petechiae"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fat embolism syndrome: marrow fat enters torn venous sinusoids, lodges in the pulmonary capillaries and causes petechiae"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pulmonary embolism from a deep vein thrombosis, which gives dyspnoea and hypoxia but no petechial rash"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sepsis from osteomyelitis at the fracture site, which gives fever and hypotension but not the rash"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Fat Embolism Syndrome (FES) is a life-threatening systemic complication occurring 24 to 72 hours following closed fractures of major long bones (especially the femur or tibia) or pelvic crush trauma. Disrupted bone marrow adipose tissue and medullary venous sinusoids allow fat globules to enter the systemic venous circulation. These fat emboli occlude pulmonary microvascular capillaries, triggering lipolysis into toxic free fatty acids that induce diffuse alveolar endothelial injury, acute lung injury (ARDS), ventilation-perfusion mismatch (severe hypoxemia), cerebral microvascular occlusion (confusion, altered mental status), and thrombocytopenic microvascular fragility (pathognomonic non-palpable petechiae over the neck, axilla, and chest). Why the other options fail: ARDS from the trauma gives bilateral infiltrates without petechiae; pulmonary embolism from a deep vein thrombosis gives dyspnoea and hypoxia without a rash; osteomyelitis sepsis gives fever and hypotension without the petechiae that appear with the confusion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Trauma complication: Femur fracture + Acute hypoxia + Neurological confusion + Axillary petechial rash = Fat Embolism Syndrome.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hyperactive sounds reflect increased secretion rather than contraction, and the fluid loss is into the peritoneum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The bowel proximal to the block dilates and becomes atonic, so sounds are absent above and tinkling below"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Proximal muscle contracts against the block while distal bowel is quiet, and fluid is lost into the lumen"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The obstruction reverses peristalsis, so the bowel empties proximally and the abdomen becomes silent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In mechanical small bowel obstruction (e.g., from postoperative adhesions or incarcerated hernias), physical blockage prevents the distal progression of chyme. Proximally, the smooth muscle layers (muscularis externa: circular and longitudinal fibers) mount vigorous hyper-peristaltic contractions to overcome the resistance, manifesting early as high-pitched, metallic 'tinkling' sounds and audible peristaltic rushes. Proximal intraluminal accumulation of swallowed air and digestive secretions (7–8 L/day of saliva, gastric juice, bile, and pancreatic fluids) increases wall tension, compresses venous drainage, causes bowel wall edema, and sequesters massive fluid into the third space, leading to hypovolemic shock and electrolyte collapse. Why the other options fail: An atonic bowel above the block reverses the findings, because the bowel above is hyperactive and the bowel below is quiet; sounds of increased secretion misread peristalsis; reversed peristalsis with a silent abdomen is not the high-pitched rush of mechanical obstruction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$GI obstruction: Muscularis externa contracts vigorously against obstruction -> high-pitched tinkling sounds proximal to obstruction -> intraluminal third-spacing and hypovolemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Preformed anti-A IgM binds donor A antigen, fixes complement and lyses red cells intravascularly, releasing free haemoglobin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Donor red cells contain potassium that precipitates with calcium in the renal tubules, causing flank pain and oliguria"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Donor white cells release cytokines that activate recipient complement and cause pulmonary capillary leak"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recipient antibodies coat donor platelets, causing their sequestration in the spleen and a febrile reaction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Individuals with type O blood produce pre-formed naturally occurring IgM antibodies (isohemagglutinins) against both A and B erythrocyte surface antigens. When type A red blood cells are erroneously infused, recipient anti-A IgM antibodies immediately bind to the donor red cell A antigens, triggering the classical complement pathway (membrane attack complex C5b-9). This causes rapid, catastrophic intravascular hemolysis, releasing free hemoglobin into plasma (causing hemoglobinuria and acute tubular necrosis/flank pain), activating clotting factor XII (disseminated intravascular coagulation), and releasing inflammatory anaphylatoxins (C3a, C5a) that produce hypotension, bronchospasm, and shock. Why the other options fail: option b invents potassium-calcium stones, which are not a transfusion reaction. Option c blames albumin neutralising white cells, which is not a mechanism. Option d has donor blood mutating recipient marrow, which cannot happen within ten minutes or at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Transfusion immunology: Type O recipients have anti-A and anti-B antibodies. Infusing type A blood causes immediate complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis, shock, and acute renal failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Platelets expose phospholipid and release calcium, which converts prothrombin directly into fibrin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Thrombin activates protein C, which cleaves fibrinogen into the insoluble mesh that forms the clot"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Factor Xa converts prothrombin to thrombin, which cleaves fibrinogen into cross-linked fibrin strands"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tissue factor converts fibrinogen directly into fibrin without the need for thrombin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hemostasis occurs in two interconnected phases: 1) Primary hemostasis involves transient vasoconstriction, von Willebrand factor binding to exposed subendothelial collagen, and platelet adhesion, activation, and aggregation to form an unstable platelet plug; 2) Secondary hemostasis involves the coagulation cascade (intrinsic and extrinsic pathways), which converge at the common pathway: Factor X is activated to Factor Xa (with Factor Va, calcium, and phospholipids forming the prothrombinase complex). This complex converts prothrombin into active THROMBIN. Thrombin then cleaves soluble circulating FIBRINOGEN into insoluble FIBRIN monomers, which are cross-linked by Factor XIIIa into a resilient, insoluble fibrin mesh that stabilizes the clot. Why the other options fail: option a stops at platelet phospholipid and converts prothrombin without thrombin. Option b uses protein C, which is an anticoagulant and does the opposite. Option d skips thrombin, and fibrinogen cannot be converted to fibrin without it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Coagulation cascade: The common pathway converges on Factor Xa converting Prothrombin to Thrombin, which cleaves Fibrinogen into stable Fibrin mesh.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Widespread coagulation consumes platelets and factors, so the patient bleeds while clots form in the microcirculation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vasodilation and capillary leak produce the bleeding, while the coagulation cascade remains inactive throughout"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Microthrombi consume platelets and factors, but fibrinolysis is suppressed, so the patient clots without bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fibrinogen is consumed faster than the liver can replace it, so bleeding occurs while coagulation tests stay normal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) is a complex consumptive coagulopathy triggered by systemic endothelial injury, endotoxins, or tissue factor release in severe sepsis, trauma, or obstetric emergencies. Massive systemic activation of thrombin creates widespread microvascular fibrin thrombi that occlude small arterioles and capillaries, causing end-organ ischemia and tissue infarction (e.g., gangrene of digits, renal cortical necrosis). Paradoxically, this relentless micro-thrombosis completely exhausts the body's circulating stores of platelets, fibrinogen, and clotting factors (consumptive phase), while secondary hyperfibrinolysis generates high levels of fibrin degradation products (D-dimer) that act as potent anticoagulants, producing catastrophic spontaneous hemorrhage (oozing from IV sites, mucosal bleeding, internal hematomas). Why the other options fail: option a describes polycythaemia, the opposite of the consumptive thrombocytopenia of DIC. Option c suppresses fibrinolysis, but DIC produces both clotting and bleeding. Option d turns fibrinogen into gastric acid, which is not a process.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DIC pathophysiology: Widespread microvascular thrombosis -> consumption of platelets and clotting factors -> simultaneous thrombosis and severe hemorrhagic bleeding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tachycardia shortens diastole, when most coronary flow and ventricular filling occur, while oxygen demand rises"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tachycardia increases blood velocity, which shears red cells and reduces oxygen delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tachycardia compresses the coronary arteries during systole and reduces flow to the subendocardium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tachycardia increases oxygen extraction to its maximum, so any further demand cannot be met"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Unlike all other systemic vascular beds which are perfused during systole, the left ventricular myocardium is perfused almost entirely during ventricular DIASTOLE. During systole, high intramyocardial compressive forces compress subendocardial coronary capillaries, and open aortic valve leaflets partially shield the coronary ostia. When the aortic valve closes in diastole, coronary perfusion pressure peaks. At rapid heart rates (e.g., >150–180 bpm), the cardiac cycle shortens primarily at the expense of diastolic duration (diastolic filling time plummets), severely curtailing coronary perfusion time. Simultaneously, tachycardia skyrockets myocardial metabolic oxygen consumption (MVO2), producing acute supply-demand mismatch and profound subendocardial ischemia (angina and ST depressions). Why the other options fail: Shear haemolysis from higher blood velocity does not limit oxygen delivery at this rate; systolic compression puts the squeeze in the wrong phase, because coronary flow is predominantly diastolic; oxygen extraction is already near maximal at rest, but that is not the mechanism that produces ischaemia when demand rises.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Coronary physiology: Coronary blood flow occurs during DIASTOLE. Tachycardia shortens diastolic time + increases oxygen demand -> triggers myocardial ischemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Obstruction raises left ventricular end-diastolic pressure, which backs up into the pulmonary veins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Obstruction causes blood to back up into the vena cavae, raising jugular venous pressure before the lungs are affected"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obstruction increases left ventricular systolic pressure, causing concentric hypertrophy and aortic dilatation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obstruction to left ventricular filling raises left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures, causing congestion and later right heart failure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The mitral (bicuspid) valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle. In mitral stenosis, fusion and calcification of the valve leaflets obstruct blood flow during diastole from the left atrium into the left ventricle. This anatomical restriction creates a high pressure gradient, causing marked left atrial dilation and elevated left atrial pressure. Because pulmonary veins lack valves, this elevated pressure transmits retrograde directly into the pulmonary venous circulation and pulmonary capillaries. When hydrostatic capillary pressure exceeds plasma oncotic pressure (~25 mmHg), fluid transudates into the pulmonary interstitium and alveoli (orthopnea, dyspnea, crackles, pulmonary edema). Chronic pulmonary venous hypertension induces pulmonary arteriolar remodeling, pulmonary arterial hypertension, and secondary right ventricular hypertrophy and failure. Why the other options fail: option a puts the obstruction after the ventricle, but the block is at the mitral valve, so ventricular filling pressure is low, not high. Option b backs blood into the vena cavae first, which happens late and only after the lungs. Option c raises systolic pressure, which stenosis does not do.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Valvular hemodynamics: Mitral stenosis -> elevated left atrial pressure -> retrograde pulmonary venous congestion -> pulmonary edema and right heart failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Venodilation reduces afterload so the left ventricle empties more completely, and the failing ventricle recovers its stroke volume"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Venodilation increases venous capacitance, so venous return falls, end-diastolic pressure drops and pulmonary congestion eases"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Venodilation dilates the pulmonary arterioles directly, so blood is redistributed away from the congested alveoli"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Venodilation increases coronary perfusion pressure, so the ischaemic myocardium recovers its contractility"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cardiac Output is determined by Heart Rate and Stroke Volume (CO = HR x SV). Stroke volume depends on PRELOAD (ventricular stretch/end-diastolic volume), AFTERLOAD (resistance to ventricular ejection), and CONTRACTILITY. According to the Frank-Starling law, physiological stretching of myocardial fibers optimizes sarcomere actin-myosin overlap. However, in decompensated heart failure, excessive volume overload stretches fibers past their optimal length onto the descending limb of the Starling curve, producing severe wall stress and pulmonary venous congestion. Venodilators like nitroglycerin pool blood in peripheral capacitance veins, decreasing venous return (preload) to the heart. This relieves excessive ventricular end-diastolic wall tension and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure, alleviating dyspnea and pulmonary edema without imposing extra metabolic workload. Why the other options fail: option a reduces afterload rather than preload, which is the effect of an arterial dilator. Option c dilates the pulmonary arterioles, which is not what nitroglycerin does at venous doses. Option d improves coronary perfusion, which is not the Frank-Starling mechanism asked for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cardiac output determinants: Preload = ventricular end-diastolic volume/stretch. Nitroglycerin venodilation reduces venous return (preload), lowering pulmonary congestion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Baroreceptors fire more rapidly when the pressure falls, driving vagal bradycardia and vasodilation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Falling stretch reduces afferent firing, disinhibiting the medulla, so sympathetic outflow raises rate, contractility and resistance"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Baroreceptors trigger histamine release, producing vasodilation and capillary leak that restore flow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Baroreceptors stop firing at low pressure, so the medulla loses all control of vascular tone until volume is restored"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Arterial baroreceptors (stretch-sensitive mechanoreceptors located within the carotid sinus [innervated by CN IX] and aortic arch [innervated by CN X]) constantly monitor arterial wall distension. In acute hemorrhage, decreased intravascular volume lowers mean arterial pressure and decreases wall stretch, leading to a marked decrease in afferent nerve firing to the nucleus tractus solitarius in the medulla oblongata. This loss of inhibitory input disinhibits sympathetic autonomic outflow while simultaneously withdrawing parasympathetic (vagal) tone. Sympathetic stimulation releases norepinephrine at beta-1 receptors (causing compensatory tachycardia and increased stroke volume) and alpha-1 receptors (causing arteriolar vasoconstriction, elevating systemic vascular resistance, and venous constriction to autotransfuse blood to the central core). Why the other options fail: option a has the receptors firing faster as pressure falls, when stretch falls and firing stops. Option c brings in histamine, which is not part of the baroreflex. Option d says the reflex stops working at low pressure, when that is exactly when it is most active.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Baroreceptor reflex: Hypovolemia -> decreased baroreceptor stretch -> sympathetic activation (tachycardia, vasoconstriction, increased contractility) to support MAP.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Compensatory vasoconstriction fails in the arterioles, but the venules constrict and the back-pressure forces fluid into the tissues"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis lowers ATP, so the precapillary sphincters close while the venules dilate, and blood is trapped in the capillary bed without leaking"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prolonged hypoperfusion causes vasodilation of both arterioles and venules, so the pressure falls without capillary leak"}, {"id": "d", "text": "ATP depletion opens capillary beds but the venules dilate first, so blood pools and lactate falls as flow improves"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the progressive (decompensated) stage of shock, homeostatic compensatory reflexes are overwhelmed. Persistent severe tissue hypoperfusion starves cells of oxygen, forcing prolonged anaerobic metabolism that produces heavy lactic acidosis (lactate >4–6 mmol/L). Local acidemia and hyperkalemia paralyze the precapillary arteriolar sphincters (causing them to dilate), while postcapillary venules remain constricted due to sympathetic acidosis resistance. This anatomical imbalance skyrockets hydrostatic capillary pressure, driving fluid and plasma proteins out of the vascular space into the interstitium (third-spacing and hemoconcentration). Sludged red cells aggregate in microvessels, producing microthrombi and irreversible end-organ cellular necrosis (MODS). Why the other options fail: Failure of arteriolar tone with the venules still constricted is close, but it is the capillary hydrostatic pressure that drives the leak, not back-pressure; vasodilation of both sides would not produce sludging with third-spacing; venules dilating first contradicts the sequence, and lactate rises rather than falls as flow worsens.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stages of shock: Progressive stage = compensatory failure -> anaerobic lactic acidosis -> precapillary sphincter failure with postcapillary venular constriction -> massive capillary leak and MODS.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lymph nodes produce albumin, so removing them lowers plasma oncotic pressure throughout the body"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Axillary lymphatics removed, so protein-rich interstitial fluid cannot drain and accumulates, with later fibroadipose thickening"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The incision severed the brachial artery, so arterial inflow stops and the limb swells"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lymphatic obstruction raises capillary hydrostatic pressure by increasing venous pressure in the limb"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under normal microvascular Starling equilibrium, capillary hydrostatic pressure filtration slightly exceeds plasma colloid osmotic reabsorption, resulting in an obligate net filtration of 2 to 4 liters of fluid per day into the interstitial space. The lymphatic system functions as the exclusive drainage network that vacuums up this fluid and extravasated macromolecules (proteins, fats, cellular debris) and channels it back into the venous circulation via the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct into the subclavian veins. Surgical resection or radiation of axillary lymph nodes obliterates this anatomical drainage conduit. High-protein fluid stagnates in subcutaneous tissue, elevating tissue oncotic pressure, attracting more fluid, triggering chronic inflammation, fibroblast activation, and progressive fibroadipose deposition (lymphedema). This is why no blood pressure cuffs, IV insertions, or venipunctures are permitted on the affected arm. Why the other options fail: option a has lymph nodes making albumin, which they do not. Option c severs the brachial artery, which would cause ischaemia, not oedema. Option d raises capillary hydrostatic pressure, which is the mechanism of venous oedema, not of lymphatic obstruction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lymphatic physiology: Lymphatics return interstitial fluid and extravasated proteins to the venous circulation. Resection causes high-protein interstitial accumulation (lymphedema).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Surfactant is produced by type I cells and coats the alveoli, preventing them from over-expanding during inspiration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Surfactant increases surface tension in small alveoli so that they empty into larger ones and equalise pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Surfactant is a lipoprotein that keeps the alveolar walls adherent, preventing them from separating during inspiration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Surfactant is produced by type II cells and lowers surface tension, so small alveoli do not collapse at end-expiration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Law of Laplace states that the collapsing inward pressure (P) inside an elastic sphere is directly proportional to surface tension (T) and inversely proportional to radius (r): P = 2T / r. Without surfactant, smaller alveoli (smaller r) would generate much higher collapsing pressures than larger alveoli, causing smaller alveoli to empty their air into larger ones and collapse completely (diffuse micro-atelectasis). Pulmonary surfactant (a complex lipoprotein predominantly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine secreted by Type II alveolar pneumocytes beginning around 24–28 weeks and maturing around 35 weeks) intersperses between water molecules at the alveolar air-liquid interface. It markedly reduces surface tension, equalizes pressure across alveoli of varying sizes, increases pulmonary compliance, and prevents end-expiratory collapse. In NRDS, lack of surfactant causes massive atelectasis, severe V/Q mismatch, and respiratory failure. Why the other options fail: Giving surfactant to type I cells is wrong, because type I cells cover the gas-exchange surface and do not secrete it; raising surface tension is the opposite of surfactant action; making the alveolar walls adherent would prevent expansion rather than prevent collapse.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alveolar biophysics: Type II pneumocytes secrete surfactant (dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine), which reduces alveolar surface tension (Laplace law) and prevents end-expiratory atelectasis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The curve shifts right as pH falls and temperature rises, so haemoglobin releases oxygen more readily to the tissues"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The curve shifts left as carbon dioxide rises, so oxygen is held more tightly by haemoglobin in the tissues"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The curve shifts right, but the effect is small and oxygen delivery is unchanged while the acidosis persists"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The curve is unaffected by pH, because only 2,3-DPG changes the affinity of haemoglobin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve describes the relationship between partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2) and percentage of hemoglobin saturation (SaO2). A shift to the RIGHT (Bohr effect) occurs when tissues are metabolically active and starved for oxygen, triggered by: 1) Decreased pH (acidosis / elevated H+); 2) Increased PaCO2 (hypercapnia); 3) Increased temperature (fever/hyperthermia); and 4) Increased 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG). A rightward shift lowers hemoglobin's binding affinity for oxygen, meaning hemoglobin more readily releases and unloads oxygen into ischemic capillary beds at any given PaO2. Conversely, a shift to the LEFT (caused by alkalosis, hypothermia, hypocapnia, or carboxyhemoglobin) increases oxygen affinity, holding oxygen tightly and impairing tissue offloading. Why the other options fail: option a shifts the curve left, which would hold oxygen and worsen delivery. Option b shifts it left as carbon dioxide rises, when carbon dioxide shifts it right. Option d denies the effect of pH, carbon dioxide and temperature altogether.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Oxyhemoglobin curve: CADET face RIGHT (CO2, Acidity/H+, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temp). Right shift = decreased affinity = easier O2 unloading to tissues.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Arterial carbon dioxide, sensed by central chemoreceptors in the medulla that respond to the hydrogen ions it generates"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Arterial oxygen saturation, sensed by peripheral chemoreceptors that fire when saturation falls below 95%"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arterial pH alone, sensed by aortic baroreceptors that adjust both rate and depth"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Carbon dioxide, sensed by carotid body chemoreceptors that respond directly to the gas itself"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In healthy individuals, the primary chemical driver of ventilation is arterial PaCO2 (normal 35–45 mmHg), not oxygen. Carbon dioxide is lipid-soluble and diffuses freely across the blood-brain barrier into cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). In CSF, CO2 hydrates to form carbonic acid (H2CO3), which dissociates into bicarbonate (HCO3-) and hydrogen ions (H+). Because CSF contains minimal protein buffering capacity, even minor rises in PaCO2 (hypercapnia) cause rapid drops in CSF pH. These free H+ ions directly excite Central Chemoreceptors situated on the ventrolateral surface of the medulla oblongata, signaling the respiratory rhythmicity center to increase minute ventilation (rate and tidal volume) to blow off excess CO2. Peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) respond primarily to severe hypoxemia (PaO2 < 60 mmHg). Why the other options fail: option a senses potassium in the kidney, which does not drive ventilation. Option b uses tactile corpuscles for oxygen saturation, which do not exist. Option c uses glucose and hypothalamic osmoreceptors, which regulate thirst and ADH, not breathing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Control of ventilation: Primary chemical drive is PaCO2 stimulating central chemoreceptors in the medulla oblongata via CSF pH/H+ changes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "NSAIDs constrict the efferent arteriole while the ACE inhibitor dilates the afferent, so blood is shunted from the cortex"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both drugs constrict the afferent arteriole, so renal blood flow falls and creatinine rises without a change in filtration pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "NSAIDs constrict the afferent arteriole by removing prostaglandins, and the ACE inhibitor dilates the efferent, so filtration pressure collapses"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The ACE inhibitor reduces aldosterone, so sodium loss drops the blood pressure and the creatinine rises from prerenal causes alone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) depends on intraglomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, which is regulated by opposing tone in the afferent (inflow) and efferent (outflow) arterioles. 1) Renal prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2) selectively vasodilate the AFFERENT arteriole to maintain inflow during low perfusion states. NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX), blocking prostaglandin synthesis, causing afferent vasoconstriction and starving the glomerulus of blood flow; 2) Angiotensin II selectively vasoconstricts the EFFERENT arteriole, maintaining intraglomerular pressure (like putting a thumb on a garden hose). ACE inhibitors block angiotensin II, causing efferent vasodilation and depressurizing the glomerular capillary bed. Combining NSAIDs (afferent clamp) and ACE inhibitors/ARBs (efferent open) destroys intraglomerular pressure, precipitating acute prerenal kidney injury ('triple whammy' with diuretics). Why the other options fail: option a reverses the arterioles, giving the NSAID the efferent and the ACE inhibitor the afferent. Option b makes both drugs constrict the afferent, which is not how ACE inhibitors act. Option d explains the creatinine by prerenal volume loss alone, which cannot account for a rise this size in five days.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Renal hemodynamics: Prostaglandins dilate Afferent arteriole; Angiotensin II constricts Efferent arteriole. NSAID (afferent constriction) + ACEI (efferent dilation) collapses GFR.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Renin from juxtaglomerular cells activates angiotensinogen directly to angiotensin II, bypassing the pulmonary step, so angiotensin-converting enzyme plays no part in the cascade at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The macula densa releases aldosterone, which drives sodium reabsorption, retains water and raises the blood pressure without any change in renin secretion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Juxtaglomerular cells release renin, which converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I; ACE in the lung converts it to angiotensin II, which constricts vessels and drives aldosterone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Juxtaglomerular cells release antidiuretic hormone, which retains water and restores the circulating volume without changing vascular tone or sodium handling anywhere"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS) is the body's primary long-term regulator of blood pressure and extracellular fluid volume: 1) Juxtaglomerular granular cells in the afferent arteriole detect decreased renal perfusion pressure (and macula densa senses low tubular NaCl), releasing the enzyme RENIN; 2) Renin cleaves hepatically produced Angiotensinogen into Angiotensin I; 3) Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE, predominantly in pulmonary vascular endothelium) cleaves Angiotensin I into ANGIOTENSIN II; 4) Angiotensin II exerts potent systemic vasoconstriction, stimulates hypothalamic thirst and ADH release, and stimulates the adrenal cortex (zona glomerulosa) to secrete ALDOSTERONE; 5) Aldosterone upregulates Na+/K+ ATPase pumps in distal tubules and collecting ducts, reabsorbing sodium and water while excreting potassium and H+. Why the other options fail: Giving aldosterone to the macula densa assigns a hormone to a sensor; activating angiotensinogen straight to angiotensin II skips the pulmonary ACE step, and angiotensin I is inactive until then; antidiuretic hormone comes from the posterior pituitary, not from the juxtaglomerular cells.$q$,
  strategy = $q$RAAS cascade: Low renal perfusion -> Renin -> Angiotensin I -> ACE -> Angiotensin II (vasoconstriction) -> Aldosterone (Na+/H2O retention, K+ excretion).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The kidneys excrete hydrogen ions and regenerate bicarbonate, which is the only compensation in DKA"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The ketoacids consume bicarbonate, and the lungs retain carbon dioxide to replace the lost buffer"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The low bicarbonate is the compensation for a primary respiratory alkalosis, and the lungs are still overventilating"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The ketoacids consume bicarbonate, and the lungs blow off carbon dioxide so carbonic acid falls and pH rises toward normal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The carbonic acid-bicarbonate buffer system operates according to the equilibrium: CO2 + H2O <-> H2CO3 <-> H+ + HCO3-. In DKA, excessive ketoacids (acetoacetic acid, beta-hydroxybutyric acid) dissociate into hydrogen ions (H+), which bind to and consume circulating bicarbonate (HCO3-), causing a primary severe metabolic acidosis (pH 7.15, HCO3- 8 mEq/L). The respiratory center in the brainstem detects the high arterial H+ and responds rapidly by triggering Kussmaul respirations (deep, rapid breathing). Blowing off CO2 lowers arterial PaCO2 (24 mmHg), which pulls the chemical equilibrium to the left, decreasing H2CO3 and free H+ ions and elevating pH back toward normal physiological range (partial respiratory compensation). Why the other options fail: Retaining carbon dioxide would add acid rather than remove it; calling the picture a primary respiratory alkalosis reverses the primary disorder; renal compensation is real but slow, and the immediate compensation in ketoacidosis is the deep Kussmaul breathing that blows off carbon dioxide.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Acid-base compensation: Primary metabolic acidosis triggers respiratory hyperventilation (Kussmaul breathing) to blow off CO2 and reduce carbonic acid/H+ concentration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pressure rises only when the mass exceeds the volume of the whole cranial cavity, so the curve is flat until herniation begins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The skull expands slowly like an elastic shell, so pressure rises only after the vault reaches its limit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The skull is rigid and its three contents have a fixed volume, so once CSF and venous blood are displaced, any added volume raises pressure steeply"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pressure rises because brain tissue swells and displaces CSF into the spinal canal faster than it can be reabsorbed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Monro-Kellie doctrine states that the rigid, inelastic cranial vault is a closed box containing a constant total volume comprised of three distinct compartments: Brain parenchymal tissue (~80%), Intravascular blood (~10%), and Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF, ~10%). Because the skull cannot expand, an increase in the volume of one component (or an added mass like a hematoma or tumor) must be compensated by an equal decrease in the volume of the other components to maintain normal ICP (5–15 mmHg). Early compensation occurs by displacing CSF into the thecal sac and compressing cerebral venous sinuses. Once these compensatory buffers are exhausted (the compliance curve breakpoint), even a tiny addition of 1 to 2 mL of volume causes an exponential, catastrophic surge in ICP, collapsing cerebral perfusion pressure and causing fatal brainstem herniation. Why the other options fail: An elastic skull contradicts the fixed volume the hypothesis rests on; a flat pressure-volume curve until the whole cavity is filled is the opposite of the exponential rise; brain tissue cannot swell into air, and displacement of cerebrospinal fluid is one of the first compensations rather than the cause of the surge.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neurophysiology: Monro-Kellie hypothesis = rigid skull contains fixed volume (brain 80%, blood 10%, CSF 10%). Once CSF/blood displacement buffers fail, ICP spikes exponentially.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sympathetic: adrenergic, alpha and beta, mydriasis, tachycardia, bronchodilation, glycogenolysis. Parasympathetic: cholinergic, muscarinic, miosis, bradycardia, digestion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sympathetic: cholinergic, miosis, bradycardia, digestion. Parasympathetic: adrenergic, mydriasis, tachycardia, bronchodilation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sympathetic: adrenergic, beta only, tachycardia and bronchodilation. Parasympathetic: cholinergic, muscarinic, bradycardia and miosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sympathetic: adrenergic, alpha only, vasoconstriction and mydriasis. Parasympathetic: cholinergic, nicotinic, all gut and bladder effects"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Autonomic Nervous System consists of two anatomically and functionally distinct branches: 1) SYMPATHETIC ('fight-or-flight', thoracolumbar outflow): Postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine (and adrenal medulla releases epinephrine) targeting alpha-1 (vasoconstriction, mydriasis), beta-1 (increased heart rate and myocardial contractility), and beta-2 receptors (bronchodilation, hepatic glycogenolysis, decreased gut motility); 2) PARASYMPATHETIC ('rest-and-digest', craniosacral outflow via CN III, VII, IX, X and pelvic splanchnic nerves): Postganglionic fibers release Acetylcholine targeting Muscarinic receptors (M1–M5), producing pupillary constriction (miosis), lacrimation, salivation, vagal bradycardia, bronchoconstriction, and stimulation of peristalsis and bladder emptying. Why the other options fail: option b swaps the two divisions completely. Option c limits the sympathetic side to beta receptors, which omits alpha-mediated vasoconstriction and mydriasis. Option d limits it to alpha receptors, which omits beta-mediated tachycardia and bronchodilation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Autonomic nervous system: Sympathetic = Adrenergic (norepinephrine) -> fight-or-flight (mydriasis, tachycardia, bronchodilation). Parasympathetic = Cholinergic (ACh) -> rest-and-digest (bradycardia, miosis, digestion).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cranial nerve II, whose crossing fibres are stretched as the temporal lobe is pushed medially"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cranial nerve III, whose superficial pupilloconstrictor fibres are compressed against the tentorial edge"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cranial nerve VI, which is stretched along the clivus as the brainstem shifts posteriorly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cranial nerve IV, which is compressed in the ambient cistern as the midbrain rotates"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The pupillary light reflex relies on afferent signaling via the Optic nerve (CN II) and efferent parasympathetic motor signaling via the Oculomotor nerve (CN III). The parasympathetic fibers responsible for pupillary constriction (innervating the iris sphincter pupillae muscle) travel on the outer, superficial perimeter of the third cranial nerve. In uncal (transtentorial) herniation, the medial aspect of the temporal lobe (uncus) herniates downward across the rigid edge of the tentorial notch, directly compressing the ipsilateral CN III against the petroclinoid ligament. Because pupilloconstrictor fibers lie on the outside of the nerve trunk, external mechanical compression selectively knocks them out first, allowing unopposed sympathetic pupillodilator action and producing an ipsilateral fixed, dilated 'blown' pupil (an ominous sign of impending brainstem failure). Why the other options fail: The optic nerve carries vision and does not constrict the pupil; the abducens nerve is stretched along the clivus and gives a lateral rectus palsy, not a blown pupil; the trochlear nerve supplies the superior oblique and cannot produce this picture.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neuroanatomy: Uncal herniation compresses Cranial Nerve III (Oculomotor nerve) parasympathetic fibers against the tentorium -> ipsilateral dilated, fixed (blown) pupil.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cushing triad: hypertension with a widened pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular breathing, from rising intracranial pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Virchow triad: stasis, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury, from immobility after head injury"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Beck triad: hypotension, muffled heart sounds and distended neck veins, from pericardial tamponade"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Charcot triad: fever, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain, from ascending cholangitis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cushing's Triad is a classic physiological response to terminal intracranial hypertension and medullary ischemia: 1) Severe Systolic Hypertension with WIDENING PULSE PRESSURE: When ICP approaches mean arterial pressure, brainstem hypoperfusion and ischemia trigger a massive sympathetic surge (Cushing reflex) to forcibly drive blood flow into cerebral circulation; 2) BRADYCARDIA: Extreme systemic hypertension stimulates carotid and aortic baroreceptors, triggering intense vagal outflow to slow the heart rate; 3) IRREGULAR RESPIRATIONS (Cheyne-Stokes or ataxic breathing): Mechanical compression and distortion of the respiratory centers in the medulla oblongata and pons cause erratic breathing. Cushing's triad is a late, grave clinical sign indicating impending or ongoing cerebral herniation requiring emergency hyperosmolar therapy (mannitol/3% saline) and neurosurgical decompression. Why the other options fail: Virchow triad describes stasis, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury, which do not raise the blood pressure and slow the pulse; Beck triad describes tamponade, which gives hypotension rather than hypertension; Charcot triad describes cholangitis, which has nothing to do with a widening pulse pressure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neurological emergencies: Cushing's Triad = Systolic hypertension with widening pulse pressure + Bradycardia + Irregular respirations = severe intracranial hypertension / impending herniation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Client 1 has septic shock in its cold phase; client 2 has neurogenic shock from loss of sympathetic tone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Client 1 has obstructive shock from tamponade; client 2 has hypovolaemic shock with low filling pressures"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Both are hypovolaemic, but client 1 is colder because the vasoconstriction is more intense"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Client 1 has low-output cardiogenic shock with congestion; client 2 has high-output distributive shock with low resistance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Shock is classified into distinct hemodynamic profiles based on Cardiac Output (CO), Preload (CVP/PCWP), and Afterload (SVR): 1) CARDIOGENIC SHOCK (Client 1): Primary myocardial pump failure (e.g., massive anterior MI). Stroke volume plummets (low CI), blood backs up into the pulmonary circulation (elevated PCWP) and systemic venous circulation (elevated CVP/JVD), and sympathetic compensatory reflex vasoconstricts peripheral arterioles (high SVR), causing cold, clammy, cyanotic extremities; 2) DISTRIBUTIVE / SEPTIC SHOCK (Client 2): Inflammatory cytokines and nitric oxide induce widespread peripheral vasodilation (profoundly low SVR/afterload). Blood pools peripherally, making skin warm, flushed, and bounding early on (hyperdynamic warm shock), with low filling pressures (low CVP/PCWP). Why the other options fail: Making client 1 obstructive ignores the raised filling pressures on both sides and the warm low-resistance findings in client 2; calling both hypovolaemic cannot explain a warm flushed periphery with low filling pressures; a cold septic phase does not give the warm bounding profile described, and neurogenic shock gives bradycardia rather than the tachycardia that is present.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hemodynamic profiles: Cardiogenic = pump failure -> high preload (PCWP/JVD), low CO, high SVR (cold/clammy). Distributive/Septic = vasodilation -> low SVR, low preload, warm/flushed peripheries.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP2-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Prednisone blocked the adrenal cortisol receptors, so endogenous cortisol could not act despite normal secretion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The pituitary continued to secrete ACTH throughout treatment, and the cortex exhausted its stored cortisol"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prednisone induced hepatic enzymes that clear cortisol so rapidly that levels fall as soon as a dose is missed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sustained exogenous cortisol suppressed CRH and ACTH, so the adrenal cortex atrophied and cannot respond when the drug stops"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis functions under strict negative feedback. When high levels of systemic glucocorticoids are present, they signal the hypothalamus to suppress CRH and the anterior pituitary to suppress ACTH. Over weeks of therapeutic glucocorticoid therapy, lack of trophic ACTH stimulation leads to progressive disuse atrophy of the adrenal cortex (specifically the zona fasciculata and zona reticularis). If exogenous steroids are abruptly stopped rather than gradually tapered, the atrophic adrenal cortex cannot immediately resume cortisol synthesis. The resulting acute secondary adrenal insufficiency (Addisonian crisis) causes vascular collapse (cortisol is permissive for catecholamine vasoconstriction), hypoglycemia (loss of cortisol-stimulated gluconeogenesis), nausea, vomiting, and distributive shock. Why the other options fail: option a invents pancreatic destruction, and the pancreas is not the site of cortisol production. Option b blames the thyroid for consuming cortisol, which no thyroid hormone does. Option c describes one gland becoming another, which does not happen; atrophy is the mechanism, not metamorphosis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Endocrine physiology: Exogenous glucocorticoids suppress CRH and ACTH, causing adrenal atrophy. Abrupt cessation triggers acute adrenal crisis (hypotension, hypoglycemia, shock).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Central diabetes insipidus from damage to the posterior pituitary, so ADH is not secreted and the ducts cannot reabsorb water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "SIADH from unregulated ADH release by the injured pituitary, retaining water and concentrating the urine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus from contrast used in theatre, so the collecting ducts ignore normal ADH levels"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary hyperparathyroidism after surgery, with calcium-driven osmotic diuresis and a dilute urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH, also known as arginine vasopressin) is synthesized by supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus and transported axonally to the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) for storage and release. ADH acts on V2 receptors on the basolateral membrane of renal medullary collecting duct principal cells, triggering cAMP-mediated exocytic insertion of Aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membrane, which allows free water reabsorption into the hypertonic medullary interstitium. Surgical damage to the posterior pituitary stalk during hypophysectomy causes Central Diabetes Insipidus. Without ADH, the collecting ducts remain water-impermeable, causing massive, unabated excretion of dilute urine (polyuria, low urine specific gravity < 1.005, urine osmolality < 200 mOsm/kg), systemic hemoconcentration, and hypernatremia (Na > 145 mEq/L). SIADH causes water retention and dilutional hyponatremia. Why the other options fail: option b reverses the physiology, because SIADH concentrates the urine and lowers the sodium rather than producing 600 mL/hour of dilute urine at a sodium of 158. Option c would stop urine altogether as filtration fails, not give a specific gravity of 1.002. Option d would give hypercalcaemia and a milder diuresis, not this abrupt postoperative picture.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Posterior pituitary physiology: ADH inserts aquaporin-2 channels to reabsorb water. Central DI = lack of ADH -> massive dilute polyuria (low specific gravity) and hypernatremia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autoimmune adrenalitis destroying the cortex, so cortisol falls and the pituitary drives a compensating catecholamine surge"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pheochromocytoma: a chromaffin-cell tumour of the adrenal medulla that autonomously secretes catecholamines"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aldosterone-secreting adenoma of the zona glomerulosa, with secondary catecholamine release from volume depletion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pituitary infarction with loss of ACTH, leaving the medulla unopposed and producing episodic hypertension"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The adrenal gland consists of an outer cortex (derived from mesoderm: zona glomerulosa, fasciculata, reticularis) and an inner ADRENAL MEDULLA (derived from neural crest ectoderm, essentially a modified sympathetic ganglion). Medullary chromaffin cells are innervated by preganglionic sympathetic cholinergic fibers and synthesize catecholamines (roughly 80% epinephrine, 20% norepinephrine). A PHEOCHROMOCYTOMA is a chromaffin cell neuroendocrine tumor that autonomously produces massive, episodic surges of catecholamines. Alpha-1 receptor stimulation causes intense systemic arteriolar vasoconstriction (paroxysmal malignant hypertension), while beta-1 stimulation causes tachycardia and forceful myocardial contractions (palpitations, pounding headache, diaphoresis). Catecholamines are metabolized into metanephrines, normetanephrines, and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA), measured in urine to confirm diagnosis. Why the other options fail: option a gives cortisol deficiency, which produces hypotension and hyperkalaemia rather than paroxysmal hypertension with raised metanephrines. Option c gives aldosterone excess, which produces hypertension with hypokalaemia and suppressed renin, not episodic headache with diaphoresis. Option d removes ACTH and cannot raise catecholamines.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Adrenal medulla physiology: Chromaffin cells secrete catecholamines (epinephrine/norepinephrine). Pheochromocytoma = chromaffin tumor -> 5 P's (Pressure/HTN, Pain/headache, Perspiration, Palpitations, Pallor).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leydig cells produce seminal fluid under FSH, and Sertoli cells carry sperm to the epididymis under LH"}, {"id": "b", "text": "LH drives Leydig cells to make testosterone; FSH drives Sertoli cells to nourish sperm, form the blood-testis barrier and secrete inhibin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Both cell types are stimulated by testosterone alone, and neither responds to pituitary gonadotropins"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sertoli cells secrete testosterone under LH, and Leydig cells form the blood-testis barrier under FSH"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Testicular function relies on two distinct cell populations regulated by anterior pituitary gonadotropins: 1) LEYDIG CELLS (interstitial cells located in connective tissue between seminiferous tubules): Stimulated by LUTEINIZING HORMONE (LH), they synthesize and secrete TESTOSTERONE, which promotes spermatogenesis, secondary male sex characteristics, and anabolic metabolism; 2) SERTOLI CELLS (sustentacular 'nurse' cells lining seminiferous tubules): Stimulated by FOLLICLE-STIMULATING HORMONE (FSH) and testosterone, they form tight junctions that create the immunologically vital BLOOD-TESTIS BARRIER (protecting haploid germ cells from autoimmune attack), provide physical and metabolic nourishment to developing spermatogonia, phagocytose excess spermatid cytoplasm, and secrete Androgen-Binding Protein (ABP, maintaining high intratesticular testosterone) and Inhibin B (which provides negative feedback on pituitary FSH). Why the other options fail: option a assigns prostate fluid to Leydig cells and urine to Sertoli cells, neither of which is a testicular function. Option c denies both the division of labour and the pituitary control that drives it. Option d swaps the two cells and their tropic hormones, which is the commonest error in this item.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Male reproductive physiology: LH stimulates Leydig cells -> Testosterone. FSH stimulates Sertoli cells -> Spermatogenesis support & blood-testis barrier.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hyperplasia of the transition zone that encircles the prostatic urethra, compressing the outlet as the nodules enlarge"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperplasia of the peripheral zone, which surrounds the rectum and narrows the urethra from behind"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertrophy of the detrusor muscle alone, with no change in the gland and no fixed obstruction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Adenocarcinoma of the central zone invading the bladder neck and producing a smooth, non-tender gland"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The prostate gland is anatomically divided into zones: 1) TRANSITION ZONE: A central zone comprising roughly 5% of prostate volume that immediately encircles the proximal urethra (prostatic urethra). BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERPLASIA (BPH) originates almost exclusively in this periurethral transition zone, driven by dihydrotestosterone (DHT). Glandular and stromal proliferation produces nodules that circumferentially compress the prostatic urethra and distort the bladder outlet, producing classic obstructive and irritative symptoms (hesitancy, decreased caliber, straining, post-void dribbling, nocturia, and detrusor hypertrophy/trabeculation); 2) PERIPHERAL ZONE: The largest posterior zone (70% of gland), easily palpated on digital rectal exam, which is the predominant site (70–80%) of prostate adenocarcinoma. Why the other options fail: option b puts the hyperplasia in the peripheral zone, which is the site of carcinoma and does not encircle the urethra. Option c blames the detrusor alone, which cannot explain a smooth symmetrically enlarged gland on examination. Option d describes cancer, which would feel irregular and hard rather than smooth and non-tender.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prostate anatomy: BPH originates in the Transition (periurethral) zone -> compresses prostatic urethra -> urinary hesitancy and weak stream. Prostate cancer originates in Peripheral zone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The pectoralis fascia and the skin over it, so the muscle pulls the skin inward when the arm moves"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cooper ligaments tethered by tumour cause the dimpling and retraction; blocked dermal lymphatics cause the peau d'orange"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lactiferous ducts obstructed by secretions, so the duct dilates and pits the skin over the areola"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Subcutaneous fat replaced by fibrous tissue after radiotherapy, which tethers the skin in a small area"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Normal breast architecture consists of glandular lobules and lactiferous ducts embedded in adipose tissue and supported by a fibrous framework called COOPER'S SUSPENSORY LIGAMENTS, which run from the deep pectoral fascia to the overlying dermis: 1) SKIN DIMPLING occurs when an invasive carcinoma invades and tethers these fibrous Cooper's ligaments; fibrous desmoplasia contracts the ligaments, pulling the overlying skin inward; 2) NIPPLE RETRACTION occurs when a subareolar tumor infiltrates and shortens major lactiferous ducts; 3) PEAU D'ORANGE (orange peel sign) is a hallmark of inflammatory breast cancer or advanced tumor infiltration, occurring when malignant cells occlude subdermal lymphatic capillaries. Blocked lymphatic drainage causes intense dermal edema, but because sweat glands and hair follicles remain tethered to the underlying tissue, the swollen skin bulges outward between the tethered pits, creating a pitted orange-skin appearance. Why the other options fail: option a detaches a muscle, which does not produce dimpling, retraction or pitting. Option c turns ducts into bone, which does not occur. Option d invokes scurvy, which gives perifollicular haemorrhage and poor wound healing, not a fixed mass with peau d'orange over it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Breast anatomy & pathology: Tumor traction on Cooper's ligaments = skin dimpling. Tumor occlusion of dermal lymphatics = peau d'orange (lymphedema with tethered follicles).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A sharp fall in progesterone as the corpus luteum regresses releases the oocyte from inhibition"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A second, smaller surge of FSH from the anterior pituitary acts directly on the follicle to rupture it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rising oestradiol from the dominant follicle triggers the mid-cycle LH surge, which ruptures the follicle 24 to 36 hours later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A surge of progesterone from the maturing follicle softens the follicular capsule until it gives way"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$During the early to mid-follicular phase, growing ovarian follicles secrete estradiol, which exerts classic negative feedback on pituitary FSH and LH. However, by Days 12–13, the dominant Graafian follicle secretes high, sustained levels of estradiol (>200 pg/mL for >48 hours). This high estradiol concentration reaches a critical threshold that paradoxically switches feedback at the hypothalamus and pituitary from negative to POSITIVE. This positive feedback triggers a massive, explosive release of Luteinizing Hormone (the mid-cycle LH surge) and a smaller FSH surge. The LH surge activates proteolytic enzymes (collagenases, plasminogen), degrades the follicular wall, and induces resumption of meiosis I, culminating in follicle rupture and OVULATION (release of the secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II) roughly 24 to 36 hours after the LH surge onset (Day 14). The ruptured follicle becomes the corpus luteum, secreting progesterone. Why the other options fail: option a has the corpus luteum regressing before ovulation, which is the wrong end of the cycle. Option b assigns follicle rupture to FSH, when the mid-cycle surge that ruptures it is LH. Option d gives progesterone the surge role, and progesterone rises after ovulation, not before it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Menstrual cycle: High sustained estradiol triggers the LH SURGE via positive feedback -> induces ovulation (Day 14) and corpus luteum formation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fertilisation occurs in the cervix, and shoulder pain comes from cervical nerves that synapse in the arm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fertilisation occurs in the ampulla; blood under the diaphragm irritates the phrenic nerve (C3-C5), referring pain to the shoulder"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fertilisation occurs in the uterine cavity, and shoulder pain comes from irritation of the aorta"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fertilisation occurs in the ampulla, and shoulder pain is referred from the ovarian plexus to the neck"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The fallopian tube consists of four anatomical regions: the infundibulum (with fimbriae), ampulla, isthmus, and interstitial (intramural) segment. The AMPULLA is the widest, longest segment and is the normal physiological site of human fertilisation (sperm capacitation, acrosome reaction, and zygote formation). If previous salpingitis or pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) damages ciliated tubal epithelium, embryo transit is blocked, leading to ampullary ectopic implantation (roughly 70–80% of tubal pregnancies). As the trophoblast erodes tubal vessels, rupture produces acute internal hemorrhage (hemoperitoneum). Intraperitoneal blood pools beneath the hemidiaphragm when the client is supine, irritating sensory fibers of the Phrenic Nerve (spinal roots C3, C4, C5). Because the supraclavicular sensory nerves share the same C3–C4 cervical spinal cord segments, diaphragmatic irritation manifests as referred ipsilateral shoulder tip pain (Kehr's sign). Why the other options fail: option a puts fertilisation in the cervix and refers pain through cervical nerves, neither of which is anatomically possible. Option c puts fertilisation in the uterine cavity, where implantation occurs but fertilisation does not. Option d has the right site but the wrong pathway, because the shoulder pain comes from the diaphragm through the phrenic nerve, not from the ovarian plexus.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Reproductive anatomy: Fertilisation occurs in the Ampulla of the fallopian tube. Rupture causes hemoperitoneum -> phrenic nerve irritation (C3-C5) -> referred shoulder pain (Kehr's sign).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "They act as a chemical spermicide in the vagina, killing sperm before they can reach the cervix"}, {"id": "b", "text": "They thicken the cervical mucus and seal the external os with scar tissue after the first cycle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "They suppress FSH and LH, blocking recruitment and the LH surge, while progestin thickens mucus and thins the endometrium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "They prevent the endometrium from shedding, so a blastocyst cannot implant in a stable lining"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Combined oral contraceptives (COCs) contain synthetic estrogen (ethinyl estradiol) and a progestin. They prevent conception through multiple synergistic neuroendocrine and reproductive mechanisms: 1) Estrogen exerts negative feedback on the anterior pituitary, suppressing Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH), which prevents ovarian follicle recruitment and selection; 2) Progestin suppresses Luteinizing Hormone (LH) secretion, completely blocking the mid-cycle LH surge and thereby preventing ovulation (the primary mechanism); 3) Progestin thickens cervical mucus, making it hostile and impermeable to ascending spermatozoa; and 4) Progestin creates an atrophic, asynchronous endometrium unfavorable for blastocyst implantation, and slows tubal peristalsis. Why the other options fail: option a treats a hormonal contraceptive as a spermicide, which would not suppress ovulation or change the endometrium. Option b describes scarring that pills do not cause. Option d reverses the effect on the endometrium, which becomes thin and unsupportive rather than shedding continuously.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contraceptive pharmacology: Estrogen/progestin suppress pituitary FSH and LH -> blocks follicle development and LH surge (inhibits ovulation) + thickens cervical mucus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute haemorrhage from a concealed abruption, with blood loss enough to drop both haemoglobin and pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Marrow suppression by pregnancy hormones, so red cell production falls below the rising plasma volume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aortocaval compression by the uterus, which also sequesters red cells in the splenic pool and lowers the count"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Plasma volume rises more than red cell mass, diluting the haemoglobin; supine, the uterus compresses the vena cava and drops venous return"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pregnancy induces profound maternal cardiovascular remodeling: 1) PHYSIOLOGIC ANEMIA OF PREGNANCY: Total blood volume increases by ~45%, but plasma volume expands disproportionately (~45–50%) compared to red blood cell mass (~20–30%). This relative hemodilution causes physiological decreases in hemoglobin (normal nadir in 2nd/3rd trimester is >= 10.5 g/dL) and hematocrit (~32–33%), reducing blood viscosity to optimize uteroplacental perfusion; 2) SUPINE HYPOTENSIVE SYNDROME (Vena Cava Syndrome): After 20 weeks, the gravid uterus compresses the Inferior Vena Cava (IVC) and abdominal aorta against the vertebral column when supine. Obstructed venous return drastically decreases right atrial preload, stroke volume, and cardiac output, dropping systemic blood pressure and causing maternal cerebral hypoperfusion (dizziness, diaphoresis) and fetal bradycardia. Tilting the client to the LEFT LATERAL position displaces the uterus off the IVC, instantly restoring venous return and blood pressure. Why the other options fail: option a explains the haemoglobin with haemorrhage, but not the immediate recovery on turning left. Option b blames marrow suppression, when red cell mass actually rises in pregnancy. Option c invokes splenic sequestration, which does not occur, and still does not explain the dilutional haemoglobin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pregnancy cardiovascular physiology: Plasma expansion > RBC expansion = physiologic hemodilutional anemia. Gravid uterus compresses IVC in supine position -> Supine Hypotensive Syndrome (relieved by left lateral position).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "hPL increases maternal insulin sensitivity, so glucose is cleared faster and hypoglycaemia is common in the third trimester"}, {"id": "b", "text": "hPL stimulates maternal lipogenesis, so fat stores rise and glucose is spared by reduced hepatic output"}, {"id": "c", "text": "hPL antagonises insulin, sparing glucose for the fetus; where beta-cell reserve is short, gestational diabetes follows"}, {"id": "d", "text": "hPL blocks placental glucose transfer, so maternal glucose rises while the fetus relies on ketones"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Human Placental Lactogen (hPL, also termed human chorionic somatomammotropin [hCS]) is a polypeptide hormone synthesized by the syncytiotrophoblast of the placenta, with secretion peaking in late gestation (weeks 24–28) proportional to placental mass. Functionally, hPL is a diabetogenic hormone: it acts as a competitive insulin antagonist that impairs peripheral glucose uptake in maternal skeletal muscle and adipose tissue while increasing lipolysis (free fatty acids). This physiological maternal insulin resistance serves a vital evolutionary purpose: it elevates maternal postprandial glucose levels, ensuring a continuous concentration gradient for facilitated glucose diffusion (via GLUT1) and amino acid transport across the placenta to meet fetal growth demands. In pregnant women with limited pancreatic beta-cell reserve who cannot triple insulin secretion, this anti-insulin effect results in GESTATIONAL DIABETES MELLITUS (tested routinely at 24–28 weeks). Why the other options fail: option a reverses the hormone, which raises rather than lowers maternal glucose. Option b assigns lipogenesis, when hPL drives lipolysis. Option d blocks the wrong step, because hPL spares glucose for transfer rather than preventing transfer.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Placental endocrinology: hPL induces maternal insulin resistance to spare glucose for the fetus. Failure of maternal pancreatic compensation causes Gestational Diabetes Mellitus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cervical stretch signals the hypothalamus to release oxytocin from the posterior pituitary, which strengthens contractions and stretches the cervix further"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cervical stretch triggers a vagal reflex that increases uterine blood flow and strengthens contractions through oxygen delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stretch receptors signal the posterior pituitary to release prostaglandins, which soften the cervix and intensify contractions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cervical stretch stimulates the adrenal medulla, and circulating adrenaline drives stronger myometrial contractions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Ferguson Reflex is a classic neuroendocrine POSITIVE FEEDBACK loop governing parturition: 1) As uterine contractions push the fetal presenting part into the birth canal, mechanical distension and stretching of the cervix and vaginal wall stimulate sensory stretch receptors; 2) Afferent neural signals travel via pelvic nerves up the anterolateral spinal cord to the hypothalamus (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei); 3) Hypothalamic magnocellular neurons fire, releasing pulses of OXYTOCIN from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY into systemic circulation; 4) Oxytocin binds to upregulated G-protein coupled oxytocin receptors on myometrial smooth muscle cells, increasing intracellular calcium and triggering more forceful uterine contractions; 5) Stronger contractions force the fetus deeper against the cervix, generating even greater cervical stretch and further oxytocin release. This self-amplifying cycle continues until expulsion of the fetus breaks the mechanical stimulus. Why the other options fail: option b sends the reflex through the vagus and oxygen delivery, which is not a positive feedback loop. Option c names prostaglandins, which are local mediators and are not released from the pituitary. Option d recruits the adrenal medulla, and adrenaline is not the oxytocin loop.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parturition neuroendocrinology: Ferguson reflex = cervical stretch -> hypothalamic sensory signals -> posterior pituitary OXYTOCIN pulsatile release -> stronger myometrial contractions (positive feedback loop).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "25% carrier, 50% affected and 25% unaffected, because two carrier parents produce mostly affected children"}, {"id": "b", "text": "25% homozygous disease, 50% asymptomatic carrier and 25% unaffected, with no difference between sons and daughters"}, {"id": "c", "text": "50% affected and 50% unaffected, because one copy of the sickle gene is enough to cause disease"}, {"id": "d", "text": "No child can be affected, because each parent carries only one copy and the trait skips a generation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sickle Cell Disease is inherited in an AUTOSOMAL RECESSIVE Mendelian pattern caused by a single point mutation (substitution of valine for glutamic acid at codon 6 of the beta-globin chain on chromosome 11). When both biological parents are asymptomatic heterozygous carriers (HbAS x HbAS), each conception has independent probabilities determined by a Punnett square: 1) 25% probability (1 in 4) of inheriting both mutant alleles (HbSS) and manifesting Sickle Cell Disease; 2) 50% probability (2 in 4) of inheriting one mutant and one normal allele (HbAS) and carrying the asymptomatic Sickle Cell Trait; 3) 25% probability (1 in 4) of inheriting two normal alleles (HbAA) and being completely non-affected and non-carrier. Gender has no influence because the beta-globin gene is located on an autosome (chromosome 11), not a sex chromosome. Why the other options fail: option a inverts the carrier and affected proportions. Option c treats the sickle gene as dominant, which it is not. Option d invents skipping generations, which autosomal recessive inheritance does not do.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Genetics: Autosomal recessive carrier parents (Aa x Aa) = 25% affected (aa), 50% carrier (Aa), 25% unaffected non-carrier (AA). Equal distribution in males and females.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Every son has a 50% chance of disease and every daughter a 50% chance of disease, because both inherit one maternal X"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sons cannot be affected because the father passes a normal Y, and daughters are carriers only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "All sons are affected and all daughters are carriers, because the mother has two mutated X chromosomes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Each son has a 50% chance of disease, and each daughter a 50% chance of being a carrier with no disease"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hemophilia A (Factor VIII deficiency) is inherited in an X-LINKED RECESSIVE pattern. The mother is heterozygous (XHXh, where Xh carries the mutation; she is protected by Lyonization/the normal XH allele). The father has normal karyotype (XHY). For each son (XY), the mother contributes either the normal XH or the mutant Xh (50% probability). Because males are HEMIZYGOUS (they have only one X chromosome and a Y chromosome with no factor VIII locus), inheriting the mutant Xh inevitably manifests the disease (50% of sons will have hemophilia A). For each daughter (XX), the father always contributes his normal XH, so no daughter can be homozygous affected (0% risk of manifesting disease); however, each daughter has a 50% chance of inheriting the maternal Xh, becoming an asymptomatic carrier (XHXh). Why the other options fail: option a gives daughters a 50% chance of disease, but they inherit a normal paternal X and can only be carriers. Option b says sons cannot be affected, yet they inherit the maternal X and half of them will be. Option c gives the mother two mutated X chromosomes, which would make her affected rather than a carrier.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Genetics: X-linked recessive carrier mother (XHXh) x Normal father (XHY) = 50% of sons affected; 50% of daughters are carriers; 0% daughters affected.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The fetal period is more vulnerable than the embryonic period, so folate is continued through the third trimester"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Weeks 3 to 8 are when the placenta forms, and folate is needed to build the placental villi; the neural tube closes at 12 weeks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Weeks 3 to 8 are organogenesis, and the neural tube closes by day 28, so folate must be present before conception to prevent neural tube defects"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Weeks 3 to 8 are when the embryo implants, and folate prevents early miscarriage rather than malformation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Human embryological development is divided into three distinct phases: 1) Pre-embryonic period (Weeks 1–2): All-or-none phenomenon—teratogenic insults either kill the conceptus or damaged cells are replaced without congenital malformations; 2) EMBRYONIC PERIOD (Weeks 3–8): The period of active ORGANOGENESIS. The primary germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm) rapidly differentiate into all rudimentary organ systems (cardiovascular, neural, musculoskeletal, craniofacial). Exposure to teratogens during this window produces severe, irreversible major structural congenital malformations; 3) Fetal period (Weeks 9–birth): Growth and functional maturation, where teratogens cause physiological or growth defects rather than gross structural deformities. The NEURAL TUBE closes completely by Day 28 post-conception (often before a woman even realizes she has missed a menstrual period). Preconception folic acid supplementation (400–800 mcg daily) supports nucleotide synthesis and DNA methylation, reducing neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly) by over 70%. Why the other options fail: option a moves the vulnerable window to the third trimester, when the organs are already formed. Option b ties folate to placental villi and puts neural tube closure at 12 weeks, when it closes by day 28. Option d restricts folate to preventing miscarriage, which is not its role here.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Embryology: Weeks 3-8 = Organogenesis (highest teratogenic susceptibility). Neural tube closes by Day 28, requiring preconception folic acid (400 mcg) to prevent NTDs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The lens swells and blocks the pupil, so fluid is trapped in the posterior chamber and pushes the vitreous forward"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aqueous humour is overproduced by an inflamed ciliary body, exceeding the drainage capacity of the trabecular meshwork"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aqueous humour cannot drain through the trabecular meshwork because the iris is pushed forward into the angle by pupillary block"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The canal of Schlemm collapses from high episcleral venous pressure, so drainage stops even though the angle is open"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Aqueous humor is continuously secreted by the ciliary body epithelium into the posterior chamber, flows through the pupil into the anterior chamber, and drains out of the eye via the iridocorneal filtration angle through the trabecular meshwork into the CANAL OF SCHLEMM. In clients with an anatomically narrow anterior chamber angle (e.g., hyperopes, older adults with enlarging lenses), pupillary dilation (mydriasis in darkness or from anticholinergics) bunches up the peripheral iris. Pupillary block traps aqueous humor in the posterior chamber, bowing the peripheral iris forward (iris bombé) against the trabecular meshwork. This acute mechanical occlusion blocks aqueous outflow while ciliary production continues, driving intraocular pressure from normal (10–21 mmHg) to extreme heights (>50–60 mmHg). The sudden ocular hypertension produces corneal edema ('steamy' cornea), severe pain, colored halos, and ischemic necrosis of the optic nerve (CN II) with irreversible blindness unless urgently treated with cholinergic miosis (pilocarpine), aqueous suppressants (timolol, acetazolamide), and laser peripheral iridotomy. Why the other options fail: Overproduction by an inflamed ciliary body is not the mechanism, because outflow is blocked while secretion continues; lens swelling that pushes the vitreous forward misplaces the block, which sits at the pupil and the angle; a collapsed canal of Schlemm with an open angle describes open-angle glaucoma, not this acute attack.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ocular anatomy: Aqueous humor drains via trabecular meshwork into Canal of Schlemm. Angle-closure glaucoma = iris bows forward, blocking drainage -> dangerous IOP spike and optic nerve ischemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rods give high-acuity colour vision in the fovea, while cones are the dim-light receptors spread through the periphery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rods and cones are evenly distributed, so peripheral and central vision have the same acuity and colour sense"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cones respond to light but rods respond only to colour, so night vision is monochromatic and colourless"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rods carry rhodopsin, dominate the periphery and give dim-light and motion vision without colour; cones fill the fovea and give acuity and colour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The neural retina contains two distinct classes of photoreceptor neurons: 1) RODS (~120 million): Concentrated in the peripheral retina, rods contain the visual photopigment RHODOPSIN (requiring Vitamin A). They exhibit high convergent neural wiring (many rods synapse onto one bipolar cell), making them exceptionally sensitive to single photons of light. They mediate SCOTOPIC VISION (dim light, night vision, peripheral motion detection, and shades of gray) but have low spatial resolution and zero color discrimination; 2) CONES (~6 million): Concentrated primarily in the macula lutea and exclusively packed in the FOVEA CENTRALIS (the area of sharpest vision). Cones contain three opsin photopigments sensitive to specific wavelengths (short/blue, medium/green, long/red). With low neural convergence (often a 1:1 ratio with bipolar and ganglion cells), cones mediate PHOTOPIC VISION (bright daylight), high visual acuity (resolving fine details), and full-color vision. Why the other options fail: option a swaps the two receptors. Option b flattens the retinal distribution, which would abolish the fovea and the peripheral field together. Option c reverses colour and dim-light function, which belong to cones and rods respectively.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Retinal histology: Rods = Rhodopsin, periphery, dim light/scotopic vision, black/white, high sensitivity. Cones = Color (red/green/blue), central fovea, bright light/photopic vision, high acuity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ossicular otosclerosis fixes the stapes in the oval window, so sound is not conducted and the pressure builds in the middle ear"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perilymph leaks through a ruptured round window, so the cochlea loses fluid and the canals fire irregularly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A vestibular schwannoma compresses the eighth nerve, producing episodic vertigo with progressive high-frequency loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Endolymph pressure rises within the membranous labyrinth, distorting hair cells and the ampullae, and producing the attacks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The inner ear (labyrinth) consists of a bony labyrinth containing Perilymph (high in Na+, resembling ECF) and an inner membranous labyrinth containing ENDOLYMPH (high in K+, resembling ICF, secreted by the stria vascularis). The membranous labyrinth comprises the Cochlea (hearing transduction via the Organ of Corti) and the Vestibular apparatus (semicircular canals sensing rotational acceleration, and utricle/saccule sensing linear motion and gravity). In MÉNIÈRE'S DISEASE, impaired resorption of endolymph through the endolymphatic sac leads to ENDOLYMPHATIC HYDROPS (dilation and increased fluid pressure within the entire membranous labyrinth). Hydrops causes micro-ruptures of the membrane, mixing high-potassium endolymph with perilymph, which depolarizes and paralyzes vestibulocochlear nerve fibers (CN VIII). Clinically, this manifests as episodic rotational vertigo, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and aural fullness. Why the other options fail: A perilymph fistula follows trauma or straining and does not give fluctuating hearing loss with aural fullness; a vestibular schwannoma gives progressive unilateral loss with steady rather than episodic vertigo; otosclerosis fixes the stapes and gives progressive conductive loss without hours-long vertigo and roaring tinnitus.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inner ear physiology: Ménière's disease = Endolymphatic hydrops (excess endolymph pressure in membranous labyrinth) -> episodic vertigo + fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss + tinnitus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Thyroid hormones raise beta-adrenergic receptor density and uncouple mitochondrial phosphorylation, so metabolic rate and heat production climb"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Thyroid hormones increase peripheral conversion of T4 to reverse T3, which drives the adrenergic surge of the crisis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thyroid hormones block the sodium-potassium pump, so intracellular sodium rises and the heart rate climbs in compensation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Thyroid hormones bind directly to cardiac myocytes and increase contractility without changing metabolic rate or heat production"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) act as nuclear transcription factors regulating basal metabolic rate and cellular protein synthesis across all organ systems: 1) ADRENERGIC RECEPTOR UPREGULATION: T3 massively increases the density and sensitivity of beta-adrenergic receptors in cardiac myocytes and vascular smooth muscle, creating an exaggerated, hyper-responsive state to endogenous catecholamines (manifesting as profound tachycardia, systolic hypertension, atrial fibrillation, and tremors); 2) MITOCHONDRIAL UNCOUPLING & CALORIGENESIS: T3 stimulates the Na+/K+ ATPase pump and induces uncoupling proteins (UCPs) in mitochondria. Instead of storing energy efficiently as ATP, cellular proton gradients dissipate directly as heat (calorigenesis), skyrocketing basal metabolic rate and producing hyperpyrexia (temperatures exceeding 40 °C), profound diaphoresis, and severe dehydration; 3) HIGH-OUTPUT CARDIAC FAILURE: T3-induced vasodilation combined with extreme metabolic oxygen demands increases cardiac output initially, but unrelenting tachyarrhythmias precipitate acute high-output cardiac decompensation. Why the other options fail: Conversion of T4 to reverse T3 produces an inactive metabolite and cannot drive the adrenergic surge; blocking the sodium-potassium pump is not the mechanism at these concentrations; binding myocytes directly without raising metabolic rate or heat production does not explain the fever and the oxygen demand.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thyroid physiology: T3/T4 upregulate beta-adrenergic receptors + uncouple mitochondrial phosphorylation -> extreme calorigenesis (hyperpyrexia), hypermetabolism, and adrenergic storm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Contraction of the criss-crossing myometrial fibres compresses the spiral arterioles like living ligatures, and atony leaves them open"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The spiral arterioles are sealed by clot in the placental bed, and atony prevents the clot from forming"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Haemostasis depends on the cervix closing after delivery, and atony leaves the os open and bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The uterine arteries are kinked at the internal os by contraction, and a boggy uterus cannot kink them"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$At term, the uteroplacental circulation receives approximately 10% to 15% of total maternal cardiac output (roughly 500 to 700 mL of blood per minute). When the placenta detaches from the uterine wall, dozens of high-flow, low-resistance maternal spiral arterioles are sheared open. The body achieves immediate hemostasis NOT via the clotting cascade (which is far too slow to prevent exsanguination within minutes), but through mechanical vasoconstriction: the myometrium consists of three interwoven muscular layers, with the middle layer arranged in a criss-crossing, figure-of-eight network. As the uterus contracts down in response to endogenous oxytocin release, these interlacing myometrial fibers act as 'LIVING LIGATURES,' physically constricting and clamping down on the open spiral arteries like biological tourniquets. In UTERINE ATONY (the cause of >80% of primary PPH), myometrial smooth muscle fails to contract (often due to prolonged labor, uterine overdistension, or bladder distension), leaving spiral vessels wide open to hemorrhage. Immediate fundal massage and uterotonics (oxytocin) stimulate myometrial contractions to halt bleeding. Why the other options fail: option b relies on clot alone, which cannot seal arterioles carrying 500 to 700 mL a minute without the myometrial clamp. Option c shifts haemostasis to the cervix, which plays no part in it. Option d kinks vessels at the internal os, which is not the mechanism of post-placental haemostasis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Postpartum hemostasis: Middle myometrial muscle fibers ('living ligatures') contract in figure-8 pattern to mechanically clamp severed spiral arterioles. Uterine atony fails to clamp them -> massive PPH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP3-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Focus on beta-cell failure and the HbA1c, because the metabolic problem is what the treatment targets"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explore how the disease, psychological stressors and social circumstances interact to shape the client's attendance"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat the missed appointments as a competence problem and arrange closer supervision at each visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add an antidepressant first, because depression is the most common reason for missing appointments"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$George Engel's Biopsychosocial Model posits that health, illness, and healthcare delivery cannot be fully understood by biological pathology alone. It requires examining the dynamic interplay of biological factors (glycemic control, genetics), psychological components (illness perceptions, coping styles, emotional distress, self-efficacy), and social determinants (socioeconomic status, family dynamics, work schedule, cultural beliefs). Narrow biomedical reductionism ignores the real-world behavioral drivers of chronic disease; shaming the client and unwarranted neuroimaging are non-therapeutic. Why the other options fail: option a stays inside the biomedical lens the question asks the nurse to move beyond. Option c replaces assessment with judgement. Option d adds a treatment for a problem the assessment has not yet established.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Biopsychosocial Model: Always assess biological, psychological, and social dimensions concurrently to understand client health behaviors.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The right to be excused from work indefinitely, with no obligation to seek treatment or return to normal roles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The right to be temporarily excused from normal roles, with the obligation to see illness as undesirable and to cooperate with treatment to get well"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The right to decide the treatment plan alone, with the obligation to refuse any advice offered by clinicians"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The right to be cared for without question, with the obligation to remain dependent on the family until cured"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Talcott Parsons' functionalist sociological model describes the 'Sick Role' as carrying two institutionalized rights and two core obligations: Rights: 1) The sick person is exempt from normal social and work responsibilities (proportional to illness severity); 2) The sick person is not blamed or held morally responsible for their condition. Obligations: 1) The person must define sickness as undesirable and want to get well; 2) The person must seek competent professional medical help and cooperate with the treatment regimen. Why the other options fail: option a makes the exemption permanent and removes the obligation. Option c gives the client the clinical decisions alone. Option d keeps the client dependent indefinitely, which the sick role does not.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parsons' Sick Role: Exemption from normal duties (right) paired with the duty to seek professional help and strive for recovery (obligation).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Elicit the client's own explanation of the illness, then negotiate a plan that respects it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refer the client to a cultural broker who can persuade them that the spiritual explanation is wrong"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that the biomedical model is the only evidence-based one, and ask the client to accept the prescription first"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Adjust the prescription to fit the client's belief, and give a lower dose so the drug does not cool the body too quickly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Arthur Kleinman's Explanatory Model framework encourages clinicians to explore how clients and families understand their sickness: etiology, timing, pathophysiology, severity, and acceptable treatment. Eliciting the client's perspective fosters trust and cultural humility, enabling the nurse to bridge clinical recommendations with the client's cultural framework (negotiating safe medical adherence while respecting spiritual practices that do not cause harm). Ridiculing the client or issuing ultimatums causes immediate care disengagement. Endorsing abandonment of evidence-based therapy is dangerous malpractice. Why the other options fail: Explaining that the biomedical model is the only evidence-based one dismisses the client's explanation and ends the negotiation before it starts; referring to a broker to persuade them that the explanation is wrong contradicts the belief rather than exploring it; lowering the dose to fit the belief abandons effective treatment.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Kleinman's Explanatory Model: Understand the client's perspective on illness etiology and treatment expectations before negotiating medical therapy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sensorimotor stage: prepare the child through touch and movement, since reasoning is not yet developed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Preoperational stage: explain in simple stories and use play to prepare the child for the cast"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Formal operational stage: explain the mechanics of the reduction in abstract terms, because the child can reason logically"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Concrete operational stage: use models and show the plaster so the child can see and touch what will happen"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Children aged 7 to 11 years are in Piaget's Concrete Operational stage of cognitive development. They can perform logical operations on tangible, concrete objects and understand cause-and-effect, conservation, and physical realities, but they struggle with purely abstract hypotheses. Explaining procedures with tangible models, letting them handle plaster casting gauze, and explaining how the cast holds the bone straight matches their cognitive capabilities. Sensorimotor is ages 0–2; Preoperational is ages 2–7 (prone to magical thinking and guilt); Formal operational begins around age 12 with abstract deductive reasoning. Why the other options fail: Preoperational describes a younger child who needs stories and play; formal operations begin around age 12, so abstract mechanics will not help a 9-year-old; sensorimotor describes infancy, where reasoning is not yet developed at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Piaget's stages: Sensorimotor (0–2), Preoperational (2–7), Concrete Operational (7–11: logical, tangible objects/models), Formal Operational (12+: abstract reasoning).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Protest, with crying, screaming and rejecting the nurse's comfort"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Despair, with crying that has stopped and a withdrawn, apathetic child"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Detachment, with the child appearing cheerful and indifferent to the parent's return"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autistic isolation, with withdrawal that predates the separation rather than following it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$John Bowlby and James Robertson described three sequential stages of separation anxiety in young children (especially 6 to 30 months): 1) PROTEST: The child vigorously cries, screams, clings, watches eagerly for parents, and angrily rejects unfamiliar caregivers; 2) DESPAIR: The crying stops, motor activity declines, and the child appears depressed, withdrawn, sad, and apathetic; 3) DETACHMENT (Denial): The child superficially adapts, appears interested in toys, but shows detachment from returning parents (a defense mechanism against deep emotional pain). Why the other options fail: option b is despair, when the crying has stopped and the child is withdrawn. Option c is detachment, when the child seems content and ignores the returning parent. Option d is not a phase of separation anxiety at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bowlby's separation anxiety stages: Protest (screaming, kicking, crying) -> Despair (quiet sadness, withdrawal) -> Detachment (superficial adjustment, ignoring parents).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trust versus mistrust, because the adolescent cannot rely on adults to keep them safe"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Initiative versus guilt, because the adolescent feels guilty about needing help"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Generativity versus stagnation, because the adolescent wants to contribute to others"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Identity versus role confusion, because peer opinion shapes the adolescent's sense of self"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Adolescents (ages 12 to 18 years) navigate Erikson's developmental stage of Identity versus Role Confusion. The primary developmental task is developing a sense of self, personal values, and peer group belonging. Fitting in with peers is paramount; chronic illnesses and visible regimens (like injections or blood glucose testing) threaten peer conformity and identity formation. The nurse should address this by integrating peer support groups, exploring discrete injection routines, and empowering autonomous self-care. Trust vs Mistrust is infancy; Initiative vs Guilt is early childhood (3–6 yrs); Generativity vs Stagnation is middle adulthood (40–65 yrs). Why the other options fail: option a belongs to infancy. Option b belongs to the preschool years. Option c belongs to midlife, when the task is contributing to the next generation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Erikson's stages: Adolescence (12–18) = Identity vs. Role Confusion. Peer conformity and fitting in are critical drivers of health behaviors.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ego integrity versus despair, because the client is reviewing life and seeking peace with it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Autonomy versus shame, because the client is struggling to remain independent in hospital"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Industry versus inferiority, because the client measures their worth by past achievement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intimacy versus isolation, because the client is seeking closeness in later life"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Older adulthood (65+ years) centers on Erikson's stage of Ego Integrity versus Despair. The developmental task is life review: looking back over one's life to achieve a sense of fulfillment, meaning, and coherence (Ego Integrity) versus experiencing bitter regret, despair, and fear of impending death. Engaging in life review is a healthy, therapeutic process that nurses should facilitate through active listening and validation. Autonomy vs Shame is toddlerhood (1–3 yrs); Industry vs Inferiority is school age (6–12 yrs); Intimacy vs Isolation is young adulthood (18–40 yrs). Why the other options fail: option b belongs to toddlerhood. Option c belongs to school age. Option d belongs to young adulthood.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Erikson's stages: Older adult (65+) = Ego Integrity vs. Despair. Life review and reflecting on one's life story are core developmental milestones.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The alarm stage, with the initial surge of catecholamines and a brief rise in blood pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A eustress phase, in which beneficial stress improves performance and adaptation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The resistance stage, with cortisol maintaining glucose and blood pressure despite the strain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The exhaustion stage, with adrenal atrophy, hypoglycaemia and collapse after prolonged stress"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hans Selye's General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) comprises three distinct biological stages: 1) ALARM REACTION: Acute 'fight-or-flight' activation of the sympathetic nervous system and hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis (tachycardia, hypertension, hyperglycemia); 2) RESISTANCE: Sustained corticosteroid release and physiological adaptation to resist the ongoing stressor; 3) EXHAUSTION: Depletion of adaptive energy reserves following prolonged, relentless stress, resulting in organ system breakdown, adrenal insufficiency, blunted immune function, shock, or death. Why the other options fail: option a is the first stage, with a catecholamine surge rather than collapse. Option c is not a stage of the syndrome. Option d is where the body holds out at a cost, before its resources run down.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Selye's GAS stages: Alarm (acute fight-or-flight) -> Resistance (coping/adaptation) -> Exhaustion (depletion of physiological reserves, collapse).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Displacement, because anger at the employer is directed at a safer target, the nurse"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sublimation, because the anger has been channelled into a socially useful activity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reaction formation, because the client behaves in a way opposite to their true feeling"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intellectualisation, because the client reasons about the situation without feeling it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Displacement is an unconscious defense mechanism in which emotional anger, hostility, or distress triggered by a powerful, threatening person or situation (e.g., the corporate employer) is redirected onto a less threatening, safer substitute target (e.g., the triage nurse). Sublimation is redirecting unacceptable impulses into socially constructive pursuits (e.g., vigorous exercise). Reaction Formation is expressing the exact opposite of unconscious unacceptable feelings (e.g., behaving with fawning affection toward someone you detest). Intellectualization is analyzing an emotional event purely through sterile logic to avoid feeling. Why the other options fail: option b channels anger into something useful, which is not what shouting at a nurse does. Option c would show behaviour opposite to the feeling, such as excessive friendliness. Option d would show detached reasoning rather than abuse.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Defense mechanism definitions: Displacement shifts emotions from the true target to a safer, vulnerable target.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Regression, when a child under stress returns to earlier behaviours such as bedwetting and thumb sucking"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Repression, when the child pushes the frightening memory of admission out of awareness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fixation, when the child becomes stuck at the stage at which the trauma occurred"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reaction formation, when the child behaves in the opposite way to how they actually feel"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Regression is a common, adaptive, and temporary ego defense mechanism in young children facing severe stress, physical illness, hospitalization, or separation from family. The child retreats to behaviors associated with an earlier, safer developmental stage (e.g., thumb-sucking, enuresis, wanting a bottle) where they felt completely nurtured and protected. Reassuring parents that this behavior is normal and will resolve as health and security return prevents punitive parental reactions that worsen distress. Punishment increases anxiety; attributing this to brain damage or manipulation is clinically inaccurate. Why the other options fail: Repression is unconscious forgetting rather than a return to earlier behaviour; fixation is a developmental arrest rather than a temporary retreat; reaction formation would show behaviour opposite to the true feeling, not the behaviour of a younger child.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Defense mechanisms: Regression is retreating to an earlier developmental milestone during acute stress. It is normal and temporary in hospitalized children.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client's financial reserves, insurance cover and the availability of legal aid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client's physiological stress markers, including cortisol and blood pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Realistic perception of the event, adequate situational supports and effective coping mechanisms"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client's previous psychiatric history and adherence to past treatment plans"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Donna Aguilera's Crisis Intervention methodology identifies three critical 'balancing factors' that determine whether a stressful life event triggers a state of emotional equilibrium or decompensates into a psychological crisis: 1) Realistic perception of the event (accurate understanding without catastrophic distortion or denial); 2) Adequate situational supports (available family, friends, community, and social resources to buffer distress); and 3) Adequate coping mechanisms (positive past behavioral strategies for problem-solving and emotional regulation). If any balancing factors are absent, crisis ensues. Financial metrics and lab values do not constitute Aguilera's model. Why the other options fail: option a is financial capital, not a balancing factor in the model. Option b is physiological measurement. Option d is background history, none of which predicts whether this crisis will resolve.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Aguilera's 3 Balancing Factors in Crisis: 1) Realistic perception, 2) Situational support, 3) Coping mechanisms.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Transfer her care to a clinic nearer her home and close the record if she does not attend"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Conduct a home visit to find structural barriers and link the family to transport, childcare and social support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain the risks of missed appointments and send a formal reminder before the next visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Report the mother to social services for neglect, because three children are at risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The WHO Social Determinants of Health (SDOH) emphasizes that conditions in which people are born, grow, live, work, and age (e.g., poverty, transport deficits, childcare lack, insecure employment) drive more than 80% of health outcomes and clinic non-attendance. Labeling individuals 'non-compliant' ignores structural inequities. A compassionate home visit uncovers real barriers and allows tailored solutions (CHPS home visits, mobile outreach, transport subsidies, flexible clinic hours). Punitive threats and patient abandonment worsen maternal-fetal mortality and violate human rights. Why the other options fail: Closing the record after moving her nearer relocates care without removing the barrier that stopped her and breaks continuity; explaining the risks and sending a reminder treats a structural problem as a reminder problem; reporting her for neglect attributes the missed visits to her character.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SDOH principle: Before attributing missed care to poor personal motivation, identify and address structural socioeconomic barriers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Remove the tea from the bedside, explain why it is unsafe, and document the conversation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Allow the tea because family beliefs matter, and monitor the electrolytes and weight closely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain the interaction, validate the wish for strength, and agree a safe alternative with the family"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the family to stop bringing remedies and restrict their visits until the client stabilises"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cultural competence requires balancing respect for traditional cultural health practices with patient physiological safety. Licorice root contains glycyrrhizin, which inhibits 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, leading to mineralocorticoid excess, severe hypokalemia, hypertension, and heart failure decompensation. The nurse explains the mechanism respectfully without denigrating their beliefs, validates their positive intention to heal, and negotiates safe alternatives (e.g., safe herbal teas without cardiac interactions or prayer/rituals). Aggressive confiscation destroys trust; passive negligence precipitates fatal heart failure; security ejection alienates the support system. Why the other options fail: option a removes the tea but not the belief, and the family will bring it again. Option b allows a substance that is actively harmful in heart failure. Option d punishes the family and guarantees that the next remedy is hidden.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Negotiating cultural health practices: Respect the intention, educate on specific physiological risks, and negotiate safe cultural practices that avoid harm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Contemplation, because the client has begun to think about quitting within the next six months"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Precontemplation, because the client does not see the behaviour as a problem and has no intention of changing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Preparation, because the client has set a date and made a plan for the next month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maintenance, because the client has already stopped and is sustaining the change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Transtheoretical Model (TTM) stages are: 1) PRECONTEMPLATION: No intention to change behavior in the foreseeable future (typically defined as within the next 6 months); unaware or in denial of negative consequences; 2) CONTEMPLATION: Aware a problem exists and seriously considering change within 6 months, but ambivalent; 3) PREPARATION: Intending to take action within the next 30 days and taking initial small steps; 4) ACTION: Actively modifying behavior (within the past 6 months); 5) MAINTENANCE: Sustaining behavior change for over 6 months and preventing relapse. This client has zero intention to quit, fitting Precontemplation. Why the other options fail: Contemplation would require the client to be considering quitting within six months, which they explicitly are not; preparation would require a date and a plan; maintenance would require the client to have already stopped.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stages of Change: Precontemplation = No intention to change ('I don't have a problem'); Contemplation = Ambivalent, thinking about change; Preparation = Planning to change soon.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cues to action alone, because a reminder letter is what brings women to screening"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perceived severity and self-efficacy, because she needs to fear cancer before she will act"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Perceived susceptibility and perceived barriers, because she does not feel at risk and sees the clinic as far away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Perceived benefits alone, because screening uptake follows once the purpose is explained"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Health Belief Model (HBM) posits that health-seeking behavior depends on: 1) Perceived Susceptibility (belief about chances of contracting a condition); 2) Perceived Severity (belief about seriousness and consequences); 3) Perceived Benefits (belief in efficacy of advised action); 4) Perceived Barriers (tangible and psychological costs of the action); 5) Cues to Action; and 6) Self-Efficacy. The woman exhibits low perceived susceptibility ('I feel healthy, so it can't happen to me') and high perceived barriers ('clinics are too far'). Psychoanalytic and Pavlovian models do not guide health screening behavior. Why the other options fail: Fear and confidence are not the two beliefs she stated; reminders alone isolate the cue from the beliefs that make it work; perceived benefits alone ignores both of her statements, that she does not feel at risk and that the clinic is far away.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Health Belief Model: Health action requires sufficient Perceived Susceptibility and Severity, recognized Benefits, and low Perceived Barriers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explore what the client enjoys about drinking before discussing any change"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Point out that the client is in denial and that continued drinking will kill them"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise switching from spirits to beer while the client considers cutting down"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reflect both sides of the ambivalence and ask what steps might protect the marriage and the liver"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Motivational Interviewing (MI) is a collaborative, person-centered counseling approach designed to strengthen personal motivation for and commitment to change by resolving ambivalence. Key principles include expressing empathy, developing discrepancy between current behavior and core values, rolling with resistance, and supporting self-efficacy. Reflecting ambivalence ('On one hand. but on the other hand.') and asking an open question invites the client to voice their own arguments for change ('change talk'). Confrontational labeling, minimizing alcoholism, and shaming trigger defensiveness and entrench resistance. Why the other options fail: Argument about denial raises resistance; advising a switch to beer colludes with continued drinking; exploring what the client enjoys leaves the ambivalence untouched, when reflecting both sides is what moves it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Motivational Interviewing: Reflect ambivalence without judgment, align with client values, and ask open-ended questions that elicit the client's own reasons for change.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A score of 15, which indicates moderately severe depression and a routine referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A score of 10, which is the threshold for mild depression and needs no action"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A score of 5, which indicates minimal symptoms despite the reported weight loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A score of 21 with active suicidal ideation, which indicates severe depression needing urgent review"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The PHQ-9 is a standardized 9-item depression screening tool scored from 0 to 27: 0–4 = minimal/none; 5–9 = mild; 10–14 = moderate; 15–19 = moderately severe; 20–27 = severe depression. A score of 20 or higher signifies severe major depressive disorder. Any endorsement of item 9 (thoughts of being better off dead or hurting oneself) demands immediate comprehensive suicide risk assessment and emergency psychiatric referral. Involuntary ECT is never indicated for mild depression; discharging a suffering client constitutes medical negligence. Why the other options fail: option a scores the symptoms as minimal, which the weight loss and anhedonia contradict. Option b treats a mild score as an emergency and names a treatment that requires consent and referral. Option c calls the presentation malingering, which no score supports.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PHQ-9 interpretation: Scores 20–27 represent severe depression. Item 9 mandates immediate, direct suicide risk assessment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask about mood and sleep first, and raise suicide only if the client mentions it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that things will improve and return to the assessment later in the shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask directly about suicidal thoughts, plan and means, and keep the client under one-to-one observation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Promise confidentiality so the client feels safe enough to disclose more"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Suicide risk assessment requires direct, unambiguous, objective questioning. Asking directly about suicidal ideation does NOT plant the idea in a person's head; rather, it provides relief and opens communication. The nurse must assess: 1) ideation; 2) plan specificity; 3) lethality of chosen method; and 4) immediate access to means. If risk is present, the client must never be left alone (initiating continuous 1-to-1 visual observation and removing environmental hazards). False reassurance trivializes despair; promising secrecy is dangerous malpractice; leaving the client alone allows suicide completion. Why the other options fail: Reassuring and returning later reassures without assessing; asking about mood and sleep first waits for the client to raise suicide, when the nurse must ask; promising confidentiality offers a secrecy the nurse cannot keep and must not offer.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Suicide assessment rule: Ask directly about suicidal thoughts, plan, and means. Never promise secrecy, and never leave an actively suicidal client alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain that antipsychotics are essential and that the prayer camp is actively harmful, then ask the family to choose between the two so that treatment can continue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Accept the family's decision to stop treatment, on the grounds that the client's own community is the only proper authority on what care is acceptable for them"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Engage the family and prayer camp leaders, explain psychosis as a treatable brain condition, and agree that prayer and medication can run together"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Arrange an involuntary admission under the mental health law so that antipsychotic treatment can continue without the family's involvement or consent at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In many West African settings, mental health care intersects with deeply rooted traditional and faith-based healing frameworks. Antagonistic approaches or police raids alienate families and drive patients into hiding. Complete abandonment leaves vulnerable clients without care. The evidenced-based, culturally congruent approach championed by the Mental Health Authority and WHO is intersectoral collaboration: engaging faith leaders and family elders respectfully, delivering non-shaming psychoeducation, and negotiating co-existence (allowing prayers/supportive faith rituals to continue while ensuring continuous medical antipsychotic therapy). Why the other options fail: Forcing a choice between treatment and belief is resolved in favour of the camp; accepting the family's decision leaves the psychosis untreated; an involuntary admission removes the client from the very support system that sustains them.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Intercultural mental health: Collaborate with community faith leaders to bridge traditional frameworks with continuous evidence-based psychotropic therapy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Encourage the client to breathe into a paper bag until the tingling stops"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Teach the client about the autonomic nervous system once the attack has settled"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Move the client to a quiet room and check back every fifteen minutes until the attack passes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stay with the client, reduce stimulation, speak in short calm sentences and guide slow breathing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$During panic-level anxiety (the highest level on Peplau's anxiety continuum), perceptual fields are completely closed, logical reasoning is suspended, and the individual experiences terror and physiological fight-or-flight surges. The nurse must: 1) stay with the client (never abandon someone in panic); 2) reduce environmental stimuli (dim lights, quiet room); 3) use a calm, low, reassuring tone; 4) speak in concise, simple sentences; and 5) guide physical breathing to reverse respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. Lengthy teaching cannot be processed; leaving the client alone intensifies terror; shouting escalates panic. Why the other options fail: Teaching about the autonomic nervous system, even once the attack has settled, is not the immediate action the panic requires; checking back every fifteen minutes leaves the client alone at the peak; a paper bag is no longer recommended and can worsen hypoxaemia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Interventions for panic-level anxiety: Stay with the client, minimize environmental stimuli, speak in short, calm, simple sentences, and guide slow rhythmic breathing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 48-year-old patient with severe sepsis, blood pressure 82/46 mmHg despite 2 L crystalloid resuscitation, and serum lactate 4.8 mmol/L"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An 82-year-old patient with metastatic lung cancer admitted with somnolence and comfort measures only requested by family"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 24-year-old alert patient with an isolated non-displaced radial fracture awaiting surgical pin placement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 62-year-old patient 48 hours post-elective uncomplicated hip arthroplasty with mild postoperative pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$ICU triage prioritizes critically ill patients with acute, reversible organ dysfunction who derive the greatest survival benefit from invasive monitoring and continuous titration of life-sustaining therapy (Priority 1). The 48-year-old patient is in septic shock refractory to initial fluid boluses with elevated lactate and urgently requires continuous vasopressor infusion and central venous/arterial line monitoring. Comfort-care patients are managed in palliative units; non-displaced fractures and uncomplicated post-op patients do not meet critical care admission criteria. Why the other options fail: option b has comfort measures only requested, which makes this a palliative admission rather than a critical care one. Option c has an isolated non-displaced fracture and is waiting for a minor procedure, which is ward care. Option d is 48 hours after an uncomplicated arthroplasty with mild pain, which is routine postoperative ward care.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Triage prioritization: Target reversible acute physiological collapse (septic shock) over futile terminal illness or non-critical stable conditions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Silence the audible alarm permanently to prevent disturbing other patients on the unit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Relocate the sensor to the forehead or earlobe and individualise alarm limits within orders"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Set the low SpO2 alarm threshold to 70% so the alarm will only trigger during true catastrophic desaturations"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discontinue continuous pulse oximetry monitoring entirely since the patient is awake and restless"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Mitigating alarm fatigue without compromising patient safety requires addressing technical causes of artifact and individualizing alarm limits based on the patient's baseline. In vasoconstricted or hypoperfused patients, digits often produce weak plethysmographic signals; moving the sensor to a centrally perfused site (forehead or earlobe) improves accuracy. Permanently silencing alarms or dropping thresholds below safe limits (such as 70%) violates patient safety standards and risks undetected hypoxic arrest. Why the other options fail: option a removes the alarm altogether, and with it the safety net. Option c sets the threshold at 70%, which permits profound hypoxaemia before anything sounds. Option d abandons monitoring on a patient whose restlessness may itself be hypoxia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alarm fatigue: Address signal artifacts at the sensor source (central vs peripheral site) rather than disabling safety thresholds.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis with hypercapnia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis with hypoxaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis with compensatory hypoventilation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis with severe hypoxaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A pH of 7.23 is acidic (normal 7.35–7.45). The PaCO2 of 70 mmHg is markedly elevated (normal 35–45 mmHg), which explains the acidaemia, confirming a respiratory cause. The serum bicarbonate (HCO3-) of 26 mEq/L is within normal limits (22–26 mEq/L), demonstrating that renal compensation has not yet occurred (uncompensated). PaO2 of 54 mmHg represents acute hypoxaemic failure. The patient requires immediate escalation (e.g., non-invasive positive pressure ventilation such as BiPAP or invasive mechanical ventilation). Why the other options fail: option a pairs a raised bicarbonate with a raised CO2, which is the compensation pattern of metabolic alkalosis, not an acute respiratory acidosis. Option b has a pH above 7.45. Option c makes bicarbonate the primary abnormality, but here the bicarbonate is normal at 26 and the CO2 is the problem.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ABG analysis: pH < 7.35 (acidosis) + high PaCO2 matches acidosis (respiratory) + normal HCO3 (uncompensated).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis; tachypnoea indicates imminent respiratory muscle failure requiring immediate paralytic intubation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis; the low bicarbonate has restored blood pH to the physiological reference range"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis; hyperventilation is a physiological compensatory mechanism to blow off carbon dioxide"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary respiratory alkalosis caused by acute hyperventilation syndrome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The pH is 7.28 (acidic), and HCO3- is 13 mEq/L (markedly low), indicating primary metabolic acidosis from ketoacid accumulation. The PaCO2 is 29 mmHg (low, normal 35–45 mmHg), showing that the respiratory system is attempting to compensate by blowing off volatile acid (Kussmaul respirations). Because the pH has not fully normalized, this represents partially compensated metabolic acidosis. Kussmaul breathing is an appropriate protective compensatory response and should not be suppressed. Why the other options fail: option a mistakes the primary disorder, because the bicarbonate of 13 identifies the metabolic process and the CO2 is falling to compensate. Option b calls it fully compensated, but the pH is still 7.28. Option d puts the pH on the wrong side of 7.35 and ignores the bicarbonate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DKA compensation: Low HCO3 matches low pH (metabolic acidosis); low PaCO2 shows active respiratory blowing off of acid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An immediate drop in mean arterial pressure (MAP) accompanied by reflex bradycardia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A marked rise in central venous pressure (CVP) with no change in pulse contour stroke volume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A rise in cardiac output or stroke volume of at least 10% to 15% during leg elevation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An increase in systemic vascular resistance (SVR) of greater than 30%"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Passive leg raising (PLR) shifts approximately 300 mL of venous blood from the lower extremities to the intrathoracic compartment, acting as a reversible 'autologous fluid bolus'. A transient increase in stroke volume or cardiac output of >= 10–15% within 60–90 seconds confirms preload responsiveness on the Frank-Starling curve, demonstrating that administering additional crystalloids will improve perfusion without precipitating pulmonary edema. Unchanged stroke volume despite elevated filling pressures indicates non-responsiveness. Why the other options fail: option a describes a fall in pressure with bradycardia, which is a vagal response, not a preload response. Option b describes a rise in filling pressure without a rise in stroke volume, which is the pattern of a patient who will not respond. Option d describes a rise in resistance, which is the afterload effect of a vasopressor, not the effect of raising the legs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dynamic fluid responsiveness: Look for an increase in stroke volume/cardiac output >= 10-15% during reversible autotransfusion (PLR).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately discontinue the arterial catheter without physician consultation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Re-zero the transducer while holding it at the level of the patient's ear"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer a rapid bolus of 500 mL normal saline through the arterial catheter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Check the tubing for air bubbles, clots, or kinks and perform a fast-flush square-wave test"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Overdamping of an arterial line is commonly caused by compliance dampeners such as air bubbles in the transducer tubing, blood clots at the catheter tip, loose connections, or anatomical kinks. The nurse should inspect the entire pressure tubing system, eliminate microbubbles, check catheter position, and execute a fast-flush (square-wave) test to evaluate dynamic system response. Fluids and medications must NEVER be bolused through an arterial line due to severe risk of limb ischaemia and arterial thrombosis. Why the other options fail: option a removes the line and the monitoring with it, before the cause is found. Option b zeroes the transducer at the wrong reference, which must be the phlebostatic axis at the level of the right atrium. Option c injects fluid into an artery, which risks limb ischaemia and is never done through an arterial line.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Arterial line troubleshooting: Overdamping = bubbles/clots/kinks. Inspect tubing and run square-wave test; NEVER bolus through an A-line.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the infusion, leave the cannula to aspirate residual drug, and prepare phentolamine for local infiltration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Increase the infusion rate to clear the peripheral vein and apply ice packs tightly over the forearm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately pull out the cannula and apply firm direct manual pressure over the site for 15 minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Flush the line vigorously with 20 mL of sterile water to dilute the vasopressor in local tissues"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Norepinephrine is a potent alpha-1 adrenergic agonist; peripheral extravasation causes intense local vasoconstriction leading to ischaemic tissue necrosis and gangrene. Immediate management: stop the infusion, leave the catheter in place to aspirate any residual vesicant, notify the provider, and administer the specific antidote phentolamine mesylate (an alpha-adrenergic antagonist) subcutaneously in a circle around the extravasated area to reverse vasoconstriction. Ice and vigorous flushing are contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option b increases the infusion of the vesicant and adds ice, which worsens vasoconstriction and necrosis. Option c removes the only route for aspirating the drug before it is absorbed. Option d dilutes with sterile water, which is not the antidote and adds volume to the injured tissue.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vasopressor extravasation: Stop immediately, do NOT pull catheter before aspirating, inject phentolamine (alpha-blocker) antidote.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Severe peripheral vasoconstriction and cold digits from alpha-1 receptor activation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Significant bradycardia with complete heart block and asystole"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypoglycaemia with metabolic alkalosis and hypokalaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ventricular tachyarrhythmias and tachycardia from beta-1 stimulation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dobutamine is a synthetic inotrope with predominantly beta-1 adrenergic receptor agonist activity (and mild beta-2 and alpha-1 activity). By augmenting myocardial contractility and AV nodal conduction, it increases myocardial oxygen consumption and can provoke sinus tachycardia, premature ventricular contractions (PVCs), and ventricular tachyarrhythmias. Continuous cardiac rhythm monitoring and blood pressure surveillance are mandatory. Why the other options fail: option a describes the effect of norepinephrine, an alpha agonist, not dobutamine. Option b describes a beta-blocker or a vagal effect, and dobutamine raises the heart rate. Option c describes neither the receptor profile nor the metabolic effect of a beta-1 agonist, which raises glucose rather than lowering it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inotrope pharmacology: Beta-1 agonists (dobutamine) increase contractility but carry high arrhythmogenic risk (tachycardia, PVCs, VT).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Silence the alarm and raise the high-pressure limit to 80 cmH2O so the set volume is delivered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a double dose of neuromuscular blocker without performing a physical assessment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Extubate at the bedside immediately and place a non-rebreather mask"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assess chest rise, check for tube biting or secretions, auscultate, then bag-ventilate if distress persists"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a high-pressure ventilator alarm triggers, the nurse first assesses the patient (DOPE mnemonic: Dislodgement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment). Common causes of high peak pressure include airway secretions (needing suctioning), patient biting the tube (needing a bite block), tubing kink, bronchospasm, or tension pneumothorax. If the cause is not immediately identifiable or the patient deteriorates, the nurse must immediately disconnect the patient from the ventilator and manually ventilate with 100% O2 via bag-valve-mask to assess lung compliance and maintain oxygenation. Why the other options fail: option a silences the alarm and raises the limit, which lets the ventilator push against an obstruction and cause barotrauma. Option b paralyses a patient who may have a blocked tube, which removes the only sign of distress. Option c extubates a patient who may still need the tube, without finding out why the pressure rose.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ventilator emergencies: Check patient first (DOPE: tube biting, secretions, pneumothorax). If distress continues, disconnect and manually bag with 100% O2.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "High tidal volumes of 12-15 mL/kg to re-expand collapsed alveoli and keep PaCO2 below 30 mmHg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Zero end-expiratory pressure to prevent barotrauma and right ventricular strain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Low tidal volumes of 4-6 mL/kg of predicted body weight, plateau pressure at or below 30 cmH2O, titrated PEEP"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperventilation to induce a respiratory alkalosis with a target pH above 7.55"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The ARDSNet protocol establishes that lung-protective ventilation utilizing low tidal volumes (4–8 mL/kg, targeting 6 mL/kg of predicted/ideal body weight) and keeping inspiratory plateau pressures <= 30 cmH2O significantly reduces mortality by mitigating ventilator-induced lung injury (volutrauma, barotrauma, biotrauma). PEEP is titrated to maintain alveolar recruitment and prevent atelectotrauma. Permissive hypercapnia is tolerated as long as severe acidaemia (pH < 7.15–7.20) is avoided. Why the other options fail: option a uses high tidal volumes, the strategy that causes volutrauma and the one ARDSNet abandoned. Option b removes PEEP, which lets the recruited alveoli collapse again with every breath. Option d targets an alkalosis that is not a goal, because permissive hypercapnia is accepted instead.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ARDSNet standard: 6 mL/kg ideal body weight + plateau pressure <= 30 cmH2O + permissive hypercapnia to protect fragile lung tissue.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Continue the trial for the full 30 minutes because transient tachypnoea is expected during weaning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the trial, return to the previous ventilator settings, reassure the patient, and look for the cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 10 mg of intravenous morphine to calm the patient and continue the trial"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Extubate the patient straight to non-invasive positive pressure ventilation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Criteria for failing a spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) include tachypnoea (respiratory rate > 35 breaths/min for >= 5 min), hypoxaemia (SpO2 < 88–90%), tachycardia (HR > 120 or >= 20% increase), accessory muscle use/paradoxical abdominal breathing, diaphoresis, and acute agitation or somnolence. The nurse must terminate the trial immediately, reconnect the patient to full ventilator support, provide reassurance, and investigate reversible causes (e.g., fatigue, secretions, bronchospasm, anxiety, electrolyte imbalances) before reattempting the next day. Why the other options fail: option a continues a trial that is failing, which exhausts the patient and delays the return to support. Option c sedates the respiratory distress instead of treating it and masks the failure. Option d extubates a patient who has just failed the test of breathing without the tube.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Weaning safety: RR > 35, SpO2 < 90%, HR > 120, or retractions = abort SBT immediately and restore full resting support.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "CPP is 96 mmHg; the nurse should administer an antihypertensive agent to prevent cerebral hyperperfusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "CPP is 52 mmHg; the nurse should notify the physician because CPP is below the target threshold of 60 to 70 mmHg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "CPP is 74 mmHg; the nurse should document that perfusion is optimal and maintain current management"}, {"id": "d", "text": "CPP is 22 mmHg; the nurse should hyperventilate the patient to a PaCO2 of 20 mmHg"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cerebral Perfusion Pressure is calculated as MAP minus ICP (CPP = MAP - ICP). Here, CPP = 74 mmHg - 22 mmHg = 52 mmHg. Evidence-based neurocritical care guidelines (Brain Trauma Foundation) recommend maintaining CPP between 60 and 70 mmHg to prevent secondary ischaemic brain injury. A CPP of 52 mmHg with elevated ICP (> 20 mmHg) requires immediate intervention, which may include titrating vasopressors to elevate MAP, administering hyperosmolar therapy (hypertonic saline or mannitol), and maintaining head-of-bed elevation. Why the other options fail: option a adds rather than subtracts, and 74 plus 22 is not a CPP of 96. Option c reports the MAP as if it were the CPP. Option d subtracts in the wrong order and hyperventilates to a CO2 of 20, which reduces cerebral blood flow further.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CPP formula: CPP = MAP - ICP. Target CPP is 60-70 mmHg; < 60 leads to secondary cerebral ischaemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give high-dose dexamethasone, wait 4 hours for urine cultures, and fluid-restrict to prevent pulmonary oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a 500 mL fluid challenge over two hours and reassess before starting antibiotics"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Measure lactate, obtain cultures before antibiotics, give broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, then 30 mL/kg crystalloid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give norepinephrine first and defer all fluids until the central line is in place"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Surviving Sepsis Campaign 1-Hour Bundle mandates five key actions initiated immediately upon recognizing sepsis/septic shock: (1) measure serum lactate (remeasure within 2–4 hours if elevated), (2) obtain blood cultures prior to administering antimicrobials, (3) administer broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials, (4) begin rapid administration of 30 mL/kg IV crystalloids for hypotension (MAP < 65) or lactate >= 4 mmol/L, and (5) apply vasopressors (norepinephrine first-line) if fluid resuscitation does not restore MAP >= 65 mmHg. Why the other options fail: option a delays antibiotics for cultures already drawn, gives steroids to every septic patient, and restricts fluid in one who needs it. Option b delays the antibiotics and gives a fraction of the 30 mL/kg that the bundle requires. Option d starts a vasopressor before the fluid bolus and waits on a line.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Surviving Sepsis 1-Hour Bundle: Lactate + blood cultures BEFORE antibiotics + broad-spectrum IV antibiotics + 30 mL/kg crystalloids.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A significant air embolism entering the arterial access catheter"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Impending clotting of the haemofilter, needing a circuit check and possible filter change"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Excessive systemic hypocoagulation with risk of major haemorrhage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Patient hypovolaemia requiring an immediate 1-litre saline bolus through the access line"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Transmembrane Pressure (TMP) represents the hydrostatic pressure difference across the semipermeable haemofilter membrane. A steadily rising TMP combined with an increasing filter pressure drop indicates protein layering and microthrombus formation within the hollow fibers (impending filter clotting). The nurse should assess the circuit, check anticoagulation delivery (e.g., regional citrate or heparin), and prepare to change the circuit if clotting progresses to avoid losing blood sequestered in a clotted filter. Why the other options fail: option a would show as a pressure drop with an air alarm, not a rising transmembrane pressure. Option c describes systemic coagulopathy, which is a bleeding risk in the patient and is not read from filter pressures. Option d describes hypovolaemia, which lowers the pressures rather than raising the TMP.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CRRT alarms: Elevated TMP and rising filter pressure drop indicate circuit/filter clotting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To sterilise the bloodstream directly and replace broad-spectrum antimicrobial therapy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To rapidly lower blood glucose and promote an osmotic diuresis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To induce systemic sedation and lower cerebral metabolic demand"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To reverse vasopressor-refractory shock caused by critical illness-related steroid insufficiency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In septic shock refractory to fluid resuscitation and escalating doses of vasopressors (norepinephrine >= 0.25 mcg/kg/min plus vasopressin), Surviving Sepsis guidelines recommend intravenous hydrocortisone at 200 mg per day. Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (CIRCI) and down-regulated vascular adrenergic receptors are common in severe sepsis. Low-dose hydrocortisone restores vascular tone, upregulates alpha-1 adrenergic receptors, and accelerates shock reversal without inducing immunosuppressive harm. Why the other options fail: option a gives hydrocortisone a sterilising role it does not have, and it never replaces antibiotics. Option b describes insulin, not hydrocortisone. Option c describes sedation, which is the opposite of what a patient in refractory shock needs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Refractory shock: IV hydrocortisone (200 mg/day) treats CIRCI and restores vascular responsiveness to catecholamines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute hypocalcaemia caused by citrate binding to circulating ionized calcium"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Severe hypercalcaemia from excessive bone resorption during massive transfusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypernatraemia resulting from high sodium content in packed red cells"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe hypokalaemia caused by rapid red cell potassium uptake"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Stored blood products contain sodium citrate as an anticoagulant. When massive volumes are infused rapidly (e.g., > 1 unit every 5 minutes), the liver's metabolic capacity to clear citrate is overwhelmed, particularly in hypothermic or shocked patients. Citrate chelates circulating ionized calcium, causing acute hypocalcaemia characterized by QT prolongation, neuromuscular excitability (Trousseau sign, Chvostek sign, twitching), decreased cardiac contractility, and hypotension. The nurse should anticipate IV calcium chloride or calcium gluconate replacement. Why the other options fail: option b is the opposite calcium change, which follows immobilisation or malignancy rather than rapid transfusion. Option c misplaces the sodium load, which is real but gives hypernatraemia without the QT prolongation and twitching described. Option d is the wrong direction for potassium and is not caused by citrate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Massive transfusion complication: Citrate toxicity binds ionized calcium -> hypocalcaemia (long QT, twitching/Trousseau sign). Treat with IV calcium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypothermia, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hyperkalaemia, hypercalcaemia, and hypermagnesaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tachycardia, hypoxaemia, and hypocapnia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The 'lethal triad' of trauma consists of hypothermia, metabolic acidosis, and coagulopathy. Each component exacerbates the others: hypothermia impairs coagulation enzyme cascades and platelet function; hypoperfusion causes lactic acidosis; and acidosis further inhibits thrombin generation. Failure to interrupt this vicious cycle leads to uncontrollable microvascular bleeding and death. Management requires active rewarming, damage control surgery, dynamic fluid resuscitation, and balanced blood component transfusion (1:1:1 PRBCs, FFP, platelets). Why the other options fail: option a lists hypertension and bradycardia, the opposite of the shocked trauma patient. Option c lists three electrolyte rises, and none of them is part of the triad. Option d describes a respiratory picture; tachycardia and hypocapnia may accompany shock but are not what drives the bleeding.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Trauma lethal triad: Hypothermia + Acidosis + Coagulopathy. Break the cycle with rewarming, perfusion restoration, and balanced transfusion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer intravenous naloxone continuous infusion to reverse central nervous system depression"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give high-dose intravenous flumazenil to antagonise benzodiazepine receptors"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Start the N-acetylcysteine protocol promptly to replenish glutathione and prevent hepatic necrosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse sodium polystyrene sulfonate to accelerate gastrointestinal excretion of the drug"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acetaminophen overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation pathways, shunting metabolism to cytochrome P450, which produces the toxic metabolite NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine). When endogenous glutathione is depleted, NAPQI binds hepatocytes causing centrilobular hepatic necrosis. N-acetylcysteine (NAC) serves as a glutathione precursor and substitute, detoxifying NAPQI. Initiating NAC within 8 hours of acute ingestion provides virtually 100% hepatoprotection. Why the other options fail: option a reverses opioid effect, and paracetamol is not an opioid, so naloxone does nothing for NAPQI. Option b antagonises benzodiazepines that the history does not mention, and flumazenil can precipitate seizures. Option d binds potassium in the gut and has no effect on hepatic glutathione.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Acetaminophen antidote: N-acetylcysteine (NAC) replenishes glutathione to neutralize toxic NAPQI metabolite.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Identify and treat the medical triggers, reorient the patient, promote sleep, and mobilise early"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Immediately administer high-dose intravenous haloperidol every 2 hours without investigating underlying physiological causes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply four-point leather restraints and keep the window blinds closed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Initiate continuous deep benzodiazepine sedation targeting a RASS score of -5"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$ICU delirium is a clinical syndrome of acute brain dysfunction characterized by fluctuating inattention and altered awareness. First-line management focuses on non-pharmacological interventions and identifying modifiable physiological causes (hypoxia, sepsis, metabolic derangements, pain, anticholinergic/sedative medications, urinary retention). Evidence-based bundles (ABCDEF bundle) emphasize pain control, daily sedation vacation, spontaneous breathing trials, delirium monitoring, early physical mobilization, and sleep optimization. Benzodiazepines and unnecessary restraints worsen delirium. Why the other options fail: option b sedates with an antipsychotic before the cause is found, and haloperidol prolongs the QT without treating the hypoxia or the sepsis. Option c restrains and darkens the room, which worsens disorientation. Option d deepens sedation to a RASS of -5, which is a coma and the opposite of the early mobilisation the bundle requires.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ICU delirium (CAM-ICU positive): Non-pharmacologic bundle first (treat causes, mobilize, reorient, optimize sleep). Avoid benzodiazepines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep systolic pressure above 100 mmHg, urine output 1-3 mL/kg/h, PaO2 above 100 mmHg, and normothermia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Permit profound hypotension and hypothermia to slow down organ cellular metabolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict all intravenous fluids and stop mechanical ventilation before the operating room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maintain continuous deep sedation with high-dose propofol and fentanyl infusions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Brain death produces autonomic storm followed by loss of sympathetic vascular tone, diabetes insipidus, poikilothermia, and endocrine failure. To preserve organ viability for transplantation, the nurse maintains optimal physiological goals (often remembered as the 'Rule of 100s'): systolic blood pressure >= 100 mmHg (MAP >= 65), PaO2 >= 100 mmHg (SaO2 >= 95%), urine output 100–200 mL/h (or 1–3 mL/kg/h), blood glucose 100–150 mg/dL, and core body temperature > 36 °C. Hormonal resuscitation (levothyroxine/T4, methylprednisolone, vasopressin) is standard. Why the other options fail: option b permits the hypotension and hypothermia that injure the organs being preserved. Option c withdraws the ventilation and fluids that maintain oxygenation and perfusion. Option d deepens sedation, which suppresses the neurological assessment and the sympathetic tone the donor depends on.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Organ donor management (Rule of 100s): SBP > 100, PaO2 > 100, urine output 100 mL/h (1-3 mL/kg/h), normothermia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Begin door-to-door immunisation and household assessment straight away, and introduce yourself to the elders at the first community durbar"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pay a formal courtesy visit to the chief, elders, queen mother, assembly member and unit committee, explaining the mission and asking their cooperation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Meet the district assembly first and let its officers inform the chief, since the CHPS compound is a government facility"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold an open meeting at the market for mothers, since the women are the main users of the service and their consent is sufficient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Community entry is the formal, socially accepted process of entering a community to initiate public health programmes. In Ghana and West African community health practice, the nurse must first pay a formal courtesy call on traditional authority figures (the Chief, elders, Queen Mother) alongside elected political representatives (Assembly Member, Unit Committee members). Bypassing traditional authority breaches cultural protocol, generates community distrust, and leads to resistance to health initiatives. Once permission, blessing, and partnership are secured, a community durbar is organized to introduce health staff to the wider community. Why the other options fail: option a skips the courtesy call that secures permission and trust, and a later durbar cannot repair the breach. Option c routes the entry through the district assembly and leaves out the traditional authority the community actually looks to. Option d assumes that mothers' attendance substitutes for the chief's consent, which it does not.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Community entry protocol: Always start with traditional leadership (Chief, Queen Mother, elders) and local governance representatives before initiating any field activities.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Community ownership and intersectoral partnership, with members planning, mobilising local resources and overseeing the facility"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Clinical supervision of the nurse's prescriptions and diagnostic decisions, through a committee of senior clinicians"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Collection of user fees to fund the district health directorate's outreach programme and staff allowances"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Day-to-day management of the CHPS compound's paid staff, including recruitment, postings and discipline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the Alma-Ata declaration and the Ghana CHPS policy, Primary Health Care relies heavily on community participation and intersectoral collaboration. The Community Health Management Committee (CHMC) consists of respected, diverse community representatives (women leaders, youth representatives, elders, teachers). Their role is to foster community ownership, maintain the CHPS compound, mobilize communal labor and local resources, support community emergency transport systems (CETS), and provide liaison between community members and health staff. Why the other options fail: option b gives the committee clinical authority it does not have, because supervision of practice belongs to the health system rather than to community representatives. Option c turns a community body into a revenue collector for the directorate, which is not its function. Option d gives it employment powers over staff, which belong to the district health administration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PHC principles: Community Health Management Committees (CHMC) represent community participation and ownership, not clinical supervision.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To assess property values so that the district assembly can levy household rates on the community"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To identify illegal structures for referral to the municipal building inspector before services begin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To decide which households should pay for services and which should be excluded from free immunisation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To map boundaries, household locations, water sources, vulnerable groups and local resources, so that interventions can be targeted"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A community profile and social mapping exercise is a participatory epidemiological assessment conducted by the Community Health Officer with community volunteers. It maps out geographical zones, household distribution, pregnant women, under-five children, water points, latrines, refuse dumps, and local resources. This enables the CHO to establish a functional household register, plan outreach routes, track defaulters, and prioritize high-risk families. Why the other options fail: option a turns a public health exercise into a tax assessment and guarantees resistance. Option b turns it into code enforcement, which is not its purpose and would deter households from being mapped at all. Option c uses the profile to exclude households from services, when its purpose is to find the ones that need them.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Social mapping: Tool to visualize community layout, household populations, vulnerable groups, and public health risks for targeted outreach.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Advocating for national and municipal policies that ban trans fats and tax tobacco and sugar-sweetened drinks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administering measles-rubella vaccine to infants at 9 months and vitamin A to children under five"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Screening random blood glucose at a community health fair and referring raised readings for confirmation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Providing physiotherapy and occupational rehabilitation to an elder after a stroke"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Primordial prevention targets the underlying economic, social, environmental, and behavioral conditions and policies to prevent the emergence and establishment of risk factors themselves (e.g., legislation banning trans-fats, taxing tobacco/sugary drinks, urban planning for sidewalks). Primary prevention prevents disease onset in individuals who already possess or are exposed to risk factors (e.g., vaccination, bed nets). Secondary prevention involves early detection and prompt treatment (e.g., screenings). Tertiary prevention aims to reduce disability and rehabilitate established illness. Why the other options fail: option b is primary prevention, immunising an infant before exposure. Option c is secondary prevention, detecting disease early in an asymptomatic person. Option d is tertiary prevention, limiting disability after a stroke. None acts on the risk factors before they become established.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prevention levels: Primordial = policies/laws stopping risk factors from emerging; Primary = stopping disease before it begins; Secondary = screening; Tertiary = rehabilitation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "IMR = (1,200 / 20,000) * 100,000 = 6,000 infant deaths per 100,000 population"}, {"id": "b", "text": "IMR = (1,200 / 20,000) * 1,000 = 60 infant deaths per 1,000 live births"}, {"id": "c", "text": "IMR = (20,000 / 1,200) * 1,000 = 16.6 per 1,000 live births"}, {"id": "d", "text": "IMR = (1,200 / 18,800) * 100 = 6.38 percent mortality"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Infant Mortality Rate (IMR) is defined as the number of deaths of infants under one year of age per 1,000 live births in a given population during a specific year. Formula: (Number of infant deaths under 1 year / Total live births) * 1,000. Here, (1,200 / 20,000) * 1,000 = 60 deaths per 1,000 live births. IMR is universally recognized as one of the most sensitive indicators of general community health, socioeconomic development, and healthcare system quality. (Note: Maternal Mortality Ratio uses a multiplier of 100,000). Why the other options fail: option a multiplies by 100,000, the convention for the maternal mortality ratio, not the infant rate. Option c inverts the fraction and divides births by deaths. Option d divides deaths by survivors and multiplies by 100, which is neither the rate nor a percentage of live births.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IMR calculation: (Deaths under 1 year / Live births) * 1,000. Expressed per 1,000 live births.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait for laboratory confirmation of at least 50 cases before opening a line list or alerting the district team"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Send the stool specimens first and begin the case search only after the laboratory reports the organism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rehydrate and isolate patients, notify the district disease control officer, define a case, and line-list by time, place and person"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inspect the river and the water sources first, and begin clinical care once the source has been confirmed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The initial steps in outbreak investigation demand parallel action: (1) provide immediate clinical care and infection control (rehydration and isolation), (2) notify the District Health Management Team (DHMT) within 24 hours, (3) verify the diagnosis with laboratory stool specimens (Cary-Blair medium), (4) establish a clear case definition, and (5) construct a line list characterizing cases by person (age, sex, occupation), place (hamlet, water source), and time (date and hour of onset) to construct an epidemic curve and identify the source of exposure. Why the other options fail: option a waits for a case count that no protocol sets and delays the notification that starts the investigation. Option b puts laboratory confirmation ahead of the case search, so the outbreak grows while the results are awaited. Option d investigates the environment before treating patients, when care, notification and the line list run in parallel and come first.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Outbreak investigation sequence: Treat and isolate -> notify DHMT immediately -> establish case definition -> line list cases (time, place, person).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Imposing fines on households that have not built a latrine, collected by the unit committee"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A lecture on faecal-oral transmission illustrated with a projector, delivered at the community centre"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The transect walk to open defecation sites, followed by placing fresh faeces beside food to show fly transmission"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Distributing free cement and slabs to every household so that latrines can be built without cost"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Community-Led Total Sanitation (CLTS) is an innovative methodology for mobilizing communities to completely eliminate open defecation without external subsidies. The triggering process uses participatory rural appraisal tools: the transect walk ('walk of shame/praise') where villagers guide facilitators to open defecation areas, calculation of medical costs, feces calculation (tons produced annually), and the 'food and shit' demonstration where flies move from feces directly onto food. Triggering collective shame and disgust prompts the community to declare an emergency action plan to achieve Open Defecation Free (ODF) status using locally available materials. Why the other options fail: option a substitutes a penalty for participation, which is a subsidy by another name and does not create collective motivation. Option b is a lecture, and CLTS works through the community's own discovery rather than being told. Option d provides hardware, which is the one thing CLTS deliberately withholds.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CLTS triggering: Uses participatory demonstrations (transect walk, fly-feces-food demo) to ignite collective disgust and community-led latrine building.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bring water to a rolling boil for at least 1 minute, cool it, and store it covered in a narrow-necked container with a tap or ladle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave water in an open bucket in the sun for 10 minutes, which is enough to kill cysts and helminth eggs before drinking"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Strain water through a single layer of nylon mesh and drink it immediately, without boiling or disinfection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add a few drops of kerosene to settle the sediment, then pour off the clear water for infants to drink"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Boiling water to a vigorous rolling boil for at least 1 minute (or 3 minutes at high altitudes) kills bacteria, viruses, and protozoan cysts (including Giardia and Cryptosporidium). Safe storage is equally vital to prevent recontamination: water must be stored in a clean, covered container with a narrow neck or a fitted tap. If wide-mouthed buckets are used, unwashed hands dipping cups into the water introduce pathogens. Water treatment without safe storage fails to interrupt the faecal-oral cycle. Why the other options fail: option b relies on ten minutes of sun in an open bucket, which does not inactivate cysts or helminth eggs. Option c strains out debris but removes neither bacteria nor viruses, and drinking immediately adds recontamination. Option d adds kerosene, which clears the appearance of the water and poisons the infant.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Household water safety: Rolling boil for 1 minute + safe storage in narrow-necked covered container with tap to prevent recontamination.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wash and scrub the sprayed internal walls with soap and water as soon as the team has left, so that children do not touch the deposit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Seal every window and door for 48 hours and keep the household out of the compound for that time"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remove food, utensils and animals, cover furniture, stay out for 2 hours, and do not wash or plaster the walls for 6 months"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Repaint the walls with white latex within 24 hours, which seals the insecticide into the plaster and prolongs its effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Indoor Residual Spraying (IRS) applies a long-lasting insecticide to the interior walls and ceilings where endophilic Anopheles mosquitoes rest after a blood meal. Community preparation requires: (1) removing food, water, utensils, and animals; (2) moving heavy furniture to the center and covering it; (3) sweeping floors before spraying; (4) remaining outside for 2 hours post-spraying; and (5) critically, refraining from washing, scrubbing, plastering, or repainting sprayed walls for at least 6 months to maintain the residual insecticide deposit. Why the other options fail: option a washes the insecticide off the walls within hours and removes the residual effect the spray is meant to give. Option b seals the house and keeps the family out, which is neither necessary nor safe. Option d paints over the deposit, which hides it from the mosquito as effectively as washing it off.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IRS community education: Protect food/pets, vacate for 2 hours, and DO NOT wash, plaster, or paint walls for 6 months.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Growth faltering: a flat weight curve over 3 months is an early danger sign and needs a feeding assessment and follow-up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Optimal growth, because a weight between the median and -2 SD lines is within the normal range"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Excess adipose tissue, which needs calorie restriction and a reduction in the number of breast feeds"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Normal plateauing at this age, which needs no investigation and can be reviewed at the next routine visit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Growth monitoring evaluates the trajectory (trend) of growth over time, not merely a single point in time. A horizontal or downward curve on the growth chart signifies growth faltering (growth stagnation), which precedes clinical wasting or stunting. Even if the weight point is currently within the 'green' or normal zone, failure to gain weight over consecutive months indicates inadequate energy intake, recurrent subclinical infection (e.g., giardiasis, urinary infection), or poor complementary feeding. The nurse must explore complementary feeding practices, check for illness, and schedule a 2-week follow-up. Why the other options fail: option b reads a single point as normal and ignores the flat trajectory, which is the finding. Option c diagnoses overfeeding in a child who is not gaining weight at all. Option d calls three months without weight gain normal plateauing, when growth faltering is the earlier and treatable signal.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Growth chart interpretation: The DIRECTION of the curve matters most. A flat or falling line means growth faltering regardless of where it plots.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trace defaulters through the child register and phone numbers, and send community volunteers to bring them for the missed doses"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restart the whole pentavalent series from dose 1 for every child who missed a date, so that immunity is complete"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Suspend outreach sessions until attendance improves, so that staff time is not wasted on small numbers"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Report the community to the district assembly for failing to meet the national immunisation coverage target"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An EPI dropout rate (Penta 1 to Penta 3) exceeding 10% indicates poor service utilization, accessibility barriers, or loss to follow-up. National EPI protocols mandate active defaulter tracing using the clinic immunization register, appointment tickler files, and Community Health Volunteers (CHVs). CHVs trace caregivers at home to identify barriers (e.g., maternal illness, relocation, fear of adverse events) and encourage attendance. Missed doses are given immediately without restarting the primary series. Why the other options fail: option b restarts a series that does not need restarting, which wastes vaccine and gives children doses they do not need. Option c suspends the service that defaulters need in order to return. Option d treats a health-system problem as a community offence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$EPI dropout management: A dropout rate > 10% requires active defaulter tracking through home visits by volunteers. Never restart missed vaccine series.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the mother a contraceptive and start the infant on cereal porridge, since breastfeeding alone is not enough by day 2"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess the newborn's breathing, temperature, feeding and cord, and the mother's involution, lochia and blood pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise the mother to stay indoors for 40 days without opening the windows, which protects her from cold and evil spirits"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bathe the newborn in cold herbal water, which hardens the skin and prevents rash in the first week"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The first 48 hours postpartum carry the highest risk of neonatal and maternal mortality. Recommended postnatal home visits occur on day 1 (within 24 hours), day 3, day 7, and at 6 weeks. The CHO must examine the newborn for danger signs (tachypnoea > 60 bpm, chest indrawing, hypothermia < 36.5 °C, lethargy, poor sucking, cord bleeding/pus) and promote chlorhexidine 7.1% or clean dry cord care and exclusive breastfeeding. The mother is evaluated for postpartum haemorrhage, uterine tone, foul lochia, puerperal sepsis, and elevated blood pressure. Why the other options fail: option a adds a contraceptive and complementary food to a visit whose purpose is to find danger signs in the first 48 hours. Option c imposes a 40-day confinement without examining either patient. Option d applies cold herbal water to a newborn, which causes hypothermia rather than preventing rash.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Postnatal home visit priorities: Evaluate maternal bleeding/uterus/BP and newborn breathing/temperature/breastfeeding/cord care.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A combined oral contraceptive, started now, because oestrogen does not affect milk supply after 6 weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A combined transdermal patch, which avoids the liver and therefore the effect on breast milk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A progestogen-only injectable, implant or copper IUD, all of which are safe during exclusive breastfeeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A monthly combined injectable, which gives steadier hormone levels than a daily pill"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to the WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC), estrogen-containing contraceptives (combined oral contraceptives, combined injectables, and contraceptive patches) are Category 4 (unacceptable health risk) in the first 3 weeks postpartum and Category 3 (risks outweigh benefits) between 3 weeks and 6 weeks (or 6 months if exclusively breastfeeding) because estrogens suppress breast milk volume and composition and increase venous thromboembolism risk. In contrast, progestin-only methods (DMPA injections, levonorgestrel/etonogestrel implants, progestin-only pills) and copper IUDs do not impair lactation and are MEC Category 1 or 2 at 6 weeks postpartum. Why the other options fail: option a is an oestrogen-containing method in a mother who is exclusively breastfeeding, where combined methods are not first-line. Option b wrongly assumes that a transdermal route spares lactation, when the oestrogen still enters the circulation. Option d is a combined method by another route, with the same objection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contraception in breastfeeding mothers: Estrogen suppresses milk production (Category 3/4). Choose progestin-only methods (implants, DMPA, POPs) or copper IUDs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Admit for inpatient stabilisation with intravenous dextrose, because a MUAC below 11.5 cm always requires admission"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the mother a 2-week supply of fortified porridge and review the child at the next monthly clinic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a single dose of vitamin A and deworming and discharge, since the child has no oedema and is alert"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Enrol in the outpatient therapeutic programme: weekly RUTF, a 7-day course of oral amoxicillin, and weekly review"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under CMAM protocols, Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) is defined as MUAC < 11.5 cm (red) or weight-for-height Z-score < -3 SD or bilateral pitting edema. Children with SAM who are alert, clinically stable, have no medical complications, and pass the appetite test (eagerly consuming RUTF) are managed as outpatients in the Outpatient Therapeutic Programme (OTP). Management includes: weekly weight-based sachets of RUTF (Plumpy'Nut), a routine 7-day course of oral amoxicillin, single-dose deworming, Vitamin A (if not given in past month), and weekly clinic monitoring. Why the other options fail: option a admits a child who meets the criteria for outpatient care, which separates him from his family and uses a bed he does not need. Option b offers no therapeutic food and sets a review a month away. Option c gives micronutrients alone and treats severe acute malnutrition as if the problem were a deficiency.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CMAM triage: SAM (MUAC < 11.5 cm) + PASSED appetite test + NO medical complications = Outpatient Therapeutic Programme (OTP) with RUTF.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the mother two cartons of RUTF and ask her to feed the child by spoon every 2 hours at home, day and night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a rapid 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline over 15 minutes to correct the hypovolaemia caused by oedema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the child warm, give 10% glucose or sugar water, and refer urgently to an inpatient centre for F-75 feeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give oral amoxicillin and a vitamin A dose and review in 3 days, since the oedema will resolve with treatment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) with complications (severe +3 edema, failed appetite test, hypothermia, lethargy, or severe infection) cannot be managed safely in the community. Such children have reductive adaptation, impaired hepatic/cardiac function, and are at high risk of sudden death from hypothermia, hypoglycaemia, and fluid overload. The CHO must warm the child (skin-to-skin or warm clothing), give 10% oral glucose or sugar water (50 mL) to prevent hypoglycaemia during transit, and immediately refer to an Inpatient Stabilization Center where feeding starts with starter formula F-75. Rapid IV fluid boluses are strictly contraindicated in kwashiorkor due to acute heart failure. Why the other options fail: option a sends a child who failed the appetite test home with food he will not eat. Option b gives a rapid bolus that is contraindicated in kwashiorkor, where the myocardium cannot tolerate a sudden volume load. Option d treats a complicated case as if it could be managed at home, and misses the hypothermia, oedema and failed appetite test that define a complication.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SAM with complications: Failed appetite test or +3 edema = Inpatient Stabilization Center referral. Keep warm, give sugar water, NO rapid IV boluses.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure him that a reading without symptoms needs no treatment, and repeat the measurement at the next annual screening"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give sublingual nifedipine to bring the pressure down at once, then discharge him without a review appointment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise him to stop drinking plain water and to take only small amounts of fluid, so that blood volume falls"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain the reading, advise less salt and fewer bouillon cubes, more activity and no tobacco, and confirm the diagnosis within 2 weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hypertension is termed the 'silent killer' because it is usually asymptomatic until target-organ damage occurs (stroke, heart failure, chronic kidney disease). In West Africa, high dietary sodium from processed foods and bouillon seasoning cubes is a major contributor to hypertension. The nurse should explain the reading clearly, advise dietary modification (DASH approach, reducing sodium, increasing fruits/vegetables), smoking cessation, alcohol reduction, and regular exercise. The patient requires scheduled confirmatory re-evaluation within 1–2 weeks before establishing a formal diagnosis and pharmacological initiation. Why the other options fail: option a repeats the silent-killer error, treating the absence of symptoms as the absence of disease. Option b treats a raised reading with a single dose and removes the follow-up, which is the opposite of what the reading needs. Option c reduces fluid rather than sodium, which does not lower blood pressure and risks dehydration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Community hypertension screening: Educate on asymptomatic nature, promote dietary sodium reduction (bouillon cubes), and book confirmatory recheck.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place the bag on the floor beside the bed so that it does not soil the client's table, and wash your hands when you leave"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the bag on a clean barrier on a raised surface, clean your hands, take out only what is needed, and disinfect items before they go back"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the bag open during care so that items can be reached quickly, and wipe the outside with a damp cloth afterwards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Put used dressings in the main compartment and dispose of them at the health centre, so that the household bin is not contaminated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The community nursing bag is a vital tool that carries essential clinical instruments into home environments. Bag technique is an infection control procedure designed to prevent cross-contamination between households. Key principles: (1) place the bag on a clean plastic/paper barrier on an elevated dry surface (never directly on the floor); (2) perform hand hygiene before opening the bag; (3) extract only needed equipment onto a clean field; (4) used items (thermometer, scissors) must be cleaned and disinfected before returning to the bag; (5) contaminated biological waste is packaged separately and never placed inside the clean bag. Why the other options fail: option a puts a contaminated bag on the floor and cleans your hands only after you leave, so the next household is contaminated from the first touch. Option c leaves the bag open and wipes the outside afterwards, which does not clean the items inside it. Option d stores used dressings in the bag that carries clean equipment into the next home.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bag technique: Elevated surface + clean paper barrier + hand hygiene BEFORE opening bag + disinfect equipment before replacing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Exclude affected pupils until the rash has cleared, and tell the assembly why, so that other parents are reassured"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treat only the pupils with visible lesions, since scabies spreads through direct contact and not through households"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Spray the classrooms with insecticide while the pupils are seated, which kills the mites in the desks and floors"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give single-dose albendazole to all pupils, treat affected children and their households with permethrin, and tell parents privately"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$School health services provide screening, preventive treatment, health education, and a healthy school environment. Helminth infections cause iron-deficiency anaemia and cognitive impairment; routine school mass deworming with single-dose oral albendazole 400 mg (or mebendazole 500 mg) is standard. Scabies requires treating the affected pupils and all household members simultaneously with 5% permethrin cream (or benzyl benzoate) while washing and sun-drying bedclothes. Actions must preserve pupil dignity and confidentiality to avoid stigmatization and school dropouts. Why the other options fail: option a isolates children for a condition that is curable within a day and announces it publicly, which drives families away from treatment. Option b treats only the visible cases and leaves the household reservoir untreated, so reinfection follows. Option c sprays an insecticide into an occupied classroom, which does not kill mites living in skin and exposes children to a pesticide.$q$,
  strategy = $q$School health: Annual mass deworming (albendazole 400 mg) + simultaneous household treatment for scabies + anti-stigma protection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the grandmother to the police for child abuse, and remove the infant from the family's care pending investigation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave the practice alone, because traditional cord care is a cultural right and challenging it would damage trust"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell the mother to keep the grandmother away from the infant, and give the family no further explanation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clean the cord with clean water, explain the tetanus risk, demonstrate clean dry cord care or chlorhexidine gel, and involve the grandmother"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Applying substances like cow dung, ash, or mud to the umbilical stump introduces Clostridium tetani spores, causing neonatal tetanus (trismus, inability to suck, spasms, and high mortality). The nurse must provide culturally sensitive health education without belittling elders: gently wash away the substance, educate the family on the link between unsterile applications and tetanus, demonstrate the proper use of 7.1% chlorhexidine digluconate gel (or clean dry cord care), and empower the grandmother by involving her in safe newborn care. Why the other options fail: option a turns a health problem into a criminal case before anyone has explained it, and removes the infant from the family. Option b leaves a practice that causes neonatal tetanus unchallenged in the name of culture. Option c separates the grandmother from the infant without teaching anyone, so the practice returns when the nurse leaves.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Harmful traditional practices: Educate with cultural humility, clean the contamination, demonstrate safe alternatives (chlorhexidine), and partner with family elders.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Persuade her to leave the village quietly for a few weeks, so that the tension settles without involving the authorities"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Protect her safety and rights, alert the police, assembly member and social welfare, and teach the community how malaria is transmitted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refer the case to the chief and elders and accept their ruling, since witchcraft accusations fall under customary law"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain that the accusation is not a health matter and confine your role to treating the child's malaria"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Community health nurses are ethical advocates for human rights, vulnerable populations, and social justice. Witchcraft accusations in rural West Africa frequently target vulnerable, destitute elderly women with dementia, depression, or eccentricities. The nurse has a duty to protect the client by immediately contacting local law enforcement, community leadership (Chief, Assembly Member), and the Department of Social Welfare. Concurrently, the nurse conducts public health education explaining the medical etiology of illnesses like severe malaria, meningitis, and stroke to dismantle harmful supernatural scapegoating. Why the other options fail: option a removes the client from her home and rewards the accusation with her absence. Option c hands the case to customary arbitration, which cannot protect her from a mob. Option d confines the nurse to prescribing for malaria and abandons the advocacy duty that the threat makes immediate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Community advocacy: Protect vulnerable clients against human rights abuses, engage law enforcement/social welfare, and demystify illness etiology through health education.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CHN-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Isolate under VHF precautions and report the suspected case to the district disease control officer within 24 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait for the RT-PCR and serology results before notifying the district, so that the report is laboratory-confirmed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Record the case in the routine outpatient ledger and review the pattern at the end of the quarter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discharge with oral antipyretics and review in a week, since the fever has lasted only 3 days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the WHO and Ghana Health Service Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response (IDSR) strategy, conditions with epidemic potential (such as acute viral haemorrhagic fevers, yellow fever, cholera, and meningococcal meningitis) mandate immediate suspected case reporting within 24 hours by telephone, SMS, or direct call to the District Health Directorate, alongside immediate strict contact/droplet barrier isolation. Notification must NEVER be delayed pending laboratory confirmation, as rapid containment prevents catastrophic community transmission. Why the other options fail: option b waits for confirmation, which is exactly what the 24-hour rule forbids for a disease with epidemic potential. Option c files the case for quarterly review, which delays any response by months. Option d discharges a patient with a suspected haemorrhagic fever, which removes him from isolation and returns him to the community.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IDSR notification rules: Epidemic-prone conditions (haemorrhagic fevers, yellow fever, cholera) require immediate reporting within 24 hours upon SUSPICION, before lab results.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wash with soap and running water, then begin 28 days of three-drug PEP immediately, ideally within 2 hours and never after 72"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Squeeze the wound to express blood, apply concentrated bleach, and wait 7 days for the baseline HIV test before starting PEP"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Start PEP only if the worker's rapid antibody test is positive at the time of injury, since the drugs are toxic"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take a single dose of zidovudine within 24 hours, which is sufficient and needs no follow-up"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Immediate management of occupational blood exposure requires washing the wound thoroughly with soap and water (never scrubbing, squeezing, or applying caustic bleach). Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) with a 3-drug antiretroviral regimen (e.g., tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir) should be initiated as urgently as possible, ideally within 2 hours of exposure. PEP is ineffective if delayed beyond 72 hours. The full course lasts 28 days with baseline and follow-up HIV testing at 6 weeks and 3 months. Why the other options fail: option b squeezes and bleaches the wound, which damages tissue and has not been recommended for years, and it delays PEP by a week. Option c makes prophylaxis conditional on a test that will be negative in early infection. Option d gives a single drug for one day, when the regimen is three drugs for 28 days.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Occupational HIV PEP: Wash with soap/water (no squeezing), start 3-drug PEP within 2 hours (cutoff 72 hours), complete full 28-day course.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give it as a continuous 72-hour infusion in concentrated dextrose, so that the parasitaemia falls steadily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a single dose of crushed artemether-lumefantrine down the nasogastric tube and avoid parenteral therapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infuse intravenous chloroquine over 4 hours and monitor for hypotension, since chloroquine is the traditional first-line"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then once daily until a full 3-day oral ACT course can be taken"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$WHO and Ghana National Malaria Control Programme guidelines mandate intravenous or intramuscular artesunate (2.4 mg/kg for adults, 3.0 mg/kg for children < 20 kg) as the first-line treatment for severe malaria. The dosing schedule is 0, 12, and 24 hours, then once every 24 hours thereafter. A minimum of 3 parenteral doses must be given. Once the patient is awake and tolerating oral intake, a complete 3-day course of an oral artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT, such as artemether-lumefantrine or artesunate-amodiaquine) must be completed to prevent recrudescence. Why the other options fail: option a gives artesunate by continuous infusion, which is not its pharmacology and would miss the dose schedule. Option b relies on an enteral drug in a comatose patient and skips the parenteral doses the guidelines require. Option c returns to chloroquine, which falciparum resistance has made unusable for severe disease.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe malaria first-line: IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 hours (minimum 3 doses), followed by a complete 3-day oral ACT course.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Recrudescence of vivax malaria, which needs primaquine to clear the liver-stage parasites"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis, needing haemoglobin monitoring and transfusion if severe"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aplastic anaemia from permanent marrow suppression caused by the artesunate course"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura triggered by mosquito salivary antigens, which needs steroids"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Post-artemisinin delayed haemolysis (PADH) is a recognized phenomenon occurring 1 to 4 weeks (typically around days 7–21) after parenteral artesunate therapy in patients with high initial parasitaemia. Artesunate causes splenic 'pitting', where dead parasites are extracted from erythrocytes, leaving once-parasitized erythrocytes ('once-infected RBCs') with shortened lifespans that undergo delayed intravascular clearance. Patients present with haemolytic anaemia, jaundice, and dark urine. Management involves supportive care and blood transfusion if severe. Why the other options fail: option a names a recurrent infection, but the parasitaemia has cleared and the reticulocyte count is up, which points to destruction rather than relapse. Option c describes permanent marrow failure, which does not recover and does not raise reticulocytes. Option d gives a platelet disorder, which does not explain the falling haemoglobin and the indirect bilirubin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Post-artesunate surveillance: Watch for delayed haemolysis (PADH) 1-3 weeks post-treatment in patients with high initial parasitaemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Normal convalescence, since defervescence marks viral clearance and the end of the illness"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Critical warning signs of severe dengue: plasma leakage with impending shock, needing immediate crystalloid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A vasovagal reaction to the antipyretics, which needs rest and a further dose of paracetamol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Allergic gastroenteritis from an NSAID, which needs an antihistamine and a bland diet"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In dengue virus infection, the critical phase begins at defervescence (typically days 3–7 of illness). The development of dengue warning signs (severe abdominal pain, persistent vomiting, fluid accumulation/ascites/pleural effusion, mucosal bleeding, lethargy/restlessness, hepatomegaly > 2 cm, and rapid rise in haematocrit with sharp drop in platelets) signals systemic capillary permeability, plasma leakage, and impending Dengue Shock Syndrome (DSS). The nurse must immediately admit the patient for close monitoring and judicious isotonic IV fluid resuscitation. Why the other options fail: option a reads defervescence as recovery, when in dengue it marks the start of the critical phase. Option c attributes warning signs to a vagal response, which does not cause mucosal bleeding. Option d invents a drug reaction that does not produce abdominal pain with bleeding and lethargy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dengue warning signs: Defervescence + abdominal pain + persistent vomiting + mucosal bleed = critical phase plasma leakage / shock.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A shared bay with a cloth mask for the patient while sleeping, since the diagnosis is not yet confirmed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Standard precautions only until the sputum smear is positive, because cavitation alone does not transmit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Airborne isolation in a negative-pressure room, with fit-tested N95 respirators for all staff on entry"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Contact precautions with sterile gowns and shoe covers, since the organism survives on surfaces"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Mycobacterium tuberculosis is transmitted via droplet nuclei that remain suspended in the air. Any patient with presumptive active pulmonary tuberculosis (cavitary disease, chronic cough, haemoptysis) must be placed immediately in an Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR) with negative pressure (>= 6–12 air exchanges per hour) venting to the outside. Staff must wear fit-tested N95 (or FFP2) particulate respirators upon room entry. If transported, the patient must wear a surgical mask. Sputum GeneXpert MTB/RIF is the rapid molecular test of choice to identify TB and rifampicin resistance. Why the other options fail: option a leaves a cavitary case in a shared bay, which is the setting in which droplet nuclei spread. Option b waits for a smear, which is often negative and never a reason to relax precautions. Option d uses contact precautions, which do not address an organism carried in the air.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tuberculosis isolation: Airborne precautions (negative pressure room + fitted N95 respirator). Patient wears surgical mask during transport.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rifampicin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Isoniazid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pyrazinamide"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ethambutol"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Ethambutol causes dose-dependent retrobulbar optic neuritis, manifesting as decreased visual acuity, central scotomas, and loss of red-green color discrimination. Baseline and periodic visual acuity and color vision testing (Ishihara plates) are indicated. If visual toxicity occurs, ethambutol must be discontinued immediately to prevent permanent blindness. In contrast, rifampicin causes orange-red discoloration of body fluids; isoniazid causes peripheral neuropathy (prevented with pyridoxine/vitamin B6) and hepatotoxicity; and pyrazinamide causes hyperuricaemia and arthralgias. Why the other options fail: option a causes orange-red discolouration of body fluids, not visual loss. Option b causes peripheral neuropathy and hepatitis, prevented with pyridoxine. Option c causes hyperuricaemia and arthralgia. None of the three affects the optic nerve.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TB medication toxicities: Ethambutol = Eyes (optic neuritis, red-green color loss); Isoniazid = Neurons (neuropathy / B6 deficiency); Rifampicin = Red secretions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Switch to second-line protease inhibitor therapy now, since a detectable load means the regimen has failed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop all antiretrovirals for 4 weeks, so that the resistant mutants are cleared before restarting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Start enhanced adherence counselling for 3 monthly sessions and repeat the viral load in 3 months before any switch"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure the client that 3,200 copies/mL still counts as suppression, and review in a year"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In national and WHO HIV treatment guidelines, virological non-suppression is defined as a viral load >= 1,000 copies/mL on two consecutive measurements 3 months apart. When an elevated viral load is detected in a patient reporting missed doses, the immediate protocol is not an automatic drug switch; instead, the nurse initiates Enhanced Adherence Counseling (EAC) monthly for 3 months to identify and overcome barriers to 95%+ pill adherence. A repeat viral load is conducted after 3 months; if still >= 1,000 copies/mL despite documented adherence, treatment failure is confirmed and the patient is switched to second-line therapy. Why the other options fail: option a switches regimen on one result and before adherence has been addressed, which risks losing the second line as well. Option b stops treatment, which allows the virus to rebound. Option d calls 3,200 copies/mL suppression, when the threshold is below 1,000 on two occasions.$q$,
  strategy = $q$HIV viral non-suppression (VL >= 1,000): Provide Enhanced Adherence Counseling (EAC) for 3 months and retest before switching regimens.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start oral lamivudine for the infant and delay all vaccinations until the 6-week visit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give HBIG alone at the 6-week child welfare clinic, when the vaccine schedule begins"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give hepatitis B vaccine and HBIG within 12 hours, at separate intramuscular sites"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give no prophylaxis if the baby was delivered by elective caesarean section, because there was no exposure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Perinatal transmission from HBsAg-positive mothers carries up to a 90% risk of chronic neonatal infection leading to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma in adulthood. Immediate prevention requires administering Hepatitis B vaccine (monovalent birth dose) and Hepatitis B Immune Globulin (HBIG, 0.5 mL) within 12 hours of birth at two separate anatomical sites (e.g., left and right anterolateral thighs). This combined active-passive immunoprophylaxis reduces transmission rates by over 90–95%. The infant then completes the standard pentavalent vaccine series at 6, 10, and 14 weeks. Why the other options fail: option a gives an antiviral to the infant and postpones the vaccine that prevents carriage. Option b gives passive antibody alone and six weeks late, by which time transmission may already have occurred. Option d treats caesarean delivery as protection, when the risk comes from maternal blood and the birth process itself.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hepatitis B perinatal prophylaxis: Monovalent HepB vaccine + HBIG given within 12 hours of birth at separate injection sites.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give oral rehydration solution by spoon and withhold intravenous fluid until the pulse returns"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Begin WHO Plan C: Ringer's lactate 100 mL/kg, 30 mL/kg in the first 30 minutes then 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give intravenous loperamide to stop the stools, so that the fluid losses slow enough to catch up"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse 500 mL of 5% dextrose over 8 hours, which avoids the risk of hypernatraemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The patient presents with severe cholera dehydration with hypovolaemic shock (lethargy, absent peripheral pulse, skin tenting > 2 s), which is rapidly fatal within hours without aggressive volume restoration. Under WHO Plan C for cholera, adult fluid resuscitation requires 100 mL/kg of IV Ringer's lactate: 30 mL/kg within the first 30 minutes, followed by 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours. As soon as the patient is able to drink, oral rehydration salts (ORS) and oral zinc are initiated, followed by single-dose oral doxycycline or azithromycin to reduce duration and shedding. Antimotility drugs (like loperamide) are strictly contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option a gives oral fluid to a patient with no radial pulse, which cannot be absorbed fast enough. Option c gives an antimotility drug, which is contraindicated in cholera. Option d gives dextrose without electrolyte at a rate far too slow for shock.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe cholera dehydration (Plan C): Rapid IV Ringer's lactate (100 mL/kg: 30 mL/kg in 30 min, 70 mL/kg in 2.5 h). NEVER give loperamide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Typhoid intestinal perforation of the terminal ileum, with acute peritonitis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Benign ileocaecal spasm from the oral rehydration, which settles with an antispasmodic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Amoebic colitis, which responds to oral metronidazole without surgery"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute cholecystitis with intact peritoneal boundaries, which needs an ultrasound"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Salmonella Typhi invades the Peyer patches in the lymphoid tissue of the terminal ileum. During the late second to third week of illness, necrosis and ulceration of Peyer patches can lead to intestinal perforation, the most lethal surgical complication of typhoid fever. Clinical signs include sudden severe abdominal pain, board-like involuntary guarding, rebound tenderness, free intraperitoneal air causing loss of hepatic dullness on percussion, and hypovolaemic/septic shock. The nurse must make the patient nil by mouth, initiate resuscitation, start broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, and alert the surgical team immediately. Why the other options fail: option b calls a rigid abdomen with lost liver dullness a spasm, which delays surgery. Option c gives a medical diagnosis and an oral drug to a patient in shock. Option d keeps the peritoneum intact, when free air and guarding show that it is not.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Typhoid fever red flags: Late 2nd/3rd week sudden abdominal pain + rigid abdomen + shock = ileal perforation. Emergency laparotomy needed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Airborne precautions in a negative-pressure room for 3 weeks, since meningococcus survives in air"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Droplet precautions until 24 hours of effective antibiotic, with ciprofloxacin prophylaxis for hostel contacts"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contact precautions only, with no prophylaxis for roommates because they were vaccinated"}, {"id": "d", "text": "No isolation once IV ceftriaxone is started, since the drug renders the patient non-infectious at once"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Meningococcal meningitis (caused by Neisseria meningitidis) is transmitted via large respiratory droplets. Droplet precautions (surgical mask within 1–2 metres, private room) must be maintained until 24 hours after initiation of effective antimicrobial therapy (e.g., IV ceftriaxone or penicillin). Close contacts (household members, dorm roommates, healthcare workers with direct oral secretion exposure such as during CPR or endotracheal intubation) must receive post-exposure chemoprophylaxis with single-dose oral ciprofloxacin (500 mg), rifampicin, or IM ceftriaxone as soon as possible. Why the other options fail: option a over-isolates and under-treats, since meningococcus is a droplet organism and 3 weeks is not the rule. Option c drops prophylaxis for the contacts at highest risk. Option d ends isolation on the first dose rather than after 24 hours of treatment.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Meningococcal disease: Droplet precautions for 24 hours after starting antibiotics + chemoprophylaxis (ciprofloxacin/rifampicin) for close contacts.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Two age-appropriate doses of high-dose vitamin A, 200,000 IU for a child of 12 months or more, 24 hours apart"}, {"id": "b", "text": "High-dose oral aspirin every 4 hours for 7 days, to control the fever and the rash"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prophylactic oral chloramphenicol for 14 days, to prevent secondary bacterial infection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Systemic corticosteroids to suppress the rash, which shortens the illness and prevents croup"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$WHO and UNICEF protocols mandate that all children diagnosed with acute measles receive two doses of high-potency oral Vitamin A given on consecutive days (50,000 IU for infants < 6 months, 100,000 IU for 6–11 months, and 200,000 IU for >= 12 months; a third dose is added at 2–4 weeks if signs of xerophthalmia exist). Measles rapidly depletes vitamin A stores, which can lead to corneal ulceration, blindness, severe pneumonia, and croup. Vitamin A therapy reduces overall measles mortality and morbidity by up to 50%. Aspirin is contraindicated due to the risk of Reye syndrome. Why the other options fail: option b gives aspirin, which is contraindicated in measles because of Reye syndrome. Option c gives an antibiotic that does not treat a viral illness and does not reduce mortality. Option d gives steroids, which suppress immunity in a child already made vulnerable by measles.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Measles therapy: High-dose Vitamin A (2 doses on consecutive days) restores epithelial integrity and reduces mortality by 50%. Never give aspirin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Airborne isolation for 6 weeks, since the cough spreads organisms over long distances"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Standard precautions only, with oral metronidazole for 10 days, since the infant is already coughing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intramuscular penicillin G, with routine observations only, since apnoea is uncommon after 4 months"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Droplet precautions, with oral azithromycin for 5 days, and continuous monitoring for apnoea and bradycardia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bordetella pertussis causes whooping cough, which in infants under 6 months presents with severe coughing spells, post-tussive cyanosis, apnoea, and bradycardia. Transmission occurs through large infectious respiratory droplets; the infant must be placed on droplet precautions until 5 days of effective macrolide therapy (such as azithromycin, 10 mg/kg on day 1, then 5 mg/kg on days 2–5) have been completed. Continuous cardiopulmonary and oxygen saturation monitoring is critical because infants are at high risk of sudden apnoea and death. Why the other options fail: option a uses airborne isolation, which pertussis does not require, and ciprofloxacin, which is not the drug of choice in a 4-month-old. Option b uses standard precautions only and metronidazole, which does not cover Bordetella. Option c gives penicillin and abandons monitoring in the age group most likely to stop breathing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pertussis management: Droplet precautions for 5 days of macrolide therapy (azithromycin). Monitor closely for apnoea and bradycardia in infants.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Suture the lacerations tightly, apply a herbal poultice, and give a single dose of tetanus toxoid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wash with soap and running water for 15 minutes, infiltrate RIG around the wound, and vaccinate on days 0, 3, 7 and 14"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give intravenous dexamethasone and observe for 10 days for signs of hydrophobia before treating"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clean with dry cotton without washing, so that the virus is not spread, and give oral amoxicillin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Rabies is virtually 100% fatal once clinical symptoms develop. Category III exposure (transdermal bites or scratches, licking of broken skin) mandates three immediate interventions: (1) immediate and vigorous washing of all bite wounds with soap, water, and povidone-iodine for at least 15 minutes (which physically removes and inactivates the envelope of the rabies virus); (2) prompt infiltration of Rabies Immune Globulin (RIG, 20 IU/kg) into and around the wound margins to provide immediate passive immunity; and (3) initiating the active cell-culture rabies vaccine series on days 0, 3, 7, and 14. Primary suturing of rabies wounds is contraindicated as it inoculates virus deeper into tissues. Why the other options fail: option a sutures a contaminated bite and leaves the virus in the tissue without immunoglobulin or vaccine. Option c gives steroids, which are not indicated, and waits for the disease, which is almost always fatal once it starts. Option d avoids washing, which removes the one intervention with proven benefit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rabies Category III PEP: Immediate 15-minute soap-and-water wash + infiltrate RIG around wound + complete 4-dose vaccine (0, 3, 7, 14). NEVER suture primarily.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous ribavirin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oral oseltamivir"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravenous ganciclovir"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral acyclovir"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Lassa fever is an acute arenavirus viral haemorrhagic fever endemic to West Africa, transmitted via the multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis) urine/faeces or human body fluids. Intravenous ribavirin is the specific, proven antiviral agent that significantly reduces mortality if initiated within the first 6 days of illness. In addition to ribavirin, strict viral haemorrhagic fever (VHF) barrier nursing precautions with impermeable personal protective equipment, fluid balance management, and avoidance of intramuscular injections are mandatory. Why the other options fail: option b treats influenza. Option c treats cytomegalovirus. Option d treats herpes simplex and varicella. None has activity against an arenavirus, and ribavirin is the only agent with a mortality benefit in Lassa fever.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lassa fever management: VHF barrier isolation + intravenous ribavirin started within the first 6 days of illness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oral albendazole 400 mg twice daily for 28 days, which clears the flukes in the bladder wall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intramuscular benzathine penicillin weekly for 3 weeks, to sterilise the urinary tract"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A single oral dose of praziquantel 40 mg/kg, taken immediately after food"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral metronidazole 500 mg three times daily for 10 days, which kills the adult worms"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Schistosoma haematobium is a digenetic blood fluke inhabiting the vesical venous plexus of the bladder and pelvis, causing urinary schistosomiasis (bilharzia) characterized by terminal haematuria, dysuria, and bladder wall calcification (with long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the bladder). Praziquantel at a single oral dose of 40 mg/kg taken after a meal is the drug of choice recommended by WHO and Ghana Health Service Neglected Tropical Disease (NTD) control programmes. Why the other options fail: option a is a deworming regimen that does not kill schistosomes. Option b treats syphilis. Option d treats anaerobic and protozoal infections but not the flukes in the vesical plexus.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Schistosomiasis treatment: Oral praziquantel 40 mg/kg single dose taken after food cures both S. haematobium and S. mansoni.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous vancomycin for 5 days, followed by oral cephalexin, which covers the usual skin organisms"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Immediate above-knee amputation, to prevent systemic dissemination of the infection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oral fluconazole for 6 months without local wound dressing, since the lesion is fungal in origin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rifampicin 10 mg/kg daily plus clarithromycin 7.5 mg/kg twice daily for 8 weeks, with wound care and physiotherapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Buruli ulcer is caused by Mycobacterium ulcerans, which produces the cytotoxic, immunosuppressive toxin mycolactone, leading to extensive painless necrotic skin ulceration with characteristic undermined edges. WHO and Ghana Health Service guidelines establish oral rifampicin (10 mg/kg once daily) combined with clarithromycin (7.5 mg/kg twice daily) for 8 weeks as the first-line treatment. This fully oral regimen has replaced older streptomycin injections. Early antimicrobial treatment combined with regular aseptic dressing and limb physiotherapy prevents joint contractures and disabling deformities. Why the other options fail: option a covers ordinary skin flora and does not touch Mycobacterium ulcerans. Option b amputates a limb that responds to oral therapy in 8 weeks, which is the outcome the regimen exists to prevent. Option c treats a fungal infection that the PCR has excluded.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Buruli ulcer treatment: Oral rifampicin + clarithromycin daily for 8 weeks + daily aseptic dressings and physiotherapy to avoid contractures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give intravenous loperamide and use alcohol rub between patients, since the diarrhoea is the main risk"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Switch to intravenous gentamicin and let the patient share a commode, since the spores are not on the skin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Airborne isolation with oral amoxicillin, since the spores become airborne during episodes of diarrhoea"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Contact precautions, hand washing with soap and water, stop the inciting antibiotics, and give oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clostridioides difficile is an anaerobic, spore-forming bacillus that proliferates following broad-spectrum antimicrobial destruction of normal colonic flora. Spores are resistant to alcohol-based hand rubs; healthcare workers MUST wash hands thoroughly with soap and water to physically remove spores. The patient requires Contact Precautions (dedicated gown, gloves, dedicated equipment) and disinfection with 0.5% hypochlorite (bleach). Treatment entails discontinuing the inciting antibiotic and administering oral vancomycin (125 mg 4 times daily) or oral fidaxomicin. Antimotility agents are contraindicated as they cause toxic megacolon. Why the other options fail: option a gives an antimotility drug, which risks toxic megacolon, and alcohol rub, which does not remove spores. Option b switches to a drug that does not treat C. difficile and shares a commode, which spreads spores. Option c uses airborne isolation for a contact organism and amoxicillin, which is one of the drugs that causes the disease.$q$,
  strategy = $q$C. difficile protocol: Soap and water hand hygiene (NOT alcohol rub) + Contact Precautions + Oral vancomycin. Antimotility drugs are contraindicated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The Litmus Acidity Strip Test"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The Bacterial Culture Plating Test"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The Shake Test"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The Specific Gravity Urinometer Test"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Aluminium-adsorbed vaccines (such as DTP-HepB-Hib pentavalent, Tetanus-diphtheria/Td, and Hepatitis B) are irreversibly damaged when frozen. Freezing breaks the antigen-adjuvant lattice, causing the aluminium to form heavy, agglomerated granules that precipitate rapidly and lose immunogenicity while increasing local reactogenicity. The WHO Shake Test compares a suspected frozen vial with a known deliberately frozen control vial: if the test vial sediments as fast as or faster than the frozen control (clearing within 15–30 minutes), the vaccine has been freeze-damaged and must be discarded. Why the other options fail: option a is used to check acidity, which is not what freezing changes. Option b cultures for sterility, which freezing does not necessarily breach. Option d measures urine density and has nothing to do with vaccines. Only the shake test detects the agglutination of aluminium adjuvant that freezing causes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vaccine cold chain safety: The Shake Test is the validated WHO method to detect irreversible freeze damage in aluminium-adsorbed vaccines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-COMM-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Secondary hypothyroidism caused by anterior pituitary failure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tertiary hypothyroidism due to hypothalamic TRH deficiency"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Primary hypothyroidism resulting from intrinsic thyroid gland dysfunction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Subclinical hyperthyroidism with peripheral hormone resistance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the negative feedback loop of the thyroid axis, low circulating free T4 triggers the anterior pituitary gland to release excess TSH to stimulate thyroid hormone production. When the thyroid gland itself fails (primary hypothyroidism, most commonly Hashimoto thyroiditis), T4 remains low despite high TSH stimulation. Conversely, secondary (pituitary) hypothyroidism would present with low or inappropriately normal TSH alongside low T4. Why the other options fail: option a describes secondary hypothyroidism, in which the pituitary fails and TSH is low or inappropriately normal, not high. Option b puts the lesion in the hypothalamus, which would also leave TSH low. Option d puts the error at the receptor and predicts high T4 with high TSH, not low T4, and subclinical hyperthyroidism by definition has a low TSH.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Endocrine Feedback Rule: High TSH + Low Free T4 = Primary Hypothyroidism (the problem is at the thyroid gland; pituitary is trying hard to stimulate it).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Discard the first void on day one, then collect all urine for 24 hours, including day two's first void"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect only the first morning urine specimen each day for three consecutive days and send them together"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Save the first morning urine on day one and stop collecting exactly at midnight that night"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the 24-hour container at room temperature and shake it well before each void"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Proper 24-hour urine collection protocol requires starting with an empty bladder: discard the first morning void on day 1 (record start time), then collect all urine produced over the subsequent 24 hours into a preservative container kept on ice or in the refrigerator. Exactly 24 hours from the start time on day 2, the client voids and adds that final specimen to the container. Why the other options fail: option b collects three first-morning specimens, which measure spot cortisol on three days rather than a 24-hour integral. Option c stops at midnight, which collects a daytime fraction, not 24 hours. Option d leaves the container at room temperature, which allows cortisol degradation; it must be kept on ice or refrigerated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$24-hour urine collection rule: Discard 1st morning void -> collect ALL urine for 24h on ice -> include final void at 24h mark.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately start subcutaneous basal-bolus insulin and arrange diabetes education"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that an HbA1c below 7.0% excludes diabetes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange a 2-week lifestyle trial and repeat the HbA1c in three months"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Repeat the fasting glucose or HbA1c on a subsequent day to confirm the diagnosis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to standard clinical guidelines (ADA / WHO), in the absence of unequivocal symptomatic hyperglycemia (polyuria, polydipsia, unexplained weight loss), a diagnosis of diabetes mellitus requires two abnormal test results from either the same sample or two separate test occasions (e.g., fasting plasma glucose >= 126 mg/dL [7.0 mmol/L], 2-hour OGTT >= 200 mg/dL [11.1 mmol/L], or HbA1c >= 6.5%). Why the other options fail: option a treats a diagnosis that has not been confirmed, and insulin is not started on one abnormal screen. Option b uses the treatment target of 7.0% as a diagnostic threshold, which it is not; the diagnostic cut-off is 6.5%. Option c postpones confirmation for three months, when a single repeat test on another day settles it now.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diabetes diagnostic criteria: Fasting glucose >= 126 mg/dL (7.0 mmol/L) or HbA1c >= 6.5%. If asymptomatic, confirm with repeat testing on a separate day.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give 4 units of rapid-acting lispro into the thigh before the workout to prevent hyperglycaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cancel the workout, because exercise is contraindicated below 150 mg/dL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Halve the next insulin dose and start the workout without eating anything"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eat a 15-30 g carbohydrate snack before exercise and carry rapid-acting glucose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Exercise increases skeletal muscle glucose uptake independent of insulin. For clients with type 1 diabetes, exercising when blood glucose is below 100 mg/dL (5.5 mmol/L) creates a severe risk of exercise-induced hypoglycemia. The client should consume a 15-30g complex carbohydrate snack prior to exercise and carry fast-acting glucose. (Exercise is avoided only if blood glucose is > 250 mg/dL WITH ketones present). Why the other options fail: option a adds insulin when the glucose is already 92 mg/dL and exercise is about to start, which compounds the risk of hypoglycaemia. Option b cancels exercise on a threshold that does not exist; 92 mg/dL is safe once carbohydrate is taken. Option c halves insulin but gives no carbohydrate, so the fall in glucose is unopposed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Type 1 diabetes & exercise: If pre-exercise BG < 100 mg/dL, eat a 15g carb snack first! Never inject insulin into active exercising limbs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediate twice-daily glibenclamide alongside the current diet"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Medical nutrition therapy, daily activity, and blood glucose self-monitoring"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Begin NPH insulin at bedtime and continue the current diet unchanged"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Admit for inpatient continuous glucose monitoring until delivery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$First-line management for gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) is medical nutrition therapy (MNT) with personalized carbohydrate distribution across meals/snacks, moderate daily physical activity, and frequent self-monitoring of blood glucose (fasting target < 95 mg/dL; 1-hour postprandial < 140 mg/dL). Pharmacotherapy (subcutaneous insulin) is introduced only if glycemic targets are not achieved with lifestyle modifications. Why the other options fail: option a starts a sulfonylurea before lifestyle change has been tried, and glibenclamide is not first-line in GDM. Option c starts insulin without addressing diet, which is the first intervention and often sufficient on its own. Option d admits the client for monitoring that is done at home, without changing the glycaemia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gestational diabetes first step: Nutritional counseling + glucose self-monitoring + moderate exercise. Insulin is added if lifestyle alone fails.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reduce metformin to 500 mg on the morning of the scan and resume the full dose at the next meal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop metformin 24 hours before the scan and restart it as soon as the scan is finished"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withhold metformin on the day of the scan and for 48 hours after, until renal function is normal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Continue metformin unchanged, because biguanides do not interact with iodinated contrast"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Iodinated radiocontrast agents can induce acute contrast-induced nephropathy. In clients taking metformin, decreased renal clearance causes dangerous accumulation of metformin, predisposing to life-threatening lactic acidosis (mortality ~50%). Metformin must be held on the day of the contrast procedure and withheld for at least 48 hours afterward until repeat serum creatinine confirms baseline renal function. Why the other options fail: option a reduces the dose when the drug must be stopped entirely for the procedure. Option b stops only 24 hours before and restarts immediately after the scan, when the risk window is the 48 hours after contrast while creatinine is still rising. Option d states a false premise; the danger is to the kidney, not a reaction with the contrast molecule.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Metformin + Contrast Rule: Stop metformin on procedure day and hold for 48 hours post-contrast until renal function is verified normal. Prevents lactic acidosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inject 10 units of air into Regular -> Inject 25 units of air into NPH -> Withdraw 25 units of NPH -> Withdraw 10 units of Regular"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inject 25 units of air into NPH -> Inject 10 units of air into Regular -> Withdraw 10 units of Regular -> Withdraw 25 units of NPH"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withdraw 25 units of NPH -> Withdraw 10 units of Regular without air injection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withdraw 10 units of Regular -> Inject 25 units of air into NPH -> Withdraw 25 units of NPH"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The standard clinical rule for mixing insulins in a single syringe is 'Clear before Cloudy' (Regular is clear; NPH is cloudy). Sequence: 1) Inject air equal to dose into cloudy (NPH) without touching liquid, 2) Inject air equal to dose into clear (Regular), 3) Invert and withdraw exact clear (Regular) dose, 4) Insert needle into cloudy (NPH) and withdraw exact cloudy dose. This prevents contaminating the short-acting regular vial with intermediate protamine. Why the other options fail: option a withdraws NPH before regular, which contaminates the clear vial with protamine and makes the short-acting dose unreliable. Option c omits the air injections, so neither vial is pressurised and the measured doses drift. Option d draws regular before NPH without the air-transfer step and leaves cloudy insulin in the needle.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Insulin mixing rule: RN (Regular before NPH) / 'Clear before Cloudy'. Inject air into NPH, air into Regular, draw Regular, draw NPH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Severe bacterial cellulitis requiring surgical incision and drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Lipohypertrophy leading to erratic, delayed, and unpredictable insulin absorption"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insulin antibody formation at the site, causing resistance and rising dose requirements"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Local fat necrosis with chronic discharge and sinus formation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Repeatedly injecting insulin into the exact same localized area causes lipohypertrophy—a benign accumulation of localized fibrous fat tissue stimulated by the lipogenic action of insulin. Injecting into lipohypertrophic tissue causes erratic, blunted, and unpredictable insulin absorption, leading to unexplained bouts of hyperglycemia and sudden refractory hypoglycemia. Clients must systematically rotate injection sites. Why the other options fail: option a describes infection, which this lesion is not; lipohypertrophy is firm, spongy and non-tender, without erythema or warmth. Option c describes immune-mediated resistance, which is systemic and not caused by using one site. Option d describes fat necrosis, which follows trauma and leaves a depressed scar, not a raised spongy mass.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lipohypertrophy = rubbery lump from repeated injections in one spot -> erratic insulin absorption. Rotate sites within anatomical zones.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give a complex carbohydrate meal with protein and re-check in one hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give 150 mL of full-cream milk with two chocolate bars for sustained calories"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 15 mg of intravenous glucagon now, since the client cannot be trusted to eat"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, wait 15 minutes, then re-check the glucose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The 'Rule of 15' for conscious hypoglycemia (blood glucose < 70 mg/dL): 1) Administer 15g of simple, fast-acting carbohydrates (120 mL fruit juice or regular soda, 3-4 glucose tablets, or 1 tablespoon honey/sugar), 2) Wait 15 minutes and re-test blood glucose, 3) If blood glucose remains < 70 mg/dL, repeat with another 15g of carbohydrates. High-fat foods (chocolate, whole milk) should be avoided because dietary fat slows glucose absorption. Why the other options fail: option a gives carbohydrate bound with fat and protein, which is absorbed too slowly to correct a glucose of 52 mg/dL. Option b is high in fat, and fat slows gastric emptying and glucose absorption, which is why chocolate is excluded. Option c gives glucagon by a route and dose for an unconscious client; this client is alert and can swallow.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hypoglycemia Rule of 15: Give 15g fast-acting simple carbs -> wait 15 min -> recheck BG -> repeat if < 70. Avoid fats (chocolate) which delay absorption.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start 0.9% saline resuscitation and have the provider replete potassium before insulin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give an immediate IV bolus of 0.15 units/kg regular insulin, then start the infusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 100 mEq of intravenous sodium bicarbonate as a slow push"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start the insulin infusion at 0.1 units/kg/hour without waiting for the potassium result"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In DKA, fluid resuscitation with isotonic crystalloids (0.9% NS) is the absolute first priority to restore intravascular volume and renal perfusion. Crucially, insulin drives potassium from the extracellular fluid into cells. Starting insulin when serum potassium is already hypokalemic (< 3.3 mEq/L) will trigger lethal cardiac dysrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Potassium must be repleted >= 3.3 mEq/L before initiating insulin therapy. Why the other options fail: option b starts insulin when the potassium is 3.1 mEq/L, which drives potassium into cells and can arrest the heart, and boluses are no longer used. Option c treats acidosis that will correct with fluids and insulin, and bicarbonate is reserved for severe acidaemia. Option d is the same error as a in a slower form; the potassium must reach 3.3 mEq/L first.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DKA Golden Rule: Fluids (0.9% NS) first! Check K+ before insulin: if K+ < 3.3 mEq/L, hold insulin and replace potassium first to prevent fatal arrhythmias.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous Regular insulin with 50% dextrose bolus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravenous 3% hypertonic saline 100 mL bolus to correct the sodium"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intravenous Hydrocortisone sodium succinate (100 mg bolus)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral Fludrocortisone acetate 0.1 mg"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The client is in acute adrenal (Addisonian) crisis—a life-threatening shock state precipitated by physiological stress (infection, vomiting) in someone with primary adrenal insufficiency who cannot mount a cortisol response. Hallmarks include refractory distributive shock, severe hyponatremia, and hyperkalemia. Immediate life-saving treatment is rapid high-dose IV Hydrocortisone (100 mg bolus) along with aggressive IV isotonic saline resuscitation. Why the other options fail: option a treats the potassium with insulin and dextrose, which does lower potassium but does nothing for the hypotension and adds hypoglycaemia to a patient who cannot mount a cortisol response. Option b corrects the sodium before the cortisol, and the hyponatraemia of adrenal crisis resolves with hydrocortisone and saline. Option d is an oral mineralocorticoid, which a vomiting, hypotensive patient cannot absorb and which acts too slowly.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Addisonian Crisis Triad: Severe hypotension + Hyponatremia + Hyperkalemia. Immediate life-saving drug is IV Hydrocortisone stat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A loop diuretic such as furosemide to lower the blood pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An NSAID such as ibuprofen, which reduces glomerular hyperfiltration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril, or an ARB such as losartan"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A thiazide diuretic such as hydrochlorothiazide to reduce intraglomerular pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Microalbuminuria is the earliest clinical manifestation of diabetic nephropathy (glomerular hyperfiltration and basement membrane breakdown). ACE inhibitors (lisinopril) and ARBs (losartan) dilate the efferent renal arteriole, reducing intraglomerular hydrostatic pressure. This slows protein leakage and halts progressive renal fibrosis, providing proven renal protection in diabetics even when systemic blood pressure is normal. Why the other options fail: option a lowers systemic pressure but not intraglomerular pressure, and does not reduce albuminuria. Option b names the wrong mechanism: NSAIDs block prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilation, which lowers filtration and can precipitate acute kidney injury. Option d lowers pressure but has no effect on the efferent arteriole, so the albumin leak continues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diabetic nephropathy early sign: Microalbuminuria. Renal protective drug of choice: ACE inhibitors or ARBs (lowers intraglomerular pressure).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"I will use a hand mirror every day to inspect the bottoms of my feet and between my toes for cuts or blisters.\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "\"I will soak my feet in hot water and Epsom salts for 30 minutes every evening to improve my circulation.\""}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"I will always wear clean cotton socks and never walk barefoot, even inside my own home.\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"I will cut my toenails straight across and file the edges gently with an emery board.\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Soaking feet in hot water is strictly contraindicated in diabetic neuropathy because loss of thermal sensation prevents the client from detecting scalding temperatures, causing severe burns. Furthermore, prolonged soaking causes skin maceration, fissuring, and breakdown, creating an entry portal for severe bacterial infection and osteomyelitis. Feet should be washed daily in lukewarm water, dried thoroughly (especially between toes), and never soaked. Why the other options fail: option a is the correct technique for finding lesions the client cannot feel. Option c is correct footwear practice. Option d is the correct way to cut nails, and curved cutting is what risks an ingrowing nail.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diabetic foot safety rules: NEVER soak feet, NEVER walk barefoot, NEVER use hot water bottles, ALWAYS inspect with a mirror, cut nails straight across.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Aspirin stimulates thyroid peroxidase and drives further hormone synthesis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aspirin prevents propranolol from crossing the blood-brain barrier"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aspirin displaces thyroid hormone from binding globulin and raises free T3 and T4"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Aspirin blocks peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 and deepens the crisis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In thyroid storm, aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is strictly contraindicated. Salicylates compete with thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) for binding sites on thyroid-binding globulin (TBG), displacing bound hormone and dramatically elevating circulating free (active) T3/T4 concentrations, exacerbating the hypermetabolic crisis. Paracetamol (acetaminophen) and external cooling blankets are the antipyretics of choice. Why the other options fail: option a describes hormone synthesis, which is not how salicylates act and not a mechanism of aspirin at antipyretic doses. Option b is a drug interaction that does not exist; propranolol is given in thyroid storm and reaches its target. Option d reverses the physiology, because blocking T4-to-T3 conversion is therapeutic and is exactly what aspirin does not do.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thyroid storm fever rule: Use Paracetamol (acetaminophen), NEVER Aspirin! Aspirin displaces T3/T4 from binding proteins, worsening the storm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reinforce the neck dressing with extra pressure gauze and elevate the head of the bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an ice collar and give an oral lozenge to reduce the swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Encourage vigorous coughing to clear pooled mucus from the larynx"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Open the neck incision at the bedside to evacuate the haematoma and call the surgeon"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Post-thyroidectomy neck hematoma is a catastrophic, rapidly progressive airway emergency. Bleeding into the tight fascial neck compartments compresses the trachea, causing acute mechanical suffocation and death within minutes. External bleeding on the dressing is often minimal. The emergency action is immediate bedside release of the neck incision sutures/clips to evacuate the hematoma and decompress the airway, followed by emergency surgical exploration. Why the other options fail: option a presses on a neck that is already tense and adds dressing bulk over the airway. Option b treats the pain and not the compression, and an oral lozenge cannot relieve stridor from an obstructed trachea. Option c uses coughing against an obstruction that is outside the trachea, which raises the pressure in the bleeding tissue.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Post-thyroidectomy hematoma: Neck swelling + stridor = mechanical tracheal compression. Cut bedside sutures stat to relieve pressure. Keep suture removal kit at bedside.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cushing syndrome (hypercortisolism)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Addison disease (hypoadrenalism)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pheochromocytoma"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary aldosteronism (Conn syndrome)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cushing syndrome results from chronic excess glucocorticoids (cortisol). Cortisol stimulates gluconeogenesis and lipogenesis in the trunk while breaking down protein in muscle and connective tissues. Classic signs include: truncal obesity, buffalo hump, moon face, purple abdominal striae, thin fragile skin with ecchymoses, muscle wasting, hyperglycemia, and hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis. Why the other options fail: option b is the mirror image, insufficient cortisol, which gives hypotension, hyperkalaemia and weight loss rather than striae and moon face. Option c is catecholamine excess, which gives episodic hypertension, palpitations and sweating. Option d is aldosterone excess, which gives hypertension and hypokalaemia with normal cortisol and no striae.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cushing syndrome hallmarks: 'Cushion of fat' (moon face, buffalo hump, purple striae), high glucose, low potassium, thin skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone (SIADH); administer hypertonic 3% saline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Central diabetes insipidus (DI); administer subcutaneous or nasal desmopressin (DDAVP)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute kidney injury from surgical hypoperfusion; give intravenous loop diuretics"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cerebral salt wasting; restrict all fluid intake to 500 mL per day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Surgical manipulation of the posterior pituitary or hypothalamus can disrupt the synthesis and release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH / vasopressin), triggering central Diabetes Insipidus (DI). Lack of ADH impairs renal collecting duct water reabsorption, causing massive dilute polyuria (> 300 mL/hr), low urine specific gravity (< 1.005), low urine osmolality, and systemic hemoconcentration with hypernatremia. Treatment is synthetic ADH (Desmopressin / DDAVP). Why the other options fail: option a follows the opposite physiology, because SIADH gives concentrated urine and low sodium, not dilute urine at 600 mL/hour with a sodium of 152. Option c would give oliguria with a rising creatinine, not a specific gravity of 1.002. Option d wastes salt and is treated with saline, and fluid restriction is the treatment for SIADH, not for this picture.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diabetes Insipidus (DI) = 'Dry Inside' (High urinary volume + Low urine specific gravity < 1.005 + High serum Na+). Treat with Desmopressin (DDAVP).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Aggressive IV 0.9% saline at 200-300 mL/hour, then a loop diuretic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Strict oral fluid restriction to 800 mL a day to reduce the calcium load"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subcutaneous calcitonin 4 units/kg every 12 hours as the only initial measure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Thiazide diuretics, which enhance renal calcium clearance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe hypercalcemia (serum calcium > 12 mg/dL) causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, volume depletion, and renal failure. The cornerstone of immediate management is rapid IV hydration with 0.9% normal saline (200-300 mL/hr) to expand intravascular volume, restore glomerular filtration, and promote urinary calcium excretion. Loop diuretics (furosemide) can be added once volume is restored; thiazide diuretics are strictly contraindicated because they increase renal calcium reabsorption. Why the other options fail: option b restricts the fluid needed to excrete the calcium and would deepen both the dehydration and the hypercalcaemia. Option c is an adjunct given after volume is restored, not a first measure, and it does not correct the dehydration. Option d is the wrong diuretic class, because thiazides increase calcium reabsorption and worsen hypercalcaemia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hypercalcemia management: 1) Normal saline IV hydration first (dilutes and flushes Ca2+), 2) Loop diuretics (furosemide). Never use Thiazides (which raise Ca2+).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "In the morning on an empty stomach, 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the medication with calcium and iron supplements at bedtime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Crush the tablet and stir it into a cup of hot espresso coffee"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take it immediately after dinner with a high-fibre meal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Levothyroxine oral bioavailability is significantly compromised by food, fiber, caffeine, and polyvalent cations (calcium, iron, antacids). To ensure consistent gastrointestinal absorption and stable serum hormone levels, clients must take levothyroxine on an empty stomach with a full glass of plain water in the morning, at least 30-60 minutes before breakfast (or at bedtime at least 3-4 hours after the last meal). Co-administration with iron or calcium requires a 4-hour separation window. Why the other options fail: option b puts polyvalent cations in the same swallow, and calcium and iron both bind levothyroxine and block absorption. Option c destroys part of the dose with heat and adds caffeine, which the same rationale names as an absorption problem. Option d adds dietary fibre at the moment of absorption, which binds the tablet in the gut.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Levothyroxine administration rule: Take in morning on an EMPTY STOMACH with water 30-60 min before breakfast. Separate from Calcium/Iron by at least 4 hours.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "14 mL/hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "70 mL/hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "0.7 mL/hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "7 mL/hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Calculation steps: 1) Desired dose per hour: 0.1 units/kg/hour x 70 kg = 7 units/hour. 2) Solution concentration: 250 units in 250 mL = 1 unit/mL. 3) Infusion pump rate: 7 units/hour ÷ 1 unit/mL = 7 mL/hour. Why the other options fail: option a is a tenfold error, because 14 mL/hour delivers 0.2 units/kg/hour, twice the prescribed rate. Option b mistakes the patient's weight for the rate and would deliver 70 units/hour. Option c is a decimal slip that delivers 0.01 units/kg/hour, one tenth of the order.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Insulin drip math: Dose = 0.1 x 70 = 7 units/hr. Concentration = 250 units / 250 mL = 1 unit/mL. Pump rate = 7 mL/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-ENDO-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Normal cognitive ageing, because memory and orientation decline steadily and falls become more common in the eighties"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Anxiety related to the move into residential care, since disorientation and falls often follow a change of environment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An absence of infection, because a temperature of 36.6 C makes a urinary tract infection unlikely in an older adult"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Blunted immune and thermoregulatory responses mean infection can present as delirium and falls before fever appears"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A hallmark of geriatric medicine is atypical presentation of illness. In older adults, blunted hypothalamic thermoregulation, decreased interleukin/cytokine response, and diminished physiological reserve often prevent the development of a classic febrile response. Consequently, common infections (such as urinary tract infections or pneumonia) frequently present without fever or tachypnoea, instead manifesting as acute delirium, functional decline, anorexia, lethargy, or new-onset falls ('off legs'). The nurse must recognize sudden acute confusion in an elder as an emergency medical trigger until proven otherwise. Why the other options fail: option a treats a change that happened in a day as normal ageing, when normal ageing does not produce acute confusion, and delirium is a medical emergency. Option b attributes the presentation to attention-seeking, which no assessment finding supports and which delays treatment of an infection. Option c treats a normal temperature as proof against infection, when blunted thermoregulation is precisely why older adults do not mount a fever.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Atypical presentation in elders: Blunted febrile response means infections often present as acute delirium, functional decline, or falls rather than fever.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Face the client at eye level in good light, reduce background noise, speak clearly at a normal volume in a lower pitch, and check understanding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use a high-pitched, sing-song voice and call the client sweetie or grandma, because warmth and familiarity aid comprehension"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Raise your voice and stand behind the client, since speaking loudly into the better ear compensates for high-frequency loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client's adult children what is needed and address replies to them, so that information is not misunderstood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Presbycusis involves progressive loss of high-frequency hearing and impaired speech discrimination. Shouting or using high-pitched sing-song tones ('elderspeak') actually distorts sound frequencies and is patronizing, causing diminished self-esteem. Therapeutic communication requires facing the patient directly so visual lip-reading cues can assist, ensuring good lighting, reducing ambient noise, and speaking clearly in a deeper (lower-pitched), normal-volume voice. Elderspeak (e.g., using terms like 'sweetie', 'honey', using collective pronouns like 'did we take our pills?') is an insidious form of ageism associated with increased resistance to care. Why the other options fail: option b uses the high-pitched voice and the endearments that define elderspeak, both of which distort speech and reduce the client to a child. Option c shouts from behind, which removes the lip-reading and visual cues she depends on and distorts the sound further. Option d removes the client from the conversation altogether, which is exclusion rather than a communication strategy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Geriatric communication: Face directly at eye level, reduce ambient noise, lower voice pitch (not volume/shouting), and NEVER use patronizing elderspeak.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Managing finances, balancing a chequebook and paying monthly utility bills"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transferring independently from bed to armchair, using a frame or the bed rail"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bathing safely in the shower without help, once grab rails have been fitted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maintaining urinary and faecal continence through the day and night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Basic Activities of Daily Living (ADLs, measured by the Katz Index) encompass fundamental self-care tasks essential for survival: bathing, dressing, toileting, transferring, continence, and feeding. In contrast, Instrumental Activities of Daily Living (IADLs, measured by the Lawton scale) require higher-order executive functioning and neuropsychological integration to live independently in the community: managing finances, using the telephone, shopping, meal preparation, housekeeping, laundry, transportation, and managing medications. Loss of IADLs typically occurs before loss of basic ADLs in progressive cognitive decline. Why the other options fail: option b is a basic ADL, transferring on the Katz index. Option c is a basic ADL, bathing. Option d is a basic ADL, continence. All three are self-care tasks needed for survival and none requires the executive planning that defines an instrumental activity.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ADLs vs IADLs: Basic ADLs = fundamental self-care (bathing, feeding, toileting). IADLs = community living skills (finances, cooking, phone, medications).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Under 20 seconds is normal for a client of this age, so reassure her and give no walking aid until the time exceeds 30 seconds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The result is not interpretable without knowing her gait speed, so repeat the test on three separate mornings before acting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Over 12 seconds means high fall risk; begin a multifactorial bundle: medication review, balance training and home hazard modification"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nineteen seconds indicates a low risk of falling, because the threshold for referral is 30 seconds in community-dwelling elders"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Timed Up and Go (TUG) test is a validated clinical screening tool for balance, gait speed, and fall risk in older adults. Most community-dwelling older adults complete the TUG in under 10–12 seconds. A score of >= 12 seconds (or >= 14 seconds depending on guideline cutoff) indicates clinically significant gait instability, frailty, and high risk of falling. Interventions must be multifactorial: comprehensive medication review (especially psychotropics and antihypertensives), referral to physical therapy for balance/strength training, environmental hazard abatement (loose rugs, grab bars), and proper walking aid fitting. Why the other options fail: option a inverts the threshold and would delay a walking aid for a client who is already slow. Option b invents a repeat-testing requirement, when the TUG is validated as a single measurement. Option d quotes a 30-second threshold that belongs to no version of the test, and would reassure a client who needs intervention now.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TUG test: Scores >= 12 seconds indicate high fall risk requiring a multifactorial fall prevention bundle. Never restrict mobility with bed rest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A low-protein, calorie-restricted diet to protect the glomeruli from hyperfiltration, with protein given mainly as clear broth"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A trial of oral feeding for 2 weeks, then a percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy if the weight continues to fall"}, {"id": "c", "text": "High-protein energy-dense meals in small frequent portions, oral supplements between meals, and treatment of dental or denture problems"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Two litres of water before each meal to distend the stomach, which is the standard first step for weight loss in older adults"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Involuntary weight loss and sarcopenia in older adults lead to immune dysfunction, pressure injuries, fall-related fractures, and increased mortality. Age-related anabolic resistance requires higher protein intake (1.2–1.5 g/kg/day in older adults with acute or chronic illness, compared to 0.8 g/kg in younger adults) along with progressive resistance exercises. Strategies include: energy- and protein-dense foods (eggs, milk, groundnut paste, fish), small frequent meals, pleasant dining environments, oral nutritional supplements between meals (so appetite for regular meals is not blunted), and inspecting the oral cavity for poorly fitting dentures or stomatitis. Why the other options fail: option a prescribes the opposite of what sarcopenia needs, and protein restriction accelerates muscle loss. Option b moves to tube feeding before oral strategies have been tried, which guidelines reserve for their failure. Option d fills the stomach with water before meals, which reduces intake in a client whose intake is already inadequate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Geriatric malnutrition & sarcopenia: Higher protein needs (1.2-1.5 g/kg/day) + small frequent energy-dense meals + check oral/denture health.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insidious onset over months to years with progressive memory loss and clear consciousness until the late stages"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute onset over hours to days, a fluctuating course, marked inattention and altered consciousness, triggered by a medical cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subacute onset with prominent worthlessness, slowed speech and 'I don't know' answers on cognitive testing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A permanent and irreversible decline in cognition that does not resolve when the underlying illness is treated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Differentiating the '3 Ds' is critical in gerontological nursing: Delirium has an ACUTE onset (hours to days), a fluctuating course throughout the day, altered level of consciousness (hyperactive, hypoactive, or mixed), severe inattention, and is a medical emergency caused by reversible underlying physiological insults (infection, medications, hypoxia, electrolyte shifts). In contrast, Dementia has an INSIDIOUS, gradual onset (months to years), progressive decline in memory and executive function, with preserved alertness until late stages. Depression has a subacute onset, prominent dysphoria, vegetative signs, and clients often answer cognitive questions with 'I don't know' (pseudodementia). Why the other options fail: option a describes dementia, with its insidious onset and preserved consciousness until late. Option c describes depression, with its subacute onset, worthlessness and 'I don't know' answers. Option d gives permanence, which is the opposite of delirium's defining reversibility.$q$,
  strategy = $q$The 3 Ds: Delirium = Acute + Fluctuating + Altered consciousness/inattention. Dementia = Insidious + Progressive + Clear consciousness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply bilateral wrist restraints to the bed rails, releasing them every 2 hours, to protect the cannula and prevent a fall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intravenous lorazepam every 2 hours until the agitation settles, then review the precipitating cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Assign one-to-one supervision, reorient gently, replace glasses and hearing aids, treat pain and retention, and avoid restraints"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dim the room and close the door to reduce stimulation, and nurse the client in a side room away from the desk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Physical restraints are strictly contraindicated as first-line management in delirium; they increase agitation, injury, strangulation, pressure ulcers, and functional decline. Evidence-based delirium care prioritizes multicomponent non-pharmacological interventions: one-on-one observation/family presence, frequent gentle reorientation, using sensory aids (glasses, hearing aids), early mobilization, optimizing hydration, assessing for constipation or urinary retention, treating uncontrolled pain (a major delirium driver), and avoiding deliriogenic medications (such as benzodiazepines and anticholinergics). Why the other options fail: option a uses physical restraint, which is contraindicated in delirium and increases agitation, injury and strangulation. Option b sedates a client whose delirium has an untreated cause, and benzodiazepines are themselves deliriogenic. Option d isolates a client who needs supervision and orientation, and removes the sensory cues that keep him oriented.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delirium management: NEVER restrain (escalates agitation). Reorient, place glasses/hearing aids, treat pain/retention, ensure continuous companion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the client in bed in a darkened room during the day, so that sleep pressure builds and the evening restlessness settles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Offer tea or coffee in the late afternoon to keep the client alert, so that agitation does not build before bedtime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Correct the hallucinations firmly each time they occur, so that the client learns to distinguish them from reality"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maximise morning daylight, keep a predictable routine, light the room before dusk, and keep evenings calm with music and low noise"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sundowning is a circadian rhythm disruption common in dementia, characterized by worsening confusion, agitation, and motor restlessness in the late afternoon and evening. Effective non-pharmacological interventions include: (1) maximizing exposure to bright natural sunlight in the morning to stabilize the circadian pacemaker; (2) maintaining a consistent, structured daily schedule for meals, exercise, and activities; (3) turning on indoor lighting before sunset to prevent shadows and perceptual illusions; (4) minimizing evening noise and visitors; and (5) avoiding caffeine or daytime napping. Arguing with hallucinations triggers catastrophic emotional reactions. Why the other options fail: option a removes daytime light and activity, which weakens the circadian signal and makes the evening worse. Option b adds a stimulant at the time of day the client is already over-aroused. Option c confronts the hallucination, which provokes a catastrophic reaction in a client who cannot test reality.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sundowning interventions: Morning sunlight, turn on lights BEFORE sunset, structured routine, calm quiet evening, avoid confrontation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tachyphylaxis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The prescribing cascade"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Idiosyncratic allergic reaction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "First-pass bioequivalence failure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A prescribing cascade occurs when an adverse drug effect is misinterpreted as a new, unrelated medical condition, prompting the prescription of a second medication to treat the side effect of the first. The second drug exposes the vulnerable older adult to additional adverse effects and drug interactions (e.g., amlodipine peripheral vasodilation edema -> furosemide added -> hypokalaemia/dehydration -> orthostatic fall). The gerontological nurse must always consider: 'Could this new symptom be an adverse effect of an existing medication?' before accepting a new diagnosis and additional prescriptions. Why the other options fail: option a is loss of response to a drug over time, which is not what happened here. Option c is an immune-mediated reaction, whereas ankle oedema from amlodipine is a predictable pharmacological effect. Option d concerns the fraction of a dose reaching the circulation after first-pass metabolism, which does not explain a new diagnosis and a second drug.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prescribing cascade: When a drug side effect is misdiagnosed as a new illness and treated with another drug. Always review the drug chart first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Diazepam is renally cleared, and reduced renal blood flow in older adults allows the drug to reach toxic concentrations within one dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Older adults metabolise benzodiazepines faster, so a 5 mg dose is ineffective and a higher dose would be needed for sleep"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Diazepam causes acute hyperthermia and malignant hypertension in older adults, which is why it is listed as potentially inappropriate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Long-acting benzodiazepines accumulate in older adults because fat mass is higher and hepatic clearance is lower, causing sedation, ataxia and fractures"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Beers Criteria strongly advises against using benzodiazepines (especially long-acting agents like diazepam, flurazepam, or chlordiazepoxide) for insomnia in older adults. Age-related changes in body composition (increased proportion of body fat, decreased body water) expand the volume of distribution for lipid-soluble drugs, while reduced hepatic cytochrome P450 oxidation dramatically prolongs elimination half-lives (diazepam's half-life can exceed 100 hours in frail elders). This leads to accumulation, prolonged hangover sedation, impaired motor coordination, confusion, and more than a twofold increased risk of falls and hip fractures. Why the other options fail: option a invents an irreversible renal injury that diazepam does not cause and places the clearance in the wrong organ. Option b reverses the pharmacokinetics, since clearance falls rather than rises with age. Option c attributes hyperthermia and malignant hypertension to a drug whose toxicity is sedation, ataxia and respiratory depression.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Beers criteria & benzodiazepines: Lipid-soluble long-acting drugs linger for days in older bodies, causing daytime ataxia, confusion, and hip fractures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A creatinine of 0.8 mg/dL confirms normal filtration in this client, so the standard dose can be given without adjustment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reduced muscle mass lowers creatinine production, so a normal creatinine can hide a substantially reduced glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Enoxaparin is cleared by the liver and bile, so renal function does not influence its dose or accumulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A normal creatinine means the dose should be doubled, because low muscle mass also lowers the volume of distribution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Creatinine is an endogenous breakdown product of skeletal muscle. In frail, sarcopenic older adults, muscle mass is markedly reduced; therefore, baseline creatinine production is very low. Even when Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) drops by 50% or more due to normal renal ageing, serum creatinine may remain within 'normal' reference limits (e.g., 0.8 mg/dL). Clinicians who rely solely on serum creatinine overlook severe renal impairment. Calculating CrCl using the Cockcroft-Gault formula reveals the true impairment, avoiding fatal accumulation of renally cleared drugs like enoxaparin, digoxin, and aminoglycosides. Why the other options fail: option a reads a single normal value as proof of normal filtration, which is the error the item tests. Option c is wrong about the route, since enoxaparin is renally cleared and accumulates in renal impairment. Option d doubles a dose on the strength of a value that may be masking impairment, which would increase the risk of bleeding.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Geriatric renal clearance: Sarcopenia masks renal impairment with falsely normal serum creatinine. Always calculate CrCl (Cockcroft-Gault) for drug dosing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect the family's privacy, record the observations without comment, and raise the concern at the next routine review, since the caregiver is present"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Remove the elder to a place of safety immediately and without notifying any authority, because the danger to her is obvious"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give wound care and fluids, document the findings, and report suspected neglect and financial exploitation to social welfare and adult protection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Confront the caregiver, demand return of the pension and give 24 hours to clean the room before deciding whether to report"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nurses have a legal, ethical, and professional duty as mandated reporters of suspected elder abuse and neglect. The physical signs (multiple severe pressure ulcers, fecal soiling, emaciation, dehydration) demonstrate severe neglect (the failure of a caregiver to provide necessary food, shelter, hygiene, or medical care). Diverting the client's pension constitutes financial exploitation. The nurse's immediate actions are: (1) provide emergency clinical stabilization (first aid, wound cleansing, hydration); (2) objectively photograph (with consent) and document injuries; and (3) notify the Department of Social Welfare and adult protective authorities. Why the other options fail: option a defers a mandated report to a routine review and leaves the client in danger. Option b removes the client without notifying anyone, which bypasses the protective authorities the nurse is required to involve. Option d negotiates with the caregiver and delays the report, giving time for evidence to be lost and for retaliation against the client.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Elder abuse & neglect: Mandated reporting obligation. Provide immediate clinical care -> Document objective findings -> Report to Social Welfare.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Follow the son's instruction and belt him into the wheelchair, because a concerned family member may consent to restraint on the client's behalf"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Respect his decision, reduce the risk with supervision, physiotherapy and non-skid footwear, and explain that restraining a capacitous adult is unlawful"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Convene a best-interest meeting and keep him in the wheelchair until the meeting agrees that he may walk again"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask him to sign a form accepting all risk, then allow walking only when his son is not on the unit to see it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A cognitively competent adult possesses the legal and ethical right to make autonomous decisions regarding their body and mobility, including accepting reasonable, informed risks (risk enablement/dignity of risk). Relatives cannot override the capacitous choices of an adult. Enclosing the client in a wheelchair with a belt constitutes unlawful physical restraint (false imprisonment). The nurse acts as patient advocate: respects the client's autonomy, implements risk-reduction strategies (physical therapy, balance training, escort supervision), and educates the family on the hazards of forced immobility (deconditioning, depression, contractures). Why the other options fail: option a lets a relative consent to restraint on behalf of a capacitous adult, which no relative can do. Option c defers the decision to a meeting while the restraint continues, which is detention by another name. Option d makes walking conditional on his son's absence, conceding the autonomy the nurse is meant to protect.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Autonomy vs Beneficence: Competent adults have the right to informed risk (dignity of risk). Relatives cannot mandate physical restraints.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "PEG feeding does not prevent aspiration, prolong life or improve comfort in advanced dementia; offer careful hand feeding and mouth care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the PEG now, because tube feeding is the only way to prevent aspiration in a client who coughs on all oral intake"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the PEG, because it removes the risk of aspiration pneumonia by bypassing the pharynx entirely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withhold all food, fluid and mouth care, and allow no oral contact, to avoid provoking another aspiration event"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Extensive clinical trials and professional guidelines (American Geriatrics Society, British Geriatrics Society) demonstrate that enteral tube feeding (PEG tubes) in advanced dementia does NOT prolong survival, does not improve nutritional status, does not prevent aspiration (patients aspirate oropharyngeal secretions and gastric contents), and does not reduce pressure ulcers. Tube feeding often leads to physical restraints to prevent tube pulling, fluid overload, and loss of the pleasure of taste. Geriatric palliative care recommends 'careful hand feeding' (comfort-focused oral feeding for pleasure) and meticulous oral hygiene. Why the other options fail: option b offers a benefit the evidence does not support, since a tube does not stop aspiration of oral secretions. Option c claims the tube removes aspiration risk, when gastric reflux and oropharyngeal secretions remain. Option d withdraws comfort as well as nutrition, when hand feeding and mouth care are the recommended alternative.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Advanced dementia & tube feeding: PEG tubes do NOT prevent aspiration or prolong life. Evidence supports careful comfort hand feeding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Four weeks of bed rest with passive range-of-motion only, so that the muscles recover before any loading begins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Seated deep-breathing exercises three times a day, which improve oxygen delivery to the quadriceps and rebuild strength"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Daily high-impact plyometric jumping for 60 minutes, which loads the muscle most and builds strength fastest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Progressive resistance training for the major muscle groups 2 to 3 days a week, with balance work and adequate protein"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Progressive resistance training (PRT) is the gold standard, evidence-based intervention to stimulate muscle protein synthesis, reverse muscle atrophy (sarcopenia), and restore functional independence in older adults, even in nonagenarians. Resistance training (using body weight, resistance bands, or light weights) 2–3 times weekly increases myofibrillar cross-sectional area and neuromuscular coordination. Combining PRT with balance exercises (e.g., tandem stance, tai chi) and dietary protein (1.2–1.5 g/kg/day) provides maximal protection against falls and fractures. Why the other options fail: option a imposes the immobility that caused the weakness and will worsen it. Option b does not load the muscle, so it cannot reverse sarcopenia. Option c applies high-impact loading that a frail, deconditioned client cannot tolerate and that risks injury without building strength.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Reversing sarcopenia: Progressive resistance/strength training 2-3 times/week + balance exercises + adequate protein. Bed rest accelerates atrophy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stress incontinence; teach pelvic floor exercises and advise her to cough before she stands up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Overflow incontinence; teach her to void by suprapubic pressure and review her anticholinergic burden"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Functional incontinence; restrict fluids to under 300 mL a day so that the bladder never fills enough to leak"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Urge incontinence; start timed voiding, bladder retraining, pelvic floor exercises and removal of caffeine and alcohol"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Urge incontinence is characterized by sudden, involuntary detrusor muscle contractions causing an uncontrollable urge to void followed by leakage of moderate-to-large amounts of urine (overactive bladder). First-line interventions are behavioral: bladder retraining (gradually increasing intervals between scheduled voiding), pelvic floor muscle (Kegel) training, prompt toileting, and avoiding dietary bladder irritants (caffeine, artificial sweeteners, alcohol). Stress incontinence involves small-volume leakage during exertion/coughing; functional incontinence is due to physical/cognitive barriers to reaching the toilet. Why the other options fail: option a describes stress incontinence, which leaks small volumes on exertion rather than after an uncontrollable urge. Option b describes overflow incontinence, which presents as constant dribbling from an over-distended bladder. Option c mislabels the problem as functional and answers it with fluid restriction, which concentrates the urine and irritates the bladder further.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Incontinence types: Sudden intense urge + large leak = Urge incontinence (overactive detrusor). Treat with bladder training, Kegels, avoid caffeine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hold the cane in the right hand, on the same side as the affected knee, and advance it after both legs have moved"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hold the cane in the left hand but advance it with the left leg, so that the stronger side leads every step"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hold the cane in the left hand, opposite the affected knee; move the cane and the right leg together, then step through with the left"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold the cane in the right hand and advance it first, then bring the right leg up to the cane before the left"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Proper cane technique requires holding the cane on the UNAFFECTED (stronger) side (the 'COAL' acronym: Cane Opposite Affected Leg). This widens the base of support and shifts the body's center of gravity away from the damaged joint. Sequence of ambulation: (1) advance the cane and the affected (weaker) leg forward simultaneously, distributing body weight between the cane and the weak leg; (2) step forward with the unaffected (stronger) leg past the cane. When navigating stairs, remember: 'Up with the good (strong leg leads), down with the bad (cane and weak leg lead)'. Why the other options fail: option a holds the cane on the affected side, which increases the load through the painful knee. Option b advances the cane with the stronger leg, which removes support at the moment the weak leg takes weight. Option d advances the cane first and then the affected leg, a sequence that does not distribute weight safely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cane ergonomics: Hold on UNAFFECTED (strong) side (COAL). Move cane and weak leg forward together, then follow with strong leg.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reconcile the medicines, check the home for hazards, train the spouse in transfers and swallowing, and follow up within 48 to 72 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the spouse a written rehabilitation manual at discharge and ask her to telephone the ward with any question"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange a clinic review at 6 weeks and advise her to call the emergency services if anything changes before then"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the antihypertensive and antiplatelet drugs at discharge, so that falls and bleeding are not compounded at home"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Care transitions between hospital and home are high-risk periods for frail elders; nearly 20% of discharged older adults experience hospital readmission within 30 days. Evidence-based transitional care bundles emphasize: (1) comprehensive medication reconciliation to resolve discrepancies and educate on new prescriptions; (2) practical caregiver training on transfers, positioning, and dysphagia precautions; (3) home hazard evaluation (grab bars, lighting, tripping hazards); (4) scheduling follow-up medical visits; and (5) proactive post-discharge follow-up (home visit by community nurse or telephone call) within 48–72 hours. Why the other options fail: option b hands over information without teaching or follow-up, which is a handout rather than a transition plan. Option c leaves a 6-week gap across the highest-risk period, when most readmissions occur. Option d stops the secondary prevention that prevents the next stroke and would raise rather than lower the risk.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Transitional care in elders: Medication reconciliation + caregiver transfer/swallow training + follow-up within 48-72 hours prevent 30-day readmissions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Investigate unmet needs such as pain, thirst and a full bladder, move him near the nurses' station, and give purposeful activity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply double-loop cotton restraints to both wrists so that the cannula is not pulled out, and check them hourly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fit a vest restraint and raise all four bed rails, which is the least restrictive option that prevents wandering"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give intramuscular haloperidol every 4 hours around the clock, which settles the agitation and prevents night wandering"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In dementia and delirium, motor restlessness and wandering are purposeful expressions of unmet physical or emotional needs (pain, full bladder/bowel, thirst, hunger, boredom, or fear). Restraint-free care requires investigating and satisfying these underlying needs first: offer fluids, assist with toileting, evaluate pain, optimize lighting, move the client closer to the nursing desk for visibility, and provide therapeutic diversion (folding towels, sorting socks, listening to favorite music). Restraints and 4 bed rails increase entrapment, asphyxiation, and fall injuries. Why the other options fail: option b restrains both wrists, which increases agitation and injury and is not a first-line measure. Option c adds a vest and four rails, the most restrictive combination, and carries entrapment risk. Option d sedates an older adult with dementia around the clock and masks the unmet need instead of meeting it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Restraint alternatives: Address unmet physical needs (toileting, pain, thirst), place close to nurses' station, and use purposeful tactile tasks.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait until the dementia is severe, because preferences expressed now cannot cover decisions that arise later"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Advance care planning now: execute a living will and appoint a durable power of attorney for healthcare while he still has capacity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the daughter to confirm his wishes verbally at the time, which is sufficient without any written document"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain that advance directives cannot be used in Ghana and that the family will decide by consensus instead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Advance Care Planning (ACP) enables individuals to articulate their values and preferences regarding future medical treatments and to appoint a substitute decision-maker (Durable Power of Attorney for Healthcare / Healthcare Proxy). This must be initiated EARLY in the disease course while the individual retains decisional legal capacity. Key components include: (1) a Living Will detailing preferences regarding life-sustaining treatments (CPR, mechanical ventilation, artificial feeding); and (2) designating a trusted healthcare proxy. Once capacity is lost, the patient can no longer legally execute these advance instruments. Why the other options fail: option a waits for capacity to be lost, at which point the instruments can no longer be executed. Option c relies on a verbal instruction given in crisis, which carries no legal weight and invites family conflict. Option d states a legal position that is not correct and leaves the client with no documented voice in his own care.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Advance care planning: Must be established EARLY while the client has decisional capacity. Designate Healthcare Proxy and document treatment preferences.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GERO-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Auscultation, inspection, palpation, percussion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Palpation, percussion, inspection, auscultation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inspection, auscultation, percussion, palpation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The correct sequence for abdominal assessment is inspection, auscultation, percussion, and palpation (I-A-P-P). Auscultation must precede percussion and palpation because mechanical manipulation, pressure, or percussion of the abdominal wall stimulates peristalsis and artificially alters the frequency and character of bowel sounds, potentially leading to inaccurate diagnostic findings. Why the other options fail: option a palpates before auscultating, and palpation alters the bowel sounds that are then recorded. Option b auscultates before it looks at the abdomen, so the inspection findings are lost. Option c starts with the most intrusive step of all and distorts every sign that follows.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the abdominal assessment rule: Look (inspect), Listen (auscultate), then Feel (percuss and palpate). Never touch or press before listening.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Listen for 15 seconds in the right lower quadrant only"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Listen continuously over each abdominal quadrant for at least 3 to 5 minutes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Palpate the right upper quadrant vigorously while listening with the stethoscope bell"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer an oral prokinetic agent and reassess bowel sounds 10 minutes later"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$To accurately declare that bowel sounds are absent, the nurse must listen continuously with the diaphragm of the stethoscope for a minimum of 3 to 5 full minutes in each quadrant (or throughout the abdomen). Normal bowel sounds occur irregularly between 5 and 30 times per minute. True absence indicates paralytic ileus or peritonitis. Brief listening (15 seconds) is inadequate to detect hypoactive peristalsis. Why the other options fail: option a listens for 15 seconds in one quadrant, which cannot distinguish hypoactive from absent sounds. Option c palpates while listening, which creates the sounds it is trying to hear. Option d gives a drug before assessing, and a prokinetic changes the baseline the nurse needs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the examination benchmark: Absent bowel sounds require continuous listening for 3 to 5 continuous minutes before being officially documented.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Maintain strict NPO status, position in low-Fowler with knees flexed, and notify the surgeon at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer 30 mL of liquid aluminum hydroxide antacid via oral syringe"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a rectal tube to relieve suspected lower bowel flatulent distension"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a warm heating pad across the epigastrium to alleviate muscle guarding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sudden severe pain, a rigid board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, and absent bowel sounds are classical hallmarks of acute gastrointestinal perforation, causing chemical peritonitis from leaking gastric contents. This is a life-threatening surgical emergency. The nurse must immediately maintain strict NPO status, position the client in low-Fowler position with knees flexed (to localize peritonitis to the pelvis and decrease abdominal wall tension), obtain vital signs, and summon the surgeon immediately for emergency laparotomy. Oral antacids and heat applications are contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option b gives an oral agent to a patient who must be NPO for theatre, and antacids do not treat a perforation. Option c inserts a rectal tube into a patient with free air in the abdomen. Option d applies heat to a peritonitic abdomen, which increases perfusion, bleeding and the risk of further perforation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the surgical abdomen: 'Sudden sharp pain + rigid board-like abdomen' = Perforation and peritonitis. First action = NPO + notify surgeon immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Consume large meals twice daily to minimize gastric acid secretory cycles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Raise the head of the bed on 6-inch blocks and stay upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Drink a cup of hot peppermint tea immediately before retiring to bed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lie flat on the back for 45 minutes after eating to facilitate gastric emptying"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Elevating the head of the bed 6 inches on blocks utilizes gravity to prevent acid reflux into the esophagus during sleep. Remaining upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals allows time for gastric emptying and prevents reflux. Peppermint, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, fatty foods, and smoking reduce lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone and worsen reflux. Lying down flat after meals dramatically increases reflux events. Why the other options fail: option a takes large meals, which raise gastric volume and pressure against a weak sphincter. Option c chooses peppermint, which relaxes the lower oesophageal sphincter and worsens reflux. Option d lies flat after eating, which removes gravity from the antireflux mechanism.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Focus on anti-reflux biomechanics: Elevate the head of bed, remain upright after eating, eat small frequent meals, and avoid LES-relaxing foods (peppermint, chocolate, caffeine).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Eat small, frequent high-protein meals, avoid fluids with meals, and lie down for 20 to 30 minutes after"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Consume high-carbohydrate smoothies with large amounts of refined sugar at mealtime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Drink at least 500 mL of iced water during each meal to dilute gastric secretions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ambulate briskly around the ward immediately after consuming heavy meals"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dumping syndrome results from the rapid transit of hyperosmolar chyme into the jejunum, pulling intravascular fluid into the bowel lumen (hypotension, dizziness, cramping) and triggering reactive hypoglycemia. Dietary management includes: (1) small, frequent meals, (2) low-carbohydrate, moderate-to-high protein and complex fat content to slow gastric transit, (3) consuming liquids between meals rather than with meals (at least 30 to 45 minutes apart) to prevent accelerated gastric emptying, and (4) reclining or lying down for 20 to 30 minutes after meals to slow intestinal transit. Why the other options fail: option b loads refined sugar, which increases the osmotic load in the jejunum and provokes the reactive hypoglycaemia. Option c drinks a large volume with the meal, which accelerates gastric emptying. Option d exercises immediately after eating, which speeds transit and worsens the dumping.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the dumping syndrome dietary triad: High protein/fat + Low carbs + No fluids with meals + Recline after meals.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rely exclusively on alcohol-based hand rub formulations for rapid sanitization"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse hands thoroughly with antimicrobial soap and warm water after removing gloves"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on airborne precautions in a negative-pressure isolation room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disinfect bedside medical equipment using standard quaternary ammonium wipes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clostridioides difficile forms hardy bacterial endospores that are resistant to alcohol-based hand sanitizers. Healthcare personnel must wash hands vigorously with soap and water; the mechanical friction and lather rinse the spores off the skin. C. diff requires Contact Precautions (gown and gloves upon entry, dedicated equipment). Disinfection of patient rooms and equipment must be performed with sporicidal, bleach-based (sodium hypochlorite) agents, not quaternary ammonium wipes. Airborne isolation is unnecessary. Why the other options fail: option a relies on alcohol, which does not kill C. difficile spores. Option c uses airborne precautions when the route is contact. Option d uses a quaternary ammonium wipe, which does not kill spores; the agent has to be a sporicidal bleach.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the C. diff rule: Spores survive alcohol! Soap and water handwashing is mandatory. Clean surfaces with bleach.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dehydration from the continuing high fever, corrected with oral fluids"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute cholelithiasis resulting in common bile duct obstruction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intestinal perforation of the terminal ileum with developing septic shock"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Constipation from the prolonged bed rest, relieved by an enema"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$During the second and third weeks of typhoid fever, hyperplastic Peyer's patches in the terminal ileum ulcerate and may necrose, causing life-threatening intestinal perforation and severe lower GI hemorrhage. A sudden hypothermic drop in temperature, tachycardia, hypotension, and generalized peritoneal signs (tenderness, guarding) in week 3 indicate free bowel perforation and acute septic peritonitis. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, and emergent exploratory laparotomy. Why the other options fail: option a describes the bacteraemia resolving, which is not what a sudden temperature drop with tachycardia and guarding means. Option b is biliary disease, which does not produce this sequence in the third week of typhoid. Option d is a self-limiting enlargement, which does not explain shock and peritonism.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the classic presentation: Typhoid fever in week 3 with sudden hypothermia, hypotension, and acute abdominal guarding = Intestinal perforation (Peyer's patch necrosis).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer a warm mineral oil enema to relieve suspected obstipation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Establish an intravenous line for 0.9% normal saline infusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Maintain the client on strict NPO status"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain blood for complete blood count and serum electrolytes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In suspected acute appendicitis, administering enemas, laxatives, or applying heat to the abdomen is strictly contraindicated because increased intraluminal pressure or hyperemic vasodilation can cause sudden appendix perforation and catastrophic diffuse peritonitis. The nurse must question any prescription for enemas, laxatives, or local heat. Keeping the client NPO, starting IV fluids, and obtaining baseline labs are standard and essential preparatory steps. Why the other options fail: option b is a correct preparatory step, as is option c for keeping the bowel at rest and option d for baseline bloods. The prescription to question is the enema, because it raises intraluminal pressure in an appendix that is already inflamed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Safety rule in suspected appendicitis: Never give enemas/laxatives, and never apply direct heat (risk of appendix rupture).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Encourage a high-fiber, high-residue diet with fresh raw vegetables to form bulk"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Monitor for hypovolemia, electrolyte depletion (especially hypokalemia), and signs of toxic megacolon"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer daily rectal stimulant suppositories to expedite bowel evacuation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict oral fluid intake to 500 mL per 24 hours to reduce stool frequency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe flare-ups of ulcerative colitis cause extensive mucosal ulceration in the colon and rectum, leading to frequent bloody diarrhea (10–20 stools/day), significant blood loss, dehydration, and severe electrolyte depletion (especially potassium and sodium). Furthermore, severe inflammation can cause paralysis of the colon and rapid dilation (toxic megacolon), predisposing to perforation. The nurse must monitor hydration, electrolytes, hemoglobin, and watch for fever, leukocytosis, and abdominal distension. High-fiber diets exacerbate bowel inflammation during acute flares. Why the other options fail: option a adds bulk and raw fibre to an acutely inflamed colon. Option c uses stimulant suppositories, which increase urgency and mucosal trauma during a flare. Option d restricts fluid in a patient who is losing volume and potassium through 10 to 20 stools a day.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the life-threats of acute colitis flares: Dehydration, hypokalemia, and toxic megacolon (fever, distension, tachycardia).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer oral polyethylene glycol bowel preparation to clear the obstruction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Provide a regular diet with high-protein supplements to prevent catabolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a nasogastric (NG) tube and attach to low intermittent suction, and initiate IV fluids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer high-dose subcutaneous loperamide to control vomiting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In mechanical small bowel obstruction, fluid and gas accumulate proximal to the blockage, resulting in massive luminal distension, third-spacing, dehydration, vomiting, and risk of bowel ischemia or perforation. Decompression of the stomach and upper GI tract via a nasogastric tube attached to low intermittent suction relieves distension, alleviates nausea/vomiting, and prevents pulmonary aspiration. Intravenous fluid resuscitation corrects hypovolemia. Oral intake, laxatives, and oral bowel preps are contraindicated because they exacerbate luminal pressure against the mechanical blockage. Why the other options fail: option a gives an oral bowel preparation against a mechanical obstruction, which raises the pressure behind the block. Option b feeds a patient who cannot pass what she takes. Option d gives an antimotility drug, which retains the distension and the vomiting.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Small bowel obstruction protocol: NPO + NG tube decompression to low intermittent suction + IV fluid rehydration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client develops watery, persistent diarrhea exceeding 8 bowel movements daily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client has complete absence of bowel movements for 48 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client's serum ammonia level increases above 150 micromol/L"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client passes 2 to 3 soft acidic stools daily and is mentally clearer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Lactulose is a non-absorbable disaccharide that acidifies the colon (converting absorbable ammonia [NH3] into non-absorbable ammonium [NH4+]), promoting its rapid excretion in stool. The therapeutic goal of lactulose in hepatic encephalopathy is achieving 2 to 3 soft, formed bowel movements per day, leading to reduced serum ammonia levels and improved mental status. Excessive diarrhea (> 4–5 stools/day) causes dehydration and hypokalemia, which worsens encephalopathy. Why the other options fail: option a describes the excessive diarrhoea the dose is meant to avoid, and it causes dehydration and hypokalaemia. Option b has no bowel movement at all, which means the dose is too low. Option c is the ammonia still rising while the patient is under-treated; the goal is a fall, tracked through the stools and the mental state.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Memorize the lactulose target: The goal is 2 to 3 soft stools per day, NOT profuse watery diarrhea. Clearer mentation reflects reduced ammonia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An extra liter of sterile water for balloon irrigation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A bottle of oral activated charcoal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A tracheostomy dilator tray"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A pair of surgical scissors"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$If the gastric balloon of a Sengstaken-Blakemore or Minnesota tube ruptures or accidentally deflates, the esophageal balloon can migrate upward into the oropharynx, causing complete, acute airway obstruction and asphyxiation. The nurse must keep a pair of scissors at the bedside at all times; in the event of acute respiratory distress, the nurse immediately cuts all lumens of the tube to deflate the balloons instantly and pulls the tube out. Why the other options fail: option a adds volume to a balloon that may already be misplaced. Option b treats a poisoning that is not the problem here. Option c prepares for a tracheostomy this patient does not have, and none of the three can deflate a migrated balloon.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High-yield NCLEX safety rule: Sengstaken-Blakemore / Minnesota tube at bedside = SCISSORS kept at bedside to cut lumens immediately if airway obstruction occurs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Benign cutaneous hyperpigmentation from elevated bilirubin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage and extensive pancreatic necrosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute lower urinary tract extravasation of urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Superficial bacterial cellulitis of the abdominal subcutaneous tissue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cullen's sign (periumbilical ecchymosis) and Turner's (Grey Turner's) sign (flank ecchymosis) represent tracking of blood from retroperitoneal tissue into subcutaneous tissue plane walls. In acute pancreatitis, they are ominous physical signs indicating severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage, tissue necrosis, and high mortality. The client requires immediate aggressive crystalloid fluid resuscitation, strict NPO, intensive hemodynamic monitoring, and surgical evaluation. Why the other options fail: option a reads the bruising as jaundice, which stains yellow rather than purple and does not follow the flank. Option c is urinary extravasation, which gives different signs and no periumbilical bruising. Option d is superficial infection, which gives erythema and warmth rather than ecchymosis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the eponyms: Cullen's sign (around the 'C'ircle of the belly button) and Turner's sign ('Turn' over to see the flank) = Retroperitoneal bleeding in severe acute pancreatitis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute diverticulitis of the sigmoid colon"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute appendicitis with early perforation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Viral gastroenteritis with dehydration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute cholecystitis with a distended gallbladder"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A positive Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest elicited by sharp tenderness when palpating the gallbladder beneath the right costal margin as the diaphragm descends on deep inspiration) is the classic physical diagnostic sign of acute cholecystitis (gallbladder inflammation, usually caused by cystic duct obstruction from gallstones). Pain is typically precipitated by fatty or fried meals (which stimulate cholecystokinin release) and frequently radiates to the right subscapular area or right shoulder. Why the other options fail: option a gives left lower quadrant pain with fever and a change in bowel habit. Option b migrates from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa. Option c gives diarrhoea and cramping without inspiratory arrest on right upper quadrant palpation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Connect Murphy's sign with location: Murphy's sign in RUQ = Acute Cholecystitis (gallbladder).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Document the finding as normal initial postoperative venous engorgement"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an ice pack directly to the stoma surface to reduce swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately notify the surgeon of suspected stomal ischemia and necrosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Irrigate the stoma with 500 mL of warm normal saline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A healthy, viable stoma should be moist, beefy red or pink, and warm. A stoma that is pale, dusky, dark purple, brown, or black indicates compromised arterial blood supply or severe venous congestion resulting in ischemia and tissue necrosis. This is a surgical emergency that requires immediate notification of the surgical team for potential urgent surgical re-exploration. Never apply ice or wait, as prolonged ischemia leads to stoma retraction and intraperitoneal perforation. Why the other options fail: option a documents a compromised stoma as normal, and the window for salvage closes. Option b applies ice to tissue that is already ischaemic. Option d irrigates a stoma whose blood supply is failing and adds pressure to it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the abnormal color: Normal stoma = beefy red and moist. Purple/dusky/black stoma = dead tissue/ischemia! Immediate surgeon notification.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Infuse 10% dextrose in water (D10W) at the same infusion rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Infuse 0.9% normal saline at a keep-vein-open (KVO) rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infuse sterile water without dextrose"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clamp the central venous catheter line completely until the new bag arrives"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$TPN solutions contain high concentrations of dextrose (typically 20% to 50%), stimulating continuous, high-level pancreatic insulin secretion. If a TPN infusion is suddenly stopped or runs out, circulating insulin levels remain high while glucose delivery abruptly drops, precipitating severe rebound hypoglycemia (confusion, diaphoresis, tremors, seizures, coma). To prevent severe rebound hypoglycemia while awaiting a new TPN bag, the nurse must immediately infuse 10% dextrose in water (D10W) at the same prescribed rate. Why the other options fail: option b keeps the vein open but delivers no glucose, so the insulin already circulating has nothing to act on. Option c gives free water without dextrose, which does not correct hypoglycaemia. Option d stops all dextrose delivery and lets the glucose fall fastest of all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the golden rule of TPN gaps: Never stop TPN abruptly. If the next TPN bag is delayed, hang D10W at the same rate to prevent rebound hypoglycemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Routine upper GI barium swallow series"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Colonoscopy with tissue biopsy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transabdominal pelvic ultrasound"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In an adult older than 50 years, new-onset iron deficiency anemia, unexplained fatigue, change in bowel habits (such as pencil-thin stools), or rectal bleeding must be treated as colorectal cancer until proven otherwise. A colonoscopy allows direct visualization of the entire large intestine from the rectum to the cecum and enables immediate biopsy of any suspicious lesions or polyp removal. It is the gold standard diagnostic procedure. Why the other options fail: option a images the upper tract with barium, which cannot see the colon and would delay the diagnosis. Option b examines the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum, all above the lesion. Option d images the pelvis and cannot inspect the colonic mucosa or take a biopsy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Screening red flag: Unexplained iron deficiency anemia + change in bowel caliber/pencil-thin stools in an older adult = Colorectal cancer evaluation via Colonoscopy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fractures from osteoporosis, hypomagnesaemia, and C. difficile colitis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypercalcemia, severe hypernatremia, and gouty arthritis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertension, acute hemolytic anemia, and fluid overload"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, and peptic ulcer recurrence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Long-term use of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) suppresses gastric acid, which impairs the intestinal absorption of calcium and magnesium, leading to hypomagnesemia and significantly increased risks of osteoporosis and bone fractures (hip, wrist, and spine). Furthermore, the loss of the gastric acid bactericidal barrier allows pathogen colonization, increasing the risk of Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea and pneumonia. Clients on long-term PPIs require periodic bone density scans and magnesium level monitoring. Why the other options fail: option b lists hypercalcaemia and gout, neither of which is a PPI effect. Option c describes haemolysis and overload, which belong to transfusion rather than acid suppression. Option d describes thyroid and adrenal failure, which are not on the PPI risk list.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate long-term PPI risks: Low acid = low calcium absorption (osteoporosis/fractures) + low magnesium (hypomagnesemia) + lost bacterial barrier (C. diff infection).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the next scheduled dose on time with a full glass of water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client that these movements are benign and will subside with continued therapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discontinue the metoclopramide and immediately notify the healthcare provider"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply warm compresses to the bilateral masseter muscles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Metoclopramide is a central dopamine antagonist that crosses the blood-brain barrier. It carries a Black Box warning for tardive dyskinesia—a serious, often irreversible extrapyramidal movement disorder characterized by involuntary, repetitive movements of the face, tongue, and limbs (facial grimacing, tongue protrusion, lip smacking, rapid blinking). At the earliest sign of tardive dyskinesia, the medication must be stopped immediately and the provider notified. Continued administration risks permanent neurological dysfunction. Why the other options fail: option a continues the drug that is causing the movements. Option b calls a black-box reaction benign, and tardive dyskinesia does not subside with continued exposure. Option d treats the jaw with heat while the cause continues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the critical side effect: Metoclopramide blocks dopamine. Involuntary facial movements/tongue thrusting = Tardive Dyskinesia! Stop the drug immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "25 mL/hr"}, {"id": "b", "text": "100 mL/hr"}, {"id": "c", "text": "10 mL/hr"}, {"id": "d", "text": "50 mL/hr"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$To calculate the infusion rate: Concentration = 500 mcg / 500 mL = 1 mcg/mL. Desired dose = 50 mcg/hr. Infusion rate = (Desired dose / Concentration) = 50 mcg/hr / (1 mcg/mL) = 50 mL/hr. Octreotide (a somatostatin analog) reduces portal venous pressure through selective splanchnic vasoconstriction, controlling active variceal hemorrhage. Why the other options fail: option a halves the dose, giving 25 mcg/hour. Option b doubles it to 100 mcg/hour. Option c delivers 10 mcg/hour, one fifth of the order. The pump has to run at 50 mL/hour to give 50 mcg/hour at 1 mcg/mL.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Calculate step-by-step: 500 mcg in 500 mL means 1 mcg = 1 mL. For 50 mcg/hr, infuse 50 mL/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that a parent or guardian sign written authorisation before any examination, because she is under 18 years old"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Examine her with the door closed but without a chaperone, since she has asked for confidentiality and a third person would breach it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Provide auditory and visual privacy, explain the examination, obtain informed consent, offer a documented chaperone, and keep the visit confidential"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain consent and examine her with a chaperone present, but do not discuss the procedure in advance in case it increases her anxiety"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under national and international adolescent sexual and reproductive health guidelines, sexually active adolescents have the right to confidential, non-judgmental counseling, screening, and contraceptive services. For an intimate pelvic examination, the nurse must: (1) guarantee visual and auditory privacy; (2) explain the indications, instruments, and sensations to alleviate anxiety; (3) secure the client's voluntary informed consent; and (4) provide and document the presence of an impartial chaperone to protect both client dignity and provider safety. Why the other options fail: option a applies a parental-authorisation rule that adolescent sexual and reproductive health standards do not impose, and it denies the service she came for. Option b keeps the record confidential but exposes her body, which is not confidentiality and breaches both dignity and the chaperone requirement. Option d keeps the chaperone but removes the explanation, so the consent it takes is not informed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gynaecological examination standards: Privacy + explanation + informed consent + chaperone documentation + non-judgmental adolescent confidentiality.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Interpret as negative; dense acetowhitening is normal glycogenated squamous epithelium, so reassure her and repeat screening in 5 years"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Interpret as inconclusive because the acid was applied for only 1 minute, and repeat the test with a 3-minute application before deciding"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Interpret as a VIA-positive screen showing high-grade precancerous change; offer same-day thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or refer for colposcopy and biopsy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Interpret as invasive carcinoma; refer urgently for radical hysterectomy and radiotherapy without taking a biopsy of the lesion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In Visual Inspection with Acetic Acid (VIA), dilute acetic acid dehydrates cells and reversibly coagulates nuclear proteins. Precancerous dysplastic cells (cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, CIN) have high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratios, causing them to reflect light and appear dense, opaque, well-demarcated acetowhite lesions adjacent to the squamocolumnar junction (SCJ / transformation zone). Under WHO screen-and-treat guidelines for low-resource settings, eligible VIA-positive lesions are treated immediately with thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or referred for colposcopic biopsy if suspicious for invasive carcinoma. Why the other options fail: option a misreads acetowhitening as normal; glycogenated epithelium does not form a dense, well-defined lesion at the squamocolumnar junction, and a 5-year recall is wrong for a screen-positive cervix. Option b invents a 3-minute protocol, when one minute is the standard application and the lesion is already diagnostic. Option d skips histology before radical surgery, which no screening programme permits on a visual inspection alone.$q$,
  strategy = $q$VIA screening: Dense, well-defined acetowhite lesions touching the SCJ = VIA-positive -> screen-and-treat (ablation) or colposcopy referral.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Non-structural: coagulopathy (COEIN-C); request von Willebrand studies and treat the bleeding with desmopressin before referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Structural: leiomyoma (PALM-L); correct the iron-deficiency anaemia and start tranexamic acid or a levonorgestrel IUD"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Structural: adenomyosis (PALM-A); proceed to hysterectomy because medical therapy will not control bleeding of this volume"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Non-structural: ovulatory dysfunction (COEIN-O); induce ovulation with clomiphene and defer treatment of the anaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The FIGO PALM-COEIN system classifies causes of Abnormal Uterine Bleeding (AUB). PALM denotes structural causes: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma (fibroid), and Malignancy/hyperplasia. COEIN denotes non-structural causes: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, and Not yet classified. Uterine leiomyomas (L) increase the endometrial surface area and disrupt myometrial contractility, causing menorrhagia and severe secondary microcytic anaemia. Priority nursing care includes correcting anaemia with iron supplementation, administering antifibrinolytics (tranexamic acid during menses) or LNG-IUS, and surgical referral for myomectomy if childbearing is desired. Why the other options fail: option a selects a non-structural category for a lesion proved on ultrasound, and plasma exchange is not a treatment for coagulopathy. Option c names a structural category that is not present and proceeds to hysterectomy before any medical stabilization. Option d selects a non-structural category and defers the anaemia, which is the immediate threat at a haemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PALM-COEIN: Leiomyoma (L) is a structural cause of AUB. Address profound anaemia (Hb 7.8) and medical hemostasis first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ovarian failure with a rising FSH; the long-term risks are osteoporosis, hot flushes and premature cardiovascular disease"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperandrogenism with insulin resistance; the long-term risks are type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and endometrial hyperplasia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hyperprolactinaemia with suppressed GnRH; the long-term risks are galactorrhoea, headache and growth of a pituitary adenoma"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypothalamic amenorrhoea from an energy deficit; the long-term risks are low bone density and recurrent stress fractures"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) diagnosed by Rotterdam criteria requires at least 2 of 3 features: (1) oligo/anovulation; (2) clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism; and (3) polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound. Hyperinsulinaemia secondary to peripheral insulin resistance stimulates ovarian theca cell androgen production and decreases sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). Chronic anovulation leads to persistent, unopposed oestrogen stimulation of the endometrium (without cyclical progesterone from a corpus luteum), predisposing women to endometrial hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma, as well as metabolic syndrome and type 2 diabetes. Why the other options fail: option a describes ovarian failure, which gives a low oestrogen and a raised FSH with hot flushes, not hyperandrogenism with a polycystic ovarian morphology. Option c describes hyperprolactinaemia, which suppresses GnRH and causes amenorrhoea without the hirsutism and insulin resistance seen here. Option d describes hypothalamic amenorrhoea, which produces a low or normal LH and no ovarian enlargement.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PCOS pathophysiology: Hyperinsulinaemia drives androgen excess. Chronic anovulation -> unopposed oestrogen -> high risk of endometrial hyperplasia/cancer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute appendicitis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cervical incompetence"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Endometriosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary dysmenorrhoea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Endometriosis is characterized by the implantation and proliferation of functional endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity (most commonly on the ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, pouch of Douglas, and pelvic peritoneum). The classic clinical triad is: (1) secondary dysmenorrhoea; (2) deep dyspareunia; and (3) subfertility/infertility, often accompanied by dyschezia (pain on defecation) and uterosacral nodularity. Primary dysmenorrhoea occurs without pelvic pathology and lacks dyspareunia or nodularity. Definitive diagnosis is established via diagnostic laparoscopy showing 'powder-burn' or 'chocolate' endometriotic lesions. Why the other options fail: option a is an acute abdominal emergency that has no cyclical relation to menstruation, no dyschezia and no uterosacral nodularity. Option b describes painless mid-trimester cervical dilatation, which causes pregnancy loss rather than cyclic pelvic pain. Option d is primary dysmenorrhoea, which begins with the first periods, has no dyspareunia or nodularity, and does not cause infertility.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Endometriosis triad: Severe cyclic pelvic pain + deep dyspareunia + subfertility/infertility with uterosacral tenderness/nodularity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "MEC category 1: no restriction at any age or smoking load, because the oestrogen dose in a modern low-dose pill is too small to affect clotting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "MEC category 2: start a low-dose combined pill now and advise her to cut down to fewer than 15 cigarettes a day, then review in 3 months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "MEC category 3: prescribe a combined pill containing 20 micrograms of ethinylestradiol, which lowers the clot risk enough to be used"}, {"id": "d", "text": "MEC category 4: combined hormonal contraceptives are contraindicated, because her age and smoking load give an unacceptable arterial thrombosis risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the WHO and CDC Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC): Category 4 represents an absolute contraindication where health risks outweigh any benefits. Age >= 35 years combined with heavy smoking (>= 15 cigarettes/day) synergistically increases hepatic production of clotting factors and promotes arterial vasospasm/thrombosis, dramatically raising the incidence of fatal myocardial infarction, deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and ischaemic stroke. Safe, non-oestrogen alternatives for this client include copper IUD, levonorgestrel IUD, progestogen-only implants, or depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA). Why the other options fail: option a ignores the age and the smoking load, the two variables that set the category. Option b treats a category 2 as permission to start the method that is contraindicated, and adds dose escalation that exists nowhere in the guidance. Option c applies a category 3 to a client who is a 4, and assumes that lowering the oestrogen dose removes an arterial risk that it does not.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contraceptive eligibility (MEC 4): Oestrogen-containing methods are absolutely contraindicated in women >= 35 who smoke >= 15 cigarettes/day (thrombosis/stroke risk).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg now, because oral methods act faster than an IUD and she is already past the 24-hour ideal window"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ulipristal acetate 30 mg only, because it stays effective to 120 hours and an IUD cannot be fitted after unprotected intercourse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A copper IUD inserted within 5 days is the most effective method; oral options are levonorgestrel within 72 hours or ulipristal within 120 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A combined oral contraceptive, two tablets now and two in 12 hours, which is as effective as an IUD at 38 hours after intercourse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The copper IUD (Cu-IUD) is the most effective method of emergency contraception, with a failure rate of less than 0.1%, and can be inserted up to 5 days (120 hours) after unprotected intercourse (or up to 5 days after the earliest calculated day of ovulation), providing ongoing long-term contraception for up to 10–12 years. If oral options are preferred: levonorgestrel 1.5 mg is licensed up to 72 hours (efficacy decreases with time and higher BMI); ulipristal acetate (30 mg selective progesterone receptor modulator) maintains high efficacy up to 120 hours (5 days) post-coitus. Why the other options fail: option a overstates the speed of oral methods and abandons the most effective option hours inside its window. Option b is right that ulipristal works to 120 hours but wrong that an IUD cannot be fitted, which is the error the item tests. Option d describes the obsolete Yuzpe regimen, which is less effective than either the IUD or a single-dose oral method.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Emergency contraception: Copper IUD is the #1 most effective emergency method (up to 5 days / 120 h). Oral LNG up to 72 h; Ulipristal up to 120 h.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Heavy bleeding with clots is expected for the first 3 months, and she should return only if she soaks more than two pads a day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bleeding stops within 48 hours of insertion; any spotting after that suggests the device has perforated the uterus and must be removed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Periods return to their previous pattern within 6 weeks, because the device provides contraception only and does not alter flow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Irregular spotting is expected for the first 3 to 6 months; flow then becomes much lighter and many women become amenorrhoeic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) releases local progestogen that induces profound endometrial glandular atrophy. In the initial 3–6 months following insertion, users frequently experience frequent, irregular, light bleeding or spotting as the endometrium thins. By 12 months, overall menstrual blood loss is reduced by >= 90%, and 20–50% of women achieve amenorrhoea. Anticipatory guidance regarding this transient bleeding pattern is the single most effective nursing strategy to prevent client anxiety and premature device discontinuation. Why the other options fail: option a calls heavy bleeding expected and sets a return threshold that would miss expulsion or perforation. Option b promises immediate amenorrhoea and turns normal spotting into a perforation, which would prompt an unnecessary removal. Option c denies any effect on flow, when the progestogen is there precisely to thin the endometrium and reduce loss.$q$,
  strategy = $q$LNG-IUS counselling: Anticipatory guidance on 3-6 months of benign irregular light spotting, followed by dramatically lighter periods or amenorrhoea.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Threatened miscarriage with vagal syncope; give intramuscular morphine for the pain and arrange a repeat ultrasound in the morning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ruptured ectopic pregnancy with haemoperitoneum; secure two large-bore IVs, resuscitate, cross-match blood and prepare for emergency laparotomy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ovarian torsion from a ruptured corpus luteum; give analgesia and book semi-elective laparoscopy within the next 24 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute pelvic inflammatory disease with sepsis; start intravenous antibiotics and fluids and reassess after 6 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$This clinical picture is diagnostic of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy with massive haemoperitoneum causing hypovolaemic shock. The classic triad consists of amenorrhoea, unilateral abdominal/pelvic pain, and vaginal bleeding. Referred shoulder tip pain (Kehr's sign) occurs when diaphragmatic peritoneum is irritated by pooled blood (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3–C5). Marked cervical motion tenderness reflects severe peritoneal irritation. Ruptured ectopic pregnancy is a surgical emergency; immediate resuscitation with two large-bore IV cannulae (14–16 gauge), rapid fluid boluses, blood cross-matching, and urgent surgical hemostasis are mandatory to prevent maternal exsanguination. Why the other options fail: option a treats a shocked client as a threatened miscarriage and gives morphine, which masks deterioration while the bleeding continues. Option c delays surgery by 24 hours in a client with a falling blood pressure and a distended abdomen. Option d attributes the picture to infection, which produces fever and a normal or raised blood pressure, not shoulder-tip pain and shock.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ruptured ectopic pregnancy: Amenorrhoea + unilateral pain + shock + shoulder pain (phrenic irritation). Resuscitate with large-bore IVs & prep for surgery.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Days 7 to 10, after bleeding has stopped and before ovulation, with a negative pregnancy test and no active pelvic infection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Any day she is not bleeding, provided the pregnancy test is negative; active pelvic infection need not delay the procedure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Days 1 to 5, during light bleeding, so that the contrast can flush residual menstrual debris out of the uterine cavity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Days 21 to 24, in the luteal phase, so that tubal spasm is reduced and the cavity distends more evenly during injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hysterosalpingography (HSG) involves fluoroscopic radiopaque contrast injection into the endocervical canal to outline the endometrial cavity and fallopian tubes. Timing is critical: it must be performed during the early follicular phase (days 7–10 of a 28-day cycle), after menstrual flow has completely ceased (to prevent retrograde flushing of menstrual debris/endometrial tissue into the peritoneal cavity and avoid venous extravasation) but BEFORE ovulation occurs (to prevent irradiating or flushing an early fertilised ovum). Active pelvic inflammatory disease is an absolute contraindication because contrast injection can precipitate fulminant pelvic peritonitis. Why the other options fail: option b drops the infection screen, the one absolute contraindication, because contrast injection into an inflamed pelvis can precipitate peritonitis. Option c schedules during menstruation, when debris is flushed retrograde into the peritoneum and venous extravasation is more likely. Option d schedules in the luteal phase, when an early conceptus may be in the tube and tubal spasm distorts the study.$q$,
  strategy = $q$HSG timing & safety: Days 7-10 (post-menses, pre-ovulation) + negative pregnancy test + absolute exclusion of active pelvic infection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inevitable miscarriage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Incomplete miscarriage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Missed miscarriage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Threatened miscarriage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Spontaneous abortion categories in early pregnancy are classified clinically: (1) Threatened abortion: vaginal bleeding occurs, the cervical os remains CLOSED, and the fetus remains viable (cardiac activity present); the pregnancy may proceed successfully to term. (2) Inevitable abortion: bleeding with uterine cramping and the cervical os is DILATED (open), making pregnancy loss unavoidable. (3) Incomplete abortion: cervical os is open with partial expulsion of products of conception. (4) Complete abortion: all products expelled, os closed. (5) Missed abortion: embryo/fetal demise occurs without bleeding or expulsion; os closed. Why the other options fail: option a describes inevitable miscarriage, which requires a dilated os and cramping that this client does not have. Option b describes incomplete miscarriage, which requires an open os and passage of tissue. Option c describes missed miscarriage, in which the embryo has died and is retained, whereas this scan shows cardiac activity. A closed os with a viable embryo is what separates threatened from all three.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Miscarriage classification: Vaginal bleeding + CLOSED os + VIABLE fetal heartbeat = Threatened miscarriage. Reassurance and pelvic rest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treatment should be withheld until endocervical swab results are available, so that the antibiotic is correctly targeted"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Empiric treatment is justified only once all three minimum criteria are met, not on cervical motion tenderness alone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prompt treatment prevents tubal scarring and lowers the lifelong risk of infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treatment can safely wait 48 hours while a pregnancy test and pelvic ultrasound exclude an ectopic pregnancy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) is an ascending polymicrobial infection from the endocervix into the endometrium, fallopian tubes (salpingitis), ovaries, and pelvic peritoneum, commonly triggered by Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. The CDC guidelines mandate empiric antibiotic therapy in sexually active young women presenting with lower pelvic pain and at least one minimum clinical criterion (cervical motion tenderness, uterine tenderness, or adnexal tenderness). Even brief delays in treatment lead to endosalpingeal deciliation, intraluminal scarring, and tubal occlusion, causing tubal infertility (10% after 1 episode, 40% after 3 episodes), ectopic pregnancy, and chronic debilitating pelvic pain. Why the other options fail: option a delays treatment for a culture that is often negative and that CDC guidance explicitly does not require before antibiotics. Option b adds a threshold that does not exist; any one of the three minimum criteria is enough to treat. Option d gives the same delay a different justification, but ectopic pregnancy is excluded in parallel, not by withholding antibiotics.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PID urgency: Minimum criteria (cervical motion tenderness) warrants immediate empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics to prevent permanent tubal scarring and infertility.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bacterial vaginosis; treat with oral metronidazole 400 mg twice daily for 5 days and avoid vaginal azoles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vulvovaginal candidiasis; treat with a topical azole such as clotrimazole, or a single 150 mg oral dose of fluconazole"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Trichomoniasis; treat with a single 2 g oral dose of metronidazole and screen her partner at the same visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atrophic vaginitis; treat with topical oestrogen cream and review the vaginal pH after four weeks of therapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Vulvovaginal Candidiasis (typically Candida albicans) presents with intense vulvovaginal pruritus, dysuria, dyspareunia, and classic thick, curd-like, white 'cottage cheese' discharge. The vaginal pH remains acidic (normal, <= 4.5), which distinguishes it from Bacterial Vaginosis and Trichomoniasis (where pH > 4.5). KOH wet mount reveals characteristic pseudohyphae and budding blastospores. Treatment consists of topical azoles (clotrimazole, miconazole pessaries) or single-dose oral fluconazole 150 mg (oral fluconazole is avoided during pregnancy, where topical clotrimazole is preferred). Why the other options fail: option a names bacterial vaginosis, which gives a thin grey discharge with a fishy odour and a pH above 4.5, and metronidazole would not clear the pseudohyphae on the KOH mount. Option c names trichomoniasis, which gives a frothy yellow-green discharge, a pH above 4.5 and motile flagellates, and ceftriaxone does not treat it. Option d names atrophic vaginitis, which follows oestrogen loss and would not show budding yeast.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vaginitis differential: Thick curd-like discharge + intense pruritus + pH <= 4.5 + pseudohyphae = Vulvovaginal candidiasis -> Clotrimazole / Fluconazole.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A Bartholin cyst; apply warm compresses and re-examine in 6 weeks, since a cyst that is not infected needs no drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A sebaceous cyst of the labium majus; incise and express the contents under local anaesthetic and pack the cavity open"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A vulval haematoma; apply ice packs and observe for 24 hours, because incision and drainage would increase the bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bartholin gland abscess; incise and drain and insert a Word catheter or marsupialise, with sitz baths and analgesia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands are located bilaterally at the 4 and 8 o'clock positions of the posterior vaginal introitus. When the duct becomes obstructed, fluid accumulates (Bartholin cyst), which can become acutely infected and form an abscess. The patient develops rapid, severe, throbbing unilateral vulval pain and dyspareunia. Management of an abscess requires incision and drainage. Simple incision alone has an 80% recurrence rate; therefore, placing a Word catheter for 4–6 weeks (allowing epithelialization of a new tract) or performing surgical marsupialization is necessary. Why the other options fail: option a treats a fluctuant, exquisitely tender abscess as an uninfected cyst, which will not settle with compresses and risks spontaneous rupture. Option b names a sebaceous cyst, which is not fixed at the introitus in the 4 o'clock position and does not need open packing. Option c names a haematoma, which follows trauma and is not managed by observation when it is this painful.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bartholin abscess: Painful fluctuant mass at 4 or 8 o'clock of introitus. Treatment = I&D with Word catheter placement or marsupialization + sitz baths.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take a direct punch biopsy of the cervical mass, or refer urgently for colposcopy and biopsy, to obtain histological confirmation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform a cervical Pap smear now and refer only if the cytology shows high-grade dysplasia, because cytology is the diagnostic test"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat the visible lesion with cryotherapy at this visit and review her in 6 months with a repeat screening test, as for a VIA-positive cervix"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prescribe a course of metronidazole and doxycycline and re-examine the cervix once the discharge has cleared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Post-coital bleeding (contact bleeding), intermenstrual bleeding, and foul-smelling vaginal discharge are cardinal red-flag warning signs of invasive cervical carcinoma. A visible, friable, exophytic, or ulcerated cervical lesion requires an immediate tissue punch biopsy for histological evaluation. A routine cytological screening smear (Pap smear) is inappropriate and contraindicated when a gross, visible lesion is present, because cytology has a false-negative rate of up to 50% in invasive cancer due to surface necrosis and blood obscuring neoplastic cells. Why the other options fail: option b relies on cytology in the presence of a visible, friable mass, where surface necrosis and blood produce a high false-negative rate and a smear cannot stage the lesion. Option c ablates a lesion that has not been sampled, destroying the evidence and delaying diagnosis. Option d treats an assumed infection while a malignant ulcer continues to grow.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cervical cancer red flags: Post-coital bleeding + visible friable cervical lesion requires direct TISSUE BIOPSY, not a screening Pap smear.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treat any postmenopausal bleeding as endometrial cancer until it is excluded; refer for transvaginal ultrasound and endometrial biopsy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure and review in 6 months, because a few episodes of spotting after 12 years of amenorrhoea are usually atrophic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Request a cervical smear first, because postmenopausal bleeding is most often caused by cervical rather than endometrial pathology"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start combined HRT to stabilise the endometrium and reassess only if the bleeding recurs after 3 months of treatment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The cardinal clinical rule in gynaecology is that postmenopausal bleeding (PMB) is presumed to be endometrial adenocarcinoma until definitively excluded. Approximately 10% of women presenting with PMB have underlying endometrial malignancy. Initial diagnostic evaluation includes transvaginal ultrasonography (TVUS): an endometrial thickness <= 4 mm has a > 99% negative predictive value for endometrial cancer. If the endometrium is > 4 mm, irregular, or bleeding persists, outpatient endometrial biopsy (using a Pipelle aspirator) or hysteroscopy with directed biopsy is required. Why the other options fail: option b attributes the bleeding to atrophy and defers the one investigation that excludes cancer; atrophy can cause spotting, but it is a diagnosis of exclusion. Option c investigates the cervix when the endometrium is the source in most cases, and a normal smear does not exclude endometrial cancer. Option d treats the bleeding with the hormone that can drive endometrial proliferation, and delays the biopsy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Postmenopausal bleeding: Cardinal rule -> Presume endometrial cancer until proven otherwise. Urgent transvaginal ultrasound & Pipelle biopsy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It arises from the cervical epithelium and spreads without symptoms, which is why smear screening is used; work up with a Pap smear and colposcopy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Its symptoms are vague and mimic benign gut disorders, so most cases present at stage III or IV; work up with transvaginal ultrasound and CA-125"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It causes no symptoms until the tumour is palpable; work up with abdominal CT and a carcinoembryonic antigen level, which is raised in ovarian cancer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is commonest in women under 40 and grows rapidly; work up with laparoscopy and a CA-19-9 level, the tumour marker for ovarian cancer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Epithelial ovarian cancer is often dubbed the 'silent killer' because it lacks a reliable screening test (Pap smears detect cervical, not ovarian, cells) and produces non-specific, insidious symptoms—persistent bloating, early satiety, pelvic fullness, and urinary frequency—that patients and clinicians frequently attribute to irritable bowel syndrome, dyspepsia, or normal ageing. Consequently, over 70% of women are diagnosed at an advanced stage (FIGO Stage III or IV with peritoneal carcinomatosis and ascites). Workup includes transvaginal ultrasound and serum CA-125 tumor marker testing. Why the other options fail: option a confuses ovarian with cervical cancer and proposes a smear that samples the wrong organ. Option c offers CT and CEA, a marker for colorectal rather than ovarian cancer, and no ovarian mass is truly symptomless until palpable. Option d reverses the age distribution and offers CA-19-9, which is not the ovarian marker.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ovarian cancer presentation: Vague GI symptoms (persistent bloating, early satiety, abdominal distension) + solid adnexal mass -> Pelvic ultrasound & CA-125.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Unopposed oestrogen drives endometrial proliferation and raises the risk of hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma; the progestogen sheds the lining"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The progestogen reduces breast tenderness and bloating, which are the commonest reasons women stop hormone therapy in the first year"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The progestogen protects the endometrium, but it is no longer needed once 12 months have passed without a natural period"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The progestogen prevents the vasomotor flushes from rebounding after 6 months of oestrogen therapy, which is why it is added"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In postmenopausal women with an intact uterus, systemic oestrogen-only replacement therapy stimulates unchecked endometrial cell proliferation (mitosis), resulting in endometrial hyperplasia and a markedly elevated risk of endometrial adenocarcinoma. Adding a progestogen (either sequentially for cyclical withdrawal bleeding or continuously for amenorrhoea) antagonizes oestrogenic proliferative effects, converting the endometrium to a thin, secretory, atrophic state and preventing malignant transformation. Women who have had a surgical hysterectomy do not have an endometrium and therefore take oestrogen-only HRT. Why the other options fail: option b gives a comfort benefit as the reason for a therapy that is given to prevent cancer. Option c is right that the endometrium is the target but wrong to stop protection after 12 months, when the risk persists for as long as oestrogen is taken. Option d invents a rebound phenomenon and ignores the endometrium entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$HRT pharmacology: Intact uterus requires COMBINED oestrogen + progestogen (progestogen protects against endometrial cancer). Post-hysterectomy takes oestrogen alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fit a Gellhorn pessary without oestrogen and leave it in place for 12 months before the first review, since it is self-cleaning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Teach her to bear down several times a day to strengthen the levator ani so that the prolapse lifts back into place"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a weighted vaginal cone daily and remove it only once the prolapse no longer descends when she coughs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fit a vaginal ring pessary with topical oestrogen to protect the mucosa, teach cleaning and replacement, and add pelvic floor training"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) results from attenuation and stretching of the endopelvic fascia and levator ani pelvic diaphragm muscles. In frail older women or those who decline or are unfit for surgery, vaginal pessaries (e.g., silicone ring or Gellhorn pessaries) provide an effective, safe conservative solution. The pessary mechanically elevates the prolapsed pelvic organs. Nursing care includes: (1) ensuring correct size fit; (2) teaching hygiene and removal/cleaning intervals (or scheduling clinic follow-up every 3–6 months); (3) prescribing local topical oestrogen cream to prevent vaginal epithelial ulceration; and (4) pelvic floor muscle training. Why the other options fail: option a uses a pessary without oestrogen and leaves it a year, which risks mucosal erosion and ulceration in an atrophic vagina. Option b asks the client to bear down, which raises intra-abdominal pressure and worsens prolapse rather than strengthening the floor. Option c uses a weighted cone, which is not a prolapse device and cannot be left in until the prolapse stops descending.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pelvic organ prolapse: Conservative care for high surgical risk = Vaginal pessary + local oestrogen cream to prevent erosion + Kegel exercises.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ensure safety and support; with consent collect forensic evidence; give emergency contraception, HIV PEP and STI prophylaxis; arrange counselling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect the forensic evidence first and defer emotional support until the examination is over, so that the samples are not lost"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give HIV PEP only if the assailant is known to be positive, and defer emergency contraception until a pregnancy test is back"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advise her to report to the police before any examination, since evidence cannot be collected without a case number"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Management of sexual assault requires a compassionate, trauma-informed approach addressing immediate medical, legal, and psychological needs: (1) Physical and psychological safety in a private environment; (2) Forensic evidence collection (clothing, swabs) before bathing, maintaining strict chain of custody; (3) Emergency contraception (levonorgestrel, ulipristal, or copper IUD) to prevent unwanted pregnancy; (4) HIV Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) started urgently within 72 hours and continued for 28 days; (5) Empiric antimicrobial prophylaxis against gonorrhoea, chlamydia, and trichomoniasis; (6) Tetanus and Hepatitis B vaccination; and (7) Structured psychological crisis intervention. Why the other options fail: option b sequences evidence before support, when a crying, trembling client cannot give informed consent to anything until she is settled. Option c makes PEP conditional on knowing the assailant's status, which is not required, and defers contraception that is time-critical. Option d makes a police case number a precondition of care, which no service requires and which re-traumatises the client.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sexual assault care bundle: Safety/trauma-informed care + forensic chain of custody + emergency contraception + HIV PEP (< 72 h) + empiric STI prophylaxis + counseling.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Recognising that the readings are abnormal and alerting the team before the end of the shift"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Synthesising the data with clinical knowledge to recognise septic shock and start the sepsis bundle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Documenting the vital signs accurately in the electronic record so the trend can be reviewed later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Checking the monitor calibration and repeating the observations to confirm the readings are real"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The DIKW framework illustrates how raw data transitions into clinical action: 1) DATA = discrete objective facts (BP 82/44, HR 124); 2) INFORMATION = data organized and given context (vital signs show hypotension, tachycardia, tachypnea, and pyrexia); 3) KNOWLEDGE = synthesizing information with nursing science (recognizing that this cluster represents Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome / severe sepsis); 4) WISDOM = using clinical knowledge, judgment, and ethics to take optimal, timely clinical action (immediately triggering the sepsis resuscitation bundle, fluids, blood cultures, and antibiotics). Options a, c, bnd d reflect unprocessed data or negligent avoidance. Why the other options fail: option a is data without interpretation. Option c is data transcribed onto paper, which is information at most. Option d suppresses the signal and treats the heart rate as anxiety, which is the opposite of clinical wisdom.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DIKW framework: Data (raw numbers) -> Information (numbers in context) -> Knowledge (understanding clinical significance) -> Wisdom (applying judgment to intervene for optimal patient outcomes).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A coding scheme that shortens documentation by allowing nurses to enter single numbers instead of sentences"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A billing structure that converts nursing interventions into chargeable items for reimbursement"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A shared vocabulary that lets diagnoses, interventions and outcomes be compared across systems and used for quality measurement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A translation tool that converts nursing notes into medical diagnoses for the physician record"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Standardized nursing terminologies (e.g., International Classification for Nursing Practice [ICNP], SNOMED-CT, NANDA/NIC/NOC) create a consistent, structured language that represents nursing care across multidisciplinary platforms. This standardization ensures semantic interoperability between diverse electronic systems, makes nursing contributions visible in clinical documentation, allows aggregation of Big Data for nursing research, and enables evaluation of nurse-sensitive client outcomes. It does not replace assessment, monetize nursing inappropriately, or displace medical diagnoses. Why the other options fail: A billing structure turns documentation into a charging exercise rather than a clinical record; a coding scheme of single numbers is shorthand, not a terminology; a translator that converts nursing notes into medical diagnoses loses the nursing content in the medical model.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Informatics principle: Standardized nursing terminologies enable interoperability, make nursing care visible and quantifiable, and drive evidence-based outcomes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It links the payroll and attendance records of health staff to the national human resource database"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It aggregates facility and district data so outbreaks can be detected and coverage and resources tracked nationally"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It stores the monthly returns so that facilities can print their own reports at the end of the year"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It replaces the paper register at facility level, with the national office collecting the forms quarterly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$DHIMS-2 (District Health Information Management System) is the primary web-based platform utilized by the Ghana Health Service (GHS) and Ministry of Health for routine national health information management. Frontline facility data (from CHPS compounds, health centers, and hospitals) are aggregated into DHIMS-2 to monitor disease incidence (e.g., malaria, cholera, vaccine-preventable illnesses), track maternal-child health metrics, evaluate health system performance, and guide targeted budgetary and logistical resource distribution. It is not an ad platform or an employee punch clock. Why the other options fail: option b treats DHIMS-2 as an advertising platform. Option c describes an archive with no analysis. Option d reduces it to attendance monitoring.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Public health informatics: DHIMS-2 collects and aggregates population-level health data to enable disease surveillance and evidence-based health policy planning.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It carries forward findings that may no longer be true, masks deterioration and creates a record that misrepresents the assessment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It duplicates the previous shift's work, so the same assessment is charged to the client twice"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It slows the record down and consumes storage, so the system becomes unusable over time"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It breaches the software licence, because the documentation belongs to the vendor rather than the clinician"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cloning documentation via 'copy and paste' in the electronic health record is a high-risk practice condemned by regulatory bodies and patient safety organizations. Copying prior assessments propagates outdated physical findings (e.g., documenting surgical dressings as 'clean, dry, and intact' when an incision has actually dehisced or infected), masks acute deterioration, creates inaccurate legal records, and can be considered fraudulent billing in court. Every clinical assessment must reflect contemporaneous, independent evaluation. Server space and copyright laws are not the primary clinical safety concerns. Why the other options fail: option a confuses storage with safety. Option c invents a copyright claim over clinical text. Option d invents an electrical effect. The harm is that yesterday's findings are presented as today's assessment.$q$,
  strategy = $q$EHR safety rule: Never copy and paste prior clinical assessment notes. Chart real-time, independent findings to avoid propagating clinical errors.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It improves accuracy and timeliness, makes the data available to the team at once and involves the client"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It shortens the shift by removing the end-of-shift documentation period, so nurses can leave on time"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It guarantees that no medication errors can occur, because every dose is scanned at the bedside"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It removes the need to wash hands between clients, because the device is cleaned between uses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Point-of-care documentation involves recording assessments, vital signs, and medication administration in real time at the bedside. Evidence demonstrates that point-of-care charting dramatically reduces cognitive recall errors (which peak during end-of-shift batch charting), ensures real-time availability of critical lab values and vitals across the multidisciplinary team, and invites the client into the care dialogue. It should never replace eye contact or interpersonal connection, does not provide absolute immunity from all errors, and never waives hand hygiene protocols. Why the other options fail: option b shortens the shift, which is a convenience and not the rationale. Option c promises something no system can guarantee. Option d removes hand hygiene, which no documentation method can do.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Point-of-care charting: Real-time documentation at the bedside improves clinical accuracy, minimizes memory lapses, and ensures timely interdisciplinary communication.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the dose as prescribed, because the prescriber is accountable for the order"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Change the order to 5,000 units yourself and document the correction in the progress note"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withhold the dose, hold the order in the system, contact the prescriber and notify the pharmacist"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cancel the order without telling anyone, because no dose has been given"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Computerized Provider Order Entry (CPOE) significantly reduces illegibility errors but does not eliminate prescribing errors (such as wrong dose or wrong frequency selections from drop-down menus). The nurse remains the ultimate safety barrier before medication administration. When an unsafe or questionable order appears in CPOE, the nurse must withhold the drug, flag or hold the order, contact the prescriber for clarification, and consult pharmacy. Assuming computer orders are infallible leads to fatal bleeding; modifying an order without authorization or deleting records violates pharmacy and nursing practice acts. Why the other options fail: Changing the order to five thousand units without the prescriber is practising medicine and hides the error; giving it because the prescriber is accountable assumes an electronic order is correct, which is the assumption computerised ordering exists to end; cancelling silently leaves the error in the record for the next nurse to repeat.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CPOE safety: Computerized orders can still contain human selection errors. Always verify clinical appropriateness; withhold and clarify unsafe orders immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the nurses to record observations and doses on their phones and enter them when the system returns"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Activate the downtime protocol, move to paper charts and shadow records, and designate staff to track orders and admissions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop giving medicines and stop monitoring vital signs until the servers are restored"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discharge the stable clients so the unit has fewer records to reconstruct when the system returns"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$When electronic health systems experience scheduled or unscheduled downtime, clinical operations must maintain uninterrupted patient safety through an established downtime policy and disaster recovery protocol. The charge nurse activates downtime standard operating procedures: accessing downtime emergency paper binders, deploying paper medication administration records (MARs) and progress notes, tracking client movement manually, and utilizing runner messengers for urgent laboratory and pharmacy orders. Ceasing care causes preventable deaths; discharging patients recklessly or relying on human memory causes catastrophic medical errors. Why the other options fail: Recording observations on personal phones is not an approved downtime record and puts protected health information on an unmanaged device; stopping medicines and monitoring because the computer is down harms clients; discharging stable clients to shrink the reconstruction workload transfers risk to them.$q$,
  strategy = $q$EHR downtime response: Activate the facility downtime protocol immediately, deploy standardized paper charts and MARs, and maintain rigorous manual documentation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An unlawful breach of role-based access and need-to-know, subject to discipline and regulatory reporting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An acceptable practice, because the nurse knows the clients personally and meant no harm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A breach only if the records were printed or shared on social media, and not otherwise"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A minor policy deviation that carries no institutional or regulatory liability"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Access to electronic protected health information (ePHI) is legally restricted by Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) and the statutory 'need-to-know' principle under data privacy statutes (such as the Ghana Data Protection Act 2012 [Act 843], HIPAA, and GDPR). Healthcare personnel may only open the records of clients directly assigned to their clinical care. Snooping into the records of neighbors, acquaintances, or public figures without clinical justification is an intentional privacy violation resulting in disciplinary action, termination, and professional council investigation. Why the other options fail: Treating the access as acceptable because the nurse knows the clients ignores role-based access and need-to-know; making it a breach only when records are printed or posted misstates where the breach occurs; calling it a minor deviation ignores that the access was deliberate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Data privacy rule: You may ONLY access health records of clients currently assigned to your direct clinical care. Browsing unassigned records is illegal snooping.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Log in for the physician but stay at the keyboard while the order is entered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Write the username and password on a note beside the workstation for the team to use"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Let the physician enter the order under the nurse's name and countersign it themselves afterwards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Decline, log off the workstation, and explain that sharing credentials breaks accountability and policy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In healthcare cybersecurity and electronic documentation law, an individual's unique login credentials (username and password) represent their legal electronic signature. Sharing credentials compromises the entire audit trail, breaches hospital security policies, and legally binds the nurse to any order, medication, or charting entered under their account. The nurse must refuse credential sharing, log out of the computer, and direct the prescriber to IT support or emergency phone/verbal order protocols. Handing over sessions or pasting passwords are severe security violations. Why the other options fail: option a leaves the session open and the physician unverified. Option b publishes credentials, which is worse than sharing them once. Option c attributes the order to the wrong clinician, which is the harm the nurse is avoiding.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cybersecurity standard: Never share login credentials or leave active electronic sessions unlocked. Your login is your legal electronic signature.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The image is likely to be of poor quality, so the second opinion would be unreliable"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The photograph is stored on a device the hospital cannot back up, so it may be lost before review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Personal devices and consumer messaging apps are not encrypted or approved, so the image breaches privacy law and policy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Second opinions are not permitted for a client who has not been formally transferred to another facility"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Capturing client clinical images on personal, unencrypted mobile devices and transmitting them across consumer messaging platforms violates client privacy rights, institutional BYOD (Bring Your Own Device) policies, and statutory health data protection laws. Digital clinical photography requires: 1) explicit informed client consent; 2) utilization of enterprise-secured, encrypted, institutional hardware and HIPAA/Data Protection Act-compliant platforms; and 3) direct storage into the client's official medical record. Camera resolution and battery usage are irrelevant to data privacy laws. Why the other options fail: Backup of a hospital device addresses resilience, not consent or encryption; image quality is a diagnostic concern, not the privacy question; requiring a formal transfer for a second opinion overstates a rule that only requires consent and a lawful channel.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Digital privacy: Never photograph clients or transmit clinical images using personal smartphones and consumer messaging apps. Use institutional secure platforms only.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the ampicillin with an antihistamine premedication, so any reaction is blunted"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Override the alert after checking that the dose lies within the normal range for the client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dismiss the alert because documented allergies are often out of date and the reaction is usually mild"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Read the alert, withhold the ampicillin, recheck the allergy record and ask the prescriber for a non-penicillin drug"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) provide real-time, evidence-based alerts at the point of care to intercept adverse drug events. High-tier critical alerts (such as severe drug-allergy interactions) represent life-threatening safety hazards. The nurse must not succumb to 'alert fatigue' (mindlessly overriding pop-ups). The nurse must review the alert, withhold the drug, verify the documented penicillin allergy, and notify the prescriber to order a safe therapeutic alternative. Automatic overrides cause fatal anaphylaxis; antacids do not prevent immune-mediated anaphylactic shock. Why the other options fail: Overriding the alert after checking the dose leaves the documented anaphylaxis unresolved; dismissing the allergy as stale or mild is not a judgement the nurse may make at the keyboard; premedicating and giving the drug anyway blunts nothing and risks the reaction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CDSS alerts: Never bypass or override critical clinical decision support alerts without reading and investigating. Overriding allergy alerts causes fatal anaphylaxis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A benign technical malfunction with no implication for patient safety, because the nurse knows the client"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An efficient adaptation to a damaged wristband, provided the copied barcode is legible and matches the client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A software interoperability problem, since the scanner cannot read a wrinkled label, and not a nursing practice issue"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A dangerous workaround that bypasses bedside patient identification and raises the risk of a wrong-patient error"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A 'workaround' is an informal bypass of an established safety system created by staff to cope with workflow obstacles or save time. Photocopying client barcodes onto carts, badges, or door frames completely dismantles the core safety mechanism of BCMA—which is verifying the Right Patient at the bedside simultaneously with the Right Drug. If a barcode is unreadable, the nurse must reprint and apply a new wristband immediately. Labeling workarounds as innovations or benign ignores root causes of severe wrong-patient medication administration fatalities. Why the other options fail: Calling the shortcut efficient is how workarounds spread; blaming the scanner makes a software problem of a substitution the nurse chose; treating it as benign because the nurse knows the client is the assumption bedside scanning exists to remove.$q$,
  strategy = $q$BCMA safety: Never use workarounds (such as scanning surrogate barcodes away from the bedside). Always scan the client's actual wristband at the bedside.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Arrange an elective appointment with the client's physician and advise rest until then, since the consultation is virtual"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Recognise possible ACS, tell the client to call emergency services, confirm the exact location, stay on the line and advise aspirin if safe"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the client to take an antacid and call back if the pain has not settled within the hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "End the call and document it, because a telehealth service cannot take responsibility for an emergency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Telehealth nurses must uphold the same standards of clinical judgment and care as bedside clinicians. When acute life-threatening symptoms (e.g., acute myocardial infarction) are identified during virtual triage, the nurse must: 1) verify the client's physical location/address immediately; 2) coordinate emergency EMS dispatch; 3) provide interim emergency guidance (rest, chew aspirin if protocol allows and no contraindications); and 4) stay on the connection until emergency responders arrive. Delaying care, recommending physical exertion, or hanging up constitutes gross telehealth malpractice. Why the other options fail: option a delays care that must happen in minutes. Option c treats chest pain with an antacid and a callback. Option d abandons the client, when the nurse is the only clinician present.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Telehealth triage: Verify physical location first in all consultations. When red-flag emergency symptoms arise, initiate emergency dispatch and stay on the line.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It replaces paper registers entirely, because tablets work in every setting and need no network at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It moves the burden of record keeping to the mother, who reports her own visits through the application"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It provides real-time video consultation with specialists, which is the only way to close the rural gap"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It allows offline data capture and decision support on the tablet, with records synchronised when connectivity returns"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$mHealth (mobile health) applications on portable smartphones and tablets revolutionize rural primary health care (such as CHPS compounds) by supporting: 1) offline functionality with store-and-forward data synchronization; 2) algorithmic point-of-care guidance for early recognition of obstetric and neonatal danger signs; 3) automated SMS text reminders for antenatal care and infant vaccinations; and 4) real-time referral communication with district hospitals. It enhances frontline human care rather than replacing clinicians with robots or imposing unaffordable hardware burdens. Why the other options fail: Moving record keeping onto the mother transfers the burden of documentation; real-time video is only one channel and is not what offline capability gives; replacing paper registers entirely overstates it, because tablets need power, maintenance and eventually a network.$q$,
  strategy = $q$mHealth advantages: Portable, offline-capable data collection, algorithmic decision support, and SMS patient reminders in resource-constrained environments.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use Boolean AND to combine every synonym of the topic, then truncate each term to capture variants"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use NOT to exclude irrelevant populations, so the search returns only postoperative delirium studies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use OR within each concept and AND between concepts, so that each concept is captured without losing studies"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use phrase searching in quotation marks alone, which returns the most precise set with no Boolean operators"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) structure bibliographic database queries: 'OR' broadens a search by including synonyms within a concept (e.g., 'aged OR elderly'); 'AND' narrows the search by requiring that all distinct concepts must be present in the retrieved records. Grouping synonyms in parentheses with OR and connecting the concept sets with AND produces targeted, high-yield results. Using 'NOT' inappropriately excludes the target population; using 'OR' exclusively returns millions of irrelevant records; natural language phrases fail in structured indexing systems. Why the other options fail: option a ANDs every synonym, which returns almost nothing. Option b uses NOT on the very population the question is about. Option d writes a sentence rather than a query and uses no Boolean logic at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Boolean logic: Use 'OR' to combine synonyms within a concept (broadens); use 'AND' to combine different concepts together (narrows).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dock the pay of the night shift nurses until the rate falls below the national benchmark"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withdraw the metric from the dashboard until the rate has returned to the benchmark on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Introduce a policy restricting catheter use and audit compliance monthly, without examining the existing data"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Form an interprofessional improvement team, analyse insertion and dwell-time data, and trial a nurse-driven removal reminder"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clinical dashboards aggregate EHR data into visual indicators (run charts, control charts) to monitor nurse-sensitive indicators (e.g., CAUTI, CLABSI, pressure injuries, falls). When run charts demonstrate non-random negative trends (e.g., 6 consecutive points above benchmark), informatics-guided leadership investigates the underlying data (e.g., catheter dwell times), implements evidence-based interventions (such as EHR automated reminders for early catheter removal or nurse-driven discontinuation protocols), and tracks post-intervention metrics. Deleting data is fraudulent; punitive blaming destroys safety culture. Why the other options fail: Withdrawing the metric removes the signal without changing the rate; auditing a new policy without examining the existing data repeats the gap; docking pay punishes staff before the cause is investigated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical dashboards: Transform electronic clinical metrics into targeted quality improvement initiatives and monitor trend shifts on run charts.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A confidentiality breach by jigsaw identification; private chats are not exempt and the nurse faces discipline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acceptable, because the messages are end-to-end encrypted and the name was left out"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acceptable educational discussion, because no full name, date of birth or address was included"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A protected disclosure, because the nurse was raising a clinical concern in good faith"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Confidentiality breaches frequently occur through 'jigsaw identification'—combining indirect identifiers (clinical photograph, specific unit, room number, tribal background, rare congenital defect) that allow community members or hospital staff to readily identify the individual. Furthermore, using consumer messaging apps (even with encryption) to transmit protected patient health data without consent violates national data privacy statutes and professional codes. Omitting names does not prevent identification; regulatory bodies routinely revoke nursing licenses for social media and messaging app disclosures. Why the other options fail: option b confuses encryption in transit with lawful use. Option c assumes that omitting the name is sufficient, when the room, the defect and the background identify the child. Option d is not a protected disclosure, because the nurse was seeking advice rather than reporting wrongdoing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Digital professionalism: Never post client information, photos, or indirect identifiers in messaging groups or social media. Jigsaw identification constitutes a major regulatory violation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ignore the alert, because an algorithm cannot interpret borderline vital signs in an older adult"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Follow the alert without assessment, because the algorithm has been validated on a large dataset"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use the alert as decision support, assess the client at the bedside, check the labs and escalate as the findings warrant"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Repeat the observations until the algorithm withdraws the alert, then continue routine care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Artificial Intelligence (AI) and machine learning models in healthcare serve as assistive decision support, NOT replacements for professional clinical judgment. Predictive models identify subtle patterns in massive EHR datasets that human clinicians might overlook (e.g., early sepsis trajectories). The nurse must validate AI alerts by performing an in-person physical assessment, synthesizing clinical context, and verifying objective diagnostic data. Total dismissal of AI misses early warning opportunities; blind subservience to AI risks algorithmic bias and error. Why the other options fail: Following the alert without assessment is automation bias; ignoring a validated alert discards a signal the bedside assessment is meant to test; repeating observations until the alert withdraws treats the alert as noise rather than a finding to act on.$q$,
  strategy = $q$AI in nursing: AI serves as augmented decision support. The nurse retains final clinical accountability through independent assessment and professional judgment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Move to manual downtime procedures, protect patients, switch to paper, disconnect the network and notify incident command"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transfer every inpatient to another facility and close the unit until the systems are restored"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pay the ransom from petty cash so that monitoring is restored as quickly as possible"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep charting electronically on the ward computers, since the encryption affects only the servers"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A ransomware cyberattack represents a combined IT disaster and clinical emergency. The nursing team's absolute priority is maintaining client physical safety by transitioning to full manual paper-based business continuity procedures (manual vitals, paper MARs, independent medication double-checks). Concurrently, nursing leadership coordinates with hospital incident command and disconnects devices from network ports to prevent malware propagation. Paying ransoms is a law enforcement decision; abandoning clients or vigilante counter-hacking are dangerous and illegal. Why the other options fail: Paying the ransom funds criminals and restores nothing quickly; transferring every inpatient and closing the unit abandons a hospital that can still function on paper; charting on ward computers that depend on the encrypted servers records nothing that will survive.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cyberattack response: Protect client physical safety first, activate emergency manual paper downtime operations, and collaborate with incident command.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the client to enter their own history into the tablet while the nurse attends to other tasks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Position the mobile computer so eye contact is kept, explain what is being entered and involve the client in the plan"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Chart at the bedside without speaking, so the record is finished before the conversation begins"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Complete the documentation after the shift from memory, so the whole shift is spent with clients"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Technology must serve the patient, not replace the human therapeutic relationship. Nurses can humanize technology by: 1) positioning the computer screen so it does not form a physical barrier between nurse and client; 2) maintaining regular eye contact and verbal communication; 3) explaining what is being documented ('I am entering your blood pressure reading into your electronic chart now'); and 4) actively involving the client in reviewing goals, lab trends, and discharge plans. Refusing to document is illegal negligence; hiding behind computers destroys the therapeutic alliance. Why the other options fail: Delegating the history to the client's own tablet loses the clinical interview; charting at the bedside without speaking is the behaviour the nurse complained about; reconstructing the shift from memory produces inaccuracy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Human-centered informatics: Position technology to maintain eye contact, narrate your documentation to the client, and keep human connection at the center of care.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INFO-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess the anterior forearm in ordinary room light, since this site is least exposed to the sun and gives the truest reading of the underlying haemoglobin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Look for bright pink flushing over the abdomen, because the increased cutaneous blood flow of sepsis is most visible where the skin is thinnest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Record that cyanosis and pallor cannot be assessed at all in this skin tone, and rely on the arterial blood gas and serum lactate to detect hypoperfusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inspect the conjunctivae, oral mucous membranes, and lips for ashen-grey or blue discoloration, and examine the palms and nail beds rather than relying on general facial skin tone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In darkly pigmented skin, high epidermal melanin concentrations mask the subtle cutaneous color changes seen in lighter skin. Pallor presents not as obvious whiteness, but as a dull, ashen-grey color in dark skin or a yellowish-brown tinge in olive/brown skin. Cyanosis in dark-skinned individuals is best detected by inspecting areas of least pigmentation and rich capillary vascularity: the conjunctivae, buccal mucosa, hard palate, tongue, lips, and palmar surfaces of hands and soles of feet. Additionally, the nurse must palpate the skin for localized warmth, edema, and induration rather than relying solely on erythema. Why the other options fail: option a is how blood pressure and rashes are often checked, but the forearm carries the same melanin as the face and gives no better signal. Option b looks for flushing rather than for the ashen or blue change the question asks about, and flushing in sepsis is inconsistent. Option c is the belief the item exists to correct: central and mucosal surfaces are assessable in every skin tone, and relying on the gas misses the hours before lactate rises.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dark skin assessment: Inspect mucous membranes (buccal mucosa, tongue, conjunctivae) and palms/soles for pallor/cyanosis; palpate for warmth/induration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Macule: A flat, non-palpable, circumscribed area of skin color change measuring less than 1 cm in diameter without elevation or depression"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Papule: A raised, fluid-filled lesion more than 1 cm across, sitting deep in the subcutaneous fat and collapsing when pressure is applied"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bulla: A solid, raised, firm lesion formed from dense fibrous scar tissue that persists after the original injury has healed over"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wheal: A permanent cavity that extends through the dermis into the underlying muscle, leaving a depressed scar once it closes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Primary skin lesion taxonomy is fundamental to clinical dermatology: (1) A macule is a flat, non-palpable change in skin color < 1 cm (e.g., freckle, petechia); if > 1 cm, it is a patch. (2) A papule is an elevated, palpable, solid mass < 1 cm (e.g., mole, wart); if > 1 cm, it is a plaque. (3) A vesicle is an elevated cavity filled with clear serous fluid <= 0.5–1 cm (e.g., herpes simplex); if > 1 cm, it is a bulla (blister). (4) A wheal is a transient, raised, edematous, erythematous area with irregular borders caused by dermal fluid extravasation (e.g., urticaria/hives). Why the other options fail: option b attaches a fluid-filled cavity and a deep location to a papule, which is solid and superficial, and a fluid-filled lesion over 1 cm is a bulla rather than a papule. Option c describes a scar or a nodule, not a blister; a bulla is defined by being fluid-filled and larger than 1 cm. Option d describes an ulcer reaching muscle, whereas a wheal is a transient oedematous swelling of the dermis that resolves without scarring.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lesion taxonomy: Macule = flat color change < 1 cm. Papule = solid raised < 1 cm. Vesicle = fluid blister < 1 cm. Bulla = fluid blister > 1 cm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leave the slough undisturbed so that the wound is not traumatised, and cover the ulcer with a dry dressing changed once weekly to keep the wound bed warm and reduce disturbance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse the ulcer with hydrogen peroxide and leave it open to the air between dressing changes, so that the exudate can dry and the adherent slough lifts away on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a hydrocolloid sheet and review at seven days, since an occlusive dressing softens slough and absorbs exudate without any need for active debridement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Debride the non-viable slough and manage the heavy exudate with an absorbent dressing, keeping the bed moist while protecting the surrounding skin from maceration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The TIME framework optimizes the chronic wound microenvironment: (1) Tissue non-viable: Slough and necrotic eschar promote bacterial biofilm and must be debrided (autolytic, enzymatic, sharp, or mechanical); (2) Infection/Inflammation: Control bacterial burden and biofilm; (3) Moisture imbalance: Chronic wounds heal fastest in a physiological moist (not wet, not dry) environment; excessive exudate macerates surrounding skin and contains destructive metalloproteinases, requiring absorbent dressings (calcium alginates, hydrofibers, foam); and (4) Edge advancement: Epidermal cells require a healthy granulating matrix to migrate across wound margins. Why the other options fail: option a addresses neither letter of the framework: slough left in place harbours biofilm, and a dry weekly dressing violates moisture balance in a heavily exuding wound. Option b dries the bed out to loosen slough, which is the opposite of moist wound healing, and hydrogen peroxide is cytotoxic to the fibroblasts trying to close the wound. Option c is the nearest miss: hydrocolloid does soften slough autolytically, but it cannot cope with heavy exudate and will leak and macerate the peri-wound skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TIME wound care: Non-viable slough requires debridement + excess exudate requires absorbent dressing to balance moisture and prevent maceration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stage 1 pressure injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stage 3 pressure injury"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stage 2 pressure injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Unstageable pressure injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to NPUAP/EPUAP staging: Stage 1 is intact skin with localized non-blanchable erythema (or localized warmth, firmness, or discoloration in dark skin). Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss of skin with exposed dermis, presenting as a shallow open ulcer with a viable, red or pink, moist wound bed without slough, eschar, or granulation tissue, OR presenting as an intact or open/ruptured serum-filled blister. Stage 3 involves full-thickness skin loss with visible adipose tissue and granulation; Stage 4 involves exposed bone, tendon, or muscle. Unstageable occurs when slough or eschar completely obscures the base. Why the other options fail: option a is intact skin with non-blanchable erythema and no loss of skin, which is not the shallow open wound described. Option b extends through the dermis into subcutaneous fat, which this wound does not. Option d cannot be staged because slough or eschar obscures the base, when here the base is visible and pink.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pressure injury staging: Partial-thickness dermis loss with red/pink bed OR intact serum blister = Stage 2. Full-thickness into fat = Stage 3.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reposition every four hours and massage the reddened prominences with a moisturising cream at each turn, since massage restores capillary blood flow to ischaemic tissue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reposition every 2 hours with a 30-degree lateral tilt, use a pressure-redistributing mattress, float the heels on pillows, and keep the skin dry and barrier-protected"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nurse the client in the high Fowler position at 90 degrees and place a ring cushion under the sacrum, so that pressure is distributed around rather than over the bony point"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reposition every two hours but keep the head of the bed elevated at 60 degrees for comfort, and use a donut cushion to lift the sacrum clear of the mattress"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A Braden score <= 12 signifies high risk for pressure injury development (scores range from 6 to 23; lower scores mean higher risk). Evidence-based prevention mandates: (1) frequent repositioning (at least every 2 hours in bed, every 1 hour in a chair); (2) using a 30-degree lateral tilted position (avoids direct vertical pressure on the trochanter and sacrum); (3) floating heels off the mattress using pillows under calves; (4) pressure-redistribution support surfaces; (5) moisture barriers for incontinence; and (6) avoiding elevation of the head of the bed > 30 degrees (reduces shear forces). Vigorously massaging bony prominences causes deep tissue capillary damage. Why the other options fail: option a doubles the interval and adds massage over an area that may already be ischaemic, which damages the deep capillary bed rather than restoring flow. Option c raises the head to 90 degrees, which converts pressure into shearing force through the sacrum, and a ring cushion concentrates pressure on the rim. Option d gets the interval right and then does both of the things that cause harm: sustained 60-degree elevation and a donut cushion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pressure ulcer prevention: Reposition q2h + 30-degree tilt + float heels + pressure-relieving mattress. NEVER massage bony prominences or use donut rings.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "27% TBSA"}, {"id": "b", "text": "36% TBSA"}, {"id": "c", "text": "45% TBSA"}, {"id": "d", "text": "18% TBSA"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the adult Wallace Rule of Nines: Head and neck = 9%; Anterior trunk = 18% (chest 9% + abdomen 9%); Posterior trunk = 18% (upper back 9% + lower back/buttocks 9%); Each arm = 9% total (anterior 4.5% + posterior 4.5%); Each leg = 18% total (anterior 9% + posterior 9%); Perineum/genitalia = 1%. In this client: Anterior chest (9%) + Entire abdomen (9%) + Entire left arm (9%) + Anterior left leg (9%) = 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 36% TBSA. Why the other options fail: option a at 27% omits the whole left arm. Option c at 45% adds the posterior trunk, which the description does not burn. Option d at 18% counts only the trunk and leaves out both the arm and the leg.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rule of Nines calculation: Chest (9%) + Abdomen (9%) + Entire arm (9%) + Anterior leg (9%) = 36% TBSA.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 5,600 mL; infuse half in the first 8 hours and the remainder over 16 hours, targeting a urine output of 30 to 50 mL per hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 22,400 mL; give the whole volume in the first 8 hours, because capillary leak is greatest immediately after the burn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Total 11,200 mL; give 5,600 mL in the first 8 hours from injury, at 800 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours, then 5,600 mL over 16 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 11,200 mL; infuse at 700 mL per hour evenly across the first 16 hours, since the first 8 hours have already elapsed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Parkland (Baxter) formula states: Total 24-hour fluid = 4 mL x weight (kg) x % TBSA burns. For this client: 4 mL x 70 kg x 40% = 11,200 mL of balanced crystalloid (Lactated Ringer's). Delivery schedule: 50% (5,600 mL) must be infused within the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF INJURY, and the remaining 50% (5,600 mL) over the next 16 hours (350 mL/hr). Because 1 hour elapsed before arrival, the initial 5,600 mL must be infused over the remaining 7 hours: 5,600 mL / 7 hours = 800 mL/hr. The nurse titrates this rate to maintain adult urine output at 0.5–1.0 mL/kg/hr (30–50 mL/hr). Why the other options fail: option a halves the formula, which delivers about half the resuscitation volume an adult with 40 per cent burns needs and will leave the client oliguric. Option b doubles the coefficient and then front-loads everything into the first 8 hours, which is the fastest route to pulmonary oedema. Option d gets the total right, which makes it the hardest distractor, but spreads it evenly and so abandons the 50 per cent rule and the eight-hour clock that starts at the time of injury, not arrival.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parkland resuscitation: 4 mL x kg x % TBSA. 50% given in first 8 hours FROM TIME OF BURN (adjust for delay). Target urine output 0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give humidified oxygen by face mask and admit for observation, since a normal PaO2 shows the airway is not yet compromised"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prepare immediately for endotracheal intubation before progressive laryngeal edema produces complete upper airway obstruction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure the family that singed nasal hair is a cosmetic finding, and treat the hoarseness with voice rest and warm fluids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recheck the arterial blood gas in 4 hours and call the anaesthetist if the stridor has worsened by then, to avoid an unnecessary tube"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Physical findings of singed nasal hairs, facial burns, carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, and STRIDOR are pathognomonic of acute thermal inhalation injury and impending upper airway compromise. Supraglottic thermal injury causes rapid, massive laryngeal and vocal cord edema that peaks within 12–24 hours. Once stridor appears, the airway lumen is critically narrowed; delayed intubation frequently becomes impossible due to anatomical distortion, necessitating emergency surgical cricothyroidotomy. Proactive, early endotracheal intubation is the definitive lifesaving priority. Why the other options fail: option a rests on the normal PaO2, which is the trap in the stem: oxygenation is preserved until the airway closes, so a normal gas does not exclude an impending obstruction. Option c treats two of the four warning signs as trivial. Option d is the classic delay: stridor means the lumen is already critically narrowed, and by the time it worsens the swelling can make intubation impossible, leaving cricothyroidotomy as the only route.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inhalation injury: Facial burns + soot + hoarseness/stridor = impending complete airway closure. Secure airway with IMMEDIATE intubation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Extremity compartment syndrome caused by unyielding, inelastic burn eschar and tissue edema; prepare immediately for urgent bedside escharotomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal post-burn inflammation; elevate the limb above the level of the heart and give aspirin for the throbbing pain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Deep vein thrombosis of the forearm; start low molecular weight heparin and apply a warm compress along the tender cord"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nerve injury from the burn itself; document the sensory loss and reassess the hand at the next dressing round"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Circumferential full-thickness burns produce an inelastic, rigid, leathery rind of dead tissue (eschar). As massive fluid resuscitation expands interstitial edema within the limb, intracompartmental pressures rise rapidly, exceeding capillary perfusion pressure (> 30 mmHg). This leads to compartment syndrome: progressive neurovascular compromise characterized by the '6 Ps' (pain out of proportion/pain on passive stretch, paresthesia, pallor, pulselessness, poikilothermia, paralysis). Emergency bedside escharotomy (linear surgical incisions through the burned eschar down to subcutaneous fat) is mandatory to release constriction and restore arterial flow. Why the other options fail: option b interprets pain on passive stretch and a 5-second capillary refill as expected inflammation, and aspirin is irrelevant to a pressure problem that needs the eschar released. Option c is a plausible vascular cause of a faint pulse, but a thrombus does not produce excruciating pain on passive finger extension, and heparin would waste the window. Option d explains the sensory finding and ignores the vascular ones, and reassigning it to the next round delays a limb-threatening procedure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Circumferential burn danger: Faint pulse + pain on passive stretch = Compartment syndrome. Prepare for immediate emergency escharotomy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erysipelas, which can be managed at home with oral paracetamol and flucloxacillin, since the erythema is well demarcated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Contact dermatitis from a laundry detergent; stop the product and apply a topical corticosteroid to the affected leg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Necrotizing fasciitis (a surgical emergency requiring immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and emergency surgical debridement)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Venous stasis pigmentation with a superimposed ulcer; apply compression bandaging and refer to the leg ulcer clinic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Necrotizing fasciitis is a fulminant, rapidly progressive bacterial infection of the deep subcutaneous tissue and fascia with a mortality rate exceeding 25–40%. Cardinal warning signs distinguishing necrotizing fasciitis from benign cellulitis are: (1) severe, exquisite pain markedly OUT OF PROPORTION to superficial clinical findings; (2) rapid progression across marked skin margins within hours; (3) palpable subcutaneous crepitus (gas production from anaerobic pathogens); (4) bullae, skin necrosis, or dishwater-gray fluid discharge; and (5) early hemodynamic instability (septic shock). Immediate surgical fasciotomy and aggressive debridement are vital. Why the other options fail: option a names the right family of infection and the wrong depth: erysipelas is superficial, does not produce crepitus or bullae, and does not cause shock, so oral treatment at home would be fatal. Option b fits the erythema and none of the rest, and a steroid would suppress the only defence the tissue has. Option d is a chronic picture that develops over months, not an acute presentation with tachycardia, hypotension and gas in the tissues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Necrotizing fasciitis: Pain out of proportion to skin signs + crepitus/bullae + shock = Surgical emergency. Immediate radical debridement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Highly contagious: strict hand hygiene, separate towels, soak and remove crusts before mupirocin, and watch for tea-coloured urine (post-streptococcal GN)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Impetigo spreads only by direct contact with the crusts, so the child can attend school provided the lesions are covered with a light bandage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Scrub the crusts off with a loofah at each bath so the ointment reaches the skin, and keep the child home until every lesion has healed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Impetigo is a self-limiting viral rash; keep the skin dry, avoid antibiotics, and return only if the child develops a fever or the rash spreads"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Impetigo is a highly contagious superficial bacterial skin infection caused primarily by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep). Lesions typically begin as fragile vesicles that rupture, releasing exudate that dries into characteristic 'honey-colored crusts'. Nursing care focuses on: (1) preventing transmission (contact precautions, hand hygiene, separate towels/bedding, keeping child home until 24 hours of antimicrobial therapy); (2) crust removal (soaking with warm soapy water before applying topical mupirocin 2%); and (3) monitoring for post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (periorbital edema, gross hematuria/tea-colored urine, hypertension) 1–3 weeks post-infection. Why the other options fail: option c has the right instinct about crusts and the wrong method, since scrubbing spreads the exudate and breaks the skin further. Option d misclassifies a bacterial infection as viral and withholds the treatment that shortens it. Option b is the most tempting, because exclusion from school is genuinely no longer required after 24 hours of treatment, but the option drops the household measures and the renal follow-up that the question is asking for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Impetigo management: Honey-colored crusts = highly contagious. Warm soak crusts + mupirocin ointment + monitor for tea-colored urine (PSGN).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply permethrin only to the visible burrows on the wrists and wash it off after ten minutes, since the mite is confined to the burrow itself"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treat only the household members who are currently itchy, because those without symptoms are not infested and do not need any treatment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight for 8 to 14 hours, treat all household contacts at the same time, and hot-wash bedding used in the past 3 days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight, but delay treating the household contacts until the index child is symptom-free"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Scabies is an infestation caused by the microscopic mite Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis. Mites burrow into the stratum corneum and deposit eggs/feces, triggering a delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction characterized by severe nocturnal pruritus and pathognomonic linear burrows in interdigital web spaces, flexor wrists, axillae, umbilicus, and genitalia. Successful eradication mandates: (1) topical 5% permethrin cream applied from the jawline/neck down over the entire body (including under fingernails and between toes; in infants, the scalp and face are included) for 8–14 hours; (2) treating ALL household contacts simultaneously regardless of symptoms (incubation period is 4–6 weeks); and (3) hot-water laundering of linens. Why the other options fail: option a treats only what can be seen, but mites are present in clinically normal skin between burrows, and ten minutes is far short of the 8 to 14 hours needed for the cream to work. Option b ignores the four to six week incubation period, during which a contact is infectious without itching. Option d is the best of the three and still wrong on the one point that drives reinfestation: simultaneous treatment, because a delay simply re-infests the treated child.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Scabies eradication: 5% permethrin from neck down overnight (8-14 h) + treat ALL household contacts simultaneously + wash bedding in hot water.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bathe the child three times daily in hot soapy water and scrub the skin with a dry loofah, so that the scale is removed before the emollient is applied"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the skin as dry as possible and avoid all moisturisers, because emollients trap bacteria in the lichenified plaques and worsen the eczema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a daily dilute bleach bath and leave the skin to air dry completely before applying any topical corticosteroid to the raw creases"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bathe in lukewarm water for 10 minutes, pat damp, and apply a thick fragrance-free emollient within 3 minutes (the 'soak and seal' method)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal skin barrier dysfunction (filaggrin deficiency), transepidermal water loss, and immune dysregulation ('the itch that rashes'). The cornerstone of maintenance therapy is the 'soak and seal' technique: (1) short lukewarm baths (not hot water, which strips natural lipids); (2) mild soap-free cleansers; (3) patting skin lightly so it remains slightly damp; and (4) liberal application of lipid-rich, fragrance-free emollients (ointments like petrolatum or thick creams) within 3 minutes of stepping out of the water to lock in hydration. Topical corticosteroids are reserved for acute inflammatory flares. Why the other options fail: option a uses heat and friction, both of which strip the lipid barrier and drive the itch-scratch cycle the plan exists to break. Option b inverts the treatment: emollients are the maintenance therapy, not a cause of infection. Option c borrows a real adjunct, dilute bleach bathing, and then air-dries the skin and jumps to a steroid, which skips the emollient that replaces the missing barrier.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Eczema barrier repair: Soak and seal -> Lukewarm bath + pat damp + apply thick ointment/cream within 3 minutes. Avoid hot water and fragrances.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tinea corporis exhibiting the Darier sign"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Plaque psoriasis exhibiting the Auspitz sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Malignant melanoma exhibiting the Breslow phenomenon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lichen planus exhibiting Wickham striae"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Plaque psoriasis (psoriasis vulgaris) is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory dermatosis driven by T-lymphocytes and cytokines (IL-17, IL-23, TNF-alpha), resulting in hyperproliferation of epidermal keratinocytes (epidermal turnover accelerates from 28 days to 3–4 days). The hallmark lesions are sharply demarcated erythematous plaques covered with silvery, micaceous scales on extensor surfaces. The Auspitz sign is the appearance of punctate capillary bleeding spots when scales are scraped off, caused by thinned suprapapillary epidermal plates over dilated, tortuous dermal capillaries. Lesions developing at sites of skin trauma demonstrate the Koebner phenomenon. Why the other options fail: option a gives tinea the Darier sign, but Darier sign is the urticarial response produced by rubbing a mastocytoma. Option c pairs melanoma with Breslow correctly, but Breslow thickness is a histological measurement rather than a bedside sign. Option d pairs lichen planus with Wickham striae correctly, but the silvery scale and pinpoint bleeding described belong to psoriasis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Psoriasis hallmarks: Silvery-white scales on extensor surfaces + pinpoint bleeding when scraped (Auspitz sign) + new lesions at trauma sites (Koebner).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "TEN/SJS overlap; stop the sulfonamide immediately and transfer to a burns intensive care unit for fluid and wound management"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A morbilliform drug exanthem; stop the cotrimoxazole, give an oral antihistamine, and review in the outpatient clinic in one week"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; start intravenous flucloxacillin and keep the skin moist with paraffin gauze until it re-epithelialises"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Erythema multiforme major; continue the antibiotic, give systemic corticosteroids, and reassess the mucosal lesions daily on the ward"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) are life-threatening Severe Cutaneous Adverse Reactions (SCAR) characterized by widespread epidermal apoptosis and detachment triggered primarily by medications (sulfonamides, antiepileptics like carbamazepine/phenytoin, allopurinol, nevirapine). SJS involves < 10% TBSA epidermal detachment; SJS/TEN overlap involves 10–30% TBSA; TEN involves > 30% TBSA. Mucosal involvement (lips, oral cavity, eyes, genitalia) is present in > 90% of cases. A positive Nikolsky sign (sloughing of epidermis with light tangential shear) reflects epidermal-dermal cleavage. Immediate cessation of the causative drug and transfer to a specialized burn unit are vital. Why the other options fail: option b describes the common benign drug rash and misses mucosal erosion and a positive Nikolsky sign, either of which rules it out, and a week's outpatient review is far too slow. Option c is a real blistering disease but it is caused by staphylococcal toxin in young children, splits at the granular layer, and does not follow a sulfonamide. Option d keeps the culprit drug running, which is the single most dangerous thing to do in TEN.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SJS / TEN: Sulfonamide trigger + mucosal erosion + positive Nikolsky sign (epidermal sloughing). STOP DRUG IMMEDIATELY and treat like severe burns in ICU.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Superficial spreading melanoma; the commonest subtype in all populations, arising on sun-exposed skin and evaluated with the ABCDE criteria"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Benign subungual lentigo; a harmless pigment band that needs no biopsy and can be reviewed yearly with clinical photography"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Basal cell carcinoma; a slow-growing, pearly, sun-related tumour of the face that rarely metastasises and is treated by excision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acral lentiginous melanoma, the commonest subtype in Black populations, arising on palms, soles and nail beds; assessed with ABCDE"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$While overall melanoma incidence is lower in dark-skinned individuals due to the photoprotective effect of eumelanin against UV radiation, when melanoma does occur in African, Black, and Asian populations, it is disproportionately Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM), accounting for 50–70% of all melanomas in these populations. Unlike other melanomas, ALM is NOT related to UV radiation/sun exposure and characteristically develops on glabrous skin: palms, soles, and subungual nail beds (Hutchinson's sign). The ABCDE mnemonic (Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, Evolution/change) guides identification, and suspicious lesions mandate urgent excisional biopsy. Why the other options fail: option a is the commonest melanoma overall and the wrong answer for this population, and it is described as sun-related, which the acral form is not. Option b reassures on a lesion with irregular borders that is enlarging, which is the description that mandates biopsy. Option c is a pigmented facial tumour, not a subungual or plantar one, and it does not fit an enlarging irregular macule on the sole.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Melanoma in dark skin: Acral lentiginous melanoma (palms, soles, nail beds) is NOT sun-related and is the most common subtype in Black populations. Use ABCDE.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erythema toxicum neonatorum; a benign, self-limiting newborn eruption that needs no treatment and resolves within 1 to 2 weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Congenital syphilis; start intravenous benzylpenicillin, screen the mother, and isolate the infant from other babies on the postnatal ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; obtain blood cultures, start intravenous flucloxacillin, and prepare the infant for skin grafting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neonatal herpes simplex infection; perform a lumbar puncture and start high-dose intravenous aciclovir on the neonatal unit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Erythema toxicum neonatorum is the most common benign cutaneous eruption in healthy full-term neonates (affecting 30–70% of newborns), typically appearing on days 1–4 of life. It presents with erythematous macules, papules, and sterile pustules with a classic 'flea-bitten' appearance over the trunk and proximal extremities, sparing palms and soles. Wright or Giemsa staining of pustular fluid reveals eosinophils (distinguishing it from bacterial or candidal pustules with neutrophils). It is self-limiting, causes no systemic symptoms, and resolves spontaneously within 7–14 days without treatment. Parental reassurance is the only indicated intervention. Why the other options fail: option b is the invasive infection to consider in a sick neonate, but this infant is feeding, afebrile and active, and syphilis does not produce eosinophil-filled pustules. Option c causes widespread tender erythema and epidermal peeling rather than scattered flea-bitten pustules on an erythematous base. Option d is the correct response to a genuinely unwell baby with vesicles, and none of the features that define herpes, such as grouped vesicles, are present.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Newborn rashes: Flea-bitten pustules on healthy term neonate + eosinophils on smear = Erythema toxicum neonatorum. Benign, self-limiting -> Reassure parents.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trim the flap away with scissors and clean the exposed dermis with alcohol, since a raised flap traps bacteria and delays epithelial migration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse with normal saline, re-approximate the flap without stretching, and secure it with a silicone non-adherent dressing or retention bandage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a high-adhesion zinc oxide tape directly over the raw dermis and change it every four hours, so that the wound is kept clean and visible"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the wound uncovered and open to the air, so that a firm scab can form over the denuded dermis and protect it from further shearing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In older adults, epidermal thinning, flattening of the dermal-epidermal junction (rete ridges), loss of dermal collagen, and microvascular atrophy make the skin exceptionally fragile, predisposing to skin tears from minor shear or friction. According to the International Skin Tear Advisory Panel (ISTAP): for a Type 1 skin tear (where the flap can cover the entire wound bed), the nurse must: (1) gently irrigate with normal saline; (2) gently re-approximate the viable epidermal flap using a moist sterile swab or gloved finger; (3) secure with a non-adherent dressing (silicone foam or mesh); and (4) NEVER apply adhesive tape directly to the fragile peri-wound skin. Why the other options fail: option a discards viable tissue that acts as a natural biological dressing and applies alcohol to a raw dermis, which is painful and cytotoxic. Option c uses the adhesive the guidance explicitly warns against, on the same fragile peri-wound skin that tore to begin with, and changes it four-hourly. Option d leaves the denuded dermis to dry and scab, which is slower and more painful, and gives no protection against the next shear.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Skin tear management: Cleanse gently -> Roll back/re-approximate viable flap -> Cover with non-adherent silicone dressing. NO adhesive tape on frail skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply the entire 60 g tube like thick cake icing twice daily, so that the eczema is fully suppressed before the tube runs out rather than under-treated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Potent topical steroids are safe on any site for any duration, so apply clobetasol to the eyelids and face twice daily and continue it indefinitely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1 FTU (about 0.5 g from the finger crease to tip) covers two adult handprints; use the least amount needed and avoid potent steroids on face, groin and axillae"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a fingertip unit measuring about 5 g to the back only, since the unit is calculated for the trunk and larger areas need proportionally more"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Fingertip Unit (FTU) is the standardized metric for topical dermatological medications: 1 FTU is the amount of ointment/cream expressed from a standard 5 mm nozzle tube from the distal skin crease to the tip of an adult index finger (~0.5 g). One FTU adequately covers a surface area equivalent to TWO adult handprints (with fingers joined). Potent and super-potent topical corticosteroids (like clobetasol) carry serious risks of local adverse effects (epidermal atrophy, telangiectasia, striae, hypopigmentation, secondary infection) and systemic absorption (HPA axis suppression, Cushing syndrome). They are strictly avoided on high-absorption, thin-skinned areas such as the face, neck, groin, and axillae. Why the other options fail: option a wastes the tube and increases systemic absorption without improving the flare, and a whole-body application of a super-potent steroid is a route to adrenal suppression. Option b states the opposite of the guidance: thin sites absorb far more per unit area, so the face, groin and axillae are exactly where atrophy and striae appear first. Option d inflates the unit tenfold, which turns a measure of 0.5 g into 5 g per application.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Topical steroid dosing: 1 FTU = 0.5 g = 2 adult handprints. Use sparingly, short-term. Avoid potent steroids on face, groin, and axillae (atrophy risk).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A thin semi-permeable film dressing, which seals bacteria out and lets exudate evaporate through the membrane between changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dry single-ply cotton gauze, changed whenever it becomes saturated, because cotton wicks exudate away from the bed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A hydrocolloid sheet changed every three days, since it forms a gel that absorbs exudate and protects the wound"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Calcium alginate or hydrofiber rope/pad dressings covered by an absorbent secondary foam dressing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dressing selection must match wound exudate levels according to the moisture balance principle. Calcium alginates (derived from seaweed) and hydrofiber dressings are highly absorbent, capable of absorbing 15–20 times their weight in exudate. When contacting wound fluid, sodium ions in exudate exchange with calcium ions in the alginate, forming a hydrophilic non-adherent gel that traps bacteria, maintains moisture balance, and prevents periwound maceration. In contrast, thin film dressings have zero absorption capacity and would trap fluid causing massive maceration. Dry gauze adheres to the wound and traumatizes new capillary buds upon removal. Why the other options fail: option a is the dressing for a wound with no exudate at all; a film has no absorption capacity, so fluid pools beneath it and macerates the skin it was meant to protect. Option b looks absorbent and is not: gauze holds little, dries out, and adheres to a granulating bed, so every change strips new tissue. Option c is right for moderate exudate and overwhelmed by copious drainage, and a three-day interval with pads saturating in two hours guarantees leakage.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dressing selection: Heavy exudate = Calcium alginate or Hydrofiber + Foam. Dry/necrotic wound = Hydrogel. Minimal drainage = Thin film / Hydrocolloid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 58-year-old client with chronic stable angina who reports dull retrosternal discomfort relieved by sublingual nitroglycerin 30 minutes ago"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 42-year-old client with type 1 diabetes whose pre-meal capillary blood glucose is 12.2 mmol/L (220 mg/dL) awaiting scheduled subcutaneous insulin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 16-year-old with acute asthma whose wheezing has stopped, who is restless, gasping and unable to speak in sentences"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 68-year-old client post-operative day 3 from elective knee replacement requesting assistance to walk to the bathroom"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Using the Airway, Breathing, Circulation (ABC) and acute-versus-chronic prioritization frameworks, the client with acute asthma whose wheezing has stopped (the 'silent chest' phenomenon) has impending fatal respiratory failure. The cessation of wheezing in a severely dyspneic, restless asthmatic client reflects severe airway constriction, muscular fatigue, and critical hypoventilation—an immediate airway and breathing emergency requiring rapid bronchodilator nebulization, oxygen, and preparation for emergent endotracheal intubation. The other three clients represent chronic stable conditions or non-immediate needs. Why the other options fail: option a describes angina that has already been relieved, which is a stable pattern rather than the most urgent problem. Option b is a glucose of 12.2 mmol/L awaiting a scheduled dose, high but not immediately life-threatening. Option d is a routine request for help to walk on post-operative day 3. None of the three carries an airway or breathing threat.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prioritization rule: Acute ABC threats outrank chronic stable conditions. A silent chest in severe asthma indicates imminent asphyxiation and respiratory arrest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "SBAR: state the respiratory compromise, the surgical history, the vital-sign trend, and a clear request for immediate review"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A narrative account of the shift so far, given in the order the events happened, and ending with the vital signs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A handwritten memo left in the registrar's pigeonhole, to be read at the start of the next working day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A message passed on through a colleague, asking the registrar to call the ward when it is convenient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) is the validated, standard healthcare communication framework designed to deliver critical, concise, and actionable clinical information between professionals during urgent clinical deterioration: (1) Situation: Introduce oneself, client, unit, and the immediate problem (e.g., acute tachypnoea and tachycardia); (2) Background: Relevant admission diagnosis, surgical date, and baseline status; (3) Assessment: Current objective findings, vital sign trends, and clinical judgment; and (4) Recommendation: Explicit request for action (e.g., 'I need you to evaluate this client at the bedside immediately'). Why the other options fail: option b narrates the shift in chronological order and buries the vital signs at the end, which is the opposite of what an urgent call needs. Option c puts the request in writing for the next day, which is useless in a deterioration happening now. Option d hands the urgency to a third party and asks for a call at the registrar's convenience.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical escalation: SBAR framework standardizes urgent communication. Rising respiratory rate is the earliest and most sensitive predictor of arrest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Performing the initial comprehensive admission assessment on a newly arrived heart failure client"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Providing discharge dietary teaching regarding a low-sodium diet to a diabetic hypertensive client"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Evaluating the efficacy of intravenous morphine administered 30 minutes ago for severe acute pancreatitis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Measure and record routine vital signs and the volume drained from an indwelling catheter in a stable client"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The registered nurse cannot delegate tasks requiring nursing clinical judgment, critical thinking, or professional evaluation. The classic 'E-A-T' mnemonic reminds nurses that the RN must never delegate: Evaluation, Assessment, or Teaching. Collecting routine objective data (e.g., vital signs, measuring urine output from a drainage bag) and assisting with basic Activities of Daily Living on stable clients are within the UAP's role. Performing admission assessments, formulating nursing care plans, client education, and evaluating medication efficacy are exclusive legal responsibilities of the licensed professional nurse. Why the other options fail: option a is an initial assessment, which only the registered nurse can perform. Option b is teaching, which the nurse cannot delegate. Option c is evaluation of a drug's effect, which requires nursing judgement about efficacy. All three fall under the assess-evaluate-teach exclusion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delegation rules: Do NOT delegate 'E-A-T' (Evaluation, Assessment, Teaching). UAP can perform routine vital signs, hygiene, and output measurement on STABLE clients.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A newly admitted unstable client presenting with diabetic ketoacidosis on an intravenous regular insulin titration infusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A stable client 3 days after laparoscopic cholecystectomy needing routine oral medicines and a dry dressing change"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A critically ill trauma client receiving ongoing massive blood transfusions and invasive arterial monitoring"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A client newly diagnosed with acute leukemia requiring initial chemotherapy education and psychological crisis intervention"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The scope of practice for the LPN/LVN focuses on providing routine care for stable clients with predictable clinical outcomes under the supervision of an RN. LPNs/LVNs can administer oral, subcutaneous, and intramuscular medications (in most jurisdictions, excluding IV push high-alert drugs or complex titration infusions), perform sterile dressing changes on healing wounds, monitor findings, and perform routine catheter care. Unstable clients, complex IV titration infusions (e.g., DKA insulin titrations), initial clinical assessments, and complex discharge/chemotherapy teaching must be managed directly by the RN. Why the other options fail: option a is an unstable client on a titrated insulin infusion, which needs the registered nurse. Option c is a trauma client on massive transfusion with invasive monitoring, which needs critical care competence. Option d needs initial chemotherapy teaching and crisis intervention, which the licensed practical nurse cannot provide.$q$,
  strategy = $q$LPN/LVN scope: Stable clients with predictable outcomes. Administer routine oral/IM meds and perform standard wound dressings. RN takes unstable clients and IV titrations.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the transfer and correct the assistant in front of the client and visitors, so that everyone learns from the mistake"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Step in at once and help with the transfer using a gait belt and safe technique, then teach the assistant in private"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Say nothing at the time and raise it at the assistant's next annual appraisal, to avoid undermining her confidence"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to say if the arm hurts, and record the incident in the ward diary at the end of the shift"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Supervision is the fifth right of delegation and requires active monitoring of delegated care and immediate intervention when client safety is jeopardized. Pulling on a hemiplegic arm risks shoulder joint dislocation (subluxation) and severe brachial plexus traction injury. The nurse's immediate priority is client safety: intervene calmly and physically assist with the transfer using proper biomechanics and gait belt. Once the client is safe and settled, the nurse provides private, professional, constructive feedback and re-education regarding transfer techniques. Why the other options fail: option a corrects the assistant in public, which damages the working relationship and the client's confidence in both of them. Option c defers the correction for two weeks while the unsafe practice continues. Option d shifts responsibility for safety onto the client, who cannot prevent a shoulder injury.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Supervisory intervention: Protect client safety FIRST by stepping in immediately -> Provide corrective instruction in PRIVATE (never embarrass in public).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Laissez-faire: each team member works from their own judgement, which keeps the team flexible when the situation changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Democratic: a quick show of hands decides the next intervention, so that everyone is committed to the plan"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transactional: each member is assigned a task and rewarded for completing it, which is what fixes the roles"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autocratic leadership: in an arrest, rapid decisions and fixed roles save lives, and debate has no place until it is over"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Leadership styles are situational: (1) Autocratic (authoritarian) leadership is characterized by centralized decision-making, top-down directives, and strict task allocation. While suboptimal for routine team morale, it is the most effective and essential leadership style in acute resuscitations, cardiac arrests, trauma codes, and disasters where immediate, synchronized, decisive execution is necessary to preserve life. (2) Democratic leadership promotes shared governance and consensus-building, ideal for unit quality improvement. (3) Transformational leadership inspires long-term innovation and organizational growth. (4) Laissez-faire provides minimal direction, leading to chaos in emergencies. Why the other options fail: option a leaves the team to improvise in an arrest, which produces duplicated and missed actions. Option b puts a vote in the middle of a resuscitation, when decisions are needed in seconds. Option c describes a transactional assignment of tasks, which does not capture the directive style an arrest requires.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Leadership styles: Autocratic = Emergency/cardiac arrest (rapid decisive direction). Democratic = Quality improvement/team consensus. Transformational = Vision/change.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the available antihypertensives, call the district hospital, arrange an escorted ambulance, and complete the referral form"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep her at the CHPS compound on bed rest, repeat the blood pressure in 24 hours, and send for magnesium if it rises"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discharge her home with a referral letter and ask her to attend the district hospital if a seizure begins"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give magnesium sulphate if any can be found in the store, and deliver the baby at the compound if she convulses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the tiered health system (CHPS -> Health Centre -> District Hospital -> Regional/Teaching Hospital), primary care nurses must promptly identify conditions that exceed the structural and legal scope of their facility. Severe pre-eclampsia carries immediate risks of eclamptic convulsions, stroke, and maternal/fetal mortality. Effective referral coordination mandates: (1) prompt clinical stabilization within facility capability (e.g., initial oral labetalol/nifedipine or loading magnesium sulphate if trained and equipped); (2) early notification of the receiving facility ('warm handover' call ahead); (3) securing dedicated transport with a trained clinical escort; and (4) complete handover documentation. Why the other options fail: option b keeps a woman with severe pre-eclampsia at a facility that cannot treat her and reviews her a day later. Option c sends her home to travel during a seizure. Option d proposes treatment beyond both the facility's supply and its scope of practice.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Health system referral: Stabilize within capability -> Call receiving hospital ahead -> Arrange emergency transport with clinical escort -> Document handover.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A septic shock client on three titrated inotropes who is stable at present but needs frequent titration"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A stable client awaiting ward transfer who needs core medical-surgical skills, with an ICU nurse as resource"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the floated nurse one stable ICU client and ask her to manage the ventilator settings independently"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Send her home if no ICU-trained nurse can supervise her, and cover the shift with the remaining staff"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$When allocating assignments to a 'floating' nurse, the charge nurse must evaluate the nurse's competencies, licensure, and experience relative to client acuity. A floated medical-surgical nurse has core competencies in medication administration, assessment, and routine post-operative/medical care, but lacks specialized training in critical care technologies (e.g., CRRT, intracranial pressure monitoring, complex vasoactive inotrope titration). The charge nurse should assign the most stable, low-acuity clients on the unit (e.g., stable 'step-down' clients awaiting transfer) and designate a permanent unit nurse as a mentor and clinical resource. Why the other options fail: option a gives the floated nurse an unstable client on three inotropes, which is beyond her competence and unsafe for the client. Option c asks her to manage ventilator settings independently. Option d removes a pair of hands from the unit and leaves it below minimum staffing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Floating nurse assignment: Match assignment to core competencies. Assign the most STABLE clients with predictable needs. Never assign high-acuity specialized devices.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Avoiding: the manager separates the nurses onto different shifts, so the disagreement no longer delays the rounds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collaborating: both nurses examine the workload and co-design a joint schedule, so that neither loses and patients gain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Competing: the senior nurse sets the schedule herself and tells the junior to follow it without discussion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Accommodating: the junior nurse lets the senior have her way, and raises it again if the delay recurs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the Thomas-Kilmann Conflict Mode Instrument, five modes exist: Competing, Accommodating, Avoiding, Compromising, and Collaborating. Collaborating represents high assertiveness and high cooperation (the 'win-win' approach). Both parties work together to explore mutual concerns, identify root problems, and create an integrative solution where neither party compromises core clinical standards. It fosters trust, enhances teamwork, and yields long-lasting resolutions in healthcare settings. Why the other options fail: option a avoids the conflict by separating the nurses, so the underlying scheduling problem stays. Option c is competing: one nurse wins and the other complies under threat, which does not fix the workflow. Option d is accommodating: one nurse gives way and the delays continue.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Conflict resolution: Collaborating = Win-Win. Joint problem-solving to address root concerns and create an optimal integrated solution.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the observations confidentially to the nurse manager at once, so that clients are protected and she can be helped"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confront her privately and insist on a urine drug screen before she takes another shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Say nothing until there is proof, so that a colleague is not wrongly accused of diverting drugs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the other staff on the ward quietly, so that they check her narcotic counts after every shift"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Impaired nursing practice due to substance use disorder directly threatens client safety and patient outcomes. Warning signs include: mood changes, frequent bathroom breaks, offering to medicate colleagues' patients for pain, discrepancies in controlled drug counts, and signs of intoxication (miosis, somnolence, ataxia). The nurse's paramount ethical, legal, and professional duty is patient protection. The nurse must NOT ignore, confront in anger, or cover up for the colleague. The nurse must report the objective, factual observations promptly to the nurse manager or nursing supervisor through institutional reporting channels. Why the other options fail: option b confronts and demands a sample, which risks a scene on the ward and is not the nurse's role. Option c waits for proof and leaves clients exposed to a colleague who is diverting opioids now. Option d broadcasts the suspicion, which destroys the colleague's reputation and the investigation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Impaired practice: Immediate confidential reporting to the nurse manager/supervisor is mandatory. Client protection is the #1 ethical duty.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the client to sign now, and have the surgeon answer her questions when he sees her in the anaesthetic room"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain the surgery and its risks yourself, then witness the signature once she says she is satisfied"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Witness the signature on the client's behalf and record that the surgeon explained the risks at the bedside"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Do not witness the form: tell the surgeon the client does not understand and needs further explanation before signing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Informed consent is the legal and ethical responsibility of the practitioner performing the procedure (the surgeon/physician). The surgeon must disclose the nature of the surgery, specific risks, anticipated benefits, alternatives, and consequences of refusal. The nurse's role in informed consent is strictly limited to: (1) witnessing the client's signature; (2) verifying that the client has decisional capacity; and (3) confirming that consent is voluntary. If the client expresses lack of understanding, the nurse must NOT explain the surgical procedure or obtain signature; the nurse must withhold witnessing and notify the surgeon to return and provide full informed disclosure. Why the other options fail: option a obtains a signature that is not informed, which is the situation the nurse is there to prevent. Option b has the nurse give the surgical explanation, which belongs to the surgeon and exceeds the nursing role. Option c signs on the client's behalf, which is forgery and voids the consent.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Informed consent roles: Physician/surgeon EXPLAINS procedure, risks, and alternatives. Nurse WITNESSES signature and confirms voluntary consent. If client has doubts -> Call surgeon.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start compressions at once, because a family member present at the arrest can override a written DNR order"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Honour the DNR order, start no CPR or defibrillation, and support the daughter calmly and privately in her grief"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Run a slow code: assemble the team and go through the motions of resuscitation without chest compressions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask security to remove the daughter from the unit, then start resuscitation once she has gone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A valid Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNR) order and advance directive represent the legally binding autonomous choices of a competent individual. Family members cannot legally revoke or override a capacitous adult's prior living will or DNR order when cardiac arrest occurs. Initiating CPR against a valid DNR violates client autonomy and constitutes battery. Performing a 'slow code' (faking resuscitation) is deceitful, unethical, and legally impermissible. The nurse must uphold the client's documented autonomous wishes, gently withhold resuscitation, support the family through the acute shock of bereavement, and contact the palliative or chaplaincy team. Why the other options fail: option a lets a relative override a valid written order at the bedside, which no relative can do. Option c performs a sham resuscitation, which deceives the family and is professionally indefensible. Option d removes the daughter so that CPR can start, which ignores the order entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DNR & Advance Directives: Legally valid DNR orders MUST be honoured. Family wishes cannot override a patient's documented advance directive. No 'slow codes'.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Non-maleficence (the duty to do no harm)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Distributive justice (the fair allocation of healthcare resources)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Veracity (the ethical duty to tell the truth and not intentionally deceive clients)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Paternalism (the belief that health workers always know best regardless of client views)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The foundational bioethical principles in nursing are: (1) Veracity: The moral obligation to tell the truth, be transparent, and avoid deception or intentional omission. The client has the right to know what medication is being administered and its intended purpose. (2) Autonomy: The right of self-determination and to accept or refuse treatment. (3) Beneficence: Doing good and acting in the client's best interest. (4) Non-maleficence: The duty to do no harm. (5) Justice: Fairness in distribution of care. (6) Fidelity: Faithfulness to commitments and promises. Why the other options fail: option a is the duty to avoid harm, and the question is specifically about telling the truth. Option b concerns fair distribution of resources, which is not at issue. Option d is the opposite of the principle being tested, because it would justify withholding the information.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bioethics definitions: Veracity = Truth-telling. Autonomy = Self-determination. Beneficence = Doing good. Non-maleficence = Do no harm. Fidelity = Keeping promises.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Duty, negligence, foreseeability and a written expert opinion that the standard of care was not met"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Breach of duty, injury, a complaint made within 1 year, and a finding by the hospital's quality committee"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Duty of care, breach of the standard of care, causation linking the breach to the injury, and actual damages"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Duty, breach, causation and a formal apology from the nurse to the family"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In healthcare law, establishing professional negligence (nursing malpractice) requires proving four essential legal elements: (1) Duty of care: A formal professional nurse-patient relationship existed; (2) Breach of duty: The nurse failed to conform to the accepted professional standard of care (did what a reasonably prudent nurse would not do, or failed to do what a prudent nurse would do); (3) Causation (proximate cause): The nurse's breach was the direct, foreseeable cause of the client's injury; and (4) Damages / Harm: The client suffered actual physical, psychological, or economic injury. Why the other options fail: option a lists criminal concepts that have no place in a negligence claim. Option b mixes administrative steps with the elements, and neither a time limit nor a committee finding creates liability. Option d substitutes an apology for damages, and a sympathetic gesture is not proof of harm.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Malpractice elements (the 4 Ds): Duty -> Dereliction (breach) -> Direct causation -> Damages. All four must be proven.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A blameless multidisciplinary review of the system: packaging, storage, ordering and checking, not the individual nurse"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Identify the nurse who prepared the dose and take disciplinary action, which shows that the standard is enforced"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict the investigation to the pharmacy, since the packaging and storage of the drug are its responsibility"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Require a second nurse to check every intravenous drug for 6 months, then review whether errors have fallen"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A Just Culture balances individual accountability with a non-punitive focus on systemic latent errors. When a sentinel event occurs, conducting a Root Cause Analysis (RCA) seeks to answer 'What happened? Why did it happen? What systems can be redesigned to prevent recurrence?' Studies demonstrate that over 85–90% of medical errors stem from faulty system designs (e.g., concentrated potassium stocked on general wards, look-alike ampoules, unverified verbal orders, noisy medication rooms) rather than reckless personal sabotage. Punitive scapegoating drives errors underground, destroying safety reporting cultures. Why the other options fail: option b is the blame response that Just Culture exists to replace, and it leaves the packaging unchanged. Option c protects the organisation's records instead of the next patient and conceals the evidence the analysis needs. Option d moves the task to another group without changing the system that allowed the overdose.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Just culture & Root Cause Analysis: System-level focus on latent vulnerabilities (storage, packaging, workflows) rather than individual blame. Drives safety improvement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Note in the progress record that an incident report was filed, so that the next shift knows the event is being investigated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "File a copy of the incident report in the chart, so that the clinical and safety records stay together"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the allergy out of the record until the investigation is complete, so that the entries cannot be used in court"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Record the drug given, the assessment, the prescriber's notification and the treatment; never mention the incident report in the chart"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Incident (variance/occurrence) reports are internal, confidential administrative tools used by hospital risk management and quality improvement to identify safety vulnerabilities. In the patient's medical record, the nurse documents only objective, clinical facts: drug given, client physical findings, notification of prescriber, orders received, client response, and ongoing monitoring. Crucially, the nurse must NEVER record in the clinical chart that an incident report was completed or filed (e.g., 'Incident report completed'). Mentioning the report in the clinical chart can compromise attorney-client privilege and makes the internal risk document discoverable in legal litigation. Why the other options fail: option a mentions the report in the clinical record, which makes a privileged internal document discoverable. Option b files the report in the chart for the same reason, and mixes two records with different purposes. Option c removes the allergy from the record, which is falsification and would endanger the client if the drug were offered again.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Incident report rules: Document objective facts, client assessment, and treatments in medical record. NEVER mention or file the incident report in the patient chart.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A recorded voice note at the desk, replayed by the incoming nurse when the ward is quiet"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A written summary left in the Kardex, with the incoming nurse reading it before the round"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A verbal report in the treatment room, kept short so that neither nurse is away from the ward too long"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bedside handover with SBAR, so both nurses see the client and lines together and the client can take part"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bedside shift handover is an established patient safety practice endorsed by accreditation bodies (Joint Commission, WHO). Performing handover at the bedside: (1) enables direct visualization of the client, checking IV infusion rates, surgical dressings, and drain outputs simultaneously; (2) uses standardized communication tools (SBAR); (3) allows immediate two-way verification and clarifying questions between outgoing and incoming nurses; and (4) engages the patient and family as active partners in their care plan, eliminating discrepancies. Why the other options fail: option a gives a recorded message with no chance to ask questions and no view of the client. Option b leaves the incoming nurse to read a document alone, without the outgoing nurse's knowledge of what changed. Option c keeps the report short by holding it away from the bedside, where the lines and the client cannot be checked.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Handoff best practice: Bedside shift report using SBAR. Allows direct visualization of patient/lines, two-way questioning, and patient engagement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Population: the adult ICU clients with non-tunneled central venous catheters who are being studied"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Comparison: the standard non-medicated transparent film dressing already in use on the unit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intervention: the procedure or bundle being tested, such as chlorhexidine-impregnated dressings"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Outcome: the incidence of laboratory-confirmed CLABSI per 1,000 central line days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The PICO(T) format is the standard methodological framework for structuring clinical questions in Evidence-Based Practice (EBP): (1) P = Patient / Population / Problem (the clinical group of interest); (2) I = Intervention / Indicator (the new treatment, procedure, exposure, or clinical bundle being evaluated); (3) C = Comparison / Control (the current standard of practice or alternative intervention); (4) O = Outcome (the measurable clinical result desired); and (5) T = Timeframe (optional duration of evaluation). Why the other options fail: option a is the P of PICO, the population. Option b is the C, the comparison already in use. Option d is the O, the measured outcome. None of them names the intervention being tested.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PICO format: P = Population, I = Intervention, C = Comparison, O = Outcome. Formulates structured search queries for evidence-based practice.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Unfreezing stage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Moving (changing) stage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refreezing stage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stagnation stage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Kurt Lewin's classic planned change theory consists of three sequential stages: (1) Unfreezing: Overcoming inertia and resistance by breaking down existing mindsets, establishing a sense of urgency, sharing safety audit data, and demonstrating why change is vital; (2) Moving (Changing): Executing the planned change through education, coaching, workflow pilot testing, and implementing new behaviors (e.g., scanning barcodes); and (3) Refreezing: Integrating the new practice into permanent organizational culture through ongoing audits, policies, praise, and performance standards. Why the other options fail: option b is where the change is executed, not where the need is created. Option c is where the new practice is embedded after the change, not where it starts. Option d is not one of Lewin's stages at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lewin's change theory: 1. Unfreezing (create urgency, break old habits) -> 2. Moving (implement change, train) -> 3. Refreezing (institutionalize and sustain).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the visitor sitting nearest the desk to keep an eye on the screen until you come back"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Write the password on a note beside the monitor, so that the next nurse can log in quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Log off or lock the terminal immediately, so that protected health information cannot be seen by anyone else"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Photograph the results on your phone so that you can review them while you answer the call bell"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Protection of digital health data under data protection acts and privacy legislation (e.g., Ghana Data Protection Act 2012 / Act 843, HIPAA) is a strict professional and legal requirement. Leaving an active, logged-in computer workstation unattended exposes confidential patient health data to unauthorized visitors, staff, and other clients. The nurse must lock or log off the terminal screen every time they step away, even momentarily. Passwords must never be shared or displayed, and photographing patient records on personal mobile devices is a gross privacy violation. Why the other options fail: option a hands custody of the screen to a visitor, who has no authority to see it. Option b exposes the password to anyone who passes the desk. Option d copies protected information onto a personal device, which is a disclosure as well as a security breach.$q$,
  strategy = $q$EHR security: Always log off or lock terminals before stepping away. Never share passwords or post patient data on mobile devices.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-LEAD-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client lacks financial insight and is rapidly depleting the family's life savings"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The family can no longer cope with the client's behaviour at home and requests admission now"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client has stopped taking prescribed oral antipsychotic medication against medical advice"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A confirmed mental disorder with imminent risk of serious harm to self or others, or severe self-neglect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under Ghana's Mental Health Act 2012 (Act 846) and standard bioethical principles, involuntary admission overrides an individual's civil liberties and is legally justified ONLY when: (1) the person has a recognized mental disorder, and (2) as a result of that disorder, presents an imminent risk of serious physical harm to self (e.g., suicide or reckless life-threatening behavior) or to others, or is incapable of caring for basic survival needs. Refusing medication, squandering money, or family distress alone does not fulfill legal criteria for involuntary confinement. Why the other options fail: option a rests on financial behaviour, which is not a statutory ground for detention. Option b rests on the family's capacity to cope, which is a social problem rather than a legal criterion. Option c treats non-adherence alone as sufficient, when the Act requires a disorder plus risk or severe self-neglect.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Involuntary psychiatric commitment requires: Mental disorder + IMMINENT RISK OF HARM TO SELF OR OTHERS. Financial waste or eccentric behavior alone does not justify depriving a patient of freedom.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Summon security at once and apply four-point physical restraints to the bed rails"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confront the client firmly and warn that shouting will cost him his ward privileges"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give rapid intramuscular sedation without attempting any verbal engagement first"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stand at a safe distance, speak calmly and low, acknowledge the distress and offer a quiet area"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Least Restrictive Care dictates that verbal de-escalation must always be attempted before physical or chemical restraints. The nurse should: (1) Maintain personal safety by standing at an angle outside the client's personal space (at least two arm's lengths away) with hands visible and uncrossed; (2) Speak in a calm, controlled, non-threatening, low-pitched voice; (3) Validate the client's emotional distress ('I see that you are very upset'); and (4) Offer to move together to a low-stimulus environment. Confrontation escalates violence, while physical restraint is an emergency measure of last resort. Why the other options fail: options a and c jump to restraint and sedation, which the least-restrictive principle reserves for imminent violence after verbal measures fail. Option b confronts and threatens, which predictably escalates agitation rather than reducing it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$De-escalation hierarchy: Verbal de-escalation & low-stimulus environment FIRST -> PRN oral medication -> Seclusion/Physical restraint ONLY as an absolute last resort when imminent violence occurs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "You have a wonderful family who loves you dearly; you shouldn't think that way at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "You are feeling overwhelmed and worthless right now. Are you thinking about ending your life?"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Why do you believe you have failed your family? Everything will look brighter tomorrow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I understand exactly how you feel; I felt the same when I went through hard times"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Therapeutic communication acknowledges and reflects the client's feelings without judgment, minimization, or false reassurance, while directly addressing suicide risk. When a client expresses hopeless thoughts ('everyone would be better off if I were dead'), the nurse must directly assess suicidal ideation ('Are you thinking about ending your life?'). Why the other options fail: option a minimises the feeling and offers false reassurance. Option c opens with a why question that implies blame and adds another reassurance. Option d turns the exchange onto the nurse's own experience instead of the client's.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NCLEX Rule for Suicide Assessment: ALWAYS ask DIRECT, explicit questions about suicidal intent. Acknowledge feelings and never give false reassurance ('everything will be fine') or ask 'why' questions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assign a nursing assistant to carry out fifteen-minute scheduled visual safety checks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the prescribed oral lorazepam so that the client settles and sleeps tonight"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on continuous one-to-one observation within arm's reach and remove all hazards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to sign a written no-harm safety contract and document it in the chart"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a client communicates explicit, active suicidal intent with a plan, the client requires immediate, continuous One-to-One (1:1) direct visual observation. The staff member must remain within arm's length at all times, including during bathroom and hygiene routines. The environment must be thoroughly searched to eliminate strangulation hazards (belts, shoelaces, bedsheets, cords), glass, plastic bags, and sharps. Periodic 15-minute checks leave unsupervised windows where suicide can occur. 'No-harm contracts' have no proven efficacy in preventing suicide in high-risk patients. Why the other options fail: Fifteen-minute visual checks leave unobserved windows a determined client can use; sedation removes neither the means nor the intent; a written no-harm contract has no evidence of effectiveness and is no substitute for continuous observation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Active suicidal intent + plan = MANDATORY 1:1 continuous visual observation within arm's reach. 15-minute checks are unsafe for actively suicidal patients.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I do not hear any voices, but I see that you are frightened. What are the voices telling you?"}, {"id": "b", "text": "There is nobody in that corner shouting at you; you are imagining the whole thing again"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Why don't you simply tell the voices to go away and leave you alone for a while?"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I hear the shouting too, but we are quite safe here in the hospital so do not worry"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When responding to auditory hallucinations, the nurse should: (1) Validate the client's reality and feeling while stating the nurse's reality ('I do not hear any voices, but I see that you are frightened'), and (2) Immediately assess for COMMAND hallucinations ('What are the voices telling you to do?'). Command hallucinations instructing self-harm or violence represent a psychiatric emergency. The nurse must never validate the hallucination as real, argue with or mock the client, or give advice to talk to the voices. Why the other options fail: option b denies the experience outright and damages the trust needed for assessment. Option c hands the problem to the client and skips the command-content assessment. Option d colludes with the hallucination, which reinforces it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hallucination protocol: Present reality calmly ('I don't hear them') + Validate emotion ('I see you are frightened') + ASSESS CONTENT ('What are they telling you to do?') to rule out command hallucinations.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Taste a spoonful of the food in front of the client to prove that it is not poisoned"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain that the kitchen staff are trained professionals who would never poison food"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acknowledge the fear without arguing, and offer factory-sealed foods the client can open"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withhold all food until the client accepts that the delusion is irrational and unfounded"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Delusions are fixed, false beliefs that cannot be corrected by logical reasoning, debate, or proof. Arguing or trying to 'prove' the food is safe (e.g., tasting it) reinforces the delusional dynamic or suggests the nurse is in collusion. The therapeutic approach is to validate the underlying emotion ('It must be terrifying to feel unsafe about what you eat') without validating the false belief, and offer pragmatic solutions such as unopened, factory-sealed packages (e.g., unopened cans, bread, fruit in peels) that the client can inspect and open personally. Why the other options fail: Tasting the food colludes with the delusion and may deepen it; telling the client that the staff would never poison food argues with a fixed belief that cannot be reasoned away; withholding food until the delusion breaks risks starvation and destroys trust.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Managing delusions of poisoning: Do not argue or debate. Offer factory-sealed, unopened foods/containers so the client feels safe eating.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that the client sit quietly at the dining table for thirty minutes at every meal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Engage the client in competitive group sports in the recreation hall to burn off energy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Offer high-calorie, high-protein finger foods and fortified drinks he can take while walking"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the client in four-point physical restraints until the hyperactivity ceases"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$During an acute manic phase, clients experience extreme hyperactivity, shortened attention span, and poor nutritional intake, placing them at severe risk of physical exhaustion, severe dehydration, and cardiovascular collapse. The priority physiological intervention is providing frequent, high-calorie, high-protein 'finger foods' (sandwiches, boiled eggs, fruit, cheese sticks) and fortified fluids that the client can consume on the move without sitting. Competitive sports are overstimulating and provoke aggression; insisting on sitting at a table causes escalation. Why the other options fail: Demanding stillness at every meal asks for a control a manic client cannot sustain and escalates the behaviour; competitive sport adds stimulation and injury risk to an already exhausted client; four-point restraint treats mania as if it were violence and leaves the exhaustion untreated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Manic patient care: High-calorie finger foods + Portable drinks + Low-stimulus private room. Manic clients can die from physical exhaustion and dehydration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The antidepressant has finally worked and the depressive episode is now completely cured"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client is at imminent risk of suicide, because energy has returned before hopelessness lifted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client has switched into a hypomanic episode and needs lithium titrated this evening"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client is recovered enough for immediate discharge to outpatient community follow-up"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A sudden, dramatic lift in mood, uncharacteristic cheerfulness, and giving away prized possessions in a severely depressed client (especially shortly after starting antidepressants or during early recovery) is a classic warning sign of impending suicide. When severe depression begins to lift, the patient's psychomotor retardation improves before mood improves; the patient now possesses the physical energy and cognitive focus required to execute a previously formulated suicide plan. Giving away possessions signifies finalizing personal affairs. Suicide precautions must be intensified immediately. Why the other options fail: option a misreads the lift in mood, which precedes recovery rather than proving it. Option c is a differential, but the timing and the giving away of possessions point to suicide risk, which must be acted on first. Option d discharges a client at the point of highest risk.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NCLEX Classic Trap: Sudden improvement in depression + giving away possessions = HIGHEST RISK OF SUICIDE. Energy returned before hopelessness resolved.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stay with the client, speak in short calm sentences, and coach slow diaphragmatic breathing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave the client alone in a quiet, empty side room to reduce sensory stimulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain the neurobiology of the sympathetic fight-or-flight response in some detail"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client firmly that the physical sensations are harmless and to snap out of it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$During panic-level anxiety, perceptual fields are drastically narrowed, cognitive processing is severely impaired, and the client feels imminent doom. The nurse must: (1) STAY with the client (never leave a panicked client alone; physical presence provides safety), (2) Speak in short, simple, calm phrases, (3) Reduce ambient noise and lighting, and (4) Model and coach slow, deep diaphragmatic breathing to counter respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. Detailed psychoeducation is impossible during panic and should occur only after anxiety decreases to mild levels. Why the other options fail: option b abandons a terrified client, which intensifies panic. Option c teaches at a moment when processing is narrowed to nothing. Option d dismisses the experience and tells the client to control what he cannot yet control.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe panic intervention: STAY with the patient + Short, calm sentences + Breathe with them. Never leave them alone and never attempt teaching during panic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lock the bathroom door before meals to extinguish the handwashing behaviour immediately"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confront the client with the irrationality of spending forty-five minutes washing his hands"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Require the client to clean the ward kitchen whenever handwashing exceeds five minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Allow time for the ritual at first, then agree limits as coping strategies are learned"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Compulsive rituals in OCD serve as a psychological defense mechanism to neutralize overwhelming anxiety generated by intrusive obsessive thoughts. Abruptly blocking or forbidding the ritual in the early stages of hospitalization triggers panic-level anxiety. The nurse should initially accommodate the ritual by planning the schedule around it (e.g., starting earlier), ensure skin protection, and establish a trusting relationship. Subsequently, the multidisciplinary team works collaboratively with the client to gradually limit ritual time (exposure and response prevention) while teaching alternative anxiety-reduction strategies. Why the other options fail: option a blocks the ritual abruptly and triggers panic-level anxiety. Option b confronts the client with the irrationality of a compulsion, which does not respond to argument. Option c punishes the symptom and damages the alliance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$OCD initial rule: Allow time for the ritual early in admission to prevent panic, then gradually negotiate limits while teaching new coping mechanisms. Never abruptly stop rituals on Day 1.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Allow the client to eat meals alone in her bedroom to reduce social anxiety"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Offer high-calorie milkshakes whenever she requests them between midnight and dawn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Observe her directly during meals and for one hour after, with bathroom visits supervised"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Weigh the client three times a day, immediately after each meal, on the same scale"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the inpatient management of severe anorexia nervosa, clients frequently experience overwhelming guilt following food consumption and may attempt to purge (vomiting), discard food in napkins, or engage in vigorous compensatory exercise. Direct nursing supervision is mandatory during meals (typically 30–45 minutes) and for at least 60 minutes postprandially. Bathrooms must be locked or supervised to prevent vomiting or water-loading. Daily weighing is conducted once in the morning upon waking, after voiding, wearing only a hospital gown, not after meals. Why the other options fail: Eating alone in her bedroom removes the supervision that prevents hiding or purging; unmeasured milkshakes at night add calories outside the plan and disrupt refeeding; weighing after every meal measures intake rather than trend and feeds the preoccupation with weight.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anorexia postprandial rule: Observe client during meal AND for 1 hour after meal to prevent vomiting/purging or hiding food.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I agree; the night nurse has a harsh manner that several patients have complained about"}, {"id": "b", "text": "You should be grateful that anyone on this unit is willing to care for you at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I will make sure the night nurse is never assigned to look after you again on any shift"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The whole team follows one agreed care plan; let us raise any concern about night care together"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clients with Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD) frequently use the primitive defense mechanism of 'splitting' (viewing people as entirely all-good or all-bad), which creates conflict, manipulation, and division among nursing staff. The therapeutic response to splitting is team solidarity and consistency: non-defensively stating that all staff adhere to a unified treatment plan, refusing to accept flattery that disparages colleagues, and redirecting the client to address concerns openly with the team. Never agree with the client's criticism of other staff members. Why the other options fail: option a joins the split and confirms the idealisation. Option b is punitive and shames the client for seeking care. Option c lets the client dictate staffing, which entrenches splitting rather than resolving it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Borderline splitting response: Staff consistency + Neutral boundary. State that all team members follow the same care plan; never take sides or validate idealization/devaluation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Postoperative sepsis from a paralytic ileus, with a fever and tachycardia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Alcohol withdrawal delirium, around forty-eight hours after the last drink"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute opioid withdrawal after stopping postoperative analgesia on day two"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Akathisia from an antiemetic, with restlessness and visual disturbance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Delirium Tremens (DTs) is a medical emergency that typically emerges 48 to 72 (up to 96) hours after the last alcoholic drink in alcohol-dependent individuals. Diagnostic features include autonomic hyperactivity (severe hypertension, tachycardia, fever, drenching sweats), gross coarse tremors, severe agitation, disorientation, and perceptual disturbances (vivid visual or tactile hallucinations, such as insects or animals). Untreated DTs has a mortality rate of up to 15% from cardiac arrhythmias or hyperthermia. Immediate treatment requires parenteral benzodiazepines (e.g., diazepam, lorazepam). Why the other options fail: option a does not explain the fever, hypertension and tactile hallucinations that appear together at forty-eight hours. Option c gives dilated pupils, cramps and craving, not fever with visual hallucinations. Option d gives motor restlessness alone, without autonomic storm or perceptual disturbance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alcohol withdrawal timeline: Tremors/anxiety at 6–12 hrs; Seizures at 12–48 hrs; Delirium Tremens (fever + tachycardia + tactile/visual hallucinations) at 48–72 hrs. Treatment: Benzodiazepines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dextrose neutralises thiamine and makes it therapeutically inert in the circulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Thiamine is needed to convert glucose into fat cells and prevent hypertriglyceridaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thiamine stimulates insulin secretion and so prevents diabetic ketoacidosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Glucose metabolism consumes the remaining thiamine, precipitating Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Thiamine (vitamin B1) is an essential cofactor for enzymes in glucose carbohydrate metabolism (pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase). In thiamine-deficient patients with alcohol use disorder, infusing intravenous glucose without thiamine stimulates rapid carbohydrate oxidation, which completely depletes the body's remaining trace thiamine stores. This triggers acute cerebral metabolic failure, hemorrhagic necrosis of the mamillary bodies, and irreversible Korsakoff psychosis (anterograde amnesia and confabulation) or fatal coma. Thiamine must always precede or accompany glucose. Why the other options fail: option a invents an interaction in which dextrose destroys thiamine. Option b invents a lipogenesis role that has nothing to do with the risk. Option c invents an insulin effect, when the danger is cerebral thiamine depletion during glucose metabolism.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alcoholism resuscitation rule: THIAMINE BEFORE DEXTROSE (T before D). Giving sugar first depletes B1 and destroys the mamillary bodies.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute onset, fluctuating course, inattention and a treatable cause such as infection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insidious onset over years, a steadily progressive course and no reversible cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prominent early visual hallucinations, whereas delirium spares perception entirely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Normal vital signs throughout, whereas dementia presents with severe hypertension"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The key clinical discriminators: (1) Delirium: ACUTE onset (hours to days), fluctuating course, impaired attention, altered level of consciousness (hyperactive or hypoactive), perceptual disturbances (hallucinations), and typically reversible once the underlying trigger (UTI, pneumonia, electrolyte disturbance, medication anticholinergic toxicity) is resolved; (2) Dementia: INSIDIOUS onset (months to years), progressive decline in memory and executive function, stable level of consciousness until late stages, and irreversible. Why the other options fail: option b describes dementia rather than delirium. Option c reverses the pattern, since visual hallucinations are characteristic of delirium. Option d invents a vital-sign rule that discriminates nothing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delirium vs Dementia: Delirium = ACUTE + FLUCTUATING + INATTENTION + REVERSIBLE (find the cause: UTI, sepsis, meds). Dementia = CHRONIC + GRADUAL + IRREVERSIBLE.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure the mother that postpartum blues are normal and will resolve by week two"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the infant with a responsible family member and arrange urgent psychiatric admission"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave mother and baby alone in a dark room to encourage maternal-infant bonding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recommend an over-the-counter herbal calming tea and review her again in one month"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The presentation (delusions involving the infant, paranoia, extreme insomnia, command thoughts of infanticide) represents Postpartum Psychosis, an acute psychiatric emergency affecting approximately 1 to 2 per 1,000 childbirths. It carries a severe risk of infanticide (up to 4%) and maternal suicide (up to 5%). The absolute priority is immediate physical separation of the infant into safe protective family care (never leave mother and infant alone) and emergent psychiatric hospitalization for antipsychotic and mood-stabilizing treatment. Why the other options fail: Attributing these symptoms to the blues miscasts a psychosis that the blues never include; leaving mother and baby alone in a dark room places the infant with a mother who is delusional about it; an unproven herbal tea with a review in a month leaves the psychosis untreated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Postpartum Psychosis = PSYCHIATRIC EMERGENCY. Delusions of infant harm/demonization carry high risk of infanticide and suicide. Protect the baby immediately and admit mother.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the dose at bedtime so that the child sleeps deeply and restfully through the night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give it in the morning after breakfast and monitor height, weight and blood pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Crush the extended-release tablet and mix it into a warm evening cocoa drink"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Double the dose at weekends whenever the child is especially boisterous and active"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Central nervous system stimulants (such as methylphenidate and dextroamphetamine) can cause anorexia, weight loss, growth suppression, insomnia, and tachycardia/hypertension. To prevent insomnia, stimulants should be administered in the morning with or immediately after breakfast (food helps minimize gastrointestinal distress and appetite suppression). Pediatric nurses must regularly plot height and weight on standardized growth charts and monitor cardiovascular parameters (blood pressure and heart rate). Crushing extended-release formulations is contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option a gives a stimulant at bedtime, which causes the insomnia it is meant to avoid. Option c destroys the extended-release mechanism and gives the dose at the wrong time. Option d doubles a controlled dose without an order.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ADHD stimulants (Methylphenidate): Give in morning with/after food (prevents insomnia & anorexia) + Monitor GROWTH (height & weight) and blood pressure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and the haloperidol must be stopped at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tardive dyskinesia, which calls for a lower maintenance dose of the haloperidol"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An acute dystonic reaction, which calls for intravenous procyclidine immediately"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Serotonin syndrome, which calls for cyproheptadine and aggressive active cooling"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) is a life-threatening idiosyncratic reaction to dopamine-receptor antagonists (antipsychotics, particularly typical agents like haloperidol and fluphenazine). Hallmark clinical tetrad: (1) Severe 'lead-pipe' muscle rigidity, (2) Hyperthermia (> 38°C, often > 40°C), (3) Autonomic instability (labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis, tachypnea), and (4) Altered mental status, accompanied by rhabdomyolysis (massively elevated serum CK) and acute kidney injury. Immediate action: STOP the neuroleptic immediately, initiate aggressive cooling and IV hydration, and transfer to ICU for dantrolene or bromocriptine therapy. Why the other options fail: option b is a late-onset movement disorder, without hyperthermia or rhabdomyolysis. Option c is muscle spasm and oculogyration without fever or a raised CK. Option d requires a serotonergic agent and gives hyperreflexia and clonus rather than lead-pipe rigidity.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) tetrad: FEVER = Fever (hyperthermia) + Encephalopathy + Vitals unstable + Elevated CK + Rigidity ('lead-pipe'). Action: STOP drug immediately!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lithium toxicity; hold the dose, notify the prescriber and draw an urgent lithium level"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mild expected side effects; advise the client to drink a strong cup of black tea"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute manic relapse; increase the lithium dose by three hundred milligrams daily"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyponatraemia; restrict all fluids until the sodium level returns to normal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Lithium has an extremely narrow therapeutic index (therapeutic maintenance: 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L). Early toxicity begins at levels > 1.5 mEq/L, presenting with gastrointestinal distress (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea), coarse hand tremors (contrasted with mild fine tremors seen at therapeutic levels), slurred speech, lethargy, muscle weakness, and ataxia. Severe toxicity (> 2.0–2.5 mEq/L) causes seizures, coma, renal failure, and arrhythmias. The nurse must hold the lithium, check an emergent serum level, hydrate the client, and promote lithium excretion. Dehydration and low sodium intake drastically increase lithium toxicity risk. Why the other options fail: option b calls a picture with ataxia and coarse tremor benign, and the tea does nothing. Option c raises the dose into deeper toxicity. Option d restricts fluid, which concentrates lithium further when the client needs volume repletion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lithium toxicity signs: COARSE hand tremor + Vomiting/Diarrhea + Ataxia/Slurred speech. Normal therapeutic range: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L. Above 1.5 = TOXICITY.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Serotonin syndrome causes hyporeflexia and hypothermia, while NMS causes hyperreflexia and fever"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Serotonin syndrome occurs only with antipsychotics, while NMS occurs with antidepressant drugs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Serotonin syndrome: hyperreflexia, clonus, dilated pupils, onset in hours; NMS: lead-pipe rigidity over days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Serotonin syndrome is reversed with dantrolene, while NMS is reversed with cyproheptadine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Both Serotonin Syndrome (SS) and Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) present with hyperthermia and autonomic instability, but key distinguishing features exist: (1) Serotonin Syndrome: RAPID onset (within hours of combining serotonergic agents like SSRIs, MAOIs, tramadol, St. John's wort), neuromuscular HYPERACTIVITY with hyperreflexia, shivering, and spontaneous clonus, hyperactive bowel sounds/diarrhea, and mydriasis (dilated pupils); treated with cyproheptadine. (2) NMS: gradual onset over days, profound 'lead-pipe' muscle rigidity with hyporeflexia, normal pupils, and normal bowel sounds; treated with dantrolene/bromocriptine. Why the other options fail: option a reverses both patterns. Option b reverses the drug classes, since serotonin syndrome follows serotonergic drugs and NMS follows dopamine antagonists. Option d reverses the antidotes, which are cyproheptadine for serotonin syndrome and dantrolene for NMS.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SS vs NMS: Serotonin Syndrome = HYPER-reflexia + CLONUS + Dilated pupils (fast onset). NMS = Lead-pipe RIGIDITY + Hyporeflexia (slower onset).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "3,420 kcal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1,520 kcal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1,770 kcal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1,320 kcal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nutrient energy calculations use the Atwater physiological fuel values: carbohydrate 4 kcal/g, protein 4 kcal/g, fat 9 kcal/g (alcohol 7 kcal/g). This plan gives carbohydrate 250 g x 4 = 1,000 kcal; protein 80 g x 4 = 320 kcal; fat 50 g x 9 = 450 kcal; total 1,000 + 320 + 450 = 1,770 kcal. Why the other options fail: 3,420 kcal applies 9 kcal/g to every macronutrient, which overstates carbohydrate and protein, both of which yield 4 kcal/g. 1,520 kcal applies 4 kcal/g to fat, understating fat at 9 kcal/g. 1,320 kcal counts only carbohydrate and protein and omits the 450 kcal contributed by fat entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Macronutrient caloric math: Carbs = 4 kcal/g, Protein = 4 kcal/g, Fat = 9 kcal/g. Multiply each by its multiplier and sum the products.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the iron with a vitamin C source such as orange juice or baobab pulp, and keep tea, coffee, milk, and antacids at least 2 hours away from the dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the iron with the largest meal of the day, because food buffers gastric irritation and improves absorption"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the iron with a glass of milk or a calcium supplement, which coats the stomach and prevents nausea"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the iron with strong tea or cocoa to disguise the metallic taste, since this improves tolerance of the tablets"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Non-heme iron (plant sources and oral iron supplements) exists in the insoluble ferric (Fe3+) state. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) reduces ferric iron to the soluble, easily absorbed ferrous (Fe2+) state and prevents formation of unabsorbable complexes, significantly enhancing intestinal absorption. Conversely, polyphenols and tannins (found in tea, coffee, cocoa), phytates (in unrefined bran, whole grains), calcium (in dairy products), and antacids bind iron and inhibit absorption by up to 60–80%. The nurse must teach the client to space tea, coffee, and dairy at least 2 hours away from iron doses. Why the other options fail: taking iron with a meal (option b) is the commonest patient habit and is plausible because it reduces nausea, but food binds the ferrous ion and cuts absorption by roughly 40 to 50 percent, so timing relative to meals matters. Milk and calcium (option c) are the trap of a familiar comfort measure: calcium competes directly with iron for absorption in the enterocyte. Tea and cocoa (option d) are pleasant to take but their polyphenols and tannins complex ferric iron in the gut lumen and can reduce uptake by more than half.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Iron bioavailability: Vitamin C enhances iron absorption (Fe3+ -> Fe2+). Tannins (tea), phytates (bran), and calcium (milk) inhibit absorption; space by 2 hours.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bilateral painless enlargement of the parotid glands, with loss of the buccal fat pad"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transverse white bands across the fingernails, with thinning of the hair and easy pluckability"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Swollen, friable gums that bleed on gentle brushing, with loose teeth in otherwise sound molars"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Angular cheilitis with fissuring at the mouth corners, together with an atrophic, smooth, beefy-red tongue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The clinical ('C') component of the ABCD nutritional assessment evaluates physical signs of nutrient deficiency on mucosal surfaces, skin, hair, and nails. Angular cheilitis (stomatitis with fissuring and maceration at labial commissures) and glossitis (inflammation, erythema, and papillary atrophy of the tongue) are classic physical indicators of riboflavin (vitamin B2), pyridoxine (vitamin B6), iron, or folate/B12 deficiencies. Koilonychia (spoon nails) reflects chronic severe iron deficiency, not vitamin C excess. Why the other options fail: bilateral parotid enlargement (option a) is a real nutritional sign but points to bulimia, starvation, or HIV salivary gland disease, none of which the stem describes, and the buccal fat pad is lost in wasting rather than in B2 deficiency. Transverse white nail bands (option b) are Mees' lines, seen after arsenic exposure, severe systemic illness, or chemotherapy, not riboflavin or iron deficiency. Friable bleeding gums with loose teeth (option c) are the gingival picture of scurvy, which is vitamin C deficiency; it is a mucosal sign, but a different nutrient from the one the stem asks about.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical signs of deficiency: Angular cheilitis and glossitis indicate riboflavin (B2), B-complex, or iron deficiency. Inspect oral mucosa closely.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Classify as moderate acute malnutrition, give a single dose of vitamin A, and review the child at the clinic in 4 weeks once the oedema has settled"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Enrol the child for outpatient care only if bilateral pitting oedema is absent, and refer for inpatient stabilisation only once MUAC falls below 10.5 cm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Repeat the MUAC measurement after 2 weeks of supplementary feeding at home, and assign a classification only if the value has not improved by then"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Classify as severe acute malnutrition, do an appetite test with ready-to-use therapeutic food, check for pitting oedema and danger signs, and enrol in outpatient or inpatient care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$MUAC is a rapid, validated, age-independent screening tool for acute wasting in children aged 6 to 59 months: (1) Red (< 11.5 cm) = Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM), carrying a 9- to 11-fold increased risk of mortality; (2) Yellow (11.5 to 12.4 cm) = Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM); (3) Green (>= 12.5 cm) = Normal nutritional status. A child with a MUAC of 11.2 cm has SAM. Management requires: assessment for bilateral pitting edema, checking for medical complications (fever, hypothermia, severe anemia, hypoglycemia, respiratory distress), conducting an RUTF appetite test, and admitting to Inpatient Stabilization (if complications present or failed appetite test) or Outpatient Therapeutic Program (OTP). Why the other options fail: option a under-calls the severity. The yellow MAM band runs from 11.5 to 12.4 cm, and 11.2 cm sits below it in the red band, so a single vitamin A dose and a four-week review leaves a child with a nine- to eleven-fold mortality risk untreated. Option b invents a threshold that does not exist: MUAC below 10.5 cm is not an admission criterion, and oedema makes a child severely malnourished at any MUAC rather than excluding them from care. Option c delays a decision that must be made now; two weeks of home feeding in a child at this risk is time the child does not have.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pediatric MUAC cutoffs: < 11.5 cm = RED (Severe Acute Malnutrition). Urgent appetite test + medical screening -> OTP with RUTF or Inpatient care.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Albumin responds within 48 hours of adequate intake, so it detects acute nutritional recovery earlier than prealbumin does"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prealbumin is filtered and reabsorbed by the renal tubule, so its serum level reflects glomerular filtration rather than intake"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prealbumin has a half-life of 2 to 3 days against 20 days for albumin, so it tracks real-time changes in visceral protein synthesis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Albumin has a shorter half-life than prealbumin, so it falls faster during acute illness and recovers faster with feeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Serum prealbumin (transthyretin) has a short circulating half-life of 2–3 days and a small total body pool, making it highly sensitive to acute changes in protein-calorie intake; levels rise rapidly within 48–72 hours of adequate nutritional support. In contrast, albumin has a long half-life (approximately 20 days) and a large extravascular reserve, making it a poor indicator of acute nutritional change. Furthermore, albumin is a negative acute-phase reactant whose hepatic synthesis is suppressed by cytokines during inflammation, infection, trauma, and liver disease, and serum levels drop dramatically during fluid overload due to hemodilution. Why the other options fail: option a inverts the half-lives. Albumin turns over over about 20 days and sits on a large extravascular reserve, so a nutritional change takes weeks to register. Option b attributes prealbumin's level to renal handling; transthyretin is not a filtration marker in this sense, and its fall reflects reduced hepatic synthesis, not tubular loss. Option d is exactly backwards: albumin's long half-life is why it lags, and its fall during inflammation reflects cytokine suppression of synthesis and haemodilution, not a fast turnover.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nutritional biomarkers: Prealbumin half-life = 2-3 days (acute, sensitive). Albumin half-life = 20 days (poor acute marker, confounded by inflammation/hydration).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Folic acid 5 mg daily from the first antenatal visit, because the neural tube closes during the second trimester"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Folic acid 400 mcg daily once pregnancy is confirmed by ultrasound at 8 weeks, continued until delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "No supplement is needed when the diet is rich in green leafy vegetables and legumes, provided iron stores are normal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Folic acid 400 mcg daily from at least 1 month before conception through the first trimester, to prevent neural tube defects"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The embryonic neural tube closes completely between day 21 and day 28 post-conception (approximately 3 to 4 weeks after fertilization), often before a woman realizes she is pregnant. Periconceptional folic acid supplementation (400 mcg or 0.4 mg daily for average-risk women) started at least 1 month prior to conception and continued through the 12th week of gestation reduces the incidence of neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly, encephalocele) by up to 70%. High-risk women (history of previous NTD, maternal diabetes, obesity, sickle cell, or taking antiepileptic drugs) require a higher dose of 4–5 mg daily. Why the other options fail: option a carries the high-risk dose and the wrong clock. Five milligrams is reserved for women with a previous affected pregnancy, diabetes, obesity, sickle cell disease, or anticonvulsant use, and the neural tube is closed by day 28, long before the second trimester. Option b starts after the window has shut; ultrasound confirmation at 8 weeks is five weeks past closure. Option c trusts diet alone, but dietary folate is poorly bioavailable and the protective evidence rests on periconceptional supplementation, so a good diet does not substitute for the tablet.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Periconceptional folate: 400 mcg daily starting >= 1 month BEFORE conception through 12 weeks gestation. Neural tube closes by day 28 post-conception.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give boiled water between feeds in hot weather, because breast milk alone cannot replace the water lost through sweating"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Introduce thin maize porridge at 4 months, since breast milk no longer meets energy needs after 3 months of age"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Continue exclusive breastfeeding to 6 months; breast milk is about 88% water and meets all fluid and nutrient needs even in hot weather"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Offer sugar water after each feed to prevent hypoglycaemia until the infant is 6 months old and eating solids"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$WHO and UNICEF recommend Exclusive Breastfeeding (EBF) for the first 6 months of life (no other liquids or solids, not even water, except for vitamins, mineral supplements, or oral rehydration salts/medicines). Breast milk is 87–88% water with low osmolarity, satisfying all infant hydration requirements even in arid tropical climates. Giving water, teas, or thin porridges fills the infant's small stomach, decreases breast suckling (lowering maternal prolactin and milk supply), and introduces waterborne pathogens causing diarrhoeal diseases, the leading cause of infant mortality in low-resource settings. Why the other options fail: option a is the grandmother's argument in clinical clothing. It sounds like sensible hydration, but breast milk is low in solute and high in water, so an exclusively breastfed infant in a hot climate is not water-depleted, and top-up water displaces a feed and its protein. Option b brings complementary feeding forward by two months, against WHO guidance, and thin porridge is energy-dilute, which is how infants become malnourished rather than better nourished. Option d adds sugar without protein or micronutrients and fills the small stomach, reducing suckling and therefore prolactin-driven milk production.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Exclusive breastfeeding: EBF for first 6 months (zero water/solids). Breast milk is 88% water and provides complete hydration even in tropical heat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Atrophic gastritis with hypochlorhydria reduces acid and pepsin, so protein-bound B12 cannot be cleaved for absorption"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of intrinsic factor alone, because parietal cell loss is the sole cause of B12 malabsorption in older adults"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bacterial overgrowth in the small bowel, which competes for dietary B12 before it can reach the terminal ileum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Enhanced renal excretion of transcobalamin-bound B12, secondary to an age-related fall in glomerular filtration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Age-related atrophic gastritis affects 10–30% of older adults, leading to loss of gastric parietal cells, hypochlorhydria (decreased hydrochloric acid), and diminished pepsin secretion. Dietary vitamin B12 is bound to animal proteins; gastric acid and pepsin are mandatory to cleave B12 from food proteins so it can bind to haptocorrin and subsequently to intrinsic factor for terminal ileal absorption. When gastric acid is low, protein-bound B12 malabsorption develops despite adequate dietary intake. Older adults frequently require crystalline B12 supplements (which do not require acid cleavage) or parenteral B12 injections. Why the other options fail: option b names a genuine mechanism but makes it exclusive. This client eats fish and eggs, so the food-bound fraction is precisely the part that fails first, and intrinsic factor loss is a contributing factor here rather than the sole explanation the stem asks for. Option c is a real cause of B12 deficiency in blind loops and strictures, but nothing in the history suggests stasis, surgery, or a structural lesion. Option d invents a route of loss; B12 is conserved through enterohepatic circulation, and falling filtration rate does not strip transcobalamin-bound vitamin from plasma.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Geriatric B12 malabsorption: Atrophic gastritis -> hypochlorhydria -> inability to cleave protein-bound B12 -> B12 deficiency and neurological deficits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Half the plate brown rice and boiled yam, one quarter fruit, and one quarter lean fish or skinless chicken"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Half the plate non-starchy vegetables, one quarter lean protein such as fish or beans, and one quarter complex carbohydrate such as boiled yam"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Half the plate lean protein, one quarter starchy vegetables, and one quarter fruit, with no grain at any meal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The whole plate non-starchy vegetables, with two slices of whole-grain bread and a banana taken on the side"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Diabetes Plate Method is a visual, practical tool for portion control and carbohydrate consistency: (1) Half the plate (50%) consists of non-starchy vegetables (greens, tomatoes, okra, cabbage, peppers) which are rich in fiber, micronutrients, and water with minimal impact on blood glucose; (2) One quarter (25%) consists of lean protein sources (fish, chicken without skin, legumes, eggs); (3) One quarter (25%) consists of complex, high-fiber, low-glycemic carbohydrates (unrefined grains, brown rice, yam, plantain, whole wheat). This structure prevents postprandial glycemic spikes and promotes satiety. Why the other options fail: option a looks healthier than it is, because brown rice, yam, and fruit are all carbohydrate, so half the plate plus a fruit quarter delivers a carbohydrate load closer to three quarters. Option c over-corrects in the protein direction and still stacks starchy vegetables with fruit, and its ban on grain is not part of the plate method. Option d keeps the vegetables but puts the carbohydrate outside the plate, where it is no longer being portioned, which defeats the visual control the method exists to provide.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diabetes plate method: 1/2 non-starchy vegetables + 1/4 lean protein + 1/4 complex carbohydrate. Maximizes fiber and controls postprandial glucose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "High in potassium, calcium, magnesium, and fibre, with sodium below 2,300 mg/day and ideally 1,500 mg/day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "High in potassium and calcium from fruit and low-fat dairy, with sodium limited to 3,000 mg/day for blood pressure control"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Low in saturated fat, but potassium and magnesium are unrestricted because they do not influence blood pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Emphasises whole grains and nuts, with sodium replaced at every meal by potassium-based salt substitutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The DASH diet is an evidence-based nutritional intervention that lowers systolic blood pressure by 8–14 mmHg. It emphasizes: (1) rich intake of fruits and vegetables (4–5 servings of each daily); (2) low-fat dairy products (providing calcium); (3) whole grains, poultry, fish, and nuts (providing magnesium and potassium); and (4) marked reduction in sodium, red meats, sweets, and saturated fats. Potassium, calcium, and magnesium promote vascular smooth muscle relaxation and urinary sodium excretion. Restricting sodium to < 2,300 mg/day (or <= 1,500 mg/day in hypertensive individuals) provides additive blood pressure lowering. Why the other options fail: option b gets the foods right and the number wrong. Three thousand milligrams is above the upper limit that defines the plan, and the blood-pressure benefit is dose-dependent, which is why 1,500 mg is the stronger target. Option c states the opposite of the mechanism: potassium and magnesium are among the nutrients that lower blood pressure, and their intake is a defining feature of DASH rather than an irrelevance. Option d sounds like DASH but swaps a dietary pattern for a product, and salt substitutes are hazardous in the renal impairment that commonly accompanies hypertension.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DASH diet: High potassium, magnesium, calcium + high fiber + sodium <= 1,500-2,300 mg/day. Proven to lower BP significantly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase protein to 1.5 g/kg/day to replace losses, and restrict only sodium and fluid until dialysis begins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Protein 0.6 to 0.8 g/kg/day, with potassium, phosphorus, and sodium restricted and potassium-based salt substitutes avoided"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict potassium and phosphorus only, since protein does not raise urea until dialysis has been started"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict sodium and phosphorus only, and use potassium-based salt substitutes freely to make the diet palatable"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In advanced pre-dialysis CKD (Stage 4–5): (1) Protein is controlled to 0.6–0.8 g/kg/day to decrease nitrogenous waste production (blood urea nitrogen) and slow renal functional decline (once dialysis begins, protein is increased to 1.2 g/kg to replace dialysate amino acid loss); (2) Potassium restriction (< 2–3 g/day) is essential because failing nephrons cannot excrete potassium, risking fatal cardiac arrhythmias; (3) Phosphorus restriction (< 800–1,000 mg/day) prevents hyperphosphatemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism, and vascular calcification; and (4) Sodium restriction (< 2 g/day) controls fluid retention and hypertension. Why the other options fail: option a imports the dialysis prescription into the pre-dialysis stage. Protein rises to about 1.2 g/kg only once dialysis replaces amino acids lost in the dialysate, and 1.5 g/kg at an eGFR of 22 generates a nitrogen load the failing kidney cannot clear. Option c denies the central mechanism of uraemia, which is protein catabolism raising blood urea nitrogen well before dialysis. Option d removes the two restrictions that matter least here and adds a potassium load, which is the most dangerous omission in a client who cannot excrete potassium.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CKD pre-dialysis nutrition: Moderate protein (0.6-0.8 g/kg) + restrict potassium + restrict phosphorus + restrict sodium. Avoid potassium salt substitutes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Eliminate rice, maize, and cassava, which cross-react with gliadin, while allowing wheat in small amounts"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Avoid all grains including rice and millet, and base the diet on oats that have not been certified gluten-free"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Eliminate wheat, rye, barley, and contaminated oats, substituting rice, maize, cassava, yam, millet, and sorghum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eliminate gluten strictly for 6 months, then reintroduce wheat gradually once the villi have recovered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Celiac disease is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by ingestion of gluten storage proteins (gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, hordein in barley) in genetically susceptible individuals, causing immune-mediated mucosal inflammation, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy in the small intestine. The sole effective treatment is strict, lifelong elimination of all gluten-containing grains: Barley, Rye, Oats (unless certified gluten-free), and Wheat (the 'BROW' mnemonic). Safe naturally gluten-free alternatives include: rice, maize, cassava, yams, plantains, potatoes, soy, millet, sorghum, arrowroot, and buckwheat. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the safe and unsafe lists. Rice, maize, and cassava contain no gluten and do not cross-react with gliadin, while wheat is the single most important grain to remove. Option b is a double error: it eliminates grains that are safe and then builds the diet on ordinary oats, which are routinely contaminated with wheat during milling and transport. Option d treats coeliac disease as a temporary elimination diet, but the enteropathy recurs with any reintroduction, so the restriction is lifelong.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Celiac disease diet: Eliminate 'BROW' (Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat). Safe staples = Rice, Maize, Yam, Cassava, Plantain, Millet, Sorghum.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Marasmus, because the oedema reflects protein-energy wasting with loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle bulk"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Kwashiorkor, the oedematous form of severe acute malnutrition, driven by protein deficiency with hypoalbuminaemic third spacing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nutritional dwarfism, because the child is short for age with preserved subcutaneous fat and a normal appetite"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Moderate acute malnutrition, because the pitting oedema is confined to the lower limbs and the hair changes are reversible"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Kwashiorkor is edematous Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) caused by severe dietary protein deficiency relative to energy intake (often occurring when a toddler is weaned abruptly from protein-rich breast milk to a high-carbohydrate starch gruel). Key diagnostic hallmarks include: (1) bilateral pitting nutritional edema (starting in feet/ankles and progressing upwards); (2) 'flaky-paint' / enamel dermatosis (desquamating pigmented skin); (3) sparse, hypopigmented hair ('flag sign'); (4) fatty liver (hepatomegaly) due to impaired apolipoprotein synthesis; and (5) profound apathy and misery. Marasmus, in contrast, presents with severe somatic wasting ('skin and bones'), no edema, and an alert, ravenous child. Why the other options fail: option a is the classic confusion, and it fails on its defining feature. Marasmus is non-oedematous by definition, with gross wasting and an alert, hungry child, whereas this child is oedematous and apathetic. Option c describes stunting, which is linear growth failure with preserved weight for height and no oedema. Option d misreads oedema as a severity marker rather than a category: bilateral pitting oedema of nutritional origin makes a child severely acutely malnourished on its own, whatever the site or the hair findings.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Kwashiorkor vs Marasmus: Bilateral edema + flaky-paint skin + moon face + plucky hair = Kwashiorkor. Extreme wasting + NO edema = Marasmus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hyperkalaemia from the rapid cellular release of potassium and magnesium as feeding is started after starvation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refeeding syndrome: the carbohydrate load triggers insulin release, which drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency, which causes confusion, weakness, and cardiac arrhythmia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypoglycaemia from the insulin surge, which depletes serum glucose and causes tremor, confusion, and arrhythmia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Refeeding syndrome is a potentially fatal metabolic complication occurring when malnourished, starved individuals are refed too rapidly. During prolonged starvation, the body shifts from carbohydrate to fat/ketone metabolism, and total-body electrolyte stores (especially phosphorus, potassium, and magnesium) become profoundly depleted despite near-normal serum levels. Reintroducing carbohydrates triggers a massive surge of endogenous insulin. Insulin stimulates intracellular uptake of glucose, phosphate, potassium, and magnesium. Rapid intracellular phosphorylation of glucose rapidly consumes remaining extracellular phosphate, causing severe hypophosphatemia, impaired ATP production, diaphragmatic weakness, hemolytic anemia, and lethal arrhythmias. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the electrolyte direction. Insulin drives potassium into cells, so refeeding produces hypokalaemia, not hyperkalaemia, and it does not explain a phosphorus of 0.4 mmol/L. Option c is a genuine hazard of refeeding and explains the confusion, but thiamine deficiency does not lower serum phosphate and the stem's laboratory value is the discriminator. Option d names the right hormone and the wrong substrate; glucose is being driven into cells and is generally normal or high, while the measured deficit is phosphate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Refeeding syndrome: Rapid feeding -> insulin surge -> intracellular shift of phosphate, potassium, magnesium -> profound hypophosphatemia & cardiac arrest. 'Start low, go slow' + thiamine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vitamin A deficiency causing xerophthalmia; give 200,000 IU orally on day 1, day 2, and again at least 2 weeks later"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vitamin A deficiency; give a single 200,000 IU dose and repeat only if night blindness persists after 6 weeks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Riboflavin deficiency; give oral riboflavin 5 mg daily until the Bitot's spots have resolved completely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vitamin A deficiency; apply topical antibiotic ointment to the plaques and review the child in 4 weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Vitamin A is essential for rhodopsin synthesis in retinal rod cells (for scotopic/night vision) and for maintaining epithelial integrity of ocular surfaces. Deficiency causes xerophthalmia: Stage XN (night blindness), Stage X1A (conjunctival xerosis), Stage X1B (Bitot's spots — keratinized epithelial debris on the bulbar conjunctiva), Stage X2 (corneal xerosis), and Stage X3 (corneal ulceration and keratomalacia, resulting in irreversible blindness). WHO treatment protocol for clinical xerophthalmia: 200,000 IU orally (> 12 months; 100,000 IU for infants 6–11 months) immediately on Day 1, Day 2, and Day 14. Why the other options fail: option b gets the drug and the diagnosis right and the schedule wrong. The WHO regimen is three doses, on day 1, day 2, and day 14, because hepatic stores are empty and a single dose does not restore them before the cornea is at risk. Option c treats the eye as a local riboflavin problem; Bitot's spots are keratinised debris of vitamin A deficiency, and riboflavin does not address the corneal melting risk. Option d manages the plaque as an infection, but topical ointment does nothing for the systemic deficiency driving it and the four-week delay risks keratomalacia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vitamin A deficiency (Xerophthalmia): Night blindness + Bitot spots -> WHO high-dose Vitamin A (Day 1, Day 2, and 2 weeks later) prevents permanent corneal melting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pellagra from niacin or tryptophan deficiency; the 4 Ds are dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia, and death"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Scurvy from vitamin C deficiency, in which perifollicular haemorrhage, gum disease, and poor wound healing accompany the rash"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Beriberi from thiamine deficiency, which produces dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia in alcohol-dependent clients"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Riboflavin deficiency, which produces a symmetrical photosensitive rash on sun-exposed skin with glossitis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pellagra is caused by a severe deficiency of niacin (nicotinic acid / vitamin B3) or its precursor amino acid tryptophan. It is historically associated with diets based almost exclusively on un-nixtamalized maize or chronic alcoholism with poor dietary intake. The hallmark is the classic '4 Ds': (1) Photosensitive Dermatitis (symmetrical, well-demarcated hyperkeratotic rash with burning sensation, classically encircling the neck as 'Casal necklace'); (2) Diarrhea (frequent watery stools, malabsorption, glossitis); (3) Dementia (headache, insomnia, depression, confusion, hallucinations); and (4) Death if untreated. Prompt administration of oral nicotinamide cures the condition. Why the other options fail: option b is tempting because the client is an alcoholic with a poor diet, but scurvy produces perifollicular haemorrhage and gingival disease rather than Casal's necklace, and it has no 4 Ds mnemonic. Option c draws on the same alcohol history and sounds close, yet thiamine deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy and high-output cardiac failure, not a photosensitive rash with diarrhoea and dementia. Option d gets the rash distribution right and the vitamin wrong: riboflavin deficiency causes angular stomatitis and a scrotal or nasolabial dermatitis, not the collar-line plaque the stem describes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pellagra triad/tetrad: Niacin (B3) deficiency -> 4 Ds: Dermatitis (Casal's necklace), Diarrhea, Dementia, Death. Rapid response to nicotinamide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Danger zone 5 to 60 °C; cold foods may be held at 10 °C and hot foods at 55 °C during service"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Danger zone 5 to 60 °C; hold cold food below 5 °C, hot food above 60 °C, and cook to a core temperature above 70 °C"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Danger zone 0 to 5 °C, because bacteria multiply fastest in food held just above freezing point"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The danger zone applies only to meat; cooked rice, stews, and soups may be held at room temperature for 6 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under the WHO 'Five Keys to Safer Food' (Keep clean; Separate raw and cooked; Cook thoroughly; Keep food at safe temperatures; Use safe water and raw materials): The Temperature Danger Zone is 5 °C to 60 °C (41 °F to 140 °F). Within this range, common foodborne bacteria (Salmonella, Staphylococcus aureus, E. coli, Bacillus cereus) divide rapidly, doubling every 20 minutes. Safe food handling rules dictate: (1) hot food must be held above 60 °C; (2) cold food must be chilled below 5 °C; (3) cooked foods must reach a core temperature of >= 70 °C for >= 2 minutes; and (4) perishable foods must never sit in the danger zone for > 2 hours. Why the other options fail: option a quotes the right range and then sets holding temperatures inside it, at 10 °C and 55 °C, which defeats the purpose of naming a zone at all. Option c compresses the zone to the refrigeration range; bacterial growth is not fastest near freezing, it is fastest through the middle of the 5 to 60 °C band. Option d singles out meat, but cooked rice, stews, and gravies support Bacillus cereus and Clostridium perfringens growth, and six hours at room temperature is well beyond the two-hour limit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Food safety temperature danger zone: 5 °C to 60 °C. Cold foods < 5 °C, hot foods > 60 °C, thorough cooking core temp >= 70 °C.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Confirm placement with the auscultatory whoosh test, and keep the head of the bed flat during and after the feed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Verify by aspirating and checking that the fluid is green or bile-stained, and nurse the client semi-prone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confirm by X-ray before every feed, and elevate the head of the bed to 15 degrees during the infusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Aspirate gastric fluid and confirm pH 5.5 or below, with initial placement checked by X-ray, and keep the head of the bed at 30 to 45 degrees"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Aspiration pneumonia is a major, preventable complication of enteral feeding. Bedside placement confirmation standards: Testing the pH of aspirated fluid (gastric aspirate is acidic, with a pH <= 5.5, whereas respiratory or intestinal secretions typically have a pH >= 6.0–7.0) is the validated bedside standard; abdominal X-ray is the gold standard for initial placement. The auscultatory 'whoosh test' (air insufflation) is unreliable and dangerous because air sounds transmit clearly from the pleural space, bronchus, or esophagus, leading to fatal pulmonary infusion. To minimize aspiration, the head of the bed must remain elevated 30–45 degrees during and for 1 hour after feeding. Why the other options fail: option a is retained practice in many units, which is exactly why it is a good distractor; insufflated air transmits from the pleura and bronchus as readily as from the stomach, so the whoosh test cannot distinguish oesophageal or pulmonary placement and has caused fatal intrapulmonary infusion. Option b substitutes colour for pH, but bile staining is neither sensitive nor specific and says nothing about whether the tip is in the airway. Option c is right about X-ray for initial placement and wrong about doing it before every feed, and 15 degrees is too low to protect against aspiration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NG tube feeding safety: Verify position via aspirate pH <= 5.5 (NEVER the whoosh test). Keep head of bed elevated 30-45 degrees to prevent fatal aspiration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hang 0.9% sodium chloride at the same rate until the new bag arrives, then resume the TPN as prescribed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pause the infusion and restart the new TPN at half the previous rate once it arrives from the pharmacy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hang 5% dextrose at the same rate and monitor capillary glucose hourly until the new bag is available"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hang 10% dextrose in water at the same rate, because the constant insulin drive will cause rebound hypoglycaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$TPN solutions contain high concentrations of hypertonic glucose (typically 15–25% dextrose), stimulating constant, high-level endogenous insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells. If a TPN infusion is suddenly stopped or interrupted without tapering, circulating hyperinsulinaemia persists in the absence of exogenous glucose supply, causing rapid and severe rebound hypoglycemia (diaphoresis, tremors, altered mental status, seizures, and coma). If a TPN bag runs dry and the next bag is unavailable, the nurse must immediately hang 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) at the existing TPN rate until the new TPN solution arrives. Why the other options fail: option a maintains the line and the volume but not the glucose, and saline does nothing to blunt a hyperinsulinaemic state. Option b is the most dangerous of the three: stopping the infusion abruptly produces the same rebound hypoglycaemia, and restarting at half rate prolongs it. Option c is the strongest distractor because 5% dextrose is a dextrose solution, but it delivers half the glucose of the 10% bag the client was receiving, so the same insulin drive outpaces the replacement.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TPN interruption protocol: If TPN is interrupted or delayed, hang 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) immediately to prevent rebound hypoglycemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Universal fortification of wheat flour with iodine, supported by annual iodized oil capsules for schoolchildren"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Annual oral iodine supplementation for all women of childbearing age, delivered through antenatal clinics"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Universal salt iodization, mandatory fortification of all edible commercial salt with potassium iodate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Iodized oil injection campaigns for schoolchildren every 2 years, with no change to household salt"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Iodine is an essential trace element required for thyroid hormone (thyroxine [T4] and triiodothyronine [T3]) synthesis. Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD) represent the single greatest cause of preventable brain damage and mental impairment worldwide. Manifestations include endemic goiter, hypothyroidism, impaired cognitive development in children, increased pregnancy loss, stillbirths, and congenital iodine deficiency syndrome (cretinism: severe irreversible cognitive impairment, deaf-mutism, spastic diplegia, and dwarfism). Universal Salt Iodization (USI) is recognized by WHO as the most equitable, cost-effective, and sustainable public health intervention to eliminate IDD globally. Why the other options fail: option a widens the vehicle to a food that is not eaten by everyone in the same quantity as salt and leaves the poorest households uncovered, which is the reason flour fortification is neither sufficient nor the chosen strategy. Option b reaches only women who attend antenatal services, the coverage gap salt iodization exists to close, and oral iodine is not the delivery route used in mass programmes. Option d is a repeated injection campaign that reaches children after the critical neurodevelopmental window has already passed and leaves household salt unfortified.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Public health nutrition: Universal Salt Iodization is the gold-standard population intervention preventing endemic goiter, cretinism, and cognitive deficits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oral morphine is cleared by renal tubular secretion before it can reach the systemic circulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravenous morphine is largely inactivated by plasma esterases before it reaches opioid receptors"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism lowers the systemic bioavailability of oral morphine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Binding excipients in the tablet neutralise a large part of the active drug before absorption"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$First-pass metabolism occurs when an orally administered drug is absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract into the portal circulation and undergoes significant enzymatic metabolism in the liver before reaching systemic circulation. Morphine has a low oral bioavailability (approximately 20% to 40%) due to extensive first-pass hepatic glucuronidation. Consequently, the oral dose must be substantially higher (roughly 3 times the parenteral dose) to achieve equivalent therapeutic blood levels and analgesia. IV administration bypasses the portal circulation, providing 100% bioavailability. Why the other options fail: option a moves the loss to the kidney, but morphine's problem is what the liver does to it before it reaches the systemic circulation. Option b invents plasma esterase inactivation, which is the route for remifentanil and not for morphine. Option d blames excipients for neutralising the drug, when the difference is metabolic and predictable.$q$,
  strategy = $q$First-pass effect: Oral drugs absorbed through portal vein are metabolized by the liver before entering systemic circulation, requiring higher doses than IV.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withhold the next dose and check the pulse every 4 hours, telling the prescriber only if it falls below 40"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess pulse, blood pressure and consciousness, notify the prescriber, monitor for bradycardia, and file an incident report"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give activated charcoal if the client is alert, then record the dose as given on the medication chart"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give atropine 1 mg intravenously now, before assessing the pulse, to pre-empt any bradycardia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a medication error occurs, patient safety is the paramount priority. The nurse must immediately: (1) assess the patient's physiological status (vital signs, heart rate, rhythm, blood pressure), (2) alert the prescribing physician and charge nurse with objective assessment data, (3) implement prescribed monitoring or corrective interventions, and (4) complete an internal incident/variance report. Incident reports are quality improvement tools and must never be referenced in the patient's legal medical record or used punitively in a just culture. Why the other options fail: option a delays both assessment and disclosure, so the client is unwatched during the period of greatest risk. Option c records the dose given as though it were the dose prescribed, which is falsification. Option d gives an antidote before assessing the client and without an order.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Medication error response: ASSESS THE PATIENT FIRST -> Notify physician -> Implement corrective care -> Document and submit incident report.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask for immediate-release or liquid alternatives, flush with 15 to 30 mL of water before and between drugs, and give each separately"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Crush the extended-release metoprolol into a fine powder and dissolve it in warm water before giving it down the tube"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Open the omeprazole capsule, crush the enteric granules finely, and mix them with the paracetamol elixir"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add all three medicines to the enteral feed bag and let them infuse together over the next 24 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Enteral tube medication safety principles require: (1) NEVER crushing enteric-coated (EC), delayed-release, or extended-release (ER/XR/XL/SR) medications, as destroying their release matrix causes rapid absorption, potential toxicity ('dose dumping'), and tube occlusion; (2) consulting the pharmacy for liquid, dispersible, or immediate-release substitutes; (3) administering medications separately rather than mixing them together; (4) flushing the tube with 15–30 mL of water before the first drug, 5–10 mL between drugs, and 15–30 mL after the last drug; and (5) never mixing medications directly into enteral feeding formula. Why the other options fail: option b destroys an extended-release matrix and releases the whole dose at once, which can cause toxicity and occlude the tube. Option c crushes enteric granules that exist to protect the drug from gastric acid, and mixes drugs that should be given separately. Option d puts medicines into a feed running for 24 hours, so the dose is neither complete nor timed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Enteral tube medications: Never crush ER/EC formulations (request liquid/immediate-release). Administer separately and flush with water between drugs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take two rapid shallow breaths through the nose immediately after actuating the canister into the spacer"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Swallow the water used for mouth rinsing, so that none of the corticosteroid dose is wasted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Actuate four puffs into the spacer in quick succession, then take one deep breath from it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Exhale fully, actuate once, inhale slowly over 3 to 5 seconds, hold for 10 seconds, then rinse the mouth and spit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Correct MDI technique with a spacer requires: shaking the inhaler, exhaling completely away from the mouthpiece, placing the mouthpiece in the mouth with a tight seal, actuating ONE puff into the spacer, inhaling slowly and deeply over 3–5 seconds, and holding the breath for up to 10 seconds to allow drug deposition in lower airways. When using inhaled corticosteroids, the patient must rinse the mouth thoroughly with water and SPIT IT OUT to prevent local immunosuppression leading to oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) and dysphonia. Multiple puffs must be taken one at a time with a 1-minute interval. Why the other options fail: option a takes rapid shallow breaths through the nose, which deposits the drug in the oropharynx rather than the airways. Option b swallows the rinse water, which is exactly how local steroid exposure causes thrush. Option c actuates four puffs before inhaling, so most of the dose settles on the chamber walls.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inhaled corticosteroid technique: Slow deep inhalation -> 10-second breath-hold -> RINSE AND SPIT to prevent oral thrush.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe before injecting, to avoid an air embolism"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inject into the anterolateral abdominal wall at 90 degrees, keep the air bubble, and do not rub the site"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aspirate for blood before injecting, then massage the site for 2 minutes to speed absorption"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inject within 1 cm of the umbilicus at 45 degrees, which is the preferred site in an obese client"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Enoxaparin injection guidelines specify: (1) inject subcutaneously into the 'love handles' (anterolateral or posterolateral abdominal wall at least 2 inches away from the umbilicus); (2) DO NOT expel the air bubble from the pre-filled manufacturer syringe, as the bubble seals the medication path upon needle withdrawal to prevent tracking into dermis and bruising; (3) pinch a skinfold and insert at 90 degrees; (4) DO NOT aspirate (causes tissue trauma); and (5) DO NOT rub or massage the injection site (causes extensive subcutaneous haematoma and bruising). Why the other options fail: option a expels the bubble that seals the track and stops the drug leaking back into the dermis. Option c aspirates, which traumatises tissue, and massages, which causes bruising and haematoma. Option d injects too close to the umbilicus and at an angle that does not reach subcutaneous fat reliably.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Enoxaparin technique: Abdomen (love handles) -> 90 degrees -> DO NOT expel air bubble -> DO NOT aspirate -> DO NOT rub.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "18.0 mL"}, {"id": "b", "text": "4.5 mL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "12.0 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "9.0 mL"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Step 1: Convert weight from pounds to kilograms: 33 lbs / 2.2 = 15 kg. Step 2: Calculate total daily dose: 15 kg * 30 mg/kg/day = 450 mg/day. Step 3: Calculate dose per administration (divided into 2 equal doses): 450 mg / 2 = 225 mg per dose. Step 4: Calculate volume using available concentration (125 mg / 5 mL = 25 mg/mL): Volume = 225 mg / 25 mg/mL = 9.0 mL per dose. (Or: [225 mg / 125 mg] * 5 mL = 9.0 mL). Why the other options fail: option a doubles the dose, the common error of forgetting to divide the daily total. Option b halves it, the error of reading 125 mg/5 mL as 125 mg/mL. Option c converts pounds to kilograms with a factor of 2 rather than 2.2, which gives a lighter child and a smaller dose.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Paediatric dosing: Convert lbs to kg -> multiply by mg/kg/day -> divide by number of daily doses -> calculate volume from concentration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "60 drops per minute"}, {"id": "b", "text": "50 drops per minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "100 drops per minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "16 drops per minute"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT — 100 drops per minute. With a microdrip set the drop factor is 60 gtt/mL, so 100 mL over 60 minutes = 1.67 mL per minute, and 1.67 x 60 = 100 drops per minute. There is a useful shortcut with microdrip tubing: drops per minute equals millilitres per hour, because the drop factor and the minutes in an hour are both 60. Why the other options fail: option a applies the shortcut to 60 mL rather than to the 100 mL prescribed. Option b would deliver only 50 mL in the hour, half the prescription. Option d is what a 10 gtt/mL paediatric set would give for 100 mL over an hour, so it belongs to a different drop factor. Always state the drop factor before calculating, because a microdrip and a macrodrip set give very different rates for the same volume.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Microdrip tubing is 60 gtt/mL, so drops per minute equals millilitres per hour.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "2.6 mL"}, {"id": "b", "text": "3.5 mL"}, {"id": "c", "text": "3.0 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "0.75 mL"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$When calculating dosage from reconstituted powders, always use the final concentration specified by the manufacturer rather than the diluent volume added (powder displacement increases the total liquid volume). Prescribed dose = 750 mg. Resulting concentration = 250 mg/mL. Volume = Prescribed dose / Available concentration = 750 mg / 250 mg/mL = 3.0 mL. The nurse draws up exactly 3.0 mL. Why the other options fail: option a divides by the diluent volume rather than the final concentration, which ignores powder displacement. Option b draws the whole 3.5 mL, which delivers 875 mg rather than the prescribed 750 mg. Option d divides the dose by the grams in the vial instead of by the concentration in mg/mL.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Reconstitution calculations: Divide prescribed dose by the stated final concentration (not diluent volume): 750 mg / 250 mg/mL = 3.0 mL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Draw up the 40 mEq into a 10 mL syringe and give it over 2 minutes, to correct the ECG changes quickly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the potassium deep intramuscularly into the dorsogluteal site, since the peripheral route is too slow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Add the potassium to the unit of packed cells and run it rapidly, so that the infusion is not delayed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Never give it as a bolus: dilute it, infuse by pump at no more than 10 mEq/h peripherally, and monitor the ECG"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Potassium chloride is a high-alert medication that is LETHAL if given by direct intravenous push or rapid bolus (it halts myocardial electrical conduction in diastole, causing instant asystole). Safety standards mandate: (1) NEVER give IV push; (2) always dilute in compatible IV solutions; (3) maximum peripheral infusion rate is 10 mEq/hour (up to 20 mEq/hour via central venous catheter with continuous ECG monitoring); (4) always use an electronic infusion pump; (5) maximum peripheral concentration is 40 mEq/L; and (6) verify adequate renal function and urine output before administration. Why the other options fail: option a is the direct push that stops the heart in diastole. Option b puts a hypertonic irritant into muscle, which causes necrosis and does not correct the deficit. Option c co-infuses potassium with blood, which is incompatible and can haemolyse the cells, and running it rapidly compounds the danger.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Potassium safety: NEVER give IV push. Max peripheral rate is 10 mEq/hr on an electronic infusion pump with cardiac monitoring.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "0.9% Normal Saline at a keep-vein-open (TKO) rate of 10 mL/h"}, {"id": "b", "text": "10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) at the same infusion rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lactated Ringer's solution bolus of 500 mL"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sterile water for injection without additives"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$TPN solutions contain high concentrations of dextrose (typically 20% to 50%), which stimulates continuous high endogenous insulin secretion by pancreatic beta cells. If TPN is abruptly stopped, circulating hyperinsulinaemia persists in the absence of exogenous glucose, precipitating severe rebound hypoglycaemia (diaphoresis, confusion, seizures, coma). If a TPN bag runs out or the pump fails, the nurse must immediately infuse 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) centrally (or 5% Dextrose peripherally) at the same infusion rate until the new TPN solution is ready. Why the other options fail: option a contains no glucose, so it does nothing to prevent the hypoglycaemia that follows abrupt withdrawal of a high-dextrose infusion. Option c gives fluid without dextrose at a bolus volume that risks overload. Option d is hypotonic and would cause haemolysis; it is never used as a replacement fluid.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TPN interruption: Abrupt cessation causes severe rebound hypoglycaemia. Always hang 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Slow the infusion to 10 mL/h and give oral paracetamol for the fever, then reassess in 30 minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the transfusion, disconnect the blood tubing, and run normal saline through new tubing to keep the vein open"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Flush the blood tubing with 50 mL of sterile water to clear any micro-clots before continuing the unit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure the client that back pain is a common reaction, and continue the transfusion at half the rate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The client is experiencing an Acute Haemolytic Transfusion Reaction (AHTR), a medical emergency typically caused by ABO/Rh incompatibility. Host antibodies attack donor red cells, causing rapid intravascular haemolysis, hemoglobinuria (reddish urine), flank/back pain, hypotension, DIC, and acute renal failure. Immediate management: (1) STOP the transfusion immediately; (2) disconnect the blood tubing at the cannula hub (do NOT flush remaining blood in the tubing into the patient); (3) connect a new sterile IV line with 0.9% normal saline to maintain vascular access; (4) notify physician and blood bank; (5) send the remaining blood unit, tubing, and fresh blood/urine samples to the lab for workup. Why the other options fail: option a leaves the incompatible blood running while haemolysis continues. Option c flushes the remaining donor cells into the circulation, which increases the antigen load. Option d continues a transfusion through a reaction that can progress to renal failure and DIC.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Transfusion reaction: STOP transfusion -> Disconnect tubing at catheter hub -> Run normal saline with NEW tubing -> Notify provider and blood bank.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "TACO: stop the transfusion, sit the client upright with the legs dependent, give oxygen and IV furosemide as ordered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "TRALI: give a 1-litre bolus of normal saline and lay the client flat, since the lungs are under-perfused"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Febrile non-haemolytic reaction: give oral diphenhydramine, restart the transfusion, and monitor the temperature"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Anaphylaxis: give subcutaneous adrenaline, then resume the transfusion at full rate once the pressure settles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload (TACO) occurs when blood products are infused faster than the circulatory system can accommodate, especially in elderly, cardiac, or renal patients. Manifestations include acute pulmonary edema: tachypnoea, hypoxaemia, hypertension, elevated JVD, and bilateral crackles. In contrast, TRALI features acute non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema with hypotension and fever. Interventions for TACO: stop the transfusion, place the patient upright with legs dependent (reduces venous return/preload), apply high-flow oxygen, and administer intravenous loop diuretics (furosemide). Why the other options fail: option b treats a hypertensive, fluid-overloaded client with more fluid. Option c names a febrile reaction when the picture is pulmonary oedema, and restarting the unit would worsen it. Option d names anaphylaxis, which presents with hypotension and wheeze rather than hypertension and raised venous pressure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TACO vs TRALI: TACO features hypertension + elevated JVD + bibasilar crackles (hypervolaemia). Treat by sitting upright, oxygen, and IV furosemide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Add 5% dextrose to the intravenous fluid and continue the insulin, so that ketones clear without hypoglycaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the insulin infusion and give oral orange juice, since the glucose has fallen below the target range"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Double the insulin rate to drive the glucose below 100 mg/dL, and withhold dextrose until it does"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switch the fluid to sterile water and give subcutaneous glargine to cover the next 24 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In DKA management, when serum blood glucose reaches approximately 200–250 mg/dL (11.1–13.9 mmol/L), 5% to 10% dextrose MUST be added to intravenous fluids (e.g., D5 0.45% NaCl). The insulin infusion rate is continued or adjusted to maintain glucose between 150 and 200 mg/dL until acidosis and ketosis resolve (anion gap normalizes, venous pH > 7.30, serum bicarbonate >= 18 mEq/L). Stopping insulin prematurely causes rebound ketoacidosis, whereas failing to add dextrose causes precipitously dropping plasma osmolality and lethal cerebral edema. Why the other options fail: option b stops the insulin while ketones are still present, which allows ketoacidosis to rebound. Option c drives the glucose down without adding substrate, risking cerebral oedema and hypoglycaemia. Option d gives hypotonic fluid and long-acting insulin, neither of which addresses the acute deficit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DKA insulin titration: When blood glucose reaches ~250 mg/dL, add 5% Dextrose to IV fluids and CONTINUE insulin to clear ketoacidosis safely.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immune thrombocytopenia: increase the heparin rate to overcome antibody consumption, and check the count in 48 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dilutional thrombocytopenia: transfuse two units of platelets and continue the heparin at the current rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis: stop the heparin and start oral aspirin instead"}, {"id": "d", "text": "HIT: stop all heparin at once, including line flushes, and start a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) is an immune-mediated drug reaction caused by antibodies against platelet factor 4 (PF4)-heparin complexes. It typically develops 5 to 10 days after heparin initiation, marked by a >= 50% drop in platelet count. Rather than causing bleeding, HIT is a pro-thrombotic emergency: antibodies cause widespread platelet activation leading to venous and arterial thrombosis (DVT, PE, stroke, limb gangrene). Immediate action: STOP all heparin products (including heparin line flushes), record heparin allergy, avoid platelet transfusions (fuels thrombosis), and initiate a non-heparin direct thrombin inhibitor (e.g., argatroban, bivalirudin) or fondaparinux. Why the other options fail: option a is the opposite of the required action, because HIT is prothrombotic and more heparin worsens it. Option b transfuses platelets into a syndrome driven by platelet activation, which can fuel the thrombosis. Option c is the wrong diagnosis, and aspirin does not treat HIT.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT): Platelets drop > 50% after 5-10 days + thrombosis. STOP ALL HEPARIN immediately; switch to argatroban.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give small titrated IV doses, 0.04 to 0.1 mg every 2 to 3 minutes, enough to restore breathing without full reversal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a rapid 2 mg intravenous bolus straight from the vial, which reverses the opioid effect completely and at once"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give naloxone as a 500 mL subcutaneous infusion over 24 hours, so that the reversal is sustained through the night"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mix naloxone with a further dose of morphine, so that receptor occupancy is balanced between the two drugs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In postoperative clients who are opioid-dependent or require analgesia, administering high-dose rapid boluses of naloxone (e.g., 2 mg IV push) causes immediate, complete reversal of analgesia, precipitating severe pain, agitation, sympathetic surge (tachycardia, hypertensive crisis, pulmonary edema), and cardiac arrest. The recommended practice is to dilute 0.4 mg of naloxone in 9 mL of normal saline (giving 0.04 mg/mL) and administer small incremental doses (0.04 to 0.1 mg) every 2–3 minutes until ventilation improves (respiratory rate >= 10–12 bpm) while preserving analgesia. Because naloxone has a shorter half-life (30–90 min) than most opioids, monitoring for resedation is critical. Why the other options fail: option b fully reverses the opioid and precipitates acute pain, agitation and a sympathetic surge. Option c cannot titrate an emergency, and subcutaneous absorption is far too slow for respiratory depression. Option d adds more opioid to a client who is already over-sedated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Naloxone titration in post-op: Titrate small incremental doses (0.04-0.1 mg) to restore adequate breathing without precipitating acute pain crisis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Confirm that the client has eaten within the past hour, so that muscle glycogen can sustain the diaphragm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confirm that sedation and analgesia are running continuously and that the airway is secured on the ventilator"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Disconnect the monitoring, so that electrical interference from the pump does not disturb the drug delivery"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the sedative infusion, so that the client can signal distress by hand movements while paralysed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Neuromuscular blocking agents (paralytics such as vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) induce complete skeletal muscle flaccidity, including the diaphragm and intercostal muscles. Crucially, paralytics possess ZERO analgesic, sedative, or anxiolytic properties. Administering a paralytic to an awake, inadequately sedated patient causes conscious paralysis, horror, and profound psychological trauma. The nurse must verify that: (1) an advanced airway is secured on mechanical ventilation, (2) adequate continuous IV sedation and analgesia are actively infusing, and (3) emergency bag-valve-mask equipment and suction are immediately operational. Why the other options fail: option a has no bearing on paralysis and risks aspiration. Option c removes the monitoring that a paralysed client depends on entirely. Option d withdraws sedation, which leaves a paralysed client conscious, the very harm the item tests.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neuromuscular blocker safety: Paralytics do NOT sedate or relieve pain. ALWAYS ensure adequate sedation/analgesia and secure mechanical ventilation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Exactly 30 minutes after the infusion is complete, which is when the peak is drawn"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Midway through the 8-hour interval, at the fourth hour after the dose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Within 30 minutes, immediately before the next scheduled dose"}, {"id": "d", "text": "At any time during the shift, provided the sample is sent promptly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Therapeutic drug monitoring for aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin, amikacin) and glycopeptides (vancomycin) requires drawing peak and trough levels to maximize bactericidal efficacy and prevent toxicity. The TROUGH level reflects the lowest drug concentration in the body and is drawn within 30 minutes immediately prior to the next scheduled dose. An elevated trough level indicates impaired drug clearance and is the most sensitive predictor of aminoglycoside-induced nephrotoxicity (acute tubular necrosis) and irreversible ototoxicity (vestibular and cochlear damage). The PEAK level is drawn 30 minutes after completion of an IV infusion. Why the other options fail: option a is when the peak is drawn, not the trough. Option b samples the middle of the interval, where the level reflects neither peak nor trough. Option d ignores timing altogether, so the result cannot be interpreted against a therapeutic range.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Trough drug levels: Always draw within 30 minutes IMMEDIATELY BEFORE the next dose. High troughs predict nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Age-related cataract, which explains the halos and the blurred vision, with a routine optometry referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Viral gastroenteritis, which explains the nausea and anorexia, treated with oral rehydration salts"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Digoxin toxicity worsened by diuretic-induced hypokalaemia: hold the digoxin, notify the prescriber, and monitor the ECG"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute hyperkalaemia, treated at once with oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate and a glucose-insulin infusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Digoxin has a narrow therapeutic index (0.5 to 2.0 ng/mL, with optimal heart failure levels 0.5–0.9 ng/mL). Potassium competes with digoxin for binding at the myocardial Na+/K+ ATPase pump. When serum potassium is low (hypokalaemia from loop diuretics like furosemide), more digoxin binds to the pump, dramatically potentiating toxicity even at normal serum digoxin levels. Classic signs of digoxin toxicity include gastrointestinal upset (anorexia, nausea, vomiting), neurological/visual disturbances (yellow-green halos, blurred vision), and cardiac dysrhythmias (bradycardia, heart blocks, ventricular ectopy). Immediate action: hold digoxin, check digoxin/electrolyte levels, replace potassium, and prepare antidote Digoxin Immune Fab (Digibind) if life-threatening dysrhythmias occur. Why the other options fail: option a attributes a drug effect to cataract and misses the bradycardia and the potassium. Option b treats the nausea and ignores the pulse and the visual halos. Option d inverts the electrolyte problem, since a potassium of 3.1 is low and the drug to give is potassium, not a binder.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Digoxin toxicity: Hypokalaemia potentiates toxicity. Look for GI upset + yellow halos + bradycardia. Hold drug and check levels.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lisinopril is teratogenic at every stage and must stop now; switch to labetalol, nifedipine or methyldopa"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Lisinopril is safe until the third trimester, so it can be continued and stopped at 28 weeks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Double the lisinopril dose, since better blood pressure control lowers the risk of pre-eclampsia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switch to losartan, because ARBs are safer in pregnancy than ACE inhibitors and carry no fetal risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitors (e.g., lisinopril, enalapril) and Angiotensin Receptor Blockers (ARBs, e.g., losartan) are strictly contraindicated in all trimesters of pregnancy. They cause severe fetopathy, including renal dysgenesis, fetal oligohydramnios (leading to pulmonary hypoplasia and limb contractures), skull hypoplasia, intrauterine growth restriction, and fetal death. The nurse must notify the provider immediately to discontinue the ACE inhibitor and transition to safe, first-line antihypertensive agents approved in pregnancy: labetalol, extended-release nifedipine, or methyldopa. Why the other options fail: option b delays the switch into the period when fetal renal damage occurs. Option c raises the dose of the drug that must be stopped. Option d swaps one renally acting blocker for another of the same class, both of which cause fetal renal failure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pregnancy contraindications: ACE inhibitors and ARBs are teratogenic (cause fetal renal failure and oligohydramnios). Switch to labetalol, nifedipine, or methyldopa.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Typical progression of Alzheimer's disease, unrelated to the recent medication changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An acute cholinergic toxidrome from organophosphate pesticide exposure at home or in the garden"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Serotonin syndrome, which needs high-dose bromocriptine without waiting for the clonus to appear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cumulative anticholinergic toxicity from two Beers-listed drugs, diphenhydramine and amitriptyline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Beers Criteria (American Geriatrics Society) identifies medications that pose high risks of adverse drug events in older adults. First-generation antihistamines (diphenhydramine) and tricyclic antidepressants (amitriptyline) possess potent anticholinergic properties. In elderly patients, reduced cholinergic receptor density and slower drug clearance lead to cumulative anticholinergic burden: central effects (acute delirium, confusion, memory impairment, sedation) and peripheral effects (urinary retention, dry mouth, blurred vision, constipation, tachycardia, orthostatic hypotension, and falls). Management requires deprescribing inappropriate anticholinergics and adopting non-pharmacological sleep hygiene. Why the other options fail: option a ignores the temporal relationship to two new drugs and calls it progression. Option b names the opposite toxidrome; organophosphates cause bradycardia, salivation and diarrhoea, not dry mouth and retention. Option c treats serotonin syndrome, which this client does not have and for which bromocriptine is not the first choice.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Geriatric pharmacology (Beers Criteria): Anticholinergics (diphenhydramine, amitriptyline) cause acute confusion, urinary retention, and falls in older adults.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Democratic leadership, with the team voting on each shock energy before it is delivered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Laissez-faire leadership, with each member acting on their own judgement and reporting later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transformational leadership, with the leader motivating the team by long-term vision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autocratic leadership, with brief orders, named roles and closed-loop communication"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In acute life-or-death resuscitations where seconds dictate survival, an autocratic (directive) leadership style is clinically indicated. The code leader must maintain command, make rapid high-stakes decisions, assign clear roles (chest compressions, airway, defibrillator, medication recorder), and demand closed-loop communication. Laissez-faire leads to chaos and delayed defibrillation; democratic consensus voting wastes critical seconds when immediate CPR is required; transformational lecturing is completely inappropriate during an emergency arrest. Why the other options fail: option a removes the leader in an arrest, when seconds decide the outcome. Option b puts a vote where a decision is needed. Option c motivates over the long term, which is not what a pulseless patient needs in the next thirty seconds.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Leadership styles: Use autocratic (directive) leadership in immediate life-threatening crises; use democratic/transformational leadership for policy creation, change management, and unit committees.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess acuity, prioritise time-critical care, delegate basic care and escalate the shortfall in writing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the assistant to take the stable patients and give antibiotics while nurses do dressings"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lock the unit doors and refuse emergency transfers until the manager has been contacted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Do the work of three nurses without escalation, because reporting looks like incompetence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When faced with an acute staffing shortage, the charge nurse must: 1) re-triage and prioritize care by client acuity, protecting time-critical treatments (airway, urgent medications, vital signs, post-op monitoring); 2) delegate routine, non-complex tasks within assistive personnel scope; and 3) escalate the staffing crisis formally and in writing to nursing management/supervision to seek float coverage. Delegating IV medications to an assistant is illegal malpractice; unilaterally locking doors violates institutional emergency response; abandoning the shift constitutes gross patient abandonment. Why the other options fail: option b gives an unqualified assistant a delegated clinical task while the two nurses split across tasks. Option c closes the unit to emergencies, which is not within the charge nurse's authority. Option d abandons the unit and the licence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Managing understaffing: Prioritize essential care by acuity, delegate within legal scope, and formally escalate the shortage to nursing administration in writing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Discard it alone at the sink and document the waste later in the same shift"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Return the 8 mg to pharmacy at the end of the shift for credit against ward stock"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Waste the 8 mg into the approved receptacle with a second nurse watching, then both co-sign"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the remaining 8 mg in a labelled syringe for the client's next scheduled dose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Controlled substances (such as opioids) are subject to strict statutory chain-of-custody protocols to prevent diversion and abuse. Any unused portion of a single-use narcotic must be destroyed immediately in the presence of a second licensed healthcare professional (RN or pharmacist). Both practitioners must directly witness the physical destruction and immediately sign/authenticate the narcotic record. Stashing leftover narcotics in pockets is illegal and facilitates diversion; un-witnessed sink wasting violates drug regulations; giving hospital narcotics to families constitutes illicit drug trafficking. Why the other options fail: option a wastes without a witness and documents after the fact. Option b returns a controlled drug to stock, which breaks the chain of custody. Option d gives a controlled drug to a family member, which is diversion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Controlled substances rule: Wastage of any unused portion requires immediate direct witnessing by a second licensed nurse and simultaneous co-signing of the narcotic register.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A lecture series covering the protocols from first principles, with a written exam at the end"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Self-directed reading of the unit policies, with a short quiz at the start of each shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Problem-centred simulation built on their own clinical experience, with self-directed solving"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Observing a senior clinician for two weeks, with no set objectives and no feedback"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Malcolm Knowles' Andragogy theory emphasizes that adult learners: 1) are self-directed and internally motivated; 2) bring rich, diverse life and clinical experience that serves as a foundation for new learning; 3) are problem-centered and task-oriented (preferring real clinical dilemmas over theoretical rote memorization); and 4) learn best when the information has immediate, practical clinical utility. Passive rote lectures, infantilizing adult practitioners, and punitive shaming create hostility and inhibit adult learning. Why the other options fail: option a teaches protocols from first principles to people who already hold them. Option b leaves learning to reading with no application. Option d offers exposure without objectives or feedback.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Knowles' Adult Learning Principles: Adults are self-directed, experience-driven, problem-centered, and value immediate clinical relevance.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Preceptorship is a formal teaching qualification, while mentorship is informal peer support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Preceptorship is for new graduates only, while mentorship is for failed competency reviews"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Both are structured programmes of the same length, differing only in whether they are paid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Preceptorship is time-limited and competence-focused; mentorship is long-term and career-focused"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Preceptorship is a formal, assigned, time-limited partnership between an experienced staff nurse (preceptor) and a novice nurse/student to teach clinical skills, socialize them into the unit, ensure patient safety, and verify clinical competence. In contrast, mentorship is typically an ongoing, voluntary, mutually agreed-upon professional relationship focused on long-term career growth, leadership development, scholarly aspirations, and personal fulfillment. Why the other options fail: option a turns preceptorship into a qualification and strips mentorship of any purpose. Option b invents eligibility rules for both. Option c makes them identical, which is the distinction the question asks for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Preceptorship = formal, time-limited, competency and skill-focused onboarding. Mentorship = long-term, voluntary, holistic professional career guidance.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the procedure and send the new nurse out while a senior colleague finishes the catheter"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Say nothing during the procedure and raise it at the next annual appraisal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Correct the technique at the bedside in front of the client so that it is remembered"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pause to protect sterility, restore asepsis, then debrief privately using the SBI framework"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a break in sterility occurs, the preceptor's first duty is client safety—gently stopping the breach and replacing sterile supplies without shaming the novice in front of the client. Afterward, constructive feedback should occur in a private, supportive setting using structured models like SBI (Situation: during catheter insertion; Behavior: touching the unsterile bed rail with sterile gloves; Impact: introduces infection risk). This preserves psychological safety and fosters clinical growth. Public humiliation damages morale; ignoring contamination causes CAUTI; jumping to board revocation is inappropriate for a learning error. Why the other options fail: option a removes the learner from the procedure, so no learning occurs. Option b delays the feedback until it cannot change behaviour. Option c corrects in front of the client, which teaches fear rather than technique.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Feedback principle: Intervene immediately to maintain client safety, then deliver formative feedback privately, objectively, and constructively.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Adult intensive care clients with central venous catheters, which is the population"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% isopropyl alcohol, which is the intervention"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Povidone-iodine topical skin preparation, the comparison intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The incidence of catheter-related bloodstream infection, which is the outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The PICO framework structures clinical research questions: P = Population/Patient problem (Adult ICU clients with central lines); I = Intervention (Chlorhexidine skin prep); C = Comparison/Control intervention (Povidone-iodine); O = Outcome (CLABSI incidence rate). In this scenario, povidone-iodine is the standard/comparator intervention ('C'). Why the other options fail: option a is the population, which is the P. Option b is the intervention, the I rather than the C. Option d is the outcome, the O.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember PICO: P = Population, I = Intervention, C = Comparison, O = Outcome. Identify each component methodically.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A single large randomised controlled trial published in a high-impact journal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A systematic review and meta-analysis of several rigorous randomised trials"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A prospective cohort study of two thousand patients followed for five years"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An expert consensus guideline written by a national professional body"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the hierarchy of clinical evidence, systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) occupy the peak (Level I) because they synthesize data across multiple high-powered, rigorously controlled experimental studies, minimizing bias. Single case-control studies and qualitative phenomenological reports provide lower levels of evidence (Levels IV–VI). Expert opinion, editorials, and blogs reside at the very bottom (Level VII). Why the other options fail: option a is a single trial, which sits below a synthesis of trials. Option c is observational, which sits below any randomised design. Option d is expert opinion, which sits near the base of the pyramid.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hierarchy of evidence: Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of RCTs sit at the apex (Level I), while expert opinion and anecdotes sit at the base (Level VII).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Written consent taken by the ward clerk on the day, with the risks explained afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Verbal consent from a relative, because the client is poor and may not understand the form"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A consent form with the risks left out, so that the client is not frightened away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Voluntary written consent, with risks, benefits and the right to withdraw explained"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Declaration of Helsinki and modern Institutional Review Board (IRB) codes mandate that human clinical research must uphold participant autonomy through voluntary informed consent, full disclosure of risks and benefits, and guarantees that participants may withdraw at any time without compromising clinical care. Coercive financial incentives, withholding standard curative care from control groups as in the unethical Tuskegee study, and publishing confidential identifiers are gross violations of bioethics and international human rights law. Why the other options fail: Taking written consent through the ward clerk on the day and explaining the risks afterwards inverts the order; verbal consent from a relative is not the client's consent; leaving the risks out so the client is not frightened voids the consent.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Research ethics: Voluntary informed consent, IRB approval, full risk disclosure, protection of vulnerable populations, and zero penalty for study withdrawal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Define the problem, set the aim and identify the root causes of the delay"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Analyse the results of the pilot and compare them against the predicted baseline"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Implement the change across the whole hospital as a standing policy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Run the change as a small pilot, document what happens and note unexpected problems"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Deming/IHI PDSA cycle operates as follows: 1) PLAN = define the problem, aim, metrics, and intervention; 2) DO = test the change on a small scale (pilot), execute the plan, and record observations/obstacles; 3) STUDY = analyze post-intervention data against baseline and evaluate lessons learned; 4) ACT = decide whether to adopt, adapt, or abandon the change based on results. Why the other options fail: Defining the problem and the aim is the Plan phase, which comes before the test; analysing the pilot results is Study, which follows it; rolling the change across the whole hospital is Act, which waits for the study.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PDSA cycle stages: Plan (design), Do (test small pilot), Study (analyze results), Act (adopt, adapt, or abandon).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Individual carelessness by the nurse, to be handled through the disciplinary process"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Active failures alone, because latent conditions cannot contribute to one overdose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Latent system conditions that aligned with active failures to defeat the defences"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An unforeseeable event, which no model of accident causation can explain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$James Reason's Swiss Cheese Model posits that complex organizational accidents rarely stem from isolated individual carelessness. Instead, defense barriers (layers of cheese) contain inherent holes (latent failures: look-alike drug packaging, poor equipment maintenance, excessive mandatory overtime, poor lighting/distractions). When these holes align across multiple institutional defenses, an active human error penetrates and harms the client. System engineering focuses on eliminating latent hazards rather than merely blaming the last human touchpoint. Why the other options fail: Blaming individual carelessness stops the analysis where the model begins; separating active failures from latent conditions contradicts the model, which holds that they align; an unforeseeable event is not what the model finds when the defences were defeated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Swiss Cheese Model: Complex medical errors result from alignment of latent system failures (organizational defects) and active human errors across defense barriers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "At-risk behaviour: coach the nurse and remove the confusing menu in the next update"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reckless behaviour: refer the nurse for a fitness-to-practise review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Human error: console the nurse, examine the pump interface and add a safeguard"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sabotage: remove the nurse from clinical duties pending the investigation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Just Culture categorizes workplace behaviors into three distinct categories: 1) Human error (inadvertent mistake, slip, or lapse while performing care) -> console the worker and fix system/device vulnerabilities; 2) At-risk behavior (taking shortcuts where risk is mistakenly believed to be negligible or justified) -> coach the worker and remove incentives for shortcuts; 3) Reckless behavior (conscious, intentional disregard of substantial, unjustifiable risk) -> disciplinary sanction. Misprogramming due to confusing user-interface design is a classic human error deserving consolation and system redesign. Why the other options fail: option a is at-risk behaviour, which requires a known risk the nurse accepted. Option b is reckless behaviour, which requires conscious disregard. Option d is sabotage, which requires intent to harm.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Just Culture framework: Human error = Console and redesign system; At-risk behavior = Coach; Reckless behavior = Discipline.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give magnesium sulphate and an antihypertensive, stabilise, then refer urgently with handover"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give magnesium and keep her at the CHPS compound until the blood pressure settles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral antihypertensives and send her home to return if the headache worsens"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Arrange a taxi to the district hospital without magnesium, since loading needs a hospital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under Ghana Health Service primary care and CHPS protocols, severe pre-eclampsia is an obstetric emergency requiring urgent stabilization followed by expedited referral up the healthcare cascade. The Community Health Officer administers a loading dose of magnesium sulphate (anticonvulsant) and rapid-acting antihypertensive (such as oral labetalol or hydralazine) within standing orders, secures transport, notifies the receiving District Hospital, and accompanies or coordinates skilled handover. Retaining the patient at CHPS causes eclamptic seizures and maternal death; discharging home is fatal malpractice; uncoordinated commercial transport fails basic emergency transfer standards. Why the other options fail: option b delays referral for a condition that can progress to eclampsia within hours. Option c sends a woman with severe pre-eclampsia home. Option d moves her without the loading dose that standing orders permit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CHPS referral pathway: Stabilize with protocol emergency therapy (magnesium sulphate), communicate with receiving facility, and ensure rapid escorted transfer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Removing financial barriers at the point of care and preventing catastrophic out-of-pocket payment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Free care for registered members, with the cost recovered from higher non-member charges"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A contributory scheme that covers the formal sector and expands to the informal sector"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A basic package for every citizen, funded from general taxation with no contribution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS) was established (under Act 650, revised Act 852) to eliminate the punitive 'cash-and-carry' system that forced patients to pay out-of-pocket before receiving emergency or essential healthcare. Its primary objective is risk pooling and social financial protection to ensure that all citizens, especially the vulnerable and indigent, can access essential healthcare without experiencing catastrophic poverty, advancing Universal Health Coverage (UHC). NHIS is a social public health protection mechanism, not a private for-profit venture. Why the other options fail: Recovering the cost from non-member charges is the opposite of removing a financial barrier; contributory cover that stops at the formal sector leaves the informal majority outside; a tax-funded basic package is a different financing model, not the removal of point-of-care fees.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Health financing policy: NHIS eliminates 'cash-and-carry' and prevents catastrophic out-of-pocket spending to achieve Universal Health Coverage.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Present the mortality figures to the assembly and ask the members to fund a new vehicle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use district data to find the worst maternal outcomes, target resources there and build transport"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lobby for more midwives at the district hospital, where most deliveries take place"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Campaign for a maternity wing at every facility, starting nearest the district capital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nurses in health administration and policy leadership exercise policy voice by using empirical public health data (such as DHIMS-2 metrics) to highlight healthcare disparities, maternal-neonatal morbidity trends, and resource bottlenecks. Advocating for targeted resource allocation, ambulance networks, and training for frontline primary healthcare providers drives systemic population-level improvements. Apathy, urban misallocation, and eliminating nursing cadres actively worsen health outcomes. Why the other options fail: Presenting mortality figures to ask for a vehicle does not target the worst outcomes; concentrating midwives at the district hospital ignores where the deaths are occurring; a maternity wing in every facility spreads resources evenly rather than toward the worst outcomes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nursing policy voice: Leverage epidemiological health data (DHIMS-2) to advocate for equitable resource allocation and address maternal-child health disparities.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Register the business with the assembly and open after an environmental health inspection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "HeFRA facility accreditation, NMC good standing, and practice within scope and referral protocols"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confirm NMC registration and open, since a licence to practise licenses the facility"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain HeFRA accreditation and open, since the facility regulator also covers the nurse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Health entrepreneurship requires strict adherence to statutory regulatory frameworks. Under Ghanaian law (Health Institutions and Facilities Act 829), all healthcare facilities—including nurse/midwife-led private clinics and maternity homes—must be inspected, accredited, and licensed by the Health Facilities Regulatory Agency (HeFRA). Furthermore, the practitioner must maintain active licensure and good standing with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC), ensure a defined clinical scope, and maintain formal emergency referral links with a higher-level facility. Operating without HeFRA licensing is illegal and subject to criminal closure. Why the other options fail: option a registers a business without licensing the facility or checking professional standing. Option c treats a personal licence as a facility licence. Option d accredits the building and leaves the practitioner unchecked.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Health entrepreneurship: Private health facilities require formal licensing from regulatory authorities (HeFRA) and active council registration (NMC).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Publish research, write in the media, and join policy panels and professional associations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A national advertising campaign showing nurses at work, funded by the association"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Speak only through the ministry's communications office so the message stays consistent"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Focus on clinical work and let the image improve as patients experience good care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The public image of nursing directly influences public trust, recruitment of top candidates, legislative healthcare funding, and the profession's policy-making influence. The International Council of Nurses (ICN) and national associations advocate for proactive media engagement: publishing clinical research, writing thought-leadership articles, speaking as expert panelists on national health topics, and showcasing nursing's scientific, autonomous clinical contributions. Silence and unkempt presentation perpetuate harmful stereotypes. Why the other options fail: An advertising campaign spends money on image rather than on evidence and expertise; speaking only through the ministry's communications office channels the profession's voice through another body; waiting for patients to notice good care assumes an image changes by itself.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Professional image: Elevate nursing through thought leadership, clinical research publication, media engagement, and professional association advocacy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Structured debriefing, safe staffing ratios, confidential counselling and peer support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mandatory resilience training for the nurses, with attendance recorded and appraised"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A roster giving each nurse three consecutive days off every month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rotate staff through the oncology unit quickly so no one is exposed for long"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Compassion fatigue and burnout are occupational hazards in high-stress, emotionally demanding healthcare specialties. Evidence-based organizational interventions include: regular clinical multidisciplinary debriefings (such as Schwartz Center Rounds) to discuss emotional aspects of care, ensuring safe staffing ratios, providing confidential mental health support programs (EAP), and fostering peer support. Punitive threats, increasing workload, and demanding emotional apathy exacerbate psychological trauma and accelerate nurse turnover. Why the other options fail: Mandatory resilience training makes the individual responsible for a system problem; three consecutive days off every month adds rest without changing the workload that caused the exhaustion; rotating staff quickly reduces exposure instead of fixing it and spreads the burnout.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Combating burnout: Address systemic staffing ratios, offer structured emotional debriefings (Schwartz rounds), and provide confidential employee counseling.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Implement the system overnight and train afterwards, so no one has time to resist"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Help staff see the limits of paper charting and address their anxieties, creating readiness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Set a compliance target and audit it monthly, reporting the results to the manager"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Set up a staff committee to decide whether the system should be introduced at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Kurt Lewin's 3-Step Change Theory consists of: 1) UNFREEZING = creating motivation and readiness for change by challenging the status quo, showing the need for innovation (e.g., highlighting medication error risks in paper records), and addressing emotional resistance; 2) MOVING (CHANGING) = executing the transition, providing hands-on training, and coaching through the new workflow; 3) REFREEZING = stabilizing and institutionalizing the change into permanent standard operating procedures. Why the other options fail: Implementing overnight and training afterwards skips unfreezing and guarantees resistance; a compliance target audited monthly is refreezing, which comes after the change; a staff committee deciding whether to introduce the system delegates a decision the manager has already been given.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lewin's Change Theory: Unfreezing (create awareness and readiness), Moving (implement change and train), Refreezing (institutionalize and sustain).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Present the findings to the surgical team and agree to review the rate again next year"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Analyse causes, apply evidence-based changes, and re-audit after a set period to close the loop"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Introduce the WHO checklist and record compliance, without repeating the audit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refer the unit for external accreditation review, since internal audit cannot change practice"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A clinical audit is a quality improvement process that seeks to improve client care and outcomes through systematic review of care against explicit criteria and the implementation of change. The complete clinical audit cycle requires: 1) identifying standard criteria; 2) measuring current practice; 3) comparing results with standards; 4) implementing corrective evidence-based changes; and 5) re-auditing practice to verify that improvements occurred ('closing the audit loop'). Hiding the report or falsifying data violates clinical governance and ethics. Why the other options fail: option a records the finding without changing anything. Option c introduces the change but never measures it, so the loop stays open. Option d hands the problem to another body, which does not close the cycle.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical audit cycle: Measure practice against standards, implement evidence-based corrective changes, and re-audit to close the audit loop.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mask or respirator first, then gown, then gloves, then eye protection, with hand hygiene performed only after leaving the room"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Gown and gloves together, rolled inside out into a bundle, then eye protection and mask together at the door before hand hygiene"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Eye protection first while the gloves are still on, then gloves, then gown, then mask, with hand hygiene after the mask is removed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gloves first, followed by eye protection (goggles/face shield), gown, mask or respirator, and perform immediate hand hygiene"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The outer surfaces of gloves and the front of the gown are heavily contaminated during patient care. The CDC recommended sequence for doffing PPE minimizes transmission to clean skin and mucous membranes: (1) Gloves are removed first (glove-in-glove technique) because they are the most heavily contaminated item; (2) Goggles or face shield are removed by grasping the clean headband/earpieces; (3) Gown is unfastened, peeled away from neck and shoulders, turned inside out, and rolled into a bundle; (4) Mask or respirator is removed last (touching only the clean elastic strings/ties, never the front surface) after exiting the room (or just before exiting for non-airborne precautions); and (5) Hand hygiene is performed immediately. Why the other options fail: removing the mask first (option a) feels right because the mask covers the face, but it is the last item removed in a doffing sequence because the hands must be free of contamination before they touch the straps behind the head. Option b bundles the two most contaminated items together, which means handling a soiled gown with soiled gloves and then carrying that contamination to the eye protection. Option c reverses the first two steps: removing goggles while wearing gloves transfers glove contamination directly to the face and hair.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PPE doffing order: Gloves first (most contaminated) -> Goggles/face shield -> Gown -> Mask/respirator last -> Immediate hand hygiene.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Document in the notes that the client left against advice and ask the ward clerk to prepare the discharge summary, because a competent adult cannot be detained"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the client to sign the DAMA form before any further discussion and supply the prescribed medications, so the treatment plan continues at home"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confirm decision-making capacity, have the physician explain the specific risks, explore reasons for leaving, obtain the DAMA signature, and provide discharge medications and a welcome to return"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain the risks once and let the client leave without further documentation, since a refusal signed under pressure cannot be relied on in law"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A mentally competent adult has the legal right to refuse medical care and leave a healthcare facility against medical advice (DAMA). Restraining the client or withholding belongings constitutes false imprisonment and battery. The nurse's professional responsibility includes: (1) confirming the client has decisional capacity (understands situation and consequences); (2) facilitating an urgent discussion where the physician explains specific risks (e.g., sudden cardiac death); (3) exploring underlying barriers (childcare, finances, business) to find alternatives; (4) offering the DAMA release form for signature (or documenting refusal to sign); and (5) providing discharge medications, essential advice, and explicitly welcoming the client back if symptoms recur. Why the other options fail: option a gets the legal principle right and the nursing obligation wrong. A competent adult cannot be detained, but documenting the exit without assessing capacity or arranging the risk discussion leaves out the part that protects the client and the nurse. Option b reverses the order: taking a signature before the risks have been explained produces a form that records nothing of value. Option d abandons documentation altogether, and the reasoning it offers is the opposite of the law, which is that a contemporaneous record of risks explained is exactly what makes a refusal defensible.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DAMA protocol: Confirm capacity -> Explain specific life-threatening risks -> Explore compromises -> Request DAMA signature -> Provide discharge meds & welcome back.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An auscultatory gap — record 168/84 and, at later readings, inflate beyond the point where the radial pulse disappears so the true systolic is not missed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An auscultatory gap; record 128/84, because the higher sounds are arterial wall artefact rather than true systolic pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A normal Korotkoff sequence; record 150/84, treating the point at which sounds disappear as the systolic pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A widened phase IV; record 168/128 and repeat the measurement on the opposite arm to confirm the finding before documenting it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An auscultatory gap is a silent interval between the first Korotkoff sound and the return of phase II sounds, seen most often in older hypertensive clients and in aortic stenosis. It matters because if the cuff is inflated only to the expected systolic, the first sounds fall inside the gap and the true systolic is missed, so the reading is falsely low. The safeguard is to palpate the radial artery while inflating 20 to 30 mmHg beyond the point where the pulse disappears, then deflate at 2 to 3 mmHg per second. The first sound heard is the systolic (168) and the final disappearance is the diastolic (84). Why the other options fail: recording 128/84 (option b) mistakes the return of phase II for the systolic and underestimates the pressure by 40 mmHg. Option c calls a 22 mmHg silent interval between phases I and II a normal sequence, and takes a disappearance point as systolic, which no Korotkoff phase ever is. Option d names a phase IV change rather than a gap and pairs the true systolic with the phase II return as though that were the diastolic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$A silent interval between phases I and II is an auscultatory gap. Record the first sound heard as systolic and the last sound heard as diastolic, and inflate well above the palpated systolic so the gap cannot hide it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Disinfect with an alcohol swab and draw both sets immediately from the same venepuncture site, sparing the client a second needle puncture and a second episode of pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Palpate the site again after the chlorhexidine has dried, then take 5 mL per bottle through an existing peripheral cannula that has been in place for four days"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the first antibiotic dose and then draw 4 mL per bottle from the arm not receiving the infusion, so that the sample is not diluted by the running fluid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disinfect the venepuncture site with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol for 30 seconds and let it dry; draw 8 to 10 mL per bottle from two separate sites before antibiotics"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Diagnostic blood cultures require meticulous aseptic technique to prevent false-positive contamination with skin flora (e.g., coagulase-negative staphylococci). Best practices dictate: (1) draw blood cultures BEFORE administering antibiotics; (2) cleanse the culture bottle rubber stoppers with alcohol; (3) disinfect the skin using 2% chlorhexidine in 70% isopropyl alcohol for 30 seconds and allow to air-dry completely (antimicrobial action occurs upon drying); (4) collect adequate blood volume (8–10 mL per bottle in adults; underfilling leads to false-negative results); and (5) obtain two distinct sets from separate venepuncture sites (not from existing indwelling peripheral IV lines). Why the other options fail: option a gets the antiseptic nearly right and then draws both sets through one puncture, so a single skin-flora contamination event makes both bottles positive and impossible to interpret. Option b repalpates a disinfected site, which reintroduces skin commensals, and takes too small a volume from an indwelling line. Option c draws the cultures after antibiotics have started, which is the commonest avoidable cause of a false-negative culture in sepsis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Blood culture standards: Before antibiotics + clean site with chlorhexidine & air dry + 8-10 mL per bottle + 2 separate venepuncture sites.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lateral recumbent 'fetal' position with knees flexed to the abdomen and chin tucked to the chest; keep the client flat and supine for 4 to 6 hours afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Seat the client upright on the edge of the bed with the chin extended and the lumbar spine held in extension, then keep them sitting upright for 4 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client prone with a pillow under the abdomen to open the interspinous spaces, then keep them prone for six hours to compress the puncture site and seal the leak"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Position the client supine with the head of the bed at 45 degrees and the knees straight, then keep the head elevated for eight hours to reduce intracranial pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$For a lumbar puncture (spinal tap), the client is positioned in a lateral decubitus (side-lying) 'fetal' posture with knees drawn up to the chest and chin tucked to the chest (or seated leaning forward over an overbed table). This maximizes flexion of the lumbar spine, widening the interspinous spaces between L3–L4 or L4–L5 to permit safe needle entry into the subarachnoid space below the conus medullaris. Post-procedure, keeping the client flat in bed in a supine position for 4–6 hours reduces cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leakage through the dural puncture site, significantly decreasing the risk of post-dural puncture headache. Why the other options fail: option b is the seated position used for some punctures, but taken with the spine extended rather than flexed, which narrows the interspinous spaces the needle has to pass through. Option c opens the spaces in the wrong direction and then keeps the client prone, which does nothing for the dural leak. Option d raises the head of the bed, the single thing most likely to worsen a post-dural puncture headache, because a dependent leak is what the flat position is meant to limit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lumbar puncture care: Lateral recumbent fetal position widens interspinous space. Post-procedure: Lie flat (supine) for 4-6 hours to prevent spinal headache.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the bed flat at its lowest height, stand close to the client, bend from the waist with the knees straight so the legs stay clear, and pull firmly on the draw sheet"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Raise the head of the bed to 45 degrees, place a slide sheet under the client, and have one nurse pull while the other lifts the client's shoulders clear of the mattress"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bed at elbow height of the shorter nurse, head flat, friction-reducing slide sheet under the torso, knees and hips bent, wide base, and move on a synchronized count"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use a hoist without checking that the sling size matches the client, and ask the client to hold the nurses' shoulders and pull themselves up to help with the transfer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Safe patient handling and ergonomic guidelines protect both health workers and clients: (1) elevate the bed surface to the working height of the caregivers (elbow/waist level) to eliminate spinal stooping; (2) flatten the bed (lowering head of bed) so gravity does not oppose upward movement; (3) use friction-reducing slide sheets or draw sheets to reduce shear forces and friction that damage fragile skin; (4) maintain proper body mechanics (wide base of support with feet shoulder-width apart, knees and hips flexed, spine neutral, lifting by shifting body weight using leg and gluteal muscles rather than lumbar spine muscles); and (5) coordinate movements on a verbal count. Why the other options fail: option a is the classic injury posture: bending from the waist with straight knees loads the lumbar spine and puts the nurses at risk rather than the client. Option b raises the head of the bed, so gravity now opposes the move, and lifting the shoulders clear of the mattress converts a slide into a lift. Option d uses the right equipment carelessly and then adds a pull on the nurses' shoulders, which is both a client fall risk and an injury risk for the staff.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ergonomic transfer: Bed at elbow height + flat head of bed + friction-reducing slide sheet + bend knees/wide base + shift weight together.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Retract the foreskin, cleanse the glans outward from the meatus, and leave it retracted so that the area can be observed and kept dry until the next round of care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Retract the foreskin, cleanse the glans from the meatus outward in circles, wash down the shaft, then replace the foreskin immediately to prevent paraphimosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wash from the scrotum upward toward the meatus in one stroke, replacing the foreskin afterwards, so the most contaminated area is cleaned first"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retract the foreskin and cleanse with an alcohol-based rub to reduce bacterial colonisation, then replace it and apply powder to keep the skin dry and free of odour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Perineal care for an uncircumcised male requires retracting the prepuce (foreskin) to clean accumulated smegma and secretions. The nurse washes from the cleanest area to the dirtiest: starting at the urethral meatus and wiping in circular outward motions around the glans toward the corona. Crucially, the nurse must REPOSITION (reduce) the foreskin back over the glans immediately after cleansing. Leaving the foreskin retracted causes constricting bands around the penile shaft, venous stasis, severe edema, arterial compromise, and penile ischemia—a surgical emergency known as paraphimosis. Why the other options fail: option a gets the cleansing right and then leaves the foreskin retracted, which is precisely the mechanism of paraphimosis, and a constricting band behind the glans can cause arterial compromise within hours. Option c reverses the direction of cleaning, carrying faecal organisms from the scrotum to the meatus and into the catheter. Option d adds an alcohol rub to mucous membrane, which is painful and damaging, and powder under a replaced foreskin promotes maceration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Male perineal care: Retract foreskin -> Cleanse meatus outwards in circles -> IMMEDIATELY replace foreskin to prevent fatal paraphimosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inflate the balloon as soon as urine appears in the tubing, because the eyelets and the balloon sit at the same level in the bladder"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inflate the balloon with 10 mL of air and then withdraw the catheter until resistance is felt, which seats the balloon at the bladder neck"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withdraw the catheter 5 cm, inflate the balloon with normal saline, and then advance it again until urine flows freely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advance the catheter another 2.5 to 5 cm (1 to 2 inches) further into the bladder before slowly inflating the balloon with sterile water"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Urine flow begins as soon as the catheter eyelets enter the bladder neck. However, the retention balloon is located 1.5 to 2.5 cm behind the eyelets. If the balloon is inflated immediately upon observing initial urine return, the balloon may still reside within the delicate female urethra. Inflating the balloon inside the urethra causes excruciating pain, severe mucosal tearing, and urethral rupture. Therefore, the nurse must advance the catheter an additional 2.5–5 cm (1–2 inches) further into the bladder lumen before inflating the balloon with the manufacturer-specified volume of sterile water (saline can crystallize and jam the inflation valve). Why the other options fail: option a states the error directly, and the premise is false: the balloon sits 1.5 to 2.5 cm behind the eyelets, so urine can flow while the balloon is still in the urethra. Option b withdraws rather than advances, which pulls the balloon back into the urethra and then inflates it there. Option c does the same in two steps and adds saline, which can crystallise in the inflation channel and jam the valve.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Catheter balloon safety: Once urine appears, advance 2.5-5 cm FURTHER into the bladder before inflating balloon. Prevents urethral rupture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute kidney injury from reflex vasoconstriction, with oliguria for 24 hours and a rising creatinine that requires fluid restriction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transient hypotension, post-obstructive diuresis with electrolyte depletion, and decompression hematuria"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertensive crisis from the sudden fall in intra-abdominal pressure, with headache and a systolic pressure above 180 mmHg"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gross haematuria from traumatic insertion, needing continuous bladder irrigation through a three-lumen catheter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Rapid evacuation of a massively distended bladder (> 1,000 mL) abruptly relieves intra-abdominal and pelvic venous compression, leading to splanchnic venous pooling and transient orthostatic hypotension. In addition, relief of prolonged bilateral urinary obstruction can trigger 'post-obstructive diuresis'—a massive polyuria due to impaired renal tubular concentrating ability, risking severe dehydration, hypokalaemia, and hyponatraemia. Rupture of engorged mucosal capillaries in the rapidly collapsing bladder wall can also cause decompression hematuria. The nurse monitors vital signs, true urine output hourly, and serum electrolytes. Why the other options fail: option a predicts oliguria, but the physiological response to relieving prolonged obstruction is the opposite: the tubules have lost their concentrating ability, so large volumes of dilute urine are passed. Option c reverses the haemodynamic direction; decompression causes venodilation and hypotension, not hypertensive crisis. Option d attributes bleeding to insertion trauma, but decompression haematuria comes from ruptured mucosal capillaries in a bladder wall that has suddenly collapsed, and it does not usually need irrigation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bladder decompression risks: Rapid drainage of > 1,000 mL can cause hypotension, post-obstructive diuresis (electrolyte loss), and decompression hematuria.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withdraw the tube completely, let the coughing settle, and reinsert a fresh tube through the other nostril once the saturation has come back above 94 per cent"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop advancing, leave the tube at its current depth, give oxygen by mask, and then continue advancing slowly while the client swallows between breaths"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withdraw the tube slightly into the nasopharynx, stop advancing, assess airway and saturation, let the client rest, then re-advance with chin tucked to chest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to cough forcefully to clear the tube from the airway, then deflate the cuff if present and advance the tube further towards the stomach"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Coughing, choking, dyspnoea, stridor, or cyanosis during NG tube insertion indicates that the tube has entered the larynx/trachea rather than the esophagus. The nurse must immediately stop advancing and pull the tube back into the posterior nasopharynx to relieve airway compromise. Once the client recovers, takes deep breaths, and normal color returns, the nurse instructs the client to tuck their chin down toward their chest (which closes the glottis over the trachea and opens the esophagus) and advance the tube gently as the client takes sips of water through a straw. Why the other options fail: option a is the over-reaction that costs time and trauma; the tube only needs withdrawing to the nasopharynx, not removing and restarting in the other nostril, and the airway must be assessed first. Option b leaves the tube in the airway while oxygen is applied and then advances it further, which pushes it deeper into the trachea. Option d asks a choking client to cough around an obstructing tube and then advances it, worsening the obstruction instead of relieving it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NG tube respiratory distress: Coughing/choking/cyanosis = tube in trachea! Stop immediately, withdraw slightly into nasopharynx, let client breathe, then advance with chin-tuck and swallows.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mucosal sloughing after colectomy, which is expected in the first 72 hours and usually separates to leave healthy pink tissue behind"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The stoma is necrotic due to vascular compromise/ischemia; notify the surgical team immediately as this requires urgent surgical re-exploration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Venous congestion from a tight stoma support; apply warm moist compresses and reassess the colour at the next dressing round"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retained blood pigment from theatre; clean the stoma gently with saline and chlorhexidine to reveal the true mucosal colour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A healthy, viable newly created gastrointestinal stoma is beefy red or pink, moist, warm, and slightly edematous (resembling buccal oral mucosa). A dusky, dark red, purple, brown, or black stoma indicates inadequate arterial blood supply or severe venous congestion resulting in stomal ischemia and necrosis. This is a post-operative surgical emergency. The nurse must immediately notify the surgeon for urgent evaluation, inspect the depth of necrosis using a small glass test tube with a penlight (to evaluate whether necrosis extends below the peritoneal fascial level), and prepare the client for potential surgical revision. Why the other options fail: option a describes a real phenomenon but the wrong one: slough is a superficial yellow-white film over pink mucosa, whereas a uniformly purple-black, flaccid, dry stoma is full-thickness ischaemia. Option c identifies congestion but prescribes a dressing-round reassessment, which delays the only intervention that saves the stoma. Option d treats a surgical emergency as a cleaning problem, and chlorhexidine on an ischaemic stoma adds chemical injury to the existing insult.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stoma viability: Normal stoma = Beefy red/pink & moist. Purple/dark/black stoma = ISCHEMIA/NECROSIS. Immediate surgical notification required.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use the dorsogluteal site with a 21-gauge needle, aspirate, and inject rapidly so that the iron does not track back along the needle track into the fat"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use the deltoid with a 1-inch needle, pinch the muscle belly, and massage the site afterwards to help the viscous fluid disperse through the muscle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use the ventrogluteal site without the Z-track, inject slowly over 30 seconds, and massage the site firmly afterwards to spread the medication through the muscle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ventrogluteal site with the Z-track technique: pull the skin 2.5 cm laterally, insert at 90 degrees, inject slowly, wait 10 seconds, then release"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The ventrogluteal site (gluteus medius and minimus) is the preferred anatomical site for deep intramuscular injections in adults because it is free of major blood vessels and large nerves (unlike the dorsogluteal site, which poses high risk of sciatic nerve injury and superior gluteal artery puncture), and has a thick muscle belly. The Z-track method is mandatory for irritating or dark medications (e.g., iron, haloperidol): displacing the skin and subcutaneous tissue laterally prior to injection creates a zigzag needle track upon needle release, effectively sealing the drug within the deep muscle plane and preventing painful subcutaneous leakage and skin tattooing. Why the other options fail: option a picks the site with the highest risk of sciatic nerve injury and then injects rapidly, which increases the chance of tracking along the track. Option b uses a 1-inch needle in the deltoid, too short to reach muscle in most adults and too small a muscle for 2 mL of an irritating preparation, and massaging drives the iron into subcutaneous fat. Option c chooses the right muscle and omits the Z-track, which is the one step that prevents the subcutaneous leakage and tattooing the irritant causes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IM injections: Ventrogluteal is the #1 safest adult site (no major nerves/vessels). Z-track prevents subcutaneous tracking and staining of irritating drugs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chemical phlebitis; slow the infusion, apply a cool compress to the cord, and continue through the same cannula so that the vein is not lost and access is preserved"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Superficial thrombophlebitis; use the other lumen of the same catheter and apply heparinoid gel along the palpable cord to disperse the clot"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infiltration from the cannula tip dislodging into tissue; aspirate the cannula, then restart the infusion proximal to the swelling on the same arm"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Phlebitis (Grade 3); stop the infusion, remove the cannula, apply a warm moist compress, elevate the arm, and restart access in the opposite arm"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Phlebitis is chemical, mechanical, or bacterial inflammation of the vein tunica intima. The classic clinical presentation includes erythema, localized warmth, pain/tenderness, and a palpable indurated venous cord (Grade 3 on the Infusion Nurses Society Phlebitis Scale). Priority management: (1) immediately stop the infusion and remove the cannula; (2) apply a warm, moist compress to relieve inflammation and promote venous blood flow; (3) elevate the affected extremity; (4) document findings and score; and (5) resite the new IV line in the contralateral extremity if ongoing therapy is required. In contrast, infiltration presents with coolness, blanching, and soft edema. Why the other options fail: option a uses the right word and the wrong action: a Grade 3 phlebitis, with a palpable cord 4 cm along the vein, needs the cannula out, not slowed, and continuing to infuse prolongs endothelial injury. Option b keeps the device in the same vessel and treats a chemical injury as a thrombotic one. Option c misidentifies the complication; infiltration produces cool blanched swelling, not warmth and a tender cord, so flushing and restarting proximal to it would be the wrong manoeuvre entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Phlebitis vs Infiltration: Phlebitis = Warmth, redness, palpable cord -> Discontinue cannula + warm compress. Infiltration = Coolness, swelling, pallor.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Abdominal wall at least 5 cm from the umbilicus, pinch a fold, 90 degrees, keep the air bubble, do not aspirate, do not massage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Select the deltoid, aspirate before injecting to confirm that the needle is not in a vessel, and massage the site afterwards to speed absorption of the dose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe, inject into the abdomen at 45 degrees, and apply firm pressure to the site afterwards to stop any oozing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inject into the umbilicus itself at 15 degrees, since this is the most vascular part of the abdominal wall and will absorb the dose most reliably"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Best-practice technique for subcutaneous enoxaparin administration: (1) use the anterolateral or posterolateral abdomen (the 'love handles'), alternating sides and staying >= 2 inches away from the umbilicus; (2) DO NOT expel the air bubble from the prefilled syringe (the bubble ensures the entire medication dose is delivered and creates an air seal preventing subcutaneous tracking); (3) pinch a skin fold throughout the injection and insert the needle at 90 degrees (or 45 degrees in thin individuals); (4) DO NOT aspirate for blood (aspiration causes tissue damage and hematoma); and (5) DO NOT massage or rub the injection site after needle withdrawal (rubbing causes capillary rupture and severe bruising). Why the other options fail: option b sends a subcutaneous drug into muscle and then aspirates, which is unnecessary for heparin and increases tissue trauma. Option c removes the air bubble that is there to deliver the full dose and seal the track, and firm pressure after the injection promotes the bruising the technique is meant to avoid. Option d injects into umbilical tissue, which is thin, poorly vascular for absorption and painful, and a 15-degree angle is too shallow for a 2 cm pinched fold.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Enoxaparin injection rules: Abdominal wall >= 2 inches from umbilicus + keep air bubble + 90-degree angle + NO aspiration + NO rubbing/massaging.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply continuous suction while inserting the catheter, limit the whole pass to 30 seconds, and give oxygen by nasal cannula between passes to maintain saturation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperoxygenate with 100% O2 for 30 to 60 seconds, advance without suction, suction only on withdrawal for 10 to 15 seconds, 1 minute between passes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hyperventilate the client by hand for 10 seconds, insert the catheter until resistance is met, and apply suction for 20 seconds in a single uninterrupted pass"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instil 5 mL of sterile saline to loosen the secretions, suction for 15 seconds, and repeat every 2 minutes until the chest sounds clear on auscultation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tracheostomy and endotracheal suctioning carries risks of hypoxemia, mucosal trauma, cardiac arrhythmias, and vagal-mediated bradycardia. Evidence-based protocol mandates: (1) pre-oxygenate (hyperoxygenate) with 100% O2 for 30–60 seconds prior to suctioning; (2) insert catheter gently WITHOUT suction until resistance is met or client coughs, then pull back 1–2 cm; (3) apply suction ONLY while withdrawing and twirling the catheter; (4) limit suction duration to <= 10–15 seconds per pass; (5) re-oxygenate between passes and allow at least 1 minute of recovery; and (6) routine normal saline instillation is strictly contraindicated as it flushes colonized biofilm into lower airways. Why the other options fail: option a sucks during insertion, which strips oxygen from the airway for the whole pass, and 30 seconds is twice the safe duration. Option c pre-oxygenates for too short a time and then applies 20 seconds of continuous suction, which is a recognised cause of hypoxaemia and vagal bradycardia. Option d instils saline, which is no longer recommended because it dislodges biofilm into the lower airways, and repeating every two minutes without recovery time causes cumulative hypoxia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tracheostomy suctioning: Pre-oxygenate with 100% O2 + NO suction during insertion + suction ONLY on withdrawal for <= 10-15 seconds max. Never instill saline.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clamp the tube with padded forceps to stop air entering the chest, releasing the clamp briefly every 15 minutes so that any trapped air can escape"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Submerge the distal open end 2 to 4 cm in a bottle of sterile water to re-establish a water seal, and prepare a new drainage unit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tape the open end to the drainage unit, position the client on the affected side, and call the medical team to reconnect the system at the bedside"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Remove the chest tube completely and cover the insertion site with an occlusive dressing until the medical team arrives"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a chest tube disconnects from its drainage canister, atmospheric air can rush into the negative-pressure pleural space, precipitating a tension pneumothorax or collapsing the lung. The immediate lifesaving action is to submerge the distal open tip of the chest tube 2 to 4 cm (1 to 2 inches) into a sterile bottle of water or normal saline kept at the bedside. This creates an immediate temporary water seal, allowing air to escape from the pleural space during exhalation while preventing air from being sucked back in during inspiration. Clamping the tube is contraindicated because trapped air cannot escape, causing tension pneumothorax. Why the other options fail: option a is the most common wrong answer, and it is dangerous: clamping a disconnected tube in a client with a pneumothorax traps air in the pleural space and can convert a simple pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax. Option c leaves the system open to atmosphere and simply tapes it, so air is drawn back in with every breath. Option d removes a tube that may still be draining and seals the chest, which risks both re-expansion failure and tension.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Chest tube disconnection: Submerge distal tube 2-4 cm in bottle of sterile water to create immediate water seal. NEVER clamp a disconnected chest tube.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop removal, apply Steri-Strips across the separated area to approximate the edges, place a sterile dressing, and notify the surgeon at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continue removing the remaining staples so that the wound edges can be inspected in full, then apply a sterile dressing and inform the surgeon afterwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Approximate the edges with Steri-Strips, cover with a sterile dressing, and arrange wound review at the next outpatient appointment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a sterile dressing, teach the client to splint the abdomen when coughing, and record the finding so that it can be raised at the surgical review"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When removing surgical sutures or staples, the standard procedure is to remove alternate (every other) staples first to evaluate wound tensile strength and structural integrity. If wound separation (dehiscence) occurs upon removing initial staples, the nurse must STOP removal immediately. The nurse should support the dehiscence with wound closure strips (Steri-Strips) placed perpendicular to the incision, apply a sterile dressing, keep the client calm in low Fowler's position with knees flexed (to reduce abdominal tension), and notify the attending surgeon promptly. Why the other options fail: option b removes the remaining staples, which are the only thing still holding a wound that has already begun to fail, and turns an early dehiscence into a full one. Option c does the right local action and then defers the escalation: dehiscence can progress to evisceration, so the surgeon is notified now, not at an outpatient review. Option d documents and teaches but neither supports the wound nor escalates it, which is the error that turns a dehiscence into a returning theatre case.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Staple removal & dehiscence: If edges separate during removal -> STOP REMOVAL IMMEDIATELY. Apply Steri-Strips to support incision and notify surgeon.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Compress at 80 to 100 per minute to a depth of 4 cm, allowing full recoil, and pause for 15 seconds after every 30 compressions to check for a pulse"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Compress at 100 to 120 per minute to a depth of 6 cm without allowing full recoil, so that coronary perfusion pressure is maintained continuously"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rate 100 to 120 per minute, depth at least 5 cm (max 6 cm), full recoil after each compression, pauses under 10 seconds, ratio 30:2"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Compress at 100 to 120 per minute to a depth of 5 cm, pausing after every 30 compressions for two rescue breaths of 2 seconds each"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$High-quality CPR is the primary determinant of cardiac arrest survival: (1) Compression rate of 100 to 120 beats per minute; (2) Compression depth of at least 5 cm (2 inches) but not exceeding 6 cm (2.4 inches) in adults; (3) Allow full chest recoil after each compression (leaning prevents coronary perfusion by impeding venous return); (4) Minimize all pauses in compressions (chest compression fraction > 60–80%, pauses < 10 seconds); and (5) Compression-to-ventilation ratio of 30:2 in adults without advanced airway, rotating compressors every 2 minutes to prevent fatigue. Why the other options fail: option a slows the rate below 100 and reduces depth below 5 cm, both of which cut cardiac output, and a 15-second pulse check every cycle destroys the compression fraction. Option b gets rate and depth right and then forbids recoil, which raises intrathoracic pressure and reduces venous return, the exact mechanism that makes leaning harmful. Option d is close but the pauses exceed 10 seconds once the two breaths are given slowly, and no target for minimising interruption is stated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High-quality CPR metrics: Rate 100-120/min + Depth 5-6 cm (2-2.4 inches) + Full recoil (don't lean) + Minimize pauses (< 10 s) + 30:2 ratio.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the client to drink 2 litres of water before the procedure so the bladder is easy to identify on the scan, and give a colloid bolus for every 3 litres drained"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Catheterise the client and clamp the catheter during drainage, giving albumin only if the systolic pressure falls after the tap has been completed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Have the client void immediately before the tap to avoid puncturing the bladder; give IV albumin 6 to 8 g per litre of ascites removed once over 5 litres"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fast the client from midnight and give fresh frozen plasma after the tap rather than albumin, to replace the volume that has been removed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Prior to abdominal paracentesis, having the client void immediately beforehand is critical: an empty bladder collapses into the deep pelvic cavity, preventing catastrophic accidental trocar laceration or perforation of the urinary bladder. For large-volume paracentesis (> 5 liters of ascitic fluid removed), rapid fluid shifts cause profound splanchnic vasodilation and arterial hypovolemia (paracentesis-induced circulatory dysfunction, PICD), leading to hepatorenal syndrome and death. To maintain effective oncotic pressure and intravascular volume, intravenous salt-poor albumin (6–8 g per liter of ascites drained beyond 5 L) is infused. Why the other options fail: option a does the reverse of the preparation: a full bladder rises out of the pelvis into the path of the trocar, which is the injury the instruction exists to prevent. Option b prevents puncture but gives albumin only after hypotension appears, when the point of prophylaxis was to prevent it. Option d substitutes plasma for albumin, which carries transfusion risk without the oncotic effect that reverses the splanchnic vasodilation of PICD.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Paracentesis care: VOID immediately before procedure (prevents bladder puncture). For > 5 L drained, infuse IV albumin (6-8 g/L) to prevent circulatory collapse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Raise the head of the bed 15 to 30 degrees or put a pillow under the head to prevent facial lividity, close the eyes, and support the jaw"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place a pillow under the shoulders and tilt the bed head-down, so that blood drains away from the face and the family can see the body more clearly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Close the eyes and jaw, leave the bed flat, and open the window to cool the room, since cooling the body prevents discoloration of the face"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wash the body, remove all tubes and lines, and keep the head flat under a sheet until the family arrives so that the face is not disturbed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Post-mortem care honors client dignity and provides compassionate closure for grieving families: (1) elevate the head of the bed 15–30 degrees or place a pillow under the head promptly to prevent dependent blood pooling (livor mortis) in the face, which causes purple discoloration; (2) close the eyelids gently; (3) insert dentures and support the lower jaw with a rolled towel under the chin before rigor mortis sets in; (4) remove non-forensic invasive tubes/catheters (unless an autopsy/coroner's inquest is mandated); (5) cleanse soiled body areas and replace soiled bed linens; and (6) position clean sheets neatly to the shoulders for family viewing. Why the other options fail: option b tilts the head down, which is the fastest way to produce the facial lividity the question asks how to avoid, and it does so for a reason (better visibility) that sounds considerate. Option c leaves the body flat, so dependent pooling continues, and cooling the room does not stop it. Option d keeps the head flat for the same cosmetic reason and reaches it by the opposite route, and it also strips every line, which pre-empts a coroner's or autopsy decision that is not the nurse's to make.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Post-mortem care: Elevate head with pillow (prevents facial blood pooling/lividity) + close eyes & support jaw + wash & clean linens before family viewing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Doubling of creatinine represents an acute loss of about 50% of the baseline glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A circadian fluctuation, since creatinine varies through the day within normal physiological limits"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Increased dietary protein catabolism, with a preserved glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Permanent end-stage renal fibrosis, which now requires immediate peritoneal dialysis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Serum creatinine is an inverse biomarker of glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Under steady-state conditions, doubling of serum creatinine (from 1.0 to 2.1 mg/dL) reflects an approximate 50% reduction in GFR and renal functional capacity. In acute dehydration, this elevation points to acute kidney injury (prerenal azotemia) requiring rapid fluid resuscitation before acute tubular necrosis occurs. Why the other options fail: option b calls a doubling of creatinine a circadian variation, when normal variation is a few micromoles and never a doubling in one illness. Option c blames dietary protein for a change that dehydration explains, and BUN rises with protein while creatinine does not. Option d diagnoses irreversible fibrosis from a single acute value, before any attempt at resuscitation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Renal Physiology Rule: Serum creatinine has an inverse relationship with GFR. A doubling of creatinine signifies an approximate 50% drop in renal filtration rate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "True total-body fluid deficit across all compartments, which would explain the ascites and the oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravascular overload with a raised central venous pressure, since the client is oedematous"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Third-space sequestration of fluid with severe intravascular depletion, or effective arterial hypovolaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An intracellular shift causing cellular hyperosmolality, which would draw fluid out of the vessels"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In cirrhosis and severe hypoalbuminemia, loss of plasma oncotic pressure combined with splanchnic arterial vasodilation allows intravascular fluid to transudate into the peritoneal cavity (third-spacing: ascites and interstitial edema). Despite massive total-body fluid accumulation, the client suffers from critical intravascular hypovolemia (manifested by hypotension, tachycardia, and oliguria), creating a prerenal state. Why the other options fail: option a denies the ascites and oedema that show total body fluid is increased, not reduced. Option b reads oedema as intravascular filling, when the pressure and the urine output show the vessels are empty. Option d invents an intracellular shift, which would not produce ascites or a low blood pressure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Third-spacing pathology: Massive fluid trapped in interstitial/peritoneal spaces WHILE intravascular space is empty -> hypotension, tachycardia, oliguria.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The 24-hour intake and output chart, which records every fluid in and out at the bedside"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Daily morning weight, taken at the same time on the same scale after voiding"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Skin turgor over the anterior forearm, which reflects interstitial hydration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Visual assessment of pitting oedema at the ankles, graded from 1+ to 4+"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Daily body weight is the single most sensitive, objective, and accurate indicator of acute fluid balance fluctuations. One kilogram (2.2 lbs) of acute weight change is equivalent to 1,000 mL (1 liter) of retained or lost fluid. Intake and output charts are prone to human measurement error and unrecorded losses (sweat, stool), and physical signs (edema, skin turgor) manifest only after significant volume shifts. Why the other options fail: option a depends on charting that misses insensible losses and is often estimated. Option c changes late and is unreliable in older adults, whose skin is lax anyway. Option d is a late sign that appears only after litres have accumulated, and it is graded by eye.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fluid monitoring gold standard: Daily body weight (same scale, same time, after voiding). 1 kg weight change = 1 liter (1,000 mL) of fluid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give oral rehydration solution, 200 mL by cup every 10 minutes, and reassess the skin pinch after an hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intramuscular metoclopramide to stop the vomiting before replacing fluid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Establish large-bore IV access and give 1,000 mL of Ringer's lactate or 0.9% saline over 15 to 30 minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Collect a stool specimen for dark-field microscopy before starting resuscitation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe dehydration with signs of hypovolemic shock (hypotension, thready tachycardia, delayed skin pinch, lethargy) represents a critical medical emergency (WHO Plan C). Oral rehydration alone is insufficient and unsafe in shock or altered consciousness. The immediate priority is rapid intravenous resuscitation with balanced isotonic crystalloids (Ringer's lactate or 0.9% normal saline) through large-bore IV access to restore intravascular volume. Why the other options fail: option a gives oral fluid to a lethargic client in shock, which is unsafe and far too slow. Option b treats the vomiting and leaves the circulation empty. Option d puts a laboratory test ahead of resuscitation in a client with a systolic pressure of 74 mmHg.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe dehydration shock (Plan C): Large-bore IV access -> Rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloids (Ringer's lactate / Normal Saline) immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lay the client flat and give a 500 mL bolus of 5% dextrose, to support the circulation while the cause is found"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform chest percussion and walk the client round the ward to clear the secretions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 100 mL of 20% mannitol by rapid intravenous push, and withhold oxygen until the provider reviews"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sit the client upright, give high-flow oxygen, stop the IV fluids, and call the provider for diuretic or dialysis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acute pulmonary edema resulting from iatrogenic fluid overload in an oliguric/anuric client is a life-threatening emergency. Immediate nursing priorities: 1) Position in high-Fowler's with legs dependent to drop cardiac preload and ease ventilation, 2) Apply high-flow oxygen immediately, 3) Shut off IV fluid infusions immediately, and 4) Alert the medical team for urgent loop diuretics or emergent ultrafiltration/hemodialysis. Why the other options fail: option a lies the client flat, which increases venous return into already flooded lungs, and gives more fluid. Option b exercises a hypoxic client and does nothing for the oedema. Option c gives an osmotic load by push and withholds oxygen from a client saturating at 84%.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fluid overload pulmonary edema sequence: High-Fowler's position -> Stop IV fluids -> Apply High-flow O2 -> Urgent dialysis/diuretics.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Infuse by pump, check neurological status often, and keep the sodium rise within 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Run it by gravity through a 22-gauge hand vein, so that the infusion can be stopped quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Run it alongside 500 mL of 0.45% saline, so that the sodium is corrected more evenly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client to drink 2 litres of water, so that the sodium is distributed through the body"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hypertonic saline (3% NaCl) is a high-alert intravenous solution. Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia causes sudden osmotic dehydration of brain cells, resulting in catastrophic Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis), causing irreversible flaccid quadriplegia and 'locked-in' syndrome. Guidelines mandate strictly limiting the sodium correction rate to no more than 8-10 mEq/L over 24 hours, infused via pump with close neuro checks. Why the other options fail: option b gives a high-alert solution by gravity through a small peripheral vein, which cannot control the rate. Option c adds hypotonic fluid to a hypertonic infusion, which defeats the correction. Option d gives free water to a client who is already water-loaded.$q$,
  strategy = $q$3% Hypertonic Saline rule: High-alert infusion! Max correction rate: 8-10 mEq/L per 24 hours. Rapid correction causes fatal osmotic demyelination syndrome.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "5% dextrose at 150 mL/hour, to dilute the sodium and relieve the cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 100 mL bolus of 3% hypertonic saline over 10 to 15 minutes, to relieve the cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subcutaneous desmopressin 4 micrograms, to stop the inappropriate antidiuresis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral sodium chloride tablets with 1 litre of free water, which is safer than hypertonic saline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Severe acute hyponatremia (< 120 mEq/L) with neurological emergencies (seizures, coma, brainstem herniation) is caused by severe cerebral edema from osmotic water entry into brain cells. The emergency intervention is 100-150 mL of IV 3% hypertonic saline infused over 10-15 minutes (repeatable up to 2 times if seizures persist) to acutely raise serum sodium by 4-6 mEq/L, reversing brain swelling and halting seizures. Why the other options fail: option a gives free water, which lowers the sodium further and worsens the cerebral oedema. Option c treats the antidiuresis rather than the emergency, and subcutaneous desmopressin acts too slowly. Option d gives salt with a litre of water, which does not raise the sodium when the client is already retaining water.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe hyponatremic seizures (< 120 mEq/L): Immediate IV 3% hypertonic saline bolus to shrink cerebral edema and arrest seizures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL infused over 2 to 3 minutes to stabilise the myocardium"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insulin 10 units with 50 mL of 50% dextrose, which shifts potassium into the cells fastest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate 30 g, which removes potassium from the body"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg as a bolus, which excretes the potassium in the urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In severe hyperkalemia with electrocardiographic changes (peaked T waves, QRS widening), the client is at imminent risk of fatal ventricular fibrillation and asystole. The immediate first-line drug is IV Calcium gluconate (or calcium chloride). Calcium does not lower potassium; it directly antagonizes potassium's membrane-depolarizing effects, stabilizing the myocardial cell membrane within 1-3 minutes. Insulin and dextrose are given next to shift potassium into cells. Why the other options fail: option b is the correct second drug, but it shifts potassium over 15 to 30 minutes and does not protect the myocardium now. Option c works over hours in the gut. Option d depends on kidneys that are not making urine. Only calcium stabilises the membrane within minutes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hyperkalemia emergency sequence: 1) IV Calcium gluconate FIRST (stabilizes heart membrane), 2) IV Insulin + D50 (shifts K+ into cells), 3) Eliminate K+ (dialysis / kayexalate).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give it as a slow direct push over 60 seconds, which is safe when the volume is small"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Run it by gravity through a 24-gauge vein in the dorsum of the hand, to dilute it quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warm the bag in hot water first, since warmth prevents the vein irritation potassium causes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Never give it as a push or bolus: use a pump and keep the peripheral rate at or below 10 mEq/hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Intravenous potassium chloride is a high-alert medication. Direct IV push or rapid bolus administration causes sudden lethal cardiac arrest (used in lethal injection protocols). Safety mandates: 1) NEVER give IV push, 2) Always infuse diluted in IV fluids using a dedicated electronic infusion pump, 3) Limit peripheral infusion rate to a maximum of 10 mEq/hour (maximum 20 mEq/hr via central venous line with continuous cardiac monitoring). Why the other options fail: option a is a direct push, which is the lethal practice the item tests. Option b uses gravity through the smallest available cannula, so neither the rate nor the dilution can be controlled. Option c warms a high-alert solution without addressing the rate, and warmth does not prevent phlebitis from potassium.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Potassium safety rule: NEVER IV PUSH (causes immediate cardiac arrest!). Max peripheral rate = 10 mEq/hour. Always use an infusion pump.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Visual acuity and intraocular pressure, which fall early in magnesium toxicity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Patellar deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate and hourly urine output"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Capillary glucose every 15 minutes, which detects the hypoglycaemia magnesium causes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bowel sounds in all four quadrants, which disappear as magnesium accumulates"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$During intravenous magnesium sulfate therapy, toxicity manifests progressively: loss of deep tendon reflexes (DTRs) occurs first at serum levels > 4-5 mEq/L, followed by respiratory depression (< 12 breaths/min) at > 8-10 mEq/L, and cardiac arrest at > 12-15 mEq/L. Magnesium is excreted renally; oliguria causes dangerous drug accumulation. The nurse must assess patellar reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output before and during infusion. Calcium gluconate is the antidote. Why the other options fail: option a describes parameters that magnesium does not affect early, and the eye is not the target organ. Option c looks for hypoglycaemia, which is not the recognised toxicity pattern. Option d is a late and non-specific finding, whereas reflexes are lost first and are the earliest reliable sign.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Magnesium toxicity surveillance: Loss of Deep Tendon Reflexes (DTRs) is the EARLIEST sign! Stop infusion and give IV Calcium gluconate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Irrigate the catheter daily with 50 mL of sterile saline, to keep the lumen clear"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hang the bag on the side rail while repositioning, so that it is not left on the floor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Disconnect the catheter from the tubing each morning, to empty and measure the urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the system closed and unobstructed, and hang the bag below the level of the bladder"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CAUTI prevention bundle evidence-based guidelines include: 1) Maintain a sterile, continuously closed drainage system (never disconnect tubing), 2) Keep the drainage bag below the level of the bladder at all times to prevent gravity backflow of contaminated urine into the bladder, 3) Hang bag on the bed frame (never on moving side rails or resting on the floor), 4) Secure catheter to the thigh to prevent urethral traction, and 5) Avoid routine antimicrobial bladder irrigation. Why the other options fail: option a irrigates a closed system routinely, which introduces organisms. Option b hangs the bag on a moving rail, where it can rise above the bladder and allow backflow. Option c breaks the closed system daily, which is the single commonest route to infection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CAUTI prevention rules: Bag ALWAYS below bladder level -> Keep system closed -> Secure to thigh -> Never hang on moving side rails.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediate oliguria and hyperkalemic cardiac arrest"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute nephrotic syndrome with severe hypoalbuminemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Post-obstructive diuresis, hypotension, and transient hematuria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe metabolic alkalosis from ammonia retention"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Rapid relief of chronic high-pressure urinary retention (> 800-1,000 mL) can trigger two well-known physiological responses: 1) Post-obstructive diuresis—a dramatic polyuric state caused by loss of medullary concentrating gradient and accumulated urea, requiring strict fluid balance monitoring to prevent hypovolemic shock, and 2) Transient hematuria and hypotension from sudden pelvic venous decompression and mucosal stretching. Why the other options fail: option a describes the opposite problem, since the risk after relief is polyuria rather than oliguria. Option b is a glomerular disease unrelated to decompression. Option d describes an acid-base consequence of vomiting or diuretics, not of relieving retention.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe urinary retention decompression: Monitor for post-obstructive diuresis (massive urine output leading to volume depletion and hypotension).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Minimal change nephrotic syndrome, which gives heavy proteinuria without haematuria"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which follows bloody diarrhoea with anaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute bacterial cystitis, which gives dysuria and frequency without oedema"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN) is an immune-complex mediated type III hypersensitivity reaction occurring 1-3 weeks after Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis or impetigo. Immune complexes deposit in glomerular basement membranes, causing inflammation and red blood cell leakage. Hallmarks: 1) Periorbital edema, 2) Tea- or cola-colored urine (gross hematuria with RBC casts), 3) Hypertension from sodium/water retention, and 4) Mild-to-moderate proteinuria. Why the other options fail: option a gives proteinuria without haematuria, and the cola-coloured urine is blood. Option b follows a diarrhoeal illness with anaemia and a low platelet count, not a sore throat two weeks earlier. Option d causes dysuria and frequency without oedema, hypertension or haematuria.$q$,
  strategy = $q$APSGN triad: Recent strep infection + Tea/cola-colored urine (hematuria) + Periorbital edema & hypertension. Contrast with Nephrotic (massive proteinuria, no gross blood).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A high-sodium diet with liberal fluid, to restore the oncotic pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Strict infection precautions, with daily weight and abdominal girth measurements"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complete immobilisation with strict bed rest for 4 weeks, to reduce protein loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High-dose intravenous potassium chloride, to replace the losses in the urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by massive proteinuria, severe hypoalbuminemia, generalized edema (anasarca), and hyperlipidemia. Crucial nursing considerations: 1) Infection risk is critically elevated because immunoglobulin G (IgG) and complement proteins are lost in urine, and the child is treated with corticosteroids, 2) Daily weights and abdominal girth measurements track ascites, 3) Sodium restriction reduces edema, and 4) Meticulous skin care prevents breakdown of edematous tissues. Why the other options fail: option a adds sodium and fluid to a child who is already anasarcous. Option c imposes immobility, which increases the risk of thrombosis and skin breakdown in oedematous tissue. Option d gives potassium to a child who has not been shown to be hypokalaemic, and the risk here is infection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nephrotic Syndrome hallmarks: Massive Proteinuria + Hypoalbuminemia + Severe Edema (anasarca). Priority: Protect from infection (lost antibodies in urine).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take them on an empty stomach at bedtime, with a full glass of water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take them 2 hours after breakfast, together with the iron supplement"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dissolve the tablets in grapefruit juice an hour before lunch, which improves absorption"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the tablets with meals and snacks, so that they bind dietary phosphate in the gut"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In advanced CKD, failing kidneys cannot excrete phosphorus, causing hyperphosphatemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism. Phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer) work locally in the gut lumen by binding dietary phosphorus into insoluble, unabsorbable complexes that are excreted in feces. For phosphate binders to be effective, they MUST be taken WITH meals and snacks (during or immediately after eating). If taken on an empty stomach, calcium acetate acts as a systemic calcium supplement without binding phosphorus. Why the other options fail: option a takes the binder on an empty stomach, when there is no dietary phosphate for it to bind. Option b separates it from the meal and adds iron, which it can also bind. Option c dissolves it in juice before food, which achieves the same failure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Phosphate binder golden rule: Take WITH MEALS AND SNACKS! It must bind the food phosphorus in the gut before it gets absorbed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An absence of pulsation or sound over the anastomosis, which shows the vein is not distended"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A blood pressure of 120/80 measured with a cuff on the fistula arm, which shows flow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cool, pale skin with absent radial pulses in the left hand, which shows a steal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Palpate a palpable thrill and auscultate a continuous blowing bruit over the fistula site"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$An arteriovenous fistula surgically anastomoses a high-pressure artery to a low-pressure vein, producing high-velocity turbulent blood flow. Functional patency is confirmed by: 1) Palpating a purring vibration ('thrill'), and 2) Auscultating a continuous whooshing sound ('bruit') over the access site. Absence of a thrill or bruit indicates fistula thrombosis and acute access occlusion, requiring immediate surgical vascular intervention. Why the other options fail: option a describes the absence of flow, which is the sign of a thrombosed fistula. Option b takes a blood pressure on the access arm, which can occlude the fistula and should never be done. Option c describes steal or occlusion, not patency.$q$,
  strategy = $q$AV Fistula patency: 'Feel the thrill, hear the bruit!' No thrill or bruit = clotted access (vascular emergency!). Never take BP or draw blood from fistula arm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Send the effluent for cell count and culture, notify the nephrologist, and start intraperitoneal antibiotics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that cloudy fluid is expected from dietary protein breakdown"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warm the dialysate bag in a microwave before the next dwell, to dissolve the sediment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clamp the catheter permanently and stop all exchanges for a week, to rest the peritoneum"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cloudy or turbid peritoneal dialysate effluent is the earliest and most classic clinical hallmark of bacterial peritonitis in peritoneal dialysis (most commonly caused by touch contamination of the catheter spike during fluid exchanges). Normal dialysate drainage should be completely clear and straw-colored. The nurse must collect fluid for white blood cell count (> 100/mcL with > 50% neutrophils) and culture, and administer intraperitoneal broad-spectrum antibiotics. Why the other options fail: option b treats a sign of infection as normal, which delays treatment of peritonitis. Option c warms the bag in a microwave, which damages the glucose in the dialysate and does not treat infection. Option d stops dialysis altogether, which leaves the infection untreated and the client without clearance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Peritoneal dialysis red flag: CLOUDY/TURBID effluent drainage + abdominal pain = PERITONITIS. Send fluid for culture and start intraperitoneal antibiotics.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Air embolism through the venous line, which presents with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An acute pyrogenic reaction to endotoxin in the dialysate, with rigors and fever"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome, from rapid solute clearance and cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe hypovolaemic collapse from excessive ultrafiltration during the session"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS) occurs primarily during initial hemodialysis sessions in clients with severely elevated BUN. Rapid clearance of urea from the vascular compartment creates a transient osmotic gradient between the blood and brain cells (where urea clears slowly due to the blood-brain barrier). Free water shifts into brain tissue, causing acute cerebral edema and increased ICP (headache, nausea, confusion, seizures, herniation). Management: slow blood flow, stop dialysis, administer hypertonic saline or mannitol. Why the other options fail: option a presents with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain, not headache and hypertension. Option b produces rigors and fever at the start of the session, not focal neurology. Option d produces hypotension, whereas this client is hypertensive and bradycardic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome: First-time dialysis + severe BUN -> rapid urea drop -> cerebral edema (headache, nausea, seizures). Prevent by slow initial dialysis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the IV furosemide slowly at a rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the entire 160 mg dose as a rapid IV push over 15 seconds"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Mix the furosemide in 50 mL of 10% dextrose and infuse over 1 minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Combine the furosemide in the same syringe with an aminoglycoside antibiotic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$High-dose intravenous loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide) can cause permanent sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and ototoxicity if administered too rapidly, especially in clients with impaired renal clearance. Safety standards mandate that high-dose intravenous furosemide must be infused slowly at a controlled rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute (160 mg should be infused over at least 40 minutes, either by slow injection or controlled IV piggyback). Why the other options fail: option b pushes a high dose in 15 seconds, which is the fastest way to cause the ototoxicity the item is about. Option c still runs the dose in a minute, which far exceeds 4 mg/min. Option d mixes a loop diuretic with an aminoglycoside in one syringe, which compounds the same toxicity.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Furosemide rate limit: Never exceed 4 mg/min! Rapid bolus causes permanent ototoxicity (tinnitus and deafness), especially in renal impairment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "21 gtt/min"}, {"id": "b", "text": "31 gtt/min"}, {"id": "c", "text": "42 gtt/min"}, {"id": "d", "text": "125 gtt/min"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Standard IV drop rate formula: Drops per minute (gtt/min) = [Total Volume (mL) x Drop Factor (gtt/mL)] ÷ Total Time (minutes). Calculation: 1) Total volume = 1,000 mL. 2) Drop factor = 15 gtt/mL. 3) Total time = 8 hours x 60 minutes = 480 minutes. 4) (1,000 x 15) ÷ 480 = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25 drops/min, which rounds to 31 drops/minute. Why the other options fail: option a uses a 10 gtt/mL set for the calculation. Option c doubles the volume to 2,000 mL. Option d divides by the drop factor instead of multiplying. The rate follows only from 1,000 mL times 15 gtt/mL over 480 minutes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Drip Rate Formula: (Volume in mL x Drop factor) / Time in minutes. (1,000 x 15) / 480 = 15,000 / 480 = 31 gtt/min.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Turning to the left splints the right bronchus and increases its airway resistance, which diverts blood away from the diseased lobe and towards the healthy one"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The left lateral position compresses the heart against the mattress and increases left ventricular stroke volume, which raises pulmonary blood flow to both lungs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lying on the left lung reduces surfactant breakdown in the diseased right alveoli, because the healthy lung takes over the work of surfactant production"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gravity increases blood perfusion to the healthy dependent left lung, improving ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In unilateral lung disease, positioning the healthy, unaffected lung in the dependent (downward) position promotes gravity-dependent blood flow to the well-ventilated healthy lung tissue. This optimizes ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) matching and maximizes systemic oxygenation ('good lung down'). Placing the diseased lung downward causes blood to perfuse poorly ventilated alveoli, causing intrapulmonary shunting and hypoxemia. Why the other options fail: option a treats the good result as a mechanical splinting of the diseased bronchus, but airway resistance is not what determines where blood flows; hypoxic vasoconstriction is, and it is opposed by gravity. Option b invents a stroke volume effect that would not raise saturation this quickly or this selectively. Option c gives a surfactant explanation for a change that occurred within five minutes, far too fast for surfactant turnover to alter.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Unilateral lung disease positioning rule: 'Good lung down' (dependent). Gravity maximizes blood flow to the well-ventilated lung, improving V/Q ratio.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Decreased tactile fremitus, hyperresonance to percussion, and absent breath sounds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Decreased tactile fremitus, tympany to percussion, and low-pitched musical rhonchi"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Absent tactile fremitus, stony dullness to percussion, and faint tracheal breath sounds"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Increased tactile fremitus, dullness to percussion, and bronchial breath sounds with egophony"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Alveolar consolidation (fluid, exudate, and cellular debris replacing air in pneumonia) conducts sound vibrations better than normal air-filled lung tissue. Hallmarks over consolidated tissue: increased tactile fremitus (enhanced vibration on palpation), dullness to percussion (fluid-filled tissue), loud bronchial breath sounds (high-pitched tubular sounds heard in peripheral fields), and positive egophony (spoken 'ee' sounds like 'ay'). Why the other options fail: option a describes a pneumothorax, where air in the pleural space both reduces the transmission of sound and hyper-resonates on percussion. Option b mixes decreased fremitus with tympany, the percussion note of a hyperinflated or gas-filled space rather than consolidated lung. Option c describes a large pleural effusion, where fluid blocks transmission completely and gives stony dullness.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Consolidation physics: Dense liquid conducts sound better than air -> increased fremitus, dull percussion, bronchial breath sounds, egophony.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase oxygen flow to 20 L/min and administer a bolus of intravenous naloxone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client to breathe into a paper bag to retain carbon dioxide, since the somnolence suggests the carbon dioxide level has fallen too far on high-flow oxygen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Replace the non-rebreather mask with a Venturi mask titrated to maintain SpO2 between 88% and 92%, and prepare for ventilatory support"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer an intravenous sedative to treat oxygen-induced panic, because the agitation and resistiveness suggest the client is fighting the mask"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In chronic CO2 retainers with advanced COPD, central chemoreceptors become desensitized to chronic hypercapnia, and hypoxemia becomes a major respiratory drive. Indiscriminate high-flow oxygen blunts this hypoxic drive and worsens ventilation-perfusion mismatch (halting hypoxic vasoconstriction and dead space ventilation), causing acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (CO2 narcosis). The nurse must switch to controlled, precise oxygen delivery (Venturi mask targeting SpO2 88-92%) and prepare for non-invasive positive-pressure ventilation. Why the other options fail: option a increases the oxygen that caused the problem and adds naloxone, which reverses opioids and not carbon dioxide. Option b is the rebreathing treatment for hyperventilation, which is the opposite acid-base disturbance. Option d sedates a client whose respiratory drive is already failing, which risks respiratory arrest rather than treating the cause.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CO2 retention / COPD target: SpO2 88-92% via Venturi mask. Sudden somnolence and bradypnea on high-flow O2 indicates acute CO2 narcosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pre-oxygenate with 100% oxygen for at least 30-60 seconds and limit suction catheter application to 10-15 seconds only during withdrawal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply continuous suction pressure at 180 mmHg throughout insertion and withdrawal, since a higher pressure removes thick secretions more completely and shortens the time the catheter is in the airway"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instil 10 mL of sterile normal saline into the tube before suctioning to loosen thick secretions, since moistening the mucus makes it easier to clear and reduces the number of passes required"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rotate the catheter continuously for 30 seconds while applying maximum suction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Evidence-based endotracheal suctioning guidelines mandate: 1) Pre-oxygenate with 100% O2 for 30-60 seconds, 2) Advance catheter without applying suction until resistance or carina is reached, then pull back 1-2 cm, 3) Apply intermittent suction only while withdrawing and rotating the catheter, 4) Limit suction duration to no more than 10-15 seconds to avoid severe hypoxemia and vagal bradycardia, and 5) Maintain suction pressure between 80-120 mmHg. Routine saline instillation is contraindicated because it dislodges bacteria into lower airways. Why the other options fail: option b applies suction during insertion at a pressure above the recommended range, which strips oxygen and traumatises the mucosa on the way down. Option c instils saline routinely, which is no longer recommended because it dislodges biofilm into the lower airways. Option d applies maximum suction for 30 seconds, twice the safe duration, and risks hypoxaemia and vagal bradycardia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Suction safety rules: Pre-oxygenate 100% -> No suction on insertion -> Max 10-15 seconds on withdrawal -> No routine saline instillation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Call for immediate emergency help, extend the client's neck, open the stoma with a tracheal dilator, and gently reinsert a spare lubricated tube with the obturator"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cover the stoma with an airtight occlusive dressing and instruct the client to breathe deeply through the mouth, since the trachea will re-open spontaneously"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a large nasogastric tube into the stoma to hold the airway patent, since the tube will stent the tract open until the surgical team arrives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the client in the prone position and give small oral sips of water to lubricate the larynx, so that the upper airway can open and airflow resumes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Accidental decannulation in an immature tracheostomy tract (under 7 days post-op) is a critical airway emergency because the stomal tract collapses rapidly. The nurse must immediately call for assistance, maintain neck extension to open the stoma, gently grasp stay sutures or use a tracheal dilator to spread the stoma, and insert the spare lubricated tracheostomy tube with the obturator (removing the obturator immediately once inserted). If reinsertion is impossible, ventilate via bag-valve-mask over the stoma or mouth. Why the other options fail: option b seals the only route the client has for breathing, which converts a partial obstruction into a complete one. Option c passes a feeding tube into an airway that has no cartilage to hold it, which neither stents the tract nor delivers oxygen. Option d puts a distressed client face down and gives fluid by mouth, both of which worsen an airway that is already compromised.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fresh tracheostomy decannulation: Immediate help -> extend neck -> use dilator / stay sutures -> insert spare tube with obturator. Always keep spare tube and obturator at bedside.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Compensated metabolic acidosis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Step-by-step ABG analysis: 1) pH 7.26 is below 7.35, indicating acidosis. 2) PaCO2 58 mmHg is above 45 mmHg (acidic), indicating that respiratory retention of carbon dioxide is the primary cause of the acidosis. 3) HCO3 24 mEq/L is within normal limits (22-26 mEq/L), meaning renal compensation has not yet occurred. Therefore, this is uncompensated respiratory acidosis secondary to opioid-induced hypoventilation. Why the other options fail: option a is wrong on both counts, since the pH is acidotic rather than compensated and the bicarbonate is normal rather than raised. Option c treats a high carbon dioxide with a low pH as alkalosis, which confuses the direction of the primary change. Option d names metabolic alkalosis, which would require a raised bicarbonate and an alkaline pH.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ABG Drill: pH < 7.35 (acidosis) + high PaCO2 (respiratory) + normal HCO3 (uncompensated) = Uncompensated Respiratory Acidosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respiratory alkalosis from excessive CO2 exhalation; coach the client in slow, controlled diaphragmatic breathing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Metabolic acidosis from lactic acid accumulation during the panic attack, which requires intravenous sodium bicarbonate to correct the pH before the underlying anxiety is addressed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Respiratory acidosis from alveolar fatigue; initiate immediate mechanical ventilation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Metabolic alkalosis from gastric acid loss during the episode, which requires a litre of normal saline infused rapidly to restore the chloride and correct the bicarbonate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tachypnea and hyperventilation blow off excessive carbon dioxide (hypocapnia, PaCO2 25 mmHg), driving blood pH up to 7.54 (respiratory alkalosis). Hypocapnia induces cerebral vasoconstriction (lightheadedness) and acute hypocalcemia from increased albumin-calcium binding (perioral tingling, tetany). The primary intervention is coaching the client into calm, slow, deep abdominal breathing or rebreathing to restore normal arterial PaCO2. Why the other options fail: option b attributes the picture to lactic acidosis, but the pH is high and the bicarbonate is normal, which rules out a metabolic cause. Option c calls a high pH with a low carbon dioxide respiratory acidosis, inverting the finding. Option d invents gastric acid loss and answers it with a fluid bolus that does nothing for a respiratory alkalosis driven by hyperventilation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hyperventilation = blowing off CO2 -> low PaCO2 + high pH = Respiratory Alkalosis. Calming breathing coach is first-line.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Primary respiratory alkalosis driving compensatory renal bicarbonate excretion, because the deep rapid breathing has lowered the carbon dioxide and the kidneys are shedding bicarbonate in response"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis, with Kussmaul respirations acting as the compensatory respiratory mechanism to blow off CO2"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis requiring immediate endotracheal intubation, because the carbon dioxide of 28 with a pH of 7.24 indicates the lungs are failing to clear sufficient acid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fully compensated metabolic alkalosis with secondary alveolar hypoventilation, because both the bicarbonate and the carbon dioxide are abnormal and neither value has returned to its reference range"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The primary pathology is metabolic acidosis driven by ketoacid accumulation: pH 7.24 (acidosis) and HCO3 11 mEq/L (severely depressed). In response, the medullary respiratory center stimulates Kussmaul breathing (deep, rapid respirations) to eliminate carbonic acid as CO2. Because PaCO2 is lowered to 28 mmHg in an attempt to normalize pH, but pH remains below 7.35, this is partially compensated metabolic acidosis. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the direction of the primary disorder; a bicarbonate of 11 with a pH of 7.24 is a metabolic acidosis, and the low carbon dioxide is compensation rather than a primary alkalosis. Option c calls it respiratory even though the carbon dioxide is falling and the bicarbonate is the abnormal value. Option d describes metabolic alkalosis, which would show a raised bicarbonate and an alkaline pH.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Kussmaul breathing is the body's compensatory respiratory response to severe metabolic acidosis (DKA). It blows off volatile acid (CO2) to raise pH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rapid clinical improvement after the bronchodilator, because the disappearance of wheeze means the bronchospasm has been reversed by the salbutamol"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Resolution of the bronchospasm allowing laminar airflow, since a quiet chest is the finding that shows the airways are no longer narrowed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Imminent respiratory arrest due to severe airflow limitation and alveolar exhaustion ('silent chest')"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Spontaneous conversion of asthma to bronchitis, since the change from wheeze to a quiet chest suggests the inflammation has moved to the larger airways"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The 'silent chest' is an ominous, life-threatening emergency sign in acute severe asthma. The complete cessation of wheezing in an exhausted, drowsy client does NOT indicate recovery; it indicates that bronchospasm, mucus plugging, and muscle fatigue have so severely reduced airflow that the client cannot move enough air to create wheezing sounds. Immediate endotracheal intubation and mechanical ventilation are required. Why the other options fail: option a reads the disappearance of wheeze as improvement, which is the classic error the item is testing; in an exhausted client a silent chest means airflow has fallen too low to generate sound. Option b gives the same misreading a physiological justification that does not hold. Option d invents a change of diagnosis, when what has changed is the movement of air.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Asthma warning sign: The 'silent chest' (disappearance of wheezing with continued distress/exhaustion) signals impending respiratory arrest!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It stimulates the diaphragm to contract at twice its normal velocity, which increases the volume of air moved with each breath and reduces the respiratory rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It increases rapid dead space ventilation to blow off residual nitrogen, which resets the respiratory centre and reduces the sensation of breathlessness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It creates positive expiratory backpressure in the airways, preventing premature bronchiolar collapse during exhalation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It directly relaxes the vascular smooth muscle of the pulmonary capillary bed, which lowers pulmonary artery pressure and improves gas exchange"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In COPD, loss of lung elastic recoil leads to dynamic collapse of small, floppy bronchioles during expiration, trapping air inside hyperinflated alveoli. Pursed-lip breathing (inhaling through the nose for 2 seconds, exhaling slowly through pursed lips for 4 seconds) creates positive expiratory backpressure throughout the airways, splinting them open, promoting alveolar emptying, and reducing work of breathing. Why the other options fail: option a attributions a velocity change to the diaphragm that pursed-lip breathing does not produce. Option b describes increased dead space and nitrogen elimination, neither of which is the mechanism and both of which would worsen gas exchange. Option d locates the effect in the pulmonary vasculature, whereas the benefit is in the airways, where the back pressure holds collapsing bronchioles open.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pursed-lip breathing mechanism: Generates positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) to prevent airway collapse and reduce air trapping.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer IV ceftriaxone -> Apply oxygen -> Collect blood cultures -> Administer paracetamol"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply oxygen -> Collect blood and sputum cultures -> Administer IV ceftriaxone -> Administer paracetamol"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer paracetamol -> Collect blood cultures -> Administer IV ceftriaxone -> Apply oxygen"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Collect sputum culture in 24 hours -> Administer IV ceftriaxone immediately -> Apply oxygen"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Priority sequence: 1) Oxygenation first (ABC principle) to relieve hypoxemia. 2) Diagnostic microbiologic specimens (blood cultures from two separate sites and sputum culture) MUST be collected BEFORE initiating antimicrobial therapy to avoid sterilizing specimens and compromising organism identification. 3) Initiate broad-spectrum antibiotics promptly within 1-2 hours. 4) Administer antipyretic for symptom relief. Why the other options fail: option a gives the antibiotic before the cultures, which sterilises the specimen the team is about to take. Option c postpones oxygen behind three other tasks, when the client is hypoxaemic and confused. Option d delays the sputum specimen by 24 hours, which is the opposite of the same error: a specimen taken that late is likely to grow nothing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sepsis & Pneumonia rule: Oxygen first -> Cultures BEFORE antibiotics -> Start antibiotics promptly. Never give antibiotics before drawing cultures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Airborne Infection Isolation Room (AIIR) with negative air pressure, fit-tested N95 respirator mask for staff, and surgical mask on client during transport"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Droplet precautions in a private room with a surgical mask for staff within three feet of the client, eye protection for airway procedures, and the door kept closed during care"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contact precautions with gown and gloves on entering the room, a dedicated stethoscope left inside the room, and hand hygiene before and after every contact with the client"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Standard precautions with a regular adult surgical mask for all nursing staff, gloves for body fluid contact, and no restriction on the client moving around the ward"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Mycobacterium tuberculosis is transmitted via droplet nuclei (< 5 micrometers) that remain suspended in the air. Mandatory airborne precautions include: 1) Negative-pressure airborne infection isolation room (AIIR) exhausting at least 6-12 air changes/hour, 2) Healthcare personnel must wear a fit-tested particulate respirator (N95 or higher) prior to entering, and 3) The client wears a standard surgical mask if leaving the room for essential transport. Why the other options fail: option b uses droplet precautions, which suit organisms spread by large droplets over short distances, not the small droplet nuclei of tuberculosis. Option c uses contact precautions, which do not address airborne transmission at all. Option d offers standard precautions with a surgical mask, which does not filter the particle size that carries tuberculosis and is fitted to no one.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tuberculosis transmission = AIRBORNE. Requires: Negative-pressure room (AIIR) + N95 respirator mask for staff + surgical mask on patient during transport.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop all the medications immediately and attend the emergency department for renal function tests, since discoloured urine indicates acute renal failure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "This indicates severe hepatic failure from pyrazinamide, so double the fluid intake and return to the clinic for liver function tests in the morning"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take two iron tablets daily to replace the haemoglobin being lost in the urine, and increase dietary protein until the colour returns to normal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"This reddish-orange discoloration is an expected, harmless side effect of Rifampicin and is not a sign of bleeding or drug toxicity.\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Rifampicin causes benign reddish-orange discoloration of all bodily fluids (urine, sweat, saliva, tears, feces). It may permanently stain soft contact lenses. The nurse should reassure the client that this is harmless and does not indicate hematuria or liver damage. In contrast, hepatitis (yellow jaundice) or Ethambutol-induced optic neuritis (loss of red-green visual acuity) requires immediate medical evaluation. Why the other options fail: option a stops therapy on the basis of a harmless effect, which risks resistance and relapse. Option b attributes the colour to hepatic failure, which does not discolour urine orange and would come with jaundice rather than a red-orange tint. Option c treats an imagined haemoglobin loss with iron, which does not change the colour and duplicates a drug the client may already be taking.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rifampicin = Red/Orange body fluids (benign!). Ethambutol = Eyes (optic neuritis). Isoniazid = INH -> Neuropathy (give B6 / pyridoxine).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Left lateral decubitus with the diseased lung dependent, so that gravity holds the fluid against the chest wall and away from the mediastinum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Flat supine with the legs elevated in Trendelenburg, so that the effusion pools away from the diaphragm and the needle can be inserted lower"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sitting upright on the edge of the bed, leaning forward over an overbed table padded with pillows"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prone with a pillow beneath the abdomen, so that the weight of the chest falls forward and widens the posterior intercostal spaces"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The optimal position for thoracentesis is upright sitting on the edge of the bed, leaning forward with arms resting on a pillow-padded overbed table. This position widens the intercostal spaces, spreads the ribs, allows fluid to pool at the base of the pleural space, and provides safe access for needle insertion while minimizing visceral pleural puncture. Why the other options fail: option a lies the client on the diseased side, which lets the effusion pool against the site of puncture and restricts breathing on the good side. Option b positions the client head-down, which pushes the diaphragm and the effusion upwards into the field and makes the tap more difficult. Option d places the client face down, which neither widens the intercostal spaces nor gives access to the posterior chest.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thoracentesis positioning: Upright, leaning forward over a padded bedside table (opens intercostal spaces and pools fluid at the base).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The chest tube is functioning normally and the lung has fully re-expanded, since bubbling shows that air is moving freely through the system"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An air leak is present in the drainage system or pleural space"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The suction control chamber is set to an inadequate negative pressure, so the wall suction should be turned up until the bubbling stops"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Subcutaneous emphysema is resolving into the intrapleural space, which is why the bubbling has become continuous rather than intermittent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In a closed underwater-seal drainage system: Tidaling (fluid fluctuations with respirations) is normal. Intermittent bubbling on exhalation is expected initially when air is evacuated from a pneumothorax. Continuous, vigorous bubbling in the water-seal chamber indicates an air leak—either in the system tubing/connections or inside the client's chest (bronchopleural fistula or unresolved large pleural tear). The nurse must locate the leak immediately. Why the other options fail: option a treats continuous bubbling as evidence of re-expansion, when it is the sign of an ongoing air leak. Option c confuses the water-seal chamber with the suction control chamber; bubbling in the latter is normal and bubbling in the former is not. Option d invents a mechanism by which subcutaneous emphysema would resolve into the pleural space, which is not how gas moves.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Chest drain chamber rule: Continuous bubbling in WATER-SEAL chamber = AIR LEAK. Continuous bubbling in SUCTION control chamber = NORMAL suction.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediately re-insert the expelled chest tube into the stoma with sterile forceps"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an airtight occlusive dressing taped securely on all four sides with heavy silk tape"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Clamp the remaining tubing and submerge the tip in sterile water"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cover the insertion site immediately with a sterile petroleum gauze dressing taped on THREE sides"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a chest tube is accidentally dislodged, the priority is to seal the wound while preventing tension pneumothorax. Cover the site immediately with sterile petrolatum gauze taped on THREE sides. This creates a one-way flutter valve: it prevents external atmospheric air from being sucked into the pleural cavity during inspiration, but allows trapped intrapleural air to escape during expiration, averting tension pneumothorax. Why the other options fail: option a re-inserts a contaminated tube into the pleural space, which risks infection and creates a fresh tract. Option b seals the wound on all four sides, which prevents inspired air escaping and can produce the tension pneumothorax the dressing is meant to avoid. Option c clamps and submerges, which is the correct response to a tube that has disconnected from the drain, not one that has come out of the client.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Accidental chest tube removal drill: Sterile gauze taped on 3 SIDES (flutter valve avoids tension pneumothorax). If tube disconnects from unit, submerge in sterile water.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Transport the client to the ultrasound room for lower limb Doppler imaging, since confirming the diagnosis before treatment avoids unnecessary anticoagulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give oral aspirin 300 mg with water, since platelet inhibition limits further clot propagation and can be given without waiting for imaging"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instruct the client to cough vigorously to clear a suspected mucus plug, since the sudden dyspnoea and pleuritic pain suggest large airway obstruction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Elevate the head of the bed to high-Fowler's position, apply high-flow oxygen, and activate the rapid response team"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acute pulmonary embolism causes sudden obstruction of pulmonary arterial blood flow, producing acute alveolar dead space, severe hypoxemia, and acute right heart strain. Immediate priority nursing actions: 1) Position in high-Fowler's to maximize diaphragmatic excursion and ease breathing mechanics, 2) Apply high-flow oxygen immediately to treat tissue hypoxia, 3) Activate emergency/rapid response team, and 4) Establish IV access for anticoagulation (unfractionated heparin). Why the other options fail: option a leaves a hypoxaemic client with a heart rate of 124 to go for a leg scan before receiving oxygen. Option b gives an antiplatelet that does not treat a pulmonary embolus and delays oxygen and anticoagulation. Option c mistakes a vascular emergency for a mucus plug and asks the client to cough while severely hypoxaemic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Suspected PE sequence: High-Fowler's position -> High-flow Oxygen -> Activate Rapid Response Team. Never leave patient or delay O2 for imaging.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Prone positioning increases cardiac preload by compressing the inferior vena cava, which raises the pressure driving blood through the pulmonary circulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prone positioning decreases tidal volume, which reduces barotrauma and the risk of pneumothorax in already stiff ARDS lungs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prone positioning recruits collapsed dorsal lung units and improves ventilation-perfusion matching"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prone positioning halts alveolar exudate secretion by reversing the hydrostatic gradient, so that fluid stops leaking into the alveolar spaces"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In ARDS, alveolar collapse and inflammatory consolidation occur predominantly in dependent, dorsal lung regions. In the supine position, the weight of the heart and ventral tissue further compresses these dorsal lung units. Placing the client in the prone position (proning) relieves cardiac compression, recruits large dorsal alveolar regions, and redistributes pulmonary blood flow, markedly improving ventilation-perfusion matching and arterial oxygenation. Why the other options fail: option a describes a compression that would reduce, not increase, venous return and has no relation to shunt physiology. Option b presents a reduction in tidal volume as the goal, when lung-protective ventilation already sets it and proning does not lower it. Option d attributes the improvement to a change in fluid secretion, whereas proning works by opening dorsal lung units that were perfused but not ventilated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ARDS proning rationale: Recruits collapsed dorsal lung alveoli, relieves heart compression on lung bases, and enhances V/Q matching.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Swallow a glass of warm milk immediately after the second puff, since the milk coats the pharynx and washes the steroid off the mucosa"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rinse mouth thoroughly with water and spit it out after each inhalation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Inhale rapidly through the mouth and exhale immediately through the nose, since a fast inhalation delivers more of the drug to the lower airways"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use the fluticasone inhaler only as a rescue medication when wheezing starts, since using it every day leads to tolerance of its effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Inhaled corticosteroids (fluticasone, budesonide) deposit small drug particles onto the oral pharyngeal mucosa, which can suppress local immunity and cause oral candidiasis (thrush) and dysphonia (hoarseness). Using a spacer device reduces oropharyngeal deposition, and rinsing the mouth with water and spitting it out immediately after each use clears residual steroid particles from oral tissues. Why the other options fail: option a coats the mucosa with milk rather than removing the drug from it. Option c describes a rapid inhalation, which increases oropharyngeal deposition and worsens the very problem the rinse is meant to solve. Option d treats a daily maintenance corticosteroid as a rescue bronchodilator, which both under-treats the asthma and exposes the client to thrush daily.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inhaled steroid rule: Use spacer + RINSE AND SPIT after inhalation to prevent oral thrush and hoarseness. Steroids are maintainers, not rescue bronchodilators.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the aminophylline infusion immediately, notify the prescriber, and anticipate a stat serum theophylline level"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Increase the infusion rate by 5 mL per hour to overcome tachyphylaxis, since the return of wheeze means the current dose is no longer adequate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer an oral antiemetic and reassure the client that the tremor is an expected effect, since it usually settles after the first week of therapy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer 100 mg of oral potassium chloride for suspected hypokalaemia, since theophylline drives potassium into cells and causes the arrhythmia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Aminophylline/theophylline has a narrow therapeutic range (typically 10-20 mcg/mL). Toxicity manifests initially with gastrointestinal symptoms (nausea, vomiting), neurological excitation (tremors, restlessness, seizures), and cardiovascular dysrhythmias (tachycardia, PVCs, ventricular tachycardia). The nurse must immediately stop the infusion, alert the physician, obtain a stat serum theophylline concentration, and prepare supportive antidotes (activated charcoal, beta-blockers). Why the other options fail: option b increases the dose of the drug producing the toxicity. Option c treats the nausea and leaves the infusion running, when the serum level is likely above the therapeutic range. Option d assumes a hypokalaemia that theophylline does not reliably cause in this way, and gives potassium without a level while ignoring the drug that is actually responsible.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Theophylline toxicity signs: Nausea/vomiting + tremors + tachycardia/PVCs (narrow therapeutic window 10-20 mcg/mL). Stop infusion stat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RESP-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Research is always local and unpublished, while QI is always multicentre and generalisable"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Research needs board review only when it is funded, while QI never requires ethical oversight"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Research and QI differ only in name, and the same methods and approvals apply to both"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Research tests hypotheses to produce generalisable knowledge; QI improves processes and outcomes within one facility"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nursing research is a systematic scientific inquiry designed to generate new generalizable knowledge, discover relationships, or test biological/behavioral hypotheses that can be applied beyond the local institution. Quality Improvement (QI), by contrast, uses established evidence and systematic data-guided activities to bring about immediate, practical improvements in local healthcare delivery processes and outcomes within a specific clinical setting. Research typically requires formal IRB ethical oversight; research is intended for dissemination; and they have distinct methodologies and governance. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the ethical requirement, because both research and QI can need review depending on risk. Option b makes publication the distinction, which is false. Option c collapses a real methodological and regulatory difference into a matter of naming.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Research vs. QI: Research generates new, generalizable knowledge; Quality Improvement optimizes local clinical processes using existing evidence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The independent variable is hospital discharge status; the dependent variable is room temperature"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The independent variable is the 10-minute warm foot bath; the dependent variable is sleep quality"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The independent variable is sleep quality; the dependent variable is the warm foot bath"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The independent variable is client age; the dependent variable is the nurse's years of experience"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In scientific research, the Independent Variable (IV) is the experimental intervention, treatment, or condition manipulated by the researcher (the presumed cause—here, the 10-minute warm foot bath). The Dependent Variable (DV) is the outcome or response measured to determine the effect of the intervention (the presumed effect—here, the client's sleep quality). Why the other options fail: option a names variables that the study does not manipulate or measure. Option c reverses independent and dependent. Option d names the client's age, which is not the intervention, and the nurse's experience, which is not an outcome.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Variables: Independent variable = Intervention/Cause (manipulated). Dependent variable = Outcome/Effect (measured).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Null hypothesis, predicting no difference between the two groups"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Non-directional hypothesis, predicting a difference without stating which group will do better"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Directional research hypothesis, predicting that one named group will score lower than the other"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Complex qualitative hypothesis, drawn from themes rather than a measured outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A directional research hypothesis explicitly predicts the direction of the expected relationship or difference between groups (e.g., specifying that group A will have 'lower anxiety' or 'higher scores' than group B). A non-directional hypothesis predicts a difference exists without specifying which group will be higher or lower (e.g., 'there will be a difference in anxiety scores'). A null hypothesis states that no relationship or difference exists between the variables (e.g., 'there will be no difference in anxiety scores'). Qualitative studies explore phenomena rather than testing statistical hypotheses. Why the other options fail: option a predicts no difference, which this hypothesis does not. Option b is non-directional, but this hypothesis names which group will do better. Option d is not a qualitative hypothesis, because it specifies groups and a measured outcome.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hypotheses: Directional specifies direction (higher, lower, greater); Non-directional predicts difference without direction; Null predicts no difference.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Manipulation of the independent variable, a control group and random allocation to the arms"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pre-test and post-test measurement, a convenience sample and blinding of the statistician only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Randomisation and a sample size calculation, because manipulation is implied by the intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Manipulation and control alone, because randomisation is optional if the sample is large"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A true experimental design or Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT) requires three essential scientific pillars: 1) MANIPULATION: The researcher actively introduces an intervention (the independent variable) to some participants; 2) CONTROL: The researcher maintains a control or comparison group that does not receive the experimental treatment (e.g., placebo, standard care); 3) RANDOMIZATION: Participants are randomly assigned to groups, ensuring every subject has an equal probability of assignment and balancing confounding baseline characteristics. Why the other options fail: option a omits randomisation, which is what distinguishes a true trial from a quasi-experiment. Option b describes a weak observational design. Option c lists desirable features but not the three defining properties.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pillars of a true experiment (RCT): Manipulation, Control group, and Randomization.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Case-control study, comparing past exposures in people with and without the outcome"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prospective cohort, following exposed and unexposed people forward for the outcome"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cross-sectional survey, measuring exposure and outcome at the same time in one sample"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retrospective cohort, following a defined exposed group back through the records for the outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A case-control study is a retrospective observational design that begins with the outcome: individuals who already have the disease or condition (cases) are matched with individuals who do not (controls), and researchers look back in time (retrospectively) to determine differences in prior exposure to risk factors (calculating an Odds Ratio). A prospective cohort study starts with exposed and unexposed groups free of disease and follows them forward in time. A cross-sectional study measures exposure and outcome simultaneously at a single point in time. An RCT involves active experimental randomization. Why the other options fail: option b follows people forward from exposure, which is the opposite direction. Option c measures exposure and outcome at one time. Option d starts from exposure, not from the outcome.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Study designs: Case-control starts with diseased cases vs. healthy controls and looks backward for past exposures (retrospective). Cohort follows groups forward in time.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A statistical power calculation of 80%, which sets the number of participants needed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A type II statistical error, which arises when the sample is too small to detect an effect"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Data saturation, the point at which further interviews yield no new themes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cronbach alpha of 0.88, which shows the interview guide is internally consistent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In qualitative research, sample size is not determined by pre-study statistical mathematical formulas (power analysis). Instead, sampling continues until 'Data Saturation' (or informational redundancy) is achieved—the point at which gathering additional participant data no longer reveals new perspectives, properties, or themes. Statistical power, Type II errors, and Cronbach's alpha are quantitative psychometric and inferential statistical concepts irrelevant to qualitative sampling closure. Why the other options fail: option a is a quantitative sample-size calculation. Option b is a statistical error about failing to detect an effect. Option d is a measure of internal consistency, not of thematic exhaustion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Qualitative research standard: Sample size is determined by Data Saturation—continuing interviews until no new themes or information emerge.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Double-blind randomisation of transcripts to two coders, so neither knows the hypothesis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Member checking, returning transcripts and themes to participants to confirm the findings"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cluster sampling of participants, so that each adolescent's account is weighted equally"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prolonged engagement in the field, so that the researcher becomes a familiar presence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Member checking (also known as participant validation) is a core method used in qualitative research to establish Credibility (the qualitative parallel to internal validity). The researcher shares transcripts, analytic categories, or preliminary thematic findings with study participants to allow them to confirm whether the interpretations accurately and authentically represent their true lived realities. Randomization, cluster sampling, and regression belong exclusively to quantitative statistical methods. Why the other options fail: Double-blind allocation of transcripts guards against coder bias but does not return findings to participants; cluster sampling changes how participants are selected and says nothing about trustworthiness; prolonged engagement builds familiarity over time but is not a check on whether the themes match what participants meant.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Qualitative trustworthiness: Member checking involves sharing findings with participants to verify the accuracy of the researcher's interpretations.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stratified random sampling, with the roster divided into tiers and participants drawn at random from each"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Purposive sampling, with key informants chosen from each region and type of hospital"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Quota sampling, with the researcher filling each regional tier to a set number by convenience"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cluster sampling, with whole facilities selected at random and every nurse in them surveyed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Stratified random sampling is a probability sampling method in which the target population is first divided into mutually exclusive, homogeneous subgroups or strata (e.g., geographical regions, hospital tiers, clinical cadres), and random samples are drawn proportionally from each stratum. This ensures that smaller or minority sub-populations are adequately and representatively reflected in the final sample, minimizing sampling error. Snowball, convenience, and purposive are non-probability sampling methods prone to severe selection bias. Why the other options fail: Purposive sampling selects informants deliberately and cannot give proportional representation; quota sampling fills each tier by convenience rather than at random; cluster sampling selects whole facilities and does not guarantee proportional representation across every tier.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sampling methods: Stratified random sampling divides the population into strata (subgroups) and randomly samples within each stratum to ensure representativeness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It shows the scale is invalid, because alpha must be below 0.05 to be significant"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It shows an unacceptable error rate of 88%, so the tool needs revision"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It shows strong internal consistency, so the items correlate and measure the same construct"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It shows high content validity, because a high alpha means the items cover the construct"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cronbach's alpha ($\alpha$) is a measure of internal consistency reliability for multi-item psychometric scales, evaluating the degree to which items that propose to measure the same general construct produce similar scores. A Cronbach's alpha of 0.70 to 0.79 is considered acceptable, 0.80 to 0.89 is good/strong, and $\ge 0.90$ is excellent. An alpha of 0.88 confirms strong internal reliability. It is not a p-value where $< 0.05$ is sought; it does not denote an error rate; and reliability does not automatically guarantee content validity, which evaluates whether items cover the full conceptual domain. Why the other options fail: option a applies a p-value threshold to a reliability coefficient. Option b misreads 0.88 as an error rate. Option d treats internal consistency as evidence of content coverage, which it is not.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Psychometrics: Cronbach's alpha measures internal consistency reliability. Values $\ge 0.70\text{--}0.80$ indicate good-to-strong reliability.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Test-retest reliability, because the same clients are assessed on two occasions"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Inter-rater reliability, because two raters scoring the same clients are being compared"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Predictive validity, because the Braden score forecasts who will develop a pressure injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Internal consistency, because the six Braden subscales are summed into one score"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Inter-rater (or inter-observer) reliability measures the degree of consistency or agreement between two or more independent raters or observers scoring the same subjects using the same instrument at the same point in time (often quantified by Cohen's kappa or intra-class correlation). High inter-rater reliability ensures that assessment scores reflect true client characteristics rather than idiosyncratic examiner biases. Predictive validity examines whether a score predicts future events; construct validity tests whether an instrument measures a theoretical trait; face validity is superficial inspection. Why the other options fail: Test-retest compares one rater on two occasions; predictive validity asks whether the score forecasts who develops an injury; internal consistency asks whether the subscales hang together, when the question is agreement between two people.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Measurement reliability: Inter-rater reliability assesses consistency between two or more independent observers scoring the same subjects.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Obtain the child's assent alone, because the child is the participant and the parent cannot consent"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Obtain parental permission alone, because the child is legally unable to assent before age 18"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obtain parental permission and the child's developmentally appropriate assent, respecting a refusal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain permission from the school head teacher, who acts in loco parentis during school hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In pediatric research ethics, minors lack legal competence to provide legal informed consent. Ethical standards mandate obtaining: 1) legal informed consent (permission) from the parent(s) or legal guardian; and 2) affirmative, developmentally appropriate 'Assent' from the child (typically children aged 7 and older who possess cognitive capability). If a child refuses to assent to a non-therapeutic research protocol, their dissent must be respected and cannot be overridden by parents or teachers. Community permission complements but never replaces individual family consent. Why the other options fail: Parental permission alone omits the child's assent; the child's assent alone inverts the requirement, since the parent consents and the child assents; a school head teacher acting in loco parentis cannot consent to research on the child's behalf.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pediatric research ethics: Parental informed permission + Child developmentally appropriate assent. Respect the child's dissent.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Misconduct, negligence and malpractice, which are the three legal categories of research fraud"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fabrication, falsification and duplication, where duplication is publishing the same study twice"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Plagiarism, self-plagiarism and salami slicing, where the same data are split across papers"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fabrication, falsification and plagiarism: making up data, altering data, and using text without attribution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The internationally recognized federal and academic definition of Scientific Research Misconduct encompasses 'FFP': 1) FABRICATION: Making up data or results and recording or reporting them as real; 2) FALSIFICATION: Manipulating research materials, equipment, or processes, or changing/omitting data such that research is not accurately represented; and 3) PLAGIARISM: Appropriating another person's ideas, processes, results, or words without giving appropriate credit. Options a, b, and c refer to clinical torts, crimes, or communications rather than scientific research misconduct. Why the other options fail: Substituting duplication for plagiarism names three related publishing practices rather than the three acts; naming plagiarism, self-plagiarism and salami slicing describes how data are sliced and reported; misconduct, negligence and malpractice are not the three categories of research fraud.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Research misconduct: FFP = Fabrication (making up data), Falsification (altering data), Plagiarism (copying without citation).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The median, because it is resistant to extreme outliers and reflects the midpoint of a skewed distribution"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The mean, because the arithmetic average uses every value and is always the best summary"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The mode, because the most frequent value is the only valid average for skewed data"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The standard deviation, because it describes the spread around the arithmetic centre"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When continuous data are normally distributed (bell-shaped symmetrical curve), the Mean and standard deviation are the preferred parametric measures. However, when data are skewed (asymmetrical with extreme outliers, such as length of stay or healthcare costs), the arithmetic Mean is heavily distorted by extreme values. The Median (the 50th percentile) is non-parametric and resistant to outliers, making it the most accurate measure of central tendency for skewed datasets. The standard deviation is a measure of dispersion, not central tendency. Why the other options fail: option b is pulled upward by the 90-day outliers, which is the problem. Option c ignores all but the most frequent value. Option d measures spread rather than centre.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Biostatistics rule: Use the Mean for symmetrical, normally distributed data. Use the Median for skewed data with extreme outliers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "250 is the incidence and 1,200 the prevalence: incidence counts new cases, prevalence all existing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "250 is the prevalence and 1,200 the incidence, because prevalence counts new cases each year"}, {"id": "c", "text": "250 is the incidence rate and 1,200 the cumulative incidence measured over the same period"}, {"id": "d", "text": "250 is the case fatality rate and 1,200 the attack rate among close household contacts"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Incidence and prevalence are fundamental epidemiological measures. INCIDENCE is the number of NEW cases arising in a population at risk during a stated period: here, 250 newly diagnosed cases in 2025. PREVALENCE is the TOTAL number of existing cases, new and old, alive with the disease at a point or over a period: here, 1,200 people under treatment during the year. The same population of 100,000 is the denominator, so 250 is an incidence and 1,200 a prevalence, not a rate expressed per population. Why the other options fail: option b swaps the two definitions and claims that prevalence counts new cases, which is the definition of incidence. Option c invents a cumulative incidence for 1,200, which is a count of existing cases rather than a measure of new ones. Option d uses mortality and contact measures, neither of which describes 250 new cases or 1,200 existing cases.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Epidemiology: Incidence = NEW cases (inflow). Prevalence = ALL existing cases (total burden).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A sampling error, because the sample was not representative of the population"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A type I error, rejecting a true null hypothesis and declaring an inert drug effective"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A type II error, failing to detect a real effect because the sample was too small"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A confounding error, because a third variable produced the apparent effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In hypothesis testing: A TYPE I ERROR (alpha error, false positive) occurs when the researcher rejects the null hypothesis when the null hypothesis is actually true in reality (concluding that a treatment works when it is actually ineffective). A TYPE II ERROR (beta error, false negative) occurs when the researcher fails to reject the null hypothesis when it is actually false (concluding that a treatment does not work when it actually does, often due to inadequate sample size/power). Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error. Why the other options fail: A sampling error is about who was selected, not about the inference drawn; a type II error is failing to detect a real effect, which is the opposite of what happened; confounding by a third variable is not in play when the drug is inert and the hypothesis is false.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Statistical errors: Type I error = False Positive (claiming a difference when none exists). Type II error = False Negative (missing a real difference).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An odds ratio below 1.0 means the rinse increases the odds of pneumonia rather than reducing them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The interval is wide, so the finding is inconclusive until a much larger trial is completed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The point estimate of 0.62 lies close to 1.0, so the effect is clinically negligible"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The interval excludes 1.0, so the reduction in odds is statistically significant and the rinse is protective"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$For ratio statistics such as Odds Ratios (OR) and Relative Risk (RR), the null value (indicating no difference between groups) is 1.0. If the 95% Confidence Interval does NOT include 1.0, the finding is statistically significant at $p < 0.05$. An OR of 0.62 with a 95% CI of [0.48 to 0.81] means that clients receiving chlorhexidine have 38% lower odds of developing pneumonia, and because the upper bound (0.81) is below 1.0, the protective effect is statistically significant. If the CI crossed 1.0 (e.g., [0.75 to 1.25]), it would not be statistically significant. Why the other options fail: Asking for a larger trial ignores that the interval already excludes one point zero; judging clinical importance from how close the point estimate sits to one point zero confuses statistical with clinical significance; reading an odds ratio below one point zero as harm reverses its direction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Interpreting Odds Ratios & 95% CI: For ratios (OR/RR), the null value is 1.0. If the 95% CI excludes 1.0, the finding is statistically significant.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A chi-square test of independence, which compares two categorical variables"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A Kruskal-Wallis test, which compares three or more independent group medians"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An independent samples t-test, which compares two independent group means"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A Kaplan-Meier analysis, which compares time to an event between groups"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$An Independent Samples Student's t-test is the standard parametric inferential statistical test used to determine whether there is a statistically significant difference between the means of two independent groups on a continuous, normally distributed dependent variable (e.g., mean blood pressure in mmHg). A Chi-square test compares categorical proportions between groups. Kruskal-Wallis is non-parametric for 3 or more groups. Kaplan-Meier analyzes time-to-event survival data. Why the other options fail: option a compares categorical variables. Option b compares medians across three or more groups. Option d compares time to an event.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Choosing statistical tests: Comparing continuous means between 2 independent groups = Independent t-test. Comparing categorical proportions = Chi-square.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sensitivity 92% and specificity 96%, because the healthy clients supply the true positive rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sensitivity 96% and specificity 92%, and the positive predictive value must equal the sensitivity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sensitivity 4% and specificity 8%, because these are the proportions of false results in each group"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sensitivity 96% and specificity 92%, because 96 of 100 cases and 92 of 100 non-cases were correctly classified"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$SENSITIVITY is the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals who HAVE the disease (True Positive Rate = True Positives / Total Diseased = 96 / 100 = 96%). SPECIFICITY is the ability of a test to correctly identify individuals who DO NOT HAVE the disease (True Negative Rate = True Negatives / Total Non-diseased = 92 / 100 = 92%). High sensitivity rules OUT disease when negative (SnNOut); high specificity rules IN disease when positive (SpPIn). Why the other options fail: The version that swaps the two measures gives the non-case rate to sensitivity; adding a predictive value that cannot be derived from these numbers overreaches; reporting the false-result proportions as sensitivity and specificity misreads what was counted.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diagnostic accuracy: Sensitivity = ability to detect true positives (sick people). Specificity = ability to identify true negatives (healthy people).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask, acquire, appraise, apply and assess, in that order, beginning with a PICO question"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess, apply, appraise, acquire and ask, working backwards from the outcome"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acquire, appraise, apply, assess and ask, with the question formed after the search"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask, appraise, acquire, apply and assess, with appraisal done before the search"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The evidence-based practice process follows the classic 5 A's sequential framework: 1) ASK: Convert clinical need into a structured, answerable clinical inquiry (PICO); 2) ACQUIRE: Efficiently search biomedical databases for the best available evidence; 3) APPRAISE: Critically evaluate the evidence for validity, reliability, and clinical applicability; 4) APPLY: Integrate critical evidence with clinical expertise, patient values/circumstances, and available resources; 5) ASSESS: Evaluate the outcomes of the practice change and identify areas for ongoing improvement. Why the other options fail: option a begins with the search before the question. Option c puts appraisal before the question. Option d appraises before acquiring the evidence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$The 5 A's of EBP: Ask -> Acquire -> Appraise -> Apply -> Assess.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Closing the audit loop, because the re-audit shows the changes achieved the standard"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The reporting phase, because the loop closes when the results are published"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The first audit, because the baseline measurement is what starts the cycle"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The research phase, because a re-audit is equivalent to a controlled trial"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A clinical audit is incomplete without 'closing the audit loop'. The complete audit cycle consists of: 1) setting standards; 2) measuring baseline practice; 3) comparing practice against standards; 4) implementing targeted changes; and 5) re-auditing practice. The re-audit verifies whether implemented interventions achieved sustained compliance with standards, closing the loop and establishing clinical governance. Audits are quality improvement activities, not punitive misconduct trials or falsifications. Why the other options fail: The reporting phase closes the loop on publication rather than on measured improvement; the first audit is the baseline that started the cycle; calling a re-audit a controlled trial overstates what it is.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical audit cycle: Setting standards -> Measuring -> Implementing change -> Re-auditing (Closing the loop).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RSCH-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Complementary medicine refers to non-mainstream healing practices used together WITH conventional biomedical care, whereas alternative medicine refers to non-mainstream practices used IN PLACE OF conventional biomedical care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Complementary medicine is used IN PLACE OF conventional treatment and replaces it entirely, whereas alternative medicine is used ALONGSIDE conventional treatment and never instead of it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complementary medicine refers only to mind-body therapies such as meditation and yoga, whereas alternative medicine refers only to herbal products and traditional botanical preparations"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The two terms are interchangeable in everyday clinical use, and the only real distinction is whether the practitioner holds a current state licence to treat clients"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to WHO and NCCIH definitions: 1) COMPLEMENTARY MEDICINE involves using non-mainstream healing practices (e.g., acupuncture, herbal teas, guided imagery) alongside and in conjunction with conventional evidence-based medical treatment. 2) ALTERNATIVE MEDICINE involves using non-mainstream practices to substitute for or replace conventional medical treatment (e.g., using a botanical infusion instead of chemotherapy for invasive cancer). 3) INTEGRATIVE HEALTH brings conventional and complementary approaches together in a coordinated, evidence-informed manner. Options b, c, and d mischaracterize both terms. Why the other options fail: option b reverses the two definitions; option c assigns each term to a therapy type rather than describing how it is used with or instead of conventional care; option d denies a distinction that both WHO and NCCIH define.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TCAM definitions: Complementary = used TOGETHER WITH conventional medicine. Alternative = used INSTEAD OF conventional medicine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The Medical and Dental Council together with the Pharmacy Council, which license both the individual practitioners and the herbal products they sell in their clinics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The Traditional Medicine Practice Council (TMPC) under Act 575 (for practitioner and premises licensing) and the Food and Drugs Authority (FDA) Ghana (for herbal product registration and safety certification)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The Ghana Health Service together with the Ministry of Chieftaincy and Religious Affairs, which jointly oversee all traditional healing practice and its premises"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The Food and Drugs Authority acting alone, which registers herbal products for sale and also licenses traditional practitioners and accredits their clinics"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under Ghanaian statutory law, the Traditional Medicine Practice Act 2000 (Act 575) established the Traditional Medicine Practice Council (TMPC) under the Ministry of Health to set standards, register, license, and regulate traditional medical practitioners (herbalists, bonesetters, naturopaths). Concurrently, the Food and Drugs Authority (FDA) Ghana (under the Public Health Act 851) evaluates the safety, efficacy, quality, toxicology, and labeling of all manufactured commercial herbal products before issuing formal FDA registration numbers (e.g., FDA/HD.xx). The EPA, Police, and Lands Commission have no mandate over clinical TCAM regulation. Why the other options fail: professional councils license clinicians, not herbal products or traditional practitioners; the Ghana Health Service delivers care rather than registering remedies; and the FDA registers products but does not license traditional practitioners.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ghana TCAM regulation: TMPC regulates practitioners and clinics (Act 575); FDA Ghana evaluates and registers herbal medicines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect the client's autonomy by making no comment on the vendor's claims, record the conversation accurately in the nursing notes, and let the oncology team raise the issue at the next scheduled review"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the client to postpone the next oncology review for two weeks so that the herbal mixture can be given a fair trial before any further imaging or chemotherapy is arranged"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that unregulated mixtures lack standardized dosing and quality control, warn against fraudulent 'cure-all' claims, emphasize that delaying proven oncology care allows cancer to metastasize, and inspect for FDA Ghana registration while reinforcing evidence-based therapy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Confiscate the jug and tell the client that all herbal products are toxic and should never be used for any reason, since no amount of regulation can make a botanical preparation safe"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Unregulated street concoctions present severe health hazards due to unstandardized dosing, microbial contamination, heavy metal adulteration, and dangerous herb-drug interactions. Furthermore, predatory 'cure-all' claims lead clients to abandon life-saving oncology care during curative windows, resulting in preventable metastasis and death. The nurse must validate the client's search for healing, provide objective risk education regarding uncertified remedies, check for FDA registration, and advocate for evidence-based cancer staging and treatment. Endorsing street mixtures or tasting client medicines is dangerous and unethical. Why the other options fail: saying nothing leaves a fraudulent cure claim unchallenged during a curative treatment window; postponing oncology review spends that window; and condemning all herbal products is both inaccurate and likely to end the conversation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Unregulated herbs: Warn against 'cure-all' claims and lack of quality control, emphasize the fatal risk of delaying conventional oncology treatment, and look for FDA certification.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Deep vein thrombosis of the calf; keep the limb elevated above the level of the heart and start anticoagulation with low-molecular-weight heparin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute compartment syndrome secondary to constrictive traditional splinting, requiring immediate splint removal and emergency orthopedic surgical fasciotomy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cellulitis complicating the splint; cut the splint off, elevate the limb and start intravenous antibiotics"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Delayed union of the tibial shaft; continue immobilisation in the same splint and arrange orthopaedic clinic review in six weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Traditional bonesetter gangrene (TBS gangrene) and acute compartment syndrome are well-documented severe pediatric surgical complications in Sub-Saharan Africa. Overly tight, unyielding traditional bamboo or wooden splints encasing an acutely fractured, swelling limb elevate intracompartmental pressures above capillary perfusion pressure. This causes ischemia, nerve necrosis, and muscle infarction (the 6 P's: Pain out of proportion, Pressure/tense calf, Paresthesia, Pallor, Paralysis, Pulselessness). The nurse must immediately cut off constricting splints, notify the orthopedic surgeon, and prepare for emergent surgical fasciotomy to prevent amputation. Why the other options fail: DVT does not render a limb pulseless and anticoagulation would not relieve compartment pressure; cellulitis does not cause pulselessness; and delayed union is a late radiographic finding, not an acute limb-threatening event.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Traditional bonesetter injury: Tight wooden splints on fractures cause Acute Compartment Syndrome (the 6 P's). Cut off splints immediately and prepare for emergency fasciotomy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uterine hyperstimulation and impending uterine rupture secondary to unmonitored herbal oxytocics, requiring immediate tocolysis, oxygen, IV resuscitation, and emergent cesarean delivery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal active first stage of labour; encourage ambulation and oral fluids while awaiting the next vaginal examination and partograph plotting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obstructed labour from cephalopelvic disproportion; prepare for an assisted vaginal delivery in theatre with neonatal resuscitation available"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uterine atony with postpartum haemorrhage; start an oxytocin infusion, massage the fundus and prepare for blood transfusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Indigenous herbal uterotonics (such as *kaligbali*, *aworoworo*, or *Cassia* extracts) contain potent oxytocic alkaloids that cause uncoordinated, tetanic uterine contractions without periods of physiological relaxation. When administered during obstructed or prolonged labor without fetal monitoring, this causes severe placental hypoperfusion, profound fetal bradycardia, and catastrophic uterine rupture with maternal exsanguination. The nurse must call the obstetrician immediately, position left lateral, administer oxygen, start large-bore IV fluid resuscitation, administer tocolytics if protocol directs, and rush the patient to the operating theater. Calling this normal is fatal negligence. Why the other options fail: normal labour does not produce tetanic contractions with a fetal heart rate of 60 beats/min; cephalopelvic disproportion does not itself cause tetanic contraction; and oxytocin in an already hyperstimulated uterus increases the risk of rupture.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Herbal uterotonics: Indigenous oxytocic herbs administered during labor cause uterine hyperstimulation, fetal asphyxia, and uterine rupture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Allow the prayer and anointing straight away, since spiritual rituals carry no infection risk and delaying them would show disrespect for the family's faith at a frightening time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Decline the visit until the client is discharged, because religious gatherings are a recognised source of hospital-acquired infection on inpatient wards and in side rooms"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Facilitate the pastoral prayer and anointing visit at a mutually suitable time, ensuring standard infection control (hand hygiene) is maintained, while ensuring that intravenous antibiotics and medical treatments continue uninterrupted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Postpone the visit until the antibiotic course is finished, so that prayer does not interfere with the action of the intravenous antibiotic or reduce its effectiveness"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In pluralist healthcare environments, spiritual beliefs and faith-based practices play a vital therapeutic role in client coping, psychological well-being, and hope. The nurse should support beneficial spiritual rituals (prayer, anointing) provided they do not introduce infection risks or disrupt necessary medical therapy. Coordinating visiting times, enforcing hand hygiene, and maintaining continuous antibiotic administration bridges faith and evidence-based science harmoniously. Arresting clergy causes emotional distress; stopping antibiotics leads to septic death; pouring oil into IV lines causes fatal oil embolism and sepsis. Why the other options fail: spiritual rituals are not risk-free and still require infection control; refusing the visit confuses infection prevention with spiritual care; and prayer does not interfere with antibiotic action.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Faith-based integration: Accommodate and support spiritual rituals that bring comfort while ensuring infection control and uninterrupted medical therapy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "St John's Wort is a potent inhibitor of hepatic CYP3A4 and raises dolutegravir concentrations, risking central nervous system toxicity, insomnia and abnormal dreams within the first fortnight"}, {"id": "b", "text": "St John's Wort has no pharmacokinetic interaction with antiretroviral drugs at all, but it commonly causes photosensitivity and should be stopped for that reason alone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "St John's Wort chelates dolutegravir in the gut lumen, and separating the two doses by at least two hours completely prevents the interaction from occurring"}, {"id": "d", "text": "St John's Wort is a potent inducer of CYP3A4 and intestinal P-glycoprotein, accelerating antiretroviral metabolism, lowering plasma concentrations, and leading to treatment failure and viral drug resistance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$St. John's Wort (*Hypericum perforatum*) is a classic, high-yield NCLEX herb-drug interaction. It is a powerful inducer of hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes and P-glycoprotein (efflux transporter). Co-administration significantly lowers the serum concentration of drugs metabolized by CYP3A4—including antiretrovirals (protease inhibitors, NNRTIs, integrase inhibitors), oral contraceptives (causing unintended pregnancy), cyclosporine/tacrolimus (causing organ transplant rejection), and warfarin. It also causes serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs. St. John's Wort does not cure HIV and has extensive interactions. Why the other options fail: St John's Wort induces rather than inhibits CYP3A4, so concentrations fall; it is not pharmacokineticially inert; and the interaction is enzyme induction, not gut chelation, so separating doses does not correct it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High-yield NCLEX interaction: St. John's Wort INDUCES CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein, dropping blood levels of antiretrovirals, birth control, and immunosuppressants.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Garlic and ginkgo induce hepatic CYP2C9 and lower the INR, so the warfarin dose should be increased and the INR rechecked after one week of combined use"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Garlic is rich in vitamin K and directly antagonises warfarin, reducing its anticoagulant effect and raising the risk of valve thrombosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ginkgo biloba and Garlic possess intrinsic antiplatelet properties and inhibit platelet aggregation, which synergistically potentiates warfarin anticoagulation and dramatically increases severe hemorrhage risk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ginkgo and garlic have no measurable effect on haemostasis at all; only dietary vitamin K alters the INR during chronic warfarin therapy in a stable client"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The '4 G's' of herbal medicine—Ginkgo biloba, Garlic, Ginseng, and Ginger (plus Feverfew and Saw Palmetto)—exhibit antiplatelet properties and impair platelet aggregation. When taken concurrently with anticoagulants (warfarin, heparin, NOACs) or antiplatelet agents (aspirin, clopidogrel), they synergistically elevate bleeding risks, manifesting as spontaneous epistaxis, gastrointestinal bleeding, hematuria, or intracranial hemorrhage. The nurse must recognize this synergistic antiplatelet interaction and advise immediate discontinuation. Garlic does not clot blood or inhibit marrow vitamin K. Why the other options fail: the interaction is pharmacodynamic antiplatelet additivity, not CYP2C9 induction; garlic does not contain vitamin K; and haemostasis is affected well beyond dietary vitamin K.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NCLEX rule: The '4 G's' (Ginkgo, Garlic, Ginseng, Ginger) increase bleeding risk when taken with anticoagulants or antiplatelets.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chronic interstitial nephritis that carries no malignant potential once the offending tea is stopped and renal function is supported"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute tubular necrosis that recovers fully after withdrawal of the tea, with normal renal function documented at follow-up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Membranous nephropathy with nephrotic-range proteinuria but preserved glomerular filtration rate and no cancer risk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Aristolochic Acid Nephropathy (fibrosing interstitial nephritis) leading to end-stage renal disease, alongside a high risk of upper urothelial tract carcinoma"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Aristolochic acid (found in plants of the *Aristolochia* genus, often used in traditional slimming teas and rheumatic remedies) is a potent nephrotoxin and human carcinogen. It causes Aristolochic Acid Nephropathy (previously known as Chinese Herb Nephropathy or Balkan Endemic Nephropathy), characterized by extensive renal interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy without glomerular lesions, and rapid progression to end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Crucially, aristolochic acid forms DNA adducts that induce high rates of upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma. It does not cause reversible pancreatitis or cardiomyopathy. Why the other options fail: aristolochic acid nephropathy is fibrosing and irreversible with a documented urothelial cancer risk; it is not acute tubular necrosis that recovers; and it is not a podocytopathy with preserved filtration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Herbal toxicity: Aristolochic acid (*Aristolochia*) is severely nephrotoxic, causing irreversible fibrosing interstitial nephritis and urothelial carcinoma.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Continue the supplements and simply tell the anaesthetist on the morning of surgery that they have been taken, so the team can plan around them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop only St John's Wort and continue ginkgo and ginseng up to the day of surgery, since only the antidepressant herb affects anaesthesia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discontinue all herbal supplements immediately (at least 2 to 3 weeks prior to elective surgery) to prevent intraoperative hemorrhage, unstable hemodynamic fluctuations, and adverse anesthetic drug interactions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Continue all three but halve the dose for the week before surgery, so that the blood-thinning effect falls without losing the memory benefit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) and surgical safety guidelines mandate that all herbal remedies and dietary supplements should be discontinued at least 2 to 3 weeks prior to elective surgery. Herbals pose multiple perioperative hazards: Ginkgo and Ginseng inhibit platelet function and provoke intraoperative hemorrhage; St. John's Wort induces hepatic drug metabolism, altering anesthetic clearance and prolonging sedative recovery; Ephedra/Ginseng can trigger intraoperative cardiac arrhythmias and hypertensive crises. Doubling doses or hiding supplement use creates life-threatening surgical emergencies. Why the other options fail: disclosure on the morning of surgery is too late to reverse platelet inhibition or enzyme induction; leaving ginkgo and ginseng in place preserves the bleeding risk; and dose reduction does not remove the interaction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Perioperative rule: Stop all herbal supplements at least 2 to 3 weeks prior to elective surgery to prevent bleeding and anesthesia interactions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Herbal teas are acceptable in pregnancy provided they are taken as dilute infusions rather than concentrated capsules, since the active dose delivered to the fetus is much lower"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only herbs traditionally used for morning sickness are safe during the first trimester, because their safety in that specific indication is well established in practice"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Herbal preparations should be strictly avoided in pregnancy unless approved by an obstetric provider, because many herbs contain active compounds with emmenagogue or uterotonic properties that trigger uterine contractions, miscarriage, or fetal teratogenicity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Herbal remedies are acceptable after 12 weeks of gestation, once organogenesis is complete and the fetus is no longer vulnerable to malformation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Herbal supplements are not inherently safe in pregnancy. Many botanical preparations (e.g., Blue Cohosh, Pennyroyal, *Cryptolepis*, *Cassia*) possess potent emmenagogue, abortifacient, or uterotonic properties that stimulate uterine contractions, precipitate spontaneous abortion, or cause fetal teratogenic malformations. Furthermore, lack of standardization and risk of heavy metal contamination make unmonitored herbal ingestion particularly dangerous during first-trimester organogenesis. The myth that 'natural equals safe' is a widespread misconception that nurses must actively correct. Why the other options fail: infusion form does not make a uterotonic herb safe; herbs used for morning sickness include known emmenagogues; and organogenesis is not the only period of risk, because uterine activity can still cause loss later in pregnancy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Maternal-fetal safety: Avoid unprescribed herbal medicines during pregnancy; many herbs possess uterotonic properties that cause miscarriage or teratogenicity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stimulating specific neurovascular acupoints modulates autonomic pathways, triggers the release of endogenous endorphins and serotonin, and activates the Gate Control mechanism to suppress emetic and nociceptive signalling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It stimulates the vagus nerve directly at the wrist point, producing bradycardia and abolishing the vomiting reflex at its source in the medulla oblongata"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It works purely as a placebo; controlled trials show no measurable neurochemical or autonomic change beyond the psychological ritual of treatment itself"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It blocks the phrenic nerve on the affected side, paralysing the diaphragm and preventing the physical movement of vomiting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Acupuncture and acupressure (such as pressure at the P6 Neiguan point on the anterior forearm) are evidence-supported complementary therapies recognized by the WHO and oncology guidelines for managing postoperative and chemotherapy-induced nausea and pain. Physiological research demonstrates that acupoint stimulation activates A-beta and A-delta sensory fibers, modulates central autonomic nervous system centers, releases endogenous opioids (beta-endorphins, enkephalins), alters serotonin transmission, and engages the Gate Control Theory to blunt emetic and pain pathways. It does not destroy nerves or paralyze the diaphragm. Why the other options fail: acupressure does not stimulate the vagus directly to produce bradycardia; trial evidence shows measurable autonomic and neurochemical effects rather than pure placebo; and it does not paralyse the diaphragm.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Complementary therapies: Acupressure/acupuncture stimulates neurovascular pathways, releasing endogenous endorphins and modulating autonomic emetic and pain reflexes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chronic venous insufficiency with long-standing ankle oedema and brown skin staining of the lower leg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Active DVT in the affected limb, due to the risk of dislodging the thrombus and causing a fatal pulmonary embolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Osteoarthritis of the hip with restricted range of movement, morning stiffness and crepitus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Type 2 diabetes with symmetrical peripheral neuropathy and reduced sensation in both feet"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Therapeutic massage and manual soft tissue therapies are contraindicated in acute Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT), thrombophlebitis, local burns, infected wounds, and severe bleeding disorders. Massaging an extremity harboring an acute venous thrombus exerts mechanical shearing forces that dislodge the clot into systemic venous circulation, causing acute pulmonary embolism, cardiovascular collapse, and sudden death. Tension headaches, anxiety, and mild soreness are classic clinical indications for relaxation massage. Why the other options fail: venous insufficiency, osteoarthritis and diabetic neuropathy are relative cautions at most, and massage is commonly used for each; only an active thrombus carries the dislodgement risk that makes massage absolute.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Massage contraindications: NEVER massage an extremity with suspected or diagnosed DVT—mechanical friction dislodges the clot into a fatal pulmonary embolism.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A sharp rise in plasma catecholamines with increased heart rate, raised blood pressure and cold, clammy hands"}, {"id": "b", "text": "No measurable physiological change at all; the benefit of guided imagery is entirely subjective and cannot be measured"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Marked parasympathetic excess with a heart rate below 50 beats/min, hypotension and dizziness needing intravenous fluids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Decreased sympathetic nervous system output, reduced heart rate and respiratory rate, lowered blood pressure, and decreased peripheral muscle tension"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Herbert Benson's 'Relaxation Response' is the physiological opposite of the sympathetic fight-or-flight stress reaction. Mind-body complementary therapies (guided imagery, progressive muscle relaxation, meditation, deep diaphragmatic breathing) stimulate the parasympathetic nervous system via the vagus nerve. This causes decreased circulating catecholamines (epinephrine, norepinephrine) and cortisol, reduction in heart rate and respiratory rate, decreased blood pressure, dilation of peripheral blood vessels, and relaxation of skeletal muscle tension. Sympathetic surges or acidosis indicate worsening stress or pathology. Why the other options fail: a catecholamine surge is the stress response being treated, not the relaxation response; the effect is measurable rather than purely subjective; and symptomatic bradycardia with hypotension indicates overstimulation, not a normal relaxation response.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Relaxation response: Elicits parasympathetic activation—decreased heart rate, lower blood pressure, reduced respiratory rate, and muscle relaxation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Why did you stop listening to your doctor and start drinking herbal tea instead of taking the prescribed medication?"}, {"id": "b", "text": "'What do you call your problem, what do you think caused it, and what kind of treatment do you believe works best for you?'"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Do you understand that continuing this herbal tea will damage your liver within a month if you do not stop it now?"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Can you sign this form confirming that you have refused standard medical advice and accept the consequences?"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Arthur Kleinman's Explanatory Model questions elicit the client's personal understanding and cultural construction of their illness without passing judgment: 'What do you call your problem? What do you think caused it? Why do you think it started when it did? What does your sickness do to your body? What kind of treatment do you think you should receive?' Asking these open-ended, respectful questions uncovers health beliefs, explains herbal remedy use, and creates a non-defensive foundation for collaborative care. Condescending 'Why' questions and threats shut down dialogue. Why the other options fail: the remaining three options are judgemental or confrontational and close the interview; Kleinman's model requires open questions about the client's own explanation of the illness.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Kleinman's Explanatory Model: Use open, non-judgmental questions to elicit what the client believes caused their illness and how they think it should be treated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain the risks of uncontrolled hypertension, including stroke and kidney damage, and tell the client that the tablets must be taken exactly as prescribed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collaborate with the client on an agreed plan that keeps safe cultural preferences such as prayer and culinary garlic while maintaining daily antihypertensives and shared home blood pressure monitoring"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Listen carefully to the client's concerns about the tablets, then recommend the standard regimen without any further discussion of herbal alternatives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acknowledge the client's cultural beliefs, then agree to withhold antihypertensives for one month while the garlic water is given a fair trial"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The LEARN model is an evidence-based cross-cultural communication framework: 1) LISTEN with empathy; 2) EXPLAIN your clinical perceptions; 3) ACKNOWLEDGE and discuss differences and similarities; 4) RECOMMEND treatment; 5) NEGOTIATE agreement. In the Negotiation step, the nurse and client collaborate on a culturally acceptable, clinically safe compromise: accommodating the client's cultural and spiritual values (prayer, culinary garlic) while maintaining essential life-saving biomedical therapies (antihypertensive pills). Coercive threats provoke rejection; abandoning medicine leads to hypertensive stroke. Why the other options fail: explaining and instructing is the Explain and Recommend steps rather than Negotiate; recommending without discussion is also Recommend; and agreeing to withhold antihypertensives abandons the clinical bottom line instead of negotiating around it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$LEARN model: Negotiate a culturally congruent plan that honors the client's values while preserving essential evidence-based medical treatments.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "'Do you take any herbal teas, tonics, roots, vitamins, botanical mixtures, traditional enemas, or dietary supplements that you prepare at home or purchase from herbal practitioners?'"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Record 'no medications' exactly as stated and revisit the medication history at the point of discharge, when the client is less anxious"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask only about prescription medicines, since herbal products and traditional preparations are not medicines and do not need recording"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask whether the client takes any supplements at all, without naming specific examples or asking where they were obtained"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clients frequently do not consider herbal teas, roots, tonics, vitamins, dietary supplements, or traditional enemas (*bentua*) to be 'medicines' because they perceive them as natural foods or spiritual preparations. Consequently, simply asking 'Do you take any medications?' yields false negative responses. Best clinical practice requires asking explicit, routine follow-up questions detailing specific modalities: herbal teas, botanical mixtures, roots, enemas, vitamins, and over-the-counter supplements. Leaving records blank misses lethal interactions; searching luggage violates rights. Why the other options fail: recording a bare 'no' produces a false-negative history; restricting the question to prescription medicines reproduces the original error; and a general prompt about supplements does not cue traditional teas, roots or enemas.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Medication reconciliation: Explicitly ask about herbal teas, roots, tonics, enemas, and dietary supplements—clients rarely view these as 'medicines'.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Conducting scientific research on plant medicines, evaluating botanical safety, toxicology and efficacy, and standardising herbal formulations for national healthcare delivery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Licensing traditional medical practitioners, accrediting their premises and disciplining those who practise without valid registration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Supplying free herbal alternatives to public hospitals and clinics whenever conventional medicines are out of stock"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Setting the retail price of imported herbal products and approving the advertising claims that manufacturers make for them"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Centre for Plant Medicine Research (CPMR) at Mampong-Akuapem (established in 1975) is Ghana's premier national biomedical research institute dedicated to the scientific investigation of medicinal plants. CPMR conducts rigorous botanical, phytochemical, microbiological, pharmacological, and toxicological analyses on traditional herbal medicines, develops standardized efficacy-tested botanical products (e.g., *Cryptolepis* for malaria), and advises the Ministry of Health and FDA Ghana on safe integration into the public health system. Options b, c, bnd d are fictional absurdities. Why the other options fail: practitioner licensing is the TMPC's role; supplying medicines is a procurement function; and price setting is not a research function.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ghanaian plant research: The Centre for Plant Medicine Research (CPMR) at Mampong conducts scientific research, safety profiling, and standardization of botanical medicines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the product and advise the client to return to clinic if the jaundice deepens or confusion develops over the following week"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continue the product at a reduced dose, stop alcohol, and monitor liver enzymes monthly until they return to normal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Report the reaction only if a second client presents with the same pattern of liver injury within the same month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the product, secure the packaging and batch number for laboratory analysis, give supportive care, and file an Adverse Drug Reaction report with FDA Ghana"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$When an Adverse Drug Reaction (ADR) or toxic organ injury (e.g., herbal hepatotoxicity) is suspected: 1) the offending product must be discontinued immediately; 2) the physical product, packaging, and batch/lot numbers should be preserved for toxicological investigation; 3) medical supportive care initiated; and 4) an official Adverse Drug Reaction (ADR) report submitted to the national regulatory pharmacovigilance authority (such as the FDA Ghana Safety Watch System / Yellow Form). This enables regulatory alerts, recalls of contaminated batches, and public protection. Throwing evidence away, doubling toxic doses, or hiding events violates pharmacovigilance duty. Why the other options fail: watchful waiting delays the regulatory report that protects other clients; continuing the product sustains the injury; and waiting for a second case defeats the purpose of spontaneous reporting.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Herbal pharmacovigilance: Stop the offending herbal, preserve product batch details for analysis, and file an official Adverse Drug Reaction (ADR) report with regulatory authorities.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect the family's spiritual beliefs and delay insulin until the prayer sessions and any associated fasting are complete"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the spouse to leave the unit and exclude the family from all further discharge teaching about insulin and monitoring"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that faith and insulin are incompatible and ask the client to choose one approach or the other before discharge"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Provide empathetic, culturally congruent education on the lifelong necessity of insulin in type 1 diabetes, explore their beliefs without mockery, and negotiate spiritual practices that do not interrupt insulin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In type 1 diabetes, pancreatic beta-cell destruction causes absolute insulin deficiency; discontinuing insulin is rapidly fatal (DKA). Shaming, insulting, or berating pluralist clients creates deep psychological defensiveness, guilt, and alienation, driving them further away from conventional care. The nurse must validate their desire for spiritual comfort, respectfully explain the pathophysiology of type 1 diabetes (the body requires insulin just like it requires oxygen and water), emphasize that insulin is non-negotiable for life, and negotiate ways their spiritual beliefs and prayers can support their healing while maintaining strict daily insulin adherence. Why the other options fail: delaying insulin in DKA is lethal; excluding the family removes the people who administer care at home; and framing faith and insulin as incompatible forces a choice the client does not have to make.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pluralist health coaching: Respect spiritual beliefs, clearly explain non-negotiable biological therapies (like insulin in type 1 diabetes), and bridge cultural values with medical safety.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 460
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch32.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 32: MH. Regenerated from src/db after the letter-reference cleanup.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client lacks financial insight and is rapidly depleting the family's life savings"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The family can no longer cope with the client's behaviour at home and requests admission now"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client has stopped taking prescribed oral antipsychotic medication against medical advice"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A confirmed mental disorder with imminent risk of serious harm to self or others, or severe self-neglect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under Ghana's Mental Health Act 2012 (Act 846) and standard bioethical principles, involuntary admission overrides an individual's civil liberties and is legally justified ONLY when: (1) the person has a recognized mental disorder, and (2) as a result of that disorder, presents an imminent risk of serious physical harm to self (e.g., suicide or reckless life-threatening behavior) or to others, or is incapable of caring for basic survival needs. Refusing medication, squandering money, or family distress alone does not fulfill legal criteria for involuntary confinement. Why the other options fail: option a rests on financial behaviour, which is not a statutory ground for detention. Option b rests on the family's capacity to cope, which is a social problem rather than a legal criterion. Option c treats non-adherence alone as sufficient, when the Act requires a disorder plus risk or severe self-neglect.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Involuntary psychiatric commitment requires: Mental disorder + IMMINENT RISK OF HARM TO SELF OR OTHERS. Financial waste or eccentric behavior alone does not justify depriving a patient of freedom.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Summon security at once and apply four-point physical restraints to the bed rails"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confront the client firmly and warn that shouting will cost him his ward privileges"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give rapid intramuscular sedation without attempting any verbal engagement first"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stand at a safe distance, speak calmly and low, acknowledge the distress and offer a quiet area"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Least Restrictive Care dictates that verbal de-escalation must always be attempted before physical or chemical restraints. The nurse should: (1) Maintain personal safety by standing at an angle outside the client's personal space (at least two arm's lengths away) with hands visible and uncrossed; (2) Speak in a calm, controlled, non-threatening, low-pitched voice; (3) Validate the client's emotional distress ('I see that you are very upset'); and (4) Offer to move together to a low-stimulus environment. Confrontation escalates violence, while physical restraint is an emergency measure of last resort. Why the other options fail: options a and c jump to restraint and sedation, which the least-restrictive principle reserves for imminent violence after verbal measures fail. Option b confronts and threatens, which predictably escalates agitation rather than reducing it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$De-escalation hierarchy: Verbal de-escalation & low-stimulus environment FIRST -> PRN oral medication -> Seclusion/Physical restraint ONLY as an absolute last resort when imminent violence occurs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "You have a wonderful family who loves you dearly; you shouldn't think that way at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "You are feeling overwhelmed and worthless right now. Are you thinking about ending your life?"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Why do you believe you have failed your family? Everything will look brighter tomorrow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I understand exactly how you feel; I felt the same when I went through hard times"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Therapeutic communication acknowledges and reflects the client's feelings without judgment, minimization, or false reassurance, while directly addressing suicide risk. When a client expresses hopeless thoughts ('everyone would be better off if I were dead'), the nurse must directly assess suicidal ideation ('Are you thinking about ending your life?'). Why the other options fail: option a minimises the feeling and offers false reassurance. Option c opens with a why question that implies blame and adds another reassurance. Option d turns the exchange onto the nurse's own experience instead of the client's.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NCLEX Rule for Suicide Assessment: ALWAYS ask DIRECT, explicit questions about suicidal intent. Acknowledge feelings and never give false reassurance ('everything will be fine') or ask 'why' questions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assign a nursing assistant to carry out fifteen-minute scheduled visual safety checks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the prescribed oral lorazepam so that the client settles and sleeps tonight"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on continuous one-to-one observation within arm's reach and remove all hazards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to sign a written no-harm safety contract and document it in the chart"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$When a client communicates explicit, active suicidal intent with a plan, the client requires immediate, continuous One-to-One (1:1) direct visual observation. The staff member must remain within arm's length at all times, including during bathroom and hygiene routines. The environment must be thoroughly searched to eliminate strangulation hazards (belts, shoelaces, bedsheets, cords), glass, plastic bags, and sharps. Periodic 15-minute checks leave unsupervised windows where suicide can occur. 'No-harm contracts' have no proven efficacy in preventing suicide in high-risk patients. Why the other options fail: Fifteen-minute visual checks leave unobserved windows a determined client can use; sedation removes neither the means nor the intent; a written no-harm contract has no evidence of effectiveness and is no substitute for continuous observation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Active suicidal intent + plan = MANDATORY 1:1 continuous visual observation within arm's reach. 15-minute checks are unsafe for actively suicidal patients.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I do not hear any voices, but I see that you are frightened. What are the voices telling you?"}, {"id": "b", "text": "There is nobody in that corner shouting at you; you are imagining the whole thing again"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Why don't you simply tell the voices to go away and leave you alone for a while?"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I hear the shouting too, but we are quite safe here in the hospital so do not worry"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$When responding to auditory hallucinations, the nurse should: (1) Validate the client's reality and feeling while stating the nurse's reality ('I do not hear any voices, but I see that you are frightened'), and (2) Immediately assess for COMMAND hallucinations ('What are the voices telling you to do?'). Command hallucinations instructing self-harm or violence represent a psychiatric emergency. The nurse must never validate the hallucination as real, argue with or mock the client, or give advice to talk to the voices. Why the other options fail: option b denies the experience outright and damages the trust needed for assessment. Option c hands the problem to the client and skips the command-content assessment. Option d colludes with the hallucination, which reinforces it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hallucination protocol: Present reality calmly ('I don't hear them') + Validate emotion ('I see you are frightened') + ASSESS CONTENT ('What are they telling you to do?') to rule out command hallucinations.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Taste a spoonful of the food in front of the client to prove that it is not poisoned"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain that the kitchen staff are trained professionals who would never poison food"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acknowledge the fear without arguing, and offer factory-sealed foods the client can open"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withhold all food until the client accepts that the delusion is irrational and unfounded"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Delusions are fixed, false beliefs that cannot be corrected by logical reasoning, debate, or proof. Arguing or trying to 'prove' the food is safe (e.g., tasting it) reinforces the delusional dynamic or suggests the nurse is in collusion. The therapeutic approach is to validate the underlying emotion ('It must be terrifying to feel unsafe about what you eat') without validating the false belief, and offer pragmatic solutions such as unopened, factory-sealed packages (e.g., unopened cans, bread, fruit in peels) that the client can inspect and open personally. Why the other options fail: Tasting the food colludes with the delusion and may deepen it; telling the client that the staff would never poison food argues with a fixed belief that cannot be reasoned away; withholding food until the delusion breaks risks starvation and destroys trust.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Managing delusions of poisoning: Do not argue or debate. Offer factory-sealed, unopened foods/containers so the client feels safe eating.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that the client sit quietly at the dining table for thirty minutes at every meal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Engage the client in competitive group sports in the recreation hall to burn off energy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Offer high-calorie, high-protein finger foods and fortified drinks he can take while walking"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the client in four-point physical restraints until the hyperactivity ceases"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$During an acute manic phase, clients experience extreme hyperactivity, shortened attention span, and poor nutritional intake, placing them at severe risk of physical exhaustion, severe dehydration, and cardiovascular collapse. The priority physiological intervention is providing frequent, high-calorie, high-protein 'finger foods' (sandwiches, boiled eggs, fruit, cheese sticks) and fortified fluids that the client can consume on the move without sitting. Competitive sports are overstimulating and provoke aggression; insisting on sitting at a table causes escalation. Why the other options fail: Demanding stillness at every meal asks for a control a manic client cannot sustain and escalates the behaviour; competitive sport adds stimulation and injury risk to an already exhausted client; four-point restraint treats mania as if it were violence and leaves the exhaustion untreated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Manic patient care: High-calorie finger foods + Portable drinks + Low-stimulus private room. Manic clients can die from physical exhaustion and dehydration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The antidepressant has finally worked and the depressive episode is now completely cured"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client is at imminent risk of suicide, because energy has returned before hopelessness lifted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client has switched into a hypomanic episode and needs lithium titrated this evening"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client is recovered enough for immediate discharge to outpatient community follow-up"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A sudden, dramatic lift in mood, uncharacteristic cheerfulness, and giving away prized possessions in a severely depressed client (especially shortly after starting antidepressants or during early recovery) is a classic warning sign of impending suicide. When severe depression begins to lift, the patient's psychomotor retardation improves before mood improves; the patient now possesses the physical energy and cognitive focus required to execute a previously formulated suicide plan. Giving away possessions signifies finalizing personal affairs. Suicide precautions must be intensified immediately. Why the other options fail: option a misreads the lift in mood, which precedes recovery rather than proving it. Option c is a differential, but the timing and the giving away of possessions point to suicide risk, which must be acted on first. Option d discharges a client at the point of highest risk.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NCLEX Classic Trap: Sudden improvement in depression + giving away possessions = HIGHEST RISK OF SUICIDE. Energy returned before hopelessness resolved.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stay with the client, speak in short calm sentences, and coach slow diaphragmatic breathing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave the client alone in a quiet, empty side room to reduce sensory stimulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain the neurobiology of the sympathetic fight-or-flight response in some detail"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client firmly that the physical sensations are harmless and to snap out of it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$During panic-level anxiety, perceptual fields are drastically narrowed, cognitive processing is severely impaired, and the client feels imminent doom. The nurse must: (1) STAY with the client (never leave a panicked client alone; physical presence provides safety), (2) Speak in short, simple, calm phrases, (3) Reduce ambient noise and lighting, and (4) Model and coach slow, deep diaphragmatic breathing to counter respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. Detailed psychoeducation is impossible during panic and should occur only after anxiety decreases to mild levels. Why the other options fail: option b abandons a terrified client, which intensifies panic. Option c teaches at a moment when processing is narrowed to nothing. Option d dismisses the experience and tells the client to control what he cannot yet control.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe panic intervention: STAY with the patient + Short, calm sentences + Breathe with them. Never leave them alone and never attempt teaching during panic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lock the bathroom door before meals to extinguish the handwashing behaviour immediately"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Confront the client with the irrationality of spending forty-five minutes washing his hands"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Require the client to clean the ward kitchen whenever handwashing exceeds five minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Allow time for the ritual at first, then agree limits as coping strategies are learned"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Compulsive rituals in OCD serve as a psychological defense mechanism to neutralize overwhelming anxiety generated by intrusive obsessive thoughts. Abruptly blocking or forbidding the ritual in the early stages of hospitalization triggers panic-level anxiety. The nurse should initially accommodate the ritual by planning the schedule around it (e.g., starting earlier), ensure skin protection, and establish a trusting relationship. Subsequently, the multidisciplinary team works collaboratively with the client to gradually limit ritual time (exposure and response prevention) while teaching alternative anxiety-reduction strategies. Why the other options fail: option a blocks the ritual abruptly and triggers panic-level anxiety. Option b confronts the client with the irrationality of a compulsion, which does not respond to argument. Option c punishes the symptom and damages the alliance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$OCD initial rule: Allow time for the ritual early in admission to prevent panic, then gradually negotiate limits while teaching new coping mechanisms. Never abruptly stop rituals on Day 1.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Allow the client to eat meals alone in her bedroom to reduce social anxiety"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Offer high-calorie milkshakes whenever she requests them between midnight and dawn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Observe her directly during meals and for one hour after, with bathroom visits supervised"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Weigh the client three times a day, immediately after each meal, on the same scale"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In the inpatient management of severe anorexia nervosa, clients frequently experience overwhelming guilt following food consumption and may attempt to purge (vomiting), discard food in napkins, or engage in vigorous compensatory exercise. Direct nursing supervision is mandatory during meals (typically 30–45 minutes) and for at least 60 minutes postprandially. Bathrooms must be locked or supervised to prevent vomiting or water-loading. Daily weighing is conducted once in the morning upon waking, after voiding, wearing only a hospital gown, not after meals. Why the other options fail: Eating alone in her bedroom removes the supervision that prevents hiding or purging; unmeasured milkshakes at night add calories outside the plan and disrupt refeeding; weighing after every meal measures intake rather than trend and feeds the preoccupation with weight.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anorexia postprandial rule: Observe client during meal AND for 1 hour after meal to prevent vomiting/purging or hiding food.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I agree; the night nurse has a harsh manner that several patients have complained about"}, {"id": "b", "text": "You should be grateful that anyone on this unit is willing to care for you at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I will make sure the night nurse is never assigned to look after you again on any shift"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The whole team follows one agreed care plan; let us raise any concern about night care together"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clients with Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD) frequently use the primitive defense mechanism of 'splitting' (viewing people as entirely all-good or all-bad), which creates conflict, manipulation, and division among nursing staff. The therapeutic response to splitting is team solidarity and consistency: non-defensively stating that all staff adhere to a unified treatment plan, refusing to accept flattery that disparages colleagues, and redirecting the client to address concerns openly with the team. Never agree with the client's criticism of other staff members. Why the other options fail: option a joins the split and confirms the idealisation. Option b is punitive and shames the client for seeking care. Option c lets the client dictate staffing, which entrenches splitting rather than resolving it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Borderline splitting response: Staff consistency + Neutral boundary. State that all team members follow the same care plan; never take sides or validate idealization/devaluation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Postoperative sepsis from a paralytic ileus, with a fever and tachycardia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Alcohol withdrawal delirium, around forty-eight hours after the last drink"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute opioid withdrawal after stopping postoperative analgesia on day two"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Akathisia from an antiemetic, with restlessness and visual disturbance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Delirium Tremens (DTs) is a medical emergency that typically emerges 48 to 72 (up to 96) hours after the last alcoholic drink in alcohol-dependent individuals. Diagnostic features include autonomic hyperactivity (severe hypertension, tachycardia, fever, drenching sweats), gross coarse tremors, severe agitation, disorientation, and perceptual disturbances (vivid visual or tactile hallucinations, such as insects or animals). Untreated DTs has a mortality rate of up to 15% from cardiac arrhythmias or hyperthermia. Immediate treatment requires parenteral benzodiazepines (e.g., diazepam, lorazepam). Why the other options fail: option a does not explain the fever, hypertension and tactile hallucinations that appear together at forty-eight hours. Option c gives dilated pupils, cramps and craving, not fever with visual hallucinations. Option d gives motor restlessness alone, without autonomic storm or perceptual disturbance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alcohol withdrawal timeline: Tremors/anxiety at 6–12 hrs; Seizures at 12–48 hrs; Delirium Tremens (fever + tachycardia + tactile/visual hallucinations) at 48–72 hrs. Treatment: Benzodiazepines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dextrose neutralises thiamine and makes it therapeutically inert in the circulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Thiamine is needed to convert glucose into fat cells and prevent hypertriglyceridaemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thiamine stimulates insulin secretion and so prevents diabetic ketoacidosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Glucose metabolism consumes the remaining thiamine, precipitating Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Thiamine (vitamin B1) is an essential cofactor for enzymes in glucose carbohydrate metabolism (pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase). In thiamine-deficient patients with alcohol use disorder, infusing intravenous glucose without thiamine stimulates rapid carbohydrate oxidation, which completely depletes the body's remaining trace thiamine stores. This triggers acute cerebral metabolic failure, hemorrhagic necrosis of the mamillary bodies, and irreversible Korsakoff psychosis (anterograde amnesia and confabulation) or fatal coma. Thiamine must always precede or accompany glucose. Why the other options fail: option a invents an interaction in which dextrose destroys thiamine. Option b invents a lipogenesis role that has nothing to do with the risk. Option c invents an insulin effect, when the danger is cerebral thiamine depletion during glucose metabolism.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alcoholism resuscitation rule: THIAMINE BEFORE DEXTROSE (T before D). Giving sugar first depletes B1 and destroys the mamillary bodies.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute onset, fluctuating course, inattention and a treatable cause such as infection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insidious onset over years, a steadily progressive course and no reversible cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prominent early visual hallucinations, whereas delirium spares perception entirely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Normal vital signs throughout, whereas dementia presents with severe hypertension"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The key clinical discriminators: (1) Delirium: ACUTE onset (hours to days), fluctuating course, impaired attention, altered level of consciousness (hyperactive or hypoactive), perceptual disturbances (hallucinations), and typically reversible once the underlying trigger (UTI, pneumonia, electrolyte disturbance, medication anticholinergic toxicity) is resolved; (2) Dementia: INSIDIOUS onset (months to years), progressive decline in memory and executive function, stable level of consciousness until late stages, and irreversible. Why the other options fail: option b describes dementia rather than delirium. Option c reverses the pattern, since visual hallucinations are characteristic of delirium. Option d invents a vital-sign rule that discriminates nothing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delirium vs Dementia: Delirium = ACUTE + FLUCTUATING + INATTENTION + REVERSIBLE (find the cause: UTI, sepsis, meds). Dementia = CHRONIC + GRADUAL + IRREVERSIBLE.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure the mother that postpartum blues are normal and will resolve by week two"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the infant with a responsible family member and arrange urgent psychiatric admission"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave mother and baby alone in a dark room to encourage maternal-infant bonding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recommend an over-the-counter herbal calming tea and review her again in one month"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The presentation (delusions involving the infant, paranoia, extreme insomnia, command thoughts of infanticide) represents Postpartum Psychosis, an acute psychiatric emergency affecting approximately 1 to 2 per 1,000 childbirths. It carries a severe risk of infanticide (up to 4%) and maternal suicide (up to 5%). The absolute priority is immediate physical separation of the infant into safe protective family care (never leave mother and infant alone) and emergent psychiatric hospitalization for antipsychotic and mood-stabilizing treatment. Why the other options fail: Attributing these symptoms to the blues miscasts a psychosis that the blues never include; leaving mother and baby alone in a dark room places the infant with a mother who is delusional about it; an unproven herbal tea with a review in a month leaves the psychosis untreated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Postpartum Psychosis = PSYCHIATRIC EMERGENCY. Delusions of infant harm/demonization carry high risk of infanticide and suicide. Protect the baby immediately and admit mother.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the dose at bedtime so that the child sleeps deeply and restfully through the night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give it in the morning after breakfast and monitor height, weight and blood pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Crush the extended-release tablet and mix it into a warm evening cocoa drink"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Double the dose at weekends whenever the child is especially boisterous and active"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Central nervous system stimulants (such as methylphenidate and dextroamphetamine) can cause anorexia, weight loss, growth suppression, insomnia, and tachycardia/hypertension. To prevent insomnia, stimulants should be administered in the morning with or immediately after breakfast (food helps minimize gastrointestinal distress and appetite suppression). Pediatric nurses must regularly plot height and weight on standardized growth charts and monitor cardiovascular parameters (blood pressure and heart rate). Crushing extended-release formulations is contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option a gives a stimulant at bedtime, which causes the insomnia it is meant to avoid. Option c destroys the extended-release mechanism and gives the dose at the wrong time. Option d doubles a controlled dose without an order.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ADHD stimulants (Methylphenidate): Give in morning with/after food (prevents insomnia & anorexia) + Monitor GROWTH (height & weight) and blood pressure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and the haloperidol must be stopped at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tardive dyskinesia, which calls for a lower maintenance dose of the haloperidol"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An acute dystonic reaction, which calls for intravenous procyclidine immediately"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Serotonin syndrome, which calls for cyproheptadine and aggressive active cooling"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) is a life-threatening idiosyncratic reaction to dopamine-receptor antagonists (antipsychotics, particularly typical agents like haloperidol and fluphenazine). Hallmark clinical tetrad: (1) Severe 'lead-pipe' muscle rigidity, (2) Hyperthermia (> 38°C, often > 40°C), (3) Autonomic instability (labile blood pressure, tachycardia, diaphoresis, tachypnea), and (4) Altered mental status, accompanied by rhabdomyolysis (massively elevated serum CK) and acute kidney injury. Immediate action: STOP the neuroleptic immediately, initiate aggressive cooling and IV hydration, and transfer to ICU for dantrolene or bromocriptine therapy. Why the other options fail: option b is a late-onset movement disorder, without hyperthermia or rhabdomyolysis. Option c is muscle spasm and oculogyration without fever or a raised CK. Option d requires a serotonergic agent and gives hyperreflexia and clonus rather than lead-pipe rigidity.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) tetrad: FEVER = Fever (hyperthermia) + Encephalopathy + Vitals unstable + Elevated CK + Rigidity ('lead-pipe'). Action: STOP drug immediately!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lithium toxicity; hold the dose, notify the prescriber and draw an urgent lithium level"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mild expected side effects; advise the client to drink a strong cup of black tea"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute manic relapse; increase the lithium dose by three hundred milligrams daily"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyponatraemia; restrict all fluids until the sodium level returns to normal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Lithium has an extremely narrow therapeutic index (therapeutic maintenance: 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L). Early toxicity begins at levels > 1.5 mEq/L, presenting with gastrointestinal distress (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea), coarse hand tremors (contrasted with mild fine tremors seen at therapeutic levels), slurred speech, lethargy, muscle weakness, and ataxia. Severe toxicity (> 2.0–2.5 mEq/L) causes seizures, coma, renal failure, and arrhythmias. The nurse must hold the lithium, check an emergent serum level, hydrate the client, and promote lithium excretion. Dehydration and low sodium intake drastically increase lithium toxicity risk. Why the other options fail: option b calls a picture with ataxia and coarse tremor benign, and the tea does nothing. Option c raises the dose into deeper toxicity. Option d restricts fluid, which concentrates lithium further when the client needs volume repletion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lithium toxicity signs: COARSE hand tremor + Vomiting/Diarrhea + Ataxia/Slurred speech. Normal therapeutic range: 0.6–1.2 mEq/L. Above 1.5 = TOXICITY.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Serotonin syndrome causes hyporeflexia and hypothermia, while NMS causes hyperreflexia and fever"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Serotonin syndrome occurs only with antipsychotics, while NMS occurs with antidepressant drugs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Serotonin syndrome: hyperreflexia, clonus, dilated pupils, onset in hours; NMS: lead-pipe rigidity over days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Serotonin syndrome is reversed with dantrolene, while NMS is reversed with cyproheptadine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Both Serotonin Syndrome (SS) and Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS) present with hyperthermia and autonomic instability, but key distinguishing features exist: (1) Serotonin Syndrome: RAPID onset (within hours of combining serotonergic agents like SSRIs, MAOIs, tramadol, St. John's wort), neuromuscular HYPERACTIVITY with hyperreflexia, shivering, and spontaneous clonus, hyperactive bowel sounds/diarrhea, and mydriasis (dilated pupils); treated with cyproheptadine. (2) NMS: gradual onset over days, profound 'lead-pipe' muscle rigidity with hyporeflexia, normal pupils, and normal bowel sounds; treated with dantrolene/bromocriptine. Why the other options fail: option a reverses both patterns. Option b reverses the drug classes, since serotonin syndrome follows serotonergic drugs and NMS follows dopamine antagonists. Option d reverses the antidotes, which are cyproheptadine for serotonin syndrome and dantrolene for NMS.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SS vs NMS: Serotonin Syndrome = HYPER-reflexia + CLONUS + Dilated pupils (fast onset). NMS = Lead-pipe RIGIDITY + Hyporeflexia (slower onset).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MH-020$q$;

