BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Speaking three distinct two-syllable words with specific meaning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Walking independently while holding onto furniture"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sitting unsupported with steady head and trunk control"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Building a tower of three small wooden blocks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$By 8 to 9 months of age, an infant should be able to sit unsupported without hand propping (tripod sitting is expected by 6 months; stable independent sitting by 8-9 months). Inability to sit steadily without support at 9 months represents a significant gross motor delay requiring immediate developmental and neurological evaluation. Walking independently (12–15 months), building a 3-block tower (15–18 months), and speaking multiple specific words (12 months) are developmental milestones expected at later stages. Why the other options fail: Speaking three distinct two-syllable words, cruising along furniture and stacking three blocks are all milestones of the second year, well beyond nine months. Steady head and trunk control in an unsupported sit is what a nine-month-old should have.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall key gross motor milestones: rolling at 4-5 months, sitting unsupported by 8-9 months, cruising/standing by 9-10 months, and independent walking by 12-15 months.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Despair"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Detachment (denial)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Regression"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Protest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Separation anxiety in toddlers typically progresses through three distinct phases: (1) Protest: crying loudly, screaming, kicking, clinging to parents, and rejecting stranger contact; (2) Despair: quiet, withdrawn, crying silently, depressed, and disinterested in toys or environment; and (3) Detachment (denial): superficial adjustment where the toddler interacts with caregivers but shows indifference or avoidance when the parent returns. Regression is a coping mechanism (e.g., bedwetting or demanding a bottle), not a primary stage of separation anxiety. Why the other options fail: Despair follows protest and shows as withdrawal and apathy rather than active crying and searching; detachment is the stage where the child appears to have given up on the parent and shows no interest on return; regression is a return to earlier behaviour, not the protest itself.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the active, aggressive behaviour (screaming, kicking, clinging) as the initial 'Protest' phase. Despair is passive sadness; Detachment is artificial indifference.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Perform a detailed feeding assessment, screen for recent illnesses, and counsel on energy-dense complementary foods"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the mother that growth naturally plateaus during the second half of infancy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately admit the infant to the inpatient nutritional stabilization centre"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advise the mother to discontinue breastfeeding and switch entirely to cow's milk formula"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A flat (horizontal) growth curve on the Road to Health chart indicates growth faltering (failure to gain weight), which is a critical early warning sign of impending undernutrition. The priority intervention is to take an in-depth nutritional history, assess the frequency, consistency, and energy density of complementary feeding (which begins around 6 months), screen for recent diarrhea or fever, and provide practical infant feeding counselling. Routine inpatient admission is reserved for severe acute malnutrition with complications. Discontinuing breastfeeding is contraindicated. Why the other options fail: Reassurance that growth plateaus in the second half of infancy is false, because growth should continue; immediate inpatient admission skips the assessment step that should come first; stopping breastfeeding for cow's milk formula removes the protection and the nutrition the infant needs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Growth curve direction matters more than a single spot measurement. A horizontal curve indicates growth faltering requiring urgent diet assessment and feeding intervention before severe wasting develops.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Initiate chest compressions at a 3:1 compression-to-ventilation ratio before any ventilation has been given"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply high-flow blow-by oxygen at 10 litres per minute and continue tactile stimulation until breathing starts"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer intravenous adrenaline (epinephrine) 1:10,000 through the umbilical vein as the first intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Initiate positive-pressure ventilation (PPV) with a bag-mask device at 40 to 60 breaths/minute"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to the Neonatal Resuscitation Program (NRP) and WHO Helping Babies Breathe guidelines, if a newborn is gasping, apnoeic, or has a heart rate below 100 beats/minute after initial drying, warming, and positioning, the single most critical and immediate step is positive-pressure ventilation (PPV) with room air (21% O2 for term infants) at 40–60 breaths/min. Chest compressions are indicated ONLY if the heart rate remains below 60 beats/minute despite 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation with chest rise. Why the other options fail: Compressions come after effective ventilation has been established in a newborn, not before; adrenaline is reserved for a heart rate that stays low despite good ventilation; blow-by oxygen and continued stimulation do not open the lungs of a baby who is not breathing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Airway and effective ventilation are the cornerstone of newborn resuscitation. PPV is started when HR < 100 bpm; chest compressions are added only when HR remains < 60 bpm after effective ventilation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the baby flat in a prone position with the neck flexed tightly against the mother's chest so the airway stays open"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dress the infant in two thick cotton shirts before securing the baby to the mother's bare chest for warmth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Position the baby upright between the mother's breasts, skin-to-skin, with the head turned to one side and slightly extended"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Maintain kangaroo mother care for no more than thirty minutes at a time to prevent maternal fatigue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Proper Kangaroo Mother Care (KMC) positioning requires placing the infant upright (vertical) between the maternal breasts with continuous skin-to-skin contact (infant dressed only in a nappy, cap, and socks). The baby's head must be turned to one side in a 'sniffing' position (slightly extended, neither hyperextended nor flexed) to keep the airway completely patent, with the hips and arms in a flexed 'frog' posture. Neck flexion obstructs the small infant's airway. KMC should be as continuous as possible (ideally 20+ hours/day). Why the other options fail: A flat prone position with the neck flexed closes the airway rather than opening it; extra clothing defeats the skin-to-skin contact that provides the warmth; limiting care to thirty-minute blocks removes the continuous thermal support that is the point of the method.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In newborn care, neck flexion compromises airway patency. The head must be turned to the side with slight neck extension to maintain an open airway.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cover the infant's eyes with opaque eye shields and leave only the diaper area covered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply moisturizing oil to the infant's skin to prevent photo-induced drying"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Turn off phototherapy during night hours to establish normal circadian rhythms"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict enteral feeds to reduce frequent loose greenish stools"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$During phototherapy, maximum skin surface exposure is required to convert unconjugated bilirubin into water-soluble photo-isomers (lumirubin). The infant should wear only a small diaper. Opaque eye patches must be securely applied over closed eyes to prevent retinal damage from the blue light spectrum. Oils or lotions must never be applied because they can cause severe skin burns. Phototherapy should be continuous except during feeding, and enteral feeding should be encouraged to promote stooling and bilirubin excretion. Why the other options fail: Oil on the skin traps heat and is not part of phototherapy care; switching the lights off at night removes the treatment and lets the bilirubin climb; restricting feeds removes the milk that speeds bilirubin excretion through the gut.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Phototherapy essentials: Eye shields (retinal protection) + Maximum skin exposure (diaper only) + No oils/lotions (prevents burns) + Frequent feeding (clears bilirubin in stool).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The internal refrigerator thermometer reads 4.5 degrees Celsius between the recommended limits"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pentavalent (DTP-HepB-Hib) vaccine vials are stored directly touching the ice-lining evaporator plates"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oral polio vaccine is stored on the top shelf, clear of the evaporator plate and the freezer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Water bottles are stored in the bottom compartment to hold the temperature steady when the lid is opened"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Adsorbed vaccines containing aluminium adjuvants (such as Pentavalent, PCV, Td, and Hepatitis B) are freeze-sensitive and lose potency permanently if frozen. Placing these vials in direct contact with cold evaporator plates or frozen icepacks causes accidental freezing and adjuvant precipitation. Freeze-sensitive vaccines must be kept between +2°C and +8°C, buffered away from freezing zones. The 'shake test' is used to verify freeze damage. OPV and measles vaccines are heat-sensitive and tolerate freezing. Why the other options fail: A reading of 4.5 degrees is inside the recommended range, not a breach; oral polio vaccine belongs on the top shelf and is not freeze-sensitive; water bottles in the bottom compartment do hold the temperature steady. Vials touching the evaporator plate freeze and destroy the adjuvant.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember freeze-sensitive vs heat-sensitive vaccines: Pentavalent/PCV/Hepatitis B must NEVER freeze (keep away from freezer plates). OPV and Measles tolerate freezing but degrade with heat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restart the entire immunisation schedule from the birth doses so that the child is protected from the beginning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the 6-week vaccines (Pentavalent 1, PCV 1, OPV 1, Rota 1) today and schedule the 10-week doses 4 weeks later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer both the six-week and the ten-week vaccines simultaneously in different limbs at this visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withhold all vaccines until the child reaches fourteen weeks of age so that the doses align with the final schedule"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In routine immunisation schedules, an interrupted or delayed schedule is never restarted; previous valid doses always count. The nurse should administer the overdue 6-week vaccines (Pentavalent 1, PCV 1, OPV 1, Rotavirus 1) today. The minimum recommended interval between sequential multi-dose vaccines (such as Pentavalent 1 and 2) is 4 weeks; therefore, the 10-week doses should be scheduled 4 weeks after today's administration. Why the other options fail: Restarting from birth gives doses the child does not need and delays protection; giving both visits at once is not the schedule; waiting to fourteen weeks leaves the child unprotected for weeks. Current vaccines are given and the schedule continues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Golden rule of immunisation: NEVER restart a vaccine series due to a delay. Give the next due dose immediately and maintain the minimum interval (typically 4 weeks) before the subsequent dose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Temperature of 39.2 degrees Celsius"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Respiratory rate of 46 breaths/minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Maculopapular rash on the trunk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inability to drink or breastfeed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$According to IMCI guidelines, the four universal General Danger Signs in children aged 2 months to 5 years are: (1) Inability to drink or breastfeed, (2) Vomiting everything, (3) History of convulsions during the current illness, and (4) Abnormally sleepy, lethargic, or unconscious. The presence of ANY general danger sign classifies the child as having a very severe disease requiring urgent pre-referral treatment (e.g., initial antibiotic, rectal artesunate) and immediate hospital referral. Why the other options fail: A temperature of thirty-nine, a respiratory rate of forty-six and a rash are all signs but none is a general danger sign; the four danger signs are inability to drink or breastfeed, vomiting everything, convulsions and lethargy or unconsciousness.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Memorize the 4 IMCI General Danger Signs: Cannot drink/breastfeed, Vomits everything, Convulsions, and Lethargic/unconscious. Fast breathing alone without chest indrawing or danger signs is classified as pneumonia, not severe illness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start intravenous Ringer's lactate 100 mL/kg immediately (30 mL/kg in the first 30 minutes, then 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer 75 mL/kg of oral rehydration salts solution over four hours and reassess the dehydration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a nasogastric tube and give oral rehydration salts at 20 mL/kg per hour until the eyes refill"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give intramuscular loperamide and ondansetron to stop the diarrhoea and vomiting before rehydrating"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The child exhibits classic signs of Severe Dehydration (lethargy, sunken eyes, skin pinch > 2 seconds, unable to drink), which falls under IMCI Plan C. Severe dehydration is a medical emergency requiring rapid intravenous fluid resuscitation with Ringer's lactate or normal saline at 100 mL/kg: for children aged 12 months to 5 years, give 30 mL/kg over 30 minutes, followed by 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours. Oral rehydration (Plan B) is impossible because the lethargic child cannot drink. Why the other options fail: Seventy-five millilitres per kilogram of oral salts over four hours treats some dehydration, not severe dehydration; nasogastric salts at that rate are too slow for a shocked child; loperamide is contraindicated in young children and neither drug replaces the fluid.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IMCI Dehydration classifications: Some dehydration (restless/irritable, drinks eagerly) = Plan B (ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 hours). Severe dehydration (lethargic, unable to drink, skin pinch > 2s) = Plan C (IV Ringer's lactate 100 mL/kg).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer high-protein, high-calorie catch-up feeds immediately to reverse wasting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Infuse rapid intravenous fluid boluses of 0.9% normal saline to expand circulating volume"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer high-dose intravenous iron dextran to correct severe anaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Feed with F-75 therapeutic formula (low protein, low sodium) in small, frequent amounts day and night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$During the initial stabilization phase (Phase 1) of Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM / Kwashiorkor), the child's metabolic systems are fragile with reduced sodium pumps and impaired hepatic/cardiac function. Starting high-protein feeds or rapid IV fluids causes fatal refeeding syndrome and acute heart failure. The child must be started on starter formula F-75 (75 kcal and 0.9 g protein per 100 mL), fed every 2 to 3 hours day and night to prevent hypoglycaemia and hypothermia. Iron supplementation is contraindicated in Phase 1 because free iron promotes bacterial proliferation. Why the other options fail: High-protein catch-up feeds are withheld in the stabilization phase because they overload a child who cannot yet handle them; iron is not given during stabilization; rapid boluses can precipitate heart failure in severe malnutrition. F-75 in small frequent feeds is the standard.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In SAM stabilization: F-75 first (NOT high-protein F-100 or solid food). NO iron in Phase 1. Prevent the 3 deadly H's: Hypoglycaemia, Hypothermia, and Heart failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "No pneumonia: cough or cold"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pneumonia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Severe pneumonia or very severe disease"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute bronchiolitis without respiratory distress"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Under IMCI guidelines, fast breathing thresholds are: >= 50 breaths/min for infants 2–11 months, and >= 40 breaths/min for children 12–59 months. Although this 10-month-old has fast breathing (54 bpm), the presence of chest indrawing (the lower chest wall moves IN when the child breathes in) automatically elevates the classification to 'Severe Pneumonia or Very Severe Disease'. This classification requires immediate urgent referral to hospital and an initial pre-referral dose of intramuscular/intravenous antibiotic (e.g., ampicillin plus gentamicin or ceftriaxone). Why the other options fail: A child with cough or difficult breathing and lower chest wall indrawing has severe pneumonia whatever the respiratory rate, so the milder classification would undertreat; bronchiolitis is not an IMCI classification and does not describe indrawing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IMCI Respiratory Hierarchy: Fast breathing only = Pneumonia (oral amoxicillin). Fast breathing + Chest indrawing OR stridor at rest OR danger sign = Severe Pneumonia (urgent pre-referral antibiotic and hospital admission).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insert a padded tongue blade between the teeth to protect the tongue from laceration, hold the head still with both hands, and wait for the jerking to stop on its own"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the child in a lateral recovery position, maintain airway patency, administer high-flow oxygen, and administer prescribed anticonvulsant (rectal diazepam or IV midazolam)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Submerge the child in an ice-water bath to bring the core temperature down rapidly, since cooling the outside of the body is what stops the seizure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrain the child's limbs firmly against the trolley with soft ties so that the motor jerking cannot continue and the seizure burns itself out"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A seizure lasting longer than 5 minutes represents status epilepticus requiring immediate emergency termination. Priority nursing care includes positioning the child on their side (lateral recovery position) to maintain an open airway and prevent aspiration, administering high-flow oxygen, suctioning secretions gently, and administering first-line anticonvulsant therapy (e.g., rectal diazepam 0.5 mg/kg or IV/buccal midazolam). Never insert anything into the mouth (risk of tooth fracture and airway obstruction), never restrain the child, and never use cold/ice baths (causes shivering and peripheral vasoconstriction). Why the other options fail: A tongue blade in a convulsing child can break teeth and obstruct the airway; an ice bath risks hypothermia and does not stop the seizure; restraining the limbs does not stop the cortical discharge and can cause injury. The airway, oxygen and a benzodiazepine are what terminate it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Seizure management: Airway + Side-lying recovery position + Oxygen + Anticonvulsant (if > 5 min). Never put objects into the mouth and never restrain limbs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inspecting the posterior pharynx using a tongue depressor to visualize the epiglottis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Allowing the parent to hold the child quietly while providing humidified blow-by oxygen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Alerting the anaesthetist and ENT surgeon for emergent airway management"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Preparing emergency endotracheal intubation equipment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The presentation (tripod position, drooling, dysphagia, distress, inspiratory stridor, high fever) is classic for acute epiglottitis (a medical airway emergency typically caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b or Streptococcus). Inspecting the throat with a tongue depressor or attempting invasive oral procedures can trigger sudden, fatal complete laryngospasm and airway closure. The nurse must keep the child calm in the parent's arms, avoid agitating procedures (no oral exams, no painful IV attempts until airway is secured), administer blow-by oxygen, and mobilize the airway team immediately. Why the other options fail: Holding the child quietly with humidified oxygen, alerting the anaesthetist and ENT surgeon, and preparing intubation equipment are all correct management. Instrumenting the pharynx with a tongue depressor can trigger complete obstruction and is strictly avoided.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Suspected epiglottitis (drooling + tripod + stridor): NEVER use a tongue depressor or inspect the throat! Doing so triggers catastrophic complete airway obstruction.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "90 mL of 5% dextrose in water over one hour to correct the presumed hypoglycaemia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1,800 mL of 0.45% saline over twenty-four hours as maintenance fluid replacement"}, {"id": "c", "text": "360 mL of 0.9% Normal Saline or Ringer's Lactate infused rapidly over 10 to 20 minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "180 mL of 20% albumin over four hours to expand the intravascular volume"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$In pediatric hypovolemic or septic shock, immediate fluid resuscitation requires 20 mL/kg of an isotonic crystalloid solution (0.9% Normal Saline or Ringer's Lactate) administered as a rapid intravenous/intraosseous push or pressure-bag infusion over 10 to 20 minutes. For an 18 kg child: 18 kg x 20 mL/kg = 360 mL. Dextrose solutions are contraindicated for volume resuscitation because rapid infusion causes osmotic diuresis and dangerous fluid shifts. The child must be reassessed immediately after the bolus for signs of fluid responsiveness or overload (hepatomegaly, crackles). Why the other options fail: Dextrose in water is not a resuscitation fluid and does not expand volume; a large volume of hypotonic saline over a day is maintenance, not resuscitation; albumin is not the first-line bolus. Twenty millilitres per kilogram of isotonic crystalloid is the PALS bolus.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PALS Shock Fluid Rule: 20 mL/kg of isotonic crystalloid (NS or LR) over 10–20 minutes. Calculate: 18 kg x 20 mL/kg = 360 mL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer intravenous digoxin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the infant in a knee-to-chest position"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Initiate bag-mask ventilation with 100% oxygen"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Infuse a 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$During a hypercyanotic ('Tet') spell in Tetralogy of Fallot, infundibular spasm increases right-to-left shunting across the ventricular septal defect, causing severe hypoxaemia. The FIRST and most immediate nursing action is placing the child in the knee-to-chest position (or holding an older child in a deep squat). This increases systemic vascular resistance (afterload), which reverses the shunt by forcing deoxygenated blood through the pulmonary vasculature to be oxygenated. Subsequent steps include high-flow calm oxygen, subcutaneous/IV morphine, and IV fluid. Why the other options fail: Digoxin does not relieve infundibular spasm; bag-mask ventilation with oxygen helps but does not address the spasm as directly as posture; a fluid bolus treats dehydration, not a Tet spell. Knee-to-chest increases systemic resistance and reduces the right-to-left shunt.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tet spell reflex action: KNEE-TO-CHEST position first. It immediately raises systemic vascular resistance, decreasing right-to-left shunting of deoxygenated blood into the aorta.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply cold ice packs directly to the painful femurs to reduce local inflammation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer PRN oral paracetamol only when the pain score exceeds 8 out of 10"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Enforce strict fluid restriction to prevent splenic sequestration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer aggressive intravenous hydration and scheduled opioid analgesia around the clock"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The cornerstone of managing an acute vaso-occlusive pain crisis in sickle cell disease is: (1) vigorous intravenous and oral hydration (typically 1.5 times maintenance) to decrease blood viscosity and prevent erythrocyte sickling in microvessels, and (2) prompt, aggressive analgesia using scheduled (around-the-clock) opioids (such as IV morphine) with adjuvant NSAIDs for severe pain. Ice packs or cold compresses are strictly contraindicated because cold causes vasoconstriction, worsening microvascular occlusion and infarction. Warm compresses are soothing and safe. Why the other options fail: Ice packs cause vasoconstriction and worsen sickling; fluid restriction is dangerous in a crisis that needs hydration; paracetamol alone at a threshold of eight is nowhere near adequate. Hydration with around-the-clock opioid analgesia is the cornerstone.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sickle cell vaso-occlusive crisis treatment: HOP = Hydration + Oxygen (if hypoxic) + Pain relief (scheduled opioids). Never apply cold/ice packs!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute bacterial peritonitis and encapsulated bacterial infections"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Severe polycythaemia with hypercalcaemia from prolonged immobility and steroid exposure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An acute post-streptococcal hypertensive crisis with encephalopathy and seizures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Metabolic alkalosis secondary to the hypokalaemia caused by diuretic therapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Children with active nephrotic syndrome are highly vulnerable to life-threatening bacterial infections, particularly spontaneous bacterial peritonitis and pneumonia caused by encapsulated organisms like Streptococcus pneumoniae. This heightened susceptibility results from: (1) massive urinary loss of immunoglobulins (IgG) and complement proteins (factor B), (2) immunosuppressive corticosteroid therapy, and (3) ascites fluid acting as a culture medium. Prompt antibiotic therapy and pneumococcal vaccination are essential preventive measures. Why the other options fail: Polycythaemia and hypercalcaemia are not the classic complications of nephrotic syndrome; a post-streptococcal hypertensive crisis is a feature of nephritic syndrome; metabolic alkalosis is not the risk. Loss of immunoglobulin and complement in the urine leaves the child open to peritonitis and encapsulated organisms.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nephrotic syndrome triad: Massive proteinuria, Hypoalbuminemia, and Severe edema. Greatest risk: Severe infection (loss of immunoglobulins in urine) and thromboembolism (loss of antithrombin III).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "1,200 mL/day at 50 mL/hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2,400 mL/day at 100 mL/hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1,580 mL/day at 66 mL/hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1,920 mL/day at 80 mL/hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Using the Holliday-Segar method for a 24 kg child: First 10 kg: 10 kg x 100 mL/kg = 1,000 mL. Next 10 kg (11-20 kg): 10 kg x 50 mL/kg = 500 mL. Remaining 4 kg (21-24 kg): 4 kg x 20 mL/kg = 80 mL. Total 24-hour maintenance volume = 1,000 + 500 + 80 = 1,580 mL/day. Hourly infusion rate = 1,580 mL / 24 hours = 65.8 mL/hour (rounds to 66 mL/hour). Using the 4-2-1 hourly rule gives: (10 x 4) + (10 x 2) + (4 x 1) = 40 + 20 + 4 = 64–66 mL/hour. Why the other options fail: 1,200 mL is the requirement for a 20 kg child; 2,400 mL is the 4-2-1 rate for a much larger child; 1,920 mL applies to 32 kg. Holliday-Segar for 24 kg is 1,000 plus 500 plus 80, which is 1,580 mL a day.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Holliday-Segar calculation: 1st 10 kg = 1,000 mL; 2nd 10 kg = 500 mL; Each additional kg = 20 mL. For 24 kg: 1,000 + 500 + (4 x 20) = 1,580 mL/day (~66 mL/hr).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "7.2 mL per dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "4.8 mL per dose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "9.6 mL per dose"}, {"id": "d", "text": "14.4 mL per dose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Step 1: Calculate total daily dose: 16 kg x 45 mg/kg/day = 720 mg/day. Step 2: Divide into 3 equal doses (every 8 hours): 720 mg / 3 doses = 240 mg per dose. Step 3: Calculate liquid volume using formula (Desired / Have) x Volume: (240 mg / 250 mg) x 5 mL = 0.96 x 5 mL = 4.8 mL per dose. Why the other options fail: 7.2 mL is the dose for 24 kg; 9.6 mL is the dose for 32 kg; 14.4 mL is the daily total rather than a single dose. Sixteen kilograms at 45 mg/kg/day is 720 mg, a third of which is 240 mg, and at 50 mg/mL that is 4.8 mL.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Always check whether the dose is total daily dose or per dose. Step 1: 16 x 45 = 720 mg/day. Step 2: 720 / 3 = 240 mg/dose. Step 3: (240 / 250) x 5 mL = 4.8 mL.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PAED-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

