-- Batch 40: NEURO. Regenerated from src/db after the letter-reference cleanup.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "GCS is 11; place the client in a high-Fowler position and administer oral analgesics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "GCS is 5; place the client in the Trendelenburg position to optimize cerebral perfusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "GCS is 9; perform hourly neurological assessments and request a routine brain MRI"}, {"id": "d", "text": "GCS is 7; prepare for endotracheal intubation and definitive airway protection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) assesses eye opening (1–4), verbal response (1–5), and motor response (1–6). In this client: eye opening to pain = 2; incomprehensible sounds = 2; decorticate abnormal flexion = 3, yielding a total GCS of 7 (2 + 2 + 3 = 7). A GCS score of 8 or less indicates severe traumatic brain injury and comatose state, carrying an inability to maintain or protect the airway. The clinical rule is 'GCS 8 or less, intubate.' Trendelenburg position is contraindicated because it increases intracranial pressure. Oral medications are strictly contraindicated in an unconscious client. Why the other options fail: A score of eleven would need a much higher verbal and motor response than this client shows; nine would mean the client is not yet at the intubation threshold; five would be a deeper coma than the described responses give. Eye opening to pain, confused speech and withdrawal add to seven, at which the airway must be protected.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Calculate each GCS component: Eye to pain = 2, Verbal incomprehensible = 2, Motor flexion = 3 (Total = 7). Recall the foundational emergency axiom: GCS ≤ 8 demands immediate airway protection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Provide thin liquids through a straw to assess independent swallowing function"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the client on strict NPO status and keep emergency suction at the bedside"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Position the client flat on their back to facilitate bilateral airway drainage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage frequent sips of ice water to stimulate thermal vocal cord reflexes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Impairment of cranial nerves IX (glossopharyngeal) and X (vagus) compromises pharyngeal motor and sensory innervation, abolishing the gag reflex and causing dysphagia with high risk of silent pulmonary aspiration. The highest nursing priority is maintaining strict NPO status until a formal swallow assessment is performed and ensuring functional bedside suction is immediately available. Thin liquids through a straw significantly increase aspiration risk. A flat supine position impairs clearance of secretions and promotes aspiration. Why the other options fail: Thin liquids through a straw are the most likely to be aspirated, not a test of swallowing; lying flat encourages aspiration rather than drainage; ice water sips do not test or protect a compromised pharyngeal swallow. Nil by mouth with suction ready is the safe response.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Relate the cranial nerves to clinical safety: Cranial nerves IX and X protect the airway and swallow reflex. Absence of gag reflex = immediate NPO + suction ready.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Decorticate posturing, indicating a superficial cerebral hemisphere cortical lesion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Purposive withdrawal response, indicating intact spinal reflexes and improving cognition"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Decerebrate posturing, indicating deeper, severe dysfunction of the midbrain or brainstem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Positive Babinski sign, indicating an isolated lower motor neuron peripheral injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Decerebrate (extensor) posturing is characterized by rigid extension and adduction of the arms, pronation of the forearms, flexion of the wrists/fingers, and plantar extension of the feet. It signifies severe injury or herniation affecting the midbrain or upper brainstem and carries a worse prognosis than decorticate (flexor) posturing, where arms flex inward toward the chest ('core'). It is an ominous clinical sign requiring immediate physician notification. Why the other options fail: Decorticate posturing is flexion of the arms with internal rotation, which is a hemispheric lesion rather than this picture; purposive withdrawal means the client localises or withdraws meaningfully, which is not what extension and pronation show; a Babinski sign is a plantar response and does not describe the limbs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Differentiate posturing: Decorticate brings hands to the 'cord/core' (flexion); Decerebrate has lots of 'e's for Extension away from the body, indicating midbrain/brainstem injury.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Neurogenic shock requiring immediate rapid crystalloid fluid resuscitation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Spinal shock characterized by complete flaccidity and transient hyperdynamic circulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Autonomic dysreflexia triggered by uninhibited sympathetic outflow from fecal impaction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cushing's triad, signaling life-threatening elevated intracranial pressure and impending brain herniation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cushing's triad is a classic, late physiological response to significantly increased intracranial pressure (ICP) and impending brainstem herniation. It consists of: (1) severe systolic hypertension with a widening pulse pressure (184/60 represents a pulse pressure of 124 mm Hg), (2) profound bradycardia (HR 46 beats/min), and (3) irregular respirations (such as Cheyne-Stokes breathing). It requires emergency interventions: elevating the head of the bed 30 degrees with head midline, hyperosmolar therapy (mannitol or 3% hypertonic saline), and urgent neurosurgical consultation. Neurogenic shock presents with hypotension, not hypertension. Why the other options fail: Neurogenic shock gives hypotension with bradycardia and warm dry skin, not a rising pressure with a widening pulse; autonomic dysreflexia occurs in spinal cord injury above T6 and is triggered by a noxious stimulus; spinal shock is flaccidity with absent reflexes, not a hyperdynamic circulation. The pattern is Cushing's triad.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Memorize Cushing's triad: Hypertension with wide pulse pressure + Bradycardia + Irregular respirations. Contrast this with shock (hypotension + tachycardia).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chronic subdural hematoma involving slow bridging cortical venous bleeding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Subarachnoid hemorrhage secondary to a ruptured anterior communicating aneurysm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Epidural hematoma involving rapid middle meningeal arterial laceration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Diffuse axonal injury with microscopic shearing of white matter tracts"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The hallmark clinical presentation of an epidural hematoma is a brief initial loss of consciousness, followed by a temporary period of lucidity ('lucid interval'), followed by rapid neurological collapse as the arterial hematoma expands within the tight skull cavity. Epidural bleeds typically result from a temporal bone fracture lacerating the middle meningeal artery. The expanding high-pressure arterial bleed leads to uncal herniation, presenting with an ipsilateral dilated, fixed pupil (compression of CN III) and contralateral hemiparesis. It is a surgical emergency requiring immediate emergent craniotomy/burr hole decompression. Why the other options fail: A chronic subdural develops over weeks from venous bleeding in an older client; a subarachnoid haemorrhage from an aneurysm gives a sudden thunderclap headache without a lucid interval; diffuse axonal injury gives immediate and prolonged coma with no lucid interval. A brief loss of consciousness followed by a lucid interval is the epidural signature.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the buzzword phrase: 'brief loss of consciousness followed by a lucid interval and rapid collapse' = Epidural hematoma (arterial, middle meningeal artery).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insert a nasogastric tube to decompress the stomach and prevent vomiting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect the drainage on a sterile gauze pad to inspect for a classic halo ring sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instruct the client to blow their nose forcefully into a sterile specimen container"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pack the left nostril tightly with sterile petroleum gauze to tamponade fluid leakage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Clear fluid draining from the nose (rhinorrhea) or ears (otorrhea) following basilar skull fracture is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leaking through a dural tear. The nurse should collect the drainage on a sterile gauze; if CSF is mixed with blood, the blood coalesces in the center and a yellowish ring forms around the perimeter ('halo sign' or 'target sign'). A chemical strip can also check for glucose (CSF contains glucose, unlike nasal mucus). Nasogastric tubes, nasal packing, and forceful nose-blowing are strictly contraindicated because they can increase intracranial pressure, introduce bacteria directly into the subarachnoid space causing meningitis, or penetrate the cribriform plate into the brain. Why the other options fail: A nasogastric tube can pass through a fractured cribriform plate into the brain; packing the nostril traps contaminated fluid and invites meningitis; blowing the nose forces air and bacteria into the cranial cavity. Inspecting the drainage for a halo sign is safe and diagnostic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rule out interventions that violate dural tear safety: never pack the nose, never insert NG tubes in basilar skull fracture, and never have the client blow their nose. Test for CSF with the halo sign or glucose strip.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start the alteplase infusion immediately so that the strict three-hour therapeutic window is not missed while the pressure is managed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer prescribed intravenous antihypertensive therapy (e.g., labetalol or nicardipine) to reduce blood pressure below 185/110 mm Hg before infusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cancel the alteplase infusion permanently, because a blood pressure at this level is an absolute contraindication to thrombolysis in stroke"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give subcutaneous heparin to provide alternative anticoagulation while the alteplase decision is deferred until the stroke team reviews"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$To safely administer IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke, blood pressure must be maintained below 185/110 mm Hg before starting the infusion and below 180/105 mm Hg for at least 24 hours after infusion. Uncontrolled hypertension during thrombolysis dramatically increases the risk of catastrophic intracranial hemorrhage. The nurse should promptly administer prescribed IV antihypertensives (such as labetalol or nicardipine). Alteplase is not cancelled unless the blood pressure cannot be maintained below 185/110 mm Hg despite treatment. Why the other options fail: Infusing into a pressure above the threshold raises the bleeding risk and breaches protocol; cancelling permanently is wrong, because the pressure can be lowered and the drug then given; heparin adds bleeding risk and does not treat the pressure. The pressure is brought down first and the infusion follows.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the exact BP parameter for tPA in acute stroke: Blood pressure must be < 185/110 mm Hg before starting thrombolytic therapy. If elevated, treat BP first, then infuse tPA.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"It dissolves microscopic blood clots within the arachnoid villi to restore CSF flow.\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "\"It stimulates bone marrow production of clotting factors to seal the aneurysm dome.\""}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"It prevents and relieves cerebral arterial vasospasm to protect brain tissue from secondary ischemic injury.\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"It acts as a potent osmotic diuretic to drain excess fluid from brain parenchyma.\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Subarachnoid hemorrhage exposes cerebral arteries to free hemoglobin and breakdown products, which frequently triggers severe cerebral arterial vasospasm between days 3 and 14 post-bleed, leading to secondary ischemic brain damage or stroke. Nimodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with high lipophilicity that selectively relaxes cerebral vasculature, preventing and treating delayed cerebral ischemia from vasospasm. It does not dissolve clots, seal aneurysms, or act as an osmotic diuretic. Why the other options fail: Nimodipine does not dissolve clot in the arachnoid villi, stimulate clotting factor production, or act as an osmotic diuretic. It is a calcium channel blocker given after subarachnoid haemorrhage to prevent the delayed cerebral vasospasm that causes secondary ischaemia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate nimodipine specifically with subarachnoid hemorrhage: Nimodipine = prevention of cerebral vasospasm and secondary ischemic deficits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Direct the client to flex their neck and tuck their chin toward the chest when swallowing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client to tilt their head backward and chin upward while swallowing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client in a semi-Fowler position at a 30-degree incline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Provide liquids through a flexible drinking straw placed on the affected left side"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$The chin-tuck maneuver (flexing the neck and tucking the chin toward the chest during swallowing) widens the vallecular space and pushes the epiglottis backward over the laryngeal opening, protecting the airway and directing food safely into the esophagus. Clients should sit upright at 90 degrees (high-Fowler) during meals and for at least 30 minutes afterward. Tilting the head back opens the airway and drastically increases aspiration risk. Drinking straws and placing food on the weak/affected side both promote pocketing and aspiration. Why the other options fail: A thirty-degree semi-Fowler position is too low and does not protect the airway; tilting the head back and lifting the chin opens the airway for breathing but is the opposite of a safe swallow; a straw on the weak side delivers fluid where sensation and control are poorest. A chin tuck protects the airway during the swallow.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the protective biomechanics of swallowing: Chin-tuck closes the trachea and widens the esophagus. Sitting upright at 90° with food placed on the unaffected side.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Force a padded wooden tongue blade between the teeth to protect the tongue from lacerations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Turn the client onto their side and remove hard or sharp objects from the immediate vicinity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrain the client's extremities firmly to the bed rails to prevent orthopedic fractures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer 50 mL of oral glucose solution to reverse potential acute hypoglycemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$During an active generalized tonic-clonic seizure, the priority nursing interventions are airway maintenance and physical safety: turn the client onto their side (lateral recovery position) to allow saliva/emesis to drain and prevent tongue occlusion of the airway, protect the head, loosen constrictive clothing, and remove hard or dangerous objects. Never force anything into the client's mouth (can cause dental fractures, aspiration, or airway obstruction), never forcefully restrain limbs (increases risk of bone fractures and tendon tears), and never give oral substances to an unconscious client. Why the other options fail: A tongue blade forced between the teeth can break teeth and obstruct the airway; restraining the limbs against the rails risks fracture and does not stop the seizure; oral glucose cannot be swallowed safely during a convulsion. Turning the client and clearing the area protects the airway and the head.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Follow seizure safety ABCs: Turn on side, clear hazards, never insert anything into mouth, never restrain extremities.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous lorazepam bolus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oral carbamazepine suspension via feeding tube"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subcutaneous regular insulin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intramuscular morphine sulfate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Status epilepticus is a medical emergency defined as continuous seizure activity lasting 5 minutes or longer, or recurrent seizures without regaining baseline consciousness between episodes. The first-line pharmacological treatment is a rapid-acting IV benzodiazepine—specifically intravenous lorazepam (or IV diazepam) to rapidly terminate neuronal hyperexcitability by enhancing GABA activity. Once the seizure is aborted, a longer-acting antiepileptic drug (e.g., levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate) is infused to prevent seizure recurrence. Oral medications cannot be administered during active status epilepticus. Why the other options fail: An oral suspension through a feeding tube is far too slow for status epilepticus and absorption is unreliable; insulin has no role; intramuscular morphine neither stops seizures nor acts quickly enough. A benzodiazepine given intravenously is the first-line termination.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the status epilepticus drug ladder: Benzodiazepine (IV lorazepam) FIRST to stop the seizure, followed by longer-acting antiepileptics (levetiracetam/fosphenytoin) to keep it stopped.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"Stop taking the medication immediately as soon as you are seizure-free for 7 consecutive days.\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "\"Practice meticulous daily flossing and soft-bristle toothbrushing to reduce gingival hyperplasia.\""}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"Double your dose if you experience morning drowsiness or mild coordination changes.\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"Restrict your dietary intake of calcium and vitamin D to avoid medication binding.\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Gingival hyperplasia (overgrowth of gum tissue) is a frequent and well-documented adverse effect of long-term phenytoin therapy, occurring in up to 50% of clients. Meticulous dental hygiene, regular brushing with a soft toothbrush, daily flossing, and routine dental visits significantly minimize gum overgrowth. Phenytoin must never be abruptly discontinued because sudden withdrawal can precipitate status epilepticus. Drowsiness and ataxia are signs of phenytoin toxicity (therapeutic level is 10–20 mcg/mL), requiring provider notification and serum level testing rather than dose escalation. Why the other options fail: Stopping the drug when seizure-free withdraws the treatment and invites recurrence; doubling the dose for drowsiness increases toxicity rather than managing it; restricting calcium and vitamin D does not prevent binding and risks bone loss. Gum overgrowth is the adverse effect to watch for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Connect phenytoin with its signature adverse effect: Phenytoin = gingival hyperplasia (good oral hygiene) + narrow therapeutic range (10-20 mcg/mL).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer oral antipyretics and dim the room lights"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transport the client to the radiology department for a non-contrast brain CT scan"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on droplet transmission precautions in a private room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prepare the client for a diagnostic lumbar puncture before initiating any isolation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bacterial meningitis (commonly caused by Neisseria meningitidis or Streptococcus pneumoniae) is highly contagious and transmitted via respiratory droplets. The immediate priority upon clinical suspicion is instituting droplet transmission precautions (private room, surgical mask for healthcare personnel within 3 feet, client masked during transport) to prevent hospital outbreaks. Once isolation is secured, blood cultures should be drawn, followed immediately by empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and dexamethasone—without delaying therapy for lumbar puncture or CT imaging if delays occur. Why the other options fail: A CT scan and a lumbar puncture are both needed, but neither comes before source isolation; antipyretics and dim lights are comfort measures; a lumbar puncture performed before isolation exposes staff and other clients. Droplet precautions come first.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prioritize infection control: When suspected infectious airborne or droplet disease enters the hospital, isolation precautions come first before diagnostic workups.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer systemic ophthalmic mydriatic eye drops to constrict pupils"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Irrigate both eyes vigorously with sterile tap water every 2 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply high-flow humidified blow-by oxygen directly over the client's open orbits"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instill prescribed preservative-free artificial tear drops and apply protective eye shields"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In an unconscious or comatose client with incomplete eyelid closure (lagophthalmos) and absent corneal reflex, the corneal surface dries out rapidly, predisposing the client to corneal abrasions, ulcerations, scarring, and permanent blindness. The nurse must instill prescribed artificial tear drops during the day, apply lubricating ophthalmic ointment at night, and cover the eyes with sterile moisture chambers or protective eye patches. Tap water irrigation causes corneal irritation and introduces waterborne pathogens. Blowing oxygen directly onto the eyes exacerbates corneal desiccation. Why the other options fail: Tap water is not sterile and repeated irrigation injures the cornea; oxygen blown over open orbits dries them further; mydriatic drops dilate the pupil and do not close the lids or moisten the eye. Artificial tears with protective shields address the exposure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the vulnerability: Loss of blink reflex leaves the cornea exposed to drying. Essential prevention = artificial tears and eye lubrication/shielding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give an extra intravenous bolus of normal saline to raise the blood pressure and relieve the headache"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the client in the Trendelenburg position so that venous return to the heart and brain is improved"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately elevate the head of the bed to 90 degrees (high-Fowler) and loosen tight clothing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain a twelve-lead electrocardiogram and send blood for cardiac enzymes before doing anything else"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$This client is exhibiting textbook signs of autonomic dysreflexia (hyperreflexia), an acute, life-threatening medical emergency occurring in spinal cord injury at or above T6. Noxious stimuli below the lesion (most commonly bladder distension/blocked Foley or bowel impaction) trigger massive uninhibited sympathetic discharge, causing severe vasoconstriction, dangerous hypertension, and diaphoresis above the injury, while the intact vagal nerve causes reflexive bradycardia. The immediate first action is to sit the client upright at 90 degrees (high-Fowler) with legs dangling if possible (to induce orthostatic blood pooling and lower intracranial arterial pressure), loosen all constricting garments, and immediately check the urinary catheter for kinks or distension. Why the other options fail: Trendelenburg worsens the raised pressure the dysreflexia has produced; a fluid bolus does not remove the triggering stimulus; an ECG and enzymes investigate the wrong problem. Sitting the client upright lowers the pressure while the cause, usually a full bladder or bowel, is found and relieved.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the first action in autonomic dysreflexia: Sit them UP (90 degrees) to lower BP gravitationally, loosen tight clothes, and search for the trigger (check bladder/Foley first).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Neurogenic shock causes loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone, leading to vasodilation, warm skin, and bradycardia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypovolemic shock produces marked bradycardia and dry skin, whereas this client is displaying neurogenic shock"}, {"id": "c", "text": "This condition represents septic shock caused by immediate wound infection at the spinal injury site"}, {"id": "d", "text": "This client is experiencing cardiogenic shock caused by acute anterior myocardial infarction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Neurogenic shock results from disruption of descending sympathetic outflow in cervical or high-thoracic spinal cord injuries (T6 and above). The unopposed parasympathetic tone produces profound systemic vasodilation (relative hypovolemia), venous pooling, warm and dry extremities (due to vasodilation rather than peripheral vasoconstriction), hypotension, and bradycardia (loss of sympathetic cardiac accelerator fibers). In contrast, hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock present with compensatory tachycardia, cool, clammy extremities, and delayed capillary refill due to intense peripheral vasoconstriction. Why the other options fail: Hypovolaemic shock gives tachycardia and cool clammy skin, not bradycardia with warm dry skin; sepsis gives fever and warm skin with tachycardia; cardiogenic shock gives cool skin with pulmonary congestion. Loss of sympathetic tone gives vasodilation, bradycardia and warm dry skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contrast shock states: Classic hypovolemia = hypotension + tachycardia + cool/clammy skin. Neurogenic shock = hypotension + bradycardia + warm/dry skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the medication 30 to 45 minutes before meals"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the medication immediately after completing large meals"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the medication right before going to sleep at night"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the medication only when feeling severe muscle exhaustion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pyridostigmine is an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that enhances neuromuscular transmission by preventing acetylcholine breakdown. In myasthenia gravis, chewing and swallowing muscles fatigue quickly. Administering pyridostigmine 30 to 45 minutes prior to meals ensures that the peak therapeutic effect coincides exactly with mealtime, maximizing muscle strength for chewing and swallowing and drastically reducing the risk of choking and aspiration. Taking it after meals leaves the client vulnerable to fatigue and aspiration during eating. Why the other options fail: Taking it after large meals delays the effect until the meal is over; taking it at bedtime leaves the client weakest in the morning; taking it only when exhausted means the drug is used as a rescue rather than to enable eating. Thirty to forty-five minutes before meals peaks the effect at mealtime.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Connect the timing to eating: Pyridostigmine must peak when the client is chewing. Peak onset is ~30-45 minutes; therefore, take 30-45 min BEFORE meals.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bilateral pupillary light reflexes and accommodation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hourly deep tendon reflex grading in the lower extremities"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Daily serum blood urea nitrogen and creatinine clearance"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Forced vital capacity (FVC) and negative inspiratory force"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) features acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy causing symmetric ascending paralysis. When the paralysis ascends to involve the intercostal nerves and diaphragm (phrenic nerve, C3–C5), respiratory failure can occur rapidly. Serial monitoring of bedside pulmonary mechanics—specifically forced vital capacity (FVC) and negative inspiratory force (NIF)—is the gold standard to detect diaphragmatic weakness before severe hypoxia occurs. An FVC falling below 15–20 mL/kg or NIF less negative than -20 cm H2O indicates urgent need for endotracheal intubation. Why the other options fail: Reflex grading tracks the neuropathy but says nothing about the respiratory failure that kills in Guillain-Barré; renal function is not the threatened system; pupillary reflexes do not predict respiratory decline. Vital capacity and inspiratory force are what decide when ventilation is needed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate Guillain-Barré with respiratory monitoring: GBS ascends; when it reaches the diaphragm, respiratory arrest threatens. Monitor forced vital capacity (FVC).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withhold all neurological assessments during the infusion to avoid client stimulation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the solution through a rapid IV push over 30 seconds without a pump"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infuse the drug concurrently with 5% dextrose in water through the same primary line"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warm the vial and inspect for microscopic crystals, and use a dedicated in-line IV filter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Mannitol is a potent hyperosmolar sugar alcohol that crystallizes readily at cooler room temperatures or higher concentrations (such as 20% or 25%). The nurse must inspect the vial carefully for crystals before administration; if crystals are present, the vial should be warmed and shaken until redissolved, then cooled to body temperature. An in-line IV filter (typically 5-micron) must always be used during administration to prevent microscopic crystals from entering the client's circulation and causing microembolism. Rapid IV push without an infusion device can cause acute circulatory overload and pulmonary edema. Why the other options fail: A rapid push without a pump risks a bolus that defeats the controlled osmotic effect; dextrose in the same line promotes crystallisation and incompatibility; withholding neurological assessments removes the very monitoring the infusion requires. Warming and filtering the solution prevents crystal infusion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the technical administration rule for mannitol: Mannitol crystallizes easily. Always inspect for crystals, warm if needed, and administer using an in-line IV filter.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dilute the phenytoin exclusively in 0.9% sodium chloride and infuse at a rate not exceeding 50 mg/min"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mix the phenytoin in 5% dextrose in water (D5W) and infuse as a rapid 1-minute bolus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer the medication via continuous infusion over 24 hours without cardiac monitoring"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Flush the intravenous line with lactated Ringer's solution immediately before and after injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Intravenous phenytoin is chemically incompatible with dextrose solutions (it will rapidly precipitate into insoluble crystals) and must only be mixed or flushed with 0.9% normal saline. Furthermore, rapid peripheral IV administration causes severe hypotension, sinus bradycardia, heart blocks, and fatal ventricular arrhythmias due to propylene glycol toxicity. Therefore, the maximum peripheral infusion rate is strictly limited to 50 mg/min in adults (often 25 mg/min in older adults), requiring continuous cardiac and blood pressure monitoring throughout the infusion. Why the other options fail: Dextrose makes phenytoin precipitate, and a one-minute bolus risks hypotension and arrhythmia; a twenty-four-hour infusion without monitoring ignores the maximum rate and the cardiac risk; lactated Ringer's is incompatible and will precipitate the drug. Normal saline at no more than 50 mg/min is correct.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the two inviolable rules of IV phenytoin: (1) Normal saline ONLY (never dextrose, which precipitates); (2) Never infuse faster than 50 mg/minute (prevents fatal arrhythmias/hypotension).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch41.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 41: NG-RESP, whole bank (20 items).
--
-- The bank was balanced 5/5/5/5 on a-e but the key ran a, b, c, d, a, b, c, d
-- for all twenty items, a positional cycle a student can learn in four
-- questions. Keys were redistributed and the distractor texts rewritten:
-- several were one- or two-word non-answers ('Prone', 'Simple fatigue',
-- 'Ignore the change'). Every item now carries a lettered distractor block.
-- Mean key/distractor length ratio 2.21 -> 1.14, worst item 1.33.
-- No stem changed. UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure the patient and reassess her observations in one hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sit her upright and give prescribed oral fluids slowly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat as impending respiratory failure and call for urgent assistance"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place the patient flat so that blood returns to the heart"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A silent chest with severe work of breathing means airflow is critically reduced, so this is impending respiratory failure and needs urgent airway and ventilatory support rather than observation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Reassuring the patient and reviewing her observations in an hour allows a silent chest to progress to respiratory arrest.
• B — Oral fluids cannot be given safely to a patient who cannot complete a sentence, and the risk is aspiration.
• D — Lying flat reduces lung expansion and increases the work of breathing in a patient who is already exhausted.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In asthma, a quiet chest can be more dangerous than loud wheezing because airflow is severely limited.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess ventilation and prepare prescribed ventilatory support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give high-flow oxygen at 15 litres per minute without monitoring"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a prescribed sedative to reduce the work of breathing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lay the patient flat and encourage deep breathing exercises"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Drowsiness with shallow breathing and a rising PaCO2 indicates hypercapnic respiratory failure; oxygen is titrated to the prescribed target while ventilation is assessed and escalated.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Unlimited high-flow oxygen without monitoring can worsen carbon dioxide retention and cause oxygen toxicity in COPD.
• C — Sedatives depress the respiratory drive and would deepen the hypercapnia and the drowsiness.
• D — Lying flat impairs diaphragm movement and worsens ventilation in a patient who is already retaining carbon dioxide.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In COPD, treat hypoxia while avoiding unmonitored oxygen excess and assess ventilation, not saturation alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Silence the alarm and document it for the next shift to review"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Increase the sedation so the patient stops fighting the ventilator"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Disconnect the ventilator and leave the patient to rest quietly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Check for kinks, secretions and tube obstruction while assessing the patient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A high-pressure alarm usually means increased resistance from kinking, secretions or obstruction, so the patient is assessed and the airway and circuit are checked immediately.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Silencing the alarm removes the warning without finding or relieving the obstruction.
• B — Increasing sedation without assessment hides the deterioration and removes the patient's ability to compensate.
• C — Disconnecting the ventilator leaves a patient who cannot breathe without any ventilatory support.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Always assess the patient first, then trace the ventilator circuit for obstruction.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Massage the calf firmly to relieve the pain and improve the circulation in the leg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sit her upright, give prescribed oxygen and escalate urgently for suspected pulmonary embolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give an oral analgesic and reassess her pain score and saturation in one hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage her to walk to the bathroom with assistance to improve her circulation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sudden breathlessness with hypoxaemia and pleuritic pain after major hip surgery is pulmonary embolism until proved otherwise, and it can progress to cardiac arrest within minutes, so oxygen, upright positioning and immediate escalation come first.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Massaging the calf can dislodge further thrombus and worsen the embolism.
• C — An oral analgesic treats the pain and misses a diagnosis that is time-critical.
• D — Mobilising a hypoxaemic patient with a possible embolus increases demand on an already compromised circulation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$After hip surgery, sudden breathlessness and hypoxia mean pulmonary embolism. Oxygen and escalate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Simple fatigue caused by the infection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An expected response to the antibiotics"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Septic shock and organ hypoperfusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Mild dehydration from poor oral intake"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Confusion, hypotension, tachypnoea and cool skin in a patient with pneumonia indicate systemic hypoperfusion, which is septic shock until proved otherwise.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Simple fatigue does not produce hypotension, confusion and a respiratory rate of 34.
• B — This is not an expected response to antibiotics; it is deterioration while on treatment.
• D — Mild dehydration does not explain cool skin, confusion and hypotension, which point to shock.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In infection, new confusion and circulatory compromise are red flags for sepsis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clamp the chest tube for 30 minutes and then reassess the drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess the patient and tubing for obstruction or disconnection and call for help"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Strip the tubing forcefully to clear it of any clot or fluid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ignore the change because the bubbling in the chamber has stopped"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sudden dyspnoea with a water seal that has stopped fluctuating may mean obstruction, disconnection or a worsening pneumothorax, so the patient and the system are assessed immediately and help is called.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Clamping the tube traps air in the pleural space and can convert a pneumothorax into a tension pneumothorax.
• C — Stripping the tubing generates high negative pressure that can damage lung tissue.
• D — Ignoring the change misses a developing pneumothorax or a disconnected system.$q$,
  strategy = $q$A sudden clinical change plus altered chest-tube function requires assessment, not routine clamping or stripping.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Prepare for immediate decompression while supporting oxygenation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait for a routine chest radiograph to confirm the diagnosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the patient prone to improve oxygenation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage coughing to reinflate the collapsed lung"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tension pneumothorax obstructs venous return and requires immediate decompression; treatment must not wait for imaging.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Waiting for a chest radiograph in tension pneumothorax allows cardiovascular collapse to proceed.
• C — Prone positioning worsens ventilation and delays the decompression.
• D — Coughing raises intrathoracic pressure and cannot relieve a tension pneumothorax.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Shock plus unilateral absent breath sounds is a decompression emergency.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Flat supine to improve venous return"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prone with the head turned to one side"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Trendelenburg to support the circulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High Fowler with legs dependent if tolerated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Upright positioning improves lung expansion and reduces venous return, which relieves pulmonary congestion while oxygen and prescribed treatment begin.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Lying flat increases venous return to an overloaded heart and floods the lungs further.
• B — Prone positioning impairs ventilation and access to the airway in an unstable patient.
• C — Trendelenburg increases venous return and worsens pulmonary congestion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$For pulmonary oedema, sit upright and support oxygenation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use sterile gloves and a sterile single-use kit, keeping the catheter tip sterile throughout"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wear clean examination gloves and reuse the catheter if the first attempt fails"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Clean the meatus with the same antiseptic swab used for the surrounding skin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Set the drainage bag on the floor beside the bed so that it does not pull"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Catheter insertion is an aseptic procedure, so sterile gloves and a sterile single-use kit are used and the catheter tip stays sterile from the moment it leaves its packaging until it enters the urethra.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A failed catheter is discarded, never reused, and clean examination gloves do not maintain asepsis.
• C — Using one swab for the meatus and the surrounding skin drags contamination towards the meatus; a new swab is used for each downward stroke.
• D — A drainage bag left on the floor puts the outlet in contact with a recognised source of catheter-associated infection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sterile gloves, sterile kit, tip never touched. A failed catheter is thrown away, not reused.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A pain score out of ten"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A dietary history"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The urine output"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Airway, then breathing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cyanosis and unresponsiveness mean an immediate ABC emergency, so airway and breathing are assessed and supported before any secondary data are collected.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A pain score is a secondary assessment and cannot be obtained from an unresponsive patient.
• B — A dietary history is irrelevant to an immediate airway emergency.
• C — Urine output is a late marker of perfusion and plays no part in the first minute.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Use ABC priorities in every collapse, regardless of the diagnosis suspected.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pneumothorax"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pleural effusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Catheter-site infection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Local cellulitis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Insertion of a central venous catheter can puncture the pleura, so sudden dyspnoea with sharp chest pain immediately afterwards is a pneumothorax until imaging shows otherwise.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A pleural effusion collects over hours to days and does not cause sudden dyspnoea within minutes of insertion.
• C — Catheter-site infection presents with fever, redness and tenderness, not with sharp pleuritic pain.
• D — Cellulitis is a local skin infection and does not produce sudden breathlessness.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Link sudden respiratory symptoms after central-line insertion with pneumothorax.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer prescribed sedatives and recheck in an hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess the patient and improve probe position or peripheral perfusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Document the value as accurate in the observation chart"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Remove all supplemental oxygen to test the reading"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A poor waveform with cold fingers makes the reading unreliable, so the patient is assessed directly while the probe position and peripheral perfusion are corrected.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Sedating a patient with a genuinely low saturation would remove their drive to breathe.
• C — Documenting a value from a poor waveform as accurate records an artefact as a clinical finding.
• D — Removing oxygen from a patient reading 82 per cent could be fatal if the reading is real.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Never treat an isolated number without checking signal quality and clinical signs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wearing only a surgical mask whenever entering the room"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keeping the room door open so the patient can be observed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Turning off the extraction to reduce the noise in the room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wearing a fit-tested N95 respirator whenever entering the room"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tuberculosis is airborne, so staff entering the room need a fit-tested respirator that filters particles at the size the organism travels on; a surgical mask does not seal and does not protect the wearer.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A surgical mask does not seal to the face, so it cannot filter airborne droplet nuclei.
• B — An open door defeats the negative pressure and lets contaminated air escape into the corridor.
• C — Switching off the extraction removes the ventilation that keeps the organism contained in the room.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Airborne means a fit-tested respirator for staff. A surgical mask is for the patient, not for you.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Squeeze the wound aggressively for ten minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply household bleach to the puncture site"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wash with soap and water and report immediately"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clean the site and continue the shift without reporting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Washing with soap and water and reporting immediately allow timely exposure assessment, baseline testing and post-exposure prophylaxis when indicated.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Squeezing the wound is no longer advised: it forces contaminated blood deeper and damages tissue.
• B — Bleach is caustic, damages the wound and is not a first-aid measure.
• D — Concealing the injury forfeits post-exposure prophylaxis during the window when it works.$q$,
  strategy = $q$After a sharps injury: wash, report and obtain urgent occupational-health review.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A simple face mask at 8 litres per minute"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A nasal cannula at 1 to 2 litres per minute"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A non-rebreathe mask at 15 litres per minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A Venturi mask set to 60 per cent oxygen"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A nasal cannula delivers roughly 24 per cent at 1 litre per minute and adds about 4 per cent for each further litre, so 1 to 2 litres gives the 24 to 28 per cent band asked for. The concentration is variable because it depends on inspiratory flow and entrained room air, which is acceptable when the target range is broad.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A simple face mask at 8 litres per minute delivers around 40 to 60 per cent, well above the target.
• C — A non-rebreathe mask delivers close to 100 per cent and is reserved for critical illness.
• D — A Venturi mask is fixed-performance, but 60 per cent is far above the target range.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nasal cannula: 1 litre is about 24 per cent, and each extra litre adds about 4 per cent.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Offload pressure, reposition and document/escalate the skin change"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Massage the reddened area vigorously to improve blood flow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply direct heat to the area to increase circulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the patient in the same position and recheck tomorrow"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Non-blanching erythema may be a stage 1 pressure injury, so pressure is relieved, the area is monitored and the change is documented and escalated.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Massaging reddened skin damages tissue that is already ischaemic.
• C — Direct heat raises the metabolic demand of tissue that cannot meet it and can cause burns.
• D — Leaving the patient in the same position allows the injury to progress to ulceration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Non-blanching redness means pressure damage has begun; remove pressure rather than massage.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restart the tablets only on days when you have a headache or feel unwell"}, {"id": "b", "text": "You must take the tablets every day or you will have a stroke soon"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertension can damage organs silently; let us discuss your concerns and a workable plan"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That is your decision and it is not something I need to discuss"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hypertension is usually asymptomatic while damaging the heart, brain and kidneys, so a non-judgemental conversation that explores barriers and agrees a workable plan is more effective than instruction.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Taking tablets only for a headache treats a symptom that hypertension usually does not cause.
• B — A threat does not build the partnership adherence requires, and hypertension is silent.
• D — Dismissing the patient's decision ends the conversation and guarantees non-adherence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Use shared decision-making and explain silent risk rather than using threats.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that she look at the stoma now so that she gets used to it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave her alone and arrange the stoma teaching for a later admission"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the family to do all the stoma care and stop teaching her"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Accept the feeling, stay with her, and introduce the stoma gradually when she is ready"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Refusal to look at a new stoma is a normal grief reaction to a change in body image; it resolves with time and support, so the feeling is acknowledged and contact is introduced gradually while the nurse demonstrates care.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Forcing contact overrides her coping and damages the trust the teaching depends on.
• B — Deferring all teaching to a later admission abandons skills she needs before discharge.
• C — Handing all care to the family leaves her dependent and unprepared to care for herself.$q$,
  strategy = $q$A new stoma is a loss first and a skill second. Accept the feeling, then teach in small steps.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pre-oxygenate and limit each suction pass to about 10 to 15 seconds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Suction continuously for a full minute to clear everything at once"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use the largest catheter that will pass to shorten the procedure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instil 5 mL of normal saline to loosen secretions before each pass"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Suctioning removes oxygen as well as secretions, so the patient is pre-oxygenated, each pass is limited to about 10 to 15 seconds, and the catheter is withdrawn with rotation without suction on the way in.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A full minute of continuous suction causes prolonged hypoxaemia.
• C — The largest catheter occludes the airway and increases trauma and hypoxaemia.
• D — Instilling saline is no longer recommended: it does not loosen secretions and it pushes them deeper, causing desaturation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pre-oxygenate, ten to fifteen seconds a pass, suction on the way out only.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oxygen is of no benefit at all in COPD, because the lungs are already permanently damaged by smoking"}, {"id": "b", "text": "In some people with chronic high carbon dioxide, too much oxygen can worsen retention and cause drowsiness and respiratory failure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The target range is set only to save money on the oxygen supply, not to keep the patient safe"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High-flow oxygen causes the lung to collapse immediately in every patient with COPD, without exception"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In a minority of patients with chronic hypercapnia the hypoxic drive contributes to ventilation, and a high inspired oxygen can raise carbon dioxide further through several mechanisms, producing drowsiness, acidosis and eventually respiratory failure. The prescribed target range balances adequate oxygenation against that risk, with blood gases and the level of consciousness monitored.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Oxygen is essential in COPD; it is titrated to a target rather than withheld.
• C — The target is a clinical safety range, not a cost measure.
• D — Lung collapse is a pneumothorax, which is not caused by oxygen therapy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$COPD oxygen is titrated to 88 to 92 per cent. Enough to oxygenate, not enough to suppress breathing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch42.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 42: NG-PHARM, whole bank (20 items).
--
-- The keys ran a, b, c, d five times over and the key/distractor length ratio
-- averaged 1.86, worst 2.94 at -0015. Eleven items carried no distractor block,
-- and -0020's rationale pointed at 'Options A and D' when the key was D and the
-- absorption offenders were A and C.
-- Keys were re-ordered, every distractor rewritten to plausible clinical weight,
-- and every block lettered to the final layout. Mean ratio 1.86 -> 1.16.
-- -0014's protamine distractor became atropine so that three of its four options
-- are no longer shared verbatim with NG-DRC-2430.
-- No stem changed. UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the dose with food to settle the nausea and recheck the pulse in an hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withhold the dose, notify the prescriber and arrange an urgent digoxin level, potassium and ECG"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the next dose early so that the blood level stays steady through the day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the client to walk to the bathroom so that the weakness can be assessed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Nausea, yellow-tinged vision and bradycardia are the classic triad of digoxin toxicity, and with a pulse of 48 the next dose could precipitate a dangerous arrhythmia. The dose is withheld, the prescriber is told, and a level, potassium and ECG are arranged, because hypokalaemia worsens toxicity.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Giving the dose with food masks a systemic warning sign without removing the drug or checking the level.
• C — Giving the next dose early adds to a level that is already toxic.
• D — Walking a bradycardic client who may be unstable risks a collapse that the assessment would have prevented.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Visual disturbance plus nausea plus bradycardia on digoxin equals toxicity. Hold, and check potassium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the next dose early to keep the level above the target"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ignore the value because vancomycin troughs are not monitored"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Double the next dose to make sure the infection is covered"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold the dose and notify the prescriber, anticipating a dose or interval change"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A vancomycin trough of 25 mg/L is well above the usual target range, and the risk of nephrotoxicity rises steeply with sustained high exposure, so the dose is withheld and the prescriber informed for a dose or interval change. Levels are drawn immediately before the next dose so that the result reflects the trough.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Giving the dose early keeps the level above target and prolongs the exposure causing the kidney injury.
• B — Ignoring a high trough abandons the monitoring that keeps the drug safe.
• C — Doubling the dose is the opposite of what a high level calls for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High vancomycin trough means hold and re-check. Never chase a trough upwards.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An order to withhold the next warfarin dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An order to give oral vitamin K and repeat the INR"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A new order for aspirin for mild joint pain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An order to recheck the INR in the morning"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The INR is far above target and the gums are already bleeding, so adding an antiplatelet drug that also damages the gastric mucosa would compound a serious bleeding risk. This is the order the nurse should query.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Withholding the next warfarin dose is appropriate here, so it is not the order to question.
• B — Oral vitamin K is the reversal agent for an over-anticoagulated, bleeding patient and is appropriate.
• D — Rechecking the INR in the morning is appropriate to confirm the response to holding the dose.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Already bleeding and over-anticoagulated? The antiplatelet is the order to stop and question.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop all bronchodilators permanently because of the tremor and the fast heart rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess oxygenation, work of breathing and the ECG, and report a marked or persistent tachycardia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a beta blocker to slow the heart rate and settle the tremor"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assume the asthma is worsening and give another nebuliser without assessing her"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tremor and tachycardia are expected beta2-agonist effects and are usually tolerable, so the question is whether the patient is better or worse. Assessing saturation, work of breathing, speech and the ECG distinguishes expected side effects from a worsening attack or an arrhythmia.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Stopping bronchodilators removes the treatment that is keeping the airway open.
• C — A beta blocker would precipitate bronchospasm in a patient with acute severe asthma.
• D — Treating a side effect as if it were the disease skips the assessment that tells them apart.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Salbutamol always causes tremor and tachycardia. Judge the patient, not the reading.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the dose and document the rising creatinine and the tinnitus afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Double the next dose so that the antibiotic level stays ahead of the infection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give an NSAID for the tinnitus and continue the gentamicin course as prescribed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withhold the dose and notify the prescriber urgently, anticipating a level and a change of antibiotic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Gentamicin is nephrotoxic and ototoxic, and a rising creatinine with new tinnitus means both organs are being damaged; the damage can become permanent, so the drug is stopped and the prescriber informed for levels and an alternative.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Giving the dose gives more of the drug that is causing the injury.
• B — Doubling the dose is the opposite of what is required.
• C — An NSAID adds a nephrotoxic drug to a kidney that is already failing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gentamicin: creatinine up and ears ringing means stop the drug, not adjust the paperwork.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "After breakfast or with food in the morning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "At bedtime so that it does not upset the stomach"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only on days when the symptoms occur"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Before breakfast on an empty stomach"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Morning administration after food reduces gastric irritation and follows the normal cortisol rhythm, and the drug must not be stopped abruptly after prolonged use.

Why the other options fail:
• D — Taking prednisolone before breakfast on an empty stomach increases gastric irritation.
• B — A bedtime dose disturbs sleep and does not match the early-morning cortisol peak.
• C — Taking it only when symptoms occur is wrong for a drug that suppresses inflammation continuously and must be tapered.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Steroids are usually taken with food in the morning unless the prescription says otherwise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Into the deltoid after removing all clothing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Into the abdomen after lifting the shirt"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Through the outer mid-thigh clothing if necessary"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Deep into the gluteal muscle after removing the trousers"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The auto-injector is delivered into the outer mid-thigh and can be given through clothing if needed; removing clothes first delays treatment in a condition that kills within minutes.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Moving to the deltoid wastes time and is not the recommended site.
• B — The abdomen is not recommended and absorption is less reliable than from the thigh.
• D — The gluteal site is not used for self-injection and delays treatment while the clothing is removed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$For anaphylaxis, give IM epinephrine promptly in the outer mid-thigh.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Crush the tablet without checking with anyone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Contact pharmacy or the prescriber for a suitable formulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Force the patient to swallow it with a large drink"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask a relative to give the medicine instead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The nurse must not alter a medicine without checking its formulation and stability; pharmacy can identify a liquid or an alternative that the prescriber can order.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Crushing an unverified tablet can destroy a modified-release preparation and release the dose at once.
• C — Forcing a patient to swallow risks aspiration and is never acceptable.
• D — Handing the dose to a relative takes administration outside the people accountable for it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Difficulty swallowing requires formulation review, not unauthorised crushing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dehydration from the increased urine output"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Water intoxication or hyponatraemia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Iron deficiency from chronic blood loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperglycaemia from the diabetes insipidus"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Excess desmopressin causes water retention and dilutional hyponatraemia, which produces headache, nausea, confusion and drowsiness.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Dehydration does not explain drowsiness and headache in a patient taking a drug that retains water.
• C — Iron deficiency causes fatigue and pallor, not acute drowsiness with headache.
• D — Hyperglycaemia is not the effect of desmopressin, which is an antidiuretic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Desmopressin toxicity looks like water excess; monitor sodium, weight and neurological status.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Diluting it and infusing it by a volumetric pump"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Checking the serum potassium and renal function first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Monitoring the cardiac rhythm during the infusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Giving it as a direct intravenous bolus over a few minutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Concentrated potassium given as a bolus reaches the myocardium at a concentration that can stop the heart, which is why it is always diluted and infused at a controlled rate, usually no faster than 10 mmol per hour peripherally.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Diluting and infusing by pump is correct practice, so it is not the unsafe action.
• B — Checking potassium and renal function first is correct, because they determine the dose.
• C — Monitoring the rhythm during the infusion is correct, because it detects the effect.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Never push potassium. Dilute it, pump it, and watch the rhythm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withholding the next dose and considering vitamin K as prescribed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Giving the usual dose because the patient is not bleeding"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Giving an extra dose of warfarin to bring the INR down"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switching to heparin immediately without seeking advice"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An INR of 5.5 is well above the therapeutic range of 2 to 3 for most indications, and although the patient is not bleeding the risk of haemorrhage is high. The prescription is usually withheld, the INR rechecked, and vitamin K considered, with a search for the cause such as a new interacting drug.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The usual dose adds to the anticoagulation in a patient who is already over-anticoagulated.
• C — An extra dose is a contradiction: warfarin raises the INR, so more would raise it further.
• D — Switching to heparin is a prescriber's decision, not an automatic substitution.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High INR without bleeding: hold the dose, recheck, and expect vitamin K to be considered.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report it, because the prescriber may switch to an alternative such as an ARB"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the drug permanently without telling the prescriber"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure the patient that the cough will always resolve on its own"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Increase the dose so that the cough settles within a few weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A dry, tickly cough affects a significant minority of patients on ACE inhibitors and is caused by bradykinin accumulation. It does not settle for most people, and if it is troublesome the prescriber usually substitutes an angiotensin receptor blocker, which does not cause it.

Why the other options fail:
• C — The cough is characteristically persistent, so reassurance that it will always resolve is wrong.
• B — Stopping a drug the patient needs without review is unsafe.
• D — Increasing the dose would not help and could worsen hypotension.$q$,
  strategy = $q$ACE inhibitor cough is common and persistent; the answer is usually an ARB.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give an extra dose of metformin before the contrast"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Verify the plan to withhold metformin and assess renal function"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Encourage dehydration so that the contrast clears faster"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop all diabetic treatment permanently before the scan"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Contrast-associated kidney injury can increase metformin accumulation and the risk of lactic acidosis, so the local protocol for withholding metformin and restarting it after renal assessment is followed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — An extra dose before contrast increases the risk of lactic acidosis when renal function is already reduced.
• D — Stopping all diabetes treatment permanently is not a nurse's decision and would be harmful.
• C — Encouraging dehydration worsens renal perfusion at the moment contrast stresses the kidneys.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contrast and renal impairment require medication reconciliation, especially with metformin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vitamin K"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Flumazenil"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Naloxone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atropine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Pinpoint pupils with a respiratory rate of 7 and difficulty rousing indicate opioid-induced respiratory depression; naloxone reverses the opioid while airway and ventilation are supported.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not opioids.
• A — Vitamin K reverses warfarin and has nothing to do with opioid depression.
• D — Atropine treats bradycardia and cholinergic toxicity, not opioid depression.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prioritise respiratory rate and consciousness; opioid respiratory depression requires naloxone and ventilation support.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop phenytoin abruptly until the gums settle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Double the dose to control the seizures faster"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Avoid all fluids to reduce the gum swelling"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use meticulous oral hygiene and attend dental reviews"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Gingival hyperplasia is a chronic effect of phenytoin; meticulous oral hygiene and regular dental review reduce its complications, and abrupt withdrawal can precipitate seizures.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Stopping phenytoin abruptly can precipitate seizures; changes are made only under medical direction.
• C — Restricting fluids does not treat gingival hyperplasia and risks dehydration.
• B — Doubling the dose increases the very effect that made the gums swell.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Know chronic adverse effects and teach prevention without stopping anticonvulsants suddenly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hold the drug and notify the prescriber before the next dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the next dose because spironolactone protects the heart"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Encourage a diet high in potassium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give an extra dose because it lowers the potassium"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Spironolactone is a potassium-sparing diuretic, so a potassium of 6.1 mmol/L is dangerous enough to threaten the myocardium. The next dose is withheld and the prescriber informed, with the ECG and renal function reviewed; treatment may include stopping the drug and giving a potassium binder or a diuretic that wastes potassium.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The next dose adds to the retention that produced the high level.
• C — A high-potassium diet worsens the hyperkalaemia.
• D — The drug raises potassium rather than lowering it, so another dose is the opposite of treatment.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Potassium-sparing diuretics raise potassium. At 6.1, hold the drug and call.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the scheduled dose of insulin because the meal is due"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage the patient to walk to the day room"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict carbohydrates until the glucose is checked"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Check the blood glucose and treat suspected hypoglycaemia promptly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Sweating, pallor and confusion are adrenergic and neuroglycopenic signs of hypoglycaemia; check the glucose if possible and give fast-acting carbohydrate or IV dextrose as appropriate.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Giving the scheduled insulin to a hypoglycaemic patient can be fatal.
• B — Exercise lowers the blood glucose further and would deepen the hypoglycaemia.
• C — Restricting carbohydrate removes the treatment at the moment it is needed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Treat suspected hypoglycaemia immediately; never give more insulin before assessment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The medicine can be stopped as soon as the cough improves and the fever settles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It has no important interactions with other medicines, including the contraceptive pill"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Urine, sweat and tears may turn orange-red, and contact lenses can be permanently stained"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Missed doses can safely be doubled the next day to catch up on the treatment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The orange-red discolouration is harmless but alarming if unexpected, and it permanently stains soft contact lenses, so warning the client prevents both distress and damage. Rifampicin is also a powerful enzyme inducer, which makes the interaction teaching essential.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Stopping as soon as the cough improves risks relapse and resistance.
• B — Rifampicin reduces the effect of oral contraceptives, warfarin and many antiretrovirals.
• D — Doubling a missed dose is never correct for an antimicrobial.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rifampicin stains everything orange and induces liver enzymes. Warn about both.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Double the next dose if one dose is forgotten during the day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrict all fluids to less than one litre a day so the level stays steady"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep salt and fluid intake steady and report vomiting or diarrhoea promptly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the drug as soon as the mood improves so that the levels do not build up"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Lithium is a salt that competes with sodium: when the body is short of sodium the kidney reabsorbs more lithium, the plasma level rises and toxicity follows. Anything that depletes salt or water — heat with heavy sweating, vomiting, diarrhoea, a low-salt diet or diuretics — raises the level, so intake is kept steady and illness is reported promptly.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Restricting fluid concentrates the lithium and invites toxicity.
• A — Doubling a missed dose risks a double dose; missed doses are discussed with the prescriber.
• D — Stopping invites relapse, because lithium is continued to prevent further episodes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lithium follows sodium. Loss of salt or water raises the level, so steady intake and prompt reporting matter.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take it with vitamin C such as orange juice, expect dark stools, and avoid tea at the same time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take it with an antacid so that the stomach lining is protected from the iron"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take it with a cup of strong tea so that the taste is masked and absorption is improved"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop it as soon as the stools darken and the fatigue improves"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Vitamin C keeps iron in the ferrous form that is absorbed, while tea contains tannins and antacids raise the gastric pH; both substantially reduce uptake. Dark stools are the harmless result of unabsorbed iron and are not a reason to stop.

Why the other options fail:
• B — An antacid raises gastric pH and impairs the absorption this prescription depends on.
• C — Tea tannins bind iron and reduce absorption.
• D — Stopping for dark stools abandons treatment for a normal effect.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vitamin C up, tea and antacids down. Black stools are expected, not a reason to stop.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 20
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch43.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 43: NG-PEDS, whole bank (20 items).
--
-- Same two defects as NG-PHARM: keys on a five-times a, b, c, d cycle, and
-- only three items carried a distractor block. Mean ratio 1.71, worst 2.52.
-- Keys re-ordered, all twenty options rewritten, a block written for every item
-- and lettered to the final layout. Mean ratio 1.71 -> 1.22.
-- No stem changed. UPDATE only: no deletes, no reseed.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A normal finding at this age that needs no action and no follow-up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Raised intracranial pressure producing a tense, bulging fontanelle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Poor feeding only, with no fluid deficit or weight loss to explain it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dehydration with reduced circulating volume, needing urgent assessment and fluids"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$A fontanelle that sinks below the level of the surrounding skull means the intravascular and extravascular spaces are depleted; it is a late and reliable sign in an infant. Paired with dry mucous membranes and eight hours without urine, it describes significant dehydration, and the child needs urgent assessment, a weight, vital signs and a plan for fluid replacement, oral if tolerated and intravenous if not.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A soft, flat fontanelle is normal, whereas a sunken one is not, and the oliguria and dry mucosa settle the point.
• B — Raised intracranial pressure produces a bulging, tense fontanelle with a high-pitched cry and altered consciousness.
• C — Poor feeding alone does not explain a sunken fontanelle, dry mucosa and eight hours without urine.

A sunken fontanelle is one of the few direct windows on an infant's volume status.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sunken fontanelle means volume loss. Bulging fontanelle means raised pressure. Flat and soft is normal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report at once and check the bandages and traction ropes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassess the toes again in one hour and document the colour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a warm compress over the toes to improve the circulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Document the finding and continue the routine traction care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Cool, pale toes with delayed capillary refill mean the circulation is being obstructed, most often by a tight bandage, a slipped traction rope or a cast. The obstruction is relieved immediately and the surgeon informed, because untreated ischaemia leads to permanent damage.

Why the other options fail:
• B — An hour of ischaemia is enough to damage muscle and nerve.
• C — Warming the surface does not restore perfusion and hides the change.
• D — This is an emergency, not an observation to record and leave.

Reference: Ghana STG — traction care; neurovascular observation$q$,
  strategy = $q$In traction, the toes report the circulation — compare colour, warmth, refill and movement each shift.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell the child off for behaving like a baby in front of the other children"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Recognise it as a stress response to hospitalisation and offer reassurance and play"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask for an immediate psychiatric referral for the enuresis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict fluids from the afternoon onwards until it settles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Toddlers and preschool children commonly regress under stress, and admission is a major stress. Bedwetting in a previously trained child is a normal, temporary response that improves when the child feels safe, is prepared for procedures and is given play and routine.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Telling the child off adds shame to fear and can prolong the regression.
• C — A psychiatric referral medicalises a normal response to admission.
• D — Fluid restriction is a dehydration risk and does not address the cause.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Regression in hospital is a communication, not a discipline problem. Reassure, prepare and play.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop feeding from both breasts for two days to rest them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bind the breasts tightly with a firm bandage for support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Feed frequently and express milk if the breast stays full"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict fluids until the engorgement has completely settled"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Engorgement is milk stasis plus oedema, and the treatment is to keep the milk moving: frequent feeding with good attachment, expressing after feeds if the breast remains hard, and a warm shower before feeding. Analgesia and a cold pack between feeds ease the discomfort.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Stopping feeding worsens stasis and is the commonest route to mastitis.
• B — Binding compresses ducts and causes blocked ducts and abscess.
• D — Fluid restriction does not reduce milk production and dehydrates the mother.

Reference: Ghana STG — breastfeeding; engorgement$q$,
  strategy = $q$Engorgement is treated by emptying, not by resting or binding the breast.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Niacin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vitamin A"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thiamine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vitamin C"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Night blindness with dry conjunctivae and Bitot's spots is xerophthalmia, the ocular manifestation of vitamin A deficiency, which in Ghana is addressed through supplementation and dietary advice about dark green leaves, mango, pawpaw and liver. Untreated, it can progress to corneal ulceration and permanent blindness.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Niacin deficiency causes pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhoea and dementia.
• C — Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi and Wernicke's encephalopathy.
• D — Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy with bleeding gums and poor wound healing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Night blindness plus Bitot's spots means vitamin A, not niacin or thiamine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 3-year-old with severe recession and grunting who remains breathless on high-flow oxygen"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 5-year-old with mild diarrhoea who is alert and drinking freely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 7-year-old waiting for a chest radiograph with a temperature of 37.9°C"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 9-year-old who is dressed and waiting for his discharge letter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Grunting, severe recession and no response to oxygen is a child in respiratory failure, which is the most time-critical finding in any waiting room and takes precedence over every other patient listed.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Mild illness with a protected airway and a child who is drinking can wait.
• C — A child who is stable and awaiting imaging is not deteriorating in front of you.
• D — A child well enough to be discharged does not need the first assessment.

In prioritisation questions, the child who is failing to respond to treatment outranks the one who has not yet been treated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Assess first the child who looks worst despite treatment, not the one who is merely waiting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rotate injection sites systematically so that lumps and unpredictable absorption do not develop"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use the same site every day so that the technique is familiar to the child"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Aspirate after inserting the needle and before injecting the insulin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reuse the needle until it becomes blunt to reduce the cost of supplies"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Repeated injection into the same spot causes lipohypertrophy, which absorbs insulin unpredictably and produces unexplained highs and lows, so sites are rotated within and between regions.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Using the same site every day is the direct cause of lipohypertrophy.
• C — Aspiration is unnecessary for subcutaneous insulin and adds pain.
• D — A reused needle becomes bent and burred, causing pain and inaccurate dosing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rotate sites to protect absorption. A lump is not just cosmetic, it changes the dose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rapid intravenous Ringer's lactate at twice the usual rate for the child's age"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Large volumes of plain water by mouth to replace the fluid losses"}, {"id": "c", "text": "No fluids until the diarrhoea stops and the child begins to ask for food"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral rehydration with ReSoMal under the severe-malnutrition protocol, with close monitoring"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In severe acute malnutrition the myocardium and the sodium-potassium balance are both compromised, so standard rehydration fluids and rates can precipitate heart failure. The specific low-sodium, high-potassium solution is given slowly under the malnutrition protocol with feeding continued and the child watched.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The classic error: rapid Ringer's lactate in severe malnutrition can kill by volume overload.
• B — Plain water gives no electrolytes and risks hyponatraemia.
• C — Withholding fluid allows shock to develop.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In severe malnutrition, rehydrate slowly with the malnutrition-specific fluid, never with rapid standard boluses.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Attempt to push the bowel gently back inside the abdomen before the transfer"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cover the bowel with warm sterile saline-soaked gauze, keep the baby warm and refer for surgery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a dry adherent dressing directly over the membrane and the bowel"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give small oral feeds to maintain hydration during the journey to theatre"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$An omphalocele is covered by a membrane, but the bowel is still exposed to heat and fluid loss, so it is covered with warm sterile saline gauze and plastic wrap if available, the baby is kept warm and nil by mouth, and a nasogastric tube is passed while urgent surgical referral is made.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Pushing the bowel back risks perforation and contamination.
• C — A dry dressing adheres to the membrane and tears it.
• D — Oral feeds are unsafe before the defect is closed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cover with warm saline gauze, keep warm, no feeds, nasogastric tube, refer. Do not push it back.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pulmonary artery to the descending aorta"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Aorta to the pulmonary artery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Right atrium to the left atrium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Umbilical vein to the inferior vena cava"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$In fetal life the lungs are fluid-filled and collapsed and pulmonary vascular resistance is high, so most blood entering the pulmonary artery is shunted through the ductus arteriosus into the descending aorta and back to the placenta. It is kept open by circulating prostaglandins and by low oxygen tension. After birth, breathing expands the lungs, pulmonary resistance falls and oxygen rises; prostaglandin levels fall and the ductus constricts into the ligamentum arteriosum.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Reversing the direction describes the left-to-right shunt seen after birth when the ductus fails to close.
• C — Right atrium to left atrium describes the foramen ovale.
• D — Umbilical vein to inferior vena cava describes the ductus venosus, which bypasses the liver.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ductus arteriosus: pulmonary artery to aorta. Ductus venosus: umbilical vein to inferior vena cava.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Essential hypertension inherited from the parents"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A normal finding that needs no further assessment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A secondary cause such as renal or cardiovascular disease"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Anxiety about the clinic visit and the cuff size"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Hypertension in a young child is uncommon and is much more likely to be secondary, with renal parenchymal disease and coarctation of the aorta heading the list, so the child needs urinalysis, renal function and imaging rather than reassurance.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Essential hypertension is the usual pattern in adolescents and adults, not in a 3-year-old.
• B — Reassurance would miss a treatable cause of significant harm.
• D — Anxiety does not explain persistently raised readings at three separate visits.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High blood pressure in a small child is a clue to an underlying disease, usually renal or cardiac.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Isoniazid with pyridoxine deficiency risk"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Digoxin—calcium deficiency as the typical direct deficiency"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Phenytoin with vitamin D deficiency risk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Broad-spectrum antibiotics with vitamin K deficiency risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$The question asks which pairing is incorrect, and digoxin is the one that does not belong: it is associated with toxicity and electrolyte interactions, not with a typical calcium-deficiency state.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Isoniazid does cause pyridoxine deficiency, so the pairing is correct.
• C — Phenytoin does affect vitamin D metabolism, so the pairing is correct.
• D — Broad-spectrum antibiotics do reduce vitamin K production by gut flora, so the pairing is correct.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Check whether the medication causes a recognised nutrient deficiency rather than merely an electrolyte interaction.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An 18 gauge, because a bigger catheter is always better for fluids"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 14 gauge central line inserted into the femoral vein"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 22 or 24 gauge, the smallest that allows the fluids and rate required"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A butterfly needle taped loosely over the antecubital fossa"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$The right catheter is the smallest one that will carry the prescribed therapy at the prescribed rate, because a smaller gauge causes less vein trauma and is easier to site in a toddler. For rehydration in a stable child, 22 or 24 gauge is usually adequate.

Why the other options fail:
• A — An 18 gauge is unnecessarily traumatic and harder to site in small veins.
• B — A central line is invasive and disproportionate for a child who is not shocked.
• D — A butterfly needle is unstable for a continuous infusion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Choose the smallest catheter that delivers the therapy. Bigger is not better in a toddler's vein.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pull the pinna up and back before instilling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pull the pinna down and back before instilling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instil the drops with the child sitting upright"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warm the drops in a microwave before use"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$In a child under three the external canal runs upwards and forwards, so the pinna is pulled gently down and back to straighten it. The child lies on the opposite side and stays there for a few minutes afterwards.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Up and back is the technique for an adult or an older child, not a toddler.
• C — Sitting allows the drops to run straight out of the canal.
• D — Microwaving heats unevenly and can scald the canal; drops are warmed in the hand.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — paediatric medication administration$q$,
  strategy = $q$Under three years pull down and back; over three years pull up and back.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the bruises immediately as a suspected non-accidental injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Arrange a skeletal survey before asking anything else"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise the mother to stop the child from standing for now"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Explain that these are the usual bruises of a child who is learning to walk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bruises on the shins, knees and forehead are the expected pattern in a child who is mobile and pulling to stand, because these are the bony points that meet furniture and the floor, and the history matches the developmental stage and the sites.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Treating a normal finding as abuse distresses the family and triggers an unnecessary investigation.
• B — A skeletal survey is disproportionate to accidental bruising of the shins.
• C — Stopping the child from standing interferes with a normal developmental milestone.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Match the bruise to the developmental stage. Shins and knees belong to a walker, not a baby who cannot move.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Haemophilia A inherited through the mother's family"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura with a low platelet count"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Disseminated intravascular coagulation caused by sepsis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vitamin K deficiency bleeding, because prophylaxis was not given at birth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Late vitamin K deficiency bleeding presents between two weeks and three months, classically in exclusively breastfed babies who did not receive vitamin K at birth, and it can present as intracranial haemorrhage, so it is a medical emergency.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Haemophilia is an inherited factor deficiency presenting with joint and soft-tissue bleeding, not umbilical bleeding in the first weeks.
• B — Immune thrombocytopenia causes petechiae and bruising with a low platelet count.
• C — Disseminated intravascular coagulation comes with a sick, febrile baby.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Late bleeding in an unprophylaxed breastfed newborn is vitamin K deficiency until proved otherwise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Punish the regression firmly so that it does not become a habit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ignore the older child until the bedwetting behaviour stops"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the older child consistent one-to-one attention and a role caring for the baby"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Move the older child to a separate room until he is dry again at night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Regression to an earlier behaviour is a normal response to the arrival of a sibling, and it is best managed by giving the older child predictable, individual attention and a role in caring for the baby, which restores his sense of position in the family.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Punishment worsens the anxiety that produced the regression.
• B — Withdrawing attention compounds the sense of displacement.
• D — Isolating the child does nothing for the underlying cause and adds another loss.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Regression after a new baby is displacement, not misbehaviour: give attention and a role.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use a firm pillow under the head to keep the head raised during sleep"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the child on their front once they can roll over confidently"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Line the cot with thick padding on all four sides to prevent bruising"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the cot clear of pillows, thick blankets and soft toys, and place the child on the back"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Once a child can roll, the risk changes from SIDS to suffocation and entrapment, and the principle stays the same: a firm, flat surface, nothing loose in the cot, no pillows or padded bumpers, and the child placed on their back.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A pillow adds a soft surface that can cover the face.
• B — Placing the child prone increases the risk of rebreathing.
• C — Padding reintroduces the soft, loose materials the cot should not contain.$q$,
  strategy = $q$A safe cot is an empty cot: firm flat mattress, nothing loose, child on the back.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use a rear-facing car seat in the back, and never hold the baby in someone's arms"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hold the baby securely on an adult's lap for short trips"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use a forward-facing seat so that the baby can see out"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lay the baby flat on the back seat with the seatbelt over the blanket"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A rear-facing seat spreads the forces of a crash across the whole back of an infant's body and protects the relatively heavy head and weak neck, and it is used in the back seat away from the front passenger airbag.

Why the other options fail:
• B — An adult cannot hold a child in a crash; this is the most dangerous option.
• C — Forward-facing turns the fragile neck into the direction of the impact far too early.
• D — Lying flat under a seatbelt provides no restraint at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rear-facing, back seat, never on a lap. That is the whole of infant car safety.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Frequent visitors and bright lighting so that the spasms can be observed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vigorous physiotherapy of the jaw and limbs to prevent stiffness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A quiet, darkened, single room with clustered care and minimal handling"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Withholding sedation until the spasms have stopped completely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Tetanus toxin blocks the release of inhibitory transmitters, so any stimulus can trigger a generalised spasm that obstructs the airway or fractures the spine. The environment is the treatment: a quiet, dark, single room, minimal handling, clustered care, and sedation with muscle relaxation prescribed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Visitors and bright light supply the stimuli that cause spasms.
• B — Vigorous physiotherapy can provoke spasm and injury.
• D — Withholding sedation lets the spasms continue unchecked.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In tetanus, the room is part of the prescription. Quiet, dark, minimal handling, sedated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS-0020$q$;

