-- Batch 55: five duplicates rewritten (CHP x2, DRG x3).
--
-- Five duplicate items rewritten from the NG-CHP and NG-DRG reads. Both families
-- were read end to end (50 items each); no wrong key was found in either.
-- 
--   NG-CHP-2254 repeated NG-EPH-2077  (poor outreach attendance)   -> CHPS community health committee
--   NG-CHP-2280 repeated NG-MCB-2547  (zero report)                -> acute flaccid paralysis surveillance
--   NG-DRG-1003 repeated NG-FLD-2162  (glucose in ORS)             -> small frequent sips of ORS
--   NG-DRG-1007 repeated NG-MHN-0824  (neuroleptic malignant syndrome) -> artemether-lumefantrine counselling
--   NG-DRG-1008 repeated NG-MHN-0822  (lithium plus dehydration)   -> phenytoin toxicity
-- 
-- Each keeps its key letter: NG-CHP stays 13/13/12/12, NG-DRG stays 13/13/12/12.
-- Every replacement topic was checked against all 5,510 items first.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newly opened CHPS compound has an elected community health committee. What is the committee's **main** role?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To interview and appoint the community health officer for the zone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To take over the day-to-day running of the compound from the nurse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To raise funds that replace the district's budget for the facility"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To link the community and the service, plan with it and hold it accountable"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "To link the community and the service, plan with it and hold it accountable". The community health committee is the governance bridge of the CHPS zone: it is elected by the community, it agrees the priorities and the outreach calendar with the community health officer, it mobilises local support such as accommodation for the nurse, it carries complaints in both directions, and it reports back to the people it represents. That two-way link is what makes the compound a community service rather than a clinic that happens to be nearby.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The community health officer is recruited and posted by the Ghana Health Service through the district; the committee is consulted, not the employer.
• B — Clinical and administrative running of the compound remains with the community health officer and the sub-district, not with a lay committee.
• C — The committee may mobilise small local contributions, but the recurrent budget and the staff come from the district and must not be replaced by fundraising.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — CHPS implementation guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$The committee links and holds to account. It does not hire, run the clinic or fund it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHP-2254$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A volunteer reports a child who became unable to stand three weeks after a fever and now has weak, floppy legs. What does this represent for the surveillance system?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A case that need not be reported now that polio has been eradicated from the region"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A surgical problem to be referred to the district hospital through the usual route"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute flaccid paralysis, which must be notified and investigated at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A case to be entered in the weekly return and reviewed at the end of the month"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Acute flaccid paralysis, which must be notified and investigated at once". Any child under fifteen with sudden weakness or floppiness of a limb, with or without fever, is a case of acute flaccid paralysis until proven otherwise, and AFP surveillance is the system that would detect a return of polio. The report goes to the district the same day, two stool specimens are collected 24 to 48 hours apart and sent to the designated laboratory, the child is reviewed at 60 days to see whether the weakness has persisted, and the immediate neighbourhood is checked for other cases and for vaccination coverage. None of that happens if the case is filed as a routine return.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Eradication in a region is exactly when surveillance must stay sensitive: the risk that matters is importation, and it is detected only by reporting every flaccid case.
• B — Weakness after a fever is not a surgical problem, and referral alone bypasses the specimen collection and the 60-day review that the system needs.
• D — A case of AFP is immediately notifiable, not a weekly count; a month's delay loses both the specimens and the chance to find other cases.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — IDSR technical guidelines; AFP surveillance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Floppy limb in a child is AFP first. Notify the same day, two stools, review at 60 days.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHP-2280$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother is shown how to give oral rehydration solution to her child with diarrhoea. Why is it given in small frequent sips with a spoon rather than as one large volume?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A large volume at once is too expensive for most households to prepare"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Small amounts are absorbed despite vomiting, while a large volume comes back up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sips are used only because the child is likely to refuse a cup"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Small volumes increase the concentration of sodium in each swallow"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Small amounts are absorbed despite vomiting, while a large volume comes back up". Rehydration depends on what stays down and crosses the gut wall, not on what is offered. A child who is vomiting will usually keep a few millilitres given slowly by spoon or cup every one to two minutes, while a full cup tipped in at once distends the stomach, provokes another vomit and is lost. The mother is taught to keep going, to give more if the child wants it, and to watch for the signs that fluid is going in: passing urine, a moist mouth and a softer fontanelle in an infant. If the child cannot drink at all, or vomits everything, the route has failed and the child needs intravenous fluid.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Cost does not change with how the same solution is divided; the point is tolerance.
• C — Refusal is handled by patience and by offering the solution from a cup or spoon the child accepts, not by the size of the sip.
• D — The concentration is fixed by the sachet and the water added; dividing the volume does not change it.

Reference: Ghana STG — diarrhoeal disease in children; WHO ORS guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$It is what stays down that rehydrates. Small and often beats one big cup.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRG-1003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother is given artemether-lumefantrine dispersible tablets for her child's uncomplicated malaria. What must she be told about giving them?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give them on an empty stomach so that the medicine is absorbed faster"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the course once the fever has settled, usually after the second dose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give them with iron-rich food to replace what the malaria has destroyed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give them with milk or a fatty meal, and complete all six doses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Give them with milk or a fatty meal, and complete all six doses". The lumefantrine in the combination is poorly absorbed unless there is fat in the stomach, so the tablet is taken with breast milk, milk, or a meal that contains some oil. Without it the blood level stays too low to clear the parasites, which is how recrudescence and eventually resistance are produced. The course is six doses over three days, at 0, 8, 24, 36, 48 and 60 hours, and it is finished even when the fever has gone. The child is also weighed so the dose matches the weight band.

Why the other options fail:
• A — An empty stomach reduces absorption of lumefantrine; the fat is part of how the drug works.
• B — Stopping early leaves parasites behind and is a direct cause of treatment failure and resistance.
• C — Iron has nothing to do with the malaria or the drug, and giving iron during the acute illness is not the priority.

Reference: Ghana STG — malaria in children; WHO ACT dosing guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lumefantrine needs fat, and malaria needs all six doses. Milk with it, finish the course.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRG-1007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian man taking phenytoin for epilepsy develops unsteadiness, slurred speech and jerky movements of the eyes. What has happened?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A viral illness that will settle without any change to his medicines"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Under-treatment, so the dose should be increased at the next visit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An expected effect showing he has reached a steady level in the blood"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Phenytoin toxicity, because the level has risen above the therapeutic range"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Phenytoin toxicity, because the level has risen above the therapeutic range". Nystagmus, ataxia and dysarthria are the classic dose-related signs of too much phenytoin, and they appear in that order as the level climbs past the therapeutic range of about 10 to 20 mg/L. The margin is narrow and easily crossed: a modest dose increase, a change in another drug that competes for the same liver enzymes, or a fall in the albumin that carries the drug all raise the free level. The nurse withholds the next dose, reports the signs and arranges a blood level rather than adjusting anything.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The combination of eye signs, ataxia and slurred speech in a patient on an anticonvulsant is not a coincidental viral illness.
• B — Increasing the dose in the presence of toxicity deepens it; the level is already too high.
• C — A steady level inside the range produces no symptoms; these signs mean the level is above it.

Reference: Ghana STG — epilepsy; British National Formulary phenytoin monitoring$q$,
  strategy = $q$Eyes jerk, then the gait, then the speech. Phenytoin toxicity climbs in that order.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRG-1008$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch56.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 56: three duplicates rewritten in NG-MDA.
--
-- Three duplicate items rewritten from the NG-MDA read (50 items, no wrong key).
-- 
--   NG-MDA-1092 repeated NG-MED-0314  (diarrhoea, metabolic acidosis)     -> capillary glucose sampling
--   NG-MDA-1093 repeated NG-MED-0316  (deep rapid breathing in DKA)       -> peak flow technique
--   NG-MDA-1103 repeated NG-DRC-2472  (concentrated KCl as a push)        -> rings, watches and nail varnish
-- 
-- Each keeps its key letter, so NG-MDA stays 13/13/12/12. Every replacement topic -
-- finger-prick technique, peak flow, hand hygiene jewellery - returned zero coverage
-- when searched across all 5,510.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse takes a capillary blood glucose sample from a patient's finger. Which practice is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use the side of the fingertip, rotate sites, and wipe away the first drop if soiled"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Squeeze the fingertip hard so that a large drop is obtained quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use the pad of the thumb, because it has the richest blood supply"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clean the finger with spirit and prick it while the skin is still wet"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Use the side of the fingertip, rotate sites, and wipe away the first drop if soiled". The side of the fingertip has fewer nerve endings than the pad, so it hurts less and is less likely to be used repeatedly until it is sore; rotating between fingers and sites protects the skin of a patient who will be tested for years. The hand is washed and dried, and if sugar or fruit has been handled the first drop is wiped away and a second allowed to form, because contamination reads falsely high.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Squeezing hard breaks red cells and draws tissue fluid into the drop, which lowers the reading; the finger is held down and massaged gently instead.
• C — The thumb and index finger are the ones used for grasping and are best avoided; the pulp of the thumb is also thick and less sensitive to the puncture.
• D — Alcohol must be allowed to dry completely; while wet it dilutes the drop and stings, and either can distort the result.

Reference: Ghana STG — diabetes; WHO guidelines on drawing blood$q$,
  strategy = $q$Side of the finger, rotate the site, let the spirit dry, and do not milk the finger.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1092$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with asthma is taught to use a peak flow meter at home. Which instruction is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take one gentle breath out into the meter and record whatever number appears"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stand upright, breathe in fully, then blow out as hard and as fast as possible"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use the meter only on days when the wheeze is troublesome enough to notice"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait at least an hour after using a reliever inhaler before any measurement"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Stand upright, breathe in fully, then blow out as hard and as fast as possible". A peak flow reading is only comparable with the last one if the effort is the same, so the technique is the measurement: stand or sit upright, take the deepest breath possible, seal the lips round the mouthpiece, and blow out as hard and fast as can be managed, three times, recording the best of the three. The reading is compared with the patient's own best, not with a predicted value from a chart, because the personal best is what the current number means. Effort falls as asthma worsens, which is precisely why the test is useful and why technique has to be checked at each review.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A gentle breath measures almost nothing and gives a number that cannot be trended.
• C — Daily readings, morning and evening, are what show the diurnal variation and the fall before an attack; measuring only when symptomatic loses the warning.
• D — A reliever is expected to improve the reading within minutes, and the reading taken after it shows how much reversibility there is; waiting an hour destroys that information.

Reference: Ghana STG — asthma; WHO/NHLBI asthma management guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Same position, same effort, best of three. The number means nothing without the technique.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1093$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A ward sister asks the nurses to remove rings, wristwatches and nail varnish before clinical work. What is the **main** reason?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Metal and polish react with alcohol hand rub and shorten its shelf life"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Jewellery can scratch patients during moving and handling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Skin under rings and chipped polish harbours organisms hand rub cannot reach"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The dress code exists mainly to present a tidy appearance to patients"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Skin under rings and chipped polish harbours organisms hand rub cannot reach". Hand hygiene works where the product reaches. A ring leaves a band of skin that is missed every time, and it stays moist, so organisms accumulate there and are transferred to the next patient; chipped polish and long or artificial nails do the same, and outbreaks have been traced to them. That is why the guidance is not about appearance: the jewellery comes off, nails are kept short and clean, and sleeves are bare to the elbow so that the wrist is covered by the rub.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Alcohol rub is not affected in any meaningful way by contact with metal; the problem is the skin underneath.
• B — A scratch is a plausible small harm, but it is not why the rule exists and is not the reason organisms are carried between patients.
• D — Appearance is a by-product; a nurse in bare, clean hands and short nails presents well because of the infection control, not instead of it.

Reference: Ghana National Infection Prevention and Control Guidelines; WHO five moments for hand hygiene$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hand rub only kills what it touches. Rings and chipped polish hide a patch it never touches.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1103$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch57.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 57: NG-TRA and NG-EBP read in full, eight duplicates rewritten.
--
-- NG-TRA read in full (50 items) and NG-EBP read in full (50 items). Eight duplicate items
-- rewritten, each replacing a question that another item already asked.
-- 
-- 1862 asked what NG-PER-1570 asks (which burn finding means the airway is threatened) and is
-- now the lap-belt sign and the hollow-viscus injury it predicts.
-- 1875 asked what NG-SUR-0338 and NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0799 both ask (how to prevent DVT after major
-- surgery) and is now the fight bite: irrigate, never close.
-- 1876 asked what NG-PER-1561 asks (where to put a drowsy patient so secretions drain) and is now
-- the five-second head-lift test for residual neuromuscular blockade.
-- 1879 asked what NG-MSN-2754 asks (a burst laparotomy wound) and is now blood at the meatus
-- after a pelvic fracture, where the catheter must be withheld.
-- 1883 asked what NG-MSN-2772 asks (bladder irrigation that turns bloody after prostate surgery)
-- and is now the mechanism of a degloving injury.
-- NG-SRG-0537 was a third copy of the NG-TRA-1876 / NG-PER-1561 positioning question, with the
-- same four options and the same key, and is now local anaesthetic systemic toxicity.
-- NG-EBP-1615 asked what NG-RSM-0965 asks (which questionnaire item is badly built - both were the
-- double-barrelled one) and is now a response scale that cannot answer a frequency question.
-- NG-EBP-1601 asked what NG-RSM-0984 asks (whether a foreign trial applies here) and is now
-- heterogeneity in a meta-analysis.
-- 
-- Two pairs sat below the 0.40 stem threshold (0.38 and 0.35) and were caught only by reading the
-- EBP bank in full; that is the argument for reading rather than thresholding.
-- 
-- Every key kept its letter, so no bank's a/b/c/d balance moves: TRA stays a13 b13 c12 d12, EBP
-- stays a13 b13 c12 d12, and the global count stays a1,402 b1,393 c1,362 d1,353. Stem Jaccard
-- against the whole bank stays at 0.38 or below and full-text Jaccard at 0.24 or below; the single
-- 0.38 is NG-CPD-1976, a children's blood-test item that shares only the question scaffolding. The
-- keyed option is not the longest in any of the eight, and option length parity runs 1.05 to 1.18.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A driver who was wearing only a lap belt has bruising across the lower abdomen and increasing abdominal pain. What should the nurse suspect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Simple bruising of the abdominal wall that will settle down"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bowel crushed between the belt and the spine, which may have torn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A fractured pelvis with blood loss into the surrounding tissues"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Trapped air in the abdominal cavity from the force of the impact"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Bowel crushed between the belt and the spine, which may have torn". A lap belt crosses the lower abdomen while the spine behind it is fixed, so <strong>the bowel is crushed between the two</strong>. Bruising in the line of the belt, with pain or tenderness, is the seat-belt sign and means a hollow viscus may have been injured. <strong>The abdomen can feel soft and the x-ray can look normal at first</strong>, because a small tear leaks slowly; peritonitis and free air may take hours to appear. The patient is kept nil by mouth, given intravenous access and analgesia, and observed closely for pulse, tenderness and abdominal girth, with early surgical review.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A belt-line bruise after a crash marks the force that was applied, not a minor injury.
• C — A pelvic fracture would not explain the belt-line bruising, and the abdomen still has to be assessed.
• D — Free air is the consequence of a perforation, not the injury itself, and is often absent early.

Reference: ATLS 10th edition — abdominal trauma; Ghana STG$q$,
  strategy = $q$A bruise in the belt line after a crash means a crushed hollow organ until it is proved otherwise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1862$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man has a small puncture wound over the knuckles after punching another person in the mouth. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Suture the small wound so that it heals without a scar"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply an antiseptic cream and cover it with a dry dressing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wash it with surgical spirit and bandage it firmly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Irrigate it thoroughly and leave it open to drain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Irrigate it thoroughly and leave it open to drain". A wound over the knuckles from a punch is a fight bite: with the fist clenched, <strong>the mouth's organisms are driven through the skin into the joint and tendon sheath</strong>. It looks trivial with the hand open and becomes a deep infection with the hand closed. Treatment is copious irrigation, <strong>no primary closure</strong>, a prescribed antibiotic active against oral anaerobes, tetanus cover, splinting and elevation, with surgical review if the joint or tendon is involved.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Closing it seals the mouth's organisms inside the joint space.
• B — A dry dressing without irrigation leaves the inoculum in the deep tissue.
• C — Spirit on an open wound is tissue-toxic and does not irrigate the depth of the tract.

Reference: Ghana STG — wound management; WHO surgical site infection guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$A fight bite is a joint infection waiting to happen. Irrigate it, never suture it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1875$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient in the recovery room cannot lift his head off the pillow or grip the nurse's hand firmly. What does this indicate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "He is still sleepy from the anaesthetic and needs more time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The muscle relaxant has not worn off and his airway is at risk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He is guarding his neck because the operation was painful"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He is ready to return to the ward once his observations are stable"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The muscle relaxant has not worn off and his airway is at risk". Holding the head off the pillow for five seconds, and a firm grip, are the bedside tests of recovery from neuromuscular blockade. <strong>If he cannot do them, the muscles of the airway and of breathing are still partly paralysed</strong>, and the danger is obstruction, aspiration and respiratory failure after transfer. A nasal voice, double vision and shallow breathing point the same way. The nurse keeps him in recovery with oxygen, suction and monitoring, and informs the anaesthetist, because reversal may be needed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Sleepiness does not stop a patient lifting the head; weakness does.
• C — Pain restricts movement of the wound, not the strength of the neck flexors or the hand.
• D — Stable observations and an ability to answer questions do not test muscle power.

Reference: AAGBI — immediate post-anaesthetic recovery; Ghana STG — perioperative care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Five-second head lift is the test. If he fails it, he is not safe to leave recovery.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1876$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with a fractured pelvis has a drop of blood at the urinary meatus. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insert a urinary catheter to measure the urine output hourly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave the catheter out and report the finding immediately"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Press on the lower abdomen to empty the bladder of urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give a diuretic and record the volume of urine passed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Leave the catheter out and report the finding immediately". Blood at the meatus after a pelvic fracture suggests <strong>the urethra has been torn</strong>, and a catheter pushed blindly can turn a partial tear into a complete one and infect the pelvic haematoma. The other signs are perineal or scrotal bruising, a high-riding prostate on rectal examination, and inability to pass urine with a full bladder. The diagnosis is made by retrograde urethrogram, and bladder drainage, if it is needed, is by suprapubic catheter placed by the urologist.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Passing the catheter is the action that can complete the tear.
• C — Pressing the bladder can force urine through the tear into the tissues.
• D — A diuretic makes more urine against a blocked outlet and does nothing for the injury.

Reference: ATLS 10th edition — genitourinary trauma; Ghana STG — urology$q$,
  strategy = $q$Blood at the meatus after pelvic trauma means no catheter until the urethra has been imaged.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1879$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why can a degloving injury of the thigh threaten life out of proportion to the size of the wound?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stripped skin tears its vessels and bleeds into the tissue planes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The exposed muscle loses heat and can no longer warm the body"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The pain is far greater than in other wounds and causes fainting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Air enters the tissue planes and causes a rapidly spreading infection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Stripped skin tears its vessels and bleeds into the tissue planes". In a degloving injury the skin and fat are sheared off the fascia, and the vessels that crossed that plane are torn with them, so <strong>litres can bleed into the space under the flap while the surface looks like a graze</strong>. The raw surface also loses fluid and heat, and the devitalised fat becomes a medium for infection. That is why the patient is resuscitated and watched for shock before the wound is judged by its size, and why the area goes to theatre for debridement rather than being stitched.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Heat loss is real but contributes far less than the concealed bleeding.
• C — The pain is severe, but pain alone does not make the injury life-threatening.
• D — Gas in the tissues comes later, from infection, and is not the mechanism of the initial danger.

Reference: ATLS 10th edition — soft tissue injury; Ghana STG — wound management$q$,
  strategy = $q$Judge a degloving by what has bled into it, not by the size of the hole.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1883$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Ten minutes after a large field block the patient becomes confused and says his lips are tingling. What is happening?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The local anaesthetic has reached toxic levels in his blood"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The adrenaline added to the mixture is causing this"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He is panicking about the operation and hyperventilating"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The block has failed, so he is beginning to feel the surgery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "The local anaesthetic has reached toxic levels in his blood". Tingling around the mouth, a metallic taste, ringing in the ears and confusion are the <strong>early warning signs of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity</strong>, and they come before seizures, coma and cardiac arrest. A large volume is absorbed faster than it is cleared. The nurse stops the injection, calls for help, gives oxygen, monitors the ECG and prepares the resuscitation trolley, and <strong>requests lipid emulsion early, because it is the specific antidote</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Adrenaline causes palpitations, tremor and pallor, not tingling of the lips with confusion.
• C — Hyperventilation causes dizziness and cramp, but not a metallic taste with ringing in the ears.
• D — A failed block produces pain, not neurological warning signs.

Reference: AAGBI — local anaesthetic toxicity; Ghana STG — anaesthesia$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tingling lips and a metallic taste after a block means toxicity. Stop, oxygen, lipid emulsion.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-SRG-0537$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A meta-analysis pools ten trials and finds that their results differ widely from one another. What is this called?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Publication bias, because negative trials went unpublished"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Heterogeneity, which must be examined before pooling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confounding, because the groups were not comparable"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Attrition, because participants dropped out of the trials"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Heterogeneity, which must be examined before pooling". When trials differ in their patients, doses or definitions, <strong>their results vary more than chance alone would explain</strong>. That is heterogeneity: it is measured, and it has to be examined before a pooled estimate is trusted. If it is high, the review explains the differences or splits the studies into subgroups instead of averaging them away. Publication bias is about which studies were published, confounding is a third factor distorting a comparison, and attrition is the loss of participants during a study.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Publication bias is a missing-study problem, shown by an asymmetric funnel plot, not by disagreement between the studies that were found.
• C — Confounding distorts the comparison inside a study rather than describing variation between studies.
• D — Attrition is people leaving a trial, not trials disagreeing with each other.

Reference: Cochrane Handbook; standard research-methods texts$q$,
  strategy = $q$Trials that disagree are heterogeneous. Explain the difference before you average it away.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EBP-1601$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A questionnaire asks "How often did you miss a dose of your medicine?" and offers strongly agree to strongly disagree. What is wrong with this item?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It is too long for a busy respondent to read through"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It asks about a subject that people will not discuss honestly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It uses a scale that cannot answer a frequency question"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It gives the respondent no way to stay anonymous at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "It uses a scale that cannot answer a frequency question". "How often" needs a frequency response — never, sometimes, often, always, or a number of missed doses — while <strong>strongly agree to strongly disagree answers a statement of opinion, not a count</strong>. When the options do not fit the question, respondents tick something arbitrary and the data cannot be interpreted. The common faults in questionnaire items are a scale that does not match the question, two ideas in one item, a leading wording, jargon, and a recall period longer than memory can carry.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Length matters, but it is not the defect that makes this item unanswerable.
• B — Missed doses may be sensitive, but a sensitive topic can still be measured well; the scale is the fault here.
• D — Anonymity is a separate safeguard, and the item would be wrong even if it were anonymous.

Reference: standard research-methods texts — questionnaire design$q$,
  strategy = $q$The response scale must fit the question. Frequency questions need frequency answers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EBP-1615$q$;

-- Verify: expect 8
COMMIT;

