-- Batch 58: NG-PNC read in full, eight duplicates rewritten.
--
-- NG-PNC read in full (all 50 items). Eight items were carrying another item's question and are
-- rewritten, each keeping its key letter so the family stays a13 b13 c12 d12.
-- 
-- 1888 repeated NG-MAT-2365 - the same Naegele calculation on the same last menstrual period, 12
-- March, with 19 December and 5 December printed as options in both - and its key contradicted the
-- bank's own arithmetic: the three other Naegele items use +7 days then -3 months, which gives 19
-- December, while 1888 marked that answer wrong and keyed 17 December. It is now cervical
-- insufficiency and the cervical stitch.
-- 1898 repeated NG-ORIGINAL-OBS-0547 (why no vaginal examination while the placental site is
-- unknown), a question the bank already carried four times over, and is now physiological weight
-- loss in a breastfed newborn.
-- 1899 repeated NG-MCH2-0004 (hard tender uterus, little visible bleeding, shocked mother =
-- concealed abruption) and is now why breathlessness in a woman with a valve lesion is serious.
-- 1901 repeated NG-MAT-2359 (a gush of clear fluid at about 32 weeks, same four actions, one item
-- asking which to take and the other which to avoid) and is now the safe storage of expressed
-- breast milk. Warming is deliberately excluded because NG-NUT-0277 already asks it.
-- 1907 repeated NG-OBS-0554 (foetal heart about 90 staying low after the contraction) and is now
-- thick meconium-stained liquor.
-- 1913 repeated NG-OBS-0559 (steady bleeding with a firm uterus = genital tract trauma) and is now
-- intrauterine balloon tamponade for atonic haemorrhage.
-- 1917 repeated NG-MAT-2316 (placenta undelivered at 35 to 40 minutes with no bleeding) and is now
-- the three signs of placental separation.
-- 1922 repeated NG-CHD-1738 (limp newborn not breathing after drying = start bag and mask) and is
-- now hypoxic ischaemic encephalopathy after a difficult birth.
-- 
-- Full-text Jaccard is 0.23 or below for all eight against every other item in the bank. No keyed
-- option is the longest and option parity runs 1.07 to 1.19. Global key balance unchanged:
-- a1,402 b1,393 c1,362 d1,353, chi-square 1.22. Length gate still holds, worst 2.00.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman has lost three pregnancies between 16 and 20 weeks, each after her cervix opened without pain or bleeding. What does this history most suggest?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That every future birth she has will have to be by caesarean section"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That each of the babies died from an infection rising from the vagina"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That her cervix opens early and may need a stitch next time"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That she should be delivered early in the next pregnancy as well"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "That her cervix opens early and may need a stitch in the next pregnancy". Loss in the middle of pregnancy, after a cervix that opens <strong>without pain and without bleeding</strong>, points to cervical insufficiency rather than to the baby or to infection. The cervix is mechanically weak and gives way once the pregnancy is heavy enough to press on it. The treatment is a cervical stitch (cerclage), usually placed at 12 to 14 weeks and removed at about 37 weeks or sooner if labour begins.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The way the cervix behaves does not decide the mode of delivery, and a normal vaginal birth is still possible.
• B — Infection can end a pregnancy late, but it brings pain, discharge and fever; a painless, bloodless opening is the cervix, not an ascending infection.
• D — Delivering early again would repeat the loss rather than prevent it.

Reference: Ghana STG — antenatal care; RCOG — cervical insufficiency$q$,
  strategy = $q$Painless opening in mid-pregnancy, three times over, is the cervix. Move the stitch, not the delivery date.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1888$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A breastfed baby weighs 8% less on day three than at birth. The mother is alarmed. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That the baby must be started on formula at once because the milk is not enough"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That this means her breast milk supply has already failed completely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That this is normal and the weight is regained within about two weeks"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That the baby should be weighed only once a week from now on instead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "That this is normal and the weight is regained within about two weeks". A breastfed newborn commonly loses up to about <strong>7 to 10% of birth weight in the first three to five days</strong>, from meconium, urine and the small volumes of colostrum taken before the milk comes in. The weight is back to birth weight by about <strong>day 10 to 14</strong>. What is not normal is a loss beyond 10%, no regain by two weeks, or a baby who is drowsy with few wet nappies and dark urine — that is a feeding problem needing assessment. The answer is help with attachment and frequency, not a bottle.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A normal weight loss does not mean the milk is insufficient, and formula reduces the stimulation that builds the supply.
• B — 8% by day three is the expected pattern, not a supply failure.
• D — Weighing once a week misses the baby who is not regaining, so early checks are needed.

Reference: WHO/UNICEF — breastfeeding counselling; Ghana STG — newborn care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Up to 10% in the first days is physiology. Back to birth weight by two weeks is the test.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1898$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with a known heart valve problem is now 34 weeks pregnant and says she is more breathless than before. Why must this be taken seriously?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because breathlessness at 34 weeks is always caused by anaemia alone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because the extra blood volume strains an already damaged heart"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the baby is pressing on her diaphragm and nothing can be done"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because she will certainly need a caesarean under general anaesthesia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because the extra blood volume strains an already damaged heart". Pregnancy raises plasma volume by nearly half and cardiac output by a third, and labour adds a further surge of venous return with every contraction. <strong>A valve that was coping may fail under that load</strong>, producing pulmonary oedema. Breathlessness that is worsening, orthopnoea, a cough with pink froth or basal crackles all need urgent assessment and senior obstetric and cardiac review — cardiac disease is a leading indirect cause of maternal death.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Anaemia is common and must be checked, but it does not explain away new breathlessness in a woman with a valve lesion.
• C — The enlarged uterus does splint the diaphragm, but that is a mild discomfort, not a sign of decompensation.
• D — The mode of delivery is decided case by case by the obstetric and cardiac team.

Reference: Ghana STG — cardiac disease in pregnancy; RCOG — cardiac disease$q$,
  strategy = $q$A damaged valve plus a 40% larger circulating volume is the equation for heart failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1899$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother returning to market work each day expresses breast milk for her baby. Which practice is safe?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "She keeps it covered in the coolest place and uses it within a few hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "She leaves it at room temperature all day for her husband to use that evening"}, {"id": "c", "text": "She keeps yesterday's milk and adds today's to the same unwashed container"}, {"id": "d", "text": "She puts the unused portion back in the cold after the baby has been fed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "She keeps it covered in the coolest place and uses it within a few hours". Freshly expressed milk is safe for a few hours at room temperature in a cool place, for up to four days in a refrigerator at 4 °C, and for up to three months in a freezer at −18 °C. It is stored in <strong>small separate portions in clean containers marked with the date</strong>, and the oldest is used first.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Milk left out warm all day grows bacteria and is not safe by the evening.
• C — Adding fresh milk to yesterday's leftovers in an unwashed container contaminates the whole batch.
• D — Milk that has been at the baby's mouth, or thawed and then partly used, must be thrown away rather than chilled again.

Reference: WHO — safe storage of expressed breast milk; Ghana STG — infant feeding$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clean, covered, dated, small portions — and nothing that has been out all day or already used.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1901$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman in labour has thick, dark green liquor draining. What does this most suggest?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That the baby has passed its first stool because it is overdue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That the membranes have been ruptured for too long already"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That the baby may be short of oxygen and needs close monitoring"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That an infection in the uterus is producing the discoloured fluid"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "That the baby may be short of oxygen and needs close monitoring". Meconium is passed when a fetus is <strong>hypoxic and its anal sphincter relaxes</strong>. Thin meconium in a post-term baby is common, but thick, dark green liquor — and especially meconium with an abnormal foetal heart pattern — means the baby is compromised. The midwife listens to the foetal heart more frequently, calls for obstetric review, and has resuscitation equipment ready at the birth. A baby who is not vigorous at birth has the airway cleared by suction under direct vision.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Post-term meconium is thin and pale, not thick and dark; the grading matters.
• B — Length of time since rupture is a risk for infection, not for the colour of the liquor.
• D — Infection gives maternal fever, a tender uterus and offensive discharge, not green liquor on its own.

Reference: WHO — intrapartum care; Ghana STG — labour$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thick green liquor means the fetus is starved of oxygen until the heart trace says otherwise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1907$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman keeps bleeding after delivery because her uterus will not contract. What does a condom catheter filled with water and placed inside the uterus achieve?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It delivers a clotting drug directly into the wall of the uterus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It measures how much blood the uterus has already lost"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It keeps the cervix open so that any clots can drain freely away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It presses on the inside of the uterus and stops the bleeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "It presses on the inside of the uterus and stops the bleeding". A condom tied over a catheter and filled with 250 to 500 mL of sterile water becomes a <strong>balloon that presses the placental site from within</strong>, and the pressure slows the bleeding from the vessels the atony has left open. It is a temporising measure for haemorrhage that has not responded to uterotonics: blood is cross-matched, and surgery is arranged while the balloon buys time.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The balloon is filled with plain sterile water; it carries no drug.
• B — Blood loss is estimated by weighing swabs and pads and by the clinical signs, not by the balloon.
• C — If the cervix is left open the balloon falls out and the pressure that is doing the work is lost.

Reference: WHO — management of postpartum haemorrhage; Ghana STG — PPH$q$,
  strategy = $q$The balloon works by pressure, not by drugs. It buys time, and it must stay inside.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1913$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Which set of signs shows that the placenta has separated and is ready to be delivered?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The cord shortens, the uterus sinks and all bleeding stops at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The cord lengthens, a gush of blood appears and the uterus rises up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The woman bears down and the fundus drops towards the symphysis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The pulse slows, the blood pressure rises and the cord stops pulsating"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The cord lengthens, a gush of blood appears and the uterus rises up". These are the three signs of separation: the cord lengthens as the placenta descends, <strong>a small gush of blood</strong> escapes as it peels away, and the uterus becomes globular and rises in the abdomen. Only once they are present is controlled cord traction with counter-pressure on the uterus safe to attempt.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The cord lengthens rather than shortens, and the uterus rises rather than sinks.
• C — The fundus rises out of the pelvis as the placenta moves down; bearing down before separation risks cord avulsion and uterine inversion.
• D — Pulse and blood pressure changes are signs of volume loss, not of placental separation.

Reference: WHO — active management of the third stage; Ghana STG — labour$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cord longer, a gush of blood, uterus up and globular. Then — and only then — pull.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1917$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A baby who needed prolonged resuscitation at birth is now having fits at 12 hours of age. What does this suggest?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A low blood sugar that will settle once the baby has been fed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Brain injury from lack of oxygen around the time of birth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An infection that the baby acquired from the mother during delivery"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A normal startle response in a newborn that needs no investigation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Brain injury from lack of oxygen around the time of birth". Seizures starting in the first day after a difficult birth, in a baby with abnormal tone and a poor suck, are the picture of <strong>hypoxic ischaemic encephalopathy</strong>. Care is supportive and urgent: keep oxygen and perfusion adequate, check and correct glucose and calcium, control the temperature without overheating, treat the seizures, and arrange cooling where a unit offers it, because therapeutic hypothermia helps only if started within about six hours.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Hypoglycaemia is checked and corrected in every fitting newborn, but it does not explain fits after a documented resuscitation.
• C — Sepsis can cause seizures, but usually later and with fever, poor feeding and jaundice; it is treated while HIE is assessed, not instead of it.
• D — A startle is a response to handling; a fit is not.

Reference: WHO — neonatal care; Ghana STG — sick newborn$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fits in the first day after a difficult birth are hypoxic injury until proved otherwise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1922$q$;

-- Verify: expect 8
COMMIT;

