-- 2039 was one of three copies of "when should control measures begin in a suspected cholera
-- outbreak" (with NG-PUB-0618 at 0.64, the highest pair in the family, and NG-MCB-2543); it is now
-- the open vial policy.
-- 2042 repeated the attack-rate calculation in NG-MCB-2544 with different numbers and is now the
-- maternity waiting home.
-- 2056 repeated NG-HPR-0412 (which single measure interrupts the most faecal-oral routes), and sat
-- beside NG-3500-0019 giving a different answer to the same question; it is now the community
-- scorecard.
-- 2061 repeated NG-HCB-1298 (rice held at room temperature) and is now abattoir meat inspection.
-- 2071 repeated NG-TRA-1855 (motorcycle helmets) and is now snakebite prevention for farmers.
-- 2072 repeated NG-PDT-0565 (a heat-damaged vial monitor) and is now temporal association against
-- causation after a vaccination.
-- 2074 repeated NG-CHD-1782 (mild illness is not a contraindication) and is now conditioning ice
-- packs for a cold box.
-- 2086 repeated NG-PUB-0628 (what accompanies a referral) and is now the counter-referral that must
-- come back.
-- 
-- Both sums in the bank were recomputed by hand: 30/120 = 25% attack rate, 24/200 = 12% case
-- fatality. Full-text Jaccard is 0.23 or below for all eight; no keyed option is the longest;
-- option parity runs 1.06 to 1.18. Global key balance unchanged: a1,402 b1,393 c1,362 d1,353.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$At the end of a session a multi-dose vaccine vial is still more than half full and its vial monitor shows no heat damage. What does the open vial policy allow?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The vial must be discarded at the end of that session in every case"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It may be kept for 28 days if kept cold and the septum stays clean"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It may be used at the next session only if it was frozen overnight"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It may be kept until its printed expiry date, opened or not"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "It may be kept for 28 days if kept cold and the septum stays clean". Under the open vial policy a multi-dose vial of the vaccines that carry a vial monitor may be used across sessions for up to <strong>28 days after it was first opened</strong>, provided it has not passed its expiry date, the monitor still shows it has not been overheated, it has been kept cold, and the septum has not been contaminated. Discarding every part-used vial wastes vaccine; keeping one indefinitely endangers the children.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Discarding at the end of every session wastes most of the vial and is not what the policy requires.
• C — Freezing damages the adsorbed vaccines; an overnight freeze would make the vial unusable, not safer.
• D — The 28-day limit runs from the day of opening, whatever the printed expiry date says.

Reference: WHO — open vial policy; Ghana EPI cold chain guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$Twenty-eight days from opening, and only while cold, clean and not heat-damaged.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2039$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman lives three hours from the nearest hospital and had a caesarean section last year. Which arrangement most reduces her risk this pregnancy?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Travelling to the hospital only once her contractions have begun"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Asking a neighbour to lend her a mobile phone for the delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Moving to a maternity waiting home near the hospital before labour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Delivering at home with a traditional birth attendant as she did before"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Moving to a maternity waiting home near the hospital before labour". A woman with a previous caesarean carries a risk of scar rupture, and she is three hours from surgery. A maternity waiting home lets her <strong>move close to the hospital in the last weeks so that the delay that kills is removed</strong> before labour starts. It is the third delay — reaching care — that is being answered, and it is answered by geography rather than by advice.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Beginning a three-hour journey in established labour may bring her to hospital after the rupture has happened.
• B — A borrowed phone helps her call for help but does not shorten the journey.
• D — A home birth with a previous caesarean puts her beyond the reach of surgery if the scar gives way.

Reference: WHO — birth preparedness and complication readiness; Ghana STG — antenatal care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Move the woman nearer the theatre before labour, not during it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2042$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A district wants each community to judge the quality of its own clinic and agree with staff what should change. Which approach is this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A quarterly meeting of the district health management team alone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A household survey carried out by interviewers sent from the capital"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An inspection of the clinic carried out by the regional directorate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A community scorecard, in which members rate services with staff"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A community scorecard, in which members rate services with staff". A community scorecard brings service users and providers together to <strong>score the service against agreed criteria and then negotiate an action plan</strong>. Because the community sets the criteria and sits in the same room as the staff, the result is a plan the clinic can be held to, rather than a report written about the clinic by somebody else.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The management team is the provider side only; the community does not judge anything.
• B — A survey measures what the questionnaire asks, not what the community thinks should change.
• C — An inspection is an outside judgement, and the community takes no part in it.

Reference: WHO — community monitoring of health services; Ghana CHPS guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$Score it with the community in the room, then agree the action plan.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2056$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why must cattle be slaughtered at an inspected abattoir rather than at home before the meat is sold?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "So that disease in the carcass is found before people eat the meat"}, {"id": "b", "text": "So that the meat can be sold at a higher price in the market"}, {"id": "c", "text": "So that the farmer is paid more quickly for the animal he raised"}, {"id": "d", "text": "So that the hide can be removed without being damaged in the process"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "So that disease in the carcass is found before people eat the meat". Ante-mortem and post-mortem inspection at an abattoir catches the animal that was sick and the carcass that is unfit — tuberculosis, anthrax, cysticercosis, abscesses and contamination during dressing — <strong>before any of it reaches a customer</strong>. Slaughter at home, or the butchering of an animal that died on its own, is how zoonoses travel from a herd into a community.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Price is an argument about the market, not about safety, and inspected meat often costs more to produce.
• C — Payment speed has nothing to do with inspection.
• D — Care of the hide is a by-product concern; a damaged hide is not a public health event.

Reference: Ghana Public Health Act — meat inspection; WHO — zoonoses$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inspect the animal and the carcass before sale, because a sick beast does not look sick on a plate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2061$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Farmers in a district are frequently bitten by snakes while working in the fields. Which measure would prevent the most bites?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wearing boots and long trousers, and clearing grass around the home"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Carrying a tourniquet to every farm to apply if a bite occurs"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keeping supplies of antivenom at every village shop in the district"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Avoiding all farm work during the rainy season when snakes are active"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Wearing boots and long trousers, and clearing grass around the home". Most bites are struck at the foot and ankle of someone working or walking after dark. <strong>Boots, long trousers and a cleared, well-lit compound keep the snake and the skin apart</strong>, which is prevention; everything else on the list is preparation for a bite that has already happened. Snakes are part of the farming environment all year, so abandoning the fields is neither possible nor necessary.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A tourniquet is harmful in snakebite and treats the bite rather than preventing it.
• C — Stocking antivenom is treatment, and antivenom kept in a village shop will not be kept cold or used correctly.
• D — Snakes are active in the warm season generally; abandoning the farm is not a preventive strategy.

Reference: WHO — snakebite envenoming prevention; Ghana STG$q$,
  strategy = $q$Boots and a cleared compound stop the bite. Everything else treats one.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2071$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A child develops a rash two days after vaccination and the mother is sure the vaccine caused it. Why can the nurse not conclude this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because vaccines are tested so thoroughly that reactions cannot occur"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because a rash two days later is always caused by something else entirely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the rash would have appeared at about that time in any case"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the mother cannot be expected to describe a rash accurately"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Because the rash would have appeared at about that time in any case". Children fall ill all the time, and an illness that happens after a vaccination would have happened at about that moment whether or not the vaccine was given. <strong>A temporal association is not a causal one</strong>: many children are vaccinated and many children have rashes, so some will have both on the same day by coincidence. Causation needs the pattern across many children, which is why events are reported and assessed together rather than one at a time.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Vaccines do cause reactions; that is exactly why events are reported and monitored.
• B — A rash can be caused by the vaccine; the point is that this cannot be concluded from one child.
• D — The mother's description is a valid starting point; the problem is the reasoning, not the witness.

Reference: WHO — adverse events following immunisation; causality assessment$q$,
  strategy = $q$After is not because. Report it, and let the pattern across many children decide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2072$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse packs vaccines into a cold box for an outreach session in a hot vehicle. What protects their potency?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pressing the vaccine vials directly against the frozen ice packs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Filling the box completely so that no air can move around inside it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Packing the box the night before so that it starts out very cold"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Conditioning the ice packs so that they do not freeze the vials"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Conditioning the ice packs so that they do not freeze the vials". Ice packs are conditioned by leaving them at room temperature until the frost on the outside has melted and water can be heard when the pack is shaken. Packed that way they <strong>hold the box at 2 to 8 °C instead of freezing the vaccines against them</strong> — and a freeze damages the adsorbed vaccines such as pentavalent and tetanus, silently and irreversibly.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Direct contact with a frozen pack freezes the vial and destroys the vaccine.
• B — A tightly filled box restricts the cold air the vaccines need and crushes the vials.
• C — Packing the night before with unconditioned packs simply gives the freezer longer to do its damage.

Reference: WHO — vaccine cold chain and carrier packing; Ghana EPI guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$Condition the packs until they slosh. Cold is the aim; frozen is the failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2074$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A CHPS nurse has referred a woman to the district hospital. What should come back to the nurse afterwards?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A written reply saying what was found and done, so care can continue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A receipt showing only that the hospital has registered the patient there"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nothing at all, because the hospital now takes over all of her care fully"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A bill for the services the woman received while she was there"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A written reply saying what was found and done, so care can continue". A referral is a handover in one direction; the reply — the counter-referral — closes the loop. It tells the referring nurse <strong>the diagnosis, the treatment given and what follow-up is now needed</strong>, so that the woman is not lost between two facilities and the nurse learns from the cases she sends. Without it the CHPS zone cannot follow up, and the same patient returns later with an untreated problem.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Registration proves only that she arrived; it says nothing about what was found.
• C — The hospital takes over the episode, not the woman's ongoing care in her own community.
• D — A bill is an administrative document, not a clinical reply.

Reference: Ghana CHPS referral guidelines; WHO — continuity of care$q$,
  strategy = $q$A referral without a reply is a patient posted into the dark.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EPH-2086$q$;

