UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A doctor asks a nurse to send an HIV test on an unconscious patient admitted after a collapse. What should the nurse consider?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The test should be sent immediately, without any further consideration at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Testing may proceed only where it is necessary for the patient's own care"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The patient's relatives may consent on his behalf in all circumstances whatsoever"}, {"id": "d", "text": "HIV testing is never permitted without the patient's own written signature"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Testing may proceed only where it is necessary for the patient's own care". HIV testing normally requires <strong>informed consent with pre- and post-test counselling</strong>. Where a patient lacks capacity and the result is necessary for their immediate care, testing may proceed in their best interests, with <strong>strict confidentiality</strong> and counselling provided once capacity returns. Results are never disclosed to relatives or staff without justification.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Testing without consideration of consent, confidentiality and clinical necessity breaches both ethical and legal standards.
• C — Relatives cannot generally consent for an adult. Decisions are made in the patient's best interests by the clinical team.
• D — An absolute rule would prevent testing that is genuinely necessary to treat an unconscious patient.

Reference: Ghana AIDS Commission; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct and Ethics$q$,
  strategy = $q$For any sensitive test, three principles apply: consent where possible, best interests where not, and confidentiality always. HIV testing carries additional stigma, so confidentiality is especially strict.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LAB-0156$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with chronic kidney disease is persistently anaemic despite adequate iron intake. Which renal function explains this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The kidney stores iron that is needed for red cell production"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The kidney manufactures haemoglobin directly from stored iron"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The kidney filters out damaged red cells from the circulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The kidney produces erythropoietin to stimulate red cells"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The kidney produces erythropoietin to stimulate red cells". Besides excreting waste, the kidney produces <strong>erythropoietin</strong> in response to low tissue oxygen, and this hormone stimulates the bone marrow to produce red cells. In chronic kidney disease, erythropoietin production falls and anaemia develops that does not respond to iron alone. The kidney also activates vitamin D and helps regulate blood pressure.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Iron is stored mainly in the liver, spleen and bone marrow as ferritin, not in the kidney.
• B — Haemoglobin is synthesised within developing red cells in bone marrow, not by the kidney.
• C — Aged and damaged red cells are removed by the spleen and liver.

Reference: Ghana STG — chronic kidney disease$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the kidney does more than make urine. It produces erythropoietin, activates vitamin D, and regulates blood pressure through renin. These explain the anaemia, bone disease and hypertension of chronic kidney disease.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ANP-0090$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian nurse decides which services to offer, what to charge, where to locate and how to tell people about the clinic. She also pays for a radio slot. Which statement separates what she is doing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Marketing and advertising are the same thing, and the radio slot is both"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Marketing is the whole approach; advertising is one paid part of it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advertising is the whole approach; marketing is only the radio slot"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Marketing is only what happens after a client has attended the clinic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Marketing covers the entire approach, the service, price, place and promotion, while advertising is one part of promotion, the paid messages. The two are not synonyms, advertising is not the larger term, and marketing begins long before a client attends.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Marketing is the whole plan; advertising is one paid channel inside it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BUS-0713$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with a long history of schizophrenia and poor control shouts insults and obscenities at the nurse during morning care. Which response is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Warn him that his privileges will be withdrawn if he continues"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tell him firmly that he must apologise before any care continues"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remain calm, finish the care and treat the words as a symptom"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ignore him completely and ask another nurse to take over the care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Remain calm, finish the care and treat the words as a symptom". Hostile language in psychosis is usually driven by <strong>fear, paranoia or disorganised thinking</strong>, not personal dislike. A <strong>calm, non-reactive, respectful</strong> approach keeps the situation from escalating, preserves the therapeutic relationship and models self-control. Clear limits are set only for behaviour that threatens safety.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Withdrawing attention entirely abandons the patient and may be experienced as rejection; handing over may be needed only if the nurse cannot stay calm.
• B — Threats and punishment are not therapeutic and can provoke aggression.
• D — Demanding an apology turns care into a power struggle and may increase agitation.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Act 2012 — rights of persons with mental illness; therapeutic communication principles$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in a psychiatric communication item, choose the option that stays calm, keeps the patient's dignity and treats behaviour as a symptom.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with poorly controlled hypothyroidism is admitted for a hernia repair. The nurse should caution the team that she is especially sensitive to:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Warm environments, which may provoke a thyroid crisis in her body"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Light physical exercise, which may make her collapse suddenly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "High-fibre foods, which may cause a painful bowel obstruction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Opioids and sedatives, which depress breathing and consciousness"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Opioids and sedatives, which depress breathing and consciousness". Hypothyroidism slows drug metabolism and blunts the respiratory drive, so ordinary doses of <strong>opioids, benzodiazepines and anaesthetics</strong> can produce prolonged sedation, hypoventilation and even <strong>myxoedema coma</strong>. Doses are reduced and the patient is monitored closely.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Hypothyroid patients feel cold and tolerate warmth well; heat intolerance and thyroid crisis belong to hyperthyroidism.
• B — Fibre helps the constipation of hypothyroidism rather than causing obstruction.
• C — Gentle exercise is encouraged to combat weight gain and constipation.

Reference: Ghana STG — hypothyroidism and peri-operative considerations$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: a slow metabolism means drugs last longer — in hypothyroidism the danger is anything that depresses the CNS.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0024$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with type 2 diabetes controlled by metformin has a right hemicolectomy. On the first post-operative day he is nil by mouth and the surgeon prescribes a sliding scale of soluble insulin. Which explanation to the patient is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"Your diabetes has now become permanent type 1, so you will need insulin from now on\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "\"Insulin is needed because the anaesthetic has damaged the cells of your pancreas\""}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"Surgery raises your glucose, and with no food we adjust it hour by hour with insulin\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"The insulin is only to help the wound heal faster, and it stops when the wound is closed\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — ""Surgery raises your glucose, and with no food we adjust it hour by hour with insulin"". The <strong>stress response</strong> to surgery releases cortisol, catecholamines and glucagon, which push glucose up, while <strong>metformin is withheld</strong> around surgery because of the risk of lactic acidosis in a dehydrated or unwell patient. Short-acting insulin titrated to capillary glucose gives <strong>flexible, safe control</strong> until normal eating resumes.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Type 2 diabetes does not convert to type 1; peri-operative insulin is temporary.
• B — Anaesthesia does not damage the pancreas.
• D — Good glucose control does support healing, but the primary reason is the stress response combined with not eating.

Reference: Ghana STG — peri-operative management of diabetes$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: 'why insulin now?' combines two facts — stress hyperglycaemia and the oral drug being stopped. The best answer contains both.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0027$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient tells the nurse, "The night nurse was rough with me and shouted when I asked for a bedpan." Which is the **most appropriate** response by the nurse?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell the patient that the night nurse is always tired and did not mean it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Promise the patient that the night nurse will never care for him again"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confront the night nurse at the next handover in front of the team"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Listen, record it factually and report it to the nurse in charge"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Listen, record it factually and report it to the nurse in charge". An allegation of rough handling and verbal abuse is a <strong>safeguarding and professional-conduct concern</strong>. The nurse's duty is to <strong>take it seriously, record exactly what was said, and escalate through the proper channel</strong> so that it can be investigated fairly; the patient is told what will happen next.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A public confrontation is unprofessional and prejudices the investigation.
• B — The nurse cannot guarantee staffing arrangements and should not pre-judge the outcome.
• C — Excusing the behaviour dismisses the complaint and may leave an abusive practice unaddressed.

Reference: NMC Ghana — code of conduct; Ghana Health Service patient charter and incident reporting$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: a patient complaint about a colleague is reported, not judged — listen, document, escalate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0156$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman is receiving a unit of packed red cells. Fifteen minutes after it starts she complains of lower back pain, chills and a feeling of dread; her temperature has risen by 1.5 °C and her pulse is 120 beats per minute. After **stopping the transfusion**, which action should the nurse take next?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Slow the transfusion to 10 drops per minute and observe for another 15 minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the vein open with saline through a new set and call the doctor and blood bank"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the prescribed paracetamol and continue the transfusion through the same set"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disconnect the cannula and discard the blood unit and giving set in the clinical waste"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Keep the vein open with saline through a new set and call the doctor and blood bank". Back pain, chills, fever and a sense of dread early in a transfusion suggest an <strong>acute haemolytic reaction</strong>, usually from ABO incompatibility. After stopping the blood, the nurse <strong>maintains intravenous access with saline through a fresh set</strong> (so no more incompatible blood is flushed in), <strong>repeats the bedside identity check</strong>, calls for medical help and returns the unit, set and fresh samples to the blood bank. Urine output is monitored for haemoglobinuria and renal failure.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Treating the fever and continuing is appropriate only for a mild febrile non-haemolytic reaction confirmed by the doctor — not for a picture suggesting haemolysis.
• C — Any further incompatible blood increases the haemolysis; the transfusion must not continue.
• D — The unit and set are evidence for investigation and must be returned, and the cannula is needed for fluids and drugs.

Reference: Ghana National Blood Service — transfusion reaction management; Ghana STG — blood transfusion$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: stop, keep the line, re-check identity, call, send everything back — and never flush the old set.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0247$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is prescribed intramuscular diazepam and intramuscular pethidine for an anxious patient before a procedure, and plans to mix both in one syringe to spare the patient a second injection. Which action is **correct**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mix them together, since combining injections always reduces pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give them in separate syringes, because diazepam is incompatible"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give both drugs intravenously instead, to avoid the problem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Omit the diazepam, because pethidine alone sedates everyone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Give them in separate syringes, because diazepam is incompatible". Drugs are <strong>never mixed in one syringe unless compatibility is documented</strong> — diazepam precipitates with most aqueous drugs, which can block the needle, inactivate the dose or injure tissue. The safe default is separate syringes and separate sites, rotating intramuscular sites and documenting each drug.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Changing the route without a prescription is outside nursing scope and intravenous diazepam has its own risks.
• C — Mixing incompatible drugs causes precipitation and unpredictable absorption; kindness never justifies it.
• D — Omitting a prescribed drug on personal judgement denies ordered care; both drugs have distinct purposes.

Reference: Ghana STG — safe injection practice; drug compatibility$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: two drugs in one barrel need proof of compatibility — without it, separate syringes every time.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0271$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$After **IM pethidine for renal colic**, which symptom is **most important** to hand over?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Nausea with a single small vomit after the injection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pain rated five out of ten at the site of the stone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "No known drug allergies recorded on the chart"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Change in the location and character of the pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Change in the location and character of the pain". <strong>Migrating stone pain maps the stone's journey</strong> — flank-to-groin shifts or colic-to-constant changes reveal movement, obstruction or complications the next shift must track. Nausea commonly shadows colic, a five rates routine, and allergy status (though vital) is already charted baseline.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Mid-scale pain rates routine post-analgesia.
• B — Colic nausea is expected company, not the headline.
• C — Allergy status is filed baseline, not new warning.

Reference: Ghana STG — renal colic; pain-migration monitoring$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: stones speak through moving pain — migration hands over first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0604$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$At her first antenatal visit a woman at 9 weeks says, "I have been so nauseated every morning and I don't understand why." Which response by the nurse is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"It is only because you are so tired at the moment and not sleeping well\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "\"Your body is burning energy much faster now, and that upsets the stomach\""}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"If you change the kinds of food you eat, the nausea will go away completely\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"Nausea is caused by the pregnancy hormones and eases after three months\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — ""Nausea is caused by the pregnancy hormones and eases after three months"". Nausea and vomiting of early pregnancy track the <strong>rapid rise in human chorionic gonadotrophin and oestrogen</strong>, peaking at 8–12 weeks and usually settling by 14–16 weeks. Giving the real cause reassures her, and the nurse adds practical measures — dry biscuit before rising, small frequent meals, avoiding fatty or strong-smelling foods — and the warning signs of hyperemesis.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Dietary changes may reduce symptoms but will not abolish them, and this promise is misleading.
• B — The basal metabolic rate rises only modestly and later in pregnancy; it is not the mechanism.
• C — Fatigue accompanies early pregnancy but does not cause the nausea.

Reference: Ghana Safe Motherhood Protocol — minor disorders of pregnancy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: the best response gives accurate physiology plus reassurance — reject explanations that are wrong or over-promise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0132$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A **postpartum mother** reports **leg pain with warmth and redness**. Which action comes **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply warm compresses to the calf for an hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage her to walk about so that the leg does not stiffen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Massage the calf gently to help the clot disperse"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep her on bed rest with the affected leg elevated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Keep her on bed rest with the affected leg elevated". <strong>Suspected clots rested elevated, never massaged</strong> — bed rest with elevation guards the thrombus while diagnosis proceeds; massage embolises, compresses await orders, and walking dislodges. Rested guarded; massaged never.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Walking dislodges thrombi.
• B — Massage embolises clots fatally.
• C — Compresses await ordered care.

Reference: Ghana — postpartum thrombophlebitis; rest and elevation$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: postpartum clots rested — elevated guarded, massaged never.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0862$q$;

