UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A doctor telephones the ward and asks the nurse to give furosemide 40 mg intravenously STAT to a woman with acute pulmonary oedema. Which action by the nurse is **correct**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Read back the order, give the dose, and have the doctor countersign it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the dose and enter it as a routine once-daily ward prescription"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withhold the dose until the doctor has come and written the order in person"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give the dose now and repeat it every six hours until told otherwise"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Read back the order, give the dose, and have the doctor countersign it". A <strong>STAT order is a single dose given immediately</strong>. A verbal or telephone order is accepted in an emergency only when it is <strong>read back and confirmed</strong>, documented as a telephone order with the prescriber's name, and <strong>countersigned</strong> within the policy period. The nurse remains accountable for checking that the drug and dose are appropriate.

Why the other options fail:
• B — STAT does not mean 'start a regular schedule'; further doses require a new order.
• C — Recording a one-off emergency dose as a daily prescription falsifies the record and risks repeated dosing.
• D — Refusing an appropriate emergency dose would delay life-saving treatment; the countersignature can follow.

Reference: Ghana Health Service medication policy — verbal and telephone orders; NMC Ghana code of conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: emergency plus telephone order equals read-back, single dose, countersign — the nurse acts safely but never expands the order.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$After 6 mg of intravenous morphine for postoperative pain, a patient sleeps quietly with pulse 68, blood pressure 100/68 and respirations **8 per minute**. Which action is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give oxygen alone and recheck her breathing again in one hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep adrenaline at the bedside in case of an allergic reaction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rouse her, give oxygen, give naloxone as prescribed and call the doctor"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave her to sleep undisturbed, because rest is what relieves the pain most"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Rouse her, give oxygen, give naloxone as prescribed and call the doctor". <strong>Eight breaths per minute with difficult arousal after IV morphine is opioid-induced respiratory depression.</strong> The nurse stimulates the patient, opens the airway, gives oxygen, administers titrated IV naloxone per protocol and calls the doctor — then monitors continuously, because naloxone wears off sooner than morphine and breathing can fail again.

Why the other options fail:
• A — There are no allergy signs (rash, wheeze, swelling) — the problem is overdose effect, treated with naloxone.
• B — Leaving her asleep allows hypoxia and carbon dioxide retention to deepen into arrest.
• D — Oxygen alone does not reverse the depressed respiratory drive; the opioid effect must be antagonised and watched.

Reference: Ghana STG — opioid analgesia; naloxone for respiratory depression$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: breathing always beats comfort — a rate of 8 after morphine is reversed, not watched.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0255$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A family member asks the nurse why the medical team refers to a hospitalized elder's acute delirium as 'reversible' when the elder appears profoundly confused. What is the nurse's best physiological explanation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Delirium is an acute encephalopathy that resolves once its cause is treated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Laboratory testing is never required for a patient with acute confusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Delirium never causes significant physiological distress to the patient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cognitive decline in delirium is permanent and cannot be reversed in any patient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Delirium is considered a reversible medical syndrome because it is caused by an acute physiological insult (e.g., systemic infection, hypoxemia, electrolyte imbalance, anticholinergic medications, withdrawal). Once the underlying etiology is diagnosed and promptly treated, neurotransmitter balance is restored and the patient generally returns to baseline cognitive status.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delirium reversibility: delirium is secondary to a medical insult; treat the underlying physical illness and the delirium resolves.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0165$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man who had a right pneumonectomy this morning is back on the ward. He reports pain of 7/10 and shallow breathing; his chest drain site is dry. Which nursing action is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withhold the analgesia so that his breathing is not further depressed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage him to breathe shallowly to protect the surgical site from any strain at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the analgesia, then help him deep breathe and cough, splinting the incision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nurse him flat on the unoperated side and avoid sitting him up at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Give the analgesia, then help him deep breathe and cough, splinting the incision". After thoracic surgery <strong>uncontrolled pain is the main barrier to effective breathing</strong>; shallow breaths lead to atelectasis and pneumonia in the remaining lung. <strong>Timely analgesia followed by breathing exercises</strong> is the correct sequence. Pneumonectomy patients are usually positioned on their back or operated side, semi-upright.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Withholding analgesia guarantees shallow breathing; opioids are titrated with monitoring, not omitted.
• B — Shallow breathing is the problem, not the goal.
• D — Lying on the unoperated side risks drowning the only remaining lung with fluid from the empty hemithorax; the bed is kept raised.

Reference: Ghana STG — thoracic surgery post-operative care; post-operative pain management$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: treat the barrier (pain) first so the protective action (deep breathing) becomes possible.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0171$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A child with severe acute malnutrition has diarrhoea, is lethargic and shows signs of shock. What is the priority action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give unlimited plain water by mouth until the child begins to improve and pass urine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a large high-protein meal as soon as the child will take one"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refer urgently, resuscitating cautiously under the severe-malnutrition protocol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait for weight gain before treating the dehydration and the shock"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Shock in severe acute malnutrition is treated with slower, smaller volumes than in a well-nourished child, because the malnourished heart and circulation tolerate rapid fluid badly and can be tipped into heart failure. The child needs urgent referral and the specific protocol. Option A does not correct shock. Option B risks refeeding syndrome and is not the immediate need. Option D leaves a shocked child untreated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In severe malnutrition, fluids are given slowly and watched closely. Fast boluses cause overload.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCH-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$How should the nurse instruct a patient to produce a good sputum specimen for tuberculosis testing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cough deeply from the chest, ideally on rising, into a sterile container"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect saliva from the mouth into the sterile container instead"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Collect the specimen immediately after eating a large meal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rinse the mouth with an antiseptic mouthwash and then spit into the pot provided"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Cough deeply from the chest, ideally on rising, into a sterile container". A diagnostic specimen must be <strong>sputum from the lower airway, not saliva</strong>. An early morning specimen contains secretions pooled overnight and has the highest yield. The patient is asked to rinse with plain water, breathe deeply and cough from the chest, ideally in a well-ventilated area or outdoors to protect others.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Saliva is the commonest reason specimens are rejected, since it does not come from the infected lung tissue.
• C — A large meal makes coughing uncomfortable and risks vomiting. It does not improve yield.
• D — Antiseptic mouthwash may inhibit the organism and interfere with the result. Plain water is used.

Reference: Ghana National Tuberculosis Programme$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask where the pathology is and make sure the specimen comes from there. For tuberculosis that means deep chest sputum, collected early, in a way that protects others from aerosol.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LAB-0194$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is leading a therapy group of patients recovering from alcohol dependence. One member repeatedly interrupts, jokes and changes the subject whenever another person describes a painful experience. Which response by the nurse is **most therapeutic**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "\"Kofi, if you cannot stop interrupting the group you will have to leave this session\""}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ignore the interruptions and go back to the member who was speaking"}, {"id": "c", "text": "\"Kofi, you joke each time someone speaks of something painful. What is happening?\""}, {"id": "d", "text": "\"Kofi, you are being disrespectful and the others deserve to be heard in peace\""}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — ""Kofi, you joke each time someone speaks of something painful. What is happening?"". The nurse <strong>describes the behaviour, links it to a pattern and invites exploration</strong> without judgement. Joking at painful moments is usually a defence against the member's own anxiety; naming it gives him and the group a chance to examine it, which is the work of group therapy.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A threat is punitive and closes down exploration.
• B — Labelling him 'disrespectful' is judgemental and will provoke defensiveness.
• D — Ignoring repeated disruption lets it undermine the group and leaves the dynamic unexamined.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Authority — group therapy and therapeutic communication$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in group work, behaviour is material — observe it aloud, invite reflection, avoid punishment and avoidance.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0185$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A burns patient on **IV morphine** develops **fading bowel sounds with distension**. Which nurse action is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Recommend that the morphine dose be reduced at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explore alternative pain control alongside the morphine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withhold the pain medicine entirely until the bowel sounds return"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Switch the medicine to another route so that the bowel is spared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Explore alternative pain control alongside the morphine". <strong>Morphine remains burns' gold standard — its gut-slowing is managed, not surrendered to</strong>, so relaxation, visualisation and positioning join (not replace) titrated opioids while bowels are watched for ileus. Dose-cutting, withholding and route-swapping all abandon pain control without fixing motility.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Dose-cutting trades pain for still-sluggish bowels.
• C — Withholding abandons suffering without restoring gut.
• D — Route-swapping moves the same gut-slowing drug.

Reference: Ghana STG — burn pain; opioid bowel effects and adjuncts$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: opioid guts adjunct, never abandon — techniques added, morphine kept.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0589$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A teenager is fitted with a **spinal brace for scoliosis**. Which statement shows teaching was **effective**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I must sharply cut my food intake while the brace is being worn"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only tub baths are allowed from now on, because showers loosen it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I will wear it day and night, taking it off only to bathe and exercise"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bed boards under the mattress will straighten the curve while I am asleep"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "I will wear it day and night, taking it off only to bathe and exercise". <strong>Braces work by near-continuous wear — typically 20–23 hours daily</strong> through the growth years, off only for hygiene and prescribed exercises, with skin checks under the pads. Bed-boards cannot correct lateral curves, growing teens need full nutrition, and showers or tub baths are both fine once the brace is off.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Either bathing method is allowed; the rule is brief removal, not bath type.
• B — Bed-boards support alignment in bed but cannot remodel a scoliotic curve.
• D — Growth demands calories — dieting stunts the spine the brace is guiding.

Reference: Paediatric orthopaedics — scoliosis bracing; wear schedule and skin care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: braces correct by hours worn — near-always on, briefly off, skin watched.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0495$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with a long history of smoking has a chest X-ray and bronchoscopy and is told that he has lung cancer. The next morning he tells the nurse, "I'm sure they mixed up the films; I have never felt better." The nurse should:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that he accept the diagnosis, because treatment cannot begin until he does"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Agree that the films may be mixed up and suggest he ask for a repeat X-ray"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Accept it as an early coping response, listen, and answer questions when he asks"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refer him to the psychiatrist at once to assess how much insight he has"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Accept it as an early coping response, listen, and answer questions when he asks". Denial is a <strong>protective first response</strong> that gives a person time to absorb devastating news. The nurse <strong>neither reinforces nor demolishes it</strong>; being present, listening and gently offering information when invited allows him to move through shock towards acceptance at his own pace, while the team ensures that essential decisions are not unsafely delayed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Forcing acceptance increases anxiety and damages trust.
• B — Denial on the first day after bad news is normal, not a psychiatric emergency.
• D — Colluding with denial reinforces it and delays treatment.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — breaking bad news and psychosocial care in cancer$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: early denial is not a problem to fix — support without arguing and without agreeing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0161$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Which feature is most characteristic of scabies?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A single scaly patch on one elbow that spreads slowly over months"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A painless ulcer on the sole of the foot with a raised edge"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intense itching, worse at night, with burrows between the fingers"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A widespread rash that spares the hands and the feet completely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Scabies produces intense itching that is characteristically worse at night, with visible burrows in the web spaces of the fingers, the wrists, the axillae and the genital area. Treatment is a topical scabicide such as permethrin applied to the whole body and left on for the stated time, and it must be given to all household and close contacts at the same time, with bedding and clothing washed hot. Option B describes a trophic ulcer. Option A suggests a localized dermatosis. Option D is unusual, because the hands are commonly involved.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Night itch with finger-web burrows: treat the whole household, not just the patient.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0038$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with cholelithiasis reports discomfort after fatty meals. Which explanation should the nurse provide?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fat is difficult for every person to digest, whether or not they are ill at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The liver cannot manufacture any bile once stones have formed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bile flow into the intestine is obstructed when the gallbladder contracts"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fat in the meal immediately causes infection of the pancreas"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$Gallstones can obstruct bile flow, especially after a fatty meal stimulates gallbladder contraction. Bile is needed for fat emulsification and digestion.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Fat is not difficult for everyone to digest; in this patient the problem is bile, not fat itself.
• B — The liver still manufactures bile in cholelithiasis; the obstruction is in flow, not production.
• D — Fat does not immediately infect the pancreas; the mechanism is enzyme activation and duct obstruction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Relate post-fat meal pain to gallbladder contraction against an obstructed outlet.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MIX-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$At a Child Welfare Clinic a mother says her 6-month-old has had every vaccine on time. According to Ghana's Expanded Programme on Immunisation, which vaccines should the nurse expect to find recorded in the child's health record?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inactivated polio vaccine, which is first given at fourteen weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Measles-rubella, which is brought forward from nine months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A third dose of rotavirus, to finish the rotavirus course"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Yellow fever, which is added once the polio course is complete and checked"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Inactivated polio vaccine, which is first given at fourteen weeks". In the Ghana schedule the birth doses are <strong>BCG and OPV 0</strong>; at 6, 10 and 14 weeks the child receives <strong>OPV, pentavalent and PCV</strong>, with <strong>rotavirus at 6 and 10 weeks</strong> and <strong>IPV at 14 weeks</strong>. <strong>Measles-rubella and yellow fever</strong> follow at 9 months (second MR at 18 months) and meningococcal A at 18 months.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Measles-rubella and yellow fever are given at 9 months, so a 6-month-old should not yet have them.
• C — Most routine antigens are given between 6 and 14 weeks, long before 9 months.
• D — Varicella and MMR are not part of Ghana's routine EPI schedule.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — Expanded Programme on Immunisation schedule$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: walk through the Ghana schedule by age — birth, 6/10/14 weeks, 9 months, 18 months — and stop at the child's current age.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0242$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is preparing to give insulin to a client who also has a heparin infusion running. Which action makes the administration safest?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Shake the cloudy insulin vigorously so that the suspension mixes well"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Draw the insulin and the heparin into one syringe to save a puncture"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Inject into an area of lipodystrophy, where absorption is slower than it should be"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Check the insulin label against the chart at preparation and again at the bedside"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Insulin is a high-alert drug, and the independent double check of the vial against the record at preparation and again at the bedside catches the wrong-vial, wrong-strength errors that cause most insulin harm. Two people and two moments, with an insulin-specific syringe. Option A is wrong because shaking denatures the suspension; it is rolled gently. Option B would mix two drugs with different routes and risks. Option C gives unpredictable absorption.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Insulin is high alert: two checks, two moments, dedicated syringe. Roll cloudy insulin, never shake.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-3500-0004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with type 1 diabetes is found unresponsive and sweaty, and capillary blood glucose reads 1.9 mmol/L. What should the nurse do first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sit the client up and give a glass of orange juice to drink"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intravenous glucose or intramuscular glucagon"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the usual dose of long-acting insulin as prescribed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait and recheck the capillary glucose again in fifteen minutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$A glucose of 1.9 mmol/L with an unresponsive client is severe hypoglycaemia, and the airway cannot be protected for oral treatment, so glucose is given parenterally or glucagon is injected while help is called. Option A is unsafe: an unconscious client cannot swallow and will aspirate. Option C would lower the glucose further. Option D allows continuing cerebral glucose deprivation, which can cause permanent injury.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Unconscious means nothing by mouth. Intravenous glucose or glucagon instead.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-3500-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with dysphagia after stroke requires which function to be addressed in the care plan?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Writing and the use of pen and paper only"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Swallowing and aspiration safety"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hair grooming and personal appearance only"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Visual acuity and the fitting of glasses only"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Dysphagia creates aspiration, malnutrition and dehydration risks. Swallow assessment, positioning and texture modification are central.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Writing may be affected by hemiplegia but is not the immediate safety issue.
• C — Hair grooming is a comfort measure, not an aspiration risk.
• D — Visual acuity may be affected by a stroke but does not threaten the airway.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Link stroke dysphagia to airway protection and nutrition.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MIX-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with obsessive-compulsive disorder washes her hands for an hour each morning until the skin is raw. The nurse understands that the **purpose** this ritual serves for her is to:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Gain attention and sympathy from her family and from the ward staff"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Temporarily reduce the overwhelming anxiety of her obsessions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Punish herself for feelings of guilt she cannot express"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Avoid the ordinary responsibilities of her daily life"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Temporarily reduce the overwhelming anxiety of her obsessions". In OCD, intrusive <strong>obsessions</strong> (for example, 'my hands are contaminated') generate intense anxiety, and the <strong>compulsion</strong> is the act that neutralises it — briefly. Because relief is short-lived the cycle repeats, and the person often recognises the behaviour as excessive but cannot stop. Treatment combines SSRIs with exposure and response prevention.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The rituals interfere with daily life rather than being a way to avoid it.
• C — Although guilt may accompany obsessions, the ritual's function is anxiety reduction rather than self-punishment.
• D — Most people with OCD hide their rituals out of shame; attention is not the function.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Authority — anxiety and obsessive-compulsive disorders; WHO mhGAP$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: compulsions are defences against anxiety — the option that names anxiety relief describes the mechanism.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0224$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn is delivered wet and placed on the resuscitaire in a cool room. Which action best reduces heat loss?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place the baby near an open window so that fresh air reaches it quickly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Delay drying the baby until after it has been weighed, measured and labelled"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dry the baby, remove the wet linen, and give skin-to-skin contact with a hat"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bathe the baby at once to clean the skin and settle it down to sleep"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$A wet newborn loses heat fast by evaporation, and cold stress drives up oxygen consumption and glucose use. Drying removes the water, dry linen and skin-to-skin contact provide warmth, and a hat reduces loss from the head, which is proportionally large. Option A increases convective loss. Option B leaves the baby wet through the weighing. Option D adds evaporative loss and is not done straight after birth.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Wet newborns cool by evaporation. Dry first, then wrap and give the baby to the mother.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCH-0015$q$;

-- Verify: expect 18
COMMIT;

