UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient admitted through casualty is wearing a gold chain and has 400 cedis in cash. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "List and witness the items with the patient and store them securely"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave them with the patient so that she can look after her own property"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give them to the relative who accompanied her to the hospital"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the ward clerk to keep them in the office until she is discharged"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "List and witness the items with the patient and store them securely". Valuables are <strong>itemised in front of the patient, signed by both, and lodged in the facility's safe or secure store with a receipt</strong>. The list is what prevents the dispute that follows when something is missing. Handing property to a relative or a colleague without that record creates exactly the accusation the procedure exists to prevent.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A patient in a hospital gown on a busy ward cannot safeguard a gold chain and cash, and the loss becomes the ward's problem.
• C — A relative may not be the person the patient wants to hold her property, and without a record there is no proof of what was handed over.
• D — The clerk has no custody procedure and no witnessed list, so the item is unprotected.

Reference: Ghana Health Service ward administration; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$Count it, sign it, lock it, receipt it. The list is the protection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2195$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is asked to witness a patient's signature on a consent form for surgery she did not hear discussed. What is the witness attesting to?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That the operation is clinically appropriate for the patient"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That the patient has understood every risk explained to him"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That the patient signed the form himself, without pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That the surgeon has explained the procedure adequately to him"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "That the patient signed the form himself, without pressure". The witness signs to the <strong>act of signing</strong> — that this patient, identified to the nurse, put his own signature on the form and was not under pressure at the time. The adequacy of the explanation is the responsibility of the person obtaining consent, and the clinical appropriateness of the operation is a medical judgement. A nurse who is asked to witness should confirm that the discussion took place before she signs, because her signature is used to imply that the process was properly carried out.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Appropriateness is a judgement for the surgeon who will perform it.
• B — Whether the patient understood is tested by the person who explained, and cannot be certified by a bystander.
• D — The nurse is not in a position to vouch for what the surgeon said unless she heard it.

Reference: Ghana Patients' Charter; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$A witness signs to the signature and the freedom to give it — nothing more.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2198$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A ward is short of two nurses and the nurse in charge asks one of them to accept a third ward on her own. What should she do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Accept it, because refusing would amount to abandoning her patients"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Accept only what she can safely staff and record the risk she has raised"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Accept it and work faster, since the shortage must be covered somehow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the ward immediately and report the nurse in charge to the Council"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Accept only what she can safely staff and record the risk she has raised". The nurse is accountable for the care she accepts, and an unsafe ratio is a risk she must <strong>escalate to the person in charge and to the next level of management, in writing and at the time</strong>, not merely absorb in silence. Accepting what can be safely done, recording the concern and the response, is what protects patients and protects the nurse when the incident that follows is reviewed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Abandonment means leaving patients unattended; raising a concern about staffing is not abandonment.
• C — Working faster does not create the second pair of hands, and it hides the risk from everyone who could act on it.
• D — Walking off the ward leaves the patients uncovered; the Council is not the first line of escalation.

Reference: NMC Ghana Code of Conduct; Ghana Health Service staffing escalation policy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Never absorb an unsafe ratio in silence. Escalate it, write it, and accept only what is safe.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2202$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is brought in dead after collapsing at home, and the cause is not known. What must the nurse do about the death certificate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the death to the police and let the proper authority certify it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Complete the certificate herself, recording the collapse as the cause"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the family to state the cause so that the certificate can be issued"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Issue the certificate once the doctor on duty has signed the ward notes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Report the death to the police and let the proper authority certify it". A death whose cause is unknown, violent or unexplained is <strong>a reportable death</strong>: it goes to the police, and the certification follows the coronial route rather than the ward's convenience. Writing a cause the team cannot know, or taking one from a relative, creates a false record and can destroy the evidence an inquest needs. The body is left undisturbed with the lines and tubes in place until the authority has been informed.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A nurse cannot certify a cause of death, and inventing one is a serious record offence.
• C — A relative's account is not a cause of death, and it is not the basis for a certificate.
• D — A signature on the ward notes does not make an unexplained death certifiable.

Reference: Ghana Public Health Act — registration of deaths; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$Unknown cause means reportable. Police first, certificate later, body undisturbed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2211$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse develops vomiting and diarrhoea on the morning of a shift on a neonatal unit. What should she do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report it and stay off duty until she has been clear for the required period"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Attend the shift and wear gloves, since the babies are in incubators"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Attend but ask to be assigned to the adult ward for the day instead"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take an antidiarrhoeal and attend, because the unit is already short-staffed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Report it and stay off duty until she has been clear for the required period". Gastrointestinal illness in a nurse caring for neonates is a <strong>transmission risk that no amount of glove use removes</strong>, because the organism is on her hands, her clothing and everything she touches before she can wash. Staff with diarrhoea and vomiting are excluded from duty until they have been symptom-free for the period the local policy sets, usually 48 hours, and report to occupational health. The neonatal unit is exactly where the consequence of a lapse is worst.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Gloves do not stop contamination of the surfaces and linen around the cot.
• C — The organism travels with the nurse to any ward, and moving her does not protect anyone.
• D — An antidiarrhoeal suppresses the symptom without removing the infectivity, and attending short-staffed is not a defence.

Reference: Ghana IPC guidelines; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct — fitness to practise$q$,
  strategy = $q$Her hands carry it to the cots. Stay off, report it, and return when clear.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2214$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient asks to see the notes made about her during her admission. What is her position in Ghana?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "She may see them only if the doctor in charge agrees to release them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "She is entitled to information about her care and may see the record"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The record belongs to the hospital, so she has no right of access"}, {"id": "d", "text": "She may see them only after she has been discharged from hospital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "She is entitled to information about her care and may see the record". Information about a patient belongs to that patient. The Patients' Charter makes information about diagnosis, treatment and prognosis a right, and a request to see the record is <strong>answered by the facility rather than refused because the paper belongs to the hospital</strong>. Where an entry could cause serious harm, the information is shared with the patient in a way that handles that — it is not withheld altogether.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The treating doctor explains the record; release is not a personal favour that can be declined.
• C — Ownership of the paper does not extinguish the patient's right to the information in it.
• D — Access does not depend on being discharged, and inpatients ask routinely.

Reference: Ghana Patients' Charter; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$The file belongs to the hospital; the information belongs to the patient.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2225$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with advanced dementia has been made not for resuscitation after discussion with his family. His daughter arrives and demands that everything be done. What should the team do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cancel the decision at once, because a relative has objected to it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ignore her, because the decision has already been written in the notes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Meet her, explain how and why the decision was made, and review it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell her the decision is a clinical matter that is not hers to discuss"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Meet her, explain how and why the decision was made, and review it". A not-for-resuscitation decision is made with the patient where possible, and in their best interests when they cannot take part, and it is <strong>reviewed whenever circumstances or the family's understanding change</strong>. The daughter is not a stranger to the decision — she may hold information about what her father would have wanted, and she may simply have been given the news badly. Listening, explaining the reasoning and reviewing is what the decision requires; neither surrendering it to whoever objects loudest nor treating it as beyond discussion is correct.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A relative's objection is a reason to review, not an automatic reversal of a decision made in the patient's interests.
• B — Ignoring a distressed daughter abandons the family at the moment the decision has to be understood.
• D — The decision is clinical in its reasoning, but the patient's family are entitled to an explanation and to be heard.

Reference: NMC Ghana Code of Conduct; Ghana STG — end-of-life care$q$,
  strategy = $q$A DNAR decision is reviewed, not defended. Explain the reasoning and listen for what the family knows.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-JUS-2228$q$;

-- Verify: expect 7
COMMIT;

