-- Batch 63: NG-PKD read and three duplicates rewritten.
--
-- Batch 63 — NG-PKD read in full (50 items, keys a13 b13 c12 d12), three rewrites.
-- NG-PKD-1228  verbal order, 0.40 vs NG-FND-2604 and 0.36 vs NG-DRC-2424
--              -> cumulative paracetamol across a cold remedy the patient bought (key c).
-- NG-PKD-1237  teach-back, 0.41 vs NG-PFC-0662 and 0.36 vs NG-DRG-1035
--              -> why eight-hourly doses must be evenly spaced, not clustered in the day (key a).
-- NG-PFC-0662  the surviving half of the teach-back pair, twin of NG-DRG-1035
--              -> consent for a patient who cannot read: read it, check it, record it (key d).
-- Also repairs seventeen rationales that my patch script had truncated to the keyed
-- option text (seven NG-JUS, these three, and seven long-answer tranche-two items).
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has had two doses of paracetamol 1 g today and has also taken a cold and flu remedy he bought himself. He now asks for another dose for headache. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the dose, because two grams in a day is well inside the safe limit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the dose and record it, since the cold remedy was not prescribed here"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Add up the paracetamol in everything he has taken before giving any more"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refuse, and tell him he must never buy a medicine for himself again"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Add up the paracetamol in everything he has taken before giving any more". Paracetamol is the active ingredient in a long list of cold, flu and headache remedies sold without prescription, and <strong>the liver does not care which box the drug came from</strong>. The adult ceiling is four grams in twenty-four hours, and less in a patient with liver disease, low body weight or regular alcohol use. The nurse's task is to <strong>total every source before adding another dose</strong>, and if the total is close to the ceiling, to withhold the dose and explain why rather than simply record it.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Two grams on the ward chart says nothing about the paracetamol inside the remedy he bought.
• B — Recording the dose does not remove what he has already swallowed, and where it was prescribed is irrelevant to his total.
• D — A blanket ban is not the issue; the same man will need analgesia again tomorrow, and the count is what makes it safe.

Reference: Ghana STG — paracetamol dosing; Ghana nursing curriculum — RGN 207 drug safety$q$,
  strategy = $q$Paracetamol appears in more places than the drug chart. Total the day before adding to it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PKD-1228$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient on a ward asks why his antibiotic cannot be given at 8 am, 1 pm and 6 pm so that he can sleep through the night. What is the nurse's best explanation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Even spacing keeps the level above what the bacteria need all night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The times fit the ward round, so doses are given while doctors are present"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The drug is absorbed only while the patient is awake and sitting upright"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Closer spacing during the day would exceed the daily limit for the drug"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Even spacing keeps the level above what the bacteria need all night". Most antibiotics kill by staying above the concentration the organism is susceptible to for as long as possible, not by reaching a high peak. Six-hourly or eight-hourly dosing exists to <strong>hold that level steady across the whole twenty-four hours</strong>. Give all three doses between breakfast and bedtime and the patient gets peaks he does not need and then a long overnight trough, which is exactly when the surviving bacteria multiply. The practical answer is to keep the interval even and shift the whole schedule earlier — six in the morning, two in the afternoon, ten at night — rather than to squeeze the doses into the day.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Ward convenience is not a pharmacological reason, and clustering the doses is the harm being avoided.
• C — Absorption does not depend on being awake, and a patient can be roused briefly for a dose.
• D — The twenty-four-hour total is unchanged by clustering; the problem is the trough, not the total.

Reference: Ghana STG — antimicrobial prescribing; Ghana nursing curriculum — RGN 207 drug safety$q$,
  strategy = $q$Space it evenly. Clustering the doses buys sleep and loses the level.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PKD-1237$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient who cannot read is asked to sign a consent form for an operation. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask him to make a thumbprint on the form instead of signing his name"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sign the form on his behalf, since he cannot read the words himself"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask a relative to read the form to him and then witness his signature"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Read it to him, check that he understands, and record that you did"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Read it to him, check that he understands, and record that you did". Being unable to read is <strong>not the same as being unable to consent</strong>. The right that has to be protected is the right to information he can understand, so the form is read aloud — in his own language, with an interpreter where the nurse does not share it — and the nurse satisfies herself that he knows what the operation is, why it is being done and what the alternatives are. The record then shows how consent was obtained, which is what protects both the patient and the team.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A thumbprint is an acceptable mark, but it is only a signature; it does not show that anything was explained to him.
• B — Only the patient can give consent, and a nurse's signature in his place is a false record.
• C — A relative may filter or soften the information, and the duty to inform rests with the team, not the family.

Reference: Ghana Patients' Charter; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct — consent$q$,
  strategy = $q$Illiteracy is not incapacity. Read it, check it landed, write down how you did it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PFC-0662$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

