-- Batch 64: NG-PRC read and five duplicates rewritten.
--
-- Batch 64 — NG-PRC read in full (50 items, keys a13 b13 c12 d12), five rewrites.
-- NG-PRC-1378  raised-ICP pupil, 0.49 vs NG-TRA-1859 (same question, same answer)
--              -> nasogastric tube removal: deep breath and hold (key a).
-- NG-PRC-1341  enema position, 0.44 vs NG-FND-2633 suppository position
--              -> how far the enema nozzle is inserted in an adult (key b).
-- NG-PRC-1364  NG feed position, 0.38 vs NG-NUT-0238
--              -> tracheostomy suction on indication, not on a timetable (key c).
-- NG-PRC-1374  pin-site discharge, 0.38 vs NG-SGY-0778
--              -> direction of wound cleaning, clean to dirty, new swab each stroke (key d).
-- NG-PRC-1363  drainage bag below bladder, 0.36 vs NG-FND-2626
--              -> temperature of water for ear syringing, body temperature (key c).
-- Key letters unchanged; no replacement exceeds 0.28 against any item; parity 1.02 to 1.17.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient's nasogastric tube is to be removed. What should the nurse ask him to do while the tube is being withdrawn?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take a deep breath and hold it as the tube comes out"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Swallow repeatedly so the tube does not catch on the way"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cough as hard as possible through the whole procedure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sip water slowly while the tube is being pulled out"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Take a deep breath and hold it as the tube comes out". Holding the breath after a deep inspiration <strong>closes the glottis</strong>, so the tube passes through the pharynx without anything being drawn into the airway, and the patient cannot swallow against it as it comes up. The tube is pinched or clamped and withdrawn steadily in one movement, and any residual fluid is cleared afterwards. Swallowing, coughing and drinking all put the patient at risk of aspirating at the moment the tube leaves the pharynx.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Swallowing closes the pharynx around the tube and can drag it, and the reflex invites inhalation.
• C — Coughing on a tube that is being pulled stimulates gagging and vomiting, not clearance.
• D — Fluid given while a tube is being withdrawn is exactly what gets aspirated.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — nasogastric procedures; RGN 207$q$,
  strategy = $q$Deep breath, hold it, pull once. Anything the patient swallows on the way out can go the wrong way.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1378$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$How far should the nozzle of an enema be inserted into the rectum of an adult?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Just inside the anal margin, about two centimetres"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Seven to ten centimetres, aimed towards the umbilicus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fifteen to twenty centimetres, into the sigmoid colon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Only until resistance is felt, whatever the depth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Seven to ten centimetres, aimed towards the umbilicus". The nozzle is passed <strong>past the anal canal and the internal sphincter into the rectum</strong>, roughly seven to ten centimetres in an adult, and directed slightly anteriorly along the line of the rectum towards the umbilicus. That places the fluid where it can be retained and run into the colon, without trauma to the rectal wall.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Two centimetres leaves the tip in the anal canal, where the fluid runs straight back out.
• C — Pushing to fifteen centimetres or more risks perforating the rectum, which curves at the rectosigmoid junction.
• D — Resistance means the tip is against the wall or a stricture; forcing past it is how perforation happens.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — bowel care procedures; RGN 207$q$,
  strategy = $q$Past the sphincter, not past the bend: seven to ten centimetres, angled to the umbilicus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1341$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with a tracheostomy is awake, breathing quietly and clearing his own secretions. When should the nurse suction him?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "At the start of every shift, whether or not he needs it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Every hour through the day and every two hours at night"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only when secretions are audible or he cannot clear them"}, {"id": "d", "text": "After every meal and every drink he takes during the day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Only when secretions are audible or he cannot clear them". Suction is a <strong>procedure with its own harms</strong> — it strips oxygen, traumatises mucosa and carries organisms down the airway — so it is done for an indication, not to a timetable. Audible or visible secretions, a bubbling stoma, a rising respiratory rate or a patient who cannot cough effectively are the indications. A patient who is breathing quietly and clearing his own secretions needs observation and humidification, not a catheter.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Routine suctioning at handover finds nothing in most patients and causes harm in some.
• B — A fixed hourly schedule ignores the patient in front of the nurse and is the habit that damages the airway.
• D — Suctioning after every meal and drink interrupts rest, and swallowing does not fill the airway.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — airway care; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$Suction for a reason you can hear. A quiet chest and a good cough are the reason not to.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1364$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse cleans a sutured abdominal wound. How should the swab be moved?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Back and forth across the incision, using one swab throughout"}, {"id": "b", "text": "In circles over the wound, working from the drainage site inwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "From the outer edge inwards, finishing at the centre of the wound"}, {"id": "d", "text": "From the clean part outwards, using a new swab for each stroke"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "From the clean part outwards, using a new swab for each stroke". Cleaning moves from <strong>the least contaminated area to the most contaminated</strong>: from the incision outwards, and from a clean wound towards a drain site rather than the other way about. A fresh swab for each stroke means organisms lifted on the first pass are not carried back to the wound, which is the whole point of cleaning rather than wiping.

Why the other options fail:
• A — One swab dragged back and forth returns the organisms it has just picked up.
• B — Working inwards from a drain site carries the heaviest contamination into the wound.
• C — Finishing at the centre puts the dirtiest stroke last, on the part that must stay cleanest.

Reference: Ghana IPC guidelines — wound care; Ghana nursing curriculum$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clean to dirty, one stroke per swab. Never carry the far end back to the near end.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1374$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$At what temperature should the water be for syringing a patient's ear?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cold, straight from the tap, to numb the canal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "As hot as the patient can comfortably tolerate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "At body temperature, around thirty-seven degrees"}, {"id": "d", "text": "At room temperature, whatever the clinic happens to be"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "At body temperature, around thirty-seven degrees". The inner ear carries the <strong>vestibular apparatus</strong>, and fluid far from body temperature sets up convection currents in the semicircular canals. Cold or hot water therefore produces vertigo, nausea and nystagmus in a patient already having his ear syringed. Water at about thirty-seven degrees is neither a caloric stimulus nor a burn, and it dissolves wax without shocking the canal.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Cold water is the standard caloric test stimulus; it makes the patient violently dizzy.
• B — Hot water scalds the canal and irritates the drum, and it is a stronger vestibular stimulus still.
• D — Room temperature is well below body heat in most clinics, so it produces the same vertigo.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — ear care; RGN 207$q$,
  strategy = $q$Match the body. Anything colder or hotter turns a syringing into a caloric test.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1363$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

