UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Intravenous promethazine 25 mg is prescribed for severe vomiting. **Before** injecting it, the nurse should give the highest priority to checking:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The colour and clarity of the solution in the ampoule"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The patient's temperature, pulse and respiratory rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That the cannula is patent and sited in a large vein"}, {"id": "d", "text": "When the patient last received an analgesic or sedative"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "That the cannula is patent and sited in a large vein". Promethazine is <strong>strongly irritant</strong>; if it leaks into tissue or an artery it causes <strong>severe pain, necrosis and even gangrene</strong>. It should be diluted, given slowly through a well-flowing line in a large vein, and the injection stopped immediately if the patient reports burning. Where possible, deep intramuscular or oral routes are preferred.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Promethazine potentiates opioids, so the analgesic history is relevant to sedation, but extravasation is the greater immediate hazard.
• B — Vital signs are not the key pre-dose check for promethazine, although sedation is monitored afterwards.
• D — Checking the solution is routine for every injection but does not address the specific danger of this drug.

Reference: Ghana STG — antiemetics; manufacturer and FDA warnings on intravenous promethazine$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: for a vesicant or irritant drug, the pre-administration priority is always the integrity of the vein.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0149$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with severe pre-eclampsia is given intravenous hydralazine. Which observation is **most important** immediately afterwards?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Maternal temperature, taken every four hours through the day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Blood pressure and foetal heart rate, checked frequently"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Daily weight, to detect any fluid retention developing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Urine specific gravity, measured once in every shift"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Blood pressure and foetal heart rate, checked frequently". Hydralazine lowers blood pressure by direct arteriolar dilation and can do so abruptly. An excessive fall reduces placental perfusion, so the foetus can be compromised by the very treatment given to protect the pregnancy, which is why maternal blood pressure and the foetal heart rate are monitored together and frequently. The aim is controlled reduction to a safe range rather than normalisation. Maternal tachycardia, headache and flushing are expected effects of the vasodilation. The principle is that in obstetric care every maternal intervention has a foetal consequence, so both must be monitored.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Temperature is a routine observation and is not the parameter at risk from acute vasodilation.
• C — Daily weight is far too slow a measure to detect a haemodynamic change occurring within minutes.
• D — Urine specific gravity gives no useful information about the immediate response to an intravenous antihypertensive.

Reference: Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines — severe pre-eclampsia$q$,
  strategy = $q$With any drug given in pregnancy, ask what it does to placental perfusion. Monitoring answers that include the foetus are usually preferred to those that watch the mother alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2462$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a routine gastrointestinal health history assessment, which question by the nurse evaluates the health perception and health management pattern?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "'Can you describe the consistency of your stools, and any recent change?'"}, {"id": "b", "text": "'How many bowel movements do you have in a normal day, and when?'"}, {"id": "c", "text": "'Does emotional stress trigger abdominal cramping or loose stools?'"}, {"id": "d", "text": "'Do you take any medicines, including herbal ones, without asking first?'"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In Gordon's Functional Health Patterns, the health perception-health management pattern asks how the client sees their own health and what they do about it, including self-prescribed medicines, herbal preparations and whether they follow advice. Bowel frequency, stress-triggered cramping and stool consistency belong to the elimination pattern.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Categorise the pattern before judging the wording: questions about self-treatment and health practices assess health perception/management; bowel frequency and stool form assess elimination.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0063$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with asthma is wheezing and can speak only single words. What is the priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess airway and breathing, then give rapid-relief treatment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask him to lie flat and rest until the wheeze settles on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Offer a heavy meal to settle him and help him sleep afterwards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Defer the assessment until the next ward round in the morning"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Being unable to speak in sentences is a marker of severe asthma, and a silent chest is the step before arrest. The nurse should sit the patient up, give oxygen to the target saturation, give repeated doses of a short-acting beta-2 agonist by nebuliser or spacer, add ipratropium and systemic steroids as prescribed, and call for senior help early. Option B worsens the work of breathing. Options C and D delay treatment during a deterioration that can kill within minutes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Single words mean severe asthma. A silent chest means it is about to stop.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0041$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$When speaking with a woman admitted with Ménière's disease, the nurse deliberately stands directly in front of her at eye level. The **main** reason for this is that:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It keeps her full attention fixed on what the nurse is saying"}, {"id": "b", "text": "She can talk without turning her head and provoking vertigo"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Her peripheral visual field is narrowed by the disease process"}, {"id": "d", "text": "She needs to read the nurse's lips because of her hearing loss"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "She can talk without turning her head and provoking vertigo". Ménière's disease causes episodes of <strong>disabling vertigo, tinnitus and fluctuating hearing loss</strong>. Sudden head movement can trigger or worsen vertigo, so care is organised to <strong>minimise head turning</strong>: approach from the front, place items within reach, move slowly, and dim lights during attacks.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Visual fields are normal in Ménière's disease.
• C — Hearing loss in Ménière's is usually one-sided and fluctuating; lip-reading is not the reason for the position.
• D — Attention is not the problem; motion-provoked vertigo is.

Reference: Ghana STG — vertigo and inner-ear disorders$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: link the nursing position to the dominant symptom — vertigo triggered by head movement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0145$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse comes upon a man lying motionless beside a road after a collision. Traffic is still moving past. What should the nurse do **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess the scene for danger and make it safe first"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Check whether the man is breathing and has a pulse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Begin chest compressions immediately where he lies"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Turn him into the recovery position on the road"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Assess the scene for danger and make it safe first". Every first aid sequence begins with <strong>danger</strong>. A rescuer who is struck by a vehicle becomes a second casualty and removes the only help available. Making the scene safe — stopping or diverting traffic, using warning triangles, engaging bystanders — takes seconds and must precede any patient contact.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Checking breathing requires approaching the casualty, which is unsafe while traffic is still moving. Assessment follows scene safety.
• C — Compressions may well be needed, but starting them in a live traffic lane endangers both people. Response and breathing have not been assessed either.
• D — The recovery position is for an unresponsive casualty who is breathing normally, which has not been established, and it still requires safe approach.

Reference: Ghana National Ambulance Service — first aid guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Danger, Response, Shout, Airway, Breathing, Circulation — in that order, every time. The commonest error in emergency questions is skipping straight to treatment. If an option involves touching the casualty before the scene is safe, it is wrong.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EMG-0061$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A ten-year-old is well above the expected weight for his height. Which approach is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place the child on a strict reducing diet and weigh him weekly at the clinic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tell the child in front of the family that he must stop eating so much"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take no action, since childhood weight always corrects itself at puberty"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Work with the family on activity and food, aiming for slow steady growth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Work with the family on activity and food, aiming for slow steady growth". A growing child usually does not need to lose weight. Holding weight relatively steady while height increases allows the body mass index to fall naturally, which is gentler and more sustainable than restriction. The intervention must involve the whole family, because a child cannot choose what is bought or cooked, and singling him out breeds shame and secret eating. The practical targets are sugary drinks, portion sizes, screen time and daily active play. Weight talk directed at children is associated with disordered eating later, so the framing should be about health, strength and energy rather than appearance or numbers.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Strict reducing diets in children risk compromising growth and micronutrient intake, and they are associated with later disordered eating. Frequent weighing intensifies the focus on the number.
• B — Public criticism humiliates the child, damages the therapeutic relationship and predicts secretive eating and poorer outcomes. It is among the least effective possible responses.
• C — Childhood obesity frequently persists into adulthood rather than resolving at puberty. Waiting wastes the years in which family habits are most changeable.

Reference: WHO ending childhood obesity report; Ghana Health Service school health programme$q$,
  strategy = $q$In a growing child, growing into the weight beats losing it. Any option that isolates or shames the child is wrong regardless of its nutritional content.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3132$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse examines a soft-tissue injury following blunt impact. Which assessment finding is NOT a characteristic feature of an uncomplicated contusion?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Full-thickness tearing and breach of the skin surface"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Localised erythema and bruising over the injured area"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tenderness and discomfort when the area is touched"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Subcutaneous oedema and mild swelling of the tissue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$A contusion (bruise) is by definition a closed injury: the overlying skin surface remains intact without tearing. Ecchymosis, tenderness, and localized swelling are hallmark features of contusions. Tearing of the skin describes an open wound (laceration).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the key distinction: contusions have intact skin. If the skin is torn or open, it is a laceration or open wound.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0078$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A community health nurse plans her home visits for the morning. Which client should she see **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A mother breastfeeding her two-day-old baby, born at 38 weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A man discharged yesterday on rivaroxaban after a deep vein thrombosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An elderly woman discharged three days ago after pneumonia treatment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A man with heart failure, off furosemide, now coughing pink froth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A man with heart failure, off furosemide, now coughing pink froth". Pink frothy sputum in a heart-failure patient who has stopped his diuretic indicates <strong>acute pulmonary oedema</strong> — an airway and breathing emergency that will progress to respiratory failure. He needs to be seen immediately, sat upright and transferred to hospital for oxygen and intravenous diuretics.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A term newborn feeding on day 2 is a routine postnatal visit.
• B — A recovering pneumonia patient needs chest assessment and encouragement to mobilise and drink, but nothing in the data suggests deterioration.
• C — A patient on a new anticoagulant needs assessment and teaching about bleeding, but he is stable.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — community health nursing priorities; Ghana STG — acute heart failure$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: find the least stable client using ABCs — breathing compromised by pulmonary oedema outranks every stable follow-up.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0096$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 48-year-old man newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes asks, "Is there anything special I should do when I buy shoes?" Which response is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Buy shoes half a size bigger than you need so that they are always roomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Buy plastic or vinyl shoes because they keep their shape and are easy to clean"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is best to buy shoes early in the morning, when your feet are freshest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Have both feet measured standing, shop late in the day, break them in slowly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Have both feet measured standing, shop late in the day, break them in slowly". Neuropathy means a diabetic foot may not feel a shoe that rubs. Shoes that <strong>fit correctly</strong> — measured each time because feet change, chosen in the afternoon when feet are slightly swollen, made of soft <strong>leather or breathable fabric</strong>, with a wide toe box — prevent the blisters and pressure ulcers that lead to amputation. New shoes are worn for an hour or two at a time at first and the feet inspected afterwards.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Non-breathable materials trap moisture and encourage fungal infection and maceration.
• B — Over-large shoes slide and rub, causing blisters just as tight shoes do.
• C — Feet are smallest in the morning, so shoes bought then may be too tight later in the day.

Reference: Ghana STG — diabetic foot care; IWGDF foot-care guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in diabetic foot teaching the aim is 'no pressure, no friction, no moisture' — choose the option that delivers all three.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0172$q$;

-- Verify: expect 10
COMMIT;

