UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A tourniquet is inflated on a patient's arm for hand surgery. What is the main danger of leaving it inflated too long?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The patient's blood pressure rises as the limb is compressed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The bone underneath the cuff may fracture under the pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The anaesthetic wears off before the tourniquet is released"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nerve and muscle damage from the limb being starved of blood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Nerve and muscle damage from the limb being starved of blood". A tourniquet works by <strong>stopping all blood flow to the limb</strong>, which is why the field is dry and why the clock matters. Peripheral nerves are the most sensitive tissue to ischaemia and begin to suffer within an hour or two; muscle follows. The inflation time is written on the theatre record the moment the cuff goes up, and most units keep it under about ninety minutes to two hours. Released, the limb is reperfused slowly and the patient watched for the transient fall in blood pressure and the tourniquet pain that follows.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Pressure rises a little with inflation but is not the danger being timed.
• B — Fracture under a padded cuff is not a recognised consequence of duration.
• C — Anaesthesia is managed by the anaesthetist; tourniquet time is not a dosing decision.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — theatre practice; WHO surgical care guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$The dry field costs the limb its blood. Write the time down the moment the cuff goes up.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1562$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why is an antiemetic often given before an operation rather than after the patient starts to vomit?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It reduces the dose of anaesthetic drug needed to keep the patient asleep"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It stops the patient retching while the airway is being secured"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Preventing the vomiting reflex is easier than stopping it once started"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It dries the airway secretions before the anaesthetic is given"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Preventing the vomiting reflex is easier than stopping it once started". Post-operative nausea and vomiting is driven by several triggers at once — the anaesthetic agents themselves, opioid analgesia, handling of the gut and motion — and <strong>once the reflex is established, a single antiemetic often fails to break it</strong>. Prophylaxis given before induction keeps the trigger below the threshold at which vomiting begins. It also matters most where aspiration is dangerous: a patient with a full stomach, a recent meal or an emergency operation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Antiemetics do not reduce the anaesthetic requirement in any clinically useful way.
• B — Preventing retching on induction is a by-product, not the reason prophylaxis is timed this way.
• D — Drying secretions is the job of an antimuscarinic drug, not an antiemetic.

Reference: Ghana STG — perioperative care; Ghana nursing curriculum$q$,
  strategy = $q$Block the reflex before it starts. An antiemetic given after vomiting is a race it often loses.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1538$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient will be in the lithotomy position for a two-hour operation. What must the nurse do to prevent injury?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place both legs straight out on the table without any support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Raise the legs as high as possible to give the surgeon access"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the legs unsupported so that the patient can reposition them"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Support the legs in stirrups and avoid pressure behind the knee"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Support the legs in stirrups and avoid pressure behind the knee". In lithotomy the legs are lifted into stirrups and the hips flexed, which <strong>stretches the sciatic and femoral nerves and puts pressure on the tissues behind the knee</strong>, where the common peroneal nerve and the popliteal vessels lie. Both legs are raised and lowered together, the buttocks must not overhang the table, and the knees and calves are supported in padded stirrups rather than hung over a bar.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Unsupported legs drop into hip extension and drag on the nerves.
• B — Extreme hip flexion is exactly what stretches the sciatic nerve.
• C — A sedated or anaesthetised patient cannot reposition herself; the nurse must.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — theatre positioning; WHO surgical care guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Legs up together, knees padded, nothing pressing behind the knee.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1556$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why must a patient show that she can move her legs before leaving the recovery area after spinal anaesthesia?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Movement proves that the spinal drug has been absorbed by the body"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The surgeon requires it before writing the operation in the notes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Moving the legs prevents a clot forming in the deep veins of the calf"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Until it wears off she cannot protect her airway or move herself"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Until it wears off she cannot protect her airway or move herself". A spinal block takes out sensation, power and sympathetic tone below its level, and <strong>the same block that anaesthetised the operation also paralyses the muscles that cough, shift position and call for help</strong>. Returning leg movement is the practical test that the block is wearing off and that the patient can protect herself on an open ward. Blood pressure must also be stable, since sympathetic tone returns with the motor fibres.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Drug absorption is not what the movement demonstrates; the block is wearing off.
• B — Recovery criteria are clinical, not a signature the surgeon supplies.
• C — Leg movement helps circulation, but clot prevention is not why she must wait.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — recovery and anaesthesia; Ghana STG$q$,
  strategy = $q$Movement returned means the block is going. Until then the ward cannot keep her safe alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1558$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why are a patient's dentures removed before a general anaesthetic?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loose teeth or a plate can obstruct the airway or be inhaled"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dentures interfere with the monitors attached during anaesthesia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The anaesthetic gas damages the material the dentures are made of"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Patients are not allowed to wear them inside the operating theatre"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Loose teeth or a plate can obstruct the airway or be inhaled". Induction and intubation involve instruments passing through the mouth and pharynx while the patient cannot protect her own airway, and <strong>a denture or a loose tooth can be dislodged into the pharynx or the trachea</strong>. A full set is removed and kept safe and labelled; a partial plate or a loose tooth is identified before induction so the anaesthetist knows the risk.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Monitoring is unaffected by dentures; the airway is the issue.
• C — Anaesthetic gases do not damage denture material.
• D — There is no theatre dress rule about dentures; the reason is airway safety.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — anaesthesia safety; WHO surgical care guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anything loose in the mouth can end up in the airway. Take it out and label it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1540$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

