UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with type 2 diabetes taking a sulfonylurea wishes to fast from dawn to dusk during Ramadan. What is the **most appropriate** nursing action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Refer him for a pre-fasting review and teach him when to break the fast"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tell him that fasting is forbidden with diabetes and must not be attempted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise him to continue the same doses at the same clock times throughout"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Suggest he stop all diabetes medication for the month of the fast"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Refer him for a pre-fasting review and teach him when to break the fast". Many people with well-controlled type 2 diabetes can fast safely with preparation, and a blanket prohibition simply means the patient fasts anyway without telling anyone, which is far more dangerous. Sulfonylureas carry a real hypoglycaemia risk during a long daylight fast, so doses are commonly reduced and the larger dose moved to the evening meal. The patient needs to know that hypoglycaemia, hyperglycaemia or dehydration require the fast to be broken, that most religious traditions explicitly permit this, and that blood glucose testing does not invalidate the fast. Respectful preparation preserves both safety and the patient's religious practice.

Why the other options fail:
• B — An outright prohibition ignores the strength of the patient's religious commitment and usually results in unsupervised fasting. It also overstates the risk for many well-controlled patients.
• C — Keeping the same doses at the same clock times means a sulfonylurea acting through the longest fasting hours with no food, which is precisely the recipe for daytime hypoglycaemia.
• D — Stopping all medication for a month risks marked hyperglycaemia, dehydration and hyperosmolar states. The goal is adjustment, not abandonment.

Reference: International Diabetes Federation and DAR guidance on diabetes and Ramadan; Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$When a patient's practice conflicts with a regimen, adjust the regimen rather than forbidding the practice. Forbidding it usually means losing sight of what they actually do.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3104$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An ICU nurse prepares to suction a 48-year-old man with an endotracheal tube. Which technique is **correct**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pre-oxygenate, insert without suction, then suction on withdrawal under 15 seconds"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insert the catheter ten centimetres and apply continuous suction for thirty seconds"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply suction while advancing the catheter, then withdraw it slowly again"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Insert it with suction applied, pump it in and out several times, then withdraw"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Pre-oxygenate, insert without suction, then suction on withdrawal under 15 seconds". Safe endotracheal suctioning means <strong>pre-oxygenation</strong>, a sterile catheter no wider than half the tube's internal diameter, <strong>insertion without suction</strong> to avoid mucosal trauma, withdrawal of 1 cm when resistance is felt at the carina, and <strong>intermittent suction for a maximum of 10–15 seconds</strong> while rotating the catheter out. This clears secretions while limiting hypoxia, vagal bradycardia and airway injury.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Thirty seconds of continuous suction causes profound hypoxia and bradycardia; passes are limited to 10–15 seconds.
• C — Suction on insertion strips oxygen from the airway and damages the mucosa.
• D — An in-and-out pumping motion with suction applied traumatises the trachea and is never recommended.

Reference: Ghana STG — airway management; AARC clinical practice guideline on endotracheal suctioning$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: a procedure question — pick the option that protects oxygenation (ABCs) at every step: oxygen first, no suction going in, short intermittent suction coming out.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with stable heart failure is started on a beta blocker. Why is it begun at a very low dose and increased slowly?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The drug works only when it is given at very low doses"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A low dose avoids the need for any monitoring of the patient"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The drug has to be stopped again after a few weeks of use"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A rapid rise can weaken the heart and worsen failure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Beta blockers improve survival in heart failure by blocking the harmful effects of sustained sympathetic drive, but they also reduce contractility, so starting low and titrating over weeks allows the heart to adapt. Option A is wrong: the target dose is the one shown to improve outcomes. Option B is wrong because blood pressure, heart rate and weight are all monitored through titration. Option C is wrong because the drug is continued long term unless it is not tolerated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Beta blockers help the failing heart in the long run but hurt it briefly, so start low and go slow.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-REVIEW-CARDIO-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A child has a partial-thickness burn to the forearm being managed on the ward. Which dressing principle applies?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leave the burn exposed to the air so that a hard crust can form"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply a tight compression bandage firmly over the burn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A non-adherent dressing keeping the wound moist and protected"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply dry gauze directly to the burn surface and leave it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A non-adherent dressing keeping the wound moist and protected". Partial-thickness burns heal best under a <strong>non-adherent dressing maintaining a moist environment</strong>, which reduces pain, protects the exposed nerve endings and provides a barrier against infection. Burns are highly susceptible to infection because the skin barrier is destroyed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Exposure allows drying, deepens the injury and offers no protection against contamination.
• B — Tight compression over a burn that will swell impairs circulation and can cause further tissue damage.
• D — Dry gauze adheres to the raw surface and causes severe pain and tissue loss at the next dressing change.

Reference: Ghana STG — burns management$q$,
  strategy = $q$Burns follow the same principles as other wounds with two additions: they swell, so nothing constrictive, and they are highly infection-prone, so a protective barrier matters. Non-adherent is essential because these wounds are exquisitely painful.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0141$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Which record of a wound assessment is **most** useful clinically?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The wound looks better today than at the previous dressing change"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dressing changed as instructed by the medical team this morning"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sacral wound 4 by 3 cm, 0.5 cm deep, 70 percent granulation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wound satisfactory on inspection and the patient is comfortable"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Sacral wound 4 by 3 cm, 0.5 cm deep, 70 percent granulation". Useful documentation is <strong>objective and measurable</strong>, allowing any nurse to determine whether the wound has improved. Recording site, dimensions, depth, tissue type by proportion, exudate, peri-wound skin and odour creates a baseline against which change can be judged and dressing decisions justified.

Why the other options fail:
• A — “Better” is subjective and unverifiable. Another nurse cannot judge progress against it.
• B — This records an action rather than an assessment and says nothing about the wound.
• D — “Satisfactory” is an opinion with no measurable content.

Reference: NMC Ghana Code — record keeping; wound assessment$q$,
  strategy = $q$Wound notes should let a colleague picture the wound without seeing it. Measure it, describe the tissue by proportion, and note exudate and surrounding skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0189$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with 35 % total body surface area burns from a kerosene stove explosion arrives at the emergency department two hours after injury. Two large-bore intravenous cannulas are inserted. The nurse understands that intravenous therapy in the first 24 hours is **primarily** aimed at:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Providing enough calories and protein for the burn to heal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Replacing plasma lost into burned tissue to prevent shock"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Delivering intravenous antibiotics to prevent infection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Correcting the hyperkalaemia caused by cell destruction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Replacing plasma lost into burned tissue to prevent shock". Major burns cause massive <strong>capillary leak</strong>; plasma pours into the interstitial space for 24–48 hours. Without rapid <strong>crystalloid resuscitation</strong>, calculated from weight and burn area (for example, the Parkland formula) and titrated to a <strong>urine output of 0.5–1 mL/kg/hour</strong>, hypovolaemic shock and acute kidney injury follow.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Nutritional support is vital but starts after resuscitation, usually enterally.
• C — Hyperkalaemia may occur and is monitored, but fluid volume, not potassium, is the primary purpose of early IV therapy.
• D — Prophylactic antibiotics are not routinely given in the first 24 hours; infection prevention relies on wound care.

Reference: Ghana STG — burns management; WHO emergency burn care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in the first day after a major burn, think circulation — the intravenous line exists to replace lost plasma volume.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A sputum sample for culture and sensitivity is requested for a man with a productive cough. Which instruction by the nurse is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "First thing tomorrow, rinse your mouth, cough deeply and spit into this container"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect every bit of sputum you produce over the next three days in this covered jar"}, {"id": "c", "text": "After your nebuliser this evening, spit your saliva into the sterile container"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Do some pursed-lip breathing first and then spit whatever comes into your mouth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "First thing tomorrow, rinse your mouth, cough deeply and spit into this container". Secretions pool overnight, so an <strong>early-morning, deep-cough specimen</strong> gives the highest yield of organisms. Rinsing the mouth with water (not mouthwash) reduces contamination, and the sample must be <strong>sputum from the lower airways, not saliva</strong>, collected in a sterile container and sent to the laboratory promptly.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Pursed-lip breathing eases breathlessness but does not mobilise lower-airway secretions; 'whatever comes' is likely to be saliva.
• C — Saliva is not sputum, and the specimen should be the first of the day rather than an evening sample.
• D — A pooled three-day collection is not used for culture; it would be overgrown with contaminants.

Reference: Ghana Health Service laboratory specimen-collection manual$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: for any culture, pick the instruction that gets the right material (deep sputum), at the best time (early morning), with the least contamination.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0078$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 46-year-old client with type 1 diabetes mellitus is admitted to the intensive care unit in severe Diabetic Ketoacidosis (blood glucose 36.2 mmol/L [652 mg/dL], arterial pH 7.12, serum bicarbonate 8 mmol/L, positive serum ketones). The client's spouse explains that two weeks ago, a faith-healer instructed the client to stop all subcutaneous insulin injections and drink concentrated bitter leaf and mahogany bark infusions to 'kill the spiritual diabetes root.' Following emergency insulin and fluid resuscitation, how should the nurse approach discharge education?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect the family's beliefs and delay insulin until the prayer sessions are over"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the spouse to leave and exclude the family from any discharge teaching"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that faith and insulin are incompatible and ask the client to choose"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give culturally congruent teaching and negotiate the spiritual practice"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$In type 1 diabetes, pancreatic beta-cell destruction causes absolute insulin deficiency; discontinuing insulin is rapidly fatal (DKA). Shaming, insulting, or berating pluralist clients creates deep psychological defensiveness, guilt, and alienation, driving them further away from conventional care. The nurse must validate their desire for spiritual comfort, respectfully explain the pathophysiology of type 1 diabetes (the body requires insulin just like it requires oxygen and water), emphasize that insulin is non-negotiable for life, and negotiate ways their spiritual beliefs and prayers can support their healing while maintaining strict daily insulin adherence. Why the other options fail: delaying insulin in DKA is lethal; excluding the family removes the people who administer care at home; and framing faith and insulin as incompatible forces a choice the client does not have to make.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pluralist health coaching: Respect spiritual beliefs, clearly explain non-negotiable biological therapies (like insulin in type 1 diabetes), and bridge cultural values with medical safety.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client taking warfarin for atrial fibrillation needs emergency surgery within six hours. The INR is 6.2. What should the nurse anticipate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate before surgery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Two units of packed red cells given to dilute the warfarin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Protamine sulfate given to reverse the warfarin effect"}, {"id": "d", "text": "No action, because the surgery itself will stop any bleeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$An INR of 6.2 means the patient is profoundly anticoagulated and cannot be operated on safely, so the team reverses the warfarin with vitamin K plus a prothrombin complex concentrate, which replaces the clotting factors within minutes, then rechecks the INR. Option B does not restore clotting factors. Option C is the antidote to heparin, not warfarin. Option D leaves the patient at risk of uncontrollable surgical bleeding.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Warfarin is reversed with vitamin K and clotting factors. Protamine is for heparin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-3500-0017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why do fluid requirements per kilogram differ between a newborn and an older child?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Newborns need less fluid per kilogram because they are small and inactive"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Newborns and older children have identical requirements at every age"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Newborns lose no fluid through the skin because it is thin and protected"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Newborns have more body water and kidneys that concentrate poorly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Newborns have more body water and kidneys that concentrate poorly". Three features combine. Total body water forms a much larger fraction of a newborn's weight than an adult's, and a greater share of it sits in the extracellular compartment where it is readily lost. Surface area relative to body mass is far higher, so evaporative losses through the skin are proportionally large, and greater still in a preterm infant or one nursed under a radiant warmer. The immature kidney cannot concentrate urine efficiently, so obligatory water loss continues even when the infant is dry. Requirements per kilogram are therefore high and change rapidly over the first days, which is why neonatal fluid prescriptions are recalculated daily against weight and urine output.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Requirements per kilogram are higher in the newborn, not lower. Small size is precisely why surface area relative to mass is large and evaporative losses are proportionally high.
• B — Requirements change substantially with age and over the first days of life. A single figure applied to every age would badly under or over hydrate most patients.
• C — Newborn skin is thin and immature, particularly in preterm infants, so transepidermal water loss is high rather than absent. Humidified incubators exist to reduce it.

Reference: WHO care of the newborn guidance; Ghana Health Service newborn protocols$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask about surface area, body water fraction and kidney maturity. Small immature patients lose proportionally more and conserve less.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-3144$q$;

-- Verify: expect 10
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch68.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 68: NG-FAM read and nine duplicates rewritten.
--
-- Batch 68 — NG-FAM read in full (50 items, keys a13 b13 c12 d12), nine rewrites.
-- NG-FAM-1712  child injection preparation, the same item as NG-PED-0482
--              -> food brought in by the family: agree it with the dietitian and count it (key a).
-- NG-FAM-1706  signed consent she cannot describe, the same item as NG-JUS-2190 and NG-PFC-0653
--              -> a relative staying overnight with a confused patient (key c).
-- NG-FAM-1725  dying patient asks, the same item as NG-PFC-0656
--              -> the family want their pastor to pray at the bedside (key c).
-- NG-FAM-1724  family ask staff not to tell, the same item as NG-JUS-2197
--              -> the patient's own request not to be told, and who may be told instead (key b).
-- NG-FAM-1703  dignity during catheterisation, the same item as NG-PRC-1340
--              -> a request that only female staff carry out the procedure (key d).
-- NG-FAM-1697  child used as interpreter, the same item as NG-JUS-2205
--              -> the husband interpreting for his wife as she is told her diagnosis (key c).
-- NG-FAM-1729  what to establish before last offices, the same item as NG-OPR-1187
--              -> explaining a legally required post-mortem to the family (key d).
-- NG-FAM-1704  money in thanks, the same item as NG-JUS-2201
--              -> a donation offered to the ward, and the proper route for it (key d).
-- NG-FAM-1691  carer exhaustion, overlapping NG-GER-0606, NG-ELD-2026 and NG-PSY-1417
--              -> a twelve-year-old carrying the caring role at home (key a).
-- Key letters unchanged; no replacement reaches 0.30 against any item; parity 1.06 to 1.15.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A twelve-year-old is doing the cooking and personal care for a parent with advanced illness. What should the nurse recognise?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A child is doing an adult's caring work and the family needs help"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The child is being neglected by the other adults in the family"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The arrangement is acceptable if the child attends school each day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nothing needs addressing while the parent remains safe at home"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A child is doing an adult's caring work and the family needs help". A child who cooks, washes and manages medicines for a parent is a <strong>young carer</strong>, and the role costs them school, sleep and their own development. It is not a sign that the family are failing — usually there is no adult available — so the nurse's response is to name what is happening and bring in help: another adult relative, a community nurse or CHPS visit, and practical support with the tasks that are too much.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Neglect is a judgement about the other adults, and the pressing need is support rather than blame.
• C — Attending school does not measure what the child is doing before and after it.
• D — Physical safety at home is not the same as the child being all right.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — family and community care; NMC Code$q$,
  strategy = $q$A child doing the caring is a signal for support, not a verdict on the family.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1691$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A husband offers to interpret for his wife while she is told her diagnosis. What should the nurse consider first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That a relative is always the best interpreter for a patient"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That the wife should be asked to learn enough English"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That he may soften what she is told, so use an interpreter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That the consultation can wait until she brings someone else"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "That he may soften what she is told, so use an interpreter". A relative interpreting <strong>holds two roles at once</strong>, and the one that usually wins is protector: bad news gets softened, questions get answered on her behalf and her own words never reach the nurse. An independent interpreter keeps the information intact and keeps the conversation between the nurse and the patient. Where no interpreter exists, the nurse explains the limits, checks understanding with the patient directly and records who interpreted.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Familiarity is exactly what makes a relative's interpretation unreliable here.
• B — The responsibility to communicate belongs to the service, not to the patient.
• D — Postponing leaves a diagnosis undiscussed while a suitable arrangement is looked for.

Reference: Ghana Patients' Charter; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct — communication$q$,
  strategy = $q$A relative who interprets is also a relative who protects. Keep the message intact.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1697$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman asks that only female staff catheterise her. How should the nurse respond?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain that staff are allocated by duty and she cannot choose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tell her the request is unreasonable and delay the procedure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Perform the procedure herself without discussing the request"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Accept the request and arrange it wherever staffing allows"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Accept the request and arrange it wherever staffing allows". A request about who touches an intimate part of the body is <strong>a reasonable request about dignity, not an obstruction</strong>, and the ward can usually meet it by allocating the duty to a female colleague. The nurse says what she can arrange and when, and if it genuinely cannot be met at that moment she explains why and offers what she can — a chaperone, a screen, a short wait.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Dismissing the request over a rota tells the patient her dignity is not part of the plan.
• B — Calling a dignity request unreasonable is a complaint waiting to be made, and delay harms her.
• C — Proceeding without answering the request leaves her feeling unheard at the worst moment.

Reference: Ghana Patients' Charter; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct — dignity$q$,
  strategy = $q$Who touches her is her call where the ward can meet it. Arrange it, do not argue the rota.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1703$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A family asks to make a donation to the ward in gratitude for their father's care. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Accept the money herself and hand it to the ward afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Decline, since any donation from a patient's family is unethical"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Suggest they give it to whichever nurse cared for him most"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Direct them to the proper channel and record what was given"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Direct them to the proper channel and record what was given". A donation <strong>to the institution</strong> is a different act from a payment to the nurse who happened to be on duty. It is accepted through the facility's own route — administration or the ward fund — receipted and recorded, so that it cannot be mistaken for a reward to an individual and so that it benefits the ward it was meant for. The nurse's own hands stay out of it.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Money passing through the nurse's hands creates exactly the suspicion the proper channel avoids.
• B — Refusing all generosity cuts the family off from a legitimate way of giving thanks.
• C — Choosing a favoured nurse turns a gift to the ward into a personal payment.

Reference: NMC Ghana Code of Conduct — gifts and conflicts of interest$q$,
  strategy = $q$Give it to the institution, not to a person. Receipted and recorded, it stays a gift.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1704$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$The family of a confused patient ask that one of them stay with him overnight. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Refuse, because no relative is allowed on the ward at night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Allow it, but ask them to keep out of the way of the staff"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Agree, arrange a chair beside the bed and show them how to call"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Agree only if the family pay for a private room for the night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Agree, arrange a chair beside the bed and show them how to call". A familiar person at the bedside is <strong>one of the most effective things available for a confused patient</strong>: it reduces agitation, keeps him from climbing out of bed and stops the night-time falls that follow. The nurse makes it work — a chair, a blanket, a clear explanation of how to summon help and what to do if he tries to get up — and includes the relative in the plan rather than merely tolerating them.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A blanket refusal removes the support that keeps him safe and increases the risk the family fear.
• B — Leaving them to keep out of the way wastes the help they came to give.
• D — Charging for company turns a clinical arrangement into a transaction.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — family-centred care; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct$q$,
  strategy = $q$A relative at the bedside is part of the plan for a confused patient. Set them up to help.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1706$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient on a renal diet asks whether his family may bring food from home. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Agree what is allowed with the dietitian and count it in his intake"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refuse, because the ward rules forbid any food being brought in"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Allow whatever the family brings, since familiar food cheers him up"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the family to bring only fruit and give no further guidance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Agree what is allowed with the dietitian and count it in his intake". Food from home is <strong>part of the care, not a threat to it</strong> — it keeps a patient eating when hospital food is refused, and it keeps the family involved. The nursing task is to make it safe: agree with the dietitian which foods fit the restriction, tell the family plainly, and include what is eaten in the fluid and intake record so the prescription still adds up.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A blanket ban removes the family from care and often means the patient eats nothing at all.
• C — Uncontrolled potassium, sodium or fluid from home cooking defeats the diet the patient needs.
• D — Fruit is exactly what is restricted in some renal patients; vague advice is worse than none.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — nutrition and family-centred care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Home food keeps a patient eating. Make it fit the prescription and record it, do not ban it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1712$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient says she does not want to be told her diagnosis and asks that her son be told instead. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell her anyway, because she has a right to know her own diagnosis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Respect her wish and agree what her son may be told about it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refuse, since such information may only be given to the patient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask her son to decide if his mother should be told the diagnosis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Respect her wish and agree what her son may be told about it". The right to information belongs to the patient, and so does the <strong>right not to receive it</strong>. Refusing information is a decision she is entitled to make, and in many Ghanaian families the relative who is told is the one who then carries the decisions. What the nurse must do is establish with her — not with the son — how much she wants to know, who may be told on her behalf, and what she wants done if she later changes her mind. Her wishes are recorded.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Overriding her refusal takes away the very right that is being invoked.
• C — Refusing to speak to the family leaves her without the support she has chosen.
• D — The son does not decide what his mother is told; she does.

Reference: Ghana Patients' Charter; NMC Ghana Code of Conduct — confidentiality$q$,
  strategy = $q$The right to know includes the right not to be told. Ask her who may hear it instead.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1724$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$The family of a dying patient ask that their pastor be allowed to pray at the bedside. How should the nurse respond?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain that visitors are restricted, so prayer must wait for visiting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Allow the visit, but ask the pastor to keep the prayers brief and quiet"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Welcome it, and arrange privacy and time for the visit to take place"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refer the request to the doctor, since it is a clinical decision"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Welcome it, and arrange privacy and time for the visit to take place". Spiritual care is part of care of the dying, and <strong>the wish to pray is not an interruption to the work of the ward but one of the needs being met</strong>. The nurse arranges what makes it possible: a screen or side room, a chair, and enough time that the family are not watching the clock. What she does not do is make the family negotiate for it or treat it as a concession.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Visiting rules exist for the running of the ward, not to refuse a dying patient's rites.
• B — Allowing it while hurrying it tells the family their beliefs are tolerated rather than respected.
• D — Prayer is not a clinical decision and does not need medical authorisation.

Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — spiritual and end-of-life care; NMC Code$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prayer at the bedside is care. Make space and time for it rather than permission.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1725$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient dies unexpectedly and the family is told a post-mortem is required. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That the examination is routine for every death on the ward"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That the family will be charged for the examination afterwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That they may not see the body once it has been taken away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That it is required by law, and their rites can still go ahead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "That it is required by law, and their rites can still go ahead". An unexpected death is a reportable one, and the examination that follows is a <strong>legal requirement rather than a clinical choice</strong> — it protects the family as much as the hospital, because it establishes why the death occurred. What families fear most is that the body will be treated without respect or that their burial rites will be impossible, so the nurse explains plainly what will happen, that cultural and religious practice is respected wherever it can be, and how the body will be released.

Why the other options fail:
• A — It is not routine; it is required because the death was unexpected.
• B — Payment is not the issue, and raising it confirms the family's worst suspicions.
• C — Viewing and rites are usually possible, and saying otherwise causes distress that can be avoided.

Reference: Ghana Public Health Act — registration and reportable deaths$q$,
  strategy = $q$An unexpected death is a legal matter. Say so plainly, and say how their rites will be respected.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FAM-1729$q$;

-- Verify: expect 9
COMMIT;

