-- Batch 69: answer cue, tranche five.
--
-- Batch 69 — answer-length cue, tranche five (10 items, 1.66 to 1.71 before).
-- Keyed option cut back to the statement it makes, explanation left in the rationale,
-- distractors extended so each states the mechanism or setting it presumes.
-- No key letter changed: NCX-0010 d, FLD-3168 c, ELD-2031 b, NCX-0323 c, NCX-0244 d,
-- NCX-0075 c, NCX-0056 b, NCX-0735 c, NCX-0126 a, CLINICAL-0037 d.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An older woman recovering from a left-hemisphere stroke has apraxia. Which nursing intervention is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Speak loudly and slowly, since she may also have a hearing problem"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform her personal care for her, so that she is not frustrated"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place objects on her affected side to encourage her to look that way"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Break tasks into simple steps, demonstrate, allow extra time"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Break tasks into simple steps, demonstrate, allow extra time". Apraxia is the <strong>inability to carry out purposeful, learned movements</strong> — using a comb, dressing — even though strength, sensation and understanding are intact. Care uses <strong>step-by-step cues, demonstration and repetition</strong>, allowing time, so that she regains function rather than losing it through dependence.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Apraxia is not a hearing or comprehension problem; volume does not help.
• B — Doing everything for her prevents relearning and promotes learned helplessness.
• C — Placing objects on the affected side is a strategy for unilateral neglect, usually after a right-hemisphere stroke.

Reference: Ghana STG — stroke rehabilitation; WHO rehabilitation guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: name the deficit precisely — apraxia means she knows what to do but cannot sequence the movement — and pick the intervention that rebuilds that sequence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A dehydrated older patient with diabetes is listed for a scan requiring intravenous contrast. Which nursing action **best** reduces the risk of kidney injury?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restrict fluids before the scan so that the contrast is not diluted"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a diuretic beforehand so that the contrast is cleared faster"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ensure hydration before and after, and review nephrotoxic drugs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take no action, since contrast medium has no effect on the kidneys"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Ensure hydration before and after, and review nephrotoxic drugs". Contrast-associated kidney injury is most likely in patients who are already dehydrated or who have diabetes, existing renal impairment or advanced age, and this patient carries several of those risks at once. Adequate hydration before and after the procedure maintains renal perfusion and helps clear the contrast, and it is the single most effective preventive measure available. At the same time, nephrotoxic drugs such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatories, aminoglycosides and sometimes metformin should be reviewed. Renal function should be checked before the scan and again afterwards where risk is high.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Restricting fluid before contrast is the opposite of correct practice and substantially increases the risk of kidney injury in an already dehydrated patient.
• B — Diuretics deplete volume further and have not been shown to protect the kidney from contrast. They increase risk rather than reducing it.
• D — Contrast media are a recognised cause of acute kidney injury, particularly in this risk group. Taking no action would be negligent.

Reference: Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines — radiology and renal care; WHO diagnostic imaging guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Before any nephrotoxic exposure, protect perfusion first. Hydration beats every pharmacological measure, and dehydration multiplies the risk.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-3168$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Where should a nurse direct a very poor older person seeking the LEAP cash transfer?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The nearest National Health Insurance district office"}, {"id": "b", "text": "District Social Welfare Officer at the Assembly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The administrator of the regional teaching hospital"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The pensions office of SSNIT in the regional capital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — ""District Social Welfare Officer at the Assembly"". LEAP targets extremely poor households, <strong>including those with a member aged 65 and above, and enrolment runs through District Social Welfare Officers based at the District Assembly</strong>. LEAP households are also an NHIS exempt category, so one referral can secure both. SSNIT is irrelevant here for a reason worth remembering: <strong>most older Ghanaians worked informally and therefore have no contributory pension at all</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — NHIS handles insurance, not the cash transfer.
• C — Hospital administration has no role in social protection enrolment.
• D — SSNIT pays contributory pensions to former formal-sector workers only.

Reference: Ghana LEAP programme, Ministry of Gender, Children and Social Protection$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cash transfer means Social Welfare at the District Assembly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ELD-2031$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with chronic back pain says a **herbal ointment** eases him and asks, 'Should I continue using it?' Which response is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Your beliefs about pain are wrong and need to change"}, {"id": "b", "text": "No, traditional remedies are all harmful and must be stopped"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell me what is in it, keep what helps, and review it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Yes, though I personally doubt that it does anything"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Tell me what is in it, keep what helps, and review it". <strong>Respect plus safety checks is the therapeutic path.</strong> Herbal preparations are part of Ghanaian life and some help — but they can irritate skin, interact with prescribed drugs or delay effective care. The nurse takes a full history of the remedy, checks for harm and interactions, involves the doctor and pharmacist, and builds a combined pain plan the patient will actually follow.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Faint endorsement laced with doubt insults the patient while teaching nothing.
• B — Attacking beliefs attacks the person; cultural humility keeps the door open for safe care.
• D — Blanket dismissal drives remedies underground and destroys the trust needed for safety monitoring.

Reference: Ghana — traditional medicine integration; culturally sensitive pain care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: respect the remedy, verify the safety — assess, involve the doctor, then decide together.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0323$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse teaches a mother how to reduce the risk of sudden infant death in her 2-month-old. Which statement shows the mother has understood?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I will lay him on his tummy so he does not choke if he vomits in bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "I will put a pillow under his head and a soft blanket over him at night"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He will sleep in our bed with us at night so that I can keep him warm"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I will put him on his back on a firm mat, and nobody will smoke"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "I will put him on his back on a firm mat, and nobody will smoke". The <strong>'back to sleep'</strong> position on a <strong>firm, flat surface free of pillows, soft toys and loose bedding</strong>, in a <strong>smoke-free home</strong>, with room-sharing but not bed-sharing, and breastfeeding, are the measures proven to reduce sudden unexpected infant death.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Pillows and loose blankets are suffocation hazards.
• B — Prone sleeping multiplies the risk; healthy infants protect their airway when supine.
• C — Bed-sharing, especially with parents who smoke or are very tired, increases the risk; the cot should be in the parents' room instead.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — essential newborn care and safe sleep; AAP safe infant sleep recommendations$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: back, firm, bare, smoke-free, same room — the option that covers all of these is the one that shows understanding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0244$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with painless haematuria is scheduled for an intravenous urogram (IVU). The nurse explains that the purpose of the test is to:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Measures the volume of urine left in the bladder after voiding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cultures the urine to identify the organism causing the infection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Outlines the kidneys, ureters and bladder with contrast"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Looks directly inside the bladder with a fine telescope"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Outlines the kidneys, ureters and bladder with contrast". An IVU injects iodinated contrast that the kidneys concentrate and excrete; serial X-rays then <strong>outline the collecting systems, ureters and bladder</strong>, showing stones, tumours, obstruction and impaired excretion. The nurse checks for contrast allergy and renal function and ensures hydration before and after.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Post-void residual volume is measured by bladder scan or catheterisation.
• B — Identifying an organism requires a urine culture, not imaging.
• D — Direct visualisation of the bladder is cystoscopy.

Reference: Ghana STG — haematuria investigation; radiology patient-preparation guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: match the investigation to what it visualises — contrast X-ray shows structure and drainage; a scope shows the lining; a culture grows organisms.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0075$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Which entry best documents a patient's emotional response to admission to the intensive care unit?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Patient appears depressed and rather anxious about the operation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rang the bell 14 times an hour, begged her to stay, and wept"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Patient cannot manage activities of daily living without help"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Family live far away and can visit only once a week at present"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Rang the bell 14 times an hour, begged her to stay, and wept". Good documentation is <strong>objective, specific and observable</strong>: what the patient did and said, how often, and when. Behaviours such as repeated call-bell use, pleading and crying let any reader judge the emotional state without the nurse's interpretation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Family visiting is social context, not the patient's emotional response.
• C — Dependence in daily living describes physical function, not emotional adjustment.
• D — 'Appears depressed and anxious' is an interpretation with no observable evidence; it cannot be verified.

Reference: NMC Ghana — standards for record keeping; Ghana Health Service documentation policy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in documentation questions choose the entry a stranger could verify — behaviours and quotes beat adjectives.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0056$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A distressed client's **respirations are 42/min with pH 7.5** (hyperventilation). Which **initial** nurse action fits?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start an intravenous infusion of dextrose and saline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer oxygen at a high flow rate through a mask"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Coach slow controlled breathing and stay with her"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Have her breathe repeatedly into a paper bag for relief"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Coach slow controlled breathing and stay with her". <strong>Coached slow breathing corrects alkalosis without the bag's dangers</strong> — paper-bag rebreathing is abandoned (it risks hypoxia and masks real pathology), while calm presence with paced breaths restores CO₂ safely. IV fluids and oxygen treat problems hyperventilation does not bring.

Why the other options fail:
• A — She is over-breathing, not hypoxic — oxygen is unneeded.
• B — Paper bags are obsolete and potentially harmful — never used.
• D — IV fluids address no part of hyperventilation.

Reference: Ghana — hyperventilation; breathing coaching, paper bag abandoned$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: blown-off CO₂ breathes back slowly — coaching, never bags.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0735$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A violent, acutely psychotic man has just been placed in limb restraints on the psychiatric emergency unit. Which nursing action is **most important** immediately afterwards?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Check each restraint and that circulation beyond it is intact"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Draw up a dose of PRN antipsychotic in case it is needed later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Read the hospital policy on how long restraints may be used for"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Plan how his meals and fluids will be given while he is restrained"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Check each restraint and that circulation beyond it is intact". A restrained patient cannot protect himself, so the nurse's first duty is <strong>physiological safety</strong>: restraints must be snug enough to hold but not impair <strong>circulation or nerve function</strong>, and the patient's position must allow breathing. Checks are repeated at least every 15–30 minutes and documented with his behaviour and the clinical justification.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Nutrition and hydration are attended to once immediate safety is confirmed.
• C — Staff should know the policy before restraining; reading it afterwards is not the priority.
• D — Medication may be part of the plan, but preparing it does not make the patient safe now.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Act 2012 — use of restraint; Ghana Health Service patient safety policy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: after any restrictive intervention, assess the patient first — circulation and breathing before paperwork or planning.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0126$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with suspected bacterial meningitis deteriorates. Why does this require immediate action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It cannot be passed from the patient to anyone else"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It usually resolves without any treatment at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It affects only the hearing and the sense of balance"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It can progress rapidly to sepsis and raised ICP"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$Bacterial meningitis can move from headache and neck stiffness to meningococcal sepsis, shock and raised intracranial pressure within hours, so antibiotics are given within an hour of the suspicion and do not wait for the lumbar puncture. Droplet precautions are needed, dexamethasone may be prescribed, and the nurse watches consciousness, pupils, blood pressure and sodium, because SIADH is common. Option B is false and dangerous. Option C understates the harm, since deafness is only one possible sequela. Option A is wrong, because close contacts may need prophylaxis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Meningitis kills by sepsis and raised pressure. Antibiotics within the hour.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0037$q$;

