-- Batch 70: NG-LTC read and two duplicates rewritten.
--
-- Batch 70 — NG-LTC read in full (50 items, keys a13 b13 c12 d12), two rewrites.
-- NG-LTC-1811  insulin during acute illness, 0.42 vs NG-CLN-2969
--              -> why a subcutaneous infusion is used when the mouth fails (key a).
-- NG-LTC-1803  position in acute LVF, 0.40 vs NG-ADV-0176
--              -> what an ejection fraction of 30 per cent means (key d).
-- Key letters unchanged; neither replacement reaches 0.30 against any item; parity 1.03 and 1.12.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A dying patient can no longer swallow, and her pain and vomiting are not controlled. What is a subcutaneous infusion used for?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To keep symptoms controlled steadily when she cannot swallow"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To give her the fluids she can no longer take by mouth each day"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To replace her tablets at exactly the same doses and the same times"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To keep a vein open in case an emergency drug is needed for her"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "To keep symptoms controlled steadily when she cannot swallow". A subcutaneous infusion — the syringe driver — delivers a continuous dose of an opioid with an antiemetic, and if needed a sedative, over twenty-four hours from a single site. It is chosen when the <strong>oral route has failed</strong>: the patient cannot swallow, is vomiting, has a bowel obstruction, or is too drowsy for tablets. A steady level avoids the peaks and troughs of four-hourly injections, and the combination is reviewed and changed as the symptoms change.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Hydration at the end of life is a separate decision, and a syringe driver is not a drip.
• C — Converted doses differ from the oral ones, so the same dose is not simply transferred.
• D — The site is subcutaneous, not intravenous, and it is not kept open for emergencies.

Reference: Ghana STG — palliative care; WHO guidelines on persisting pain in adults$q$,
  strategy = $q$When the mouth fails, the driver takes over: a steady level from one site, reviewed daily.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LTC-1811$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with heart failure asks what it means that her ejection fraction is 30 per cent. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That a third of the muscle of her heart has stopped working for good"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That her heart is beating about thirty per cent faster than it should"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That her body needs thirty per cent more blood than her heart supplies"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That each beat empties about thirty per cent of her ventricle's blood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "That each beat empties about thirty per cent of her ventricle's blood". The ejection fraction is the <strong>proportion of the blood in the ventricle that is pumped out with each contraction</strong>. A normal ventricle empties more than half of what it holds; at thirty per cent the pump is weak, which is why she is breathless on exertion and why her legs swell. It is a measure of how well the heart is pumping, and it guides which medicines she is offered — not a measure of how much muscle has died.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The figure describes output per beat, not the amount of dead muscle.
• B — It says nothing about the rate; a heart can be fast or slow at any ejection fraction.
• C — It is a fraction of what the ventricle holds, not a shortfall in the body's demand.

Reference: Ghana STG — cardiovascular disease; Ghana nursing curriculum$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ejection fraction is how much of the tank leaves with each stroke. Thirty per cent means a weak pump.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LTC-1803$q$;

-- Verify: expect 2
COMMIT;

