-- Batch 71: answer cue, tranche six.
--
-- Batch 71 — answer-length cue, tranche six (10 items, 1.67 to 1.69 before).
-- Keyed option cut back to the statement it makes, the explanation left in the rationale,
-- distractors extended so none is a bare phrase beside a full sentence.
-- No key letter changed: CLINICAL-0021 a, NCX-0186 d, NCX-0163 a, NCX-0101 c, NCX-0348 d,
-- BUS-0719 a, NCX-0001 b, MAN-0849 b, NCX-0193 b, EMG-0073 b.
-- NCX-0124 is left alone: its four options are ages, and numeric options are exempt.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Which set of findings suggests a fluid-volume deficit?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dizziness on standing with poor skin turgor and dark urine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A bounding pulse with raised neck veins and swollen ankles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bibasal crackles with a rapid gain in weight over two days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A full, slow pulse with warm dry skin and flushed cheeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Volume deficit shows as a dry, empty circulation: postural dizziness and tachycardia, dry mucous membranes, poor skin turgor, low jugular venous pressure, oliguria with concentrated urine and weight loss. Options B, C and D all describe volume overload, where the circulation is full: a bounding pulse, raised neck veins, crackles and rapid weight gain. Reading the direction of the findings before choosing is what makes these items straightforward.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dry and empty means deficit. Wet and full means overload.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0021$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 34-year-old woman is referred with irritable bowel syndrome. Which pattern would the nurse **expect** in her history?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bloody stools with cramping pain about an hour after eating a meal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ten to twelve loose stools a day for several months, with weight loss"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Vomiting with severe distension and absolute constipation for days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Alternating diarrhoea and constipation, bloating, pain eased by stool"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Alternating diarrhoea and constipation, bloating, pain eased by stool". Irritable bowel syndrome is a <strong>functional disorder of gut motility</strong> without inflammation: abdominal pain related to defecation, a change in stool frequency or form, bloating and mucus, often worse with stress and certain foods. <strong>Blood, weight loss, fever and night symptoms are red flags</strong> that point to another diagnosis.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Vomiting and distension suggest obstruction.
• B — Rectal bleeding is never explained by irritable bowel syndrome and must be investigated.
• C — Frequent diarrhoea with weight loss suggests inflammatory bowel disease, infection or malabsorption.

Reference: Ghana STG — irritable bowel syndrome; Rome IV criteria$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: functional disorders change how the bowel behaves but leave no blood and no weight loss — those red flags eliminate three options.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0186$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman who survived a serious road traffic accident six months ago has nightmares, avoids travelling by road, startles easily and says she feels 'numb'. Which response by the nurse is **most helpful** at the first assessment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "These are recognised reactions to trauma and treatment helps"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Six months is long enough; most people are over it by now"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Try not to think about it and keep yourself very busy instead"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start travelling by road again today or the fear will grow"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "These are recognised reactions to trauma and treatment helps". The symptoms meet the pattern of <strong>post-traumatic stress disorder</strong>: re-experiencing, avoidance, hyperarousal and emotional numbing lasting beyond a month. <strong>Naming the reaction as recognised and treatable</strong> reduces shame, and an open invitation to explore the impact builds the relationship needed for trauma-focused therapy referral.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Minimising her experience is judgemental and inaccurate; PTSD often persists for years untreated.
• C — Suppression and avoidance maintain PTSD.
• D — Exposure is part of treatment but is graded and planned within therapy, not imposed abruptly.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Authority — trauma and PTSD; WHO mhGAP module on conditions related to stress$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: normalise without minimising, name the condition as treatable, and invite the story — the therapeutic first response to trauma.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0163$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 19-year-old university student with anorexia nervosa is referred by the nurse to an assertiveness-skills group as part of her care plan. The nurse understands that this referral addresses which **underlying** problem?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of contact with reality and fixed false beliefs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Uncontrolled aggressive impulses towards other people"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Poor self-esteem and a weak sense of personal identity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Impaired short-term memory and concentration at school"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Poor self-esteem and a weak sense of personal identity". People with eating disorders typically have <strong>low self-worth and define themselves through others' approval</strong>, so they struggle to refuse requests, voice needs or accept praise. Assertiveness training — saying no, setting limits, giving and receiving feedback — <strong>builds a sense of self</strong> that does not depend on weight or shape.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Memory is not the target of assertiveness training.
• B — Anger is usually suppressed and turned inward in anorexia; aggression is not the presenting problem.
• D — Anorexia distorts body image but does not involve psychosis; reality testing is intact.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Authority — eating disorders; WHO mhGAP$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: ask what an intervention is designed to change — assertiveness skills change how a person values and asserts herself.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0101$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse teaches a 40-year-old man with a **flaccid (lower motor neuron) bladder** to perform intermittent self-catheterisation. Which instruction is **correct**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Buy a new sterile catheter for every single pass, without exception"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Catheterise only once every twenty-four hours, at bedtime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict all fluids so that catheterisation is needed less often"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Try to empty the bladder by pressing over it before catheterising"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Try to empty the bladder by pressing over it before catheterising". <strong>Bearing down (Valsalva) or pressing over the bladder (Credé) before the pass empties what the flaccid bladder can</strong>, reducing residual urine and infection. Home technique is clean (washed hands, washed reusable or single-use catheter), done every 4–6 hours, with generous fluids to flush bacteria — restriction concentrates urine and feeds infection.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Fluid restriction thickens urine and invites stones and sepsis; fluids are encouraged.
• B — Once-daily drainage guarantees over-distension and infection — 4–6 hourly is the standard.
• C — Home self-catheterisation uses clean technique; hospital-style sterility for every pass is unnecessary.

Reference: Fundamentals of nursing — intermittent self-catheterisation teaching$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: squeeze first, catheterise clean, drink freely — empty often and dilute always.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0348$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian nurse calculates that with income of GH₵ 12,000 a month she exactly covers her GH₵ 12,000 of costs. What has she identified?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The break-even point, where income equals costs exactly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A profitable month, because income has reached GH₵ 12,000"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A cash-flow crisis, because no surplus has been generated"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The mark-up, because income and costs have been compared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$Break-even is precisely the point at which total income equals total costs, so the business makes neither profit nor loss. Breaking even is not profit, no surplus does not amount to a cash crisis, and mark-up is a pricing calculation rather than a comparison of total income and total cost.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Income equals costs exactly: that is break-even, not profit.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BUS-0719$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with Addison's disease stopped his hydrocortisone three days ago because of vomiting. Which observation should make the nurse suspect an Addisonian crisis?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Gradual tiredness with muscle weakness over some days"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restlessness, a rapid weak pulse and falling pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Poor appetite with occasional nausea and weight loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Darkening of the skin creases, gums and old scars"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Restlessness, a rapid weak pulse and falling pressure". Acute adrenal insufficiency causes <strong>profound hypotension and hypovolaemic shock</strong> from cortisol and aldosterone deficiency, with tachycardia, confusion, severe vomiting, hypoglycaemia and hyperkalaemia. It is a medical emergency needing <strong>immediate IV hydrocortisone and fluids</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Anorexia and nausea are everyday symptoms of Addison's disease rather than markers of collapse.
• C — Hyperpigmentation develops slowly from chronic ACTH excess and is a sign of long-standing, not acute, disease.
• D — Fatigue and weakness are chronic features; they do not define the crisis.

Reference: Ghana STG — adrenal insufficiency and adrenal crisis$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: the word crisis means circulation — look for the option describing shock.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian ward manager is told that her budget has two lines: one pays for electricity, food and dressings every month, and the other has bought a new refrigerator that should last ten years. Why must she keep the two apart?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because capital items always cost less than recurrent ones over a year"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Recurrent costs repeat monthly while capital buys assets used for years"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because recurrent spending needs no approval while capital spending does"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because donors fund recurrent costs and government funds capital costs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$Recurrent expenditure recurs each month for running the ward, while capital expenditure buys durable assets that last years; the two are planned, approved and accounted for separately. Neither is inherently cheaper, both ordinarily require approval, and the funding source is not fixed to one or the other as the distractors claim.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Monthly running costs are recurrent; long-lived assets are capital - never mix the two lines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAN-0849$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 19-year-old university student is brought in by friends after taking a 'large handful' of paracetamol tablets four hours ago following a relationship break-up. She now says she feels fine and wants to go home. The nurse's **best** response is:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell her she is being dramatic and to come back if pain develops"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain the liver risk, take a blood level now and arrange assessment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discharge her with a counselling leaflet and an appointment next week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Accept her decision to go home, since she is an adult of sound mind"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Explain the liver risk, take a blood level now and arrange assessment". Paracetamol overdose is <strong>often symptom-free for 24 hours</strong> while hepatic necrosis develops; a <strong>4-hour plasma level</strong> determines the need for <strong>N-acetylcysteine</strong>, which is most effective within 8 hours. Every episode of self-harm also requires a <strong>psychosocial and risk assessment</strong> before discharge, with a safety plan and follow-up.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Discharge without medical treatment or risk assessment could be fatal.
• C — Her capacity to refuse must be assessed in light of the overdose and her mental state, and she needs accurate information to decide.
• D — Belittling a person who has self-harmed increases shame and future risk.

Reference: Ghana STG — paracetamol poisoning; Ghana Mental Health Authority — assessment after self-harm$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: 'feels fine' after paracetamol means nothing — treat the liver on the clock and assess the mind before the door.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0193$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A bus collision brings many casualties to a district hospital at once. Which principle should guide the nurse's triage decisions?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treat the most severely injured first, whatever the likely outcome"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Do the greatest good for the greatest number with what you have"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat casualties strictly in the order in which they arrive"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treat children and pregnant women before all other adults"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Do the greatest good for the greatest number with what you have". Disaster triage reverses the everyday rule. When need exceeds resources, the goal becomes the <strong>greatest good for the greatest number</strong>, so casualties who are salvageable with the resources available are treated first. A patient requiring enormous resource with little prospect of survival is categorised expectant so that several others can be saved.

Why the other options fail:
• A — This is correct in everyday practice but fails in a mass casualty event, where devoting a whole team to one unsalvageable patient costs several preventable deaths.
• C — Order of arrival bears no relation to clinical urgency, and the most severely injured often arrive last.
• D — Age alone is not a triage criterion. Children are triaged by the same physiological principles, using paediatric parameters.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — mass casualty management$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember that disaster triage inverts normal priority. Everyday care treats the sickest first; disaster care treats the most salvageable first. Expect at least one distractor that states the everyday rule.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EMG-0073$q$;

