UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 34 weeks with a known placenta praevia reports painless vaginal bleeding. What should the nurse do first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Perform a gentle vaginal examination now"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a warm bath and start an oxytocin drip"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withhold examination and call the doctor"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask her to walk to the toilet to pass urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Withhold examination and call the doctor". <strong>Painless bleeding in late pregnancy with a known placenta praevia warns of major haemorrhage</strong>. A digital examination can dislodge the placenta from the lower segment and provoke catastrophic bleeding, so it is withheld until the placental site is known and theatre and blood are ready. A warm bath and oxytocin have no place here, and oxytocin would be dangerous before delivery is arranged. Walking to the toilet risks a faint and a bleed away from help. The nurse secures intravenous access, records observations, keeps her resting and calls the doctor.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A vaginal examination can start a catastrophic bleed.
• B — Oxytocin is for labour, not for a praevia bleed.
• D — Walking risks a faint and a haemorrhage away from help.

Reference: Ghana STG — antepartum haemorrhage; placenta praevia$q$,
  strategy = $q$In late pregnancy, painless bleeding is placenta praevia until proved otherwise, and the examining finger is what kills.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0219$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has a metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap. Which cause fits this picture?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lactic acidosis from prolonged septic shock"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Diabetic ketoacidosis with accumulating ketones"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prolonged diarrhoea with loss of bicarbonate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advanced renal failure with retained acids"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Prolonged diarrhoea with loss of bicarbonate". <strong>A normal anion gap means bicarbonate was lost, not that an acid was added</strong> — in diarrhoea, bicarbonate-rich fluid leaves the gut and the kidney retains chloride, so the gap stays normal while the bicarbonate falls. Ketoacidosis, lactic acidosis and uraemic acidosis all add unmeasured acid anions, which widen the gap. Reading the gap beside the bicarbonate is what separates the two families of cause and it changes treatment: bicarbonate loss is replaced with fluid and, if severe, bicarbonate, while the added-acid causes are treated by correcting the underlying process.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Lactate is an unmeasured anion, so the gap widens.
• B — Ketoacidosis adds ketone anions and widens the gap.
• D — Retained acids of renal failure also widen the gap.

Reference: Ghana STG — acid–base disorders; anion gap$q$,
  strategy = $q$Read the gap with the bicarbonate: a normal gap means bicarbonate was lost, a wide gap means an acid was added.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-1029$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with myeloma has a serum calcium of 3.4 mmol/L. Which ECG change should the nurse expect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Prominent U waves with a long QT interval"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tall, peaked T waves with a widened QRS"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dropped beats with a prolonged PR interval"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A shortened QT interval with a flat T wave"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A shortened QT interval with a flat T wave". <strong>Calcium shortens the plateau of the ventricular action potential</strong>, so the QT interval shortens; as the level climbs further the T wave flattens and the rhythm can degenerate. Tall peaked T waves with a widening QRS belong to hyperkalaemia, and that is the pair most often confused at the bedside. Prominent U waves with a long QT belong to hypokalaemia. Dropped beats with a prolonged PR interval suggest heart block or drug effect, not calcium.

Why the other options fail:
• A — U waves with a long QT indicate hypokalaemia.
• B — Peaked T waves with a wide QRS are the potassium picture.
• C — Prolonged PR and dropped beats suggest heart block.

Reference: Ghana STG — hypercalcaemia; ECG changes$q$,
  strategy = $q$Learn the two pictures as a pair: potassium peaks the T wave, calcium shortens the QT.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-1060$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man started on simvastatin asks why he must avoid grapefruit juice.$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It blocks the enzyme that clears the statin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It adds potassium, which makes the statin stronger"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It binds the statin in the gut so less is absorbed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It reduces the cholesterol the statin works on"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "It blocks the enzyme that clears the statin". <strong>Grapefruit juice inhibits intestinal CYP3A4</strong>, the enzyme that breaks simvastatin down, so more of the drug reaches the circulation and the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis rises. It is not a binding interaction in the gut — that is the mechanism for tetracyclines and calcium. Grapefruit adds no potassium and does not change the amount of cholesterol the drug has to work on: the interaction is on the drug's disposal, not on its target. Advise water or another juice, or a statin handled by a different route such as pravastatin.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The interaction is metabolic, not electrolyte.
• C — Binding in the gut is the tetracycline–calcium mechanism.
• D — Grapefruit does not alter the cholesterol load.

Reference: Ghana STG — lipid-lowering drugs; food interactions$q$,
  strategy = $q$Drug–nutrient interactions are about metabolism or absorption. Grapefruit blocks metabolism; calcium and tea block absorption.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-1065$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a vaginal examination in labour the midwife feels the umbilical cord lying ahead of the presenting part. What should she do first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask her to push hard with the next contraction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Call for help and elevate the presenting part"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Push the cord back above the presenting part"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave her alone to arrange an urgent caesarean"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Call for help and elevate the presenting part". <strong>This is cord prolapse, and the cord is being compressed between the presenting part and the pelvis</strong>, cutting the baby's oxygen supply. The midwife calls for help, lifts the presenting part off the cord with her hand and keeps it there, positions the woman knee-chest or head-down, and prepares for immediate delivery. Pushing the cord back is never done: handling it causes spasm and worsens compression. Asking her to push drives the presenting part further onto the cord, and leaving her alone delays the only measure that relieves it.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Pushing presses the presenting part onto the cord.
• C — Handling the cord provokes spasm and deeper compression.
• D — Help must be summoned before anything can be arranged.

Reference: Ghana STG — cord prolapse; obstetric emergency$q$,
  strategy = $q$In cord prolapse the hand that lifts the presenting part is the treatment: call, lift, then deliver.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-1087$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why does the nurse mark a nasogastric tube at the point where it leaves the nose?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To measure how much of the tube is inside"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To record the tube size on the fluid chart"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To show which nostril the tube went into"}, {"id": "d", "text": "So that slipping out is noticed at once"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "So that slipping out is noticed at once". The mark is a reference point: if more tube is visible outside the nose than when it was secured, the tip has moved upwards and may now sit in the oesophagus or above it, so feeding must stop until position is rechecked. It is not a measure of how much tube is inside — the insertion length is recorded separately at the time of insertion. The tube size is written on the care plan, not on the tube, and the nostril used is recorded in the notes. Marking alone does not confirm position: it detects a change, which is why it is checked before every feed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$A mark shows change, not position. Compare it with the recorded length before every feed and stop if it has moved.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-0237$q$;

