UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An enteral feeding tube stops draining and the feed will not run. What should the nurse try first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pass a stiff guide wire down the tube to clear it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Raise the suction pressure to pull the block out"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Flush gently with warm water and a syringe"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instil cold water and clamp the tube for an hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Flush gently with warm water and a syringe". CORRECT (C) — "Flush gently with warm water and a syringe". A blocked tube is first cleared with a gentle warm water flush, using a piston syringe in a push-pull motion and letting the water soften the feed or the crushed tablet that is plugging it; this clears most blocks without damaging the tube. Cold water softens feed poorly and clamping for an hour leaves the patient unfed. A guide wire can perforate the tube or the gut and is never passed by a nurse. Raising suction does not clear tube feedings, and strong suction pulls mucosa into the ports and makes the block worse.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A guide wire can perforate the tube or the gut and is not a nursing action.
• B — Suction pulls mucosa into the ports and worsens the block.
• D — Cold water softens feed poorly and an hour of clamping delays feeding.

Reference: Ghana STG — enteral feeding; blocked tube$q$,
  strategy = $q$A blocked tube is cleared with warm water and patience, never with a wire or more suction.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0851$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A post-operative order reads 'vital signs every 15 minutes'. Which observations should the nurse record at each check?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respirations only, as they rise before anything else"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Temperature and pulse, because fever is the first sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pulse and blood pressure, since these change fastest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Respirations only, as they rise before anything else". CORRECT (A) — "Temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure". The phrase 'vital signs' means the full set — temperature, pulse, respirations and blood pressure — and an order written that way is not an instruction to drop one of them. Pulse and blood pressure alone would miss a rising temperature or a falling respiratory rate, and both are early warnings after anaesthesia and surgery. Temperature and pulse alone miss hypotension, which is the finding that most often changes management. Respirations alone are the least informative of the four taken by themselves, though a rising rate is often the earliest sign of deterioration.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Omitting blood pressure hides the hypotension that changes management.
• C — Does not address the priority identified in the stem.
• D — Omitting temperature and respirations hides early sepsis and opioid effect.

Reference: Ghana STG — postoperative monitoring; vital signs$q$,
  strategy = $q$Read the order literally: vital signs are four observations, not the convenient two.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0941$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A breastfeeding mother has **burning nipple pain** and her baby has **white patches on the tongue and cheeks** that **do not wipe off**. What should the nurse advise?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treat mother and baby together, because thrush passes both ways"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop breastfeeding for a week until the patches have gone completely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wipe the white patches off with warm water before each feed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treat only the baby's mouth, since the mother has no rash to treat"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Treat mother and baby together, because thrush passes both ways". White patches that stay on the mucosa when wiped are oral candida, and the burning nipple pain is the same yeast on the breast; each feeds the other, so both are treated at the same time. The baby's mouth and the mother's nipples receive a topical antifungal such as nystatin or miconazole, feeding continues, and teats, dummies and any expressed milk equipment are sterilised. Treating the baby alone leaves the maternal reservoir and the infection returns at the next feed. Stopping breastfeeding is unnecessary and risks engorgement and a falling supply, and the patches cannot be wiped away because they are attached to the mucosa, not milk curd.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Stopping breastfeeding risks engorgement and a falling supply.
• C — The patches are attached to the mucosa, not curd, and cannot be wiped off.
• D — The baby's mouth alone leaves the maternal reservoir, so it returns.

Reference: Ghana — postnatal care; maternal and infant candidiasis$q$,
  strategy = $q$Look for the pair of sites the same organism is using.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0976$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$At what age does an infant usually begin to show stranger anxiety?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "About 2 to 3 months old, as vision sharpens"}, {"id": "b", "text": "About 10 to 12 months old, once she is walking"}, {"id": "c", "text": "About 4 to 5 months old, as feeding settles"}, {"id": "d", "text": "About 6 to 8 months, with object permanence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "About 6 to 8 months, with object permanence". CORRECT (D) — "About 6 to 8 months, with object permanence". Stranger anxiety appears at about six to eight months and runs alongside the growth of object permanence: the infant now holds a mental image of the familiar carer and can tell a stranger from it, so an unfamiliar face is alarming. Two to three months is too early — social smiling is the milestone then, and a baby of that age does not yet discriminate. Four to five months is still before the usual onset. Ten to twelve months is when the reaction is at its height, not when it begins, and it fades during the second year.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Two to three months is the age of social smiling, not of discrimination.
• B — Ten to twelve months is the peak of the reaction, not its beginning.
• C — Four to five months is before the usual onset of stranger anxiety.

Reference: Ghana STG — child development milestones$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stranger anxiety arrives with object permanence: the baby can now tell a familiar face from an unfamiliar one.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-1104$q$;

