UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with **no immune defences of his own** is in **protective (reverse) isolation**. Which measure is **required** of everyone who enters?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wearing gloves for the whole shift without changing them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Thorough handwashing before any contact with him"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keeping the door closed so that the room stays warm"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Admitting visitors freely so that his spirits stay high"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Thorough handwashing before any contact with him". Hands carry the organisms a patient with no defences cannot resist, so washing before any contact is the measure that must happen every time, with gown, gloves and mask added when the task calls for them. Visitors are limited and screened rather than admitted freely, and a closed door belongs to airborne precautions, not to protective isolation. Gloves worn for a whole shift become a second skin that carries organisms from one patient and one surface to the next, and they never replace handwashing.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Gloves worn all shift carry organisms instead of blocking them.
• C — A closed door belongs to airborne precautions, not reverse isolation.
• D — Visitors are screened and limited in protective isolation, not free.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — protective isolation; hand hygiene$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask which measure must happen every time, not which barriers exist.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0908$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An older woman who has fallen twice at home walks about indoors in **loose slippers**. Which single change is **most likely** to prevent another fall?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Buying her a walking stick to use when outdoors"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Telling her to stay indoors after dark every evening"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fitting her with firm shoes with nonskid soles"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Asking her to hold on to the wall as she walks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Fitting her with firm shoes with nonskid soles". The cue is the footwear: a loose slipper slides on the foot and on smooth floors, so a shoe that stays on and grips the floor removes the hazard that is actually present. Holding the wall depends on a wall being within reach and does not stop her foot sliding inside the slipper. A stick used only outdoors leaves every indoor walk unprotected. Keeping her indoors after dark restricts her life without touching the slippery footwear, and it adds the harms of inactivity and isolation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A stick used outdoors leaves the indoor walking unprotected.
• B — Staying in after dark restricts her without removing the hazard.
• D — Wall-holding does not stop the foot sliding inside a loose slipper.

Reference: Gerontological nursing — falls; footwear$q$,
  strategy = $q$Find the hazard the stem has planted, then choose the change that removes it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0971$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A **feverish adult** who can drink is **dehydrated** and asks what he should drink between meals. Which is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cola"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Broth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Water"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Water". For ordinary fluid lost with fever, plain water is the best of these four: it restores volume without adding salt, sugar or caffeine. Broth carries a heavy salt load that is not wanted in simple fever dehydration, tea and cola both contain caffeine, which encourages further urine loss, and cola adds sugar on top. Diarrhoeal dehydration is a different case, where oral rehydration solution with its measured sodium and glucose is the correct fluid and plain water alone is not enough.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Cola adds sugar and caffeine on top of the fluid.
• B — Broth adds a salt load that simple fever dehydration does not need.
• D — Tea contains caffeine, which increases urine loss.

Reference: Fundamentals of nursing — oral fluids; fever$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask which fluid replaces water without adding salt, sugar or a diuretic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0999$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch77.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 77: Sickle cell fever cluster: three of four rewritten.
--
-- Batch 77 — fever-in-sickle-cell, four items with one answer.
--   0912  "temperature 38.9 in a child with SCD" -> hand-foot syndrome
--         (dactylitis) in an infant (key c)
--   1956  "temperature 38.6 in a five-year-old with SCD" -> priapism in
--         a teenager, a urological emergency (key d)
--   0016  "temperature 38.6 in a young adult with SCD" -> aplastic crisis
--         after parvovirus (key d)
--   CHI-3022 stays as the single fever item, because it carries the full
--   answer (cultures and parenteral antibiotics without waiting).
--   A first attempt at 0912 (splenic sequestration) was scrapped before it
--   was written: NG-CPD-1957 already asks exactly that.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A **nine-month-old with sickle cell disease** has **swollen, painful hands and feet** and screams when they are touched. What is this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Juvenile arthritis, which needs long-term anti-inflammatory treatment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A skin infection, which needs a course of antibiotics at once"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sickled cells blocking the small bones of the hands and feet"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A vitamin deficiency, which needs supplements for several months"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Sickled cells blocking the small bones of the hands and feet". This is dactylitis, or hand-foot syndrome, the painful swelling of the hands and feet that is often the first sign of sickle cell disease in a baby between about six and eighteen months. Sickled cells obstruct the small vessels in the bones, so the child holds the hands and feet still and cries when they are handled. It is treated with hydration, analgesia and warmth, and it settles over days to weeks without antibiotics or surgery. Juvenile arthritis develops over months with morning stiffness rather than overnight swelling in an infant, a skin infection shows redness, warmth and a source, and no vitamin deficiency swells both hands and both feet like this.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In an infant with sickle cell disease, symmetrical painful swelling is the disease itself.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IMD-0912$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A **14-year-old boy with sickle cell disease** has had a **painful erection for three hours**. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell him it is a normal teenage change and will settle down"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Advise a warm bath and review him again the following week"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give him an analgesic and send him home to rest quietly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treat it as an emergency and get him reviewed at once"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Treat it as an emergency and get him reviewed at once". Priapism in sickle cell disease is sickling inside the penile vessels, and it is a urological emergency. Beyond about four hours the tissue is at risk of permanent damage and erectile failure, so the boy needs prompt review for analgesia, hydration and the urological treatment that relieves the trapped blood. Reassurance, a warm bath or analgesia alone all spend the safe window while the penis remains engorged. It is also worth telling him that repeated episodes are a reason to be reviewed by the sickle cell team rather than endured quietly.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask how long the organ has been without its circulation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CPD-1956$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A **child with sickle cell disease** develops **sudden severe anaemia** with **very few reticulocytes**, one week after a **viral illness**. What is the likely cause?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Iron deficiency, because growth has used up his iron stores"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A painful crisis, which always lowers the haemoglobin briefly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Splenic pooling of blood, which removes cells from the count"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Parvovirus has stopped the marrow making red cells for now"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Parvovirus has stopped the marrow making red cells for now". A viral illness a week earlier, a sudden drop in haemoglobin and almost no reticulocytes together describe an aplastic crisis. Parvovirus B19 infects the red cell precursors, so the marrow stops producing red cells for about a week to ten days while the existing cells age out; the marrow recovers on its own, but the anaemia in the meantime can be severe enough to need transfusion. Sequestration also drops the haemoglobin but the spleen enlarges and the reticulocyte count rises. A painful crisis does not suppress reticulocytes, and iron deficiency develops slowly with small pale cells rather than overnight.$q$,
  strategy = $q$The reticulocyte count tells you whether the marrow is working.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-USER-0016$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch78.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 78: Febrile neutropenia cluster: three of four rewritten.
--
-- Batch 78 — febrile neutropenia, four items with one answer.
--   0195  "neutrophils 0.4 and temperature 38.5" -> mouth care in
--         mucositis (key c)
--   0350  the same case in a teaching hospital -> vincristine must never
--         be given intrathecally (key b)
--   1515  "temperature 38.5 on chemotherapy" -> anthracycline
--         cardiotoxicity from doxorubicin (key d)
--   CPD-1972 stays as the single febrile-neutropenia item, because it
--   carries the timing ("within the hour").
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient on chemotherapy has **painful mouth ulcers** and has stopped eating. Which action is **correct**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use a strong alcohol-based mouthwash four times a day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop all oral fluids until the ulcers have healed completely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rinse with salt or soda water and give soft, cool foods"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Scrub the ulcers with a toothbrush to keep them clean"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Rinse with salt or soda water and give soft, cool foods". Chemotherapy mucositis is treated by keeping the mouth clean and comfortable: frequent rinses with saline or bicarbonate of soda, soft cool food, adequate analgesia and a soft brush. Strong alcohol-based mouthwashes dry and sting the mucosa, scrubbing ulcers tears them and invites bleeding, and stopping oral fluids removes the cleansing and hydration the mouth needs. Mouth care is done gently and often, and the nurse inspects the mouth each shift because ulcers can become the entry point for the infection this patient cannot fight.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Choose the measure that cleans and soothes without injuring.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LAB-0195$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A doctor asks a nurse to give **vincristine by lumbar puncture**. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give it slowly, diluted in saline, as the doctor has asked"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refuse, because vincristine is fatal if given intrathecally"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Check the dose and give half of it by lumbar puncture"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask a second nurse to witness it and then give the injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Refuse, because vincristine is fatal if given intrathecally". Vincristine is for intravenous use only. Given into the cerebrospinal fluid it causes ascending paralysis and death, and there is no antidote, so a request to give it by lumbar puncture must be refused and escalated at once. Diluting it, halving the dose or finding a witness all carry out the order rather than stopping it. The same error has killed patients where the drug, the route and the intrathecal syringe were not separated, which is why vincristine is now handled apart from lumbar puncture trays.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Some orders are refused, not adjusted.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-SUR-0350$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman **midway through doxorubicin** reports **breathlessness and swollen ankles**. What should the nurse consider?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "That the swelling is from the salt in her chemotherapy fluids"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That breathlessness is expected in anyone having chemotherapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That she has developed a chest infection from the treatment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That the drug may be harming her heart and needs review"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "That the drug may be harming her heart and needs review". Doxorubicin is an anthracycline, and its dose-limiting harm is to the heart muscle. Breathlessness, swollen ankles, a new cough and fatigue partway through treatment point to anthracycline cardiotoxicity, so the nurse reports it before the next dose and asks for cardiac assessment, with an echocardiogram or a measurement of ejection fraction as ordered. Fluid from the infusion is far too small to explain heart failure, breathlessness is not an expected effect of chemotherapy in general, and an infection would usually bring fever and a focal chest picture rather than symmetrical oedema.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask which organ this drug is known to damage at its dose limit.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CVM-1515$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch79.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 79: TB sputum cluster: three of four rewritten.
--
-- Batch 79 — TB sputum collection, four items with one answer.
--   0194  "how to produce a good sputum specimen" -> gastric aspirate
--         in a child too young to expectorate (key a)
--   0440  the same instruction again -> how a sputum specimen is
--         stored and sent, with the contrast against CSF spelled out
--         (key a)
--   2503  the fourth copy of "which specimen is valid" -> sputum
--         induction with hypertonic saline (key d)
--   CLP-2799 stays as the collection item, because it alone names the
--   well-ventilated place as well as the early-morning deep cough.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A **three-year-old** cannot cough up sputum for tuberculosis testing. Which specimen will the nurse expect to collect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An early-morning gastric aspirate before the child gets up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Saliva collected while the child is awake and playing happily"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A quick throat swab with a sterile cotton applicator"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A sample of vomit collected after the child's breakfast"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "An early-morning gastric aspirate before the child gets up". Young children swallow what they cough up and cannot produce a sputum specimen, so the swallowed secretions are collected from the stomach. The aspirate is taken early in the morning before the child sits up, eats or takes any medicine, because overnight the swallowed bacilli have collected there and the stomach is still full of them. It is repeated on three consecutive mornings and the specimen is sent to the laboratory at once, since gastric acid destroys the organisms with time. Saliva and throat swabs contain no lower-airway secretions and will not find tuberculosis in the lungs, and vomit after a meal is diluted and contaminated by food.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask where a small child's coughed-up sputum actually ends up.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LAB-0194$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A sputum specimen for tuberculosis is collected at a rural clinic and must travel to the laboratory. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Refrigerate it if delayed, and send it sealed that day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep it on the ward bench until the next clinic day comes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Freeze it overnight so nothing inside the specimen is lost"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Add formalin to the pot so that it cannot leak in transit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Refrigerate it if delayed, and send it sealed that day". A sputum specimen is put in a clean, leak-proof container, sealed inside a second bag, and sent to the laboratory on the day it is collected. If a delay cannot be avoided it is kept in the refrigerator at about two to eight degrees, never in the freezer and never on a warm bench, because heat lets other organisms overgrow the specimen and the acid in it begins to digest the bacilli. Sputum is not cerebrospinal fluid: cerebrospinal fluid must go to the laboratory at once and is never refrigerated, whereas sputum survives a short refrigerated delay. Formalin is a fixative, not a preservative here: it kills the organisms and makes culture impossible even when the smear can still be read, and freezing damages the cells the laboratory needs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Handle a specimen the way the laboratory needs to receive it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-APR-0440$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An adult with a **dry cough** cannot produce any sputum. Which method will obtain a specimen?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take a nasal swab instead, since the disease is in the lungs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask him to spit repeatedly until the pot is half full"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send him home and try again another morning without help"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nebulise hypertonic saline, then ask for a deep cough"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Nebulise hypertonic saline, then ask for a deep cough". Sputum induction is used when a patient cannot cough up a specimen: sterile hypertonic saline, usually three per cent, is nebulised for about ten to fifteen minutes, which draws fluid into the airways and provokes a deep productive cough. The specimen is then collected in the usual way and sent to the laboratory. Induction generates aerosols, so it is done in a well-ventilated room with airborne precautions and staff wearing the right protection. A nasal swab does not sample the lower airway, repeated spitting produces saliva, and sending the patient away simply delays a diagnosis that the clinic can obtain in one visit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$When the patient cannot produce the specimen, make the airway produce it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2503$q$;

-- Verify: expect 3
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch80.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 80: TB drug clusters: orange urine, pyridoxine, interrupted treatment.
--
-- Batch 80 — the TB drug clusters.
--   0373  alarmed by orange urine -> pyrazinamide and painful joints
--         from raised uric acid (key a)
--   1211  "warn about a harmless rifampicin effect" -> treatment
--         interrupted for three weeks and what that means (key a)
--   0897  pyridoxine, the third copy -> what a treatment supporter
--         actually does (key b)
--   2442  pyridoxine again -> when antiretroviral treatment starts in
--         TB/HIV co-infection (key c)
--   DRC-2441 (which drug turns the urine orange), PHARM-0018 (fullest
--   rifampicin teaching) and PHM-0374 (pyridoxine) are kept.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient **two weeks into tuberculosis treatment** reports **painful, swollen joints**. Which drug is the likely cause?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pyrazinamide, which raises the blood uric acid"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rifampicin, which damages the joints directly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Isoniazid, which washes calcium out of bone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ethambutol, which inflames the small joints and hands"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Pyrazinamide, which raises the blood uric acid". Pyrazinamide commonly raises the serum uric acid, and that produces arthralgia and swollen, tender joints in the first weeks of treatment. The pain is usually managed with a simple analgesic such as paracetamol and settles as the course continues; the nurse reports it so the prescriber can decide whether anything more is needed, but it is not a reason to stop treatment. Rifampicin does not damage joints, isoniazid does not deplete calcium from bone in this way, and ethambutol's serious effect is on the eyes rather than the joints.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Match the symptom to the drug whose known effect it is.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHM-0373$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient **stops his tuberculosis medicines for three weeks**, then returns feeling well. What should the nurse expect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "His treatment must be reviewed and probably restarted"}, {"id": "b", "text": "He can simply carry on from the tablets he has left in the packet"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He no longer needs any treatment, because he feels completely well"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He needs only half the dose for the remaining months of the course"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "His treatment must be reviewed and probably restarted". A break of several weeks means the treatment has to be reviewed before it continues. Interrupted courses let the surviving bacilli multiply and select for resistance, so the regimen is usually restarted rather than resumed where it stopped, and the prescriber decides this after examining the patient and the sputum results. Continuing with the leftover tablets assumes nothing has changed, and stopping because he feels well is exactly the mistake that produces relapse and drug-resistant disease. Reducing the dose would give the organism a low concentration to adapt to, which is the surest way to breed resistance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Weigh a treatment gap by what it does to the bacilli, not by how the patient feels.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PKD-1211$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In Ghana a **treatment supporter** is named for a patient starting tuberculosis treatment. What is that person's role?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To pay for the medicines whenever a supply runs out"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To see that the medicines are swallowed every day"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To sign the patient's certificate when the course ends"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To give the injections when the patient cannot attend"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "To see that the medicines are swallowed every day". Treatment is supervised so that every dose is actually swallowed. A member of the family, a community volunteer or a health worker agrees to watch the patient take each dose and to record it, which is what keeps the course continuous and stops the irregular taking that breeds resistance. The supporter is not a payer, does not certify the outcome, and does not give injections; their whole task is witnessing the daily dose and encouraging the patient to keep attending.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask what the named person actually watches happen.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IMD-0897$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has **tuberculosis and HIV**. When should antiretroviral treatment be started?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Only after the full tuberculosis course has been completed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only if the CD4 count falls below a particular number"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Within a few weeks of starting the tuberculosis treatment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Before the tuberculosis treatment, so that it is not delayed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Within a few weeks of starting the tuberculosis treatment". Antiretroviral treatment is started within weeks of beginning tuberculosis treatment, not months later. Early treatment lowers mortality and cuts the risk of further opportunistic infection, and the two regimens are adjusted to work together, which is why rifampicin's effect on dolutegravir has to be handled. Waiting until the tuberculosis course finishes leaves the patient immune-compromised for months, and tying the start to a particular CD4 threshold delays it for people who need it. Starting before the tuberculosis treatment is usually the wrong order, because the tuberculosis is the immediate danger.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask which of the two conditions is the immediate threat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2442$q$;

-- Verify: expect 4
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch81.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 81: peak flow: technique and interpretation.
--
-- peak flow, asked twice. ADV-0181 gave the technique and MDA-1093 gave
-- the same technique again in a home setting, with the same key, so one
-- of them had to move.
-- 
-- 0181  stays on technique: full breath in, lips sealed, one hard blast.
--       Its two weak distractors were extended so the key is no longer
--       the longest line.
-- 1093  now carries what nothing else in 5,510 asked: how a reading is
--       judged. Against the patient's own best, not a predicted value on
--       a printed chart, and not only when he feels wheezy, because the
--       fall comes before the wheeze. Bad-day readings are kept, not
--       binned.
-- 
-- Peak flow was thin, not crowded: two items in the whole bank, so the
-- covering facet is added rather than deleted. No overlap above 0.20.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$How should a nurse instruct a patient to perform a peak expiratory flow measurement?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Breathe out slowly and steadily for as long as the patient possibly can"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take a deep breath in, seal the lips and blow out as hard and fast as possible"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Breathe in and out normally into the device for a full minute, then read the number shown"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take a shallow breath in and blow out gently into the mouthpiece, so that the effort stays the same each time"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Take a deep breath in, seal the lips and blow out as hard and fast as possible". Peak flow measures the <strong>maximum speed of expiration</strong>, so it requires a full inspiration followed by a short, sharp, maximal blast. The patient should be standing or sitting upright, and the best of three attempts is recorded. Poor technique produces falsely low readings that may prompt unnecessary treatment changes.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A slow prolonged breath measures a different parameter entirely and gives a low peak reading.
• C — Tidal breathing does not measure peak flow at all.
• D — A shallow gentle breath produces a falsely low result and may be misread as deterioration.

Reference: Ghana STG — asthma monitoring
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Peak flow measures speed, not volume, so the instruction is short and forceful rather than long and slow. Full breath in, tight seal, one hard blast, and the best of three is the number that counts.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0181$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with asthma has recorded his peak flow every morning for three months. How should he judge each day's reading?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Against the predicted value for his height on the clinic chart, since that is what the chart is printed for"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Against his own best reading, recorded on a day when his asthma was at its best and he felt well"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only when he also feels wheezy, because a low number on a good day does not matter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discard the readings from bad days, since poor effort makes them falsely low and misleading"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Against his own best reading, recorded on a day when his asthma was at its best and he felt well". A peak flow number means nothing on its own. It means something only against the same patient's own best, taken with the same technique on a day when his control was good. That personal best becomes the ceiling he is working against, and every later reading, good day or bad, is judged against it, because a fall from his own best can come a day or two before he feels any wheeze.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A predicted value is a population average for a height; this patient's true best may sit well above or below it, so the chart mislabels him in either direction.
• C — The whole point of a daily record is that the number falls before symptoms appear, so waiting for wheeze throws away the early warning.
• D — Bad-day readings are the most informative ones; a low number is a signal to check technique and reliever use, not a reason to throw the evidence away.

Reference: Ghana STG — asthma monitoring; GINA asthma management
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Compare with the patient's own best, never with a printed predicted value, and act on a fall before symptoms appear.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1093$q$;

-- Verify: expect 2
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch82.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 82: snake bite: one first-aid item, five new facets.
--
-- snake bite was asked six ways, and five of the six were the same
-- question: immobilise, keep calm, transfer, do not tourniquet, cut or
-- ice. EMG-0064 already carries that teaching in its sharpest form - the
-- tourniquet is already on and the nurse has to take it off.
-- 
-- TRA-1849  the 20-minute whole blood clotting test, the bedside test
--           for the coagulopathy viper venom causes
-- KID-3217  a child gets the adult dose, because the snake injects the
--           same venom into both
-- IMD-0932  which findings mean the venom has entered the circulation
-- CLP-2894  an anaphylactoid reaction during the infusion: stop first
-- FND-2654  treat the syndrome, not the species; the dead snake waits
-- 
-- Left alone: EMG-0064 (first aid), EPH-2071 (prevention). Seven
-- snake-bite items now, seven different questions. Antivenom dose,
-- clotting test, reaction and syndrome-led treatment had no coverage at
-- all. Largest stem overlap 0.269.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A farmer bitten on the ankle by a snake now has swelling spreading up the leg. Which bedside test should the nurse do to detect a clotting defect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Send a sample for a full blood count and wait for the platelet count to come back before deciding anything"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Put 2 mL of venous blood in a clean dry glass tube, leave it upright for 20 minutes, then tip it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Time how long the puncture wound goes on oozing once the needle is out, and write that time on the chart"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tie a tourniquet on the arm for five minutes, then look for pin-point bruises below it on the forearm"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Put 2 mL of venous blood in a clean dry glass tube, leave it upright for 20 minutes, then tip it". This is the <strong>20-minute whole blood clotting test</strong>, the bedside test the WHO recommends where laboratories are thin. Blood that has formed a complete clot when the tube is tipped means clotting is intact; blood that is still liquid means the venom has consumed the clotting factors, which is systemic envenoming and an indication for antivenom. It costs one tube and twenty minutes, needs no reagents, and is repeated six-hourly after antivenom until clotting returns.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A platelet count says nothing about the clotting cascade that viper venom destroys, and waiting for it delays antivenom.
• C — Oozing from the puncture is not a measure of clotting; bleeding time is not used to decide antivenom.
• D — The tourniquet test looks for capillary fragility, not for the defibrination this venom causes.

Reference: WHO guidelines for the management of snakebites; Ghana STG — snake bite
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Four tubes of ideas, one tube of blood: the 20WBCT is the answer whenever a snakebite patient needs a clotting answer at the bedside.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1849$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A four-year-old is admitted after a snake bite and antivenom is prescribed. His mother asks whether such a small child will be given a smaller dose. What should the nurse say?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Yes — the dose is worked out from his weight, so it will be much smaller than an adult's"}, {"id": "b", "text": "No — he gets the same dose as an adult, because the snake put in the same amount of venom"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Yes — he gets half an adult dose now, and a second half if the swelling keeps spreading"}, {"id": "d", "text": "No — he gets a larger dose than an adult, because a smaller blood volume concentrates the venom"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "No — he gets the same dose as an adult, because the snake put in the same amount of venom". Antivenom neutralises venom, and the amount injected depends on the snake, not on the size of the child, so the dose at four years is the dose at forty. A weight-based reduction is wrong precisely because it leaves circulating venom unneutralised, and children can deteriorate faster than adults since the same venom load is spread through a smaller body. The one paediatric adjustment is fluid: the antivenom is diluted in a smaller volume so that the infusion does not overload him.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Weight-based reduction under-doses him; the dose follows the venom, not the kilogram.
• C — Halving the dose leaves him under-treated from the first vial, and topping up later delays neutralisation.
• D — A larger dose is not recommended either; the standard dose is repeated only if envenoming persists.

Reference: WHO guidelines for the management of snakebites; Ghana STG — snake bite
$q$,
  strategy = $q$The dose treats the venom, not the patient. Same venom load, same dose, whatever the age — only the diluent volume changes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-KID-3217$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man was bitten on the calf by a snake two hours ago. Which set of findings tells the nurse that venom has entered the circulation and antivenom is needed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bleeding from the gums, bruising away from the bite, and blood that will not clot"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sharp pain at the bite, a swollen calf and two puncture marks where the fangs went in"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A feeling of panic, sweating and a rapid pulse in the first hour after the bite"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Numbness round the bite, a blister at the site and a bruise that fades within a day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Bleeding from the gums, bruising away from the bite, and blood that will not clot". Antivenom is given for <strong>systemic envenoming</strong>, and the signs of it are the ones that show venom has entered the circulation: spontaneous bleeding from the gums, nose or an old wound, bruising that appears away from the bitten limb, and blood that stays liquid. Neurotoxic signs count too — drooping eyelids, difficulty swallowing, weakness — as do a falling blood pressure and passing little urine. None of these needs the snake to be identified first.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Pain, swelling and fang marks are local effects; a painful swollen leg alone does not mean venom has entered the circulation.
• C — Fear and a fast pulse are the ordinary response to being bitten, and mistaking them for envenoming means antivenom given for fright.
• D — A small blister and a fading bruise stay local, and do not amount to systemic envenoming.

Reference: WHO guidelines for the management of snakebites; Ghana STG — snake bite
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Local signs say where the bite is; systemic signs say whether the venom is circulating. Antivenom follows the systemic signs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IMD-0932$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Thirty minutes into a snake antivenom infusion a patient becomes restless and wheezes, and his blood pressure falls. What should the nurse do first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Speed the infusion up so the remaining vials are in before the reaction gets worse"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give the rest of the antivenom by intramuscular injection instead of by infusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the infusion, call for help, and prepare adrenaline and intravenous fluid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Note the reaction, carry on the infusion slowly and reassess him in fifteen minutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Stop the infusion, call for help, and prepare adrenaline and intravenous fluid". Early reactions to antivenom are common and are usually <strong>anaphylactoid</strong>: the antivenom's proteins trigger histamine release directly rather than through IgE, so they can come on within minutes of the first vial even in someone who has never had antivenom before. The first action is to stop the infusion, then treat as for anaphylaxis — adrenaline into the outer thigh, oxygen, intravenous fluid, lying flat. Once the reaction is controlled the antivenom can usually be restarted slowly, because it is the only treatment for the venom and abandoning it leaves him envenomed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Running more antigen in faster deepens the reaction.
• B — Intramuscular antivenom is poorly absorbed and is not a treatment route.
• D — Watching a hypotensive, wheezing patient for fifteen minutes risks cardiac arrest.

Reference: WHO guidelines for the management of snakebites; Ghana STG — snake bite
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stop first, treat as anaphylaxis second, and restart the antivenom slowly once he is stable — the venom still needs its antidote.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2894$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A relative arrives carrying the dead snake in a bag and asks the nurse to look at it before the antivenom is given. What is the best response?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Refuse to give antivenom until the medical officer has identified the snake"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Open the bag and lay the snake out on the bench so that its markings can be seen clearly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send the snake to the laboratory and start antivenom only when the result is back"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Put the bag aside, treat on his signs, and give antivenom if envenoming is present"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Put the bag aside, treat on his signs, and give antivenom if envenoming is present". Treatment is led by the syndrome in front of the nurse — bleeding and non-clotting blood, drooping eyelids and weakness, spreading swelling — and by which snakes are known in the district, not by a species name. The snake is worth keeping for the district's records, but it is handled with a stick or tongs and never with bare hands, because a freshly killed snake can still strike. Antivenom that covers the local species is started on the clinical picture; waiting for an identification that may never come costs the hours in which antivenom works best.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Withholding antivenom until the snake is named leaves him untreated through the hours that matter.
• B — Laying out a dead snake by hand risks a reflex bite, and the markings would not settle the treatment anyway.
• C — No laboratory test identifies the snake, and a result would arrive long after the decision.

Reference: WHO guidelines for the management of snakebites; Ghana STG — snake bite
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Treat the syndrome, not the species. Keep the snake for the records, handle it with tongs, and never let identification delay antivenom.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FND-2654$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch83.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 83: perineal aftercare, shoulder dystocia, three HIT facets.
--
-- two more duplicated pairs and one over-asked cluster.
-- 
-- perineal tear   PNC-1919 already asks the classification; MAT-2317 asked it
--                 again. MAT-2317 now carries the aftercare instead: the
--                 stool softener, because constipation tears a fresh
--                 sphincter repair where a soft stool does not.
-- turtle sign     MAT-2310 already asks the first manoeuvre, so MOD-OBS-014
--                 now asks the one thing the team must never do - fundal
--                 pressure, which drives the shoulder harder into the
--                 symphysis and snaps the plexus.
-- heparin         four items all asked "platelets halved on day 7, what now".
--                 MOD-PHARM-014 keeps that, with argatroban named, and the
--                 other three take the parts nobody asked: no platelet
--                 transfusion, warfarin waits because early warfarin
--                 gangrenes the limb, and the day-4-to-14 monitoring window.
-- 
-- Largest stem overlap 0.250. Nothing contradicts MOD-PHARM-014: it stops
-- the heparin, these three explain what happens next.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Three days after the repair of a third degree tear, a woman says she is frightened to open her bowels in case the stitches give way. What is the best advice?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Avoid bulky food and hold the bowels back for a few days, so that the repair can settle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask for an enema, so that the bowel is emptied once and then leaves the stitches alone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the lactulose prescribed, because a soft stool strains the repair less than constipation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Strain gently with a pad pressed on the perineum, and use a glycerin suppository if nothing comes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Take the lactulose prescribed, because a soft stool strains the repair less than constipation". A repaired anal sphincter is at its most vulnerable in the first week, and what threatens it is not a soft stool but the straining that comes from hard, impacted faeces. A stool softener such as lactulose is given routinely after repair of a third or fourth degree tear for exactly this reason, with plenty of fluid and a gentle return to an ordinary diet. It is continued until she is passing soft stool without effort, and she is reviewed to check that the repair has held and that she is not leaking flatus or stool.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Holding the bowels back lets the faeces harden, and the strain when they finally pass is far worse for the repair than a soft stool on day three.
• B — An enema drives stool and fluid against a fresh repair and can disrupt it.
• D — Bearing down against a hand is what the softener exists to avoid, and a suppository provokes the same sudden effort.

Reference: WHO recommendations for postnatal care of the mother and newborn; Ghana STG — obstetric care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$After a sphincter repair the enemy is constipation, not the stool. Soft, easy stool for the first weeks, and ask about leakage at every review.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAT-2317$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A shoulder dystocia has just been recognised. Which action must the team avoid?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask the woman to stop pushing and take slow breaths between efforts"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Note the time the head was born and call for extra help and a paediatrician"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Turn her onto all fours if the first manoeuvres do not release the shoulder"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Press hard on the fundus from above while pulling on the baby's head"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Press hard on the fundus from above while pulling on the baby's head". Fundal pressure is the one manoeuvre that must not be used. The shoulder is already jammed against the symphysis pubis, and pushing the uterus down drives it harder into the bone while adding nothing to the room at the outlet; the combination with traction on the head is what snaps the brachial plexus and can rupture the uterus. The pressure that helps is <strong>suprapubic</strong> — downwards and laterally above the bladder, behind the anterior shoulder — and it is used together with the McRoberts manoeuvre, not instead of it.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Telling her to stop pushing is correct; each push drives the impacted shoulder further into the symphysis.
• B — Calling for help and noting the time of the head's birth are both required, because the decision clock for the later manoeuvres starts there.
• C — All fours is a recognised manoeuvre when the first steps fail, and it is safe.

Reference: WHO recommendations on intrapartum care; Ghana STG — obstetric emergencies
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Suprapubic, never fundal. Push the shoulder from behind the pubis and never add traction to a jammed shoulder.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia has a platelet count of 85 × 10⁹/L and is not bleeding. His relative asks why he is not being given platelets. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Platelets are not given, because in HIT the antibody is activated by them and the risk is clotting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Platelets are held back only until the count falls below 50, and then two units are transfused slowly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Platelets would be given if he started to bleed, but they are not used as a routine top-up"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Platelets are unnecessary because the count comes back by itself within twenty-four hours of stopping heparin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Platelets are not given, because in HIT the antibody is activated by them and the risk is clotting". Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is not a bleeding disorder. Antibodies against the heparin-platelet factor 4 complex switch platelets on, so the count falls while clots form in veins and arteries, and giving platelets adds fuel to that process. The treatment that matters is stopping every heparin product, including line flushes, and starting a non-heparin anticoagulant such as argatroban. Transfusion is reserved for bleeding from another cause or an invasive procedure, not for the number on the chart.

Why the other options fail:
• B — There is no threshold at which routine transfusion becomes right in HIT; a falling count is the expected course while the antibody clears.
• C — Even with bleeding, platelets are a last resort here, because they can worsen the thrombosis that is the real danger.
• D — The count recovers over days once heparin stops, not in twenty-four hours, and waiting without an alternative anticoagulant leaves him thrombosing.

Reference: British Society for Haematology — HIT guidelines; Ghana STG — anticoagulation
$q$,
  strategy = $q$In HIT the platelet count is a thrombosis warning, not a bleeding warning. No platelets, no heparin, alternative anticoagulant.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$His platelets halved on day seven of heparin treatment and a clot formed in his calf, so the heparin has been stopped. The prescriber asks why warfarin should not start today. What is the reason?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Warfarin cannot be started until the platelet count is above 300, or it will not work at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Warfarin started too early can cause limb gangrene, so it waits until the platelets recover"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warfarin and the heparin antibody together cause bleeding into the brain in the first week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warfarin is never used again in anyone who has had heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Warfarin started too early can cause limb gangrene, so it waits until the platelets recover". Warfarin lowers protein C before it lowers the clotting factors, and in a patient already switched into a prothrombotic state by the heparin antibody that tips the balance towards <strong>venous limb gangrene</strong> — tissue death in a limb that is already thrombosing. So warfarin is deferred until the platelet count has recovered, usually to above 150 × 10⁹/L, and it is then overlapped with a non-heparin anticoagulant for at least five days. Warfarin can be used again afterwards; it is the timing that was wrong, not the drug.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The threshold is about recovery of platelets and control of the thrombosis, not a figure above 300.
• C — The hazard of early warfarin in HIT is venous gangrene, not intracranial bleeding.
• D — Warfarin is not permanently forbidden; it is started later, under cover of another anticoagulant.

Reference: British Society for Haematology — HIT guidelines; Ghana STG — anticoagulation
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stop heparin, cover with a non-heparin anticoagulant, and let warfarin wait until the platelets are back. Early warfarin costs the limb.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is starting unfractionated heparin. Which platelet monitoring should the nurse arrange?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A baseline count, then a count every two to three days from day four until day fourteen"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A count every day for the first three days, then none unless he starts to bleed or bruise at a puncture site"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A count only when a dose is changed, and again when the heparin is stopped"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A count once weekly for the duration of the treatment, and once more at discharge"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A baseline count, then a count every two to three days from day four until day fourteen". Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia is immune-mediated, and the antibody takes about five days to appear on a first exposure, so the danger window opens on day four or five and runs to about day fourteen. A baseline count gives the comparison that makes a 50 per cent fall visible; sampling every two to three days across the window catches it while it is still a number rather than a thrombosed leg. A patient who has had heparin in the past three months can fall within a day, so an early count is added for them.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Daily counts for three days then nothing watches the window that matters least and stops watching before the risk begins.
• C — Tying the count to dose changes misses the fall entirely, because the antibody does not follow the dose.
• D — Weekly sampling can miss a fall that develops and thromboses inside a few days.

Reference: British Society for Haematology — HIT guidelines; Ghana STG — anticoagulation
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Watch the window, not the dose. Baseline, then every two to three days from day four to day fourteen.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-HAEM-018$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch84.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 84: paracetamol overdose: two questions asked three times.
--
-- paracetamol overdose was asked seven times, and six of them were two
-- questions asked three times each.
-- 
-- "she feels fine"   PHM-0365, IMD-0929, KID-3216 and ORIGINAL-NCX-0193
--                    all said it. NCX-0193 keeps it, because it also
--                    carries the risk assessment. The other three move:
--                    0365 the four-hour rule that makes a level readable,
--                    0929 why the liver dies - NAPQI, once glutathione is
--                    gone, 3216 what a mother is told on the telephone.
-- "which antidote"   DRC-2430, MOD-EMERG-014 and MOD-CC-018 all said
--                    acetylcysteine. EMERG-014 keeps it, with the
--                    nomogram. 2430 takes the eight-hour window and the
--                    transfer, 018 takes what liver failure looks like on
--                    day three - rising INR, falling glucose, rising
--                    ammonia.
-- 
-- MOD-MH-004, the suicidal patient, is left alone; it was never a
-- duplicate of the toxicology items.
-- 
-- Largest stem overlap 0.261.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman who swallowed a large number of paracetamol tablets two hours ago has blood taken for a paracetamol level. Why is an early level not used to decide treatment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the laboratory cannot measure paracetamol accurately in the first six hours after it is swallowed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because absorption is still going on, so a level before four hours cannot be read against the nomogram"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the antidote must be started before any level is taken, so the result would arrive too late"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because a level taken early always reads high, and would lead to the antidote being given needlessly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because absorption is still going on, so a level before four hours cannot be read against the nomogram". Paracetamol is still being absorbed from the gut for the first few hours, so a level drawn early under-reads the eventual peak and would plot falsely safe. The treatment line on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram was drawn from levels taken at four hours or later, and only a level taken at or after that point can be compared with it. The practical rule is therefore that the decision sample is taken at four hours; treatment that is already indicated — a massive dose, or a patient who cannot give a time — is started without waiting for the number.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The assay is reliable; it is the patient's absorption that has not finished, not the laboratory that cannot measure.
• C — The antidote is not withheld for a level, but the level is still what decides whether it is needed, and it is taken at four hours.
• D — Early levels read low, not high, which is exactly why an early result is dangerous rather than merely cautious.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; WHO/International Programme on Chemical Safety — paracetamol overdose
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Four hours is the number that makes the level readable. Before four hours the only question is whether treatment is already indicated anyway.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHM-0365$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Why does paracetamol destroy the liver when it is taken in overdose?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A toxic metabolite builds up once the liver's glutathione is used up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The drug crystallises in the small bile ducts and blocks the flow of bile out of the liver"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An allergic reaction against the drug itself destroys the liver cells over the first day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The liver turns the drug into alcohol faster than the alcohol can be cleared from the blood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A toxic metabolite builds up once the liver's glutathione is used up". Most of a paracetamol dose is conjugated and excreted safely, but a small share is oxidised by cytochrome P450 2E1 into <strong>NAPQI</strong>, a reactive metabolite. At ordinary doses glutathione mops NAPQI up as fast as it is made. In overdose the safe pathways saturate, more goes down the P450 route, glutathione is exhausted, and unbound NAPQI binds liver cell proteins — producing the centrilobular necrosis that appears a day or two later. Acetylcysteine works because it replenishes glutathione.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Paracetamol does not crystallise in bile ducts; that is not the mechanism of its liver injury.
• C — The injury is metabolic, not immune, and it is not an allergy to the drug.
• D — The liver does not turn paracetamol into alcohol; the toxic product is NAPQI.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; WHO/International Programme on Chemical Safety — paracetamol overdose
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Saturation, then depletion, then NAPQI. Acetylcysteine is a glutathione substitute, not a direct antidote to paracetamol.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IMD-0929$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother telephones the clinic because her two-year-old has just been found with an open bottle of paracetamol tablets and some are missing. What should the nurse tell her?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Make him vomit with a glass of salt water, then watch him at home for the rest of the day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bring him and the bottle straight away, and do not try to make him vomit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give him milk to line the stomach, then bring him if he seems unwell later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait until the morning clinic, since he looks well and may have swallowed none"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Bring him and the bottle straight away, and do not try to make him vomit". The bottle tells the team how many tablets were in it and how strong they were, which is what turns 'some are missing' into a dose per kilogram. Inducing vomiting with salt water is dangerous in a small child — it causes hypernatraemia and aspiration and does not empty the stomach reliably. He is brought at once because charcoal, if it is going to be used, works within the first hour, and because a child who looks well now can still develop liver injury a day or two later.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Salt water to force vomiting can cause salt poisoning and choking, and watching at home wastes the hour in which charcoal works.
• C — Milk does not bind paracetamol and does not protect the liver; delay is the harm.
• D — Waiting until morning loses both the charcoal window and the four-hour level.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; WHO — childhood poisoning management
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bring the child and bring the bottle. Never force vomiting, and never let a well-looking toddler wait until morning.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-KID-3216$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$The paracetamol level of a man who overdosed nine hours ago is above the treatment line, but the antidote is not stocked at the health centre. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait for the referral hospital to repeat the level before arranging any transfer at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Send him home to come back if he turns yellow, since nine hours have already passed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send him at once, and start the antidote as soon as it is available, because delay costs lives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give activated charcoal now and review him in the morning before deciding whether he needs transfer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Send him at once, and start the antidote as soon as it is available, because delay costs lives". Acetylcysteine is most effective when it is started within eight hours of the overdose, and that window has already closed — which makes the next hours matter more, not less. Benefit continues to fall with every hour of delay, so the transfer is arranged now, the receiving unit is told he is coming so the antidote is ready on arrival, and it is started there without waiting for a repeat level. A level above the treatment line is already an indication.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Repeating a level that is already above the line adds delay and changes nothing.
• B — Nine hours is late, not too late; sending him home abandons him in the window where treatment still helps.
• D — Charcoal is only useful in the first hour, and reviewing in the morning would push treatment past a day.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; WHO/International Programme on Chemical Safety — paracetamol overdose
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Past eight hours the answer is not 'too late' but 'sooner'. Transfer, pre-alert, and start the antidote on arrival.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2430$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Three days after a paracetamol overdose a patient becomes drowsy and jaundiced. Which set of results tells the nurse that his liver is failing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A falling international normalised ratio, a rising blood glucose and a raised plasma bicarbonate level"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A rising platelet count, a falling white cell count and a normal serum bilirubin level"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A rising international normalised ratio, a falling blood glucose and a rising blood ammonia level"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A raised amylase, a low serum calcium and a clotting screen that is entirely normal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A rising international normalised ratio, a falling blood glucose and a rising blood ammonia level". A liver that is failing stops doing the three things this set measures. It stops making clotting factors, so the international normalised ratio climbs and the patient bleeds; it stops making glucose, so the blood glucose falls; and it stops clearing ammonia through the urea cycle, so ammonia rises and the drowsiness deepens into encephalopathy. Jaundice on day three, falling glucose and a widening INR together define acute liver failure, and this patient needs referral to a unit that can manage it, not another paracetamol level.

Why the other options fail:
• A — This reverses every one of the three; a failing liver does not shorten the INR or raise the glucose.
• B — The platelet count may fall rather than rise, and the bilirubin in a jaundiced patient is not normal.
• D — Amylase and calcium are not the markers of hepatic synthetic failure, and the clotting screen would not be normal in a failing liver.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; WHO/International Programme on Chemical Safety — paracetamol overdose
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Synthetic failure shows as a rising INR, falling glucose and rising ammonia. In liver failure the clotting time lengthens, it does not shorten.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-CC-018$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch85.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 85: four triples: hypertensive emergency, blood gas rule, digoxin antibody, maternal gaps.
--
-- four more triples, and two false starts worth recording.
-- 
-- hypertensive emergency  MSN-2694 asks what makes it an emergency and
--                         NCD-0490 asks what to do, so both stay.
--                         ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0013 now asks how fast to bring
--                         the pressure down: a quarter of the excess in
--                         the first hour, not to normal.
-- blood gas               FLD-3143 and FLD-2168 both read the same acidosis.
--                         3143 keeps the worked example; 2168 now teaches
--                         the rule that settles it - bicarbonate moving with
--                         the pH means the metabolic side is the cause.
--                         LAB-0148 is a different gas and was left alone.
-- digoxin                 all three described the same triad. CVM-1503 keeps
--                         the action, DRC-2407 keeps the potassium level,
--                         and DRG-0998 now takes the step after both -
--                         antibody fragments, because the potassium is high
--                         and must not be replaced.
-- magnesium toxicity      three items all said stop the infusion. BATCH2-0020
--                         keeps it. MAT-0017 and MCH-0006 moved to placenta
--                         accreta and obstetric cholestasis.
-- 
-- First drafts of those last two went to cord prolapse and antenatal
-- steroids and were thrown away: cord prolapse is asked three times
-- already (NCX-1087, OBS-0555, PNC-1908) and the steroid course twice
-- (MAT-2318, MNH-3055). Keyword greps missed both. Largest overlap 0.211.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with a hypertensive emergency is having his blood pressure lowered. How fast should it come down in the first hour?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "By about a quarter of the excess, not all the way to normal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To a normal reading within the hour, so that the organs are protected"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only after the headache has settled, whatever the pressure reads"}, {"id": "d", "text": "By half of the excess every fifteen minutes until it is below 140/90"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "By about a quarter of the excess, not all the way to normal". The brain and kidneys have spent days adapting to a high pressure, and their autoregulation has shifted upwards. Dropping the pressure to normal within an hour takes them below the range in which they can still perfuse, and the result is a stroke or a kidney that shuts down in a patient who was talking to you an hour ago. So the first hour is deliberately modest — roughly a quarter of the excess, or a fall of about 20 to 25 per cent in mean arterial pressure — and the rest is taken down over the following day. Aortic dissection and eclampsia are the exceptions, because the target organ is being torn or seizing now.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Normalising the pressure in an hour is the commonest way to cause cerebral ischaemia in these patients.
• C — Treatment follows the pressure and the organ damage, not the headache; waiting risks progression.
• D — Halving the excess every quarter hour is still far faster than autoregulation can follow.

Reference: Ghana STG — hypertension; WHO/ISH cardiovascular risk guidance
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Controlled, not quick. A quarter of the excess in the first hour, and the rest over the day; only dissection and eclampsia go faster.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In a blood gas the pH is low and the bicarbonate is also low. Which system is responsible for the acidosis?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The respiratory system, because it is the lungs that retain carbon dioxide and push the pH down"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Neither system; a low bicarbonate alongside a low pH is always a laboratory error"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The metabolic system, because bicarbonate has moved in the same direction as the pH"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both systems equally, since the two values have both fallen from their normal ranges"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "The metabolic system, because bicarbonate has moved in the same direction as the pH". There is a single rule that settles most gases before any arithmetic. Look at the bicarbonate and the pH: when they move in the <strong>same</strong> direction, the metabolic side is the cause, because bicarbonate is the metabolic buffer and its fall is what dragged the pH down with it. When they move in <strong>opposite</strong> directions, the respiratory side is the cause, because carbon dioxide is the acid gas and the bicarbonate change is the body compensating. Here both are low, so the cause is metabolic, and a low carbon dioxide would mean the lungs are compensating.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A respiratory acidosis has a high carbon dioxide and a bicarbonate that rises in compensation, not falls.
• B — A low bicarbonate with a low pH is the ordinary picture of metabolic acidosis, not an error.
• D — Only one system causes an acidosis; the other compensates, and the direction rule tells you which is which.

Reference: Ghana STG — fluid, electrolyte and acid-base disorders
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Same direction means metabolic cause; opposite directions mean respiratory cause. The bicarbonate moves with the pH when it is the culprit.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-2168$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with digoxin toxicity has a potassium of 6.8 mmol/L and a ventricular arrhythmia despite the infusion being stopped. Which treatment is now indicated?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Repeat the serum digoxin level and wait for the laboratory result before any further treatment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give a further dose of digoxin so that the level can be rechecked more accurately later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give intravenous potassium to bring the level into range, and repeat the ECG in an hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Digoxin-specific antibody fragments, which bind the drug and reverse the toxicity"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Digoxin-specific antibody fragments, which bind the drug and reverse the toxicity". Once there is a life-threatening arrhythmia or a potassium this high, stopping the drug is no longer enough, because the digoxin already bound to the heart is what is driving the arrhythmia and the potassium leak. Antibody fragments bind digoxin in the plasma and pull it off its receptor, and they are the treatment of choice for exactly this picture. Potassium is <strong>high</strong>, not low, because digoxin blocks the sodium-potassium pump and pushes potassium out of cells — which is why giving more potassium here would be dangerous.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Waiting for a level in a patient with a ventricular arrhythmia wastes the time the antibody needs.
• B — More digoxin deepens the toxicity that is already life-threatening.
• C — The potassium is already dangerously high; giving more risks cardiac arrest.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; British National Formulary — digoxin-specific antibody fragments
$q$,
  strategy = $q$When the rhythm is dangerous or the potassium is high, bind the drug. Antibody fragments, never more potassium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRG-0998$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with placenta praevia is being assessed. Which part of her history puts her at greatest risk of placenta accreta?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Three previous caesarean deliveries, the last one two years ago"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Two first-trimester miscarriages, both managed medically at home"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Gestational diabetes in her last pregnancy, controlled on insulin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pre-eclampsia in her first pregnancy, delivered at 38 weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Three previous caesarean deliveries, the last one two years ago". Placenta accreta happens when the placenta implants over scarred decidua and grows into the myometrium instead of stopping at it, because the scar has no proper decidual layer to hold it. Each caesarean adds to that scar, so the risk climbs with the number of previous sections, and it climbs again when the placenta lies over the scar as a praevia. A woman with praevia and several previous sections should be delivered in a unit with blood, theatre and intensive care, because the placenta may not separate and the bleeding at delivery can be torrential.

Why the other options fail:
• B — First-trimester loss managed without surgery does not scar the upper segment where the placenta implants.
• C — Gestational diabetes affects fetal growth and the newborn's glucose, not the depth of placental invasion.
• D — Pre-eclampsia is a disorder of the maternal vascular response, not of implantation depth.

Reference: WHO recommendations on placenta accreta spectrum; Ghana STG — obstetric care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Scar plus praevia equals invasion. Count the caesareans whenever the placenta sits low.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAT-0017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 34 weeks complains of intense itching, worst on the palms and soles and worse at night, with no rash to see. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure her that itching is normal in late pregnancy, and suggest a moisturiser"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Report it, because it may be obstetric cholestasis and the baby is at risk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Advise a cool bath and an antihistamine, and review her in two weeks"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Test her urine for protein, since the itching suggests pre-eclampsia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Report it, because it may be obstetric cholestasis and the baby is at risk". Itch that has no rash, that settles on the palms and soles and that is worse at night is the classic picture of obstetric cholestasis, caused by bile acids backing up in the maternal circulation. The danger is not to the mother's skin — it is the raised bile acid level, which is linked to preterm labour, fetal distress and stillbirth. She needs liver function and serum bile acids measured, vitamin K considered, and planned delivery in the late preterm window rather than a wait for term. Antihistamines do not treat the cause.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Itch without a rash in the third trimester is not the ordinary itch of stretched skin, and moisturiser treats nothing.
• C — Antihistamines relieve the sensation and leave the bile acids, and the fetus, unmonitored.
• D — Pre-eclampsia causes generalised itching only rarely, and proteinuria would not explain this pattern.

Reference: WHO recommendations on maternal and newborn care; Ghana STG — obstetric care; RCOG — obstetric cholestasis
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Itch with no rash, palms and soles, worse at night: measure bile acids, because the risk is the baby, not the skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCH-0006$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch86.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 86: last four triples: praevia plan, fluid tonicity, ECG leads, Battle's sign, HELLP.
--
-- the last four triples, and four rejected drafts along the way.
-- 
-- placenta praevia   BATCH2-0019 (never do a vaginal examination) and
--                    OBS-0546 (name the diagnosis) ask different things
--                    and stay. MAT-2292 now asks the planning question:
--                    caesarean at 36-37 weeks, timed rather than forced
--                    by a bleed.
-- Holliday-Segar     DRC-2396 gives the 24-hour total and FLD-2174 the
--                    hourly rate, so both stay. NUT-3094 now asks why
--                    maintenance fluid is isotonic - a sick child holds
--                    water, and hypotonic fluid causes hyponatraemic
--                    seizures.
-- rising ICP         three items read one observation set. APR-0455 keeps
--                    it. CVM-1525 moved to 12-lead chest lead placement
--                    (no coverage), SGY-0751 to Battle's sign and raccoon
--                    eyes.
-- pre-eclampsia      MCH-0001 now asks the definitive treatment - delivery
--                    - and MNH-3047 takes HELLP, which had no coverage.
--                    NCX-0157 keeps the nursing environment item.
-- 
-- Rejected on the way, all already covered: seizure first aid (10 items),
-- status epilepticus, F-75/F-100 feeding, wound dehiscence, refeeding,
-- transfusion reactions, pupil signs, mannitol, head-up positioning,
-- 20 mL/kg boluses, cord prolapse, antenatal steroids, uterine rupture,
-- BCG, vitamin A, zinc. Largest stem overlap 0.278.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 33 weeks has placenta praevia and has had one small painless bleed, which has now settled. She and the fetus are stable. What is the usual plan?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Wait for labour to start on its own, and deliver vaginally if the bleeding stays away"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Admit her now until 40 weeks, and allow a trial of vaginal delivery at term"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a course of antibiotics and review her in the antenatal clinic in four weeks"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Plan a caesarean at about 36 to 37 weeks, before labour or a bleed forces it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Plan a caesarean at about 36 to 37 weeks, before labour or a bleed forces it". With the placenta lying over or near the os, normal labour cannot deliver the baby without tearing the placenta off first, so delivery is by caesarean and it is timed rather than waited for. Around 36 to 37 weeks is late enough for the baby to be mature and early enough to get ahead of the bleed or the labour that would turn a planned operation into an emergency one. She is admitted earlier, and delivered sooner, only if she bleeds heavily or labour starts.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Labour with a praevia is the emergency this plan exists to avoid; the placenta is between the baby and the birth canal.
• B — A trial of vaginal delivery is not offered when the placenta covers the os, whatever the gestation.
• C — Antibiotics treat infection, and there is no infection here to treat; delay risks an uncontrolled bleed.

Reference: WHO recommendations on placenta praevia; Ghana STG — obstetric care; RCOG — placenta praevia and accreta
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Praevia means caesarean, and the date is set before the bleed sets it. 36 to 37 weeks unless bleeding forces the hand.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAT-2292$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 34 weeks has severe pre-eclampsia and is now stable on magnesium sulphate and antihypertensives after 24 hours. What is the definitive treatment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Delivery, because pre-eclampsia is cured only by delivering the baby and placenta"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continue treatment until 40 weeks, then induce labour if she is still hypertensive"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop the magnesium once her blood pressure is under 150/100 and send her home"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give corticosteroids and wait a further two weeks for the lungs to mature"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Delivery, because pre-eclampsia is cured only by delivering the baby and placenta". Pre-eclampsia is a disease of the placenta, and every drug used in it buys time rather than treats the cause. Once she is past 34 weeks and has been stabilised — magnesium running, blood pressure controlled, steroids given if there is time — delivery is the treatment, because keeping the pregnancy going only lets the disease progress to eclampsia, abruption, pulmonary oedema or HELLP. The route depends on the cervix and the baby, but the decision to deliver is the decision.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Waiting to 40 weeks with severe disease courts a fit or a stillbirth; the disease does not remit with time.
• C — Magnesium is stopped after delivery, not when the pressure dips; stopping it while pregnant removes her seizure cover.
• D — Steroids are given when there is time before delivery, not as a reason to delay it in severe disease.

Reference: WHO recommendations for prevention and treatment of pre-eclampsia and eclampsia; Ghana STG — hypertensive disorders of pregnancy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pre-eclampsia is cured by the placenta leaving. Stabilise, give steroids if there is time, then deliver.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCH-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is asked to record a 12-lead ECG. Where do the six chest leads go?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "V1 and V2 either side of the sternum, V3 and V4 over the heart, V5 and V6 in the armpit line"}, {"id": "b", "text": "All six in a row across the left side of the chest, level with the nipple, from the midline outwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "V1 to V3 on the back between the shoulder blades, and V4 to V6 on the front of the chest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Anywhere on the chest, so long as the same six points are used each time the ECG is repeated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "V1 and V2 either side of the sternum, V3 and V4 over the heart, V5 and V6 in the armpit line". The chest leads look at the heart from six fixed windows, and the pattern is a sweep from the right of the sternum round to the left mid-axillary line. V1 sits in the fourth intercostal space at the right sternal edge and V2 opposite it on the left; V3 and V4 follow at the same level across to the mid-clavicular line; V5 and V6 continue round into the anterior and mid-axillary lines. The limb leads then give the other six views, and misplaced chest leads are one of the commonest reasons a tracing is unreadable or a change is missed.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A single row at nipple level loses the windows that look at the septum and the lateral wall.
• C — Leads on the back are not part of a standard 12-lead; V1 to V3 look at the anterior and septal walls from the front.
• D — Consistency alone is not enough, because the standard positions are what make the tracing comparable with every previous one.

Reference: Ghana STG — ischaemic heart disease; British Cardiovascular Society — ECG recording standards
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Six windows in a fixed sweep from the right sternal edge round to the mid-axillary line, at the fourth intercostal space.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CVM-1525$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man is brought in two hours after a fall from a ladder with bruising behind his right ear and around both eyes. What does this suggest?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A fracture of the base of the skull, which needs urgent assessment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A simple scalp haematoma that will settle on its own within a week or two"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Raised intracranial pressure from a growing extradural collection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An allergic reaction to the dressing applied in the emergency unit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A fracture of the base of the skull, which needs urgent assessment". Bruising behind the ear — Battle's sign — and bruising around both eyes without any direct blow to them — raccoon eyes — are the two external signs of a fracture through the base of the skull. Blood tracks along the tissue planes and comes out a day or two after the injury, which is why the signs appear late. The importance is what the fracture implies: a dural tear with a risk of meningitis, possible damage to the cranial nerves and vessels, and the need to look for cerebrospinal fluid leaking from the nose or ear. He needs urgent assessment and imaging.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A scalp haematoma is confined to the scalp, and it does not produce bruising around the eyes or behind the ear.
• C — Raised pressure shows as a falling conscious level, rising blood pressure and a slowing pulse, not as bruising.
• D — An allergic reaction produces weals and itching at the site of contact, not bruising at a distance from it.

Reference: Ghana STG — head injury; NICE head injury guidance
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bruising behind the ear and around both eyes means the fracture is at the base of the skull, and the danger is meningitis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-SGY-0751$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A sick child is to be given maintenance fluid. Why is isotonic saline with glucose used rather than 0.18 per cent saline with glucose?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because hypotonic fluid can lower the sodium and cause seizures in a sick child"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because hypotonic fluid is too concentrated and damages the wall of the vein at the cannula"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because isotonic fluid contains more potassium and prevents cardiac arrest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because isotonic fluid is cheaper and the difference in sodium does not matter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Because hypotonic fluid can lower the sodium and cause seizures in a sick child". A sick child holds on to water: pain, vomiting, fever and infection all raise antidiuretic hormone, so the kidney stops making dilute urine. Give hypotonic fluid on top of that and the free water has nowhere to go, the sodium is diluted, and the brain swells — a hospital-acquired hyponatraemia that presents as headache, vomiting, confusion and then a fit, often a day or two into an admission for something else entirely. Isotonic saline with glucose gives the maintenance water and sugar without the free water that drives the sodium down.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Hypotonic fluid is more dilute, not more concentrated, and it does not damage the vein wall.
• C — The potassium content is not what makes the difference; the sodium concentration is.
• D — The sodium difference is the whole point and can kill; it is not a matter of cost.

Reference: WHO — fluid management in children; NICE — intravenous fluid therapy in children
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sick children hold water. Isotonic maintenance fluid, because free water in a child with high ADH causes hyponatraemic seizures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3094$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 34 weeks with severe pre-eclampsia now has a platelet count of 88, a rising AST and a blood film showing red cell fragments. What is happening?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "She is developing a haemolytic transfusion reaction and needs the unit stopped"}, {"id": "b", "text": "She has progressed to disseminated intravascular coagulation from an abruption"}, {"id": "c", "text": "She has developed HELLP syndrome, and the pregnancy needs to be delivered"}, {"id": "d", "text": "These are the expected changes of pregnancy in the third trimester"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "She has developed HELLP syndrome, and the pregnancy needs to be delivered". The three letters are the three findings: <strong>H</strong> for haemolysis, seen as red cell fragments on the film and a rising LDH, with jaundice; <strong>EL</strong> for elevated liver enzymes, with right upper quadrant or epigastric pain and nausea; and <strong>LP</strong> for a low platelet count. It is a severe variant of pre-eclampsia and it can be there with only a modest blood pressure. The treatment is the same as for the disease itself — stabilise and deliver — because HELLP can progress to liver rupture, abruption and DIC within hours.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A transfusion reaction can cause haemolysis, but she has a low platelet count and abnormal liver enzymes as well.
• B — DIC is a complication that may follow, but the combination of haemolysis, liver enzyme rise and thrombocytopenia in pre-eclampsia is HELLP.
• D — These are not normal changes of pregnancy; a platelet count of 88 with fragments on the film is disease.

Reference: WHO recommendations for prevention and treatment of pre-eclampsia and eclampsia; Ghana STG — hypertensive disorders of pregnancy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$H for fragments, EL for the liver, LP for the platelets, and it can arrive with a normal-looking blood pressure. Deliver.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MNH-3047$q$;

-- Verify: expect 6
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch87.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 87: five pairs: avascular necrosis, exchange transfusion, shared equipment, implant, post-caesarean VTE.
--
-- five duplicate pairs closed, and four drafts rejected on probe.
-- 
-- traction      APR-0453 keeps "weights must hang free". SGY-0777 now
--               asks what a painful hip months after a fractured neck of
--               femur means: avascular necrosis, because the retinacular
--               vessels were torn.
-- burette       both items were the same drip-rate sum with different
--               numbers. CLP-2852 keeps the calculation; FLD-2176 now
--               asks what exchange transfusion does that phototherapy
--               cannot.
-- sterile field IPC-0036 keeps "out of sight means contaminated".
--               MCB-2515 now takes shared equipment: clean the cuff
--               between patients.
-- breastfeeding MNH-3054/MAT-2346 both said "progestogen-only". MAT-2346
--               keeps it; MNH-3054 now carries the implant itself - three
--               years, fertility back within weeks of removal.
-- secondary PPH both said "genital tract tear" and MAT-2313 keeps it.
--               OBS-0559 now carries thromboembolism after caesarean,
--               which had no coverage at all.
-- 
-- Rejected after probing, all already covered: cord prolapse (3 items),
-- antenatal steroids (2), hyperemesis (MAT-2303), sterile pack dampness
-- (OPR-1141), autonomic dysreflexia, neonatal hypoglycaemia, sharps
-- containers, protective isolation, organ donation, fat embolism (3).
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Six months after a fractured neck of femur was fixed, an elderly man has worsening groin pain and walks with a limp. What complication should the nurse suspect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dislocation of the hip, which happens when the leg is allowed to cross the midline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A chest infection, which commonly follows surgery on the hip in older people"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A urinary tract infection from the catheter that was used during the operation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Avascular necrosis, because the fracture cut the blood supply to the femoral head"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Avascular necrosis, because the fracture cut the blood supply to the femoral head". The head of the femur is fed by a small retinacular group of vessels that run up the neck, and a fracture through the neck tears them. If the blood supply is not restored, the bone dies while the patient walks on it, and the head slowly collapses. The warning is pain in the groin that gets worse rather than better months after the injury, with a limp and a shrinking range of movement. Early changes show on MRI long before they show on plain film, which is why a persistent painful hip after this fracture is not dismissed as expected recovery.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Dislocation follows a hip replacement, not a fixation, and it presents acutely rather than over months.
• B — A chest infection presents with fever, cough and breathlessness, not with groin pain and a limp.
• C — A urinary infection causes dysuria, frequency and fever, and would not explain a painful hip.

Reference: Ghana STG — fracture management; orthopaedic trauma guidance
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fractured neck of femur equals torn retinacular vessels. Pain that worsens months later is avascular necrosis until proven otherwise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-SGY-0777$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn with severe jaundice is to have an exchange transfusion. What does the procedure do that phototherapy cannot?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It removes bilirubin that is already bound to the brain tissue, so damage already done is reversed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It removes bilirubin and the antibody-coated red cells, and replaces them with donor blood"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It warms the baby quickly, and the bilirubin falls as the temperature returns to normal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It gives the liver time to mature by resting it while the donor blood does the work instead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "It removes bilirubin and the antibody-coated red cells, and replaces them with donor blood". Phototherapy works on bilirubin in the skin, converting it so that it can be excreted — but it works at a certain rate, and if the level is already at the exchange threshold, or the baby is showing signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy, that rate is too slow. An exchange transfusion takes the bilirubin out of the circulation directly and removes the antibody-coated red cells that are still being broken down, which is why it is done as a double-volume exchange, roughly twice the baby's blood volume. It is a treatment for the level that is already dangerous, not a substitute for phototherapy started earlier.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Bilirubin already bound in the brain is not removed by exchange, which is exactly why the decision is made before that point.
• C — Temperature is managed during the exchange, but warming is not the purpose and does not lower bilirubin.
• D — The liver is not rested; the baby's own liver goes on conjugating whatever bilirubin is left behind.

Reference: WHO — neonatal jaundice management; Ghana STG — newborn care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Phototherapy works at its own speed. Exchange transfusion is for when the level is already dangerous, and it removes both the bilirubin and the cells making it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-2176$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse has just taken the blood pressure of a patient with a discharging wound and is moving on to the next patient. What should she do with the cuff?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use it straight away, since the cuff never touches the wound itself and only sits on the arm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dust it with powder and put it away, as the cover is there to protect it from the skin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use it on the next patient and clean the cuff at the end of the shift with the rest of the trolley"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clean it with alcohol before the next patient, because it carries what the last arm carried"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Clean it with alcohol before the next patient, because it carries what the last arm carried". A blood pressure cuff is shared equipment that goes from arm to arm all day, and it picks up what the skin and any discharge leave on it. Organisms survive on fabric and plastic for weeks, and the cuff is one of the commonest things a nurse handles without thinking of it as a piece of equipment that needs cleaning between patients. The rule is that anything shared and touched must be decontaminated between patients: wipe the cuff, then the stethoscope, then wash the hands.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The cuff does not need to touch a wound to become a vehicle; the skin and the hands carry organisms onto it.
• B — Dusting powder does not disinfect and can trap organisms in the fabric.
• C — Cleaning at the end of a shift leaves the cuff carrying organisms to every patient in between.

Reference: Ghana STG — infection prevention and control; WHO — guidelines on hand hygiene and shared equipment
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Shared means cleaned between patients. Cuff, then stethoscope, then hands, every time.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2515$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman has a contraceptive implant inserted today. What should the nurse tell her about it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It works for three years, and her fertility returns quickly once it is removed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It must be replaced every six months, at the same time as her child's immunisations"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It works for three years, but the effect lasts a further year after it is taken out"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It needs a monthly injection to keep it working, given at the clinic by a nurse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "It works for three years, and her fertility returns quickly once it is removed". The implant is a small rod placed under the skin of the upper arm that releases a progestogen steadily; the etonogestrel implant is licensed for three years, and a woman should be told the date it must be changed. Because it does not contain oestrogen it suits her whether or not she is breastfeeding. Removal is a small procedure, and ovulation usually returns within a few weeks, which is what to tell a woman who may want another child. Nothing about it needs a monthly visit.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Six-monthly replacement is wrong; the implant is not tied to the immunisation schedule.
• C — The effect ends when the rod comes out; it does not linger for a year afterwards.
• D — It is not an injectable, and there is nothing to give monthly.

Reference: WHO — family planning: a global handbook for providers; Ghana STG — family planning
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A rod in the arm, three years' cover, and fertility back within weeks of removal. No oestrogen, so breastfeeding is no obstacle.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MNH-3054$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman is now 12 hours after a caesarean section. Which risk should the nurse be actively preventing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Infection of the wound, which is the main risk once the first twelve hours have safely passed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Retained products of conception, which are said to be common after an operative delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Venous thromboembolism, which is far more likely after a caesarean than after a vaginal birth"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uterine rupture, which can reportedly happen at any time in the first day after a caesarean"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Venous thromboembolism, which is many times more likely after a caesarean than a vaginal birth". Pregnancy itself makes the blood clot more readily, and a caesarean adds surgery, immobility and often obesity and pre-eclampsia on top, so the days after a section are the highest-risk time for a deep vein thrombosis and a pulmonary embolus. Prevention is unglamorous and effective: early mobilisation, leg exercises, plenty of fluid, and the prescribed low molecular weight heparin, together with compression stockings where they are used. The nurse also watches for the warning signs, a calf that is painful and swollen, or sudden breathlessness and chest pain.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Wound infection is a real risk, but it declares itself over days, while thromboembolism can kill within hours.
• B — Retained products are a complication of delivery of the placenta, not the leading danger at twelve hours.
• D — Uterine rupture is a risk of labour after a previous section, not of the hours after the operation itself.

Reference: WHO recommendations for prevention of thromboembolism in pregnancy and the puerperium; Ghana STG — obstetric care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$After a caesarean the killer is the clot. Early mobility, fluids, prescribed heparin, and watch the calf and the breath.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-OBS-0559$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

