-- Batch 89: seven pairs: phosphate, phaeo prep, carbon monoxide, methanol, ESBL, paraquat, precipitate labour.
--
-- seven pairs, all closed onto ground the bank did not cover.
-- 
-- digoxin + furosemide  ELE-2993 keeps the potassium answer, with values.
--                       FLD-2161 now asks why phosphate is checked in
--                       DKA: insulin drives it into the cells, and the
--                       weakness that follows gets blamed on the illness.
-- thyroid storm         NCD-0497 keeps the diagnosis. CLN-2914 now asks
--                       how a phaeochromocytoma is prepared for surgery -
--                       alpha blockade before beta, or the alpha effect
--                       runs unopposed.
-- Parkland              SRG-0519 keeps the first-eight-hours calculation.
--                       MSN-2765 now takes carbon monoxide: a saturation
--                       of 98 per cent is the most dangerous number in
--                       the room.
-- cancer hypercalcaemia CVM-1534 now takes methanol. The parent alcohol
--                       is harmless; the acid made from it blinds. Block
--                       the enzyme and dialyse.
-- thyroidectomy calcium MSN-2769 now takes an ESBL-producing wound
--                       organism: the cephalosporins will fail.
-- hypoglycaemia         LTC-1812 now takes paraquat - no antidote, the
--                       lung takes it up, and oxygen is held back.
-- reduced fetal moves   MAT-2367 now takes precipitate labour: ninety
--                       minutes tears what it crosses.
-- 
-- Kept both of the bladder irrigation pair: APR-0448 asks about deep-red
-- drainage and PER-1582 about stopped drainage with suprapubic pain.
-- Different scenarios, different answers.
-- 
-- Rejected for want of a gap: post-operative fever, anastomotic leak,
-- IPTp, adrenal crisis, lithium, MRSA, delirium, NG tube position - all
-- already asked. Largest stem overlap 0.250.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient in diabetic ketoacidosis is started on an insulin infusion and his potassium is being watched. Why must his phosphate be checked as well?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because insulin raises the phosphate, and a high phosphate causes muscle weakness"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because insulin drives phosphate into the cells, and a low level weakens muscles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because phosphate is lost in the urine only when the kidneys are failing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because phosphate falls only if he is also given intravenous bicarbonate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because insulin drives phosphate into the cells, and a low level weakens muscles". Insulin moves glucose and phosphate into cells together, so starting treatment for ketoacidosis pulls phosphate out of the blood even though the total body store was already low from the osmotic diuresis that brought him in. A severely low phosphate produces muscle weakness, difficulty weaning from oxygen, confusion and, at its worst, respiratory failure and rhabdomyolysis. That is why the electrolytes are checked repeatedly through the first day of treatment and phosphate replaced when it falls, alongside the potassium that everyone remembers.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Insulin lowers the plasma phosphate rather than raising it.
• C — Phosphate is lost in the urine in the osmotic diuresis of ketoacidosis, not only in renal failure.
• D — Bicarbonate is not the cause; the insulin is what shifts phosphate into the cells.

Reference: Ghana STG — diabetes and diabetic ketoacidosis; JBDS — management of diabetic ketoacidosis
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Insulin drives potassium and phosphate into cells together. Watch both, or the weakness that follows will be blamed on the illness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-2161$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with a phaeochromocytoma is being prepared for surgery. Which drug must be started first, and why?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A beta-blocker first, to slow the heart down before the operation begins and steady the rhythm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An alpha-blocker first, so that the beta-blocker cannot leave the alpha effect unopposed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A beta-blocker and an alpha-blocker together, since the order in which they are given does not matter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neither drug, because the blood pressure settles by itself once the patient is anaesthetised"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "An alpha-blocker first, so that the beta-blocker cannot leave the alpha effect unopposed". The tumour releases adrenaline and noradrenaline, and the vessels are clamped down by the alpha effect. Block the beta receptors first and you remove the vasodilating, heart-slowing half of the response while leaving the alpha constriction untouched — the blood pressure climbs instead of falling, and the patient can be tipped into a hypertensive crisis or pulmonary oedema. So the alpha blockade, usually phenoxybenzamine, is established for a week or two with generous fluid, and the beta-blocker is added afterwards for the tachycardia.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A beta-blocker first causes unopposed alpha stimulation and a dangerous rise in pressure.
• C — The order matters a great deal; simultaneous starting does not protect against the alpha surge.
• D — Anaesthesia provokes catecholamine release, which is exactly when an unprepared patient crashes.

Reference: Ghana STG — endocrine surgery; Endocrine Society — phaeochromocytoma management
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Alpha before beta, always. Blocking beta first leaves the constriction unopposed and the pressure rises.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLN-2914$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A family is brought in after a generator was left running in a closed room. One patient is confused but his oxygen saturation reads 98 per cent. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Accept the reading and observe him, since a saturation of 98 per cent excludes serious hypoxia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give high-flow oxygen at once, because the saturation reading cannot see carbon monoxide"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a low flow of oxygen through nasal prongs and repeat the saturation in an hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait for a blood gas result before starting oxygen, so that the diagnosis is not masked"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Give high-flow oxygen at once, because the saturation reading cannot see carbon monoxide". A standard pulse oximeter measures how much haemoglobin is carrying oxygen, but it cannot tell oxygen from carbon monoxide — carboxyhaemoglobin absorbs light in the same way, so the saturation stays reassuringly normal while the blood is poisoned. Carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin more than 200 times more tightly than oxygen does, and the brain and heart are the first to suffer. Oxygen is treatment and it is also the diagnostic test: high-flow oxygen is started immediately, and the diagnosis is confirmed by measuring carboxyhaemoglobin.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A normal saturation is the dangerous part of this picture; it is falsely reassuring.
• C — Low-flow oxygen through nasal prongs is nowhere near enough; the aim is to displace carbon monoxide as fast as possible.
• D — Waiting for a result delays the only treatment that works.

Reference: WHO — chemical incidents; Ghana STG — poisoning; BTS — carbon monoxide poisoning
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A normal saturation in a smoke or generator patient means nothing. Give 100 per cent oxygen and measure carboxyhaemoglobin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2765$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man is brought in drowsy after drinking from an unlabelled bottle of industrial spirit. His vision is blurred, he is breathing deeply and his blood gas shows a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Why is a blocking agent given when the alcohol itself is not the poison?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because it neutralises the acid that has already collected in his bloodstream and protects the optic nerve"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because it stops the enzyme that turns the alcohol into the acid that causes the damage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because it flushes the unchanged alcohol out through the kidneys before it can reach his eyes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because it reverses the drowsiness so that he can be assessed more reliably"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because it stops the enzyme that turns the alcohol into the acid that causes the damage". Methanol on its own produces the drowsiness but very little else; it is alcohol dehydrogenase in the liver that converts it to formaldehyde and then to formic acid, and the acid is what blinds the patient and drives the acidosis. Blocking that enzyme with fomepizole, or with ethanol where fomepizole is not available, holds the methanol unchanged while dialysis clears it — the antidote buys time rather than neutralising anything. That is why treatment is started on suspicion, before the blood level comes back.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Nothing is neutralised; the drug works upstream on the enzyme, not on the acid already present.
• C — It does not speed renal excretion of methanol; dialysis is what removes it.
• D — Reversing drowsiness is not the purpose, and would not save the eyes or the kidneys.

Reference: Ghana STG — poisoning; UKNPIS — methanol and ethylene glycol poisoning
$q$,
  strategy = $q$In methanol poisoning the parent alcohol is harmless; the acid made from it blinds and kills. Block the enzyme and dialyse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CVM-1534$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A wound swab from a patient in Ghana grows an organism reported as an ESBL producer. What does this mean for his care?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It is resistant to every antibiotic available, so treatment must be purely supportive"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It resists most cephalosporins, so a carbapenem may be needed and contact precautions apply"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is a laboratory contaminant of no importance unless the patient is in intensive care"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is resistant to penicillin only, so high-dose benzylpenicillin will still clear the wound"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "It resists most cephalosporins, so a carbapenem may be needed and contact precautions apply". ESBL stands for extended-spectrum beta-lactamase, an enzyme that breaks down the cephalosporins and monobactams that would normally cover gut organisms such as Klebsiella and E. coli. The laboratory therefore reports the organism as resistant to those drugs even when the disc looks sensitive, and the clinician is pushed towards a carbapenem or another agent that survives the enzyme. Because these organisms spread readily on hands and equipment, contact precautions and careful hand hygiene go alongside the antibiotic choice.

Why the other options fail:
• A — They are not resistant to everything; carbapenems and some other agents still work.
• C — An ESBL producer in a wound is a real infection risk and a real infection-control problem on any ward.
• D — Benzylpenicillin does not cover these gut organisms and is broken down by the enzyme.

Reference: Ghana National AMR Action Plan; WHO — global priority pathogens list
$q$,
  strategy = $q$ESBL means the cephalosporins will fail. Escalate to a carbapenem and put the contact precautions up.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2769$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A farmer is brought in after swallowing a mouthful of weedkiller from a bottle with a blue-green label. Which statement about paraquat is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An antidote given early will reverse the poisoning if the patient reaches hospital in time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "High-flow oxygen should be given to every patient, since the lungs are the organ at risk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "There is no antidote, so charcoal is given early and oxygen only for measured hypoxia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It damages only the liver, so liver function tests decide whether he will survive"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "There is no antidote, so charcoal is given early and oxygen only for measured hypoxia". Paraquat is concentrated in the lung by an active uptake system and then generates oxygen radicals there, so the lung is destroyed from the inside and the patient dies of progressive fibrosis and respiratory failure, often days after seeming to recover. There is no antidote and no way to pull it back out of the lung, so management is decontamination as early as possible — activated charcoal, or gastric lavage if within the first hour — and then careful supportive care. Supplemental oxygen is itself part of the injury in paraquat, so it is given only when the saturation is actually low, not routinely as it would be in other poisonings.

Why the other options fail:
• A — No antidote exists; nothing reverses paraquat once it is absorbed.
• B — Routine high-flow oxygen accelerates the lung injury and is specifically avoided.
• D — The lung and the kidney are the organs that fail; the liver is not the deciding factor.

Reference: Ghana STG — pesticide poisoning; WHO — paraquat poisoning
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Paraquat has no antidote and the lung takes it up. Charcoal early, oxygen only if the saturation falls.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LTC-1812$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman having her fourth baby labours for only ninety minutes and delivers before the midwife has finished preparing the trolley. Which complication must the midwife anticipate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Shoulder dystocia, because the baby comes through the pelvis far too quickly to rotate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perineal lacerations, because the tissues are given no time to stretch gradually"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cord prolapse, because a rapid descent pulls the cord down ahead of the baby"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Retained placenta, because very strong contractions grip the placenta in the uterus"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Perineal lacerations, because the tissues are given no time to stretch gradually". In a precipitate labour the head is delivered through a vulva and perineum that have had no chance to accommodate slowly, and the expulsive effort is often uncontrolled, so tears — including third- and fourth-degree tears — are common. The midwife's preparation therefore shifts towards controlled delivery of the head, warm support of the perineum, a quick check for trauma afterwards, and readiness for bleeding that has not been measured because nobody counted the blood lost at home.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Shoulder dystocia is a problem of obstruction, not of excessive speed; a small, fast baby usually passes the shoulders easily.
• C — Cord prolapse follows rupture of membranes with a poorly applied presenting part, not rapid descent.
• D — Strong contractions normally expel the placenta promptly; retention is not the expected risk of a fast labour.

Reference: Ghana STG — normal labour and puerperium; WHO — intrapartum care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A labour that ends in ninety minutes tears what it crosses. Guard the perineum and look for damage afterwards.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAT-2367$q$;

