UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A boy develops fever, a widespread itchy rash and painful swollen joints eight days after being given snake antivenom. What is this, and what does it mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Anaphylaxis, so he needs intramuscular adrenaline and must never have antivenom again"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Serum sickness, an immune-complex reaction that settles as the foreign protein clears"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A recurrence of envenoming, so a second identical dose of antivenom must be given at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Malaria acquired in the same week, so he needs a blood film and a full course of artemether"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Serum sickness, an immune-complex reaction that settles as the foreign protein clears". Horse-protein antivenom is recognised by the immune system as foreign, and about one to two weeks later antibody-antigen complexes deposit in small vessels, producing fever, urticaria, joint pain and sometimes lymphadenopathy. It is not an allergy of the immediate kind, it is not a sign that the venom is still active, and the treatment is to stop any further exposure, give an antihistamine and a short course of steroid if the reaction is troublesome, and reassure the family that it fades as the protein is cleared.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Anaphylaxis comes within minutes of the dose, not after eight days, and the management is quite different.
• C — Envenoming relapse does not produce this rash-and-joints picture, and repeating antivenom adds more foreign protein.
• D — Malaria does not follow an antivenom injection on cue, though any fever in Ghana still needs a test.

Reference: WHO — antivenom administration; BNF — serum sickness
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Antivenom given a week or two ago plus fever, rash and aching joints is serum sickness. It burns out; it does not need another dose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2407$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A trial reports that 4 in 100 patients die with placebo and 3 in 100 with the new treatment. What does the number needed to treat show?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It is 1, because the treatment lowers the risk of death by one percentage point"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It is about 100, so roughly 100 patients must be treated to save one extra life"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is about 25, which means a quarter of the patients treated will be saved by the drug"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It cannot be calculated, because a fall from 4 to 3 per cent is too small to matter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "It is about 100, so roughly 100 patients must be treated to save one extra life". The absolute risk falls by 1 percentage point, from 4 per cent to 3 per cent, and the number needed to treat is 100 divided by that absolute difference — so 100. It is the plainest way to say how much work produces one benefit, and it lets a nurse weigh that against the cost, the injections and the side effects. A smaller number needed to treat means a more powerful treatment; the relative reduction of 25 per cent sounds far more impressive than the one life saved per hundred, which is exactly why both figures are reported.

Why the other options fail:
• A — One percentage point is the absolute risk reduction, not the number needed to treat.
• C — A quarter is the relative risk reduction; it is not the number of patients treated.
• D — It can be calculated, and a small absolute effect matters greatly when the disease is common.

Reference: Cochrane — presenting benefit; Ghana Nursing and Midwifery Council — research appreciation
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Number needed to treat is one divided by the absolute risk reduction. A 1 per cent fall means 100 patients per life saved.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BST-1268$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A rice farmer comes in with fever, severe pain in both calves and redness of the eyes after working for days in flooded fields. Which diagnosis fits, and what must be watched for?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Malaria, and the nurse must watch for convulsions once the fever begins to fall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Typhoid, and the nurse must watch for a perforated bowel in the third week"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leptospirosis from rat urine in the water; watch the eyes turn yellow and the urine fail"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dengue, and the nurse must watch for bleeding from the gums as the platelets begin to fall"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Leptospirosis from rat urine in the water; watch the eyes turn yellow and the urine fail". Rats shed the organism in their urine, it survives in warm flooded water and gets in through cuts or the lining of the mouth and eyes, so rice and cane workers are the classic patients. The illness starts like many others — fever, headache, aching calves and red eyes — but in the severe form, Weil's disease, jaundice, kidney failure and bleeding appear, and that is what the nurse watches for. Penicillin or doxycycline is given, and the family is taught to wear boots and cover cuts before going back to the fields.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Malaria does not give beefy calf pain and red eyes as its signature, though a blood film is still done.
• B — Typhoid gives a stepwise fever and abdominal signs, not this watery-field exposure with conjunctival redness.
• D — Dengue is not transmitted by wading in rat-contaminated water and does not spare the kidneys in this way.

Reference: Ghana STG — zoonoses; WHO — leptospirosis
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Flooded field, rats, aching calves, red eyes: think leptospirosis, then watch the eyes turn yellow and the urine dry up.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2771$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A child is stung on the foot by a scorpion and screams with pain at the site. He is sweaty and restless but his airway, breathing and circulation are intact. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cut the skin over the sting and suck the venom out, then apply a tight band above the ankle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give analgesia, apply a cold compress, and observe for severe systemic features"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give scorpion antivenom immediately to every child who has been stung"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give intravenous adrenaline at once, since all scorpion stings cause anaphylaxis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Give analgesia, apply a cold compress, and observe for severe systemic features". The overwhelming majority of scorpion stings cause intense local pain and a surge of adrenaline-like symptoms — sweating, restlessness, a fast heart — and settle with simple pain relief, a cold compress and tetanus prophylaxis. What changes the plan is severe systemic envenoming: profuse sweating with vomiting, agitation, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmia, pulmonary oedema, or in a small child a bulbar palsy with difficulty swallowing. Those children need antivenom and intensive support, but it is not given to every sting, and cutting, sucking or bandaging the limb only adds harm.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Incision, suction and a tight bandage cause injury and delay nothing useful; venom is not removed this way.
• C — Antivenom is reserved for severe systemic envenoming, not routine local pain.
• D — Anaphylaxis is uncommon with scorpion stings; adrenaline is for anaphylaxis, not for pain.

Reference: WHO — scorpion stings; Ghana STG — envenoming
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Most scorpion stings hurt and nothing more. Treat the pain, watch for the vomiting, sweating, agitation child who needs antivenom.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2694$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with fever drinks a herbal mixture for four days before coming to the clinic. Why is that delay dangerous when the fever is malaria?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the herbs block chloroquine, so the antimalarial will no longer work afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because the herbal mixture ferments in the stomach and makes the fever much harder to measure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because malaria can become severe within a day, while a test and treatment act at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because four days is the exact incubation period, after which treatment can no longer help at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Because malaria can become severe within a day, while a test and treatment act at once". In a person with little immunity, especially a child or a pregnant woman, falciparum malaria can move from fever to convulsions, acidosis and severe anaemia in a single day, and the risk climbs with every day the parasite is left untreated. A rapid diagnostic test or a blood film settles the diagnosis in minutes and artemisinin combination therapy starts immediately. The nurse's job is not to condemn the herbal mixture but to ask about it without judgement, so that the delay is not repeated and so that any toxicity is recognised, then to test and treat at once.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Herbal mixtures do not specifically disable antimalarials; the danger is the untreated parasite.
• B — The issue is the delay, not the measurement of temperature.
• D — Four days is not a threshold beyond which treatment stops working; earlier is better at any point.

Reference: Ghana STG — malaria; WHO — management of severe malaria
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Malaria does not wait while herbs are tried. Test at once and treat at once, and ask about the remedy kindly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRD-2127$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 37 weeks feels a gush of fluid, then a small amount of bleeding, and within minutes the fetal heart falls to 70 beats per minute. What is the likely cause and what must happen?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Placental abruption, so she needs analgesia, fluids and a wait for labour to begin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vasa praevia with fetal blood loss, so she needs a caesarean without delay"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Placenta praevia, so she needs a speculum examination to see how much she is bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cord prolapse, so she needs the cord replaced inside the vagina and a gentle wait"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Vasa praevia with fetal blood loss, so she needs a caesarean without delay". When the umbilical vessels run through the membranes across the cervix instead of being protected by placental tissue, rupture of the membranes tears them and the baby bleeds — the loss looks small from the outside because the blood belongs to the fetus, and the fetal heart collapses within minutes of a bleed that would barely affect the mother. That is why a small painless bleed with immediate fetal bradycardia after the membranes rupture means theatre now, and why the diagnosis is made by ultrasound before labour whenever it is suspected.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Abruption is painful, the uterus is tense and the mother is usually unwell; waiting is wrong.
• C — A praevia bleed is maternal and the fetal heart usually holds; speculum examination does not fix the bradycardia.
• D — Pushing a prolapsed cord back up causes injury and delay; the cord is handled minimally and delivery is expedited.

Reference: RCOG — vasa praevia; Ghana STG — obstetric emergencies
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A small bleed, a gushing membrane and a fetal heart that collapses means the baby is bleeding. Theatre, not observation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2900$q$;

