UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with advanced HIV has two weeks of worsening headache, fever and neck stiffness. His CD4 count is very low and a lumbar puncture is done. Which organism is most likely, and what does the result mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Streptococcus pneumoniae, so he needs high-dose benzylpenicillin and dexamethasone together"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mycobacterium tuberculosis, so the cerebrospinal fluid will always be negative in this disease"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cryptococcus, an opportunistic yeast needing antifungals and control of raised pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neisseria meningitidis, so his close household contacts must all receive antibiotic prophylaxis today"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Cryptococcus, an opportunistic yeast needing antifungals and control of raised pressure". Cryptococcal meningitis is the commonest cause of meningitis in adults with advanced HIV, and it creeps in over days or weeks rather than hours, with headache and fever that are often surprisingly mild at the bedside while the pressure inside the head climbs. The diagnosis is made on the cerebrospinal fluid — India ink, cryptococcal antigen, or culture — and treatment is an antifungal induction regimen followed by consolidation, alongside repeated drainage for the raised pressure, because it is the pressure that makes these patients blind or dead. Screening with a blood cryptococcal antigen test before starting antiretroviral therapy prevents much of this.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Pneumococcal meningitis presents acutely, not over two weeks, and does not follow the CD4 count down in this way.
• B — Tuberculous meningitis is possible, but culture negativity is not a rule and it does not make this the likeliest organism.
• D — Meningococcal disease is acute, epidemic and not an opportunistic infection of advanced HIV.

Reference: WHO — managing advanced HIV disease; Ghana STG — HIV and opportunistic infections
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Advanced HIV, weeks of headache, very low CD4: think cryptococcus. Treat the yeast and drain the pressure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0064$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with gout and a high urate level is starting allopurinol, and he is also taking azathioprine for another condition. What must the nurse check before he starts?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Nothing, as long as the dose is increased quickly to bring the urate down within a week"}, {"id": "b", "text": "That the azathioprine dose is reviewed, since allopurinol raises its level dangerously"}, {"id": "c", "text": "That he stops allopurinol as soon as the joint pain starts, because that shows it is working"}, {"id": "d", "text": "That the allopurinol is given only during an attack, which is when the urate is highest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "That the azathioprine dose is reviewed, since allopurinol raises its level dangerously". Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, and xanthine oxidase is one of the enzymes that clears azathioprine's active metabolites, so the two together can drive the marrow into profound suppression — the azathioprine has to be reduced or stopped, and the prescriber must decide before the first dose. The same review applies to mercaptopurine. Allopurinol itself is started at a low dose and built up slowly, is not started or stopped around an acute attack without advice, and any rash is a reason to stop and report rather than to carry on.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Rapid escalation is not safe; the dose is titrated against urate over weeks.
• C — Joint pain does not mean it is working; continuing through a new rash could allow a severe hypersensitivity reaction.
• D — Treatment is continuous; starting and stopping with attacks causes urate swings and more attacks.

Reference: BNF — allopurinol interactions; Ghana STG — gout
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Allopurinol plus azathioprine is a marrow-suppression pair. Check the azathioprine first, and build allopurinol up slowly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PHARM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A preterm baby in a Ghanaian newborn unit has grunting, recessions and a rising oxygen need. Bubble CPAP is set up. Why does it help, and what must the nurse watch?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It delivers a high concentration of oxygen, so the nurse watches only the saturation probe on the foot"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It keeps the alveoli open between breaths, so the nurse watches the nose, abdomen and chest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It completely replaces the baby's breathing, so the nurse stops counting respirations"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It warms and humidifies the air, which is the whole of its effect on the work of breathing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "It keeps the alveoli open between breaths, so the nurse watches the nose, abdomen and chest". In respiratory distress the small alveoli collapse at the end of each breath and the baby must inflate them again with every breath, which is exhausting; continuous positive pressure splints them open, so the work falls and the grunting and recessions ease. The pressures are low and the baby still breathes for itself, so respiratory rate and effort are counted, not abandoned. The nurse checks the nasal prongs for septal damage from pressure, the abdomen for distension from swallowed air, and the chest for a sudden deterioration that could mean a pneumothorax.

Why the other options fail:
• A — It is a pressure device, not simply an oxygen supply, and the saturation is only one of several observations.
• C — The baby continues to breathe; the device supports, it does not ventilate.
• D — Humidification is helpful but not the mechanism that reduces the work of breathing.

Reference: WHO — newborn respiratory distress; Ghana STG — neonatal care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$CPAP splints alveoli open so the baby stops fighting to reinflate them. Watch the nose, the belly and the chest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A boy from a rural Ghanaian district has a painless, raised ulcer with a yellow crust on his shin that has been there for months. He is otherwise well and there is no genital ulcer. What is this, and what does it need?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Primary syphilis, so he needs a course of intramuscular penicillin and his partners traced"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Yaws, a non-venereal treponemal infection that resolves with a single penicillin injection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Buruli ulcer, which needs surgery and cannot be treated with any antibiotic at all"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tropical ulcer, which is always caused by fungal infection and needs a topical antifungal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Yaws, a non-venereal treponemal infection that resolves with a single penicillin injection". Yaws is caused by a treponeme that spreads by skin contact among children in poor rural communities, not by sexual contact, so a child with a painless crusted ulcer on the leg and no genital lesion fits it far better than syphilis. One intramuscular dose of benzathine penicillin heals the early lesion, household contacts are treated at the same time to break transmission, and the whole district campaign is part of Ghana's elimination effort. Left alone it moves on to destroy bone and cartilage years later.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Syphilis is sexually transmitted and the lesion is usually genital; tracing partners is not the approach here.
• C — Buruli ulcer is treated with antibiotics, including rifampicin and streptomycin or clarithromycin, and surgery is not the first step.
• D — Tropical ulcers are usually bacterial, and an antifungal alone would fail.

Reference: WHO — yaws eradication; Ghana STG — skin and treponemal infections
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Painless crusted leg ulcer in a child, no genital lesion: yaws. One penicillin injection, and treat the household.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2896$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with cirrhosis and ascites is started on norfloxacin every day although he has no infection. Why is a long-term antibiotic given to a patient who is not infected?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It lowers the gut bacteria that cross into ascitic fluid and cause spontaneous peritonitis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It treats an infection of the ascitic fluid that is always present once the liver fails"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It prevents the liver from making more ammonia, and so keeps encephalopathy away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It sterilises the bowel before a planned endoscopy, and is then stopped again the following day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "It lowers the gut bacteria that cross into ascitic fluid and cause spontaneous peritonitis". In cirrhosis the gut wall becomes leaky and bacteria cross into the ascitic fluid, where a patient with poor immunity cannot clear them; that is spontaneous bacterial peritonitis, which can kill within days and often presents with nothing more than fever or a vague deterioration. A poorly absorbed antibiotic such as norfloxacin or ciprofloxacin reduces the gut reservoir and the episodes, and it is continued while the risk remains. The nurse watches for the signs anyway — fever, abdominal pain, confusion, falling urine output — because prophylaxis lowers the risk and does not abolish it.

Why the other options fail:
• B — There is no infection present at the start; that is exactly why this is prophylaxis.
• C — Ammonia is dealt with by lactulose and rifaximin, not by norfloxacin for this purpose.
• D — It is not a single-dose bowel preparation; it is long-term prophylaxis.

Reference: Ghana STG — chronic liver disease; EASL — decompensated cirrhosis
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ascites plus a leaky gut means bacteria reach the fluid. Daily norfloxacin is prophylaxis, not treatment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with cold hands and dark nail varnish has a pulse oximeter reading of 89 per cent. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Record the reading and increase the oxygen, since 89 per cent is always a real fall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Warm the hand, move the probe to a clean nail or an ear lobe, and repeat the reading"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ignore the oximeter entirely and rely on the patient's colour and how he feels"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Replace the machine, because a reading below 90 per cent always means it is faulty"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Warm the hand, move the probe to a clean nail or an ear lobe, and repeat the reading". A pulse oximeter sends red and infrared light through the tissue and works out the ratio of haemoglobin that is carrying oxygen, so anything that interferes with the light path or with a good pulsatile signal gives a false reading: cold and vasoconstricted fingers, nail varnish or dirt, a probe on the same arm as a blood-pressure cuff, movement, bright light and poor peripheral perfusion. The answer is to correct the conditions and repeat before acting — and if the reading still does not make sense beside the patient, look at him and check a blood gas.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A low reading from a poor signal is not proof of hypoxaemia, and oxygen would be given for a machine artefact.
• C — Colour and how the patient feels are late and unreliable signs; the corrected reading is still useful.
• D — A machine fault is far less likely than poor technique, and a true reading below 90 per cent is dangerous.

Reference: WHO — pulse oximetry training manual; Ghana STG — oxygen therapy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cold fingers and painted nails lie to an oximeter. Warm the hand, move the probe, then believe the number.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Ten minutes after delivering a healthy baby, a woman becomes pale and shocked with severe lower abdominal pain, and a smooth mass is felt in the vagina. What has happened?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The uterus has turned inside out, and it must be replaced without pulling on the cord"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The placenta is retained, and the cord must be pulled firmly to bring it down"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The uterus has ruptured, and the abdomen must be opened to repair the tear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A bladder has filled and is pressing on the uterus, so a catheter will relieve the pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "The uterus has turned inside out, and it must be replaced without pulling on the cord". Acute uterine inversion follows excessive cord traction or fundal pressure on a relaxed uterus: the fundus dimples and passes down through the cervix, so the woman feels intense pain and becomes shocked out of proportion to the visible blood loss, and a smooth mass appears at the vulva or is felt in the vagina. Help is called, resuscitation starts, the placenta is left in place until the uterus is back, and the uterus is replaced — often with tocolysis to relax it — before the placenta is delivered. Pulling the cord makes it worse.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Cord traction is what causes inversion; pulling harder completes the disaster.
• C — A ruptured uterus gives a silent or tender abdomen with the fetal parts felt outside the uterus, not a vaginal mass.
• D — A full bladder displaces the uterus upwards and causes atony and bleeding, not a mass in the vagina.

Reference: WHO — managing postpartum haemorrhage; Ghana STG — obstetric emergencies
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Shock, pain and a mass in the vagina after delivery means the uterus is inside out. Replace it; never pull the cord.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MNH-3042$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman who delivered two weeks ago is using a herbal preparation in the vagina to tighten the tissues. Why should the nurse advise her to stop?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the preparation will be absorbed into breast milk and make the baby drowsy and sleepy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because it disturbs the vaginal flora, can burn healing tissue and invites infection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because it works too well, and the tightened tissues will make intercourse painful later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because it will permanently stop the return of her monthly periods after the birth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because it disturbs the vaginal flora, can burn healing tissue and invites infection". Herbal and chemical vaginal preparations are concentrated, unmeasured and often caustic; they strip the lactobacilli that keep the vagina healthy, irritate or burn tissue that is still healing after birth, and open the door to bacterial vaginosis, candidiasis, pelvic infection and, with the inflammation they cause, a higher risk of HIV acquisition. The nurse's approach is to ask without judgement what she is using and why, explain the harm plainly, offer warm water alone for cleansing, and make sure she knows which symptoms to report.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Absorption into breast milk sufficient to sedate a baby is not the reason these preparations are dangerous.
• C — The harm is infection and injury, not excessive tightening.
• D — Nothing in these preparations stops the return of menstruation.

Reference: WHO — vaginal practices and health; Ghana STG — postpartum care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Herbal douches burn, strip the normal flora and invite infection into tissue that is still healing. Warm water is enough.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRD-2094$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Using the 4-2-1 rule, what is the hourly maintenance fluid requirement for a child weighing 34 kg?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "74 mL per hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "54 mL per hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "108 mL per hour"}, {"id": "d", "text": "34 mL per hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "74 mL per hour". The 4-2-1 rule gives 4 mL/kg/h for the first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/h for the next 10 kg, and 1 mL/kg/h for every kilogram above 20. For 34 kg that is 40 + 20 + 14, which is 74 mL per hour. The same total can be reached by the daily method and divided by 24, and the two should agree closely.

Why the other options fail:
• B — 54 mL/h comes from forgetting that the third band carries 14 kg, not 6.
• C — 108 mL/h would apply 4 mL/kg to every kilogram, which over-prescribes.
• D — 34 mL/h applies 1 mL/kg to the whole weight, which under-prescribes badly.

Reference: Ghana STG — paediatric fluid therapy; APLS — maintenance requirements
$q$,
  strategy = $q$4-2-1: 40 for the first 10 kg, 20 for the next, 1 mL per kilogram for the rest. Here 40 + 20 + 14 = 74.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-2174$q$;

-- Verify: expect 9
COMMIT;

