-- Batch 92: ten pairs: intention to treat, anthrax, Bishop score, CBT, capacity, glove order, qSOFA, brow presentation, NEC, frailty.
--
-- ten pairs, all closed onto ground the bank did not cover.
-- 
-- standard deviation    STA-0396 keeps the two-ward answer. BST-1243 now
--                       takes intention-to-treat: analyse where they were
--                       randomised, or the drug flatters itself.
-- cholera outbreak      PUB-0618 keeps "begin immediately". MCB-2543 now
--                       takes cutaneous anthrax - a painless black eschar
--                       that does not spread between people.
-- HIV feeding           NUT-0278 keeps exclusive breastfeeding. MAT-2297
--                       now takes the Bishop score, and why a low score
--                       means the cervix must be ripened first.
-- olanzapine            BEH-3014 keeps the metabolic monitoring. MEN-3183
--                       now takes what CBT actually asks the patient to
--                       do - the work between sessions.
-- delirium tremens      MHN-0829 keeps the diagnosis. PSY-1407 now takes
--                       capacity: understand, weigh, communicate, and a
--                       capable refusal stands.
-- sputum for AFB        CLINICAL-0135 keeps the collection instructions.
--                       MOD-IPC-018 now takes why gloves go on last -
--                       they must cover the gown cuffs.
-- shoulder tip pain     SGY-0767 keeps the residual gas answer. MSN-2773
--                       now takes qSOFA - breathing, mentation, systolic
--                       pressure, and what two of three means.
-- wound dehiscence      NCX-0173 keeps the saline-soaked pack. NCX-0542
--                       now takes brow presentation and the section it
--                       needs when it persists.
-- moderate dehydration  FLD-3158 keeps the oral route. MDA-1101 now
--                       takes necrotising enterocolitis - stop the feeds,
--                       drain the stomach, start antibiotics.
-- sundowning            MSN-2716 keeps "look for an unmet need first".
--                       ELD-2021 now takes frailty: lost reserve, not
--                       lost years, and it can be improved.
-- 
-- The two sundowning items also contradicted each other on what to try
-- first, so only one now survives, and the survivor's answer stands.
-- 
-- Kept: Naegele dates (different dates), delegation scenarios with
-- different patients, eye care against passive movement, consent for a
-- patient who cannot explain against one who cannot read, Parkland with
-- different weights, and the evisceration pack against the evisceration
-- position, which is the fuller answer. Largest stem overlap 0.200.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A drug trial randomised 400 patients, but 60 of them stopped taking the tablets. The report analyses all 400 in the groups they were originally allocated to. What is this called and why is it done?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A per-protocol analysis, which is done to show the drug at its best in ideal conditions"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An intention-to-treat analysis, which protects the benefit of randomisation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A subgroup analysis, which is done to find out which patients responded best"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An interim analysis, which is done so that the trial can be stopped early if needed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "An intention-to-treat analysis, which protects the benefit of randomisation". Randomisation only balances the groups if they are compared as they were made. If the people who struggled with the tablets are dropped, what is left is a group of survivors who tolerated treatment, and the drug looks better than it is — the very patients most likely to do badly have been removed from the treated arm. Intention to treat keeps every randomised patient in their original group whatever happened afterwards, so the answer reflects what a real clinic would see.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A per-protocol analysis includes only those who completed treatment and is the one that flatters the drug.
• C — A subgroup analysis looks within groups and cannot restore the loss from dropping patients.
• D — An interim analysis is a look taken part-way through the trial, not a method of handling dropouts.

Reference: Cochrane Handbook — analysing trial data; Ghana NMC — research appreciation
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Analyse them where they were randomised. Dropping the dropouts leaves the healthy behind and inflates the drug.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BST-1243$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A herdsman in northern Ghana has a painless black crusted sore on his forearm after handling a dead cow. What organism is responsible, and what matters about its spread?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A fungus acquired from the soil, spread readily from person to person on the hands"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A virus carried by the animal's ticks, which needs isolation of the patient in a side room"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bacillus anthracis, a spore-former from animal hides that does not spread between people"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A staphylococcus from the animal's skin, which spreads between people who share bedding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Bacillus anthracis, a spore-former from animal hides that does not spread between people". Cutaneous anthrax begins as an itchy bump and becomes a painless black eschar with surrounding oedema, and the history of handling a dead animal or its hide is the giveaway; spores survive for years in soil and on hides and infect through broken skin. The important point for the ward is that person-to-person spread does not occur, so the patient does not need isolating for the sake of others — though the wound is covered and the correct antibiotic, ciprofloxacin or doxycycline, is given. Inhalation anthrax is a different and far more lethal illness.

Why the other options fail:
• A — It is a bacterium, not a fungus, and person-to-person spread is not the pattern.
• B — Anthrax is not a tick-borne virus and does not require isolation of the patient.
• D — The organism is not a staphylococcus, and spread between people is not how it behaves.

Reference: WHO — anthrax in humans and animals; Ghana STG — zoonoses
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Painless black eschar after a dead animal means anthrax. Treat the patient; he does not need to be isolated from others.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2543$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 41 weeks is being assessed before induction. The midwife records a Bishop score of 3. What does the score describe, and what does a low score mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The strength of the contractions, so a low score means the induction will progress quickly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "How ready the cervix is for labour, so a low score means ripening is needed first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The estimated weight of the baby, so a low score means a small baby and an easy birth"}, {"id": "d", "text": "How far the head has descended, so a low score means the head is already deep in the pelvis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "How ready the cervix is for labour, so a low score means ripening is needed first". The Bishop score adds up five findings — cervical dilatation, effacement or length, consistency, position, and the station of the head — to say how favourable the cervix is. A high score means the cervix is soft, short, open and the head is applied, and a simple induction with oxytocin or amniotomy is likely to work. A low score, such as 3, means the cervix is unripe, and that labour is likely to be long or to fail unless the cervix is ripened first with a prostaglandin or a balloon, because oxytocin acting on a firm closed cervix achieves very little.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Contractions are not part of the score.
• C — Estimated fetal weight is not part of the score either.
• D — Station is only one of the five components, and a low score does not mean the head is deep in the pelvis.

Reference: WHO — induction of labour; Ghana STG — prolonged pregnancy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bishop scores the cervix, not the contractions. A low score means ripen it first, or the induction will fail.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAT-2297$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with depression has not improved on an antidepressant. Her therapist offers cognitive behavioural therapy. Which description matches what she will be asked to do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lie on a couch and talk freely about her childhood until the causes become clear to her"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Work through practical tasks that test and change unhelpful thoughts and behaviours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Attend sessions in which she is deliberately made to feel anxious until she stops fearing it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take a weekly tablet alongside her antidepressant to strengthen the effect of both"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Work through practical tasks that test and change unhelpful thoughts and behaviours". Cognitive behavioural therapy is structured and time-limited, usually a set number of weekly sessions, and its engine is the work done between sessions: the patient records what she thought at a moment when she felt low, examines the evidence for and against that thought, and tries small behavioural experiments to see what actually happens. The aim is to give her a method she can keep using, not to uncover a hidden cause from the past or to induce distress for its own sake.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Free association on a couch describes psychodynamic therapy, not CBT.
• C — Deliberate prolonged anxiety is not the method; exposure is done in graded, planned steps.
• D — CBT is a talking therapy and does not involve adding a tablet.

Reference: NICE — depression in adults; Ghana STG — mental health
$q$,
  strategy = $q$CBT is homework: catch the thought, weigh the evidence, test it in practice. Structured, time-limited, practical.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MEN-3183$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with chronic schizophrenia refuses antibiotics for a severe leg infection. Asked about the decision, he repeats the risks back correctly and explains why he still refuses. What should guide the team?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "His illness means he cannot decide, so his wife should sign the consent form instead"}, {"id": "b", "text": "He should be treated under the mental health law, since refusing treatment proves relapse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He can understand, weigh and communicate the decision, so a capable refusal stands"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treatment should be delayed until the infection worsens enough to change his mind"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "He can understand, weigh and communicate the decision, so a capable refusal stands". Capacity is decided by how the person handles this particular decision, not by the label on his folder: can he understand the information, retain it, weigh it against his own values, and communicate the choice? A man who can do all four is entitled to refuse even if the team thinks the decision unwise, and the answer is to keep talking, treat his pain and distress, and offer the treatment again rather than to override him. A diagnosis of schizophrenia does not by itself remove capacity, and the mental health law governs treatment for mental disorder, not a leg infection.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A relative cannot consent on behalf of an adult who has capacity; capacity is not decided by diagnosis.
• B — Refusing antibiotics is not evidence of relapse, and the law is not the route to treat a physical infection against a capable refusal.
• D — Waiting for deterioration to force a decision is neither lawful nor good care.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Act 846; Ghana NMC — consent and capacity
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Capacity is about understanding, weighing and communicating this decision — not about the diagnosis on the folder.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PSY-1407$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is dressing for contact precautions. Why must the gloves be the very last item put on?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because gloves are the cheapest item, so they should go on after everything else is in place"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because they must cover the gown cuffs, or the wrists and sleeves are contaminated later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because putting gloves on first makes them tear when the gown sleeves are pulled over them"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the gloves only need to be worn when the patient is actually being touched by her"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because they must cover the gown cuffs, or the wrists and sleeves are contaminated later". The gown is put on first, then the mask, then the eye protection, and the gloves last so that their cuffs sit over the ends of the gown sleeves. If the gloves go on earlier, the sleeves end up on the outside and, when the nurse removes the gloves, her hands and wrists touch the outside of the gown — which is the part that has been against the patient and the bed. Gloves also go on last because they will be the first thing contaminated, and hand hygiene before them protects the patient as much as the nurse.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Cost is irrelevant to the sequence.
• C — Gloves do not tear for that reason; the order is about contamination.
• D — Gloves are worn for the whole contact, not only when touching the patient.

Reference: WHO — personal protective equipment; Ghana IPC guidelines
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gown, mask, eyes, gloves last. The gloves must sit over the gown cuffs, or the wrists get contaminated.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-IPC-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A ward uses qSOFA to screen for sepsis. Which findings make up the score, and what does a score of two mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Temperature, pulse and white cell count; two abnormal values mean the patient has sepsis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Respiratory rate, mental state and systolic pressure; two abnormal values mean escalate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lactate, urine output and oxygen saturation; two abnormal values mean start antibiotics"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Blood pressure, haemoglobin and platelet count; two abnormal values mean transfuse blood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Respiratory rate, mental state and systolic pressure; two abnormal values mean escalate". The three items are a respiratory rate of 22 or more, altered mentation, and a systolic pressure of 100 mmHg or less; a score of two or more identifies a patient at high risk of a poor outcome and is a prompt to get senior help, start the sepsis bundle and look for the source. It is a bedside screen, not a diagnosis: a low score does not rule sepsis out, and the full assessment — including lactate, urine output and the clinical picture — decides what happens next.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Temperature and white cell count are not part of qSOFA, and a score is not a diagnosis.
• C — Lactate and urine output belong to the fuller sepsis assessment, not the qSOFA screen.
• D — Haemoglobin and platelets are not part of it, and transfusion is not the response to a positive screen.

Reference: Singer et al. — Sepsis-3 definitions; Ghana STG — sepsis
$q$,
  strategy = $q$qSOFA is breathing, mentation and systolic pressure. Two of three means call for help, not wait for a culture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2773$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman in labour at term has a cervix that is 6 cm dilated and the head is felt high, with the brow at the top of the pelvis on examination. The membranes are intact. What does this mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A face presentation, which can deliver vaginally as long as the chin is to the front"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A brow presentation, which if it persists means obstructed labour and caesarean section"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A cord presentation, which is an emergency whether or not the membranes are ruptured"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A brow presentation, which will always rotate and deliver vaginally if she is left to push"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A brow presentation, which if it persists means obstructed labour and caesarean section". <strong>Evisceration needs slack on the suture line and zero strain</strong> — flat with knees flexed (low Fowler's at most) relaxes the abdominal wall, keeps pressure off the wound and eases breathing. The nurse adds: cover the bowel with a sterile saline-soaked dressing, give nothing by mouth, keep nil per rectum preparations ready, and summon the surgeon urgently.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Head-down positioning crams abdominal contents against the diaphragm and the wound.
• C — Full upright sitting maximises wound tension and bowel protrusion.
• D — Higher Fowler's with straight legs tensions the wound exactly where it failed.

Reference: Ghana — surgical emergencies; evisceration positioning and cover$q$,
  strategy = $q$Brow means the head cannot enter the pelvis. Watch briefly for it to flex or extend; if it persists, section.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0542$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A preterm baby on day four of life has a swollen, shiny abdomen, bilious aspirates and blood in the stool. What is the likely diagnosis, and what should happen first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Gastroenteritis, so the baby should be kept feeding and given oral rehydration solution"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Necrotising enterocolitis, so feeds stop, the stomach is drained and antibiotics start"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A hernia, so the abdomen should be massaged firmly and a warm compress applied to it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Constipation, so a glycerine suppository should be given and feeding continued as before"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Necrotising enterocolitis, so feeds stop, the stomach is drained and antibiotics start". Necrotising enterocolitis is an ischaemic and infective destruction of the immature bowel, and preterm babies fed formula are the group at risk. It announces itself with a distended abdomen that may look shiny and red, bilious aspirates, bloody stools, apnoea and temperature instability, and the radiograph may show gas in the bowel wall. Feeds are stopped at once, a nasogastric tube is left on free drainage, broad-spectrum antibiotics are started and the baby is supported with parenteral nutrition and careful fluid. Breast milk is the strongest protection.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Continuing feeds into an ischaemic bowel makes the perforation more likely.
• C — Massaging the abdomen risks perforating damaged bowel.
• D — This is not constipation, and a suppository would delay the treatment that matters.

Reference: WHO — newborn care; Ghana STG — neonatal sepsis and NEC
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Preterm baby, bilious aspirates and a bloody stool means dead bowel, not an upset stomach. Stop feeds and treat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1101$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 84-year-old woman lives alone and manages well until a chest infection, after which she cannot stand or manage her tablets. Her family call it old age. What does the nurse explain about frailty?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It is an unavoidable part of ageing, so the family should plan for permanent residential care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It is another name for depression, so she should be started on an antidepressant and reviewed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is reduced reserve, so a small illness causes a large decline, and it can improve"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It means she has had a stroke, so she needs urgent brain imaging before anything else is done"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "It is reduced reserve, so a small illness causes a large decline, and it can improve". Frailty is a loss of physiological reserve across several systems, so a chest infection that a robust person would shrug off tips a frail person into a spiral of immobility, poor intake and losing the ability to manage at home. That is not the same as inevitable ageing: comprehensive geriatric assessment, a graded exercise programme, attention to nutrition, review of unnecessary tablets and treating the infection all reduce the decline, and many patients recover their previous function. Naming frailty correctly also changes the plan — the aim is rehabilitation, not a one-way move to a care home.

Why the other options fail:
• A — It is not inevitable, and the correct response is assessment and rehabilitation.
• B — Depression can look similar and must be excluded, but it is not what frailty means.
• D — There is no reason here to suspect a stroke, and imaging would not be the first step.

Reference: British Geriatrics Society — frailty; Ghana STG — care of the older person
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Frailty is lost reserve, not lost years. A small insult causes a big collapse, and rehabilitation can reverse much of it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ELD-2021$q$;

-- Verify: expect 10
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch93.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 93: ten pairs: listeria, hypospadias, opioid observations, somatic symptoms, NPH, infant hydrocephalus, varicocele, cell salvage, perineal infection, valuables.
--
-- ten pairs, all closed onto ground the bank did not cover.
-- 
-- damp sterile pack    OPR-1141 keeps treat-as-contaminated. MCB-2583 now
--                      takes listeria in pregnancy: the soft cheese that
--                      crosses the placenta.
-- PPE removal          MOD-IPC-004 keeps the doffing sequence. MOD-PROC-001
--                      now takes hypospadias: never circumcise before
--                      the repair, because the foreskin is the graft.
-- NG tube check        MOD-NUTR-018 keeps the pH-with-initial-X-ray answer,
--                      which is the current guidance; MOD-FUND-012 now
--                      takes why observations tighten after morphine -
--                      sedation and slow breathing come first.
-- suicide risk         MHN-0813 keeps the plan-and-means answer. PSY-1399
--                      now takes somatic symptom disorder and why normal
--                      tests are not repeated.
-- GTN sublingual       DRC-2422 keeps first-pass avoidance. CLP-2817 now
--                      takes normal pressure hydrocephalus - magnetic
--                      gait, incontinence, big ventricles, and a shunt.
-- four phone messages  NCX-0361 keeps the chest pain. NCX-0639 now takes
--                      infant hydrocephalus: the tense fontanelle and
--                      sunset eyes.
-- stairs               NEURO-0003 keeps the stick-and-weak-leg-first
--                      sequence. NCX-0152 now takes varicocele - a bag of
--                      worms that enlarges on coughing.
-- bowel obstruction    MSN-2737 keeps the no-laxative safety rule, and
--                      SRG-0525's weaker "flatus confirms obstruction"
--                      item is replaced by cell salvage.
-- partograph           OBS-0553 keeps the transfer answer. PNC-1906 now
--                      takes an infected perineal repair: swab, antibiotics,
--                      heal by second intention.
-- isolation loneliness MCB-2521 keeps the increased-contact answer.
--                      FND-2685 now takes patient valuables: register,
--                      receipt, witness.
-- 
-- Two self-caught collisions: the first priapism replacement duplicated
-- an existing priapism item, and the first endometritis replacement made
-- a third item on the same postnatal fever. Both replaced again before
-- applying. Largest stem overlap 0.208.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A pregnant woman in Accra has two days of fever, headache and muscle aches after eating unpasteurised soft cheese. Which organism must be considered, and why does it matter in pregnancy?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Salmonella typhi, which is treated with ciprofloxacin only in the third trimester of pregnancy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Staphylococcus aureus, which is treated with flucloxacillin and does not affect the fetus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Listeria monocytogenes, which crosses the placenta and can cause fetal loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vibrio cholerae, which causes profuse watery diarrhoea and needs immediate rehydration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Listeria monocytogenes, which crosses the placenta and can cause fetal loss". Listeria is a Gram-positive rod that grows well at fridge temperature, so it survives in soft cheese, unpasteurised milk and cold cooked meats. In a healthy adult it may cause only a flu-like illness, but in pregnancy it crosses to the placenta and can cause fetal loss, stillbirth, preterm labour or a severely ill newborn, and in the immunosuppressed and elderly it causes meningitis. It is treated with ampicillin, often with gentamicin, and the woman is advised to avoid unpasteurised dairy and reheat chilled foods thoroughly.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Typhoid comes from contaminated water and food, not particularly from chilled cheese, and is not the classic pregnancy hazard here.
• B — Staphylococcal food poisoning is a short, violent vomiting illness, not this picture.
• D — Cholera is a waterborne diarrhoeal disease and does not cross the placenta in this way.

Reference: WHO — listeriosis; Ghana STG — food-borne infection in pregnancy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Soft cheese, flu-like illness, pregnancy: think listeria. Ampicillin, and it can reach the baby.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2583$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn boy has hypospadias and is being prepared for repair. Why must he not be circumcised before the operation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the foreskin is needed to rebuild the urethra during the repair"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because circumcision in a newborn always causes the urethra to close over"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the operation cannot be done until he has reached school age"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the repair needs the boy to be able to urinate standing up first"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Because the foreskin is needed to rebuild the urethra during the repair". In hypospadias the urethral opening lies on the underside of the penis, somewhere between the glans and the scrotum, and the curve of the shaft plus the short urethra must be corrected. The inner layer of the foreskin is the ideal tissue for reconstructing the urethra, so removing it beforehand takes away the surgeon's best material and makes a later repair much harder. Repair is usually done between about six and eighteen months, and afterwards the urinary catheter is kept draining freely — often into a double nappy — while the dressing and the stream are watched.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Circumcision does not close the urethra; the reason is the lost tissue.
• C — Repair is done in infancy, not at school age.
• D — A baby does not need to stand to pass urine; a catheter is used after surgery.

Reference: EAU — hypospadias; Ghana STG — paediatric surgery
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Never circumcise a hypospadias before repair. The foreskin is the graft the surgeon is going to need.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has just been given intravenous morphine for severe pain. Why must the observations be repeated at short, set intervals rather than at the next routine round?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the blood pressure always falls sharply within five minutes of a dose of morphine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because sedation and slowed breathing come before the changes the routine would catch"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the pain score must be recorded before the drug is allowed to take full effect"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because morphine disturbs the temperature, and a fever must be detected within the hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because sedation and slowed breathing come before the changes the routine would catch". Respiratory depression from an opioid begins with drowsiness and a falling respiratory rate, and by the time the oxygen saturation drops or the patient looks unwell the depression is established. The nurse therefore assesses sedation level, respiratory rate and saturation at set intervals after the dose — typically within the first half hour and then according to the early warning score — and keeps naloxone to hand. The same logic applies after sedation and on return from theatre, which is why the interval is prescribed rather than left to chance.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The blood pressure does not always fall, and it is not the earliest or most dangerous change.
• C — The pain score matters but is not the reason for close monitoring of breathing.
• D — Temperature is not the concern after an opioid.

Reference: Ghana STG — acute pain; NICE — postoperative care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$After morphine, watch the breathing and the sedation first. The saturation falls late, not early.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman has attended the clinic nine times in a year with abdominal pain, headache and palpitations. Every investigation has been normal. What is the most helpful approach?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell her plainly that there is nothing wrong so that she stops attending the clinic"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Arrange an exploratory operation, because an organic cause must exist somewhere"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Offer regular scheduled review, treat her distress and stop repeating normal tests"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refer her to a surgeon each time a new symptom appears, so that nothing is missed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Offer regular scheduled review, treat her distress and stop repeating normal tests". This pattern fits a somatic symptom disorder: real distress and real symptoms, held together with repeated consultations and tests that keep coming back normal. Repeated investigations are not neutral — each one carries a risk of a false positive, a complication and a message that the doctors have still not found it — so the plan is a single named clinician, appointments at fixed intervals rather than at each new symptom, attention to anxiety and depression, and a clear explanation that her symptoms are being taken seriously even though the tests are complete.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Dismissing her does not end the distress and damages the relationship.
• B — Operating on normal investigations exposes her to harm without a diagnosis.
• D — Cascading referrals repeat the problem and increase the chance of an incidental finding being over-treated.

Reference: ICD-11 — bodily distress disorder; NICE — medically unexplained symptoms
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Normal tests with real distress need a named clinician and fixed appointments, not another scan.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PSY-1399$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An older man has three months of shuffling, magnetic gait, urinary incontinence and mild confusion. His dementia screen is unremarkable and imaging shows enlarged ventricles. What is the likely diagnosis, and why does it matter?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Alzheimer's disease, and it matters because nothing can be done except long-term care planning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal pressure hydrocephalus, and it matters because shunting can reverse some of the decline"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Parkinson's disease, and it matters because levodopa will restore his gait completely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A stroke, and it matters because he needs thrombolysis within four and a half hours of onset"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Normal pressure hydrocephalus, and it matters because shunting can reverse some of the decline". The triad is a broad-based, shuffling gait that looks as if the feet are stuck to the floor, urinary incontinence and a mild cognitive slowing, with ventricles that are large on imaging although the cerebrospinal fluid pressure is normal. It is one of the few causes of dementia and gait failure that can be treated: a shunt diverts the fluid and, especially when the gait is the dominant problem and the history is short, walking can improve considerably. That is why every older patient with this triad deserves imaging rather than an assumption of untreatable dementia.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Alzheimer's presents with memory loss first and has no shunt-responsive gait disorder.
• C — Parkinson's gives tremor, rigidity and bradykinesia rather than this triad, and levodopa does not reverse hydrocephalus.
• D — There is no acute onset and no window for thrombolysis after three months.

Reference: NICE — dementia; Ghana STG — neurology of the older person
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Magnetic gait, incontinence and confusion with big ventricles is treatable. Scan the older patient before calling it dementia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2817$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A six-week-old baby's head has grown much faster than the growth chart allows, the fontanelle is tense and bulging, and his eyes seem to look downward. What is happening?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "He has rickets from lack of vitamin D, so he needs a supplement and review in three months"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It is a normal variation in head size, so the measurement should simply be repeated at the next visit"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The pressure inside the head is raised from hydrocephalus, so he needs urgent neurosurgical review"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He is dehydrated, which pulls the eyes downward, so he needs oral rehydration solution at home"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "The pressure inside the head is raised from hydrocephalus, so he needs urgent neurosurgical review". <strong>A funny feeling under a cast warns of neurovascular compromise</strong> — pressure, swelling or a tightening cast can strangle nerves and blood flow within hours, so circulation checks and urgent review come before every other callback. Itching from bile salts, infection-related nausea and arthritic insomnia all need follow-up, but none threatens a limb.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Arthritic insomnia matters for comfort, never first among these four.
• B — Hepatitis itching is miserable but stable — calamine and antihistamines can wait its turn.
• D — Nausea in osteomyelitis needs review, but it does not outrank limb threat.

Reference: Ghana — cast care; neurovascular compromise recognition$q$,
  strategy = $q$A head crossing the lines, a tense fontanelle and sunset eyes mean pressure, not a big head. Scan urgently.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0639$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 22-year-old man has a dull ache in the left scrotum that worsens at the end of the day. On examination there is a soft swelling above the testis that feels like a bag of worms and enlarges when he coughs. What is it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Epididymitis, which presents with fever and pain on passing urine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A varicocele, a dilated vein plexus that can impair fertility over time"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A hydrocele, which transilluminates brilliantly when a torch is applied"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Testicular torsion, which needs immediate surgery to save the testis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A varicocele, a dilated vein plexus that can impair fertility over time". The <strong>strong leg does the work</strong>: going down, the stick and weak leg go first so that the strong leg, still on the upper step, controls the descent; going up, the strong leg leads and the stick and weak leg follow. The memory aid is <strong>'up with the good, down with the bad'</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Leading down with the strong leg puts the weight on the weak leg.
• C — The stick must go before the weak leg to provide support.
• D — Leading down with the strong leg leaves the weak leg to lower the body — unsafe.

Reference: Ghana STG — stroke rehabilitation; mobility-aid teaching standards$q$,
  strategy = $q$A cough-responsive, ache-in-the-evening scrotal swelling is a varicocele — not an emergency, but worth referring.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0152$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient having a major operation is expected to lose a lot of blood. The theatre uses cell salvage. What does that mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Donor blood is ordered in advance and kept in a fridge beside the theatre"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The patient's blood pressure is lowered deliberately to reduce the bleeding"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Blood lost from the wound is collected, washed and given back to the patient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Blood is taken from the patient two weeks before surgery and stored for the day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Blood lost from the wound is collected, washed and given back to the patient". The suction apparatus is connected to a collecting reservoir, the red cells are washed and concentrated, and they are returned to the patient through a filter, so the patient receives his own blood rather than a donor's. That reduces exposure to donor blood and its risks and eases pressure on a scarce blood supply. It is not used when the field is heavily contaminated with bowel contents or infection, and it does not remove the need for careful cross-matching and consent, but it is a real saving where the loss is expected to be large.

Why the other options fail:
• A — That is cross-matched donor blood, not salvage.
• B — That is controlled hypotension, a different technique.
• D — That is preoperative autologous donation, which is done weeks before, not during the operation.

Reference: AABB — perioperative blood salvage; Ghana National Blood Service guidelines
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cell salvage means the patient's own lost blood is washed and returned. Fewer donor units, less risk.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-SRG-0525$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Six days after a second-degree tear was repaired, a woman has a painful perineum, the suture line has separated and there is offensive discharge. What is this and what should be done?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Normal healing, so she should be reassured that the stitches always separate a little at this stage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An infected, broken-down perineal repair, needing a swab, antibiotics and regular cleaning"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A sign that the tear was repaired with the wrong suture material, so it must be re-sutured today"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A vaginal thrush infection, so she needs a course of antifungal pessaries and should stop breastfeeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "An infected, broken-down perineal repair, needing a swab, antibiotics and regular cleaning". Infection of a perineal repair shows itself as increasing pain after the first few days, redness and separation of the edges, offensive discharge and sometimes fever. The wound is swabbed before antibiotics, the area is kept clean and dry with regular washing and sitz baths, analgesia is given, and the woman is reviewed — most wounds are left to heal by secondary intention rather than being stitched again at once, because re-suturing infected tissue simply breaks down again. Faecal and urinary continence is checked, since a third- or fourth-degree tear changes all of this.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Sutures do not normally separate, and pain should be settling rather than worsening.
• C — Suture material is not the usual cause, and immediate re-suturing of infected tissue fails.
• D — Thrush causes itching and a white discharge, not a gaping offensive perineal wound.

Reference: RCOG — perineal repair; Ghana STG — postnatal care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A perineal repair that hurts more by day six and has opened means infection. Swab, antibiotics, keep it clean.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PNC-1906$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient due for surgery hands the nurse a gold ring and asks her to keep it safe. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Put it in her own uniform pocket and return it when the patient comes back from theatre"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Record it, place it in the hospital safe with a written receipt, and note the witness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave it in the locker beside the bed, since the patient asked for it to be kept there"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give it to another patient's relative to hold until the family comes in later that day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Record it, place it in the hospital safe with a written receipt, and note the witness". Valuables are a known source of complaint and dispute, so the process is deliberate: the item is described in the nursing notes, entered in the valuables register and placed in the hospital safe, with a receipt given to the patient and a second nurse witnessing the entry. If a hospital has no safe, the family are asked to take the item home and that decision is documented. Jewellery is also removed before theatre because metal can conduct diathermy current and cause burns, and rings can injure a finger that swells.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Keeping it on her person leaves no record and makes the nurse the only witness.
• C — An undocumented locker is neither safe nor accountable.
• D — Handing a patient's property to another family is a clear route to loss and dispute.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — patient property and valuables; NMC code of conduct
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Valuables: describe, register, receipt, witness. Never a pocket, never an undocumented locker.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FND-2685$q$;

-- Verify: expect 10
COMMIT;

