BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 19-year-old has a severe sore throat on one side, cannot open his mouth fully and his voice sounds muffled. The uvula is pushed away from the painful side. What is this, and what is the concern?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Simple tonsillitis, which will settle with oral penicillin and warm salt-water gargles at home"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A peritonsillar abscess, which may need drainage and can threaten the airway"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infectious mononucleosis, which needs bed rest and avoidance of contact sports only"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A dental abscess, which needs a tooth extraction before any antibiotic is started"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A peritonsillar abscess, which may need drainage and can threaten the airway". A quinsy forms between the tonsil and the pharyngeal wall, so the pain is one-sided and severe, the jaw is held partly closed by trismus, the voice is muffled, and the swollen tonsil pushes the uvula to the opposite side. The danger is not the throat pain but the airway: the swelling can obstruct breathing, and infection can track into the parapharyngeal space or the neck. Treatment is intravenous antibiotics with needle aspiration or incision and drainage by an ENT team, and the nurse watches swallowing, voice, drooling and breathing.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Simple tonsillitis is bilateral and does not push the uvula aside or lock the jaw.
• C — Glandular fever causes bilateral enlargement and fatigue, without a unilaterally displaced uvula.
• D — A dental abscess points to a carious tooth and facial swelling, not a peritonsillar collection.

Reference: Ghana STG — upper airway infection; ENT UK — quinsy management
$q$,
  strategy = $q$One-sided throat with trismus and a displaced uvula is a quinsy. Antibiotics and drainage, and watch the airway.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2548$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A blood donor session is being held at a Ghanaian district hospital. Which donor must be deferred from giving blood today?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A 30-year-old man who weighs 70 kg and last donated four months ago, feeling well since"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 24-year-old woman who weighs 55 kg and has never donated before in her life"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A 22-year-old woman with a haemoglobin of 9.5 g/dL who wants to donate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A 45-year-old man who ate a light breakfast two hours ago and feels completely well"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A 22-year-old woman with a haemoglobin of 9.5 g/dL who wants to donate". The donor's haemoglobin is checked before every donation, and a value below the cutoff — commonly 12.5 g/dL for women and 13.0 g/dL for men — means deferral, because taking a unit from an already anaemic woman harms her and gives a poor product. The other requirements are a minimum weight of about 50 kg, an interval of at least three months since the last donation, no active infection, and being fit and well that day; afterwards the donor rests, takes fluids and a snack, and is observed for faintness before leaving.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Weight, interval and health all meet the criteria.
• B — First-time donors are welcome, and 55 kg is above the minimum.
• D — A light meal and good health are not reasons to defer.

Reference: WHO — blood donor selection; Ghana National Blood Service
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Before a unit is taken, check haemoglobin, weight, interval and health. Anaemia is a deferral, not a detail.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1356$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man has a swollen, brawny neck under the jaw, a tongue pushed upward, drooling and difficulty swallowing, two days after a toothache. What is this, and what must happen?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ludwig's angina, a spreading floor-of-mouth infection that threatens the airway"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mumps, which is viral and only needs analgesia, fluids and isolation from pregnant staff"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A simple dental abscess, which can wait for an outpatient extraction next week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tonsillitis, which needs oral penicillin and review in the outpatient clinic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Ludwig's angina, a spreading floor-of-mouth infection that threatens the airway". Infection from a lower molar tracks through the submandibular and sublingual spaces, so both sides of the floor of the mouth become hard and brawny, the tongue is lifted up and back, and swallowing and speaking become difficult. The airway can close quickly: the patient is sat upright, high-flow oxygen is given, intravenous antibiotics are started and an ENT or maxillofacial team is called for drainage, with an anaesthetist warned in case an artificial airway is needed.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Mumps swells the parotid glands bilaterally and does not give a brawny floor of mouth with drooling.
• C — This is not confined to a tooth; the swelling is spreading through tissue planes and needs urgent care.
• D — The infection is below the jaw and in the floor of the mouth, not limited to the tonsils.

Reference: Ghana STG — head and neck infection; NICE — dental abscess and Ludwig's angina
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A hard floor of mouth, tongue pushed up, drooling after toothache: sit him up, call for help, protect the airway.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2901$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Three weeks after a caesarean section a woman is breathless on climbing stairs, pale, and her haemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL with a low ferritin. What is the diagnosis and the main treatment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Anaemia of chronic disease, so she needs erythropoietin injections and no iron at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Iron deficiency anaemia after delivery blood loss, so she needs a course of oral iron"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Thalassaemia trait, so she needs no treatment and only genetic counselling for the future"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Folate deficiency, so she needs folic acid alone and a repeat count in six months"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Iron deficiency anaemia after delivery blood loss, so she needs a course of oral iron". Blood loss at delivery, on top of the demands of pregnancy and breastfeeding, empties the iron stores, and a low ferritin with a low haemoglobin confirms it. Oral iron is taken with vitamin C or citrus and away from tea, milk and antacids, and it is continued for about three months after the haemoglobin returns to normal, because the haemoglobin corrects before the stores refill. The nurse also looks for the cause — ongoing bleeding, poor diet, or a second pregnancy too soon — and the response is checked with a repeat count.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A low ferritin shows the stores themselves are empty; this is iron deficiency, not anaemia of chronic disease.
• C — Thalassaemia trait gives a low MCV with a normal ferritin, not an empty store after delivery.
• D — Folate deficiency gives a macrocytic anaemia, and it is not the likeliest cause here.

Reference: WHO — iron deficiency anaemia; Ghana STG — postnatal care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$After delivery blood loss, a low haemoglobin with low ferritin is iron deficiency. Give iron, and keep giving it after the count recovers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLN-2924$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A four-year-old has a discharging ear for ten days and now has a red, tender swelling behind the ear that pushes the pinna forward. What is this, and what does it need?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mastoiditis, which needs intravenous antibiotics and an urgent ENT opinion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A sebaceous cyst, which needs incision and drainage in the outpatient clinic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Enlarged lymph nodes, which need observation and a review in one month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Otitis externa, which needs antibiotic ear drops and a wick in the canal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Mastoiditis, which needs intravenous antibiotics and an urgent ENT opinion". A <strong>new blown pupil signals uncal herniation or third-nerve compression until proven otherwise</strong> — a neurosurgical emergency. The nurse keeps the head midline with the bed up 30 degrees, gives oxygen, runs full neuro-observations and summons the doctor for an urgent scan and possible theatre; watching and waiting can cost the patient's life.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Detailed vision testing wastes critical minutes and does not treat the underlying compression.
• C — Laying the bed flat increases intracranial pressure; the head stays elevated, never lowered.
• D — Delayed action during herniation allows irreversible brainstem compression — reassessment never replaces escalation.

Reference: Ghana STG — raised intracranial pressure; head injury emergencies$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ear discharge that will not settle, then a red tender swelling behind the ear: mastoiditis. IV antibiotics, ENT now.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0304$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man who has been drinking heavily vomits repeatedly, then brings up a large amount of fresh blood. He is not jaundiced and has no liver disease. What is the likely cause?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bleeding oesophageal varices from undiagnosed cirrhosis of the liver"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A peptic ulcer that has perforated into the abdominal cavity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A Mallory-Weiss tear at the junction of the oesophagus and stomach"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stomach cancer that has eroded through a large blood vessel"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A Mallory-Weiss tear at the junction of the oesophagus and stomach". Repeated retching raises the pressure inside the stomach against a closed sphincter and splits the mucosa at the gastro-oesophageal junction, so the haematemesis comes after a bout of vomiting rather than before it. It is usually self-limiting, but the patient is resuscitated with fluids, the bleeding is assessed and endoscopy both confirms the tear and treats it if it is still bleeding. The history of heavy drinking makes varices a fair question, which is why the absence of stigmata of liver disease and of prior vomiting-free bleeding matters.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Varices bleed without the preceding retching and are associated with signs of chronic liver disease.
• B — Perforation gives sudden severe abdominal pain and peritonism, not haematemesis as the leading feature.
• D — Cancer usually gives weight loss, anaemia and altered appetite over weeks rather than a single bleed after vomiting.

Reference: Ghana STG — upper gastrointestinal bleeding; NICE — gastrointestinal bleeding
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vomiting first, blood after: that sequence points to a Mallory-Weiss tear, not varices.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2794$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn has increasing respiratory distress, a scaphoid abdomen and bowel sounds heard in the left chest. What is the diagnosis and what must the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pneumonia, so the baby needs intravenous antibiotics and oxygen by nasal prongs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A congenital diaphragmatic hernia, so the baby needs a stomach tube and surgery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A pneumothorax, so the baby needs a chest drain inserted at the bedside immediately"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A congenital heart defect, so the baby needs an echocardiogram before anything else"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A congenital diaphragmatic hernia, so the baby needs a stomach tube and surgery". A hole in the diaphragm lets bowel herniate into the chest, which pushes the mediastinum across and compresses the lung that should be there; that is why the abdomen looks flat while breath sounds are absent on the affected side and bowel sounds are heard in the chest. A nasogastric tube is passed at once to keep the bowel empty, the baby is intubated and ventilated if needed, and surgery follows after stabilisation. Bag-and-mask ventilation is avoided because it inflates the herniated bowel and further compresses the lung.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Pneumonia does not empty the abdomen or put bowel sounds in the chest.
• C — A pneumothorax gives hyper-resonance and absent breath sounds, but not a scaphoid abdomen with bowel sounds in the chest.
• D — The picture is mechanical, and delaying decompression to obtain an echocardiogram would be harmful.

Reference: WHO — newborn surgical emergencies; Ghana STG — congenital anomalies
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Scaphoid abdomen, bowel sounds in the chest: congenital diaphragmatic hernia. Drain the stomach, avoid bagging, call the surgeons.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHD-1774$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has circular bruises and a healing burn on his back after a traditional cupping session. How should the nurse respond?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report the practitioner to the police, since cupping is a form of assault on the patient"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Say nothing about it, because commenting on a patient's cultural practice is not allowed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask about the treatment without judgement, treat the burn and explain the infection risk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell him that cupping is scientifically proven and that he should continue the sessions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Ask about the treatment without judgement, treat the burn and explain the infection risk". Cupping is widely used in Ghana and elsewhere, and the bruises it leaves are expected; burns happen when heat is applied for too long or a flammable substance is used. The nurse's job is to ask what was done and by whom, dress the burn with a simple non-adherent dressing, look for signs of infection, check tetanus status and explain how to keep the area clean — while respecting the patient's choice. A judgemental or dismissive response only ensures he will not mention it next time.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Cupping is a recognised traditional practice; it is not an assault to be reported to the police.
• B — Silence leaves the burn untreated and misses an infection.
• D — Cupping is not proven for the conditions it is used for, and advising more sessions ignores the injury.

Reference: WHO — traditional medicine; Ghana STG — burns and traditional practices
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cupping bruises are expected; burns are not. Ask without judgement, dress the burn, check tetanus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TCM-0665$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient who has taken steroids for months develops a sudden fever, abdominal pain and shock. His stool contains larvae, and the laboratory reports Strongyloides. Why is this so dangerous?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the larvae burrow through the skin and cause severe itching that cannot be treated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because steroids allow the larvae to multiply and carry gut bacteria throughout the body"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the worm blocks the bowel and causes an obstruction needing immediate surgery"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the infection is untreatable once it has reached the bloodstream from the gut"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Because steroids allow the larvae to multiply and carry gut bacteria throughout the body". Strongyloides is unusual because its larvae can reinfect the same host through the gut wall or the skin, so the infection can persist for decades without further exposure. When immunity is suppressed, especially by corticosteroids, this cycle accelerates into hyperinfection: huge numbers of larvae cross the gut carrying enteric bacteria with them, producing sepsis and meningitis, often with Gram-negative organisms and sometimes with no eosinophilia to hint at the worm. Treatment is ivermectin, and screening and treating before steroids in at-risk patients prevents it.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Itching is a minor part of the illness, and it is treatable.
• C — The worm does not obstruct the bowel; the danger is dissemination.
• D — It is treatable with ivermectin, which is why recognising it matters.

Reference: WHO — soil-transmitted helminths; CDC — strongyloidiasis hyperinfection
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Strongyloides plus steroids can become hyperinfection — larvae carrying gut bacteria through the body. Ivermectin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2516$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn has bile-stained vomiting in the first day of life and a soft, non-distended abdomen. What does bilious vomiting in a newborn always demand?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Urgent surgical assessment, because it means obstruction until proved otherwise"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A feed of dextrose water and observation for the next twelve hours on the ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treatment for reflux, since newborns commonly bring up their feeds after eating"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassurance alone, because the abdomen is soft and there is no visible distension"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Urgent surgical assessment, because it means obstruction until proved otherwise". Bile-stained vomiting in a newborn is never normal: the obstruction may be atresia, malrotation with volvulus, meconium ileus or Hirschsprung disease, and malrotation with volvulus can strangulate the entire midgut within hours. The baby is kept nil by mouth, a nasogastric tube is passed to decompress the stomach, fluids are given and a surgeon is called while imaging is arranged. The abdomen may be soft and scaphoid rather than distended, which is exactly why the sign must be taken seriously on its own.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Feeding a baby with a possible obstruction is dangerous.
• C — Reflux is non-bilious and does not begin on the first day with green vomiting.
• D — A soft abdomen does not exclude obstruction, and reassurance delays surgery.

Reference: WHO — newborn surgical emergencies; Ghana STG — neonatal intestinal obstruction
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Green vomit in a newborn means obstruction until proved otherwise. Stop feeds, drain the stomach, call the surgeon.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLN-2978$q$;

