-- Batch 96: ten pairs: granuloma, Horner, hypo unawareness, hypothermia, PONV, p value, B12, candour, AFE, episiotomy.
--
-- ten pairs that asked the same thing twice, each moved onto ground the
-- bank did not cover.
-- 
-- ORS calculation        NUT-0262 keeps the 12 kg worked example. CHD-1765
--                        now takes an umbilical granuloma - the moist pink
--                        lump that will not dry, and the red skin that
--                        means omphalitis instead.
-- myasthenia gravis      NEURO-0013 keeps the fatigable weakness.
--                        CLINICAL-0028 now takes Horner syndrome - ptosis,
--                        a small pupil and a dry face after neck injury.
-- drip rate              FLD-2175 keeps 50 drops a minute. CLP-2851 now
--                        takes impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia: the
--                        warning is lost, so check before driving.
-- post-operative delirium SGY-0756 keeps the cause-hunt. PER-1548 now
--                        takes inadvertent hypothermia at 35 degrees in
--                        recovery: it bleeds and strains the heart.
-- day-surgery discharge  MSN-2775 keeps the responsible adult. PER-1554
--                        now takes post-operative nausea that blocks oral
--                        intake, which is a failed discharge criterion.
-- confidence interval    BST-1248 keeps the interval that crosses 1.0.
--                        BST-1259 now takes what a p value actually means.
-- insulin storage        MDS-1660 keeps the clay pot. CLP-2835 now takes
--                        eight years of metformin and numb feet - check
--                        the vitamin B12 before blaming the diabetes.
-- herbal alongside drugs MDS-1671 keeps recording and checking. PFC-0657
--                        now takes duty of candour after a wrong dose.
-- placenta praevia       OBS-0546 keeps the painless bleeding. BATCH2-0019
--                        now takes amniotic fluid embolism at rupture of
--                        membranes - collapse and blood that will not clot.
-- Naegele's rule         MAT-2365 keeps the date calculation. MOD-OBS-006
--                        now takes episiotomy: mediolateral at 60 degrees,
--                        for an indication, cut at crowning.
-- 
-- Kept as distinct: the four "who first" handover items (each teaches a
-- different priority), the respiratory against the metabolic blood gas,
-- the two sickle cell inheritance questions (affected parent against
-- carrier parents), and cervical insufficiency diagnosis against its
-- management, which is now the stitch timing rather than a repeat of the
-- diagnosis. Largest stem overlap 0.261.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A three-week-old baby has a moist pink fleshy lump at the umbilicus that oozes a little clear fluid. The skin around it is normal and he is feeding well. What is it, and what should be done?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An umbilical hernia, which needs a belt and referral for surgery within the week"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An umbilical granuloma, treated with silver nitrate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Omphalitis, which needs intravenous antibiotics and admission the same day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A normal healing stump, which needs nothing and will separate when it is ready"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "An umbilical granuloma, treated with silver nitrate". When the cord separates, the raw surface sometimes heals with a small overgrowth of granulation tissue instead of drying over. The result is a soft, pink, moist lump at the umbilicus that oozes serous fluid and will not dry, while the surrounding skin stays clean and the baby feeds normally. It is treated by cauterising the tissue with a silver nitrate stick — one or two applications are usually enough — or by tying it off; the parents are taught to keep the area dry. The important thing is to be sure the skin around it is not red and the baby is not unwell, because that would be omphalitis instead.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A hernia is a bulge under the skin that gets bigger when the baby cries and can be pushed back; it is not moist tissue.
• C — Omphalitis means spreading redness, foul discharge, tenderness and a systemically unwell baby — that is an emergency, not this picture.
• D — A stump that oozes and is fleshy at three weeks, after the cord has already separated, has not healed normally.


Reference: WHO — newborn care; Ghana STG — neonatal infection
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Moist pink lump, clean skin, well baby: granuloma, cauterise. Red skin and unwell: omphalitis, emergency.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHD-1765$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man seen after a road traffic injury has a drooping eyelid and a small pupil on the left, and the left side of his face is dry. The left eye opens and the eye movements are otherwise full. What does this combination mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A third-nerve palsy, because the eyelid droops and the pupil is abnormal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A simple ptosis of the eyelid, which is cosmetic and needs no further action"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Horner syndrome, from injury to the sympathetic pathway"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A painful red eye from the injury, which needs drops and a clinic review next week"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Horner syndrome, from injury to the sympathetic pathway". The sympathetic fibres that supply the eye travel up the neck with the carotid artery. When they are interrupted the eyelid droops, the pupil stays small, and sweating is lost on that side; the eye also looks slightly sunken. After trauma to the neck, new Horner syndrome raises the possibility of a carotid artery injury or dissection, which can cause a stroke, so it is not a cosmetic finding. In other patients the cause may be an apical lung tumour or a brainstem lesion, and the search for it is the same — urgent medical review.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A third-nerve palsy gives a widely dilated pupil and the eye is turned down and out, which is the opposite pupil finding.
• B — New ptosis with miosis is not simple or cosmetic; it points to a pathway that runs beside the carotid artery.
• D — Nothing here describes a red painful eye; the eye is pain-free and moves fully.


Reference: Ghana STG — head and neck injury; NICE — Horner syndrome
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ptosis + small pupil + dry face = Horner. New after neck trauma means carotid injury until excluded.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0028$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 26-year-old with type 1 diabetes says he no longer feels shaky or sweaty when his sugar drops, and he has had two severe hypos in a month. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Repeated lows blunt the warning, so check before driving"}, {"id": "b", "text": "He has become allergic to insulin, so the dose should be reduced and reviewed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "His insulin has gone out of date, and a fresh vial will bring the warnings back"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The sweating and shaking were never true symptoms of a low sugar at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Repeated lows blunt the warning, so check before driving". When blood glucose falls repeatedly, the body adapts and the adrenaline surge that normally produces shaking, sweating and a racing heart becomes weaker, so the first thing the patient notices may be confusion or a seizure. This is called impaired awareness of hypoglycaemia. It is dangerous because the warning is lost before the fall is severe, so the plan is to prevent lows: checking glucose before driving and at bedtime, agreeing a target with the team, and carrying fast-acting carbohydrate. Avoiding lows for a few weeks often allows the warning to return.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Allergy to insulin causes redness and swelling at the injection site, not loss of warning symptoms.
• C — Expired insulin loses potency and causes higher, not lower, sugars; it does not remove symptoms.
• D — Shakiness and sweating are genuine warning symptoms that have been blunted by repetition.


Reference: ADA — hypoglycaemia and impaired awareness; Ghana STG — diabetes
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Repeated hypos erase the warning. The fix is fewer lows — check before driving and at bedtime.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2851$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man is brought to recovery after a three-hour laparotomy. His core temperature is 35.1 °C and he is shivering. Why does this matter, and what should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It is harmless; a warm blanket is enough and he should be left to shiver it off"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It only matters if he later develops a fever, so no action is needed at this stage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It impairs clotting and strains the heart, so warm him"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Shivering means he has already rewarmed, so the temperature need not be measured"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "It impairs clotting and strains the heart, so warm him". Core temperature falls under anaesthesia because the drugs stop the body's normal heat conservation, the theatre is cool, the skin is exposed and cold fluids are infused. At 35 °C the clotting enzymes work less well, the immune response is depressed, and shivering increases oxygen demand on a heart that may be at risk, which is why passive rewarming alone is too slow. The nurse measures core temperature, applies forced-air warming, gives warmed intravenous fluids and keeps the patient covered; shivering is evidence of heat loss, not evidence that it has been corrected.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Leaving him to shiver raises oxygen consumption and helps the temperature fall further; passive cover alone is too slow.
• B — A fever is a late sign of infection and unrelated to heat loss in theatre.
• D — Shivering generates a little heat but does not mean the core has recovered; it must be measured directly.


Reference: NICE — inadvertent perioperative hypothermia; AORN — patient warming
$q$,
  strategy = $q$35 °C in recovery bleeds, infects and strains the heart. Warm actively and measure, don't watch.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1548$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman recovering from day-case gynaecological surgery is ready to go home. She has mild nausea and has not managed a meal. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Discharge her, because nausea after an anaesthetic is expected and settles at home"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treat the nausea, and delay discharge until she keeps fluids down"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a strong oral antiemetic and send her home with a supply of tablets to take"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refer her to the emergency department for intravenous fluids and overnight admission"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Treat the nausea, and delay discharge until she keeps fluids down". Day surgery works because the patient can care for herself the same day. Persistent vomiting after anaesthesia defeats that: she cannot take pain relief or fluids by mouth, risks dehydration, and a large proportion of the patients who return with complications are those sent home unwell. The nurse reassesses her against the discharge criteria — pain and nausea controlled, able to take oral fluids and pass urine where required, a responsible adult escort, and instructions understood. For nausea with no red flags, a prescribed antiemetic from an appropriate class and a period of observation usually settle things.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Nausea that prevents oral intake is a discharge criterion failure; sending her home risks dehydration and readmission.
• C — A patient who has just vomited will not keep tablets down, and it leaves the criteria unmet.
• D — There is no surgical red flag here; referral is for pain out of proportion, bleeding, fever or cardiovascular change, and would overwhelm the department.


Reference: Ghana STG — day surgery; RCoA — day-case discharge criteria
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nausea that blocks oral intake is a failed discharge criterion. Treat, observe, then decide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1554$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A trial of a new dressing reports that it healed wounds better than the standard dressing, with p = 0.03. What does that number mean?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A difference this size would arise by chance 3 times in 100"}, {"id": "b", "text": "There is a 3 per cent probability that the new dressing really does work better"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The new dressing will improve healing in exactly 3 out of every 100 patients treated"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The trial must be discarded, because any p value above 0.01 is unreliable evidence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A difference this size would arise by chance 3 times in 100". The p value answers one question only: if there were no real difference between the treatments, how often would a difference at least this big appear through random variation? A value of 0.03 means about three times in a hundred. That is surprising enough for the result to be called statistically significant, but it says nothing about how big the benefit is, whether it will matter to a patient, or how well the study was run. The size of the benefit comes from the effect estimate, and usefulness comes from the confidence interval.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The p value is a statement about the data under a null hypothesis, not the probability that a treatment is active.
• C — That is an absolute risk difference, which needs the numbers healed in each group; p alone cannot give it.
• D — There is no threshold below which 0.01 is the only trustworthy figure; the value is judged with the design, size and effect.


Reference: BMJ — statistics at square one; STROBE reporting
$q$,
  strategy = $q$p = 0.03 means a 3-in-100 fluke if there were no effect. It is not the chance the drug works.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BST-1259$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man who has taken metformin for eight years for type 2 diabetes reports numb feet and a tingling in his fingers. His glucose control is reasonable. What should the nurse consider?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Diabetic neuropathy from high glucose, so the metformin dose should be increased"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The numbness is unrelated to any medicine and needs no investigation at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Metformin must be stopped permanently, because any neuropathy on it is drug damage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Long-term metformin lowers vitamin B12, so check the level"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Long-term metformin lowers vitamin B12, so check the level". Metformin reduces the absorption of vitamin B12, and after several years of treatment a proportion of patients develop a deficiency that shows itself as numbness and tingling in the feet and hands, sometimes with a macrocytic anaemia or sore tongue. Glucose control that is reasonable makes diabetic neuropathy less likely as the only explanation, though both can coexist. The nurse checks the B12 level and reports it; if it is low, replacement is given and the metformin can usually continue, with the level rechecked.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Neuropathy from high glucose is possible, but the glucose is reasonable and raising the dose would not treat it.
• B — Loss of sensation has causes that include treatable deficiency, and ignoring it allows progression and falls.
• C — Metformin is not stopped permanently for a B12 deficiency; the deficiency is corrected and the drug is usually continued.


Reference: BNF — metformin and vitamin B12; NICE — diabetic foot care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Years of metformin → check B12 before labelling numb feet as diabetic neuropathy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2835$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient receives the wrong dose of an antibiotic. He is not harmed, but he is angry and asks what happened. What is the nurse's professional duty?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Say nothing until the incident review is finished, so as not to alarm the patient"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain that the dose was within the normal range, even though it was not, to protect the team"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell him what happened, apologise and explain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the whole explanation to the manager, because the nurse cannot discuss an incident"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Tell him what happened, apologise and explain". Being open with patients when something has gone wrong is a professional duty, not a matter of choice. The patient is told promptly that an error occurred and what is known, given a sincere apology, told what will be done to put it right and to stop it happening again, and records are made. An apology is not an admission of legal liability; withholding the truth is what damages trust and, when discovered, damages the organisation far more. The incident is reported through the local system at the same time.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Waiting for a review leaves the patient with unanswered questions and is the opposite of candour.
• B — Misstating the dose is dishonesty, which destroys the trust the profession depends on.
• D — The nurse who gave the dose is exactly the person who should begin the conversation, with support from seniors.


Reference: NMC — duty of candour; Ghana Health Service — incident reporting
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Say it early, say it plainly, apologise and explain the fix. Silence is the failure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PFC-0657$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at term has just had her membranes ruptured artificially. Within minutes she becomes breathless and hypotensive, then unresponsive, and the blood oozing from her cannula site will not clot. What is happening?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Amniotic fluid embolism, needing resuscitation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Anaphylaxis to the oxytocin, which will settle once the infusion is stopped immediately"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A vasovagal reaction to the procedure, which needs reassurance and a cool drink only"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The normal discomfort of labour, which needs pain relief and continued close observation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Amniotic fluid embolism, needing resuscitation". Amniotic fluid entering the maternal circulation sets off a violent reaction: sudden breathlessness, hypoxia, hypotension and collapse during labour, at rupture of membranes or immediately after delivery, followed by haemorrhage that will not clot because of disseminated intravascular coagulation. It is rare and unpredictable, and there is no specific antidote — survival depends on recognising it and calling for help at once, then supporting breathing and circulation, replacing blood and clotting factors and delivering the baby. The fetal heart also falls, so the team prepares for delivery.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Anaphylaxis is possible with any drug, but it does not explain the bleeding that will not clot.
• C — A vasovagal faint does not leave a woman breathless, hypotensive and coagulopathic.
• D — Collapse with bleeding is never normal labour pain; treating it as such loses the minutes that matter.


Reference: RCOG — amniotic fluid embolism; Ghana STG — obstetric emergencies
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Collapse at membrane rupture plus bleeding that will not clot = amniotic fluid embolism. Resuscitate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A midwife is delivering a woman having her first baby. The head is crowning and the fetal heart has just slowed, so the doctor asks for an episiotomy. What is the correct practice?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cut in the midline, straight towards the anus, because it heals most easily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cut mediolaterally at about 60 degrees, for a clear indication"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cut routinely for every first delivery, to prevent an uncontrolled tear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cut after the head is born, to make room for the shoulders"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Cut mediolaterally at about 60 degrees, for a clear indication". Episiotomy is not routine: it is done for a reason — a fetal heart that has fallen and delivery must be quickened, a shoulder dystocia, an instrumental delivery, a breech, a rigid or scarred perineum, or a tear that is already extending. The cut is mediolateral, starting at the posterior fourchette and directed at about 60 degrees from the midline, which keeps it away from the anal sphincter; local anaesthetic is infiltrated first if there is time, and the cut is timed to crowning when the perineum is thin. A mediolateral cut at the right angle lowers the risk of third and fourth degree tears compared with a midline cut, and the repair is done in layers with the apex identified.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A midline cut heals neatly but runs straight into the sphincter, and it is the angle that causes the worst tears.
• C — Routine episiotomy for every first birth increases blood loss and perineal pain without preventing tears; current practice is restrictive.
• D — Cutting after the head is out does not help the head and adds a wound the woman did not need.

Reference: WHO — intrapartum care; Ghana STG — perineal care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Episiotomy is for an indication, not a ritual: mediolateral at 60 degrees, cut at crowning.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-006$q$;

