UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man is switched from an insulin syringe to a pen device. He is shown how to dial his dose. What else must he be taught?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the needle attached after every injection so the pen is always ready to use"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prime with two units before each injection and hold the needle in for about ten seconds"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Store the pen he is using in the freezer so that the insulin stays fresh to the last unit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Use the same spot on the abdomen each time so that the absorption is always the same"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Prime with two units before each injection and hold the needle in for about ten seconds". An insulin pen needs priming because air can enter the cartridge when a new needle is fitted; dialling two units and injecting into the air until a drop appears proves the needle is patent and the dose will be accurate. Once the dose is given the needle is left in place for about ten seconds, so that the full volume leaves the pen rather than leaking back, and the needle is then removed. Leaving a needle on between doses lets air in and insulin crystallise in the needle, and it also changes the dose the pen delivers. Sites are rotated within the same general area to prevent lumps that absorb unpredictably.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A needle left on lets air into the cartridge and blocks the flow; it is removed after each dose.
• C — Freezing damages the insulin protein; the in-use pen stays at room temperature.
• D — Injecting the same point repeatedly causes lipohypertrophy, which makes absorption erratic.


Reference: Ghana STG — diabetes; Diabetes UK — insulin pen technique
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pen: prime two units, hold ten seconds, needle off, sites rotated, never frozen.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2450$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 68-year-old is admitted for a total hip replacement. The team prescribes enoxaparin, compression stockings and early walking. Why are all three used?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "They are given only to patients who already have a clot, to stop it growing larger"}, {"id": "b", "text": "They are routine for every patient over 60 and have no relation to the operation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hip surgery carries a high risk of clot, and drug, stockings and movement each reduce it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "They are used to prevent bleeding into the wound, which is the main risk after hip surgery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Hip surgery carries a high risk of clot, and drug, stockings and movement each reduce it". Surgery on the hip and pelvis is one of the highest-risk situations for deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism, because the operation damages veins, the patient lies still and the blood is in a low-flow state for days. Prevention is layered: an anticoagulant such as enoxaparin reduces clot formation, graduated compression stockings counter venous stasis, and early mobilisation restores the calf muscle pump. The nurse checks the platelet count and renal function, watches for bleeding, gives the injection subcutaneously into the abdominal wall without rubbing the site, and makes sure the stockings are correctly fitted.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Prophylaxis is given before any clot exists; treating an established clot needs a therapeutic dose.
• B — Age adds risk but the operation itself is the reason these measures are prescribed.
• D — These measures prevent clotting; bleeding is a side effect to watch for, not the target.


Reference: NICE — venous thromboembolism prevention; Ghana STG — perioperative care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$High-risk surgery gets all three: drug, stocking, movement. Check platelets, kidney and bleeding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2439$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother in a village health post has a child with diarrhoea and some dehydration. There is no sachet of oral rehydration salts. What should the nurse teach her to make at home?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "One litre of clean water with six level teaspoons of sugar and half a level teaspoon of salt"}, {"id": "b", "text": "One litre of clean water with six level teaspoons of sugar and two level teaspoons of salt, which is stronger and works faster"}, {"id": "c", "text": "One litre of fruit juice with one teaspoon of salt added, given with a spoon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "One litre of clean water with sugar alone, because the salt makes the child thirsty"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "One litre of clean water with six level teaspoons of sugar and half a level teaspoon of salt". Where a sachet is not available, the home mixture is one litre of clean water, six level teaspoons of sugar and half a level teaspoon of salt — mixed until the sugar dissolves and tasting no saltier than tears. The sugar is absorbed with the salt and drags water across the gut wall, which is why both are needed; the salt must be measured level with the back of a knife, because too much salt can raise the sodium dangerously, especially in a small child. It is given by cup and spoon after every loose stool, and any mixture left after 24 hours is thrown away.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Two teaspoons of salt is four times too much and risks hypernatraemia, especially in an infant.
• C — Fruit juice is high in sugar and low in salt, and the balance is wrong for rehydration.
• D — Sugar without salt cannot drive water absorption, and the child becomes more dehydrated.


Reference: WHO — diarrhoea treatment guidelines; Ghana STG — childhood diarrhoea
$q$,
  strategy = $q$One litre, six sugar, half salt, level spoons, discarded after a day.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-2174$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man has a nephrostomy tube in place after a blocked ureter. What should the nurse do to keep it safe and working?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clamp the tube at night so that he can sleep without the bag pulling on the kidney"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Change the bag every two hours, because urine becomes infected once it stands in the bag"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lie him flat after insertion, so that the tube does not kink inside the kidney"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the bag below the kidney, secure the tube, never clamp without an order"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Keep the bag below the kidney, secure the tube, never clamp without an order". A nephrostomy drains urine straight from the renal pelvis to a bag outside the body, and it works by gravity. The bag stays below the kidney so urine cannot flow back and infect it, the tube is secured with tape so that a pull does not displace it, and the bag is emptied regularly with the spout cleaned to prevent contamination. The tube is never clamped unless a doctor orders it — clamping with a blocked or single kidney can back up urine and cause sepsis. The nurse also watches the volume and colour of urine, the dressing for leakage, and the temperature.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Clamping with no order blocks drainage and can cause infection or kidney injury.
• B — Draining and cleaning the bag when it is two-thirds full is enough; changing it hourly risks contamination and trauma.
• C — He is nursed at his own comfort; lying flat does nothing for kinking and promotes stasis and chest infection.


Reference: Ghana STG — urology; RCN — catheter and nephrostomy care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gravity drainage, tube secured, bag below kidney, no clamping without an order.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2770$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with known rheumatic heart disease is now 12 weeks pregnant. She is breathless on walking uphill and has been told her pregnancy is high risk. Why does the nurse arrange joint cardiology and obstetric review?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pregnancy raises blood volume and cardiac output, so her heart may fail later"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The pregnancy will damage her valves further, and she will certainly need valve surgery this year"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rheumatic heart disease is dangerous only to the baby, not to the mother, so the review is for the fetus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "She should stop all cardiac medicines now, because any heart drug harms the unborn baby"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Pregnancy raises blood volume and cardiac output, so her heart may fail later". Pregnancy increases plasma volume and cardiac output by a third or more, peaking in the third trimester and staying high for the first days after delivery, so a heart with narrowed or leaking valves is asked to do much more work. The danger period is late pregnancy, labour and the immediate postpartum, when pulmonary oedema and heart failure can appear suddenly. That is why she is seen early, booked with a team that includes cardiology and obstetrics, monitored for breathlessness, orthopnoea and a new cough, and planned for a delivery that avoids the strain of a long second stage.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Pregnancy does not itself destroy valves, and most women are managed medically through pregnancy; surgery is not automatic.
• C — Rheumatic heart disease is a major cause of maternal death; both mother and baby are at risk.
• D — Some cardiac drugs are continued and others are changed, but stopping them all is dangerous — the decision is made case by case.


Reference: WHO — cardiac disease in pregnancy; Ghana STG — medical disorders in pregnancy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Volume goes up by a third: watch for failure in the third trimester and after delivery.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0023$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A two-week-old has not regained his birth weight. His mother has cracked, painful nipples, and feeds are noisy with clicking. The tongue cannot lift to the roof of the mouth or poke past the gums. What is the problem and what is needed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "He has a cleft palate that was missed at birth, and he needs immediate surgical repair"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The milk supply is low, so the mother should stop breastfeeding and use formula only"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A tight tongue tie is preventing a deep latch, so he needs lactation support and referral"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He has gastro-oesophageal reflux, so feeds should be thickened and he should sleep upright"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A tight tongue tie is preventing a deep latch, so he needs lactation support and referral". The frenulum that anchors the tongue is short and tight in ankyloglossia, so the baby cannot cup the breast or seal and strip milk; the feed is shallow and noisy, the mother's nipples become cracked and painful, and weight gain falters because transfer is inefficient. The examination confirms it when the tongue cannot lift to the palate or protrude beyond the lower gum. Care begins with positioning and latch support, and if feeding remains ineffective the baby is referred for a frenotomy, which is quick and usually improves feeding at once. The mother is also helped to express and keep her supply while the latch is fixed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A cleft palate would show a gap in the roof of the mouth and milk coming down the nose; the tongue restriction would not be the finding.
• B — Stopping breastfeeding removes the best food because of a mechanical problem that can be corrected.
• D — Reflux causes possetting and arching after feeds, not a clicking latch with a tongue that cannot lift.


Reference: WHO — infant feeding; NICE — ankyloglossia in infants
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clicking feeds, cracked nipples and a tongue that cannot lift: tongue tie. Support the latch, then refer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHD-1757$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is sedated and ventilated in the intensive care unit after emergency surgery. Why does the nurse clean his mouth and teeth several times a day?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because a dry mouth makes the lips crack and this is the main source of ward infection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because the antiseptic used on the gums prevents stomach ulcers in ventilated patients"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because plaque is aspirated into the lungs and causes pneumonia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because cleaning the mouth stops the tube from becoming blocked with secretions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Because plaque is aspirated into the lungs and causes pneumonia". A ventilated patient cannot swallow or clear his mouth, so plaque builds up on the teeth within a day or two and is rich in the organisms that cause pneumonia. Microaspiration around the cuff carries those organisms into the lower airway, and ventilator-associated pneumonia follows, prolonging ventilation and increasing mortality. Cleaning the teeth and gums with a soft brush, using the prescribed antiseptic, suctioning the secretions and keeping the head of the bed raised all reduce that risk. The nurse also checks the mouth for ulcers and dryness, because a dry mouth is uncomfortable even when the patient cannot say so.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Cracked lips are uncomfortable but are not the pathway by which pneumonia develops.
• B — Oral antiseptic is given to reduce plaque and oral organisms, not to prevent gastric ulcers.
• D — Tube blockage comes from secretions and kinking; mouth care does not keep the tube patent.


Reference: NICE — hospital-acquired pneumonia; Ghana STG — critical care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Plaque is aspirated around the cuff. Brush, suction, antiseptic, head up.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-OPR-1161$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A family in a rural district has been eating cassava flour that was dried quickly in the sun after a poor harvest. A man presents with confusion, deep rapid breathing and a severe metabolic acidosis. What should the nurse consider?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Organophosphate poisoning from pesticide sprayed on the cassava, needing atropine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Alcohol intoxication from fermented cassava, needing only observation and thiamine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cyanide poisoning from poorly processed cassava, needing oxygen and the antidote"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Food poisoning from bacterial contamination, needing antibiotics and intravenous fluids"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Cyanide poisoning from poorly processed cassava, needing oxygen and the antidote". Cassava contains cyanogenic glycosides, and proper processing — soaking, fermenting, drying for long enough — removes them. When the flour is dried quickly after a poor harvest, the cyanide remains. Acute poisoning blocks the body's ability to use oxygen, so the tissues switch to anaerobic metabolism and a severe lactic acidosis develops with confusion, deep breathing, convulsions and collapse; the venous blood can look bright red because oxygen is not being used. The patient needs oxygen, removal from the source and the antidote — hydroxocobalamin where available, or sodium thiosulfate — while the acidosis is corrected.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Organophosphates cause a cholinergic picture with slow heart rate, small pupils and excessive secretions, not this acidosis.
• B — Alcohol does not produce a severe lactic acidosis of this kind, and thiamine alone would not treat collapse.
• D — Food poisoning causes vomiting and diarrhoea; it does not explain the acidosis and confusion together.


Reference: WHO — cassava cyanide and konzo; Ghana STG — poisoning
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cassava, acidosis and confusion = cyanide. Oxygen plus antidote, and ask about the drying.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2733$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 7-year-old has fever, pain on chewing and swelling of both cheeks. He has had no immunisation. What is the diagnosis and what matters most?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mumps, which spreads by droplets and can cause meningitis and deafness"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A dental abscess, which needs extraction of the offending tooth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bacterial parotitis, which needs intravenous antibiotics and admission"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Measles, which needs vitamin A and isolation for seven days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Mumps, which spreads by droplets and can cause meningitis and deafness". Mumps is caused by a paramyxovirus and produces fever and painful swelling of one or both parotid glands, so the child complains of pain when chewing and the swelling fills the angle of the jaw. It spreads by droplets before the swelling appears and while it lasts, so he is kept away from other children, and the illness is notifiable. Most children recover, but the complications matter: meningitis, deafness, pancreatitis and, after puberty, orchitis that can affect fertility. Two doses of measles-mumps-rubella vaccine prevent it, and the case is a reminder to check the immunisation status of the household.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A dental abscess is painful and localised to a tooth with a cavity and a swelling pointing in the mouth, not bilateral cheek swelling.
• C — Bacterial parotitis is usually unilateral, in a debilitated or dehydrated patient, with pus from the duct.
• D — Measles gives a rash, cough and red eyes, not isolated parotid swelling.


Reference: WHO — mumps; Ghana STG — vaccine-preventable disease
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fever plus both cheeks, worse on chewing, in an unimmunised child: mumps. Droplets, deafness, MMR.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2495$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with pneumonia has a temperature of 39 °C, a pulse of 124, a respiratory rate of 28 and a systolic pressure of 88. The sepsis pathway is triggered. Which set of actions belongs to the first hour?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Oxygen, cultures, IV antibiotics, fluids and lactate, watching urine output"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait for the chest X-ray and the full blood count before giving any antibiotic at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral antibiotics and send him home, because the pressure is only slightly low"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give a diuretic for the fast breathing and repeat the observations in four hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Oxygen, cultures, IV antibiotics, fluids and lactate, watching urine output". Sepsis kills by the hour, and the first-hour bundle is built around that: give oxygen to keep the saturations in range, take blood cultures before antibiotics where this does not delay them, give intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics within the first hour, start a fluid bolus and reassess the response, measure lactate, and catheterise or monitor urine output so that the kidney is watched. The source is looked for at the same time, and the patient is handed over with the time each element was completed. Waiting for results that will not change the decision is the commonest reason the hour is lost.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The X-ray and blood count must not delay antibiotics; cultures are taken and treatment starts.
• C — This is sepsis with hypotension, which needs admission and intravenous treatment, not tablets at home.
• D — Fast breathing in sepsis is compensation for acidosis and hypoxaemia; a diuretic does not treat the cause and worsens the hypovolaemia.


Reference: Surviving Sepsis Campaign; Ghana STG — sepsis in adults
$q$,
  strategy = $q$First hour: oxygen, cultures, IV antibiotics, fluids, lactate, urine output. Do not wait for the X-ray.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2754$q$;

-- Verify: expect 10
COMMIT;

