-- Batch 98: nine pairs: teething, terminal clean, test dose, dyspepsia alarm, adjustment reaction, referral note, verbal autopsy, catatonia, organ donation.
--
-- nine pairs that asked the same thing twice. Ten were picked; one was
-- pulled at the last moment because its replacement already existed and
-- has been left for the next batch rather than forced.
-- 
-- cholera rehydration    IPC-0008 keeps the rapid Ringer's lactate.
--                        IPC-0011 now takes the terminal clean after a
--                        patient with diarrhoea leaves the room.
-- insulin mixing         ADV-0110 keeps clear before cloudy. DRC-2412 now
--                        takes the test dose: given to catch anaphylaxis
--                        while only a little has gone in.
-- postnatal weight       PNC-1898 keeps the 8 per cent loss. CHD-1771 now
--                        takes teething - dribbling yes, fever no, and
--                        the malaria that hides behind "it is only
--                        teething".
-- stomal ischaemia       MOD-PERIOP-017 keeps the dusky stoma.
--                        MOD-GI-015 now takes dyspepsia with weight loss
--                        and dysphagia, which is an urgent endoscopy.
-- delirium               GER-0592 keeps the overnight confusion.
--                        MHN-0835 now takes an adjustment reaction three
--                        weeks after redundancy, and the features that
--                        would make it a depression.
-- verbal orders          ADV-0117 keeps write-read-back-sign. FND-2604 now
--                        takes what a referral note must contain.
-- natural is safe        TCM-0668 keeps the active-chemicals answer.
--                        TRD-2100 now takes consent for organ donation -
--                        voluntary, and the family is part of it.
-- herbal jaundice        TCM-0671 keeps the Food and Drugs Authority
--                        report. TRD-2135 now takes the verbal autopsy and
--                        why deaths at home are counted.
-- pain assessment        PDT-0586 keeps the behavioural tool. PSY-1437 now
--                        takes catatonia - stupor, waxy flexibility and a
--                        young man who has not eaten for two days.
-- 
-- Kept: both hypothermia and both hypomagnesaemia pairs on inspection
-- turned out to be different questions, and the dose-too-high pair
-- (MDS-1655/DRC-2482) is left for the next batch because every
-- replacement topic I probed for it was already taken. Largest stem
-- overlap 0.25.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A six-month-old is dribbling, chewing his fists and fretful at night, and his mother has felt a slight warmth. She is sure it is teething and does not want him seen. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Teething explains the drooling and chewing, but a fever needs another cause looked for"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Teething causes a high fever and diarrhoea, so no other cause ever needs to be looked for"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Teething is not a real phenomenon, and the child should be investigated for a brain problem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The child is developing normally and nothing else needs to be asked about or checked at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Teething explains the drooling and chewing, but a fever needs another cause looked for". Teething does cause dribbling, a urge to chew, mild gum swelling, disturbed sleep and sometimes a slightly raised temperature, and the nurse can help the mother with a cool teething ring, a clean finger rubbed on the gum and simple analgesia in the right dose. What it does not cause is a fever above about 38 °C, diarrhoea, vomiting, a rash or a child who will not feed or be consoled. Those need a full assessment — malaria, respiratory infection, ear infection, gastroenteritis — because a real illness is easily put down to teething and missed.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Attributing high fever and diarrhoea to teething is the classic way a malaria or a gastroenteritis is missed.
• C — Teething is a normal developmental stage, not a sign of a neurological problem.
• D — Accepting the mother's explanation without asking about fever, feeding and wet nappies leaves illness undetected.


Reference: WHO — integrated management of childhood illness; Ghana STG — child health
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dribbling and chewing: teething. Fever, diarrhoea or a child who will not feed: look for a cause.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHD-1771$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with a diarrhoeal illness has been discharged from a single room on the ward. What must happen to that room before another patient is put in it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Change the bed linen, because the mattress, the floor and the toilet do not pass infection on"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A terminal clean: every surface, the bed, the toilet and the curtains"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Open the door and leave the room to air for an hour, since the organism cannot survive in air"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wipe the bed frame once with a detergent wipe, which is all a discharged room needs before re-use"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A terminal clean: every surface, the bed, the toilet and the curtains". Organisms that cause diarrhoea, and Clostridioides difficile and norovirus in particular, survive for days or weeks on bed rails, call bells, door handles, toilets and floors, and they resist many ordinary cleaners. A terminal clean is done once the patient has left: all surfaces are cleaned with detergent and then disinfected with a chlorine-based product at the right strength, the bed and mattress are checked, the toilet and bathroom are cleaned, and the curtains are changed and laundered. The cleaner wears the right gloves and apron, and the room is not re-let until the clean is complete and documented.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Linen alone leaves the surfaces that hands touch contaminated, which is how the next patient is infected.
• C — Airing does not disinfect a room; spores in particular survive on surfaces indefinitely.
• D — A single detergent wipe of one surface removes neither the soil nor the organisms from the rest of the room.


Reference: NHS — terminal cleaning standards; Ghana IPC guidelines
$q$,
  strategy = $q$After a diarrhoeal patient leaves: detergent, then chlorine, on every surface — including the toilet and curtains.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IPC-0011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is to start a new antibiotic, and the prescription includes a small test dose before the full dose. Why is this done?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To find out whether the drug works, so that the full dose can be withheld if it does not"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To check that the patient can swallow a tablet before the injection is changed to oral treatment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To detect a severe reaction while only a tiny amount has been given"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To measure how quickly the kidneys clear the drug, so the maintenance dose can be calculated"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "To detect a severe reaction while only a tiny amount has been given". A test dose is given where the risk of a severe hypersensitivity reaction is real — a history of reaction to a related drug, a known allergen, or a desensitisation programme. A small amount is given and the patient is observed closely for rash, itching, swelling, wheeze, a fall in blood pressure or a rising pulse; the route, dose and observation period come from the protocol. If a reaction starts, it is treated at once with the emergency drugs to hand, and the rest of the dose is not given. The observation must be active, not a glance, because a reaction can begin within minutes.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Whether the drug works is judged over days by the patient's condition, not by a small dose.
• B — Swallowing is a separate assessment and has nothing to do with a test dose of an antibiotic.
• D — Kidney clearance is judged by renal function and drug levels, not by a test dose.


Reference: BNF — test doses and hypersensitivity; Ghana STG — antimicrobials
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A test dose is about anaphylaxis, not efficacy. Give small, watch closely, have adrenaline ready.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2412$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 52-year-old has had indigestion for six weeks. He has lost weight without trying and says food sticks on the way down. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Refer urgently for endoscopy, because these are alarm features"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Advise an antacid and review him in three months, as indigestion is usually benign and self-limiting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure him that the symptoms are caused by stress and suggest a change of diet and more rest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Request a barium meal in a year, when the next endoscopy list has space for non-urgent cases"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Refer urgently for endoscopy, because these are alarm features". Most indigestion is benign, but a small group of features marks a higher chance of cancer or of a stricture, and they change the pathway from treat-and-review to investigate now. They are: new indigestion over the age of 45 to 50, unintentional weight loss, difficulty or pain on swallowing, vomiting, bleeding or anaemia, an abdominal mass, and a family history. This man has two of them. He is referred for urgent endoscopy, and the nurse explains why, keeps him on the list, and records the features in the referral so the urgency is understood.

Why the other options fail:
• B — An antacid with a three-month review is the pathway for simple indigestion without alarm features, not for this man.
• C — Attributing weight loss and dysphagia to stress delays a diagnosis that is treatable when it is made early.
• D — A test booked a year away is not urgent investigation; the delay could allow a cancer to advance.


Reference: NICE — dyspepsia and gastro-oesophageal reflux; Ghana STG — upper GI cancer
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Age, weight loss, dysphagia, bleeding: any alarm feature turns dyspepsia into an urgent endoscopy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Three weeks after being made redundant, a man has low mood, poor sleep, and cannot stop replaying the loss. He still works around the house, meets friends and says he will look for work. What is the most likely explanation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A major depressive episode, which needs an antidepressant started at the first appointment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An adjustment reaction to a clear stressor, which usually settles with support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A psychotic illness, because he cannot stop thinking about the events of that month"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Normal and nothing to ask about, so he should be sent away without any assessment today"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "An adjustment reaction to a clear stressor, which usually settles with support". An adjustment disorder begins within about a month of an identifiable stressor and shows as low mood, anxiety, worry or a sense of being unable to cope, but the criteria for a depressive episode are not met and the person is still functioning. It is common, it is distressing, and it usually improves as the situation is worked through. What helps is listening, naming the reaction, practical problem-solving, sleep and routine, and the offer of a review. The nurse watches for the features that would change the diagnosis: pervasive low mood and loss of interest for two weeks or more, hopelessness, or thoughts of self-harm.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Antidepressants are not the first step for a short-lived reaction to a stressor in someone who is still functioning.
• C — Rumination about a real event is not psychosis; psychosis means loss of contact with reality, such as delusions or hallucinations.
• D — He is distressed enough to need assessment, and a brief assessment is what separates a reaction from an illness.


Reference: WHO — mhGAP; ICD-11 — adjustment disorder
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stressor within a month, functioning preserved: adjustment reaction. Watch for the shift into depression.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MHN-0835$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient at a CHPS compound needs referral to the district hospital. What must the referral note contain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Only the patient's name and the diagnosis, so that the hospital does not repeat any unnecessary tests"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The reason, findings, treatment given, allergies and what the patient has been told"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A complete copy of the entire health record, because the hospital has no way to obtain one itself"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The nurse's opinion alone, with the clinical findings left out so the doctor is not influenced by them"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The reason, findings, treatment given, allergies and what the patient has been told". A referral note is a handover of care, and what the next clinician needs is a compact, accurate picture: why the patient is being sent, what has been found on examination and testing, what treatment has already been given and when, what the patient is allergic to, and what the patient has been told and agreed to. Treatment already given matters especially — a dose of an antibiotic or of quinine changes what the receiving team does next. The note carries the nurse's name and contact, the patient's identifiers, and the time of referral, and a copy stays in the record.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Name and diagnosis alone tells the receiving team nothing about what has been done, and errors follow.
• C — Copying a whole record buries the reason for referral; the summary is what gets read.
• D — Omitting the findings removes the evidence the doctor needs and invites repeated examination and delay.


Reference: Ghana Health Service — referral guidelines; WHO — continuity of care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A referral is a handover: why, what was found, what was given, allergies, and what the patient knows.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FND-2604$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In a rural district a man dies at home with no doctor in attendance. Why does the health system carry out a verbal autopsy?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To decide whether the family should be prosecuted for failing to bring him to hospital in time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To replace the medical certificate of cause of death, which is optional in rural districts of Ghana"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To work out a probable cause of death, so that deaths are counted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To help the family claim an insurance payout, which requires a stated cause of death to be paid"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "To work out a probable cause of death, so that deaths are counted". Most deaths in many low-income settings happen at home, uncounted and uncertified, and a health system that cannot say what people die of cannot plan services or measure progress. A verbal autopsy uses a structured interview with the family, often with a questionnaire and a review by clinicians or an algorithm, to assign a probable cause. It gives population-level information — how much is malaria, how much is stroke, how much is maternal — and it is not a legal determination for an individual death, nor a substitute for a certificate where one is required.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The purpose is public health measurement, not blame; a blame-framed process would destroy the cooperation it depends on.
• B — It supplements, rather than replaces, the legal certification system, and it cannot name a cause with certainty in an individual.
• D — Insurance and legal processes require formal certification, not a research interview.


Reference: WHO — verbal autopsy standards; Ghana — vital registration
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Verbal autopsy counts deaths for planning. It is public health data, not a legal cause of death.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRD-2135$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A young man on the psychiatric ward stands motionless, does not answer, and keeps his arm where the nurse places it. He has not eaten for two days. What is this, and what does it need?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Catatonia, which needs urgent assessment, fluids and treatment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Deliberate non-cooperation, which is managed by withdrawing privileges until he chooses to respond"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A depressive stupor that needs nothing more than time and a quiet room until the episode passes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Malingering, because he is clearly able to stand and to keep his arm where it has been placed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Catatonia, which needs urgent assessment, fluids and treatment". Stupor, mutism, waxy flexibility — holding a limb where it is positioned — posturing and negativism together make up catatonia. It is a syndrome, not a diagnosis: it occurs in schizophrenia and mood disorders but also in medical illness, including encephalitis and other organic causes, so a physical screen comes first. It is dangerous: the patient does not eat or drink, cannot protect his airway, and is at risk of pressure injury, clot and aspiration. Treatment begins with a benzodiazepine challenge and supportive care, with electroconvulsive therapy for the severe or refractory case.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Withdrawing privileges treats a sign of illness as bad behaviour and leaves a deteriorating patient untreated.
• C — A stuporose patient who has not eaten for two days is a medical emergency, not something to wait out.
• D — Waxy flexibility is an involuntary sign; it is not something a person can perform convincingly.


Reference: Royal College of Psychiatrists — catatonia; Ghana STG — psychiatry
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Stupor plus waxy flexibility is catatonia: screen for a physical cause, then benzodiazepine and ECT.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PSY-1437$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A family whose relative has been declared dead is asked about organ donation. What should the nurse know about consent?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A donor card signed in life lets the team proceed even if the family objects at the bedside"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Donation can be assumed unless the family registers an objection in writing before the death"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only a doctor may say anything about donation, so the nurse must end the conversation at once"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Donation is voluntary and needs the family's agreement, given without pressure"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Donation is voluntary and needs the family's agreement, given without pressure". Organ and tissue donation rests on valid consent, and in Ghana, as in most systems, the family is part of that decision. Brain death is confirmed by defined clinical criteria, separately from any discussion of donation, and the conversation is held by trained staff in a private place, in plain language, once the family has understood that the relative has died. The nurse's role is to support the family, answer what she can, and pass questions on — never to push. A decision either way is respected, and it does not change the care the family receives.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A card expresses the person's wish but does not override a family who object; the discussion still happens.
• B — Assumed consent is not the standard, and a written-objection rule would be a different legal system.
• C — Ending the conversation abandons a family who have asked a direct question; the nurse answers within her competence and refers on.


Reference: WHO — organ and tissue donation; Ghana Health Service — transplantation policy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Donation is voluntary and the family is part of consent. Explain, support, never push.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRD-2100$q$;

-- Verify: expect 9
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch99.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 99: nine pairs: prophylaxis timing, NSAID in CKD, warfarin preconception, verifying death, Kawasaki, herbal bath, lithium, heel prick, dissection.
--
-- nine pairs that asked the same thing twice.
-- 
-- tablet arithmetic       DRC-2390 keeps the microgram conversion. ADV-0106
--                         now takes antibiotic prophylaxis timing - in the
--                         tissue before the knife goes in.
-- adult IM site           MDS-1648 keeps the ventrogluteal site. CLP-2834 now
--                         takes ibuprofen in chronic kidney disease, and the
--                         prostaglandin that keeps the kidney perfused.
-- wrong drug given        MAN-0873 keeps assess-then-report. PKD-1231 now
--                         takes warfarin before conception - embryopathy and
--                         fetal bleeding.
-- catheter asepsis        NCX-0206 keeps the labia held apart. RESP-0009 now
--                         takes verifying death and what the nurse records.
-- breast attachment       MAT-2329 keeps the attachment signs. CHD-1745 now
--                         takes Kawasaki disease before day ten.
-- natural means safe      TRD-2106 keeps the active-chemical answer.
--                         TCM-0668 now takes a herbal bath for a newborn -
--                         ask, explain, agree plain water.
-- dose too high           MDS-1655 keeps withhold-and-query. DRC-2482 now
--                         takes lithium before conception: stopping abruptly
--                         relapses, continuing needs counselling.
-- post-catheter bleeding  NCX-1120 keeps pressure on the site. PEDS2-0015 now
--                         takes the newborn screening heel prick - the outer
--                         heel, warmed, never the centre.
-- silent infarct          MSN-2692 keeps the blunted cardiac pain. CVM-1494
--                         now takes aortic dissection - tearing pain to the
--                         back and unequal arm pressures.
-- 
-- Kept after reading both: the respiratory against the metabolic blood gas,
-- sickle disease against sickle trait inheritance, low sodium against high
-- sodium correction, and the two renal versus post-streptococcal items that
-- only shared a template. Largest stem overlap 0.333.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is having a hip replacement. The surgeon asks why the antibiotic prophylaxis must be running before the knife goes in. What is the nurse's answer?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tissue levels must be high at incision, so it is given within 60 minutes before"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It is given after the wound is closed, to treat any organisms that have landed on the wound"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is given only if the operation lasts more than four hours, when infection becomes likely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is given the night before, so that a full course has time to work through the bloodstream"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Tissue levels must be high at incision, so it is given within 60 minutes before". Surgical prophylaxis is about preventing infection in the wound, and the critical moment is when the incision is made. Giving the drug within about 60 minutes before the knife goes in means that the tissue concentration is at its peak when the wound opens and the first organisms are introduced; that is why the timing is checked with the theatre team and recorded. Some drugs need to start earlier — vancomycin takes an hour to infuse — and prophylaxis is repeated during a long operation or after heavy blood loss. The course is short; continuing it for days is not prophylaxis and breeds resistance.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Giving it after closure misses the moment of contamination, when prophylaxis actually works.
• C — Duration is not the trigger; the drug is given before every incision, and a long operation means a repeat dose, not a first dose.
• D — A dose the night before gives tissue levels that have already fallen by the morning and encourages resistance.


Reference: NICE — surgical site infection; SIGN 104; Ghana STG — perioperative care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prophylaxis must be in the tissue before the knife: within 60 minutes, repeated for long cases.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0106$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with chronic kidney disease asks whether he can take ibuprofen for his knee pain. What should the nurse advise?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Yes, provided he drinks a lot of water with each tablet to protect the kidneys"}, {"id": "b", "text": "No: NSAIDs reduce kidney blood flow, so use paracetamol and discuss other options"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Yes, because a short course of a NSAID can never harm a kidney that is already damaged"}, {"id": "d", "text": "No: all painkillers are dangerous in kidney disease, so he should endure the pain without anything"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "No: NSAIDs reduce kidney blood flow, so use paracetamol and discuss other options". Prostaglandins keep the afferent arteriole open, and that flow is what maintains the filtration pressure in a kidney already working with reduced capacity. NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis, so the vessel narrows, blood flow falls and acute kidney injury can follow, along with fluid retention and a rise in potassium. The risk rises with dehydration, heart failure, older age and other kidney-toxic drugs. Paracetamol at the right dose is the first choice, with topical NSAID gels or a short course under supervision only if the prescriber judges it safe and the creatinine is watched.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Extra water does not prevent the vasoconstriction that a NSAID causes; hydration does not make it safe.
• C — The damaged kidney has less reserve, which makes it more vulnerable, not less.
• D — Untreated pain is harmful too, and paracetamol, heat, weight loss and a supervised plan all help without this mechanism.


Reference: KDIGO — drug dosing in chronic kidney disease; BNF — NSAIDs
$q$,
  strategy = $q$NSAIDs narrow the vessel that keeps a failing kidney perfused. Paracetamol first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2834$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with a mechanical heart valve is planning a pregnancy. Why must warfarin be changed before she conceives?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because warfarin makes the blood too thick and raises the risk of a clot in the valve"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because warfarin cannot be taken at the same time as folic acid in early pregnancy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because it crosses the placenta and can cause malformations and fetal bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the dose has to be doubled in pregnancy, and that is unsafe for the mother's bone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Because it crosses the placenta and can cause malformations and fetal bleeding". Warfarin crosses the placenta and blocks vitamin K–dependent clotting factor synthesis in the fetus. Between about 6 and 12 weeks it can cause warfarin embryopathy — nasal hypoplasia and stippled epiphyses — and later in pregnancy it can cause bleeding into the fetal brain, especially at delivery. That is why a woman with a mechanical valve who is planning a pregnancy is switched, before conception where possible, to a low molecular weight heparin, which does not cross the placenta; the valve needs protection throughout, so the switch is planned with cardiology and obstetrics and never stopped altogether.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Warfarin thins the blood, and stopping it would expose the valve to clot; the concern is the fetus.
• B — Folic acid is recommended in any pregnancy; there is no such interaction driving the change.
• D — The dose is not simply doubled; the whole drug is changed because of fetal harm.


Reference: NICE — anticoagulation in pregnancy; RCOG — heart disease in pregnancy
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Warfarin crosses the placenta: embryopathy at 6–12 weeks, fetal bleeding later. Switch before conception.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PKD-1231$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient on the ward has died. What is the nurse's responsibility at that moment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Certify the death in the notes so that the family can be given a death certificate immediately"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Say nothing to the family until the body has been taken to the mortuary and the papers are complete"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remove the dentures and pack the body before any doctor is informed, to save time later"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Confirm and record absence of response, breathing and pulse, and inform the doctor"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Confirm and record absence of response, breathing and pulse, and inform the doctor". Verification of death means establishing that the patient is not breathing, has no central pulse, does not respond and has fixed, dilated pupils, and documenting the time. The nurse who is present makes those observations and records them, and calls the doctor, who completes the certification and the medical certificate of cause of death. The family is told promptly and with dignity, in private. Last offices — washing and positioning the body, removing lines and dentures as local practice requires — come after, and the body is labelled and handled with respect for the family's religious and cultural wishes.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Certifying and completing the certificate is the doctor's legal act; the nurse documents her observations.
• B — The family should be told as soon as possible, not after paperwork; delay adds to their distress.
• C — The doctor must be informed first, and last offices follow the agreed procedure, not a shortcut.


Reference: Ghana Health Service — care of the dying; NMC — professional standards
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Verify and record, call the doctor, then tell the family, then last offices.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-RESP-0009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A two-year-old has had a fever for six days, red eyes, cracked lips, a rash and red swollen hands. What is the diagnosis, and why does it matter?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Measles, which needs vitamin A and isolation, and the fever will settle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Kawasaki disease, needing IVIG and aspirin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Scarlet fever, which needs penicillin and will settle the rash in a week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A viral illness that needs only fluids and rest at home"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Kawasaki disease, needing IVIG and aspirin". Six days of fever that does not respond to ordinary treatment, with conjunctivitis, red cracked lips and strawberry tongue, a polymorphous rash, a single large neck node and red swollen hands and feet that later peel, is Kawasaki disease. It affects small and medium arteries, and the danger is coronary artery aneurysm, which can cause a myocardial infarction in a child. Treatment with intravenous immunoglobulin and high-dose aspirin within the first ten days reduces that risk sharply, so the child also needs an echocardiogram and follow-up. No cause is known, and it is not infectious.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Measles gives a rash with cough, coryza and the mouth spots, and does not cause six days of fever with peeling hands.
• C — Scarlet fever has a sore throat, a sandpaper rash and a coated tongue, and the fever falls quickly on penicillin.
• D — Treating this as a simple viral illness is what allows a coronary aneurysm to form untreated.


Reference: WHO — Kawasaki disease; AHA — diagnosis and treatment
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Five or more days of fever with red eyes, lips, rash and swollen hands: IVIG before day 10.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHD-1745$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother wants to give her three-day-old baby a herbal bath with a decoction her own mother used. How should the nurse respond?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Respect the wish, explain the risks, and agree a plain warm bath with the cord kept dry"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refuse to discuss it, because a trained nurse must never talk about traditional practices"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell her that herbal baths are harmless in a newborn and she should use the strongest decoction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the baby into hospital care, because the family cannot be trusted to keep him safe at home"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Respect the wish, explain the risks, and agree a plain warm bath with the cord kept dry". Herbal bathing of a newborn is common and carries meaning for the family, so the conversation starts by asking what is used and why. The real risks are specific and worth naming: unsterilised preparations can introduce infection through the cord stump or broken skin, some plants irritate or sensitise the skin, boiling water causes scalds, and anything added to the bath water can get into the eyes. The nurse does not dismiss the practice but agrees what is safe: plain warm water, the cord kept clean and dry, no substances applied to the stump, and hands washed. The mother's concerns are heard, and the baby's safety is protected at the same time.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Refusing to discuss it leaves the practice unchanged and loses the chance to reduce harm.
• C — Calling it harmless and urging a stronger decoction endorses the very things that cause infection and burns.
• D — Removing the baby over a bathing practice is disproportionate and damages the relationship that keeps the family engaged.


Reference: WHO — newborn care; Ghana STG — traditional medicine and newborn health
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask, explain the risks, agree plain water and a dry cord. Do not dismiss and do not endorse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TCM-0668$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with bipolar disorder has been stable on lithium for years and now wants to conceive. What must happen before she stops the drug herself?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Nothing: lithium is the safest mood stabiliser in pregnancy and can continue at the same dose throughout"}, {"id": "b", "text": "She should simply stop the lithium today, since any drug is dangerous in the first three months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "She should switch to a high dose of a sedative, which will control her mood in place of lithium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A planned review, because stopping abruptly risks relapse, and lithium itself carries fetal risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A planned review, because stopping abruptly risks relapse, and lithium itself carries fetal risk". Both paths carry risk, so the decision is made with psychiatry and obstetrics before conception, not in a hurry after it. Stopping lithium suddenly in a woman who depends on it leads to early relapse, especially in the postpartum period, and relapse carries its own dangers for mother and baby. Continuing it means counselling about Ebstein's anomaly and other cardiac effects, checking the dose against a pre-pregnancy level, monitoring the level more often in pregnancy, and planning around delivery and breastfeeding. The one option that is not safe is a self-made decision in either direction.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Lithium is not risk-free; it is associated with cardiac malformations, and the dose and level need planning.
• B — Abrupt withdrawal is the classic trigger for relapse, and untreated illness in pregnancy is a serious risk.
• C — A sedative is not a mood stabiliser and does not treat bipolar disorder.


Reference: NICE — bipolar disorder in pregnancy; Ghana STG — psychiatry
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Plan it: abrupt stopping relapses, continuing needs counselling and level monitoring.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2482$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn is to have a blood spot taken for screening before discharge. Where should the nurse obtain the sample?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The outer side of the heel, after warming it, avoiding the centre"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The centre of the heel pad, where the skin is thickest and the blood flows best"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The tip of the big toe, because the toe is small and easy to hold steady"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The back of the hand, after a tight squeeze to fill the capillary tube quickly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "The outer side of the heel, after warming it, avoiding the centre". Newborn screening in Ghana includes a blood spot for sickle cell disease, and the sample has to be taken safely and be adequate. The heel is warmed first, then a lancet is used on the outer or inner aspect of the heel, where the skin is thin and the bone is deep; the first drop is wiped away and the next drops are touched onto the card so that it fills from both sides of the circle. Squeezing hard or using the centre of the heel risks bruising the bone, and the fingers and toes of a newborn are avoided because infection and injury follow easily.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The centre of the heel sits over the calcaneus, and a puncture there can cause osteomyelitis.
• C — The toe is small, poorly perfused at the tip and prone to infection; it is not a screening site.
• D — A tight squeeze damages tissue and dilutes the sample with tissue fluid; fingers are avoided in newborns.


Reference: WHO — newborn screening; Ghana — newborn screening for sickle cell disease
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Outer heel, warmed, first drop wiped. Never the centre of the heel or a newborn's finger.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man is brought in with sudden tearing chest pain that went through to his back, and his right arm pressure is much lower than his left. What must the nurse do first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give a loading dose of aspirin and prepare for thrombolysis, as for a heart attack"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Call for senior help, control pain and prepare for urgent imaging and surgery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give morphine and send him home for review, since the pain is settling with rest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start a large intravenous fluid infusion as fast as possible, to fill the aorta and the circulation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Call for senior help, control pain and prepare for urgent imaging and surgery". Tearing pain that moves to the back, a difference in blood pressure or pulse between arms, and a new murmur of aortic regurgitation are the picture of an aortic dissection. The danger is that the false channel ruptures, which can kill within minutes, so the patient needs senior help at once, good analgesia, and heart rate and blood pressure brought down so that the force tearing the wall apart is reduced — then urgent imaging and the surgeon or cardiologist. Thrombolysis or anticoagulation, which is right for a heart attack, is actively lethal here, which is why the distinction matters.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Aspirin and thrombolysis are for an occluded coronary artery; in dissection they make the bleeding worse.
• C — Sending home a patient with a possible dissection is a fatal error; the pain settling means nothing.
• D — Aggressive fluid loading raises the pressure driving the dissection; the aim is to lower it, not to fill.


Reference: ESC — aortic dissection; Ghana STG — chest pain
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tearing pain to the back with unequal arm pressures: not a heart attack. Control the pressure, scan, operate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CVM-1494$q$;

