UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$The surgeon uses diathermy throughout a long operation. Why does the theatre nurse insist on smoke evacuation rather than relying on the team's masks?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the smoke makes the theatre too hot for the patient to be safely cooled during surgery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because the smell is unpleasant for the team and can cause headaches by the end of a long list"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the smoke carries chemicals and possibly viable cells, and masks do not filter it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the smoke sets off the theatre fire alarm, which interrupts the operation and wastes time"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Because the smoke carries chemicals and possibly viable cells, and masks do not filter it". Diathermy and laser smoke is not just a smell. It contains fine particulate matter, irritant and carcinogenic chemicals such as benzene and formaldehyde, and potentially viable cells, including blood-borne viruses from the patient. An ordinary surgical mask does not seal at the face and does not filter particles of that size, so it gives little protection to the team and none to the air in the room. The control is extraction at the point of generation — a smoke evacuator with the nozzle close to the tip — together with adequate theatre ventilation. Proper masks, eye protection and the correct extraction are used together.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Theatre temperature is controlled by the ventilation system and has nothing to do with the smoke evacuator.
• B — The smell is a clue, not the hazard; the risk is what the smoke carries.
• D — Alarms are unrelated, and treating the hazard as an annoyance is how staff are exposed.


Reference: NICE — diathermy and surgical smoke; AORN — surgical smoke evacuation
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Surgical smoke carries chemicals and cells; a mask does not filter it. Extract at the tip.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0176$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient had sutures inserted in a cut on the forearm ten days ago. He asks when they can come out. What determines the timing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "All sutures come out at seven days, wherever they are on the body"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The site: face and neck early, limbs later, and over joints later still"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The number of sutures used, because more sutures mean the wound is stronger"}, {"id": "d", "text": "How the wound looks on the day, with no reference to where it is on the body"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The site: face and neck early, limbs later, and over joints later still". Wounds heal at different rates because the skin is different and because of the forces across it. Sutures on the face and neck can come out at about five to seven days, the scalp and trunk at seven to ten, and the limbs at ten to fourteen, while a wound over a joint or on the back may need longer. Other things matter too — age, nutrition, diabetes, steroid use, infection and how much tension the wound carries — so the decision is made by the clinician who reviews the wound, and the nurse checks that the edges are soundly united before removal.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A single time for every site would take face sutures out too late and limb sutures out too early.
• C — The number of sutures does not tell you how the tissue has healed; more sutures do not mean a stronger wound.
• D — Appearance alone, without the site and the patient's risk factors, is an incomplete guide.


Reference: Ghana STG — wound care; NICE — surgical site infection
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sutures are timed by site and healing: face 5–7 days, trunk 7–10, limbs 10–14.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-SUR-0345$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient returns from surgery with a closed suction drain in the wound. When should it be removed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "On the second day after surgery, whatever the volume and character of the drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only when the patient is ready for discharge, so that nothing is left behind in the wound"}, {"id": "c", "text": "When the drain has stopped draining completely for at least five days on the ward"}, {"id": "d", "text": "When the drainage has fallen and the wound is ready, as prescribed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "When the drainage has fallen and the wound is ready, as prescribed". A drain is there to remove blood, serum or pus that would otherwise collect and become an abscess, and to let the nurse see what is coming out. It is removed when its job is done: the volume has fallen to a low level for the local protocol, the fluid is no longer bloodstained or purulent, and the patient is recovering. Removal too early allows a collection to form; removal too late gives organisms a track into the wound and keeps the patient tethered to a bottle. The nurse checks the prescription, records the 24-hour volume, and watches the wound and the temperature after removal.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Removing on a fixed day ignores how much is draining and can leave a collection behind.
• B — Leaving the drain until discharge means it sits in the wound for days longer than it needs to.
• C — Waiting for five completely dry days keeps an unnecessary foreign body in a healing wound.


Reference: Ghana STG — wound and drain care; NICE — surgical drains
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A drain comes out when output falls and the wound is ready — not on a fixed day.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PER-1566$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman had a classical caesarean section two years ago, with an incision in the upper segment of the uterus. She is now 36 weeks and asks about a normal delivery. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A previous classical incision means planned caesarean, because labour risks a uterine tear"}, {"id": "b", "text": "She can labour safely, because any scar in the uterus behaves the same way in a later pregnancy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "She can be induced with oxytocin, and the scar will simply stretch as the contractions increase"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The type of the previous incision makes no difference at all to how this pregnancy will be managed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A previous classical incision means planned caesarean, because labour risks a uterine tear". The risk of scar rupture depends on where the uterus was cut. A lower-segment incision has a low rupture risk, which is why some women can plan a vaginal birth after caesarean. A classical incision runs through the thicker, contractile upper segment and heals less well, so the rupture risk in labour is several times higher, and rupture can kill the fetus and bleed the mother catastrophically. For that reason a planned caesarean before labour is the usual plan. The nurse explains why, records the type of the previous incision, and ensures the plan is in the notes and understood by the woman.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Scars behave very differently, and treating all scars alike is how a rupture is missed.
• C — Oxytocin increases the forces on the scar and is avoided; induction does not make a classical scar safe.
• D — The incision type is one of the most important facts in planning the delivery.


Reference: RCOG — birth after caesarean; Ghana STG — operative delivery
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A classical (upper segment) scar means no labour: rupture risk is far higher than a lower-segment scar.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAT-2341$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has a transdermal hyoscine patch prescribed for travel sickness. What must the nurse teach?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place it on the chest over the heart, and leave it on for a month before changing it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Behind the ear, applied hours ahead, hands washed after handling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place it on any hairy part of the body, since hair improves how well the drug is absorbed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take it only once the nausea has already started, because it works within a few minutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Behind the ear, applied hours ahead, hands washed after handling". Hyoscine (scopolamine) is absorbed through the skin, so it is applied to clean, dry, hairless skin behind the ear, where the drug enters steadily. For motion sickness it is applied some hours before travel, because it takes time to reach an effective level; it is not a rescue medicine for nausea that has already started. Removing the patch needs care: the residue on the fingers can dilate the pupil and blur vision if it gets into the eye, so the hands are washed after handling. It is avoided in narrow-angle glaucoma and in urinary retention, and it causes a dry mouth and drowsiness.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The patch belongs behind the ear, not over the heart, and it is changed according to the prescription, not monthly.
• C — Hair reduces contact and absorption; the skin must be clean and dry.
• D — It is a prophylactic, not a rescue drug; it takes hours, not minutes.


Reference: BNF — hyoscine; Ghana STG — antiemetics
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Behind the ear, hours ahead, hands washed. It prevents sickness, it does not treat it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHM-0369$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with type 2 diabetes is started on a variable-rate intravenous insulin infusion after surgery. What must the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Check capillary glucose hourly and adjust the rate to the protocol"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Set the rate once in the morning and review it at the next ward round"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the usual long-acting insulin at the same time to keep the rate steady"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Stop the infusion as soon as one reading is in the target range"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Check capillary glucose hourly and adjust the rate to the protocol". Variable-rate intravenous insulin exists because surgery, infection and starvation change glucose unpredictably, and the only way to keep it in range is to measure often and adjust. The nurse checks capillary glucose at least hourly — more often if the level is falling quickly or is out of range — and changes the rate according to the written protocol, with a glucose source running alongside to prevent hypoglycaemia. The infusion is stopped only when the patient is eating and a subcutaneous regimen is in place; stopping it without cover leaves the patient with no insulin at all. Potassium is watched, because insulin drives it into cells.

Why the other options fail:
• B — A rate set once and reviewed hours later ignores the changes that the infusion exists to manage.
• C — Adding long-acting insulin on top of a variable infusion is not the usual practice and risks hypoglycaemia unless prescribed.
• D — Stopping at the first in-range reading removes the control and lets glucose climb again.


Reference: JBDS — the variable rate intravenous insulin infusion; Ghana STG — perioperative diabetes
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hourly glucose, rate per protocol, glucose source running, stop only when eating with subcutaneous cover.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHM-0014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is to receive a blood transfusion for symptomatic anaemia. What must happen before the first unit is started?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Nothing in particular, because a transfusion is a routine treatment like an intravenous infusion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only the group and cross-match result, since a discussion about risk would worry the patient"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A signature from a relative, because the patient cannot consent to receiving blood products"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A specific consent discussion covering the risks, benefits and alternatives, documented"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A specific consent discussion covering the risks, benefits and alternatives, documented". Blood is a treatment with real risks — febrile and allergic reactions, haemolytic reactions from incompatibility, fluid overload, and the small residual risk of transfusion-transmitted infection — so the patient is told why it is needed, what the alternatives are, and what might go wrong, and the discussion is recorded. In an emergency where the patient cannot consent and the transfusion is life-saving, it proceeds and the reason is documented. Consent is not a signature collected in a corridor; it is a conversation, and the nurse checks that it has happened before the unit is requested and started.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Blood is not a routine infusion; it needs consent and two-person identity checks at the bedside.
• B — Withholding the risks removes the patient's ability to decide and is not consent.
• C — A competent adult consents for herself; a relative's signature is not a substitute.


Reference: Ghana National Blood Service — safe transfusion; NMC — consent
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Transfusion needs its own consent conversation, documented, plus bedside identity checks.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ANP-0304$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Several people in one district fall ill with watery diarrhoea within a week. Why does the outbreak team write a case definition before counting cases?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "So that the number of cases can be made to look smaller while the cause is still unknown"}, {"id": "b", "text": "So that every worker counts the same thing, and cases can be compared over time and place"}, {"id": "c", "text": "So that treatment can be started without waiting for any laboratory confirmation of the cause"}, {"id": "d", "text": "So that only the most severely ill patients are recorded, which saves work in a busy district"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "So that every worker counts the same thing, and cases can be compared over time and place". A case definition sets out who counts: the clinical features, the time period, the place, and any laboratory criteria, often with a simple, probable and confirmed level. Without it, one clinic counts every child with diarrhoea and another counts only those admitted, and the numbers cannot be compared, the size of the outbreak cannot be judged, and the effect of control measures cannot be measured. It is written early and revised as the picture becomes clearer — widening if cases are being missed, narrowing if the net is catching unrelated illness.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A definition improves accuracy; it is not a tool for making numbers look better.
• C — Treatment of individual patients is a separate decision and does not wait for the definition.
• D — Counting only severe cases underestimates the outbreak and hides its true extent and source.


Reference: WHO — outbreak investigation; Ghana — Integrated Disease Surveillance and Response
$q$,
  strategy = $q$A case definition makes everyone count the same thing: person, place, time, clinical and lab criteria.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PUB-0614$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with recurrent severe depression writes, while well, that if she becomes unwell again she would not want electroconvulsive therapy and wants her sister involved in decisions. She is later admitted severely depressed and cannot decide. How should the team use what she wrote?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ignore it, because a decision made when she is well cannot apply when she is ill"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use it as evidence of her wishes, weigh it with her current needs and involve her sister"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat it as absolutely binding, even when the situation is different from the one she imagined"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Destroy it, so that it cannot influence a decision that must be made on clinical grounds alone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Use it as evidence of her wishes, weigh it with her current needs and involve her sister". A statement written when someone is well records their values, what matters to them and what they would want, and it is valuable precisely because it was made without the pressure of illness. It is not a substitute for assessing the person now: the team considers it carefully, checks whether the circumstances are those the person had in mind, and records its reasons if it departs from it. The nominated person is involved. Where a jurisdiction gives a formal refusal legal force, that is a separate matter and is checked with the law; a statement of wishes is guidance that must be taken seriously, not ignored and not treated as unchangeable.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Ignoring it discards the clearest evidence of what she values.
• C — A statement made in advance cannot anticipate every change in circumstances, which is why it is weighed rather than applied blindly.
• D — Destroying it removes the patient's voice from the decision.


Reference: WHO — mental health and human rights; Ghana Mental Health Act 2012 (Act 846)
$q$,
  strategy = $q$An advance statement is evidence of her wishes: weigh it, check the circumstances, involve the named person.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MHN-0790$q$;

-- Verify: expect 9
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch101.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 101: nine pairs: MUST screening, transfusion threshold, TWOC, VBAC provision, tube feed residuals, ectopic methotrexate, iron overdose, health education planning, cytotoxic handling.
--
-- nine pairs that asked the same thing twice.
-- 
-- orthostatic BP          GER-0594 keeps lying-and-standing pressures.
--                         ELD-1992 now takes malnutrition screening: a
--                         BMI of 18.4 in a widower is not age, it is a
--                         MUST score and a dietitian.
-- transfusion reaction    ADV-0099 keeps stop-the-transfusion. CLP-2857
--                         now takes the restrictive threshold - one unit,
--                         then reassess.
-- ear syringing           APR-0460 keeps the perforated eardrum. PRC-1385
--                         now takes the planned trial without catheter.
-- active third stage      OBS-0556 keeps the AMTSL bundle. MNH-3046 now
--                         takes what a trial of labour needs after a
--                         lower-segment scar.
-- site marking            TRA-1873 keeps halt-and-mark. MSN-2746 now takes
--                         gastric residual volumes in tube feeding.
-- ruptured ectopic        OBS-0548 keeps the diagnosis. MNH-3057 now takes
--                         methotrexate for the small stable ectopic, and
--                         the hCG follow-up it demands.
-- chain of infection      MIC-0049 keeps the mode-of-transmission link.
--                         CLN-2963 now takes iron overdose and
--                         desferrioxamine.
-- broken water pipeline   PUB-0637 keeps working with the assembly.
--                         CHP-2249 now takes planning a health education
--                         session - audience first, objectives second.
-- dose too high           MDS-1655 keeps withhold-and-query. DRC-2482 now
--                         takes safe handling of cytotoxic drugs - gown,
--                         double gloves, eye protection, spill kit ready.
-- 
-- Two drafts were pulled after the neighbour check: the catheter specimen
-- question duplicated PRC-1357 and delayed cord clamping duplicated
-- MAT-2336. Both were replaced with ground I had probed and found empty.
-- Largest stem overlap 0.269.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 82-year-old man has lost weight since his wife died and now has a body mass index of 18.4. He says he has no appetite. What should the nurse do first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell him to eat more of what he already eats and review him in six months"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Screen him with a malnutrition tool"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange a barium swallow, because weight loss in older people is always cancer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Weigh him again next year, because weight falls with age and this is expected"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Screen him with a malnutrition tool". A body mass index under 20 in an older adult, combined with recent weight loss and poor appetite, is a red flag that will be missed if it is put down to age or grief. The MUST tool scores body mass index, unplanned weight loss and acute illness, and a score of two or more means high risk: the patient is referred to a dietitian, and the nurse starts the things that can be done at once — food first, small energy-dense meals, a drink between meals rather than with them, and treatable contributors looked for, such as tooth problems, swallowing difficulty, depression, poor housing and medicines that blunt appetite.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Asking a man with no appetite to eat more, without finding out why, changes nothing and delays help.
• C — Cancer is one cause among many; a barium swallow before screening and assessment is the wrong order.
• D — Unintentional weight loss in an older person is never simply expected and needs investigating.


Reference: NICE — nutrition support in adults; MUST; Ghana STG — older people's care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$BMI under 20 plus weight loss plus no appetite: screen with MUST and refer. Do not blame age.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ELD-1992$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A stable patient on the ward has a haemoglobin of 7.4 g/dL, is walking about and has no breathlessness at rest. The team is considering transfusion. What is current practice?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Transfuse two units at once, because a haemoglobin under 8 always needs correcting quickly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Withhold transfusion until the haemoglobin falls below 6, whatever the patient's symptoms"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A restrictive threshold, with transfusion only for symptoms or a lower haemoglobin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transfuse plasma instead, because it carries oxygen and is safer than red cells"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A restrictive threshold, with transfusion only for symptoms or a lower haemoglobin". Modern practice is restrictive: red cells are given to relieve symptoms or to correct a haemoglobin below the local threshold, commonly around 7 g/dL for most stable adults, and higher only for specific groups such as patients with ischaemic heart disease. The evidence shows that a restrictive policy gives the same or better outcomes with fewer units, less overload and fewer reactions. A single unit is given and the patient reassessed, rather than a routine pair. The nurse checks the indication, the patient's symptoms and fluid status, and records why each unit was given.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Two units by routine exposes the patient to twice the risk with no added benefit; give one and reassess.
• B — A fixed number under 6 ignores symptoms and the patient's cardiac history.
• D — Plasma carries clotting factors, not oxygen, and is not a substitute for red cells.


Reference: NICE — blood transfusion; AABB — red cell transfusion thresholds
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Restrictive threshold: transfuse one unit for symptoms or a low haemoglobin, then reassess.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2857$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man had an indwelling catheter for three days after surgery and is now passing urine normally. What should the nurse plan before removing it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Remove it in the evening so that he sleeps without a bag, and put it back if he cannot pass urine by morning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave it in until discharge, because a second catheterisation is worse than a few more days of drainage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Remove it when he is mobile and can reach a toilet, and record the volume passed afterwards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clamp the catheter for a full day first, so that the bladder can stretch back to its normal size"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Remove it when he is mobile and can reach a toilet, and record the volume passed afterwards". Removing a catheter is a trial without catheter, and it succeeds best when it is planned rather than incidental. The nurse checks that the reason for the catheter has passed, that the patient can walk to a toilet or use a bottle and can manage his clothing, and that he is not sedated or in severe pain. The catheter is removed in the morning where possible, fluids are encouraged, and every void is measured and recorded; retention is checked by scan rather than by waiting for symptoms. If he does not pass urine, the team is told and a decision is made about recatheterisation or a further trial.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Evening removal leaves him unsupported overnight, when retention is likely to go unnoticed until the bladder is over-distended.
• B — Leaving it in prolongs the risk of infection and stones and makes the eventual trial harder, not easier.
• D — Clamping for a day does not restore bladder tone and risks infection and discomfort; it is not a recognised preparation.


Reference: NICE — catheter care; Ghana STG — postoperative urinary care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Plan the trial: mobile, morning, fluids encouraged, every void measured.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PRC-1385$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman who had one previous caesarean section for fetal distress has a lower-segment scar and now wants to try for a vaginal birth. What must be in place before that is agreed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Nothing extra: a previous caesarean has no bearing on how this labour should be managed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A plan made in advance, with continuous monitoring and immediate access to theatre and blood"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An oxytocin infusion from the start of labour, to make the labour shorter and safer for the scar"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A guarantee that she will not need a caesarean this time, because a repeat operation is more dangerous"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A plan made in advance, with continuous monitoring and immediate access to theatre and blood". A previous lower-segment caesarean does not rule out vaginal birth, but it changes what the unit must be able to do. The plan is made and documented before labour, the woman is told the chance of success and the risk of scar rupture, and she labours where continuous fetal monitoring is available and where theatre and blood can be reached within minutes if the scar gives way. Induction and augmentation are used with caution, because they increase the forces on the scar. A classical or unknown uterine scar is different and is managed with a planned caesarean instead.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A scar is one of the most important facts in planning a labour; ignoring it is unsafe.
• C — Oxytocin increases the strain on the scar and is not routine in this situation.
• D — No one can guarantee a vaginal birth; the plan has to include the possibility of caesarean.


Reference: RCOG — birth after previous caesarean; NICE — intrapartum care
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lower-segment scar may labour, but only with a plan, continuous monitoring and theatre on call.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MNH-3046$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is being fed through a nasogastric tube. The nurse is about to give the next feed and wonders whether to check for residual stomach contents. What is current practice?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Aspirate and discard everything that comes back, then give a double feed to make up for it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Check a residual every hour and stop feeding whenever any fluid is aspirated at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Never check, because the presence of any gastric fluid means the tube is in the lung"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Follow the unit protocol on residuals"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Follow the unit protocol on residuals". Routine checking of gastric residual volumes has been abandoned in most units, because a large aspirate is a poor predictor of aspiration and stopping feeds for it leaves patients underfed. What matters more is whether the patient is tolerating the feed: abdominal distension, vomiting, pain, a rising gastric aspirate beyond the local threshold, or a change in consciousness. The nurse keeps the head of the bed raised at about 30 to 45 degrees during and after feeds, confirms tube position by the approved method, flushes between medicines, and acts on the clinical signs rather than on a fixed volume in isolation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Discarding the aspirate throws away gastric acid and electrolytes, and doubling a feed is unsafe.
• B — Hourly checks with automatic stopping cause underfeeding and do not prevent aspiration.
• C — Gastric fluid is expected in the stomach; tube position is confirmed by the proper method, not by the presence of fluid.


Reference: NHS — nasogastric feeding guidance; BAPEN; Ghana STG — enteral nutrition
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Residuals do not prevent aspiration. Watch for distension and vomiting, keep the head raised.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MSN-2746$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at seven weeks with a small unruptured tubal pregnancy is stable, with no pain and no free fluid on scan. Her hCG is 900. What treatment may be offered instead of surgery?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Methotrexate, with hCG monitored until it falls and a plan for follow-up"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oxytocin infusion, to empty the uterus and let the tube resolve on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Misoprostol by mouth, which works for any early pregnancy outside the uterus"}, {"id": "d", "text": "No treatment at all, because an ectopic pregnancy resolves without intervention"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Methotrexate, with hCG monitored until it falls and a plan for follow-up". A small, unruptured ectopic pregnancy in a haemodynamically stable woman, with a low hCG, no fetal heartbeat and a mass under about 3.5 cm, can be treated medically with methotrexate, which stops the trophoblast dividing. Before it is given the woman needs bloods and liver and kidney function checked, and she must be reliable for follow-up because the drug can fail and the tube can rupture later. hCG is measured on set days until it is undetectable, and she is warned to return at once with pain, dizziness or heavy bleeding. Surgery remains the option where she is unstable or the criteria are not met.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Oxytocin acts on the uterus and has no effect on a pregnancy in the tube.
• C — Misoprostol does not reliably treat a tubal pregnancy and is not a substitute for methotrexate or surgery.
• D — An untreated ectopic can rupture and kill; doing nothing is never the plan.


Reference: RCOG — ectopic pregnancy and miscarriage; Ghana STG — early pregnancy complications
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Methotrexate for a small stable ectopic with low hCG, plus hCG follow-up and a rupture warning.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MNH-3057$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A toddler has swallowed a handful of his grandmother's iron tablets an hour ago. He is vomiting and has bloody diarrhoea. What is the priority treatment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give activated charcoal, because it binds the iron and stops it being absorbed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Desferrioxamine, the iron chelator"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral rehydration solution and review him in the clinic the following morning"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give vitamin C, which helps the body handle the iron more quickly and safely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Desferrioxamine, the iron chelator". Iron is directly corrosive to the gut and, once absorbed, damages the liver, heart and mitochondria; the child who is vomiting and passing blood has significant poisoning. The antidote is desferrioxamine, a chelator that binds free iron and allows it to be excreted, given intravenously in a severely poisoned child after urgent advice and with the dose guided by the serum iron level and the clinical picture. Supportive care matters as much: fluids, correction of acidosis, and treatment of shock. Activated charcoal does not bind iron well and, in a child who is vomiting, adds aspiration risk.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Charcoal binds iron poorly and is hazardous in a vomiting child.
• C — Rehydration alone leaves the absorbed iron to damage the liver and heart.
• D — Vitamin C increases iron absorption and can worsen the poisoning.


Reference: WHO — childhood poisoning; BNF for Children — desferrioxamine
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Iron overdose is corrosive plus systemic. Chelate with desferrioxamine, support the circulation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLN-2963$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is asked to run a session on hand hygiene for mothers at a rural clinic. What should she do first when planning it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Write down everything she knows about hand hygiene, and read it out at the session"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Print the national guidelines and hand them out, because the content is the important part"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Find out what the mothers already do and what they want to know, and set objectives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Book the largest room available, because a bigger audience means better value for the visit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Find out what the mothers already do and what they want to know, and set objectives". Health education that changes behaviour starts from the learner, not the teacher. Before any content is prepared, the nurse finds out what the group already knows and does, what their circumstances allow — water, soap, time, a place to wash — and what they see as their main problem. From that she sets two or three specific objectives, chooses a method that lets them practise and discuss rather than listen, and plans how she will know afterwards whether anything changed. The session is then delivered in the language the group uses, with demonstrations and return demonstrations.

Why the other options fail:
• A — A lecture from a list teaches little and does not account for what the group already knows.
• B — Handing out guidelines does not ensure they are read, understood or possible in that setting.
• D — A large room suits a lecture, not learning; room size is not the planning question.


Reference: WHO — health education and promotion; Ghana Health Service — CHPS
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Start from the audience and their circumstances, set objectives, then choose the method.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CHP-2249$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is asked to prepare and give a cytotoxic chemotherapy drug. What must she have before she starts?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Only gloves, because the drug is given through a closed intravenous line"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A cytotoxic spill kit, protective equipment and a prepared administration area"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A second nurse to hold the bag, since two people are needed for every dose"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Permission from the patient's family, because chemotherapy is a hazardous treatment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A cytotoxic spill kit, protective equipment and a prepared administration area". Preparing and giving cytotoxic drugs exposes the nurse to a hazardous substance, so the equipment is set out before anything is opened: a gown, two pairs of gloves, eye and face protection, a absorbent pad on a designated surface, luer-lock connections and closed-system transfer where available. A spill kit must be within reach, because a break, a leak or a dropped syringe is a foreseeable event and the response has to be immediate. Pregnant and breastfeeding staff are excluded from handling these drugs, and waste goes into the cytotoxic bin, not the ordinary one.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Gloves alone leave the forearms, face and eyes exposed to splashes and aerosols.
• C — Two nurses are used for checking, not for physically holding the bag.
• D — Consent is obtained for the treatment, not for the nurse's handling of it.


Reference: NIOSH — hazardous drug handling; Ghana STG — chemotherapy safety
$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cytotoxics need gown, double gloves, eye protection and a spill kit ready before you start.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRC-2482$q$;

-- Verify: expect 9
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch102.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 102: eight duplicates replaced.
--
-- Two pairs were asking the same thing twice about the same ground: an NG feed
-- position, a potassium infusion, a suicide plan with means, a donor-funded
-- programme, a daily weight in heart failure, an ACE-inhibitor cough, a
-- discharge-planning start, and what to delegate. Each duplicate was replaced
-- with a question whose ground the bank does not occupy: irradiated blood,
-- implanted port access, silver sulfadiazine, trachoma and the SAFE strategy,
-- tracheostomy suction depth, nebuliser technique, supernumerary students, and
-- near-miss review.
-- 
-- Two drafts died in the neighbour check: the sepsis first-hour bundle because
-- MSN-2754 already asks it with the same numbers, and insulin storage because
-- MDS-1660 already asks it for a patient with no refrigerator.
-- 
-- Consolidated file: expect 2,740 statements.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with Hodgkin lymphoma is about to be transfused. The prescription states that the red cells must be irradiated. Why is the unit irradiated?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because irradiation kills any bacteria that entered the bag while it was being collected from the donor"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because irradiation removes the potassium that leaks out of red cells during storage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because irradiation matches the unit more closely to the patient's own tissue type"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because irradiation stops the donor lymphocytes from dividing and attacking the patient"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Because irradiation stops the donor lymphocytes from dividing and attacking the patient”. A red cell unit contains viable donor T lymphocytes. In a recipient who cannot reject them, they can engraft and mount an attack on the patient’s skin, gut, liver and marrow — transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease, which is almost always fatal and for which there is no effective treatment. Irradiation with 25 to 30 Gy arrests the DNA of those lymphocytes so that they can no longer divide, while leaving the red cells usable. It is indicated for patients with Hodgkin lymphoma, stem-cell transplant recipients, and babies receiving exchange transfusion. Why the other options fail: irradiation is not a sterilising step and does nothing to bacteria in the bag (A); it does not alter potassium leak or storage age (B); and it has nothing to do with ABO or tissue matching (C).$q$,
  strategy = $q$For anything entering the stomach in a patient who cannot protect their airway, gravity is the intervention. Look for the option that keeps the head above the stomach.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-0238$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has an implanted port in the chest wall and needs a two-week course of intravenous antibiotics. Which needle and technique should the nurse use?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A standard hypodermic needle, because it is cheap, available everywhere and can be changed at every dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A non-coring needle, inserted with sterile technique and checked that it is fully seated before flushing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Any needle, provided it is passed through the same puncture hole each time to spare the skin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A cannula in the antecubital fossa instead, since an implanted port is used only for taking blood"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “A non-coring needle, inserted with sterile technique and checked that it is fully seated before flushing”. An implanted port sits under the skin and is entered through a self-sealing silicone septum. A non-coring needle has a deflected, side-opening tip, so it parts the silicone instead of cutting a plug out of it; the septum then closes behind the needle and survives hundreds of punctures. An ordinary bevelled needle cores the septum, which leaks, and eventually the port has to be replaced. The site is cleaned, the needle is seated fully, and correct position is confirmed before the flush and the drug, because a partly seated needle can extravasate into the chest wall. Why the other options fail: a standard needle destroys the septum (A); reusing one hole does not stop coring and risks infection (C); and a peripheral cannula does not reach the central circulation the port was placed for (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$For any electrolyte affecting the heart, the rate of change matters more than the amount given.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2856$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman has a partial-thickness burn to her forearm and the ward is using silver sulfadiazine cream. Which instruction about it is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply it thickly and cover it with a tight bandage that stays undisturbed for a week so that the burn is protected"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply it to the eyelids as well, so that the burn infection cannot spread to the eyes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stop it and report at once if a rash appears, and do not use it if she is allergic to sulfonamides"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Once it is on, the wound no longer needs to be looked at until the burn has healed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Stop it and report at once if a rash appears, and do not use it if she is allergic to sulfonamides”. Silver sulfadiazine is a sulfonamide antibiotic applied as a thin layer to a clean partial-thickness burn. Because it is a sulfonamide, it can cause a rash and, rarely, a fall in the white cell count, so a new rash, a sore throat or a dropping count means it is stopped and reported. It must never be put near the eyes or on mucous membranes, and it is avoided in late pregnancy and in glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency. Why the other options fail: a thick layer under an occlusive dressing macerates the wound and slows healing (A); it is not for the eyes (B); and the burn must still be inspected at every dressing change, because infection is what kills (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Specific means plus imminent timing overrides routine rapport-building or no-harm contracts.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0029$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A community health nurse in northern Ghana is helping to plan trachoma control for a district. Which combination of measures does the SAFE strategy describe?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Surgery for in-turned lashes, antibiotics for infection, facial cleanliness and environmental improvement"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait for the consultant's ward round in the morning so that the plan is made by a doctor before any treatment is started at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Spectacles for poor vision, aspirin for inflammation, fumigation of houses and draining of all wells"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Steroid eye drops, antifungal ointment, spraying of flies and free distribution of sunglasses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Surgery for in-turned lashes, antibiotics for infection, facial cleanliness and environmental improvement”. Trachoma is caused by Chlamydia trachomatis and is spread from eye to eye by fingers, cloths and flies that are drawn to the discharge of an infected child. Repeated infection scars the inner eyelid, the lashes turn inward and rub the cornea, and that is what causes blindness. SAFE works on each part of the chain: surgery corrects the in-turned lashes, mass azithromycin treats the infection in the community, face washing removes the discharge the flies feed on, and better water and latrines reduce the fly population. Why the other options fail: screening and supplements do not interrupt transmission (B); aspirin and fumigation do nothing to the organism or the fly breeding sites (C); and steroids without antibiotics can worsen an infection while sunglasses merely hide the eye (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask at the design stage what survives the closing of the project office.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-HCB-1317$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with a tracheostomy needs suctioning. How far should the nurse pass the catheter?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To the pre-measured depth, about the length of the tube plus a little, stopping at any resistance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "As far as it will go, until it will not pass any further, so that the airway is completely cleared of secretions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Only into the stoma itself, so that the tip does not touch the inside of the tube"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ten centimetres beyond the tip of the tube on every pass to reach the secretions lower down"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “To the pre-measured depth, about the length of the tube plus a little, stopping at any resistance”. The catheter is marked and inserted only as far as the length of the tracheostomy tube plus one or two centimetres, which reaches the secretions that matter without touching the carina. It is passed without force, suction is applied only on withdrawal and for no more than ten to fifteen seconds, and the patient is given oxygen before and after. This limits mucosal trauma, bleeding, hypoxia and the introduction of infection. Why the other options fail: forcing the catheter until it stops injures the carina and the tracheal wall (B); suction above the tube clears nothing (C); and passing ten centimetres beyond the tip guarantees trauma and a hypoxic episode (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$When a simple measurement is repeated daily, ask what it detects before the eye can. Weight beats visible oedema by several days.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NCD-0492$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient in the emergency unit is given nebulised salbutamol for an acute asthma attack. Which practice is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use a nasal mask so that the mist is breathed in through the nose as well as the mouth and reaches both passages"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Set the gas at one litre per minute so that the nebuliser lasts for the whole treatment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sit the patient upright, use a mouthpiece with a good seal and run the gas at six to eight litres per minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the patient alone during the treatment so that he is not disturbed while he breathes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Sit the patient upright, use a mouthpiece with a good seal and run the gas at six to eight litres per minute”. A nebuliser only produces particles small enough to reach the bronchi when the driving gas flows at six to eight litres a minute. Sitting upright improves lung expansion and diaphragm movement, and a mouthpiece with a tight seal delivers far more drug to the airway than a loose mask, with less exposure of the staff and the patient’s eyes to the mist. The treatment continues until the chamber stops misting, usually five to ten minutes. Why the other options fail: breathing through the nose filters out much of the drug (A); at one litre a minute the droplets are too large and the treatment takes far too long (B); and a patient in severe asthma must be watched, because silence in the chest is a warning, not an improvement (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$When a symptom starts soon after a new drug, suspect the drug before adding a diagnosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRG-0993$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A second-year student nurse is counted in the ward numbers for a night shift, filling the gap left by a staff nurse who is sick. What is the correct position?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Students are supernumerary, so they must never be counted as part of the staff needed to run the ward"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A second-year student may take the shift alone provided that she has done a night shift before and knows the ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Counting students is acceptable whenever the ward is short of staff and no one else is available"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The student may cover the shift as long as she reports anything unusual to the doctor on call"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Students are supernumerary, so they must never be counted as part of the staff needed to run the ward”. A student is a learner and not an employee. She is supernumerary, which means she is additional to the staff needed to run the ward and always works under the supervision of a registered practitioner who remains accountable for the care. Counting her in the numbers leaves the ward short of a registered nurse and places a learner in a position of responsibility that her registration does not carry. Why the other options fail: having done a night shift before does not make a student accountable for a ward (B); being short-staffed is exactly the situation the rule is written for, not an exception to it (C); and telling the doctor afterwards does not make an unsupervised learner safe (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask what cannot be arranged on the day. Anything needing another person, money or transport must start early.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PFC-0661$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse almost gives a medicine to the wrong patient but notices at the last moment, and the patient comes to no harm. What should the ward manager do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take no action at all, because no harm was done, the patient never noticed and no one else needs to know"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Discipline the nurse quietly so that the rest of the team learn to be more careful"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Record and review it as a near miss, and look for the system failure that allowed it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the nurse to keep it to herself so that the ward's safety record stays clean"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Record and review it as a near miss, and look for the system failure that allowed it”. A near miss is an error that was caught before it reached the patient, which makes it free information: nothing was lost and the whole failure pathway is visible. Reviewing it shows what actually allowed the mistake — look-alike packaging, a shared drug trolley, interruptions during the round, a missing identification check — and that is what gets fixed, so the next nurse is protected. Staff must be thanked for reporting, because blame drives errors underground and the next one will not be reported. Why the other options fail: doing nothing throws away the lesson and leaves the trap in place (A); quiet punishment teaches the team to hide errors (B); and concealment is dishonest and leaves the hazard for the next patient (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delegate doing, never deciding. Data collection can be delegated; interpreting that data cannot.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MAN-0858$q$;

-- Verify: expect 8
COMMIT;

