-- Batch 103: five duplicates replaced.
--
-- Five duplicates replaced: a second ascending-paralysis vignette, a second
-- urine dipstick reading, a second dementia-wandering answer, a second cholera
-- rehydration answer, and a second silent-chest answer. Replacements sit on
-- ground the bank does not hold: epidural block checks, dipstick interpretation
-- for a urinary infection, night sedation in the very old, diphtheria antitoxin,
-- and denture care in a dependent adult.
-- 
-- Two more drafted replacements died in the neighbour check: breast
-- self-examination timing (PRC-1372 already asks it) and oxygen precautions at
-- the bedside (ADV-0125 already asks it). They are queued, not forced.
-- 
-- Consolidated file: expect 2,745 statements.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has an epidural infusion running for pain relief after major surgery. Which observation must be recorded at each check?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The number of times he has passed urine since the epidural was started and the colour of that urine"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The level of the sensory block, his leg power, his blood pressure and his breathing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The volume of the epidural bag remaining, so that a replacement can be ordered in good time"}, {"id": "d", "text": "His temperature only, because infection at the epidural site is the one serious complication"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “The level of the sensory block, his leg power, his blood pressure and his breathing”. The block is checked by finding the level at which cold or touch is no longer felt, and by testing that he can lift his legs. A block that is climbing higher than expected, or legs that will not move, may mean the drug is reaching the spinal cord or the wrong space, and must be reported at once. Local anaesthetic also dilates the vessels, so blood pressure falls and the patient can become hypotensive and bradycardic; respiratory depression is the other danger. Why the other options fail: urine output matters, but it does not assess the block (A); the bag volume is stock control, not monitoring (C); and temperature alone would miss hypotension, a high block and weakness (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Read weakness by its distribution: one-sided means brain, symmetrical and ascending means peripheral nerve.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLN-2907$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman has burning when she passes urine. The ward dipstick shows nitrites positive, leucocytes positive and a trace of blood. How should the nurse interpret this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Nitrite and leucocytes together point to a urinary tract infection, so a midstream sample should be sent for culture"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The trace of blood is the only finding that matters, and it means she has a tumour of the kidney until this is proved otherwise"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A positive nitrite means the sample was contaminated while it was being collected, so the result should be thrown away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "All three findings are normal in a woman of her age and nothing further needs to be done for her at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Nitrite and leucocytes together point to a urinary tract infection, so a midstream sample should be sent for culture”. Nitrite is produced when Gram-negative coliforms such as E. coli reduce nitrate in the urine, and leucocyte esterase reflects pus cells, so both being positive in a woman with burning on micturition is strongly suggestive of infection. A midstream specimen is sent for culture before antibiotics so that the organism and its sensitivities are known; the trace of blood may come from the infection or from menstruation. Nitrite can be falsely negative if she voids frequently or if the organism does not reduce nitrate. Why the other options fail: blood alone is not a cancer diagnosis (B); contamination would not manufacture nitrite (C); and a symptomatic infection that is left untreated ascends to the kidney (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$For growth hormone, ask whether the growth plates were open. Open gives height, fused gives thickness.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLN-2920$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 82-year-old woman in a care home has been waking at night, and her family asks the nurse for a sedative. What should the nurse advise?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A benzodiazepine at bedtime is the kindest and safest way to make sure that she sleeps right through the night"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Look for pain, a full bladder, hunger, cold or noise, and try sleep hygiene before any drug is considered"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Night sedation is harmless in older people, because the dose can simply be halved if she is drowsy the next day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A long-acting sedative is best, so that one dose at bedtime covers the whole night and the following morning"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Look for pain, a full bladder, hunger, cold or noise, and try sleep hygiene before any drug is considered”. Waking at night in an older person is usually a symptom rather than a disease: pain from arthritic hips, a full bladder or a urinary infection, constipation, hunger, a cold room, noise or fear. Finding and treating that cause costs nothing and carries no risk, and simple measures such as daylight, a warm drink and a predictable routine help. Sedatives in this age group cause next-day confusion, unsteadiness, falls and hip fracture, and they are habit-forming. Why the other options fail: benzodiazepines roughly double the risk of a fall in an older person (A); being drowsy the next day is the harm, not a reason to keep going (C); and a long-acting drug hangs over into the morning, when the patient is most likely to fall (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Restriction presented as safety is usually the harm. Ask what she is looking for.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ELD-2022$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A frail man in a care home can no longer brush his own teeth, and he wears a full upper denture. Which care should the nurse give?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Mouth care is unnecessary for a man who eats very little, so simply rinse his mouth with water once a week and leave the rest of it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Leave the denture in place day and night, so that his face keeps its shape and he is not distressed by its removal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Clean his own teeth and gums twice a day and take the denture out at night, cleaning it and leaving the mouth to rest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Scrub the denture with ordinary hard soap and a stiff brush once a week, and store it dry in a bedside drawer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Clean his own teeth and gums twice a day and take the denture out at night, cleaning it and leaving the mouth to rest”. Oral bacteria are aspirated, so a mouth left uncleaned in a dependent person is a direct route to pneumonia as well as to pain and tooth loss. The remaining teeth and the gums are brushed twice daily, and a denture is removed at night so that the mucosa can rest; it is cleaned and stored in water so that it does not warp. The nurse also looks inside the mouth for ulcers, thrush and a dry, coated tongue. Why the other options fail: eating little is exactly when a coated mouth and infection take hold (A); a denture left in continuously causes stomatitis and pressure damage (B); and a dry denture warps and no longer fits (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$A silent chest in asthma is the emergency. Ask what the absence of a sign means.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LTC-1798$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A child has a grey, adherent membrane over the tonsils, a brassy cough and a swollen neck. Diphtheria is suspected. Which action comes first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the diphtheria antitoxin as soon as it can be obtained, without waiting for the laboratory to confirm"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start oral penicillin and wait for the throat swab result before giving anything else, so that the treatment is targeted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nurse the child in the open ward with paracetamol for the sore throat, since the membrane separates on its own"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Send the child home with a course of antibiotics, because hospital admission only exposes other children"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Give the diphtheria antitoxin as soon as it can be obtained, without waiting for the laboratory to confirm”. The danger in diphtheria is the toxin, which damages the heart muscle and the nerves. Antitoxin neutralises toxin that is still circulating but cannot undo what has already bound to tissue, so every hour of delay costs myocarditis or a palsy. It is therefore given on clinical suspicion, and antibiotics such as penicillin or erythromycin follow to clear the organism and reduce spread. The child needs droplet precautions and close watching for airway obstruction. Why the other options fail: waiting for the swab before antitoxin is exactly how children are lost (B); an open ward exposes others and paracetamol does nothing to the toxin (C); and discharge with antibiotics alone leaves the toxin unopposed (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify what is actually killing the patient. In cholera it is volume loss, so fluid outranks the antibiotic every time.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2550$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

