UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse must draw up morphine from a glass ampoule for a patient in severe pain. Why is a filter needle used to draw the drug up, and what is done before the injection is given?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To trap any glass particles, and a fresh needle is fitted before the injection is given"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To sterilise the solution as it is drawn up, and the same needle is then used for the injection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To measure the dose more accurately than the markings moulded on the barrel of the syringe"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To draw the drug quickly from the narrow neck of the ampoule without losing any of it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “To trap any glass particles, and a fresh needle is fitted before the injection is given”. Snapping the neck of a glass ampoule throws microscopic shards into the solution, and drawing that up unfiltered injects glass into the tissue. A filter needle has a fine mesh in its hub that holds the fragments back as the drug is withdrawn. It is a draw-up device only: it is taken off and replaced with an ordinary needle, and the ordinary needle must also be cleared of air and checked again, because a filter needle is blunt compared with an injection needle and is not designed to pierce skin. Why the other options fail: the solution in a sealed ampoule is already sterile, so nothing has to be sterilised at the bedside (B); the volume is measured by the graduations on the syringe barrel, not by the needle (C); and drawing up quickly through a narrow neck is exactly how the glass shards are swept into the syringe (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lithium follows sodium. Loss of salt or water raises the level, so steady intake and prompt reporting matter.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHARM-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian man started on haloperidol for an acute psychotic episode asks his sister why he is still hearing voices after five days. What should the nurse explain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The drug should act at once, so if the voices continue the dose must be far too low"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sedation comes within hours, but the antipsychotic effect builds over days to a few weeks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The voices may never stop completely, so he should be taught to accept them as they are and not expect change"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He should stop the tablets now and return only when the voices become unbearable for him"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Sedation comes within hours, but the antipsychotic effect builds over days to a few weeks”. Haloperidol blocks dopamine D2 receptors quickly, and its sedating and calming effect is felt within hours, which is why the agitation often settles first. The antipsychotic effect on hallucinations and delusions is slower: the drug has to reach a steady tissue level and the limbic system has to adapt, so clear improvement takes days and a full response can take four to six weeks. Missing this distinction is why patients stop a drug that is working; he needs to be told that the voices should quieten gradually if he keeps taking it as prescribed. Why the other options fail: the drug does not act on hallucinations at once, so a persisting voice does not mean the dose is wrong and it must never be raised without the prescriber (A); telling a patient to accept persisting voices is therapeutic nihilism and removes hope of the symptom settling (C); and stopping the tablets is the commonest cause of relapse in first-episode psychosis (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anything that changes body water changes the lithium level — teach that link.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0030$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient returns to the ward after a percutaneous renal biopsy. What is the nurse's priority in the first few hours?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Keep the patient lying flat, watch the puncture site and check each urine specimen for blood"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage the patient to walk about early so that the kidney does not become stiff"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Withhold all oral fluids for twenty-four hours after the procedure so that the kidney can rest completely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Send the patient home as soon as the puncture wound is dry, with no further observation needed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Keep the patient lying flat, watch the puncture site and check each urine specimen for blood”. A renal biopsy passes a needle through the skin and muscle into a kidney that is rich in blood at systemic pressure, so the complication to fear is bleeding — into the urine, around the kidney as a perirenal haematoma, or into the retroperitoneum. Lying flat for several hours reduces the chance of re-bleeding and keeps the patient still. The nurse then watches the puncture site for swelling or oozing, checks the pulse and blood pressure for a fall, asks about flank or back pain, and inspects every specimen of urine for frank or microscopic blood. Oral fluids are encouraged, not withheld, so that the urinary tract is flushed through, and the patient avoids lifting and straining for about a week. Why the other options fail: early walking raises the risk of bleeding from the puncture (B); fluid restriction concentrates the urine and makes haematuria harder to detect while doing nothing to rest the kidney (C); and discharging the patient as soon as the skin is dry ignores the internal bleeding that is the real hazard (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fluid monitoring gold standard: Daily body weight (same scale, same time, after voiding). 1 kg weight change = 1 liter (1,000 mL) of fluid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A monitor at the bedside of a patient in a Ghanaian ward begins to smoulder and small flames appear at its casing. Which extinguisher should the nurse use?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Water, because it cools the burning casing faster than anything else available"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Carbon dioxide, because it does not conduct electricity and leaves no residue"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A wet blanket, wrung out and laid over the machine to smother the flames"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Foam, because it coats the equipment and excludes air from the burning casing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Carbon dioxide, because it does not conduct electricity and leaves no residue”. An electrical fire is a fire in energised equipment, so the first concern is that the extinguishing agent and the nurse can both become part of the circuit. Carbon dioxide is not a conductor, it smothers the fire by displacing oxygen, and it leaves no residue to damage the machine or the ward. The sequence around it still matters: raise the alarm and get help, switch the equipment off at the socket if that can be done safely, evacuate anyone at risk, and only then tackle a small fire with the correct extinguisher. Why the other options fail: water conducts electricity and a jet of it on live equipment can electrocute the nurse (A); a wet blanket carries the same conducting risk and has to be laid over a live machine to work (C); and foam also conducts and leaves a residue that ruins the equipment (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sequence fire actions by what protects the most people first: alarm, rescue, contain, then fight or evacuate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-CLP-2883$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A district in Ghana has repeated outbreaks of diarrhoea among young children. Which single measure would do most to prevent them?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Handwashing with soap at the key times, taught and supported in every household"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treating every child with oral rehydration salts as soon as the diarrhoea starts"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Giving all children a course of antibiotics at the beginning of each rainy season"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Building a new district hospital with a dedicated ward for children with diarrhoea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Handwashing with soap at the key times, taught and supported in every household”. Childhood diarrhoea spreads by the faecal-oral route, on hands, water, food and utensils. Handwashing with soap after defecation, after cleaning a child and before preparing food or eating interrupts that route at its narrowest point, and it is the intervention with the largest effect on diarrhoea incidence of any single measure. It also works before the outbreak, which is what prevention means. Why the other options fail: oral rehydration salts treat the dehydration a child already has — they save lives, but they do not stop the next child being infected (B); routine seasonal antibiotics do not prevent diarrhoea, breed resistance and expose children to unnecessary drugs (C); and a hospital treats severe cases after they occur, which is tertiary care rather than prevention (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Match the hazard to the transmission route it serves. Crowding and poor air point to airborne disease; poor water points to faecal-oral disease.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-HPR-0416$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

