-- six drafts died first, including negative pressure wound therapy
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A clinical thermometer breaks on the floor of a Ghanaian treatment room and beads of mercury scatter across the tiles. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vacuum the floor thoroughly so that none of the mercury is left where a patient can touch it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sweep the beads into the drain and run water for several minutes to wash them away"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ventilate the room, keep people out, and collect the beads into a sealed labelled container"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wipe the area with a wet cloth and put the cloth in the ordinary clinical waste bag"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Ventilate the room, keep people out, and collect the beads into a sealed labelled container”. Mercury is liquid at room temperature and gives off vapour continuously, and the vapour is what is toxic: it is inhaled, absorbed in the lungs and accumulates in the nervous system and kidneys. The room is therefore ventilated and cleared of people, and the beads are gathered with a stiff card, a pipette or a commercial spill kit into a container that seals, is labelled as hazardous waste, and is not put into the ordinary clinical waste stream. Skin contact is avoided and the hands are washed afterwards. Why the other options fail: a vacuum cleaner is the single worst thing to use, because it breaks the beads into a fine mist and blows mercury vapour through the exhaust into the room (A); washing it into the drain sends the mercury into the water supply and the food chain, and it settles in the trap where it keeps vaporising (B); and a wet cloth spreads the mercury over a larger area and then carries it into the waste bag, where it goes on giving off vapour (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Grade the obstruction before you act. Effective cough means encourage; ineffective cough means intervene.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FND-2644$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$On a busy Ghanaian ward, three extension leads are chained together from the single wall socket to reach four pieces of equipment, and the plug tops are warm. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leave the arrangement exactly as it is, since the equipment is being used for patient care at this moment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the equipment off the chained leads and report the inadequate socket provision for repair"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tape the adaptors together so that they cannot work loose, and carry on using them"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Run the equipment from the lighting circuit instead, so that the sockets are not overloaded"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Take the equipment off the chained leads and report the inadequate socket provision for repair”. Each socket and each lead is rated for a maximum current, and daisy-chaining adaptors adds the loads together while bypassing the fuse that was designed for the circuit. The current then heats the plugs, sockets and cable until the insulation fails or a fire starts, and in a ward that fire is beside oxygen and immobile patients. Warm plug tops are the warning sign. The load is reduced at once, the equipment moved to properly rated outlets, and the lack of sockets reported so that the estate or maintenance team can install a safe supply. Why the other options fail: patient care is exactly why the risk cannot be left, because a ward fire puts every patient on it in danger (A); taping the adaptors together keeps them connected but does nothing about the current or the heat (C); and moving the load onto the lighting circuit overloads a circuit never designed for medical equipment and defeats the protection that is supposed to trip (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask whether the particles fall or float. Large droplets fall within about two metres, so a surgical mask suffices. Droplet nuclei from TB, measles and chickenpox float and need a respirator and a room. When in doubt with a chronic cough, isolate first and de-escalate later.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IPC-0051$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An elective cholecystectomy is carried out and the biliary tract is opened under controlled conditions, with no unusual spillage. How should the nurse classify this wound?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Clean-contaminated, because a tract containing micro-organisms is entered in a controlled way"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Clean, because no infection was present anywhere and the operation had been planned well in advance"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Contaminated, because any operation that opens a hollow organ inevitably spills bacteria"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Dirty, because the biliary tract carries organisms and the wound must be treated as infected"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Clean-contaminated, because a tract containing micro-organisms is entered in a controlled way”. The four surgical wound classes run clean, clean-contaminated, contaminated and dirty. A wound is clean-contaminated when the respiratory, alimentary, genital or urinary tract is entered under controlled conditions and without unusual contamination — an elective cholecystectomy, a planned bowel resection, a routine hysterectomy. The bacteria are there, but they are contained, so the expected infection rate sits between the clean and the contaminated group, and prophylaxis is often given. Why the other options fail: a clean wound is one in which no hollow viscus is entered at all, such as a hernia repair or a breast biopsy (B); a contaminated wound is one with gross spillage of visceral contents, a traumatic wound, or an operation where the sterile technique has been broken (C); and a dirty or infected wound is one that already holds pus or a perforated viscus at the time of surgery (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask which end the fluid left from. Losses from above cause alkalosis; losses from below cause acidosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1094$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with an indwelling urinary catheter has drained nothing into the bag for four hours, and urine is now leaking around the outside of the tube. What is the most likely explanation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The kidneys have stopped making urine, so the bladder is empty and there is nothing at all to drain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The balloon has burst and the catheter has slipped down the urethra, so urine escapes beside it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The catheter is blocked, so urine builds up behind it and escapes around the tube instead"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The patient is drinking more than the bag can drain, so the excess urine spills out beside the tube"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “The catheter is blocked, so urine builds up behind it and escapes around the tube instead”. A catheter bypasses the urinary sphincter, so a correctly draining tube empties the bladder and no urine should appear beside it. When the lumen blocks — with sediment, mucus, blood clot or crystals, or because the tube is kinked or the bag is above the bladder — urine cannot leave through the tube, the bladder fills, and the pressure forces urine out along the path of least resistance, which is the space between the catheter and the urethral wall. The nurse checks the tubing for kinks, makes sure the bag hangs below the bladder, and reports so that the catheter can be irrigated or changed as prescribed. Why the other options fail: if the kidneys had stopped making urine there would be no leakage, because an empty bladder has nothing to push out (A); a burst balloon lets the catheter slide out altogether, and the tip is then visible rather than sitting in place (B); and drinking more makes the bag fill faster, not overflow through the urethra, as long as the drainage path is open (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask what the healing cells physically need to do. Cells migrate through moisture, not across crust.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-OPR-1160$q$;

-- Verify: expect 4
COMMIT;

