-- Batch 111: four more duplicate pairs closed.
--
-- four more duplicate pairs closed from the similarity scan
-- sterilisation versus disinfection, hair removal, PPE order, gentamicin levels
-- first batch chosen by stem-and-option cosine rather than by topic probing
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$While assessing a Stage 3 pressure injury the nurse finds that tissue loss extends sideways beneath the intact skin edge. How should this be recorded, and why does it matter?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "As maceration, because the skin edge around the wound has become softened and waterlogged by the exudate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "As undermining, because it changes the dressing choice and shows whether the wound is closing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "As a sinus tract, because the cavity runs in a narrow channel towards another opening"}, {"id": "d", "text": "As slough, because the yellow tissue in the base is the part that has broken down entirely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “As undermining, because it changes the dressing choice and shows whether the wound is closing”. Undermining is tissue loss that extends laterally under the wound edge, so the surface opening is smaller than the cavity beneath it. It is measured by inserting a probe gently under the edge with the clock face as a guide and recording the depth and the direction, for example 2 cm from four to eight o'clock, alongside the wound's length, width and depth. It matters for two reasons: the cavity has to be packed so that it heals from the base upwards, and if the undermining lessens from one dressing change to the next, the wound is closing. If it enlarges, the wound is deteriorating or the dressing is not reaching the cavity. Why the other options fail: maceration describes skin damaged by too much moisture at the edge, which is a surrounding-skin problem rather than a cavity under the edge (A); a sinus tract is a narrow blind channel, not the broad ledge of undermining that follows the whole rim (C); and slough is dead tissue lying on the wound bed, which is recorded separately as a percentage of the surface (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask whether spores survive. That single question separates disinfection from sterilisation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-OPR-1138$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Four patients on a Ghanaian ward are colonised with the same multi-resistant organism, and the ward is short of single rooms. What does cohorting them involve?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Moving each patient to a separate cubicle and keeping every door closed at all times"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Nursing them in the open ward but with a fresh pair of gloves for each episode of care"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sending them home until they are no longer colonised, to protect the other patients"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nursing them together in one bay with staff and equipment kept for that group alone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Nursing them together in one bay with staff and equipment kept for that group alone”. Cohorting is what a ward does when it has more colonised patients than single rooms. Patients carrying the same organism are grouped together in a defined area, and the nurses, the equipment and the cleaning arrangements are dedicated to that group so that the organism cannot be carried out to other patients on the same shift. It contains the spread without isolating each patient alone, and it is reviewed as soon as the numbers or the accommodation allow. Why the other options fail: separate cubicles are the ideal, but this ward has none to give, which is exactly the situation cohorting exists to solve (A); gloves do not stop the organism being carried between patients on hands, clothing and shared equipment (B); and sending colonised patients home is neither necessary nor safe, because colonisation is not illness and the patient may still need treatment (C).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Watch for interventions that damage the skin barrier. In infection control the question is often whether the preparation itself creates the portal of entry.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2529$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse on a Ghanaian ward has been wearing a surgical mask through a long morning of patient care. When should she change it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "At the end of the shift, since a surgical mask is designed to last for a full day of work"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Every two hours exactly, whether or not the mask has become damp during the shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "As soon as it becomes damp, soiled or torn, and never wear it hanging round the neck"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Only when it begins to smell, because that is the first sign that it has lost its protection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “As soon as it becomes damp, soiled or torn, and never wear it hanging round the neck”. A surgical mask works as a physical barrier and its filtering depends on the fabric staying dry. Once it is damp from breath, or soiled with blood or sputum, or torn, the barrier is lost and the mask becomes a warm wet surface held against the face, which is worse than no mask. It is removed by the ties without touching the front, and discarded into the clinical waste. Wearing it pulled down around the neck or pushed up on the head contaminates the inside and carries organisms from the mask to the skin. Why the other options fail: a mask is not designed to be worn for a whole shift, and length of wear is not the test — condition is (A); a fixed two-hourly change is arbitrary and can leave a soaking mask on the face in between (B); and smell is not the indicator, because the barrier is lost long before any odour appears (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Reason it out rather than memorising: gloves are dirtiest so they come off first, the mask protects the face so it comes off last. That logic reconstructs the sequence every time.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCB-2510$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient is receiving gentamicin and the prescriber asks for trough and peak serum levels. Why are these measurements needed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Because the gap between the effective and toxic dose is narrow, and the kidney clears the drug"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Because gentamicin is destroyed in the stomach, so the level shows how much was actually absorbed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Because the level proves the patient has taken the doses, which is otherwise impossible to know"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Because the level is used to choose the next antibiotic if the organism turns out to be resistant"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Because the gap between the effective and toxic dose is narrow, and the kidney clears the drug”. Gentamicin is an aminoglycoside with a low therapeutic index: the concentration that kills the organism is close to the concentration that damages the kidney and the eighth cranial nerve. It is also eliminated almost entirely unchanged by the kidney, so anything that reduces renal function — age, dehydration, sepsis, other nephrotoxic drugs — makes the drug accumulate. The trough is measured to show that clearance is adequate and the peak to show that the dose is high enough; the dose and interval are then adjusted. Why the other options fail: gentamicin is given parenterally and is not absorbed from the gut, so absorption from the stomach is irrelevant (B); measuring the level is not a test of adherence, and it is used to adjust dose rather than to police the patient (C); and susceptibility is decided by culture and sensitivity of the organism, not by how much drug is in the blood (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$When an observation is the reason for a check, act on it before giving the drug, not after.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-DRG-0992$q$;

-- Verify: expect 4
COMMIT;

