-- Batch 112: five duplicate pairs closed.
--
-- five duplicate pairs closed; eleven drafts died finding replacement ground
-- religious modesty in bathing, post-spinal headache, sacral eschar, CD countersignature, hand-rub duration
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient tells the nurse that he cannot be bathed by a woman, for religious reasons. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain to him that the bath is a necessary part of his nursing care and carry on with the bath as planned"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Respect the request and arrange for a male member of staff to help him wash"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask the patient's wife to bathe him on the ward so that the staff need not"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Record that he has refused care and leave him unwashed until the next day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Respect the request and arrange for a male member of staff to help him wash”. Washing is intimate care, and a request about who may see and touch the body is a legitimate expression of the patient's dignity, culture and religion. The nurse's duty is to deliver the care, not to insist on who delivers it, so the request is accepted without argument and the rota is rearranged: a male colleague, or a relative present with the patient's consent, can provide the bath. If no male staff are on duty, the bath is postponed to the next shift and that is recorded as an agreed plan rather than a refusal. Why the other options fail: overriding a religious or modesty objection is a failure of consent and damages trust, and the patient may then decline care altogether (A); handing intimate care to an untrained relative on a busy open ward removes the nurse's assessment of the skin and exposes the patient's body to everyone nearby (C); and writing it up as a refusal misrepresents what happened — he did not refuse care, he asked for a different carer, and leaving him unwashed is neglect (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Narrow cuff reads high, wide cuff reads low. For any measurement question, ask which direction the error pushes the number — a falsely normal result is more dangerous than an obviously wrong one, because nobody questions it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FUN-0025$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient has returned to the ward after surgery performed under spinal anaesthesia. Why is he nursed flat for the first few hours?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "To stop the anaesthetic from leaking out of the spinal canal and paralysing his legs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "To reduce the risk of a post-dural puncture headache while the puncture seals over"}, {"id": "c", "text": "To keep the blood pressure raised, because a spinal anaesthetic lowers it for a whole day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "To prevent the wound from bleeding, since lying flat reduces the pressure inside it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “To reduce the risk of a post-dural puncture headache while the puncture seals over”. The spinal needle leaves a small hole in the dura, and cerebrospinal fluid can continue to leak through it. As the pressure of the fluid around the brain falls, the brain sags slightly on its supporting structures and the patient develops a severe frontal or occipital headache that is worse on sitting or standing and relieved by lying down, sometimes with neck stiffness, nausea and photophobia. Lying flat for the first few hours reduces the leak and gives the puncture time to close. The nurse watches for that headache and reports it. Why the other options fail: the anaesthetic does not need to be kept in, because it is taken up by the nerve roots and its effect wears off regardless of position (A); a spinal does lower the blood pressure, but that is managed with fluids and vasopressors in theatre, and lying flat for hours is not the treatment for it (C); and the wound is not what is being protected here, so its pressure is irrelevant to the reason for the position (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$For any reservoir device, the bag must stay inflated throughout the respiratory cycle. If it collapses on inspiration, the flow is too low.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0126$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An elderly man who has been bedbound for a week has a black, hard, leathery patch over one heel that does not blanch. How should the nurse manage it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rub the patch firmly several times a day so that the blood supply returns to the tissue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Soak the heel and then cut away the black tissue with sterile scissors at the bedside"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep it clean and dry, keep all pressure off the heel, and report it for assessment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a warm compress to soften the black tissue and massage the skin around it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Keep it clean and dry, keep all pressure off the heel, and report it for assessment”. A black, hard, leathery patch that will not blanch is necrotic tissue — an eschar — over a pressure injury, and because the dead tissue hides the base the wound cannot be staged. It must not be rubbed, massaged or cut at the bedside: massage damages the already ischaemic tissue and can extend the injury, and blind cutting risks infection and damage to deeper structures. The heel is off-loaded completely with a device that floats it clear of the mattress, the eschar is kept dry and intact, and the wound is reported so that it can be assessed and any debridement planned by those trained to do it. Why the other options fail: rubbing and massaging reddened or blackened pressure areas is contraindicated, because it shears capillaries in tissue that has no blood supply to spare (A, D); and cutting into the eschar at the bedside is unsafe and painful, and it must not be done by the ward nurse (B).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sudden relief in a surgical abdomen means something burst. Escalate immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-TRA-1867$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is asked by a colleague to sign the controlled drugs register as the second checker for a dose that she did not see prepared or given. What should she do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Sign it, since the colleague is experienced and has already checked the balance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sign it, and add a note that she was not present so that the record stays honest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refuse to sign it, because the second signature must confirm a check she made"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the patient afterwards whether the injection was given, and then sign"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Refuse to sign it, because the second signature must confirm a check she made”. A controlled drug register is a legal record and the second signature is not a formality: it attests that the signatory personally saw the drug measured, saw the balance reconciled, and saw the dose administered or witnessed its destruction. Signing for a check that was never carried out makes the nurse personally accountable for another person's error, and it destroys the only audit trail there is for a drug that is both dangerous and open to diversion. The correct action is to decline, and to say why, and to offer to witness the next entry properly. Why the other options fail: the colleague's experience is not evidence that this particular dose was right, and the register records the checker, not the reputation of the person checked (A); a note that she was absent does not rescue a signature that certifies something untrue, and the entry becomes misleading rather than honest (B); and asking the patient afterwards cannot confirm that the right drug and dose were given or that the balance was correct (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Enoxaparin injection rules: Abdominal wall >= 2 inches from umbilicus + keep air bubble + 90-degree angle + NO aspiration + NO rubbing/massaging.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PROC-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$How long should a nurse spend rubbing her hands with alcohol-based hand rub during routine hand hygiene?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "20 to 30 seconds, covering every surface of both hands until they feel dry"}, {"id": "b", "text": "About five seconds, because the alcohol kills organisms on contact with the skin"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A full minute, scrubbing between each finger under running water as she goes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Only until the hands look and feel clean, since no fixed time is needed at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “20 to 30 seconds, covering every surface of both hands until they feel dry”. Alcohol-based hand rub has to reach every part of the hands — palms, backs, between the fingers, the thumbs, the fingertips and the wrists — and be rubbed in for about 20 to 30 seconds, long enough for the alcohol to act and short enough that staff will actually do it every time. The hands are rubbed until they are dry and are not wiped on a towel afterwards, because wiping removes the product before it has finished working. Washing with soap and water takes longer, about 40 to 60 seconds. Why the other options fail: five seconds does not give the alcohol time to act across the whole surface, and some organisms take longer to be inactivated (B); a full minute under running water describes soap-and-water washing, a different procedure with a different timing (C); and visible cleanliness is no test at all, because organisms cannot be seen and the hands look the same before and after (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$For spore-forming organisms, use soap and water and dedicated equipment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BATCH2-0039$q$;

-- Verify: expect 5
COMMIT;

