  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Clean the eye, start an antibiotic eye drop and teach the mother how to instil it at home”. Purulent conjunctivitis in a child is managed syndromically: the eye is cleaned, a topical antibiotic is started without waiting for a laboratory, and the mother is shown how to instil it, to wash her hands and to keep the child's towel separate. The child is reviewed, and referred only if the eye does not improve in two or three days or if there is pain, photophobia or a cloudy cornea. Why the other options fail: A — a compress alone does not treat a bacterial infection and lets it spread; C — padding both eyes stops the discharge draining and can hide a spreading infection, and it frightens a child; D — a swab is not needed for a first episode and referral takes the treatment away from the family for no gain.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Where follow-up is uncertain, treat everything the syndrome could be at the first contact.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MDA-1122$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An observational study finds that people who drink more sugary drinks have more diabetes. What can the nurse reasonably conclude?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The sugary drinks caused the diabetes, so the mechanism has now been proved by this study"}, {"id": "b", "text": "There is an association, but another factor may explain both, so causation is not proved"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nothing at all can be concluded, because observational studies are worthless"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The study is invalid because the participants were not allocated at random"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “There is an association, but another factor may explain both, so causation is not proved”. An observational study watches what people do; it can show that two things travel together but not which caused which. People who drink a lot of sugary drinks are also more likely to be heavier, older or less active, and any of those could raise the diabetes risk on its own. Randomisation or strong causal reasoning is needed before a cause is claimed. Why the other options fail: A — the direction and the mechanism are exactly what an observational design cannot settle; C — far too strong, since observational work established the link between smoking and lung cancer; D — the study is not invalid, it is simply limited in what it can show, and calling it invalid throws away useful evidence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask out of how many before believing any count. The denominator decides the answer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BST-1281$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 8 weeks' gestation asks the antenatal nurse whether she may take bitter herbal roots and blue cohosh for morning sickness. What should the nurse advise?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dilute infusions are safe in pregnancy because the dose reaching the fetus is much lower than the mother's"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only herbs traditionally used for morning sickness are safe during the first trimester of pregnancy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Avoid all herbal preparations unless an obstetric provider approves them, since many are uterotonic"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Herbal remedies may be started after 12 weeks, once organogenesis is complete in the fetus"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Avoid all herbal preparations unless an obstetric provider approves them, since many are uterotonic”. Herbal does not mean harmless, and pregnancy is the time when that matters most. Blue cohosh, pennyroyal, cassia and many bitter roots contain emmenagogue or uterotonic compounds that stimulate the uterus, and some are teratogenic; the dose in a cup of infusion is not standardised, so nobody can say how much the woman or the fetus received. The safe answer is that nothing is taken in pregnancy unless the obstetric team has approved it, and the woman is told why rather than simply told no. Why the other options fail: A — dilution does not make an active compound inactive, and the fetal dose is exactly what is unknown; B — a herb being traditional for morning sickness says nothing about its safety in pregnancy, and several traditional remedies are precisely the abortifacients; D — twelve weeks is when major organ formation ends, but growth, brain development and uterine sensitivity continue, so the risk does not switch off.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Maternal-fetal safety: Avoid unprescribed herbal medicines during pregnancy; many herbs possess uterotonic properties that cause miscarriage or teratogenicity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with stage III cervical cancer shows the nurse an unlabelled bottle from a roadside vendor, who promised it would cure cancer, HIV and diabetes within fourteen days without surgery. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Make no comment, respect the client's autonomy and let the oncology team raise it at the next review"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the client to postpone the next oncology review for two weeks so the mixture can be fairly tried"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that it is unregulated, check for FDA Ghana registration and warn that delay lets cancer spread"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Confiscate the bottle and tell the client that all herbal products are toxic and should never be used at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Explain that it is unregulated, check for FDA Ghana registration and warn that delay lets cancer spread”. Three things are wrong with the bottle and the nurse addresses all three: it has no label, so the dose and the contents are unknown; it makes a cure-all claim, which no single preparation can support; and it is being offered as a substitute for treatment in a cancer that is still treatable. The nurse is honest about the risk of delaying oncology care, checks whether the product carries Ghana FDA registration, and keeps the door open so the client does not simply stop attending. Why the other options fail: A — saying nothing is not respect, it is abandonment of a client about to make a dangerous choice; B — a two-week trial of an unproven mixture is exactly the delay that allows a treatable cancer to metastasise; D — confiscation is not the nurse's to do and the blanket claim that all herbals are toxic is false, which costs the nurse credibility.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Unregulated herbs: Warn against 'cure-all' claims and lack of quality control, emphasize the fatal risk of delaying conventional oncology treatment, and look for FDA certification.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client receiving chemotherapy has severe nausea and asks about acupressure at the P6 point on the inner wrist. How should the nurse explain its action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It stimulates acupoints that modulate autonomic pathways and release endorphins and serotonin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It stimulates the vagus nerve at the wrist, slowing the heart and abolishing the vomiting reflex"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It acts purely as a placebo; trials show no measurable neurochemical or autonomic change at all"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It blocks the phrenic nerve, paralysing the diaphragm and so preventing the physical act of vomiting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “It stimulates acupoints that modulate autonomic pathways and release endorphins and serotonin”. Acupressure at P6 is one of the complementary measures that guidelines accept alongside antiemetics, not instead of them. The best current explanation is that stimulating the point alters autonomic signalling and triggers release of endogenous opioids and serotonin, which dampens the emetic and pain pathways at the level of the brainstem and the gate in the dorsal horn. Why the other options fail: B — the vagus is not stimulated at the wrist in this way, and abolishing the reflex outright is not what acupressure does; C — controlled trials do report measurable effects and guideline bodies do recommend it, so calling it pure placebo overstates the evidence in the other direction; D — the phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm from the neck and has nothing to do with the wrist point, and paralysing the diaphragm would be a catastrophe rather than a treatment.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Complementary therapies: Acupressure/acupuncture stimulates neurovascular pathways, releasing endogenous endorphins and modulating autonomic emetic and pain reflexes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A community health nurse is teaching a group of market women about preventing cervical cancer. Which statement by the nurse is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Screening finds changes before they become cancer, and HPV vaccination protects girls"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only women with many children need screening, because the cervix is injured during each childbirth"}, {"id": "c", "text": "If you feel well there is no need to be screened, because early changes always cause bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cervical cancer cannot be prevented, only treated, because the virus stays in the body for life"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Screening finds changes before they become cancer, and HPV vaccination protects girls". Cervical cancer is almost always caused by <strong>persistent human papillomavirus infection</strong> and develops slowly through pre-cancerous stages that <strong>screening detects and simple treatment (cryotherapy or thermal ablation) cures</strong>. <strong>HPV vaccination</strong> of girls aged 9–14 prevents most cases. Early disease has no symptoms, which is why well women are screened.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Every sexually active woman is at risk regardless of parity.
• C — Pre-cancer and early cancer cause no symptoms; waiting for symptoms means waiting for advanced disease.
• D — Cervical cancer is one of the most preventable cancers through vaccination and screening.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — cervical cancer screening and HPV vaccination programme$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: prevention teaching should name both arms — vaccinate before exposure, screen before symptoms.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0235$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with leg weakness is booked for electromyography (EMG). Which explanation should the nurse give?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It is completely painless because nothing touches the skin; a scanner records the muscle from a distance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A very fine needle records the muscle activity at rest and when you tighten it, and you need not fast"}, {"id": "c", "text": "You will be given a light sedative an hour before, because the needle stimulation is uncomfortable"}, {"id": "d", "text": "You must not eat for four to six hours before the test, because a full stomach disturbs the recordings"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A very fine needle records the muscle activity at rest and when you tighten it, and you need not fast". EMG records the electrical activity of muscle fibres through <strong>needle electrodes</strong>, often combined with nerve-conduction studies using surface electrodes, to distinguish nerve from muscle disease. Brief discomfort and small bruises are expected; the patient should avoid creams on the skin and tell the technician if she takes anticoagulants.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Sedation would alter muscle activity and is not given.
• C — Needle insertion is part of the test, so 'nothing touches you' is inaccurate and sets up mistrust.
• D — There is no fasting requirement.

Reference: Neurology diagnostic procedure guidance; Ghana nursing procedure manual — patient preparation$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: accurate preparation builds trust — choose the option that tells the patient honestly what will happen and what will not.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0175$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A school nurse performs the Rinne test on a six-year-old girl. Which technique is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hold the vibrating stem on the mastoid until the sound fades, then move the prongs in front of the ear"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hold the vibrating fork beside the ear canal until the sound fades, then move it away and record the time"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the stem on the middle of the forehead and ask her in which ear the sound seems louder"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hold the fork about 5 cm behind the head and document how long she can hear the sound for"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Hold the vibrating stem on the mastoid until the sound fades, then move the prongs in front of the ear". The Rinne test compares <strong>bone conduction</strong> (fork on the mastoid) with <strong>air conduction</strong> (fork beside the ear canal). Normally air conduction is better, so the sound is heard again when the fork is moved to the ear. If bone conduction is longer, a <strong>conductive hearing loss</strong> such as glue ear or wax is likely.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Holding a fork behind the head tests nothing standardised.
• C — A fork on the forehead is the Weber test, which lateralises sound.
• D — Starting at the canal and timing alone does not compare air with bone conduction.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — school health screening; clinical examination of hearing$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: Rinne compares two routes — bone then air; Weber uses the forehead. Keep the two tests separate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0006$q$;

-- Verify: expect 13
COMMIT;

