-- Ten more items rebalanced; TCAM-010 rewritten as a birth-attendant referral item, closing its conflict with TCM-0678.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 75-year-old woman with osteoarthritis of the knees is prescribed oral naproxen. Which statement is **most accurate** about this drug in her age group?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It may be taken for as long as she needs because it can be bought without a prescription"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time, because of bleeding and kidney risk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Any dose is safe provided it is swallowed with a full glass of water and not on an empty stomach"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is a weak drug that works for osteoarthritis only when it is given in high doses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Use the lowest effective dose for the shortest time, because of bleeding and kidney risk". Reduced renal clearance, a more fragile gastric mucosa and frequent co-prescription of antihypertensives, anticoagulants and diuretics make NSAIDs <strong>high-risk medicines in older adults</strong>. Naproxen is given <strong>with food</strong>, often with gastric protection, and the nurse watches for black stools, reduced urine output and ankle swelling.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Over-the-counter availability does not make a drug safe; older adults need medical supervision.
• C — Naproxen is a potent NSAID; higher doses only increase toxicity.
• D — Water reduces oesophageal irritation but does nothing to prevent bleeding, renal injury or heart failure.

Reference: Ghana STG — osteoarthritis and NSAID use; Beers criteria for older adults$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: NSAID plus older adult — think stomach, kidney and heart, and pick the option that limits dose and duration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0068$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man in an acute manic episode is shouting, threatening staff and throwing furniture on admission. Which is the nurse's **initial** priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Attend to his hygiene and nutrition, which have been neglected for several days before admission"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep him and others safe: move him somewhere quiet, de-escalate and give prescribed sedation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Involve him gradually in unit activities so that his energy is channelled into the group programme"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Teach him about his diagnosis and the importance of lithium before he leaves the unit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Keep him and others safe: move him somewhere quiet, de-escalate and give prescribed sedation". A combative manic patient is a <strong>danger to himself and others</strong>, and his internal agitation will not settle by environmental measures alone. <strong>Safety comes first</strong>: fewer people and less noise, a calm firm approach, and prescribed sedation (for example a benzodiazepine with or without an antipsychotic) so that assessment and longer-term treatment can begin.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Teaching is impossible while he cannot concentrate and is unsafe.
• C — Group activity increases stimulation and risk during acute mania.
• D — Hygiene and nutrition are important but cannot be addressed until behaviour is controlled.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Act 2012 — least-restrictive care; Ghana STG — mania and rapid tranquillisation$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: aggression means safety first — environment, de-escalation and medication together — before any other need.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0248$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse sitting in the hospital cafeteria observes a visitor suddenly stand up, clutch their throat with both hands (universal sign of choking), and become unable to speak or cough. What sequence of first aid actions should the nurse perform?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Offer a glass of water and encourage small sips, so that the fluid lubricates the bolus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform a blind finger sweep, because a bolus at the larynx can usually be hooked out"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lay the victim flat and begin rescue breathing at once, because ventilation must be restored first"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give up to five firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then up to five abdominal thrusts"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Give up to five firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then up to five abdominal thrusts”. Clutching the throat, silence and an inability to cough are the signs of severe airway obstruction, and the adult sequence is five back blows followed by five abdominal thrusts, repeated until the object comes out or the victim becomes unresponsive. If he becomes unresponsive he is lowered to the floor and CPR is started, and the airway is checked each time the breaths are given. Why the other options fail: A — fluid cannot pass an obstructed airway and sips risk aspiration; B — a blind finger sweep can push a visible object deeper and is no longer taught outside a witnessed, visible object in a child; C — rescue breathing alone does not relieve a solid obstruction and wastes the seconds when thrusts would clear it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe choking management: 5 Back blows -> 5 Abdominal thrusts (repeat). If patient goes unconscious -> Begin CPR (compressions first). NEVER do blind finger sweeps!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-FUND-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a night shift a nurse on a 30-bed ward has one enrolled nurse and one health care assistant. Four call bells ring at once: a post-operative patient says his dressing is soaked with fresh blood; a patient with dementia is climbing over the bed rails; a patient wants her analgesic that is now due; and a relative is asking for a blanket. How should the nurse **best** organise the response?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the analgesic first, because it is now due and that patient has already waited a long time for it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "See the bleeding patient herself, send the nurse to the dementia patient, and delegate the blanket"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Send the health care assistant to assess the bleeding so that she can manage the dementia patient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the relative to sit with the dementia patient, because a familiar face will calm her down"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "See the bleeding patient herself, send the nurse to the dementia patient, and delegate the blanket". The nurse <strong>keeps the patient with the greatest physiological threat (haemorrhage) for her own assessment</strong>, delegates the immediate safety need (fall risk) to the enrolled nurse who can stay with and reassure the patient, and gives the assistant a task within her scope. The analgesic is important but a few minutes' delay is not dangerous, and medicines cannot be delegated to the assistant.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Assessing bleeding requires a registered nurse; it cannot be delegated to an assistant.
• C — Routine medication does not take precedence over possible haemorrhage or an imminent fall.
• D — Relatives are not staff; a patient climbing over rails needs trained supervision, and the bleeding patient has been ignored.

Reference: NMC Ghana — delegation and accountability; prioritisation frameworks (ABC, safety, then comfort)$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: rank by threat — circulation first, then injury risk, then comfort — and match each task to the most appropriate person's scope.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0223$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman who delivered 3 days ago telephones the postnatal clinic saying her breasts are hard, hot, swollen and painful, and the baby cannot latch. Which advice should the nurse give?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop breastfeeding for 48 hours so that the breasts rest, and express only enough to relieve pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bind the breasts tightly and restrict fluids for 48 hours so that the milk supply reduces"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Feed or express often, warmth before and cold after, soften the areola, and give paracetamol"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start antibiotics at once because this is mastitis, and stop feeding from that breast until it settles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Feed or express often, warmth before and cold after, soften the areola, and give paracetamol". Bilateral hard, swollen, painful breasts on day 3 are <strong>engorgement</strong>, caused by milk coming in plus vascular congestion. The remedy is <strong>frequent, effective milk removal</strong>: warmth beforehand to help flow, <strong>reverse-pressure softening or hand expression</strong> so the baby can latch, cold afterwards for comfort, and analgesia. Untreated engorgement leads to blocked ducts and mastitis.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Binding and fluid restriction are outdated and painful and do not help.
• B — Stopping feeds worsens engorgement and reduces supply.
• D — Mastitis is usually unilateral with a wedge of redness and fever; antibiotics are not indicated for simple engorgement.

Reference: Ghana Safe Motherhood Protocol — postnatal breast care; WHO breastfeeding counselling$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: engorgement is a drainage problem — empty the breast more often, not less, and make latching possible.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0165$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A hospitalized client receiving intravenous ceftriaxone for severe bacterial pneumonia requests that their church pastor and family prayer group be allowed into the room to perform anointing with holy oil, lay hands, and pray for divine healing. What is the nurse's culturally and clinically competent action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Allow the prayer and anointing at once, since spiritual rituals carry no infection risk and delay is disrespectful"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Decline the visit until discharge, because religious gatherings are a recognised source of hospital infection"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange the prayer at a suitable time, keep hand hygiene, and let the antibiotics continue uninterrupted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Postpone the visit until the antibiotic course is finished, so that prayer cannot reduce its effectiveness"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Arrange the prayer at a suitable time, keep hand hygiene, and let the antibiotics continue uninterrupted”. Spiritual care is part of nursing, and a request for prayer and anointing at a frightening time is reasonable. The nurse makes it possible: a mutually suitable time, the fewest people needed, hand hygiene before and after, and the infusion and antibiotics running to schedule. Nothing about the ritual interferes with the treatment, and refusing it would damage trust for no clinical gain. Why the other options fail: A — allowing a group in without any thought about hand hygiene or crowding ignores a real infection risk in a patient with severe pneumonia; B — refusing outright is not justified, since the risk is manageable; D — prayer has no effect on the action of ceftriaxone, so postponing the visit has no clinical basis and simply withholds support the patient asked for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Faith-based integration: Accommodate and support spiritual rituals that bring comfort while ensuring infection control and uninterrupted medical therapy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newly diagnosed young man with type 1 diabetes asks the nurse, "Why is my insulin called human insulin? Is it taken from people?" Which response is **best**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It prevents the eye, kidney and nerve damage that animal insulins cause, because it is purer"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It is made by bacteria or yeast with genetic technology, so it matches the insulin your pancreas makes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It is purified from donated human blood plasma, so it is exactly the insulin a healthy pancreas makes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It means you will have no low blood sugar at all, because it matches your own insulin exactly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "It prevents the eye, kidney and nerve damage that animal insulins cause, because it is purer". Human insulin is produced by <strong>recombinant DNA technology</strong>: the human insulin gene is inserted into bacteria or yeast, which then manufacture insulin with the <strong>same amino-acid sequence as endogenous insulin</strong>. Because it is not foreign protein, immune reactions and antibody formation are rare. Analogue insulins are modified versions designed to act faster or longer.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Long-term complications result from blood glucose control, not the type of insulin.
• C — Insulin is never extracted from human blood or tissue.
• D — Any insulin can cause hypoglycaemia if dose, food and activity do not match.

Reference: Ghana STG — type 1 diabetes; WHO essential medicines — insulin$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: the best teaching response is accurate and answers the actual question; eliminate options that over-promise or misstate the source.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0129$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman is scheduled for an MRI scan of her lumbar spine. Which statement shows that the nurse's teaching has been **effective**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The wires they stick on my head and chest will not hurt, because they only record activity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "I will lie still in a narrow tunnel for 30 to 45 minutes; it is noisy but painless, and no metal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The dye they inject will turn my urine green for about a day, which is harmless and expected"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I will be put to sleep for the scan and will wake up back on the ward two hours later, with no memory of it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "I will lie still in a narrow tunnel for 30 to 45 minutes; it is noisy but painless, and no metal". MRI uses a <strong>powerful magnet and radio waves</strong>, not X-rays. The patient lies still in a tunnel for 30–60 minutes while the machine knocks loudly; it is painless. <strong>All metal must be removed</strong>, and pacemakers, cochlear implants, some aneurysm clips and metal fragments are contraindications. Claustrophobic patients may need a mild sedative.

Why the other options fail:
• A — General anaesthesia is not used for a routine MRI.
• C — No electrodes or wires are attached — that describes an EEG or ECG.
• D — No urine-colouring dye is used; gadolinium contrast is sometimes given but does not change urine colour.

Reference: Radiology patient-preparation guidelines; Ghana nursing procedure manual — diagnostic investigations$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: a teaching-evaluation item — the correct statement contains only true features (still, noisy, painless, no metal).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0086$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man known to drink heavily becomes restless, tremulous and irritable on the second day after surgery. The doctor prescribes chlorpromazine for the agitation. The nurse should **query** this prescription because:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "She does not know whether he is allergic to chlorpromazine, and a second dose could kill him outright"}, {"id": "b", "text": "These symptoms are alcohol withdrawal; chlorpromazine lowers the seizure threshold, so use a benzodiazepine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He is not psychotic, and an antipsychotic must never be used outside the psychiatric setting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nurses should query every prescription written by a junior doctor, because junior doctors make more errors than consultants"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "These symptoms are alcohol withdrawal; chlorpromazine lowers the seizure threshold, so use a benzodiazepine". Restlessness, tremor and irritability 24–72 hours after the last drink are <strong>alcohol withdrawal</strong>, which carries a risk of seizures and delirium tremens. Phenothiazines <strong>lower the seizure threshold, cause hypotension and mask progression</strong>. The standard treatment is a <strong>benzodiazepine such as diazepam</strong>, with thiamine, fluids and close monitoring.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Unknown allergy status applies to any new drug and is not the specific reason to query this order.
• C — Antipsychotics are sometimes used in non-psychotic agitation; the issue is the specific danger in withdrawal.
• D — Nurses question prescriptions on clinical grounds, not routinely by seniority.

Reference: Ghana STG — alcohol withdrawal; WHO mhGAP$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: match the agitation to its cause first — withdrawal needs a benzodiazepine, and the wrong sedative can precipitate a seizure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0109$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman in a village has been in labour for twelve hours with the traditional birth attendant and the baby has still not been born. What should the attendant do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Continue to wait, because a first labour commonly lasts longer than twelve hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give her a herbal preparation to strengthen the contractions and keep waiting at home"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arrange immediate referral to the health centre and send a note of the labour with her"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask the family to bring a second attendant who has more experience of difficult births"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Arrange immediate referral to the health centre and send a note of the labour with her”. Twelve hours without delivery is prolonged labour, and the attendants at a village birth are judged on recognising it early rather than on finishing at home. The woman is referred without delay to the health centre, with a note of when labour began, the membranes, the contractions and any bleeding, and someone goes with her. Why the other options fail: A — waiting turns a delay into an obstructed labour, which is where ruptured uterus and stillbirth come from; B — herbal oxytocics can cause a hypertonic uterus or rupture and keep the woman away from care; D — a second attendant at home does not provide the monitoring or the theatre that a prolonged labour may need.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Perioperative rule: Stop all herbal supplements at least 2 to 3 weeks prior to elective surgery to prevent bleeding and anesthesia interactions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-010$q$;

-- Verify: expect 10
COMMIT;

