UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A community nurse plans a diabetes screening outreach at a market in Kumasi. Which approach is **most likely** to reach the people at highest risk?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place posters at the regional hospital inviting people to a weekday diabetes clinic there"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Work with the market queen and traders to screen where they work, at a quiet hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Screen only those already recorded as having hypertension in their health record"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hand out leaflets about the symptoms of diabetes at the market, without any testing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Work with the market queen and traders to screen where they work, at a quiet hour". Market traders — often middle-aged women with sedentary work, irregular meals and limited time — are a high-risk group that rarely attends hospital-based services. <strong>Taking the service to where people are, engaging community gatekeepers, fitting the timing to their work and giving immediate results with a referral pathway</strong> are the principles of effective community screening.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Hospital-based promotion reaches people who already use health services, not the hard-to-reach.
• C — Information without testing does not identify undiagnosed disease.
• D — Restricting to known hypertensives misses many undiagnosed diabetics.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — community health and NCD screening outreach guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: screening finds disease only if the right people come — go to them, use their leaders, and close the loop with results and referral.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0164$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A ward in-charge notices that a newly qualified nurse has twice documented medicines as given before actually administering them. Which action should the in-charge take **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Report her to the Nursing and Midwifery Council immediately, before a patient is harmed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Announce at the ward meeting that documentation errors will no longer be tolerated by anyone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Speak to her privately, name the incidents, explain the risk and agree a supervision plan"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassign the nurse to duties that do not involve medicines, without giving her any explanation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Speak to her privately, name the incidents, explain the risk and agree a supervision plan". Documenting before administering is a serious practice issue because a dose may be missed and no one will know. Good leadership addresses it <strong>directly, privately and specifically</strong>, finds out why (workload, misunderstanding, habit), teaches correct practice and arranges <strong>supervision and follow-up</strong>. Escalation follows if the behaviour continues or harm has occurred.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Reassignment without feedback leaves the nurse unaware of the problem and unable to improve.
• B — A general announcement avoids the real conversation and humiliates without teaching.
• D — Regulatory referral is for serious or persistent misconduct after local measures; the first step is local correction.

Reference: NMC Ghana — code of professional conduct; medicines administration record standards$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: performance problems are managed privately, specifically and developmentally first — public or punitive steps only when that fails.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0245$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with mechanical heart valve replacement taking chronic oral warfarin therapy begins consuming large daily doses of Ginkgo biloba and high-potency Garlic supplements to 'improve memory and heart health.' When the client reports spontaneous epistaxis and extensive ecchymoses, which herb-drug interaction mechanism must the nurse identify?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Garlic and ginkgo induce hepatic CYP2C9 and lower the INR, so increase the warfarin dose and recheck in a week"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Garlic is rich in vitamin K and antagonises warfarin, reducing its effect and risking valve thrombosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ginkgo and garlic both inhibit platelet aggregation, which adds to warfarin and greatly raises bleeding risk"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neither herb affects haemostasis; only dietary vitamin K alters the INR during stable warfarin therapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Ginkgo and garlic both inhibit platelet aggregation, which adds to warfarin and greatly raises bleeding risk”. Ginkgo, garlic, ginseng and ginger all reduce platelet aggregation, and taken with warfarin they add a second anticoagulant effect on top of the first. In a patient with a mechanical valve the result is epistaxis and bruising at an INR that may look acceptable, so the herbs are stopped and the bleeding is assessed rather than the warfarin simply increased. Why the other options fail: A — the interaction is on platelets, not on CYP2C9 induction, and increasing the dose would make the bleeding worse; B — garlic does not contain enough vitamin K to antagonise warfarin, and this reverses the mechanism; D — both herbs measurably affect haemostasis, which is precisely why the bleeding occurred.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NCLEX rule: The '4 G's' (Ginkgo, Garlic, Ginseng, Ginger) increase bleeding risk when taken with anticoagulants or antiplatelets.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 78-year-old man with moderate dementia becomes agitated and tries to leave the ward every evening, saying he must 'go home to feed the goats'. Which nursing approach is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain firmly each evening that he no longer keeps goats and that this is a hospital"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply a lap restraint each evening, because he is not safe walking about after dark"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the evening calm and predictable, reduce noise, engage him and redirect him"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give a sedative every evening before the behaviour starts, because prevention beats crisis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Keep the evening calm and predictable, reduce noise, engage him and redirect him". Late-day restlessness ('sundowning') is common in dementia and worsens with fatigue, noise, poor lighting and unmet needs. <strong>Validation and redirection</strong> — acknowledging the feeling behind the statement and guiding him to a soothing activity — along with a <strong>structured, low-stimulus environment</strong>, reduce distress without the harms of restraint or sedation.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Restraints increase agitation, injury and deconditioning and are a last resort.
• B — Repeated correction confuses and distresses a person who cannot retain the information.
• D — Routine sedation raises fall and aspiration risk; non-drug approaches are tried first.

Reference: Ghana STG — dementia and behavioural symptoms; WHO mhGAP dementia module$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: for behaviour in dementia, change the environment and the approach before reaching for restraint or medication.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0189$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A community health nurse assesses pain in an 84-year-old client with osteoarthritis. What key principle should guide the nurse's evaluation of pain in older adult patients?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Older adults often underreport pain from fear of addiction or of losing independence"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Older people gradually lose their pain receptors, so they feel pain much less intensely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Severe persistent pain is a normal and expected consequence of growing older"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Older adults cannot use a numeric rating scale reliably, so their pain must be guessed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Older adults often underreport pain from fear of addiction or of losing independence”. Pain is never a normal part of growing older, and the older adult is at particular risk of having it undertreated. Many underreport it because they fear becoming addicted to analgesia, because they fear losing independence or becoming a burden, or because they believe that pain is simply inevitable at their age; some understate it or dismiss it as part of ageing itself. The nurse must therefore screen actively at every contact, accept the patient's report without waiting for outward signs of distress, and advocate for analgesia and non-drug measures rather than assume that quiet means comfortable. Why the other options fail: pain receptors are not lost with age and pain thresholds do not simply rise, so ageing does not blunt pain (B); persistent pain is not a healthy consequence of growing older, and left untreated it reduces mobility, sleep, appetite and independence (C); and most older adults can use a simple 0 to 10 scale, a verbal descriptor scale or a faces scale reliably once sight, hearing and language are accommodated (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Core gerontological truth: Pain is NEVER a normal consequence of aging. Actively assess older adults because they tend to underreport.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0106$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man taking phenelzine, a monoamine oxidase inhibitor, is brought to the emergency unit with a throbbing occipital headache, blood pressure 220/130 mmHg, palpitations and sweating after a meal of momoni (fermented fish) and aged cheese. Which action should the nurse anticipate **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give intravenous phentolamine or labetalol to lower the blood pressure urgently, with monitoring"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage oral fluids and rest in a quiet, dark room until the headache settles by itself"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give intravenous diazepam to control his anxiety and bring the blood pressure down slowly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give a loading dose of oral phenelzine to restore the enzyme levels that the tyramine-rich meal has depleted"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Give intravenous phentolamine or labetalol to lower the blood pressure urgently, with monitoring". MAOIs prevent the breakdown of <strong>tyramine</strong>, found in fermented, aged and pickled foods such as momoni, kenam, aged cheese, soy sauce and tap beer. Ingestion triggers a massive release of noradrenaline — a <strong>hypertensive crisis</strong> that can cause stroke or myocardial infarction. Treatment is <strong>urgent parenteral blood-pressure reduction</strong> with an alpha-blocker or labetalol, and ECG monitoring.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Sedation does not lower blood pressure adequately in this situation and may mask deterioration.
• C — More phenelzine would worsen the crisis.
• D — A dark room helps the headache but does nothing for a blood pressure that threatens a stroke.

Reference: Ghana STG — hypertensive emergency; MAOI dietary interactions$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: MAOI plus tyramine food plus severe hypertension equals hypertensive crisis — bring the pressure down with a parenteral drug now.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0167$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 6-year-old with sickle cell disease is admitted with a vaso-occlusive crisis affecting both legs. Which nursing intervention is the **priority**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give analgesia promptly and regularly, score the pain and keep the child well hydrated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apply ice packs to the painful legs to reduce the swelling and numb the pain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict fluids to prevent pulmonary oedema developing during the painful crisis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage vigorous exercise to improve the circulation and break up the sickled cells in the legs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Give analgesia promptly and regularly, score the pain and keep the child well hydrated". A vaso-occlusive crisis is caused by sickled cells blocking small vessels, producing severe ischaemic pain. The priorities are <strong>effective analgesia within 30 minutes of arrival</strong> (paracetamol, NSAIDs and opioids as needed), <strong>hydration</strong> to reduce blood viscosity, <strong>warmth</strong>, oxygen if saturations fall, and a search for a precipitating infection.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Cold causes vasoconstriction and worsens sickling; warmth is applied instead.
• C — Exercise increases oxygen demand and pain; rest is advised during a crisis.
• D — Dehydration promotes sickling; fluids are given at maintenance or above with monitoring.

Reference: Ghana STG — sickle cell disease: management of vaso-occlusive crisis$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in sickle crisis think 'pain, fluids, warmth, oxygen' — and reject anything cold, dry or strenuous.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0218$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Six days after a laparotomy, a man coughs and feels 'something give'. The nurse finds the wound edges separated with a loop of bowel visible. Which action should the nurse take **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Push the bowel gently back into the abdomen and apply a firm pressure dressing to hold it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sit him up on the edge of the bed to ease the pressure on the wound and give him a drink of water"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cover the bowel with warm saline-soaked dressings, lay him flat, and call the surgeon urgently"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the wound exposed to the air so that the surgeon can inspect it as soon as he arrives"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Cover the bowel with warm saline-soaked dressings, lay him flat, and call the surgeon urgently". This is <strong>evisceration</strong>, a surgical emergency. The priorities are to <strong>keep the bowel moist and protected</strong> (dry bowel becomes ischaemic), <strong>reduce intra-abdominal pressure</strong> (supine, knees bent, no coughing or straining), keep him <strong>nil by mouth</strong> and prepare for return to theatre, while monitoring for shock.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Exposed bowel dries and dies within minutes; it must be covered.
• B — Sitting up increases abdominal pressure and may extrude more bowel; oral intake is stopped because surgery is imminent.
• D — Replacing bowel outside theatre introduces infection and can damage the gut; pressure dressings constrict the loop.

Reference: Ghana STG — post-operative complications: wound dehiscence and evisceration$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: moist, covered, pressure off, surgeon called — in that order; never put anything back.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0173$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 10-month-old is admitted with bronchiolitis. The nurse finds a respiratory rate of 68 breaths per minute, marked subcostal recession, nasal flaring, grunting and an oxygen saturation of 88 % in air; the baby is too breathless to feed. Which action is the **priority**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give a nebulised bronchodilator and reassess the work of breathing and the saturation in 20 minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage the mother to keep breastfeeding so that the baby stays calm and hydrated"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Start oral antibiotics and discharge the baby with advice to return if the breathing worsens"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give humidified oxygen to keep saturations above 92 %, feed by tube or drip, and tell the doctor"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Give humidified oxygen to keep saturations above 92 %, feed by tube or drip, and tell the doctor". Grunting, severe recession, a rate above 60, hypoxia and inability to feed are signs of <strong>severe bronchiolitis with impending respiratory failure</strong>. <strong>Oxygen</strong> is the main treatment; a breathless infant must not be fed orally because of <strong>aspiration risk</strong>, so fluids are given by tube or vein, and the baby needs close monitoring for the need of high-flow or ventilatory support.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Bronchodilators have little benefit in bronchiolitis and must not delay oxygen.
• B — A baby breathing at 68 per minute cannot coordinate sucking and breathing and may aspirate.
• C — Bronchiolitis is viral; antibiotics are not indicated, and this baby is far too sick for discharge.

Reference: Ghana STG — bronchiolitis; WHO pocket book of hospital care for children$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: count the danger signs (grunting, saturation under 90 %, cannot feed) — oxygen first and nothing by mouth.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0250$q$;

-- Verify: expect 9
COMMIT;

