-- Batch 119: 11 length coding, tranche 5.
--
-- Eleven items where the correct option was the longest of the four, brought into
-- one length band. Five ncx items keep their explain text and only have the
-- auto-generated quote regenerated. Six carry rewritten rationales: MOD-OBS-011,
-- MOD-TCAM-017 and PEDS2-0008 previously had no CORRECT quote at all and now do.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man admitted with an asthma attack is on an intravenous aminophylline infusion. He now has clear breath sounds and is breathing comfortably when his cannula tissues. Which action by the nurse is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the infusion, tell the doctor he has improved and ask whether inhaled therapy can replace it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Re-site the cannula in another vein and restart the infusion at exactly the same rate as before"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a warm compress, insert a new cannula elsewhere and continue all the intravenous medicines unchanged"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wait two hours, reassess him carefully and restart the infusion only if he starts to wheeze again"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Stop the infusion, tell the doctor he has improved and ask whether inhaled therapy can replace it". Aminophylline has a <strong>narrow therapeutic index</strong> and intravenous access carries its own risks, so therapy is stepped down as soon as the patient improves. The infiltrated line gives the nurse a natural point to <strong>report the assessment findings and ask for the plan to be reviewed</strong> rather than automatically re-establishing a drug that may no longer be needed.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Continuing 'unchanged' ignores the nurse's assessment that he has improved.
• C — Restarting without review continues a potentially unnecessary, toxic infusion.
• D — Leaving a prescribed infusion off for two hours without informing the prescriber is unsafe; the decision belongs with the doctor, informed by the nurse.

Reference: Ghana STG — acute asthma; aminophylline monitoring$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: when the patient has changed, the plan should change — report the assessment and recommend, rather than mechanically restoring the old order.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0153$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Following the normal vaginal delivery of a vigorous infant, the midwife carries out Active Management of the Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL) to prevent postpartum haemorrhage. Which triad of clinical actions defines standard AMTSL?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pushing hard on the fundus after delivery, removing the placenta by hand within five minutes, and applying ice to the perineum"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Clamping the cord within five seconds, asking the mother to bear down to expel the placenta, and packing the uterus with gauze"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Repairing the episiotomy, giving an intravenous ergometrine bolus before the shoulders deliver, and waiting sixty minutes before traction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oxytocin 10 IU IM within a minute of birth, controlled cord traction with counter-traction, and fundal massage after the placenta"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Oxytocin 10 IU IM within a minute of birth, controlled cord traction with counter-traction, and fundal massage after the placenta". Active management reduces primary postpartum haemorrhage by more than half, and it works because each of the three actions empties the uterus or keeps it contracted. A prophylactic uterotonic is given within one minute of birth, once a second twin has been excluded; controlled cord traction is applied during a contraction while the other hand counter-tracts on the lower uterine segment above the symphysis, which stops the uterus being pulled down or inverted; and the fundus is palpated and massaged as soon as the placenta is out. Why the other options fail: pushing on the fundus, pulling the placenta out by hand within five minutes and icing the perineum are not part of the triad, and manual removal is reserved for a retained placenta (A); clamping the cord within seconds deprives the newborn of placental transfusion, and uterine packing is not part of active management (B); ergometrine given before the shoulders are delivered can entrap a second twin, and an hour of expectant waiting is the opposite of active management (C).$q$,
  strategy = $q$AMTSL Triad: 1. Uterotonic (Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 min); 2. Controlled cord traction with suprapubic counter-traction; 3. Uterine massage after delivery of placenta.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-OBS-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 7-year-old boy's mother is dying of cancer. Which understanding of death should the nurse expect him to have?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "He still sees death as temporary and reversible, like sleep, and expects his mother to come back to him soon"}, {"id": "b", "text": "He already has a fully adult understanding of death as universal, irreversible and certain for everyone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "He has no concept of death at all and will not react to her illness or to her death in any way"}, {"id": "d", "text": "He is beginning to grasp that death is final but may believe his bad behaviour caused it and feel guilty"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "He is beginning to grasp that death is final but may believe his bad behaviour caused it and feel guilty". School-age children (about 6–9 years) are moving from the pre-schooler's reversible view of death towards understanding its <strong>finality</strong>, yet their thinking is still concrete and <strong>magical</strong>: they may believe death is a punishment, that it is catching, or that they caused it. Simple honest explanations, reassurance that nothing he did caused the illness, and permission to ask questions are key.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Seeing death as temporary and reversible is typical of pre-school children (3–5 years).
• B — Even toddlers react to loss and separation; a 7-year-old certainly has a concept of death.
• C — An abstract adult concept of death — universal, irreversible, inevitable — usually develops from about 9–10 years and in adolescence.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — child and adolescent psychosocial care; developmental concepts of death$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: match the concept of death to the developmental stage — pre-school reversible, school-age final-but-magical, adolescent abstract.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0216$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During an admission medication reconciliation, a nurse asks, 'Do you take any medications?' The client responds, 'No, I don't take any pills.' To obtain a truly complete and accurate medication history that captures traditional, complementary, and alternative remedies, what follow-up inquiry must the nurse make?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Do you take any herbal teas, roots, tonics, traditional enemas, vitamins or supplements you make or buy?"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Record 'no medications' as stated and revisit the medication history at discharge, when the client is less anxious"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ask only about prescription medicines, since herbal products and traditional preparations are not medicines"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask whether the client takes any supplements at all, without naming examples or asking where they came from"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Do you take any herbal teas, roots, tonics, traditional enemas, vitamins or supplements you make or buy?". Clients do not count herbal teas, roots, tonics, vitamins, dietary supplements or traditional enemas (bentua) as medicines, because they think of them as foods, tonics or spiritual preparations, so the bare question "Do you take any medications?" produces a confident and wrong "none". The nurse has to name the categories: ask about herbal teas and roots, tonics, botanical mixtures, traditional enemas, vitamins and over-the-counter supplements, and ask whether they are made at home or bought from a herbal practitioner. Only a named and specific prompt retrieves these, and the answer matters, because several of them interact with prescribed drugs. Why the other options fail: recording a bare "no" as stated leaves a false-negative history and is the very error being corrected (B); limiting the question to prescription medicines repeats the assumption that only pills are medicines (C); and a vague prompt about supplements does not cue teas, roots or enemas, which the client does not classify as supplements at all (D). Reference: Ghana STG — traditional and complementary medicine; medication reconciliation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Medication reconciliation: Explicitly ask about herbal teas, roots, tonics, enemas, and dietary supplements—clients rarely view these as 'medicines'.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A student says he drinks several caffeinated energy drinks each night to study. He reports palpitations and poor sleep. What is the **best** advice?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Continue the drinks but add a sedative at night so that he can get to sleep"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Switch to a larger quantity of the sugar-free version, since sugar is the only ingredient that matters"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reduce and then stop the drinks, because caffeine and sugar cause the palpitations and poor sleep"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure him that energy drinks are harmless in any quantity for a healthy young person like him"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Reduce and then stop the drinks, because caffeine and sugar cause the palpitations and poor sleep". High caffeine intake causes tachycardia, palpitations, tremor, anxiety and insomnia, and the doses in energy drinks are often far higher than in a cup of coffee, with several consumed in a session. The irony is central to the counselling: sleep is when memory is consolidated, so the drinks undermine the studying they are taken to support. Reduction should be gradual, because abrupt cessation after heavy use causes headache and irritability. The sugar load adds dental and metabolic harm. Palpitations that persist after caffeine is stopped warrant assessment, since they should resolve if caffeine was the cause.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Adding a sedative to counteract a stimulant treats the symptom while leaving the cause untouched, and introduces dependence risk in a young person. It is poor practice.
• B — Removing the sugar addresses only part of the problem, and increasing the quantity raises the caffeine load, which is what causes the palpitations and insomnia.
• D — Energy drinks are not harmless in quantity. Cardiac events have been reported in young people with high intake, and this student is already symptomatic.

Reference: WHO guidance on caffeine and adolescent health; Ghana Food and Drugs Authority$q$,
  strategy = $q$When a remedy causes the problem it is meant to solve, name the loop. Stimulants taken to study destroy the sleep that consolidates learning.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3125$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman with severe pre-eclampsia is receiving magnesium sulphate. Why must intravenous fluids be given cautiously and urine output monitored closely?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fluids should be given rapidly to raise her blood pressure back towards the normal range"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Magnesium sulphate causes excessive urine output, so large volumes of fluid must always be infused to keep up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Fluid restriction is unnecessary because the kidneys are not affected in pre-eclampsia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Capillary leak and low oncotic pressure favour pulmonary oedema, and magnesium is cleared renally"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Capillary leak and low oncotic pressure favour pulmonary oedema, and magnesium is cleared renally". Two dangers run in parallel. Endothelial damage in pre-eclampsia produces capillary leak, and plasma oncotic pressure is low, so infused fluid readily enters the lungs and pulmonary oedema is a leading cause of maternal death in this condition. At the same time magnesium sulphate is cleared entirely by the kidney, so falling urine output allows the level to climb towards toxicity. This is why the monitoring regimen checks respiratory rate at sixteen or above, urine output of at least 100 mL over four hours, and the presence of the patellar reflex before each dose, with calcium gluconate available as the antidote.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Rapid fluid loading in pre-eclampsia precipitates pulmonary oedema. Blood pressure is controlled with antihypertensives such as labetalol, methyldopa or nifedipine, not with fluid.
• B — Magnesium sulphate does not cause a diuresis. Urine output tends to fall in severe pre-eclampsia, which is exactly why toxicity becomes a risk.
• C — Renal function is frequently impaired in severe pre-eclampsia, and oliguria is part of the disease. Assuming normal kidneys would allow magnesium to accumulate unnoticed.

Reference: WHO recommendations for prevention and treatment of pre-eclampsia and eclampsia; Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$When a drug is cleared by the kidney, urine output becomes a drug safety measurement, not just a fluid one.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-3164$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nursing student asks how Erikson's theory differs from Freud's account of development. Which statement is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erikson described the psychosexual stages, while Freud described the psychosocial task of each age"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Both theories end at adolescence and deny any further development in adult life"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Erikson's stages are purely biological, while Freud's account is purely social and environmental"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Erikson described psychosocial conflicts across the lifespan; Freud's stages end at adolescence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Erikson described psychosocial conflicts across the lifespan; Freud's stages end at adolescence". Freud built his account on psychosexual stages — oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital — and treated the personality as largely settled by the end of adolescence. Erikson kept the idea of stages but moved the engine from sexuality to the relationship between the person and their social world, and he ran eight of them from infancy to old age, each turning on a conflict that has to be resolved: trust against mistrust, autonomy against shame, intimacy against isolation, and at the end integrity against despair. That is why a nurse can use Erikson with a toddler and with an eighty-year-old, whereas Freud's frame stops at the adolescent. Why the other options fail: option A swaps the two theorists and gives the psychosexual stages to Erikson (A); Erikson was arguing precisely against the idea that development ends at adolescence, so B misdescribes the whole point of his theory (B); and neither account is purely biological or purely social, since both are interactions between the child and the environment, which is why they are called psychosexual and psychosocial (C). Reference: Ghana nursing curriculum — developmental theories.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Erikson equals psychosocial and lifespan. Freud equals psychosexual and childhood.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PEDS2-0008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Two days after a major fire at a market, a nurse is supporting survivors. A woman is tearful, sleeping poorly and repeatedly describing the event. What is the **most appropriate** initial response?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Refer her at once for psychiatric assessment and medication, before she is allowed to leave the unit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Advise her to avoid thinking or talking about the fire, so that she can put it behind her and recover"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell her that she is fortunate to have survived when so many other people at the market did not"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reassure her that such reactions are normal, listen to her account, and meet her practical needs"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Reassure her that such reactions are normal, listen to her account, and meet her practical needs". Tearfulness, disturbed sleep and repeated retelling are <strong>normal acute stress reactions</strong> in the days after a traumatic event, not symptoms of mental illness. Psychological first aid consists of listening without pressing for detail, normalising the reaction, and meeting practical needs such as shelter, food and contacting family. Most people recover without formal intervention.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Immediate psychiatric referral pathologises a normal reaction. Referral is reserved for those with severe or persistent symptoms, or risk of self-harm.
• B — Advising avoidance is unhelpful and can impede natural processing. Talking at her own pace is part of recovery.
• C — Comparing her to those who died induces guilt and dismisses her distress, closing the conversation.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — mental health in emergencies; WHO psychological first aid$q$,
  strategy = $q$Psychological first aid is practical, not therapeutic: look, listen, link. Normalise the reaction, meet basic needs, connect people with family and services. Reserve referral for persistent, severe or high-risk presentations.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-EMG-0076$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Haloperidol 5 mg intramuscularly is given to a young man for acute agitation in the emergency department. For which **two adverse effects** should the nurse monitor him over the following hours?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hearing loss with an unsteady, staggering gait that develops gradually over the next few hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute dystonic reactions such as neck twisting, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome with fever"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nystagmus and an inability to look upward, which persist for several weeks afterwards"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Loss of consciousness with increasing confusion and a fluctuating level of alertness"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Acute dystonic reactions such as neck twisting, and neuroleptic malignant syndrome with fever". Haloperidol is a potent dopamine blocker. <strong>Acute dystonia</strong> — torticollis, oculogyric crisis, laryngeal spasm — can appear within hours, especially in young men, and is treated with an anticholinergic such as benzhexol or IM procyclidine. <strong>Neuroleptic malignant syndrome</strong> (hyperthermia, muscle rigidity, autonomic instability, confusion) is rare but life-threatening. QT prolongation is a further reason to monitor the pulse.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Hearing loss and ataxia are not effects of haloperidol.
• C — Transient upward gaze occurs in oculogyric crisis, but persistent gaze palsy with nystagmus is not a haloperidol effect.
• D — Haloperidol is relatively non-sedating; coma and worsening confusion are not expected effects.

Reference: Ghana STG — acute agitation and rapid tranquillisation; antipsychotic adverse effects$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: for a high-potency antipsychotic, think movement (dystonia, EPS) and temperature (NMS) — both clauses must be true for the option to stand.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0085$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse assesses orientation in an 82-year-old woman admitted last night. Which principle should guide the nurse's interpretation of the findings?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Older people cannot learn new information while they are in hospital, because the environment is unfamiliar"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A decline in mental status is a normal and expected part of ageing, especially after the age of eighty"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Short-term memory is more efficient than long-term memory in older adults, so recent events are recalled better"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The unfamiliar ward can itself cause confusion, so disorientation on admission does not mean dementia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The unfamiliar ward can itself cause confusion, so disorientation on admission does not mean dementia". Admission removes an older person's familiar cues, routine and sleep, and often adds illness, pain, dehydration and new drugs — all triggers of <strong>delirium</strong>, an acute, fluctuating and reversible confusion. Baseline function from family, repeated assessment, reorientation and treating causes distinguish it from dementia.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The reverse is true: short-term memory declines before long-term memory.
• B — Older adults can learn in hospital; they may need more time, repetition and written reminders.
• C — Normal ageing slows processing slightly but does not cause confusion or decline in mental status.

Reference: Ghana STG — delirium in older adults; WHO integrated care for older people$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: new confusion in an older patient is delirium until proven otherwise — look for the reversible cause rather than labelling it dementia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0090$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse plans the day for a woman admitted with myasthenia gravis. Which consideration is **most important**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Plan bathing, physiotherapy and meals for early in the day or after rest, because weakness worsens with use"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrict her fluids to about a litre a day, so that her electrolyte balance is not disturbed by the excess"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Avoid anything that could raise her intracranial pressure, such as coughing or straining at stool"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Protect her from falls caused by vertigo, keeping the bed low and the call bell within easy reach at all times"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Plan bathing, physiotherapy and meals for early in the day or after rest, because weakness worsens with use". In myasthenia gravis, antibodies block acetylcholine receptors, so <strong>muscle strength fades with repeated use and recovers with rest</strong>. Patients are strongest in the morning and after medication. Planning energy-demanding care around those times, and watching swallowing and breathing late in the day, prevents exhaustion and aspiration.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Intracranial pressure is unrelated to this neuromuscular-junction disorder.
• C — Vertigo is not a feature of myasthenia; weakness, not dizziness, causes problems.
• D — Fluid and electrolyte balance is not characteristically disturbed.

Reference: Ghana STG — myasthenia gravis$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: know the pattern of the disease — fatigable weakness — and plan care to the time of greatest strength.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0095$q$;

