BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with advanced lung cancer and a documented 'do not attempt resuscitation' decision is on a continuous morphine infusion. The nurse finds him unresponsive with a respiratory rate of 6 and blood pressure 84/50 mmHg. Naloxone 0.4 mg IV is prescribed. Which point is **most important** for the nurse's plan?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Naloxone will also reverse any benzodiazepine or barbiturate that is contributing to his drowsiness here"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Naloxone must not be given at all, because he has a documented decision that resuscitation is not to be attempted"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Naloxone is shorter acting than morphine, so the dose may need repeating and he must be watched closely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "His blood pressure and respirations must recover fully before any second dose of naloxone is given"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Naloxone is shorter acting than morphine, so the dose may need repeating and he must be watched closely". Naloxone's effect lasts <strong>30–90 minutes</strong>, far shorter than that of an infused opioid, so respiratory depression can <strong>return</strong>; repeated small doses or an infusion may be required. In palliative care small titrated doses are preferred so that pain control is not abruptly lost.

Why the other options fail:
• A — If the first dose has not reversed the depression, a second dose is given promptly — waiting for recovery would be illogical.
• B — Naloxone is a pure opioid antagonist; it has no effect on benzodiazepines or barbiturates.
• D — A DNACPR decision applies to cardiopulmonary resuscitation if the heart stops; it does not prevent treating a reversible drug effect.

Reference: Ghana STG — opioid toxicity; palliative care guidance on opioid reversal$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: antidote questions turn on duration — when the antidote wears off before the poison, plan to repeat and keep watching.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0131$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man returns from theatre after a total laryngectomy with a permanent tracheostomy. In planning his nutrition for the first few days, which understanding should guide the nurse?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "He can never again take food or fluids by mouth now that he has a permanent tracheostomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "All feeding is withheld for about three weeks to eliminate any risk of aspirating food"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Feeds go through a nasogastric tube at first so that the pharyngeal suture line is not disturbed while it heals"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Feeding must wait until he has been weaned from the ventilator after the operation, which usually takes several days"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Feeds go through a nasogastric tube at first so that the pharyngeal suture line is not disturbed while it heals". After laryngectomy the pharynx is closed with sutures that must heal before swallowing is attempted. <strong>Nasogastric (or gastrostomy) feeding</strong> starts once bowel sounds return and continues for about 7–10 days; oral intake resumes after the surgeon confirms the suture line is intact. Because the airway and oesophagus are now completely separate, he can eat normally later with no aspiration risk.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Three weeks without nutrition would impair healing; enteral feeding starts within a day or two.
• B — A laryngectomy separates the airway from the food passage permanently, so eating by mouth is safe once healing is complete.
• D — A laryngectomy patient breathes spontaneously through the stoma; ventilation is not routinely required.

Reference: Ghana STG — head and neck cancer surgery; ENT nursing care after laryngectomy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: ask what structure needs protecting (the pharyngeal suture line) and choose the feeding route that bypasses it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0213$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse uses the LEARN communication model (Listen, Explain, Acknowledge, Recommend, Negotiate) when counselling a client with hypertension who wants to replace all prescribed antihypertensives with garlic water and prayer. Which nursing action exemplifies the 'Negotiate' step of the LEARN framework?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain the risks of uncontrolled hypertension, such as stroke and kidney damage, and insist that the tablets be taken as prescribed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collaborate on an agreed plan that keeps prayer and culinary garlic while maintaining daily antihypertensives and home checks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Listen carefully to the client's concerns about the tablets, then recommend the standard regimen without discussing herbal alternatives"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acknowledge the client's cultural beliefs, then agree to withhold antihypertensives for a month while the garlic water gets a fair trial"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Collaborate on an agreed plan that keeps prayer and culinary garlic while maintaining daily antihypertensives and home checks". LEARN is a five-step cross-cultural consultation: Listen to the client's own account of the illness and its meaning, Explain your clinical view in plain words, Acknowledge where the two accounts differ and what is at stake, Recommend a plan you can defend clinically, and then Negotiate. Negotiation is the step where the nurse and client build a plan together that is acceptable to the client and safe: prayer and garlic as culinary seasoning can stay, because neither harms the client and refusing them would end the conversation, while the antihypertensives and the home blood pressure record remain the non-negotiable core of the plan. Why the other options fail: explaining the risks and insisting on the tablets is the Explain and Recommend steps delivered as an instruction, with no negotiation in it (A); listening and then recommending without discussing the alternatives is also Recommend, and it wastes the listening (C); and agreeing to stop the tablets for a month gives away the part of the plan that cannot be given away, which is a failure to negotiate rather than a success at it (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$LEARN model: Negotiate a culturally congruent plan that honors the client's values while preserving essential evidence-based medical treatments.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 39 weeks has active genital herpes lesions and her membranes have just ruptured. Why is caesarean birth recommended?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Caesarean birth cures the mother's herpes infection and stops the lesions from ever coming back again"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The active lesions make a vaginal delivery physically impossible to perform safely in any circumstances"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Passing through an infected birth canal exposes the newborn to herpes, which can cause severe infection"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Herpes infection causes the uterus to stop contracting in labour, so delivery cannot progress without surgery"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Passing through an infected birth canal exposes the newborn to herpes, which can cause severe infection". Neonatal herpes is acquired mainly during passage through an infected birth canal, and the risk is highest when the mother has active lesions and the membranes have been ruptured for some time, which is exactly this woman's situation. Disseminated neonatal herpes and herpes meningoencephalitis carry high mortality and leave survivors with neurological damage, so a caesarean birth performed before or soon after membrane rupture substantially reduces the exposure dose the baby receives. Why the other options fail: surgery does not treat the mother's infection, which still needs antiviral treatment, and it does not prevent recurrence (A); the lesions do not make vaginal birth mechanically impossible, and the recommendation rests on the risk of transmission, not on feasibility (B); and herpes does not arrest uterine contraction or stop labour from progressing, so D invents a mechanism that does not exist (D). Reference: WHO and Ghana STG — prevention of mother-to-child transmission of herpes simplex.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Active herpes at term: the risk is to the baby in the birth canal, not to the uterus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCH2-0003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse sets up a continuous nasogastric feed using an open system (formula decanted into a feeding container). Which practice **correctly** reduces the risk of bacterial contamination?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hang 12 hours' worth of formula to reduce handling, and change the giving set every 72 hours to save time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Add fresh formula to the container on top of whatever remains from the previous feed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rinse the giving set with tap water after each feed and reuse the same set for 48 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hang no more than 4 hours of formula at a time and change the giving set every 24 hours, labelled"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Hang no more than 4 hours of formula at a time and change the giving set every 24 hours, labelled". Decanted formula is an excellent culture medium at room temperature. For <strong>open systems the hang time is limited to about 4 hours</strong>, the container is emptied and rinsed before each refill, and the <strong>administration set is changed every 24 hours</strong>. Closed ready-to-hang systems can run for 24–48 hours. Hand hygiene and clean technique when decanting are essential.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Reused, tap-water-rinsed sets are a recognised source of feed contamination and gastroenteritis.
• B — 'Topping up' mixes old, possibly contaminated formula with fresh.
• C — Twelve hours of hang time gives bacteria time to multiply to dangerous levels; set changes every 72 hours are too infrequent for an open system.

Reference: ESPEN/ASPEN enteral nutrition safety guidance; Ghana nursing procedure manual — enteral feeding$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: for open enteral systems remember '4 and 24' — four hours of feed, twenty-four hours per set.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0180$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 4-year-old girl is admitted with suspected bacterial meningitis. Which nursing action should be carried out **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Place her in a single room with droplet precautions and prepare for the lumbar puncture and drugs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Encourage her parents to bring in her favourite foods from home while she settles into the ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Begin teaching the parents about the hearing tests she will need after recovery, while she is settled"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Place her in a brightly lit room so that the staff can observe her easily through the day and night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Place her in a single room with droplet precautions and prepare for the lumbar puncture and drugs". Bacterial meningitis is a medical emergency and, for meningococcal disease, a <strong>transmissible one</strong>: <strong>droplet precautions</strong> protect other children and staff for the first 24 hours of therapy, and the <strong>sooner antibiotics follow the lumbar puncture (or precede it if there is delay), the better the outcome</strong>. Seizure precautions, neurological observations and a dim quiet room reduce complications.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Nutrition is a later concern; the child may be nil by mouth initially because of vomiting and reduced consciousness.
• C — Audiology follow-up is important but belongs to discharge planning.
• D — Children with meningitis are photophobic; bright light increases distress.

Reference: Ghana STG — meningitis; Ghana Health Service IPC policy: droplet precautions$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: emergency plus transmissible means isolate and treat in the same breath — everything else waits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0187$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A primigravida at 41 weeks gestation is brought to the district hospital maternity unit after 28 hours of labor at the home of an untrained Traditional Birth Attendant (TBA). The TBA administered multiple doses of an indigenous herbal uterotonic infusion (*kaligbali* / *aworoworo*) to 'force the baby out.' The nurse notes tetanic uterine contractions without relaxation, fetal heart rate decelerations to 60 beats/min, and severe maternal vaginal bleeding. Which catastrophe is the nurse actively witnessing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uterine hyperstimulation and impending rupture from unmonitored herbal oxytocics, needing tocolysis, oxygen and emergency caesarean"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal active first stage of labour; encourage walking and oral fluids while waiting for the next vaginal examination and partograph"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Obstructed labour from cephalopelvic disproportion; prepare for an assisted vaginal delivery in theatre with neonatal resuscitation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Uterine atony with postpartum haemorrhage; start an oxytocin infusion, massage the fundus and prepare for blood transfusion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Uterine hyperstimulation and impending rupture from unmonitored herbal oxytocics, needing tocolysis, oxygen and emergency caesarean". Indigenous herbal uterotonics such as kaligbali and aworoworo contain oxytocic alkaloids of unknown dose, and this woman has had repeated doses over twenty-eight hours with no fetal monitoring. The result is uncoordinated, tetanic uterine contraction with no relaxation between contractions: placental perfusion falls, the fetal heart rate decelerates, and the uterus can rupture, which is catastrophic and can kill the mother through exsanguination. The nurse's immediate job is to call the obstetrician, position her on the left side, give oxygen, start large-bore intravenous fluids, give a tocolytic if the protocol directs, and move her to theatre for caesarean delivery. Why the other options fail: calling a tetanic, non-relaxing uterus a normal first stage is the most dangerous error available here, and partograph plotting cannot make an over-contracted uterus normal (B); cephalopelvic disproportion does not produce tetanic contractions without relaxation, and an assisted vaginal birth in the presence of fetal decelerations and a contracting uterus would be unsafe (C); and atony produces a soft, boggy uterus after delivery, whereas this uterus is contracting continuously before delivery (D). Reference: Ghana STG — obstructed and prolonged labour; management of uterine hyperstimulation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Herbal uterotonics: Indigenous oxytocic herbs administered during labor cause uterine hyperstimulation, fetal asphyxia, and uterine rupture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 24-year-old woman is admitted to a psychiatric unit with major depression after a serious overdose. Her care plan includes checks every 15 minutes. The nurse should also make sure that she is:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Allowed to keep her own medicines in her locker, because taking them herself gives back a sense of control"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Left alone whenever she asks for it, because privacy and autonomy matter most during a depressive illness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Assessed for suicidal thoughts at every shift and whenever her mood changes, with means of self-harm removed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Given a private room away from the nurses' station, because the ward noise is stopping her from resting properly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Assessed for suicidal thoughts at every shift and whenever her mood changes, with means of self-harm removed". A recent serious attempt is the strongest predictor of another. Observation levels are only part of safety: <strong>repeated direct assessment of suicidal ideation, plan and intent</strong> — particularly as mood starts to lift and energy returns — and <strong>removal of belts, cords, sharps and hoarded medicines</strong> are essential.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Her stated wish for solitude must be balanced against risk; she is observed within sight or at the agreed interval.
• B — A room far from staff reduces observation; high-risk patients are placed close to the nurses' station.
• D — Patients at risk never hold their own medication.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Authority — suicide prevention and observation policy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: after an attempt, assume continuing risk — assess directly, repeatedly, and remove means.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0183$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man who tested positive for Helicobacter pylori is prescribed triple therapy — omeprazole, amoxicillin and clarithromycin — for 14 days. Which statement by the man shows he has understood the teaching?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I can stop the antibiotics after a week, because the pain will have gone by then and the germ with it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "I will take the tablets on the days when the pain is bad, and stop them once it has settled down again"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I must finish all the tablets even when the pain goes, and come back for a test to be sure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I will take them with a glass of palm wine in the evening, because the alcohol kills the germ as well"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "I must finish all the tablets even when the pain goes, and come back for a test to be sure". <strong>Eradication fails if the course is incomplete</strong>, and treatment failure breeds antibiotic resistance and ulcer recurrence. The patient completes the full course, takes the proton-pump inhibitor before meals, avoids alcohol and NSAIDs, and attends for a <strong>test of cure (urea breath test or stool antigen) at least four weeks after finishing antibiotics</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Intermittent dosing guarantees failure and resistance.
• B — Symptom relief comes early; stopping antibiotics then leaves the organism alive.
• D — Alcohol irritates the gastric mucosa and interacts with the medicines.

Reference: Ghana STG — peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori eradication$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: for eradication therapy the teaching points are 'all of it, every day, and come back to prove it worked'.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0237$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In Ghana, which statutory regulatory bodies are legally mandated to regulate traditional and herbal medicine practitioners, accredit herbal clinics, and register commercial botanical remedies?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The Medical and Dental Council with the Pharmacy Council, which license the individual practitioners and the herbal products they sell in their clinics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The Traditional Medicine Practice Council under Act 575 for practitioners and premises, and the Food and Drugs Authority for product registration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The Ghana Health Service with the Ministry of Chieftaincy and Religious Affairs, which jointly oversee all traditional healing practice and premises"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The Food and Drugs Authority acting alone, which registers herbal products and also licenses traditional practitioners and their clinics"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The Traditional Medicine Practice Council under Act 575 for practitioners and premises, and the Food and Drugs Authority for product registration". Two statutes split the work. The Traditional Medicine Practice Act 2000 (Act 575) created the Traditional Medicine Practice Council under the Ministry of Health, and the Council sets standards and registers, licenses and regulates traditional medical practitioners — herbalists, bonesetters and naturopaths — together with their premises and clinics. The Food and Drugs Authority, acting under the Public Health Act 2012 (Act 851), reviews the safety, efficacy, quality and labelling of manufactured herbal products and issues the registration number that a commercial remedy must carry before it can be sold. Practitioner regulation and product regulation are therefore two different authorities doing two different jobs. Why the other options fail: the Medical and Dental Council and the Pharmacy Council regulate doctors, dentists and pharmacists, not traditional practitioners (A); the Ghana Health Service delivers services and the Ministry of Chieftaincy and Religious Affairs has no statutory licensing role over healing practice (C); and the FDA registers products but does not license traditional practitioners or accredit their clinics, which is the Council's function (D). Reference: Traditional Medicine Practice Act 2000 (Act 575); Public Health Act 2012 (Act 851).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ghana TCAM regulation: TMPC regulates practitioners and clinics (Act 575); FDA Ghana evaluates and registers herbal medicines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman arrives with heavy postpartum bleeding. Her haemoglobin taken minutes after the bleeding began is 11 g/dL. How should the nurse interpret this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The bleeding is not significant, since the haemoglobin is still near the normal range and she is stable"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The haemoglobin should simply be repeated in one week to see whether it has fallen in the meantime"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transfusion will definitely not be required at any point during this admission, whatever the repeat shows"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The result may not yet reflect the loss, because haemoglobin falls only after fluid shifts occur"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The result may not yet reflect the loss, because haemoglobin falls only after fluid shifts occur". In <strong>acute haemorrhage the patient loses whole blood</strong>, so the concentration of haemoglobin in the remaining blood is initially unchanged. It falls only over hours as fluid moves from the interstitium into the vascular space, or after intravenous fluid is given. Management is guided by the clinical picture — pulse, blood pressure, conscious level, visible loss — not by an early haemoglobin.

Why the other options fail:
• A — This is the dangerous misreading. A normal early haemoglobin gives false reassurance during active haemorrhage.
• B — A week is far too long. In active bleeding the measurement is repeated within hours alongside continuous clinical assessment.
• C — Transfusion may become urgently necessary. The decision rests on clinical status and ongoing loss, not a single early value.

Reference: Ghana Safe Motherhood Protocol — postpartum haemorrhage$q$,
  strategy = $q$Match the test to the timing. Some results lag behind the event: haemoglobin in acute bleeding, creatinine in early kidney injury, cardiac markers in early infarction. Treat the patient in front of you, not the number.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-LAB-0143$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 17-year-old student has lost considerable weight, exercises for hours daily, wears heavy clothing in warm weather and insists she is overweight despite a body mass index of 15 kg/m². Which response is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reassure her that many young women diet and simply advise her to eat a little more at each meal and snack"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Recognise features suggesting an eating disorder and arrange prompt referral for specialist assessment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell her firmly that she is dangerously thin and must start gaining weight immediately before she collapses"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Weigh her weekly in the clinic and take no further action for as long as the weight stays stable at this level"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Recognise features suggesting an eating disorder and arrange prompt referral for specialist assessment". Marked weight loss with a distorted body image, compulsive exercise and concealment of the body are core features of anorexia nervosa, and a body mass index of 15 kg/m² carries substantial medical risk including arrhythmia, hypoglycaemia, bone loss and death. Eating disorders have the highest mortality of any psychiatric condition, so this is not a phase to be waited out. The nursing role is to recognise the pattern, respond without judgement or comment on appearance, screen for physical instability, and refer promptly to a service able to provide combined medical and psychological care. Refeeding must be medically supervised because of the risk of refeeding syndrome.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Normalising this as ordinary dieting misses a serious and potentially fatal illness, and advice to eat more addresses neither the distorted body image nor the compulsion that drives the restriction.
• C — Confronting her about her appearance and demanding weight gain typically increases shame and concealment. Eating disorders are not corrected by instruction, and the approach damages the trust needed for engagement.
• D — Monitoring without referral allows a high-risk condition to progress. Stable weight at a body mass index of 15 kg/m² is not reassurance; it is sustained severe illness.

Reference: WHO mental health guidance; Ghana Mental Health Act 846 and Standard Treatment Guidelines$q$,
  strategy = $q$Weight loss plus a distorted body image plus concealment is a psychiatric emergency, not a diet. Refer rather than advise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3108$q$;

