UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered nurse supervises a student changing the dressing on a newly inserted peritoneal dialysis catheter. The nurse should **intervene** when the student:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Cleans the exit site with a circular motion that starts at the outer skin and moves in towards the catheter"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Secures the edges of the new dressing with hypoallergenic tape so that it does not lift away from the skin at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cleans the exit site with sterile gauze soaked in povidone-iodine before applying the new sterile dressing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Covers the exit site with sterile pre-cut gauze squares after cleaning and drying the skin around it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Cleans the exit site with a circular motion that starts at the outer skin and moves in towards the catheter". Cleaning must move <strong>from the exit site outward</strong>, in widening circles, so that skin flora is carried away from the catheter and not towards it. Exit-site infection can track along the catheter and cause <strong>peritonitis</strong>, the leading complication of peritoneal dialysis, so every step of the dressing is aseptic.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Sterile pre-cut gauze is the correct dressing for a drain or catheter site.
• C — Hypoallergenic tape secures the dressing without damaging the skin.
• D — Povidone-iodine on sterile gauze is an acceptable antiseptic for the exit site.

Reference: Ghana STG — peritoneal dialysis care; International Society for Peritoneal Dialysis catheter-care recommendations$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: 'intervene' means find the wrong technique — in aseptic cleaning, direction matters: clean to dirty, inside to outside.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0136$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is preparing to give an intramuscular injection. Which action **best** prevents a needle-stick injury?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Activate the safety device or drop the uncapped needle straight into a sharps container at the point of use"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Recap the needle carefully using both hands, then carry it to the sharps bin at the end of the drug round"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bend the needle back on itself after the injection so that it cannot be picked up and reused by anyone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the used syringe lying on the dressing tray and dispose of all of them when the medication round is over"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Activate the safety device or drop the uncapped needle straight into a sharps container at the point of use". Most needle-stick injuries occur <strong>during recapping or after use before disposal</strong>. The safe sequence is to <strong>never recap</strong>, use the device's safety shield if present, and <strong>dispose of the sharp immediately into a puncture-proof container kept within reach</strong>, filling it no more than three-quarters.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Bending or breaking needles causes splash and injury.
• C — Two-handed recapping is the classic cause of needle-stick injury; if recapping is unavoidable, a one-handed scoop technique is used.
• D — Carrying used sharps around the ward exposes the nurse and others.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — infection prevention and control policy: sharps management; WHO injection safety$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: the sharp is most dangerous between use and the bin — shorten that interval to nothing and never recap.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0249$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother asks a nurse at a health centre for antibiotics for her child, who has had a runny nose and mild cough for two days without fever. What is the **most appropriate** response?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain that the illness is likely viral, antibiotics will not help, and name the danger signs to return for"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Provide a short course of antibiotics to reassure the mother, since her child has now been unwell for two days"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell her that antibiotics are currently unavailable at this facility and that she should come back next week"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advise her to buy a full course of antibiotics from the chemical seller nearby if the symptoms carry on"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Explain that the illness is likely viral, antibiotics will not help, and name the danger signs to return for". A brief coryzal illness without fever is almost always <strong>viral</strong>, and antibiotics offer no benefit while contributing to resistance, side effects and cost. Effective practice combines a clear explanation with <strong>safety-netting</strong> — telling the mother precisely which danger signs mean she must return, which is what makes it safe to withhold treatment.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Prescribing to satisfy an expectation drives antimicrobial resistance, exposes the child to unnecessary adverse effects, and reinforces the belief that every illness needs antibiotics.
• C — A false statement about availability may be expedient but is dishonest, and it leaves the mother believing antibiotics were needed but unobtainable.
• D — Directing a mother to unregulated purchase encourages self-medication with incomplete courses, one of the strongest drivers of resistance.

Reference: Ghana STG — rational use of antimicrobials; IMCI$q$,
  strategy = $q$When declining a treatment a patient has requested, the complete answer always has two parts: the explanation and the safety net. An option that only refuses is incomplete; one that gives in to the request is unsafe.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-IPC-0055$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In which situation should the midwife expect anti-D immunoglobulin to be given within 72 hours of delivery?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The mother is Rh-positive and the baby is Rh-negative, so the baby's cells could sensitise the mother"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The mother is Rh-negative and unsensitised, the baby is Rh-positive and the direct Coombs test is negative"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The mother is Rh-negative and already has anti-D antibodies from an earlier pregnancy with an Rh-positive baby"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Both the mother and the baby are Rh-negative, while the father of the baby is known to be Rh-positive himself"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The mother is Rh-negative and unsensitised, the baby is Rh-positive and the direct Coombs test is negative". Anti-D immunoglobulin <strong>destroys Rh-positive fetal cells that enter the mother's circulation</strong> before her immune system can make its own antibodies, protecting the next pregnancy. It is given to an <strong>unsensitised Rh-negative mother after an Rh-positive birth</strong>, and also after miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, amniocentesis and antepartum bleeding.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Once a woman has formed her own antibodies, anti-D no longer helps; that pregnancy is managed by fetal surveillance.
• C — If the baby is Rh-negative there are no Rh-positive cells to react against; no anti-D is needed.
• D — An Rh-positive mother cannot become sensitised against the Rh antigen.

Reference: Ghana Safe Motherhood Protocol — Rhesus isoimmunisation prophylaxis$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: three conditions must all be true — mother Rh-negative, baby Rh-positive, mother not yet sensitised.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0217$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In Ghana, several public regional and district hospitals operate integrated herbal medicine pilot units staffed by Medical Herbalists trained at Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and Technology (KNUST). What is the primary role of the Centre for Plant Medicine Research (CPMR) at Mampong-Akuapem in supporting this integration?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Researching plant medicines, assessing botanical safety, toxicology and efficacy, and standardising herbal formulations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Licensing traditional medical practitioners, accrediting their premises and disciplining those who practise without registration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Supplying free herbal alternatives to public hospitals and clinics whenever the conventional medicines run out of stock"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Setting the retail price of imported herbal products and approving the advertising claims that the manufacturers make"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Researching plant medicines, assessing botanical safety, toxicology and efficacy, and standardising herbal formulations". The Centre for Plant Medicine Research at Mampong-Akuapem was established in 1975 and is Ghana's national institute for the scientific study of medicinal plants. Its work is research: botanical and phytochemical analysis, microbiology, pharmacology and toxicology on the plants that traditional healers actually use, the development of standardised and efficacy-tested products such as Cryptolepis for malaria, and technical advice to the Ministry of Health and the Food and Drugs Authority on how herbal medicine can be integrated into public care safely. That is why the pilot units in regional and district hospitals can be staffed by KNUST-trained medical herbalists and still operate to a standard the hospital can defend. Why the other options fail: licensing practitioners, accrediting premises and disciplining the unregistered is the statutory work of the Traditional Medicine Practice Council under Act 575, not of a research institute (B); supplying medicines to hospitals is a procurement and supply-chain function of the Ghana Health Service, not research (C); and setting retail prices and approving advertising claims belongs to the FDA and the Ministry of Trade, not to CPMR (D). Reference: Centre for Plant Medicine Research, Mampong-Akuapem; Traditional Medicine Practice Act 2000 (Act 575).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ghanaian plant research: The Centre for Plant Medicine Research (CPMR) at Mampong conducts scientific research, safety profiling, and standardization of botanical medicines.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man with advanced chronic kidney disease has a serum potassium of 6.4 mmol/L. Which dietary advice is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase his fruit intake, since fruit is natural and healthy and supports the work of the kidneys"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Limit high-potassium foods such as ripe plantain, avocado and coconut water, and boil vegetables in plenty of water"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict all protein completely until the serum potassium has come back down into the normal range"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage coconut water, which is a natural fluid that replaces the electrolytes lost through the kidneys every day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Limit high-potassium foods such as ripe plantain, avocado and coconut water, and boil vegetables in plenty of water". Failing kidneys cannot excrete a potassium load, and at 6.4 mmol/L this man is at risk of fatal arrhythmia. Dietary control means identifying the high-potassium foods actually eaten in his household, which in Ghana commonly means ripe plantain, avocado, citrus, tomato paste, coconut water, palm nut soup and yam. A practical technique is to cut vegetables small and boil them in a generous volume of water that is then discarded, since potassium leaches out. Advice must be specific to local foods to be usable, and hyperkalaemia at this level also requires urgent medical review with an electrocardiogram rather than dietary measures alone.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Fruit is a major potassium source, so a general instruction to eat more of it is dangerous here. Healthy in general does not mean safe for this patient.
• C — Protein is restricted in a measured way in advanced kidney disease, not eliminated. Complete restriction causes malnutrition and muscle breakdown, which itself releases potassium into the blood.
• D — Coconut water is exceptionally high in potassium. Recommending it to a hyperkalaemic patient could precipitate a cardiac arrest, and it is a common and dangerous misconception that it is universally healthy.

Reference: Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines — chronic kidney disease; WHO dietary guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Translate every dietary restriction into the specific foods on that patient's table. A list written for another country protects nobody.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3090$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A teenager drinks three bottles of soft drink daily and asks why this matters when he is not overweight. What is the **most accurate** reply?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It does not matter at all, since his weight is normal and body weight is the only measure that counts for health"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The main concern is that the caffeine in the drinks will interfere with his sleep and concentration"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Liquid sugar adds energy without causing fullness and raises the risk of tooth decay and type 2 diabetes anyway"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Soft drinks are harmful only because they replace water, so drinking water as well cancels the effect"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Liquid sugar adds energy without causing fullness and raises the risk of tooth decay and type 2 diabetes anyway". Energy taken as liquid is poorly compensated: the body does not reduce later food intake the way it does after solid food of the same energy, so total intake creeps up unnoticed. Beyond weight, frequent sugar exposure bathes the teeth in substrate for acid-producing bacteria, and dental caries is among the commonest reasons young people lose teeth. Repeated rapid glucose loads also drive insulin resistance over time, and a person of normal weight can still be metabolically affected, particularly where visceral fat accumulates. Framing the harm around weight alone gives a false reassurance to exactly the person in this scenario.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Body weight is one outcome among several. Dental caries and metabolic change occur independently of weight, so a normal weight does not exclude harm.
• B — Caffeine content varies and is not the main concern with three bottles daily. The sugar load is far more consequential than the stimulant effect.
• D — Displacement of water and milk is a genuine problem, but adding water alongside does not remove the sugar already consumed. The intake itself must fall.

Reference: WHO guideline on sugars intake; Ghana Health Service non-communicable disease programme$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ask what the exposure does beyond the obvious outcome. Sugar harms teeth and metabolism directly, not only through body weight.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3103$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man has an above-knee amputation with a rigid plaster dressing applied in theatre so that a temporary prosthesis can be fitted early. Which nursing action is **most important** in the first 24 hours?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Observe the rigid dressing for slipping, blood staining through the plaster and signs of pressure, and keep the stump aligned"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Measure the drainage from the Penrose drains every hour and record it carefully on the fluid balance chart"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep the stump elevated on pillows for at least 48 hours, to reduce the swelling before the prosthesis is fitted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Begin stump bandaging in a figure-of-eight pattern as soon as the plaster is dry, to shape the end of the stump for the prosthesis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Observe the rigid dressing for slipping, blood staining through the plaster and signs of pressure, and keep the stump aligned". An immediate post-operative rigid dressing <strong>controls oedema, protects the wound, prevents flexion contracture and shapes the stump</strong>. If it slips off or is soaked with blood the benefits are lost and haemorrhage may be hidden, so the nurse checks it frequently, marks any staining, and ensures the straps and suspension stay secure.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Elevation on pillows promotes hip flexion contracture; the rigid dressing already controls swelling.
• C — Drains are not normally used with a rigid dressing; the cast itself provides compression.
• D — Elastic bandaging is used when the stump is dressed softly, not when a cast is in place.

Reference: Ghana STG — amputation and rehabilitation; prosthetic and orthotic service guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: when a device replaces conventional dressings, nursing care centres on the device — check it, protect it, keep it in place.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0177$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newly qualified nurse awaiting her PIN is asked by a ward sister to independently administer intravenous medication because the ward is short-staffed. What is the nurse's **most appropriate** response?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the medication, since the ward sister has given the instruction and takes responsibility for it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explain that she cannot practise independently until her registration is complete, and offer to assist under supervision"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Refuse outright and leave the ward straight away to report the ward sister to the Nursing and Midwifery Council that day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer the medication but ask a registered colleague to document it under that colleague's name instead"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Explain that she cannot practise independently until her registration is complete, and offer to assist under supervision". Under the NMC Ghana framework a nurse may not practise independently until registered and issued a PIN. The professional response both <strong>protects the patient</strong> and <strong>preserves the working relationship</strong>: state the limit clearly and offer what you <em>can</em> lawfully do — assist under the supervision of a registered practitioner.

Why the other options fail:
• A — An instruction from a senior does not extend your legal scope. Accountability for practising unregistered remains with the individual nurse.
• C — Refusal is correct in principle, but abandoning the ward compromises patient safety and escalating externally before raising it internally is disproportionate.
• D — Falsifying documentation is fraud and a serious professional offence, compounding the original breach rather than resolving it.

Reference: NMC Ghana Code of Conduct and Ethics; Health Professions Regulatory Bodies Act 857 (2013)$q$,
  strategy = $q$In scope-of-practice questions, eliminate any option that involves doing the unlawful act, abandoning the patient, or falsifying records. The survivor is almost always: state the boundary, then offer the lawful alternative.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ETH-0006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse realises she has recorded a set of observations in the wrong patient's chart. What should she do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Use correction fluid to cover the entry completely and then rewrite it neatly in the correct patient's chart instead"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Remove the page from the chart and rewrite the whole record so that the notes remain neat and consistent"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the entry where it is and add an explanatory note at the end of the shift when the ward is quieter"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Draw a single line through the entry, write “written in error”, sign and date it, then record in the correct chart"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Draw a single line through the entry, write “written in error”, sign and date it, then record in the correct chart". A health record is a <strong>legal document</strong>. An error is corrected by striking through with a single line so the original remains readable, annotating it as an error, and signing and dating the correction. This preserves the audit trail and shows the correction was made openly rather than concealed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Correction fluid obliterates the original entry. In any investigation, an obscured record is treated as concealment even when the intent was innocent.
• B — Removing a page destroys part of a legal record. Tidiness never justifies altering documentation.
• C — Delaying leaves incorrect information in another patient's chart, where a colleague may act on it in the meantime.

Reference: NMC Ghana Code of Conduct and Ethics — record keeping$q$,
  strategy = $q$For documentation questions, the correct answer always keeps the original visible, identifies who changed it and when, and happens immediately. Anything that hides, removes or delays is wrong.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ETH-0021$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$After running a community session on complementary feeding, a nurse wants to know whether it worked. Which evaluation gives the **most meaningful** information?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Counting how many mothers attended the session on the day, and comparing it with the number invited"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Asking at the end of the session whether everyone found the teaching useful and understood the messages"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Following up a sample of mothers weeks later to see what feeding practices have actually changed at home"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recording how many handouts and leaflets were distributed to the mothers who came to the session that day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Following up a sample of mothers weeks later to see what feeding practices have actually changed at home". Health education aims to change behaviour, so evaluation must eventually measure behaviour. Attendance, satisfaction and materials distributed are process measures: they describe what was delivered, not what changed. A mother can attend, enjoy the session and still feed exactly as before, most often because of cost, time, family pressure or a grandmother's advice rather than lack of knowledge. Following up a sample of households weeks later reveals what actually changed and, just as valuably, what obstacles prevented change, which is the information needed to design the next session. It costs more effort, which is precisely why it is so often skipped.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Attendance measures reach, not effect. A well-attended session that changes nothing has still failed, and the number alone cannot distinguish the two.
• B — End-of-session satisfaction is influenced by politeness and rapport, and it is collected before anyone has had a chance to try the advice at home. It correlates poorly with actual change.
• D — Counting handouts records distribution of paper. It says nothing about whether the material was read, understood or acted upon, and literacy may limit its use entirely.

Reference: WHO health promotion evaluation guidance; Ghana Health Service community health programme$q$,
  strategy = $q$Distinguish what was delivered from what changed. Only follow-up in the real setting measures whether teaching altered behaviour.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3119$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A detained patient assessed as at high risk of suicide cannot be found on the ward. What is the **immediate** priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Initiate the absconding procedure at once, search the ward and grounds and notify the team, police and family"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait for two hours in case the patient comes back of their own accord before raising the alarm with anyone at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complete the incident form fully and accurately before beginning any search of the ward or of the hospital grounds"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discharge the patient from the ward list as having left of their own accord, and inform the next of kin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Initiate the absconding procedure at once, search the ward and grounds and notify the team, police and family". A detained patient at high risk of suicide who is missing is a potential emergency in which time is the critical variable. The absconding procedure is activated immediately: search the ward and grounds, inform the nurse in charge and the responsible clinician, and notify police and the identified next of kin according to the agreed plan and risk level. Detention exists precisely because the person cannot at present keep themselves safe. Documentation matters greatly but follows the search rather than preceding it, and a detained patient cannot be discharged simply because they have left.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Waiting two hours in a high-risk detained patient could be the difference between life and death, and no policy supports a delay of that kind.
• C — Paperwork before searching inverts the priorities. The form is completed once the immediate safety response is under way.
• D — A patient subject to detention cannot be treated as having self-discharged. Removing them from the list would abandon both the legal duty and the person.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Act 846; WHO patient safety guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$When a detained high-risk patient is missing, act first and document second. The legal detention exists because waiting is unsafe.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MEN-3188$q$;

