UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn with a large ventricular septal defect has persistently low oxygen saturation despite breathing well. Why does a ventricular septal defect cause less cyanosis than a comparable right-to-left shunt?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The shunt runs left to right, so oxygenated blood is recirculated through the lungs, not bypassed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The foramen ovale closes within minutes of birth in every baby, so no blood can cross over it afterwards"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The ductus arteriosus carries oxygenated blood straight into the aorta, so the lungs are bypassed entirely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fetal haemoglobin keeps the oxygen saturation normal whatever the direction of the shunt turns out to be"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "The shunt runs left to right, so oxygenated blood is recirculated through the lungs, not bypassed". In a ventricular septal defect the pressures in the two ventricles are not equal: the left ventricle is the higher-pressure chamber, so blood crosses the defect from left to right, from the oxygenated side into the pulmonary circulation. That extra flow returns to the left heart and is pumped round again, so the lungs receive too much blood and too little of it is ever shunted past them. The baby is therefore not cyanosed; the problems are pulmonary overcirculation, breathlessness, poor feeding and failure to thrive. Cyanosis appears only at a late stage, when pulmonary vascular resistance has climbed until the shunt reverses and deoxygenated blood starts to reach the aorta — Eisenmenger physiology. Why the other options fail: the foramen ovale does close, but whether it has closed does not determine the direction of a ventricular shunt, and closure is not guaranteed within minutes in every baby (B); the ductus arteriosus carries mixed blood from the pulmonary artery to the aorta in fetal life and is not a route that delivers oxygenated blood to the body after birth (C); and fetal haemoglobin shifts the oxygen dissociation curve but cannot keep saturation normal in the face of a right-to-left shunt, which is why it is not the explanation (D). Reference: Ghana STG — congenital heart disease; paediatric cardiology.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Left-to-right shunts cause breathlessness, not cyanosis. Cyanosis means the shunt has reversed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MCH2-0001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman develops hypoparathyroidism after total thyroidectomy. Which dietary advice should the nurse give?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Restrict potassium-rich foods such as plantain, oranges and coconut water for a few weeks after the surgery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Eat plenty of phosphorus-rich foods such as fizzy drinks, organ meats and processed cheese to build the bones back up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Follow a low-sodium diet for a few weeks to protect the kidneys from any further strain after the operation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Choose calcium-rich, low-phosphorus foods such as green leafy vegetables, and take the calcium and vitamin D"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Choose calcium-rich, low-phosphorus foods such as green leafy vegetables, and take the calcium and vitamin D". Parathyroid hormone keeps calcium up and phosphate down; without it the patient becomes <strong>hypocalcaemic and hyperphosphataemic</strong>, risking tetany and laryngospasm. Management is <strong>calcium and active vitamin D supplements</strong> plus a diet <strong>high in calcium and low in phosphorus</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Sodium restriction is for hypertension, heart failure and renal disease, not for this hormone deficiency.
• B — Potassium is not the electrolyte disturbed by loss of parathyroid function.
• C — Phosphorus-rich foods push the phosphate higher and the calcium lower.

Reference: Ghana STG — hypocalcaemia and post-thyroidectomy care$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: name the hormone, state what it raises and lowers, then reverse the deficiency with diet and supplements.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An elderly man presents with sigmoid volvulus. Which cluster of findings would the nurse expect with an obstruction **low in the large bowel**, as opposed to one high in the small bowel?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Early profuse vomiting with little abdominal distension and visible peristalsis right across the whole abdomen"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Explosive watery diarrhoea with cramping pains and a scaphoid, sunken appearance of the whole abdomen"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Burning epigastric pain with black tarry stools and anaemia, from slow chronic blood loss over months"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Marked distension, absolute constipation with no flatus, and late vomiting that may be faeculent"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Marked distension, absolute constipation with no flatus, and late vomiting that may be faeculent". The further down the bowel the block, the more bowel there is to <strong>fill with gas and fluid</strong>, so distension is gross and <strong>constipation is absolute</strong>, while vomiting comes <strong>late</strong> and may be faeculent. High small-bowel obstruction does the reverse: early vomiting and little distension. Sigmoid volvulus is a common cause of large-bowel obstruction in older adults in West Africa.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Early vomiting with minimal distension is the picture of a high obstruction.
• B — Diarrhoea does not occur in complete obstruction; nothing passes below the block.
• C — Epigastric burning and melaena suggest a bleeding peptic ulcer.

Reference: Ghana STG — intestinal obstruction; surgical nursing assessment$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: high obstruction vomits early and distends little; low obstruction distends a lot and vomits late. Place the block, then pick the picture.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0130$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman at 36 weeks' gestation is admitted with a blood pressure of 170/112 mmHg, 3+ proteinuria, headache and visual disturbance. Which nursing action is the **priority**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Encourage her to walk around the ward, because activity lowers the blood pressure and reduces the risk of a clot forming in the leg"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Nurse her in a quiet, dimly lit room with suction and oxygen to hand, and prepare the magnesium and antihypertensive"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict her fluid intake to 500 mL per day, to reduce the oedema and bring the blood pressure down gradually"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Insert a nasogastric tube to protect the airway if she should have a seizure on the ward during the night"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Nurse her in a quiet, dimly lit room with suction and oxygen to hand, and prepare the magnesium and antihypertensive". Headache and visual disturbance with severe hypertension are <strong>warning signs of imminent eclampsia</strong>. Care aims to <strong>prevent seizures (magnesium sulfate), lower the blood pressure safely (labetalol, hydralazine or nifedipine) and protect her from injury</strong>: reduced stimulation, padded side rails, airway equipment ready, and preparation for delivery once stabilised.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Activity increases stimulation and does not lower blood pressure; bed rest in a calm environment is appropriate.
• C — Fluids are carefully balanced, not severely restricted, because these women have reduced intravascular volume and are at risk of renal impairment.
• D — A nasogastric tube is not routine; the airway is protected by positioning and suction if a seizure occurs.

Reference: Ghana Safe Motherhood Protocol — severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: severe pre-eclampsia with neurological symptoms means 'seizure is coming' — prevent it and prepare for it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0157$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient with obsessive-compulsive disorder must check that the ward door is locked exactly twelve times before she can go to bed. Early in her admission, which nursing approach is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lock the door yourself and tell her firmly that she no longer needs to check it before going to bed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Allow time for the ritual, avoid interrupting it, and negotiate limits gradually as her anxiety settles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Physically prevent her from checking the door, so that she learns for herself that it is already locked"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Point out how illogical the behaviour is each time she does it, so that she can see it for herself"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Allow time for the ritual, avoid interrupting it, and negotiate limits gradually as her anxiety settles". Abruptly blocking a compulsion produces <strong>overwhelming anxiety, even panic</strong>, before any therapeutic relationship exists. The nurse first <strong>accepts the ritual, builds rapport, schedules time so the ritual does not cause lateness</strong>, and then — within a structured plan such as exposure and response prevention — helps her gradually reduce it.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Reassurance does not remove the obsessive doubt; she will still need to check.
• C — She already knows the behaviour is irrational; pointing it out adds shame without reducing anxiety.
• D — Forcible prevention is traumatic and damages the therapeutic relationship.

Reference: Ghana Mental Health Authority — obsessive-compulsive disorder nursing care; exposure and response prevention principles$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in OCD, 'stop the ritual' is the end point, not the starting point — begin with acceptance and time, reduce gradually.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0212$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is conducting a community health education seminar on healthcare modalities. According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health (NCCIH), what is the definitive distinction between 'Complementary Medicine' and 'Alternative Medicine'?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Complementary medicine is used together WITH conventional biomedical care, while alternative medicine is used IN PLACE OF it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Complementary medicine is used IN PLACE OF conventional treatment, while alternative medicine is used ALONGSIDE it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complementary medicine means only mind-body therapies such as meditation, while alternative medicine means only herbal products"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The two terms are interchangeable in ordinary clinical use, and the only real distinction is whether the practitioner holds a licence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Complementary medicine is used together WITH conventional biomedical care, while alternative medicine is used IN PLACE OF it". The distinction turns on how a non-mainstream practice is used, not on what the practice is. WHO and the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health define complementary medicine as a non-mainstream practice used *together with* conventional care — acupuncture alongside analgesia, herbal tea alongside antihypertensives — and alternative medicine as a non-mainstream practice used *in place of* conventional care, such as a botanical infusion taken instead of chemotherapy for a curable cancer. Integrative health, the third term, is the coordinated use of both under one evidence-informed plan. Why the other options fail: option B reverses the two definitions exactly, so it gives the correct words in the wrong order (B); option C sorts the two terms by the type of therapy rather than by how it is used, and both mind-body therapies and herbal products can be either complementary or alternative depending on the context (C); and option D denies a distinction that both bodies do define, with the licensing of the practitioner being a separate question from the classification of the therapy (D). Reference: WHO traditional medicine strategy; NCCIH definitions.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TCAM definitions: Complementary = used TOGETHER WITH conventional medicine. Alternative = used INSTEAD OF conventional medicine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCAM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman in shock has cold peripheries. The pulse oximeter reads 88 percent but the trace is weak and intermittent. How should the nurse interpret this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The reading may be unreliable because of poor perfusion and must be read with the clinical assessment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The reading is accurate and the oxygen should be increased at once to correct the saturation properly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The oximeter is evidently faulty and should be taken out of service and discarded immediately"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oxygen saturation cannot be affected by the circulation, so the trace quality is of no importance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "The reading may be unreliable because of poor perfusion and must be read with the clinical assessment". Pulse oximetry depends on detecting a <strong>pulsatile arterial signal</strong>. Cold peripheries, hypotension, vasoconstriction, movement and nail varnish all degrade the trace and make readings unreliable. A poor waveform means the number cannot be trusted alone, so the nurse assesses colour, conscious level, respiratory rate and effort, warms the limb or moves the probe, and escalates.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Acting on an unreliable number risks both under-treatment and over-treatment. The waveform quality must be assessed first.
• C — The device is behaving as designed. The limitation lies in the patient's perfusion, not the equipment.
• D — Poor circulation profoundly affects the oximeter's ability to detect a pulsatile signal.

Reference: Ghana STG — monitoring in shock$q$,
  strategy = $q$Never read a monitor without checking whether it can be believed. For oximetry, look at the waveform or signal quality first. For any monitor, correlate with what you see in the patient.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ADV-0130$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian nurse notices that diabetic patients in her town travel two hours for foot care. She leases a room, buys dressing supplies with her own savings, and opens a weekly foot clinic without knowing whether enough patients will come. Which term best describes her?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A volunteer, because she has no guaranteed income from the venture and gives her time freely"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An employee, because she is paid a wage for the nursing care that she provides each day"}, {"id": "c", "text": "An entrepreneur, because she spots an unmet need and takes on risk to organise resources to meet it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A manager, because she organises the room and the supplies into a service that runs smoothly every single day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “An entrepreneur, because she spots an unmet need and takes on risk to organise resources to meet it”. Entrepreneurship is spotting an unmet need and bearing the risk of organising the resources to meet it. She has identified a service nobody in the town provides, put her own savings into it, and has no guarantee that enough patients will come to recover the money — that combination of initiative and personal financial risk is what defines the role. Why the other options fail: a volunteer gives time without expecting the venture to earn and carries no financial risk (A); an employee is paid a wage by someone else and does not put her own money at stake (B); and a manager runs an organisation that already exists, carrying little of the personal risk that is the essence of what she is doing (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Unmet need plus personal risk plus organising resources equals entrepreneur.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BUS-0688$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A Ghanaian nurse opens a clinic that charges fees and covers its costs, but the surplus is used to fund free antenatal care for women who cannot pay. How should this venture be described?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A social enterprise, because it trades for income but exists mainly to achieve a social purpose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A charity, because it provides some of its services completely without charge to those in need"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A conventional business, because it charges fees for its services and has to cover all of its own costs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A public service, because it is funded from the public purse through the district health budget"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "A social enterprise, because it trades for income but exists mainly to achieve a social purpose". A social enterprise is defined by the relationship between trading and purpose: it earns income by selling something, and it does so in order to pursue a social mission rather than to distribute profit to owners. Charging fees for a clinic while spending the surplus on free antenatal care for women who cannot pay fits that description exactly — the trading is real, and the trading is not the point. Why the other options fail: it is not a charity, because a charity is funded by donations and grants rather than by the sale of its services, and giving some care free does not make the organisation charitable (B); it is not a conventional business, because in a conventional business the surplus belongs to the owners and profit is the purpose rather than a means (C); and it is not a public service, because it receives nothing from the public purse and is not accountable to a government budget line (D). Reference: social enterprise and health financing in Ghana; Ghana Health Service private-sector engagement.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Earns its own income, spends the surplus on a social purpose: social enterprise.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-BUS-0702$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Public health nutrition programmes emphasise the first 1,000 days. Which period does this describe, and why does it **matter**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "From birth to the third birthday, because this is the period when most of the serious childhood illness occurs"}, {"id": "b", "text": "From conception to the second birthday, because growth and brain development then are largely irreversible"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The first 1,000 days of school, because learning and school performance depend on adequate nutrition"}, {"id": "d", "text": "From conception to the first birthday, because breastfeeding normally ends at twelve months of age"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "From conception to the second birthday, because growth and brain development then are largely irreversible". The first 1,000 days runs from conception through pregnancy and the first two years of life, roughly 270 days of gestation plus 730 days. This window matters because linear growth and brain development proceed faster during it than at any other time, and the deficits acquired then are largely permanent. A child stunted by the age of two rarely regains that height, and the associated losses in cognitive development and later earning capacity persist into adulthood. Interventions therefore concentrate here: maternal nutrition and supplementation, early and exclusive breastfeeding, timely and adequate complementary feeding, and the prevention and treatment of infection.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Starting the count at birth omits pregnancy, when foetal growth and neural development are exquisitely sensitive to maternal nutrition. Maternal undernutrition is a major determinant of low birth weight.
• C — The concept concerns early childhood, not school age. By the time a child starts school, the window in which stunting can be prevented has largely closed.
• D — Ending at the first birthday cuts the window in half and misstates breastfeeding guidance, which recommends continuing to two years and beyond alongside complementary foods.

Reference: WHO and UNICEF global nutrition targets; Ghana Health Service nutrition policy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Count the window from conception, not birth. Any option that leaves out pregnancy has missed the period of fastest growth.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3086$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient on a surgical ward in Accra has been losing a litre a day through a high-output ileostomy. Which replacement approach is **most appropriate**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Replace the daily loss with five per cent dextrose, since the priority is to supply energy to the body"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Restrict his fluid intake, so that the ileostomy output falls and the losses become easier to manage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Replace it with a sodium-containing crystalloid such as normal saline or Ringer's, matched to the loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give plain water by mouth in the volume lost, since the fluid coming out is mainly water with a little salt"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Replace it with a sodium-containing crystalloid such as normal saline or Ringer's, matched to the loss". Ileostomy fluid is rich in sodium, and to a lesser extent potassium and bicarbonate, so it must be replaced with a fluid that resembles what was lost. A balanced salt solution such as Ringer's lactate or normal saline does this; the volume is guided by measured output and the response is judged by urine output, pulse, blood pressure and daily weight. Replacing sodium-rich losses with water or dextrose is the classic error: the water is retained and distributed across the body while the sodium deficit persists, producing hyponatraemia on top of continuing volume depletion.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Five per cent dextrose is effectively water once the glucose is metabolised. It does not replace sodium, distributes throughout total body water and will worsen hyponatraemia.
• B — Restricting fluid does not reduce ileostomy output meaningfully and simply accelerates dehydration, which is the commonest reason these patients are readmitted.
• D — Ileostomy fluid is not mainly water; it carries a substantial sodium load. Replacing it with plain water dilutes the remaining sodium and can cause symptomatic hyponatraemia.

Reference: Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines — surgical care; NICE intravenous fluid therapy guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Replace like with like. Ask what that particular fluid contained, then choose the replacement whose composition resembles it.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FLD-3141$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Following an acute soft-tissue sprain, the nurse advises rest and immobilisation for the first 48 hours. What is the main therapeutic reason for this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It maximises the release of cytokines at the site of the injury and speeds up healing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It increases the local production of corticosteroids, which reduces the swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It reduces metabolic demand and limits bleeding, swelling and further tissue strain around the injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It stimulates immediate muscle hypertrophy around the joint, so that the joint is protected from strain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "It reduces metabolic demand and limits bleeding, swelling and further tissue strain around the injury". In the first forty-eight hours after a sprain the capillaries at the injury are leaking, the tissue is trying to form a clot, and every movement disturbs that repair. Rest and immobilisation reduce the metabolic demand of the damaged tissue, limit further microvascular disruption so that less blood and fluid escape into the tissues, and hold down swelling, pain and the strain on healing ligaments. That is why rest sits first in the rest, ice, compression and elevation sequence. Why the other options fail: immobilisation does not maximise cytokine release; cytokines are part of the inflammatory response that rest is trying to keep proportionate, not drive harder (A); rest does not increase local corticosteroid production, which is not a mechanism the body uses in this way (B); and muscle hypertrophy is a training effect that takes weeks of loading, so it cannot be produced by two days of doing the opposite (D). Reference: Ghana STG — soft tissue injury; PRICE management.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In the first 48 hours the aim is to stop making it worse: rest, ice, compression, elevation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-CLINICAL-0057$q$;

-- Verify: expect 12
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch123.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 123: 12 length coding, tranche 9.
--
-- Twelve items where the correct option was the longest of the four, brought into
-- one length band. Eight ncx items keep their explain text. Four object-style items
-- carry rationales: PHM-0019 had no CORRECT quote and now does.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 66-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes refuses finger-prick glucose testing and checks her urine for sugar instead. The nurse explains that blood glucose monitoring is preferred **because**:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The renal threshold for glucose rises with age, so the urine can test negative while the blood level is high"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Urine testing is more expensive and harder to perform than testing a single drop of capillary blood at home"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Urine strips give a false-positive result in most older adults, so a negative strip cannot be relied upon at all"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The colour chart on the urine strips cannot be read by people whose eyesight is poor or beginning to fail"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "The renal threshold for glucose rises with age, so the urine can test negative while the blood level is high". Glucose appears in urine only when blood glucose exceeds the <strong>renal threshold</strong>, normally about 10 mmol/L. With ageing kidneys this threshold <strong>rises</strong>, so urine may remain negative despite marked hyperglycaemia. Urine testing also <strong>cannot detect hypoglycaemia</strong>, the greater day-to-day danger for an older person on glucose-lowering drugs.

Why the other options fail:
• B — The usual problem in older adults is a false negative, not a false positive.
• C — Urine strips are cheap and simple; cost is not the reason blood testing is preferred.
• D — Poor eyesight affects reading any strip and is a practical issue, not the physiological reason.

Reference: Ghana STG — diabetes mellitus; IDF guidance on glucose monitoring in older people$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: a 'because' question asks for physiology. Pick the option that explains why the test misses what matters — high and low blood glucose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0033$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A man learning to irrigate his descending colostomy asks why the nurse insists on the same time every day. The **best** explanation is:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The irrigating solution only works at certain hours of the day, so the timing of the washout matters"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A regular routine makes the appliance last longer and saves the patient money on the supplies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Irrigation at irregular times damages the stoma and makes the stoma surface bleed more easily"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A regular time trains the bowel to empty predictably, so output between irrigations is less likely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A regular time trains the bowel to empty predictably, so output between irrigations is less likely". Colostomy irrigation works by <strong>conditioning the bowel</strong>. Performed at the same time each day — ideally after a meal, when the gastro-colic reflex is active — it produces a <strong>predictable evacuation</strong> and may allow the person to wear only a small cap or dressing between irrigations.

Why the other options fail:
• A — The solution is plain lukewarm water; its effect does not depend on the clock.
• B — Irregular timing reduces effectiveness but does not injure the stoma.
• C — Appliance life depends on skin care and output, not on the timing of irrigation.

Reference: Ghana STG — stoma care; World Council of Enterostomal Therapists guidance$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: a 'why' question about a routine almost always rests on physiology — here, bowel conditioning and the gastro-colic reflex.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0048$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newborn with features of fetal alcohol spectrum disorder has a weak suck, is irritable and startles at the slightest noise. Which nursing consideration is **most important** in the first week?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Limit holding and skin contact, so that the baby is not over-stimulated by too much handling and by noise"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start iron supplements from the first day of life, because babies with this condition are usually anaemic already"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Offer small, frequent feeds patiently in a quiet, dimly lit room, and monitor weight gain closely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Feed the baby by nasogastric tube routinely, so that he uses no energy on sucking and grows"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Offer small, frequent feeds patiently in a quiet, dimly lit room, and monitor weight gain closely". Alcohol-exposed newborns combine <strong>poor feeding with hypersensitivity to stimuli</strong>, so they tire and lose weight. A <strong>calm, low-stimulation setting, unhurried small feeds, swaddling</strong> and close weight monitoring address both problems. Feeding difficulties are the main cause of early failure to thrive in these infants.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Iron supplementation is not an immediate newborn priority and does not address the feeding problem.
• B — Tube feeding is reserved for babies who cannot take enough orally after patient attempts; it is not routine.
• D — Gentle holding, swaddling and skin contact soothe these babies; it is noise, light and handling changes that over-stimulate.

Reference: Ghana Health Service — essential newborn care; WHO guidance on substance exposure in pregnancy$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: in a newborn, nutrition and growth are survival needs — pick the option that secures feeding while respecting the baby's fragile nervous system.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0111$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Two days after a severe head injury, a young man begins to pass very large volumes of urine. Which set of findings would **confirm** the nurse's suspicion of diabetes insipidus?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Glucose in the urine, osmotic diuresis and a blood glucose of 18 mmol/L after the injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A weight gain of 2 kg, a serum sodium of 118 mmol/L and a concentrated, high-specific-gravity urine"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Urine output of 4.5 L in 24 hours at a specific gravity of 1.003, dry mucous membranes and rising sodium"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Decerebrate posturing, blood pressure 170/100 mmHg and a pulse of 52 beats per minute, with pupils fixed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Urine output of 4.5 L in 24 hours at a specific gravity of 1.003, dry mucous membranes and rising sodium". Injury to the hypothalamic–pituitary axis stops the release of antidiuretic hormone, so the kidneys cannot concentrate urine. The result is <strong>polyuria of dilute urine (specific gravity below 1.005)</strong>, intense thirst, <strong>dehydration and hypernatraemia</strong>. Fluid balance is charted hourly and desmopressin is given as prescribed.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Glycosuria with hyperglycaemia is diabetes mellitus, a different cause of polyuria.
• B — Weight gain, dilutional hyponatraemia and concentrated urine describe SIADH — the opposite disorder, also seen after head injury.
• D — Posturing with hypertension and bradycardia is the Cushing response to raised intracranial pressure.

Reference: Ghana STG — traumatic brain injury complications; diabetes insipidus management$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: polyuria after head injury — check the specific gravity: dilute urine with rising sodium is diabetes insipidus; concentrated urine with low sodium is SIADH.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0123$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 35-year-old woman presents with a round face, weight gain around the trunk, hypertension and a fasting glucose of 8.2 mmol/L. Which result would **confirm** the nurse's suspicion of Cushing's syndrome?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A low serum sodium with a high serum potassium, indicating a mineralocorticoid deficiency at the adrenal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A low early-morning cortisol with a high ACTH level, indicating primary adrenal failure of the cortex"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A random cortisol level that falls within the normal reference range for the time of day it was taken"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Low-dose overnight dexamethasone fails to suppress her cortisol, and urinary free cortisol is raised"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Low-dose overnight dexamethasone fails to suppress her cortisol, and urinary free cortisol is raised". In Cushing's syndrome <strong>cortisol is produced autonomously</strong>, so it is <strong>not suppressed by dexamethasone</strong>, the normal daily rhythm is lost and 24-hour urinary free cortisol is high. Supporting findings are <strong>hyperglycaemia, hypokalaemia and hypernatraemia</strong>. The nurse plans care for infection risk, skin fragility, mood change and glucose control.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Low sodium and high potassium is the pattern of Addison's disease (adrenal insufficiency), the opposite condition.
• B — A single random cortisol can be normal in Cushing's because of the lost rhythm; it neither confirms nor excludes the diagnosis.
• C — Low cortisol with high ACTH indicates primary adrenal insufficiency.

Reference: Ghana STG — adrenal disorders; Endocrine Society guidance on diagnosis of Cushing's syndrome$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: too much cortisol that will not switch off — look for the test that shows loss of suppression, not a single level.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0240$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$According to Talcott Parsons' sociological concept of the 'Sick Role', which right and corresponding obligation apply to a client diagnosed with an acute medical illness?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The right to be excused from work indefinitely, with no obligation to seek treatment or return to normal roles at any point"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The right to be excused from normal roles for a time, with the obligation to seek help and want to get well"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The right to decide the treatment plan alone, with the obligation to refuse any advice that the clinicians offer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The right to be cared for without question, with the obligation to remain dependent on the family until cured"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "The right to be excused from normal roles for a time, with the obligation to seek help and want to get well". Parsons described illness as a social role rather than a purely biological event, and the role carries two rights and two obligations. The rights are that the person is exempted from normal social roles — work, household duties, caring responsibilities — and that the person is not held personally responsible for being ill. The obligations are that the person must regard being ill as undesirable and must want to get well, and must seek competent help and cooperate with it. The exemption is temporary and conditional on the effort to recover, which is what separates the sick role from malingering. Why the other options fail: the exemption from work is temporary and conditional, not indefinite, and it carries the obligation to seek treatment rather than freeing the person of it (A); the sick role does not confer authority to dictate treatment, and the obligation is to cooperate with competent care rather than to refuse it (C); and dependence is not the defining feature, since the role requires the person to work towards leaving it (D). Reference: Parsons, The Social System; sociology of health and illness.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parsons' Sick Role: Exemption from normal duties (right) paired with the duty to seek professional help and strive for recovery (obligation).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-BEH-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse begins a shift on a 28-bed ward. Four patients need attention. Which should be seen **first**?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A woman reporting abdominal pain of 8 out of 10, after her prescribed analgesia was delayed by the drug round"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A man whose intravenous cannula site has become red and slightly swollen around the entry point overnight"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A man with a chest infection whose respiratory rate has risen from 22 to 34 and who is now confused"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A woman due for discharge today, who is still waiting for her medicines and her health education talk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A man with a chest infection whose respiratory rate has risen from 22 to 34 and who is now confused". A rising respiratory rate with <strong>new confusion</strong> is a breathing problem producing cerebral hypoxia. Respiratory rate is the earliest and most sensitive indicator of deterioration, and altered mentation means compensation is already failing. This patient may be developing respiratory failure or sepsis and needs assessment, oxygen and escalation now.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Severe pain is urgent and must be treated quickly, but pain does not kill in the next ten minutes. Urgent is not the same as life-threatening.
• B — Early phlebitis needs the cannula resited, but it is a localised, slowly evolving problem.
• D — Discharge teaching is important and time-consuming, which is exactly why it tempts a busy nurse. It is not clinically urgent.

Reference: NMC Ghana scope of practice; early warning principles$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sort every option into 'stable and expected' or 'new and changing'. Then apply ABC to what is changing. A rising respiratory rate with new confusion outranks severe pain, local inflammation and any scheduled task.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-FUN-0013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 1,000 mL infusion prescribed to run over 8 hours is found to have 600 mL left after 4 hours. What should the nurse do?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the infusion altogether, because the original prescription is no longer valid at this stage of the run"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Open the clamp fully so that the infusion catches up to the prescribed schedule as quickly as it possibly can"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the rate unchanged and simply document the shortfall at the end of the shift for the next nurse to see"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recalculate the rate for the remaining volume and time, and tell the team the infusion is running behind"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Recalculate the rate for the remaining volume and time, and tell the team the infusion is running behind". After four hours of an 8-hour litre, 500 mL should have run and 500 mL should be left; 600 mL remaining means the infusion is 100 mL behind. The safe correction is arithmetic rather than force: divide what is left by the time that is left and set the new rate, then tell the prescriber or the team that the infusion has fallen behind, because the delay may reflect a blocked line, a tissued cannula or a pump fault that needs finding. Why the other options fail: stopping the infusion removes a prescribed therapy on the nurse's own authority, and the prescription has not expired (A); opening the clamp fully delivers the deficit as a bolus, and rapid boluses overload patients who tolerate volume poorly — children, older adults and anyone with cardiac or renal disease (B); and leaving the rate unchanged while writing a note at the end of the shift means the patient receives less than was prescribed for the whole of the shift while the nurse watches it happen (C). Reference: Ghana nursing procedure manual — intravenous infusion management; NMC standards on medication administration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$An infusion that is behind is recalculated, never caught up by opening the clamp.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-PHM-0019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A woman is admitted with hypoparathyroidism and a serum calcium of 1.6 mmol/L. Which nursing action has the **highest** priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Begin the teaching plan about lifelong calcium and vitamin D, because she is going home on both of them"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Protect her from chest infection by limiting visitors, because an infection would worsen the tetany"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Keep IV calcium gluconate and airway equipment at the bedside, and watch for stridor and dysrhythmias"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Review the trend in her phosphate level, because a high phosphate level would explain the low calcium"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Keep IV calcium gluconate and airway equipment at the bedside, and watch for stridor and dysrhythmias". Severe hypocalcaemia causes <strong>neuromuscular irritability</strong> that can progress to <strong>laryngospasm (airway obstruction), seizures and life-threatening dysrhythmias</strong> from a prolonged QT interval. Readiness to give intravenous calcium and to support the airway, with continuous observation, protects life; everything else follows.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Teaching is essential for discharge but cannot come before the threat to airway and circulation is controlled.
• B — Reviewing results is useful but is a lower priority than preparing to treat the emergency the results predict.
• D — Respiratory infection is not a particular risk of hypoparathyroidism.

Reference: Ghana STG — hypocalcaemia; emergency management of tetany$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: ABCs — low calcium threatens the airway and the heart; choose the option that prepares for both.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0137$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$The care plan for a man one day after a thoracotomy includes turning, coughing and deep breathing every two hours. The nurse understands that the **main** purpose of this is to:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Raise the blood pH and restore the bicarbonate balance that the anaesthetic has disturbed during surgery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prevent respiratory alkalosis by increasing the amount of oxygen that is taken in with each deep breath"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reduce the amount of analgesia he needs by keeping the muscles of the chest wall moving freely all day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Expand the lungs and clear secretions, keeping ventilation and preventing CO2 retention and collapse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Expand the lungs and clear secretions, keeping ventilation and preventing CO2 retention and collapse". Pain and anaesthesia make post-thoracotomy patients breathe shallowly and avoid coughing, so alveoli collapse and secretions pool. Regular <strong>deep breathing, incentive spirometry, coughing with the wound supported, and position changes</strong> keep the alveoli open and secretions moving, preventing <strong>atelectasis, pneumonia and respiratory acidosis</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Raising pH is not a goal; the aim is to prevent the fall in pH that hypoventilation causes.
• B — Good analgesia is a prerequisite for effective coughing, not something the exercises replace.
• C — Alkalosis results from over-breathing, which this measure does not prevent.

Reference: Ghana STG — post-operative pulmonary care; thoracic surgery nursing standards$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: breathing exercises target ventilation — the movement of air and removal of carbon dioxide — rather than oxygen alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0192$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A patient in the last days of life is no longer eating or drinking. The family insists a drip be started so that he does not starve. What is the **most appropriate** nursing response?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain gently that less eating and drinking is part of dying, and that fluids may add to secretions and discomfort"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start intravenous fluids immediately, because withholding them from a dying patient would be a form of neglect"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Tell the family that nothing more can be done for him, and that their request is unreasonable at this stage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Insert a nasogastric tube, so that the feeding can be continued whatever the circumstances and the family are reassured"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Explain gently that less eating and drinking is part of dying, and that fluids may add to secretions and discomfort". Loss of appetite and thirst is an expected part of the dying process rather than a cause of it. Artificial hydration at this stage does not prolong life or relieve the sensation of thirst, and it can cause harm through increased respiratory secretions, worsening oedema, and the discomfort of cannulation. What relieves thirst is scrupulous mouth care with moistened swabs and lip care, which the family can be shown how to give. That participation matters, because the request for a drip usually expresses a fear of abandoning the patient. Acknowledging that fear, explaining the physiology kindly and offering a role transforms the conversation.

Why the other options fail:
• B — Starting fluids reflexively to satisfy distress substitutes a procedure for a conversation, and may add secretions and oedema to a patient's final days without benefit.
• C — Telling a family nothing can be done is untrue and cruel. A great deal can be done for comfort, and dismissing the request leaves them feeling their relative has been abandoned.
• D — A nasogastric tube at the end of life is burdensome and offers no benefit. It carries aspiration risk and is distressing for a dying patient to have inserted and maintained.

Reference: WHO palliative care guidance; Ghana Standard Treatment Guidelines — palliative care$q$,
  strategy = $q$At the end of life, ask whether an intervention adds comfort or only adds activity. Address the family's fear directly rather than treating it with a procedure.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-NUT-3087$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse teaching a community group of older adults explains that the **main** reason constipation becomes more common with age is:$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The small intestine gradually stops absorbing water from the stool, which becomes hard and difficult to pass"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reduced physical activity and weaker abdominal and intestinal muscle tone slow the passage of stool"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The nerve supply to the bowel is destroyed by the ageing process, so peristalsis cannot be triggered"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Older people lose the sensation that tells them it is time to open their bowels, so stool is held back"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Reduced physical activity and weaker abdominal and intestinal muscle tone slow the passage of stool". Age brings <strong>less activity, weaker abdominal muscles and slower colonic transit</strong>, often compounded by low fluid and fibre intake and constipating medicines. These are modifiable, which is why teaching focuses on <strong>walking, fluids, fruit, vegetables and whole grains</strong>.

Why the other options fail:
• A — Rectal sensation may blunt slightly but is not the main driver of constipation in most older adults.
• C — Normal ageing does not destroy the enteric nervous system.
• D — Water absorption in the colon actually increases with slow transit, hardening the stool; the small intestine does not stop absorbing water.

Reference: Ghana STG — constipation; WHO healthy ageing$q$,
  strategy = $q$Decision tree: 'main reason' in a health-education question points to the modifiable cause the nurse can act on.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-ORIGINAL-NCX-0032$q$;

-- Verify: expect 12
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch124.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 124: 15 no-quote rationales, three wrong keys fixed.
--
-- First batch of the no-quote sweep. All fifteen items in ap1-module-scenarios.ts
-- carried no CORRECT (X) quote, so the build verifier - which only checks a quote
-- when one is present - had never inspected them.
-- 
-- Three keys were wrong and are corrected: AP1-008 key a said the growth plate has
-- no nerve supply and injuries are painless (false; the plate is the growth zone
-- and the risk is premature closure), now key b; AP1-012 key c blamed the superior
-- gluteal artery rather than the sciatic nerve, now key a; AP1-015 key d claimed
-- only fat malabsorbs because the lacteals are blocked, now key a. Their distractor
-- explanations described options that no longer existed and have been rewritten.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is teaching nursing students about physiological homeostasis. Which scenario accurately exemplifies a Negative Feedback homeostatic control mechanism?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Uterine cervical stretch during labour triggers oxytocin secretion, which produces stronger uterine contractions that further increase cervical stretch until the baby is delivered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Platelet adhesion to a ruptured vessel wall triggers the release of ADP and thromboxane A2, recruiting more platelets in an accelerating cascade until a clot forms"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Elevated blood glucose triggers pancreatic beta-cells to secrete insulin, which promotes cellular glucose uptake and returns blood glucose back toward normal baseline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lactation suckling stimulates prolactin and oxytocin release, which triggers milk let-down and then stimulates further vigorous infant suckling at the breast"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Elevated blood glucose triggers pancreatic beta-cells to secrete insulin, which promotes cellular glucose uptake and returns blood glucose back toward normal baseline”. A Negative Feedback mechanism is a primary homeostatic regulatory loop that detects deviations from a set point and triggers biological responses that reverse or oppose the initial stimulus, restoring equilibrium. When blood glucose rises, insulin release lowers glucose back to baseline (opposing the change). In contrast, Positive Feedback mechanisms amplify, reinforce, and accelerate the initial stimulus until a discrete endpoint is reached, as in oxytocin-mediated labor contractions, platelet aggregation in hemostasis, and the suckling-milk ejection reflex. Why the other options fail: option a is a positive feedback loop, because contractions increase the stimulus that produced them. Option b is also positive feedback, with each platelet recruiting more. Option d is positive feedback again, since suckling begets more suckling rather than returning the system to baseline.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Homeostasis: Negative feedback opposes/reverses the stimulus to restore baseline equilibrium (e.g., blood glucose, body temperature). Positive feedback amplifies the stimulus (e.g., labor, clotting).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client undergoes a computed tomography (CT) scan of the abdomen. The radiologist describes cross-sectional cross-cuts dividing the body into superior (upper) and inferior (lower) portions. Which anatomical plane was used for these imaging slices?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Midsagittal (median) plane, dividing the body into equal right and left halves through the midline"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Frontal (coronal) plane, dividing the body into anterior and posterior portions from head to foot"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Parasagittal plane, dividing the body into unequal right and left portions off the midline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transverse (horizontal or axial) plane, dividing the body into superior and inferior portions"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Transverse (horizontal or axial) plane, dividing the body into superior and inferior portions”. The Transverse plane (also called horizontal or axial plane) runs horizontally, dividing the body or an organ into superior (upper) and inferior (lower) sections; standard axial CT and MRI cross-sections are viewed along this plane. A Sagittal (or midsagittal) plane divides the body vertically into right and left halves. A Frontal (or coronal) plane divides the body vertically into anterior (ventral/front) and posterior (dorsal/back) portions. Why the other options fail: option a splits the body into right and left halves, not upper and lower. Option b splits it into anterior and posterior. Option c is a vertical cut parallel to the midline, which still produces left and right portions.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Anatomical planes: Transverse (axial) = superior/inferior. Sagittal = right/left. Coronal (frontal) = anterior/posterior.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with a penetrating stab wound to the left chest develops severe respiratory distress, tracheal deviation to the right, and absent breath sounds on the left. In anatomical terms, what pathology has occurred in the thoracic serous membranes?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Air entered the pleural cavity between the visceral and parietal pleura, abolishing the negative intrapleural pressure and collapsing the lung on that side"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Fluid accumulating in the retroperitoneal space behind the abdomen, displacing the left kidney upwards and splinting the diaphragm from below throughout the illness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Air trapped in the subarachnoid space after a dural tear, raising intracranial pressure and splinting the chest wall indirectly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bile leaking between the visceral and parietal peritoneum, irritating the undersurface of the diaphragm and causing referred chest pain at rest"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Air entered the pleural cavity between the visceral and parietal pleura, abolishing the negative intrapleural pressure and collapsing the lung on that side”. The lungs are enclosed by a double-layered serous membrane: the visceral pleura (covering the lung surface) and the parietal pleura (lining the thoracic wall), separated by the pleural cavity containing a thin film of serous fluid under negative pressure. When penetrating trauma breaches this seal, air rushes into the potential space (pneumothorax). In a tension pneumothorax, a one-way valve effect traps air, increasing intrapleural pressure, collapsing the ipsilateral lung, shifting the mediastinum and trachea to the opposite side, and compressing the vena cava, causing obstructive shock. Why the other options fail: option b puts fluid in the retroperitoneum, which lies behind the abdominal cavity and does not collapse the lung. Option c puts air in the subarachnoid space, which is inside the skull and spinal canal. Option d puts bile in the peritoneum, which irritates the diaphragm but leaves the pleural space intact.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Thoracic anatomy: Pleural cavity lies between visceral pleura (on lungs) and parietal pleura (on chest wall). Air entry collapses negative pressure, causing pneumothorax.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with acute cerebral edema from traumatic brain injury is prescribed an intravenous infusion of 3% Hypertonic Saline. Based on the cellular principles of osmosis and tonicity, what fluid movement occurs across cerebral capillary endothelial cell membranes?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Water moves from the intravascular space into the already swollen brain cells, because the hypertonic fluid cannot cross the capillary wall at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Water moves from the swollen brain interstitium into the hypertonic intravascular space, reducing cerebral oedema and intracranial pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sodium pulls water into the brain interstitium first, and the oedema resolves only when the kidneys excrete the excess sodium and water"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The hypertonic gradient draws sodium into the brain cells, and water follows the sodium, so the intracellular volume rises further"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Water moves from the swollen brain interstitium into the hypertonic intravascular space, reducing cerebral oedema and intracranial pressure”. Osmosis is the net movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration (higher water concentration) to an area of higher solute concentration (lower water concentration). Hypertonic solutions (such as 3% sodium chloride, osmolarity ~1,026 mOsm/L) have a higher solute concentration than normal intracellular fluid (~285 mOsm/L). Infusing 3% saline raises intravascular osmolarity, creating an osmotic gradient that pulls excess water out of swollen brain cells and interstitial tissue into cerebral capillaries, thereby reducing brain volume and intracranial pressure (ICP). Why the other options fail: option a reverses the gradient, because water leaves the brain when the plasma is hypertonic. Option c describes a delayed renal effect rather than the immediate osmotic shift. Option d moves sodium and water into the cells, which is the effect of hypotonic fluid.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Tonicity & Osmosis: Hypertonic fluids pull water OUT of cells into blood vessels (shrinks swollen cells). Hypotonic fluids push water INTO cells (cells swell).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During acute myocardial infarction, coronary occlusion cuts off oxygen delivery to myocardial cells. What immediate cellular pathophysiological sequence occurs as a result of hypoxia?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "ATP depletion opens the potassium channels first, so potassium enters the cell and water follows it in, causing the cell to swell and burst"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, the sodium-potassium pump fails, so sodium and water enter and the cell swells and acidifies"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Anaerobic glycolysis raises ATP above its normal level, and the excess energy drives calcium into the cell from the extracellular fluid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The cell membrane becomes impermeable to all ions after hypoxia, so the cell can no longer regulate its volume and swells"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Oxidative phosphorylation stops, ATP falls, the sodium-potassium pump fails, so sodium and water enter and the cell swells and acidifies”. Oxygen is the final electron acceptor in mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation. Hypoxia causes: 1) cessation of aerobic ATP synthesis; 2) switch to anaerobic glycolysis, generating lactic acid and lowering cellular pH; 3) failure of the ATP-dependent Na+/K+ pump, causing sodium (and obligate water) to accumulate inside the cell while potassium leaks out, leading to hydropic cellular swelling; and 4) influx of extracellular calcium activating destructive intracellular enzymes (proteases, lipases, endonucleases), culminating in irreversible necrosis. Why the other options fail: option a has potassium entering the cell first, when the failing pump loses its gradient and sodium accumulates inside. Option c has anaerobic glycolysis raising ATP, which it cannot do as efficiently as oxidation. Option d makes the membrane impermeable to ions, which is the opposite of pump failure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cellular pathophysiology: Hypoxia -> ATP depletion -> Na+/K+ pump failure -> intracellular Na+ and water accumulation -> cell swelling and lactic acidosis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A bronchial biopsy in a client with a 40-pack-year history of cigarette smoking reveals that the normal pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium of the respiratory tract has been replaced by stratified squamous epithelium. What adaptive cellular alteration is this?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Metaplasia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypertrophy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Atrophy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hyperplasia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Metaplasia”. Metaplasia is a reversible cellular adaptation in which one adult mature cell type (e.g., pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium) is replaced by another mature adult cell type (e.g., stratified squamous epithelium) that is better able to tolerate chronic physical or chemical irritation (such as hot cigarette smoke). However, this adaptation results in the loss of vital mucus secretion and ciliary escalator function, predisposing to chronic bronchitis, and if irritation persists, can progress to dysplasia and bronchogenic squamous cell carcinoma. Hypertrophy is increased cell size; Atrophy is decreased cell size; Hyperplasia is increased cell number. Why the other options fail: option b increases cell size without changing cell type. Option c shrinks cells without changing type. Option d increases the number of the same cells, which is not the substitution of one mature epithelium for another that the biopsy shows.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cellular adaptations: Metaplasia = conversion of one mature cell type to another (e.g., respiratory ciliated columnar to stratified squamous in smokers).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A post-menopausal client with severe osteoporosis asks the nurse how bones maintain their calcium strength. How do Osteoblasts, Osteoclasts, and Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) interact in skeletal calcium homeostasis?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Osteoblasts resorb bone while osteoclasts build it, and PTH lowers serum calcium by blocking the action of both cell types"}, {"id": "b", "text": "PTH raises serum calcium by increasing intestinal absorption of calcium alone, without acting on the bone cells at any stage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Osteoclasts both build and resorb bone, and PTH simply switches them from one role to the other as the calcium level changes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoblasts build bone, osteoclasts resorb it, and PTH drives osteoclastic resorption to raise serum calcium when it falls"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Osteoblasts build bone, osteoclasts resorb it, and PTH drives osteoclastic resorption to raise serum calcium when it falls”. Bone is dynamic living tissue continuously remodeled by two primary cell types: 1) OSTEOBLASTS ('bone builders') synthesize collagen matrix and promote mineral deposition (osteogenesis); 2) OSTEOCLASTS ('bone chewers/resorbers') secrete lysosomal enzymes and acids to break down bone matrix, releasing ionized calcium into systemic circulation. When serum calcium falls below normal, Parathyroid Hormone (PTH) is secreted, stimulating osteoclast activity, renal calcium reabsorption, and vitamin D activation to elevate serum calcium. Calcitonin opposes PTH. In post-menopausal osteoporosis, estrogen deficiency accelerates osteoclastic resorption over osteoblastic formation. Why the other options fail: option A reverses the two cells, since osteoblasts build bone and osteoclasts resorb it, so the sentence inverts the basic anatomy (A); option B gives PTH an intestinal action only and denies it any effect on bone, whereas PTH acts on the kidney and, through signalling from osteoblasts, on bone to drive resorption (B); and option C makes one cell type do both jobs, when building and resorbing belong to two separate lineages that PTH coordinates rather than converts (C).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bone cells: Osteoblasts = Build bone (deposition). Osteoclasts = Chew/break down bone (resorption). PTH stimulates osteoclasts to raise serum calcium.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 8-year-old child sustains a traumatic fracture through the distal femoral growth plate (epiphyseal plate). Why is an injury to this specific anatomical region of a long bone a critical pediatric orthopedic concern?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The plate fuses by about eight years of age, so an injury after that age cannot affect growth or the final length of the limb at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The plate is the growth zone, and injury can cause premature or asymmetric closure with length discrepancy or angulation"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The plate is the weakest part of the bone, so fractures there heal more slowly than shaft fractures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The plate carries the blood supply to the epiphysis, so injury causes avascular necrosis of the whole bone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “The plate is the growth zone, and injury can cause premature or asymmetric closure with length discrepancy or angulation”. In a growing child, longitudinal bone growth happens only at the epiphyseal plate, the physis, where columns of chondrocytes proliferate and are then replaced by bone through endochondral ossification. That is why an injury which would be trivial in an adult shaft is critical here: a Salter-Harris fracture can tear across the proliferative zone, disturb its blood supply, and then heal by laying down bone where cartilage should be, a premature epiphyseal arrest. If the whole plate closes the limb ends up short; if only part of it closes, growth continues unevenly and the bone angulates, and the younger the child the more remaining growth is at risk. Why the other options fail: the physis does not fuse at eight years, and the distal femoral growth plate stays open into the mid-teens, so the remaining growth is precisely what puts the child at risk (A); the plate is indeed the weakest part of a child's long bone, but that is why it fractures readily rather than slowly, and the concern is disordered growth rather than delayed union (C); and while the physis does have its own blood supply, injury does not as a rule produce avascular necrosis of the whole bone, which is the risk at the femoral head and the scaphoid (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pediatric bone anatomy: Epiphyseal plate fractures (Salter-Harris) damage growth cartilage, risking premature growth arrest and permanent limb discrepancy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is comparing joint pathologies in a medical clinic. Which structural feature and pathophysiology distinguish Osteoarthritis (OA) from Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) in a synovial joint?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Osteoarthritis follows a low-grade synovial infection, while rheumatoid arthritis is a purely genetic disorder with no inflammatory component"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Osteoarthritis is degenerative cartilage loss with subchondral remodelling, while rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune synovitis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Rheumatoid arthritis affects the weight-bearing joints asymmetrically, while osteoarthritis is always symmetric and spares the hands"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Osteoarthritis affects the synovium first, while rheumatoid arthritis begins in the subchondral bone and spares the synovium entirely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — “Osteoarthritis is degenerative cartilage loss with subchondral remodelling, while rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune synovitis”. A synovial (diarthrodial) joint consists of articular hyaline cartilage, a fibrous capsule lined by a synovial membrane, and synovial lubricating fluid. OSTEOARTHRITIS is primarily a biomechanical degenerative disorder characterized by progressive degradation of articular cartilage, osteophyte (bone spur) formation, and joint space narrowing, classically exacerbated by weight-bearing and use. RHEUMATOID ARTHRITIS is a systemic autoimmune disease where autoantibodies target the synovial membrane, causing inflammatory synovitis, microvascular proliferation, and invasive hyperplastic pannus that erodes articular cartilage and bone symmetrically. Why the other options fail: option A makes osteoarthritis an infection and rheumatoid arthritis a purely genetic disease, when osteoarthritis is mechanical and degenerative and rheumatoid arthritis is autoimmune and inflammatory (A); option C reverses the distribution, since rheumatoid arthritis is characteristically symmetric and involves the small joints of the hands, while osteoarthritis favours weight-bearing joints and is often asymmetric (C); and option D puts each disease in the wrong tissue, because both destroy articular cartilage and rheumatoid arthritis begins in the synovium rather than sparing it (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Synovial joint pathology: Osteoarthritis = cartilage wear-and-tear (subchondral bone spurs). Rheumatoid arthritis = autoimmune synovial inflammation (pannus formation).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During skeletal muscle contraction, what sequence of molecular events occurs according to the Sliding Filament Theory?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Calcium binds tropomyosin, which pulls troponin aside so that the myosin heads can bind actin and shorten the sarcomere during a contraction"}, {"id": "b", "text": "ATP hydrolysis extends the actin filaments while the myosin filaments shorten, which draws the Z lines closer together in the sarcomere"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sodium entering through the T-tubules binds troponin and exposes the actin sites for the myosin heads to attach to during a contraction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, moving tropomyosin so myosin heads cross-bridge and shorten the sarcomere"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin, moving tropomyosin so myosin heads cross-bridge and shorten the sarcomere”. The sliding filament mechanism of skeletal muscle contraction follows a precise molecular sequence: 1) Motor neuron action potential releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction; 2) End-plate depolarization propagates down transverse tubules (T-tubules); 3) Voltage-gated channels trigger calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum; 4) Calcium binds to TROPONIN, inducing a conformational shift in TROPOMYOSIN that uncovers active myosin-binding sites on ACTIN; 5) Energized MYOSIN heads bind to actin, forming cross-bridges; 6) The power stroke pulls actin filaments toward the sarcomere center (M-line), hydrolyzing ATP; 7) New ATP binding releases myosin from actin. Filaments do not change length; they slide past each other. Why the other options fail: option a dissolves the myosin heads, which are not consumed by contraction. Option b lengthens actin and shortens myosin, when the filaments slide past each other without changing length. Option c gives the trigger to sodium and collagen, when calcium and troponin are the switch.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Muscle contraction sequence: Action potential -> Calcium release from sarcoplasmic reticulum -> Calcium binds troponin -> Tropomyosin moves -> Myosin-actin cross-bridge power stroke.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 32-year-old female presents with fluctuating muscle fatigue, bilateral ptosis, diplopia, and difficulty swallowing that worsens with exertion and improves with rest. The physician diagnoses Myasthenia Gravis. What neuromuscular junction anatomy and pathophysiology underlie this disorder?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autoantibodies block the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor end-plate, so the muscle membrane cannot depolarise properly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Excess acetylcholinesterase at the end-plate breaks down acetylcholine before it can act on the muscle membrane at all during a contraction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Autoantibodies destroy the presynaptic calcium channels, so acetylcholine is never released into the synaptic cleft at any point in the disease"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autoantibodies block the muscarinic receptors on the muscle membrane, preventing depolarisation of the muscle fibre during voluntary effort"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Autoantibodies block the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the motor end-plate, so the muscle membrane cannot depolarise properly”. Myasthenia Gravis (MG) is an autoimmune disease targeting the neuromuscular junction. Circulating autoantibodies (primarily against the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor [AChR] on the postsynaptic muscle sarcolemma) block acetylcholine binding and accelerate receptor degradation. With repetitive motor neuron firing, acetylcholine stores deplete while fewer functional receptors remain available, resulting in progressive muscle fatigability that worsens with activity and improves with rest (e.g., ptosis, diplopia, dysarthria, dysphagia, and potentially fatal myasthenic respiratory crisis). ALS destroys motor neurons; Guillain-Barré causes demyelination. Why the other options fail: Excess acetylcholinesterase would break acetylcholine down faster than it is released, which is not the antibody mechanism; destruction of the presynaptic calcium channels is Lambert-Eaton, which improves with use; muscarinic blockade on the muscle membrane is not how the end-plate fails.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Neuromuscular junction: Myasthenia Gravis = autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors -> muscle weakness and fatigability.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse prepares to administer a 3 mL deep intramuscular injection of an irritating viscous medication. Why is the Ventrogluteal site (gluteus medius and minimus) the anatomically preferred site over the Dorsogluteal site for adult intramuscular injections?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It lies away from the sciatic nerve and major vessels, and has thick muscle for a viscous drug"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It has fewer nerve endings, so an irritating drug causes less pain and is absorbed more slowly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It avoids the superior gluteal artery, and it lies within the safe triangle bounded by the iliac crest"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It lies over the gluteus maximus, which has a larger surface area than the dorsogluteal site"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “It lies away from the sciatic nerve and major vessels, and has thick muscle for a viscous drug”. The ventrogluteal site places the needle into gluteus medius and minimus, in the wedge held between the palm on the greater trochanter and the index finger on the anterior superior iliac spine, with the middle finger spread back towards the iliac crest. That wedge is thick muscle under a fairly consistent layer of fat, and it lies well clear of the sciatic nerve — the structure the dorsogluteal site puts at risk, with foot drop as the injury. Dose volume matters here: 3 mL of an irritating drug needs a deep belly with good blood flow, not a thin sheet of muscle over bone. Why the other options fail: a site is not chosen for having fewer nerve endings, and slowing the absorption of an irritant does not protect the tissue (B); the ventrogluteal site is not preferred specifically for avoiding the superior gluteal artery, which is one of the hazards of the dorsogluteal route, and the triangle it sits in is defined by the anterior superior iliac spine, the iliac crest and the greater trochanter rather than by the iliac crest alone (C); and the site lies over gluteus medius and minimus, so its surface area is smaller than the dorsogluteal area over gluteus maximus, not larger (D). Reference: Ghana nursing procedure manual — intramuscular injection; site selection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IM injection anatomy: Ventrogluteal (gluteus medius/minimus) is safest—deep muscle belly, no major nerves. Dorsogluteal is unsafe due to proximity to the Sciatic Nerve.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who underwent a total gastrectomy for gastric adenocarcinoma is scheduled for discharge. Based on gastric anatomy and mucosal histology, which lifelong deficiency and hematological disorder will develop unless treated with parenteral replacement?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of chief cells removes pepsinogen, so protein cannot be digested and iron absorption falls, causing a microcytic anaemia over time"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of acid allows bacterial overgrowth in the small bowel, which consumes vitamin B12 and causes a macrocytic anaemia over months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Loss of intrinsic factor reduces folate absorption in the jejunum, causing a macrocytic anaemia that responds to folate therapy alone"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum, causing a macrocytic anaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — “Loss of parietal cells removes intrinsic factor, so vitamin B12 cannot be absorbed in the terminal ileum, causing a macrocytic anaemia”. The gastric mucosa contains specialized secretory glands in the fundus and body: 1) PARIETAL CELLS secrete Hydrochloric Acid (HCl, establishing an acidic pH for pepsin activation and pathogen destruction) and INTRINSIC FACTOR (IF). Intrinsic factor binds dietary Vitamin B12 (cobalamin), protecting it from digestive degradation and facilitating receptor-mediated absorption in the terminal ileum. Following total gastrectomy (or autoimmune destruction of parietal cells in pernicious anemia), intrinsic factor is completely absent, preventing B12 absorption and resulting in macrocytic megaloblastic anemia and progressive neurological demyelination (subacute combined degeneration). Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Why the other options fail: option a blames chief cells and pepsinogen for calcium, which they do not regulate. Option b blames acid loss for sickle cell disease, which is inherited. Option c blames pepsinogen for haemophilia, which is a clotting factor deficiency.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gastric physiology: Parietal cells secrete HCl and Intrinsic Factor. Intrinsic Factor is mandatory for Vitamin B12 absorption in the terminal ileum.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with gallstone obstruction of the common bile duct (choledocholithiasis) presents with clay-colored (acholic) stools, dark amber urine, jaundice, and steatorrhea. Which physiological function of bile and bilirubin excretion has been interrupted?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bile salts from the gallbladder emulsify fat, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea, while bilirubin is excreted by the kidney"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bile salts are needed for fat digestion, but bilirubin reaches the gut in the blood and is excreted in the urine instead"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bile salts from the liver emulsify fat and carry bilirubin into the gut, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea and pale stools"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bile acids activate pancreatic lipase in the duodenum, and obstruction blocks the enzyme rather than the bile"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — “Bile salts from the liver emulsify fat and carry bilirubin into the gut, so obstruction causes steatorrhoea and pale stools”. Bile is produced continuously by hepatocytes in the liver, concentrated in the gallbladder, and released into the duodenum via the common bile duct in response to cholecystokinin (CCK). Bile salts act as biological detergents, emulsifying large dietary fat globules into microscopic micelles to provide a vast surface area for pancreatic lipase digestion. Bile also excretes conjugated bilirubin into the intestinal lumen, where normal bacterial flora metabolize it into stercobilin (giving feces their brown color). When choledocholithiasis obstructs bile outflow, fats cannot be absorbed (steatorrhea), feces lack stercobilin (clay-colored stools), and water-soluble conjugated bilirubin backs up into circulation, spilling into urine (dark amber) and skin (jaundice). Why the other options fail: option A attributes bile production to the gallbladder and sends bilirubin to the kidney, but the liver makes bile and obstruction backs conjugated bilirubin into the blood, which is what turns the urine dark (A); option B sends bilirubin to the gut through the bloodstream, which is not a route it takes, and still treats the gallbladder as the source of bile (B); and option D gives bile acids a job in activating pancreatic lipase and then blames the enzyme rather than the blocked duct, when lipase is activated by colipase and the problem here is that no bile reaches the gut at all (D).$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hepatobiliary physiology: Bile emulsifies dietary fats and excretes bilirubin. Common bile duct obstruction causes steatorrhea, clay-colored stools, and jaundice.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A small intestinal biopsy in a client with untreated celiac disease reveals diffuse blunting and total atrophy of the intestinal villi and microvilli across the duodenum and jejunum. How does this structural damage impair physiological nutrition?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Loss of villi removes brush border enzymes and surface area, so fat, carbohydrate, protein and iron all malabsorb"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of villi reduces the surface area for water absorption alone, producing diarrhoea without nutrient loss"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The villi regenerate within days after gluten withdrawal, so the absorptive defect is transient and self-limiting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Villous atrophy blocks the lymphatic lacteals, so only fat is malabsorbed while sugars and proteins are spared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — “Loss of villi removes brush border enzymes and surface area, so fat, carbohydrate, protein and iron all malabsorb”. The small bowel multiplies its absorptive surface three times over — circular folds, then villi, then the microvilli of the brush border — until it is many hundreds of times the area of a plain tube. In untreated coeliac disease the immune response to gluten destroys enterocytes, the villi flatten and the crypts lengthen, and both things that matter are lost together: the surface itself, and the disaccharidases and peptidases mounted on it. The result is global malabsorption rather than one deficiency — fat causes steatorrhoea, carbohydrate and protein are poorly taken up, and iron and folate are absorbed in the upper bowel that has been worst damaged, so anaemia and weight loss follow. Why the other options fail: water absorption is not the only function lost, and a patient with coeliac disease loses nutrients as well as fluid (B); the mucosa does recover on a strict gluten-free diet, but over weeks to months rather than days, and not while gluten continues (C); and the lacteals are not blocked — this is a mucosal absorptive failure, not lymphatic obstruction, so fat is not the only nutrient affected (D). Reference: Ghana STG — coeliac disease; intestinal histology.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Intestinal histology: Villi and microvilli maximize mucosal absorptive surface area. Villi atrophy in celiac disease causes generalized nutrient malabsorption.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-AP1-015$q$;

