-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch128.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 128: 40 NEURO and MSK rationales quoted, whole block out of the key-longest class.
--
-- NEURO and MSK: 40 items read in full, 40 keys correct.
-- Every rationale now opens with CORRECT (X) and the keyed option's exact text.
-- All 40 are out of the key-longest class; nineteen had distractors lengthened.
-- NEURO-001 fail-bullet said "confused speech and withdrawal" where the stem
-- gives incomprehensible sounds and decorticate flexion; corrected to the
-- actual GCS components that sum to seven.
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse in the emergency department assesses a client admitted after a motor vehicle collision. The client opens their eyes only in response to a painful sternal rub, utters incomprehensible sounds, and abnormally flexes their upper extremities toward the core (decorticate posturing) when noxious stimuli are applied. What is this client's Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score, and what is the nurse's immediate priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "GCS is 11; place the client in a high-Fowler position and administer oral analgesics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "GCS is 5; place the client in the Trendelenburg position to optimize cerebral perfusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "GCS is 9; perform hourly neurological assessments and request a routine brain MRI"}, {"id": "d", "text": "GCS is 7; prepare for endotracheal intubation and definitive airway protection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "GCS is 7; prepare for endotracheal intubation and definitive airway protection" The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) assesses eye opening (1–4), verbal response (1–5), and motor response (1–6). In this client: eye opening to pain = 2; incomprehensible sounds = 2; decorticate abnormal flexion = 3, yielding a total GCS of 7 (2 + 2 + 3 = 7). A GCS score of 8 or less indicates severe traumatic brain injury and comatose state, carrying an inability to maintain or protect the airway. The clinical rule is 'GCS 8 or less, intubate.' Trendelenburg position is contraindicated because it increases intracranial pressure. Oral medications are strictly contraindicated in an unconscious client. Why the other options fail: A score of eleven would need a much higher verbal and motor response than this client shows; nine would mean the client is not yet at the intubation threshold; five would be a deeper coma than the described responses give. Eye opening to pain (2), incomprehensible sounds (2) and decorticate flexion (3) add to seven, at which the airway must be protected.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Calculate each GCS component: Eye to pain = 2, Verbal incomprehensible = 2, Motor flexion = 3 (Total = 7). Recall the foundational emergency axiom: GCS ≤ 8 demands immediate airway protection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse cares for a client who suffered a brainstem infarction. During the initial cranial nerve assessment, the nurse notes asymmetric uvular elevation, absence of a gag reflex, and a hoarse, wet voice. Which nursing action is the highest priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Provide thin liquids through a straw in order to assess independent swallowing function"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the client on strict NPO status and keep emergency suction at the bedside"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Position the client flat on their back to facilitate bilateral airway drainage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Encourage frequent sips of ice water to stimulate thermal vocal cord reflexes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Place the client on strict NPO status and keep emergency suction at the bedside" Impairment of cranial nerves IX (glossopharyngeal) and X (vagus) compromises pharyngeal motor and sensory innervation, abolishing the gag reflex and causing dysphagia with high risk of silent pulmonary aspiration. The highest nursing priority is maintaining strict NPO status until a formal swallow assessment is performed and ensuring functional bedside suction is immediately available. Thin liquids through a straw significantly increase aspiration risk. A flat supine position impairs clearance of secretions and promotes aspiration. Why the other options fail: Thin liquids through a straw are the most likely to be aspirated, not a test of swallowing; lying flat encourages aspiration rather than drainage; ice water sips do not test or protect a compromised pharyngeal swallow. Nil by mouth with suction ready is the safe response.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Relate the cranial nerves to clinical safety: Cranial nerves IX and X protect the airway and swallow reflex. Absence of gag reflex = immediate NPO + suction ready.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$While assessing a client with severe closed head trauma in the intensive care unit, the nurse applies a painful peripheral stimulus. The client rigidly extends both arms, pronates the forearms, flexes the wrists downward, and extends the legs with plantar flexion. How should the nurse document and interpret this finding?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Decorticate posturing, indicating a superficial cerebral hemisphere cortical lesion"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Purposive withdrawal response, indicating intact spinal reflexes and gradually improving cognition"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Decerebrate posturing, indicating deeper, severe dysfunction of the midbrain or brainstem"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Positive Babinski sign, indicating an isolated lower motor neuron peripheral injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Decerebrate posturing, indicating deeper, severe dysfunction of the midbrain or brainstem" Decerebrate (extensor) posturing is characterized by rigid extension and adduction of the arms, pronation of the forearms, flexion of the wrists/fingers, and plantar extension of the feet. It signifies severe injury or herniation affecting the midbrain or upper brainstem and carries a worse prognosis than decorticate (flexor) posturing, where arms flex inward toward the chest ('core'). It is an ominous clinical sign requiring immediate physician notification. Why the other options fail: Decorticate posturing is flexion of the arms with internal rotation, which is a hemispheric lesion rather than this picture; purposive withdrawal means the client localises or withdraws meaningfully, which is not what extension and pronation show; a Babinski sign is a plantar response and does not describe the limbs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Differentiate posturing: Decorticate brings hands to the 'cord/core' (flexion); Decerebrate has lots of 'e's for Extension away from the body, indicating midbrain/brainstem injury.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who sustained a severe traumatic brain injury 24 hours ago is monitored in the neuro-intensive care unit. Over the last 30 minutes, the nurse notes the client's blood pressure has changed from 132/76 mm Hg to 184/60 mm Hg, heart rate has decreased from 78 to 46 beats/min, and respirations have become irregular and shallow at 10 breaths/min. Which emergency condition does the nurse recognize?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Neurogenic shock requiring immediate rapid crystalloid fluid resuscitation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Spinal shock characterized by complete flaccidity and a transient hyperdynamic circulation with a wide pulse pressure"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Autonomic dysreflexia triggered by uninhibited sympathetic outflow from fecal impaction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cushing's triad, signaling life-threatening elevated intracranial pressure and impending brain herniation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Cushing's triad, signaling life-threatening elevated intracranial pressure and impending brain herniation" Cushing's triad is a classic, late physiological response to significantly increased intracranial pressure (ICP) and impending brainstem herniation. It consists of: (1) severe systolic hypertension with a widening pulse pressure (184/60 represents a pulse pressure of 124 mm Hg), (2) profound bradycardia (HR 46 beats/min), and (3) irregular respirations (such as Cheyne-Stokes breathing). It requires emergency interventions: elevating the head of the bed 30 degrees with head midline, hyperosmolar therapy (mannitol or 3% hypertonic saline), and urgent neurosurgical consultation. Neurogenic shock presents with hypotension, not hypertension. Why the other options fail: Neurogenic shock gives hypotension with bradycardia and warm dry skin, not a rising pressure with a widening pulse; autonomic dysreflexia occurs in spinal cord injury above T6 and is triggered by a noxious stimulus; spinal shock is flaccidity with absent reflexes, not a hyperdynamic circulation. The pattern is Cushing's triad.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Memorize Cushing's triad: Hypertension with wide pulse pressure + Bradycardia + Irregular respirations. Contrast this with shock (hypotension + tachycardia).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 22-year-old cyclist who was not wearing a helmet struck a concrete barrier. At the scene, the cyclist experienced a brief loss of consciousness for 2 minutes, woke up completely oriented, and declined emergency medical transport. Three hours later at home, the client developed a severe headache, nausea, and acute left pupil dilation with right-sided hemiparesis. What traumatic intracranial bleed should the nurse immediately suspect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chronic subdural hematoma involving slow bridging cortical venous bleeding"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Subarachnoid hemorrhage secondary to a ruptured anterior communicating aneurysm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Epidural hematoma involving rapid middle meningeal arterial laceration"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Diffuse axonal injury with microscopic shearing of white matter tracts"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Epidural hematoma involving rapid middle meningeal arterial laceration" The hallmark clinical presentation of an epidural hematoma is a brief initial loss of consciousness, followed by a temporary period of lucidity ('lucid interval'), followed by rapid neurological collapse as the arterial hematoma expands within the tight skull cavity. Epidural bleeds typically result from a temporal bone fracture lacerating the middle meningeal artery. The expanding high-pressure arterial bleed leads to uncal herniation, presenting with an ipsilateral dilated, fixed pupil (compression of CN III) and contralateral hemiparesis. It is a surgical emergency requiring immediate emergent craniotomy/burr hole decompression. Why the other options fail: A chronic subdural develops over weeks from venous bleeding in an older client; a subarachnoid haemorrhage from an aneurysm gives a sudden thunderclap headache without a lucid interval; diffuse axonal injury gives immediate and prolonged coma with no lucid interval. A brief loss of consciousness followed by a lucid interval is the epidural signature.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the buzzword phrase: 'brief loss of consciousness followed by a lucid interval and rapid collapse' = Epidural hematoma (arterial, middle meningeal artery).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who sustained facial and basilar skull trauma has clear, watery drainage trickling from the left nostril. Which action by the nurse is safe and indicated?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insert a nasogastric tube to decompress the stomach and prevent vomiting"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect the drainage on a sterile gauze pad to inspect for a classic halo ring sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Instruct the client to blow their nose forcefully into a sterile specimen container"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pack the left nostril tightly with sterile petroleum gauze to tamponade fluid leakage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Collect the drainage on a sterile gauze pad to inspect for a classic halo ring sign" Clear fluid draining from the nose (rhinorrhea) or ears (otorrhea) following basilar skull fracture is cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) leaking through a dural tear. The nurse should collect the drainage on a sterile gauze; if CSF is mixed with blood, the blood coalesces in the center and a yellowish ring forms around the perimeter ('halo sign' or 'target sign'). A chemical strip can also check for glucose (CSF contains glucose, unlike nasal mucus). Nasogastric tubes, nasal packing, and forceful nose-blowing are strictly contraindicated because they can increase intracranial pressure, introduce bacteria directly into the subarachnoid space causing meningitis, or penetrate the cribriform plate into the brain. Why the other options fail: A nasogastric tube can pass through a fractured cribriform plate into the brain; packing the nostril traps contaminated fluid and invites meningitis; blowing the nose forces air and bacteria into the cranial cavity. Inspecting the drainage for a halo sign is safe and diagnostic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rule out interventions that violate dural tear safety: never pack the nose, never insert NG tubes in basilar skull fracture, and never have the client blow their nose. Test for CSF with the halo sign or glucose strip.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An emergency department nurse prepares to initiate intravenous alteplase (tissue plasminogen activator [tPA]) for a client with an acute ischemic stroke whose symptoms started 2 hours ago. On reassessment prior to starting the infusion, the client's blood pressure is 196/114 mm Hg. Which action must the nurse take first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Start the alteplase infusion immediately so that the strict three-hour therapeutic window is not missed while the pressure is managed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer prescribed intravenous antihypertensive therapy (e.g., labetalol or nicardipine) to reduce blood pressure below 185/110 mm Hg before infusion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cancel the alteplase infusion permanently, because a blood pressure at this level is regarded as an absolute contraindication to thrombolysis in acute stroke"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give subcutaneous heparin to provide alternative anticoagulation while the alteplase decision is deferred until the stroke team reviews"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Administer prescribed intravenous antihypertensive therapy (e.g., labetalol or nicardipine) to reduce blood pressure below 185/110 mm Hg before infusion" To safely administer IV alteplase for acute ischemic stroke, blood pressure must be maintained below 185/110 mm Hg before starting the infusion and below 180/105 mm Hg for at least 24 hours after infusion. Uncontrolled hypertension during thrombolysis dramatically increases the risk of catastrophic intracranial hemorrhage. The nurse should promptly administer prescribed IV antihypertensives (such as labetalol or nicardipine). Alteplase is not cancelled unless the blood pressure cannot be maintained below 185/110 mm Hg despite treatment. Why the other options fail: Infusing into a pressure above the threshold raises the bleeding risk and breaches protocol; cancelling permanently is wrong, because the pressure can be lowered and the drug then given; heparin adds bleeding risk and does not treat the pressure. The pressure is brought down first and the infusion follows.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the exact BP parameter for tPA in acute stroke: Blood pressure must be < 185/110 mm Hg before starting thrombolytic therapy. If elevated, treat BP first, then infuse tPA.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client is admitted to the neurological step-down unit following a subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) caused by a ruptured intracranial berry aneurysm. The healthcare provider prescribes oral nimodipine 60 mg every 4 hours. The client asks the nurse why this medication is necessary. What is the nurse's best explanation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It dissolves the microscopic blood clots that have formed within the arachnoid villi, so that the circulation of cerebrospinal fluid is restored"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It stimulates the bone marrow to produce clotting factors, which then seal the dome of the ruptured aneurysm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It prevents and relieves cerebral arterial vasospasm to protect brain tissue from secondary ischemic injury."}, {"id": "d", "text": "It acts as a potent osmotic diuretic that draws excess fluid out of the brain parenchyma and into the circulation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "It prevents and relieves cerebral arterial vasospasm to protect brain tissue from secondary ischemic injury." Subarachnoid hemorrhage exposes cerebral arteries to free hemoglobin and breakdown products, which frequently triggers severe cerebral arterial vasospasm between days 3 and 14 post-bleed, leading to secondary ischemic brain damage or stroke. Nimodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with high lipophilicity that selectively relaxes cerebral vasculature, preventing and treating delayed cerebral ischemia from vasospasm. It does not dissolve clots, seal aneurysms, or act as an osmotic diuretic. Why the other options fail: Nimodipine does not dissolve clot in the arachnoid villi, stimulate clotting factor production, or act as an osmotic diuretic. It is a calcium channel blocker given after subarachnoid haemorrhage to prevent the delayed cerebral vasospasm that causes secondary ischaemia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate nimodipine specifically with subarachnoid hemorrhage: Nimodipine = prevention of cerebral vasospasm and secondary ischemic deficits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse assists with feeding a client recovering from a right hemispheric ischemic stroke who has mild left-sided facial weakness and dysphagia. Which nursing intervention most effectively minimizes the client's risk of pulmonary aspiration during oral intake?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Direct the client to flex their neck and tuck their chin toward the chest when swallowing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client to tilt their head backward and chin upward while swallowing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client in a semi-Fowler position at a 30-degree incline"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Provide liquids through a flexible drinking straw placed on the weak, affected left side of the mouth"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Direct the client to flex their neck and tuck their chin toward the chest when swallowing" The chin-tuck maneuver (flexing the neck and tucking the chin toward the chest during swallowing) widens the vallecular space and pushes the epiglottis backward over the laryngeal opening, protecting the airway and directing food safely into the esophagus. Clients should sit upright at 90 degrees (high-Fowler) during meals and for at least 30 minutes afterward. Tilting the head back opens the airway and drastically increases aspiration risk. Drinking straws and placing food on the weak/affected side both promote pocketing and aspiration. Why the other options fail: A thirty-degree semi-Fowler position is too low and does not protect the airway; tilting the head back and lifting the chin opens the airway for breathing but is the opposite of a safe swallow; a straw on the weak side delivers fluid where sensation and control are poorest. A chin tuck protects the airway during the swallow.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the protective biomechanics of swallowing: Chin-tuck closes the trachea and widens the esophagus. Sitting upright at 90° with food placed on the unaffected side.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse witnesses a hospitalized client suddenly cry out, lose consciousness, and develop generalized stiffening of all extremities followed by violent rhythmic muscle contractions. What is the nurse's priority action during the active convulsive phase?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Force a padded wooden tongue blade between the teeth in order to protect the tongue from lacerations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Turn the client onto their side and remove hard or sharp objects from the immediate vicinity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrain the client's extremities firmly to the bed rails to prevent orthopedic fractures"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer 50 mL of oral glucose solution to reverse potential acute hypoglycemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Turn the client onto their side and remove hard or sharp objects from the immediate vicinity" During an active generalized tonic-clonic seizure, the priority nursing interventions are airway maintenance and physical safety: turn the client onto their side (lateral recovery position) to allow saliva/emesis to drain and prevent tongue occlusion of the airway, protect the head, loosen constrictive clothing, and remove hard or dangerous objects. Never force anything into the client's mouth (can cause dental fractures, aspiration, or airway obstruction), never forcefully restrain limbs (increases risk of bone fractures and tendon tears), and never give oral substances to an unconscious client. Why the other options fail: A tongue blade forced between the teeth can break teeth and obstruct the airway; restraining the limbs against the rails risks fracture and does not stop the seizure; oral glucose cannot be swallowed safely during a convulsion. Turning the client and clearing the area protects the airway and the head.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Follow seizure safety ABCs: Turn on side, clear hazards, never insert anything into mouth, never restrain extremities.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse cares for a client who is experiencing continuous generalized tonic-clonic seizure activity lasting 7 minutes without regaining consciousness. The client is in status epilepticus. After securing oxygen and establishing intravenous access, which medication should the nurse anticipate administering first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous lorazepam bolus"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Oral carbamazepine suspension via feeding tube"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subcutaneous regular insulin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intramuscular morphine sulfate"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Intravenous lorazepam bolus" Status epilepticus is a medical emergency defined as continuous seizure activity lasting 5 minutes or longer, or recurrent seizures without regaining baseline consciousness between episodes. The first-line pharmacological treatment is a rapid-acting IV benzodiazepine—specifically intravenous lorazepam (or IV diazepam) to rapidly terminate neuronal hyperexcitability by enhancing GABA activity. Once the seizure is aborted, a longer-acting antiepileptic drug (e.g., levetiracetam, fosphenytoin, or valproate) is infused to prevent seizure recurrence. Oral medications cannot be administered during active status epilepticus. Why the other options fail: An oral suspension through a feeding tube is far too slow for status epilepticus and absorption is unreliable; insulin has no role; intramuscular morphine neither stops seizures nor acts quickly enough. A benzodiazepine given intravenously is the first-line termination.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the status epilepticus drug ladder: Benzodiazepine (IV lorazepam) FIRST to stop the seizure, followed by longer-acting antiepileptics (levetiracetam/fosphenytoin) to keep it stopped.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with newly diagnosed epilepsy is prescribed oral phenytoin 100 mg three times daily. When providing discharge education, which instruction should the nurse emphasize to prevent a common adverse effect and ensure therapeutic safety?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop taking the medication immediately as soon as you have been seizure-free for 7 consecutive days."}, {"id": "b", "text": "Practice meticulous daily flossing and soft-bristle toothbrushing to reduce gingival hyperplasia."}, {"id": "c", "text": "Double your dose if you experience morning drowsiness or mild coordination changes."}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict your dietary intake of calcium and vitamin D to avoid medication binding."}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Practice meticulous daily flossing and soft-bristle toothbrushing to reduce gingival hyperplasia." Gingival hyperplasia (overgrowth of gum tissue) is a frequent and well-documented adverse effect of long-term phenytoin therapy, occurring in up to 50% of clients. Meticulous dental hygiene, regular brushing with a soft toothbrush, daily flossing, and routine dental visits significantly minimize gum overgrowth. Phenytoin must never be abruptly discontinued because sudden withdrawal can precipitate status epilepticus. Drowsiness and ataxia are signs of phenytoin toxicity (therapeutic level is 10–20 mcg/mL), requiring provider notification and serum level testing rather than dose escalation. Why the other options fail: Stopping the drug when seizure-free withdraws the treatment and invites recurrence; doubling the dose for drowsiness increases toxicity rather than managing it; restricting calcium and vitamin D does not prevent binding and risks bone loss. Gum overgrowth is the adverse effect to watch for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Connect phenytoin with its signature adverse effect: Phenytoin = gingival hyperplasia (good oral hygiene) + narrow therapeutic range (10-20 mcg/mL).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 19-year-old university student is admitted with a high fever (39.5°C [103.1°F]), severe photophobia, nuchal rigidity, and positive Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs. Bacterial meningitis is suspected. Which clinical action must the nurse implement first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer oral antipyretics and dim the room lights"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transport the client to the radiology department for a non-contrast brain CT scan"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on droplet transmission precautions in a private room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prepare the client for a diagnostic lumbar puncture before initiating any isolation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Place the client on droplet transmission precautions in a private room" Bacterial meningitis (commonly caused by Neisseria meningitidis or Streptococcus pneumoniae) is highly contagious and transmitted via respiratory droplets. The immediate priority upon clinical suspicion is instituting droplet transmission precautions (private room, surgical mask for healthcare personnel within 3 feet, client masked during transport) to prevent hospital outbreaks. Once isolation is secured, blood cultures should be drawn, followed immediately by empiric broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and dexamethasone—without delaying therapy for lumbar puncture or CT imaging if delays occur. Why the other options fail: A CT scan and a lumbar puncture are both needed, but neither comes before source isolation; antipyretics and dim lights are comfort measures; a lumbar puncture performed before isolation exposes staff and other clients. Droplet precautions come first.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Prioritize infection control: When suspected infectious airborne or droplet disease enters the hospital, isolation precautions come first before diagnostic workups.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a comatose client in the intensive care unit who has lost the spontaneous corneal blink reflex and whose eyelids remain partially open. Which nursing intervention is essential to prevent permanent visual impairment?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer systemic ophthalmic mydriatic eye drops to constrict pupils"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Irrigate both eyes vigorously with sterile tap water every 2 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply high-flow humidified blow-by oxygen directly over the client's open orbits throughout the shift"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Instill prescribed preservative-free artificial tear drops and apply protective eye shields"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Instill prescribed preservative-free artificial tear drops and apply protective eye shields" In an unconscious or comatose client with incomplete eyelid closure (lagophthalmos) and absent corneal reflex, the corneal surface dries out rapidly, predisposing the client to corneal abrasions, ulcerations, scarring, and permanent blindness. The nurse must instill prescribed artificial tear drops during the day, apply lubricating ophthalmic ointment at night, and cover the eyes with sterile moisture chambers or protective eye patches. Tap water irrigation causes corneal irritation and introduces waterborne pathogens. Blowing oxygen directly onto the eyes exacerbates corneal desiccation. Why the other options fail: Tap water is not sterile and repeated irrigation injures the cornea; oxygen blown over open orbits dries them further; mydriatic drops dilate the pupil and do not close the lids or moisten the eye. Artificial tears with protective shields address the exposure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the vulnerability: Loss of blink reflex leaves the cornea exposed to drying. Essential prevention = artificial tears and eye lubrication/shielding.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with a complete T4 spinal cord injury who has been hospitalized for 3 weeks suddenly reports a throbbing, pounding headache and blurred vision. The nurse notes the client's blood pressure is 214/110 mm Hg, heart rate is 50 beats/min, and the skin above T4 is flushed and drenched in sweat while the lower extremities are pale and cold. Which action should the nurse take first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give an extra intravenous bolus of normal saline to raise the blood pressure and relieve the headache"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the client in the Trendelenburg position so that venous return to the heart and brain is improved"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Immediately elevate the head of the bed to 90 degrees (high-Fowler) and loosen tight clothing"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain a twelve-lead electrocardiogram and send blood for cardiac enzymes before doing anything else"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Immediately elevate the head of the bed to 90 degrees (high-Fowler) and loosen tight clothing" This client is exhibiting textbook signs of autonomic dysreflexia (hyperreflexia), an acute, life-threatening medical emergency occurring in spinal cord injury at or above T6. Noxious stimuli below the lesion (most commonly bladder distension/blocked Foley or bowel impaction) trigger massive uninhibited sympathetic discharge, causing severe vasoconstriction, dangerous hypertension, and diaphoresis above the injury, while the intact vagal nerve causes reflexive bradycardia. The immediate first action is to sit the client upright at 90 degrees (high-Fowler) with legs dangling if possible (to induce orthostatic blood pooling and lower intracranial arterial pressure), loosen all constricting garments, and immediately check the urinary catheter for kinks or distension. Why the other options fail: Trendelenburg worsens the raised pressure the dysreflexia has produced; a fluid bolus does not remove the triggering stimulus; an ECG and enzymes investigate the wrong problem. Sitting the client upright lowers the pressure while the cause, usually a full bladder or bowel, is found and relieved.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the first action in autonomic dysreflexia: Sit them UP (90 degrees) to lower BP gravitationally, loosen tight clothes, and search for the trigger (check bladder/Foley first).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client admitted following a cervical spine injury at C6 presents with a blood pressure of 82/44 mm Hg, heart rate of 48 beats/min, respiratory rate of 16 breaths/min, and extremities that are warm and dry to the touch. How should the nurse distinguish this condition from hypovolemic shock?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Neurogenic shock causes loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone, leading to vasodilation, warm skin, and bradycardia"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypovolemic shock produces marked bradycardia with dry skin, whereas this client is displaying true neurogenic shock"}, {"id": "c", "text": "This condition represents septic shock caused by immediate wound infection at the spinal injury site"}, {"id": "d", "text": "This client is experiencing cardiogenic shock caused by acute anterior myocardial infarction"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Neurogenic shock causes loss of sympathetic vasomotor tone, leading to vasodilation, warm skin, and bradycardia" Neurogenic shock results from disruption of descending sympathetic outflow in cervical or high-thoracic spinal cord injuries (T6 and above). The unopposed parasympathetic tone produces profound systemic vasodilation (relative hypovolemia), venous pooling, warm and dry extremities (due to vasodilation rather than peripheral vasoconstriction), hypotension, and bradycardia (loss of sympathetic cardiac accelerator fibers). In contrast, hypovolemic and cardiogenic shock present with compensatory tachycardia, cool, clammy extremities, and delayed capillary refill due to intense peripheral vasoconstriction. Why the other options fail: Hypovolaemic shock gives tachycardia and cool clammy skin, not bradycardia with warm dry skin; sepsis gives fever and warm skin with tachycardia; cardiogenic shock gives cool skin with pulmonary congestion. Loss of sympathetic tone gives vasodilation, bradycardia and warm dry skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contrast shock states: Classic hypovolemia = hypotension + tachycardia + cool/clammy skin. Neurogenic shock = hypotension + bradycardia + warm/dry skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse provides medication instructions to a client newly diagnosed with myasthenia gravis who is prescribed oral pyridostigmine. To promote safe nutrition and prevent aspiration, when should the nurse instruct the client to schedule their medication doses?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the medication 30 to 45 minutes before meals"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the medication immediately after completing large meals"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the medication right before going to sleep at night"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the medication only when feeling severe muscle exhaustion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Take the medication 30 to 45 minutes before meals" Pyridostigmine is an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that enhances neuromuscular transmission by preventing acetylcholine breakdown. In myasthenia gravis, chewing and swallowing muscles fatigue quickly. Administering pyridostigmine 30 to 45 minutes prior to meals ensures that the peak therapeutic effect coincides exactly with mealtime, maximizing muscle strength for chewing and swallowing and drastically reducing the risk of choking and aspiration. Taking it after meals leaves the client vulnerable to fatigue and aspiration during eating. Why the other options fail: Taking it after large meals delays the effect until the meal is over; taking it at bedtime leaves the client weakest in the morning; taking it only when exhausted means the drug is used as a rescue rather than to enable eating. Thirty to forty-five minutes before meals peaks the effect at mealtime.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Connect the timing to eating: Pyridostigmine must peak when the client is chewing. Peak onset is ~30-45 minutes; therefore, take 30-45 min BEFORE meals.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client hospitalized with Guillain-Barré syndrome has developed progressive ascending weakness that now involves the upper extremities and trunk. Which clinical assessment parameter is the most critical for the nurse to monitor serially to detect impending respiratory failure?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bilateral pupillary light reflexes and accommodation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hourly deep tendon reflex grading in the lower extremities"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Daily serum blood urea nitrogen and creatinine clearance"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Forced vital capacity (FVC) and negative inspiratory force"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Forced vital capacity (FVC) and negative inspiratory force" Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS) features acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy causing symmetric ascending paralysis. When the paralysis ascends to involve the intercostal nerves and diaphragm (phrenic nerve, C3–C5), respiratory failure can occur rapidly. Serial monitoring of bedside pulmonary mechanics—specifically forced vital capacity (FVC) and negative inspiratory force (NIF)—is the gold standard to detect diaphragmatic weakness before severe hypoxia occurs. An FVC falling below 15–20 mL/kg or NIF less negative than -20 cm H2O indicates urgent need for endotracheal intubation. Why the other options fail: Reflex grading tracks the neuropathy but says nothing about the respiratory failure that kills in Guillain-Barré; renal function is not the threatened system; pupillary reflexes do not predict respiratory decline. Vital capacity and inspiratory force are what decide when ventilation is needed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate Guillain-Barré with respiratory monitoring: GBS ascends; when it reaches the diaphragm, respiratory arrest threatens. Monitor forced vital capacity (FVC).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with cerebral edema following surgical evacuation of an acute intracranial hematoma is prescribed an intravenous infusion of 20% mannitol 100 g over 60 minutes. Which nursing intervention is essential during the preparation and administration of this medication?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Withhold all neurological assessments during the infusion in order to avoid any stimulation of the client"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the solution through a rapid IV push over 30 seconds without a pump"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Infuse the drug concurrently with 5% dextrose in water through the same primary line"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Warm the vial and inspect for microscopic crystals, and use a dedicated in-line IV filter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Warm the vial and inspect for microscopic crystals, and use a dedicated in-line IV filter" Mannitol is a potent hyperosmolar sugar alcohol that crystallizes readily at cooler room temperatures or higher concentrations (such as 20% or 25%). The nurse must inspect the vial carefully for crystals before administration; if crystals are present, the vial should be warmed and shaken until redissolved, then cooled to body temperature. An in-line IV filter (typically 5-micron) must always be used during administration to prevent microscopic crystals from entering the client's circulation and causing microembolism. Rapid IV push without an infusion device can cause acute circulatory overload and pulmonary edema. Why the other options fail: A rapid push without a pump risks a bolus that defeats the controlled osmotic effect; dextrose in the same line promotes crystallisation and incompatibility; withholding neurological assessments removes the very monitoring the infusion requires. Warming and filtering the solution prevents crystal infusion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the technical administration rule for mannitol: Mannitol crystallizes easily. Always inspect for crystals, warm if needed, and administer using an in-line IV filter.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A healthcare provider prescribes intravenous phenytoin 250 mg for a client experiencing cluster seizures. When preparing to administer this medication through a peripheral intravenous catheter, which nursing practice is mandatory to prevent cardiovascular collapse and catheter precipitation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dilute the phenytoin exclusively in 0.9% sodium chloride and infuse at a rate not exceeding 50 mg/min"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mix the phenytoin in 5% dextrose in water (D5W) and infuse as a rapid 1-minute bolus"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer the medication via continuous infusion over 24 hours without cardiac monitoring"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Flush the intravenous line with lactated Ringer's solution immediately before and immediately after the injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Dilute the phenytoin exclusively in 0.9% sodium chloride and infuse at a rate not exceeding 50 mg/min" Intravenous phenytoin is chemically incompatible with dextrose solutions (it will rapidly precipitate into insoluble crystals) and must only be mixed or flushed with 0.9% normal saline. Furthermore, rapid peripheral IV administration causes severe hypotension, sinus bradycardia, heart blocks, and fatal ventricular arrhythmias due to propylene glycol toxicity. Therefore, the maximum peripheral infusion rate is strictly limited to 50 mg/min in adults (often 25 mg/min in older adults), requiring continuous cardiac and blood pressure monitoring throughout the infusion. Why the other options fail: Dextrose makes phenytoin precipitate, and a one-minute bolus risks hypotension and arrhythmia; a twenty-four-hour infusion without monitoring ignores the maximum rate and the cardiac risk; lactated Ringer's is incompatible and will precipitate the drug. Normal saline at no more than 50 mg/min is correct.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the two inviolable rules of IV phenytoin: (1) Normal saline ONLY (never dextrose, which precipitates); (2) Never infuse faster than 50 mg/minute (prevents fatal arrhythmias/hypotension).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NEURO-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse performs serial neurovascular checks on a client who underwent open reduction and internal fixation (ORIF) of a closed tibial fracture 6 hours ago. Which clinical finding represents the earliest and most sensitive indicator of arterial and neurovascular compromise in the injured extremity?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Absent dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses with a cool foot"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mottled cyanosis and profound coldness spreading over the whole foot"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Total paralysis, with inability to dorsiflex the ankle on request"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Numbness and tingling with pain on passive extension of the toes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Numbness and tingling with pain on passive extension of the toes" In assessing the '6 Ps' of neurovascular compromise (Pain, Paresthesia, Pallor, Poikilothermia, Pulselessness, Paralysis), sensory changes such as paresthesia (burning, tingling, numbness) and intense pain exacerbated by passive stretch of the distal digits are the earliest and most reliable warning signs of ischemia and nerve compression. Pulselessness, cold pallor, and paralysis are late, ominous signs that indicate irreversible neuromuscular necrosis is already occurring. Why the other options fail: Pulselessness, mottled cyanosis with profound coldness, and total paralysis are all late signs that mean the limb is already at risk; none is the earliest finding, which is the sensory change.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the sequence of the 6 Ps: Pain and Paresthesia are EARLIEST; Pulselessness and Paralysis are LATE and catastrophic.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a client who had a wet plaster cast applied to the right lower leg 30 minutes ago. Which technique should the nurse employ when moving and positioning the casted extremity while it is still damp?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Grasp the cast firmly with the fingertips so that it cannot slip during turns"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Handle the damp cast on the flat palms of open hands, never the fingertips"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cover the damp cast with heavy wool blankets to speed up the curing process"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Play a hot hairdryer over the cast surface to dry it quickly from outside"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Handle the damp cast on the flat palms of open hands, never the fingertips" A wet plaster cast takes 24 to 72 hours to dry completely. While wet, handling the cast with fingertips can cause localized indentations on the inside surface of the plaster, which creates severe internal pressure points and decubitus ulceration over underlying bony prominences. The nurse must handle damp casts using only the flat, open palms of both hands. Covering a wet plaster cast with blankets traps heat and moisture, while heated blow dryers can cause thermal skin burns beneath the cast. Why the other options fail: Grasping with the fingertips presses dents into damp plaster; heavy blankets trap heat and moisture against it; a hot hairdryer can burn the skin beneath the cast.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember cast handling basics: Palms ONLY for damp plaster casts (prevents indentations and pressure ulcers). Allow to air dry uncovered.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a frail older adult client with a fractured femoral neck who is placed in Buck's skin traction awaiting hip arthroplasty tomorrow morning. Which nursing action is essential to maintain effective therapeutic traction?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Remove the weights for thirty minutes every two hours to let muscles relax"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Rest the weights securely on a stool at the foot of the bed between turns"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Raise the foot of the bed to ninety degrees to increase counter-traction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The weights hang free in mid-air, clear of the floor and the bed frame"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The weights hang free in mid-air, clear of the floor and the bed frame" Buck's traction is a temporary skin traction used to immobilize the fracture, prevent muscle spasms, and relieve pain prior to surgical repair. For traction to remain continuous and effective, the weights must hang freely in mid-air off the floor and away from the bed frame at all times. Resting weights on the floor or a stool eliminates the traction pull entirely, triggering excruciating muscle spasms and fracture displacement. Traction weights must never be removed or interrupted without a specific medical prescription. Why the other options fail: Removing the weights interrupts the pull and allows spasm and displacement; resting them on a stool takes the tension off the rope; raising the foot of the bed to ninety degrees is neither counter-traction nor a safe position.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Key rule of all traction: Weights must always HANG FREELY. Never set weights on the floor, bed, or stool.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with an unstable comminuted femur fracture is admitted in balanced suspension skeletal traction with a Thomas splint and Pearson attachment. During morning rounds, the nurse inspects the distal femoral skeletal pin sites. Which finding requires prompt escalation and nursing intervention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Purulent, foul-smelling drainage with induration and warmth at the pin site"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A small amount of clear serous crusting around the pin entry site"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The weights hanging freely over the pulleys at the foot of the bed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Palpable dorsalis pedis pulses with a capillary refill of two seconds and warm toes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Purulent, foul-smelling drainage with induration and warmth at the pin site" Skeletal traction pins penetrate directly through the skin, soft tissues, and into the bone cortex, providing a direct conduit for microorganisms to cause life-threatening osteomyelitis. Purulent (pus-filled), foul-smelling drainage, erythema, localized induration, and tenderness indicate active pin-site infection that can rapidly progress to bone infection. This requires immediate wound cultures and provider notification. In contrast, slight serous (clear/straw-colored) crusting is a normal physiological exudate. Why the other options fail: A little clear serous crusting is normal healing; weights hanging free over the pulleys and palpable pulses with a two-second refill are the correct traction and perfusion state, so neither needs escalation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Distinguish normal from infected pin sites: Clear/serous crusting = normal healing; Purulent drainage + erythema/heat = infection (risk of osteomyelitis)!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with a newly applied short-arm fiberglass cast reports severe itching deep underneath the cast near the wrist. What instructions should the nurse give the client to relieve the itching safely?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Push a plastic knitting needle or a wire coat hanger down inside to scratch"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Direct a hairdryer on the cool setting down the opening of the cast"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pour baby powder or calamine lotion down inside the cast to soothe the skin"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Soak the casted arm in a tub of warm water for fifteen minutes each evening"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Direct a hairdryer on the cool setting down the opening of the cast" Inserting foreign objects (such as knitting needles, coat hangers, or pens) inside a cast breaks skin integrity, creating hidden lacerations that can become infected and cause severe cellulitis or abscesses beneath the cast. Powders and lotions cake, trap moisture, and promote maceration and bacterial growth. Directing air from a hairdryer on the COOL setting down into the cast provides safe, effective pruritus relief without damaging the skin or lining. Antihistamines may also be prescribed. Why the other options fail: A knitting needle or a wire hanger can break the skin where the wound cannot be seen; powder or lotion cakes and traps moisture against it; soaking softens the cast and macerates the skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cast safety rule: Never stick anything inside a cast! Use a blow dryer on COOL setting or prescribed oral antihistamines for itching.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who sustained a crush injury to the right lower leg reports severe, throbbing calf pain rated 10/10 that is unresponsive to repeated intravenous hydromorphone doses. On assessment, the calf is firm and tight, and the client screams in pain when the nurse gently flexes the foot upward (passive dorsiflexion). Which action by the nurse is indicated?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Elevate the leg high above heart level on four pillows and apply ice bags"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Start vigorous active range-of-motion exercises of the ankle to stretch the calf muscle"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give another dose of hydromorphone and reassess the leg in two hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the limb at heart level, loosen the dressings, and call the surgeon now"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Keep the limb at heart level, loosen the dressings, and call the surgeon now" This client is demonstrating classic hallmark signs of acute compartment syndrome: severe, unremitting pain out of proportion to the injury, tense wooden swelling, and extreme pain with passive stretching of the compartment muscles. Increased tissue pressure within an inelastic fascial space compromises perfusion. Immediate priorities: keep the limb AT HEART LEVEL (elevating above heart reduces arterial perfusion pressure; lowering below heart increases venous hydrostatic pressure), loosen external constrictive bandages/splints/casts (bivalving), and summon the surgeon immediately for emergency fasciotomy. Ice is contraindicated because it induces vasoconstriction. Why the other options fail: Raising the limb above the heart lowers arterial perfusion and ice constricts the vessels further; vigorous exercise works a compartment that is already under pressure; another opioid with a two-hour wait withholds the only intervention that saves the limb.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recognize acute compartment syndrome: Pain out of proportion + pain on passive stretch. Actions: Keep at heart level (NEVER elevate high), remove constrictions, call surgeon for fasciotomy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 21-year-old client who sustained a closed mid-shaft femur fracture 36 hours ago suddenly becomes restless, confused, and tachypneic. The nurse notes an SpO2 of 86% on room air, respiratory rate of 32 breaths/min, and pinpoint non-palpable petechial spots across the client's anterior chest, neck, and axillary folds. What clinical emergency should the nurse immediately recognize?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fat embolism syndrome, 36 hours after a femoral shaft fracture"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Deep vein thrombosis with localised venous stasis in the calf and mild ankle swelling"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute neurogenic shock from occult spinal cord injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bacterial sepsis from a hospital-acquired catheter infection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Fat embolism syndrome, 36 hours after a femoral shaft fracture" Fat embolism syndrome (FES) is a life-threatening complication occurring 24 to 72 hours after fractures of long bones (such as the femur or pelvis) or major orthopedic trauma. Fat globules released from the disrupted marrow canal embolize to the pulmonary vasculature and systemic microcirculation. FES presents with a classic clinical triad: (1) hypoxemia and respiratory distress (dyspnea, tachypnea, low SpO2), (2) neurological abnormalities (restlessness, confusion, altered mental status), and (3) a pathognomonic petechial rash distributed over the non-dependent skin of the neck, chest, axillae, and conjunctivae (due to microvascular fat occlusion and thrombocytopenia). Why the other options fail: Deep vein thrombosis gives local swelling without hypoxaemia or a rash; neurogenic shock gives hypotension and bradycardia, not the petechial rash; sepsis is possible in hospital but does not explain a rash appearing with the confusion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the diagnostic triad of Fat Embolism Syndrome: Long bone fracture + Hypoxemia + Confusion + Petechial rash on chest/axilla.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A factory worker suffers a traumatic complete amputation of the left index and middle fingers. When instructing first responders and preparing the severed digits for transport to the replantation center, how should the nurse handle the amputated parts?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Put the digits straight into a cup of crushed ice with dry ice"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Place the digits in a bowl of warm, sterile lactated Ringer solution"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Scrub the severed digits vigorously with concentrated chlorhexidine before transport to the centre"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wrap in saline-moistened gauze, seal in a bag, and float the bag in ice water"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Wrap in saline-moistened gauze, seal in a bag, and float the bag in ice water" Proper preservation of amputated digits is critical for successful microvascular replantation. The severed part should be rinsed gently with sterile saline if grossly contaminated, wrapped in sterile gauze moistened with normal saline (never soaking wet), placed into a waterproof, sealed plastic bag, and then placed into an insulated container filled with a slurry of ice and water (cooling to ~4°C). The part must NEVER be placed directly on ice (causes freezing injury and tissue death) or submerged directly in water (causes severe tissue edema and cellular lysis). Why the other options fail: Direct contact with ice or dry ice freezes and kills the tissue; warm Ringer soaks the part and causes oedema and cell lysis; concentrated chlorhexidine is caustic to tissue that must survive to be replanted.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the 3-step rule for severed parts: (1) Wrap in moist saline gauze; (2) Put in plastic bag; (3) Put bag on ice-water slurry. Never put directly on ice or in water.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 2-month-old infant is diagnosed with developmental dysplasia of the hip (DDH) and fitted with a Pavlik harness. When providing education to the infant's parents regarding harness maintenance and skin safety, which instruction is correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Adjust the buckles and strap lengths at home whenever the infant seems uncomfortable"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wear it 23 hours a day, over a soft undershirt and knee socks, and never adjust it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the harness off whenever the infant is awake and playing on the floor"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rub thick scented lotion and baby powder under the straps twice a day"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Wear it 23 hours a day, over a soft undershirt and knee socks, and never adjust it" A Pavlik harness maintains the infant's hips in flexion and abduction to seat the femoral head deeply into the acetabulum. The harness is typically worn continuously for 23 hours a day (only removed briefly for bathing if authorized). To prevent skin breakdown from friction, parents should dress the infant in a thin, soft cotton undershirt and knee socks beneath the straps. The straps should NEVER be adjusted by parents; adjustments must only be made by the healthcare provider to ensure correct anatomical alignment. Powders and lotions promote skin maceration and irritation. Why the other options fail: Only the treating team may change the alignment, so home adjustment is unsafe; removing the harness whenever the infant is awake abandons the position it exists to hold; lotions and powders macerate the skin under the straps.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pavlik harness rules: Worn 23 hours/day, undershirt/socks under straps, parents NEVER adjust straps, no powders/lotions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman undergoes a dual-energy x-ray absorptiometry (DXA) scan that reveals a femoral neck bone mineral density T-score of -2.8. How should the nurse interpret this result, and which non-pharmacological lifestyle recommendation should the nurse prioritize?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Normal bone density; recommend high-impact aerobic jumping classes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Osteoporosis at a T-score of -2.8; weight-bearing exercise and fall-proofing her home"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Osteopenia; recommend strict and prolonged bed rest to avoid any further microfractures of the hip"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe osteoarthritis; recommend avoiding all dairy and calcium-rich foods"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Osteoporosis at a T-score of -2.8; weight-bearing exercise and fall-proofing her home" According to World Health Organization criteria, a DXA T-score of -2.5 or lower defines osteoporosis (T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 defines osteopenia; -1.0 or higher is normal). For clients with osteoporosis, regular weight-bearing exercises (e.g., walking, stair climbing) stimulate bone osteoblast activity and remodeling. Concurrently, home safety and fall prevention (removing loose throw rugs, installing grab bars, adequate night lighting) are crucial to prevent devastating fragility fractures (e.g., hip or vertebral compression fractures). Why the other options fail: A T-score below minus two point five is osteoporosis, not normal, and impact loading is unsafe on fragile bone; calling it osteopenia and prescribing bed rest accelerates bone loss; this is not osteoarthritis, and avoiding dairy removes the calcium the skeleton needs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall T-score cutoffs: T-score ≤ -2.5 = Osteoporosis. Management: Weight-bearing exercise + Fall hazard elimination (throw rugs, lighting) + Calcium/Vitamin D.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A school nurse performs a spinal screening on a 13-year-old adolescent. While performing the Adams forward bend test, which assessment finding is most indicative of structural adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Symmetrical horizontal alignment of the posterior ribs and both scapulae"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Exaggerated anterior convexity of the cervical spine on forward bending"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bilateral sacral dimples with a tuft of hair over the lower sacrum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A unilateral rib hump with paraspinal asymmetry on forward bending"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A unilateral rib hump with paraspinal asymmetry on forward bending" The Adams forward bend test is the standard screening assessment for scoliosis: the adolescent bends forward at the waist with knees straight and arms hanging loosely downward. In structural scoliosis, rotational vertebral deformities cause the ribs to project posteriorly on the convex side of the curve, producing a noticeable unilateral rib hump and asymmetrical scapular elevation. Symmetrical ribs are normal. Why the other options fail: Symmetrical posterior ribs and scapulae describe a normal spine; exaggerated cervical convexity is a postural finding, not the rotational deformity of scoliosis; sacral dimples with a tuft of hair are a cutaneous marker of occult spinal dysraphism, which this test does not show.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate Adams forward bend test with scoliosis: Forward bending reveals rib hump and asymmetric paraspinal elevation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a client on postoperative day 1 following a total hip arthroplasty performed via a posterior surgical approach. To prevent acute prosthetic hip dislocation, which postoperative nursing intervention must be enforced?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An abduction pillow in bed, with hip flexion kept under 90 degrees"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the operative leg adducted, and cross the ankles while lying in bed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Provide a low, soft lounge chair for the client to sit in during the day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Let the client bend at the waist to tie their own shoes before discharge"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "An abduction pillow in bed, with hip flexion kept under 90 degrees" Following a posterior approach total hip arthroplasty, the posterior hip capsule is disrupted, creating a high risk of prosthetic dislocation if the hip enters flexion, adduction, or internal rotation. Precautions include: (1) using an abduction wedge pillow between the legs to maintain abduction, (2) avoiding hip flexion past 90 degrees (using raised toilet seats and straight-back firm chairs, avoiding bending at the waist), and (3) avoiding internal rotation or crossing legs/ankles. Low soft chairs cause extreme hip flexion (>90°) and are strictly contraindicated. Why the other options fail: Adducting the leg and crossing the ankles is the movement that dislocates a posterior-approach hip; a low soft chair flexes the hip past ninety degrees; bending at the waist to tie shoes does the same while standing.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the hip dislocation triad for posterior hip replacement: No flexion > 90°, No crossing legs (adduction), No internal rotation. Use abduction pillow!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse compares the clinical features of rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and osteoarthritis (OA) during a clinical conference. Which constellation of symptoms specifically characterizes rheumatoid arthritis rather than osteoarthritis?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Asymmetrical wear of weight-bearing joints, with stiffness lasting under thirty minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Symmetrical inflammation, systemic fatigue, and morning stiffness over an hour"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Isolated crepitus in the knees, which worsens progressively with activity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Heberden and Bouchard nodes at the distal interphalangeal joints, without fever"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Symmetrical inflammation, systemic fatigue, and morning stiffness over an hour" Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a systemic, autoimmune inflammatory disease characterized by symmetric peripheral joint involvement (wrists, MCP, PIP joints), prolonged morning stiffness lasting greater than 1 hour (which loosens and improves with movement), and systemic symptoms (fatigue, low-grade fever, anorexia, weight loss). In contrast, osteoarthritis (OA) is a degenerative 'wear-and-tear' non-inflammatory disease characterized by asymmetric involvement of weight-bearing joints, brief morning stiffness (< 30 minutes), pain that worsens with activity and improves with rest, and bony nodes (Heberden's and Bouchard's). Why the other options fail: Asymmetrical wear of weight-bearing joints with stiffness under thirty minutes, isolated crepitus that worsens with activity, and Heberden and Bouchard nodes without fever all describe osteoarthritis, where the joint is mechanically worn rather than symmetrically inflamed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Compare RA vs OA: RA = Symmetrical, Autoimmune, Morning stiffness > 1 hour, systemic signs. OA = Asymmetrical, Degenerative, Morning stiffness < 30 min, worse with use.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client presents with an exquisitely painful, hot, swollen, and red right great toe (podagra). Serum uric acid is elevated at 9.6 mg/dL, confirming acute gouty arthritis. When providing nutritional and lifestyle teaching to prevent future gouty flare-ups, which instruction should the nurse emphasize?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase daily red meat, liver and shellfish, which are good sources of protein"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take high-dose aspirin every day to push uric acid out through the kidneys"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Two to three litres of water daily, and no alcohol, especially beer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Cut fluid intake to one litre a day to stop fluid collecting around the joints"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Two to three litres of water daily, and no alcohol, especially beer" Gout is caused by hyperuricemia and precipitation of monosodium urate crystals in synovial joints. High fluid intake of 2 to 3 liters daily maintains adequate urinary volume to promote uric acid excretion and prevent kidney stone (nephrolithiasis) formation. Purine-rich foods (organ meats, red meat, sardines, anchovies, shellfish) break down into uric acid and should be strictly avoided. Alcohol, particularly beer, increases uric acid production while reducing renal excretion. Aspirin should be avoided because salicylates interfere with renal uric acid excretion and can precipitate flare-ups. Why the other options fail: Red meat, liver and shellfish raise purine intake and therefore uric acid; high-dose aspirin retains urate rather than clearing it; cutting fluids reduces the urine volume that clears urate and invites stones.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gout lifestyle essentials: Drink 2-3 L fluids/day (flushes uric acid) + Avoid high-purine foods (organ meats, shellfish) + Avoid beer/alcohol.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with type 2 diabetes mellitus is admitted with acute hematogenous osteomyelitis of the right calcaneus. What is the standard medical and nursing management required to treat this deep bone infection effectively?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A three-day course of oral cephalexin, taken at home after discharge"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Continuous high-temperature wet packs to the heel, without any antibiotics"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cultures first, then four to six weeks of targeted intravenous antibiotics"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Immediate amputation of the foot, without attempting medical therapy first"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Cultures first, then four to six weeks of targeted intravenous antibiotics" Bone is relatively avascular compared to soft tissues, making antibiotic penetration difficult. Osteomyelitis (commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus) requires obtaining blood and bone/wound cultures first, followed by an aggressive, prolonged course of targeted intravenous antibiotics lasting 4 to 6 weeks or longer. A peripherally inserted central catheter (PICC line) is typically placed for home or outpatient IV antibiotic infusions. Short-course oral therapy is inadequate and leads to chronic osteomyelitis, bone necrosis (sequestrum), and amputation. Why the other options fail: Three days of oral cephalexin is far too short to sterilise bone; wet packs without an antibiotic leave the infection untreated; amputation is not the first step before medical therapy has been tried.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recognize osteomyelitis therapy duration: Bone infection requires long-term (4 to 6 weeks) IV antibiotics, usually via a PICC line.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with a history of chronic lower back pain from a herniated lumbar disc presents to the emergency department. Which symptom reported by the client represents an acute neurological emergency known as cauda equina syndrome?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Saddle anaesthesia with new urinary incontinence"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A mild dull ache in the lower back that increases after prolonged sitting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A localised hamstring spasm on the right that eases with massage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Gradual loss of lumbar range of motion over the past six months"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Saddle anaesthesia with new urinary incontinence" Cauda equina syndrome is a surgical emergency caused by massive central disc herniation or spinal tumor compressing the lumbosacral nerve roots of the cauda equina. Hallmark features include: (1) saddle anesthesia (loss of sensation in the buttocks, perineum, and inner thighs), (2) bowel or bladder dysfunction (urinary retention or overflow incontinence, fecal incontinence), and (3) bilateral sciatica with lower-extremity motor weakness. Immediate surgical decompression (within 24–48 hours) is mandatory to prevent permanent paraplegia and permanent loss of bowel/bladder control. Why the other options fail: A mild ache that worsens with sitting is the ordinary ache of disc disease; a localised hamstring spasm is muscular; a slow loss of range over six months is a mechanical change, not the acute compression that threatens bladder, bowel and leg function.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify Cauda Equina Syndrome red flags: Saddle anesthesia + sudden urinary/bowel incontinence or retention + bilateral weakness. Immediate surgical emergency!$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a client who had an L4–L5 laminectomy and spinal fusion 12 hours ago. Which nursing intervention is mandatory when turning and repositioning this client in bed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Have the client grip the side rail with both hands and twist the hips first in order to start the turn"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Sit the client in high Fowler for thirty minutes before every repositioning"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Log-roll with a friction sheet, keeping the spine neutral and a pillow between the knees"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Let the client dangle their feet over the side of the bed without assistance"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Log-roll with a friction sheet, keeping the spine neutral and a pillow between the knees" Following spinal surgery (laminectomy and fusion), maintaining strict neutral spinal alignment is vital to prevent graft displacement, instrumentation failure, and spinal cord trauma. The nurse must log-roll the client as a single cohesive unit: 2 to 3 staff members align the shoulders, hips, and knees simultaneously without twisting or bending the torso. A pillow is placed between the knees to maintain proper hip alignment. The client must never twist, reach for rails, or bend the spine. Why the other options fail: Gripping the side rail and twisting the hips rotates a freshly fused spine; high Fowler before every turn loads the surgical segment through the trunk; dangling unsupervised invites a fall and spinal movement at the same time.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Spine surgery repositioning rule: Always LOG-ROLL! Keep the entire spine aligned in one straight plane with a pillow between the knees.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with severe postmenopausal osteoporosis is prescribed oral alendronate 70 mg once weekly. Which specific administration instructions must the nurse emphasize to prevent devastating drug-induced esophageal ulceration?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "On waking, with a full glass of plain water, then stay upright 30 minutes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the tablet at bedtime with a small glass of orange juice"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Crush the tablet and stir it thoroughly into a bowl of hot porridge or fruit yoghurt"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the tablet with lunch, alongside a calcium carbonate supplement"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "On waking, with a full glass of plain water, then stay upright 30 minutes" Oral bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) are poorly absorbed and highly caustic to esophageal mucosa. If a tablet lodges in the esophagus, it can cause severe erosive esophagitis, ulceration, and esophageal perforation. Mandatory instructions: (1) take immediately upon waking on an empty stomach with a full 8-ounce glass of plain water (not mineral water, juice, or coffee), (2) wait at least 30 minutes before consuming any food, drink, or other medications, and (3) remain sitting or standing completely upright for at least 30 minutes (60 minutes for ibandronate). Never crush or chew. Why the other options fail: Taking it at night with juice, lying down, invites oesophageal injury; crushing a tablet that must be swallowed whole destroys the preparation; taking it with food and calcium blocks absorption.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the bisphosphonate golden rules: Morning + Full glass plain water + Empty stomach + Sit upright 30 minutes (prevents esophageal ulcers).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 32-year-old female client with severe active rheumatoid arthritis is prescribed weekly oral methotrexate. When providing medication counseling, which critical instruction must the nurse deliver?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the medicine daily with dairy products to improve absorption"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stop the medicine as soon as the joint pain improves after the first dose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reliable contraception, because it is teratogenic, and daily folic acid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Skip the routine blood tests while on maintenance therapy, since they are unnecessary"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Reliable contraception, because it is teratogenic, and daily folic acid" Methotrexate is a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (DMARD) and folate antimetabolite. It is dosed once WEEKLY for rheumatoid arthritis (daily dosing is an error that causes fatal bone marrow toxicity). Methotrexate is an FDA Pregnancy Category X drug and potent teratogen that causes congenital malformations and fetal demise; strict contraception is mandatory during therapy and for at least one ovulatory cycle after stopping. Daily folic acid supplementation is co-prescribed to prevent gastrointestinal toxicity and stomatitis. Routine complete blood counts and liver function tests are required. Why the other options fail: A weekly drug taken daily causes marrow toxicity; stopping as soon as the pain improves abandons a disease-modifying drug; skipping the blood tests removes the monitoring that catches marrow and liver toxicity.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Key methotrexate points: Weekly dosing (NOT daily), Folic acid co-administration, Teratogenic (Category X, strict birth control), Bone marrow suppression monitoring.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who underwent an open reduction and internal fixation of a pelvic fracture is receiving intravenous morphine via a patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) pump. During hourly rounds, the nurse finds the client sleeping soundly with a respiratory rate of 8 breaths/min, pinpoint pupils, and an SpO2 of 88% on 2 L/min nasal cannula. What is the nurse's priority sequence of actions?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Raise the nasal cannula to six litres and let the client sleep undisturbed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Press the PCA bolus button to test whether the lockout is working"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Arouse the client, stop the PCA, give titrated naloxone and call for help"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Document the findings as the expected deep sleep of good analgesia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Arouse the client, stop the PCA, give titrated naloxone and call for help" A respiratory rate of 8 breaths/min, somnolence, pinpoint pupils, and desaturation indicate severe opioid-induced respiratory depression—a life-threatening PCA complication. The nurse must immediately attempt physical stimulation to arouse the client, stop the PCA infusion to halt further drug delivery, administer the opioid antagonist IV naloxone (Narcan) in small titrated doses to reverse respiratory depression without precipitating severe acute withdrawal pain, maintain the airway, provide supplemental oxygen, and call for emergency assistance. Why the other options fail: Raising the oxygen and letting the client sleep masks hypoxaemia without treating its cause; pressing the bolus delivers more opioid to a client who is already over-sedated; documenting a respiratory rate of eight as expected records danger as normal.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recognize opioid toxicity triad: RR < 10, pinpoint pupils, sedation. Immediate response: Stop PCA, stimulate client, give IV naloxone, call for help.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-MSK-020$q$;

