BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 17-year-old unmarried female attends the youth-friendly reproductive health clinic seeking advice regarding vaginal discharge and contraception. She requests that her visit remain strictly confidential. During the consultation, a speculum examination is clinically indicated. What is the nurse's priority ethical and professional conduct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Insist that a parent or guardian sign written authorisation before any examination, because she is under 18 years old"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Examine her with the door closed but without a chaperone, since she has asked for confidentiality and a third person would breach it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Provide auditory and visual privacy, explain the examination, obtain informed consent, offer a documented chaperone, and keep the visit confidential"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain consent and examine her with a chaperone present, but do not discuss the procedure with her in advance in case doing so increases her anxiety"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Provide auditory and visual privacy, explain the examination, obtain informed consent, offer a documented chaperone, and keep the visit confidential" Under national and international adolescent sexual and reproductive health guidelines, sexually active adolescents have the right to confidential, non-judgmental counseling, screening, and contraceptive services. For an intimate pelvic examination, the nurse must: (1) guarantee visual and auditory privacy; (2) explain the indications, instruments, and sensations to alleviate anxiety; (3) secure the client's voluntary informed consent; and (4) provide and document the presence of an impartial chaperone to protect both client dignity and provider safety. Why the other options fail: option a applies a parental-authorisation rule that adolescent sexual and reproductive health standards do not impose, and it denies the service she came for. Option b keeps the record confidential but exposes her body, which is not confidentiality and breaches both dignity and the chaperone requirement. Option d keeps the chaperone but removes the explanation, so the consent it takes is not informed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Gynaecological examination standards: Privacy + explanation + informed consent + chaperone documentation + non-judgmental adolescent confidentiality.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a community cervical cancer screening outreach, the nurse performs Visual Inspection with Acetic Acid (VIA) on a 32-year-old multiparous woman. After applying 3% to 5% dilute acetic acid to the cervix for 1 minute, the nurse visualizes a dense, opaque, well-defined acetowhite lesion with distinct borders touching the squamocolumnar junction (SCJ). How should the nurse interpret and manage this finding?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Interpret as negative; dense acetowhitening is normal glycogenated squamous epithelium, so reassure her and repeat screening in 5 years"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Interpret as inconclusive because the acid was applied for only 1 minute, and so repeat the whole test with a full 3-minute application before any decision on treatment is made"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Interpret as a VIA-positive screen showing high-grade precancerous change; offer same-day thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or refer for colposcopy and biopsy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Interpret as invasive carcinoma; refer urgently for radical hysterectomy and radiotherapy without taking a biopsy of the lesion"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Interpret as a VIA-positive screen showing high-grade precancerous change; offer same-day thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or refer for colposcopy and biopsy" In Visual Inspection with Acetic Acid (VIA), dilute acetic acid dehydrates cells and reversibly coagulates nuclear proteins. Precancerous dysplastic cells (cervical intraepithelial neoplasia, CIN) have high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratios, causing them to reflect light and appear dense, opaque, well-demarcated acetowhite lesions adjacent to the squamocolumnar junction (SCJ / transformation zone). Under WHO screen-and-treat guidelines for low-resource settings, eligible VIA-positive lesions are treated immediately with thermal ablation or cryotherapy, or referred for colposcopic biopsy if suspicious for invasive carcinoma. Why the other options fail: option a misreads acetowhitening as normal; glycogenated epithelium does not form a dense, well-defined lesion at the squamocolumnar junction, and a 5-year recall is wrong for a screen-positive cervix. Option b invents a 3-minute protocol, when one minute is the standard application and the lesion is already diagnostic. Option d skips histology before radical surgery, which no screening programme permits on a visual inspection alone.$q$,
  strategy = $q$VIA screening: Dense, well-defined acetowhite lesions touching the SCJ = VIA-positive -> screen-and-treat (ablation) or colposcopy referral.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 35-year-old woman presents with severe menorrhagia (heavy menstrual bleeding lasting 9 days with large clots, soaking 6 pads daily) and fatigue. Pelvic ultrasound reveals two enlarged intramural uterine leiomyomas (fibroids) measuring 6 cm each. Her haemoglobin is 7.8 g/dL. Under the FIGO PALM-COEIN classification system, what structural category does this represent, and what is the initial nursing priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Non-structural: coagulopathy (COEIN-C); request von Willebrand studies and treat the bleeding with desmopressin before referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Structural: leiomyoma (PALM-L); correct the iron-deficiency anaemia and start tranexamic acid or a levonorgestrel IUD"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Structural: adenomyosis (PALM-A); proceed to hysterectomy because medical therapy will not control bleeding of this volume"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Non-structural: ovulatory dysfunction (COEIN-O); induce ovulation with clomiphene and defer treatment of the anaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Structural: leiomyoma (PALM-L); correct the iron-deficiency anaemia and start tranexamic acid or a levonorgestrel IUD" The FIGO PALM-COEIN system classifies causes of Abnormal Uterine Bleeding (AUB). PALM denotes structural causes: Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma (fibroid), and Malignancy/hyperplasia. COEIN denotes non-structural causes: Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, and Not yet classified. Uterine leiomyomas (L) increase the endometrial surface area and disrupt myometrial contractility, causing menorrhagia and severe secondary microcytic anaemia. Priority nursing care includes correcting anaemia with iron supplementation, administering antifibrinolytics (tranexamic acid during menses) or LNG-IUS, and surgical referral for myomectomy if childbearing is desired. Why the other options fail: option a selects a non-structural category for a lesion already proved on ultrasound, and it leaves the anaemia of 7.8 g/dL untreated while the bleeding continues. Option c names a structural category that is not present and proceeds to hysterectomy before any medical stabilization. Option d selects a non-structural category and defers the anaemia, which is the immediate threat at a haemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PALM-COEIN: Leiomyoma (L) is a structural cause of AUB. Address profound anaemia (Hb 7.8) and medical hemostasis first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 24-year-old female presents with irregular periods (oligomenorrhoea, menses every 65 days), facial hirsutism, cystic acne, and a BMI of 31 kg/m². Transvaginal ultrasound demonstrates enlarged ovaries with a 'string-of-pearls' peripheral follicle distribution (>= 20 antral follicles per ovary). Which endocrine and metabolic abnormality is central to this condition (PCOS), and what long-term health risk must the nurse educate her about?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ovarian failure with a rising FSH; the long-term risks are osteoporosis, hot flushes and premature cardiovascular disease"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hyperandrogenism with insulin resistance; the long-term risks are type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and endometrial hyperplasia"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hyperprolactinaemia with suppressed GnRH; the long-term risks are galactorrhoea, headache and progressive growth of a pituitary adenoma"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypothalamic amenorrhoea from an energy deficit; the long-term risks are low bone density and recurrent stress fractures"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Hyperandrogenism with insulin resistance; the long-term risks are type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease and endometrial hyperplasia" Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) diagnosed by Rotterdam criteria requires at least 2 of 3 features: (1) oligo/anovulation; (2) clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism; and (3) polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound. Hyperinsulinaemia secondary to peripheral insulin resistance stimulates ovarian theca cell androgen production and decreases sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). Chronic anovulation leads to persistent, unopposed oestrogen stimulation of the endometrium (without cyclical progesterone from a corpus luteum), predisposing women to endometrial hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma, as well as metabolic syndrome and type 2 diabetes. Why the other options fail: option a describes ovarian failure, which gives a low oestrogen and a raised FSH with hot flushes, not hyperandrogenism with a polycystic ovarian morphology. Option c describes hyperprolactinaemia, which suppresses GnRH and causes amenorrhoea without the hirsutism and insulin resistance seen here. Option d describes hypothalamic amenorrhoea, which produces a low or normal LH and no ovarian enlargement.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PCOS pathophysiology: Hyperinsulinaemia drives androgen excess. Chronic anovulation -> unopposed oestrogen -> high risk of endometrial hyperplasia/cancer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 29-year-old nulliparous woman presents with severe cyclic pelvic pain that begins 2 days prior to menstruation, deep dyspareunia (pain during intercourse), and painful defecation during menses (dyschezia). She has been unable to conceive for 2 years despite regular intercourse. On pelvic examination, there is marked tenderness and nodularity along the uterosacral ligaments. What condition should the nurse suspect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute appendicitis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cervical incompetence"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Endometriosis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Primary dysmenorrhoea"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Endometriosis" Endometriosis is characterized by the implantation and proliferation of functional endometrial glands and stroma outside the uterine cavity (most commonly on the ovaries, uterosacral ligaments, pouch of Douglas, and pelvic peritoneum). The classic clinical triad is: (1) secondary dysmenorrhoea; (2) deep dyspareunia; and (3) subfertility/infertility, often accompanied by dyschezia (pain on defecation) and uterosacral nodularity. Primary dysmenorrhoea occurs without pelvic pathology and lacks dyspareunia or nodularity. Definitive diagnosis is established via diagnostic laparoscopy showing 'powder-burn' or 'chocolate' endometriotic lesions. Why the other options fail: option a is an acute abdominal emergency that has no cyclical relation to menstruation, no dyschezia and no uterosacral nodularity. Option b describes painless mid-trimester cervical dilatation, which causes pregnancy loss rather than cyclic pelvic pain. Option d is primary dysmenorrhoea, which begins with the first periods, has no dyspareunia or nodularity, and does not cause infertility.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Endometriosis triad: Severe cyclic pelvic pain + deep dyspareunia + subfertility/infertility with uterosacral tenderness/nodularity.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 38-year-old woman comes to the family planning clinic requesting a prescription for combined oral contraceptive pills (COCs). During health history taking, she states that she smokes one pack of cigarettes per day (20 cigarettes/day). According to the WHO / US CDC Medical Eligibility Criteria for Contraceptive Use (MEC), how should the nurse categorize this request?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "MEC category 1: no restriction at any age or at any smoking load, because the oestrogen dose in a modern low-dose pill is far too small to affect clotting at all"}, {"id": "b", "text": "MEC category 2: start a low-dose combined pill now and advise her to cut down to fewer than 15 cigarettes a day, then review in 3 months"}, {"id": "c", "text": "MEC category 3: prescribe a combined pill containing 20 micrograms of ethinylestradiol, which lowers the clot risk enough to be used"}, {"id": "d", "text": "MEC category 4: combined hormonal contraceptives are contraindicated, because her age and smoking load give an unacceptable arterial thrombosis risk"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "MEC category 4: combined hormonal contraceptives are contraindicated, because her age and smoking load give an unacceptable arterial thrombosis risk" Under the WHO and CDC Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC): Category 4 represents an absolute contraindication where health risks outweigh any benefits. Age >= 35 years combined with heavy smoking (>= 15 cigarettes/day) synergistically increases hepatic production of clotting factors and promotes arterial vasospasm/thrombosis, dramatically raising the incidence of fatal myocardial infarction, deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, and ischaemic stroke. Safe, non-oestrogen alternatives for this client include copper IUD, levonorgestrel IUD, progestogen-only implants, or depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA). Why the other options fail: option a ignores the age and the smoking load, the two variables that set the category. Option b treats a category 2 as permission to start the method that is contraindicated, and adds dose escalation that exists nowhere in the guidance. Option c applies a category 3 to a client who is a 4, and assumes that lowering the oestrogen dose removes an arterial risk that it does not.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Contraceptive eligibility (MEC 4): Oestrogen-containing methods are absolutely contraindicated in women >= 35 who smoke >= 15 cigarettes/day (thrombosis/stroke risk).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 21-year-old university student presents to the clinic on Monday morning stating that a condom broke during intercourse on Saturday evening (approximately 38 hours ago). She does not want to become pregnant. Which emergency contraceptive method is the most effective overall, and what oral options are available within this timeframe?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg now, because oral methods act faster than an IUD and she is already past the 24-hour ideal window"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ulipristal acetate 30 mg only, because it stays effective to 120 hours and an IUD cannot be fitted after unprotected intercourse"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A copper IUD inserted within 5 days is the most effective method; oral options are levonorgestrel within 72 hours or ulipristal within 120 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A combined oral contraceptive, with two tablets taken now and two more in 12 hours, which is just as effective as an IUD at 38 hours after intercourse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A copper IUD inserted within 5 days is the most effective method; oral options are levonorgestrel within 72 hours or ulipristal within 120 hours" The copper IUD (Cu-IUD) is the most effective method of emergency contraception, with a failure rate of less than 0.1%, and can be inserted up to 5 days (120 hours) after unprotected intercourse (or up to 5 days after the earliest calculated day of ovulation), providing ongoing long-term contraception for up to 10–12 years. If oral options are preferred: levonorgestrel 1.5 mg is licensed up to 72 hours (efficacy decreases with time and higher BMI); ulipristal acetate (30 mg selective progesterone receptor modulator) maintains high efficacy up to 120 hours (5 days) post-coitus. Why the other options fail: option a overstates the speed of oral methods and abandons the most effective option hours inside its window. Option b is right that ulipristal works to 120 hours but wrong that an IUD cannot be fitted, which is the error the item tests. Option d describes the obsolete Yuzpe regimen, which is less effective than either the IUD or a single-dose oral method.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Emergency contraception: Copper IUD is the #1 most effective emergency method (up to 5 days / 120 h). Oral LNG up to 72 h; Ulipristal up to 120 h.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 27-year-old client has a levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) inserted for both contraception and treatment of heavy menstrual bleeding. During pre-discharge counselling, what expected change in bleeding patterns must the nurse emphasize to promote adherence and prevent premature device removal?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Heavy bleeding with clots is expected for the first 3 months, and she should return only if she soaks more than two pads a day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Bleeding stops within 48 hours of insertion; any spotting after that suggests the device has perforated the uterus and must be removed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Periods return to their previous pattern within 6 weeks, because the device provides contraception only and does not alter flow"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Irregular spotting is expected for the first 3 to 6 months; flow then becomes much lighter and many women become amenorrhoeic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Irregular spotting is expected for the first 3 to 6 months; flow then becomes much lighter and many women become amenorrhoeic" The levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine system (LNG-IUS) releases local progestogen that induces profound endometrial glandular atrophy. In the initial 3–6 months following insertion, users frequently experience frequent, irregular, light bleeding or spotting as the endometrium thins. By 12 months, overall menstrual blood loss is reduced by >= 90%, and 20–50% of women achieve amenorrhoea. Anticipatory guidance regarding this transient bleeding pattern is the single most effective nursing strategy to prevent client anxiety and premature device discontinuation. Why the other options fail: option a calls heavy bleeding expected and sets a return threshold that would miss expulsion or perforation. Option b promises immediate amenorrhoea and turns normal spotting into a perforation, which would prompt an unnecessary removal. Option c denies any effect on flow, when the progestogen is there precisely to thin the endometrium and reduce loss.$q$,
  strategy = $q$LNG-IUS counselling: Anticipatory guidance on 3-6 months of benign irregular light spotting, followed by dramatically lighter periods or amenorrhoea.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 26-year-old female presents to the emergency room with a 6-week history of amenorrhoea, sudden sharp unilateral right lower quadrant pain, dizziness, and referred right shoulder pain. Blood pressure is 82/48 mmHg, pulse is 128 beats/min, and pelvic exam reveals severe cervical motion tenderness (chandelier sign) and a bulging posterior fornix. What life-threatening condition does the nurse recognize, and what is the immediate intervention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Threatened miscarriage with vagal syncope; give intramuscular morphine for the pain and arrange a repeat ultrasound scan for the following morning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ruptured ectopic pregnancy with haemoperitoneum; secure two large-bore IVs, resuscitate, cross-match blood and prepare for emergency laparotomy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Ovarian torsion from a ruptured corpus luteum; give analgesia and book semi-elective laparoscopy within the next 24 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute pelvic inflammatory disease with sepsis; start intravenous antibiotics and fluids and reassess after 6 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Ruptured ectopic pregnancy with haemoperitoneum; secure two large-bore IVs, resuscitate, cross-match blood and prepare for emergency laparotomy" This clinical picture is diagnostic of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy with massive haemoperitoneum causing hypovolaemic shock. The classic triad consists of amenorrhoea, unilateral abdominal/pelvic pain, and vaginal bleeding. Referred shoulder tip pain (Kehr's sign) occurs when diaphragmatic peritoneum is irritated by pooled blood (innervated by the phrenic nerve, C3–C5). Marked cervical motion tenderness reflects severe peritoneal irritation. Ruptured ectopic pregnancy is a surgical emergency; immediate resuscitation with two large-bore IV cannulae (14–16 gauge), rapid fluid boluses, blood cross-matching, and urgent surgical hemostasis are mandatory to prevent maternal exsanguination. Why the other options fail: option a treats a shocked client as a threatened miscarriage and gives morphine, which masks deterioration while the bleeding continues. Option c delays surgery by 24 hours in a client with a falling blood pressure and a distended abdomen. Option d attributes the picture to infection, which produces fever and a normal or raised blood pressure, not shoulder-tip pain and shock.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ruptured ectopic pregnancy: Amenorrhoea + unilateral pain + shock + shoulder pain (phrenic irritation). Resuscitate with large-bore IVs & prep for surgery.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 31-year-old woman undergoing an infertility evaluation is scheduled for Hysterosalpingography (HSG) to assess tubal patency and uterine cavity architecture. When should the nurse instruct the client to schedule the HSG procedure, and what pre-procedure screening is essential?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Days 7 to 10, after bleeding has stopped and before ovulation, with a negative pregnancy test and no active pelvic infection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Any day she is not bleeding, provided the pregnancy test is negative; an active pelvic infection need not delay the procedure at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Days 1 to 5, during light bleeding, so that the contrast can flush residual menstrual debris out of the uterine cavity"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Days 21 to 24, in the luteal phase, so that tubal spasm is reduced and the cavity distends more evenly during injection"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Days 7 to 10, after bleeding has stopped and before ovulation, with a negative pregnancy test and no active pelvic infection" Hysterosalpingography (HSG) involves fluoroscopic radiopaque contrast injection into the endocervical canal to outline the endometrial cavity and fallopian tubes. Timing is critical: it must be performed during the early follicular phase (days 7–10 of a 28-day cycle), after menstrual flow has completely ceased (to prevent retrograde flushing of menstrual debris/endometrial tissue into the peritoneal cavity and avoid venous extravasation) but BEFORE ovulation occurs (to prevent irradiating or flushing an early fertilised ovum). Active pelvic inflammatory disease is an absolute contraindication because contrast injection can precipitate fulminant pelvic peritonitis. Why the other options fail: option b drops the infection screen, the one absolute contraindication, because contrast injection into an inflamed pelvis can precipitate peritonitis. Option c schedules during menstruation, when debris is flushed retrograde into the peritoneum and venous extravasation is more likely. Option d schedules in the luteal phase, when an early conceptus may be in the tube and tubal spasm distorts the study.$q$,
  strategy = $q$HSG timing & safety: Days 7-10 (post-menses, pre-ovulation) + negative pregnancy test + absolute exclusion of active pelvic infection.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 23-year-old female at 9 weeks gestation presents with painless bright red vaginal spotting. On speculum and digital examination, the nurse notes that the cervical os is closed, no fetal tissue has passed, and bed-side ultrasound confirms an intrauterine gestational sac with a viable embryonic heartbeat. How should the nurse classify this type of spontaneous abortion?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inevitable miscarriage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Incomplete miscarriage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Missed miscarriage"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Threatened miscarriage"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Threatened miscarriage" Spontaneous abortion categories in early pregnancy are classified clinically: (1) Threatened abortion: vaginal bleeding occurs, the cervical os remains CLOSED, and the fetus remains viable (cardiac activity present); the pregnancy may proceed successfully to term. (2) Inevitable abortion: bleeding with uterine cramping and the cervical os is DILATED (open), making pregnancy loss unavoidable. (3) Incomplete abortion: cervical os is open with partial expulsion of products of conception. (4) Complete abortion: all products expelled, os closed. (5) Missed abortion: embryo/fetal demise occurs without bleeding or expulsion; os closed. Why the other options fail: option a describes inevitable miscarriage, which requires a dilated os and cramping that this client does not have. Option b describes incomplete miscarriage, which requires an open os and passage of tissue. Option c describes missed miscarriage, in which the embryo has died and is retained, whereas this scan shows cardiac activity. A closed os with a viable embryo is what separates threatened from all three.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Miscarriage classification: Vaginal bleeding + CLOSED os + VIABLE fetal heartbeat = Threatened miscarriage. Reassurance and pelvic rest.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 22-year-old sexually active female presents with bilateral lower quadrant abdominal pain, fever (38.4 °C), and deep dyspareunia. On bimanual examination, the nurse elicits exquisite cervical motion tenderness and bilateral adnexal tenderness. Why must the nurse prioritize immediate empiric broad-spectrum antibiotic treatment (such as ceftriaxone, doxycycline, and metronidazole) rather than delaying for laboratory culture results?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treatment should be withheld until the endocervical swab results are available, so that the chosen antibiotic is correctly targeted at the organism"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Empiric treatment is justified only once all three minimum criteria are met, not on cervical motion tenderness alone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prompt treatment prevents tubal scarring and lowers the lifelong risk of infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Treatment can safely wait 48 hours while a pregnancy test and pelvic ultrasound exclude an ectopic pregnancy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Prompt treatment prevents tubal scarring and lowers the lifelong risk of infertility, ectopic pregnancy and chronic pelvic pain" Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) is an ascending polymicrobial infection from the endocervix into the endometrium, fallopian tubes (salpingitis), ovaries, and pelvic peritoneum, commonly triggered by Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. The CDC guidelines mandate empiric antibiotic therapy in sexually active young women presenting with lower pelvic pain and at least one minimum clinical criterion (cervical motion tenderness, uterine tenderness, or adnexal tenderness). Even brief delays in treatment lead to endosalpingeal deciliation, intraluminal scarring, and tubal occlusion, causing tubal infertility (10% after 1 episode, 40% after 3 episodes), ectopic pregnancy, and chronic debilitating pelvic pain. Why the other options fail: option a delays treatment for a culture that is often negative and that CDC guidance explicitly does not require before antibiotics. Option b adds a threshold that does not exist; any one of the three minimum criteria is enough to treat. Option d gives the same delay a different justification, but ectopic pregnancy is excluded in parallel, not by withholding antibiotics.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PID urgency: Minimum criteria (cervical motion tenderness) warrants immediate empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics to prevent permanent tubal scarring and infertility.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 28-year-old woman complains of an abnormal vaginal discharge and severe vulval itching. Speculum examination reveals a thick, adherent, white, curd-like ('cottage cheese') discharge coating the vaginal walls, with erythema and fissuring of the vulva. The vaginal pH is 4.2. Wet mount microscopy with 10% potassium hydroxide (KOH) demonstrates budding yeast and pseudohyphae. What is the diagnosis, and what is the treatment of choice?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bacterial vaginosis; treat with oral metronidazole 400 mg twice daily for 5 days and avoid vaginal azoles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vulvovaginal candidiasis; treat with a topical azole such as clotrimazole, or a single 150 mg oral dose of fluconazole"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Trichomoniasis; treat with a single 2 g oral dose of metronidazole and screen her partner at the same visit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Atrophic vaginitis; treat with topical oestrogen cream and review the vaginal pH again after four weeks of continuous therapy"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Vulvovaginal candidiasis; treat with a topical azole such as clotrimazole, or a single 150 mg oral dose of fluconazole" Vulvovaginal Candidiasis (typically Candida albicans) presents with intense vulvovaginal pruritus, dysuria, dyspareunia, and classic thick, curd-like, white 'cottage cheese' discharge. The vaginal pH remains acidic (normal, <= 4.5), which distinguishes it from Bacterial Vaginosis and Trichomoniasis (where pH > 4.5). KOH wet mount reveals characteristic pseudohyphae and budding blastospores. Treatment consists of topical azoles (clotrimazole, miconazole pessaries) or single-dose oral fluconazole 150 mg (oral fluconazole is avoided during pregnancy, where topical clotrimazole is preferred). Why the other options fail: option a names bacterial vaginosis, which gives a thin grey discharge with a fishy odour and a pH above 4.5, and metronidazole would not clear the pseudohyphae on the KOH mount. Option c names trichomoniasis, which gives a frothy yellow-green discharge, a pH above 4.5 and motile flagellates on the wet mount, none of which is present here. Option d names atrophic vaginitis, which follows oestrogen loss and would not show budding yeast.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vaginitis differential: Thick curd-like discharge + intense pruritus + pH <= 4.5 + pseudohyphae = Vulvovaginal candidiasis -> Clotrimazole / Fluconazole.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 25-year-old client presents with severe, throbbing vulval pain that makes sitting and walking excruciating. Physical examination reveals an extremely tender, fluctuant, erythematous, walnut-sized mass at the 4 o'clock position of the posterior labium majus near the vaginal introitus. What condition is present, and what is the definitive clinical intervention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A Bartholin cyst; apply warm compresses and re-examine in 6 weeks, since a cyst that is not infected needs no drainage"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A sebaceous cyst of the labium majus; incise and express the contents under local anaesthetic and pack the cavity open"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A vulval haematoma; apply ice packs and observe for 24 hours, because incision and drainage would increase the bleeding"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bartholin gland abscess; incise and drain and insert a Word catheter or marsupialise, with sitz baths and analgesia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Bartholin gland abscess; incise and drain and insert a Word catheter or marsupialise, with sitz baths and analgesia" The greater vestibular (Bartholin) glands are located bilaterally at the 4 and 8 o'clock positions of the posterior vaginal introitus. When the duct becomes obstructed, fluid accumulates (Bartholin cyst), which can become acutely infected and form an abscess. The patient develops rapid, severe, throbbing unilateral vulval pain and dyspareunia. Management of an abscess requires incision and drainage. Simple incision alone has an 80% recurrence rate; therefore, placing a Word catheter for 4–6 weeks (allowing epithelialization of a new tract) or performing surgical marsupialization is necessary. Why the other options fail: option a treats a fluctuant, exquisitely tender abscess as an uninfected cyst, which will not settle with compresses and risks spontaneous rupture. Option b names a sebaceous cyst, which is not fixed at the introitus in the 4 o'clock position and does not need open packing. Option c names a haematoma, which follows trauma and is not managed by observation when it is this painful.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Bartholin abscess: Painful fluctuant mass at 4 or 8 o'clock of introitus. Treatment = I&D with Word catheter placement or marsupialization + sitz baths.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 44-year-old woman attends the gynaecology clinic reporting bleeding after sexual intercourse (post-coital bleeding) and persistent, watery, foul-smelling vaginal discharge for 4 months. Speculum examination reveals an exophytic, friable, ulcerated mass on the anterior cervical lip that bleeds easily on contact. What is the most critical next diagnostic step?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take a direct punch biopsy of the cervical mass, or refer urgently for colposcopy and biopsy, to obtain histological confirmation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform a cervical Pap smear now and refer only if the cytology shows high-grade dysplasia, because cytology is the diagnostic test"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Treat the visible lesion with cryotherapy at this visit and review her in 6 months with a repeat screening test, as for a VIA-positive cervix"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Prescribe a course of metronidazole and doxycycline and re-examine the cervix once the discharge has cleared"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Take a direct punch biopsy of the cervical mass, or refer urgently for colposcopy and biopsy, to obtain histological confirmation" Post-coital bleeding (contact bleeding), intermenstrual bleeding, and foul-smelling vaginal discharge are cardinal red-flag warning signs of invasive cervical carcinoma. A visible, friable, exophytic, or ulcerated cervical lesion requires an immediate tissue punch biopsy for histological evaluation. A routine cytological screening smear (Pap smear) is inappropriate and contraindicated when a gross, visible lesion is present, because cytology has a false-negative rate of up to 50% in invasive cancer due to surface necrosis and blood obscuring neoplastic cells. Why the other options fail: option b relies on cytology in the presence of a visible, friable mass, where surface necrosis and blood produce a high false-negative rate and a smear cannot stage the lesion. Option c ablates a lesion that has not been sampled, destroying the evidence and delaying diagnosis. Option d treats an assumed infection while a malignant ulcer continues to grow.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Cervical cancer red flags: Post-coital bleeding + visible friable cervical lesion requires direct TISSUE BIOPSY, not a screening Pap smear.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 64-year-old postmenopausal woman (whose last menstrual period was 12 years ago) visits the health center reporting three episodes of painless, brown-to-red vaginal spotting over the past month. She is not taking hormone replacement therapy. What principle must guide the nurse's clinical management?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Treat any postmenopausal bleeding as endometrial cancer until it is excluded; refer for transvaginal ultrasound and endometrial biopsy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure and review in 6 months, because a few episodes of spotting after 12 years of amenorrhoea are usually atrophic"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Request a cervical smear first, because postmenopausal bleeding is most often caused by cervical rather than endometrial pathology in this age group"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Start combined HRT to stabilise the endometrium and reassess only if the bleeding recurs after 3 months of treatment"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Treat any postmenopausal bleeding as endometrial cancer until it is excluded; refer for transvaginal ultrasound and endometrial biopsy" The cardinal clinical rule in gynaecology is that postmenopausal bleeding (PMB) is presumed to be endometrial adenocarcinoma until definitively excluded. Approximately 10% of women presenting with PMB have underlying endometrial malignancy. Initial diagnostic evaluation includes transvaginal ultrasonography (TVUS): an endometrial thickness <= 4 mm has a > 99% negative predictive value for endometrial cancer. If the endometrium is > 4 mm, irregular, or bleeding persists, outpatient endometrial biopsy (using a Pipelle aspirator) or hysteroscopy with directed biopsy is required. Why the other options fail: option b attributes the bleeding to atrophy and defers the one investigation that excludes cancer; atrophy can cause spotting, but it is a diagnosis of exclusion. Option c investigates the cervix when the endometrium is the source in most cases, and a normal smear does not exclude endometrial cancer. Option d treats the bleeding with the hormone that can drive endometrial proliferation, and delays the biopsy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Postmenopausal bleeding: Cardinal rule -> Presume endometrial cancer until proven otherwise. Urgent transvaginal ultrasound & Pipelle biopsy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 58-year-old postmenopausal female presents with persistent abdominal bloating, early satiety, increased abdominal girth, and urinary urgency for the past 2 months. Pelvic examination reveals a fixed, solid, non-tender adnexal mass. Why is ovarian cancer frequently referred to as the 'silent killer', and what diagnostic workup is indicated?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "It arises from the cervical epithelium and spreads without symptoms, which is why smear screening is used; work up with a Pap smear and colposcopy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Its symptoms are vague and mimic benign gut disorders, so most cases present at stage III or IV; work up with transvaginal ultrasound and CA-125"}, {"id": "c", "text": "It causes no symptoms until the tumour is palpable; work up with abdominal CT and a carcinoembryonic antigen level, which is raised in ovarian cancer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "It is commonest in women under 40 and grows rapidly; work up with laparoscopy and a CA-19-9 level, the tumour marker for ovarian cancer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Its symptoms are vague and mimic benign gut disorders, so most cases present at stage III or IV; work up with transvaginal ultrasound and CA-125" Epithelial ovarian cancer is often dubbed the 'silent killer' because it lacks a reliable screening test (Pap smears detect cervical, not ovarian, cells) and produces non-specific, insidious symptoms—persistent bloating, early satiety, pelvic fullness, and urinary frequency—that patients and clinicians frequently attribute to irritable bowel syndrome, dyspepsia, or normal ageing. Consequently, over 70% of women are diagnosed at an advanced stage (FIGO Stage III or IV with peritoneal carcinomatosis and ascites). Workup includes transvaginal ultrasound and serum CA-125 tumor marker testing. Why the other options fail: option a confuses ovarian with cervical cancer and proposes a smear that samples the wrong organ. Option c offers CT and CEA, a marker for colorectal rather than ovarian cancer, and no ovarian mass is truly symptomless until palpable. Option d reverses the age distribution and offers CA-19-9, which is not the ovarian marker.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ovarian cancer presentation: Vague GI symptoms (persistent bloating, early satiety, abdominal distension) + solid adnexal mass -> Pelvic ultrasound & CA-125.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 51-year-old woman with an intact uterus suffers from debilitating vasomotor flushes, night sweats, and sleep disturbances secondary to the menopausal transition. Her physician prescribes combined Hormone Replacement Therapy (HRT) containing both oestrogen and a progestogen. Why is a progestogen added to oestrogen therapy in a woman who has NOT had a hysterectomy?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Unopposed oestrogen drives endometrial proliferation and raises the risk of hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma; the progestogen sheds the lining"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The progestogen reduces breast tenderness and bloating, which are the commonest reasons that women stop their hormone therapy in the first year"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The progestogen protects the endometrium, but it is no longer needed once 12 months have passed without a natural period"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The progestogen prevents the vasomotor flushes from rebounding after 6 months of oestrogen therapy, which is why it is added"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Unopposed oestrogen drives endometrial proliferation and raises the risk of hyperplasia and adenocarcinoma; the progestogen sheds the lining" In postmenopausal women with an intact uterus, systemic oestrogen-only replacement therapy stimulates unchecked endometrial cell proliferation (mitosis), resulting in endometrial hyperplasia and a markedly elevated risk of endometrial adenocarcinoma. Adding a progestogen (either sequentially for cyclical withdrawal bleeding or continuously for amenorrhoea) antagonizes oestrogenic proliferative effects, converting the endometrium to a thin, secretory, atrophic state and preventing malignant transformation. Women who have had a surgical hysterectomy do not have an endometrium and therefore take oestrogen-only HRT. Why the other options fail: option b gives a comfort benefit as the reason for a therapy that is given to prevent cancer. Option c is right that the endometrium is the target but wrong to stop protection after 12 months, when the risk persists for as long as oestrogen is taken. Option d invents a rebound phenomenon and ignores the endometrium entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$HRT pharmacology: Intact uterus requires COMBINED oestrogen + progestogen (progestogen protects against endometrial cancer). Post-hysterectomy takes oestrogen alone.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 72-year-old multiparous client presents complaining of a heavy sensation in her pelvis and 'a feeling like a ball is falling out of my vagina when I walk or lift objects.' Physical examination confirms a Stage 3 uterine prolapse and cystocele. The client is frail with severe cardiovascular comorbidities and is unfit for major pelvic reconstructive surgery. Which conservative management should the nurse teach the client?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fit a Gellhorn pessary without oestrogen and leave it in place for 12 months before the first review, since it is self-cleaning and needs no attention"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Teach her to bear down several times a day to strengthen the levator ani so that the prolapse lifts back into place"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a weighted vaginal cone daily and remove it only once the prolapse no longer descends when she coughs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Fit a vaginal ring pessary with topical oestrogen to protect the mucosa, teach cleaning and replacement, and add pelvic floor training"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Fit a vaginal ring pessary with topical oestrogen to protect the mucosa, teach cleaning and replacement, and add pelvic floor training" Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) results from attenuation and stretching of the endopelvic fascia and levator ani pelvic diaphragm muscles. In frail older women or those who decline or are unfit for surgery, vaginal pessaries (e.g., silicone ring or Gellhorn pessaries) provide an effective, safe conservative solution. The pessary mechanically elevates the prolapsed pelvic organs. Nursing care includes: (1) ensuring correct size fit; (2) teaching hygiene and removal/cleaning intervals (or scheduling clinic follow-up every 3–6 months); (3) prescribing local topical oestrogen cream to prevent vaginal epithelial ulceration; and (4) pelvic floor muscle training. Why the other options fail: option a uses a pessary without oestrogen and leaves it a year, which risks mucosal erosion and ulceration in an atrophic vagina. Option b asks the client to bear down, which raises intra-abdominal pressure and worsens prolapse rather than strengthening the floor. Option c uses a weighted cone, which is not a prolapse device and cannot be left in until the prolapse stops descending.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pelvic organ prolapse: Conservative care for high surgical risk = Vaginal pessary + local oestrogen cream to prevent erosion + Kegel exercises.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 19-year-old female presents to the emergency room accompanied by a friend, reporting she was sexually assaulted 18 hours ago. She is crying and visibly trembling. What is the nurse's comprehensive, trauma-informed clinical priority bundle for this survivor?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ensure safety and support; with consent collect forensic evidence; give emergency contraception, HIV PEP and STI prophylaxis; arrange counselling"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Collect the forensic evidence first and defer emotional support until the examination is over, so that none of the samples that might be needed are lost"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give HIV PEP only if the assailant is known to be positive, and defer emergency contraception until a pregnancy test is back"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Advise her to report to the police before any examination, since evidence cannot be collected without a case number"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Ensure safety and support; with consent collect forensic evidence; give emergency contraception, HIV PEP and STI prophylaxis; arrange counselling" Management of sexual assault requires a compassionate, trauma-informed approach addressing immediate medical, legal, and psychological needs: (1) Physical and psychological safety in a private environment; (2) Forensic evidence collection (clothing, swabs) before bathing, maintaining strict chain of custody; (3) Emergency contraception (levonorgestrel, ulipristal, or copper IUD) to prevent unwanted pregnancy; (4) HIV Post-Exposure Prophylaxis (PEP) started urgently within 72 hours and continued for 28 days; (5) Empiric antimicrobial prophylaxis against gonorrhoea, chlamydia, and trichomoniasis; (6) Tetanus and Hepatitis B vaccination; and (7) Structured psychological crisis intervention. Why the other options fail: option b sequences evidence before support, when a crying, trembling client cannot give informed consent to anything until she is settled. Option c makes PEP conditional on knowing the assailant's status, which is not required, and defers contraception that is time-critical. Option d makes a police case number a precondition of care, which no service requires and which re-traumatises the client.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Sexual assault care bundle: Safety/trauma-informed care + forensic chain of custody + emergency contraception + HIV PEP (< 72 h) + empiric STI prophylaxis + counseling.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GYN-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is assessing a deeply pigmented African client admitted with suspected sepsis and acute respiratory distress. Which assessment technique is most reliable for identifying cyanosis and pallor in clients with dark skin?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess the anterior forearm in ordinary room light, since this site is the least exposed to the sun and therefore gives the truest reading of the underlying haemoglobin level of the blood"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Look for bright pink flushing over the abdomen, because the increased cutaneous blood flow of sepsis is most visible where the skin is thinnest"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Record that cyanosis and pallor cannot be assessed at all in this skin tone, and rely on the arterial blood gas and serum lactate to detect hypoperfusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inspect the conjunctivae, oral mucous membranes, and lips for ashen-grey or blue discoloration, and examine the palms and nail beds rather than relying on general facial skin tone"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Inspect the conjunctivae, oral mucous membranes, and lips for ashen-grey or blue discoloration, and examine the palms and nail beds rather than relying on general facial skin tone" In darkly pigmented skin, high epidermal melanin concentrations mask the subtle cutaneous color changes seen in lighter skin. Pallor presents not as obvious whiteness, but as a dull, ashen-grey color in dark skin or a yellowish-brown tinge in olive/brown skin. Cyanosis in dark-skinned individuals is best detected by inspecting areas of least pigmentation and rich capillary vascularity: the conjunctivae, buccal mucosa, hard palate, tongue, lips, and palmar surfaces of hands and soles of feet. Additionally, the nurse must palpate the skin for localized warmth, edema, and induration rather than relying solely on erythema. Why the other options fail: option a is how blood pressure and rashes are often checked, but the forearm carries the same melanin as the face and gives no better signal. Option b looks for flushing rather than for the ashen or blue change the question asks about, and flushing in sepsis is inconsistent. Option c is the belief the item exists to correct: central and mucosal surfaces are assessable in every skin tone, and relying on the gas misses the hours before lactate rises.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dark skin assessment: Inspect mucous membranes (buccal mucosa, tongue, conjunctivae) and palms/soles for pallor/cyanosis; palpate for warmth/induration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse documents dermatological findings on an adult client. Which lesion description correctly matches its morphological classification?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Macule: A flat, non-palpable, circumscribed area of skin color change measuring less than 1 cm in diameter without elevation or depression"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Papule: A raised, fluid-filled lesion more than 1 cm across, sitting deep in the subcutaneous fat and collapsing completely when pressure is applied to it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bulla: A solid, raised, firm lesion formed from dense fibrous scar tissue that persists after the original injury has healed over"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Wheal: A permanent cavity that extends through the dermis into the underlying muscle, leaving a depressed scar once it closes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Macule: A flat, non-palpable, circumscribed area of skin color change measuring less than 1 cm in diameter without elevation or depression" Primary skin lesion taxonomy is fundamental to clinical dermatology: (1) A macule is a flat, non-palpable change in skin color < 1 cm (e.g., freckle, petechia); if > 1 cm, it is a patch. (2) A papule is an elevated, palpable, solid mass < 1 cm (e.g., mole, wart); if > 1 cm, it is a plaque. (3) A vesicle is an elevated cavity filled with clear serous fluid <= 0.5–1 cm (e.g., herpes simplex); if > 1 cm, it is a bulla (blister). (4) A wheal is a transient, raised, edematous, erythematous area with irregular borders caused by dermal fluid extravasation (e.g., urticaria/hives). Why the other options fail: option b attaches a fluid-filled cavity and a deep location to a papule, which is solid and superficial, and a fluid-filled lesion over 1 cm is a bulla rather than a papule. Option c describes a scar or a nodule, not a blister; a bulla is defined by being fluid-filled and larger than 1 cm. Option d describes an ulcer reaching muscle, whereas a wheal is a transient oedematous swelling of the dermis that resolves without scarring.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lesion taxonomy: Macule = flat color change < 1 cm. Papule = solid raised < 1 cm. Vesicle = fluid blister < 1 cm. Bulla = fluid blister > 1 cm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A wound care nurse utilizes the TIME framework (Tissue, Infection/Inflammation, Moisture imbalance, Edge of wound) to evaluate a chronic venous leg ulcer. The wound bed currently displays 60% yellow, fibrous, adherent slough and heavy purulent exudate causing periwound maceration. What is the nurse's priority dual objective based on the TIME principles?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Leave the slough undisturbed so that the wound is not traumatised, and cover the ulcer with a dry dressing changed once weekly to keep the wound bed warm and reduce disturbance"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse the ulcer with hydrogen peroxide and leave it open to the air between dressing changes, so that the exudate can dry and the adherent slough lifts away on its own"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a hydrocolloid sheet and review at seven days, since an occlusive dressing softens slough and absorbs exudate without any need for active debridement"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Debride the non-viable slough and manage the heavy exudate with an absorbent dressing, keeping the bed moist while protecting the surrounding skin from maceration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Debride the non-viable slough and manage the heavy exudate with an absorbent dressing, keeping the bed moist while protecting the surrounding skin from maceration" The TIME framework optimizes the chronic wound microenvironment: (1) Tissue non-viable: Slough and necrotic eschar promote bacterial biofilm and must be debrided (autolytic, enzymatic, sharp, or mechanical); (2) Infection/Inflammation: Control bacterial burden and biofilm; (3) Moisture imbalance: Chronic wounds heal fastest in a physiological moist (not wet, not dry) environment; excessive exudate macerates surrounding skin and contains destructive metalloproteinases, requiring absorbent dressings (calcium alginates, hydrofibers, foam); and (4) Edge advancement: Epidermal cells require a healthy granulating matrix to migrate across wound margins. Why the other options fail: option a addresses neither letter of the framework: slough left in place harbours biofilm, and a dry weekly dressing violates moisture balance in a heavily exuding wound. Option b dries the bed out to loosen slough, which is the opposite of moist wound healing, and hydrogen peroxide is cytotoxic to the fibroblasts trying to close the wound. Option c is the nearest miss: hydrocolloid does soften slough autolytically, but it cannot cope with heavy exudate and will leak and macerate the peri-wound skin.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TIME wound care: Non-viable slough requires debridement + excess exudate requires absorbent dressing to balance moisture and prevent maceration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A bedridden client has a localized area over the sacrum displaying partial-thickness loss of dermis presenting as a shallow, open ulcer with a viable, pink-red wound bed without slough or bruising. An adjacent area shows an intact, serum-filled blister. How should the nurse stage this pressure injury according to the NPUAP/EPUAP staging system?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stage 1 pressure injury"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stage 3 pressure injury"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stage 2 pressure injury"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Unstageable pressure injury"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Stage 2 pressure injury" According to NPUAP/EPUAP staging: Stage 1 is intact skin with localized non-blanchable erythema (or localized warmth, firmness, or discoloration in dark skin). Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss of skin with exposed dermis, presenting as a shallow open ulcer with a viable, red or pink, moist wound bed without slough, eschar, or granulation tissue, OR presenting as an intact or open/ruptured serum-filled blister. Stage 3 involves full-thickness skin loss with visible adipose tissue and granulation; Stage 4 involves exposed bone, tendon, or muscle. Unstageable occurs when slough or eschar completely obscures the base. Why the other options fail: option a is intact skin with non-blanchable erythema and no loss of skin, which is not the shallow open wound described. Option b extends through the dermis into subcutaneous fat, which this wound does not. Option d cannot be staged because slough or eschar obscures the base, when here the base is visible and pink.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pressure injury staging: Partial-thickness dermis loss with red/pink bed OR intact serum blister = Stage 2. Full-thickness into fat = Stage 3.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$The nurse scores a newly admitted stroke client on the Braden Scale for Predicting Pressure Sore Risk. The total score is 11 (high risk). Which multi-component prevention plan is most appropriate and evidence-based?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Reposition every four hours and massage the reddened prominences with a moisturising cream at each turn, since massage restores capillary blood flow to ischaemic tissue"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reposition every 2 hours with a 30-degree lateral tilt, use a pressure-redistributing mattress, float the heels on pillows, and keep the skin dry and barrier-protected"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nurse the client in the high Fowler position at 90 degrees and place a ring cushion under the sacrum, so that pressure is distributed around rather than over the bony point"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Reposition every two hours but keep the head of the bed elevated at 60 degrees for comfort, and use a donut cushion to lift the sacrum clear of the mattress"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Reposition every 2 hours with a 30-degree lateral tilt, use a pressure-redistributing mattress, float the heels on pillows, and keep the skin dry and barrier-protected" A Braden score <= 12 signifies high risk for pressure injury development (scores range from 6 to 23; lower scores mean higher risk). Evidence-based prevention mandates: (1) frequent repositioning (at least every 2 hours in bed, every 1 hour in a chair); (2) using a 30-degree lateral tilted position (avoids direct vertical pressure on the trochanter and sacrum); (3) floating heels off the mattress using pillows under calves; (4) pressure-redistribution support surfaces; (5) moisture barriers for incontinence; and (6) avoiding elevation of the head of the bed > 30 degrees (reduces shear forces). Vigorously massaging bony prominences causes deep tissue capillary damage. Why the other options fail: option a doubles the interval and adds massage over an area that may already be ischaemic, which damages the deep capillary bed rather than restoring flow. Option c raises the head to 90 degrees, which converts pressure into shearing force through the sacrum, and a ring cushion concentrates pressure on the rim. Option d gets the interval right and then does both of the things that cause harm: sustained 60-degree elevation and a donut cushion.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pressure ulcer prevention: Reposition q2h + 30-degree tilt + float heels + pressure-relieving mattress. NEVER massage bony prominences or use donut rings.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An adult client was caught in a domestic gas explosion and sustained second- and third-degree burns covering the entire anterior chest, entire abdomen, the entire left arm (circumferential), and the anterior surface of the left leg. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the calculated Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) percentage of burn injury?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "27% TBSA"}, {"id": "b", "text": "36% TBSA"}, {"id": "c", "text": "45% TBSA"}, {"id": "d", "text": "18% TBSA"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "36% TBSA" Under the adult Wallace Rule of Nines: Head and neck = 9%; Anterior trunk = 18% (chest 9% + abdomen 9%); Posterior trunk = 18% (upper back 9% + lower back/buttocks 9%); Each arm = 9% total (anterior 4.5% + posterior 4.5%); Each leg = 18% total (anterior 9% + posterior 9%); Perineum/genitalia = 1%. In this client: Anterior chest (9%) + Entire abdomen (9%) + Entire left arm (9%) + Anterior left leg (9%) = 9 + 9 + 9 + 9 = 36% TBSA. Why the other options fail: option a at 27% omits the whole left arm. Option c at 45% adds the posterior trunk, which the description does not burn. Option d at 18% counts only the trunk and leaves out both the arm and the leg.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Rule of Nines calculation: Chest (9%) + Abdomen (9%) + Entire arm (9%) + Anterior leg (9%) = 36% TBSA.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 70 kg adult male arrives at the emergency department 1 hour after sustaining 40% TBSA partial- and full-thickness flame burns. Using the Parkland formula (4 mL x kg x % TBSA of Ringer's Lactate), calculate the total fluid requirement for the first 24 hours, and determine the rate at which fluids should be administered during the remaining 7 hours of the first 8-hour window.$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 5,600 mL; infuse half in the first 8 hours and the remainder over 16 hours, targeting a urine output of 30 to 50 mL per hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 22,400 mL; give the whole volume in the first 8 hours, because capillary leak is greatest immediately after the burn"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Total 11,200 mL; give 5,600 mL in the first 8 hours from injury, at 800 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours, then 5,600 mL over 16 hours"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Total 24-hour volume = 11,200 mL; infuse at 700 mL per hour evenly across the first 16 hours, since the first 8 hours have already elapsed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Total 11,200 mL; give 5,600 mL in the first 8 hours from injury, at 800 mL/hr over the remaining 7 hours, then 5,600 mL over 16 hours" The Parkland (Baxter) formula states: Total 24-hour fluid = 4 mL x weight (kg) x % TBSA burns. For this client: 4 mL x 70 kg x 40% = 11,200 mL of balanced crystalloid (Lactated Ringer's). Delivery schedule: 50% (5,600 mL) must be infused within the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF INJURY, and the remaining 50% (5,600 mL) over the next 16 hours (350 mL/hr). Because 1 hour elapsed before arrival, the initial 5,600 mL must be infused over the remaining 7 hours: 5,600 mL / 7 hours = 800 mL/hr. The nurse titrates this rate to maintain adult urine output at 0.5–1.0 mL/kg/hr (30–50 mL/hr). Why the other options fail: option a halves the formula, which delivers about half the resuscitation volume an adult with 40 per cent burns needs and will leave the client oliguric. Option b doubles the coefficient and then front-loads everything into the first 8 hours, which is the fastest route to pulmonary oedema. Option d gets the total right, which makes it the hardest distractor, but spreads it evenly and so abandons the 50 per cent rule and the eight-hour clock that starts at the time of injury, not arrival.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Parkland resuscitation: 4 mL x kg x % TBSA. 50% given in first 8 hours FROM TIME OF BURN (adjust for delay). Target urine output 0.5-1.0 mL/kg/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client rescued from an enclosed apartment fire presents with deep facial burns, singed eyebrows and nasal vibrissae, soot-stained carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, and an inspiratory stridor. Arterial blood gas shows a normal PaO2. What is the nurse's immediate emergency action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give humidified oxygen by face mask and admit for observation, since a normal PaO2 shows the airway is not yet compromised"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prepare immediately for endotracheal intubation before progressive laryngeal edema produces complete upper airway obstruction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Reassure the family that singed nasal hair is a cosmetic finding, and treat the hoarseness with voice rest and warm fluids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Recheck the arterial blood gas in 4 hours and call the anaesthetist if the stridor has worsened by then, to avoid an unnecessary tube"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Prepare immediately for endotracheal intubation before progressive laryngeal edema produces complete upper airway obstruction" Physical findings of singed nasal hairs, facial burns, carbonaceous sputum, hoarseness, and STRIDOR are pathognomonic of acute thermal inhalation injury and impending upper airway compromise. Supraglottic thermal injury causes rapid, massive laryngeal and vocal cord edema that peaks within 12–24 hours. Once stridor appears, the airway lumen is critically narrowed; delayed intubation frequently becomes impossible due to anatomical distortion, necessitating emergency surgical cricothyroidotomy. Proactive, early endotracheal intubation is the definitive lifesaving priority. Why the other options fail: option a rests on the normal PaO2, which is the trap in the stem: oxygenation is preserved until the airway closes, so a normal gas does not exclude an impending obstruction. Option c treats two of the four warning signs as trivial. Option d is the classic delay: stridor means the lumen is already critically narrowed, and by the time it worsens the swelling can make intubation impossible, leaving cricothyroidotomy as the only route.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Inhalation injury: Facial burns + soot + hoarseness/stridor = impending complete airway closure. Secure airway with IMMEDIATE intubation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client sustained a full-thickness, leathery, circumferential third-degree burn to the right forearm and hand. Four hours post-injury, the client reports severe, progressive, throbbing distal hand pain. The nurse notes that the right radial pulse is faint, capillary refill is 5 seconds, and passive extension of the fingers elicits excruciating pain. What complication is occurring, and what procedure is urgently required?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Extremity compartment syndrome caused by unyielding, inelastic burn eschar and tissue edema; prepare immediately for urgent bedside escharotomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Normal post-burn inflammation; elevate the limb above the level of the heart and give aspirin for the throbbing pain"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Deep vein thrombosis of the forearm; start low molecular weight heparin and apply a warm compress along the tender cord before reassessing the hand"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Nerve injury from the burn itself; document the sensory loss and reassess the hand at the next dressing round"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Extremity compartment syndrome caused by unyielding, inelastic burn eschar and tissue edema; prepare immediately for urgent bedside escharotomy" Circumferential full-thickness burns produce an inelastic, rigid, leathery rind of dead tissue (eschar). As massive fluid resuscitation expands interstitial edema within the limb, intracompartmental pressures rise rapidly, exceeding capillary perfusion pressure (> 30 mmHg). This leads to compartment syndrome: progressive neurovascular compromise characterized by the '6 Ps' (pain out of proportion/pain on passive stretch, paresthesia, pallor, pulselessness, poikilothermia, paralysis). Emergency bedside escharotomy (linear surgical incisions through the burned eschar down to subcutaneous fat) is mandatory to release constriction and restore arterial flow. Why the other options fail: option b interprets pain on passive stretch and a 5-second capillary refill as expected inflammation, and aspirin is irrelevant to a pressure problem that needs the eschar released. Option c is a plausible vascular cause of a faint pulse, but a thrombus does not produce excruciating pain on passive finger extension, and heparin would waste the window. Option d explains the sensory finding and ignores the vascular ones, and reassigning it to the next round delays a limb-threatening procedure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Circumferential burn danger: Faint pulse + pain on passive stretch = Compartment syndrome. Prepare for immediate emergency escharotomy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 54-year-old diabetic male presents with acute swelling, warmth, and erythema of the left lower leg. The client is tachycardic (pulse 128 beats/min), hypotensive (BP 86/50 mmHg), and reports excruciating, agony-level pain that is markedly out of proportion to the visible skin erythema. The nurse palpates subcutaneous crepitus (crackling gas) and notes small hemorrhagic bullae forming. What life-threatening infection must be suspected?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erysipelas, which can be managed at home with oral paracetamol and flucloxacillin, since the erythema is well demarcated and the leg is not tender"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Contact dermatitis from a laundry detergent; stop the product and apply a topical corticosteroid to the affected leg"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Necrotizing fasciitis (a surgical emergency requiring immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and emergency surgical debridement)"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Venous stasis pigmentation with a superimposed ulcer; apply compression bandaging and refer to the leg ulcer clinic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Necrotizing fasciitis (a surgical emergency requiring immediate broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and emergency surgical debridement)" Necrotizing fasciitis is a fulminant, rapidly progressive bacterial infection of the deep subcutaneous tissue and fascia with a mortality rate exceeding 25–40%. Cardinal warning signs distinguishing necrotizing fasciitis from benign cellulitis are: (1) severe, exquisite pain markedly OUT OF PROPORTION to superficial clinical findings; (2) rapid progression across marked skin margins within hours; (3) palpable subcutaneous crepitus (gas production from anaerobic pathogens); (4) bullae, skin necrosis, or dishwater-gray fluid discharge; and (5) early hemodynamic instability (septic shock). Immediate surgical fasciotomy and aggressive debridement are vital. Why the other options fail: option a names the right family of infection and the wrong depth: erysipelas is superficial, does not produce crepitus or bullae, and does not cause shock, so oral treatment at home would be fatal. Option b fits the erythema and none of the rest, and a steroid would suppress the only defence the tissue has. Option d is a chronic picture that develops over months, not an acute presentation with tachycardia, hypotension and gas in the tissues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Necrotizing fasciitis: Pain out of proportion to skin signs + crepitus/bullae + shock = Surgical emergency. Immediate radical debridement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 5-year-old child presents to the paediatric clinic with perioral erythematous papules that have ruptured into thick, golden, honey-colored crusts around the nose and mouth. The child is diagnosed with non-bullous impetigo. What is the essential nursing education for the parent regarding infection transmission and potential systemic complications?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Highly contagious: strict hand hygiene, separate towels, soak and remove crusts before mupirocin, and watch for tea-coloured urine (post-streptococcal GN)"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Impetigo spreads only by direct contact with the crusts, so the child can attend school provided the lesions are covered with a light bandage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Scrub the crusts off with a loofah at each bath so the ointment reaches the skin, and keep the child home until every lesion has healed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Impetigo is a self-limiting viral rash; keep the skin dry, avoid antibiotics altogether, and return only if the child develops a fever or the rash spreads"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Highly contagious: strict hand hygiene, separate towels, soak and remove crusts before mupirocin, and watch for tea-coloured urine (post-streptococcal GN)" Impetigo is a highly contagious superficial bacterial skin infection caused primarily by Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes (Group A Strep). Lesions typically begin as fragile vesicles that rupture, releasing exudate that dries into characteristic 'honey-colored crusts'. Nursing care focuses on: (1) preventing transmission (contact precautions, hand hygiene, separate towels/bedding, keeping child home until 24 hours of antimicrobial therapy); (2) crust removal (soaking with warm soapy water before applying topical mupirocin 2%); and (3) monitoring for post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (periorbital edema, gross hematuria/tea-colored urine, hypertension) 1–3 weeks post-infection. Why the other options fail: option c has the right instinct about crusts and the wrong method, since scrubbing spreads the exudate and breaks the skin further. Option d misclassifies a bacterial infection as viral and withholds the treatment that shortens it. Option b limits transmission to the crusts and stops there, which leaves out the hand hygiene, separate towels and renal follow-up that the question asks for, even though a child may return to school once treatment has begun.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Impetigo management: Honey-colored crusts = highly contagious. Warm soak crusts + mupirocin ointment + monitor for tea-colored urine (PSGN).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A mother and two children attend the community health center complaining of intense, intractable generalized itching that worsens severely at night. Physical examination reveals erythematous papules, excoriations, and linear burrows in the finger web spaces, wrists, axillary folds, and groin. The diagnosis is scabies (Sarcoptes scabiei). What instructions must the nurse provide to ensure eradication and prevent reinfection?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply permethrin only to the visible burrows on the wrists and wash it off after ten minutes, since the mite is said to be confined to the burrow itself and nowhere else"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Treat only the household members who are currently itchy, because those without symptoms are not infested and do not need any treatment"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight for 8 to 14 hours, treat all household contacts at the same time, and hot-wash bedding used in the past 3 days"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight, but delay treating the household contacts until the index child is symptom-free"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Apply 5% permethrin from the neck down overnight for 8 to 14 hours, treat all household contacts at the same time, and hot-wash bedding used in the past 3 days" Scabies is an infestation caused by the microscopic mite Sarcoptes scabiei var. hominis. Mites burrow into the stratum corneum and deposit eggs/feces, triggering a delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction characterized by severe nocturnal pruritus and pathognomonic linear burrows in interdigital web spaces, flexor wrists, axillae, umbilicus, and genitalia. Successful eradication mandates: (1) topical 5% permethrin cream applied from the jawline/neck down over the entire body (including under fingernails and between toes; in infants, the scalp and face are included) for 8–14 hours; (2) treating ALL household contacts simultaneously regardless of symptoms (incubation period is 4–6 weeks); and (3) hot-water laundering of linens. Why the other options fail: option a treats only what can be seen, but mites are present in clinically normal skin between burrows, and ten minutes is far short of the 8 to 14 hours needed for the cream to work. Option b ignores the four to six week incubation period, during which a contact is infectious without itching. Option d is the best of the three and still wrong on the one point that drives reinfestation: simultaneous treatment, because a delay simply re-infests the treated child.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Scabies eradication: 5% permethrin from neck down overnight (8-14 h) + treat ALL household contacts simultaneously + wash bedding in hot water.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 4-year-old child with severe atopic dermatitis (eczema) presents with erythematous, lichenified, excoriated plaques in the antecubital and popliteal fossae. The mother reports the child is irritable and scratches continuously until bleeding. What core barrier-repair strategy should the nurse teach the mother?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bathe the child three times daily in hot soapy water and scrub the skin with a dry loofah, so that the scale is removed before the emollient is applied"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Keep the skin as dry as possible and avoid all moisturisers, because emollients trap bacteria in the lichenified plaques and worsen the eczema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a daily dilute bleach bath and leave the skin to air dry completely before applying any topical corticosteroid to the raw creases"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bathe in lukewarm water for 10 minutes, pat damp, and apply a thick fragrance-free emollient within 3 minutes (the 'soak and seal' method)"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Bathe in lukewarm water for 10 minutes, pat damp, and apply a thick fragrance-free emollient within 3 minutes (the 'soak and seal' method)" Atopic dermatitis is characterized by epidermal skin barrier dysfunction (filaggrin deficiency), transepidermal water loss, and immune dysregulation ('the itch that rashes'). The cornerstone of maintenance therapy is the 'soak and seal' technique: (1) short lukewarm baths (not hot water, which strips natural lipids); (2) mild soap-free cleansers; (3) patting skin lightly so it remains slightly damp; and (4) liberal application of lipid-rich, fragrance-free emollients (ointments like petrolatum or thick creams) within 3 minutes of stepping out of the water to lock in hydration. Topical corticosteroids are reserved for acute inflammatory flares. Why the other options fail: option a uses heat and friction, both of which strip the lipid barrier and drive the itch-scratch cycle the plan exists to break. Option b inverts the treatment: emollients are the maintenance therapy, not a cause of infection. Option c borrows a real adjunct, dilute bleach bathing, and then air-dries the skin and jumps to a steroid, which skips the emollient that replaces the missing barrier.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Eczema barrier repair: Soak and seal -> Lukewarm bath + pat damp + apply thick ointment/cream within 3 minutes. Avoid hot water and fragrances.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 36-year-old client presents with well-demarcated, erythematous plaques covered by thick, adherent, silvery-white micaceous scales symmetrically distributed over both elbows, knees, and presacral area. When the nurse gently lifts a scale during examination, pinpoint capillary bleeding is observed. What are this lesion type and this clinical sign?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tinea corporis exhibiting the Darier sign"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Plaque psoriasis exhibiting the Auspitz sign"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Malignant melanoma exhibiting the Breslow phenomenon"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lichen planus exhibiting Wickham striae"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Plaque psoriasis exhibiting the Auspitz sign" Plaque psoriasis (psoriasis vulgaris) is a chronic immune-mediated inflammatory dermatosis driven by T-lymphocytes and cytokines (IL-17, IL-23, TNF-alpha), resulting in hyperproliferation of epidermal keratinocytes (epidermal turnover accelerates from 28 days to 3–4 days). The hallmark lesions are sharply demarcated erythematous plaques covered with silvery, micaceous scales on extensor surfaces. The Auspitz sign is the appearance of punctate capillary bleeding spots when scales are scraped off, caused by thinned suprapapillary epidermal plates over dilated, tortuous dermal capillaries. Lesions developing at sites of skin trauma demonstrate the Koebner phenomenon. Why the other options fail: option a gives tinea the Darier sign, but Darier sign is the urticarial response produced by rubbing a mastocytoma. Option c pairs melanoma with Breslow correctly, but Breslow thickness is a histological measurement rather than a bedside sign. Option d pairs lichen planus with Wickham striae correctly, but the silvery scale and pinpoint bleeding described belong to psoriasis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Psoriasis hallmarks: Silvery-white scales on extensor surfaces + pinpoint bleeding when scraped (Auspitz sign) + new lesions at trauma sites (Koebner).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who started cotrimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) 10 days ago presents with high fever, sore throat, severe conjunctival injection, painful oral ulcerations, and a widespread dusky purpuric maculopapular rash. When the nurse applies gentle lateral friction to the client's skin, the epidermal sheet slides off easily (positive Nikolsky sign), leaving raw, denuded dermis over 25% TBSA. What condition does the nurse recognize, and what is the immediate intervention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "TEN/SJS overlap; stop the sulfonamide immediately and transfer to a burns intensive care unit for fluid and wound management"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A morbilliform drug exanthem; stop the cotrimoxazole, give an oral antihistamine, and review in the outpatient clinic in one week"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; start intravenous flucloxacillin and keep the skin moist with paraffin gauze until it re-epithelialises"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Erythema multiforme major; continue the antibiotic, give systemic corticosteroids, and reassess the mucosal lesions daily on the ward"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "TEN/SJS overlap; stop the sulfonamide immediately and transfer to a burns intensive care unit for fluid and wound management" Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) are life-threatening Severe Cutaneous Adverse Reactions (SCAR) characterized by widespread epidermal apoptosis and detachment triggered primarily by medications (sulfonamides, antiepileptics like carbamazepine/phenytoin, allopurinol, nevirapine). SJS involves < 10% TBSA epidermal detachment; SJS/TEN overlap involves 10–30% TBSA; TEN involves > 30% TBSA. Mucosal involvement (lips, oral cavity, eyes, genitalia) is present in > 90% of cases. A positive Nikolsky sign (sloughing of epidermis with light tangential shear) reflects epidermal-dermal cleavage. Immediate cessation of the causative drug and transfer to a specialized burn unit are vital. Why the other options fail: option b describes the common benign drug rash and misses mucosal erosion and a positive Nikolsky sign, either of which rules it out, and a week's outpatient review is far too slow. Option c is a real blistering disease but it is caused by staphylococcal toxin in young children, splits at the granular layer, and does not follow a sulfonamide. Option d keeps the culprit drug running, which is the single most dangerous thing to do in TEN.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SJS / TEN: Sulfonamide trigger + mucosal erosion + positive Nikolsky sign (epidermal sloughing). STOP DRUG IMMEDIATELY and treat like severe burns in ICU.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a skin screening assessment, a dark-skinned Ghanaian farmer asks the nurse to evaluate an irregular, dark-brown to black, enlarging macule with jagged borders beneath the nail bed of his right great toe (subungual) and on the plantar surface of his foot. Which type of skin malignancy is most prevalent in individuals with deeply pigmented skin, and what mnemonic guides early detection?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Superficial spreading melanoma; the commonest subtype in all populations, arising on sun-exposed skin and evaluated with the ABCDE criteria"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Benign subungual lentigo; a harmless pigment band that needs no biopsy and can be reviewed yearly with clinical photography"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Basal cell carcinoma; a slow-growing, pearly, sun-related tumour of the face that rarely metastasises and is treated by excision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acral lentiginous melanoma, the commonest subtype in Black populations, arising on palms, soles and nail beds; assessed with ABCDE"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Acral lentiginous melanoma, the commonest subtype in Black populations, arising on palms, soles and nail beds; assessed with ABCDE" While overall melanoma incidence is lower in dark-skinned individuals due to the photoprotective effect of eumelanin against UV radiation, when melanoma does occur in African, Black, and Asian populations, it is disproportionately Acral Lentiginous Melanoma (ALM), accounting for 50–70% of all melanomas in these populations. Unlike other melanomas, ALM is NOT related to UV radiation/sun exposure and characteristically develops on glabrous skin: palms, soles, and subungual nail beds (Hutchinson's sign). The ABCDE mnemonic (Asymmetry, Border irregularity, Color variegation, Diameter > 6 mm, Evolution/change) guides identification, and suspicious lesions mandate urgent excisional biopsy. Why the other options fail: option a is the commonest melanoma overall and the wrong answer for this population, and it is described as sun-related, which the acral form is not. Option b reassures on a lesion with irregular borders that is enlarging, which is the description that mandates biopsy. Option c is a pigmented facial tumour, not a subungual or plantar one, and it does not fit an enlarging irregular macule on the sole.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Melanoma in dark skin: Acral lentiginous melanoma (palms, soles, nail beds) is NOT sun-related and is the most common subtype in Black populations. Use ABCDE.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A full-term 2-day-old neonate displays a generalized, benign rash consisting of small, firm, white-to-yellow papules and pustules on an erythematous base (resembling 'flea bites') distributed over the trunk, chest, and arms, with sparing of the palms and soles. The infant is feeding vigorously, afebrile, and active. A smear of pustular content reveals abundant eosinophils. What is the diagnosis and correct nursing management?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Erythema toxicum neonatorum; a benign, self-limiting newborn eruption that needs no treatment and resolves within 1 to 2 weeks"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Congenital syphilis; start intravenous benzylpenicillin, screen the mother, and isolate the infant from other babies on the postnatal ward"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome; obtain blood cultures, start intravenous flucloxacillin, and prepare the infant for skin grafting"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Neonatal herpes simplex infection; perform a lumbar puncture and start high-dose intravenous aciclovir on the neonatal unit"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Erythema toxicum neonatorum; a benign, self-limiting newborn eruption that needs no treatment and resolves within 1 to 2 weeks" Erythema toxicum neonatorum is the most common benign cutaneous eruption in healthy full-term neonates (affecting 30–70% of newborns), typically appearing on days 1–4 of life. It presents with erythematous macules, papules, and sterile pustules with a classic 'flea-bitten' appearance over the trunk and proximal extremities, sparing palms and soles. Wright or Giemsa staining of pustular fluid reveals eosinophils (distinguishing it from bacterial or candidal pustules with neutrophils). It is self-limiting, causes no systemic symptoms, and resolves spontaneously within 7–14 days without treatment. Parental reassurance is the only indicated intervention. Why the other options fail: option b is the invasive infection to consider in a sick neonate, but this infant is feeding, afebrile and active, and syphilis does not produce eosinophil-filled pustules. Option c causes widespread tender erythema and epidermal peeling rather than scattered flea-bitten pustules on an erythematous base. Option d is the correct response to a genuinely unwell baby with vesicles, and none of the features that define herpes, such as grouped vesicles, are present.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Newborn rashes: Flea-bitten pustules on healthy term neonate + eosinophils on smear = Erythema toxicum neonatorum. Benign, self-limiting -> Reassure parents.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 84-year-old frail female sustained a skin tear over her anterior forearm during transfer from bed to chair. The epidermal flap is peeled back but remains viable, and the wound edges can be re-approximated to cover the exposed dermis (Type 1 skin tear). How should the nurse manage this wound to facilitate healing and prevent further trauma?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Trim the flap away with scissors and clean the exposed dermis with alcohol, since a raised flap traps bacteria and delays epithelial migration across it"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse with normal saline, re-approximate the flap without stretching, and secure it with a silicone non-adherent dressing or retention bandage"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Apply a high-adhesion zinc oxide tape directly over the raw dermis and change it every four hours, so that the wound is kept clean and visible"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the wound uncovered and open to the air, so that a firm scab can form over the denuded dermis and protect it from further shearing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Cleanse with normal saline, re-approximate the flap without stretching, and secure it with a silicone non-adherent dressing or retention bandage" In older adults, epidermal thinning, flattening of the dermal-epidermal junction (rete ridges), loss of dermal collagen, and microvascular atrophy make the skin exceptionally fragile, predisposing to skin tears from minor shear or friction. According to the International Skin Tear Advisory Panel (ISTAP): for a Type 1 skin tear (where the flap can cover the entire wound bed), the nurse must: (1) gently irrigate with normal saline; (2) gently re-approximate the viable epidermal flap using a moist sterile swab or gloved finger; (3) secure with a non-adherent dressing (silicone foam or mesh); and (4) NEVER apply adhesive tape directly to the fragile peri-wound skin. Why the other options fail: option a discards viable tissue that acts as a natural biological dressing and applies alcohol to a raw dermis, which is painful and cytotoxic. Option c uses the adhesive the guidance explicitly warns against, on the same fragile peri-wound skin that tore to begin with, and changes it four-hourly. Option d leaves the denuded dermis to dry and scab, which is slower and more painful, and gives no protection against the next shear.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Skin tear management: Cleanse gently -> Roll back/re-approximate viable flap -> Cover with non-adherent silicone dressing. NO adhesive tape on frail skin.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client is prescribed a potent topical corticosteroid cream (clobetasol propionate 0.05%) for a flare of severe localized eczema on the back. What principle of topical steroid administration and dosing (Fingertip Unit) must the nurse teach the client to prevent local and systemic adverse effects?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Apply the entire 60 g tube like thick cake icing twice daily, so that the eczema is fully suppressed before the tube runs out rather than being left under-treated"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Potent topical steroids are safe on any site for any duration, so apply clobetasol to the eyelids and face twice daily and continue it indefinitely"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1 FTU (about 0.5 g from the finger crease to tip) covers two adult handprints; use the least amount needed and avoid potent steroids on face, groin and axillae"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a fingertip unit measuring about 5 g to the back only, since the unit is calculated for the trunk and larger areas need proportionally more"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "1 FTU (about 0.5 g from the finger crease to tip) covers two adult handprints; use the least amount needed and avoid potent steroids on face, groin and axillae" The Fingertip Unit (FTU) is the standardized metric for topical dermatological medications: 1 FTU is the amount of ointment/cream expressed from a standard 5 mm nozzle tube from the distal skin crease to the tip of an adult index finger (~0.5 g). One FTU adequately covers a surface area equivalent to TWO adult handprints (with fingers joined). Potent and super-potent topical corticosteroids (like clobetasol) carry serious risks of local adverse effects (epidermal atrophy, telangiectasia, striae, hypopigmentation, secondary infection) and systemic absorption (HPA axis suppression, Cushing syndrome). They are strictly avoided on high-absorption, thin-skinned areas such as the face, neck, groin, and axillae. Why the other options fail: option a wastes the tube and increases systemic absorption without improving the flare, and a whole-body application of a super-potent steroid is a route to adrenal suppression. Option b states the opposite of the guidance: thin sites absorb far more per unit area, so the face, groin and axillae are exactly where atrophy and striae appear first. Option d inflates the unit tenfold, which turns a measure of 0.5 g into 5 g per application.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Topical steroid dosing: 1 FTU = 0.5 g = 2 adult handprints. Use sparingly, short-term. Avoid potent steroids on face, groin, and axillae (atrophy risk).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client has a heavily draining Stage 3 sacral pressure ulcer with copious serosanguinous exudate that is saturating multiple dressing pads within 2 hours. Which dressing category is best suited to absorb this heavy exudate, support moist wound healing, and protect periwound skin from maceration?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A thin semi-permeable film dressing, which seals bacteria out and lets exudate evaporate through the membrane between changes"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Dry single-ply cotton gauze, changed whenever it becomes saturated, because cotton wicks exudate away from the bed"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A hydrocolloid sheet changed every three days, since it forms a gel that absorbs exudate and protects the wound"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Calcium alginate or hydrofiber rope/pad dressings covered by an absorbent secondary foam dressing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Calcium alginate or hydrofiber rope/pad dressings covered by an absorbent secondary foam dressing" Dressing selection must match wound exudate levels according to the moisture balance principle. Calcium alginates (derived from seaweed) and hydrofiber dressings are highly absorbent, capable of absorbing 15–20 times their weight in exudate. When contacting wound fluid, sodium ions in exudate exchange with calcium ions in the alginate, forming a hydrophilic non-adherent gel that traps bacteria, maintains moisture balance, and prevents periwound maceration. In contrast, thin film dressings have zero absorption capacity and would trap fluid causing massive maceration. Dry gauze adheres to the wound and traumatizes new capillary buds upon removal. Why the other options fail: option a is the dressing for a wound with no exudate at all; a film has no absorption capacity, so fluid pools beneath it and macerates the skin it was meant to protect. Option b looks absorbent and is not: gauze holds little, dries out, and adheres to a granulating bed, so every change strips new tissue. Option c is right for moderate exudate and overwhelmed by copious drainage, and a three-day interval with pads saturating in two hours guarantees leakage.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dressing selection: Heavy exudate = Calcium alginate or Hydrofiber + Foam. Dry/necrotic wound = Hydrogel. Minimal drainage = Thin film / Hydrocolloid.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-INTEG-020$q$;

