UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is calculating the total daily caloric intake for a client whose prescribed nutritional meal plan provides 250 g of carbohydrates, 80 g of protein, and 50 g of fat. What is the total energy content provided by this diet?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "3,420 kcal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "1,520 kcal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "1,770 kcal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "1,320 kcal"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "1,770 kcal" Nutrient energy calculations use the Atwater physiological fuel values: carbohydrate 4 kcal/g, protein 4 kcal/g, fat 9 kcal/g (alcohol 7 kcal/g). This plan gives carbohydrate 250 g x 4 = 1,000 kcal; protein 80 g x 4 = 320 kcal; fat 50 g x 9 = 450 kcal; total 1,000 + 320 + 450 = 1,770 kcal. Why the other options fail: 3,420 kcal applies 9 kcal/g to every macronutrient, which overstates carbohydrate and protein, both of which yield 4 kcal/g. 1,520 kcal applies 4 kcal/g to fat, understating fat at 9 kcal/g. 1,320 kcal counts only carbohydrate and protein and omits the 450 kcal contributed by fat entirely.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Macronutrient caloric math: Carbs = 4 kcal/g, Protein = 4 kcal/g, Fat = 9 kcal/g. Multiply each by its multiplier and sum the products.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with microcytic iron-deficiency anaemia is prescribed oral ferrous sulphate tablets. The nurse provides dietary guidance to maximize non-heme iron absorption. Which dietary strategy should the nurse recommend?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take the iron with a vitamin C source such as orange juice or baobab pulp, and keep tea, coffee, milk, and antacids at least 2 hours away from the dose"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the iron with the largest meal of the day, because food buffers gastric irritation and improves absorption"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Take the iron with a glass of milk or a calcium supplement, which coats the stomach and prevents nausea"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the iron with a cup of strong tea or cocoa to disguise the metallic taste, since this improves tolerance of the tablets and makes the full course easier to finish"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Take the iron with a vitamin C source such as orange juice or baobab pulp, and keep tea, coffee, milk, and antacids at least 2 hours away from the dose" Non-heme iron (plant sources and oral iron supplements) exists in the insoluble ferric (Fe3+) state. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) reduces ferric iron to the soluble, easily absorbed ferrous (Fe2+) state and prevents formation of unabsorbable complexes, significantly enhancing intestinal absorption. Conversely, polyphenols and tannins (found in tea, coffee, cocoa), phytates (in unrefined bran, whole grains), calcium (in dairy products), and antacids bind iron and inhibit absorption by up to 60–80%. The nurse must teach the client to space tea, coffee, and dairy at least 2 hours away from iron doses. Why the other options fail: taking iron with a meal (option b) is the commonest patient habit and is plausible because it reduces nausea, but food binds the ferrous ion and cuts absorption by roughly 40 to 50 percent, so timing relative to meals matters. Milk and calcium (option c) are the trap of a familiar comfort measure: calcium competes directly with iron for absorption in the enterocyte. Tea and cocoa (option d) are pleasant to take but their polyphenols and tannins complex ferric iron in the gut lumen and can reduce uptake by more than half.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Iron bioavailability: Vitamin C enhances iron absorption (Fe3+ -> Fe2+). Tannins (tea), phytates (bran), and calcium (milk) inhibit absorption; space by 2 hours.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse conducts a comprehensive nutritional assessment using the ABCD approach (Anthropometric, Biochemical, Clinical, Dietary). Which physical sign observed during the clinical examination strongly suggests severe riboflavin (vitamin B2) or iron deficiency?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Bilateral painless enlargement of the parotid glands, with loss of the buccal fat pad"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Transverse white bands across the fingernails, with thinning of the hair and easy pluckability"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Swollen, friable gums that bleed on gentle brushing, with loose teeth in otherwise sound molars and old scars that fail to heal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Angular cheilitis with fissuring at the mouth corners, together with an atrophic, smooth, beefy-red tongue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Angular cheilitis with fissuring at the mouth corners, together with an atrophic, smooth, beefy-red tongue" The clinical ('C') component of the ABCD nutritional assessment evaluates physical signs of nutrient deficiency on mucosal surfaces, skin, hair, and nails. Angular cheilitis (stomatitis with fissuring and maceration at labial commissures) and glossitis (inflammation, erythema, and papillary atrophy of the tongue) are classic physical indicators of riboflavin (vitamin B2), pyridoxine (vitamin B6), iron, or folate/B12 deficiencies. Koilonychia (spoon nails) is the nail sign of chronic severe iron deficiency, and it is not what option b describes: transverse white bands are Mees' lines, not spooning. Why the other options fail: bilateral parotid enlargement (option a) is a real nutritional sign but points to bulimia, starvation, or HIV salivary gland disease, none of which the stem describes, and the buccal fat pad is lost in wasting rather than in B2 deficiency. Transverse white nail bands (option b) are Mees' lines, seen after arsenic exposure, severe systemic illness, or chemotherapy, not riboflavin or iron deficiency. Friable bleeding gums with loose teeth (option c) are the gingival picture of scurvy, which is vitamin C deficiency; it is a mucosal sign, but a different nutrient from the one the stem asks about.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical signs of deficiency: Angular cheilitis and glossitis indicate riboflavin (B2), B-complex, or iron deficiency. Inspect oral mucosa closely.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a community child health outreach, a community health nurse measures the Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC) of an 18-month-old child using a standard color-banded Shakir strip. The measurement is 11.2 cm (RED zone). How should the nurse triage and manage this child?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Classify as moderate acute malnutrition, give a single dose of vitamin A, and review the child at the clinic in 4 weeks once the oedema has settled"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Enrol the child for outpatient care only if bilateral pitting oedema is absent, and refer for inpatient stabilisation only once the MUAC has fallen below 10.5 cm on two separate measurements"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Repeat the MUAC measurement after 2 weeks of supplementary feeding at home, and assign a classification only if the value has not improved by then"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Classify as severe acute malnutrition, do an appetite test with ready-to-use therapeutic food, check for pitting oedema and danger signs, and enrol in outpatient or inpatient care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Classify as severe acute malnutrition, do an appetite test with ready-to-use therapeutic food, check for pitting oedema and danger signs, and enrol in outpatient or inpatient care" MUAC is a rapid, validated, age-independent screening tool for acute wasting in children aged 6 to 59 months: (1) Red (< 11.5 cm) = Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM), carrying a 9- to 11-fold increased risk of mortality; (2) Yellow (11.5 to 12.4 cm) = Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM); (3) Green (>= 12.5 cm) = Normal nutritional status. A child with a MUAC of 11.2 cm has SAM. Management requires: assessment for bilateral pitting edema, checking for medical complications (fever, hypothermia, severe anemia, hypoglycemia, respiratory distress), conducting an RUTF appetite test, and admitting to Inpatient Stabilization (if complications present or failed appetite test) or Outpatient Therapeutic Program (OTP). Why the other options fail: option a under-calls the severity. The yellow MAM band runs from 11.5 to 12.4 cm, and 11.2 cm sits below it in the red band, so a single vitamin A dose and a four-week review leaves a child with a nine- to eleven-fold mortality risk untreated. Option b invents a threshold that does not exist: MUAC below 10.5 cm is not an admission criterion, and oedema makes a child severely malnourished at any MUAC rather than excluding them from care. Option c delays a decision that must be made now; two weeks of home feeding in a child at this risk is time the child does not have.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pediatric MUAC cutoffs: < 11.5 cm = RED (Severe Acute Malnutrition). Urgent appetite test + medical screening -> OTP with RUTF or Inpatient care.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A surgical nurse evaluates laboratory nutritional markers for a malnourished client receiving enteral nutrition. Why is serum prealbumin (transthyretin) considered a superior marker for assessing acute nutritional recovery compared to serum albumin?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Albumin responds within 48 hours of adequate intake, so it detects acute nutritional recovery earlier than prealbumin does"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Prealbumin is filtered and reabsorbed by the renal tubule, so its serum level reflects the glomerular filtration rate rather than recent protein intake"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Prealbumin has a half-life of 2 to 3 days against 20 days for albumin, so it tracks real-time changes in visceral protein synthesis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Albumin has a shorter half-life than prealbumin, so it falls faster during acute illness and recovers faster with feeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Prealbumin has a half-life of 2 to 3 days against 20 days for albumin, so it tracks real-time changes in visceral protein synthesis" Serum prealbumin (transthyretin) has a short circulating half-life of 2–3 days and a small total body pool, making it highly sensitive to acute changes in protein-calorie intake; levels rise rapidly within 48–72 hours of adequate nutritional support. In contrast, albumin has a long half-life (approximately 20 days) and a large extravascular reserve, making it a poor indicator of acute nutritional change. Furthermore, albumin is a negative acute-phase reactant whose hepatic synthesis is suppressed by cytokines during inflammation, infection, trauma, and liver disease, and serum levels drop dramatically during fluid overload due to hemodilution. Why the other options fail: option a inverts the half-lives. Albumin turns over over about 20 days and sits on a large extravascular reserve, so a nutritional change takes weeks to register. Option b attributes prealbumin's level to renal handling; transthyretin is not a filtration marker in this sense, and its fall reflects reduced hepatic synthesis, not tubular loss. Option d is exactly backwards: albumin's long half-life is why it lags, and its fall during inflammation reflects cytokine suppression of synthesis and haemodilution, not a fast turnover.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nutritional biomarkers: Prealbumin half-life = 2-3 days (acute, sensitive). Albumin half-life = 20 days (poor acute marker, confounded by inflammation/hydration).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 26-year-old woman attends the preconception clinic planning a pregnancy within the next 3 months. She has no chronic medical conditions and no prior affected pregnancies. What folic acid supplementation should the nurse prescribe, and why is periconceptional timing essential?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Folic acid 5 mg daily from the first antenatal visit, because the neural tube closes during the second trimester"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Folic acid 400 mcg daily once pregnancy is confirmed by ultrasound at 8 weeks, continued until delivery"}, {"id": "c", "text": "No supplement is needed when the diet is rich in green leafy vegetables and legumes, provided that iron stores and haemoglobin are normal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Folic acid 400 mcg daily from at least 1 month before conception through the first trimester, to prevent neural tube defects"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Folic acid 400 mcg daily from at least 1 month before conception through the first trimester, to prevent neural tube defects" The embryonic neural tube closes completely between day 21 and day 28 post-conception (approximately 3 to 4 weeks after fertilization), often before a woman realizes she is pregnant. Periconceptional folic acid supplementation (400 mcg or 0.4 mg daily for average-risk women) started at least 1 month prior to conception and continued through the 12th week of gestation reduces the incidence of neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly, encephalocele) by up to 70%. High-risk women (history of previous NTD, maternal diabetes, obesity, sickle cell, or taking antiepileptic drugs) require a higher dose of 4–5 mg daily. Why the other options fail: option a carries the high-risk dose and the wrong clock. Five milligrams is reserved for women with a previous affected pregnancy, diabetes, obesity, sickle cell disease, or anticonvulsant use, and the neural tube is closed by day 28, long before the second trimester. Option b starts after the window has shut; ultrasound confirmation at 8 weeks is five weeks past closure. Option c trusts diet alone, but dietary folate is poorly bioavailable and the protective evidence rests on periconceptional supplementation, so a good diet does not substitute for the tablet.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Periconceptional folate: 400 mcg daily starting >= 1 month BEFORE conception through 12 weeks gestation. Neural tube closes by day 28 post-conception.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A first-time mother brings her 4-month-old infant to the child welfare clinic. The grandmother advises the mother to give the baby water and thin maize porridge because 'the weather is too hot for breast milk alone.' What evidence-based guidance should the nurse deliver?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give boiled water between feeds in hot weather, because breast milk alone cannot replace the water a baby loses through sweating and breathing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Introduce thin maize porridge at 4 months, since breast milk no longer meets energy needs after 3 months of age"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Continue exclusive breastfeeding to 6 months; breast milk is about 88% water and meets all fluid and nutrient needs even in hot weather"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Offer sugar water after each feed to prevent hypoglycaemia until the infant is 6 months old and eating solids"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Continue exclusive breastfeeding to 6 months; breast milk is about 88% water and meets all fluid and nutrient needs even in hot weather" WHO and UNICEF recommend Exclusive Breastfeeding (EBF) for the first 6 months of life (no other liquids or solids, not even water, except for vitamins, mineral supplements, or oral rehydration salts/medicines). Breast milk is 87–88% water with low osmolarity, satisfying all infant hydration requirements even in arid tropical climates. Giving water, teas, or thin porridges fills the infant's small stomach, decreases breast suckling (lowering maternal prolactin and milk supply), and introduces waterborne pathogens causing diarrhoeal diseases, the leading cause of infant mortality in low-resource settings. Why the other options fail: option a is the grandmother's argument in clinical clothing. It sounds like sensible hydration, but breast milk is low in solute and high in water, so an exclusively breastfed infant in a hot climate is not water-depleted, and top-up water displaces a feed and its protein. Option b brings complementary feeding forward by two months, against WHO guidance, and thin porridge is energy-dilute, which is how infants become malnourished rather than better nourished. Option d adds sugar without protein or micronutrients and fills the small stomach, reducing suckling and therefore prolactin-driven milk production.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Exclusive breastfeeding: EBF for first 6 months (zero water/solids). Breast milk is 88% water and provides complete hydration even in tropical heat.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 82-year-old client exhibits microcytic anaemia, mild cognitive decline, and bilateral sensory paresthesia in the lower extremities. Serum vitamin B12 level is low. The client eats fish and eggs regularly. What age-related physiological alteration of the gastrointestinal system explains this vitamin B12 deficiency?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Atrophic gastritis with hypochlorhydria reduces acid and pepsin, so protein-bound B12 cannot be cleaved for absorption"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Loss of intrinsic factor alone, because parietal cell loss is the sole cause of B12 malabsorption in older adults and no other mechanism contributes at all"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Bacterial overgrowth in the small bowel, which competes for dietary B12 before it can reach the terminal ileum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Enhanced renal excretion of transcobalamin-bound B12, secondary to an age-related fall in glomerular filtration"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Atrophic gastritis with hypochlorhydria reduces acid and pepsin, so protein-bound B12 cannot be cleaved for absorption" Age-related atrophic gastritis affects 10–30% of older adults, leading to loss of gastric parietal cells, hypochlorhydria (decreased hydrochloric acid), and diminished pepsin secretion. Dietary vitamin B12 is bound to animal proteins; gastric acid and pepsin are mandatory to cleave B12 from food proteins so it can bind to haptocorrin and subsequently to intrinsic factor for terminal ileal absorption. When gastric acid is low, protein-bound B12 malabsorption develops despite adequate dietary intake. Older adults frequently require crystalline B12 supplements (which do not require acid cleavage) or parenteral B12 injections. Why the other options fail: option b names a genuine mechanism but makes it exclusive. This client eats fish and eggs, so the food-bound fraction is precisely the part that fails first, and intrinsic factor loss is a contributing factor here rather than the sole explanation the stem asks for. Option c is a real cause of B12 deficiency in blind loops and strictures, but nothing in the history suggests stasis, surgery, or a structural lesion. Option d invents a route of loss; B12 is conserved through enterohepatic circulation, and falling filtration rate does not strip transcobalamin-bound vitamin from plasma.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Geriatric B12 malabsorption: Atrophic gastritis -> hypochlorhydria -> inability to cleave protein-bound B12 -> B12 deficiency and neurological deficits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is teaching a newly diagnosed type 2 diabetic client about Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) using the 'Plate Method'. How should the nurse instruct the client to divide a standard 9-inch dinner plate for optimal glycemic control?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Half the plate brown rice and boiled yam, one quarter fruit, and one quarter lean fish or skinless chicken"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Half the plate non-starchy vegetables, one quarter lean protein such as fish or beans, and one quarter complex carbohydrate such as boiled yam"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Half the plate lean protein, one quarter starchy vegetables, and one quarter fruit, with no grain or starchy staple allowed at any meal of the day"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The whole plate non-starchy vegetables, with two slices of whole-grain bread and a banana taken on the side"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Half the plate non-starchy vegetables, one quarter lean protein such as fish or beans, and one quarter complex carbohydrate such as boiled yam" The Diabetes Plate Method is a visual, practical tool for portion control and carbohydrate consistency: (1) Half the plate (50%) consists of non-starchy vegetables (greens, tomatoes, okra, cabbage, peppers) which are rich in fiber, micronutrients, and water with minimal impact on blood glucose; (2) One quarter (25%) consists of lean protein sources (fish, chicken without skin, legumes, eggs); (3) One quarter (25%) consists of complex, high-fiber, low-glycemic carbohydrates (unrefined grains, brown rice, yam, plantain, whole wheat). This structure prevents postprandial glycemic spikes and promotes satiety. Why the other options fail: option a looks healthier than it is, because brown rice, yam, and fruit are all carbohydrate, so half the plate plus a fruit quarter delivers a carbohydrate load closer to three quarters. Option c over-corrects in the protein direction and still stacks starchy vegetables with fruit, and its ban on grain is not part of the plate method. Option d keeps the vegetables but puts the carbohydrate outside the plate, where it is no longer being portioned, which defeats the visual control the method exists to provide.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Diabetes plate method: 1/2 non-starchy vegetables + 1/4 lean protein + 1/4 complex carbohydrate. Maximizes fiber and controls postprandial glucose.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with essential hypertension is prescribed the Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension (DASH) eating plan. Which nutrient profile characterizes the DASH diet, and what is the recommended daily sodium limit for optimal blood pressure reduction?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "High in potassium, calcium, magnesium, and fibre, with sodium below 2,300 mg/day and ideally 1,500 mg/day"}, {"id": "b", "text": "High in potassium and calcium from fruit and low-fat dairy, with sodium limited to 3,000 mg/day for blood pressure control"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Low in saturated fat, but potassium and magnesium are unrestricted because they do not influence blood pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Emphasises whole grains and nuts, with sodium replaced at every meal by potassium-based salt substitutes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "High in potassium, calcium, magnesium, and fibre, with sodium below 2,300 mg/day and ideally 1,500 mg/day" The DASH diet is an evidence-based nutritional intervention that lowers systolic blood pressure by 8–14 mmHg. It emphasizes: (1) rich intake of fruits and vegetables (4–5 servings of each daily); (2) low-fat dairy products (providing calcium); (3) whole grains, poultry, fish, and nuts (providing magnesium and potassium); and (4) marked reduction in sodium, red meats, sweets, and saturated fats. Potassium, calcium, and magnesium promote vascular smooth muscle relaxation and urinary sodium excretion. Restricting sodium to < 2,300 mg/day (or <= 1,500 mg/day in hypertensive individuals) provides additive blood pressure lowering. Why the other options fail: option b gets the foods right and the number wrong. Three thousand milligrams is above the upper limit that defines the plan, and the blood-pressure benefit is dose-dependent, which is why 1,500 mg is the stronger target. Option c states the opposite of the mechanism: potassium and magnesium are among the nutrients that lower blood pressure, and their intake is a defining feature of DASH rather than an irrelevance. Option d sounds like DASH but swaps a dietary pattern for a product, and salt substitutes are hazardous in the renal impairment that commonly accompanies hypertension.$q$,
  strategy = $q$DASH diet: High potassium, magnesium, calcium + high fiber + sodium <= 1,500-2,300 mg/day. Proven to lower BP significantly.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with Stage 4 Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD; eGFR 22 mL/min/1.73 m², not on dialysis) requires dietary counselling. Which nutritional restrictions must the nurse emphasize to prevent uremic complications, hyperkalaemia, and renal osteodystrophy?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Increase protein to 1.5 g/kg/day to replace losses, and restrict only sodium and fluid until dialysis begins"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Protein 0.6 to 0.8 g/kg/day, with potassium, phosphorus, and sodium restricted and potassium-based salt substitutes avoided"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Restrict potassium and phosphorus only, since protein does not raise urea until dialysis has been started"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict sodium and phosphorus only, and use potassium-based salt substitutes freely at every meal to make the bland diet palatable"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Protein 0.6 to 0.8 g/kg/day, with potassium, phosphorus, and sodium restricted and potassium-based salt substitutes avoided" In advanced pre-dialysis CKD (Stage 4–5): (1) Protein is controlled to 0.6–0.8 g/kg/day to decrease nitrogenous waste production (blood urea nitrogen) and slow renal functional decline (once dialysis begins, protein is increased to 1.2 g/kg to replace dialysate amino acid loss); (2) Potassium restriction (< 2–3 g/day) is essential because failing nephrons cannot excrete potassium, risking fatal cardiac arrhythmias; (3) Phosphorus restriction (< 800–1,000 mg/day) prevents hyperphosphatemia, secondary hyperparathyroidism, and vascular calcification; and (4) Sodium restriction (< 2 g/day) controls fluid retention and hypertension. Why the other options fail: option a imports the dialysis prescription into the pre-dialysis stage. Protein rises to about 1.2 g/kg only once dialysis replaces amino acids lost in the dialysate, and 1.5 g/kg at an eGFR of 22 generates a nitrogen load the failing kidney cannot clear. Option c denies the central mechanism of uraemia, which is protein catabolism raising blood urea nitrogen well before dialysis. Option d removes the two restrictions that matter most here and adds a potassium load, which is the most dangerous omission in a client who cannot excrete potassium.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CKD pre-dialysis nutrition: Moderate protein (0.6-0.8 g/kg) + restrict potassium + restrict phosphorus + restrict sodium. Avoid potassium salt substitutes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 22-year-old client is diagnosed with Celiac Disease (gluten-sensitive enteropathy) confirmed by duodenal biopsy demonstrating villous blunting. Which grains must the nurse instruct the client to eliminate completely from their diet, and which staples are safe alternatives?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Eliminate rice, maize, and cassava, which cross-react with gliadin, while allowing wheat in small amounts"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Avoid all grains including rice and millet, and base the entire diet on ordinary oats that have not been certified gluten-free"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Eliminate wheat, rye, barley, and contaminated oats, substituting rice, maize, cassava, yam, millet, and sorghum"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Eliminate gluten strictly for 6 months, then reintroduce wheat gradually once the villi have recovered"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Eliminate wheat, rye, barley, and contaminated oats, substituting rice, maize, cassava, yam, millet, and sorghum" Celiac disease is an immune-mediated enteropathy triggered by ingestion of gluten storage proteins (gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, hordein in barley) in genetically susceptible individuals, causing immune-mediated mucosal inflammation, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy in the small intestine. The sole effective treatment is strict, lifelong elimination of all gluten-containing grains: Barley, Rye, Oats (unless certified gluten-free), and Wheat (the 'BROW' mnemonic). Safe naturally gluten-free alternatives include: rice, maize, cassava, yams, plantains, potatoes, soy, millet, sorghum, arrowroot, and buckwheat. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the safe and unsafe lists. Rice, maize, and cassava contain no gluten and do not cross-react with gliadin, while wheat is the single most important grain to remove. Option b is a double error: it eliminates grains that are safe and then builds the diet on ordinary oats, which are routinely contaminated with wheat during milling and transport. Option d treats coeliac disease as a temporary elimination diet, but the enteropathy recurs with any reintroduction, so the restriction is lifelong.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Celiac disease diet: Eliminate 'BROW' (Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat). Safe staples = Rice, Maize, Yam, Cassava, Plantain, Millet, Sorghum.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 2-year-old child admitted to the paediatric stabilization unit presents with bilateral lower extremity pitting edema extending to the thighs, a swollen 'moon face', a flaky-paint dermatosis with hyperpigmented peeling skin, distended abdomen with hepatomegaly, and reddish-brown discolored hair that plucks out easily ('flag sign'). The child is apathetic and irritable. How should the nurse classify this form of malnutrition?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Marasmus, because the oedema reflects protein-energy wasting with loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle bulk"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Kwashiorkor, the oedematous form of severe acute malnutrition, driven by protein deficiency with hypoalbuminaemic third spacing"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Nutritional dwarfism, because the child is short for age with preserved subcutaneous fat and a normal appetite"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Moderate acute malnutrition, because the pitting oedema is confined to the lower limbs alone and the hair changes will reverse with feeding"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Kwashiorkor, the oedematous form of severe acute malnutrition, driven by protein deficiency with hypoalbuminaemic third spacing" Kwashiorkor is edematous Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM) caused by severe dietary protein deficiency relative to energy intake (often occurring when a toddler is weaned abruptly from protein-rich breast milk to a high-carbohydrate starch gruel). Key diagnostic hallmarks include: (1) bilateral pitting nutritional edema (starting in feet/ankles and progressing upwards); (2) 'flaky-paint' / enamel dermatosis (desquamating pigmented skin); (3) sparse, hypopigmented hair ('flag sign'); (4) fatty liver (hepatomegaly) due to impaired apolipoprotein synthesis; and (5) profound apathy and misery. Marasmus, in contrast, presents with severe somatic wasting ('skin and bones'), no edema, and an alert, ravenous child. Why the other options fail: option a is the classic confusion, and it fails on its defining feature. Marasmus is non-oedematous by definition, with gross wasting and an alert, hungry child, whereas this child is oedematous and apathetic. Option c describes stunting, which is linear growth failure with preserved weight for height and no oedema. Option d misreads oedema as a severity marker rather than a category: bilateral pitting oedema of nutritional origin makes a child severely acutely malnourished on its own, whatever the site or the hair findings.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Kwashiorkor vs Marasmus: Bilateral edema + flaky-paint skin + moon face + plucky hair = Kwashiorkor. Extreme wasting + NO edema = Marasmus.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A chronically starved client with a BMI of 13 kg/m² is admitted for nutritional rehabilitation. Twelve hours after aggressive high-carbohydrate enteral feeding is initiated, the client develops profound muscular weakness, tremors, confusion, respiratory shallowing, and ventricular arrhythmias. Serum phosphorus is critically low at 0.4 mmol/L (normal: 0.8–1.5 mmol/L). What life-threatening syndrome has developed, and what is the underlying mechanism?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hyperkalaemia from the sudden cellular release of potassium, magnesium and phosphate as aggressive feeding is started after prolonged starvation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Refeeding syndrome: the carbohydrate load triggers insulin release, which drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wernicke encephalopathy from thiamine deficiency, which causes confusion, weakness, and cardiac arrhythmia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypoglycaemia from the insulin surge, which depletes serum glucose and causes tremor, confusion, and arrhythmia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Refeeding syndrome: the carbohydrate load triggers insulin release, which drives phosphate, potassium, and magnesium into cells" Refeeding syndrome is a potentially fatal metabolic complication occurring when malnourished, starved individuals are refed too rapidly. During prolonged starvation, the body shifts from carbohydrate to fat/ketone metabolism, and total-body electrolyte stores (especially phosphorus, potassium, and magnesium) become profoundly depleted despite near-normal serum levels. Reintroducing carbohydrates triggers a massive surge of endogenous insulin. Insulin stimulates intracellular uptake of glucose, phosphate, potassium, and magnesium. Rapid intracellular phosphorylation of glucose rapidly consumes remaining extracellular phosphate, causing severe hypophosphatemia, impaired ATP production, diaphragmatic weakness, hemolytic anemia, and lethal arrhythmias. Why the other options fail: option a reverses the electrolyte direction. Insulin drives potassium into cells, so refeeding produces hypokalaemia, not hyperkalaemia, and it does not explain a phosphorus of 0.4 mmol/L. Option c is a genuine hazard of refeeding and explains the confusion, but thiamine deficiency does not lower serum phosphate and the stem's laboratory value is the discriminator. Option d names the right hormone and the wrong substrate; glucose is being driven into cells and is generally normal or high, while the measured deficit is phosphate.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Refeeding syndrome: Rapid feeding -> insulin surge -> intracellular shift of phosphate, potassium, magnesium -> profound hypophosphatemia & cardiac arrest. 'Start low, go slow' + thiamine.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 3-year-old child in a rural community presents with severe night blindness (inability to see in dim light), dry conjunctivae, and distinct, triangular, foamy, silvery-grey plaques on the temporal bulbar conjunctiva (Bitot's spots). What micronutrient deficiency is responsible, and what is the WHO high-dose therapeutic protocol?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Vitamin A deficiency causing xerophthalmia; give 200,000 IU orally on day 1, day 2, and again at least 2 weeks later"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Vitamin A deficiency; give a single 200,000 IU dose now and repeat it only if the night blindness has not resolved after six weeks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Riboflavin deficiency; give oral riboflavin 5 mg daily until the Bitot's spots have resolved completely"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Vitamin A deficiency; apply topical antibiotic ointment to the plaques and review the child in 4 weeks"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Vitamin A deficiency causing xerophthalmia; give 200,000 IU orally on day 1, day 2, and again at least 2 weeks later" Vitamin A is essential for rhodopsin synthesis in retinal rod cells (for scotopic/night vision) and for maintaining epithelial integrity of ocular surfaces. Deficiency causes xerophthalmia: Stage XN (night blindness), Stage X1A (conjunctival xerosis), Stage X1B (Bitot's spots — keratinized epithelial debris on the bulbar conjunctiva), Stage X2 (corneal xerosis), and Stage X3 (corneal ulceration and keratomalacia, resulting in irreversible blindness). WHO treatment protocol for clinical xerophthalmia: 200,000 IU orally (> 12 months; 100,000 IU for infants 6–11 months) immediately on Day 1, Day 2, and Day 14. Why the other options fail: option b gets the drug and the diagnosis right and the schedule wrong. The WHO regimen is three doses, on day 1, day 2, and day 14, because hepatic stores are empty and a single dose does not restore them before the cornea is at risk. Option c treats the eye as a local riboflavin problem; Bitot's spots are keratinised debris of vitamin A deficiency, and riboflavin does not address the corneal melting risk. Option d manages the plaque as an infection, but topical ointment does nothing for the systemic deficiency driving it and the four-week delay risks keratomalacia.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Vitamin A deficiency (Xerophthalmia): Night blindness + Bitot spots -> WHO high-dose Vitamin A (Day 1, Day 2, and 2 weeks later) prevents permanent corneal melting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 45-year-old client with chronic alcoholism and poor dietary intake presents with a symmetric, rough, scaly, hyperpigmented erythematous rash on sun-exposed areas of the neck ('Casal's necklace') and dorsal hands. The client also has chronic watery diarrhea, disorientation, memory deficits, and glossitis. What nutritional deficiency disease is this, and what is the '4 Ds' mnemonic associated with it?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Pellagra from niacin or tryptophan deficiency; the 4 Ds are dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia, and death"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Scurvy from vitamin C deficiency, in which perifollicular haemorrhage, gum disease, and poor wound healing accompany the rash"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Beriberi from thiamine deficiency, which produces dermatitis, diarrhoea, and dementia in alcohol-dependent clients"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Riboflavin deficiency, which produces a symmetrical photosensitive rash on sun-exposed skin with glossitis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Pellagra from niacin or tryptophan deficiency; the 4 Ds are dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia, and death" Pellagra is caused by a severe deficiency of niacin (nicotinic acid / vitamin B3) or its precursor amino acid tryptophan. It is historically associated with diets based almost exclusively on un-nixtamalized maize or chronic alcoholism with poor dietary intake. The hallmark is the classic '4 Ds': (1) Photosensitive Dermatitis (symmetrical, well-demarcated hyperkeratotic rash with burning sensation, classically encircling the neck as 'Casal necklace'); (2) Diarrhea (frequent watery stools, malabsorption, glossitis); (3) Dementia (headache, insomnia, depression, confusion, hallucinations); and (4) Death if untreated. Prompt administration of oral nicotinamide cures the condition. Why the other options fail: option b is tempting because the client is an alcoholic with a poor diet, but scurvy produces perifollicular haemorrhage and gingival disease rather than Casal's necklace, and it has no 4 Ds mnemonic. Option c draws on the same alcohol history and sounds close, yet thiamine deficiency causes peripheral neuropathy and high-output cardiac failure, not a photosensitive rash with diarrhoea and dementia. Option d gets the rash distribution right and the vitamin wrong: riboflavin deficiency causes angular stomatitis and a scrotal or nasolabial dermatitis, not the collar-line plaque the stem describes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Pellagra triad/tetrad: Niacin (B3) deficiency -> 4 Ds: Dermatitis (Casal's necklace), Diarrhea, Dementia, Death. Rapid response to nicotinamide.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A community health nurse provides health education to food handlers regarding the WHO 'Five Keys to Safer Food'. What temperature range is designated the 'Temperature Danger Zone' where foodborne bacterial pathogens multiply most rapidly, and what are the safe temperatures for hot holding and cold storage?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Danger zone 5 to 60 °C; cold foods may be held at 10 °C and hot foods at 55 °C during service"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Danger zone 5 to 60 °C; hold cold food below 5 °C, hot food above 60 °C, and cook to a core temperature above 70 °C"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Danger zone 0 to 5 °C, because bacteria multiply fastest in food held just above freezing point"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The danger zone applies only to meat and fish; cooked rice, stews, and soups may safely be held at room temperature for up to 6 hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Danger zone 5 to 60 °C; hold cold food below 5 °C, hot food above 60 °C, and cook to a core temperature above 70 °C" Under the WHO 'Five Keys to Safer Food' (Keep clean; Separate raw and cooked; Cook thoroughly; Keep food at safe temperatures; Use safe water and raw materials): The Temperature Danger Zone is 5 °C to 60 °C (41 °F to 140 °F). Within this range, common foodborne bacteria (Salmonella, Staphylococcus aureus, E. coli, Bacillus cereus) divide rapidly, doubling every 20 minutes. Safe food handling rules dictate: (1) hot food must be held above 60 °C; (2) cold food must be chilled below 5 °C; (3) cooked foods must reach a core temperature of >= 70 °C for >= 2 minutes; and (4) perishable foods must never sit in the danger zone for > 2 hours. Why the other options fail: option a quotes the right range and then sets holding temperatures inside it, at 10 °C and 55 °C, which defeats the purpose of naming a zone at all. Option c compresses the zone to the refrigeration range; bacterial growth is not fastest near freezing, it is fastest through the middle of the 5 to 60 °C band. Option d singles out meat, but cooked rice, stews, and gravies support Bacillus cereus and Clostridium perfringens growth, and six hours at room temperature is well beyond the two-hour limit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Food safety temperature danger zone: 5 °C to 60 °C. Cold foods < 5 °C, hot foods > 60 °C, thorough cooking core temp >= 70 °C.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is preparing to administer an intermittent enteral tube feeding via a newly inserted small-bore nasogastric (NG) tube in an alert adult client. What is the gold-standard, evidence-based bedside method to verify proper tube position prior to feeding, and what bed position must be maintained?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Confirm placement with the auscultatory whoosh test, and keep the head of the bed flat while the feed is running and for a full hour afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Verify by aspirating and checking that the fluid is green or bile-stained, and nurse the client semi-prone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confirm by X-ray before every feed, and elevate the head of the bed to 15 degrees during the infusion"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Aspirate gastric fluid and confirm pH 5.5 or below, with initial placement checked by X-ray, and keep the head of the bed at 30 to 45 degrees"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Aspirate gastric fluid and confirm pH 5.5 or below, with initial placement checked by X-ray, and keep the head of the bed at 30 to 45 degrees" Aspiration pneumonia is a major, preventable complication of enteral feeding. Bedside placement confirmation standards: Testing the pH of aspirated fluid (gastric aspirate is acidic, with a pH <= 5.5, whereas respiratory or intestinal secretions typically have a pH >= 6.0–7.0) is the validated bedside standard; abdominal X-ray is the gold standard for initial placement. The auscultatory 'whoosh test' (air insufflation) is unreliable and dangerous because air sounds transmit clearly from the pleural space, bronchus, or esophagus, leading to fatal pulmonary infusion. To minimize aspiration, the head of the bed must remain elevated 30–45 degrees during and for 1 hour after feeding. Why the other options fail: option a is retained practice in many units, which is exactly why it is a good distractor; insufflated air transmits from the pleura and bronchus as readily as from the stomach, so the whoosh test cannot distinguish oesophageal or pulmonary placement and has caused fatal intrapulmonary infusion. Option b substitutes colour for pH, but bile staining is neither sensitive nor specific and says nothing about whether the tip is in the airway. Option c is right about X-ray for initial placement and wrong about doing it before every feed, and 15 degrees is too low to protect against aspiration.$q$,
  strategy = $q$NG tube feeding safety: Verify position via aspirate pH <= 5.5 (NEVER the whoosh test). Keep head of bed elevated 30-45 degrees to prevent fatal aspiration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with extensive small-bowel resection is receiving Total Parenteral Nutrition (TPN) via a central venous catheter. The current TPN infusion bag is running out, and the replacement bag from the pharmacy is delayed by 2 hours. What intravenous fluid must the nurse infuse immediately at the same rate to prevent a life-threatening complication?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Hang 0.9% sodium chloride at the same rate until the new bag arrives, then resume the TPN exactly as it was prescribed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Pause the infusion and restart the new TPN at half the previous rate once it arrives from the pharmacy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hang 5% dextrose at the same rate and monitor capillary glucose hourly until the new bag is available"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hang 10% dextrose in water at the same rate, because the constant insulin drive will cause rebound hypoglycaemia"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Hang 10% dextrose in water at the same rate, because the constant insulin drive will cause rebound hypoglycaemia" TPN solutions contain high concentrations of hypertonic glucose (typically 15–25% dextrose), stimulating constant, high-level endogenous insulin secretion from pancreatic beta cells. If a TPN infusion is suddenly stopped or interrupted without tapering, circulating hyperinsulinaemia persists in the absence of exogenous glucose supply, causing rapid and severe rebound hypoglycemia (diaphoresis, tremors, altered mental status, seizures, and coma). If a TPN bag runs dry and the next bag is unavailable, the nurse must immediately hang 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) at the existing TPN rate until the new TPN solution arrives. Why the other options fail: option a maintains the line and the volume but not the glucose, and saline does nothing to blunt a hyperinsulinaemic state. Option b is the most dangerous of the three: stopping the infusion abruptly produces the same rebound hypoglycaemia, and restarting at half rate prolongs it. Option c is the strongest distractor because 5% dextrose is a dextrose solution, but it delivers half the glucose of the 10% bag the client was receiving, so the same insulin drive outpaces the replacement.$q$,
  strategy = $q$TPN interruption protocol: If TPN is interrupted or delayed, hang 10% Dextrose in Water (D10W) immediately to prevent rebound hypoglycemia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A public health nurse leads a district nutrition advocacy campaign. Which population-level public health nutrition intervention has been proven most cost-effective in preventing endemic goitre, maternal miscarriages, and irreversible congenital neurological impairment (cretinism) in children?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Universal fortification of wheat flour with iodine, supported by annual iodized oil capsules for schoolchildren"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Annual oral iodine supplementation for all women of childbearing age, delivered through antenatal clinics"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Universal salt iodization, mandatory fortification of all edible commercial salt with potassium iodate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Iodized oil injection campaigns for schoolchildren every 2 years, with no change to household salt"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Universal salt iodization, mandatory fortification of all edible commercial salt with potassium iodate" Iodine is an essential trace element required for thyroid hormone (thyroxine [T4] and triiodothyronine [T3]) synthesis. Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD) represent the single greatest cause of preventable brain damage and mental impairment worldwide. Manifestations include endemic goiter, hypothyroidism, impaired cognitive development in children, increased pregnancy loss, stillbirths, and congenital iodine deficiency syndrome (cretinism: severe irreversible cognitive impairment, deaf-mutism, spastic diplegia, and dwarfism). Universal Salt Iodization (USI) is recognized by WHO as the most equitable, cost-effective, and sustainable public health intervention to eliminate IDD globally. Why the other options fail: option a widens the vehicle to a food that is not eaten by everyone in the same quantity as salt and leaves the poorest households uncovered, which is the reason flour fortification is neither sufficient nor the chosen strategy. Option b reaches only women who attend antenatal services, the coverage gap salt iodization exists to close, and oral iodine is not the delivery route used in mass programmes. Option d is a repeated injection campaign that reaches children after the critical neurodevelopmental window has already passed and leaves household salt unfortified.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Public health nutrition: Universal Salt Iodization is the gold-standard population intervention preventing endemic goiter, cretinism, and cognitive deficits.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-NUTR-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered nurse is conducting the initial admission assessment for a client scheduled for major abdominal surgery tomorrow. During Hildegard Peplau's orientation phase of the therapeutic nurse-client relationship, which intervention should the nurse prioritize?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Introduce yourself by name and role, state the purpose and time frame, and ask about concerns"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that modern anaesthesia and surgery are completely safe and free of risk"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give a forty-five minute talk on spirometry before finding out what the client is worried about"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client that anxiety raises blood pressure and ask them to hold their feelings in"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Introduce yourself by name and role, state the purpose and time frame, and ask about concerns" Peplau's orientation phase is the foundational stage of the therapeutic relationship where the nurse introduces themselves by name and professional role, sets boundaries, establishes the contract (purpose, parameters, and time frame), clarifies roles, and assesses the client's perceptions, immediate concerns, and expectations. Establishing trust during this phase is essential for subsequent therapeutic work. False reassurance, premature didactic instruction without assessing the client's readiness or anxiety, and minimizing or suppressing emotions are non-therapeutic barriers that undermine trust. Why the other options fail: option b offers reassurance that is false and closes the conversation. Option c teaches before assessing readiness, so it answers the nurse's agenda rather than the client's. Option d asks the client to suppress feeling, which is neither possible nor therapeutic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In Peplau's framework, the orientation phase focuses on contracting, defining roles, establishing trust, and identifying the client's presenting concerns.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 34-year-old client diagnosed with invasive malignant melanoma tearfully confides to the nurse, 'I have two young children at home. I am terrified that I won't live to see them finish school.' Which response by the nurse demonstrates therapeutic empathy?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I know exactly what you are going through, because my own sister had cancer and we struggled too"}, {"id": "b", "text": "It sounds as though you are carrying a heavy fear about your children's future; what scares you most?"}, {"id": "c", "text": "You should not dwell on worst-case scenarios when immunotherapy has produced such good outcomes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Try to stay positive for the sake of your children, because worrying yourself sick will not change the biopsy result at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "It sounds as though you are carrying a heavy fear about your children's future; what scares you most?" Empathy involves understanding and validating the client's emotional experience from their perspective and communicating that understanding without judgment ('It sounds like you are carrying a profound weight of fear.'). It centers the client and invites further exploration. Telling the client 'I know exactly what you are going through' is sympathy or nurse-centered countertransference—every client's experience is unique. False reassurance and dismissive toxic positivity invalidate genuine fear and shut down meaningful therapeutic dialogue. Why the other options fail: option a moves the focus onto the nurse's own family. Option c dismisses the fear with statistics about outcomes. Option d tells the client to stop feeling what they feel.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Therapeutic communication rule: Acknowledge, validate, and explore the client's feelings. Eliminate responses that shift focus to the nurse ('I know how you feel') or dismiss emotions with false cheer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse enters the room of a client newly diagnosed with congestive heart failure to discuss lifestyle modifications and low-sodium nutrition. To foster an open environment and demonstrate congruent, attending non-verbal communication, which action should the nurse take?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stand at the foot of the bed with the arms folded across the chest while reading the leaflet"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stand in the doorway with one hand on the handle to signal that the visit will be brief"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Pull up a chair and sit at eye level, about three to four feet away, with an open posture"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep looking at the bedside computer screen while dictating the instructions to the client"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Pull up a chair and sit at eye level, about three to four feet away, with an open posture" Non-verbal communication conveys more than 70% of interpersonal meaning. Sitting down at eye level conveys that the nurse is not rushed and is fully present for the client, reducing power asymmetries and fostering comfort. An open posture (uncrossed arms/legs) invites interaction. Standing over a seated or bedridden client reinforces hierarchy and dominance. Standing at the door signals hurriedness and impatience. Focusing on a screen rather than the client conveys emotional detachment and disinterest. Why the other options fail: option a folds the arms and stands over the client. Option b signals that the nurse is in a hurry to leave. Option d attends to the screen rather than to the person.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Eye-level positioning and open posture (sitting rather than standing over the client) significantly increase client perception of nurse caring and engagement.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who has just been informed that their chronic lymphocytic leukemia has progressed to blast crisis lowers their head, weeps quietly, and stops speaking. What is the most therapeutic initial nursing action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Ask at once why the client is crying when bone marrow transplantation is still to be discussed"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Change the subject to the evening meal choices so that the client is distracted from the sorrow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stay quietly with the client, allowing time to cry and to process the news without interruption"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hand over a box of tissues, pat the client's back and say that everything happens for a reason"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Stay quietly with the client, allowing time to cry and to process the news without interruption" Therapeutic silence is an active nursing intervention that gives the client space, permission, and time to experience, process, and express deep emotional reactions without feeling rushed or judged. Sitting quietly with the client provides therapeutic presence. Asking 'Why' questions prompts defensiveness and intellectualization. Changing the subject is non-therapeutic avoidance by a nurse uncomfortable with grief. Platitudes like 'everything happens for a reason' invalidate suffering and minimize personal tragedy. Why the other options fail: option a opens with a why question that invites defensiveness. Option b avoids the grief altogether. Option d offers a platitude that minimises the loss.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Silence is therapeutic when a client is overwhelmed or weeping. Do not rush to fill silence with words, platitudes, or topic changes.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is conducting a psychosocial assessment using the SOLER active listening framework. Which physical behavior by the nurse correctly aligns with this evidence-based communication model?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stand with the arms folded tightly across the chest to convey clinical authority and self-control"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hold unbroken, fixed eye contact into the client's pupils for the whole of the interview"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Recline backwards with the hands clasped behind the head to show how relaxed you are"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sit squarely facing the client, open posture, lean slightly forward, eye contact, relaxed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Sit squarely facing the client, open posture, lean slightly forward, eye contact, relaxed" The SOLER acronym for active attending behaviors stands for: S = Sit squarely facing the client (signals involvement); O = Open posture (uncrossed arms and legs; non-defensive); L = Lean slightly forward toward the client (signals attentiveness and interest); E = Eye contact (appropriate, intermittent, culturally respectful); R = Relaxed posture (communicates ease and presence). Folded arms indicate defensiveness or disengagement; continuous unblinking staring is intimidating and aggressive; excessive slouching/reclining indicates indifference or unprofessionalism. Why the other options fail: option a is a closed posture. Option b is a stare that most clients find threatening. Option c is a slouched posture that reads as indifference.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the SOLER acronym: Squarely face the client, Open posture, Lean forward, Eye contact, Relaxed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for an elderly client who is trembling with intense emotional grief following the sudden death of their spouse. The nurse considers using touch to communicate comfort. Which clinical principle must guide the nurse's use of therapeutic touch?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Touch means comfort in every culture, so no explanation or permission is ever required"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Assess culture and boundaries first and ask permission before resting a hand on the forearm"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Avoid touching clients altogether, because any physical contact crosses a professional boundary and is never appropriate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give the client a sudden, tight hug without warning so that the grief is interrupted"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Assess culture and boundaries first and ask permission before resting a hand on the forearm" Therapeutic touch can convey profound empathy and comfort, particularly during acute grief or severe anxiety. However, touch is deeply personal and interpreted through cultural, gender, personal trauma, and religious lenses. The nurse should always assess the client's readiness and obtain verbal or clear non-verbal permission before touching (e.g., placing a gentle hand on the hand or forearm). Assuming universal comfort can trigger re-traumatization; completely eliminating touch strips nursing of a vital human element; and invasive unannounced hugs breach physical boundaries. Why the other options fail: option a assumes one meaning for touch in every culture. Option c removes a legitimate comfort from all care. Option d imposes contact without consent.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Touch requires clinical assessment of cultural, personal, and trauma histories. Always gauge readiness and obtain permission for therapeutic touch.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client newly diagnosed with essential hypertension states, 'I threw the prescription pills into the trash because I do not believe in putting toxic chemicals into my body.' Which response by the nurse represents the most therapeutic open-ended exploration?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Can you tell me what concerns or beliefs you have about taking blood pressure medicines?"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Why would you throw away a life-saving medicine when hypertension causes strokes?"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Do you realise that high blood pressure is called the silent killer for a very good reason?"}, {"id": "d", "text": "You should really listen to your doctor if you expect your heart health to improve at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Can you tell me what concerns or beliefs you have about taking blood pressure medicines?" Open-ended questioning invites the client to elaborate, share underlying beliefs, and articulate values without feeling judged or cornered ('Can you share with me what concerns or beliefs you have.'). This provides the assessment data needed to address medication hesitancy collaboratively. Asking 'Why' puts the client on the defensive and provokes justification. Closed-ended rhetorical scare tactics increase resistance. Authoritative advice-giving damages the collaborative therapeutic partnership. Why the other options fail: option b opens with a why question and a warning. Option c uses a scare tactic that raises resistance. Option d gives advice instead of exploring the belief.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Avoid 'Why' questions that elicit defensiveness. Choose open-ended questions starting with 'What', 'How', or 'Tell me about...' to explore client health beliefs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 54-year-old client scheduled for an urgent total laryngectomy due to advanced laryngeal cancer remarks, 'My voice is how I teach my university students and sing in our community choir. If they remove my larynx, I won't be a real human being anymore.' Which response by the nurse best reflects the client's core emotion and message?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Modern speech therapy uses electrolarynx devices and oesophageal speech that work very well"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Removing the tumour matters far more than speaking, because cancer is a life-threatening illness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "You should be grateful that the surgeons found this before it reached the lungs"}, {"id": "d", "text": "You feel that losing your voice will strip away your identity, your work and who you are"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "You feel that losing your voice will strip away your identity, your work and who you are" Reflecting and paraphrasing content and feelings communicates that the nurse has actively heard and understood the core meaning beneath the client's words. The client equates losing their voice with losing their very identity and personhood. Reflecting this back ('You feel that losing your voice will strip away your identity.') validates their profound existential grief. Rushing into technical solutions bypasses emotional validation. Minimizing their loss by comparing it to cancer survival is non-therapeutic and dismissive. Why the other options fail: option a answers grief with equipment. Option b weighs the voice against survival, which the client did not ask. Option c tells the client to be grateful.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Reflecting feelings mirrors the emotional essence of what the client expressed. Never jump to technical problem-solving before validating grief and identity loss.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During an initial clinic consultation, a client talks in a rapid, disjointed manner, jumping between chronic back spasms, severe insomnia, unemployment stress, marital arguments, and sudden lightheadedness. Which communication technique should the nurse employ to synthesize these issues and structure the clinical focus?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Interrupt at once and tell the client that this clinic handles physical complaints only, not social or marital problems"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Offer unsolicited advice on conflict mediation so that the marital discord is settled first"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Summarise the concerns, then agree to assess the dizziness and pain first because they threaten safety"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sit in total silence for the whole appointment until the client has run out of things to say"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Summarise the concerns, then agree to assess the dizziness and pain first because they threaten safety" Summarizing and focusing brings together important points of discussion to review main ideas, clarify mutual understanding, and organize priorities for action. In an overwhelmed or rambling client, summarizing validates everything they have shared while providing clear, gentle structure to focus on high-priority clinical concerns (such as dizziness and acute pain) first. Abruptly dismissing social concerns destroys trust. Giving marital advice is outside nursing scope and non-therapeutic. Unstructured silence fails to provide needed clinical guidance. Why the other options fail: option a dismisses the social concerns outright. Option b gives advice the nurse is not qualified to give. Option d offers silence with no structure or direction.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Summarizing condenses key themes; focusing directs the conversation toward high-priority clinical concerns without dismissing other issues.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A multidisciplinary oncology team is preparing to discuss poor biopsy results indicating metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma with a client. According to the evidence-based SPIKES protocol for breaking bad news, what must the healthcare team determine first before disclosing the diagnostic findings?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "What the client already understands or suspects about the illness and the tests"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The five-year survival figures, so that the client grasps the numerical prognosis at once"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The hospice referral, so that a palliative bed is ready before the results are discussed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The family's presence, so that relatives hear the diagnosis before the client does"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "What the client already understands or suspects about the illness and the tests" The SPIKES protocol consists of: S = Setting (privacy, seating, time); P = Perception (assessing what the client understands: 'What have you been told so far?'); I = Invitation (asking how much detail the client wants); K = Knowledge (delivering information in small chunks with warning shots); E = Empathy (addressing emotions); S = Strategy/Summary (collaborative next steps). Inquiring about the client's perception ('P') establishes where the client is psychologically, identifies unrealistic expectations or misinformation, and allows the clinician to tailor the disclosure appropriately. Blunt statistical dumps induce shock. Handing out hospice brochures prematurely bypasses disclosure and empathy. Forcing family out violates autonomy. Why the other options fail: option b leads with statistics before asking what the client knows. Option c introduces palliative care before the diagnosis has been given. Option d decides the family's presence for the client.$q$,
  strategy = $q$SPIKES protocol sequence: Setting -> Perception -> Invitation -> Knowledge -> Empathy -> Strategy. Always assess the client's baseline perception first.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An adult son storms up to the nurses' station visibly agitated, red-faced, and shouting: 'My mother has been ringing her call bell for over 45 minutes for pain medication, and you lazy nurses are just gossiping! You don't care if people suffer!' Which initial response by the nurse demonstrates effective clinical de-escalation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Shout back that aggression will not be tolerated and threaten to call security immediately"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Speak calmly, keep a safe distance and say you can see the distress, then go to assess the mother"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Explain that another client had a cardiac arrest on the ward, so his mother is not the only patient who needs attention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Turn away silently and lock the medication room door until he leaves the desk area"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Speak calmly, keep a safe distance and say you can see the distress, then go to assess the mother" Effective de-escalation requires: 1) maintaining a calm, unhurried, lower-volume voice; 2) maintaining safe physical positioning without cornering the person; 3) validating the emotion driving the outburst (distress over the mother's pain); and 4) taking immediate, constructive, client-focused action. Escalating with aggression risks physical violence. Defensiveness and counter-complaining invalidate his legitimate distress and escalates hostility. Abandoning the interaction leaves the client in pain and fuels feelings of neglect. Why the other options fail: option a matches aggression with a threat. Option c defends the staff instead of hearing the complaint. Option d abandons a distressed family member and leaves the mother in pain.$q$,
  strategy = $q$De-escalation principles: Stay calm, maintain safe distance, validate the underlying emotion, and direct energy toward immediate constructive action.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A family member sitting at the bedside of an unresponsive client actively dying from terminal hepatic encephalopathy whispers tearfully to the nurse, 'I don't know how I will ever go home to an empty house. My entire life is ending with theirs.' Which response by the nurse provides the most therapeutic grief support?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "You must stay strong now, because the rest of the family is depending on you to hold together"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Time heals all wounds, and in time you will rebuild your life and find happiness again"}, {"id": "c", "text": "At least your partner lived a long and fulfilled life, when many people die much younger"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Going home to an empty house feels overwhelming; I am right here with you through this"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Going home to an empty house feels overwhelming; I am right here with you through this" Therapeutic communication in anticipatory grief requires acknowledging the depth of sorrow, validating feelings of devastation without offering false solutions or clichés, and providing supportive presence ('The thought of going home to an empty house feels utterly overwhelming. I am right here with you'). Ordering someone to 'stay strong' imposes guilt and suppresses legitimate mourning. Clichés like 'time heals all wounds' or comparative minimization like 'at least they lived a long life' dismiss raw emotional pain. Why the other options fail: option a instructs the client to suppress grief. Option b offers a cliché about time. Option c compares this death with others, and so diminishes it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Eliminate clichés ('time heals', 'at least...'). Choose the response that validates grief and offers compassionate presence.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 4-year-old child admitted for severe dehydration requires an intravenous (IV) catheter insertion. The child is crying, hiding behind their mother, and clinging tightly. Which communication approach is developmentally appropriate for this preschooler?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain vein cannulation and intravascular rehydration using correct medical terminology"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Tell the child that big children do not cry and promise that the needle will not hurt"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use a teddy bear to show a magic straw, let the child touch the tourniquet, then proceed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Have four staff restrain the child without speaking, so the needle is inserted quickly"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Use a teddy bear to show a magic straw, let the child touch the tourniquet, then proceed" Preschool-age children (3 to 5 years) think concretely, exhibit magical thinking, and fear bodily injury and pain. Developmentally appropriate communication includes: using therapeutic play (demonstrating on a stuffed animal or doll), using simple, concrete, non-threatening language ('magic straw' or 'straw to give medicine/water'), allowing them to handle safe equipment (tourniquet, cotton ball), and timing preparation shortly before the procedure so anticipatory anxiety does not build. Complex explanations exceed cognitive development; shaming and lying about pain destroys trust; forceful silent restraint causes severe psychological trauma. Why the other options fail: option a uses language beyond a four-year-old's understanding. Option b shames the child and promises something untrue. Option d restrains a child without any preparation at all.$q$,
  strategy = $q$For preschoolers, use therapeutic play, concrete non-threatening terms, honest expectations, and prepare them immediately before procedures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is conducting discharge teaching for an 80-year-old client with severe bilateral presbycusis (sensorineural age-related hearing loss). Which communication strategy should the nurse implement to maximize comprehension?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Speak directly into the client's ear in a raised, high-pitched voice at the greatest volume the voice can produce"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Direct the discharge teaching to the adult child and leave the client out of it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Turn towards the computer screen and speak while finishing the documentation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Face the client in good light, cut background noise, lower your pitch, then teach back"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Face the client in good light, cut background noise, lower your pitch, then teach back" Presbycusis involves loss of high-frequency sound perception and speech discrimination. Effective interventions include: 1) facing the client directly so lip movements and facial expressions can be observed; 2) ensuring adequate lighting on the nurse's face without backlighting; 3) reducing ambient noise (shutting doors, turning off televisions); 4) lowering the vocal pitch rather than shouting (shouting distorts vowels and raises pitch, worsening comprehension); and 5) using the teach-back method to verify understanding. Shouting in a high pitch exacerbates distortion. Bypassing the client violates autonomy and dignity. Turning away eliminates visual cues. Why the other options fail: option a raises the pitch, which is the frequency band the client has lost. Option b excludes the client from their own care. Option c removes the visual cues that lip reading depends on.$q$,
  strategy = $q$For presbycusis: Lower your vocal pitch, face the client directly, reduce background noise, and avoid shouting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A non-English-speaking client arrives in the emergency department with severe right lower quadrant abdominal pain. The client is accompanied by their 11-year-old bilingual child, who offers to translate the history and examination. What is the nurse's most appropriate action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Arrange a certified medical interpreter, in person or by secure telephone, for the assessment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the eleven-year-old child to translate the history and explain the surgical risks"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Use gestures and louder English words, since an interpreter would simply delay care"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Proceed to surgery without consent, because a language barrier waives the requirement for informed consent in an emergency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Arrange a certified medical interpreter, in person or by secure telephone, for the assessment" Using minor children or family members as interpreters violates healthcare privacy laws, compromises clinical accuracy, and places inappropriate emotional burdens on children. Minor children lack medical vocabulary, may misunderstand complex clinical issues, or filter sensitive information (such as sexual history, substance use, or severe diagnoses). Professional certified medical interpreters ensure confidential, culturally competent, and legally valid translation. Pantomime is dangerous for complex clinical decisions. Language barriers do not waive consent requirements when translation services are accessible. Why the other options fail: option b burdens a child and risks misreported clinical detail. Option c abandons accuracy at the moment it matters most. Option d invents a waiver of consent that does not exist.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Standard of care: Always use a qualified, certified medical interpreter rather than minor children or family members.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered nurse is calling the on-call physician using the SBAR framework to report a post-operative client whose blood pressure has fallen to 82/46 mmHg with a heart rate of 128 beats/minute. After the nurse provides the Situation, Background, Assessment, and Recommendation, the physician verbally orders: 'Infuse 500 mL normal saline bolus STAT and administer IV ondansetron 4 mg.' Which action by the nurse is required before hanging up?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "End the call and go to the medication room to hang the fluid bolus as quickly as possible"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Read the order back word for word and get the prescriber to confirm it before hanging up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Write the order in the chart and wait for the doctor to countersign it in the morning"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ask another nurse in the corridor whether five hundred millilitres is a sensible volume"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Read the order back word for word and get the prescriber to confirm it before hanging up" Closed-loop communication and mandatory 'read-back' of verbal/telephone orders are key Joint Commission and international patient safety requirements to prevent medication errors. The receiving nurse must write down the order and read back the exact drug, dose, route, rate, and frequency verbatim, receiving clear verbal confirmation from the prescriber. Hanging up without read-back creates critical error vulnerabilities. Delaying STAT emergency fluid resuscitation for hours awaiting an in-person signature leads to clinical deterioration and hypovolemic shock. Consulting peers does not replace verification with the prescriber. Why the other options fail: option a hangs up without confirmation and loses the chance to catch an error. Option c delays a stat order until the morning. Option d asks an uninvolved nurse instead of the prescriber.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Patient safety standard: Always perform a closed-loop read-back of all verbal and telephone orders.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A staff nurse notices that an attending surgeon is about to make an incision for an open inguinal hernia repair without verifying the surgical site marking or initiating the universal protocol surgical 'time-out'. Which statement demonstrates assertive advocacy using the evidence-based CUS communication model?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "I am Concerned, I am Uncomfortable, and this is a Safety issue: we have not done the time-out"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Doctor, you are acting recklessly and breaking every rule in the hospital's policy manual"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Stay silent, because the surgeon has decades of experience and raising it would cause friction"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave theatre quietly and tell the charge nurse about it once the incision has been made"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "I am Concerned, I am Uncomfortable, and this is a Safety issue: we have not done the time-out" The CUS communication framework is an evidence-based patient safety tool from TeamSTEPPS designed to cut through interprofessional hierarchies assertively: 'I am Concerned', 'I am Uncomfortable', 'This is a Safety issue'. Stating these exact trigger words clearly alerts the entire surgical team to an immediate halt before harm occurs. Hostile accusations trigger defensiveness rather than collaboration. Passive silence allows wrong-site surgery and fatal medical errors. Delayed complaining after the fact fails the primary duty of bedside client advocacy. Why the other options fail: option b accuses rather than alerts. Option c trades the client's safety for the surgeon's comfort. Option d reports after the harm that the protocol exists to prevent.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the TeamSTEPPS CUS words: Concerned, Uncomfortable, Safety issue. Use them assertively to stop potential patient harm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a busy change-of-shift handoff in the report room, an oncoming nurse loudly snaps at the off-going nurse in front of the entire team: 'Why are day-shift nurses so incompetent? You left two IV bags nearly dry and the morning chart looks like a complete mess!' Which response by the off-going nurse demonstrates professional, assertive communication?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Shout back that his own shift leaves the unit in a mess every weekend, so he is in no position to talk about anyone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Apologise repeatedly for being incompetent, and cry in front of the whole handover group"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Say nothing, finish the report, and leave out the morning potassium result to get even"}, {"id": "d", "text": "I want the handover to be accurate, but accusations in public are unprofessional: let us finish and talk privately"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "I want the handover to be accurate, but accusations in public are unprofessional: let us finish and talk privately" Assertive communication respects both oneself and the other person by setting firm, clear boundaries without becoming aggressive or passive. Acknowledging the priority of client safety while calmly calling out inappropriate public hostility ('making personal accusations in public is unprofessional') and proposing a constructive private forum de-escalates public drama and addresses lateral violence effectively. Aggressive retaliatory shouting creates toxic escalation. Passive submission enables ongoing bullying. Passive-aggressive retaliation by withholding critical patient data is dangerous malpractice. Why the other options fail: option a answers aggression with aggression. Option b accepts a public accusation as true. Option c withholds a critical result, which is dangerous.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Assertive response to lateral hostility: Focus on client safety, label unprofessional behavior calmly and objectively, and move conflict resolution to a private setting.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Upon discharge following a complex 4-week hospitalization, a grateful client presents the primary nurse with a designer diamond wristwatch valued at $1,500 and says, 'You saved my life. I want you to have this so you remember me forever.' Which response by the nurse best adheres to professional boundary guidelines?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Accept the watch graciously and promise to wear it on every shift so that the client knows the gratitude was received"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take the watch, sell it, and give the money to the staff association without telling anyone"}, {"id": "c", "text": "I am touched, but policy does not let me accept personal gifts; a card to the team would mean a lot"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Scold the client for attempting a bribe and threaten to report them to the hospital board"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "I am touched, but policy does not let me accept personal gifts; a card to the team would mean a lot" Accepting expensive personal gifts from clients or families breaches professional boundaries, blurs the therapeutic relationship, and creates conflicts of interest or perceived favoritism. The nurse must decline individual valuable gifts graciously, citing professional ethics and institutional policy, while validating the client's gratitude. Suggesting a thank-you note, card, or general contribution to the unit/hospital foundation redirects their appreciation appropriately. Accepting the gift violates professional codes of conduct. Harshly scolding the client punishes genuine gratitude and terminates the relationship destructively. Why the other options fail: option a accepts a valuable gift. Option b converts it to money, which is still accepting it. Option d punishes genuine gratitude.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Professional boundaries: Decline individual gifts of significant value gracefully by citing professional standards, and redirect gratitude to a card or team appreciation.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$On the morning of hospital discharge after an extensive 6-week admission for Guillain-Barré syndrome rehabilitation, a client expresses acute distress: 'I am terrified to leave. You nurses have become my family and protected me. I don't think I can survive at home without you.' In Hildegard Peplau's termination phase, which intervention should the nurse implement?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give the client your personal address and mobile number so they can message when lonely"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Validate the anxiety, review the gains made, and go through the follow-up and home support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Agree that home is frightening and lobby the team to keep the client in hospital longer"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client briskly that they are being dramatic and that the bed is needed for someone else"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Validate the anxiety, review the gains made, and go through the follow-up and home support" In Peplau's termination phase, separation anxiety, regression, or ambivalence frequently arise as the client transitions away from intensive professional support. The therapeutic response is to: 1) validate their feelings of anxiety as normal; 2) summarize the therapeutic gains, goals achieved, and coping mechanisms mastered; and 3) review the concrete transition plan (home health, outpatient therapy, community resources) to empower independence. Giving personal contact information breaches professional boundaries and fosters unhealthy dependency. Unnecessarily extending hospitalization prevents recovery. Dismissive comments demonstrate abandonment and callousness. Why the other options fail: option a breaches the boundary and builds dependency. Option c prevents the discharge the client has worked towards. Option d dismisses the anxiety and the client.$q$,
  strategy = $q$In Peplau's termination phase, validate normal separation anxiety, review therapeutic achievements, reinforce independence, and review community transition resources.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-TCOM-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 40
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch134.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
-- Batch 134: 40 PRO1 + PRO2 module items quoted.
--
-- PRO1 + PRO2 module block: 40 items quoted, 16 keys de-throned.
-- All 40 stored keys re-read against the stem and judged correct; no key changed.
-- Each item gains the CORRECT (X) - "option text" prefix so the prebuild
-- verifier can check the quote against the keyed option.
-- 16 items were key-longest and had their longest distractor lengthened instead
-- of trimming the key.
-- Sentence repairs: PRO2-001 had the democratic and laissez-faire fail-bullets
-- swapped between options a and b; PRO2-003 fail-bullet described a family
-- member who is not in option d and the preamble carried an orphan trafficking
-- sentence; PRO2-006 orphan board-revocation clause; PRO2-017 orphan grooming
-- clause; PRO1-003 stem said biennial renewal where the NMC licence runs 12
-- months; PRO1-012 option b used "her" for a client of unstated sex.
-- 
-- Verify: expect 40
BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered general nurse realizes that their professional practicing licence and PIN from the regulatory nursing council expired two months ago. The nurse is currently scheduled to manage a busy medical ward shift. What is the nurse's immediate legal and professional obligation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Carry on working the rota as normal while the council's annual audit cycle comes round"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Notify the manager, stop practising until the licence is renewed, and expedite renewal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Chart under a colleague's registration PIN on the electronic medication record until renewal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Do only non-invasive tasks, and hand all intravenous medicines to the student nurses"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Notify the manager, stop practising until the licence is renewed, and expedite renewal" Practicing nursing without an active, unencumbered professional licence violates national nursing statutory laws and professional regulations (such as the Health Professions Regulatory Bodies Act and Nursing & Midwifery Council standards). Practicing with an expired licence constitutes illegal, unauthorized practice, exposing the individual to criminal penalties, disciplinary sanctions, and loss of professional indemnity coverage. The nurse must notify their supervisor immediately and step down from clinical practice until the licence is restored. Continuing to practice, charting under a colleague's credentials, or practicing selectively are fraudulent, unlawful, and breach professional standards. Why the other options fail: option a leaves the nurse in unlawful practice. Option c uses another person's registration, which is fraudulent. Option d still delivers nursing care without a licence, whatever the task.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Professional regulation standard: Active licensure is a mandatory legal prerequisite for clinical practice. Practicing with an expired license is illegal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered general nurse (RGN) is supervising a nursing team that includes an enrolled nurse (EN) and a healthcare assistant (HCA). When planning care for a newly admitted client experiencing unstable acute coronary syndrome, which responsibility must the registered nurse retain?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take routine baseline observations once the initial assessment has been documented"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Help the client with perineal hygiene and change the bed linen after the ward round"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Carry a clean urine specimen bottle down to the hospital pathology laboratory"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The assessment, the care plan and the evaluation of the response to nitroglycerin"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The assessment, the care plan and the evaluation of the response to nitroglycerin" According to professional practice acts and regulatory delegation guidelines, the registered nurse cannot delegate tasks that require specialized nursing knowledge, clinical judgment, comprehensive assessment, care planning, or clinical evaluation (the 'E-A-T' principle: Evaluate, Assess, Teach). An unstable client requires ongoing registered nurse assessment and titration of high-alert cardiovascular medications. Routine vital sign checks on stable clients, personal hygiene, and specimen transport can be safely delegated to assistive personnel within their designated job descriptions. Why the other options fail: option a is routine work that can be delegated once the assessment is written. Options b and c are personal care and portering tasks that sit within the assistant's role.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delegation rule: The RN cannot delegate Assessment, Teaching, or Evaluation (E-A-T) or any care for an unstable, complex client.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is reviewing regulatory requirements for annual licence renewal and professional re-registration. Which statement accurately describes the core purpose and requirement of Continuous Professional Development (CPD)?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An optional academic pursuit for nurses who hope to become university lecturers"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A mandatory requirement for lifelong learning, with verified competence credits"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Awarded automatically after a number of consecutive years of clinical employment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intended to replace the undergraduate pre-service nursing curriculum entirely"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A mandatory requirement for lifelong learning, with verified competence credits" Continuous Professional Development (CPD) is a statutory regulatory mandate, and in Ghana the NMC licence and PIN run for 12 months and are renewed by 31 January each year against a CPD credit requirement established by nursing regulatory councils (including the Ghana NMC, UK NMC, and US State Boards). Its primary mission is public protection by ensuring that registered practitioners continuously maintain, update, and enhance clinical knowledge, evidence-based skills, and professional ethics throughout their careers. It is not optional, cannot be acquired passively through tenure alone, and does not replace pre-service training. Why the other options fail: option a makes continuing development optional. Option c awards credit for time served without any learning. Option d confuses professional development with pre-registration training.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Regulatory principle: CPD is mandatory for all licensed nurses to guarantee ongoing competence and protect public safety.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An alert, mentally competent 45-year-old adult client with severe gastrointestinal bleeding from a peptic ulcer refuses a prescribed blood transfusion due to deeply held religious convictions, despite being fully informed that refusal could lead to hypovolemic shock and death. Which ethical principle dictates the nurse's primary duty to respect this client's decision?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Autonomy"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Paternalism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Beneficence"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Non-maleficence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Autonomy" Autonomy is the ethical and legal principle that recognizes an individual's right to self-determination, bodily integrity, and independent decision-making regarding their own medical care, provided they possess decision-making capacity. A competent adult has the absolute legal and ethical right to refuse any medical treatment, even life-sustaining interventions like blood transfusions. Paternalism overrides client choices for their perceived good. Beneficence promotes well-being, but does not legally or ethically supersede a competent client's autonomous refusal. Non-maleficence means doing no harm. Why the other options fail: option b overrides the client's choice for what others judge to be their good. Option c is the duty to promote welfare, and it does not override a competent refusal. Option d is the duty not to harm, which here means informing and supporting the decision.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Ethical rule: Autonomy grants mentally competent adults the absolute right to refuse medical interventions, even when providers believe the choice is unwise or fatal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse inadvertently administers 10 units of regular insulin instead of the prescribed 5 units to a client with diabetes mellitus. Upon realizing the error 10 minutes later, the client appears asymptomatic with a blood glucose of 6.2 mmol/L (112 mg/dL). Which action aligns with the ethical principles of Veracity and Non-maleficence?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Conceal the error and withhold the next scheduled dose to balance the day's total"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Wait quietly to see whether the client develops sweating and tremor before acting"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Change the medication record to five units before anyone reviews the chart"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assess and monitor, tell the prescriber, tell the client, treat and document"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Assess and monitor, tell the prescriber, tell the client, treat and document" Veracity is the obligation to tell the truth and avoid deception; Non-maleficence is the duty to prevent harm. When a medication error occurs, the nurse's first duty is client safety (frequent monitoring, providing oral glucose as ordered/protocol, physician notification) and honest disclosure to both the clinical team and the client. Falsifying documentation, concealing the error, or waiting for symptomatic hypoglycemic brain injury violates professional ethics, medical candor, and criminal law. Why the other options fail: option a hides the error and unbalances the day's dosing. Option b waits for harm instead of preventing it. Option c falsifies the record, which is a separate offence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Medication error protocol: Prioritize client safety, notify provider, practice veracity (honest disclosure), and document objectively. Never conceal or falsify records.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A staff nurse caring for a comatose client with multi-organ failure and terminal metastatic cancer experiences profound moral distress because the attending team continues painful invasive procedures despite prior documented advance directives expressing wishes for comfort care only. What is the most appropriate professional action for the nurse to take?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Turn off the ventilator unilaterally to end the client's suffering, without telling anyone"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Walk off the shift at once without handing over care, to protest the decisions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Post about the team's ethics on public social media to rally community pressure"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Consult the ethics committee, present the directive and request a palliative conference"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Consult the ethics committee, present the directive and request a palliative conference" Moral distress occurs when a healthcare professional knows the ethically correct action but feels powerless to act due to systemic or institutional constraints. Constructive resolution involves utilizing established institutional mechanisms: engaging the hospital ethics committee, reviewing and advocating for the client's documented advance directives, and requesting an interdisciplinary conference including palliative specialists and the family. Euthanasia/unilateral withdrawal without legal orders is illegal homicide; abandoning a shift constitutes patient abandonment; public social media venting breaches confidentiality and professional codes. Why the other options fail: option a withdraws treatment without any lawful authority. Option b abandons the client. Option c breaches confidentiality and the professional code.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Managing moral distress: Utilize institutional ethics committees and interprofessional case conferences to advocate for client advance directives.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In a medical malpractice lawsuit against a registered nurse, the plaintiff alleges negligent post-operative care following an orthopedic surgery. To establish civil liability for nursing malpractice in a court of law, which four legal elements must the plaintiff prove?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Duty, breach of duty, causation and actual damages"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intent, malice, a criminal record and the medical expenses incurred"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Informed consent, a written contract, a bad outcome and high hospital fees"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Assault, battery, slander and the emotional distress that followed"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Duty, breach of duty, causation and actual damages" The four essential legal elements of nursing malpractice (professional negligence) are: 1) Duty of care (a legal obligation to adhere to a standard of care created by the nurse-client relationship); 2) Breach of duty (failure to meet the accepted standard of care); 3) Causation/Proximate cause (a direct causal link showing that the breach caused the harm); and 4) Actual damages/injury (measurable physical, emotional, or financial harm). Malice or intent are elements of intentional torts or crimes, not negligence. An unfortunate outcome without a breach of duty does not constitute malpractice. Why the other options fail: option b lists criminal and financial elements. Option c confuses consent with negligence, since a bad outcome alone is not malpractice. Option d lists intentional torts.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the 4 D's of malpractice: Duty, Dereliction (Breach), Direct cause (Causation), and Damage (Injury).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered nurse delegates the task of obtaining hourly blood glucose measurements for a client on an intravenous insulin infusion to an experienced healthcare assistant (HCA). Two hours later, the client suffers severe hypoglycemia and loses consciousness because the HCA failed to check the blood glucose. Regarding professional accountability, which statement is legally and ethically true?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The registered nurse bears no responsibility, because the task was fully transferred"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The hospital chief executive is solely accountable for anything that happens inside it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The registered nurse keeps accountability for supervising and evaluating the task"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client is mainly at fault for not reporting the early symptoms of hunger"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "The registered nurse keeps accountability for supervising and evaluating the task" A core tenet of nursing delegation is that while authority and responsibility for carrying out a task can be delegated to another worker, professional accountability for the overall outcome of nursing care cannot be delegated away. The delegating nurse remains legally and professionally accountable for appropriate delegation (assessing competency, communicating clearly, supervising, and timely follow-up/evaluation). Shifting 100% of blame to the assistant, the CEO, or the victim ignores statutory professional accountability. Why the other options fail: option a mistakes delegation for a transfer of accountability. Option b misstates who carries professional responsibility. Option d blames the client for a failure of supervision.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Delegation law: Responsibility can be delegated, but professional accountability always remains with the registered nurse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A physician writes a telephone order for '100 mg IV digoxin STAT' for a client in atrial fibrillation. The nurse recognizes that this dosage is roughly 400 times the standard therapeutic dose (0.25 mg). The nurse administers the 100 mg dose as written, and the client dies of digoxin toxicity. What is the nurse's legal liability?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The nurse is immune, because nurses are legally obliged to obey written orders blindly"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Only the hospital pharmacy is liable, because the pharmacist dispenses every ward medicine and checks every dose before it leaves"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The nurse is immune under respondeat superior, since the doctor is captain of the ship"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The nurse is personally liable, because a nurse must question a lethal order and withhold it"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The nurse is personally liable, because a nurse must question a lethal order and withhold it" A registered nurse is an autonomous licensed professional, not an agent who blindly executes orders. Under the law and nursing practice acts, a nurse has an independent legal and ethical duty to protect client safety, which includes recognizing lethal or unreasonable medication doses, withholding them, clarifying with the prescriber, and escalating up the chain of command if necessary. Administering a patently lethal order makes the nurse personally liable for professional negligence and wrongful death. The 'captain of the ship' doctrine does not protect nurses from individual accountability for gross clinical errors. Why the other options fail: option a assumes that an order indemnifies the nurse. Option b shares liability with the pharmacy but does not remove the nurse's. Option c does not protect gross error.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Legal rule: Licensed nurses are independently accountable for every medication they administer. Blindly following an obviously unsafe physician order results in nurse malpractice liability.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered nurse is asked by a surgeon to witness a client's signature on a consent form for an elective laparoscopic cholecystectomy. What is the legal scope of the nurse's role when witnessing an informed consent document?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Explain the risks, the benefits and the alternatives, and answer the surgical questions"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Guarantee to the client that no complication or surgical error will occur"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confirm competence, the authenticity of the signature and that consent was voluntary"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sign as the surrogate decision-maker if the client hesitates about the operation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Confirm competence, the authenticity of the signature and that consent was voluntary" When witnessing a consent form, the nurse's legal responsibility is limited to three specific elements: 1) confirming the client is mentally competent to sign; 2) verifying the authenticity of the signature; and 3) verifying that consent was given voluntarily without coercion. Explaining the surgical procedure, risks, benefits, and alternatives is the non-delegable legal responsibility of the healthcare provider performing the surgery (the surgeon). The nurse never guarantees outcomes and cannot sign as a surrogate. If the client does not understand the surgery, the nurse must notify the surgeon to return and provide further explanation before signing. Why the other options fail: option a is the surgeon's non-delegable duty. Option b is a guarantee that no one can give. Option d makes the nurse the decision-maker, which the witness never is.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Informed consent rule: The physician/surgeon explains risks, benefits, and alternatives. The nurse witnesses the signature, verifies competence, and ensures voluntary consent.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A popular political figure is admitted to the intensive care unit following a motor vehicle collision. A staff nurse working in the outpatient clinic on the ground floor logs into the hospital's electronic health record (EHR) using their personal credentials to read the politician's trauma scans and laboratory results out of personal curiosity. How should this action be evaluated?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An intentional privacy breach and gross misconduct, with discipline and regulatory referral"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acceptable, because every member of staff signs a general confidentiality agreement on appointment and is therefore covered"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acceptable educational practice, provided the findings are not shared with journalists"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A minor administrative infraction that needs no action unless the client complains"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "An intentional privacy breach and gross misconduct, with discipline and regulatory referral" Accessing a client's electronic protected health information (ePHI) without a legitimate clinical 'need to know' directly violates data protection laws (e.g., Ghana Data Protection Act, HIPAA, GDPR) and nursing professional codes of conduct. Clinical staff may only access the records of clients directly assigned to their care. Snooping into records out of curiosity is an intentional privacy breach that triggers automatic audit alerts, immediate disciplinary termination, and referral to the nursing regulatory board for professional sanctions. Why the other options fail: option b ignores the need-to-know rule. Option c treats curiosity as a clinical need. Option d waits for a complaint that privacy law does not require.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Data privacy rule: Access patient health records ONLY when directly involved in their clinical care ('need-to-know' basis). Snooping out of curiosity is illegal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 70-year-old alert client diagnosed with mild cognitive impairment but intact decision-making capacity states firmly to the nurse, 'I want to go home with visiting nurse support. I do not want to go to a nursing home.' The client's adult children demand that the nurse disregard the client's wishes and initiate immediate nursing home placement papers. What is the nurse's role as a patient advocate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Follow the children's instructions, because adult children hold authority over parents"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Support the client's choice, convene a family meeting and arrange home-based support"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Sedate the client to prevent resistance and move her to the nursing home smoothly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refuse to speak to the client or the family until a guardianship hearing is held"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Support the client's choice, convene a family meeting and arrange home-based support" Patient advocacy is a cornerstone of professional nursing practice. The nurse must defend the client's right to self-determination and voice their preferences when capable of expressing them, regardless of family pressure. Having mild impairment does not strip an individual of legal competence to choose their living arrangements. The nurse advocates for the client by organizing a multidisciplinary family meeting and coordinating safe home health services. Assuming family supremacy or chemically restraining the client violates fundamental human rights and professional codes. Why the other options fail: option a gives the children an authority they do not have. Option c is chemical restraint. Option d abandons the client while a dispute is settled.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Advocacy rule: The nurse advocates for the client's expressed wishes and autonomy, not the family's convenience or desires.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$While reviewing paper medical charts at 14:00, a nurse realizes that an intramuscular analgesic injection administered at 08:30 was never charted in the client's progress notes. Which method of correcting this omission adheres to legal documentation standards?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Squeeze the entry into the margin beside the 08:30 notes in tiny handwriting, and date it for the morning"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use correction fluid to erase another nurse's entry and write the dose over it"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Write a late entry with the writing time, the actual time, the drug and dose, and sign it"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Discard the chart page and rewrite the whole shift's notes from memory"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Write a late entry with the writing time, the actual time, the drug and dose, and sign it" Legal documentation standards require that records are accurate, contemporaneous, and unadulterated. When charting is omitted, the nurse must record a 'Late Entry'. The entry must state the current date and time of writing, reference the exact time the event occurred ('Late entry for 08:30: Administered morphine 5 mg IM.'), record factual clinical details, and be signed with professional credentials. Squeezing notes into margins, using white correction fluid, or recreating chart pages are considered fraudulent record tampering in court and can lead to criminal charges and license suspension. Why the other options fail: option a makes the note appear contemporaneous when it is not. Option b alters a colleague's entry. Option d destroys the original record.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Legal documentation: When adding omitted information, label it as a 'Late Entry' with current date/time, reference the event time, and sign. Never erase, squeeze, or white-out.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A post-operative client falls in the bathroom while attempting to ambulate unassisted. The nurse assists the client back to bed, completes a thorough head-to-toe physical assessment, and notifies the physician. When documenting the event and filing an incident/variance report, which nursing action is legally and professionally correct?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Chart that an incident report was filed with the risk management committee after the fall"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Document the facts in the record and file the incident report separately"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Put a carbon copy of the completed incident report in the client's bedside binder"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Leave the fall out of the notes if there was no laceration and no fracture"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Document the facts in the record and file the incident report separately" An incident report (variance/occurrence report) is an internal administrative quality improvement and risk management tool designed to identify system defects; it is protected under institutional quality assurance legal privilege. Documenting in the client's medical chart that an incident report was completed or filing a copy in the medical record can make the internal investigative document discoverable in a malpractice lawsuit. The nurse documents objective clinical facts (fall, assessment, physician notification, vitals, interventions) in the chart and submits the incident report separately. Concealing the fall is illegal fraud. Why the other options fail: options a and c put the internal report into the clinical record, where it becomes discoverable. Option d conceals an event that the record must show.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Documentation standard: Document objective facts in the client's medical record. NEVER document that an incident report was completed in the chart.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who underwent a subtotal thyroidectomy 6 hours ago develops increasing stridor, respiratory rate of 34 breaths/min, and severe neck swelling. The bedside nurse calls the surgical resident twice over 20 minutes, but the resident dismisses the concern and refuses to evaluate the client. What is the nurse's immediate professional and legal responsibility?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Accept the resident's judgement and wait for the morning surgical round to reassess"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Escalate the chain of command: attending surgeon, rapid response, nursing supervisor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Leave the client alone and walk the corridors looking for the resident so the concern can be raised face to face"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Give an unprescribed intravenous sedative to calm the client's breathing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Escalate the chain of command: attending surgeon, rapid response, nursing supervisor" When a client is deteriorating and the primary provider fails or refuses to respond appropriately, the nurse has an affirmative legal and professional duty to activate the organizational chain of command. Post-thyroidectomy neck hematoma and stridor represent an imminent, life-threatening airway emergency. The nurse must call the emergency rapid response team, contact the attending surgeon or surgical chief directly, and notify the nursing supervisor. Waiting for rounds or giving sedatives that cause fatal respiratory arrest is gross clinical malpractice. Why the other options fail: option a waits on an airway that is closing. Option c abandons a deteriorating client. Option d sedates a client who needs an airway, and does so outside any order.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Chain of command rule: When a client is deteriorating and a physician fails to respond, escalate up the chain of command immediately. Never abandon a compromised airway.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is administering morning medications. A competent client with full decision-making capacity states, 'I do not want that injection; stop and do not touch me!' The nurse responds, 'If you do not cooperate, I will have the security guards hold you down,' and forcibly administers the injection into the client's deltoid. Which intentional torts has the nurse committed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assault and battery"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Defamation and slander"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Invasion of privacy and libel"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Breach of contract and strict liability"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Assault and battery" Assault is an intentional act that creates an apprehension or fear of immediate harmful or offensive contact (e.g., threatening the client with security guards to force compliance). Battery is the actual unauthorized, intentional, offensive, or harmful physical contact with another person without consent (e.g., forcibly administering an intramuscular injection over a competent client's explicit refusal). Forcing medical treatment on a competent individual without legal/emergency authorization constitutes both civil and criminal battery. Defamation involves harming reputation; libel is written defamation. Why the other options fail: option b concerns reputation and spoken words, not contact. Option c concerns privacy and publication. Option d concerns agreements and product liability.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Legal tort definitions: Assault is the threat that causes apprehension of harm; Battery is the actual unauthorized physical contact.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$To prevent an alert, elderly client who is mildly restless from getting out of bed without assistance, a nurse raises all four bedrails, ties the client's wrists to the bedframe with gauze bandages, and places a bedside table tightly across the bed, all without a physician's order or clinical emergency indication. Which legal tort has been committed?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Malice aforethought"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Involuntary manslaughter"}, {"id": "c", "text": "False imprisonment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Breach of confidentiality"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "False imprisonment" False imprisonment is the unlawful intentional confinement, restraint, or restriction of an individual's freedom of movement without legal authorization, clinical justification, or consent. Applying physical restraints (or four raised side rails to prevent exit) without clinical necessity, less restrictive alternatives, or a valid time-limited provider order constitutes false imprisonment. Restraints may only be used when an imminent risk of physical harm to self or others exists, and they require strict protocols, ongoing documentation, and timely orders. Why the other options fail: option a requires an intent to kill. Option b requires a death. Option d concerns information rather than movement.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Restraint law: Unlawfully restraining a client or restricting their physical freedom without justification or orders constitutes False Imprisonment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$During a pediatric wellness examination, a nurse observes multiple circular burn marks resembling cigarette burns on a 3-year-old child's back, along with unexplained bilateral spiral fractures of different healing stages on X-ray. The parent claims the injuries occurred from 'falling down carpeted stairs.' What is the nurse's statutory legal obligation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Confront the parent angrily in the waiting area and demand a signed confession"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Stay silent, to preserve the parent's confidentiality under the privacy charter"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Wait six months until the next vaccination visit to see whether more injuries appear"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Report at once to child protection and the police, as a mandatory reporter"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Report at once to child protection and the police, as a mandatory reporter" Healthcare professionals are legally designated mandatory reporters under national child protection statutes (such as the Children's Act). When a nurse has reasonable cause to suspect child abuse or neglect based on clinical findings (such as patterned burns and multiple fractures in varying stages of healing incompatible with the reported history), the nurse is legally mandated to report immediately to child welfare authorities or police. Good-faith reporting is legally protected from civil liability. Maintaining confidentiality does not apply in child abuse; delay endangers the child's life. Why the other options fail: option a risks the child and contaminates any case. Option b treats confidentiality as a shield for abuse, which it is not. Option c leaves the child at risk for half a year.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Mandatory reporting: Nurses are legally obligated to report suspected child or elder abuse immediately. Patient confidentiality does not protect abuse.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse notices that a colleague on the night shift has slurred speech, an unsteady gait, smells faintly of alcohol, and has repeatedly signed out intravenous opioids from the automated dispensing cabinet for clients who later report receiving no pain relief. What is the nurse's immediate priority professional duty?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Tell the charge nurse at once and remove the colleague from client care"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ignore the signs, because confronting a colleague damages morale and team solidarity"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give the colleague strong coffee and send them for a nap in the staff break room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Search the colleague's locker and car boot without telling them, to find evidence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Tell the charge nurse at once and remove the colleague from client care" Client safety is the nurse's paramount professional and ethical responsibility. When a colleague exhibits signs of chemical impairment on duty, the nurse has an urgent ethical and legal obligation to report the behavior immediately to the supervisor or charge nurse so that the impaired individual is safely relieved of client care duties. Suspected drug diversion also requires formal investigation and drug screening according to institutional policy. Covering up, looking away, or conducting private vigilante searches enables ongoing endangerment of vulnerable clients. Why the other options fail: option b leaves impaired practice in place. Option c leaves the colleague in clinical contact and does nothing about the missing opioids. Option d is a search with no authority behind it.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Impaired nurse protocol: Protect client safety first by immediately reporting signs of impairment to the nursing supervisor.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A newly graduated registered nurse takes a selfie on a clinical pediatric ward showing a smiling child in the background receiving chemotherapy and posts it to a public social media page with the caption: 'So blessed to care for my brave little cancer warrior on 4-West today! #nurselife #oncology'. No name is written in the post. How does professional regulation view this behavior?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An inspiring act of health promotion that the hospital press office should commend"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Completely acceptable, since the child's record number was not written in the post"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A serious confidentiality and boundary breach that warrants disciplinary action"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Permissible if the nurse deletes the post within twenty-four hours"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "A serious confidentiality and boundary breach that warrants disciplinary action" Posting photographs, videos, or identifiable descriptions of clients on personal social media accounts—regardless of intent or whether the client's name is explicitly stated—is a flagrant breach of privacy laws (HIPAA, Data Protection Act) and nursing regulatory standards. The child's image and hospital unit constitute personally identifiable protected health information. Regulatory councils (NMC, NCSBN, State Boards) impose severe disciplinary penalties, including suspension or revocation of registration, for social media boundary violations. Deleting the post later does not erase the violation. Why the other options fail: option a ignores consent and the child's privacy. Option b is wrong because the image and the unit identify the child. Option d treats deletion as an undo, when publication has already happened.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Social media ethics: Never photograph clients or post any clinical client information on social media. Zero exceptions.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO1-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A multidisciplinary resuscitation team is managing a sudden pulseless ventricular fibrillation arrest in an adult client. Which leadership style is most effective and appropriate for the team leader during this acute crisis resuscitation?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Democratic leadership, with the team voting on each shock energy before it is delivered"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Laissez-faire leadership, with each member acting on their own judgement and reporting later"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Transformational leadership, with the leader motivating the team by long-term vision"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Autocratic leadership, with brief orders, named roles and closed-loop communication"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Autocratic leadership, with brief orders, named roles and closed-loop communication" In acute life-or-death resuscitations where seconds dictate survival, an autocratic (directive) leadership style is clinically indicated. The code leader must maintain command, make rapid high-stakes decisions, assign clear roles (chest compressions, airway, defibrillator, medication recorder), and demand closed-loop communication. Laissez-faire leads to chaos and delayed defibrillation; democratic consensus voting wastes critical seconds when immediate CPR is required; transformational lecturing is completely inappropriate during an emergency arrest. Why the other options fail: option a is democratic leadership, and putting each shock energy to a vote spends seconds the rhythm does not allow. Option b is laissez-faire, which leaves each member to act alone, and that is how roles and defibrillation timing are lost. Option c motivates over the long term, which is not what a pulseless patient needs in the next thirty seconds.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Leadership styles: Use autocratic (directive) leadership in immediate life-threatening crises; use democratic/transformational leadership for policy creation, change management, and unit committees.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$At the start of a night shift, the charge nurse of a 30-bed surgical unit learns that two scheduled nurses called in sick, leaving only two registered nurses and one healthcare assistant on duty. How should the charge nurse manage this critical staffing shortage?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Assess acuity, prioritise time-critical care, delegate basic care and escalate the shortfall in writing"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Ask the healthcare assistant to take the stable patients and give their antibiotics while the two nurses do the dressings"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lock the unit doors and refuse emergency transfers until the manager has been contacted"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Do the work of three nurses without escalation, because reporting looks like incompetence"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Assess acuity, prioritise time-critical care, delegate basic care and escalate the shortfall in writing" When faced with an acute staffing shortage, the charge nurse must: 1) re-triage and prioritize care by client acuity, protecting time-critical treatments (airway, urgent medications, vital signs, post-op monitoring); 2) delegate routine, non-complex tasks within assistive personnel scope; and 3) escalate the staffing crisis formally and in writing to nursing management/supervision to seek float coverage. Delegating IV medications to an assistant is illegal malpractice; unilaterally locking doors violates institutional emergency response; abandoning the shift constitutes gross patient abandonment. Why the other options fail: option b gives an unqualified assistant a delegated clinical task while the two nurses split across tasks. Option c closes the unit to emergencies, which is not within the charge nurse's authority. Option d abandons the unit and the licence.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Managing understaffing: Prioritize essential care by acuity, delegate within legal scope, and formally escalate the shortage to nursing administration in writing.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A registered nurse prepares to administer 2 mg of IV morphine from a 10 mg/mL glass ampoule. The client receives the ordered 2 mg dose. What is the mandatory legal and regulatory procedure for discarding the remaining 8 mg of morphine?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Discard it alone at the sink and document the waste later in the same shift"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Return the 8 mg to the pharmacy at the end of the shift for credit against ward stock, which is standard practice for part-ampoules"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Waste the 8 mg into the approved receptacle with a second nurse watching, then both co-sign"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the remaining 8 mg in a labelled syringe for the client's next scheduled dose"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Waste the 8 mg into the approved receptacle with a second nurse watching, then both co-sign" Controlled substances (such as opioids) are subject to strict statutory chain-of-custody protocols to prevent diversion and abuse. Any unused portion of a single-use narcotic must be destroyed immediately in the presence of a second licensed healthcare professional (RN or pharmacist). Both practitioners must directly witness the physical destruction and immediately sign/authenticate the narcotic record. Holding a leftover portion back for a later use is illegal and facilitates diversion, and un-witnessed sink wasting violates the drug regulations. Why the other options fail: option a wastes without a witness and documents after the fact. Option b returns a controlled drug to ward stock without the pharmacy intake, which breaks the chain of custody. Option d keeps part of a single-dose opioid back for a later dose, which is exactly how diversion starts.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Controlled substances rule: Wastage of any unused portion requires immediate direct witnessing by a second licensed nurse and simultaneous co-signing of the narcotic register.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse educator is designing a clinical skills update for experienced registered nurses returning to critical care practice. According to Malcolm Knowles' Adult Learning Theory (Andragogy), which instructional strategy will result in the highest engagement and learning retention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A lecture series covering the protocols from first principles, with a written exam at the end"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Self-directed reading of the unit policies, with a short quiz at the start of each shift"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Problem-centred simulation built on their own clinical experience, with self-directed solving"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Observing a senior clinician for two weeks, with no set objectives and no feedback"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Problem-centred simulation built on their own clinical experience, with self-directed solving" Malcolm Knowles' Andragogy theory emphasizes that adult learners: 1) are self-directed and internally motivated; 2) bring rich, diverse life and clinical experience that serves as a foundation for new learning; 3) are problem-centered and task-oriented (preferring real clinical dilemmas over theoretical rote memorization); and 4) learn best when the information has immediate, practical clinical utility. Passive rote lectures, infantilizing adult practitioners, and punitive shaming create hostility and inhibit adult learning. Why the other options fail: option a teaches protocols from first principles to people who already hold them. Option b leaves learning to reading with no application. Option d offers exposure without objectives or feedback.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Knowles' Adult Learning Principles: Adults are self-directed, experience-driven, problem-centered, and value immediate clinical relevance.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A hospital introduces a formal preceptorship program for newly registered nurses, alongside a voluntary professional mentorship initiative. How do the primary goals and structures of preceptorship and mentorship differ?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Preceptorship is a formal teaching qualification, while mentorship is informal peer support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Preceptorship is for new graduates only, while mentorship is reserved for nurses who have failed a competency review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Both are structured programmes of the same length, differing only in whether they are paid"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Preceptorship is time-limited and competence-focused; mentorship is long-term and career-focused"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Preceptorship is time-limited and competence-focused; mentorship is long-term and career-focused" Preceptorship is a formal, assigned, time-limited partnership between an experienced staff nurse (preceptor) and a novice nurse/student to teach clinical skills, socialize them into the unit, ensure patient safety, and verify clinical competence. In contrast, mentorship is typically an ongoing, voluntary, mutually agreed-upon professional relationship focused on long-term career growth, leadership development, scholarly aspirations, and personal fulfillment. Why the other options fail: option a turns preceptorship into a qualification and strips mentorship of any purpose. Option b invents eligibility rules for both. Option c makes them identical, which is the distinction the question asks for.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Preceptorship = formal, time-limited, competency and skill-focused onboarding. Mentorship = long-term, voluntary, holistic professional career guidance.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A preceptor observes that a new graduate nurse struggles with maintaining sterile technique during urinary catheter insertion, contaminating the sterile gloves before insertion. Which feedback approach by the preceptor best promotes learning and psychological safety?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Stop the procedure and send the new nurse out while a senior colleague finishes the catheter"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Say nothing during the procedure and raise it at the next annual appraisal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Correct the technique at the bedside in front of the client so that it is remembered"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Pause to protect sterility, restore asepsis, then debrief privately using the SBI framework"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Pause to protect sterility, restore asepsis, then debrief privately using the SBI framework" When a break in sterility occurs, the preceptor's first duty is client safety—gently stopping the breach and replacing sterile supplies without shaming the novice in front of the client. Afterward, constructive feedback should occur in a private, supportive setting using structured models like SBI (Situation: during catheter insertion; Behavior: touching the unsterile bed rail with sterile gloves; Impact: introduces infection risk). This preserves psychological safety and fosters clinical growth. Public humiliation damages morale, and ignoring contamination is how a CAUTI happens. Why the other options fail: option a removes the learner from the procedure, so no learning occurs. Option b delays the feedback until it cannot change behaviour. Option c corrects in front of the client, which teaches fear rather than technique.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Feedback principle: Intervene immediately to maintain client safety, then deliver formative feedback privately, objectively, and constructively.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse leader is formulating an evidence-based practice (EBP) question to evaluate whether chlorhexidine skin prep reduces central line-associated bloodstream infections (CLABSI) compared to povidone-iodine in adult ICU clients. Using the PICO framework, which element represents the 'C'?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Adult intensive care clients with central venous catheters, which is the population"}, {"id": "b", "text": "2% chlorhexidine gluconate in 70% isopropyl alcohol, which is the intervention"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Povidone-iodine topical skin preparation, the comparison intervention"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The incidence of catheter-related bloodstream infection, which is the outcome"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Povidone-iodine topical skin preparation, the comparison intervention" The PICO framework structures clinical research questions: P = Population/Patient problem (Adult ICU clients with central lines); I = Intervention (Chlorhexidine skin prep); C = Comparison/Control intervention (Povidone-iodine); O = Outcome (CLABSI incidence rate). In this scenario, povidone-iodine is the standard/comparator intervention ('C'). Why the other options fail: option a is the population, which is the P. Option b is the intervention, the I rather than the C. Option d is the outcome, the O.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember PICO: P = Population, I = Intervention, C = Comparison, O = Outcome. Identify each component methodically.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$When evaluating clinical evidence to update a hospital's clinical guideline on pressure injury prevention, which source represents the highest level of evidence (Level I) on the evidence-based practice hierarchy pyramid?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A single large randomised controlled trial published in a high-impact journal"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A systematic review and meta-analysis of several rigorous randomised trials"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A prospective cohort study of two thousand patients followed for five years"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An expert consensus guideline written by a national professional body"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A systematic review and meta-analysis of several rigorous randomised trials" In the hierarchy of clinical evidence, systematic reviews and meta-analyses of randomized controlled trials (RCTs) occupy the peak (Level I) because they synthesize data across multiple high-powered, rigorously controlled experimental studies, minimizing bias. Single case-control studies and qualitative phenomenological reports provide lower levels of evidence (Levels IV–VI). Expert opinion, editorials, and blogs reside at the very bottom (Level VII). Why the other options fail: option a is a single trial, which sits below a synthesis of trials. Option c is observational, which sits below any randomised design. Option d is expert opinion, which sits near the base of the pyramid.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hierarchy of evidence: Systematic reviews and meta-analyses of RCTs sit at the apex (Level I), while expert opinion and anecdotes sit at the base (Level VII).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse researcher is conducting an experimental clinical trial evaluating a new wound dressing in vulnerable, economically disadvantaged clients. Which protocol requirement is mandatory under the Declaration of Helsinki and Institutional Review Board (IRB) ethical standards?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Written consent taken by the ward clerk on the day, with the risks explained afterwards"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Verbal consent from a relative, because the client is poor and may not understand the form"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A consent form with the risks left out, so that the client is not frightened away"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Voluntary written consent, with risks, benefits and the right to withdraw explained"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Voluntary written consent, with risks, benefits and the right to withdraw explained" The Declaration of Helsinki and modern Institutional Review Board (IRB) codes mandate that human clinical research must uphold participant autonomy through voluntary informed consent, full disclosure of risks and benefits, and guarantees that participants may withdraw at any time without compromising clinical care. Coercive financial incentives, withholding standard curative care from control groups as in the unethical Tuskegee study, and publishing confidential identifiers are gross violations of bioethics and international human rights law. Why the other options fail: Taking written consent through the ward clerk on the day and explaining the risks afterwards inverts the order; verbal consent from a relative is not the client's consent; leaving the risks out so the client is not frightened voids the consent.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Research ethics: Voluntary informed consent, IRB approval, full risk disclosure, protection of vulnerable populations, and zero penalty for study withdrawal.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A hospital quality improvement (QI) team seeks to reduce medication administration turnaround times on an inpatient ward. According to the Plan-Do-Study-Act (PDSA) quality improvement cycle, what should the team do during the 'Do' phase?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Define the problem, set the aim and identify the root causes of the delay before any change is tested on the ward"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Analyse the results of the pilot and compare them against the predicted baseline"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Implement the change across the whole hospital as a standing policy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Run the change as a small pilot, document what happens and note unexpected problems"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Run the change as a small pilot, document what happens and note unexpected problems" The Deming/IHI PDSA cycle operates as follows: 1) PLAN = define the problem, aim, metrics, and intervention; 2) DO = test the change on a small scale (pilot), execute the plan, and record observations/obstacles; 3) STUDY = analyze post-intervention data against baseline and evaluate lessons learned; 4) ACT = decide whether to adopt, adapt, or abandon the change based on results. Why the other options fail: Defining the problem and the aim is the Plan phase, which comes before the test; analysing the pilot results is Study, which follows it; rolling the change across the whole hospital is Act, which waits for the study.$q$,
  strategy = $q$PDSA cycle stages: Plan (design), Do (test small pilot), Study (analyze results), Act (adopt, adapt, or abandon).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A post-surgical client receives an accidental tenfold overdose of intravenous insulin due to a combination of look-alike packaging, an un-calibrated infusion pump, nurse fatigue from a 16-hour mandatory shift, and a distracting ward environment. According to James Reason's Swiss Cheese Model of system accidents, how are these contributory factors categorized?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Individual carelessness by the nurse, to be handled through the disciplinary process"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Active failures alone, because latent conditions cannot contribute to one overdose"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Latent system conditions that aligned with active failures to defeat the defences"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An unforeseeable event, which no model of accident causation can explain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Latent system conditions that aligned with active failures to defeat the defences" James Reason's Swiss Cheese Model posits that complex organizational accidents rarely stem from isolated individual carelessness. Instead, defense barriers (layers of cheese) contain inherent holes (latent failures: look-alike drug packaging, poor equipment maintenance, excessive mandatory overtime, poor lighting/distractions). When these holes align across multiple institutional defenses, an active human error penetrates and harms the client. System engineering focuses on eliminating latent hazards rather than merely blaming the last human touchpoint. Why the other options fail: Blaming individual carelessness stops the analysis where the model begins; separating active failures from latent conditions contradicts the model, which holds that they align; an unforeseeable event is not what the model finds when the defences were defeated.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Swiss Cheese Model: Complex medical errors result from alignment of latent system failures (organizational defects) and active human errors across defense barriers.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Under a Just Culture patient safety framework, a nurse accidentally misprograms an intravenous infusion pump because two software menus looked virtually identical on a new monitor. In the Just Culture decision matrix, which categorization and management response are appropriate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "At-risk behaviour: coach the nurse and remove the confusing menu in the next update"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reckless behaviour: refer the nurse for a fitness-to-practise review"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Human error: console the nurse, examine the pump interface and add a safeguard"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sabotage: remove the nurse from clinical duties pending the investigation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Human error: console the nurse, examine the pump interface and add a safeguard" Just Culture categorizes workplace behaviors into three distinct categories: 1) Human error (inadvertent mistake, slip, or lapse while performing care) -> console the worker and fix system/device vulnerabilities; 2) At-risk behavior (taking shortcuts where risk is mistakenly believed to be negligible or justified) -> coach the worker and remove incentives for shortcuts; 3) Reckless behavior (conscious, intentional disregard of substantial, unjustifiable risk) -> disciplinary sanction. Misprogramming due to confusing user-interface design is a classic human error deserving consolation and system redesign. Why the other options fail: option a is at-risk behaviour, which requires a known risk the nurse accepted. Option b is reckless behaviour, which requires conscious disregard. Option d is sabotage, which requires intent to harm.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Just Culture framework: Human error = Console and redesign system; At-risk behavior = Coach; Reckless behavior = Discipline.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$In Ghana's primary health care system, a Community Health Officer (CHO) at a CHPS (Community-based Health Planning and Services) compound identifies a pregnant woman in labour exhibiting signs of severe pre-eclampsia (BP 170/110 mmHg, 3+ proteinuria, hyperreflexia). What is the CHO's appropriate management and referral action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give magnesium sulphate and an antihypertensive, stabilise, then refer urgently with handover"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give magnesium and keep her at the CHPS compound until the blood pressure settles"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give oral antihypertensives and send her home to return if the headache worsens"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Arrange a taxi to the district hospital without magnesium, since a loading dose can only be given once she reaches hospital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Give magnesium sulphate and an antihypertensive, stabilise, then refer urgently with handover" Under Ghana Health Service primary care and CHPS protocols, severe pre-eclampsia is an obstetric emergency requiring urgent stabilization followed by expedited referral up the healthcare cascade. The Community Health Officer administers a loading dose of magnesium sulphate (anticonvulsant) and rapid-acting antihypertensive (such as oral labetalol or hydralazine) within standing orders, secures transport, notifies the receiving District Hospital, and accompanies or coordinates skilled handover. Retaining the patient at CHPS causes eclamptic seizures and maternal death; discharging home is fatal malpractice; uncoordinated commercial transport fails basic emergency transfer standards. Why the other options fail: option b delays referral for a condition that can progress to eclampsia within hours. Option c sends a woman with severe pre-eclampsia home. Option d moves her without the loading dose that standing orders permit.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CHPS referral pathway: Stabilize with protocol emergency therapy (magnesium sulphate), communicate with receiving facility, and ensure rapid escorted transfer.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse leader is advocating for community enrollment in the National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS) in a peri-urban district. What is the fundamental socioeconomic and health policy objective of the NHIS in advancing Universal Health Coverage (UHC)?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Removing financial barriers at the point of care and preventing catastrophic out-of-pocket payment"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Free care for all registered members, with the whole cost recovered from higher charges levied on non-members"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A contributory scheme that covers the formal sector and expands to the informal sector"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A basic package for every citizen, funded from general taxation with no contribution"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Removing financial barriers at the point of care and preventing catastrophic out-of-pocket payment" The National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS) was established (under Act 650, revised Act 852) to eliminate the punitive 'cash-and-carry' system that forced patients to pay out-of-pocket before receiving emergency or essential healthcare. Its primary objective is risk pooling and social financial protection to ensure that all citizens, especially the vulnerable and indigent, can access essential healthcare without experiencing catastrophic poverty, advancing Universal Health Coverage (UHC). NHIS is a social public health protection mechanism, not a private for-profit venture. Why the other options fail: Recovering the cost from non-member charges is the opposite of removing a financial barrier; contributory cover that stops at the formal sector leaves the informal majority outside; a tax-funded basic package is a different financing model, not the removal of point-of-care fees.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Health financing policy: NHIS eliminates 'cash-and-carry' and prevents catastrophic out-of-pocket spending to achieve Universal Health Coverage.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A senior nurse is appointed to the District Health Management Team (DHMT). How can the nurse leader most effectively exercise policy voice to improve maternal and neonatal survival across the district?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Present the mortality figures to the assembly and ask the members to fund a new vehicle"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Use district data to find the worst maternal outcomes, target resources there and build transport"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Lobby for more midwives at the district hospital, where most of the district's deliveries already take place"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Campaign for a maternity wing at every facility, starting nearest the district capital"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Use district data to find the worst maternal outcomes, target resources there and build transport" Nurses in health administration and policy leadership exercise policy voice by using empirical public health data (such as DHIMS-2 metrics) to highlight healthcare disparities, maternal-neonatal morbidity trends, and resource bottlenecks. Advocating for targeted resource allocation, ambulance networks, and training for frontline primary healthcare providers drives systemic population-level improvements. Apathy, urban misallocation, and eliminating nursing cadres actively worsen health outcomes. Why the other options fail: Presenting mortality figures to ask for a vehicle does not target the worst outcomes; concentrating midwives at the district hospital ignores where the deaths are occurring; a maternity wing in every facility spreads resources evenly rather than toward the worst outcomes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nursing policy voice: Leverage epidemiological health data (DHIMS-2) to advocate for equitable resource allocation and address maternal-child health disparities.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An experienced certified midwife wishes to establish an independent, private maternity home in an underserved rural district. Which regulatory standard must the practitioner fulfill before legally admitting clients and providing obstetric care?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Register the business with the assembly and open after an environmental health inspection"}, {"id": "b", "text": "HeFRA facility accreditation, NMC good standing, and practice within scope and referral protocols"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Confirm NMC registration and open the home, since a licence to practise is also a licence for the facility"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain HeFRA accreditation and open, since the facility regulator also covers the nurse"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "HeFRA facility accreditation, NMC good standing, and practice within scope and referral protocols" Health entrepreneurship requires strict adherence to statutory regulatory frameworks. Under Ghanaian law (Health Institutions and Facilities Act 829), all healthcare facilities—including nurse/midwife-led private clinics and maternity homes—must be inspected, accredited, and licensed by the Health Facilities Regulatory Agency (HeFRA). Furthermore, the practitioner must maintain active licensure and good standing with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC), ensure a defined clinical scope, and maintain formal emergency referral links with a higher-level facility. Operating without HeFRA licensing is illegal and subject to criminal closure. Why the other options fail: option a registers a business without licensing the facility or checking professional standing. Option c treats a personal licence as a facility licence. Option d accredits the building and leaves the practitioner unchecked.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Health entrepreneurship: Private health facilities require formal licensing from regulatory authorities (HeFRA) and active council registration (NMC).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse leader observes that mainstream news media frequently portrays nurses as passive, subservient handmaidens with low technical competence. Which professional initiative most effectively elevates the public image and voice of the nursing profession?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Publish research, write in the media, and join policy panels and professional associations"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A national advertising campaign showing nurses at work in every region, funded and run by the professional association"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Speak only through the ministry's communications office so the message stays consistent"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Focus on clinical work and let the image improve as patients experience good care"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Publish research, write in the media, and join policy panels and professional associations" The public image of nursing directly influences public trust, recruitment of top candidates, legislative healthcare funding, and the profession's policy-making influence. The International Council of Nurses (ICN) and national associations advocate for proactive media engagement: publishing clinical research, writing thought-leadership articles, speaking as expert panelists on national health topics, and showcasing nursing's scientific, autonomous clinical contributions. Silence leaves the stereotype unchallenged. Why the other options fail: An advertising campaign spends money on image rather than on evidence and expertise; speaking only through the ministry's communications office channels the profession's voice through another body; waiting for patients to notice good care assumes an image changes by itself.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Professional image: Elevate nursing through thought leadership, clinical research publication, media engagement, and professional association advocacy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse manager notes that several staff nurses on a high-mortality oncology unit are demonstrating emotional exhaustion, cynicism, chronic absenteeism, and detachment from client suffering (compassion fatigue and burnout). Which organizational strategy is most effective in combating burnout?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Structured debriefing, safe staffing ratios, confidential counselling and peer support"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Mandatory resilience training for the nurses, with attendance recorded and appraised"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A roster giving each nurse three consecutive days off every month so that people can recover between shifts"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Rotate staff through the oncology unit quickly so no one is exposed for long"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Structured debriefing, safe staffing ratios, confidential counselling and peer support" Compassion fatigue and burnout are occupational hazards in high-stress, emotionally demanding healthcare specialties. Evidence-based organizational interventions include: regular clinical multidisciplinary debriefings (such as Schwartz Center Rounds) to discuss emotional aspects of care, ensuring safe staffing ratios, providing confidential mental health support programs (EAP), and fostering peer support. Punitive threats, increasing workload, and demanding emotional apathy exacerbate psychological trauma and accelerate nurse turnover. Why the other options fail: Mandatory resilience training makes the individual responsible for a system problem; three consecutive days off every month adds rest without changing the workload that caused the exhaustion; rotating staff quickly reduces exposure instead of fixing it and spreads the burnout.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Combating burnout: Address systemic staffing ratios, offer structured emotional debriefings (Schwartz rounds), and provide confidential employee counseling.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse manager intends to introduce a new computerized barcode medication administration (BCMA) system on a medical ward where nurses have used paper charts for 25 years. The staff express strong anxiety and resistance. Applying Kurt Lewin's 3-Step Change Model, what should the manager do during the 'Unfreezing' stage?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Implement the system overnight and train afterwards, so no one has time to resist"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Help staff see the limits of paper charting and address their anxieties, creating readiness"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Set a compliance target for the new system and audit it monthly, reporting the results to the ward manager"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Set up a staff committee to decide whether the system should be introduced at all"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Help staff see the limits of paper charting and address their anxieties, creating readiness" Kurt Lewin's 3-Step Change Theory consists of: 1) UNFREEZING = creating motivation and readiness for change by challenging the status quo, showing the need for innovation (e.g., highlighting medication error risks in paper records), and addressing emotional resistance; 2) MOVING (CHANGING) = executing the transition, providing hands-on training, and coaching through the new workflow; 3) REFREEZING = stabilizing and institutionalizing the change into permanent standard operating procedures. Why the other options fail: Implementing overnight and training afterwards skips unfreezing and guarantees resistance; a compliance target audited monthly is refreezing, which comes after the change; a staff committee deciding whether to introduce the system delegates a decision the manager has already been given.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Lewin's Change Theory: Unfreezing (create awareness and readiness), Moving (implement change and train), Refreezing (institutionalize and sustain).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A clinical governance committee conducts a clinical audit on post-operative surgical site infection (SSI) rates in a regional hospital. The audit reveals an SSI rate of 14%, well above the national benchmark of 4%. To complete the clinical audit cycle and improve client outcomes, what is the mandatory next step?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Present the findings to the surgical team and agree to review the rate again next year"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Analyse causes, apply evidence-based changes, and re-audit after a set period to close the loop"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Introduce the WHO checklist and record compliance, without repeating the audit"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Refer the unit for an external accreditation review, since an internal audit has no authority to change practice"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Analyse causes, apply evidence-based changes, and re-audit after a set period to close the loop" A clinical audit is a quality improvement process that seeks to improve client care and outcomes through systematic review of care against explicit criteria and the implementation of change. The complete clinical audit cycle requires: 1) identifying standard criteria; 2) measuring current practice; 3) comparing results with standards; 4) implementing corrective evidence-based changes; and 5) re-auditing practice to verify that improvements occurred ('closing the audit loop'). Hiding the report or falsifying data violates clinical governance and ethics. Why the other options fail: option a records the finding without changing anything. Option c introduces the change but never measures it, so the loop stays open. Option d hands the problem to another body, which does not close the cycle.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Clinical audit cycle: Measure practice against standards, implement evidence-based corrective changes, and re-audit to close the audit loop.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-PRO2-020$q$;

