BEGIN;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 64-year-old client with chronic hypertension has a baseline serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (88 mcmol/L). Following an episode of severe dehydration, repeat testing reveals a serum creatinine of 2.1 mg/dL (186 mcmol/L) and blood urea nitrogen (BUN) of 42 mg/dL. How should the nurse interpret this rise in serum creatinine?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Doubling of creatinine represents an acute loss of about 50% of the baseline glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A circadian fluctuation, since creatinine varies through the day within its normal physiological limits in health"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Increased dietary protein catabolism, with a preserved glomerular filtration rate"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Permanent end-stage renal fibrosis, which now requires immediate peritoneal dialysis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Doubling of creatinine represents an acute loss of about 50% of the baseline glomerular filtration rate" Serum creatinine is an inverse biomarker of glomerular filtration rate (GFR). Under steady-state conditions, doubling of serum creatinine (from 1.0 to 2.1 mg/dL) reflects an approximate 50% reduction in GFR and renal functional capacity. In acute dehydration, this elevation points to acute kidney injury (prerenal azotemia) requiring rapid fluid resuscitation before acute tubular necrosis occurs. Why the other options fail: option b calls a doubling of creatinine a circadian variation, when normal variation is a few micromoles and never a doubling in one illness. Option c blames dietary protein for a change that dehydration explains, and BUN rises with protein while creatinine does not. Option d diagnoses irreversible fibrosis from a single acute value, before any attempt at resuscitation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Renal Physiology Rule: Serum creatinine has an inverse relationship with GFR. A doubling of creatinine signifies an approximate 50% drop in renal filtration rate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with decompensated cirrhosis has massive abdominal ascites and 3+ bilateral pedal edema. Vital signs are: blood pressure 88/54 mmHg, pulse 112 beats/min, and urine output 18 mL/hour. Which fluid compartment imbalance does this clinical scenario represent?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "True total-body fluid deficit across all compartments, which would explain both the ascites and the pedal oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Intravascular overload with a raised central venous pressure, since the client is oedematous"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Third-space sequestration of fluid with severe intravascular depletion, or effective arterial hypovolaemia"}, {"id": "d", "text": "An intracellular shift causing cellular hyperosmolality, which would draw fluid out of the vessels"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Third-space sequestration of fluid with severe intravascular depletion, or effective arterial hypovolaemia" In cirrhosis and severe hypoalbuminemia, loss of plasma oncotic pressure combined with splanchnic arterial vasodilation allows intravascular fluid to transudate into the peritoneal cavity (third-spacing: ascites and interstitial edema). Despite massive total-body fluid accumulation, the client suffers from critical intravascular hypovolemia (manifested by hypotension, tachycardia, and oliguria), creating a prerenal state. Why the other options fail: option a denies the ascites and oedema that show total body fluid is increased, not reduced. Option b reads oedema as intravascular filling, when the pressure and the urine output show the vessels are empty. Option d invents an intracellular shift, which would not produce ascites or a low blood pressure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Third-spacing pathology: Massive fluid trapped in interstitial/peritoneal spaces WHILE intravascular space is empty -> hypotension, tachycardia, oliguria.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 32-year-old presents to the cholera treatment centre with severe 'rice-water' diarrhea and vomiting for 14 hours. Assessment reveals: profound lethargy, sunken eyes, skin tenting persisting for > 3 seconds, radial pulses faint and thready at 138 beats/min, blood pressure 74/42 mmHg, and dry mucous membranes. What is the immediate priority intervention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give oral rehydration solution, 200 mL by cup every 10 minutes, and reassess the skin pinch after an hour"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Give intramuscular metoclopramide to stop the vomiting before replacing fluid"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Establish large-bore IV access and give 1,000 mL of Ringer's lactate or 0.9% saline over 15 to 30 minutes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Collect a stool specimen for dark-field microscopy before starting resuscitation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Establish large-bore IV access and give 1,000 mL of Ringer's lactate or 0.9% saline over 15 to 30 minutes" Severe dehydration with signs of hypovolemic shock (hypotension, thready tachycardia, delayed skin pinch, lethargy) represents a critical medical emergency (WHO Plan C). Oral rehydration alone is insufficient and unsafe in shock or altered consciousness. The immediate priority is rapid intravenous resuscitation with balanced isotonic crystalloids (Ringer's lactate or 0.9% normal saline) through large-bore IV access to restore intravascular volume. Why the other options fail: option a gives oral fluid to a lethargic client in shock, which is unsafe and far too slow. Option b treats the vomiting and leaves the circulation empty. Option d puts a laboratory test ahead of resuscitation in a client with a systolic pressure of 74 mmHg.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe dehydration shock (Plan C): Large-bore IV access -> Rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloids (Ringer's lactate / Normal Saline) immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Anuric acute kidney injury occurs in a client who received 4 liters of intravenous fluids over 12 hours. Assessment reveals: severe dyspnea, orthopnea, jugular venous distention to the angle of the jaw at 45 degrees, S3 gallop, and diffuse wet crackles across bilateral lower and middle lung fields. Oxygen saturation is 84% on room air. What are the nurse's immediate priority actions?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Lay the client flat and give a 500 mL bolus of 5% dextrose, to support the circulation while the cause is being found"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Perform chest percussion and walk the client round the ward to clear the secretions"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Give 100 mL of 20% mannitol by rapid intravenous push, and withhold oxygen until the provider reviews"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Sit the client upright, give high-flow oxygen, stop the IV fluids, and call the provider for diuretic or dialysis"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Sit the client upright, give high-flow oxygen, stop the IV fluids, and call the provider for diuretic or dialysis" Acute pulmonary edema resulting from iatrogenic fluid overload in an oliguric/anuric client is a life-threatening emergency. Immediate nursing priorities: 1) Position in high-Fowler's with legs dependent to drop cardiac preload and ease ventilation, 2) Apply high-flow oxygen immediately, 3) Shut off IV fluid infusions immediately, and 4) Alert the medical team for urgent loop diuretics or emergent ultrafiltration/hemodialysis. Why the other options fail: option a lies the client flat, which increases venous return into already flooded lungs, and gives more fluid. Option b exercises a hypoxic client and does nothing for the oedema. Option c gives an osmotic load by push and withholds oxygen from a client saturating at 84%.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Fluid overload pulmonary edema sequence: High-Fowler's position -> Stop IV fluids -> Apply High-flow O2 -> Urgent dialysis/diuretics.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with symptomatic severe hyponatremia is prescribed an intravenous infusion of 3% sodium chloride (hypertonic saline) at 40 mL/hour. Which nursing intervention is mandatory to ensure patient safety during the infusion?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Infuse by pump, check neurological status often, and keep the sodium rise within 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Run it by gravity through a 22-gauge hand vein, so that the infusion can be stopped quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Run it alongside 500 mL of 0.45% saline, so that the sodium is corrected more evenly"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Tell the client to drink two litres of water, so that the sodium is distributed harmlessly through the body"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Infuse by pump, check neurological status often, and keep the sodium rise within 8 to 10 mEq/L in 24 hours" Hypertonic saline (3% NaCl) is a high-alert intravenous solution. Rapid correction of chronic hyponatremia causes sudden osmotic dehydration of brain cells, resulting in catastrophic Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis), causing irreversible flaccid quadriplegia and 'locked-in' syndrome. Guidelines mandate strictly limiting the sodium correction rate to no more than 8-10 mEq/L over 24 hours, infused via pump with close neuro checks. Why the other options fail: option b gives a high-alert solution by gravity through a small peripheral vein, which cannot control the rate. Option c adds hypotonic fluid to a hypertonic infusion, which defeats the correction. Option d gives free water to a client who is already water-loaded.$q$,
  strategy = $q$3% Hypertonic Saline rule: High-alert infusion! Max correction rate: 8-10 mEq/L per 24 hours. Rapid correction causes fatal osmotic demyelination syndrome.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with small cell lung cancer develops Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone (SIADH). Serum sodium drops to 116 mEq/L (reference 135-145 mEq/L), and the client becomes disoriented, obtunded, and develops generalized tonic-clonic seizures. What is the immediate pharmacological intervention?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "5% dextrose at 150 mL/hour, to dilute the sodium and relieve the cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A 100 mL bolus of 3% hypertonic saline over 10 to 15 minutes, to relieve the cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Subcutaneous desmopressin 4 micrograms, to stop the inappropriate antidiuresis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Oral sodium chloride tablets with 1 litre of free water, which is safer than hypertonic saline"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "A 100 mL bolus of 3% hypertonic saline over 10 to 15 minutes, to relieve the cerebral oedema" Severe acute hyponatremia (< 120 mEq/L) with neurological emergencies (seizures, coma, brainstem herniation) is caused by severe cerebral edema from osmotic water entry into brain cells. The emergency intervention is 100-150 mL of IV 3% hypertonic saline infused over 10-15 minutes (repeatable up to 2 times if seizures persist) to acutely raise serum sodium by 4-6 mEq/L, reversing brain swelling and halting seizures. Why the other options fail: option a gives free water, which lowers the sodium further and worsens the cerebral oedema. Option c treats the antidiuresis rather than the emergency, and subcutaneous desmopressin acts too slowly. Option d gives salt with a litre of water, which does not raise the sodium when the client is already retaining water.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe hyponatremic seizures (< 120 mEq/L): Immediate IV 3% hypertonic saline bolus to shrink cerebral edema and arrest seizures.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client in oliguric acute kidney injury has a serum potassium level of 7.1 mEq/L (reference 3.5-5.0 mEq/L). The telemetry monitor reveals tall, peaked T waves, a prolonged PR interval, and widening of the QRS complex. What is the FIRST medication the nurse must administer immediately?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Intravenous calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL infused over 2 to 3 minutes to stabilise the myocardium"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Insulin 10 units with 50 mL of 50% dextrose, which shifts the potassium into the cells faster than any other drug"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate 30 g, which removes potassium from the body"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Intravenous furosemide 40 mg as a bolus, which excretes the potassium in the urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Intravenous calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL infused over 2 to 3 minutes to stabilise the myocardium" In severe hyperkalemia with electrocardiographic changes (peaked T waves, QRS widening), the client is at imminent risk of fatal ventricular fibrillation and asystole. The immediate first-line drug is IV Calcium gluconate (or calcium chloride). Calcium does not lower potassium; it directly antagonizes potassium's membrane-depolarizing effects, stabilizing the myocardial cell membrane within 1-3 minutes. Insulin and dextrose are given next to shift potassium into cells. Why the other options fail: option b is the correct second drug, but it shifts potassium over 15 to 30 minutes and does not protect the myocardium now. Option c works over hours in the gut. Option d depends on kidneys that are not making urine. Only calcium stabilises the membrane within minutes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Hyperkalemia emergency sequence: 1) IV Calcium gluconate FIRST (stabilizes heart membrane), 2) IV Insulin + D50 (shifts K+ into cells), 3) Eliminate K+ (dialysis / kayexalate).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A provider prescribes intravenous potassium chloride (KCl) 20 mEq in 250 mL of normal saline for a client with severe hypokalemia (potassium 2.7 mEq/L). Which nursing safety rule is absolute regarding intravenous potassium administration?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Give it as a slow direct push over 60 seconds, which is safe when the volume is small"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Run it by gravity through a 24-gauge vein in the dorsum of the hand, to dilute it quickly"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warm the bag in hot water first, since warmth prevents the painful vein irritation that potassium causes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Never give it as a push or bolus: use a pump and keep the peripheral rate at or below 10 mEq/hour"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Never give it as a push or bolus: use a pump and keep the peripheral rate at or below 10 mEq/hour" Intravenous potassium chloride is a high-alert medication. Direct IV push or rapid bolus administration causes sudden lethal cardiac arrest (used in lethal injection protocols). Safety mandates: 1) NEVER give IV push, 2) Always infuse diluted in IV fluids using a dedicated electronic infusion pump, 3) Limit peripheral infusion rate to a maximum of 10 mEq/hour (maximum 20 mEq/hr via central venous line with continuous cardiac monitoring). Why the other options fail: option a is a direct push, which is the lethal practice the item tests. Option b uses gravity through the smallest available cannula, so neither the rate nor the dilution can be controlled. Option c warms a high-alert solution without addressing the rate, and warmth does not prevent phlebitis from potassium.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Potassium safety rule: NEVER IV PUSH (causes immediate cardiac arrest!). Max peripheral rate = 10 mEq/hour. Always use an infusion pump.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with chronic alcohol dependence presents with tremors, hyperactive deep tendon reflexes, and a serum magnesium level of 1.1 mg/dL (reference 1.7-2.2 mg/dL). When the blood pressure cuff is inflated on the upper arm, carpal spasm occurs. While infusing intravenous magnesium sulfate, which assessment parameter must the nurse monitor continuously to detect hypermagnesemic toxicity?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Visual acuity and intraocular pressure, which fall early in magnesium toxicity"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Patellar deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate and hourly urine output"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Capillary glucose every 15 minutes, which detects the hypoglycaemia magnesium causes"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Bowel sounds in all four quadrants, which disappear as magnesium accumulates"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Patellar deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate and hourly urine output" During intravenous magnesium sulfate therapy, toxicity manifests progressively, and the thresholds should be read in the same units as the reference range: loss of the deep tendon reflexes occurs first, at about 5 mg/dL and above; respiratory depression below 12 breaths a minute follows as the level approaches 10 mg/dL; and cardiac arrest comes above about 15 mg/dL. That is why the patellar reflex is the earliest bedside warning, and why it is checked before the rate is increased. Magnesium is excreted renally; oliguria causes dangerous drug accumulation. The nurse must assess patellar reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output before and during infusion. Calcium gluconate is the antidote. Why the other options fail: option a describes parameters that magnesium does not affect early, and the eye is not the target organ. Option c looks for hypoglycaemia, which is not the recognised toxicity pattern. Option d is a late and non-specific finding, whereas reflexes are lost first and are the earliest reliable sign.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Magnesium toxicity surveillance: Loss of Deep Tendon Reflexes (DTRs) is the EARLIEST sign! Stop infusion and give IV Calcium gluconate.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is providing catheter care for a bedridden client with an indwelling Foley urinary catheter. Which action by the nurse represents an evidence-based practice in the catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI) prevention bundle?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Irrigate the catheter daily with 50 mL of sterile saline, to keep the lumen clear"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hang the bag on the side rail while repositioning the client, so that it is not left standing on the floor"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Disconnect the catheter from the tubing each morning, to empty and measure the urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Keep the system closed and unobstructed, and hang the bag below the level of the bladder"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Keep the system closed and unobstructed, and hang the bag below the level of the bladder" CAUTI prevention bundle evidence-based guidelines include: 1) Maintain a sterile, continuously closed drainage system (never disconnect tubing), 2) Keep the drainage bag below the level of the bladder at all times to prevent gravity backflow of contaminated urine into the bladder, 3) Hang bag on the bed frame (never on moving side rails or resting on the floor), 4) Secure catheter to the thigh to prevent urethral traction, and 5) Avoid routine antimicrobial bladder irrigation. Why the other options fail: option a irrigates a closed system routinely, which introduces organisms. Option b hangs the bag on a moving rail, where it can rise above the bladder and allow backflow. Option c breaks the closed system daily, which is the single commonest route to infection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$CAUTI prevention rules: Bag ALWAYS below bladder level -> Keep system closed -> Secure to thigh -> Never hang on moving side rails.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An older adult male with benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) has not voided for 16 hours. Assessment reveals intense suprapubic pain, agitation, and a palpable dull bladder extending above the umbilicus. A bladder ultrasound scanner estimates 950 mL of retained urine. When inserting an indwelling catheter, what should the nurse anticipate and monitor for following the release of this large volume?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Immediate oliguria with hyperkalaemic cardiac arrest, because an obstructed kidney cannot excrete the potassium load at all after relief"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute nephrotic syndrome with severe hypoalbuminaemia, from heavy glomerular protein loss during the decompression"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Post-obstructive diuresis with hypotension and transient haematuria, as the decompressed tract loses its concentrating gradient"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe metabolic alkalosis from retained ammonia, because the overdistended bladder reabsorbs urea into the bloodstream"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Post-obstructive diuresis with hypotension and transient haematuria, as the decompressed tract loses its concentrating gradient" Rapid relief of chronic high-pressure urinary retention (> 800-1,000 mL) can trigger two well-known physiological responses: 1) Post-obstructive diuresis—a dramatic polyuric state caused by loss of medullary concentrating gradient and accumulated urea, requiring strict fluid balance monitoring to prevent hypovolemic shock, and 2) Transient hematuria and hypotension from sudden pelvic venous decompression and mucosal stretching. Why the other options fail: option a describes the opposite problem, since the risk after relief is polyuria rather than oliguria. Option b is a glomerular disease unrelated to decompression. Option d describes an acid-base consequence of vomiting or diuretics, not of relieving retention.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Severe urinary retention decompression: Monitor for post-obstructive diuresis (massive urine output leading to volume depletion and hypotension).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$An 8-year-old child is brought to the clinic 2 weeks after recovering from an untreated streptococcal pharyngitis (strep throat). The parents report that the child has puffy swollen eyes in the morning, reduced urine output, and dark 'tea-colored / cola-colored' urine. Blood pressure is elevated at 134/86 mmHg. Which clinical condition does the nurse suspect?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Minimal change nephrotic syndrome, which gives heavy proteinuria and no red cells on the dipstick"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome, which follows bloody diarrhoea with anaemia and a low platelet count"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, two weeks after an untreated streptococcal pharyngitis"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute bacterial cystitis, which gives dysuria and frequency but no oedema, hypertension or haematuria"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, two weeks after an untreated streptococcal pharyngitis" Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (APSGN) is an immune-complex mediated type III hypersensitivity reaction occurring 1-3 weeks after Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis or impetigo. Immune complexes deposit in glomerular basement membranes, causing inflammation and red blood cell leakage. Hallmarks: 1) Periorbital edema, 2) Tea- or cola-colored urine (gross hematuria with RBC casts), 3) Hypertension from sodium/water retention, and 4) Mild-to-moderate proteinuria. Why the other options fail: option a gives proteinuria without haematuria, and the cola-coloured urine is blood. Option b follows a diarrhoeal illness with anaemia and a low platelet count, not a sore throat two weeks earlier. Option d causes dysuria and frequency without oedema, hypertension or haematuria.$q$,
  strategy = $q$APSGN triad: Recent strep infection + Tea/cola-colored urine (hematuria) + Periorbital edema & hypertension. Contrast with Nephrotic (massive proteinuria, no gross blood).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 4-year-old child is admitted with massive generalized edema (anasarca), severe ascites, and facial swelling. Urinalysis demonstrates 4+ proteinuria (> 3.5 g/24 hours) and oval fat bodies, but no red blood cells. Serum albumin is profoundly low at 1.8 g/dL (reference 3.5-5.0 g/dL), and serum cholesterol is elevated at 340 mg/dL. Which nursing intervention is essential in the care of this child with Nephrotic Syndrome?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "A high-sodium diet with liberal fluid, to restore the oncotic pressure"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Strict infection precautions, with daily weight and abdominal girth measurements"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Complete immobilisation with strict bed rest for 4 weeks, to reduce protein loss"}, {"id": "d", "text": "High-dose intravenous potassium chloride, to replace the losses in the urine"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Strict infection precautions, with daily weight and abdominal girth measurements" Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by massive proteinuria, severe hypoalbuminemia, generalized edema (anasarca), and hyperlipidemia. Crucial nursing considerations: 1) Infection risk is critically elevated because immunoglobulin G (IgG) and complement proteins are lost in urine, and the child is treated with corticosteroids, 2) Daily weights and abdominal girth measurements track ascites, 3) Sodium restriction reduces edema, and 4) Meticulous skin care prevents breakdown of edematous tissues. Why the other options fail: option a adds sodium and fluid to a child who is already anasarcous. Option c imposes immobility, which increases the risk of thrombosis and skin breakdown in oedematous tissue. Option d gives potassium to a child who has not been shown to be hypokalaemic, and the risk here is infection.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Nephrotic Syndrome hallmarks: Massive Proteinuria + Hypoalbuminemia + Severe Edema (anasarca). Priority: Protect from infection (lost antibodies in urine).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) Stage 5 has a serum phosphorus level of 6.8 mg/dL (reference 2.5-4.5 mg/dL) and is prescribed oral calcium acetate (a phosphate binder) 667 mg tablets. Which instruction must the nurse give regarding the timing of this medication?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Take them on an empty stomach at bedtime, with a full glass of water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Take them 2 hours after breakfast, together with the iron supplement"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dissolve the tablets in grapefruit juice an hour before lunch, which improves absorption"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Take the tablets with meals and snacks, so that they bind dietary phosphate in the gut"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Take the tablets with meals and snacks, so that they bind dietary phosphate in the gut" In advanced CKD, failing kidneys cannot excrete phosphorus, causing hyperphosphatemia and secondary hyperparathyroidism. Phosphate binders (calcium acetate, sevelamer) work locally in the gut lumen by binding dietary phosphorus into insoluble, unabsorbable complexes that are excreted in feces. For phosphate binders to be effective, they MUST be taken WITH meals and snacks (during or immediately after eating). If taken on an empty stomach, calcium acetate acts as a systemic calcium supplement without binding phosphorus. Why the other options fail: option a takes the binder on an empty stomach, when there is no dietary phosphate for it to bind. Option b separates it from the meal and adds iron, which it can also bind. Option c dissolves it in juice before food, which achieves the same failure.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Phosphate binder golden rule: Take WITH MEALS AND SNACKS! It must bind the food phosphorus in the gut before it gets absorbed.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-015$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who will receive hemodialysis has a newly created left radiocephalic arteriovenous (AV) fistula. Which nursing assessment verifies that the vascular access is patent and functioning properly?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An absence of pulsation or sound over the anastomosis, which shows the vein is not distended"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A blood pressure of 120/80 measured with a cuff on the fistula arm, which shows flow"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Cool, pale skin with absent radial pulses in the left hand, which shows a steal"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Palpate a palpable thrill and auscultate a continuous blowing bruit over the fistula site"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Palpate a palpable thrill and auscultate a continuous blowing bruit over the fistula site" An arteriovenous fistula surgically anastomoses a high-pressure artery to a low-pressure vein, producing high-velocity turbulent blood flow. Functional patency is confirmed by: 1) Palpating a purring vibration ('thrill'), and 2) Auscultating a continuous whooshing sound ('bruit') over the access site. Absence of a thrill or bruit indicates fistula thrombosis and acute access occlusion, requiring immediate surgical vascular intervention. Why the other options fail: option a describes the absence of flow, which is the sign of a thrombosed fistula. Option b takes a blood pressure on the access arm, which can occlude the fistula and should never be done. Option c describes steal or occlusion, not patency.$q$,
  strategy = $q$AV Fistula patency: 'Feel the thrill, hear the bruit!' No thrill or bruit = clotted access (vascular emergency!). Never take BP or draw blood from fistula arm.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-016$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client performing continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD) at home presents to the renal unit reporting generalized abdominal pain and fever. The nurse inspects the drained peritoneal dialysate effluent bag and notes that the fluid is cloudy, turbid, and yellow, whereas it was previously crystal clear. What is the nurse's priority action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Send the effluent for cell count and culture, notify the nephrologist, and start intraperitoneal antibiotics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Reassure the client that cloudy fluid is expected from dietary protein breakdown"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Warm the dialysate bag in a microwave before the next dwell, to dissolve the sediment"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Clamp the catheter permanently and stop all exchanges for a week, so that the peritoneum can rest and settle by itself"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Send the effluent for cell count and culture, notify the nephrologist, and start intraperitoneal antibiotics" Cloudy or turbid peritoneal dialysate effluent is the earliest and most classic clinical hallmark of bacterial peritonitis in peritoneal dialysis (most commonly caused by touch contamination of the catheter spike during fluid exchanges). Normal dialysate drainage should be completely clear and straw-colored. The nurse must collect fluid for white blood cell count (> 100/mcL with > 50% neutrophils) and culture, and administer intraperitoneal broad-spectrum antibiotics. Why the other options fail: option b treats a sign of infection as normal, which delays treatment of peritonitis. Option c warms the bag in a microwave, which damages the glucose in the dialysate and does not treat infection. Option d stops dialysis altogether, which leaves the infection untreated and the client without clearance.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Peritoneal dialysis red flag: CLOUDY/TURBID effluent drainage + abdominal pain = PERITONITIS. Send fluid for culture and start intraperitoneal antibiotics.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$Two hours into their very first haemodialysis session, a client with a severely elevated baseline blood urea nitrogen (BUN 148 mg/dL) develops a severe throbbing headache, nausea, restlessness, muscle twitching, and visual blurriness. Telemetry reveals sinus bradycardia and blood pressure climbs to 186/104 mmHg. Which life-threatening complication is this client experiencing?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Air embolism through the venous line, which presents with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain"}, {"id": "b", "text": "An acute pyrogenic reaction to endotoxin in the dialysate, with rigors and fever"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome, from rapid solute clearance and cerebral oedema"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Severe hypovolaemic collapse from excessive ultrafiltration during the session"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome, from rapid solute clearance and cerebral oedema" Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome (DDS) occurs primarily during initial hemodialysis sessions in clients with severely elevated BUN. Rapid clearance of urea from the vascular compartment creates a transient osmotic gradient between the blood and brain cells (where urea clears slowly due to the blood-brain barrier). Free water shifts into brain tissue, causing acute cerebral edema and increased ICP (headache, nausea, confusion, seizures, herniation). Management: slow blood flow, stop dialysis, administer hypertonic saline or mannitol. Why the other options fail: option a presents with sudden dyspnoea and chest pain, not headache and hypertension. Option b produces rigors and fever at the start of the session, not focal neurology. Option d produces hypotension, whereas this client is hypertensive and bradycardic.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Dialysis Disequilibrium Syndrome: First-time dialysis + severe BUN -> rapid urea drop -> cerebral edema (headache, nausea, seizures). Prevent by slow initial dialysis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with acute oliguric renal failure has severe fluid overload and is prescribed intravenous furosemide 160 mg. Which administration technique is critical to prevent permanent ototoxicity?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the IV furosemide slowly at a rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer the entire 160 mg dose as a rapid IV push over 15 seconds"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Mix the furosemide in 50 mL of 10% dextrose and infuse over 1 minute"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Combine the furosemide in the same syringe with an aminoglycoside antibiotic"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Administer the IV furosemide slowly at a rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute" High-dose intravenous loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide) can cause permanent sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus, and ototoxicity if administered too rapidly, especially in clients with impaired renal clearance. Safety standards mandate that high-dose intravenous furosemide must be infused slowly at a controlled rate not exceeding 4 mg/minute (160 mg should be infused over at least 40 minutes, either by slow injection or controlled IV piggyback). Why the other options fail: option b pushes a high dose in 15 seconds, which is the fastest way to cause the ototoxicity the item is about. Option c still runs the dose in a minute, which far exceeds 4 mg/min. Option d mixes a loop diuretic with an aminoglycoside in one syringe, which compounds the same toxicity.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Furosemide rate limit: Never exceed 4 mg/min! Rapid bolus causes permanent ototoxicity (tinnitus and deafness), especially in renal impairment.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A physician prescribes 1,000 mL of 0.9% normal saline to be infused intravenously over 8 hours for a dehydrated client. The infusion administration set has a drop factor (calibration) of 15 drops/mL (gtt/mL). At what drop rate in drops per minute (gtt/min) should the nurse regulate the gravity IV infusion?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "21 gtt/min"}, {"id": "b", "text": "31 gtt/min"}, {"id": "c", "text": "42 gtt/min"}, {"id": "d", "text": "125 gtt/min"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "31 gtt/min" Standard IV drop rate formula: Drops per minute (gtt/min) = [Total Volume (mL) x Drop Factor (gtt/mL)] ÷ Total Time (minutes). Calculation: 1) Total volume = 1,000 mL. 2) Drop factor = 15 gtt/mL. 3) Total time = 8 hours x 60 minutes = 480 minutes. 4) (1,000 x 15) ÷ 480 = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25 drops/min, which rounds to 31 drops/minute. Why the other options fail: option a uses a 10 gtt/mL set for the calculation. Option c doubles the volume to 2,000 mL. Option d divides by the drop factor instead of multiplying. The rate follows only from 1,000 mL times 15 gtt/mL over 480 minutes.$q$,
  strategy = $q$IV Drip Rate Formula: (Volume in mL x Drop factor) / Time in minutes. (1,000 x 15) / 480 = 15,000 / 480 = 31 gtt/min.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-RENAL-020$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is performing an admission physical assessment on a client admitted with acute mid-abdominal pain. In which sequence should the nurse execute the physical examination of the abdomen?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Auscultation, inspection, palpation, percussion"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Palpation, percussion, inspection, auscultation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Inspection, auscultation, percussion, palpation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Inspection, auscultation, percussion, palpation" The correct sequence for abdominal assessment is inspection, auscultation, percussion, and palpation (I-A-P-P). Auscultation must precede percussion and palpation because mechanical manipulation, pressure, or percussion of the abdominal wall stimulates peristalsis and artificially alters the frequency and character of bowel sounds, potentially leading to inaccurate diagnostic findings. Why the other options fail: option a palpates before auscultating, and palpation alters the bowel sounds that are then recorded. Option b auscultates before it looks at the abdomen, so the inspection findings are lost. Option c starts with the most intrusive step of all and distorts every sign that follows.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the abdominal assessment rule: Look (inspect), Listen (auscultate), then Feel (percuss and palpate). Never touch or press before listening.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-001$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse auscultates the abdomen of a client on postoperative day 2 following a bowel resection. Before documenting that bowel sounds are completely absent, what standard clinical assessment procedure must the nurse follow?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Listen for 15 seconds in the right lower quadrant only"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Listen continuously over each abdominal quadrant for at least 3 to 5 minutes"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Palpate the right upper quadrant vigorously while listening with the stethoscope bell"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer an oral prokinetic agent and reassess bowel sounds 10 minutes later"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Listen continuously over each abdominal quadrant for at least 3 to 5 minutes" To accurately declare that bowel sounds are absent, the nurse must listen continuously with the diaphragm of the stethoscope for a minimum of 3 to 5 full minutes in each quadrant (or throughout the abdomen). Normal bowel sounds occur irregularly between 5 and 30 times per minute. True absence indicates paralytic ileus or peritonitis. Brief listening (15 seconds) is inadequate to detect hypoactive peristalsis. Why the other options fail: option a listens for 15 seconds in one quadrant, which cannot distinguish hypoactive from absent sounds. Option c palpates while listening, which creates the sounds it is trying to hear. Option d gives a drug before assessing, and a prokinetic changes the baseline the nurse needs.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the examination benchmark: Absent bowel sounds require continuous listening for 3 to 5 continuous minutes before being officially documented.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-002$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client hospitalized with peptic ulcer disease reports sudden, excruciating epigastric pain that rapidly spreads across the entire abdomen. On examination, the client lies motionless with knees drawn up, the abdomen is rigid and board-like to light palpation, and bowel sounds are absent. What is the nurse's priority action?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Maintain strict NPO status, position in low-Fowler with knees flexed, and notify the surgeon at once"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Administer 30 mL of liquid aluminum hydroxide antacid via oral syringe"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a rectal tube to relieve suspected lower bowel flatulent distension"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply a warm heating pad across the epigastrium to alleviate the muscle guarding and to ease the severe pain"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Maintain strict NPO status, position in low-Fowler with knees flexed, and notify the surgeon at once" Sudden severe pain, a rigid board-like abdomen, rebound tenderness, and absent bowel sounds are classical hallmarks of acute gastrointestinal perforation, causing chemical peritonitis from leaking gastric contents. This is a life-threatening surgical emergency. The nurse must immediately maintain strict NPO status, position the client in low-Fowler position with knees flexed (to localize peritonitis to the pelvis and decrease abdominal wall tension), obtain vital signs, and summon the surgeon immediately for emergency laparotomy. Oral antacids and heat applications are contraindicated. Why the other options fail: option b gives an oral agent to a patient who must be NPO for theatre, and antacids do not treat a perforation. Option c inserts a rectal tube into a patient with free air in the abdomen. Option d applies heat to a peritonitic abdomen, which increases perfusion, bleeding and the risk of further perforation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the surgical abdomen: 'Sudden sharp pain + rigid board-like abdomen' = Perforation and peritonitis. First action = NPO + notify surgeon immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-003$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse provides discharge instructions to a client with severe gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and a sliding hiatal hernia. Which lifestyle modification should the nurse instruct the client to adopt?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Consume large meals twice daily to minimize gastric acid secretory cycles"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Raise the head of the bed on 6-inch blocks and stay upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Drink a cup of hot peppermint tea immediately before retiring to bed"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Lie flat on the back for 45 minutes after eating to facilitate gastric emptying into the duodenum"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Raise the head of the bed on 6-inch blocks and stay upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals" Elevating the head of the bed 6 inches on blocks utilizes gravity to prevent acid reflux into the esophagus during sleep. Remaining upright for 2 to 3 hours after meals allows time for gastric emptying and prevents reflux. Peppermint, chocolate, caffeine, alcohol, fatty foods, and smoking reduce lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone and worsen reflux. Lying down flat after meals dramatically increases reflux events. Why the other options fail: option a takes large meals, which raise gastric volume and pressure against a weak sphincter. Option c chooses peppermint, which relaxes the lower oesophageal sphincter and worsens reflux. Option d lies flat after eating, which removes gravity from the antireflux mechanism.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Focus on anti-reflux biomechanics: Elevate the head of bed, remain upright after eating, eat small frequent meals, and avoid LES-relaxing foods (peppermint, chocolate, caffeine).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-004$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who underwent a partial gastrectomy with gastrojejunostomy (Billroth II procedure) 3 weeks ago reports experiencing weakness, dizziness, palpitations, diaphoresis, and explosive abdominal cramping 20 minutes after meals. The nurse recognizes dumping syndrome. Which dietary teaching should the nurse provide?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Eat small, frequent high-protein meals, avoid fluids with meals, and lie down for 20 to 30 minutes after"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Consume high-carbohydrate smoothies with large amounts of refined sugar at mealtime to keep the energy up"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Drink at least 500 mL of iced water during each meal to dilute gastric secretions"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Ambulate briskly around the ward immediately after consuming heavy meals"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Eat small, frequent high-protein meals, avoid fluids with meals, and lie down for 20 to 30 minutes after" Dumping syndrome results from the rapid transit of hyperosmolar chyme into the jejunum, pulling intravascular fluid into the bowel lumen (hypotension, dizziness, cramping) and triggering reactive hypoglycemia. Dietary management includes: (1) small, frequent meals, (2) low-carbohydrate, moderate-to-high protein and complex fat content to slow gastric transit, (3) consuming liquids between meals rather than with meals (at least 30 to 45 minutes apart) to prevent accelerated gastric emptying, and (4) reclining or lying down for 20 to 30 minutes after meals to slow intestinal transit. Why the other options fail: option b loads refined sugar, which increases the osmotic load in the jejunum and provokes the reactive hypoglycaemia. Option c drinks a large volume with the meal, which accelerates gastric emptying. Option d exercises immediately after eating, which speeds transit and worsens the dumping.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the dumping syndrome dietary triad: High protein/fat + Low carbs + No fluids with meals + Recline after meals.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-005$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A hospitalized client receiving broad-spectrum IV antibiotics develops severe watery diarrhea and tests positive for Clostridioides difficile (C. diff). When planning infection control precautions, which practice is essential for the nursing team?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Rely exclusively on alcohol-based hand rub formulations for rapid sanitization between every client contact"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Cleanse hands thoroughly with antimicrobial soap and warm water after removing gloves"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Place the client on airborne precautions in a negative-pressure isolation room"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Disinfect bedside medical equipment using standard quaternary ammonium wipes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Cleanse hands thoroughly with antimicrobial soap and warm water after removing gloves" Clostridioides difficile forms hardy bacterial endospores that are resistant to alcohol-based hand sanitizers. Healthcare personnel must wash hands vigorously with soap and water; the mechanical friction and lather rinse the spores off the skin. C. diff requires Contact Precautions (gown and gloves upon entry, dedicated equipment). Disinfection of patient rooms and equipment must be performed with sporicidal, bleach-based (sodium hypochlorite) agents, not quaternary ammonium wipes. Airborne isolation is unnecessary. Why the other options fail: option a relies on alcohol, which does not kill C. difficile spores. Option c uses airborne precautions when the route is contact. Option d uses a quaternary ammonium wipe, which does not kill spores; the agent has to be a sporicidal bleach.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Remember the C. diff rule: Spores survive alcohol! Soap and water handwashing is mandatory. Clean surfaces with bleach.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-006$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 24-year-old client is hospitalized during the third week of untreated typhoid fever (Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi). The nurse notes a sudden drop in body temperature from 39.8°C (103.6°F) to 36.2°C (97.2°F), accompanied by tachycardia, hypotension, severe abdominal tenderness, and involuntary guarding. Which life-threatening complication must the nurse suspect immediately?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Dehydration from the continuing high fever, corrected by giving oral fluids and continuing the antibiotics"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute cholelithiasis resulting in common bile duct obstruction"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Intestinal perforation of the terminal ileum with developing septic shock"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Constipation from the prolonged bed rest, relieved by an enema"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Intestinal perforation of the terminal ileum with developing septic shock" During the second and third weeks of typhoid fever, hyperplastic Peyer's patches in the terminal ileum ulcerate and may necrose, causing life-threatening intestinal perforation and severe lower GI hemorrhage. A sudden hypothermic drop in temperature, tachycardia, hypotension, and generalized peritoneal signs (tenderness, guarding) in week 3 indicate free bowel perforation and acute septic peritonitis. This is a surgical emergency requiring immediate fluid resuscitation, broad-spectrum IV antibiotics, and emergent exploratory laparotomy. Why the other options fail: option a treats this as dehydration from the fever alone, which does not explain the temperature collapse, the tachycardia or the involuntary guarding, and oral fluid cannot correct a developing septic circulation. Option b is biliary colic, which gives right upper quadrant pain and jaundice rather than a board-like abdomen in the third week of typhoid. Option d is constipation, which never produces hypotension, tachycardia and generalised peritonism.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the classic presentation: Typhoid fever in week 3 with sudden hypothermia, hypotension, and acute abdominal guarding = Intestinal perforation (Peyer's patch necrosis).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-007$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 16-year-old client presents with right lower quadrant abdominal pain localized at McBurney's point, low-grade fever, nausea, and anorexia. The nurse suspects acute appendicitis. Which provider prescription should the nurse question and withhold?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer a warm mineral oil enema to relieve suspected obstipation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Establish an intravenous line for a 0.9% normal saline infusion and run it at the prescribed rate"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Maintain the client on strict NPO status"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Obtain blood for complete blood count and serum electrolytes"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Administer a warm mineral oil enema to relieve suspected obstipation" In suspected acute appendicitis, administering enemas, laxatives, or applying heat to the abdomen is strictly contraindicated because increased intraluminal pressure or hyperemic vasodilation can cause sudden appendix perforation and catastrophic diffuse peritonitis. The nurse must question any prescription for enemas, laxatives, or local heat. Keeping the client NPO, starting IV fluids, and obtaining baseline labs are standard and essential preparatory steps. Why the other options fail: option b is a correct preparatory step, as is option c for keeping the bowel at rest and option d for baseline bloods. The prescription to question is the enema, because it raises intraluminal pressure in an appendix that is already inflamed.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Safety rule in suspected appendicitis: Never give enemas/laxatives, and never apply direct heat (risk of appendix rupture).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-008$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is providing care for a client experiencing an acute flare-up of ulcerative colitis. The client reports passing 12 bloody, mucoid stools per day with severe tenesmus. Which clinical priority should guide the nurse's immediate interventions?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Encourage a high-fibre, high-residue diet with plenty of fresh raw vegetables, to add bulk and slow the stool"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Monitor for hypovolemia, electrolyte depletion (especially hypokalemia), and signs of toxic megacolon"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Administer daily rectal stimulant suppositories to expedite bowel evacuation"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Restrict oral fluid intake to 500 mL per 24 hours to reduce stool frequency"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Monitor for hypovolemia, electrolyte depletion (especially hypokalemia), and signs of toxic megacolon" Severe flare-ups of ulcerative colitis cause extensive mucosal ulceration in the colon and rectum, leading to frequent bloody diarrhea (10–20 stools/day), significant blood loss, dehydration, and severe electrolyte depletion (especially potassium and sodium). Furthermore, severe inflammation can cause paralysis of the colon and rapid dilation (toxic megacolon), predisposing to perforation. The nurse must monitor hydration, electrolytes, hemoglobin, and watch for fever, leukocytosis, and abdominal distension. High-fiber diets exacerbate bowel inflammation during acute flares. Why the other options fail: option a adds bulk and raw fibre to an acutely inflamed colon. Option c uses stimulant suppositories, which increase urgency and mucosal trauma during a flare. Option d restricts fluid in a patient who is losing volume and potassium through 10 to 20 stools a day.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the life-threats of acute colitis flares: Dehydration, hypokalemia, and toxic megacolon (fever, distension, tachycardia).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-009$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client is admitted with an acute mechanical small bowel obstruction (SBO) caused by postoperative adhesions. The client exhibits severe abdominal distension, high-pitched hyperactive bowel sounds with rushes, and persistent bilious emesis. Which initial collaborative intervention is the priority?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer an oral polyethylene glycol bowel preparation to clear the mechanical obstruction from below"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Provide a regular diet with high-protein supplements to prevent catabolism"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Insert a nasogastric (NG) tube and attach to low intermittent suction, and initiate IV fluids"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Administer high-dose subcutaneous loperamide to control vomiting"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Insert a nasogastric (NG) tube and attach to low intermittent suction, and initiate IV fluids" In mechanical small bowel obstruction, fluid and gas accumulate proximal to the blockage, resulting in massive luminal distension, third-spacing, dehydration, vomiting, and risk of bowel ischemia or perforation. Decompression of the stomach and upper GI tract via a nasogastric tube attached to low intermittent suction relieves distension, alleviates nausea/vomiting, and prevents pulmonary aspiration. Intravenous fluid resuscitation corrects hypovolemia. Oral intake, laxatives, and oral bowel preps are contraindicated because they exacerbate luminal pressure against the mechanical blockage. Why the other options fail: option a gives an oral bowel preparation against a mechanical obstruction, which raises the pressure behind the block. Option b feeds a client who cannot pass what is taken. Option d gives an antimotility drug, which retains the distension and the vomiting.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Small bowel obstruction protocol: NPO + NG tube decompression to low intermittent suction + IV fluid rehydration.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-010$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a client with end-stage cirrhosis who has developed hepatic encephalopathy. The client exhibits asterixis (flapping tremors of the hands) and progressive disorientation. Oral lactulose 30 mL three times daily is prescribed. What clinical finding indicates that the lactulose therapy is achieving its desired therapeutic outcome?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "The client develops watery, persistent diarrhea exceeding 8 bowel movements daily"}, {"id": "b", "text": "The client has complete absence of bowel movements for 48 hours"}, {"id": "c", "text": "The client's serum ammonia level increases above 150 micromol/L"}, {"id": "d", "text": "The client passes 2 to 3 soft acidic stools daily and is mentally clearer"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "The client passes 2 to 3 soft acidic stools daily and is mentally clearer" Lactulose is a non-absorbable disaccharide that acidifies the colon (converting absorbable ammonia [NH3] into non-absorbable ammonium [NH4+]), promoting its rapid excretion in stool. The therapeutic goal of lactulose in hepatic encephalopathy is achieving 2 to 3 soft, formed bowel movements per day, leading to reduced serum ammonia levels and improved mental status. Excessive diarrhea (> 4–5 stools/day) causes dehydration and hypokalemia, which worsens encephalopathy. Why the other options fail: option a describes the excessive diarrhoea the dose is meant to avoid, and it causes dehydration and hypokalaemia. Option b has no bowel movement at all, which means the dose is too low. Option c is the ammonia still rising while the patient is under-treated; the goal is a fall, tracked through the stools and the mental state.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Memorize the lactulose target: The goal is 2 to 3 soft stools per day, NOT profuse watery diarrhea. Clearer mentation reflects reduced ammonia.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-011$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client with decompensated alcoholic cirrhosis and known esophageal varices develops massive hematemesis. A Sengstaken-Blakemore balloon tamponade tube is placed by the healthcare provider to control active bleeding. Which item is mandatory for the nurse to keep at the client's bedside at all times?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "An extra liter of sterile water for balloon irrigation"}, {"id": "b", "text": "A bottle of oral activated charcoal"}, {"id": "c", "text": "A tracheostomy dilator tray"}, {"id": "d", "text": "A pair of surgical scissors"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "A pair of surgical scissors" If the gastric balloon of a Sengstaken-Blakemore or Minnesota tube ruptures or accidentally deflates, the esophageal balloon can migrate upward into the oropharynx, causing complete, acute airway obstruction and asphyxiation. The nurse must keep a pair of scissors at the bedside at all times; in the event of acute respiratory distress, the nurse immediately cuts all lumens of the tube to deflate the balloons instantly and pulls the tube out. Why the other options fail: option a adds volume to a balloon that may already be misplaced. Option b treats a poisoning that is not the problem here. Option c prepares for a tracheostomy this patient does not have, and none of the three can deflate a migrated balloon.$q$,
  strategy = $q$High-yield NCLEX safety rule: Sengstaken-Blakemore / Minnesota tube at bedside = SCISSORS kept at bedside to cut lumens immediately if airway obstruction occurs.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-012$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client is admitted to the intensive care unit with acute necrotizing pancreatitis. On examination, the nurse notes bluish discoloration around the umbilicus (Cullen's sign) and in the bilateral flank areas (Turner's sign). What do these clinical assessment findings indicate?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Benign cutaneous hyperpigmentation from elevated bilirubin"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage and extensive pancreatic necrosis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Acute lower urinary tract extravasation of urine"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Superficial bacterial cellulitis of the abdominal subcutaneous tissue"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$b$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (B) — "Severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage and extensive pancreatic necrosis" Cullen's sign (periumbilical ecchymosis) and Turner's (Grey Turner's) sign (flank ecchymosis) represent tracking of blood from retroperitoneal tissue into subcutaneous tissue plane walls. In acute pancreatitis, they are ominous physical signs indicating severe retroperitoneal hemorrhage, tissue necrosis, and high mortality. The client requires immediate aggressive crystalloid fluid resuscitation, strict NPO, intensive hemodynamic monitoring, and surgical evaluation. Why the other options fail: option a reads the bruising as jaundice, which stains yellow rather than purple and does not follow the flank. Option c is urinary extravasation, which gives different signs and no periumbilical bruising. Option d is superficial infection, which gives erythema and warmth rather than ecchymosis.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Recall the eponyms: Cullen's sign (around the 'C'ircle of the belly button) and Turner's sign ('Turn' over to see the flank) = Retroperitoneal bleeding in severe acute pancreatitis.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-013$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 45-year-old client presents to the emergency department reporting acute right upper quadrant pain radiating to the right scapula 1 hour after eating fried chicken. The nurse palpates beneath the right costal margin and asks the client to take a deep breath; the client suddenly stops inhaling due to sharp pain (positive Murphy's sign). Which condition is consistent with this finding?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Acute diverticulitis of the sigmoid colon, with left-sided pain, fever and a recent change in bowel habit"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Acute appendicitis with early perforation, with pain that has migrated to the right iliac fossa"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Viral gastroenteritis with dehydration, with diarrhoea and cramping but no localised tenderness"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Acute cholecystitis with a distended inflamed gallbladder, which arrests inspiration on deep palpation"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "Acute cholecystitis with a distended inflamed gallbladder, which arrests inspiration on deep palpation" A positive Murphy's sign (inspiratory arrest elicited by sharp tenderness when palpating the gallbladder beneath the right costal margin as the diaphragm descends on deep inspiration) is the classic physical diagnostic sign of acute cholecystitis (gallbladder inflammation, usually caused by cystic duct obstruction from gallstones). Pain is typically precipitated by fatty or fried meals (which stimulate cholecystokinin release) and frequently radiates to the right subscapular area or right shoulder. Why the other options fail: option a gives left lower quadrant pain with fever and a change in bowel habit. Option b migrates from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa. Option c gives diarrhoea and cramping without inspiratory arrest on right upper quadrant palpation.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Connect Murphy's sign with location: Murphy's sign in RUQ = Acute Cholecystitis (gallbladder).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-014$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A 62-year-old client presents for a routine check-up. The client's laboratory results show unexplained microcytic, hypochromic anemia (hemoglobin 8.4 g/dL). The client reports feeling fatigued and having narrower, pencil-thin stools over the past 2 months. Which diagnostic screening test should the nurse anticipate the provider ordering first?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Routine upper GI barium swallow series"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD)"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Colonoscopy with tissue biopsy"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Transabdominal pelvic ultrasound"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Colonoscopy with tissue biopsy" In an adult older than 50 years, new-onset iron deficiency anemia, unexplained fatigue, change in bowel habits (such as pencil-thin stools), or rectal bleeding must be treated as colorectal cancer until proven otherwise. A colonoscopy allows direct visualization of the entire large intestine from the rectum to the cecum and enables immediate biopsy of any suspicious lesions or polyp removal. It is the gold standard diagnostic procedure. Why the other options fail: option a images the upper tract with barium, which cannot see the colon and would delay the diagnosis. Option b examines the oesophagus, stomach and duodenum, all above the lesion. Option d images the pelvis and cannot inspect the colonic mucosa or take a biopsy.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Screening red flag: Unexplained iron deficiency anemia + change in bowel caliber/pencil-thin stools in an older adult = Colorectal cancer evaluation via Colonoscopy.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-017$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A client who has been taking high-dose omeprazole (a proton pump inhibitor [PPI]) daily for over 4 years is admitted to the clinic. The nurse should assess the client for which potential long-term adverse complications associated with prolonged acid suppression?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Fractures from osteoporosis, hypomagnesaemia, and C. difficile colitis"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Hypercalcemia, severe hypernatremia, and gouty arthritis"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Hypertension, acute hemolytic anemia, and fluid overload"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, and recurrence of the peptic ulcer the acid was suppressing"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$a$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (A) — "Fractures from osteoporosis, hypomagnesaemia, and C. difficile colitis" Long-term use of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) suppresses gastric acid, which impairs the intestinal absorption of calcium and magnesium, leading to hypomagnesemia and significantly increased risks of osteoporosis and bone fractures (hip, wrist, and spine). Furthermore, the loss of the gastric acid bactericidal barrier allows pathogen colonization, increasing the risk of Clostridioides difficile-associated diarrhea and pneumonia. Clients on long-term PPIs require periodic bone density scans and magnesium level monitoring. Why the other options fail: option b lists hypercalcaemia and gout, neither of which is a PPI effect. Option c describes haemolysis and overload, which belong to transfusion rather than acid suppression. Option d describes thyroid and adrenal failure, which are not on the PPI risk list.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Associate long-term PPI risks: Low acid = low calcium absorption (osteoporosis/fractures) + low magnesium (hypomagnesemia) + lost bacterial barrier (C. diff infection).$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-018$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A nurse is caring for a client receiving intravenous metoclopramide for severe diabetic gastroparesis. While assessing the client, the nurse notes involuntary tongue protrusion, repetitive facial grimacing, and lip-smacking movements. Which action should the nurse take immediately?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "Administer the next scheduled dose on time with a full glass of water"}, {"id": "b", "text": "Instruct the client that these movements are benign and will subside with continued therapy"}, {"id": "c", "text": "Discontinue the metoclopramide and immediately notify the healthcare provider"}, {"id": "d", "text": "Apply warm compresses to the bilateral masseter muscles"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$c$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (C) — "Discontinue the metoclopramide and immediately notify the healthcare provider" Metoclopramide is a central dopamine antagonist that crosses the blood-brain barrier. It carries a Black Box warning for tardive dyskinesia—a serious, often irreversible extrapyramidal movement disorder characterized by involuntary, repetitive movements of the face, tongue, and limbs (facial grimacing, tongue protrusion, lip smacking, rapid blinking). At the earliest sign of tardive dyskinesia, the medication must be stopped immediately and the provider notified. Continued administration risks permanent neurological dysfunction. Why the other options fail: option a continues the drug that is causing the movements. Option b calls a black-box reaction benign, and tardive dyskinesia does not subside with continued exposure. Option d treats the jaw with heat while the cause continues.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Identify the critical side effect: Metoclopramide blocks dopamine. Involuntary facial movements/tongue thrusting = Tardive Dyskinesia! Stop the drug immediately.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-019$q$;

UPDATE questions SET
  stem = $q$A healthcare provider prescribes a continuous intravenous infusion of octreotide at 50 mcg/hour for a client experiencing active bleeding from esophageal varices. The pharmacy delivers an IV infusion bag containing octreotide 500 mcg in 500 mL of 0.9% normal saline. At what rate in milliliters per hour (mL/hr) should the nurse program the electronic infusion pump?$q$,
  choices = $q$[{"id": "a", "text": "25 mL/hr"}, {"id": "b", "text": "100 mL/hr"}, {"id": "c", "text": "10 mL/hr"}, {"id": "d", "text": "50 mL/hr"}]$q$::jsonb,
  correct_choice_id = $q$d$q$,
  rationale = $q$CORRECT (D) — "50 mL/hr" To calculate the infusion rate: Concentration = 500 mcg / 500 mL = 1 mcg/mL. Desired dose = 50 mcg/hr. Infusion rate = (Desired dose / Concentration) = 50 mcg/hr / (1 mcg/mL) = 50 mL/hr. Octreotide (a somatostatin analog) reduces portal venous pressure through selective splanchnic vasoconstriction, controlling active variceal hemorrhage. Why the other options fail: option a halves the dose, giving 25 mcg/hour. Option b doubles it to 100 mcg/hour. Option c delivers 10 mcg/hour, one fifth of the order. The pump has to run at 50 mL/hour to give 50 mcg/hour at 1 mcg/mL.$q$,
  strategy = $q$Calculate step-by-step: 500 mcg in 500 mL means 1 mcg = 1 mL. For 50 mcg/hr, infuse 50 mL/hr.$q$
WHERE authored_id = $q$NG-MOD-GI-020$q$;

-- Verify: expect 37
COMMIT;

-- >>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>  question-fixes-batch137.sql  <<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<
